ations"}},{"k":"system_explorer","v":{"s":"System Explorer"}},{"k":"explore_3_different_body_systems","v":{"s":"Explore 3 different body systems"}},{"k":"spotter_pro","v":{"s":"Spotter Pro"}},{"k":"identify_3_structures_in_the_spotter_t","v":{"s":"Identify 3 structures in the Spotter Test"}},{"k":"clinical_intern","v":{"s":"Clinical Reviewer"}},{"k":"solve_2_clinical_cases","v":{"s":"Review 2 clinical cases"}},{"k":"pathway_explorer","v":{"s":"Pathway Explorer"}},{"k":"complete_2_physiological_pathways","v":{"s":"Complete 2 physiological pathways"}},{"k":"memory_master","v":{"s":"Memory Master"}},{"k":"unlock_3_anatomical_mnemonics","v":{"s":"Unlock 3 anatomical mnemonics"}},{"k":"comparative_anatomist","v":{"s":"Comparative Anatomist"}},{"k":"compare_3_pairs_of_structures","v":{"s":"Compare 3 pairs of structures"}},{"k":"challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Challenge Complete!"}},{"k":"skeletal","v":{"s":"Skeletal"}},{"k":"206_bones_support_protection_movement_","v":{"s":"206 bones — support, protection, movement, mineral storage, hematopoiesis."}},{"k":"skull_cranium","v":{"s":"Skull (Cranium)"}},{"k":"houses_meninges_brain_and_cranial_nerv","v":{"s":"Houses meninges, brain, and cranial nerve foramina. Fontanelles close during infancy and early childhood; major cranial sutures normally remain into adulthood and fuse much later, if at all."}},{"k":"mandible","v":{"s":"Mandible"}},{"k":"clavicle","v":{"s":"Clavicle"}},{"k":"sternum","v":{"s":"Sternum"}},{"k":"ribs_1_12","v":{"s":"Ribs (1-12)"}},{"k":"scapula","v":{"s":"Scapula"}},{"k":"humerus","v":{"s":"Humerus"}},{"k":"radius","v":{"s":"Radius"}},{"k":"ulna","v":{"s":"Ulna"}},{"k":"carpals","v":{"s":"Carpals"}},{"k":"vertebral_column","v":{"s":"Vertebral Column"}},{"k":"pelvis","v":{"s":"Pelvis"}},{"k":"femur","v":{"s":"Femur"}},{"k":"patella","v":{"s":"Patella"}},{"k":"tibia","v":{"s":"Tibia"}},{"k":"fibula","v":{"s":"Fibula"}},{"k":"tarsals","v":{"s":"Tarsals"}},{"k":"sacrum_coccyx","v":{"s":"Sacrum & Coccyx"}},{"k":"hyoid_bone","v":{"s":"Hyoid Bone"}},{"k":"atlas_c1_axis_c2","v":{"s":"Atlas (C1) & Axis (C2)"}},{"k":"metatarsals_phalanges_foot","v":{"s":"Metatarsals & Phalanges (Foot)"}},{"k":"metacarpals_phalanges_hand","v":{"s":"Metacarpals & Phalanges (Hand)"}},{"k":"scaphoid_bone","v":{"s":"Scaphoid Bone"}},{"k":"muscular","v":{"s":"Muscular"}},{"k":"600_muscles_movement_posture_heat_prod","v":{"s":"600+ muscles — movement, posture, heat production, joint stabilization."}},{"k":"deltoid","v":{"s":"Deltoid"}},{"k":"pectoralis_major","v":{"s":"Pectoralis Major"}},{"k":"biceps_brachii","v":{"s":"Biceps Brachii"}},{"k":"triceps_brachii","v":{"s":"Triceps Brachii"}},{"k":"rectus_abdominis","v":{"s":"Rectus Abdominis"}},{"k":"external_obliques","v":{"s":"External Obliques"}},{"k":"quadriceps_femoris","v":{"s":"Quadriceps Femoris"}},{"k":"hamstrings","v":{"s":"Hamstrings"}},{"k":"gastrocnemius","v":{"s":"Gastrocnemius"}},{"k":"trapezius","v":{"s":"Trapezius"}},{"k":"latissimus_dorsi","v":{"s":"Latissimus Dorsi"}},{"k":"gluteus_maximus","v":{"s":"Gluteus Maximus"}},{"k":"sartorius","v":{"s":"Sartorius"}},{"k":"tibialis_anterior","v":{"s":"Tibialis Anterior"}},{"k":"soleus","v":{"s":"Soleus"}},{"k":"rotator_cuff_sits","v":{"s":"Rotator Cuff (SITS)"}},{"k":"iliopsoas","v":{"s":"Iliopsoas"}},{"k":"intercostal_muscles","v":{"s":"Intercostal Muscles"}},{"k":"pelvic_floor_levator_ani","v":{"s":"Pelvic Floor (Levator Ani)"}},{"k":"diaphragm_muscle","v":{"s":"Diaphragm (Muscle)"}},{"k":"circulatory","v":{"s":"Circulatory"}},{"k":"heart_60_000_miles_of_vessels_5l_of_bl","v":{"s":"Heart, 60,000 miles of vessels, 5L of blood — delivers O₂, nutrients, hormones; removes waste."}},{"k":"heart","v":{"s":"Heart"}},{"k":"aorta","v":{"s":"Aorta"}},{"k":"superior_vena_cava","v":{"s":"Superior Vena Cava"}},{"k":"inferior_vena_cava","v":{"s":"Inferior Vena Cava"}},{"k":"pulmonary_arteries","v":{"s":"Pulmonary Arteries"}},{"k":"carotid_arteries","v":{"s":"Carotid Arteries"}},{"k":"jugular_veins","v":{"s":"Jugular Veins"}},{"k":"coronary_arteries","v":{"s":"Coronary Arteries"}},{"k":"femoral_artery","v":{"s":"Femoral Artery"}},{"k":"brachial_artery","v":{"s":"Brachial Artery"}},{"k":"hepatic_portal_vein","v":{"s":"Hepatic Portal Vein"}},{"k":"circle_of_willis","v":{"s":"Circle of Willis"}},{"k":"great_saphenous_vein","v":{"s":"Great Saphenous Vein"}},{"k":"lymphatic_vessels","v":{"s":"Lymphatic Vessels"}},{"k":"nervous","v":{"s":"Nervous"}},{"k":"cns_brain_spinal_cord_and_pns_31_spina","v":{"s":"CNS (brain + spinal cord) and PNS (31 spinal nerve pairs, 12 cranial nerve pairs, autonomic NS)."}},{"k":"brain","v":{"s":"Brain"}},{"k":"lightest_sleep_transition_from_wakeful","v":{"s":"Lightest sleep — transition from wakefulness. Slow rolling eye movements, muscle tone decreasing. Hypnic jerks (myoclonic twitches) common. Easily aroused. Vertex sharp waves appear on EEG."}},{"k":"true_sleep_begins_core_body_temperatur","v":{"s":"True sleep begins. Core body temperature drops, heart rate slows. Sleep spindles (thalamocortical bursts) protect sleep and consolidate motor memory. K-complexes are large waveforms responding to external stimuli."}},{"k":"deep_slow_wave_sleep_sws_most_restorat","v":{"s":"Deep/slow-wave sleep (SWS). Most restorative stage. Growth hormone secretion peaks. Glymphatic system clears brain waste (amyloid-β). Declarative memory consolidation occurs. Hardest to awaken from; sleep inertia if aroused."}},{"k":"dreaming_stage_brain_is_highly_active_","v":{"s":"Dreaming stage. Brain is highly active (similar to waking) but body is paralyzed (atonia via brainstem inhibition of motor neurons). Rapid conjugate eye movements. Emotional memory processing and creative problem-solving. Cycles lengthen through the night."}},{"k":"cerebral_cortex_4_lobes","v":{"s":"Cerebral Cortex (4 Lobes)"}},{"k":"brodmann_areas_map_52_cytoarchitectura","v":{"s":"Brodmann areas map 52 cytoarchitectural regions. Primary motor cortex (area 4), primary somatosensory (areas 3,1,2), primary visual (area 17), primary auditory (areas 41,42). Prefrontal cortex is last to myelinate (age ~25)."}},{"k":"cerebellum","v":{"s":"Cerebellum"}},{"k":"purkinje_cells_are_among_the_largest_n","v":{"s":"Purkinje cells are among the largest neurons, each receiving ~200,000 synaptic inputs. Cerebellar cortex has 3 layers: molecular, Purkinje, granular. Emerging research shows roles in cognition, emotion, and language."}},{"k":"brainstem_midbrain_pons_medulla","v":{"s":"Brainstem (Midbrain, Pons, Medulla)"}},{"k":"contains_nuclei_for_cranial_nerves_iii","v":{"s":"Contains nuclei for cranial nerves III–XII. Reticular activating system (RAS) is the brain's \"on switch\" — damage causes coma. Decussation of pyramids in medulla: motor crossover explains contralateral motor control."}},{"k":"hippocampus","v":{"s":"Hippocampus"}},{"k":"memory_replay_during_sleep_hippocampal","v":{"s":"Memory replay during sleep: hippocampal neurons \"replay\" daytime experiences during N3 sleep, transferring memories to neocortex. Grid cells in entorhinal cortex provide spatial coordinates to hippocampal place cells."}},{"k":"amygdala","v":{"s":"Amygdala"}},{"k":"fear_conditioning_pathway_auditory_cor","v":{"s":"Fear conditioning pathway: auditory cortex → lateral amygdala → central amygdala → hypothalamus (stress response) + PAG (freezing). Amygdala-prefrontal interactions allow emotional regulation — basis of CBT therapy."}},{"k":"thalamus","v":{"s":"Thalamus"}},{"k":"key_nuclei_vpl_vpm_somatosensory_relay","v":{"s":"Key nuclei: VPL/VPM (somatosensory relay), LGN (visual relay to V1), MGN (auditory relay to A1), pulvinar (attention), anterior nuclear group (memory, Papez circuit)."}},{"k":"hypothalamus","v":{"s":"Hypothalamus"}},{"k":"thermostat_analogy_anterior_hypothalam","v":{"s":"Thermostat analogy: anterior hypothalamus triggers cooling (vasodilation, sweating); posterior hypothalamus triggers warming (shivering, vasoconstriction). Fever = elevated set point by prostaglandins from infection."}},{"k":"corpus_callosum","v":{"s":"Corpus Callosum"}},{"k":"split_brain_experiments_revealed_hemis","v":{"s":"Split-brain experiments revealed hemispheric specialization: left hemisphere = language, logic; right hemisphere = spatial, holistic processing. Information presented to one visual field is processed by contralateral hemisphere."}},{"k":"basal_ganglia","v":{"s":"Basal Ganglia"}},{"k":"dopamine_from_substantia_nigra_modulat","v":{"s":"Dopamine from substantia nigra modulates the direct (D1, excitatory) and indirect (D2, inhibitory) pathways. Loss of this modulation in Parkinson creates the characteristic difficulty initiating movement."}},{"k":"spinal_cord","v":{"s":"Spinal Cord"}},{"k":"vagus_nerve_cn_x","v":{"s":"Vagus Nerve (CN X)"}},{"k":"sciatic_nerve","v":{"s":"Sciatic Nerve"}},{"k":"brachial_plexus","v":{"s":"Brachial Plexus"}},{"k":"median_nerve","v":{"s":"Median Nerve"}},{"k":"ulnar_nerve","v":{"s":"Ulnar Nerve"}},{"k":"femoral_nerve","v":{"s":"Femoral Nerve"}},{"k":"sympathetic_chain","v":{"s":"Sympathetic Chain"}},{"k":"cranial_nerves_i_xii","v":{"s":"Cranial Nerves (I-XII)"}},{"k":"lymphatic","v":{"s":"Lymphatic"}},{"k":"returns_interstitial_fluid_absorbs_die","v":{"s":"Returns interstitial fluid, absorbs dietary fat, immune surveillance — 600–700 lymph nodes, thymus, spleen."}},{"k":"thymus","v":{"s":"Thymus"}},{"k":"spleen","v":{"s":"Spleen"}},{"k":"tonsils_waldeyer_ring","v":{"s":"Tonsils (Waldeyer Ring)"}},{"k":"cervical_lymph_nodes","v":{"s":"Cervical Lymph Nodes"}},{"k":"axillary_lymph_nodes","v":{"s":"Axillary Lymph Nodes"}},{"k":"inguinal_lymph_nodes","v":{"s":"Inguinal Lymph Nodes"}},{"k":"thoracic_duct","v":{"s":"Thoracic Duct"}},{"k":"bone_marrow","v":{"s":"Bone Marrow"}},{"k":"organ_systems","v":{"s":"Organ Systems"}},{"k":"major_visceral_organs_respiration_dige","v":{"s":"Major visceral organs — respiration, digestion, filtration, endocrine regulation."}},{"k":"lungs","v":{"s":"Lungs"}},{"k":"liver","v":{"s":"Liver"}},{"k":"stomach","v":{"s":"Stomach"}},{"k":"kidneys","v":{"s":"Kidneys"}},{"k":"small_intestine","v":{"s":"Small Intestine"}},{"k":"large_intestine","v":{"s":"Large Intestine"}},{"k":"pancreas","v":{"s":"Pancreas"}},{"k":"gallbladder","v":{"s":"Gallbladder"}},{"k":"urinary_bladder","v":{"s":"Urinary Bladder"}},{"k":"diaphragm","v":{"s":"Diaphragm"}},{"k":"thyroid_gland","v":{"s":"Thyroid Gland"}},{"k":"adrenal_glands","v":{"s":"Adrenal Glands"}},{"k":"integumentary","v":{"s":"Integumentary"}},{"k":"skin_largest_organ_2m_hair_nails_gland","v":{"s":"Skin (largest organ, ~2m²), hair, nails, glands — barrier, thermoregulation, sensation, vitamin D synthesis."}},{"k":"epidermis","v":{"s":"Epidermis"}},{"k":"dermis","v":{"s":"Dermis"}},{"k":"hypodermis_subcutaneous","v":{"s":"Hypodermis (Subcutaneous)"}},{"k":"hair_follicles","v":{"s":"Hair Follicles"}},{"k":"sweat_glands","v":{"s":"Sweat Glands"}},{"k":"sebaceous_glands","v":{"s":"Sebaceous Glands"}},{"k":"nails","v":{"s":"Nails"}},{"k":"melanocytes_pigmentation","v":{"s":"Melanocytes & Pigmentation"}},{"k":"respiratory","v":{"s":"Respiratory"}},{"k":"oxygen_delivery_and_co_removal_12_20_b","v":{"s":"Oxygen delivery and CO₂ removal — ~12–20 breaths/min, ~6L air/min, 300 million alveoli."}},{"k":"nasal_cavity_sinuses","v":{"s":"Nasal Cavity & Sinuses"}},{"k":"pharynx","v":{"s":"Pharynx"}},{"k":"larynx_voice_box","v":{"s":"Larynx (Voice Box)"}},{"k":"trachea","v":{"s":"Trachea"}},{"k":"bronchial_tree","v":{"s":"Bronchial Tree"}},{"k":"alveoli","v":{"s":"Alveoli"}},{"k":"pleura","v":{"s":"Pleura"}},{"k":"respiratory_muscles","v":{"s":"Respiratory Muscles"}},{"k":"endocrine","v":{"s":"Endocrine"}},{"k":"hormone_producing_glands_regulate_meta","v":{"s":"Hormone-producing glands — regulate metabolism, growth, reproduction, stress, homeostasis via chemical messengers."}},{"k":"pituitary_gland_hypophysis","v":{"s":"Pituitary Gland (Hypophysis)"}},{"k":"pineal_gland","v":{"s":"Pineal Gland"}},{"k":"parathyroid_glands","v":{"s":"Parathyroid Glands"}},{"k":"islets_of_langerhans","v":{"s":"Islets of Langerhans"}},{"k":"ovaries_endocrine","v":{"s":"Ovaries (Endocrine)"}},{"k":"testes_endocrine","v":{"s":"Testes (Endocrine)"}},{"k":"hypothalamic_pituitary_axis","v":{"s":"Hypothalamic-Pituitary Axis"}},{"k":"adrenal_cortex_zones","v":{"s":"Adrenal Cortex Zones"}},{"k":"reproductive","v":{"s":"Reproductive"}},{"k":"male_and_female_reproductive_organs_ga","v":{"s":"Male and female reproductive organs — gamete production, fertilization, fetal development, hormonal regulation."}},{"k":"testes","v":{"s":"Testes"}},{"k":"epididymis","v":{"s":"Epididymis"}},{"k":"prostate_gland","v":{"s":"Prostate Gland"}},{"k":"uterus","v":{"s":"Uterus"}},{"k":"ovaries","v":{"s":"Ovaries"}},{"k":"fallopian_tubes_oviducts","v":{"s":"Fallopian Tubes (Oviducts)"}},{"k":"mammary_glands","v":{"s":"Mammary Glands"}},{"k":"placenta","v":{"s":"Placenta"}},{"k":"gas_exchange","v":{"s":"Gas Exchange"}},{"k":"the_circulatory_system_delivers_deoxyg","v":{"s":"The circulatory system delivers deoxygenated blood to the lungs, where the respiratory system loads it with oxygen and offloads carbon dioxide across the thin alveolar-capillary membrane."}},{"k":"neuromuscular_junction","v":{"s":"Neuromuscular Junction"}},{"k":"motor_neurons_from_the_nervous_system_","v":{"s":"Motor neurons from the nervous system release acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, triggering muscle fiber contraction. Without neural signals, muscles cannot move."}},{"k":"lever_system_for_movement","v":{"s":"Lever System for Movement"}},{"k":"muscles_attach_to_bones_via_tendons_an","v":{"s":"Muscles attach to bones via tendons and pull across joints, creating lever systems. The skeleton provides rigid levers; muscles provide the pulling force."}},{"k":"hormonal_regulation_of_reproduction","v":{"s":"Hormonal Regulation of Reproduction"}},{"k":"the_hypothalamus_pituitary_axis_releas","v":{"s":"The hypothalamus-pituitary axis releases FSH and LH that regulate the gonads. Estrogen, progesterone, and testosterone from reproductive organs feedback to the endocrine system."}},{"k":"immune_defense_and_fluid_balance","v":{"s":"Immune Defense and Fluid Balance"}},{"k":"the_lymphatic_system_returns_interstit","v":{"s":"The lymphatic system returns interstitial fluid to the bloodstream and deploys immune cells made in lymphoid organs. Both systems maintain fluid homeostasis and fight infection."}},{"k":"hypothalamic_pituitary_axis_2","v":{"s":"Hypothalamic-Pituitary Axis"}},{"k":"the_hypothalamus_bridges_the_nervous_a","v":{"s":"The hypothalamus bridges the nervous and endocrine systems — it integrates neural signals and translates them into hormonal commands that control the pituitary gland and all downstream hormone cascades."}},{"k":"breathing_mechanics","v":{"s":"Breathing Mechanics"}},{"k":"the_diaphragm_and_intercostal_muscles_","v":{"s":"The diaphragm and intercostal muscles physically expand and compress the thoracic cavity to move air. Lungs have no muscle themselves and rely entirely on surrounding muscles."}},{"k":"sensory_receptors_in_skin","v":{"s":"Sensory Receptors in Skin"}},{"k":"the_skin_contains_millions_of_speciali","v":{"s":"The skin contains millions of specialized nerve endings and encapsulated receptors that detect touch, pressure, temperature, and pain, feeding constant sensory data to the nervous system."}},{"k":"portal_circulation_and_nutrient_proces","v":{"s":"Portal Circulation and Nutrient Processing"}},{"k":"blood_from_the_gi_tract_drains_through","v":{"s":"Blood from the GI tract drains through the hepatic portal vein directly to the liver before entering general circulation, allowing the liver to process nutrients and detoxify substances first."}},{"k":"bone_marrow_blood_cell_production","v":{"s":"Bone Marrow Blood Cell Production"}},{"k":"red_bone_marrow_inside_the_skeleton_is","v":{"s":"Red bone marrow inside the skeleton is the birthplace of all blood cells. Red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets are all produced here through hematopoiesis."}},{"k":"the_skull","v":{"s":"The Skull"}},{"k":"the_vertebral_column","v":{"s":"The Vertebral Column"}},{"k":"the_rib_cage","v":{"s":"The Rib Cage"}},{"k":"the_femur","v":{"s":"The Femur"}},{"k":"the_pelvis","v":{"s":"The Pelvis"}},{"k":"the_diaphragm","v":{"s":"The Diaphragm"}},{"k":"the_deltoid","v":{"s":"The Deltoid"}},{"k":"rectus_abdominis_2","v":{"s":"Rectus Abdominis"}},{"k":"the_quadriceps","v":{"s":"The Quadriceps"}},{"k":"the_gastrocnemius","v":{"s":"The Gastrocnemius"}},{"k":"the_heart","v":{"s":"The Heart"}},{"k":"the_aorta","v":{"s":"The Aorta"}},{"k":"coronary_arteries_2","v":{"s":"Coronary Arteries"}},{"k":"the_carotid_arteries","v":{"s":"The Carotid Arteries"}},{"k":"the_brain","v":{"s":"The Brain"}},{"k":"the_cerebral_cortex","v":{"s":"The Cerebral Cortex"}},{"k":"the_spinal_cord","v":{"s":"The Spinal Cord"}},{"k":"the_vagus_nerve","v":{"s":"The Vagus Nerve"}},{"k":"the_thymus","v":{"s":"The Thymus"}},{"k":"the_spleen","v":{"s":"The Spleen"}},{"k":"cervical_lymph_nodes_2","v":{"s":"Cervical Lymph Nodes"}},{"k":"bone_marrow_2","v":{"s":"Bone Marrow"}},{"k":"the_lungs","v":{"s":"The Lungs"}},{"k":"the_liver","v":{"s":"The Liver"}},{"k":"the_stomach","v":{"s":"The Stomach"}},{"k":"the_kidneys","v":{"s":"The Kidneys"}},{"k":"the_epidermis","v":{"s":"The Epidermis"}},{"k":"the_dermis","v":{"s":"The Dermis"}},{"k":"hair_follicles_2","v":{"s":"Hair Follicles"}},{"k":"melanocytes","v":{"s":"Melanocytes"}},{"k":"nasal_cavity","v":{"s":"Nasal Cavity"}},{"k":"the_larynx","v":{"s":"The Larynx"}},{"k":"the_bronchial_tree","v":{"s":"The Bronchial Tree"}},{"k":"the_alveoli","v":{"s":"The Alveoli"}},{"k":"the_pituitary_gland","v":{"s":"The Pituitary Gland"}},{"k":"the_thyroid","v":{"s":"The Thyroid"}},{"k":"adrenal_cortex","v":{"s":"Adrenal Cortex"}},{"k":"islets_of_langerhans_2","v":{"s":"Islets of Langerhans"}},{"k":"the_testes","v":{"s":"The Testes"}},{"k":"the_uterus","v":{"s":"The Uterus"}},{"k":"the_ovaries","v":{"s":"The Ovaries"}},{"k":"the_placenta","v":{"s":"The Placenta"}},{"k":"the_runner_s_knee","v":{"s":"The Runner's Knee"}},{"k":"which_structure_is_most_likely_affecte","v":{"s":"Which structure is most likely affected?"}},{"k":"patella_patellofemoral_joint","v":{"s":"Patella / patellofemoral joint"}},{"k":"patellofemoral_pain_syndrome_occurs_wh","v":{"s":"Patellofemoral pain syndrome occurs when the patella does not track smoothly in its groove on the femur. Repeated stress from running causes cartilage irritation. Treatment includes quad strengthening, hip stabilization, and activity modification."}},{"k":"the_shoulder_that_won_t_lift","v":{"s":"The Shoulder That Won't Lift"}},{"k":"which_structure_is_most_likely_torn","v":{"s":"Which structure is most likely torn?"}},{"k":"supraspinatus_tendon_rotator_cuff","v":{"s":"Supraspinatus tendon (rotator cuff)"}},{"k":"the_supraspinatus_is_the_most_commonly","v":{"s":"The supraspinatus is the most commonly torn rotator cuff muscle. It runs under the acromion where it is susceptible to impingement and tears. Overhead work like painting increases this risk significantly."}},{"k":"racing_heart_after_exercise","v":{"s":"Racing Heart After Exercise"}},{"k":"which_structure_controls_the_normal_he","v":{"s":"Which structure controls the normal heart rhythm?"}},{"k":"sinoatrial_sa_node","v":{"s":"Sinoatrial (SA) node"}},{"k":"the_sa_node_in_the_right_atrium_is_the","v":{"s":"The SA node in the right atrium is the heart's natural pacemaker. During intense exercise, adrenaline can cause benign palpitations as the heart rate adjusts. Persistent arrhythmias should be evaluated to rule out structural heart disease."}},{"k":"the_numb_hand","v":{"s":"The Numb Hand"}},{"k":"which_nerve_is_being_compressed","v":{"s":"Which nerve is being compressed?"}},{"k":"median_nerve_carpal_tunnel_syndrome","v":{"s":"Median nerve (carpal tunnel syndrome)"}},{"k":"carpal_tunnel_syndrome_is_compression_","v":{"s":"Carpal tunnel syndrome is compression of the median nerve under the flexor retinaculum at the wrist. The median nerve supplies sensation to the thumb and first 3.5 fingers. Repetitive wrist use is a major risk factor."}},{"k":"the_swollen_neck_node","v":{"s":"The Swollen Neck Node"}},{"k":"what_diagnosis_must_be_urgently_ruled_","v":{"s":"What diagnosis must be urgently ruled out?"}},{"k":"lymphoma_hodgkin_lymphoma","v":{"s":"Lymphoma (Hodgkin lymphoma)"}},{"k":"painless_lymphadenopathy_with_b_sympto","v":{"s":"Painless lymphadenopathy with B-symptoms (night sweats, weight loss, fever) is the classic presentation of Hodgkin lymphoma in young adults. Biopsy showing Reed-Sternberg cells confirms the diagnosis."}},{"k":"the_diabetic_emergency","v":{"s":"The Diabetic Emergency"}},{"k":"which_cells_failed_and_what_is_the_eme","v":{"s":"Which cells failed, and what is the emergency condition?"}},{"k":"beta_cells_of_islets_of_langerhans_dia","v":{"s":"Beta cells of the pancreatic islets; diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)"}},{"k":"without_insulin_from_beta_cells_glucos","v":{"s":"With too little insulin, many tissues cannot use glucose normally and the liver increases ketone production from fat. Ketones accumulate and acidify the blood; deep Kussmaul breathing helps lower carbon dioxide. DKA is a medical emergency treated with fluids, insulin, electrolyte monitoring, and correction of the trigger."}},{"k":"the_broken_collarbone","v":{"s":"The Broken Collarbone"}},{"k":"why_is_the_middle_third_of_the_clavicl","v":{"s":"Why is the middle third of the clavicle the most common fracture site?"}},{"k":"the_middle_third_is_thinnest_and_has_n","v":{"s":"The middle third is thinnest and has no muscular reinforcement"}},{"k":"the_clavicle_is_the_most_frequently_fr","v":{"s":"The clavicle is the most frequently fractured bone. Its middle third is thinnest and lacks muscular protection. Force from a fall on an outstretched hand concentrates at this weak point. Most heal with sling immobilization."}},{"k":"breathless_at_high_altitude","v":{"s":"Breathless at High Altitude"}},{"k":"why_does_altitude_cause_these_symptoms","v":{"s":"Why does altitude cause these symptoms, and which structure is most stressed?"}},{"k":"the_alveoli_and_respiratory_muscles_re","v":{"s":"The alveoli and respiratory muscles; reduced atmospheric oxygen causes hypoxia"}},{"k":"at_high_altitude_atmospheric_pressure_","v":{"s":"At high altitude, atmospheric pressure drops, reducing the partial pressure of oxygen. Less oxygen crosses the alveolar membrane. The body compensates by breathing faster and deeper, increasing respiratory muscle work."}},{"k":"carpal_bones_proximal_to_distal","v":{"s":"Carpal Bones (Proximal to Distal)"}},{"k":"cranial_bones","v":{"s":"Cranial Bones"}},{"k":"vertebral_count","v":{"s":"Vertebral Count"}},{"k":"rotator_cuff_muscles","v":{"s":"Rotator Cuff Muscles"}},{"k":"erector_spinae_lateral_to_medial","v":{"s":"Erector Spinae (Lateral to Medial)"}},{"k":"quadriceps_muscles","v":{"s":"Quadriceps Muscles"}},{"k":"heart_valve_order_flow","v":{"s":"Heart Valve Order (Flow)"}},{"k":"aortic_arch_branches","v":{"s":"Aortic Arch Branches"}},{"k":"12_cranial_nerves","v":{"s":"12 Cranial Nerves"}},{"k":"brachial_plexus_2","v":{"s":"Brachial Plexus"}},{"k":"immune_cell_types","v":{"s":"Immune Cell Types"}},{"k":"liver_segments","v":{"s":"Liver Segments"}},{"k":"layers_of_gi_wall","v":{"s":"Layers of GI Wall"}},{"k":"epidermis_layers_superficial_to_deep","v":{"s":"Epidermis Layers (Superficial to Deep)"}},{"k":"structures_air_passes_through","v":{"s":"Structures Air Passes Through"}},{"k":"anterior_pituitary_hormones","v":{"s":"Anterior Pituitary Hormones"}},{"k":"stages_of_meiosis_i","v":{"s":"Stages of Meiosis I"}},{"k":"path_of_blood","v":{"s":"Path of Blood"}},{"k":"follow_a_red_blood_cell_through_the_en","v":{"s":"Follow a red blood cell through the entire circulatory system."}},{"k":"right_atrium","v":{"s":"Right Atrium"}},{"k":"deoxygenated_blood_from_the_body_enter","v":{"s":"Deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium via the superior and inferior vena cava."}},{"k":"right_ventricle","v":{"s":"Right Ventricle"}},{"k":"blood_passes_through_the_tricuspid_val","v":{"s":"Blood passes through the tricuspid valve into the right ventricle, which pumps it toward the lungs."}},{"k":"pulmonary_arteries_2","v":{"s":"Pulmonary Arteries"}},{"k":"the_right_ventricle_ejects_blood_throu","v":{"s":"The right ventricle ejects blood through the pulmonary valve into the pulmonary arteries toward both lungs."}},{"k":"lung_capillaries","v":{"s":"Lung Capillaries"}},{"k":"in_the_alveolar_capillaries_co2_is_rel","v":{"s":"In the alveolar capillaries, CO2 is released and O2 is picked up through the thin alveolar membrane."}},{"k":"pulmonary_veins","v":{"s":"Pulmonary Veins"}},{"k":"freshly_oxygenated_blood_returns_to_th","v":{"s":"Freshly oxygenated blood returns to the heart via the four pulmonary veins."}},{"k":"left_atrium","v":{"s":"Left Atrium"}},{"k":"the_four_pulmonary_veins_empty_oxygena","v":{"s":"The four pulmonary veins empty oxygenated blood into the left atrium, which fills and then pushes it through the mitral valve."}},{"k":"left_ventricle","v":{"s":"Left Ventricle"}},{"k":"blood_passes_through_the_mitral_valve_","v":{"s":"Blood passes through the mitral valve into the muscular left ventricle, the strongest heart chamber."}},{"k":"aorta_2","v":{"s":"Aorta"}},{"k":"the_left_ventricle_powerfully_ejects_b","v":{"s":"The left ventricle powerfully ejects blood through the aortic valve into the aorta, the body's main highway."}},{"k":"body_tissues","v":{"s":"Body Tissues"}},{"k":"arteries_branch_into_arterioles_and_ca","v":{"s":"Arteries branch into arterioles and capillaries where O2 and nutrients are delivered and CO2/waste collected."}},{"k":"venous_return","v":{"s":"Venous Return"}},{"k":"deoxygenated_blood_returns_through_ven","v":{"s":"Deoxygenated blood returns through venules and veins, assisted by muscle pumps and valves, back to the right atrium."}},{"k":"path_of_air","v":{"s":"Path of Air"}},{"k":"trace_the_journey_of_a_breath_of_air_f","v":{"s":"Trace the journey of a breath of air from nose to alveoli and back."}},{"k":"nasal_cavity_2","v":{"s":"Nasal Cavity"}},{"k":"air_enters_through_the_nostrils_and_is","v":{"s":"Air enters through the nostrils and is warmed, humidified, and filtered by mucous membranes and turbinates."}},{"k":"pharynx_2","v":{"s":"Pharynx"}},{"k":"air_passes_through_the_nasopharynx_and","v":{"s":"Air passes through the nasopharynx and oropharynx, a shared passage with the digestive tract."}},{"k":"larynx","v":{"s":"Larynx"}},{"k":"air_crosses_the_vocal_cords_in_the_voi","v":{"s":"Air crosses the vocal cords in the voice box. The epiglottis guards against food entering the airway."}},{"k":"trachea_2","v":{"s":"Trachea"}},{"k":"the_trachea_windpipe_is_reinforced_by_","v":{"s":"The trachea (windpipe) is reinforced by C-shaped cartilage rings. Cilia move mucus upward to trap debris."}},{"k":"bronchi","v":{"s":"Bronchi"}},{"k":"the_trachea_splits_into_left_and_right","v":{"s":"The trachea splits into left and right main bronchi, each entering a lung. They branch into smaller bronchioles."}},{"k":"alveoli_2","v":{"s":"Alveoli"}},{"k":"300_million_grape_like_sacs_where_gas_","v":{"s":"300 million grape-like sacs where gas exchange occurs. O2 diffuses into blood; CO2 diffuses out. Surface area equals a tennis court."}},{"k":"exhalation","v":{"s":"Exhalation"}},{"k":"the_diaphragm_relaxes_and_rises_reduci","v":{"s":"The diaphragm relaxes and rises, reducing lung volume. CO2-rich air is pushed out through the same pathway in reverse."}},{"k":"path_of_food","v":{"s":"Path of Food"}},{"k":"follow_a_meal_from_mouth_through_the_e","v":{"s":"Follow a meal from mouth through the entire digestive system."}},{"k":"mouth","v":{"s":"Mouth"}},{"k":"teeth_mechanically_break_food_down_sal","v":{"s":"Teeth mechanically break food down. Salivary amylase begins starch digestion. The tongue shapes food into a bolus."}},{"k":"pharynx_esophagus","v":{"s":"Pharynx & Esophagus"}},{"k":"swallowing_pushes_the_bolus_past_the_e","v":{"s":"Swallowing pushes the bolus past the epiglottis. Peristaltic waves move it down the 25cm esophagus in about 8 seconds."}},{"k":"stomach_2","v":{"s":"Stomach"}},{"k":"gastric_acid_ph_1_5_3_5_and_pepsin_bre","v":{"s":"Gastric acid (pH 1.5-3.5) and pepsin break down proteins. Churning produces chyme over 2-4 hours."}},{"k":"duodenum","v":{"s":"Duodenum"}},{"k":"the_first_25cm_of_the_small_intestine_","v":{"s":"The first 25cm of the small intestine receives bile from the liver/gallbladder and enzymes from the pancreas."}},{"k":"jejunum_ileum","v":{"s":"Jejunum & Ileum"}},{"k":"most_nutrient_absorption_occurs_here_v","v":{"s":"Most nutrient absorption occurs here via villi and microvilli, increasing surface area 600-fold. 6-7 meters long."}},{"k":"large_intestine_2","v":{"s":"Large Intestine"}},{"k":"water_and_electrolytes_are_reabsorbed_","v":{"s":"Water and electrolytes are reabsorbed. Gut bacteria ferment remaining fiber, producing vitamins K and B12."}},{"k":"rectum_excretion","v":{"s":"Rectum & Excretion"}},{"k":"waste_is_compacted_and_stored_in_the_r","v":{"s":"Waste is compacted and stored in the rectum until defecation. The entire journey takes 24-72 hours."}},{"k":"path_of_a_nerve_signal","v":{"s":"Path of a Nerve Signal"}},{"k":"trace_a_reflex_arc_from_stimulus_to_re","v":{"s":"Trace a reflex arc from stimulus to response."}},{"k":"receptor","v":{"s":"Receptor"}},{"k":"a_sensory_receptor_in_the_skin_detects","v":{"s":"A sensory receptor in the skin detects a painful stimulus (like touching a hot surface) and generates an electrical signal."}},{"k":"sensory_neuron","v":{"s":"Sensory Neuron"}},{"k":"the_signal_travels_along_a_sensory_aff","v":{"s":"The signal travels along a sensory (afferent) neuron toward the spinal cord at up to 120 m/s via saltatory conduction."}},{"k":"spinal_cord_2","v":{"s":"Spinal Cord"}},{"k":"in_the_dorsal_horn_the_sensory_neuron_","v":{"s":"In the dorsal horn, the sensory neuron synapses with an interneuron. For reflexes, the signal does not need to reach the brain."}},{"k":"interneuron","v":{"s":"Interneuron"}},{"k":"the_interneuron_in_the_spinal_gray_mat","v":{"s":"The interneuron in the spinal gray matter integrates the signal and relays it to a motor neuron."}},{"k":"motor_neuron","v":{"s":"Motor Neuron"}},{"k":"the_motor_efferent_neuron_carries_the_","v":{"s":"The motor (efferent) neuron carries the command signal from the ventral horn down to the target muscle."}},{"k":"effector_muscle","v":{"s":"Effector (Muscle)"}},{"k":"acetylcholine_released_at_the_neuromus","v":{"s":"Acetylcholine released at the neuromuscular junction causes muscle contraction. You pull your hand away before feeling pain."}},{"k":"brain_awareness","v":{"s":"Brain Awareness"}},{"k":"meanwhile_a_copy_of_the_signal_ascends","v":{"s":"Meanwhile, a copy of the signal ascends to the somatosensory cortex. You consciously feel pain about 0.5 seconds after the reflex."}},{"k":"skin","v":{"s":"Skin"}},{"k":"skeletal_2","v":{"s":"Skeletal"}},{"k":"muscular_2","v":{"s":"Muscular"}},{"k":"organs","v":{"s":"Organs"}},{"k":"circulatory_2","v":{"s":"Circulatory"}},{"k":"nervous_2","v":{"s":"Nervous"}},{"k":"lymphatic_2","v":{"s":"Lymphatic"}},{"k":"light","v":{"s":"Light"}},{"k":"medium","v":{"s":"Medium"}},{"k":"olive","v":{"s":"Olive"}},{"k":"brown","v":{"s":"Brown"}},{"k":"deep","v":{"s":"Deep"}},{"k":"true","v":{"s":"True"}},{"k":"false","v":{"s":"False"}},{"k":"sorry_i_could_not_connect_to_the_ai_tu","v":{"s":"Sorry, I could not connect to the AI tutor right now."}},{"k":"ai_tutor_is_not_available_in_this_envi","v":{"s":"AI tutor is not available in this environment."}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read aloud"}},{"k":"read_aloud_2","v":{"s":"Read aloud"}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"human_anatomy_explorer","v":{"s":"🫀 Human Anatomy Explorer"}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}