t."}},{"k":"neuromuscular_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Neuromuscular junction and contraction deep dive"}},{"k":"trace_muscle_activation","v":{"s":"Follow a motor-neuron signal from the axon terminal to calcium release and sarcomere shortening."}},{"k":"liver_lobule_scale_bridge","v":{"s":"Liver lobule Scale Bridge"}},{"k":"trace_liver_processing","v":{"s":"Connect the liver's body location to lobule microanatomy, portal blood processing, venous return, and bile flow."}},{"k":"intestinal_villus_scale_bridge","v":{"s":"Intestinal villus Scale Bridge"}},{"k":"trace_intestinal_absorption","v":{"s":"Zoom from the small intestine to a villus and trace how digested nutrients and water cross the epithelium into blood or lymph."}},{"k":"skin_repair_scale_bridge","v":{"s":"Skin Repair Scale Bridge"}},{"k":"trace_skin_repair","v":{"s":"Connect body surface and skin layers to the overlapping phases that stop bleeding, clear damage, rebuild tissue, and remodel a scar."}},{"k":"learning_modes","v":{"s":"Anatomy learning modes"}},{"k":"compare_homeostasis_reference_ranges","v":{"s":"Adjust the sliders to compare three adult teaching reference ranges and examine the limits of a simplified homeostasis model."}},{"k":"within_teaching_references","v":{"s":"All within teaching references"}},{"k":"one_variable_outside_reference","v":{"s":"1 variable outside reference"}},{"k":"two_variables_outside_reference","v":{"s":"2 variables outside reference"}},{"k":"three_variables_outside_reference","v":{"s":"3 variables outside reference"}},{"k":"fasting_glucose_mg_dl","v":{"s":"Fasting glucose (mg/dL)"}},{"k":"homeostasis_reference_hypothesis","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: Which reference range is narrowest, and why might the body regulate it tightly?"}},{"k":"arterial_blood_ph_reference_prompt","v":{"s":"A common arterial blood pH reference range is 7.35-7.45. Investigate why it is so narrow."}},{"k":"compare_outside_reference_counts","v":{"s":"Find settings with one, two, and three variables outside the reference ranges. What changes?"}},{"k":"explain_homeostasis_model_limit","v":{"s":"Explain why a reference-range flag alone cannot diagnose a person."}},{"k":"homeostasis_model_limit","v":{"s":"Teaching model only, not a clinical score or diagnosis. Real interpretation depends on age, context, symptoms, measurement method, trends, and rate of change."}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Homeostasis explanation"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Homeostasis hypothesis"}}],"id":473}},{"k":"angles","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_angle_reasoning_pathway","v":{"s":"Angle reasoning pathway"}},{"k":"a11y_svg","v":{"s":"SVG"}},{"k":"a11y_second_angle_ray_handle","v":{"s":"Second angle ray handle"}},{"k":"a11y_angle_ray_handle","v":{"s":"Angle ray handle"}},{"k":"a11y_pin","v":{"s":"Pin"}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"angle_explorer","v":{"s":"📐 Angle Explorer"}},{"k":"sound_effects","v":{"s":"Sound effects"}},{"k":"export_s_v_g","v":{"s":"Export S V G"}},{"k":"svg","v":{"s":"📥 SVG"}},{"k":"achievement_badges","v":{"s":"🏅 Achievement Badges"}},{"k":"angle_explorer_sections","v":{"s":"Angle Explorer sections"}},{"k":"explore_drag_the_protractor_name_the_a","v":{"s":"Explore — drag the protractor, name the angle"}},{"k":"acute_90_right_90_obtuse_90_180_straig","v":{"s":"Acute < 90°, right = 90°, obtuse 90–180°, straight = 180°, reflex > 180°. The protractor was invented around 1801; the half-circle version still runs every geometry class."}},{"k":"challenges_estimate_classify_measure","v":{"s":"Challenges — estimate, classify, measure"}},{"k":"estimation_builds_spatial_sense_precis","v":{"s":"Estimation builds spatial sense; precise measurement builds the protractor habit. Common Core 4.MD.5–7: angles as fractions of a full turn (1/360 of a rotation)."}},{"k":"learn_the_angle_relationships","v":{"s":"Learn — the angle relationships"}},{"k":"complementary_sum_to_90_supplementary_","v":{"s":"Complementary sum to 90°; supplementary to 180°; vertical pairs are equal; alternate interior angles match when lines are parallel. These four facts unlock most middle-school proofs."}},{"k":"tools_bisector_second_ray_calculator","v":{"s":"Tools — bisector, second ray, calculator"}},{"k":"bisect_cut_in_half_the_construction_wi","v":{"s":"Bisect = cut in half (the construction with compass + straightedge is in Euclid Book I, Proposition 9). Modern tools: protractor, miter saw, theodolite — all the same idea, calibrated."}},{"k":"angle","v":{"s":"Angle"}},{"k":"type","v":{"s":"Type"}},{"k":"explement","v":{"s":"Explement"}},{"k":"unit","v":{"s":"Unit"}},{"k":"angle_unit","v":{"s":"Angle unit"}},{"k":"degrees","v":{"s":"Degrees"}},{"k":"radians","v":{"s":"Radians"}},{"k":"gradians","v":{"s":"Gradians"}},{"k":"turns","v":{"s":"Turns"}},{"k":"angle_value_slider","v":{"s":"Angle value slider"}},{"k":"snap_15","v":{"s":"Snap 15°"}},{"k":"bisector","v":{"s":"Bisector"}},{"k":"2nd_ray","v":{"s":"2nd Ray"}},{"k":"pin_this_angle_on_protractor","v":{"s":"Pin this angle on protractor"}},{"k":"pin","v":{"s":"📌 Pin"}},{"k":"complementary","v":{"s":"Complementary"}},{"k":"click_to_remove","v":{"s":"Click to remove"}},{"k":"complementary_2","v":{"s":"Complementary"}},{"k":"supplementary","v":{"s":"Supplementary"}},{"k":"vertical_angle","v":{"s":"Vertical Angle"}},{"k":"vertical_angles_are_equal","v":{"s":"Vertical angles are equal!"}},{"k":"ai_angle_tutor","v":{"s":"🤖 AI Angle Tutor"}},{"k":"click_to_get_personalized_angle_tips","v":{"s":"Click to get personalized angle tips!"}},{"k":"click_to_get_personalized_angle_tips_2","v":{"s":"Click to get personalized angle tips!"}},{"k":"create","v":{"s":"Create"}},{"k":"create_2","v":{"s":"🎯 Create"}},{"k":"classify","v":{"s":"Classify"}},{"k":"classify_2","v":{"s":"🧠 Classify"}},{"k":"estimate","v":{"s":"Estimate"}},{"k":"estimate_2","v":{"s":"👁️ Estimate"}},{"k":"speed_round","v":{"s":"Speed Round"}},{"k":"speed_round_2","v":{"s":"⚡ Speed Round"}},{"k":"speed_round_3","v":{"s":"⚡ Speed Round"}},{"k":"what_t