terior angles always = 360°."}},{"k":"sin_trigonometric_functions","v":{"s":"sin Trigonometric functions"}},{"k":"soh_cah_toa_sine_opp_hyp_cosine_adj_hy","v":{"s":"SOH-CAH-TOA: Sine = Opp/Hyp, Cosine = Adj/Hyp, Tangent = Opp/Adj. The trig functions express ratios of right-triangle sides; extended to all angles via the unit circle."}},{"k":"special_angles_unit_circle","v":{"s":"★ Special angles (unit circle)"}},{"k":"these_angles_their_trig_values_are_wor","v":{"s":"These angles + their trig values are worth memorizing — they appear constantly in math, physics, and engineering."}},{"k":"angle_units_2","v":{"s":"° Angle units"}},{"k":"conversions","v":{"s":"🔁 Conversions: "}},{"k":"1_rad_180_57_296_1_180_0_01745_rad_1_g","v":{"s":"1 rad = 180/π ≈ 57.296°. 1° = π/180 ≈ 0.01745 rad. 1 gon = 0.9°. Most calculators have a degrees/radians mode toggle — make sure you're in the right mode!"}},{"k":"real_world_angles","v":{"s":"🌍 Real-world angles"}},{"k":"angle_problem_solving_shortcuts","v":{"s":"⚡ Angle problem-solving shortcuts"}},{"k":"compass_bearings_16_point","v":{"s":"🧭 Compass bearings (16-point)"}},{"k":"bearings_measured_clockwise_from_north","v":{"s":"Bearings measured CLOCKWISE from north. Standard 16-point compass has 22.5° between adjacent directions."}},{"k":"tower_of_pisa_actual_lean_4","v":{"s":"Tower of Pisa — actual lean ≈ 4°"}},{"k":"adjacent_cos","v":{"s":"adjacent (cos θ)"}},{"k":"pisa_linked_representations","v":{"s":"🗼 Pisa: linked representations"}},{"k":"three_views_of_the_same_trigonometric_","v":{"s":"Three views of the SAME trigonometric setup. Drag the angle (or tower height) slider — every representation updates simultaneously. Then take the inverse-problem quiz: solve a real measurement from only one representation."}},{"k":"1_phenomenon_the_tower","v":{"s":"1. Phenomenon — the tower"}},{"k":"2_geometric_decomposition","v":{"s":"2. Geometric decomposition"}},{"k":"3_equation","v":{"s":"3. Equation"}},{"k":"tan_opposite_adjacent","v":{"s":"tan(θ) = opposite / adjacent"}},{"k":"change_the_angle_and_watch_all_three_u","v":{"s":"Change the angle and watch all three update together — this is what \"the same idea, three representations\" means."}},{"k":"tilt_angle_in_degrees","v":{"s":"Tilt angle in degrees"}},{"k":"tower_height_in_meters","v":{"s":"Tower height in meters"}},{"k":"pisa_is_roughly_56_m_tall_leaning_4_to","v":{"s":"(Pisa is roughly 56 m tall, leaning ≈ 4° today)"}},{"k":"inverse_problem_quiz","v":{"s":"🧩 Inverse-problem quiz"}},{"k":"each_quiz_gives_you_partial_info_use_t","v":{"s":"Each quiz gives you partial info. Use the relationships you discovered above to compute the missing value."}},{"k":"your_answer","v":{"s":"your answer"}},{"k":"your_answer_2","v":{"s":"Your answer"}},{"k":"check_5","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"tip_rearrange_tan_opp_adj_if_you_know_","v":{"s":"Tip: rearrange tan(θ) = opp/adj — if you know θ and one side, you can solve for the other. If you know both sides, take arctan."}},{"k":"angles_glossary","v":{"s":"📖 Angles glossary"}},{"k":"area_base_height","v":{"s":"Area (base × height)"}},{"k":"area_heron_s_formula","v":{"s":"Area (Heron's formula)"}},{"k":"area_two_sides_included_angle","v":{"s":"Area (two sides + included angle)"}},{"k":"law_of_sines","v":{"s":"Law of Sines"}},{"k":"law_of_cosines","v":{"s":"Law of Cosines"}},{"k":"sum_of_angles","v":{"s":"Sum of angles"}},{"k":"pythagorean_theorem","v":{"s":"Pythagorean theorem"}},{"k":"triangle_inequality","v":{"s":"Triangle inequality"}},{"k":"centroid","v":{"s":"Centroid"}},{"k":"circumcenter","v":{"s":"Circumcenter"}},{"k":"incenter","v":{"s":"Incenter"}},{"k":"orthocenter","v":{"s":"Orthocenter"}},{"k":"circumference","v":{"s":"Circumference"}},{"k":"area","v":{"s":"Area"}},{"k":"arc_length","v":{"s":"Arc length"}},{"k":"sector_area","v":{"s":"Sector area"}},{"k":"chord_length","v":{"s":"Chord length"}},{"k":"inscribed_angle_theorem","v":{"s":"Inscribed angle theorem"}},{"k":"thales_theorem","v":{"s":"Thales' theorem"}},{"k":"power_of_a_point","v":{"s":"Power of a point"}},{"k":"equation_of_circle_center_h_k","v":{"s":"Equation of circle (center h,k)"}},{"k":"cube","v":{"s":"Cube"}},{"k":"rectangular_prism_box","v":{"s":"Rectangular prism (box)"}},{"k":"sphere","v":{"s":"Sphere"}},{"k":"cylinder","v":{"s":"Cylinder"}},{"k":"cone","v":{"s":"Cone"}},{"k":"square_pyramid","v":{"s":"Square pyramid"}},{"k":"triangular_prism","v":{"s":"Triangular prism"}},{"k":"tetrahedron_regular","v":{"s":"Tetrahedron (regular)"}},{"k":"octahedron_regular","v":{"s":"Octahedron (regular)"}},{"k":"dodecahedron_regular","v":{"s":"Dodecahedron (regular)"}},{"k":"icosahedron_regular","v":{"s":"Icosahedron (regular)"}},{"k":"translation","v":{"s":"Translation"}},{"k":"rotation","v":{"s":"Rotation"}},{"k":"reflection","v":{"s":"Reflection"}},{"k":"glide_reflection","v":{"s":"Glide reflection"}},{"k":"dilation_scaling","v":{"s":"Dilation (scaling)"}},{"k":"shear","v":{"s":"Shear"}},{"k":"identity","v":{"s":"Identity"}},{"k":"composition","v":{"s":"Composition"}},{"k":"cartesian_rectangular_2d","v":{"s":"Cartesian (rectangular) 2D"}},{"k":"cartesian_rectangular_3d","v":{"s":"Cartesian (rectangular) 3D"}},{"k":"polar_2d","v":{"s":"Polar 2D"}},{"k":"cylindrical_3d","v":{"s":"Cylindrical 3D"}},{"k":"spherical_3d","v":{"s":"Spherical 3D"}},{"k":"geographic_lat_lon","v":{"s":"Geographic (lat, lon)"}},{"k":"utm_universal_transverse_mercator","v":{"s":"UTM (Universal Transverse Mercator)"}},{"k":"homogeneous_projective","v":{"s":"Homogeneous (projective)"}},{"k":"quantity_with_both_magnitude_and_direc","v":{"s":"Quantity with both magnitude AND direction. Drawn as arrow. Examples: velocity, force, displacement."}},{"k":"scalar_just_a_number_mass_temperature_","v":{"s":"Scalar = just a number (mass, temperature, time). Vector = magnitude + direction."}},{"k":"place_tail_of_second_at_head_of_first_","v":{"s":"Place tail of second at head of first. Resultant = first tail to second head."}},{"k":"place_tails_together_diagonal_of_paral","v":{"s":"Place tails together. Diagonal of parallelogram = resultant."}},{"k":"vector_v_at_angle_v_v_cos_v_y_v_sin","v":{"s":"Vector v at angle θ: vₓ = v·cos θ, v_y = v·sin θ."}},{"k":"v_v_v_y_v_z_pythagoras_in_any_dimensio","v":{"s":"|v| = √(vₓ² + v_y² + v_z²). Pythagoras in any dimension."}},{"k":"magnitude_1_v_v_v_often_called_the_dir","v":{"s":"Magnitude 1. v̂ = v / |v|. Often called the \"direction\" of v."}},{"k":"a_b_a_b_cos_gives_scalar_zero_when_per","v":{"s":"a·b = |a||b|cos θ. Gives scalar. Zero when perpendicular. Negative when angle > 90°."}},{"k":"a_b_a_b_sin_n_gives_vector_perpendicul","v":{"s":"a×b = |a||b|sin θ · n̂. Gives vector PERPENDICULAR to both. Magnitude = area of parallelogram."}},{"k":"curl_fingers_from_a_to_b_thumb_points_","v":{"s":"Curl fingers from a to b. Thumb points in direction of a×b. (Right-handed coordinates.)"}},{"k":"c_a_c_b_generates_a_plane_or_line_of_v","v":{"s":"c₁·a + c₂·b. Generates a plane (or line) of vectors."}},{"k":"newton_s_2nd_law_f_ma_net_force_is_vec","v":{"s":"Newton's 2nd law: F = ma. Net force is vector sum of all forces."}},{"k":"airplane_heading_wind_vector_ground_ve","v":{"s":"Airplane heading + wind vector → ground velocity (vector sum)."}},{"k":"reflection_mirror_symmetry","v":{"s":"Reflection (mirror) symmetry"}},{"k":"rotational_symmetry","v":{"s":"Rotational symmetry"}},{"k":"translational_symmetry","v":{"s":"Translational symmetry"}},{"k":"glide_reflection_2","v":{"s":"Glide reflection"}},{"k":"point_symmetry_centrosymmetric","v":{"s":"Point symmetry (centrosymmetric)"}},{"k":"bilateral_symmetry","v":{"s":"Bilateral symmetry"}},{"k":"radial_symmetry","v":{"s":"Radial symmetry"}},{"k":"crystallographic_groups","v":{"s":"Crystallographic groups"}},{"k":"regular_tessellations_3_only","v":{"s":"Regular tessellations (3 only)"}},{"k":"equilateral_triangle_square_regular_he","v":{"s":"Equilateral triangle, square, regular hexagon. Only regular polygons that tile the plane."}},{"k":"why_only_3","v":{"s":"Why only 3?"}},{"k":"interior_angle_must_divide_360_triangl","v":{"s":"Interior angle must divide 360°. Triangle 60° (×6), square 90° (×4), hexagon 120° (×3). Pentagon 108° doesn't divide 360° → can't tile alone."}},{"k":"semiregular_archimedean_tessellations","v":{"s":"Semiregular (Archimedean) tessellations"}},{"k":"8_in_total_combine_multiple_regular_po","v":{"s":"8 in total. Combine multiple regular polygons; same arrangement at each vertex."}},{"k":"penrose_tilings_1974","v":{"s":"Penrose tilings (1974)"}},{"k":"aperiodic_tiling_never_repeats_exactly","v":{"s":"Aperiodic tiling — never repeats exactly. 5-fold symmetry. Inspired discovery of quasicrystals."}},{"k":"honeycomb_conjecture","v":{"s":"Honeycomb conjecture"}},{"k":"hexagonal_tiling_minimizes_total_perim","v":{"s":"Hexagonal tiling minimizes total perimeter for given area. Proven by Hales (1999). Why bees use hexagons."}},{"k":"voronoi_diagrams","v":{"s":"Voronoi diagrams"}},{"k":"each_region_points_closest_to_one_seed","v":{"s":"Each region = points closest to one \"seed\" point. Appears in nature (giraffe spots, mud cracks, cell organization)."}},{"k":"hyperbolic_tilings","v":{"s":"Hyperbolic tilings"}},{"k":"on_a_hyperbolic_plane_regular_pentagon","v":{"s":"On a hyperbolic plane, regular pentagons (and many other shapes) CAN tile. M.C. Escher's \"Circle Limit\" series."}},{"k":"wang_tiles_1961","v":{"s":"Wang tiles (1961)"}},{"k":"colored_squares_that_must_match_neighb","v":{"s":"Colored squares that must match neighbors on edges. Can simulate Turing machine → tiling problem is undecidable in general."}},{"k":"einstein_tile_2023","v":{"s":"\"Einstein\" tile (2023)"}},{"k":"a_single_tile_that_aperiodically_tiles","v":{"s":"A single tile that aperiodically tiles the plane (the \"hat\" and \"spectre\" shapes). Major recent discovery."}},{"k":"a_b_pythagorean_theorem","v":{"s":"a²+b² Pythagorean theorem"}},{"k":"in_a_right_triangle_a_b_c_where_c_is_t","v":{"s":"In a right triangle, a² + b² = c² where c is the hypotenuse. Sets of integers (a, b, c) that satisfy this are called Pythagorean triples. A \"primitive\" triple has no common factor."}},{"k":"generating_primitive_triples","v":{"s":"Generating primitive triples: "}},{"k":"for_positive_integers_m_n_with_no_comm","v":{"s":"For positive integers m > n with no common factor and not both odd, a = m² − n², b = 2mn, c = m² + n². Tries m=2, n=1 → (3,4,5)."}},{"k":"triangle_types_and_formulas","v":{"s":"△ Triangle types and formulas"}},{"k":"classification","v":{"s":"Classification"}},{"k":"key_formulas","v":{"s":"Key formulas"}},{"k":"circle_geometry_2","v":{"s":"◯ Circle geometry"}},{"k":"3d_solids_volumes_and_surface_areas","v":{"s":"⬢ 3D solids — volumes and surface areas"}},{"k":"geometric_transformations","v":{"s":"↻ Geometric transformations"}},{"k":"x_y_coordinate_systems","v":{"s":"(x,y) Coordinate systems"}},{"k":"vectors_2","v":{"s":"→ Vectors"}},{"k":"symmetry_2","v":{"s":"⟂ Symmetry"}},{"k":"tessellations_tilings","v":{"s":"⬡ Tessellations & tilings"}},{"k":"history_of_geometry","v":{"s":"🕰 History of geometry"}},{"k":"careers_that_use_angles_every_day","v":{"s":"💼 Careers that use angles every day"}},{"k":"classic_mathematical_theorems","v":{"s":"∎ Classic mathematical theorems"}},{"k":"circle_has_most_area_for_a_perimeter","v":{"s":"Circle has most area for a perimeter"}},{"k":"isoperimetric_inequality_4_a_p_equalit","v":{"s":"Isoperimetric inequality: 4πA ≤ P². Equality only for circles. Why soap bubbles + planets are round."}},{"k":"sphere_has_most_volume_for_a_surface_a","v":{"s":"Sphere has most volume for a surface area"}},{"k":"3d_version_of_above_single_bubbles_are","v":{"s":"3D version of above. Single bubbles are spheres; merged bubbles meet at 120° angles."}},{"k":"regular_hexagons_tile_most_efficiently","v":{"s":"Regular hexagons tile most efficiently"}},{"k":"honeycomb_conjecture_proven_1999_by_ha","v":{"s":"Honeycomb conjecture (proven 1999 by Hales): hexagonal tiling has lowest total edge length per area among any partition."}},{"k":"triangle_is_most_rigid_shape","v":{"s":"Triangle is most rigid shape"}},{"k":"cannot_be_deformed_without_changing_si","v":{"s":"Cannot be deformed without changing side lengths. Why bridges + roof trusses use triangles."}},{"k":"squares_stack_densely_100","v":{"s":"Squares stack densely (100%)"}},{"k":"square_tiles_fill_the_plane_with_no_ga","v":{"s":"Square tiles fill the plane with no gaps. Same for rectangles + parallelograms."}},{"k":"circles_tile_poorly","v":{"s":"Circles tile poorly"}},{"k":"hexagonal_packing_of_circles_fills_90_","v":{"s":"Hexagonal packing of circles fills ~90.7% of plane (best possible). Square packing only ~78.5%."}},{"k":"sphere_packing_in_3d","v":{"s":"Sphere packing in 3D"}},{"k":"fcc_hcp_both_achieve_74_density_kepler","v":{"s":"FCC + HCP both achieve ~74% density (Kepler conjecture, proven 2014 by Hales)."}},{"k":"five_regular_polyhedra","v":{"s":"Five regular polyhedra"}},{"k":"only_5_in_3d_tetrahedron_cube_octahedr","v":{"s":"Only 5 in 3D: tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron, icosahedron. (Plato discussed.)"}},{"k":"tetrahedron_is_simplest","v":{"s":"Tetrahedron is simplest"}},{"k":"4_vertices_6_edges_4_faces_minimum_3d_","v":{"s":"4 vertices, 6 edges, 4 faces. Minimum 3D shape."}},{"k":"sphere_cube_cylinder_volumes","v":{"s":"Sphere/cube/cylinder volumes"}},{"k":"for_sphere_inscribed_in_cylinder_h_2r_","v":{"s":"For sphere inscribed in cylinder (h = 2r): V_sphere = 2/3 × V_cylinder. (Archimedes's favorite result.)"}},{"k":"4d_hypercube_tesseract","v":{"s":"4D hypercube (tesseract)"}},{"k":"16_vertices_32_edges_24_faces_8_cells_","v":{"s":"16 vertices, 32 edges, 24 faces, 8 cells. Can be unfolded into 8 cubes (per Salvador Dalí)."}},{"k":"m_bius_strip","v":{"s":"Möbius strip"}},{"k":"single_sided_surface_cut_down_middle_l","v":{"s":"Single-sided surface. Cut down middle → longer single loop. Cut at 1/3 → two interlocked rings (one Möbius)."}},{"k":"klein_bottle","v":{"s":"Klein bottle"}},{"k":"closed_non_orientable_surface_inside_o","v":{"s":"Closed non-orientable surface. Inside = outside. Can be made in 3D only with self-intersection."}},{"k":"reuleaux_triangle","v":{"s":"Reuleaux triangle"}},{"k":"curve_of_constant_width_not_a_circle_c","v":{"s":"Curve of constant width (not a circle!). Can roll a board on three Reuleaux rollers smoothly. Drill bits use it."}},{"k":"antiprism","v":{"s":"Antiprism"}},{"k":"two_parallel_polygons_rotated_360_2n_c","v":{"s":"Two parallel polygons rotated 360°/2n, connected by triangles. Tetrahedron is the simplest antiprism."}},{"k":"snub_cube","v":{"s":"Snub cube"}},{"k":"has_chirality_left_right_handed_forms_","v":{"s":"Has chirality — left- + right-handed forms. One of two chiral Archimedean solids (other is snub dodecahedron)."}},{"k":"hyperbolic_paraboloid_saddle","v":{"s":"Hyperbolic paraboloid (saddle)"}},{"k":"equation_z_xy_straight_lines_lie_on_it","v":{"s":"Equation z = xy. Straight lines lie on it in 2 families. Used in Pringles + some roofs."}},{"k":"stable_polyhedron_count","v":{"s":"Stable polyhedron count"}},{"k":"gomboc_2006_is_first_known_mono_monost","v":{"s":"\"Gomboc\" (2006) is first known mono-monostatic — one stable + one unstable equilibrium. Like a self-righting Weeble."}},{"k":"egg_shape_oval_tessellation","v":{"s":"Egg shape (oval) tessellation"}},{"k":"cannot_tile_plane_alone_gaps_but_7_egg","v":{"s":"Cannot tile plane alone — gaps. But 7 egg-shapes form a hex-like cluster."}},{"k":"polyhedra_in_nature","v":{"s":"Polyhedra in nature"}},{"k":"common_in_crystals_cube_nacl_octahedro","v":{"s":"Common in crystals: cube (NaCl), octahedron (diamond, fluorite), dodecahedron (pyrite, garnet)."}},{"k":"buckyball_c","v":{"s":"Buckyball (C₆₀)"}},{"k":"truncated_icosahedron_60_carbons_like_","v":{"s":"Truncated icosahedron. 60 carbons. Like soccer ball pattern. Discovered 1985."}},{"k":"dna_double_helix","v":{"s":"DNA double helix"}},{"k":"helical_10_5_base_pairs_per_turn_major","v":{"s":"Helical, ~10.5 base pairs per turn. Major + minor grooves. Constant diameter ~2 nm."}},{"k":"honeycomb_cells","v":{"s":"Honeycomb cells"}},{"k":"hexagonal_prisms_with_rhombic_bottoms_","v":{"s":"Hexagonal prisms with rhombic bottoms. Bees do not consciously calculate — biophysics naturally minimizes surface area."}},{"k":"spaghetti_theorem","v":{"s":"\"Spaghetti theorem\""}},{"k":"drop_spaghetti_at_random_average_numbe","v":{"s":"Drop spaghetti at random — average number of intersections = (L/πd) × π × n. Used to estimate π in classroom."}},{"k":"surprising_shape_facts","v":{"s":"◇ Surprising shape facts"}},{"k":"study_of_flags_comes_from_latin_vexill","v":{"s":"Study of flags. Comes from Latin vexillum (flag carried by Roman cavalry)."}},{"k":"keep_it_simple_a_child_can_draw_from_m","v":{"s":"Keep it simple (a child can draw from memory), use meaningful symbolism, 2-3 basic colors, no lettering or seals, be distinctive."}},{"k":"most_common_2_3_e_g_france_germany_us_","v":{"s":"Most common: 2:3 (e.g., France, Germany). US is 1:1.9. UK is 1:2. Some unusual: Switzerland 1:1, Nepal non-rectangular."}},{"k":"symbols_added_to_flag_stars_crosses_an","v":{"s":"Symbols added to flag (stars, crosses, animals). Heraldic vocabulary describes positioning."}},{"k":"upper_left_rectangle_about_1_4_the_fla","v":{"s":"Upper-left rectangle (about 1/4 the flag). US stars go here. Australia, NZ also use canton design."}},{"k":"background_color_colors_of_flag","v":{"s":"Background color/colors of flag."}},{"k":"hoist_side_attached_to_pole_fly_opposi","v":{"s":"Hoist = side attached to pole. Fly = opposite (free) side."}},{"k":"red_courage_revolution_blood_blue_ocea","v":{"s":"Red = courage, revolution, blood. Blue = ocean, sky, freedom. Green = land, nature, Islam. White = peace, purity. Yellow/Gold = wealth, sun."}},{"k":"flying_flag_upside_down_where_asymmetr","v":{"s":"Flying flag upside-down (where asymmetric); flying flag at half-staff = mourning."}},{"k":"light_blue_with_map_un_or_compass_nato","v":{"s":"Light blue with map (UN) or compass (NATO). Intentionally avoid national/religious symbols."}},{"k":"flag_geometry_2","v":{"s":"🚩 Flag geometry"}},{"k":"flags_are_codified_geometry_every_nati","v":{"s":"Flags are codified geometry. Every national flag has precise specifications: aspect ratio, color codes, charge positions, star angles."}},{"k":"selected_national_organizational_flags","v":{"s":"Selected national + organizational flags"}},{"k":"flag_design_principles","v":{"s":"Flag design principles"}},{"k":"1_12_flat_ish","v":{"s":"1:12 (flat-ish)"}},{"k":"6_12_standard","v":{"s":"6:12 (standard)"}},{"k":"12_12_45","v":{"s":"12:12 (45°)"}},{"k":"24_12_steep","v":{"s":"24:12 (steep)"}},{"k":"roof_pitch_ramp_grade_tables","v":{"s":"◢ Roof pitch + ramp grade tables"}},{"k":"roof_pitches_rise_run_notation","v":{"s":"Roof pitches (rise:run notation)"}},{"k":"x_12_means_x_inches_rise_per_12_inches","v":{"s":"X:12 means X inches rise per 12 inches of horizontal run."}},{"k":"ramp_grades_transportation_slopes","v":{"s":"Ramp grades + transportation slopes"}},{"k":"total_solar_system_mass","v":{"s":"Total solar system mass"}},{"k":"sun_99_86_jupiter_0_095_everything_els","v":{"s":"Sun = 99.86%. Jupiter = 0.095%. Everything else = 0.045%."}},{"k":"astronomical_unit_au","v":{"s":"Astronomical Unit (AU)"}},{"k":"149_597_870_700_m_defined_exactly_ligh","v":{"s":"149,597,870,700 m. Defined exactly. Light takes ~8.3 min to cross 1 AU."}},{"k":"9_461_10_m_distance_light_travels_in_1","v":{"s":"9.461×10¹⁵ m. Distance light travels in 1 Julian year."}},{"k":"parsec","v":{"s":"Parsec"}},{"k":"3_26_light_years_distance_at_which_1_a","v":{"s":"~3.26 light-years. Distance at which 1 AU subtends 1 arcsecond."}},{"k":"voyager_1_distance","v":{"s":"Voyager 1 distance"}},{"k":"165_au_2024_fastest_human_made_object_","v":{"s":"~165 AU (2024). Fastest human-made object leaving solar system. Still receiving data."}},{"k":"sun_mass","v":{"s":"Sun mass"}},{"k":"1_989_10_kg_333_000_earth_masses","v":{"s":"1.989×10³⁰ kg. ~333,000 Earth masses."}},{"k":"sun_fusion","v":{"s":"Sun fusion"}},{"k":"620_million_tons_of_h_he_every_second_","v":{"s":"~620 million tons of H → He every second. Loses ~4 million tons as energy (E = mc²)."}},{"k":"solar_wind","v":{"s":"Solar wind"}},{"k":"stream_of_charged_particles_400_800_km","v":{"s":"Stream of charged particles. ~400-800 km/s near Earth. Creates auroras + sculpts comet tails."}},{"k":"notable_peaks_their_slopes","v":{"s":"⛰ Notable peaks + their slopes"}},{"k":"solar_system_planets_dwarf_planets","v":{"s":"🪐 Solar system planets + dwarf planets"}},{"k":"axial_tilt_drives_seasons_tilt_90_mean","v":{"s":"Axial tilt drives seasons. Tilt > 90° means retrograde rotation (Venus, Uranus, Pluto)."}},{"k":"solar_system_facts","v":{"s":"Solar system facts"}},{"k":"k_nigsberg_bridges","v":{"s":"Königsberg bridges"}},{"k":"can_you_cross_each_of_the_7_bridges_of","v":{"s":"Can you cross each of the 7 bridges of Königsberg exactly once?"}},{"k":"no_euler_proved_1736_you_need_each_nod","v":{"s":"No. Euler proved (1736) you need each node to have even degree, except possibly 2 odd-degree nodes. Königsberg had 4 odd-degree nodes. Founded graph theory."}},{"k":"squaring_the_circle","v":{"s":"Squaring the circle"}},{"k":"using_only_compass_straightedge_constr","v":{"s":"Using only compass + straightedge, construct a square with same area as a given circle."}},{"k":"impossible_is_transcendental_lindemann","v":{"s":"Impossible. π is transcendental (Lindemann 1882). Cannot be constructed with finite C+S operations."}},{"k":"doubling_the_cube","v":{"s":"Doubling the cube"}},{"k":"construct_a_cube_with_twice_the_volume","v":{"s":"Construct a cube with twice the volume of a given cube using compass + straightedge."}},{"k":"impossible_requires_2_which_is_algebra","v":{"s":"Impossible. Requires ∛2, which is algebraic of degree 3, but C+S only produces 2ⁿ-degree numbers."}},{"k":"trisecting_an_angle","v":{"s":"Trisecting an angle"}},{"k":"divide_an_arbitrary_angle_into_3_equal","v":{"s":"Divide an arbitrary angle into 3 equal parts with compass + straightedge."}},{"k":"impossible_in_general_some_specific_an","v":{"s":"Impossible in general. Some specific angles (e.g., 180°) can be. Origami CAN trisect arbitrary angles."}},{"k":"two_circle_problem","v":{"s":"Two-circle problem"}},{"k":"given_two_circles_how_do_they_relate","v":{"s":"Given two circles, how do they relate?"}},{"k":"5_cases_separate_externally_tangent_in","v":{"s":"5 cases: separate, externally tangent, intersecting (2 points), internally tangent, one inside the other."}},{"k":"four_color_theorem","v":{"s":"Four color theorem"}},{"k":"can_every_planar_map_be_colored_with_4","v":{"s":"Can every planar map be colored with 4 colors so no adjacent regions share a color?"}},{"k":"yes_proven_by_appel_haken_1976_first_m","v":{"s":"Yes. Proven by Appel + Haken (1976) — first major theorem proved with computer assistance."}},{"k":"brachistochrone","v":{"s":"Brachistochrone"}},{"k":"what_path_from_a_to_b_b_lower_minimize","v":{"s":"What path from A to B (B lower) minimizes time of sliding object under gravity?"}},{"k":"cycloid_posed_by_bernoulli_1696_solved","v":{"s":"Cycloid. Posed by Bernoulli (1696). Solved by Newton overnight + by 4 others including Leibniz."}},{"k":"pizza_theorem","v":{"s":"Pizza theorem"}},{"k":"cut_a_circular_pizza_into_8_sectors_wi","v":{"s":"Cut a circular pizza into 8 sectors with cuts through one point (not necessarily center). Alternating pieces have equal area."}},{"k":"true_for_any_odd_multiple_of_4_sectors","v":{"s":"True for any odd-multiple-of-4 sectors. Works for 8, 12, 16, ..."}},{"k":"mrs_miniver_problem","v":{"s":"Mrs. Miniver problem"}},{"k":"for_what_radii_of_intersecting_circles","v":{"s":"For what radii of intersecting circles do the moon-shape (\"lune\") + lens have equal area?"}},{"k":"ratio_depends_solved_analytically_roma","v":{"s":"Ratio depends — solved analytically. Romantic geometry from a 1942 novel."}},{"k":"steinmetz_solid","v":{"s":"Steinmetz solid"}},{"k":"intersection_of_two_perpendicular_cyli","v":{"s":"Intersection of two perpendicular cylinders of same radius."}},{"k":"has_4_vertices_looks_like_a_4_faced_cu","v":{"s":"Has 4 vertices, looks like a 4-faced cushion. Volume = (16/3)r³."}},{"k":"buffon_s_needle","v":{"s":"Buffon's needle"}},{"k":"drop_a_needle_of_length_l_onto_paralle","v":{"s":"Drop a needle of length L onto parallel lines spaced d apart. What's the probability it crosses a line?"}},{"k":"2l_d_for_l_d_method_to_compute_by_drop","v":{"s":"2L/(πd) for L ≤ d. Method to compute π by dropping needles!"}},{"k":"tarski_circle_squaring","v":{"s":"Tarski circle-squaring"}},{"k":"can_you_cut_a_circle_into_pieces_rearr","v":{"s":"Can you cut a circle into pieces + rearrange them into a square of equal area?"}},{"k":"yes_laczkovich_1990_with_10_pieces_cho","v":{"s":"Yes (Laczkovich 1990) — with ~10⁵⁰ pieces. Choice axiom required. Cannot use scissors-style cuts; pieces are bizarre."}},{"k":"banach_tarski_paradox","v":{"s":"Banach-Tarski paradox"}},{"k":"can_you_cut_a_ball_into_pieces_rearran","v":{"s":"Can you cut a ball into pieces + rearrange them into two balls of the same size?"}},{"k":"yes_requires_axiom_of_choice_non_measu","v":{"s":"Yes — requires axiom of choice + non-measurable pieces. Defies intuition but mathematically valid."}},{"k":"soap_film_minimal_surface","v":{"s":"Soap film minimal surface"}},{"k":"why_do_soap_films_take_certain_shapes","v":{"s":"Why do soap films take certain shapes?"}},{"k":"they_minimize_surface_area_catenoid_be","v":{"s":"They minimize surface area. Catenoid (between rings), Plateau border (junctions). Yields beautiful geometry."}},{"k":"square_tiling","v":{"s":"Square tiling"}},{"k":"simplest_regular_tiling_schl_fli_symbo","v":{"s":"Simplest regular tiling. Schläfli symbol {4,4}."}},{"k":"triangular_tiling","v":{"s":"Triangular tiling"}},{"k":"6_triangles_around_each_vertex_3_6","v":{"s":"6 triangles around each vertex. {3,6}."}},{"k":"hexagonal_tiling","v":{"s":"Hexagonal tiling"}},{"k":"3_hexagons_around_each_vertex_6_3_hone","v":{"s":"3 hexagons around each vertex. {6,3}. Honeycomb. Most efficient."}},{"k":"truncated_square_tiling","v":{"s":"Truncated square tiling"}},{"k":"octagons_squares_4_8_8_hispanic_coloni","v":{"s":"Octagons + squares (4.8.8). Hispanic colonial tile patterns."}},{"k":"truncated_hexagonal_tiling","v":{"s":"Truncated hexagonal tiling"}},{"k":"dodecagons_triangles_3_12_12_some_pave","v":{"s":"Dodecagons + triangles (3.12.12). Some pavements."}},{"k":"snub_square_tiling","v":{"s":"Snub square tiling"}},{"k":"squares_triangles_3_3_4_3_4_chiral_has","v":{"s":"Squares + triangles (3.3.4.3.4). Chiral — has handedness."}},{"k":"rhombic_tiling","v":{"s":"Rhombic tiling"}},{"k":"just_rhombi_can_be_combined_to_make_pe","v":{"s":"Just rhombi. Can be combined to make Penrose tilings."}},{"k":"penrose_tiling_p3","v":{"s":"Penrose tiling (P3)"}},{"k":"two_rhombi_fat_thin_aperiodic_never_re","v":{"s":"Two rhombi (fat + thin). Aperiodic — never repeats. 5-fold symmetry."}},{"k":"penrose_tiling_p2","v":{"s":"Penrose tiling (P2)"}},{"k":"kite_dart_aperiodic_with_5_fold_symmet","v":{"s":"Kite + dart. Aperiodic with 5-fold symmetry."}},{"k":"pinwheel_tiling","v":{"s":"Pinwheel tiling"}},{"k":"right_triangles_arranged_in_pinwheels_","v":{"s":"Right triangles arranged in pinwheels. Aperiodic. Used in tiles at Federation Square (Melbourne)."}},{"k":"hat_einstein_tile_2023","v":{"s":"\"Hat\" einstein tile (2023)"}},{"k":"single_polygon_that_tiles_aperiodicall","v":{"s":"Single polygon that tiles aperiodically. Major recent discovery by Smith, Myers, Kaplan, Goodman-Strauss."}},{"k":"spectre_einstein_tile_2023","v":{"s":"\"Spectre\" einstein tile (2023)"}},{"k":"improvement_on_hat_uses_only_direct_no","v":{"s":"Improvement on hat — uses only direct (not reflected) tiles."}},{"k":"cairo_pentagonal","v":{"s":"Cairo pentagonal"}},{"k":"5_sided_tiles_found_in_cairo_street_pa","v":{"s":"5-sided tiles. Found in Cairo street paving. Each tile has 4 angles of ~120° + one of 90°."}},{"k":"demiregular_tilings","v":{"s":"Demiregular tilings"}},{"k":"use_2_vertex_types_14_distinct_ones_so","v":{"s":"Use 2 vertex types. 14 distinct ones (some debate). More complex than Archimedean."}},{"k":"voronoi_tessellation","v":{"s":"Voronoi tessellation"}},{"k":"divide_plane_into_cells_closest_to_eac","v":{"s":"Divide plane into cells closest to each \"seed\" point. Appears in nature (giraffe spots, foam)."}},{"k":"delaunay_triangulation","v":{"s":"Delaunay triangulation"}},{"k":"triangle_mesh_with_property_that_no_po","v":{"s":"Triangle mesh with property that no point is inside any triangle's circumcircle. Dual of Voronoi."}},{"k":"15_pentagon_types","v":{"s":"15 pentagon types"}},{"k":"15_distinct_convex_pentagon_shapes_can","v":{"s":"15 distinct convex pentagon shapes can tile the plane. Last one found 2015 (Mann, McLoud, Von Derau). Marjorie Rice found 4 as amateur in 1970s."}},{"k":"hyperbolic_7_3_tiling","v":{"s":"Hyperbolic {7,3} tiling"}},{"k":"heptagons_impossible_in_euclidean_tile","v":{"s":"Heptagons (impossible in Euclidean) tile hyperbolic plane with 3 around each vertex. Drawn by Escher."}},{"k":"truchet_tiles","v":{"s":"Truchet tiles"}},{"k":"square_tiles_with_curves_or_diagonals_","v":{"s":"Square tiles with curves or diagonals. Random orientation → maze-like patterns."}},{"k":"famous_geometry_puzzles","v":{"s":"🧩 Famous geometry puzzles"}},{"k":"famous_tilings_of_the_plane","v":{"s":"◇ Famous tilings of the plane"}},{"k":"world_city_coordinates","v":{"s":"🌆 World city coordinates"}},{"k":"latitude_longitude_in_decimal_degrees_","v":{"s":"Latitude + longitude in decimal degrees. Lat: 0° = equator, ±90° = poles. Lon: 0° = Greenwich, ±180° = international date line area."}},{"k":"circle","v":{"s":"Circle"}},{"k":"ellipse","v":{"s":"Ellipse"}},{"k":"parabola","v":{"s":"Parabola"}},{"k":"hyperbola","v":{"s":"Hyperbola"}},{"k":"logarithmic_spiral","v":{"s":"Logarithmic spiral"}},{"k":"archimedean_spiral","v":{"s":"Archimedean spiral"}},{"k":"catenary","v":{"s":"Catenary"}},{"k":"brachistochrone_cycloid","v":{"s":"Brachistochrone (cycloid)"}},{"k":"tractrix","v":{"s":"Tractrix"}},{"k":"lissajous_figures","v":{"s":"Lissajous figures"}},{"k":"helix","v":{"s":"Helix"}},{"k":"sine_wave","v":{"s":"Sine wave"}},{"k":"cosine_wave","v":{"s":"Cosine wave"}},{"k":"bell_curve_gaussian","v":{"s":"Bell curve (Gaussian)"}},{"k":"heart_curve_cardioid","v":{"s":"Heart curve (cardioid)"}},{"k":"trefoil_knot","v":{"s":"Trefoil knot"}},{"k":"m_bius_strip_2","v":{"s":"Möbius strip"}},{"k":"klein_bottle_2","v":{"s":"Klein bottle"}},{"k":"pythagoras","v":{"s":"Pythagoras"}},{"k":"euclid","v":{"s":"Euclid"}},{"k":"archimedes","v":{"s":"Archimedes"}},{"k":"hypatia","v":{"s":"Hypatia"}},{"k":"fibonacci_leonardo_of_pisa","v":{"s":"Fibonacci (Leonardo of Pisa)"}},{"k":"ren_descartes","v":{"s":"René Descartes"}},{"k":"pierre_de_fermat","v":{"s":"Pierre de Fermat"}},{"k":"blaise_pascal","v":{"s":"Blaise Pascal"}},{"k":"isaac_newton","v":{"s":"Isaac Newton"}},{"k":"gottfried_leibniz","v":{"s":"Gottfried Leibniz"}},{"k":"leonhard_euler","v":{"s":"Leonhard Euler"}},{"k":"carl_friedrich_gauss","v":{"s":"Carl Friedrich Gauss"}},{"k":"variste_galois","v":{"s":"Évariste Galois"}},{"k":"bernhard_riemann","v":{"s":"Bernhard Riemann"}},{"k":"sofia_kovalevskaya","v":{"s":"Sofia Kovalevskaya"}},{"k":"georg_cantor","v":{"s":"Georg Cantor"}},{"k":"henri_poincar","v":{"s":"Henri Poincaré"}},{"k":"david_hilbert","v":{"s":"David Hilbert"}},{"k":"emmy_noether","v":{"s":"Emmy Noether"}},{"k":"srinivasa_ramanujan","v":{"s":"Srinivasa Ramanujan"}},{"k":"alan_turing","v":{"s":"Alan Turing"}},{"k":"john_von_neumann","v":{"s":"John von Neumann"}},{"k":"paul_erd_s","v":{"s":"Paul Erdős"}},{"k":"andrew_wiles","v":{"s":"Andrew Wiles"}},{"k":"maryam_mirzakhani","v":{"s":"Maryam Mirzakhani"}},{"k":"mathematical_physical_constants","v":{"s":"π Mathematical + physical constants"}},{"k":"earth_measurements_2","v":{"s":"🌎 Earth measurements"}},{"k":"famous_structures_their_angles","v":{"s":"🏛 Famous structures + their angles"}},{"k":"famous_mathematical_curves","v":{"s":"∿ Famous mathematical curves"}},{"k":"great_mathematicians","v":{"s":"👨‍🏫 Great mathematicians"}},{"k":"practical_angle_standards","v":{"s":"🛠 Practical angle standards"}},{"k":"circle_2","v":{"s":"Circle"}},{"k":"ellipse_2","v":{"s":"Ellipse"}},{"k":"parabola_2","v":{"s":"Parabola"}},{"k":"hyperbola_2","v":{"s":"Hyperbola"}},{"k":"degenerate_point","v":{"s":"Degenerate (point)"}},{"k":"degenerate_line","v":{"s":"Degenerate (line)"}},{"k":"degenerate_two_lines","v":{"s":"Degenerate (two lines)"}},{"k":"tetrahedron","v":{"s":"Tetrahedron"}},{"k":"cube_hexahedron","v":{"s":"Cube (hexahedron)"}},{"k":"octahedron","v":{"s":"Octahedron"}},{"k":"dodecahedron","v":{"s":"Dodecahedron"}},{"k":"icosahedron","v":{"s":"Icosahedron"}},{"k":"why_only_5","v":{"s":"Why only 5?"}},{"k":"at_each_vertex_you_need_3_polygons_who","v":{"s":"At each vertex, you need 3+ polygons whose angle sum is < 360° (else flat or impossible). Only 5 combinations work for regular polygons."}},{"k":"euler_s_formula","v":{"s":"Euler's formula"}},{"k":"v_e_f_2_holds_for_every_convex_polyhed","v":{"s":"V − E + F = 2 holds for every convex polyhedron (including all 5 Platonic solids)."}},{"k":"duality","v":{"s":"Duality"}},{"k":"connecting_face_centers_of_a_platonic_","v":{"s":"Connecting face centers of a Platonic solid gives its \"dual\". Cube ↔ octahedron; dodecahedron ↔ icosahedron; tetrahedron ↔ itself."}},{"k":"archimedean_solids","v":{"s":"Archimedean solids"}},{"k":"13_semi_regular_solids_same_arrangemen","v":{"s":"13 semi-regular solids — same arrangement at each vertex, but multiple polygon types. Includes truncated icosahedron (soccer ball pattern)."}},{"k":"kepler_poinsot_polyhedra","v":{"s":"Kepler-Poinsot polyhedra"}},{"k":"4_regular_star_polyhedra_with_pentagra","v":{"s":"4 regular star polyhedra with pentagram faces or vertices. Non-convex."}},{"k":"koch_snowflake","v":{"s":"Koch snowflake"}},{"k":"start_with_triangle_on_each_side_repla","v":{"s":"Start with triangle. On each side, replace middle third with two sides of a smaller triangle. Repeat. Infinite perimeter, finite area."}},{"k":"sierpinski_triangle","v":{"s":"Sierpinski triangle"}},{"k":"start_with_triangle_remove_middle_smal","v":{"s":"Start with triangle. Remove middle (smaller) triangle. Repeat on each remaining triangle. Hausdorff dimension log(3)/log(2) ≈ 1.585."}},{"k":"cantor_set","v":{"s":"Cantor set"}},{"k":"remove_middle_third_of_segment_repeat_","v":{"s":"Remove middle third of segment. Repeat on each remaining segment. Uncountably infinite but total length zero."}},{"k":"mandelbrot_set","v":{"s":"Mandelbrot set"}},{"k":"points_c_where_iterating_z_z_c_stays_b","v":{"s":"Points c where iterating z = z² + c stays bounded. Infinite detail at any zoom. Discovered 1980."}},{"k":"julia_set","v":{"s":"Julia set"}},{"k":"for_each_c_points_z_where_iterating_z_","v":{"s":"For each c, points z where iterating z = z² + c stays bounded. Each c gives a different Julia set; together they tile the Mandelbrot set."}},{"k":"dragon_curve","v":{"s":"Dragon curve"}},{"k":"fold_a_strip_of_paper_in_half_repeated","v":{"s":"Fold a strip of paper in half repeatedly; unfold each fold at 90°. Or recursive replacement of segments."}},{"k":"barnsley_fern","v":{"s":"Barnsley fern"}},{"k":"iterated_function_system_random_select","v":{"s":"Iterated function system. Random selection of 4 affine maps produces a fern shape."}},{"k":"hausdorff_dimension","v":{"s":"Hausdorff dimension"}},{"k":"fractional_dimension_coastline_1_2_1_3","v":{"s":"Fractional dimension. Coastline ~1.2-1.3 depending on country. Captures \"roughness\" beyond integer dimensions."}},{"k":"defining_property_strict_self_similari","v":{"s":"Defining property. Strict self-similarity (Koch) or statistical (coastlines)."}},{"k":"real_world_fractals","v":{"s":"Real-world fractals"}},{"k":"coastlines_trees_lungs_bronchi_blood_v","v":{"s":"Coastlines, trees, lungs (bronchi), blood vessels, river networks, mountain ranges, clouds, lightning."}},{"k":"flat_plane_parallel_postulate_holds_an","v":{"s":"Flat plane. Parallel postulate holds. Angles in triangle sum to exactly 180°."}},{"k":"surface_of_sphere_triangle_angles_sum_","v":{"s":"Surface of sphere. Triangle angles sum to MORE than 180°. Excess proportional to area. Used in navigation."}},{"k":"saddle_shaped_surface_triangle_angles_","v":{"s":"Saddle-shaped surface. Triangle angles sum to LESS than 180°. Many parallels through a point not on a line."}},{"k":"shortest_paths_on_a_surface_straight_l","v":{"s":"Shortest paths on a surface. Straight lines (Euclidean); great circles (sphere); hyperbolic lines."}},{"k":"largest_circles_on_a_sphere_equator_me","v":{"s":"Largest circles on a sphere. Equator + meridians. Plane flights follow great-circle paths."}},{"k":"euclidean_0_spherical_positive_hyperbo","v":{"s":"Euclidean = 0. Spherical = positive. Hyperbolic = negative. Curvature determines geometry."}},{"k":"on_sphere_of_radius_r_triangle_area_r_","v":{"s":"On sphere of radius R, triangle area = R² × (angle sum − π)."}},{"k":"positions_on_earth_s_surface_use_spher","v":{"s":"Positions on Earth's surface use spherical (or more precisely, ellipsoidal) geometry."}},{"k":"nyc_to_tokyo_is_shortest_via_the_arcti","v":{"s":"NYC to Tokyo is shortest via the Arctic, not straight east — that's the great circle."}},{"k":"spacetime_is_curved_by_mass_energy_geo","v":{"s":"Spacetime is curved by mass-energy. Geodesics through curved spacetime ARE the paths of free-falling objects."}},{"k":"mercator","v":{"s":"Mercator"}},{"k":"robinson","v":{"s":"Robinson"}},{"k":"winkel_tripel","v":{"s":"Winkel tripel"}},{"k":"mollweide","v":{"s":"Mollweide"}},{"k":"sinusoidal","v":{"s":"Sinusoidal"}},{"k":"albers_conic","v":{"s":"Albers conic"}},{"k":"lambert_conformal_conic","v":{"s":"Lambert conformal conic"}},{"k":"azimuthal_equidistant","v":{"s":"Azimuthal equidistant"}},{"k":"goode_s_homolosine","v":{"s":"Goode's homolosine"}},{"k":"a_vertical_or_angled_gnomon_casts_a_sh","v":{"s":"A vertical or angled \"gnomon\" casts a shadow. As Earth rotates, shadow moves across markings."}},{"k":"for_accuracy_gnomon_must_be_parallel_t","v":{"s":"For accuracy, gnomon must be parallel to Earth's axis — angle to horizontal = local latitude."}},{"k":"dial_plate_perpendicular_to_gnomon_par","v":{"s":"Dial plate perpendicular to gnomon (parallel to equator). Hour lines evenly spaced 15° apart."}},{"k":"dial_plate_parallel_to_ground_hour_lin","v":{"s":"Dial plate parallel to ground. Hour lines NOT evenly spaced — formula: tan(hour line angle) = sin(latitude) × tan(hour angle)."}},{"k":"dial_on_a_wall_hour_lines_depend_on_wa","v":{"s":"Dial on a wall. Hour lines depend on wall's azimuth + latitude."}},{"k":"sundial_time_differs_from_clock_time_b","v":{"s":"Sundial time differs from clock time by up to ~16 minutes through the year. Due to Earth's elliptical orbit + axial tilt."}},{"k":"figure_8_trace_of_sun_s_position_at_sa","v":{"s":"Figure-8 trace of sun's position at same clock time each day for a year. Shown on some advanced sundials."}},{"k":"sundial_shows_local_apparent_solar_tim","v":{"s":"Sundial shows LOCAL apparent solar time. Differs from civil time by longitude offset within timezone + DST."}},{"k":"very_precise_sundials_with_corrections","v":{"s":"Very precise sundials with corrections for equation of time + longitude. Accurate to seconds."}},{"k":"value","v":{"s":"Value"}},{"k":"phi_1_61803398874989_irrational_roots_","v":{"s":"φ (phi) ≈ 1.61803398874989... Irrational. Roots of x² = x + 1."}},{"k":"algebraic_definition","v":{"s":"Algebraic definition"}},{"k":"a_line_divided_so_that_whole_larger_la","v":{"s":"A line divided so that whole : larger = larger : smaller. (a+b)/a = a/b = φ."}},{"k":"fibonacci_connection","v":{"s":"Fibonacci connection"}},{"k":"ratio_of_consecutive_fibonacci_numbers","v":{"s":"Ratio of consecutive Fibonacci numbers approaches φ: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21 → 21/13 ≈ 1.615."}},{"k":"golden_rectangle","v":{"s":"Golden rectangle"}},{"k":"long_side_short_side_remove_a_square_r","v":{"s":"Long side / short side = φ. Remove a square → remaining rectangle is also golden."}},{"k":"golden_spiral","v":{"s":"Golden spiral"}},{"k":"logarithmic_spiral_with_growth_factor_","v":{"s":"Logarithmic spiral with growth factor φ per quarter turn. Approximated by Fibonacci squares + arcs."}},{"k":"pentagram","v":{"s":"Pentagram"}},{"k":"ratios_of_pentagram_diagonals_to_sides","v":{"s":"Ratios of pentagram diagonals to sides = φ. Pythagoras knew this."}},{"k":"in_nature","v":{"s":"In nature"}},{"k":"spiral_arrangements_in_sunflowers_pine","v":{"s":"Spiral arrangements in sunflowers, pinecones, nautilus shells. Reflects optimal packing for growing meristems."}},{"k":"in_art_overstated","v":{"s":"In art (overstated)"}},{"k":"often_claimed_in_parthenon_mona_lisa_e","v":{"s":"Often claimed in Parthenon, Mona Lisa, etc. — most claims unsupported. Real golden ratio art: 20th-century deliberate use (Le Corbusier, Dali)."}},{"k":"aesthetic_claim","v":{"s":"\"Aesthetic\" claim"}},{"k":"long_believed_humans_find_golden_ratio","v":{"s":"Long believed humans find golden ratio most beautiful. Robust evidence weaker than popular accounts suggest."}},{"k":"continued_fraction","v":{"s":"Continued fraction"}},{"k":"1_1_1_1_1_1_most_irrational_number_slo","v":{"s":"φ = 1 + 1/(1 + 1/(1 + 1/(...))). Most irrational number — slowest converging continued fraction."}},{"k":"mathematical_origami","v":{"s":"Mathematical origami"}},{"k":"active_research_area_can_solve_cubic_e","v":{"s":"Active research area. Can solve cubic equations (impossible with compass + straightedge alone). Connect to algebra."}},{"k":"maekawa_s_theorem","v":{"s":"Maekawa's theorem"}},{"k":"at_any_flat_foldable_vertex_mountain_f","v":{"s":"At any flat-foldable vertex, |# mountain folds − # valley folds| = 2."}},{"k":"kawasaki_s_theorem","v":{"s":"Kawasaki's theorem"}},{"k":"at_a_flat_foldable_vertex_alternating_","v":{"s":"At a flat-foldable vertex, alternating angles around the vertex sum to 180°."}},{"k":"7_fold_limit_paper","v":{"s":"7-fold limit (paper)"}},{"k":"standard_paper_can_be_folded_in_half_7","v":{"s":"Standard paper can be folded in half ~7 times. Each fold doubles thickness. After 7, paper is too stiff. Record (with special equipment + huge paper): 12 folds."}},{"k":"crease_pattern_3d","v":{"s":"Crease pattern → 3D"}},{"k":"flat_sheet_can_fold_into_virtually_any","v":{"s":"Flat sheet can fold into virtually any 3D shape with enough creases. Modern origami extremely complex."}},{"k":"real_world_space_telescopes","v":{"s":"Real-world: space telescopes"}},{"k":"james_webb_space_telescope_sunshield_f","v":{"s":"James Webb Space Telescope sunshield folded for launch (origami principles), unfolded in space."}},{"k":"real_world_airbags","v":{"s":"Real-world: airbags"}},{"k":"folded_efficiently_to_fit_in_compartme","v":{"s":"Folded efficiently to fit in compartment, unfold rapidly."}},{"k":"real_world_stents","v":{"s":"Real-world: stents"}},{"k":"origami_inspired_medical_stents_fold_t","v":{"s":"Origami-inspired medical stents fold tiny for insertion, expand in artery."}},{"k":"miura_fold","v":{"s":"Miura fold"}},{"k":"parallelogram_based_fold_lets_large_su","v":{"s":"Parallelogram-based fold lets large surface (solar panel) deploy from compact bundle via single pull."}},{"k":"kirigami","v":{"s":"Kirigami"}},{"k":"cuts_allowed_in_addition_to_folds_used","v":{"s":"Cuts allowed in addition to folds. Used for pop-up books + scientific structures."}},{"k":"parallel_lines_converge_to_vanishing_p","v":{"s":"Parallel lines converge to vanishing points. 1-point (looking down a road), 2-point (corner of building), 3-point (looking up at a tower)."}},{"k":"where_parallel_lines_appear_to_meet_on","v":{"s":"Where parallel lines appear to meet on horizon. Determined by viewer's position + line direction."}},{"k":"horizontal_line_at_viewer_s_eye_height","v":{"s":"Horizontal line at viewer's eye height. All vanishing points lie on it (for lines parallel to ground)."}},{"k":"objects_pointing_at_viewer_appear_comp","v":{"s":"Objects pointing at viewer appear compressed. Drawing a hand pointed at viewer = challenge."}},{"k":"divide_canvas_photo_into_9_equal_secti","v":{"s":"Divide canvas/photo into 9 equal sections. Place subjects on intersections for dynamic composition."}},{"k":"camera_tilted_off_horizontal_creates_u","v":{"s":"Camera tilted off-horizontal creates unease, instability, dynamism."}},{"k":"sun_angle_determines_shadow_length_dir","v":{"s":"Sun angle determines shadow length + direction. Low angle (golden hour) = long shadows + warm color."}},{"k":"brunelleschi_1413_demonstrated_mathema","v":{"s":"Brunelleschi (~1413) demonstrated mathematical linear perspective. Alberti formalized it (1435)."}},{"k":"distant_objects_appear_bluer_less_dist","v":{"s":"Distant objects appear bluer + less distinct due to atmospheric scattering."}},{"k":"some_traditions_use_specific_angles_ra","v":{"s":"Some traditions use specific angles + ratios (Islamic geometric patterns, Indian mandalas, gothic cathedrals)."}},{"k":"mathematical_artist_impossible_objects","v":{"s":"Mathematical artist. Impossible objects (Penrose triangles), tessellations, hyperbolic geometry."}},{"k":"folding_angles_crease_patterns_produce","v":{"s":"Folding angles + crease patterns produce 3D sculptures from flat paper."}},{"k":"conic_sections_2","v":{"s":"◉ Conic sections"}},{"k":"curves_formed_when_a_plane_intersects_","v":{"s":"Curves formed when a plane intersects a (double) cone. The angle of the plane to the cone's axis determines the curve type."}},{"k":"platonic_solids_2","v":{"s":"⬡ Platonic solids"}},{"k":"the_only_5_convex_regular_polyhedra_al","v":{"s":"The only 5 convex regular polyhedra. All faces are congruent regular polygons, same number meeting at each vertex."}},{"k":"fractals_2","v":{"s":"❄ Fractals"}},{"k":"shapes_that_exhibit_self_similarity_at","v":{"s":"Shapes that exhibit self-similarity at every scale. Coined by Benoit Mandelbrot (1975) from Latin fractus, \"broken\"."}},{"k":"non_euclidean_geometry","v":{"s":"⊕ Non-Euclidean geometry"}},{"k":"geometries_on_curved_surfaces_euclid_s","v":{"s":"Geometries on curved surfaces. Euclid's parallel postulate doesn't hold. Discovered 1820s (Lobachevsky, Bolyai, Gauss)."}},{"k":"map_projections_2","v":{"s":"🗺 Map projections"}},{"k":"projecting_curved_earth_onto_a_flat_ma","v":{"s":"Projecting curved Earth onto a flat map ALWAYS distorts something — angles, areas, distances, or shapes. Each projection optimizes for different uses."}},{"k":"sundials_2","v":{"s":"☼ Sundials"}},{"k":"golden_ratio_2","v":{"s":"φ Golden ratio"}},{"k":"reality_check","v":{"s":"⚠ Reality check: "}},{"k":"many_popular_claims_about_the_golden_r","v":{"s":"Many popular claims about the golden ratio in art, architecture, and \"beauty\" are unsupported or invented retroactively. The mathematical properties are real + beautiful; the cultural claims often aren't."}},{"k":"origami_geometry_2","v":{"s":"✦ Origami geometry"}},{"k":"origami_connects_geometry_to_engineeri","v":{"s":"Origami connects geometry to engineering, biology, and design. Mathematical theorems govern what folds are possible."}},{"k":"angles_in_sports_2","v":{"s":"🏆 Angles in sports"}},{"k":"many_athletic_skills_depend_on_launch_","v":{"s":"Many athletic skills depend on launch angles. The classic \"45° is optimal\" is true only for projectiles launched + landing at the same height (without air resistance)."}},{"k":"angles_in_art_perspective","v":{"s":"🎨 Angles in art + perspective"}},{"k":"angle_measuring_tools","v":{"s":"📏 Angle-measuring tools"}},{"k":"first_angle_desc","v":{"s":"Create your first angle"}},{"k":"right_on_desc","v":{"s":"Create a perfect 90° angle"}},{"k":"straight_up_desc","v":{"s":"Create a 180° angle"}},{"k":"full_circle_desc","v":{"s":"Explore 360°"}},{"k":"acute_eye_desc","v":{"s":"Correctly classify 5 acute angles"}},{"k":"streak_5_desc","v":{"s":"5 correct answers in a row"}},{"k":"streak_10_desc","v":{"s":"10 correct answers in a row"}},{"k":"speed_demon_desc","v":{"s":"Score 10+ in speed round"}},{"k":"estimator_desc","v":{"s":"Estimate within 5° three times"}},{"k":"all_types_desc","v":{"s":"Classify all 5 angle types correctly"}},{"k":"polygon_pro_desc","v":{"s":"Explore polygon angles for 5+ shapes"}},{"k":"century_desc","v":{"s":"Answer 100 challenges total"}},{"k":"resume_speed_round","v":{"s":"Resume speed round"}},{"k":"pause_speed_round","v":{"s":"Pause speed round"}},{"k":"resume","v":{"s":"Resume"}},{"k":"pause","v":{"s":"Pause"}},{"k":"add_fifteen_seconds","v":{"s":"Add 15 seconds"}},{"k":"angle_diagram","v":{"s":"Angle diagram showing the current tower or triangle measurements."}}],"id":474}},{"k":"applab","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_generation_cancelled","v":{"s":"Generation cancelled"}},{"k":"sr_generation_failed","v":{"s":"Generation failed"}},{"k":"sr_app_enhanced","v":{"s":"App enhanced"}},{"k":"sr_fix_applied_re_running_the_preview","v":{"s":"Fix applied. Re-running the preview."}},{"k":"architect","v":{"s":"Architect"}},{"k":"plans_the_app_structure_features_and_a","v":{"s":"Plans the app structure, features, and accessibility requirements before any code is written."}},{"k":"builder","v":{"s":"Builder"}},{"k":"writes_the_actual_html_css_and_javascr","v":{"s":"Writes the actual HTML, CSS, and JavaScript code based on the plan (or from scratch if Architect is off)."}},{"k":"reviewer","v":{"s":"Reviewer"}},{"k":"a_separate_ai_reads_the_code_with_fres","v":{"s":"A separate AI reads the code with fresh eyes to find bugs, accessibility issues, and UX problems."}},{"k":"fixer","v":{"s":"Fixer"}},{"k":"takes_the_reviewer_s_feedback_and_appl","v":{"s":"Takes the Reviewer's feedback an