lower peak honey. Ideal for hands-off IPM."}},{"k":"buckfast","v":{"s":"Buckfast"}},{"k":"brother_adam_s_century_long_hybrid_pro","v":{"s":"Brother Adam's century-long hybrid program. Balanced everything: gentle, low-swarm, disease-resistant, productive. No trait is top-tier but none are weak."}},{"k":"saskatraz","v":{"s":"Saskatraz"}},{"k":"bred_for_prairie_canada_cold_tolerance","v":{"s":"Bred for prairie Canada: cold tolerance + varroa/tracheal mite resistance. Excellent for cold-climate beekeeping (Maine, Minnesota, Scandinavia)."}},{"k":"meadow_old_field","v":{"s":"Meadow / Old Field"}},{"k":"open_field_with_mixed_wildflowers_and_","v":{"s":"Open field with mixed wildflowers and clover. The classic apiary site — balanced exposure, easy inspection access, decent forage in all seasons."}},{"k":"forest_edge","v":{"s":"Forest Edge"}},{"k":"dappled_shade_with_strong_basswood_lin","v":{"s":"Dappled shade with strong basswood/linden flow in July. Cooler mornings delay first foraging each day. Bears occasionally investigate — consider electric fencing."}},{"k":"urban_rooftop","v":{"s":"Urban Rooftop"}},{"k":"park_flowers_ornamental_gardens_no_far","v":{"s":"Park flowers + ornamental gardens + NO farm spray. Heat island makes varroa reproduce faster and crowding raises disease risk. Urban bees actually thrive despite expectations."}},{"k":"coastal_blueberry_barrens","v":{"s":"Coastal Blueberry Barrens"}},{"k":"wild_blueberry_barrens_yield_a_massive","v":{"s":"Wild blueberry barrens yield a massive but brief July flow. Salt spray stresses wings; coastal wind cuts foraging on rough days. Year-round mild climate = gentler winters."}},{"k":"mountain_valley","v":{"s":"Mountain Valley"}},{"k":"alpine_meadows_bursting_with_wildflowe","v":{"s":"Alpine meadows bursting with wildflowers. Cold nights suppress both disease and varroa. Shorter forage season — build fast or go hungry. Prized for varietal honeys."}},{"k":"first_dawn","v":{"s":"First Dawn"}},{"k":"advance_your_first_day","v":{"s":"Advance your first day"}},{"k":"month_one","v":{"s":"Month One"}},{"k":"colony_survives_30_days","v":{"s":"Colony survives 30 days"}},{"k":"full_year","v":{"s":"Full Year"}},{"k":"survive_a_complete_year_120_days","v":{"s":"Survive a complete year (120 days)"}},{"k":"sweet_reward","v":{"s":"Sweet Reward"}},{"k":"harvest_honey_for_the_first_time","v":{"s":"Harvest honey for the first time"}},{"k":"mite_slayer","v":{"s":"Mite Slayer"}},{"k":"treat_varroa_mites_3_times","v":{"s":"Treat varroa mites 3 times"}},{"k":"eco_warrior","v":{"s":"Eco Warrior"}},{"k":"complete_5_conservation_actions","v":{"s":"Complete 5 conservation actions"}},{"k":"garden_friend","v":{"s":"Garden Friend"}},{"k":"have_3_pollinator_plants_in_companion_","v":{"s":"Have 3+ pollinator plants in Companion Planting"}},{"k":"thriving_colony","v":{"s":"Thriving Colony"}},{"k":"reach_80_colony_health","v":{"s":"Reach 80+ colony health"}},{"k":"mega_hive","v":{"s":"Mega Hive"}},{"k":"grow_colony_to_25_000_workers","v":{"s":"Grow colony to 25,000+ workers"}},{"k":"bee_scholar","v":{"s":"Bee Scholar"}},{"k":"score_8_on_the_bee_knowledge_quiz","v":{"s":"Score 8+ on the Bee Knowledge Quiz"}},{"k":"hive_inspector","v":{"s":"Hive Inspector"}},{"k":"view_all_9_inspection_layers","v":{"s":"View all 9 inspection layers"}},{"k":"weather_wise","v":{"s":"Weather Wise"}},{"k":"survive_3_weather_events","v":{"s":"Survive 3 weather events"}},{"k":"varietal_master","v":{"s":"Varietal Master"}},{"k":"harvest_4_different_honey_varietals","v":{"s":"Harvest 4 different honey varietals"}},{"k":"crisis_manager","v":{"s":"Crisis Manager"}},{"k":"handle_5_colony_events","v":{"s":"Handle 5 colony events"}},{"k":"varroa_mite_surge","v":{"s":"Varroa Mite Surge"}},{"k":"varroa_destructor_mites_are_multiplyin","v":{"s":"Varroa destructor mites are multiplying on developing pupae. They feed on fat body tissue and transmit deadly viruses (DWV, ABPV)."}},{"k":"swarming_impulse","v":{"s":"Swarming Impulse!"}},{"k":"the_colony_is_crowded_and_wants_to_spl","v":{"s":"The colony is crowded and wants to split! The old queen may leave with half the workers unless you add space."}},{"k":"nectar_flow","v":{"s":"Nectar Flow!"}},{"k":"wildflowers_are_blooming_abundantly_fo","v":{"s":"Wildflowers are blooming abundantly! Foragers return heavy with nectar."}},{"k":"pesticide_drift","v":{"s":"Pesticide Drift"}},{"k":"nearby_agricultural_spraying_has_conta","v":{"s":"Nearby agricultural spraying has contaminated foraging areas. Workers are returning disoriented or dying."}},{"k":"bear_sighting","v":{"s":"Bear Sighting!"}},{"k":"a_black_bear_has_been_spotted_near_the","v":{"s":"A black bear has been spotted near the apiary, attracted by the smell of honey."}},{"k":"queen_laying_strongly","v":{"s":"Queen Laying Strongly"}},{"k":"the_queen_is_healthy_and_laying_up_to_","v":{"s":"The queen is healthy and laying up to 2,000 eggs per day — her own body weight in eggs!"}},{"k":"robbing_attempt","v":{"s":"Robbing Attempt"}},{"k":"a_weaker_nearby_colony_is_trying_to_st","v":{"s":"A weaker nearby colony is trying to steal your honey stores! Guard bees are fighting at the entrance."}},{"k":"heatwave","v":{"s":"Heatwave"}},{"k":"outside_temperatures_have_spiked_past_","v":{"s":"Outside temperatures have spiked past 100°F (38°C). Water foragers are working overtime to cool the hive."}},{"k":"drought","v":{"s":"Drought"}},{"k":"prolonged_dry_weather_has_wilted_flowe","v":{"s":"Prolonged dry weather has wilted flowers across the foraging range. Nectar flow has collapsed."}},{"k":"late_spring_frost","v":{"s":"Late Spring Frost"}},{"k":"a_sudden_overnight_frost_has_killed_ea","v":{"s":"A sudden overnight frost has killed early blossoms and trapped foragers caught outside."}},{"k":"heavy_rain_flood","v":{"s":"Heavy Rain / Flood"}},{"k":"days_of_heavy_rain_have_waterlogged_th","v":{"s":"Days of heavy rain have waterlogged the ground and grounded all foragers. Moisture is seeping into the hive."}},{"k":"nosema_infection","v":{"s":"Nosema Infection"}},{"k":"workers_are_showing_signs_of_nosema_so","v":{"s":"Workers are showing signs of Nosema — soiled comb, reduced longevity, and sluggish foraging. This single-cell gut parasite weakens bees from the inside."}},{"k":"chalkbrood_outbreak","v":{"s":"Chalkbrood Outbreak"}},{"k":"mummified_chalk_white_larvae_are_appea","v":{"s":"Mummified chalk-white larvae are appearing on the landing board. The fungus Ascosphaera apis has taken hold in damp comb."}},{"k":"deformed_wing_virus","v":{"s":"Deformed Wing Virus"}},{"k":"workers_are_emerging_with_shriveled_us","v":{"s":"Workers are emerging with shriveled, useless wings — classic signs of DWV. This virus is transmitted by varroa mites and is the #1 driver of colony collapse worldwide."}},{"k":"plant_wildflowers","v":{"s":"Plant Wildflowers"}},{"k":"sow_native_wildflower_mix_in_nearby_fi","v":{"s":"Sow native wildflower mix in nearby field"}},{"k":"reduce_lawn_mowing","v":{"s":"Reduce Lawn Mowing"}},{"k":"let_clover_and_dandelions_bloom_in_the","v":{"s":"Let clover and dandelions bloom in the lawn"}},{"k":"set_up_bee_waterer","v":{"s":"Set Up Bee Waterer"}},{"k":"place_shallow_water_dishes_with_pebble","v":{"s":"Place shallow water dishes with pebbles for landing"}},{"k":"advocate_no_spray_zone","v":{"s":"Advocate No-Spray Zone"}},{"k":"convince_neighbors_to_stop_using_neoni","v":{"s":"Convince neighbors to stop using neonicotinoids"}},{"k":"build_solitary_bee_hotel","v":{"s":"Build Solitary Bee Hotel"}},{"k":"create_nesting_habitat_for_mason_bees_","v":{"s":"Create nesting habitat for mason bees and leafcutters"}},{"k":"report_colony_data","v":{"s":"Report Colony Data"}},{"k":"submit_health_data_to_the_bee_informed","v":{"s":"Submit health data to the Bee Informed Partnership"}},{"k":"oxalic_acid_dribble","v":{"s":"Oxalic Acid Dribble"}},{"k":"2_1_oxalic_acid_in_sugar_syrup_dribble","v":{"s":"2.1% oxalic acid in sugar syrup, dribbled between frames. Organic acid; kills phoretic mites on contact."}},{"k":"most_effective_in_winter_when_there_s_","v":{"s":"Most effective in WINTER when there's no capped brood. If brood > 2,000, ~60% of mites are hiding in cells and untouchable — wait for a brood break. Legal organic treatment; leaves no residue in wax."}},{"k":"formic_acid_pads","v":{"s":"Formic Acid Pads"}},{"k":"naturally_occurring_in_ants_bees_the_o","v":{"s":"Naturally occurring in ants & bees; the only treatment that penetrates wax cappings to kill mites in capped brood."}},{"k":"temperature_sensitive_ideal_10_29_c_ab","v":{"s":"Temperature-sensitive: ideal 10–29°C. Above 32°C can kill queens and brood. Works year-round but risky in peak summer heat. Queen is briefly caged or may be superseded."}},{"k":"thymol_apiguard","v":{"s":"Thymol (Apiguard)"}},{"k":"essential_oil_from_thyme_slowly_vapori","v":{"s":"Essential oil from thyme; slowly vaporizes over 4–6 weeks. Bees tolerate it well; mites cannot."}},{"k":"needs_warm_weather_above_15_c_to_subli","v":{"s":"Needs warm weather (above 15°C) to sublime properly. Best applied in late summer / early autumn after honey harvest. Can temporarily reduce brood production."}},{"k":"powdered_sugar_dusting","v":{"s":"Powdered Sugar Dusting"}},{"k":"dust_workers_with_confectioner_s_sugar","v":{"s":"Dust workers with confectioner's sugar. Bees groom each other clean, knocking off phoretic mites through the screened bottom."}},{"k":"mechanical_no_chemicals_harmless_to_be","v":{"s":"Mechanical, no chemicals. Harmless to bees. Only affects ~5–15% of mites (those on adult bees, not in brood). Effective as monitoring + supplemental control, not as sole treatment."}},{"k":"drone_brood_removal","v":{"s":"Drone Brood Removal"}},{"k":"install_drone_foundation_frame_varroa_","v":{"s":"Install drone-foundation frame; varroa prefer drone brood 8× over worker brood. Remove and destroy the capped frame."}},{"k":"cultural_non_chemical_control_disrupts","v":{"s":"Cultural (non-chemical) control. Disrupts varroa reproductive cycle. Labor-intensive (costs 2 action points). Summer-only — no drone brood in winter. Combine with acid treatments for full control."}},{"k":"garden_wildflower","v":{"s":"Garden Wildflower"}},{"k":"a_multifloral_honey_from_a_diverse_pol","v":{"s":"A multifloral honey from a diverse pollinator garden — the richest phenolic profile and highest antioxidant content."}},{"k":"spring_clover","v":{"s":"Spring Clover"}},{"k":"pale_gold_mild_and_grassy_white_clover","v":{"s":"Pale gold, mild and grassy. White clover (Trifolium repens) is the classic North American table honey. Dominant in April–May."}},{"k":"summer_lavender","v":{"s":"Summer Lavender"}},{"k":"aromatic_purple_tinged_honey_with_a_fl","v":{"s":"Aromatic purple-tinged honey with a floral finish. Prized in Mediterranean cuisine for its gentle herbal sweetness."}},{"k":"summer_wildflower","v":{"s":"Summer Wildflower"}},{"k":"multifloral_blend_each_harvest_tastes_","v":{"s":"Multifloral blend — each harvest tastes slightly different based on bloom mix within flight range. The \"terroir\" of honey."}},{"k":"autumn_buckwheat","v":{"s":"Autumn Buckwheat"}},{"k":"dark_molasses_like_honey_with_robust_m","v":{"s":"Dark, molasses-like honey with robust malty notes. Rich in iron and antioxidants. Buckwheat (Fagopyrum) blooms late summer into autumn."}},{"k":"stored_wildflower","v":{"s":"Stored Wildflower"}},{"k":"winter_harvest_from_stored_surplus_cry","v":{"s":"Winter harvest from stored surplus — crystallized into creamy fondant. Harvest sparingly: the colony needs these reserves."}},{"k":"wildflower","v":{"s":"Wildflower"}},{"k":"a_multifloral_honey_a_snapshot_of_what","v":{"s":"A multifloral honey — a snapshot of whatever was blooming within flight range."}},{"k":"welcome_beekeeper","v":{"s":"Welcome, Beekeeper!"}},{"k":"you_re_managing_a_honeybee_colony_a_su","v":{"s":"You're managing a honeybee colony — a superorganism of 10,000 workers, a queen, and hundreds of drones. Your goal: keep the colony alive and thriving through all four seasons."}},{"k":"advance_days","v":{"s":"Advance Days"}},{"k":"click_next_day_to_simulate_one_day_wat","v":{"s":"Click \"Next Day\" to simulate one day. Watch the seasonal cycle — spring builds workers, summer brings nectar, autumn prepares for winter, and winter tests your reserves."}},{"k":"manage_your_hive","v":{"s":"Manage Your Hive"}},{"k":"use_treat_super_harvest_feed_and_inspe","v":{"s":"Use Treat, Super, Harvest, Feed, and Inspect to manage the colony. Treat varroa mites before they get above 20%. Harvest only the honey above the seasonal reserve shown in the dashboard."}},{"k":"conservation_matters","v":{"s":"Conservation Matters"}},{"k":"spend_action_points_on_conservation_ac","v":{"s":"Spend action points on conservation actions — plant wildflowers, build bee hotels, and advocate for pesticide-free zones. These improve habitat and foraging for all pollinators."}},{"k":"explore_learn","v":{"s":"Explore & Learn"}},{"k":"bee_roles","v":{"s":"Bee Roles"}},{"k":"honey_chemistry","v":{"s":"Honey Chemistry"}},{"k":"life_cycle","v":{"s":"Life Cycle"}},{"k":"waggle_dance","v":{"s":"Waggle Dance"}},{"k":"thermoregulation","v":{"s":"Thermoregulation"}},{"k":"pheromone_language","v":{"s":"Pheromone Language"}},{"k":"bee_anatomy","v":{"s":"Bee Anatomy"}},{"k":"native_bees","v":{"s":"Native Bees"}},{"k":"bloom_calendar","v":{"s":"Bloom Calendar"}},{"k":"hive_inspector_2","v":{"s":"Hive Inspector"}},{"k":"inside_the_superorganism","v":{"s":"Inside the superorganism"}},{"k":"colony_view","v":{"s":"← Colony View"}},{"k":"hive_inspection_layers","v":{"s":"Hive inspection layers"}},{"k":"plain","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"using_simple_everyday_words_and_short_","v":{"s":"using simple everyday words and short sentences"}},{"k":"grade_5","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"for_a_5th_grade_student_brief_and_frie","v":{"s":"for a 5th grade student, brief and friendly"}},{"k":"high_school","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"for_a_high_school_biology_student_scie","v":{"s":"for a high school biology student, scientifically accurate"}},{"k":"ai_tutor_for_inspection_layer","v":{"s":"AI tutor for inspection layer"}},{"k":"reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"a_worker_bee_changes_jobs_as_she_ages_","v":{"s":"A worker bee changes jobs as she ages. This \"temporal polyethism\" means the colony always has the right workers for every task:"}},{"k":"feed_larvae_1_300_times_per_day_with_r","v":{"s":"Feed larvae 1,300+ times per day with royal jelly (from hypopharyngeal glands) and bee bread (pollen + honey + enzymes). Future queens get royal jelly exclusively."}},{"k":"secrete_beeswax_from_8_abdominal_gland","v":{"s":"Secrete beeswax from 8 abdominal glands. Wax flakes are chewed and shaped into hexagonal cells — the most space-efficient structure in nature (proven by the \"honeycomb conjecture\" in mathematics)."}},{"k":"station_at_the_entrance_and_inspect_ev","v":{"s":"Station at the entrance and inspect every bee by antennating (touching antennae to read chemical ID). Each colony has a unique pheromone signature. Intruders are stung or wrestled out."}},{"k":"fly_up_to_5_miles_round_trip_visiting_","v":{"s":"Fly up to 5 miles round-trip, visiting 50-1,000 flowers per trip. They carry nectar in their honey stomach (a separate organ) and pollen in corbiculae (leg baskets). A forager makes 1/12 of a teaspoon of honey in her lifetime."}},{"k":"the_boldest_foragers_become_scouts_exp","v":{"s":"The boldest foragers become scouts — exploring new territory for nectar sources or nest sites. They perform waggle dances to report their findings. During swarming, scouts use a democratic \"quorum sensing\" process to choose a new home."}},{"k":"remove_dead_bees_and_diseased_brood_fr","v":{"s":"Remove dead bees and diseased brood from the hive — critical hygiene behavior. Colonies bred for high \"hygienic behavior\" are more resistant to varroa and disease."}},{"k":"stand_at_the_entrance_and_fan_their_wi","v":{"s":"Stand at the entrance and fan their wings at 230 beats/second to control temperature and humidity. They also fan Nasonov pheromone (lemon-scented) to guide lost bees home."}},{"k":"nectar_honey_the_chemistry","v":{"s":"🧪 Nectar → Honey: The Chemistry"}},{"k":"step_1_collection","v":{"s":"Step 1: Collection"}},{"k":"forager_sucks_nectar_80_water_20_sucro","v":{"s":"Forager sucks nectar (80% water, 20% sucrose) into her honey stomach — a separate organ from her digestive stomach, with a valve (proventriculus) between them."}},{"k":"step_2_enzymatic_processing","v":{"s":"Step 2: Enzymatic Processing"}},{"k":"bee_adds_invertase_enzyme_which_breaks","v":{"s":"Bee adds invertase enzyme, which breaks sucrose into glucose + fructose:"}},{"k":"c_h_o_h_o_c_h_o_c_h_o","v":{"s":"C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁ + H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + C₆H₁₂O₆"}},{"k":"sucrose_water_glucose_fructose","v":{"s":"(sucrose + water → glucose + fructose)"}},{"k":"also_adds_glucose_oxidase_glucose_o_gl","v":{"s":"Also adds glucose oxidase: glucose + O₂ → gluconic acid + H₂O₂. The hydrogen peroxide is why honey is antimicrobial!"}},{"k":"step_3_mouth_to_mouth_transfer","v":{"s":"Step 3: Mouth-to-Mouth Transfer"}},{"k":"house_bees_receive_the_nectar_and_pass","v":{"s":"House bees receive the nectar and pass it mouth-to-mouth (trophallaxis), adding more enzymes each transfer. This social processing is unique to honeybees."}},{"k":"step_4_evaporative_concentration","v":{"s":"Step 4: Evaporative Concentration"}},{"k":"bees_spread_thin_film_on_cell_walls_an","v":{"s":"Bees spread thin film on cell walls and fan wings (230 beats/sec) to evaporate water. Moisture drops from 80% → 18%. Takes 1-3 days."}},{"k":"step_5_capping","v":{"s":"Step 5: Capping"}},{"k":"below_18_6_moisture_the_honey_is_stabl","v":{"s":"Below about 18.6% moisture, honey is usually stable enough for capping. Stored sealed and dry, it can remain usable for a very long time; if it absorbs moisture, osmophilic yeasts can ferment it. Moisture control is therefore part of safe storage."}},{"k":"your_hive_right_now","v":{"s":"📊 Your Hive Right Now"}},{"k":"lbs_honey","v":{"s":"lbs honey"}},{"k":"lbs_pollen","v":{"s":"lbs pollen"}},{"k":"lbs_day_in","v":{"s":"lbs/day in"}},{"k":"three_castes_three_lifecycles_all_from","v":{"s":"Three castes, three lifecycles — all from the same egg. The only difference is diet:"}},{"k":"mates_once_on_nuptial_flight_with_10_2","v":{"s":"Mates with multiple drones during one or more nuptial flights early in adult life. Observed mating heights vary with terrain and conditions. She stores sperm for years, then can lay up to 2,000 eggs/day without repeating that early-life mating period."}},{"k":"all_female_cannot_mate_changes_jobs_by","v":{"s":"All female. Cannot mate. Changes jobs by age (temporal polyethism). Winter bees have more fat body and live 10× longer."}},{"k":"all_male_no_stinger_sole_purpose_mate_","v":{"s":"All male. No stinger. Sole purpose: mate with a queen from another colony. Dies immediately after mating. Surviving drones are evicted from the hive in autumn."}},{"k":"decoding_the_waggle_dance","v":{"s":"💃 Decoding the Waggle Dance"}},{"k":"the_forager_dances_a_figure_8_pattern_","v":{"s":"The forager dances a figure-8 pattern on the vertical comb face. The central \"waggle run\" encodes two pieces of information:"}},{"k":"direction","v":{"s":"Direction"}},{"k":"the_angle_of_the_waggle_run_relative_t","v":{"s":"The angle of the waggle run relative to vertical = the angle of the food source relative to the sun. Straight up = toward the sun. 60° right = 60° right of the sun. The bee uses the sun as a compass and gravity as a reference."}},{"k":"distance","v":{"s":"Distance"}},{"k":"the_duration_of_the_waggle_run_encodes","v":{"s":"The duration of the waggle run encodes distance. ~1 second of waggling ≈ 1 kilometer. A 2-second waggle run means the nectar is about 2km away. Closer sources use a simpler \"round dance\" (no direction info needed)."}},{"k":"karl_von_frisch_decoded_this_in_the_19","v":{"s":"Karl von Frisch decoded the waggle dance in the 1940s-60s and shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. It is a landmark example of symbolic referential communication in an invertebrate."}},{"k":"the_warm_blooded_superorganism","v":{"s":"🌡️ The Warm-Blooded Superorganism"}},{"k":"individual_bees_are_cold_blooded_but_t","v":{"s":"Individual bees are ectothermic, but the colony usually keeps the brood area near 34–36°C (93–97°F). Temperature varies across the nest and by brood stage. How do workers regulate it?"}},{"k":"too_cold_shivering_cluster","v":{"s":"Too Cold: Shivering Cluster"}},{"k":"bees_form_a_tight_ball_winter_cluster_","v":{"s":"In winter, bees form a tight cluster. Outer bees insulate while inner bees generate heat, and workers rotate positions. When brood is present, the colony warms the brood area; the rest of the cluster can be cooler and varies with conditions."}},{"k":"too_hot_evaporative_cooling","v":{"s":"Too Hot: Evaporative Cooling"}},{"k":"water_foragers_collect_droplets_and_sp","v":{"s":"Water foragers collect droplets and spread them on comb surfaces. Fanner bees create airflow (230 wingbeats/sec) that evaporates the water, cooling by ~10°C. If overheating continues, bees \"beard\" outside the entrance to reduce internal body heat."}},{"k":"current_brood_temperature","v":{"s":"🌡️ Current Brood Temperature"}},{"k":"pheromones_are_the_chemical_language_o","v":{"s":"Pheromones are the chemical language of the hive. Bees communicate danger, identity, location, reproduction, and social status through volatile molecules detected by 170+ odor receptors on their antennae."}},{"k":"queen_mandibular_pheromone_qmp","v":{"s":"Queen Mandibular Pheromone (QMP)"}},{"k":"the_most_powerful_pheromone_in_the_ins","v":{"s":"The most powerful pheromone in the insect world"}},{"k":"key_compounds","v":{"s":"Key compounds:"}},{"k":"suppresses_worker_ovary_development_on","v":{"s":"• Suppresses worker ovary development — only the queen reproduces"}},{"k":"attracts_workers_to_attend_and_feed_he","v":{"s":"• Attracts workers to attend and feed her (retinue response)"}},{"k":"stabilizes_colony_cohesion_without_it_","v":{"s":"• Stabilizes colony cohesion — without it, workers become agitated within hours"}},{"k":"during_nuptial_flight_attracts_drones_","v":{"s":"• During nuptial flight, attracts drones from up to 60 meters away"}},{"k":"alarm_pheromone","v":{"s":"Alarm Pheromone"}},{"k":"the_smell_of_danger","v":{"s":"The smell of danger"}},{"k":"released_from_sting_shaft_isopentyl_ac","v":{"s":"• Released from sting shaft (isopentyl acetate — smells like bananas 🍌)"}},{"k":"also_released_from_mandibles_2_heptano","v":{"s":"• Also released from mandibles (2-heptanone) when biting intruders"}},{"k":"recruits_guard_bees_and_increases_aggr","v":{"s":"• Recruits guard bees and increases aggression in nearby workers"}},{"k":"each_sting_deposits_pheromone_that_mar","v":{"s":"• Each sting deposits pheromone that marks the target for more stings"}},{"k":"this_is_why_beekeepers_use_smoke_it_ma","v":{"s":"• This is why beekeepers use smoke — it masks alarm pheromone and triggers bees to gorge on honey (preparing for possible hive evacuation), making them calmer"}},{"k":"isopentyl_acetate_ch_cooch_ch_ch_ch","v":{"s":"🧪 Isopentyl acetate: CH₃COOCH₂CH₂CH(CH₃)₂"}},{"k":"nasonov_pheromone","v":{"s":"Nasonov Pheromone"}},{"k":"come_home_the_homing_beacon","v":{"s":"\"Come home\" — the homing beacon"}},{"k":"released_from_the_nasonov_gland_on_the","v":{"s":"• Released from the Nasonov gland on the abdomen (bees fan with raised abdomen)"}},{"k":"primary_component_geraniol_and_citral_","v":{"s":"• Primary component: geraniol and citral (lemon-scented)"}},{"k":"marks_hive_entrance_so_lost_bees_can_f","v":{"s":"• Marks hive entrance so lost bees can find home"}},{"k":"used_during_swarming_to_guide_the_clus","v":{"s":"• Used during swarming to guide the cluster to the new nest site"}},{"k":"foragers_fan_it_at_water_and_food_sour","v":{"s":"• Foragers fan it at water and food sources to recruit other foragers"}},{"k":"geraniol_c_h_o_a_monoterpene_alcohol","v":{"s":"🧪 Geraniol: C₁₀H₁₈O — a monoterpene alcohol"}},{"k":"brood_pheromone","v":{"s":"Brood Pheromone"}},{"k":"the_babies_control_the_adults","v":{"s":"The babies control the adults"}},{"k":"a_blend_of_10_fatty_acid_esters_produc","v":{"s":"• A blend of 10 fatty acid esters produced by larvae"}},{"k":"stimulates_nurse_bees_to_feed_hungry_l","v":{"s":"• Stimulates nurse bees to feed — hungry larvae produce MORE pheromone"}},{"k":"inhibits_worker_ovary_development_back","v":{"s":"• Inhibits worker ovary development (backup to QMP)"}},{"k":"stimulates_foragers_to_collect_pollen_","v":{"s":"• Stimulates foragers to collect pollen (protein for larvae)"}},{"k":"controls_the_ratio_of_nurse_bees_to_fo","v":{"s":"• Controls the ratio of nurse bees to foragers — more brood = more nurses"}},{"k":"footprint_pheromone","v":{"s":"Footprint Pheromone"}},{"k":"chemical_gps","v":{"s":"Chemical GPS"}},{"k":"deposited_by_tarsal_glands_on_bee_feet","v":{"s":"• Deposited by tarsal glands on bee feet as they walk"}},{"k":"marks_visited_flowers_tells_other_fora","v":{"s":"• Marks visited flowers — tells other foragers \"already harvested, try the next one\""}},{"k":"marks_the_hive_entrance_helps_bees_loc","v":{"s":"• Marks the hive entrance — helps bees locate the entrance on return"}},{"k":"queen_footprint_pheromone_inhibits_que","v":{"s":"• Queen footprint pheromone inhibits queen cell construction — workers know she's present and walking the comb"}},{"k":"the_chemical_brain","v":{"s":"🧠 The Chemical Brain"}},{"k":"a_honeybee_has_170_odorant_receptors_h","v":{"s":"A honeybee has 170+ odorant receptors (humans have ~400, but bees are 50× more sensitive to floral scents). The colony's pheromone system is essentially a distributed nervous system — each bee is a \"neuron\" that reads and writes chemical signals. The colony thinks, decides, and responds as one organism, without any centralized brain."}},{"k":"under_the_microscope_a_worker_bee_is_a","v":{"s":"Under the microscope: a worker bee is a marvel of miniature engineering. Each structure evolved for a specific job — navigation, collection, defense, or construction."}},{"k":"head_sensory_feeding","v":{"s":"🧠 Head — Sensory & Feeding"}},{"k":"two_compound_eyes_with_6_900_hexagonal","v":{"s":"Two compound eyes with 6,900 hexagonal facets (ommatidia) each. Bees see UV light (invisible to humans) — flowers have \"nectar guide\" UV patterns that point to the nectar like runway lights."}},{"k":"three_simple_eyes_on_top_of_the_head_d","v":{"s":"Three simple eyes on top of the head detect light intensity and horizon orientation. They help the bee stay level during flight and detect sunrise/sunset."}},{"k":"12_segments_workers_queens_or_13_drone","v":{"s":"12 segments (workers/queens) or 13 (drones). Each antenna has ~170 odorant receptors, taste sensors, humidity detectors, and touch hairs. This is the bee's primary sensory organ."}},{"k":"a_retractable_tongue_up_to_6_6mm_long_","v":{"s":"A retractable \"tongue\" up to 6.6mm long. Built from the glossa (hairy tip), labium, and maxillae that lock together. The hairs flick 10× per second to lap up nectar. Different bee species have different tongue lengths matched to specific flowers."}},{"k":"powerful_jaws_used_to_chew_wax_clean_t","v":{"s":"Powerful jaws used to chew wax, clean the hive, bite intruders, and crush pollen. They also contain the 2-heptanone alarm pheromone gland."}},{"k":"thorax_locomotion_power","v":{"s":"💪 Thorax — Locomotion Power"}},{"k":"forewings_hook_into_hindwings_via_tiny","v":{"s":"Forewings hook into hindwings via tiny \"hamuli\" to form one unified airfoil. Wings beat 230× per second (12,000 rpm). Muscles contract 5–10× per nerve impulse — one of the fastest motion systems in biology. Top speed: ~15 mph; range: up to 5 miles from home."}},{"k":"indirect_flight_muscles_don_t_attach_t","v":{"s":"Indirect flight muscles don't attach to the wings directly — they deform the thorax, which snaps wings up and down. Can generate heat by shivering without moving wings (used for thermoregulation)."}},{"k":"front_legs_clean_antennae_special_notc","v":{"s":"Front legs clean antennae (special notch). Middle legs push pollen. Hind legs have the pollen basket (corbicula) — a shiny concave area ringed with hairs that packs pollen into a visible ball during foraging."}},{"k":"the_pollen_basket_on_the_hind_tibia_ca","v":{"s":"The pollen basket on the hind tibia. Can hold up to 15mg of pollen — one-third the bee's body weight. Foragers moisten pollen with nectar to make it stick."}},{"k":"abdomen_chemistry_defense","v":{"s":"🐛 Abdomen — Chemistry & Defense"}},{"k":"a_separate_storage_sac_from_the_digest","v":{"s":"A separate storage sac from the digestive stomach. A valve (proventriculus) controls whether nectar goes into storage (for the hive) or digestion (for the bee). Capacity: ~40mg — nearly half the bee's weight."}},{"k":"eight_abdominal_glands_on_workers_aged","v":{"s":"Eight abdominal glands on workers aged 12–18 days secrete liquid wax that hardens into flakes. Bees chew and shape flakes into hexagonal cells — the most space-efficient shape in geometry (proven by the Honeycomb Conjecture)."}},{"k":"on_the_dorsal_abdomen_near_tail_releas","v":{"s":"On the dorsal abdomen (near tail). Releases geraniol + citral (lemon scent) to mark the hive entrance and guide swarming bees. Workers raise their abdomens and fan wings to broadcast the signal."}},{"k":"a_modified_ovipositor_worker_stingers_","v":{"s":"A modified ovipositor. Worker stingers have barbs — once embedded in mammal skin, they rip from the bee's abdomen and she dies, but the pulsing venom sac keeps pumping apitoxin. Queens have smooth stings used only against rival queens."}},{"k":"bees_do_not_have_lungs_they_breathe_th","v":{"s":"Bees do not have lungs. They breathe through 10 pairs of spiracles connected to tracheal tubes that pipe oxygen directly to every cell. Air sacs pump like bellows when bees fly."}},{"k":"by_the_numbers","v":{"s":"📏 By the Numbers"}},{"k":"worker_body_length","v":{"s":"worker body length"}},{"k":"worker_body_mass","v":{"s":"worker body mass"}},{"k":"wing_beats","v":{"s":"wing beats"}},{"k":"neurons_humans_86_billion","v":{"s":"neurons (humans: 86 billion)"}},{"k":"there_are_over_20_000_bee_species_worl","v":{"s":"There are over 20,000 bee species worldwide "}},{"k":"and_only_7_of_them_make_honey_everythi","v":{"s":"— and only 7 of them make honey. Everything you've learned about "}},{"k":"apis_mellifera","v":{"s":"Apis mellifera"}},{"k":"the_western_honeybee_is_the_tip_of_the","v":{"s":" (the Western honeybee) is the tip of the iceberg. Most bees are "}},{"k":"solitary_native_and_ecologically_irrep","v":{"s":"solitary, native, and ecologically irreplaceable"}},{"k":"native_bees_pollinate_75_of_north_amer","v":{"s":". Native bees pollinate ~75% of North American flowering plants — many crops honeybees cannot pollinate at all."}},{"k":"mason_bees","v":{"s":"Mason Bees"}},{"k":"females_build_individual_nest_chambers","v":{"s":"Females build individual nest chambers in hollow stems or drilled wood and seal each with mud (hence \"mason\"). They carry pollen dry on their abdomen (scopa), dropping 10× more pollen than honeybees. Critical for orchard crops — apples, cherries, plums. Active only 4–6 weeks in early spring."}},{"k":"leafcutter_bees","v":{"s":"Leafcutter Bees"}},{"k":"females_snip_oval_leaf_pieces_and_roll","v":{"s":"Females snip oval leaf pieces and roll them into tubular cells. Key pollinator of alfalfa (the #1 livestock forage crop). Commercial leafcutter pollination in North America supports billions of dollars of alfalfa seed production."}},{"k":"bumblebees","v":{"s":"Bumblebees"}},{"k":"fuzzy_cold_tolerant_and_powerful_bumbl","v":{"s":"Fuzzy, cold-tolerant, and powerful. Bumblebees vibrate their flight muscles at 400Hz to shake pollen loose from tomato and blueberry flowers — something honeybees cannot do. Annual colonies die in fall except the queen, who overwinters alone. Several species (e.g. rusty-patched bumblebee) are endangered."}},{"k":"sweat_bees","v":{"s":"Sweat Bees"}},{"k":"iridescent_often_mistaken_for_small_fl","v":{"s":"Iridescent, often mistaken for small flies or wasps. They nest in bare soil and are major pollinators of wildflowers and vegetable crops. Some species have primitive social behavior (a precursor to honeybee eusociality). They'll occasionally land on people to drink salty sweat."}},{"k":"squash_bees","v":{"s":"Squash Bees"}},{"k":"males_sleep_inside_closed_squash_bloss","v":{"s":"Males sleep inside closed squash blossoms overnight. Females forage at dawn before honeybees even wake up. Without squash bees, your jack-o'-lantern, zucchini, and winter squash crops would fail. A textbook example of coevolution between plant and pollinator."}},{"k":"miner_bees","v":{"s":"Miner Bees"}},{"k":"one_of_the_largest_bee_genera_1_500_sp","v":{"s":"One of the largest bee genera (1,500+ species). Emerge very early in spring — often the first pollinators of the year. Completely harmless and don't defend their nests. If you see small mounds of dirt with holes in your lawn in April, you may be hosting miners!"}},{"k":"carpenter_bees","v":{"s":"Carpenter Bees"}},{"k":"females_bore_perfectly_round_tunnels_i","v":{"s":"Females bore perfectly round tunnels in untreated wood. Males have no stinger and are often mistaken for aggressive — they just hover and stare. Effective pollinators of passionflower, beans, and eggplant. Sometimes accused of nectar-robbing (biting holes in flower bases to skip the pollination work)."}},{"k":"how_to_help_native_bees","v":{"s":"🌍 How to Help Native Bees"}},{"k":"leave_patches_of_bare_soil_70_of_nativ","v":{"s":"Leave patches of bare soil — 70% of native bees nest in the ground"}},{"k":"build_a_bee_hotel_with_hollow_stems_6_","v":{"s":"Build a bee hotel with hollow stems (6–8mm diameter) and drilled wood blocks"}},{"k":"plant_native_flowers_exotic_ornamental","v":{"s":"Plant native flowers — exotic ornamentals often produce 4× less nectar"}},{"k":"never_use_neonicotinoids_they_are_leth","v":{"s":"Never use neonicotinoids — they are lethal to solitary bees at micro-doses"}},{"k":"report_sightings_to_bumble_bee_watch_o","v":{"s":"Report sightings to Bumble Bee Watch or iNaturalist for research data"}},{"k":"skip_the_honeybee_hive_in_urban_areas_","v":{"s":"Skip the honeybee hive in urban areas with scarce flowers — managed honeybees compete with struggling natives"}},{"k":"red_maple_willow","v":{"s":"Red Maple / Willow"}},{"k":"earliest_pollen_source_critical_for_sp","v":{"s":"Earliest pollen source — critical for spring build-up. Pussy willow catkins appear even in snow."}},{"k":"crocus_snowdrop","v":{"s":"Crocus / Snowdrop"}},{"k":"first_flowers_bees_see_plant_bulbs_in_","v":{"s":"First flowers bees see — plant bulbs in fall for emergency early pollen."}},{"k":"dandelion","v":{"s":"Dandelion"}},{"k":"a_bee_lifeline_not_a_weed_no_mow_may_l","v":{"s":"A bee lifeline, not a weed. \"No Mow May\" lets dandelions feed colonies recovering from winter."}},{"k":"fruit_trees_apple_cherry","v":{"s":"Fruit Trees (apple/cherry)"}},{"k":"massive_but_brief_apple_bloom_lasts_10","v":{"s":"Massive but brief — apple bloom lasts ~10 days. Honeybees are critical commercial pollinators."}},{"k":"white_clover","v":{"s":"White Clover"}},{"k":"the_classic_north_american_table_honey","v":{"s":"The classic North American table honey. Low, creeping, drought-tolerant — ideal lawn replacement."}},{"k":"blueberry","v":{"s":"Blueberry"}},{"k":"needs_buzz_pollination_from_bumblebees","v":{"s":"Needs buzz-pollination from bumblebees — honeybees help but bumbles are 3× more effective."}},{"k":"black_locust_basswood","v":{"s":"Black Locust / Basswood"}},{"k":"linden_basswood_honey_is_a_prized_sing","v":{"s":"Linden/basswood honey is a prized single-origin varietal. Locust supports early summer flows."}},{"k":"lavender","v":{"s":"Lavender"}},{"k":"aromatic_honey_prized_in_mediterranean","v":{"s":"Aromatic honey prized in Mediterranean cuisine. Excellent for garden borders."}},{"k":"borage_comfrey","v":{"s":"Borage / Comfrey"}},{"k":"borage_refills_nectar_every_2_minutes_","v":{"s":"Borage refills nectar every 2 minutes — one of the highest-yielding bee plants known."}},{"k":"milkweed","v":{"s":"Milkweed"}},{"k":"critical_for_monarchs_and_bees_nectar_","v":{"s":"Critical for monarchs AND bees. Nectar-rich complex flowers. Plant common or swamp milkweed."}},{"k":"sunflower","v":{"s":"Sunflower"}},{"k":"massive_composite_flower_heads_are_pol","v":{"s":"Massive composite flower heads are pollen landing pads. Choose pollen-producing (not pollen-free) cultivars."}},{"k":"buckwheat","v":{"s":"Buckwheat"}},{"k":"dark_molasses_like_honey_blooms_30_day","v":{"s":"Dark, molasses-like honey. Blooms 30 days after sowing — easy late-summer flow plant."}},{"k":"goldenrod","v":{"s":"Goldenrod"}},{"k":"autumn_winter_stores_powerhouse_often_","v":{"s":"Autumn winter-stores powerhouse. Often blamed for hay fever, but it's ragweed that actually causes allergies."}},{"k":"new_england_aster","v":{"s":"New England Aster"}},{"k":"last_abundant_nectar_before_winter_bee","v":{"s":"Last abundant nectar before winter. Bees gorge on it to fatten up for clustering."}},{"k":"witch_hazel","v":{"s":"Witch Hazel"}},{"k":"unusual_late_autumn_and_late_winter_bl","v":{"s":"Unusual late-autumn AND late-winter bloomer. On warm winter days, bees will fly to visit it."}},{"k":"early_spring_mar_apr","v":{"s":"🌱 Early Spring (Mar–Apr)"}},{"k":"late_spring_summer_may_jul","v":{"s":"☀️ Late Spring → Summer (May–Jul)"}},{"k":"late_summer_jul_aug","v":{"s":"🌻 Late Summer (Jul–Aug)"}},{"k":"autumn_aug_oct","v":{"s":"🍂 Autumn (Aug–Oct)"}},{"k":"a_bloom_gap_can_starve_a_colony","v":{"s":"A bloom gap can starve a colony. "}},{"k":"bees_need_nectar_from_earliest_spring_","v":{"s":"Bees need nectar from earliest spring through late autumn. Any 2-week dearth — especially during summer nectar gaps or after first frost — forces the colony to dip into honey reserves. Diverse native plantings smooth out the forage curve so bees always have something to eat."}},{"k":"baseline_maine_new_england_usda_zone_5","v":{"s":"Baseline: Maine / New England (USDA Zone 5). Shift ±3 weeks for warmer/colder climates."}},{"k":"current_in_game_month","v":{"s":"Current in-game month"}},{"k":"peak_bloom","v":{"s":"Peak bloom"}},{"k":"light_bloom","v":{"s":"Light bloom"}},{"k":"dormant","v":{"s":"Dormant"}},{"k":"summer_dearth_late_july_in_maine","v":{"s":"⚠️ Summer Dearth (late July in Maine)"}},{"k":"between_spring_tree_bloom_and_summer_h","v":{"s":"Between spring tree bloom and summer herbaceous flowers there's often a 2–3 week \"dearth\" with little nectar flow. Watch for robbing behavior and reduce hive entrance size. Planting late-summer bloomers (sunflower, buckwheat, goldenrod) is the single best way to close this gap."}},{"k":"white_rice_grain_egg_laid_by_queen","v":{"s":"White rice-grain egg laid by queen"}},{"k":"queen_can_lay_1_500_eggs_day_her_own_b","v":{"s":"Queen can lay 1,500 eggs/day — her own body weight"}},{"k":"c_shaped_grub_fed_royal_jelly","v":{"s":"C-shaped grub fed royal jelly"}},{"k":"larva_grows_900_in_size_royal_jelly_fo","v":{"s":"Larva grows 900× in size. Royal jelly for 3 days, then worker jelly (pollen+honey)"}},{"k":"capped_cell_metamorphosis_hidden","v":{"s":"Capped cell, metamorphosis hidden"}},{"k":"workers_cap_the_cell_with_wax_inside_e","v":{"s":"Workers cap the cell with wax. Inside: eyes, wings, legs, organs all form"}},{"k":"chews_out_of_capping_emerges","v":{"s":"Chews out of capping, emerges"}},{"k":"first_jobs_cleaning_cells_nurse_bee_gu","v":{"s":"First jobs: cleaning cells → nurse bee → guard → forager (last 3 weeks of life)"}},{"k":"queen","v":{"s":"👑 Queen"}},{"k":"worker","v":{"s":"🧑‍🏭 Worker (♀)"}},{"k":"drone","v":{"s":"🚀 Drone (♂)"}},{"k":"outer_cover","v":{"s":"Outer Cover"}},{"k":"weatherproof_telescoping_lid","v":{"s":"Weatherproof telescoping lid"}},{"k":"inner_cover","v":{"s":"Inner Cover"}},{"k":"insulating_layer_vent_hole","v":{"s":"Insulating layer + vent hole"}},{"k":"honey_super","v":{"s":"Honey Super"}},{"k":"bees_store_surplus_honey_here","v":{"s":"Bees store surplus honey here"}},{"k":"queen_excluder","v":{"s":"Queen Excluder"}},{"k":"metal_plastic_grid_workers_pass_queen_","v":{"s":"Metal/plastic grid — workers pass, queen doesn't"}},{"k":"brood_box_deep_super","v":{"s":"Brood Box (Deep Super)"}},{"k":"queen_lays_eggs_here_nursery","v":{"s":"Queen lays eggs here · nursery"}},{"k":"bottom_board_entrance","v":{"s":"Bottom Board + Entrance"}},{"k":"floor_landing_board_for_foragers","v":{"s":"Floor + landing board for foragers"}},{"k":"smoker","v":{"s":"Smoker"}},{"k":"cool_smoke_triggers_fire_eat_honey_res","v":{"s":"Cool smoke triggers \"fire! eat honey!\" response — calms bees for inspections"}},{"k":"hive_tool","v":{"s":"Hive Tool"}},{"k":"j_shaped_pry_bar_to_break_propolis_sea","v":{"s":"J-shaped pry bar to break propolis seals between frames + lift them out"}},{"k":"bee_brush","v":{"s":"Bee Brush"}},{"k":"soft_bristles_to_gently_move_bees_off_","v":{"s":"Soft bristles to gently move bees off frames during harvest — no crushed bees"}},{"k":"veil_suit","v":{"s":"Veil + Suit"}},{"k":"mesh_hood_white_coverall_bees_react_to","v":{"s":"Mesh hood + white coverall — bees react to dark colors as bear threats"}},{"k":"frame_with_foundation","v":{"s":"Frame (with foundation)"}},{"k":"wooden_rectangle_with_wax_foundation_s","v":{"s":"Wooden rectangle with wax foundation sheet — bees build comb on it"}},{"k":"hive_scale","v":{"s":"Hive Scale"}},{"k":"digital_scale_under_the_hive_tracks_da","v":{"s":"Digital scale under the hive — tracks daily weight = nectar flow"}},{"k":"honeybee","v":{"s":"Honeybee"}},{"k":"bumblebee","v":{"s":"Bumblebee"}},{"k":"mason_bee","v":{"s":"Mason Bee"}},{"k":"leafcutter","v":{"s":"Leafcutter"}},{"k":"squash_bee","v":{"s":"Squash Bee"}},{"k":"sweat_bee","v":{"s":"Sweat Bee"}},{"k":"counts_to_4","v":{"s":"Counts to 4"}},{"k":"can_tell_2_dots_from_3_3_from_4_traine","v":{"s":"Can tell 2 dots from 3, 3 from 4 — trained honeybees out-perform some birds"}},{"k":"understands_zero","v":{"s":"Understands ZERO"}},{"k":"bees_grasp_none_as_a_quantity_a_concep","v":{"s":"Bees grasp \"none\" as a quantity — a concept humans don't acquire until age 4"}},{"k":"recognizes_faces","v":{"s":"Recognizes faces"}},{"k":"trained_to_discriminate_human_faces_li","v":{"s":"Trained to discriminate human faces like dogs or sheep; uses same config cues"}},{"k":"solves_puzzles","v":{"s":"Solves puzzles"}},{"k":"pulls_strings_rolls_balls_to_get_sugar","v":{"s":"Pulls strings, rolls balls to get sugar — teaches other bees by demonstration"}},{"k":"resin","v":{"s":"Resin"}},{"k":"essential_oils","v":{"s":"Essential oils"}},{"k":"pollen","v":{"s":"Pollen"}},{"k":"other","v":{"s":"Other"}},{"k":"melittin","v":{"s":"Melittin"}},{"k":"membrane_disrupting_peptide","v":{"s":"membrane-disrupting peptide"}},{"k":"phospholipase_a","v":{"s":"Phospholipase A₂"}},{"k":"enzyme_cell_membrane_attack","v":{"s":"enzyme — cell-membrane attack"}},{"k":"apamin","v":{"s":"Apamin"}},{"k":"neurotoxin_blocks_k_channels","v":{"s":"neurotoxin · blocks K⁺ channels"}},{"k":"histamine","v":{"s":"Histamine"}},{"k":"triggers_pain_inflammation","v":{"s":"triggers pain + inflammation"}},{"k":"water_others","v":{"s":"Water + others"}},{"k":"carrier_minor_compounds","v":{"s":"carrier & minor compounds"}},{"k":"almonds","v":{"s":"Almonds"}},{"k":"apples","v":{"s":"Apples"}},{"k":"blueberries","v":{"s":"Blueberries"}},{"k":"cherries","v":{"s":"Cherries"}},{"k":"avocados","v":{"s":"Avocados"}},{"k":"strawberries","v":{"s":"Strawberries"}},{"k":"melons","v":{"s":"Melons"}},{"k":"coffee","v":{"s":"Coffee"}},{"k":"varroa_mites","v":{"s":"VARROA MITES"}},{"k":"habitat_loss","v":{"s":"HABITAT LOSS"}},{"k":"colony_collapse_disorder","v":{"s":"COLONY COLLAPSE DISORDER"}},{"k":"9_oda_acid_8_others_without_qmp_worker","v":{"s":"9-ODA acid + 8 others. Without QMP, workers start laying (unfertilized) eggs within hours."}},{"k":"released_from_stinger_1_bee_stings_10_","v":{"s":"Released from stinger. 1 bee stings → 10 more come. Never eat bananas near a hive!"}},{"k":"released_from_nasonov_gland_abdomen_ti","v":{"s":"Released from Nasonov gland (abdomen tip). Workers fan wings + expose gland = scent beacon."}},{"k":"released_by_larvae_triggers_nurse_bees","v":{"s":"Released by larvae — triggers nurse bees to supply food and causes workers to cap cells at the right time."}},{"k":"foot_printing_tarsi_glands_waggle_danc","v":{"s":"Foot-printing (tarsi glands) + waggle dance. A good find = more pheromone = more workers sent."}},{"k":"wherever_she_walks_tarsal_gland_secret","v":{"s":"Wherever she walks, tarsal gland secretion marks the surface as \"hers\". Colony recognizes home."}},{"k":"brood_chamber","v":{"s":"Brood Chamber"}},{"k":"lay_eggs_produce_new_workers","v":{"s":"Lay eggs — produce new workers"}},{"k":"honey_store","v":{"s":"Honey Store"}},{"k":"store_nectar_converted_to_honey","v":{"s":"Store nectar converted to honey"}},{"k":"pollen_vault","v":{"s":"Pollen Vault"}},{"k":"store_pollen_for_brood_food","v":{"s":"Store pollen for brood food"}},{"k":"guard_post","v":{"s":"Guard Post"}},{"k":"station_guards_to_detect_intruders","v":{"s":"Station guards to detect intruders"}},{"k":"royal_nursery","v":{"s":"Royal Nursery"}},{"k":"produce_royal_jelly_from_nurse_bees","v":{"s":"Produce royal jelly from nurse bees"}},{"k":"fanning_station","v":{"s":"Fanning Station"}},{"k":"temperature_control_scent_distribution","v":{"s":"Temperature control & scent distribution"}},{"k":"lay_worker_eggs","v":{"s":"Lay Worker Eggs"}},{"k":"fertilized_eggs_workers_in_21_days","v":{"s":"Fertilized eggs → workers in 21 days"}},{"k":"lay_drone_eggs","v":{"s":"Lay Drone Eggs"}},{"k":"unfertilized_eggs_drones_for_mating","v":{"s":"Unfertilized eggs → drones for mating"}},{"k":"emit_qmp","v":{"s":"Emit QMP"}},{"k":"release_queen_mandibular_pheromone_sup","v":{"s":"Release Queen Mandibular Pheromone — suppress worker rebellion, boost morale"}},{"k":"alarm_signal","v":{"s":"Alarm Signal"}},{"k":"release_alarm_pheromone_mobilize_guard","v":{"s":"Release alarm pheromone — mobilize guards against threat"}},{"k":"nasonov_rally","v":{"s":"Nasonov Rally"}},{"k":"release_nasonov_call_foragers_home_mar","v":{"s":"Release Nasonov — call foragers home, mark safe areas"}},{"k":"order_comb","v":{"s":"Order Comb"}},{"k":"direct_builders_to_construct_new_comb_","v":{"s":"Direct builders to construct new comb cells"}},{"k":"no_fanning_stations_brood_temperature_","v":{"s":"❄️ No fanning stations! Brood temperature dropping — larvae at risk."}},{"k":"swarm_phase_colony_is_crowded_manage_p","v":{"s":"🐝 SWARM PHASE — Colony is crowded! Manage pheromones carefully or half the workers leave with a new queen."}},{"k":"defend_phase_threats_are_increasing_bu","v":{"s":"⚔️ DEFEND PHASE — Threats are increasing. Build guard posts and keep alarm pheromone ready."}},{"k":"qmp_too_low_workers_becoming_restless_","v":{"s":"⚠️ QMP too low — workers becoming restless! Some are developing ovaries."}},{"k":"swarm_qmp_couldn_t_hold_them_half_your","v":{"s":"🐝🐝🐝 SWARM! QMP couldn't hold them — half your workers left with a rebel queen! Rebuild with stronger pheromones."}},{"k":"wasp_raider","v":{"s":"Wasp Raider"}},{"k":"a_hornet_is_probing_the_entrance","v":{"s":"A hornet is probing the entrance!"}},{"k":"robber_bees","v":{"s":"Robber Bees"}},{"k":"foreign_bees_are_trying_to_steal_honey","v":{"s":"Foreign bees are trying to steal honey!"}},{"k":"mouse_intruder","v":{"s":"Mouse Intruder"}},{"k":"a_mouse_is_trying_to_nest_inside_the_h","v":{"s":"A mouse is trying to nest inside the hive!"}},{"k":"varroa_spike","v":{"s":"Varroa Spike"}},{"k":"varroa_mites_are_multiplying_on_brood","v":{"s":"Varroa mites are multiplying on brood!"}},{"k":"hive_beetle","v":{"s":"Hive Beetle"}},{"k":"small_hive_beetles_are_tunneling_throu","v":{"s":"Small hive beetles are tunneling through comb!"}},{"k":"giant_hornet","v":{"s":"Giant Hornet"}},{"k":"a_giant_hornet_one_can_kill_40_bees_pe","v":{"s":"A giant hornet — one can kill 40 bees per minute!"}},{"k":"starvation_no_nectar_workers_are_dying","v":{"s":"🚨 STARVATION — No nectar! Workers are dying."}},{"k":"alarm","v":{"s":"Alarm"}},{"k":"nasonov","v":{"s":"Nasonov"}},{"k":"brood","v":{"s":"Brood"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"beehive_colony_simulator","v":{"s":"🐝 Beehive Colony Simulator"}},{"k":"manage_a_living_superorganism_50_000_m","v":{"s":"Manage a living superorganism — 50,000 minds, one purpose"}},{"k":"export_colony_report_to_clipboard","v":{"s":"Export colony report to clipboard"}},{"k":"keyboard_shortcuts","v":{"s":"Keyboard shortcuts"}},{"k":"keyboard_shortcuts_2","v":{"s":"Keyboard shortcuts"}},{"k":"simulation_perspective","v":{"s":"Simulation perspective"}},{"k":"beekeeper","v":{"s":"Beekeeper"}},{"k":"manage_the_colony_from_outside","v":{"s":"Manage the colony from outside"}},{"k":"queen_rts","v":{"s":"Queen RTS"}},{"k":"command_the_hive_from_within","v":{"s":"Explore a colony strategy abstraction"}},{"k":"drone_flight","v":{"s":"Drone Flight"}},{"k":"fly_the_nuptial_flight_in_3d","v":{"s":"Fly the nuptial flight in 3D"}},{"k":"toggle_fullscreen_canvas","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen canvas"}},{"k":"toggle_fullscreen","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen"}},{"k":"tip_click_the_hive_beekeeper_or_meadow","v":{"s":"💡 Tip: click the hive, beekeeper, or meadow to dive in"}},{"k":"educational_topic_views","v":{"s":"Educational topic views"}},{"k":"scene","v":{"s":"Scene"}},{"k":"anatomy","v":{"s":"Anatomy"}},{"k":"flight_physics","v":{"s":"Flight Physics"}},{"k":"lifecycle","v":{"s":"Lifecycle"}},{"k":"honey","v":{"s":"Honey"}},{"k":"waggle_dance_2","v":{"s":"Waggle Dance"}},{"k":"thermoregulation_2","v":{"s":"Thermoregulation"}},{"k":"castes","v":{"s":"Castes"}},{"k":"pheromones","v":{"s":"Pheromones"}},{"k":"threats","v":{"s":"Threats"}},{"k":"drone_nuptial_flight","v":{"s":"Drone Nuptial Flight"}},{"k":"experience_life_as_a_drone_bee_your_so","v":{"s":"Experience life as a drone bee. Fly into the game’s 100–130 ft Drone Congregation Area target and find a queen. Flight time varies by difficulty: 150 / 110 / 75 seconds. Only about 1 in 1,000 drones succeeds in this model — can you?"}},{"k":"easy","v":{"s":"🌱 Easy"}},{"k":"150s_fewer_birds_closer_queen","v":{"s":"150s, fewer birds, closer queen"}},{"k":"normal","v":{"s":"🐝 Normal"}},{"k":"110s_balanced_challenge","v":{"s":"110s, balanced challenge"}},{"k":"hard","v":{"s":"🔥 Hard"}},{"k":"75s_more_birds_distant_queen","v":{"s":"75s, more birds, distant queen"}},{"k":"arrow_keys_wasd_steer_space_climb_shif","v":{"s":"Arrow keys / WASD = steer · Space = climb · Shift = descend · Fly low near glowing markers to chain training boosts."}},{"k":"drone_flight_simulation_use_arrow_keys","v":{"s":"Drone flight simulation — use arrow keys to fly"}},{"k":"end_flight","v":{"s":"End flight"}},{"k":"end_flight_2","v":{"s":"✕ End Flight"}},{"k":"drone_science_facts_discovered","v":{"s":"📚 Drone Science Facts Discovered"}},{"k":"keep_flying_to_discover_facts","v":{"s":"Keep flying to discover facts..."}},{"k":"queen_command_hive_rts","v":{"s":"Queen Signals: Colony Strategy Model"}},{"k":"you_are_the_queen_control_your_colony_","v":{"s":"Model colony strategy through pheromone signals, worker allocation, comb building, and defense. Real queens do not issue orders: worker groups make decentralized decisions in response to chemical and environmental cues. Building and combat are game abstractions."}},{"k":"three_phases_of_your_reign","v":{"s":"📋 Three Phases of Colony Strategy"}},{"k":"establish_your_colony_build_structures","v":{"s":"Establish your colony. Build structures, lay eggs, stockpile resources. Threats are rare."}},{"k":"threats_double_wasps_robber_bees_horne","v":{"s":"Threats double. Wasps, robber bees, hornets attack. Build guard posts, keep alarm pheromone ready."}},{"k":"colony_wants_to_split_keep_qmp_above_4","v":{"s":"Colony wants to split! Keep QMP above 40 or half your workers leave. Survive the swarm impulse."}},{"k":"begin_your_reign","v":{"s":"👑 Begin Strategy Model"}},{"k":"queen_defense_game_release_pheromones_","v":{"s":"Colony strategy game: model pheromone signals and build structures to explore how a colony responds to threats"}},{"k":"pheromone_commands","v":{"s":"👑 Pheromone Signals"}},{"k":"build_comb_structures","v":{"s":"🏗️ Build Comb Structures"}},{"k":"next_day","v":{"s":"⏩ Next Day"}},{"k":"end_game","v":{"s":"End Game"}},{"k":"hive_events_log_latest_colony_events_a","v":{"s":"Hive events log — latest colony events and alerts"}},{"k":"hive_events","v":{"s":"📋 Hive Events"}},{"k":"bee_stock_collapsed","v":{"s":"Bee stock (collapsed)"}},{"k":"change","v":{"s":"Change ▾"}},{"k":"choose_honeybee_subspecies","v":{"s":"Choose honeybee subspecies"}},{"k":"choose_your_bee_stock","v":{"s":"🧬 Choose Your Bee Stock"}},{"k":"real_honeybees_come_in_distinct_geneti","v":{"s":"Real honeybees come in distinct genetic lines, each adapted to different climates. Pick a subspecies — it will modify honey yield, winter survival, spring buildup, and varroa resistance throughout your colony's life."}},{"k":"collapse_stock_picker","v":{"s":"Collapse stock picker"}},{"k":"collapse","v":{"s":"▲ Collapse"}},{"k":"selected","v":{"s":"✓ Selected"}},{"k":"choose_apiary_site","v":{"s":"Choose apiary site"}},{"k":"choose_your_apiary_site","v":{"s":"📍 Choose Your Apiary Site"}},{"k":"real_beekeepers_spend_weeks_choosing_a","v":{"s":"Real beekeepers spend weeks choosing a site. Forage range, sun exposure, wind, water, and neighbors all matter. Your choice modifies nectar yield and disease pressure for this colony's life."}},{"k":"selected_2","v":{"s":"✓ Selected"}},{"k":"skip_tutorial","v":{"s":"Skip tutorial"}},{"k":"skip","v":{"s":"Skip"}},{"k":"back_2","v":{"s":"← Back"}},{"k":"honey_harvested","v":{"s":"Honey harvested"}},{"k":"varietals_produced","v":{"s":"Varietals produced"}},{"k":"peak_workforce","v":{"s":"Peak workforce"}},{"k":"min_varroa","v":{"s":"Min. varroa"}},{"k":"conservations","v":{"s":"Conservations"}},{"k":"events_handled","v":{"s":"Events handled"}},{"k":"total_nectar_honey","v":{"s":"Total nectar → honey"}},{"k":"flower_visits","v":{"s":"Flower visits"}},{"k":"exported_colony_report","v":{"s":"Exported colony report"}},{"k":"colony_report_copy_manually","v":{"s":"📄 Colony Report — Copy Manually"}},{"k":"close_export","v":{"s":"Close export"}},{"k":"select_all_text_below_ctrl_cmd_a_then_","v":{"s":"Select all text below (Ctrl/Cmd+A), then copy:"}},{"k":"keyboard_shortcuts_3","v":{"s":"Keyboard shortcuts"}},{"k":"keyboard_shortcuts_4","v":{"s":"⌨️ Keyboard Shortcuts"}},{"k":"close_shortcuts","v":{"s":"Close shortcuts"}},{"k":"educational_views","v":{"s":"🔬 Educational Views"}},{"k":"achievement_badges","v":{"s":"Achievement badges"}},{"k":"close_badges","v":{"s":"Close badges"}},{"k":"bee_knowledge_quiz","v":{"s":"Bee Knowledge Quiz"}},{"k":"bee_knowledge_quiz_2","v":{"s":"🎓 Bee Knowledge Quiz"}},{"k":"close_quiz","v":{"s":"Close quiz"}},{"k":"choose_a_varroa_treatment","v":{"s":"Choose a varroa treatment"}},{"k":"integrated_pest_management_varroa","v":{"s":"🧪 Integrated Pest Management — Varroa"}},{"k":"close_treatment_modal","v":{"s":"Close treatment modal"}},{"k":"every_treatment_has_trade_offs_real_be","v":{"s":"Every treatment has trade-offs. Real beekeepers rotate treatments seasonally to prevent mite resistance. Effectiveness depends on brood status and temperature."}},{"k":"ideal_season","v":{"s":"✨ ideal season"}},{"k":"monitoring_threshold","v":{"s":"Monitoring threshold: "}},{"k":"treat_when_mite_count_exceeds_2_of_bee","v":{"s":"Treat when mite count exceeds ~2% of bees (about 15–20% on the Varroa meter here). Below that, the colony can usually manage mites via hygienic behavior."}},{"k":"science","v":{"s":"🔬 Science: "}},{"k":"acknowledge","v":{"s":"✓ Acknowledge"}},{"k":"colony_dashboard_showing_population_re","v":{"s":"Colony dashboard showing population, resources, and health metrics"}},{"k":"colony_status","v":{"s":"🐝 Colony Status"}},{"k":"mite_control","v":{"s":"mite control"}},{"k":"honey_stores","v":{"s":"honey stores"}},{"k":"nurses","v":{"s":"Nurses"}},{"k":"feed_larvae_royal_jelly_bee_bread_ages","v":{"s":"Feed larvae royal jelly + bee bread (ages 3–10 days)"}},{"k":"builders","v":{"s":"Builders"}},{"k":"secrete_wax_draw_comb_ages_11_17_days","v":{"s":"Secrete wax + draw comb (ages 11–17 days)"}},{"k":"foragers","v":{"s":"Foragers"}},{"k":"bring_back_nectar_pollen_water_ages_21","v":{"s":"Bring back nectar + pollen + water (ages 21+ days)"}},{"k":"guards","v":{"s":"Guards"}},{"k":"sentries_at_the_entrance_recognize_sis","v":{"s":"Sentries at the entrance, recognize sister vs intruder"}},{"k":"scouts","v":{"s":"Scouts"}},{"k":"find_new_forage_new_nest_sites_waggle_","v":{"s":"Find new forage + new nest sites (waggle dance)"}},{"k":"fanners","v":{"s":"Fanners"}},{"k":"beat_wings_at_230_hz_to_cool_evaporate","v":{"s":"Beat wings at 230 Hz to cool + evaporate honey moisture"}},{"k":"undertakers","v":{"s":"Undertakers"}},{"k":"carry_dead_bees_out_of_the_hive_colony","v":{"s":"Carry dead bees out of the hive (colony hygiene)"}},{"k":"clusterers","v":{"s":"Clusterers"}},{"k":"vibrate_flight_muscles_to_keep_the_que","v":{"s":"Vibrate flight muscles to keep the queen at 95°F all winter"}},{"k":"workforce_by_role","v":{"s":"👷 Workforce by role"}},{"k":"workers","v":{"s":"👷 Workers"}},{"k":"brood_2","v":{"s":"🥚 Brood"}},{"k":"drones","v":{"s":"♂ Drones"}},{"k":"queen_2","v":{"s":"👑 Queen"}},{"k":"healthy","v":{"s":"✓ Healthy"}},{"k":"watch","v":{"s":"⚠ Watch"}},{"k":"danger","v":{"s":"⛔ Danger"}},{"k":"what_to_do","v":{"s":"What to do: "}},{"k":"workers_2","v":{"s":"Workers"}},{"k":"honey_2","v":{"s":"Honey"}},{"k":"varroa","v":{"s":"Varroa"}},{"k":"morale","v":{"s":"Morale"}},{"k":"reflection_question","v":{"s":"Reflection question"}},{"k":"reflection_think_about_it","v":{"s":"Reflection · think about it"}},{"k":"show_another_reflection_question","v":{"s":"Show another reflection question"}},{"k":"another","v":{"s":"Another →"}},{"k":"hive_cross_section","v":{"s":"🏠 Hive Cross-Section"}},{"k":"honey_stores_2","v":{"s":"← Honey stores"}},{"k":"brood_nest_center","v":{"s":"Brood nest (center)"}},{"k":"honey_stores_3","v":{"s":"Honey stores →"}},{"k":"bees_organize_the_comb_concentrically_","v":{"s":"Bees organize the comb concentrically: brood in the warm center, pollen ring around it, honey stores on the outer edges. This pattern is universal across all Apis mellifera colonies."}},{"k":"garden_connection_active","v":{"s":"Garden Connection Active! "}},{"k":"honey_varietals_pantry","v":{"s":"Honey varietals pantry"}},{"k":"unifloral_honeys_single_flower_source_","v":{"s":"Unifloral honeys (single flower source) are rare and prized. Most supermarket honey is multifloral blend. The flavor, color, and crystallization speed all come from the botanical source."}},{"k":"pollination_impact_statistics","v":{"s":"Pollination impact statistics"}},{"k":"pollination_impact_ecosystem_services","v":{"s":"🌸 Pollination Impact · Ecosystem Services"}},{"k":"every_3rd_bite_of_food_you_eat_depends","v":{"s":"Every 3rd bite of food you eat depends on pollinators. Honeybees alone contribute ~$15 billion/year to U.S. agriculture — and that's before counting the 4,000+ native bee species that pollinate wild plants."}},{"k":"beekeeper_journal_entries","v":{"s":"Beekeeper journal entries"}},{"k":"real_beekeepers_keep_hive_journals_for","v":{"s":"Real beekeepers keep hive journals for every inspection. Patterns across weeks reveal colony trajectory that a single reading misses."}},{"k":"colony_collapse","v":{"s":"Colony Collapse"}},{"k":"your_colony_has_fallen_below_500_worke","v":{"s":"Your colony has fallen below 500 workers and can no longer sustain itself."}},{"k":"diagnosis_what_went_wrong","v":{"s":"🔬 Diagnosis: What went wrong?"}},{"k":"next_time_try","v":{"s":"💡 Next time, try:"}},{"k":"treat_varroa_mites_as_soon_as_levels_e","v":{"s":"Treat varroa mites as soon as levels exceed 15–20%"}},{"k":"feed_sugar_syrup_before_honey_drops_be","v":{"s":"Feed before stores fall below the current seasonal reserve"}},{"k":"plant_wildflowers_and_build_bee_hotels","v":{"s":"Plant wildflowers and build bee hotels to improve habitat"}},{"k":"monitor_colony_health_every_few_days_n","v":{"s":"Monitor colony health every few days, not just when problems appear"}},{"k":"plant_a_companion_garden_connects_to_t","v":{"s":"Plant a companion garden (connects to the Companion Planting tool!)"}},{"k":"start_a_new_colony_from_scratch","v":{"s":"Start a new colony from scratch"}},{"k":"start_new_colony_apply_what_you_learne","v":{"s":"🔄 Start New Colony — Apply What You Learned"}},{"k":"master_beekeeper_you_ve_seen_the_full_","v":{"s":"Master beekeeper. You've seen the full sim — try the Queen RTS or Drone Flight modes next."}},{"k":"next_badges_to_earn","v":{"s":"Next badges to earn"}},{"k":"next_badges","v":{"s":"Next Badges"}},{"k":"see_all_badges_and_their_requirements","v":{"s":"See all badges and their requirements"}},{"k":"see_all","v":{"s":"See all →"}},{"k":"local_habitat","v":{"s":"🌳 Local Habitat"}},{"k":"pesticide_exposure","v":{"s":"☠️ Pesticide Exposure"}},{"k":"next_day_2","v":{"s":"⏩ Next Day"}},{"k":"advance_30_days_1_month","v":{"s":"Advance 30 days (1 month)"}},{"k":"smoke","v":{"s":"Smoke"}},{"k":"smoke_the_hive_to_calm_bees_before_ope","v":{"s":"Smoke the hive to calm bees before opening"}},{"k":"inspect","v":{"s":"Inspect"}},{"k":"open_hive_inspector_explore_bee_biolog","v":{"s":"Open hive inspector — explore bee biology"}},{"k":"treat","v":{"s":"Treat"}},{"k":"choose_an_ipm_treatment_each_has_seaso","v":{"s":"Choose an IPM treatment — each has seasonal trade-offs"}},{"k":"hygiene","v":{"s":"Hygiene"}},{"k":"clean_comb_remove_dead_bees_improve_ve","v":{"s":"Clean comb, remove dead bees, improve ventilation (−disease risk)"}},{"k":"super","v":{"s":"Super"}},{"k":"add_honey_super_10_morale_2_wax","v":{"s":"Add honey super (+10 morale, +2 wax)"}},{"k":"harvest","v":{"s":"Harvest"}},{"k":"harvest_surplus_honey_need_15_lbs","v":{"s":"Harvest surplus honey above the current seasonal reserve"}},{"k":"feed_sugar_syrup_5_lbs_honey_5_morale","v":{"s":"Feed sugar syrup (+5 lbs honey, +5 morale)"}},{"k":"requeen","v":{"s":"Requeen"}},{"k":"install_a_new_queen_restores_queenheal","v":{"s":"Install a new queen — restores queenHealth to 100"}},{"k":"conservation_actions_help_save_bees","v":{"s":"🌍 Conservation Actions (help save bees!)"}},{"k":"plain_2","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"using_simple_everyday_words_and_short__2","v":{"s":"using simple everyday words and short sentences"}},{"k":"grade_5_2","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"for_a_5th_grade_student_brief_and_frie_2","v":{"s":"for a 5th grade student, brief and friendly"}},{"k":"high_school_2","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"for_a_high_school_biology_student_scie_2","v":{"s":"for a high school biology student, scientifically accurate"}},{"k":"ai_beehive_tutor","v":{"s":"AI beehive tutor"}},{"k":"reading_level_2","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"click_explain_for_the_ai_tutor_to_desc","v":{"s":"Click “Explain” for the AI tutor to describe the current view."}},{"k":"bee_science_quick_reference_cards","v":{"s":"Bee science quick reference cards"}},{"k":"the_waggle_dance","v":{"s":"💃 The Waggle Dance"}},{"k":"when_a_forager_finds_nectar_she_perfor","v":{"s":"When a forager finds nectar, she performs a figure-8 dance on the comb. The waggle-run angle encodes direction relative to the sun, and its duration encodes distance. Karl von Frisch shared the 1973 Nobel Prize for decoding this landmark example of symbolic referential communication in an invertebrate."}},{"k":"the_superorganism","v":{"s":"🧠 The Superorganism"}},{"k":"a_honeybee_colony_is_a_superorganism_5","v":{"s":"A honeybee colony is a superorganism — thousands of individuals coordinating as one system. The queen is the reproductive system; workers collectively provide many digestive, defensive, and information-processing functions; drones carry a reproductive role. The brood area is usually kept near 34–36°C (93–97°F) through heating, fanning, water evaporation, clustering, and worker movement."}},{"k":"from_nectar_to_honey","v":{"s":"🍯 From Nectar to Honey"}},{"k":"nectar_is_80_water_bees_convert_it_to_","v":{"s":"Nectar is mostly water. Bees turn it into honey through enzymatic processing and evaporative concentration, then cap cells when moisture is low enough. Properly ripened honey stored sealed and dry can remain stable for a very long time, but absorbed moisture can allow osmophilic yeasts to ferment it."}},{"k":"colony_collapse_disorder_2","v":{"s":"⚠️ Colony Collapse Disorder"}},{"k":"since_2006_beekeepers_have_reported_lo","v":{"s":"Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD) describes a specific syndrome in which most adult workers disappear while the queen, brood, and food remain. It is not a label for every colony loss. Honey bee health reflects interacting pressures including Varroa and viruses, nutrition and forage, pesticide exposure, weather, queen problems, and management conditions."}},{"k":"optimal_35_c","v":{"s":"🟢 Typical brood range (34–36 °C)"}},{"k":"brood_healthy_colony_thermoregulating","v":{"s":"Brood healthy. Colony thermoregulating."}},{"k":"overheating","v":{"s":"🔥 Overheating"}},{"k":"brood_at_risk_above_36_c","v":{"s":"Brood at risk above 36°C."}},{"k":"chilled_brood","v":{"s":"🧊 Chilled brood"}},{"k":"below_32_c_development_fails","v":{"s":"Below 32°C development fails."}},{"k":"within_tolerance","v":{"s":"🟡 Within tolerance"}},{"k":"maintains_range_but_costs_energy","v":{"s":"Maintains range but costs energy."}},{"k":"hive_thermoregulation_discovery","v":{"s":"🐝 Hive thermoregulation discovery"}},{"k":"sliders_for_outside_temp_fanning_bees_","v":{"s":"Sliders for outside temp, fanning bees, brood count. Discrete 4-state thermal regime. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_at_40_c_outside_how_many_fa","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: At 40°C outside, how many fanning bees keep the brood area near 34–36°C?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"brood_tolerance_is_35_0_5_c_investigat","v":{"s":"Healthy brood generally develops near 34–36°C. Investigate how bees limit temperature swings."}},{"k":"what_is_the_cost_of_constant_fanning","v":{"s":"What is the cost of constant fanning?"}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_how_the_colony_as_a_superorgan","v":{"s":"Explain how the colony as a superorganism thermoregulates."}},{"k":"design_note_discrete_4_state_thermal_m","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 4-state thermal marker; no temperature score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"current_sim_goal","v":{"s":"Current simulation goal"}},{"k":"play_focus","v":{"s":"Play focus"}},{"k":"pollination","v":{"s":"Pollination"}},{"k":"equipment","v":{"s":"Equipment"}},{"k":"cognition","v":{"s":"Cognition"}},{"k":"vision","v":{"s":"Vision"}},{"k":"propolis","v":{"s":"Propolis"}},{"k":"stingers","v":{"s":"Stingers"}},{"k":"buzz","v":{"s":"Buzz"}},{"k":"bee_field_guide","v":{"s":"Bee field guide"}},{"k":"close_field_guide","v":{"s":"Close field guide"}},{"k":"field_guide_topics","v":{"s":"Field guide topics"}},{"k":"open_field_guide","v":{"s":"Open bee field guide"}},{"k":"field_guide","v":{"s":"Field Guide — species, anatomy, waggle dance, and more"}},{"k":"split","v":{"s":"Split"}},{"k":"split_the_colony_tip","v":{"s":"Divide a crowded colony into two instead of losing half to a swarm. Spring or early summer only, and it costs this year’s crop (2 AP)"}},{"k":"winterize","v":{"s":"Winterize"}},{"k":"winterize_tip","v":{"s":"Wrap the hive, insulate over the cluster and fit a mouse guard so the colony burns less honey through winter. Autumn onward (2 AP)"}},{"k":"bay3d_hide","v":{"s":"Hide 3D"}},{"k":"bay3d_hide_details","v":{"s":"Hide details for"}},{"k":"bay3d_host_too_old","v":{"s":"The 3D view needs a newer STEAM Lab host than this build has. Everything else in this simulation still works."}},{"k":"bay3d_loading","v":{"s":"Loading the 3D view…"}},{"k":"bay3d_reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset view"}},{"k":"bay3d_rotate_left","v":{"s":"Rotate left"}},{"k":"bay3d_rotate_right","v":{"s":"Rotate right"}},{"k":"bay3d_show","v":{"s":"Show 3D"}},{"k":"bay3d_show_details","v":{"s":"Show details for"}},{"k":"bay3d_tilt_down","v":{"s":"Tilt down"}},{"k":"bay3d_tilt_up","v":{"s":"Tilt up"}},{"k":"bay3d_zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"bay3d_zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"bees_only_insects_warm_year_round","v":{"s":"Bees are the only insects that stay warm year-round. Solo they're cold-blooded — together they're collectively warm-blooded."}},{"k":"hive3d_blurb","v":{"s":"Drag to turn the hive, or use the buttons and the parts list. The comb is drawn cell by cell from this colony right now — worker and drone brood, pollen, honey, and the gaps a failing queen leaves. Supers you add appear on the stack, the meadow follows the season, and picking the frames or the queen slides the front frame out the way an inspection lifts it."}},{"k":"hive3d_close_aria","v":{"s":"Close hive: stack the boxes back together"}},{"k":"hive3d_close_label","v":{"s":"⇊ Close hive"}},{"k":"hive3d_eyebrow","v":{"s":"🧰 The hive in 3D"}},{"k":"hive3d_fallback","v":{"s":"The 3D hive could not start on this device, usually because WebGL is unavailable or blocked. The hive cross-section in the Inspect hive panel shows the same structure."}},{"k":"hive3d_frame_group_aria","v":{"s":"Choose which brood frame to read"}},{"k":"hive3d_frame_next","v":{"s":"Next frame, toward the back of the box"}},{"k":"hive3d_frame_prev","v":{"s":"Previous frame, toward the front of the box"}},{"k":"hive3d_open_aria","v":{"s":"Open hive: lift the boxes apart in inspection order — outer cover, inner cover, honey super, queen excluder, then the brood frames"}},{"k":"hive3d_open_label","v":{"s":"⇈ Open hive"}},{"k":"hive3d_pick_prompt","v":{"s":"Pick a part of the hive"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_cluster_aria","v":{"s":"Queen in cluster: the colony is wintering, so she cannot be shown on a frame"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_cluster_label","v":{"s":"❄ Queen in cluster"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_cluster_title","v":{"s":"Queen in cluster"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_find_aria","v":{"s":"Find the queen: draw out the front frame and highlight her"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_find_title","v":{"s":"Find the queen"}},{"k":"hive3d_queen_pressed_aria","v":{"s":"Find the queen: pressed. Stop highlighting her and slide the frame back"}},{"k":"hive3d_title","v":{"s":"3D hive"}},{"k":"hyaluronidase_mcd_peptide_and_more","v":{"s":"hyaluronidase, MCD peptide and more"}},{"k":"inside_vs_outside_temp_year","v":{"s":"Inside vs Outside temperature through the year:"}},{"k":"on_the_comb_vertical_surface","v":{"s":"ON THE COMB (vertical surface, in the dark hive)"}},{"k":"other_peptides_enzymes","v":{"s":"Other peptides & enzymes"}},{"k":"percentages_by_dry_weight","v":{"s":"percentages by dry weight"}},{"k":"queen3d_eyebrow","v":{"s":"🗺️ The frontline in 3D"}},{"k":"queen3d_fallback","v":{"s":"The 3D forage map could not start on this device, usually because WebGL is unavailable or blocked. The 2D battlefield above carries the same frontline reading."}},{"k":"queen3d_pick_prompt","v":{"s":"Pick something on the map"}},{"k":"queen3d_title","v":{"s":"3D forage map"}},{"k":"venom_apitoxin","v":{"s":"Venom (Apitoxin)"}},{"k":"wingbeat_waveform","v":{"s":"Wingbeat Waveform"}},{"k":"wingbeats","v":{"s":"wingbeats"}},{"k":"colony_network","v":{"s":"Colony Network"}},{"k":"colony_network_strategy_model","v":{"s":"Colony Network: Distributed Strategy Model"}},{"k":"model_distributed_colony_signals","v":{"s":"Model distributed colony signals"}},{"k":"worker_alarm_response","v":{"s":"Worker Alarm Response"}},{"k":"worker_alarm_response_desc","v":{"s":"Threatened workers release alarm pheromone, recruiting guard workers to the danger"}},{"k":"worker_nasonov_relay","v":{"s":"Worker Nasonov Relay"}},{"k":"worker_nasonov_relay_desc","v":{"s":"Scenting workers expose Nasonov glands and fan, helping displaced nestmates orient"}},{"k":"close_treatment_choices","v":{"s":"Close treatment choices"}},{"k":"treatment_forecast_intro","v":{"s":"Each card uses the current brood and season to forecast this model's result. Compare mite change, colony cost, and the safety and timing note before applying. In a real apiary, follow the product label and local guidance."}}],"id":489}},{"k":"behaviorlab","v":{"o":[{"k":"first_food","v":{"s":"First Food"}},{"k":"in_positive_reinforcement_a_consequenc","v":{"s":"In positive reinforcement, a consequence is ADDED after a behavior to INCREASE the likelihood of that behavior occurring again. Your job: click \"Deliver Food\" immediately after the mouse presses the lever. Reinforce 10 lever presses!"}},{"k":"shape_up","v":{"s":"Shape Up!"}},{"k":"shaping_uses_successive_approximations","v":{"s":"Shaping uses successive approximations. The mouse won't spin on its own! Follow this 3-step sequence: (1) Reinforce \"Turning Right\" (↪️) to increase turning. (2) Once turns are frequent, wait for \"Half-Turn\" (↩️↪️) and reinforce those. (3) Finally, wait for full \"Spinning\" (🌀) and reinforce! Shape 5 complete spins through 3 stages of approximation!"}},{"k":"the_burst","v":{"s":"The Burst"}},{"k":"when_reinforcement_is_suddenly_withhel","v":{"s":"When reinforcement is suddenly withheld, the organism often shows an extinction burst — a temporary INCREASE in the behavior before it decreases. First, reinforce 5 lever presses, then STOP reinforcing and watch what happens!"}},{"k":"on_schedule","v":{"s":"On Schedule"}},{"k":"not_every_response_needs_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Not every response needs reinforcement! A Fixed Ratio (FR) schedule reinforces after a set number of responses. Try FR-3: reinforce every 3rd lever press. Watch how the mouse responds differently than continuous reinforcement!"}},{"k":"green_means_go","v":{"s":"Green Means Go"}},{"k":"a_discriminative_stimulus_sd_signals_t","v":{"s":"A discriminative stimulus (SD) signals that reinforcement is available. The green light = SD (reinforce lever presses). Red light = S-delta (do NOT reinforce). Teach the mouse to press only when the green light is on!"}},{"k":"free_lab","v":{"s":"Free Lab"}},{"k":"welcome_to_the_free_lab_all_tools_are_","v":{"s":"Welcome to the Free Lab! All tools are unlocked. Design your own experiment. Try shaping a new behavior, testing different schedules, or building a behavior chain. Happy experimenting!"}},{"k":"chain_reaction","v":{"s":"Chain Reaction"}},{"k":"a_behavior_chain_links_multiple_behavi","v":{"s":"A behavior chain links multiple behaviors in a specific sequence. The completion of one step becomes the signal (SD) for the next. Teach the mouse this chain: Sniff ➜ Rear Up ➜ Press Lever. Reinforce ONLY when the full 3-step chain is completed!"}},{"k":"not_that","v":{"s":"Not That!"}},{"k":"dro_differential_reinforcement_of_othe","v":{"s":"DRO (Differential Reinforcement of Other behavior) means reinforcing the ABSENCE of a specific behavior for a set time interval. A countdown timer runs — if the mouse does NOT press the lever before the timer finishes, deliver food! If the mouse presses the lever, the timer resets. Deliver 5 successful DRO intervals!"}},{"k":"pavlov_s_bell","v":{"s":"Pavlov's Bell"}},{"k":"classical_conditioning_pairs_a_neutral","v":{"s":"Classical conditioning pairs a neutral stimulus (bell) with an unconditioned stimulus (food) that naturally causes a response (salivation). After repeated pairings the bell ALONE triggers salivation! Phase 1: Ring the bell — nothing happens. Phase 2: Pair bell + food 5 times. Phase 3: Ring bell alone and watch for the conditioned response!"}},{"k":"first_feeder","v":{"s":"First Feeder"}},{"k":"mastered_positive_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Mastered positive reinforcement"}},{"k":"shape_shifter","v":{"s":"Shape Shifter"}},{"k":"shaped_behavior_through_approximations","v":{"s":"Shaped behavior through approximations"}},{"k":"burst_observer","v":{"s":"Burst Observer"}},{"k":"witnessed_the_extinction_burst","v":{"s":"Witnessed the extinction burst"}},{"k":"scheduler","v":{"s":"Scheduler"}},{"k":"implemented_fr_3_schedule","v":{"s":"Implemented FR-3 schedule"}},{"k":"signal_master","v":{"s":"Signal Master"}},{"k":"taught_stimulus_discrimination","v":{"s":"Taught stimulus discrimination"}},{"k":"free_thinker","v":{"s":"Free Thinker"}},{"k":"explored_the_sandbox_lab","v":{"s":"Explored the sandbox lab"}},{"k":"chain_builder","v":{"s":"Chain Builder"}},{"k":"completed_a_behavior_chain","v":{"s":"Completed a behavior chain"}},{"k":"dro_pro","v":{"s":"DRO Pro"}},{"k":"mastered_differential_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Mastered differential reinforcement"}},{"k":"pavlovian","v":{"s":"Pavlovian"}},{"k":"demonstrated_classical_conditioning","v":{"s":"Demonstrated classical conditioning"}},{"k":"ivan_pavlov","v":{"s":"Ivan Pavlov"}},{"k":"john_b_watson","v":{"s":"John B. Watson"}},{"k":"edward_thorndike","v":{"s":"Edward Thorndike"}},{"k":"b_f_skinner","v":{"s":"B.F. Skinner"}},{"k":"albert_bandura","v":{"s":"Albert Bandura"}},{"k":"baer_wolf_risley","v":{"s":"Baer, Wolf & Risley"}},{"k":"o_ivar_lovaas","v":{"s":"O. Ivar Lovaas"}},{"k":"murray_sidman","v":{"s":"Murray Sidman"}},{"k":"attention","v":{"s":"Attention"}},{"k":"behavior_maintained_by_social_attentio","v":{"s":"Behavior maintained by social attention from others. Example: A student calls out in class to get the teacher to look at them. The attention (even if negative) reinforces the calling out."}},{"k":"behavior_maintained_by_removal_of_an_a","v":{"s":"Behavior maintained by removal of an aversive stimulus. Example: A student has a meltdown when given math work, and the teacher removes the assignment. The meltdown is negatively reinforced."}},{"k":"tangible","v":{"s":"Tangible"}},{"k":"behavior_maintained_by_access_to_a_pre","v":{"s":"Behavior maintained by access to a preferred item or activity. Example: A child screams in the store until the parent buys them candy. The screaming is reinforced by getting the candy."}},{"k":"behavior_maintained_by_the_sensory_sti","v":{"s":"Behavior maintained by the sensory stimulation it produces, independent of social consequences. The behavior itself feels good. Example: Hand-flapping may produce proprioceptive input that is reinforcing."}},{"k":"autism_services","v":{"s":"Autism Services"}},{"k":"evidence_based_intervention_for_indivi","v":{"s":"Evidence-based intervention for individuals with autism. Teaches communication, social, self-care, and academic skills through systematic reinforcement and prompting strategies."}},{"k":"education_classroom_management","v":{"s":"Education & Classroom Management"}},{"k":"token_economies_positive_behavior_supp","v":{"s":"Token economies, positive behavior support (PBS), response to intervention (RTI), and group contingencies. Making learning reinforcing and reducing challenging behavior."}},{"k":"animal_training","v":{"s":"Animal Training"}},{"k":"clicker_training_shaping_and_chaining_","v":{"s":"Clicker training, shaping, and chaining are all ABA principles. Service dogs, marine mammals, and zoo animals are all trained using operant conditioning."}},{"k":"organizational_behavior_management_obm","v":{"s":"Organizational Behavior Management (OBM)"}},{"k":"applying_aba_principles_to_improve_emp","v":{"s":"Applying ABA principles to improve employee performance, safety, and satisfaction. Feedback systems, incentive programs, and performance management."}},{"k":"sports_performance","v":{"s":"Sports Performance"}},{"k":"coaches_use_shaping_reinforcement_and_","v":{"s":"Coaches use shaping, reinforcement, and behavioral rehearsal to improve athletic performance. Video feedback and goal-setting based on behavioral principles."}},{"k":"health_fitness","v":{"s":"Health & Fitness"}},{"k":"habit_formation_self_monitoring_contin","v":{"s":"Habit formation, self-monitoring, contingency contracts for exercise. Behavioral approaches to weight management, medication adherence, and addiction treatment."}},{"k":"app_game_design","v":{"s":"App & Game Design"}},{"k":"variable_ratio_reinforcement_in_social","v":{"s":"Variable ratio reinforcement in social media (likes, notifications). Shaping in game tutorials. Streaks and badges as conditioned reinforcers. Gamification IS applied behavior analysis."}},{"k":"environmental_sustainability","v":{"s":"Environmental Sustainability"}},{"k":"behavioral_interventions_for_recycling","v":{"s":"Behavioral interventions for recycling, energy conservation, and sustainable transportation. Feedback and reinforcement can change environmental behaviors at scale."}},{"k":"duration","v":{"s":"Duration"}},{"k":"latency","v":{"s":"Latency"}},{"k":"inter_response_time_irt","v":{"s":"Inter-Response Time (IRT)"}},{"k":"partial_interval_recording","v":{"s":"Partial Interval Recording"}},{"k":"whole_interval_recording","v":{"s":"Whole Interval Recording"}},{"k":"momentary_time_sampling","v":{"s":"Momentary Time Sampling"}},{"k":"tier_1_universal","v":{"s":"Tier 1 — Universal"}},{"k":"tier_2_targeted","v":{"s":"Tier 2 — Targeted"}},{"k":"tier_3_intensive","v":{"s":"Tier 3 — Intensive"}},{"k":"what_restraint_is_and_is_not","v":{"s":"What restraint IS (and is NOT)"}},{"k":"restraint_physical_holding_that_restri","v":{"s":"Restraint = physical holding that restricts a student's freedom of movement. Three types: physical (staff hands on student), mechanical (devices that restrict — almost never appropriate in schools), chemical (medication used for behavioral control rather than treatment — never appropriate without prescription)."}},{"k":"what_seclusion_is_and_is_not","v":{"s":"What seclusion IS (and is NOT)"}},{"k":"seclusion_involuntary_confinement_of_a","v":{"s":"Seclusion = involuntary confinement of a student alone in a room or area from which the student is physically prevented from leaving. The \"physically prevented from leaving\" part is what makes it seclusion legally."}},{"k":"maine_chapter_33_the_rule","v":{"s":"Maine Chapter 33 — the rule"}},{"k":"maine_permits_emergency_physical_restr","v":{"s":"Maine permits emergency physical restraint and seclusion ONLY when there is \"imminent danger of serious physical injury\" to the student or others. Property destruction alone is not sufficient justification. Documentation, debrief, and parent notification are mandatory within specific time windows. Specific staff training (currently CPI / NCI / Mandt or equivalent) is required for anyone authorized to perform restraint."}},{"k":"less_restrictive_alternative_principle","v":{"s":"Less-restrictive-alternative principle"}},{"k":"every_restraint_or_seclusion_use_must_","v":{"s":"Every restraint or seclusion use must be the least restrictive option available to manage the immediate safety concern. If a student can be safely de-escalated by clearing the room of others, that comes before restraint. If a student can be safely supported with verbal de-escalation, that comes before clearing the room. The hierarchy is built into law and ethics — not optional."}},{"k":"after_every_incident_the_debrief","v":{"s":"After every incident — the debrief"}},{"k":"within_24_48_hours_an_incident_report_","v":{"s":"Within ~24-48 hours: an incident report (what happened, who, when, duration, less-restrictive alternatives tried first). A staff debrief about what could be done differently next time. Parent notification (Maine requires same-day or next-business-day). A team meeting to revise the BIP if the incident reveals a pattern. The student debrief — when the student is fully recovered — to hear their perspective."}},{"k":"the_disability_community_position","v":{"s":"The disability-community position"}},{"k":"major_disability_rights_organizations_","v":{"s":"Major disability-rights organizations (ASAN, AAPD, COPAA, Disability Rights Network) have called for substantial federal restriction of school restraint and seclusion. Documented harms include physical injury, psychological trauma (PTSD outcomes are well-documented), and disproportionate use against students of color and students with disabilities. Federal data show students with disabilities account for ~12% of enrollment but ~75% of restraint cases."}},{"k":"calm","v":{"s":"Calm"}},{"k":"triggers","v":{"s":"Triggers"}},{"k":"agitation","v":{"s":"Agitation"}},{"k":"acceleration","v":{"s":"Acceleration"}},{"k":"peak","v":{"s":"Peak"}},{"k":"recovery","v":{"s":"Recovery"}},{"k":"a_kid_who_is_in_pain_cannot_perform_th","v":{"s":"A kid who is in pain cannot perform the same way a kid who is not in pain can. Period."}},{"k":"schools_often_see_the_aftermath_of_a_h","v":{"s":"Schools often see the AFTERMATH of a home event hours later — student is more dysregulated than usual but cannot or will not say why."}},{"k":"predictability_is_a_reinforcer_for_man","v":{"s":"Predictability is a reinforcer for many learners. Removing it changes thresholds across the whole day, not just the moment."}},{"k":"adolescent_social_events_have_a_half_l","v":{"s":"Adolescent social events have a half-life of days, not minutes. Behavior on Wednesday may trace to Friday."}},{"k":"for_sensory_sensitive_learners_the_env","v":{"s":"For sensory-sensitive learners, the environment itself is a continuous setting event. Reduce input and threshold rises."}},{"k":"trauma_anniversaries_and_seasonal_ment","v":{"s":"Trauma anniversaries and seasonal mental-health patterns are real and predictable. Calendar awareness is a clinical tool."}},{"k":"ari_ne_eman","v":{"s":"Ari Ne'eman"}},{"k":"temple_grandin","v":{"s":"Temple Grandin"}},{"k":"damian_milton","v":{"s":"Damian Milton"}},{"k":"henny_kupferstein","v":{"s":"Henny Kupferstein"}},{"k":"kassiane_asasumasu","v":{"s":"Kassiane Asasumasu"}},{"k":"mel_baggs_1980_2020","v":{"s":"Mel Baggs (1980–2020)"}},{"k":"the_autism_community_critique","v":{"s":"The autism community critique"}},{"k":"adult_autistic_advocates_including_man","v":{"s":"Adult autistic advocates — including many who experienced early ABA — have documented serious concerns about historical practice: 40-hour-week intensity, \"indistinguishable from peers\" as a goal (which trains masking), targeting stimming and eye-contact \"deficits\" without considering their function, and the use of contingencies that look like coercion when the client cannot meaningfully consent or refuse."}},{"k":"neurodiversity_affirming_aba","v":{"s":"Neurodiversity-affirming ABA"}},{"k":"modern_ethical_practice_has_shifted_cl","v":{"s":"Modern ethical practice has shifted: client assent (not just guardian consent) is required throughout sessions, \"normalization\" goals are increasingly declined, stimming is recognized as self-regulation rather than a target for reduction, and client-chosen goals replace clinician-imposed ones. The acronym stays; the values inside it have moved."}},{"k":"trauma_informed_behavior_practice","v":{"s":"Trauma-informed behavior practice"}},{"k":"some_non_compliance_is_a_trauma_respon","v":{"s":"Some \"non-compliance\" is a trauma response, not a learning deficit. A child who freezes, flees, or fights at a familiar demand may be telling you the demand has become a threat cue. Regulation comes before reasoning; co-regulation comes before self-regulation. Contingency analysis still matters — it just is not the whole picture."}},{"k":"the_double_empathy_problem","v":{"s":"The Double Empathy Problem"}},{"k":"research_by_autistic_scholar_damian_mi","v":{"s":"Research by autistic scholar Damian Milton reframes \"social skill deficits\" as a two-way mismatch in mutual understanding between autistic and non-autistic people, not a one-sided deficit located in the autistic person. This changes what we measure as a \"behavior problem\" — and who needs to do the changing."}},{"k":"what_aba_does_well","v":{"s":"What ABA does well"}},{"k":"honest_accounting_matters_functional_c","v":{"s":"Honest accounting matters. Functional communication training for non-speaking learners has changed lives. Reduction of self-injurious or dangerous behavior is sometimes the difference between a kid going to school and a kid getting hospitalized. Self-help skills the client actively wants. Schedules of reinforcement explain real classroom outcomes. The science is real; the application has to be ethical."}},{"k":"what_aba_cannot_do_alone","v":{"s":"What ABA cannot do alone"}},{"k":"operant_conditioning_is_one_tool_it_do","v":{"s":"Operant conditioning is one tool. It does not replace mental-health treatment for trauma or anxiety. It does not address the sensory environment a building creates. It does not substitute for autistic community and identity. Good practice integrates ABA with OT (sensory), SLP (communication), mental health (regulation), and — crucially — autistic adult mentorship that the child can grow into."}},{"k":"benefit_others","v":{"s":"Benefit Others"}},{"k":"aba_practitioners_have_a_responsibilit","v":{"s":"ABA practitioners have a responsibility to promote the well-being of their clients above all other considerations."}},{"k":"least_restrictive","v":{"s":"Least Restrictive"}},{"k":"always_use_the_least_restrictive_effec","v":{"s":"Always use the least restrictive effective treatment. Try reinforcement-based procedures before considering punishment-based ones."}},{"k":"informed_consent","v":{"s":"Informed Consent"}},{"k":"clients_or_their_guardians_must_unders","v":{"s":"Clients (or their guardians) must understand and agree to all intervention procedures before they are implemented."}},{"k":"data_driven_decisions","v":{"s":"Data-Driven Decisions"}},{"k":"all_treatment_decisions_must_be_based_","v":{"s":"All treatment decisions must be based on objective data, not opinions or assumptions. If data shows the intervention isn't working, change it."}},{"k":"social_validity","v":{"s":"Social Validity"}},{"k":"goals_procedures_and_outcomes_should_b","v":{"s":"Goals, procedures, and outcomes should be acceptable and meaningful to the client and their community."}},{"k":"competence","v":{"s":"Competence"}},{"k":"only_practice_within_your_area_of_trai","v":{"s":"Only practice within your area of training and competence. Seek supervision and continuing education."}},{"k":"fixed_ratio","v":{"s":"Fixed Ratio"}},{"k":"reinforce_every_nth_response_creates_p","v":{"s":"Reinforce every Nth response. Creates post-reinforcement pause then rapid responding."}},{"k":"variable_ratio","v":{"s":"Variable Ratio"}},{"k":"reinforce_after_variable_number_of_res","v":{"s":"Reinforce after variable number of responses (avg N). Produces high, steady rate. Most resistant to extinction."}},{"k":"fixed_interval","v":{"s":"Fixed Interval"}},{"k":"reinforce_first_response_after_fixed_t","v":{"s":"Reinforce first response after fixed time interval. Creates scallop pattern — slow after reinforcement, fast near end."}},{"k":"variable_interval","v":{"s":"Variable Interval"}},{"k":"reinforce_first_response_after_variabl","v":{"s":"Reinforce first response after variable time interval (avg N). Produces slow, steady rate."}},{"k":"positive_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Positive Reinforcement"}},{"k":"adding_a_stimulus_to_increase_behavior","v":{"s":"Adding a stimulus to increase behavior"}},{"k":"negative_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Negative Reinforcement"}},{"k":"removing_an_aversive_stimulus_to_incre","v":{"s":"Removing an aversive stimulus to increase behavior"}},{"k":"positive_punishment","v":{"s":"Positive Punishment"}},{"k":"adding_an_aversive_stimulus_to_decreas","v":{"s":"Adding an aversive stimulus to decrease behavior"}},{"k":"negative_punishment","v":{"s":"Negative Punishment"}},{"k":"removing_a_desired_stimulus_to_decreas","v":{"s":"Removing a desired stimulus to decrease behavior"}},{"k":"homework_complete","v":{"s":"Homework Complete"}},{"k":"on_task_15_min","v":{"s":"On-task 15 min"}},{"k":"act_of_kindness","v":{"s":"Act of Kindness"}},{"k":"clean_up_area","v":{"s":"Clean Up Area"}},{"k":"help_a_peer","v":{"s":"Help a Peer"}},{"k":"quiet_transition","v":{"s":"Quiet Transition"}},{"k":"read_20_pages","v":{"s":"Read 20 Pages"}},{"k":"score_80","v":{"s":"Score 80%+"}},{"k":"sticker","v":{"s":"Sticker"}},{"k":"5_min_free_time","v":{"s":"5 min Free Time"}},{"k":"snack_choice","v":{"s":"Snack Choice"}},{"k":"line_leader","v":{"s":"Line Leader"}},{"k":"computer_time","v":{"s":"Computer Time"}},{"k":"homework_pass","v":{"s":"Homework Pass"}},{"k":"lunch_with_teacher","v":{"s":"Lunch with Teacher"}},{"k":"mystery_prize","v":{"s":"Mystery Prize"}},{"k":"what_function_does_the_screaming_likel","v":{"s":"What function does the screaming likely serve?"}},{"k":"what_function_does_throwing_toys_likel","v":{"s":"What function does throwing toys likely serve?"}},{"k":"what_function_does_the_rocking_likely_","v":{"s":"What function does the rocking likely serve?"}},{"k":"what_function_does_the_crying_likely_s","v":{"s":"What function does the crying likely serve?"}},{"k":"what_should_you_do","v":{"s":"What should you do?"}},{"k":"what_aba_concept_does_this_illustrate","v":{"s":"What ABA concept does this illustrate?"}},{"k":"what_should_the_therapist_do_next","v":{"s":"What should the therapist do next?"}},{"k":"which_procedure_would_be_most_appropri","v":{"s":"Which procedure would be MOST appropriate?"}},{"k":"what_do_you_expect","v":{"s":"What do you expect?"}},{"k":"what_should_the_bcba_present","v":{"s":"What should the BCBA present?"}},{"k":"what_happens","v":{"s":"What happens?"}},{"k":"what_is_the_best_advice","v":{"s":"What is the best advice?"}},{"k":"the_7_dimensions_of_aba","v":{"s":"The 7 Dimensions of ABA"}},{"k":"applied_behavioral_analytic_technologi","v":{"s":"Applied • Behavioral • Analytic • Technological • Conceptually Systematic • Effective • Generality"}},{"k":"reinforcement_rule","v":{"s":"Reinforcement Rule"}},{"k":"if_a_consequence_increases_behavior_re","v":{"s":"If a consequence INCREASES behavior = Reinforcement. + means ADD stimulus. - means REMOVE stimulus."}},{"k":"punishment_rule","v":{"s":"Punishment Rule"}},{"k":"if_a_consequence_decreases_behavior_pu","v":{"s":"If a consequence DECREASES behavior = Punishment. + means ADD stimulus. - means REMOVE stimulus."}},{"k":"three_term_contingency","v":{"s":"Three-Term Contingency"}},{"k":"a_antecedent_b_behavior_c_consequence_","v":{"s":"A (Antecedent) → B (Behavior) → C (Consequence). Also called the \"ABC\" of behavior analysis."}},{"k":"extinction","v":{"s":"Extinction"}},{"k":"withholding_reinforcement_for_a_previo","v":{"s":"Withholding reinforcement for a previously reinforced behavior. Expect an initial extinction BURST (temporary increase) before decrease."}},{"k":"schedules_of_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Schedules of Reinforcement"}},{"k":"fr_fixed_ratio_vr_variable_ratio_fi_fi","v":{"s":"FR (Fixed Ratio) • VR (Variable Ratio) • FI (Fixed Interval) • VI (Variable Interval). Ratio = responses. Interval = time."}},{"k":"motivating_operations","v":{"s":"Motivating Operations"}},{"k":"eo_establishing_operation_increases_va","v":{"s":"EO (Establishing Operation) = increases value of reinforcer. AO (Abolishing Operation) = decreases value of reinforcer."}},{"k":"ethics_first","v":{"s":"Ethics First"}},{"k":"least_restrictive_data_driven_informed","v":{"s":"Least restrictive • Data-driven • Informed consent • Social validity • Competence • Client benefit above all."}},{"k":"behavior_lab","v":{"s":"Behavior Lab"}},{"k":"skip_to_main_content","v":{"s":"Skip to main content"}},{"k":"back_to_stem_lab","v":{"s":"Back to STEAM Lab"}},{"k":"key_term","v":{"s":"📖 Key Term:"}},{"k":"plain","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"using_simple_everyday_words_and_short_","v":{"s":"using simple everyday words and short sentences, no jargon"}},{"k":"grade_5","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"for_a_5th_grade_student_with_a_concret","v":{"s":"for a 5th grade student, with a concrete classroom or family example"}},{"k":"high_school","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"for_a_high_school_psychology_student","v":{"s":"for a high school psychology student"}},{"k":"for_a_new_school_psychologist_or_bcba_","v":{"s":"for a new school psychologist or BCBA trainee, using accurate technical language"}},{"k":"ai_behavior_analysis_tutor","v":{"s":"AI behavior-analysis tutor"}},{"k":"explain_at_my_level","v":{"s":"✨ Explain at my level"}},{"k":"reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"click_explain_for_a_classroom_example_","v":{"s":"Click “Explain” for a classroom example of this concept at your chosen reading level."}},{"k":"start_experiment","v":{"s":"Start Experiment"}},{"k":"start_experiment_2","v":{"s":"🚀 Start Experiment"}},{"k":"addictive_pattern","v":{"s":"Addictive pattern"}},{"k":"vr_schedule_strong_reinforcer_no_alter","v":{"s":"VR schedule + strong reinforcer + no alternative = gambling/scrolling pattern. Hardest to extinguish."}},{"k":"durable_habit","v":{"s":"Durable habit"}},{"k":"behavior_persists_through_occasional_n","v":{"s":"Behavior persists through occasional non-reinforcement. Typical of well-trained skills."}},{"k":"moderate_persistence","v":{"s":"Moderate persistence"}},{"k":"standard_reinforcement_behavior_mainta","v":{"s":"Standard reinforcement; behavior maintained while contingency holds."}},{"k":"fragile","v":{"s":"Fragile"}},{"k":"crf_or_low_strength_alternatives_avail","v":{"s":"CRF or low strength + alternatives available. Extinction begins quickly when reinforcement stops."}},{"k":"already_extinguishing","v":{"s":"Already extinguishing"}},{"k":"reinforcer_too_weak_vs_alternative_beh","v":{"s":"Reinforcer too weak vs alternative — behavior dropping in frequency right now."}},{"k":"reinforcement_inquiry_predict_persiste","v":{"s":"🔬 Reinforcement Inquiry — Predict Persistence"}},{"k":"set_schedule_reinforcer_strength_alter","v":{"s":"Set schedule, reinforcer strength, alternatives, extinction time. Predict the persistence band. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"strength","v":{"s":"Strength "}},{"k":"alternative","v":{"s":"Alternative "}},{"k":"ext_time","v":{"s":"Ext time "}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_why_does_vr_resist_ext","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (why does VR resist extinction so much?)"}},{"k":"e_g_vr_makes_every_action_potentially_","v":{"s":"e.g., VR makes every action potentially the rewarded one — extinction never feels conclusive..."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_open_questions","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions"}},{"k":"open_questions_no_answer_key","v":{"s":"Open questions (no answer key)"}},{"k":"why_is_vr_variable_ratio_the_schedule_","v":{"s":"Why is VR (variable ratio) the schedule behind slot machines + scrolling apps?"}},{"k":"when_does_adding_an_alternative_reinfo","v":{"s":"When does adding an alternative reinforcer FASTER extinguish a behavior than just removing the original?"}},{"k":"what_pattern_of_responding_shows_durin","v":{"s":"What pattern of responding shows during an FI (fixed interval) schedule? Why \"scalloping\"?"}},{"k":"how_does_extinction_time_interact_with","v":{"s":"How does extinction TIME interact with schedule type? (Hint: partial reinforcement extinction effect.)"}},{"k":"i_can_explain_why_this_schedule_streng","v":{"s":"I can explain why this schedule + strength + alternative yields this persistence band."}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words..."}},{"k":"inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a","v":{"s":"Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. Skinner box terminology + matching-law extension. Persistence is multi-dimensional in real life; this is a teaching heuristic."}},{"k":"behavior_lab_simulation","v":{"s":"Behavior Lab Simulation"}},{"k":"skip_to_simulation","v":{"s":"Skip to simulation"}},{"k":"back_to_level_select","v":{"s":"Back to level select"}},{"k":"reinforcements","v":{"s":"Reinforcements"}},{"k":"ticks_to_complete","v":{"s":"Ticks to Complete"}},{"k":"resp_rate_min","v":{"s":"Resp Rate / min"}},{"k":"avg_latency_ticks","v":{"s":"Avg Latency (ticks)"}},{"k":"key_vocabulary","v":{"s":"📖 Key Vocabulary:"}},{"k":"knowledge_check","v":{"s":"🧠 Knowledge Check"}},{"k":"next_level","v":{"s":"Next Level"}},{"k":"next_level_2","v":{"s":"➡️ Next Level"}},{"k":"lever_proximity","v":{"s":"🐭 → Lever proximity"}},{"k":"recent_behaviors","v":{"s":"🔥 Recent Behaviors"}},{"k":"automatic_dro_food_delivered_when_time","v":{"s":"⏱ Automatic (DRO) — food delivered when timer completes"}},{"k":"use_the_classical_conditioning_panel_b","v":{"s":"🔔 Use the Classical Conditioning panel below"}},{"k":"start_extinction","v":{"s":"Start Extinction"}},{"k":"start_extinction_2","v":{"s":"🚫 Start Extinction"}},{"k":"extinction_in_progress_watch_the_burst","v":{"s":"⏳ Extinction in progress... watch the burst!"}},{"k":"target_behavior_selector","v":{"s":"Target behavior selector"}},{"k":"chain_progress","v":{"s":"🔗 Chain Progress"}},{"k":"reinforce_now","v":{"s":"🍕 REINFORCE NOW!"}},{"k":"dro_timer","v":{"s":"⏱ DRO Timer"}},{"k":"classical_conditioning","v":{"s":"🔔 Classical Conditioning"}},{"k":"association_strength","v":{"s":"Association Strength"}},{"k":"ring_bell","v":{"s":"Ring Bell"}},{"k":"pair_bell_with_food","v":{"s":"Pair Bell with Food"}},{"k":"last_behavior","v":{"s":"Last Behavior"}},{"k":"session_stats","v":{"s":"Session Stats"}},{"k":"behavior_probability_weights","v":{"s":"📊 Behavior Probability Weights"}},{"k":"abc_data_log","v":{"s":"📋 ABC Data Log"}},{"k":"export_abc_data_as_csv","v":{"s":"Export ABC Data as CSV"}},{"k":"export_csv","v":{"s":"📥 Export CSV"}},{"k":"progress_badges","v":{"s":"🏆 Progress Badges"}},{"k":"earned","v":{"s":"✓ Earned"}},{"k":"four_functions_of_behavior_fba","v":{"s":"💡 Four Functions of Behavior (FBA)"}},{"k":"every_behavior_serves_a_function_under","v":{"s":"Every behavior serves a function. Understanding WHY a behavior occurs is the key to changing it. These are the 4 functions identified by functional behavior assessment (FBA):"}},{"k":"continue_in_school_behavior_toolkit","v":{"s":"Continue in: School Behavior Toolkit"}},{"k":"applied_k_12_practice_what_school_psyc","v":{"s":"Applied K-12 practice — what school psychs and educators DO with the science"}},{"k":"if_you_have_just_learned_operant_condi","v":{"s":"If you have just learned operant conditioning here in BehaviorLab, the next step is the K-12 practice that uses it. "}},{"k":"we_deliberately_built_that_content_as_","v":{"s":"We deliberately built that content as a separate tool, not here."}},{"k":"the_skinner_box_visual_frame_should_no","v":{"s":" The Skinner-box visual frame should not be adjacent to \"how to handle a kid in crisis\" content — different tonal space, different ethical weight. The Toolkit covers PBIS three-tier framework, replacement behaviors mapped to FBA functions, setting events (slow triggers most BIPs miss), Geoff Colvin's seven-phase Acting-Out Cycle for crisis de-escalation, and Restraint & Seclusion ethics anchored in Maine Chapter 33."}},{"k":"open_stem_lab_behavioral_science","v":{"s":"Open STEAM Lab → Behavioral Science → "}},{"k":"school_behavior_toolkit","v":{"s":"\"🏫 School Behavior Toolkit.\""}},{"k":"beyond_pure_aba_neurodiversity_affirmi","v":{"s":"🧭 Beyond Pure ABA — neurodiversity-affirming + trauma-informed"}},{"k":"operant_conditioning_is_a_powerful_set","v":{"s":"Operant conditioning is a powerful set of tools AND has been used in ways the autistic community has documented as harmful. Good modern practice holds both truths at once. School psychs and BCBAs working in K-12 settings need the critical lens to apply ABA ethically — not just the technical mechanics."}},{"k":"identity_first_language_follows_commun","v":{"s":"💡 Identity-first language follows community-consensus norms (Kenny et al. 2016; Bury et al. 2020; Taboas et al. 2023). The Behavior Lab teaches the science of behavior; this panel teaches the ethics of applying it to humans who can tell us what they want."}},{"k":"continue_in_disability_voices_sel_hub","v":{"s":"Continue in: Disability Voices (SEL Hub)"}},{"k":"named_autistic_and_disabled_advocates_","v":{"s":"Named autistic and disabled advocates, not behavioral subjects"}},{"k":"if_you_have_just_read_the_critical_fra","v":{"s":"If you have just read the critical-frame panels above, the next step is to hear from the people whose work shaped — and critiqued — applied behavior analysis. "}},{"k":"we_deliberately_built_that_content_in_","v":{"s":"We deliberately built that content in the SEL Hub, not here."}},{"k":"putting_named_real_autistic_adults_ins","v":{"s":" Putting named real autistic adults inside a tool whose central image is a Skinner box would be exactly what the disability community has documented as harmful. The tool you want includes Ari Ne'eman, Temple Grandin, Damian Milton, Henny Kupferstein, Kassiane Asasumasu, Mel Baggs, Ly Xīnzhèn M. Zhǎngsūn Brown, and Patty Berne — with documented quotes, context, and a curated reading list."}},{"k":"open_sel_hub_identity_care","v":{"s":"Open SEL Hub → Identity & Care → "}},{"k":"disability_voices","v":{"s":"\"🎤 Disability Voices.\""}},{"k":"schedule_comparison","v":{"s":"📈 Schedule Comparison"}},{"k":"watch_how_different_reinforcement_sche","v":{"s":"Watch how different reinforcement schedules produce distinct response patterns. FR creates post-reinforcement pauses, VR produces high steady rates, FI shows scalloping, VI shows low steady rates."}},{"k":"toggle_schedule_animation","v":{"s":"Toggle schedule animation"}},{"k":"reset_schedule_animation","v":{"s":"Reset schedule animation"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"↻ Reset"}},{"k":"schedule_sleuth_identify_the_schedule_","v":{"s":"🕵️ Schedule Sleuth — identify the schedule from its curve"}},{"k":"you_will_see_one_unlabeled_cumulative_","v":{"s":"You will see one unlabeled cumulative-response curve. Pick which schedule produced it. The curve shape is the only clue."}},{"k":"start_schedule_sleuth","v":{"s":"Start Schedule Sleuth"}},{"k":"start_the_game","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start the game"}},{"k":"round","v":{"s":"Round "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"how_to_read_this_curve_click_to_toggle","v":{"s":"📜 How to read this curve (click to toggle)"}},{"k":"cumulative_response_curve","v":{"s":"Cumulative-response curve"}},{"k":"each_reinforced_response_adds_one_to_t","v":{"s":": each reinforced response adds one to the y-axis. Flat segments = no responding. Steep segments = rapid responding. Bumps where the curve briefly flattens = post-reinforcement pauses."}},{"k":"fixed_ratio_2","v":{"s":"Fixed Ratio"}},{"k":"variable_ratio_2","v":{"s":"Variable Ratio"}},{"k":"fixed_interval_2","v":{"s":"Fixed Interval"}},{"k":"variable_interval_2","v":{"s":"Variable Interval"}},{"k":"tip_focus_on_shape_not_absolute_height","v":{"s":"Tip: focus on shape, not absolute height. The curve below is unlabeled until you guess."}},{"k":"cumulative_responses","v":{"s":"Cumulative responses"}},{"k":"time","v":{"s":"Time →"}},{"k":"pick_the_schedule","v":{"s":"Pick the schedule"}},{"k":"next_round","v":{"s":"Next round"}},{"k":"next_round_2","v":{"s":"➡️ Next round"}},{"k":"function_sleuth_what_is_the_behavior_g","v":{"s":"🔍 Function Sleuth — what is the behavior getting?"}},{"k":"attention_2","v":{"s":"Attention"}},{"k":"escape_avoidance","v":{"s":"Escape / Avoidance"}},{"k":"tangible_2","v":{"s":"Tangible"}},{"k":"sensory_automatic","v":{"s":"Sensory / Automatic"}},{"k":"12_vignettes_for_each_pick_the_functio","v":{"s":"12 vignettes. For each, pick the function the behavior is most likely serving. Coaching after each pick names what makes this function more likely than the other three."}},{"k":"attention_3","v":{"s":"Attention"}},{"k":"escape","v":{"s":"Escape"}},{"k":"tangible_3","v":{"s":"Tangible"}},{"k":"sensory","v":{"s":"Sensory"}},{"k":"a_behavior_can_have_more_than_one_func","v":{"s":"A behavior can have more than one function in reality. For these vignettes, pick the most-likely primary function based on the contingency described."}},{"k":"start_function_sleuth","v":{"s":"Start Function Sleuth"}},{"k":"start_the_game_2","v":{"s":"🔍 Start the game"}},{"k":"round_2","v":{"s":"Round "}},{"k":"streak_2","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best_2","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"accuracy_2","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"pick_the_function","v":{"s":"Pick the function"}},{"k":"all_12_vignettes_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 12 vignettes complete"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_vignette","v":{"s":"➡️ Next vignette"}},{"k":"reinforcement_punishment_matrix","v":{"s":"⚖️ Reinforcement & Punishment Matrix"}},{"k":"the_4_quadrants_of_operant_conditionin","v":{"s":"The 4 quadrants of operant conditioning. Click each cell to learn more:"}},{"k":"add_stimulus","v":{"s":"➕ Add Stimulus"}},{"k":"remove_stimulus","v":{"s":"➖ Remove Stimulus"}},{"k":"increase_behavior","v":{"s":"⬆ Increase Behavior"}},{"k":"decrease_behavior","v":{"s":"⬇ Decrease Behavior"}},{"k":"a_stimulus_to","v":{"s":" a stimulus to "}},{"k":"real_world_examples","v":{"s":"💡 Real-world examples:"}},{"k":"token_economy_builder","v":{"s":"🎮 Token Economy Builder"}},{"k":"design_a_classroom_token_economy_award","v":{"s":"Design a classroom token economy! Award tokens for positive behaviors, then let students exchange them for rewards. This is a real ABA strategy used in schools worldwide."}},{"k":"token_balance","v":{"s":"Token Balance"}},{"k":"earn_tokens","v":{"s":"⬆ Earn Tokens"}},{"k":"spend_tokens","v":{"s":"🎁 Spend Tokens"}},{"k":"recent_activity","v":{"s":"Recent Activity"}},{"k":"operant_vs_classical_conditioning","v":{"s":"🔬 Operant vs Classical Conditioning"}},{"k":"aspect","v":{"s":"Aspect"}},{"k":"aspect_2","v":{"s":"Aspect"}},{"k":"operant","v":{"s":"🐭 Operant"}},{"k":"classical","v":{"s":"🐶 Classical"}},{"k":"behavior_intervention_plan_bip_builder","v":{"s":"📋 Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP) Builder"}},{"k":"walk_through_the_steps_of_creating_a_r","v":{"s":"Walk through the steps of creating a real Behavior Intervention Plan — the document ABA professionals use to address challenging behavior."}},{"k":"step_1_identify_target_behavior","v":{"s":"Step 1: Identify Target Behavior"}},{"k":"describe_the_behavior_you_want_to_chan","v":{"s":"Describe the behavior you want to change. Be specific and observable!"}},{"k":"e_g_student_calls_out_answers_without_","v":{"s":"e.g., Student calls out answers without raising hand (3-5 times per class)"}},{"k":"step_2_identify_antecedents","v":{"s":"Step 2: Identify Antecedents"}},{"k":"what_happens_right_before_the_behavior","v":{"s":"What happens RIGHT BEFORE the behavior? What triggers it?"}},{"k":"e_g_teacher_asks_a_question_to_the_who","v":{"s":"e.g., Teacher asks a question to the whole class"}},{"k":"step_3_hypothesize_function","v":{"s":"Step 3: Hypothesize Function"}},{"k":"why_does_the_student_engage_in_this_be","v":{"s":"WHY does the student engage in this behavior? (Attention, Escape, Tangible, Sensory)"}},{"k":"e_g_attention_student_wants_teacher_an","v":{"s":"e.g., Attention — student wants teacher and peer attention"}},{"k":"step_4_replacement_behavior","v":{"s":"Step 4: Replacement Behavior"}},{"k":"what_appropriate_behavior_should_repla","v":{"s":"What appropriate behavior should REPLACE the problem behavior? Must serve the SAME function!"}},{"k":"e_g_raise_hand_and_wait_to_be_called_o","v":{"s":"e.g., Raise hand and wait to be called on (still gets attention but appropriately)"}},{"k":"step_5_intervention_strategy","v":{"s":"Step 5: Intervention Strategy"}},{"k":"how_will_you_teach_and_reinforce_the_r","v":{"s":"How will you teach and reinforce the replacement behavior?"}},{"k":"e_g_teach_hand_raising_reinforce_with_","v":{"s":"e.g., Teach hand-raising, reinforce with praise + 2 tokens; ignore call-outs (extinction)"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"back_2","v":{"s":"← Back"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"next_2","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"your_behavior_intervention_plan","v":{"s":"✅ Your Behavior Intervention Plan"}},{"k":"target_behavior","v":{"s":"Target Behavior"}},{"k":"antecedent","v":{"s":"Antecedent"}},{"k":"hypothesized_function","v":{"s":"Hypothesized Function"}},{"k":"replacement_behavior","v":{"s":"Replacement Behavior"}},{"k":"intervention_strategy","v":{"s":"Intervention Strategy"}},{"k":"edit","v":{"s":"Edit"}},{"k":"edit_2","v":{"s":"✏️ Edit"}},{"k":"new_plan","v":{"s":"New Plan"}},{"k":"new_plan_2","v":{"s":"🔄 New Plan"}},{"k":"better_approach","v":{"s":"💡 Better approach: "}},{"k":"next_scenario","v":{"s":"Next Scenario ("}},{"k":"aba_history_timeline","v":{"s":"📅 ABA History Timeline"}},{"k":"foundations","v":{"s":"Foundations"}},{"k":"growth","v":{"s":"Growth"}},{"k":"applied","v":{"s":"Applied"}},{"k":"modern","v":{"s":"Modern"}},{"k":"quick_reference_cards","v":{"s":"📋 Quick Reference Cards"}},{"k":"three_term_contingency_2","v":{"s":"🔗 Three-Term Contingency"}},{"k":"experiment_status","v":{"s":"Experiment status"}},{"k":"current_concept","v":{"s":"Current concept"}},{"k":"path_progress","v":{"s":"Path progress"}},{"k":"data_points","v":{"s":"Data points"}},{"k":"lab_status","v":{"s":"Lab status"}},{"k":"choose_experiment","v":{"s":"Choose an experiment"}},{"k":"experiment_path_help","v":{"s":"Build from reinforcement fundamentals toward schedules, chaining, DRO, and classical conditioning."}},{"k":"experiment_levels","v":{"s":"Experiment levels"}},{"k":"advanced_reinforcement_inquiry","v":{"s":"Advanced inquiry: predict persistence"}},{"k":"explanation_label","v":{"s":"Explain your prediction"}},{"k":"observation_chamber","v":{"s":"Observation chamber"}},{"k":"schedule_comparison_chart","v":{"s":"Animated cumulative response records comparing fixed ratio, variable ratio, fixed interval, and variable interval schedules."}},{"k":"abc_ante_chain","v":{"s":"Chain complete"}},{"k":"abc_ante_chamber","v":{"s":"Chamber"}},{"k":"abc_antecedent","v":{"s":"ANTECEDENT"}},{"k":"abc_behavior","v":{"s":"BEHAVIOR"}},{"k":"abc_consequence","v":{"s":"CONSEQUENCE"}},{"k":"avg_delay_short","v":{"s":"Avg delay to reinforcement"}},{"k":"avg_delay_to_reinforcement","v":{"s":"Avg delay to reinforcement (ticks)"}},{"k":"badge_earned","v":{"s":" — Earned"}},{"k":"badge_earned_paren","v":{"s":" (Earned)"}},{"k":"badge_locked","v":{"s":" — Locked"}},{"k":"badge_locked_paren","v":{"s":" (locked)"}},{"k":"browse_arrow","v":{"s":"Browse →"}},{"k":"canvas_axis_responses","v":{"s":"Responses"}},{"k":"canvas_axis_time_ticks","v":{"s":"Time (ticks)"}},{"k":"canvas_cumulative_record","v":{"s":"CUMULATIVE RECORD"}},{"k":"canvas_cumulative_responses","v":{"s":"Cumulative responses"}},{"k":"canvas_distance_delta","v":{"s":"distance Δ"}},{"k":"canvas_extinction_burst","v":{"s":"EXTINCTION BURST"}},{"k":"canvas_extinction_burst_up","v":{"s":"EXTINCTION BURST ↑"}},{"k":"canvas_food","v":{"s":"FOOD"}},{"k":"canvas_green_reinforce","v":{"s":"✅ GREEN = Reinforce!"}},{"k":"canvas_lever","v":{"s":"LEVER"}},{"k":"canvas_paused","v":{"s":"⏸ PAUSED"}},{"k":"canvas_red_no_reinforce","v":{"s":"❌ RED = Do NOT reinforce!"}},{"k":"canvas_waiting_for_data","v":{"s":"Waiting for data…"}},{"k":"cc_ring_without_food","v":{"s":"Ring the bell WITHOUT food to weaken the association"}},{"k":"chain_press_lever","v":{"s":"⚡ Press Lever"}},{"k":"chain_rear_up","v":{"s":"🐭 Rear Up"}},{"k":"chain_sniff","v":{"s":"👃 Sniff"}},{"k":"chamber_3d_failed","v":{"s":"3D is unavailable on this device or network. Switch back to Diagram — it carries the whole simulation."}},{"k":"chamber_3d_hint","v":{"s":"A teaching model of a standard operant chamber, not a specific commercial apparatus. It renders the same simulation as the diagram — same positions, same tick."}},{"k":"chamber_3d_label","v":{"s":"Operant chamber, 3D. Interactive: drag or use arrow keys to rotate, plus and minus to zoom, zero to reset. Every part is also a button below, and the diagram view carries the same information."}},{"k":"chamber_3d_loading","v":{"s":"Loading the 3D chamber…"}},{"k":"chamber_parts","v":{"s":"Chamber parts"}},{"k":"chamber_view","v":{"s":"Chamber view"}},{"k":"cmp_fi","v":{"s":"Scallop: almost nothing early, accelerating as the interval runs out."}},{"k":"cmp_fr","v":{"s":"Break and run: a pause after every pellet, then a burst."}},{"k":"cmp_vi","v":{"s":"Low and steady. Responding does not make the pellet come sooner."}},{"k":"cmp_vr","v":{"s":"High and steady. No pauses — the next pellet could be any press."}},{"k":"compare_arrow","v":{"s":"Compare →"}},{"k":"compare_schedules_arrow","v":{"s":"Compare Schedules →"}},{"k":"deliver_food","v":{"s":"🍕 Deliver Food"}},{"k":"dro_keep_waiting","v":{"s":"Keep waiting…"}},{"k":"dro_timer_started","v":{"s":"Timer started — no lever presses needed."}},{"k":"every_response_adds_one_to_the_y_axis","v":{"s":": every response — reinforced or not — adds one to the y-axis, so the line can only rise. Flat segments = no responding. Steep segments = rapid responding. The tick marks under the line show where a reinforcer was delivered."}},{"k":"explain_btn","v":{"s":"🧠 Explain"}},{"k":"explore_arrow","v":{"s":"Explore →"}},{"k":"fr_presses_since_food","v":{"s":"Presses since the last pellet"}},{"k":"fr_ready","v":{"s":"reinforce the next press"}},{"k":"generate_ai_at","v":{"s":"Generate AI explanation at "}},{"k":"goal_l3","v":{"s":"🎯 Goal: reinforce 5 lever presses, then stop and observe."}},{"k":"h_behavior_lab","v":{"s":"Behavior Lab"}},{"k":"hide","v":{"s":"Hide"}},{"k":"hide_part_labels","v":{"s":"Hide labels"}},{"k":"key_space","v":{"s":"Space"}},{"k":"level_progress_label","v":{"s":"Level progress: "}},{"k":"mark_correct","v":{"s":" ✔ Correct"}},{"k":"mark_incorrect","v":{"s":" ✘ Incorrect"}},{"k":"mute","v":{"s":"Mute"}},{"k":"part_grid","v":{"s":"Grid floor"}},{"k":"part_grid_desc","v":{"s":"Stainless bars. In this simulation the floor is only a floor: it delivers nothing. Historically, grid floors were also used to deliver shock, which is a large part of why the chamber carries the reputation it does."}},{"k":"part_hopper","v":{"s":"Food magazine"}},{"k":"part_hopper_desc","v":{"s":"Delivers one pellet at a time into the trough. The delivery is the reinforcer only if it actually raises the future rate of the behaviour it followed — a consequence is not a reinforcer by intention."}},{"k":"part_house","v":{"s":"House light"}},{"k":"part_house_desc","v":{"s":"General illumination for the chamber. It dims here while extinction is running, so the change in conditions is visible rather than only tabulated."}},{"k":"part_lever","v":{"s":"Response lever"}},{"k":"part_lever_desc","v":{"s":"The operandum — the one thing in the box the subject can operate. A press is the operant response the schedule is defined over; everything else the mouse does is measured, not reinforced."}},{"k":"part_sdlight","v":{"s":"Stimulus light"}},{"k":"part_sdlight_desc","v":{"s":"The discriminative stimulus. Green (SD) signals that responding is reinforced; red (S-delta) signals that it is not. It does not cause the press — it sets the occasion for it."}},{"k":"part_speaker","v":{"s":"Tone generator"}},{"k":"part_speaker_desc","v":{"s":"Sounds the conditioned stimulus in the Pavlov level. Classical conditioning pairs the tone with food regardless of what the subject does — that independence from behaviour is exactly what separates it from operant conditioning."}},{"k":"part_subject","v":{"s":"Subject"}},{"k":"part_subject_desc","v":{"s":"A simulated mouse. Its behaviour is drawn from a probability distribution that reinforcement reshapes — you are watching a model of operant learning, not a recording of a real animal."}},{"k":"pause_btn","v":{"s":"⏸ Pause"}},{"k":"pause_btn2","v":{"s":"⏸ Pause"}},{"k":"pause_simulation","v":{"s":"Pause simulation"}},{"k":"phase_baseline","v":{"s":"Baseline"}},{"k":"phase_extinction","v":{"s":"Extinction"}},{"k":"phase_test_cr","v":{"s":"Test CR"}},{"k":"play_arrow","v":{"s":"Play →"}},{"k":"play_btn","v":{"s":"▶ Play"}},{"k":"practice_scenarios","v":{"s":"Practice Scenarios"}},{"k":"press_space_to_reinforce","v":{"s":"Press Space to reinforce."}},{"k":"quiz_answer_label","v":{"s":"Quiz answer: "}},{"k":"quiz_result_correct","v":{"s":"✅ Correct!"}},{"k":"quiz_result_incorrect","v":{"s":"❌ Incorrect"}},{"k":"rank_in_training","v":{"s":"⭐ Behaviour Analyst in Training"}},{"k":"rank_keep_going","v":{"s":"🐭 Keep experimenting"}},{"k":"re_explain","v":{"s":"🔄 Re-explain"}},{"k":"reading_level_label","v":{"s":"Reading level: "}},{"k":"reinforcers_in_block","v":{"s":"Reinforcers"}},{"k":"reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset the view"}},{"k":"responses_in_block","v":{"s":"Responses"}},{"k":"responses_in_each_block","v":{"s":"Responses made in each block of 20 time units, and the running total at the end of the block."}},{"k":"resume_btn","v":{"s":"▶ Resume"}},{"k":"resume_simulation","v":{"s":"Resume simulation"}},{"k":"ring","v":{"s":"RING!"}},{"k":"rotate_left","v":{"s":"Rotate view left"}},{"k":"rotate_right","v":{"s":"Rotate view right"}},{"k":"running_total","v":{"s":"Running total"}},{"k":"scope_note","v":{"s":"This lab teaches the concepts of behaviour analysis. It does not qualify anyone to run a functional behaviour assessment or write a behaviour plan — that is supervised professional work, and the function of a real behaviour is a hypothesis you test with data, not a label you read off a description."}},{"k":"sec_analysis","v":{"s":"Analysing the contingency"}},{"k":"sec_analysis_sub","v":{"s":"Naming what a consequence actually did — and telling the two kinds of conditioning apart."}},{"k":"sec_classroom","v":{"s":"Where this goes next"}},{"k":"sec_classroom_sub","v":{"s":"Everything above is a simulated mouse. The applied K-12 practice built on this science lives in two sibling tools; what stays here is the argument about the science itself."}},{"k":"sec_data","v":{"s":"Reading the data"}},{"k":"sec_data_sub","v":{"s":"The same session, four ways behaviour analysts actually record it."}},{"k":"sec_reference","v":{"s":"Reference"}},{"k":"sec_reference_sub","v":{"s":"History, terminology and quick cards — including the parts of this history the field is still arguing about."}},{"k":"sec_schedules","v":{"s":"Schedules of reinforcement"}},{"k":"sec_schedules_sub","v":{"s":"Back to the chamber: how the timing of reinforcement shapes the pattern of responding."}},{"k":"select_level_label","v":{"s":"Select level "}},{"k":"set_speed_to","v":{"s":"Set speed to "}},{"k":"show_part_labels","v":{"s":"Show labels"}},{"k":"show_the_record_as_numbers","v":{"s":"🔢 The same record, as numbers"}},{"k":"sim_paused","v":{"s":"Simulation paused"}},{"k":"sim_resumed","v":{"s":"Simulation resumed"}},{"k":"sleuth_why_fi","v":{"s":"Fixed Interval produces a *scallop*: slow responding right after reinforcement, then accelerating as the interval ends and the next reinforcer becomes available. Look for repeating concave curves."}},{"k":"sleuth_why_fr","v":{"s":"Fixed Ratio creates a *post-reinforcement pause* after each delivery, then a rapid burst of responses to reach the next reinforcer. Look for the staircase shape with brief flat plateaus."}},{"k":"sleuth_why_vi","v":{"s":"Variable Interval produces a *low, steady* rate. The next reinforcer arrives at unpredictable times, so a moderate steady rate maximizes the chance of catching it. Look for the lowest, smoothest line."}},{"k":"sleuth_why_vr","v":{"s":"Variable Ratio produces the *steepest, smoothest* climb because the next reinforcer could come at any moment. This is the slot-machine pattern — most resistant to extinction."}},{"k":"sound_off","v":{"s":"Sound Off"}},{"k":"sound_on","v":{"s":"Sound On"}},{"k":"speed_fast","v":{"s":"Fast"}},{"k":"speed_medium","v":{"s":"Medium"}},{"k":"sr_explanation_ready","v":{"s":"Explanation ready."}},{"k":"sr_l3_complete","v":{"s":"Level 3 complete. You observed the extinction burst."}},{"k":"sr_view_2d","v":{"s":"Diagram chamber view."}},{"k":"sr_view_3d","v":{"s":"Three-D chamber view. Drag or use arrow keys to look around."}},{"k":"status_live","v":{"s":"Live"}},{"k":"status_paused","v":{"s":"Paused"}},{"k":"streak_on_fire","v":{"s":" On fire!"}},{"k":"suffix_selected","v":{"s":" (selected)"}},{"k":"suffix_target_behavior","v":{"s":" (target behavior)"}},{"k":"the_aversives_in_the_history","v":{"s":"The aversives in this history"}},{"k":"the_aversives_in_the_history_desc","v":{"s":"The timeline in this tool runs through the early intensive-intervention programs without saying what was in them. It should. Lovaas’s UCLA work in the 1960s used contingent electric shock, and he published it; the 1987 study’s treatment package included aversives such as a loud “no” and a slap on the thigh. Mainstream practice moved away from aversives and the BACB now restricts them sharply — but contingent shock has not ended everywhere, and its legal status in the United States has changed more than once. When autistic adults describe early ABA as harmful, this is a large part of what they are describing. A tool that teaches positive punishment as a quadrant on a chart owes students the version where it was a real thing done to real children."}},{"k":"tick_marks_are_reinforcers","v":{"s":"Tick marks below the line = reinforcer delivered"}},{"k":"tilt_down","v":{"s":"Tilt view down"}},{"k":"tilt_up","v":{"s":"Tilt view up"}},{"k":"time_block","v":{"s":"Time block"}},{"k":"toast_abc_exported","v":{"s":"📥 ABC log exported as CSV."}},{"k":"toast_assoc_too_weak","v":{"s":"🔔 Weak response — the association is not strong enough yet. Pair some more."}},{"k":"toast_bell_neutral","v":{"s":"🔔 Bell rings… no response. It is still a neutral stimulus."}},{"k":"toast_cr_observed","v":{"s":"🤤 The dog salivates to the bell alone. That is a conditioned response."}},{"k":"toast_dro_met","v":{"s":"🍕 DRO interval met! Food delivered."}},{"k":"toast_extinction_no_reinforce","v":{"s":"⚠️ Extinction phase: do NOT reinforce."}},{"k":"toast_extinction_started","v":{"s":"🚫 Extinction phase started. Do NOT deliver food."}},{"k":"toast_extinguished","v":{"s":"✅ Association extinguished. The bell no longer produces salivation."}},{"k":"toast_l3_complete","v":{"s":"🎉 Level 3 complete! You observed the extinction burst."}},{"k":"toast_l9_complete","v":{"s":"🎉 Level 9 complete! Classical conditioning."}},{"k":"toast_pairing_phase_only","v":{"s":"⚠️ Pairing is only available during the pairing phase."}},{"k":"toast_phase_extinction","v":{"s":"➡️ Extinction phase. Ring the bell without food to weaken the association."}},{"k":"toast_phase_pairing","v":{"s":"➡️ Pairing phase. Now pair the bell WITH food."}},{"k":"toast_phase_test","v":{"s":"➡️ Test phase. Ring the bell ALONE to test for the conditioned response."}},{"k":"toast_quiz_correct","v":{"s":"✅ Correct! +5 XP bonus."}},{"k":"toast_quiz_wrong","v":{"s":"❌ Not quite — read the explanation below."}},{"k":"toast_scenario_wrong","v":{"s":"❌ Not quite — read the explanation."}},{"k":"toast_sd_only","v":{"s":"❌ Only reinforce when the GREEN light (SD) is on!"}},{"k":"toast_wait_for_chain","v":{"s":"⚠️ Wait for the full chain: Sniff ➜ Rear Up ➜ Lever!"}},{"k":"unmute","v":{"s":"Unmute"}},{"k":"view_2d","v":{"s":"Diagram"}},{"k":"view_3d","v":{"s":"3D"}},{"k":"view_arrow","v":{"s":"View →"}},{"k":"zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}}],"id":490}},{"k":"bikelab","v":{"o":[{"k":"physics_sandbox","v":{"s":"Physics Sandbox"}},{"k":"side_view_ride_with_visible_force_vect","v":{"s":"Side-view ride with visible force vectors"}},{"k":"pedal_through_flat_hilly_and_off_road_","v":{"s":"Pedal through flat, hilly, and off-road terrain. Toggle force arrows for pedal force, gravity, drag, rolling resistance, and normal force. Watch kinetic and potential energy swap as you climb and descend."}},{"k":"gearing_lab","v":{"s":"Gearing Lab"}},{"k":"chainring_cassette_mechanical_advantag","v":{"s":"Chainring × cassette → mechanical advantage"}},{"k":"pick_any_chainring_and_cog_combo_see_t","v":{"s":"Pick any chainring and cog combo. See the gear ratio, mechanical advantage, and cadence-to-speed curve. Run the climb simulator to prove why low gears win on hills — same rider power, different torque at the wheel."}},{"k":"repair_simulator","v":{"s":"Repair Simulator"}},{"k":"hands_on_procedural_practice","v":{"s":"Hands-on procedural practice"}},{"k":"seven_step_by_step_procedures_with_dra","v":{"s":"Seven step-by-step procedures with drag-and-drop tool placement, step verification, and read-aloud instructions: patch a tube, brakes, chain, derailleur, pedal swap, wheel true, headset adjust. Builds real mechanical literacy."}},{"k":"neighborhood_ride","v":{"s":"Neighborhood Ride"}},{"k":"applied_physics_commute_demo","v":{"s":"Applied-physics commute demo"}},{"k":"a_scripted_600_m_commute_to_school_fla","v":{"s":"A scripted 600 m commute to school: flat block → stop sign → Oak Hill (7% grade) → descent → wet patch → school. Apply Sandbox + Gearing + Braking concepts in one scored run."}},{"k":"bike_fit_calculator","v":{"s":"Bike Fit Calculator"}},{"k":"saddle_height_reach_bar_drop","v":{"s":"Saddle height, reach, bar drop"}},{"k":"enter_rider_height_inseam_pick_bike_ty","v":{"s":"Enter rider height & inseam; pick bike type. Get evidence-based fit numbers (LeMond formula, KOPS setback)."}},{"k":"braking_physics","v":{"s":"Braking Physics"}},{"k":"stopping_distance_weight_transfer","v":{"s":"Stopping distance & weight transfer"}},{"k":"dry_wet_gravel_ice_surfaces_front_both","v":{"s":"Dry/wet/gravel/ice surfaces × front/both/rear brake choice. Solves d = v²/(2a) with endo-limit gating."}},{"k":"hand_signals_safety","v":{"s":"Hand Signals & Safety"}},{"k":"6_question_road_safety_quiz","v":{"s":"6-question road safety quiz"}},{"k":"svg_illustrated_quiz_turn_signals_lane","v":{"s":"SVG-illustrated quiz: turn signals, lane position, stop signs, helmet fit. Instant feedback with explanations."}},{"k":"bike_parts_inspector","v":{"s":"Bike Parts Inspector"}},{"k":"19_parts_on_a_clickable_bike","v":{"s":"19 parts on a clickable bike"}},{"k":"svg_bike_diagram_with_clickable_hotspo","v":{"s":"SVG bike diagram with clickable hotspots. Each part shows its function, common failure modes, and a deep-link to the related repair job."}},{"k":"helmet_fit_checker","v":{"s":"Helmet Fit Checker"}},{"k":"position_angle_chin_strap","v":{"s":"Position, angle, chin strap"}},{"k":"adjust_helmet_height_side_strap_angle_","v":{"s":"Adjust helmet height, side-strap angle, and chin-strap snugness on a live diagram. Get instant fit feedback against the 2V1 rule (2 fingers above eyebrows, V under ears, 1 finger chin slack)."}},{"k":"maine_winter_biking","v":{"s":"Maine Winter Biking"}},{"k":"fat_bikes_ice_salt_layering","v":{"s":"Fat bikes, ice, salt, layering"}},{"k":"maine_specific_guide_fat_bike_physics_","v":{"s":"Maine-specific guide: fat bike physics, studded tires, freeze-thaw road conditions, salt corrosion care, and layering for sub-freezing rides. Includes a winterization checklist."}},{"k":"signal_reaction_drill","v":{"s":"Signal Reaction Drill"}},{"k":"match_the_signal_under_time_pressure","v":{"s":"Match the signal under time pressure"}},{"k":"a_traffic_situation_flashes_you_press_","v":{"s":"A traffic-situation flashes; you press the matching hand signal as fast as possible. Tracks reaction time across 10 rounds, with a personal-best leaderboard."}},{"k":"physics_mechanics_and_maintenance_lear","v":{"s":"Physics, mechanics, and maintenance — learn the science of cycling and the real-world skills to keep a bike rolling."}},{"k":"core_modules","v":{"s":"Core Modules"}},{"k":"quick_labs","v":{"s":"Quick Labs"}},{"k":"stem_lab_tool_side_view_2d_canvas_no_p","v":{"s":"STEAM Lab tool · Side-view 2D canvas · No plugins required"}},{"k":"bikelab_menu","v":{"s":" BikeLab Menu"}},{"k":"physics_sandbox_2","v":{"s":"Physics Sandbox"}},{"k":"bike","v":{"s":"Bike"}},{"k":"terrain","v":{"s":"Terrain"}},{"k":"rider_power","v":{"s":"Rider Power"}},{"k":"pro_300_w_amateur_150_w_casual_80_w","v":{"s":"Pro: ~300 W · Amateur: ~150 W · Casual: ~80 W"}},{"k":"gear_ratio","v":{"s":"Gear Ratio"}},{"k":"low_gear_more_torque_less_top_speed_se","v":{"s":"Low gear = more torque, less top speed. See Gearing Lab for math."}},{"k":"wind","v":{"s":"Wind"}},{"k":"drag_scales_with_v_wind_a_10_mph_headw","v":{"s":"Drag scales with (v + wind)² — a 10 mph headwind at 15 mph feels like 25 mph worth of drag."}},{"k":"rider_mass","v":{"s":"Rider Mass"}},{"k":"rider_mass_in_kilograms","v":{"s":"Rider mass in kilograms"}},{"k":"physics_sandbox_simulation_bicycle_on_","v":{"s":"Physics sandbox simulation: bicycle on terrain with live force vectors (gravity, drag, rolling resistance, propulsion) and energy graphs."}},{"k":"toggle_fullscreen_for_the_physics_sand","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen for the physics sandbox"}},{"k":"fullscreen","v":{"s":"Fullscreen"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"force_vectors","v":{"s":"→ Force Vectors"}},{"k":"energy_graph","v":{"s":"📈 Energy Graph"}},{"k":"stop_pedaling_watch_drag_rolling_and_g","v":{"s":"Stop pedaling — watch drag, rolling, and gravity decelerate the bike on their own."}},{"k":"physics_notes","v":{"s":"💡 Physics notes: "}},{"k":"extreme_cross_chain_avoid","v":{"s":"⚠ Extreme cross-chain — avoid"}},{"k":"mild_cross_chain","v":{"s":"⚠ Mild cross-chain"}},{"k":"chainline_ok","v":{"s":"✓ Chainline OK"}},{"k":"gearing_lab_2","v":{"s":"Gearing Lab"}},{"k":"gear_selection","v":{"s":"Gear Selection"}},{"k":"leftmost_largest_easiest_to_climb_righ","v":{"s":"Leftmost = largest = easiest to climb. Rightmost = smallest = fastest top speed."}},{"k":"pedal_cadence_rpm","v":{"s":"Pedal Cadence (RPM)"}},{"k":"casual_riders_60_80_trained_80_100_spi","v":{"s":"Casual riders: 60–80. Trained: 80–100. Spin classes: 100+."}},{"k":"this_gear","v":{"s":"This Gear"}},{"k":"gear_ratio_2","v":{"s":"Gear Ratio"}},{"k":"mech_advantage","v":{"s":"Mech. Advantage"}},{"k":"at_the_wheel","v":{"s":"at the wheel"}},{"k":"distance_per_rev","v":{"s":"Distance per Rev"}},{"k":"gearing_matrix_gear_inches_every_ring_","v":{"s":"Gearing Matrix — gear inches (every ring × cog)"}},{"k":"lower_easier_climbing_higher_faster_to","v":{"s":"Lower = easier (climbing); higher = faster (top speed). Green→red = easy→hard. Click any cell to select that gear."}},{"k":"ring_cog","v":{"s":"ring  cog"}},{"k":"speed_vs_cadence_this_gear","v":{"s":"Speed vs. Cadence (this gear)"}},{"k":"x_axis_pedal_cadence_rpm_y_axis_bike_s","v":{"s":"X axis: pedal cadence (RPM). Y axis: bike speed (mph). Linear in this gear — double the cadence, double the speed."}},{"k":"climb_simulator_6_grade_200_w_rider","v":{"s":"⛰️ Climb Simulator (6% grade, 200 W rider)"}},{"k":"estimated_sustained_climb_speed_at_thi","v":{"s":"Estimated sustained climb speed at this gear vs. a low gear vs. a high gear:"}},{"k":"low_gear_easy","v":{"s":"Low gear (easy)"}},{"k":"this_gear_2","v":{"s":"This gear"}},{"k":"high_gear_hard","v":{"s":"High gear (hard)"}},{"k":"mph_up_the_hill","v":{"s":"mph up the hill"}},{"k":"power_torque_angular_velocity_a_low_ge","v":{"s":"Power = torque × angular velocity. A low gear trades less angular velocity (slower wheel) for more torque (more force pushing you forward), so your 200 W moves a heavier load (gravity + you + bike) even though your top speed drops. On a climb, you don't need speed — you need enough force to overcome gravity. That's what low gear gives you."}},{"k":"repair_simulator_2","v":{"s":"Repair Simulator"}},{"k":"pick_a_procedure_each_has_step_by_step","v":{"s":"Pick a procedure. Each has step-by-step instructions and tool verification — drag the right tool to the job to advance."}},{"k":"done","v":{"s":"✓ Done"}},{"k":"repair_simulator_3","v":{"s":"Repair Simulator"}},{"k":"exit_job","v":{"s":"Exit Job"}},{"k":"current_step","v":{"s":"Current Step"}},{"k":"read_this_step_aloud","v":{"s":"Read this step aloud"}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read Aloud"}},{"k":"toolbox_drag_the_right_tool_to_the_job","v":{"s":"Toolbox (drag the right tool to the job)"}},{"k":"tip_click_or_drag_if_your_choice_match","v":{"s":"Tip: click or drag. If your choice matches the step, the job advances. Wrong choice = no penalty, try again."}},{"k":"advancing_to_next_step","v":{"s":"Advancing to next step…"}},{"k":"drop_the_correct_tool_here","v":{"s":"Drop the correct tool here"}},{"k":"previous_step","v":{"s":"← Previous Step"}},{"k":"bike_fit_calculator_2","v":{"s":"Bike Fit Calculator"}},{"k":"rider_measurements","v":{"s":"Rider Measurements"}},{"k":"height","v":{"s":"Height"}},{"k":"inseam_cm","v":{"s":"Inseam (cm)"}},{"k":"how_to_measure_inseam","v":{"s":"How to measure inseam: "}},{"k":"stand_barefoot_against_a_wall_place_a_","v":{"s":"Stand barefoot against a wall. Place a hardcover book snug between your legs. Measure from floor to the top spine of the book."}},{"k":"bike_type","v":{"s":"Bike Type"}},{"k":"flexibility_experience","v":{"s":"Flexibility / Experience"}},{"k":"less_flexible_higher_bars","v":{"s":"Less flexible = higher bars."}},{"k":"your_fit","v":{"s":"Your Fit"}},{"k":"saddle","v":{"s":"● Saddle"}},{"k":"handlebar","v":{"s":"● Handlebar"}},{"k":"saddle_height","v":{"s":"Saddle Height"}},{"k":"saddle_setback","v":{"s":"Saddle Setback"}},{"k":"reach_horizontal","v":{"s":"Reach (horizontal)"}},{"k":"bar_drop","v":{"s":"Bar Drop"}},{"k":"the_science","v":{"s":"📚 The science: "}},{"k":"saddle_height_affects_knee_extension_a","v":{"s":"Saddle height affects knee extension at the bottom of the pedal stroke. Too low = quad-dominant + knee pain. Too high = hip rock + hamstring strain + saddle sores. LeMond's 0.883 × inseam targets ~27° of knee flexion at pedal 6 o'clock — optimal sustained power with least joint stress. Saddle setback aligns knee over pedal spindle (KOPS), preventing forward-knee strain."}},{"k":"dry_pavement","v":{"s":"Dry Pavement"}},{"k":"wet_pavement","v":{"s":"Wet Pavement"}},{"k":"loose_gravel","v":{"s":"Loose Gravel"}},{"k":"braking_physics_2","v":{"s":"Braking Physics"}},{"k":"braking_distance_visualization_bicycle","v":{"s":"Braking distance visualization: bicycle stops over the calculated reaction-plus-braking distance for the selected speed and surface."}},{"k":"initial_speed","v":{"s":"Initial Speed"}},{"k":"surface","v":{"s":"Surface"}},{"k":"brake_used","v":{"s":"Brake Used"}},{"k":"rear_only_stops_3_slower_than_front","v":{"s":"Rear-only stops ~3× slower than front."}},{"k":"reaction_time","v":{"s":"Reaction Time"}},{"k":"alert_adult_1_0_s_pro_racer_0_3_s","v":{"s":"Alert adult: 1.0 s · Pro racer: 0.3 s"}},{"k":"total_stopping_distance","v":{"s":"Total Stopping Distance"}},{"k":"braking_distance","v":{"s":"Braking Distance"}},{"k":"reaction_distance","v":{"s":"Reaction Distance"}},{"k":"time_to_stop","v":{"s":"Time to Stop"}},{"k":"weight_transfer_endo_limit","v":{"s":"📐 Weight Transfer & Endo Limit"}},{"k":"hard_front_braking_transfers_weight_fo","v":{"s":"Hard front braking transfers weight forward. If deceleration exceeds a = g·(L − a_cm)/h_cm, the rear wheel lifts (endo)."}},{"k":"at_this_friction_pure_front_braking_wi","v":{"s":"⚠ At this friction, pure front braking will flip you. Shift weight back or use both brakes."}},{"k":"real_world_lesson","v":{"s":"🎯 Real-World Lesson"}},{"k":"doubling_speed_quadruples_stopping_dis","v":{"s":"Doubling speed quadruples stopping distance"}},{"k":"d_v","v":{"s":" (d ∝ v²)."}},{"k":"front_brake_does_70_of_the_work","v":{"s":"Front brake does ~70% of the work"}},{"k":"on_pavement","v":{"s":" on pavement."}},{"k":"wet_cuts_grip_nearly_in_half","v":{"s":"Wet cuts grip nearly in half."}},{"k":"on_gravel_ice_rear_brake_is_safer","v":{"s":"On gravel/ice, rear brake is safer"}},{"k":"locked_rear_skids_locked_front_over_th","v":{"s":" — locked rear skids; locked front = over the bars."}},{"k":"left_arm_straight_out_to_the_side","v":{"s":"Left arm straight out to the side"}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_up_at_90","v":{"s":"Left arm bent up at 90°"}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_down_at_90","v":{"s":"Left arm bent down at 90°"}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_up_at_90_forearm_vertica","v":{"s":"Left arm bent up at 90° (forearm vertical)"}},{"k":"right_arm_straight_out","v":{"s":"Right arm straight out"}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_down","v":{"s":"Left arm bent down"}},{"k":"left_arm_straight_down_palm_facing_bac","v":{"s":"Left arm straight down, palm facing back"}},{"k":"both_arms_out_to_the_sides","v":{"s":"Both arms out to the sides"}},{"k":"left_arm_straight_out","v":{"s":"Left arm straight out"}},{"k":"on_the_sidewalk_safer_from_cars","v":{"s":"On the sidewalk — safer from cars"}},{"k":"far_right_of_the_road_with_traffic","v":{"s":"Far right of the road, with traffic"}},{"k":"in_the_middle_of_the_right_lane","v":{"s":"In the middle of the right lane"}},{"k":"against_traffic_so_you_can_see_cars_co","v":{"s":"Against traffic so you can see cars coming"}},{"k":"speed_through_momentum_helps_balance","v":{"s":"Speed through — momentum helps balance"}},{"k":"come_to_a_full_stop_then_proceed_in_or","v":{"s":"Come to a FULL stop, then proceed in order"}},{"k":"slow_to_5_mph_but_don_t_fully_stop","v":{"s":"Slow to 5 mph but don't fully stop"}},{"k":"wait_for_cars_to_go_first_always","v":{"s":"Wait for cars to go first, always"}},{"k":"only_on_long_rides","v":{"s":"Only on long rides"}},{"k":"only_at_night","v":{"s":"Only at night"}},{"k":"every_ride_every_time","v":{"s":"EVERY ride, every time"}},{"k":"only_when_you_ride_fast","v":{"s":"Only when you ride fast"}},{"k":"1_foot","v":{"s":"1 foot"}},{"k":"2_feet","v":{"s":"2 feet"}},{"k":"3_feet","v":{"s":"3 feet"}},{"k":"no_specific_distance_just_safe","v":{"s":"No specific distance — just safe"}},{"k":"reflectors_only_no_lights_needed","v":{"s":"Reflectors only — no lights needed"}},{"k":"a_white_front_headlight_visible_500_ft","v":{"s":"A white front headlight visible 500 ft AND a red rear reflector or light"}},{"k":"just_a_rear_reflector","v":{"s":"Just a rear reflector"}},{"k":"no_lights_the_rider_just_has_to_wear_b","v":{"s":"No lights — the rider just has to wear bright clothing"}},{"k":"yell_at_drivers","v":{"s":"Yell at drivers"}},{"k":"stick_your_left_arm_straight_out_check","v":{"s":"Stick your left arm straight out, check over your shoulder, signal early"}},{"k":"just_turn_drivers_will_see_you","v":{"s":"Just turn — drivers will see you"}},{"k":"brake_first_then_turn_the_wheel","v":{"s":"Brake first, then turn the wheel"}},{"k":"loading_quiz","v":{"s":"Loading quiz…"}},{"k":"hand_signals_safety_2","v":{"s":"Hand Signals & Safety"}},{"k":"correct","v":{"s":"✓ Correct"}},{"k":"your_answer","v":{"s":"✗ Your answer"}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"your_block","v":{"s":"Your block"}},{"k":"stop_sign","v":{"s":"STOP SIGN"}},{"k":"flat_run","v":{"s":"Flat run"}},{"k":"oak_hill","v":{"s":"Oak Hill"}},{"k":"descent","v":{"s":"Descent"}},{"k":"wet_patch","v":{"s":"Wet patch"}},{"k":"school_st","v":{"s":"School St."}},{"k":"neighborhood_ride_2","v":{"s":"Neighborhood Ride"}},{"k":"neighborhood_ride_bicycle_traverses_st","v":{"s":"Neighborhood ride: bicycle traverses streets with hills, stop signs, traffic, and weather. Use the brake button or arrow keys to control."}},{"k":"toggle_fullscreen_for_the_neighborhood","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen for the neighborhood ride"}},{"k":"fullscreen_2","v":{"s":"Fullscreen"}},{"k":"session","v":{"s":"Session"}},{"k":"ride_again","v":{"s":"↻ Ride Again"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"bike_2","v":{"s":"Bike"}},{"k":"gear","v":{"s":"Gear (↑/↓)"}},{"k":"shift_to_a_lower_gear_easier_pedaling_","v":{"s":"Shift to a lower gear (easier pedaling, less top speed)"}},{"k":"lower","v":{"s":"↓ Lower"}},{"k":"shift_to_a_higher_gear_more_top_speed_","v":{"s":"Shift to a higher gear (more top speed, harder pedaling)"}},{"k":"higher","v":{"s":"↑ Higher"}},{"k":"pedal_power","v":{"s":"Pedal Power"}},{"k":"brake_space","v":{"s":"Brake (Space)"}},{"k":"hold_to_slow_or_stop","v":{"s":"Hold to slow or stop"}},{"k":"you_made_it_to_school","v":{"s":"You made it to school"}},{"k":"new_personal_best","v":{"s":"🏆 New personal best!"}},{"k":"stop_sign_2","v":{"s":"Stop Sign"}},{"k":"hill_crawl","v":{"s":"Hill Crawl"}},{"k":"wet_section","v":{"s":"Wet Section"}},{"k":"coach","v":{"s":"Coach"}},{"k":"course","v":{"s":"🧭 Course: "}},{"k":"180_m_flat_stop_sign_flat_run_oak_hill","v":{"s":"180 m flat → 🛑 stop sign → flat run → ⛰️ Oak Hill (7% grade) → descent → 🌧️ wet patch → 🏫 school (600 m total). Use ↑/↓ to shift gears, Space to brake. Apply the physics you learned in Sandbox, Gearing, and Braking labs."}},{"k":"top_tube","v":{"s":"Top Tube"}},{"k":"down_tube","v":{"s":"Down Tube"}},{"k":"seat_tube","v":{"s":"Seat Tube"}},{"k":"chainstay","v":{"s":"Chainstay"}},{"k":"seatstay","v":{"s":"Seatstay"}},{"k":"head_tube","v":{"s":"Head Tube"}},{"k":"spokes","v":{"s":"Spokes"}},{"k":"tire","v":{"s":"Tire"}},{"k":"chainring","v":{"s":"Chainring"}},{"k":"cassette","v":{"s":"Cassette"}},{"k":"chain","v":{"s":"Chain"}},{"k":"rear_derailleur","v":{"s":"Rear Derailleur"}},{"k":"shifter","v":{"s":"Shifter"}},{"k":"handlebar_2","v":{"s":"Handlebar"}},{"k":"stem","v":{"s":"Stem"}},{"k":"saddle_2","v":{"s":"Saddle"}},{"k":"brake_lever","v":{"s":"Brake Lever"}},{"k":"brake_caliper","v":{"s":"Brake Caliper"}},{"k":"bike_parts_inspector_2","v":{"s":"Bike Parts Inspector"}},{"k":"click_any_part_of_the_bike","v":{"s":"Click any part of the bike"}},{"k":"has_repair_job","v":{"s":"Has repair job"}},{"k":"function","v":{"s":"Function"}},{"k":"common_issues","v":{"s":"Common Issues"}},{"k":"open_related_repair_job","v":{"s":"🔧 Open related repair job →"}},{"k":"parts_by_category","v":{"s":"Parts by category"}},{"k":"helmet_fit_checker_2","v":{"s":"Helmet Fit Checker"}},{"k":"live_fit_diagram","v":{"s":"Live Fit Diagram"}},{"k":"eyebrow_line","v":{"s":"eyebrow line"}},{"k":"the_2v1_rule","v":{"s":"📏 The 2V1 Rule"}},{"k":"2_fingers","v":{"s":"2 fingers"}},{"k":"above_the_eyebrows_the_helmet_should_s","v":{"s":" above the eyebrows: the helmet should sit LOW on the forehead, not pushed back to expose your forehead."}},{"k":"under_each_ear_the_front_and_rear_stra","v":{"s":" under each ear: the front and rear straps should meet just below your ear, forming a clean V (or Y)."}},{"k":"1_finger","v":{"s":"1 finger"}},{"k":"chin_slack_only_one_finger_should_fit_","v":{"s":" chin slack: only one finger should fit between the chin strap and your jaw — looser and the helmet pops off in a crash; tighter and you can't breathe comfortably."}},{"k":"why_it_matters","v":{"s":"Why it matters: "}},{"k":"a_loose_or_tilted_helmet_protects_almo","v":{"s":"A loose or tilted helmet protects almost as poorly as no helmet at all. Concussion-research data shows 60-80% of cyclist head injuries happen with a helmet that was incorrectly fitted or unstrapped at the moment of impact."}},{"k":"tire_pressure_dropped_5_10_psi_for_sno","v":{"s":"Tire pressure dropped 5-10 PSI for snow/slush traction"}},{"k":"studded_tires_installed_if_riding_on_i","v":{"s":"Studded tires installed if riding on ice (or fat-bike with low PSI)"}},{"k":"front_white_rear_red_lights_installed_","v":{"s":"Front white + rear red lights installed (Maine §2065 — required at night)"}},{"k":"wet_winter_chain_lube_applied_washes_o","v":{"s":"Wet/winter chain lube applied (washes off less in slush)"}},{"k":"fenders_installed_slush_spray_wet_cold","v":{"s":"Fenders installed (slush spray = wet & cold = stops you within 1 ride)"}},{"k":"brake_pads_inspected_wet_grit_conditio","v":{"s":"Brake pads inspected — wet/grit conditions wear them 3-5x faster"}},{"k":"layered_clothing_wicking_base_insulati","v":{"s":"Layered clothing: wicking base + insulating mid + windproof shell"}},{"k":"hands_lobster_mitts_bar_mitts_feet_woo","v":{"s":"Hands (lobster mitts/bar mitts), feet (wool socks + waterproof shoes), face (balaclava)"}},{"k":"high_vis_or_reflective_outer_layer_dri","v":{"s":"High-vis or reflective outer layer (drivers see you 3x sooner)"}},{"k":"route_checked_for_plow_status_recently","v":{"s":"Route checked for plow status — recently-plowed > untouched powder > glare ice"}},{"k":"phone_in_inner_pocket_cold_drains_batt","v":{"s":"Phone in inner pocket (cold drains battery; keep it warm)"}},{"k":"post_ride_rinse_the_bike_to_remove_roa","v":{"s":"Post-ride: rinse the bike to remove road salt (corrodes drivetrain in days)"}},{"k":"maine_winter_biking_2","v":{"s":"Maine Winter Biking"}},{"k":"riding_through_a_maine_winter","v":{"s":"Riding through a Maine winter"}},{"k":"maine_s_winter_is_roughly_5_months_lon","v":{"s":"Maine's winter is roughly 5 months long. Done right, biking in -10°F can be safer than biking in summer rain — there's less traffic, the road is harder, and you're more visible. Done wrong, you crash on black ice or freeze your fingers. This guide covers the gear, technique, and Maine-specific knowledge you need."}},{"k":"fat_bikes_3_5_5_tires","v":{"s":"Fat bikes (3.5-5\" tires) "}},{"k":"float_on_packed_snow_and_run_at_5_12_p","v":{"s":"float on packed snow and run at 5-12 PSI — way below road bike pressures. The wider contact patch behaves like a snowshoe. Norway and Minnesota commute on these year-round."}},{"k":"studded_tires","v":{"s":"Studded tires "}},{"k":"have_100_300_metal_carbide_studs_that_","v":{"s":"have 100-300 metal carbide studs that bite into ice. Maine has no winter-tire law for bicycles (cars are restricted to Oct 1 – May 1, but bikes can run them anytime). They're loud and slow on bare pavement, so most riders swap them on/off seasonally."}},{"k":"pressure_rule","v":{"s":"Pressure rule: "}},{"k":"drop_5_10_psi_for_snow_slush_lower_pre","v":{"s":"drop 5-10 PSI for snow/slush. Lower pressure = larger contact patch = more grip. But not so low you pinch-flat over a curb."}},{"k":"black_ice","v":{"s":"Black ice "}},{"k":"forms_first_on_bridges_no_ground_heat_","v":{"s":"forms first on bridges (no ground heat below) and shaded curves. Just-below-freezing (28-32°F) is more dangerous than -10°F because surface meltwater pools and refreezes."}},{"k":"glare_ice","v":{"s":"Glare ice "}},{"k":"is_fully_transparent_looks_like_wet_as","v":{"s":"is fully transparent — looks like wet asphalt. If you suspect it: don't brake, don't turn. Coast straight through."}},{"k":"salt_sand","v":{"s":"Salt + sand "}},{"k":"on_roads_gives_traction_but_eats_your_","v":{"s":"on roads gives traction but eats your drivetrain. A salty chain lasts a week instead of a season."}},{"k":"plow_priority","v":{"s":"Plow priority: "}},{"k":"recently_plowed_untouched_2_powder_dee","v":{"s":"recently plowed > untouched 2\" powder > deep slush > glare ice. Plan your route around the plow schedule."}},{"k":"wicking_synthetic_or_merino_wool_never","v":{"s":"wicking synthetic or merino wool. NEVER cotton — once wet from sweat, it freezes against your skin."}},{"k":"insulating_fleece_or_light_down_trap_w","v":{"s":"insulating fleece or light down. Trap warm air without bulk that restricts pedaling."}},{"k":"windproof_and_ideally_water_resistant_","v":{"s":"windproof and ideally water-resistant. Wind chill at 15 mph adds 10-15°F of cooling."}},{"k":"rule_of_thumb","v":{"s":"Rule of thumb: "}},{"k":"dress_so_you_re_slightly_cold_for_the_","v":{"s":"dress so you're slightly cold for the first 5 minutes. If you're warm at the start, you'll be soaked in sweat 20 minutes in."}},{"k":"hands","v":{"s":"Hands "}},{"k":"fail_first_lobster_claw_mitts_3_finger","v":{"s":"fail first. Lobster-claw mitts (3-finger) are warmer than gloves and still let you brake. Bar mitts (handlebar pogies) are the warmest — they wrap the entire hand-and-grip area."}},{"k":"feet","v":{"s":"Feet "}},{"k":"fail_second_wool_socks_waterproof_shoe","v":{"s":"fail second. Wool socks + waterproof shoes + neoprene shoe covers. Toe warmers (chemical packets) work for sub-zero rides."}},{"k":"a_balaclava_ski_goggles_below_20_f_col","v":{"s":"a balaclava + ski goggles below 20°F. Cold air hurts to breathe — a thin scarf over the mouth warms inhalations."}},{"k":"below_0_f","v":{"s":"Below 0°F: "}},{"k":"cover_every_square_inch_of_skin_frostb","v":{"s":"cover EVERY square inch of skin. Frostbite begins in 10-30 minutes of exposed skin in those temps."}},{"k":"the_maine_salt_problem","v":{"s":"The Maine salt problem"}},{"k":"maine_roads_use_brine_rock_salt_all_wi","v":{"s":"Maine roads use brine + rock salt all winter. Salt + steel = rust within DAYS, not weeks. After every wet/slushy ride: rinse the bike with a hose, dry the chain, re-lube. Skip this and you'll go through chains and cassettes 3-4x faster than summer riders. Aluminum frames are mostly fine; steel frames need extra care, especially at welds."}},{"k":"winter_ride_checklist","v":{"s":"✅ Winter-Ride Checklist"}},{"k":"turning_left_at_the_upcoming_intersect","v":{"s":"Turning left at the upcoming intersection"}},{"k":"turning_right_onto_the_side_street","v":{"s":"Turning right onto the side street"}},{"k":"stopping_for_a_red_light_ahead","v":{"s":"Stopping for a red light ahead"}},{"k":"approaching_a_stop_sign","v":{"s":"Approaching a stop sign"}},{"k":"changing_to_the_left_lane_to_pass_a_pa","v":{"s":"Changing to the left lane to pass a parked car"}},{"k":"merging_right_into_the_bike_lane","v":{"s":"Merging right into the bike lane"}},{"k":"slowing_for_a_pedestrian_in_the_crossw","v":{"s":"Slowing for a pedestrian in the crosswalk"}},{"k":"turning_left_at_a_roundabout_exit","v":{"s":"Turning left at a roundabout exit"}},{"k":"turning_right_after_the_next_utility_p","v":{"s":"Turning right after the next utility pole"}},{"k":"stopping_for_an_obstacle_ahead_downed_","v":{"s":"Stopping for an obstacle ahead (downed branch)"}},{"k":"signal_reaction_drill_2","v":{"s":"Signal Reaction Drill"}},{"k":"round","v":{"s":"Round"}},{"k":"avg_correct","v":{"s":"Avg (Correct)"}},{"k":"personal_best","v":{"s":"🏆 Personal Best"}},{"k":"a_traffic_situation_will_appear_press_","v":{"s":"A traffic situation will appear. Press the matching hand signal as fast as you can. 10 rounds. Best correct-only average is your personal best."}},{"k":"start_drill","v":{"s":"▶ Start Drill"}},{"k":"drill_complete","v":{"s":"Drill complete!"}},{"k":"run_again","v":{"s":"↻ Run Again"}},{"k":"hand_signal_reference","v":{"s":"📖 Hand Signal Reference"}},{"k":"left_turn","v":{"s":"Left turn: "}},{"k":"left_arm_extended_straight_out_to_the_","v":{"s":"left arm extended straight out to the side."}},{"k":"right_turn","v":{"s":"Right turn: "}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_up_at_90_or_right_arm_ou","v":{"s":"left arm bent up at 90° (or right arm out — both legal in Maine)."}},{"k":"stop_slow","v":{"s":"Stop / slow: "}},{"k":"left_arm_bent_down_at_90_with_palm_fac","v":{"s":"left arm bent down at 90° with palm facing back."}},{"k":"spinning_zone_80_95_rpm","v":{"s":"🟢 Spinning zone (80-95 RPM)"}},{"k":"optimal_efficiency_band_sustainable_jo","v":{"s":"Optimal efficiency band. Sustainable, joint-friendly."}},{"k":"mashing_60_rpm","v":{"s":"🔴 Mashing (<60 RPM)"}},{"k":"low_cadence_high_force_per_stroke_hard","v":{"s":"Low cadence, high force per stroke. Hard on knees."}},{"k":"mid_cadence_60_80_rpm","v":{"s":"🟡 Mid cadence (60-80 RPM)"}},{"k":"functional_but_not_optimal","v":{"s":"Functional but not optimal."}},{"k":"over_spinning_110_rpm","v":{"s":"🟠 Over-spinning (>110 RPM)"}},{"k":"wasted_effort_cardio_cost_exceeds_outp","v":{"s":"Wasted effort — cardio cost exceeds output."}},{"k":"pedal_rpm","v":{"s":"Pedal RPM"}},{"k":"grade","v":{"s":"Grade (%)"}},{"k":"gear_ratio_3","v":{"s":"Gear ratio"}},{"k":"hypothesis_free_text_how_do_gear_and_g","v":{"s":"Hypothesis (free text): How do gear and grade interact to keep cadence in the spinning zone?"}},{"k":"why_does_cadence_matter_how_do_gear_an","v":{"s":"Why does cadence matter? How do gear and grade interact?"}},{"k":"patch_tube_description","v":{"s":"The #1 roadside repair. Find the puncture, rough it up, glue, patch, reassemble."}},{"k":"brake_adjust_description","v":{"s":"Rim brakes rubbing, soft lever, or uneven pads? Fix cable tension and pad alignment."}},{"k":"chain_clean_description","v":{"s":"A clean chain shifts quieter and lasts 3× longer. Degrease, dry, re-lube."}},{"k":"derailleur_index_description","v":{"s":"Chain skipping or slow to shift? Dial in cable tension one click at a time."}},{"k":"pedal_change_description","v":{"s":"Pedals wear out or you want to switch between flat, clipless, or platform. The trick: opposite threads on the left side."}},{"k":"wheel_true_description","v":{"s":"Bent rim causing brake rub? A basic true fixes side-to-side wobble with a spoke wrench. Introduces the real truing stand workflow."}},{"k":"headset_adjust_description","v":{"s":"Clunking from the front end under braking? The headset (steering bearings) needs preload adjustment. Critical safety fix."}},{"k":"rider_power_watts","v":{"s":"Rider power in watts"}},{"k":"gear_ratio_slider","v":{"s":"Gear ratio"}},{"k":"wind_speed_slider","v":{"s":"Wind speed and direction"}},{"k":"pedal_cadence_rpm_slider","v":{"s":"Pedal cadence in RPM"}},{"k":"rider_height_centimeters","v":{"s":"Rider height in centimeters"}},{"k":"rider_inseam_centimeters","v":{"s":"Rider inseam in centimeters"}},{"k":"initial_speed_mph_slider","v":{"s":"Initial speed in miles per hour"}},{"k":"reaction_time_seconds_slider","v":{"s":"Reaction time in seconds"}},{"k":"neighborhood_ride_gear_ratio","v":{"s":"Neighborhood ride gear ratio"}},{"k":"neighborhood_ride_pedal_power_watts","v":{"s":"Neighborhood ride pedal power in watts"}}],"id":491}},{"k":"birdlab","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_external_resources_for","v":{"s":"External resources 