"}},{"k":"third_candidate","v":{"s":"Third candidate"}},{"k":"other_candidate","v":{"s":"Other candidate"}},{"k":"teacher_notes","v":{"s":"Teacher Notes"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher Notes — click to expand"}},{"k":"print_this_module_page","v":{"s":"Print this module page"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"ngss_cte_standards","v":{"s":"NGSS / CTE Standards"}},{"k":"discussion_questions","v":{"s":"Discussion Questions"}},{"k":"watch_for_these_misconceptions","v":{"s":"Watch for these misconceptions"}},{"k":"extension_activity","v":{"s":"Extension Activity"}},{"k":"sources","v":{"s":"Sources"}},{"k":"i_spy_bird_spotter","v":{"s":"I-Spy Bird Spotter"}},{"k":"5_layered_habitat_scenes","v":{"s":"5 layered habitat scenes"}},{"k":"5_distinct_habitat_scenes_forest_marsh","v":{"s":"5 distinct habitat scenes (forest, marsh, backyard, coast, mountain) with animated birds at their species' real movement signatures. Click any bird to identify. Reduced-motion fallback for users who prefer static. Per-habitat \"found\" tracking."}},{"k":"field_marks_trainer","v":{"s":"Field Marks Trainer"}},{"k":"the_vocabulary_of_birding","v":{"s":"The vocabulary of birding"}},{"k":"birders_identify_by_looking_at_specifi","v":{"s":"Birders identify by looking at specific anatomy: wing bars, eye rings, supercilium, malar stripes, breast streaking, primary projection. Click parts of an annotated bird to learn what each mark is and why it matters. Phase 1 ships with the Black-capped Chickadee deep-dive."}},{"k":"beak_feet_lab","v":{"s":"Beak & Feet Lab"}},{"k":"adaptation_match_what_does_this_beak_d","v":{"s":"Adaptation match — what does this beak do?"}},{"k":"match_beak_shapes_to_diet_seedeater_fi","v":{"s":"Match beak shapes to diet (seedeater, fish-spear, raptor hook, nectar tube, insect-tweezer). Match foot shapes to lifestyle (perching, swimming, climbing, raptor talons). Cross-links to EvoLab's Galápagos Beak Lab for the deep evolution story."}},{"k":"bird_call_trainer","v":{"s":"Bird Call Trainer"}},{"k":"songs_in_plain_english","v":{"s":"Songs in plain English"}},{"k":"15_mnemonic_phrases_drink_your_tea_for","v":{"s":"15 mnemonic phrases (\"drink-your-tea\" for towhee, \"who-cooks-for-you\" for Barred Owl) + an 8-question matching quiz. Pairs with Cornell's free Merlin Bird ID app for the real audio."}},{"k":"habitat_match","v":{"s":"Habitat Match"}},{"k":"predict_birds_predict_habitats","v":{"s":"Predict birds; predict habitats"}},{"k":"two_reverse_direction_reasoning_games_","v":{"s":"Two reverse-direction reasoning games. Bird → Habitat (where would you find this species?) and Habitat → Birds (multi-select which species belong here). 12 species × 5 habitats."}},{"k":"maine_birds_spotlight","v":{"s":"Maine Birds Spotlight"}},{"k":"25_species_12_birding_hotspots","v":{"s":"25 species + 12 birding hotspots"}},{"k":"25_maine_species_cards_year_round_bree","v":{"s":"25 Maine species cards (year-round, breeders, winter visitors, migrants) with real Maine conservation stories: Project Puffin, Bald Eagle DDT recovery, Wood Thrush decline. Plus 12 Maine Birding Trail hotspots (Scarborough Marsh, Eastern Egg Rock, Acadia, Bradbury Hawkwatch, Baxter, more)."}},{"k":"migration_patterns","v":{"s":"Migration Patterns"}},{"k":"when_where_why_birds_move","v":{"s":"When, where, why birds move"}},{"k":"4_north_american_flyways_with_maine_s_","v":{"s":"4 North American flyways with Maine's role in the Atlantic Flyway. Spring + fall arrival/departure calendar by month. 8 featured migrators with strategies + distances. Cross-links to dedicated Migration & Wind Patterns Lab for flight physics."}},{"k":"citizen_science_bridge","v":{"s":"Citizen Science Bridge"}},{"k":"your_sightings_real_science","v":{"s":"Your sightings = real science"}},{"k":"8_projects_ebird_merlin_feederwatch_cb","v":{"s":"8 projects (eBird, Merlin, FeederWatch, CBC, NestWatch, GBBC, Maine Audubon, iNaturalist) with full cards + a 5-question match quiz that recommends 1–2 projects that fit your time + interests."}},{"k":"field_observation_challenge","v":{"s":"Field Observation Challenge"}},{"k":"track_a_bird_log_it_reflect","v":{"s":"Track a bird → log it → reflect"}},{"k":"a_three_phase_observation_activity_1_k","v":{"s":"A three-phase observation activity: (1) keep your binocular reticle on a moving bird for 5 cumulative seconds, (2) fill a structured field log (date, location, behavior, weather, count), (3) write a brief reflective note. Saves to a personal notebook on your device."}},{"k":"bird_photo_id_ai","v":{"s":"Bird Photo ID (AI)"}},{"k":"upload_a_photo_get_a_probable_species_","v":{"s":"Upload a photo, get a probable species + confidence"}},{"k":"upload_any_bird_photo_gemini_vision_re","v":{"s":"Upload any bird photo. Gemini Vision returns up to 3 candidate species with confidence scores, the field marks it noticed, and direct links to verify on eBird, All About Birds, Audubon, Wikipedia, and iNaturalist. Honest about uncertainty."}},{"k":"conservation_careers","v":{"s":"Conservation & Careers"}},{"k":"3_billion_missing_birds_9_careers","v":{"s":"3 billion missing birds + 9 careers"}},{"k":"rosenberg_2019_3_billion_birds_study_w","v":{"s":"Rosenberg 2019 \"3 billion birds\" study with methodology + key findings + the hopeful takeaway. 4 Maine focal species (Piping Plover, Wood Thrush, recovered Bald Eagle + Atlantic Puffin). Climate context (Gulf of Maine warming). 9 ornithology career paths with honest pay + Maine programs."}},{"k":"side_by_side_comparator","v":{"s":"Side-by-Side Comparator"}},{"k":"train_the_comparison_id_skill","v":{"s":"Train the comparison-ID skill"}},{"k":"pick_any_two_species_see_them_rendered","v":{"s":"Pick any two species — see them rendered at matching scale with their field marks, habitats, calls, and movement signatures lined up. The way an experienced birder evaluates a confusing sighting. 6 hand-curated teaching pairs (chickadee ⇄ junco, mallard ⇄ eider, etc.) plus free-pick from 15 species."}},{"k":"nest_gallery","v":{"s":"Nest Gallery"}},{"k":"20_maine_breeding_nests_with_svg_profi","v":{"s":"20 Maine breeding nests with SVG profiles"}},{"k":"20_maine_breeding_bird_species_shown_w","v":{"s":"20 Maine breeding bird species shown with their actual nest structures: stick platforms (eagle/osprey), woven cups (oriole/red-wing), cavities (chickadee/pileated), burrows (kingfisher/puffin), scrapes (plover/woodcock). Visual SVG renderings + dimensions + materials + eggs + species story."}},{"k":"egg_gallery","v":{"s":"Egg Gallery"}},{"k":"35_maine_bird_eggs_to_scale","v":{"s":"35 Maine bird eggs to scale"}},{"k":"35_maine_bird_eggs_rendered_at_relativ","v":{"s":"35 Maine bird eggs rendered at relative scale — from the pea-sized hummingbird egg to the loon's large olive-brown ovoid. Sort by size to compare; click any egg for full details on dimensions, clutch size, pattern, and species notes."}},{"k":"feather_anatomy_lab","v":{"s":"Feather Anatomy Lab"}},{"k":"interactive_labeled_feather_diagram","v":{"s":"Interactive labeled feather diagram"}},{"k":"click_8_numbered_parts_of_an_annotated","v":{"s":"Click 8 numbered parts of an annotated feather to learn its name + function: rachis, calamus, vane, barb, barbule, hooklets, downy region, aftershaft. Feathers are biology's most complex structure — 1 million microscopic hooklets per feather."}},{"k":"silhouette_quiz","v":{"s":"Silhouette Quiz"}},{"k":"20_birds_id_from_shape_alone","v":{"s":"20 birds — ID from shape alone"}},{"k":"identify_20_maine_birds_from_silhouett","v":{"s":"Identify 20 Maine birds from silhouettes alone. Habitat clue + 4 multiple-choice. Builds shape-based ID — the skill experienced birders use for distant flying birds. Each correct answer reveals the species' key flight signature."}},{"k":"seasonal_plumage_molt","v":{"s":"Seasonal Plumage + Molt"}},{"k":"14_species_through_the_year","v":{"s":"14 species through the year"}},{"k":"how_birds_change_plumage_seasonally_14","v":{"s":"How birds change plumage seasonally. 14 species showing breeding vs non-breeding plumage + molt strategy + Maine timing. From goldfinches changing color to wood ducks entering eclipse plumage to snow buntings revealing white through feather wear."}},{"k":"tracks_sign","v":{"s":"Tracks + Sign"}},{"k":"read_bird_evidence_you_never_see","v":{"s":"Read bird evidence you never see"}},{"k":"12_species_and_their_tracks_droppings_","v":{"s":"12 species and their tracks, droppings, feeding sign, and pellets. Read bird evidence: turkey scratches in leaf litter, pileated woodpecker rectangular holes, owl pellets, kingfisher fish-bone middens, hawk plucking sites."}},{"k":"flight_patterns","v":{"s":"Flight Patterns"}},{"k":"10_flight_styles_species","v":{"s":"10 flight styles + species"}},{"k":"10_flight_patterns_from_soaring_to_und","v":{"s":"10 flight patterns from soaring to undulating to hovering to plunge-diving. Behavior in flight is a field mark — how a bird moves through air identifies it from far away. Includes energy cost, timing, and ID tips for each pattern."}},{"k":"maine_owls_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Maine Owls Deep Dive"}},{"k":"8_maine_owl_species","v":{"s":"8 Maine owl species"}},{"k":"eastern_screech_great_horned_barred_sn","v":{"s":"Eastern Screech, Great Horned, Barred, Snowy, Long-eared, Short-eared, Saw-whet, Boreal — Maine's 8 owl species in detail. Size, voice, habitat, diet, Maine status, color morphs, conservation status."}},{"k":"maine_raptors_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Maine Raptors Deep Dive"}},{"k":"12_maine_raptor_species","v":{"s":"12 Maine raptor species"}},{"k":"eagles_hawks_falcons_harriers_vultures","v":{"s":"Eagles, hawks, falcons, harriers, vultures. 12 Maine raptor species with group, size, voice, diet, hunting style, breeding, conservation status. From the Bradbury Mountain broadwings to recovered peregrines on city bridges."}},{"k":"maine_warblers_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Maine Warblers Deep Dive"}},{"k":"15_hardest_to_id_maine_breeders","v":{"s":"15 hardest-to-ID Maine breeders"}},{"k":"15_maine_breeding_warblers_the_hardest","v":{"s":"15 Maine breeding warblers — the hardest ID family for new birders. Tiny, fast, seasonal. Each profile: key field mark, habitat, song mnemonic, arrival + departure dates. From yellow-rumped (most common) to magnolia + black-throated green + Blackburnian (the fire-throated jewel)."}},{"k":"birding_optics_gear","v":{"s":"Birding Optics + Gear"}},{"k":"binoculars_scopes_apps","v":{"s":"Binoculars, scopes, apps"}},{"k":"what_does_8_42_mean_binoculars_spottin","v":{"s":"What does \"8×42\" mean? Binoculars, spotting scopes, apps (Merlin + Audubon), digiscoping, harnesses, cameras. From budget starter ($80 binos) to premium gear ($4,000 Swarovski). What to buy + how + why."}},{"k":"birding_ethics","v":{"s":"Birding Ethics"}},{"k":"aba_code_of_birding_ethics","v":{"s":"ABA Code of Birding Ethics"}},{"k":"the_american_birding_association_code_","v":{"s":"The American Birding Association Code — eight principles for birders: respect birds + their environment, observe access rules, keep cats indoors, reduce window collisions, don't share rare-bird locations, help with citizen science, model good behavior for kids, build inclusion."}},{"k":"weather_birding","v":{"s":"Weather + Birding"}},{"k":"10_weather_scenarios_bird_response","v":{"s":"10 weather scenarios + bird response"}},{"k":"how_birds_respond_to_weather_10_weathe","v":{"s":"How birds respond to weather. 10 weather scenarios — cold fronts, storm passages, fog, sunny calm, tailwinds, cold snaps — with bird response + where to go for each."}},{"k":"birding_faq","v":{"s":"Birding FAQ"}},{"k":"15_common_questions_answered","v":{"s":"15 common questions answered"}},{"k":"15_of_the_most_common_questions_from_n","v":{"s":"15 of the most common questions from new birders — where to start, equipment, LBJ identification, climate change, window strikes, cats, bald eagles in Maine, learning to recognize bird songs, and more."}},{"k":"maine_waterfowl","v":{"s":"Maine Waterfowl"}},{"k":"20_ducks_geese_swans","v":{"s":"20 ducks + geese + swans"}},{"k":"maine_s_20_waterfowl_species_dabblers_","v":{"s":"Maine's 20 waterfowl species — dabblers (mallard, wood duck, black duck), divers (goldeneye, bufflehead, mergansers), sea ducks (eider, scoters, long-tailed), geese (Canada, snow, brant), swans. Male + female plumages, habitat, diet, voice, breeding biology."}},{"k":"maine_shorebirds","v":{"s":"Maine Shorebirds"}},{"k":"15_plovers_sandpipers_snipes","v":{"s":"15 plovers, sandpipers, snipes"}},{"k":"maine_s_15_shorebird_species_plovers_p","v":{"s":"Maine's 15 shorebird species. Plovers (piping, semipalmated, killdeer), yellowlegs (greater + lesser), sandpipers (spotted, solitary, sanderling, semipalmated, least, dunlin), willet, turnstone, snipe. Mostly migrants; some breeders."}},{"k":"gulf_of_maine_seabirds","v":{"s":"Gulf of Maine Seabirds"}},{"k":"12_alcids_pelagic_species","v":{"s":"12 alcids + pelagic species"}},{"k":"atlantic_puffin_razorbill_murre_black_","v":{"s":"Atlantic Puffin, razorbill, murre, black guillemot, gannet, fulmar, shearwater, storm-petrel, kittiwake, terns. Maine's offshore + Gulf of Maine seabirds with Project Puffin restoration story."}},{"k":"wing_types_lab","v":{"s":"Wing Types Lab"}},{"k":"5_wing_shapes_flight_styles","v":{"s":"5 wing shapes + flight styles"}},{"k":"elliptical_wings_songbirds_high_aspect","v":{"s":"Elliptical wings (songbirds), high-aspect-ratio (swallows + albatrosses), soaring wings with slots (eagles + vultures), high-speed tapered (falcons), aquatic (puffins). Wing shape predicts flight style + habitat."}},{"k":"foot_types_lab","v":{"s":"Foot Types Lab"}},{"k":"8_foot_types_adaptations","v":{"s":"8 foot types + adaptations"}},{"k":"perching_anisodactyl_raptor_talons_cli","v":{"s":"Perching (anisodactyl), raptor talons, climbing zygodactyl (woodpeckers), swimming palmate (ducks), wading long-thin (herons), lobed (grebes), grasping (osprey), walking (turkeys). Foot anatomy reveals diet + habitat."}},{"k":"bird_behavior_deep","v":{"s":"Bird Behavior Deep"}},{"k":"8_behavior_topics","v":{"s":"8 behavior topics"}},{"k":"courtship_displays_territorial_defense","v":{"s":"Courtship displays, territorial defense, parental care, mating systems, migration, communication, mobbing predators, foraging niche differentiation. The behaviors that shape bird life."}},{"k":"bird_physiology","v":{"s":"Bird Physiology"}},{"k":"8_biology_systems","v":{"s":"8 biology systems"}},{"k":"heart_rate_metabolism_through_flow_res","v":{"s":"Heart rate + metabolism, through-flow respiration, hollow bones, crop + gizzard, exceptional vision + UV color, hearing, magnetic + stellar navigation. Birds are physiological athletes optimized for flight."}},{"k":"bird_evolution","v":{"s":"Bird Evolution"}},{"k":"dinosaurs_to_today","v":{"s":"Dinosaurs to today"}},{"k":"160_million_years_of_bird_evolution_fr","v":{"s":"160 million years of bird evolution. From feathered theropods through Archaeopteryx through K-Pg extinction through modern bird radiation. Birds ARE dinosaurs — the only surviving theropod lineage."}},{"k":"bird_scientists","v":{"s":"Bird Scientists"}},{"k":"10_historical_contemporary_figures","v":{"s":"10 historical + contemporary figures"}},{"k":"audubon_peterson_steve_kress_puffin_re","v":{"s":"Audubon, Peterson, Steve Kress (Puffin restoration), Rachel Carson (DDT), Chapman (CBC founder), Leopold, MacArthur (niche differentiation), Margaret Morse Nice, Stutchbury (geolocators) + Maine's ornithologists."}},{"k":"gull_id_master","v":{"s":"Gull ID Master"}},{"k":"7_species_multiple_plumages","v":{"s":"7 species, multiple plumages"}},{"k":"maine_s_gulls_herring_great_black_back","v":{"s":"Maine's gulls — Herring, Great Black-backed, Ring-billed, Laughing, Bonaparte's, Iceland, Glaucous. Multiple-year plumages make gulls the hardest birding ID group. Adult winter + breeding + first-winter for each."}},{"k":"irruptive_visitors","v":{"s":"Irruptive Visitors"}},{"k":"8_winter_irruptive_species","v":{"s":"8 winter irruptive species"}},{"k":"snowy_owl_common_redpoll_pine_grosbeak","v":{"s":"Snowy Owl, Common Redpoll, Pine Grosbeak, Bohemian Waxwing, crossbills, Pine Siskin, Evening Grosbeak. Birds that appear in unpredictable years driven by northern food crop variations."}},{"k":"endangered_maine_birds","v":{"s":"Endangered Maine Birds"}},{"k":"10_listed_species","v":{"s":"10 listed species"}},{"k":"maine_s_state_federally_listed_species","v":{"s":"Maine's state + federally listed species: Piping Plover, Roseate Tern, Black-crowned Night Heron, Wood Thrush (watch list), Bicknell's Thrush, Saltmarsh Sparrow, Sedge Wren, Bald Eagle (recovered). Status + threats + actions."}},{"k":"maine_hotspots_extended","v":{"s":"Maine Hotspots Extended"}},{"k":"12_hotspots_with_deep_info","v":{"s":"12 hotspots with deep info"}},{"k":"scarborough_marsh_eastern_egg_rock_aca","v":{"s":"Scarborough Marsh, Eastern Egg Rock, Acadia, Bradbury Mountain Hawkwatch, Baxter, Petit Manan, Penobscot River, Moosehead Lake, pelagic Bar Harbor trips. Type, key species, best seasons, access, insider tips."}},{"k":"duck_id_dabbler_vs_diver","v":{"s":"Duck ID — Dabbler vs Diver"}},{"k":"5_categories_explained","v":{"s":"5 categories explained"}},{"k":"dabblers_tip_up_to_feed_divers_go_unde","v":{"s":"Dabblers tip up to feed; divers go underwater. Mergansers specialize on fish; sea ducks dive for mollusks; tree ducks nest in cavities. Anatomy + behavior + flight + diet — how to tell which kind of duck you're watching."}},{"k":"bird_vocalizations_deep","v":{"s":"Bird Vocalizations Deep"}},{"k":"7_vocalization_topics","v":{"s":"7 vocalization topics"}},{"k":"song_vs_call_song_learning_dawn_chorus","v":{"s":"Song vs call, song learning, dawn chorus reasons, song function, mimicry, citizen-science recording, acoustic environment changes. The science behind why birds sing."}},{"k":"bird_reproduction_deep","v":{"s":"Bird Reproduction Deep"}},{"k":"8_reproduction_topics","v":{"s":"8 reproduction topics"}},{"k":"egg_formation_incubation_hatching_altr","v":{"s":"Egg formation, incubation, hatching, altricial vs precocial, fledging, brood reduction, cooperative breeding, brood parasitism, monogamy reality (extra-pair paternity). The biology of how birds make more birds."}},{"k":"maine_sparrows_deep","v":{"s":"Maine Sparrows Deep"}},{"k":"15_sparrow_species","v":{"s":"15 sparrow species"}},{"k":"15_maine_sparrow_species_song_white_th","v":{"s":"15 Maine sparrow species — Song, White-throated, White-crowned, Junco, American Tree, Chipping, Field, Savannah, Swamp, Saltmarsh, Nelson's, Lincoln's, Towhee, Meadowlark, Vesper. The LBJs (Little Brown Jobs) demystified with size, habitat, key marks, song, and Maine status."}},{"k":"maine_thrushes_deep","v":{"s":"Maine Thrushes Deep"}},{"k":"8_thrush_species","v":{"s":"8 thrush species"}},{"k":"american_robin_hermit_thrush_wood_thru","v":{"s":"American Robin, Hermit Thrush, Wood Thrush, Veery, Bicknell's Thrush, Swainson's Thrush, Gray-cheeked Thrush, Eastern Bluebird. Maine's flute-voiced thrushes including the climate-vulnerable Bicknell's."}},{"k":"maine_woodpeckers_deep","v":{"s":"Maine Woodpeckers Deep"}},{"k":"8_woodpecker_species","v":{"s":"8 woodpecker species"}},{"k":"downy_hairy_pileated_northern_flicker_","v":{"s":"Downy, Hairy, Pileated, Northern Flicker, Red-bellied, Yellow-bellied Sapsucker, Black-backed, American Three-toed. Maine's 8 woodpeckers with size, voice, drumming patterns, and ecological role as keystone cavity makers."}},{"k":"maine_finches_deep","v":{"s":"Maine Finches Deep"}},{"k":"9_finch_species","v":{"s":"9 finch species"}},{"k":"american_goldfinch_purple_finch_house_","v":{"s":"American Goldfinch, Purple Finch, House Finch, Pine Grosbeak, Common Redpoll, Pine Siskin, Evening Grosbeak, Red Crossbill, White-winged Crossbill. Year-round + irruptive winter visitors. Crossbills with unique crossed mandibles."}},{"k":"maine_blackbirds_corvids","v":{"s":"Maine Blackbirds + Corvids"}},{"k":"10_species","v":{"s":"10 species"}},{"k":"red_winged_blackbird_common_grackle_eu","v":{"s":"Red-winged Blackbird, Common Grackle, European Starling (intro), Brown-headed Cowbird (parasite), Baltimore Oriole, Bobolink, American Crow, Common Raven, Blue Jay, Gray Jay. Maine's most intelligent + most controversial bird family."}},{"k":"hummingbirds_swifts","v":{"s":"Hummingbirds + Swifts"}},{"k":"3_species_biology","v":{"s":"3 species + biology"}},{"k":"ruby_throated_hummingbird_maine_s_only","v":{"s":"Ruby-throated Hummingbird (Maine's only common humming), Rufous Hummingbird (rare vagrant), Chimney Swift (cigar-shape aerial insectivore). The most specialized fliers in Maine."}},{"k":"flycatchers_vireos","v":{"s":"Flycatchers + Vireos"}},{"k":"10_species_2","v":{"s":"10 species"}},{"k":"eastern_phoebe_eastern_wood_pewee_yell","v":{"s":"Eastern Phoebe, Eastern Wood-Pewee, Yellow-bellied + Least Flycatcher, Great Crested Flycatcher, Eastern Kingbird, Olive-sided Flycatcher (declining), Red-eyed + Blue-headed + Yellow-throated Vireo. The aerial insectivores of Maine forests."}},{"k":"bird_topography_lab","v":{"s":"Bird Topography Lab"}},{"k":"interactive_labeled_bird_diagram","v":{"s":"Interactive labeled bird diagram"}},{"k":"click_22_numbered_regions_on_an_intera","v":{"s":"Click 22 numbered regions on an interactive bird diagram to learn the names + importance of each body part: crown, supercilium, lores, malar, breast, flank, rump, wing coverts, primaries, tail. The vocabulary birders use for description + ID."}},{"k":"maine_habitats_deep","v":{"s":"Maine Habitats Deep"}},{"k":"12_maine_ecosystems","v":{"s":"12 Maine ecosystems"}},{"k":"northern_hardwood_forest_spruce_fir_bo","v":{"s":"Northern hardwood forest, spruce-fir boreal, salt marsh, freshwater marsh + bog, grassland, mixed forest, young second-growth, river corridor, lake shoreline, pelagic offshore, suburban, beach + dune. Each with key birds, conservation, and Maine sites."}},{"k":"climate_change_maine_birds","v":{"s":"Climate Change + Maine Birds"}},{"k":"8_climate_impacts","v":{"s":"8 climate impacts"}},{"k":"range_shifts_phenological_mismatch_bor","v":{"s":"Range shifts, phenological mismatch, boreal forest shrinkage, sea level rise on salt marshes, ocean temperature on seabirds, storm intensity on migration, wintering range shifts, what birders can do. Maine birds are climate-vulnerable."}},{"k":"backyard_feeder_guide","v":{"s":"Backyard Feeder Guide"}},{"k":"10_feeder_types_foods","v":{"s":"10 feeder types + foods"}},{"k":"tube_hopper_suet_platform_nyjer_suet_l","v":{"s":"Tube, hopper, suet, platform, nyjer, suet log, mealworm, sugar-water, oriole, ground. Each feeder type with foods, birds attracted, and setup tips for your Maine backyard."}},{"k":"nest_box_guide","v":{"s":"Nest Box Guide"}},{"k":"12_maine_species_specs","v":{"s":"12 Maine species + specs"}},{"k":"specifications_for_nest_boxes_for_blue","v":{"s":"Specifications for nest boxes for bluebirds, tree swallows, chickadees, nuthatches, titmice, wood ducks, hooded mergansers, flickers, kestrels, screech-owls, house wrens, purple martins. Floor size, entry hole, height, placement, timing."}},{"k":"bird_diets_foraging","v":{"s":"Bird Diets + Foraging"}},{"k":"9_dietary_categories","v":{"s":"9 dietary categories"}},{"k":"granivores_insectivores_nectarivores_f","v":{"s":"Granivores, insectivores, nectarivores, frugivores, piscivores, carnivores, scavengers, mollusk specialists, aquatic-plant eaters. What birds eat shapes what they look like + how they live."}},{"k":"migration_deep_science","v":{"s":"Migration — Deep Science"}},{"k":"9_migration_topics","v":{"s":"9 migration topics"}},{"k":"why_birds_migrate_triggers_pre_migrati","v":{"s":"Why birds migrate, triggers, pre-migration body changes, in-flight physiology, navigation, stopover sites, hazards, Maine's migration calendar, endurance records. The remarkable biology of one of nature's greatest journeys."}},{"k":"complete_birding_glossary","v":{"s":"Complete Birding Glossary"}},{"k":"100_birding_terms","v":{"s":"100+ birding terms"}},{"k":"comprehensive_birding_vocabulary_accip","v":{"s":"Comprehensive birding vocabulary — accipiter, altricial, brood parasitism, dichotomous key, eclipse plumage, irruption, lek, malar, niche, primaries, raptor, supercilium, vagrant, zugunruhe + 80+ more. Searchable."}},{"k":"dichotomous_key_id_walkthrough","v":{"s":"Dichotomous Key — ID Walkthrough"}},{"k":"step_by_step_bird_id","v":{"s":"Step-by-step bird ID"}},{"k":"answer_questions_about_the_bird_you_sa","v":{"s":"Answer questions about the bird you saw — size, color, bill shape, location — and walk through a step-by-step identification tree to a likely species ID. Like a real field guide key but interactive."}},{"k":"maine_birding_by_season","v":{"s":"Maine Birding by Season"}},{"k":"year_round_maine_guide","v":{"s":"Year-round Maine guide"}},{"k":"winter_spring_summer_fall_in_maine_eac","v":{"s":"Winter, spring, summer, fall in Maine. Each season has its own bird community + birding strategy. Species, where to go, tips, hotspots for every part of the calendar."}},{"k":"bird_photography","v":{"s":"Bird Photography"}},{"k":"9_photography_topics","v":{"s":"9 photography topics"}},{"k":"equipment_settings_composition_lightin","v":{"s":"Equipment, settings, composition, lighting, ethical approach, ethical photography, action shots, editing, sharing. Photography techniques + ethics specifically for bird photographers."}},{"k":"birding_resources","v":{"s":"Birding Resources"}},{"k":"10_categories_of_resources","v":{"s":"10 categories of resources"}},{"k":"apps_field_guides_maine_organizations_","v":{"s":"Apps, field guides, Maine organizations, citizen science projects, books, websites, festivals, conservation orgs, tour operators. Everything you need to take birding from interest to expertise."}},{"k":"aging_sexing_birds","v":{"s":"Aging + Sexing Birds"}},{"k":"8_topics","v":{"s":"8 topics"}},{"k":"how_