e. Click each circle on the bird below to see the part name, what it looks like, and what it tells you about identification."}},{"k":"hotspots_explored","v":{"s":"Hotspots explored: "}},{"k":"field_mark_detail","v":{"s":"🪶 Field mark detail"}},{"k":"explored","v":{"s":"✓ Explored"}},{"k":"what_it_looks_like","v":{"s":"👁 What it looks like"}},{"k":"why_it_matters_for_id","v":{"s":"🎯 Why it matters for ID"}},{"k":"all_field_marks_keyboard_list","v":{"s":"All field marks (keyboard list)"}},{"k":"cone_seed_cracker","v":{"s":"Cone (seed-cracker)"}},{"k":"hook_raptor","v":{"s":"Hook (raptor)"}},{"k":"spear_fish","v":{"s":"Spear (fish)"}},{"k":"tube_nectar","v":{"s":"Tube (nectar)"}},{"k":"chisel_excavator","v":{"s":"Chisel (excavator)"}},{"k":"filter_dabbler","v":{"s":"Filter (dabbler)"}},{"k":"parrot_like_wedge","v":{"s":"Parrot-like wedge"}},{"k":"thin_probe_insectivore","v":{"s":"Thin probe (insectivore)"}},{"k":"perching_anisodactyl","v":{"s":"Perching (anisodactyl)"}},{"k":"raptor_talons","v":{"s":"Raptor talons"}},{"k":"climbing_zygodactyl","v":{"s":"Climbing (zygodactyl)"}},{"k":"webbed","v":{"s":"Webbed"}},{"k":"wading_long_toes","v":{"s":"Wading (long toes)"}},{"k":"shorebird_small_webbing","v":{"s":"Shorebird (small webbing)"}},{"k":"beak_feet_lab_2","v":{"s":"Beak & Feet Lab"}},{"k":"bills_and_feet_are_evolution_s_tool_ki","v":{"s":"Bills and feet are evolution's tool kit"}},{"k":"a_bird_s_bill_tells_you_what_it_eats_i","v":{"s":"A bird's bill tells you what it eats. Its feet tell you where it lives. Look at any bird and you can read its lifestyle from those two parts alone — without ever seeing it eat or move. This module shows the eight common bill shapes and six common foot patterns, and which species in BirdLab's habitats use each."}},{"k":"cross_lab_link","v":{"s":"🔗 Cross-lab link"}},{"k":"see_evolution_change_a_beak_gal_pagos_","v":{"s":"See evolution change a beak: Galápagos finches"}},{"k":"darwin_watched_13_finch_species_on_the","v":{"s":"Darwin watched 13 finch species on the Galápagos and realized they'd all evolved from a single ancestor — each beak matched a different food. Peter & Rosemary Grant later watched these beaks "}},{"k":"continue_evolving","v":{"s":"continue evolving"}},{"k":"across_droughts_for_the_deep_story_ope","v":{"s":" across droughts. For the deep story, open "}},{"k":"evolab_gal_pagos_beak_lab","v":{"s":"EvoLab → Galápagos Beak Lab"}},{"k":"8_bill_shapes_click_to_learn_what_each","v":{"s":"8 bill shapes — click to learn what each one eats"}},{"k":"diet","v":{"s":"🍽 Diet: "}},{"k":"examples","v":{"s":"🐦 Examples: "}},{"k":"6_foot_types_click_to_learn_how_each_o","v":{"s":"6 foot types — click to learn how each one moves"}},{"k":"lifestyle","v":{"s":"🚶 Lifestyle: "}},{"k":"examples_2","v":{"s":"🐦 Examples: "}},{"k":"how_to_read_a_bird_s_lifestyle_in_5_se","v":{"s":"How to read a bird's lifestyle in 5 seconds"}},{"k":"bill_shape_diet","v":{"s":"Bill shape → diet. "}},{"k":"cone_seeds_hook_meat_spear_fish_long_t","v":{"s":"Cone = seeds. Hook = meat. Spear = fish. Long thin probe = insects. Tube = nectar. Chisel = wood."}},{"k":"foot_shape_habitat","v":{"s":"Foot shape → habitat. "}},{"k":"webbed_water_talons_predator_2_and_2_c","v":{"s":"Webbed = water. Talons = predator. 2-and-2 = climbing tree. Long toes = mud / lily pads. Songbird foot = anywhere with branches."}},{"k":"a_hooked_bill_on_a_webbed_foot_you_re_","v":{"s":"a hooked bill on a webbed foot? You're looking at a fish-eating raptor — Bald Eagle, Osprey. A cone bill on a perching foot? Seedeater songbird — cardinal, finch."}},{"k":"rising","v":{"s":"Rising"}},{"k":"falling","v":{"s":"Falling"}},{"k":"steady","v":{"s":"Steady"}},{"k":"trill","v":{"s":"Trill"}},{"k":"complex","v":{"s":"Complex"}},{"k":"shape","v":{"s":"Shape"}},{"k":"bird_call_trainer_2","v":{"s":"Bird Call Trainer"}},{"k":"songs_in_plain_english_2","v":{"s":"Songs in plain English"}},{"k":"real_birders_identify_80_of_birds_by_s","v":{"s":"Real birders identify 80%+ of birds by SOUND — visual ID is for confirming. The trick is mnemonic phrases that match the bird's rhythm: \"drink-your-tea\" for towhee, \"who-cooks-for-you\" for Barred Owl. Once you hear it as those words, you can never un-hear it."}},{"k":"sound_id","v":{"s":"🎤 Sound ID"}},{"k":"for_actual_audio","v":{"s":"For actual audio"}},{"k":"free_cornell_lab","v":{"s":"✓ Free · Cornell Lab"}},{"k":"get_merlin_bird_id","v":{"s":"Get Merlin Bird ID"}},{"k":"cornell_lab_s_free_app_has_professiona","v":{"s":"Cornell Lab's free app has professional recordings of every common North American bird, plus real-time "}},{"k":"sound_id_2","v":{"s":"Sound ID"}},{"k":"that_identifies_what_s_singing_right_n","v":{"s":" that identifies what's singing right now from your phone's microphone."}},{"k":"download_on_the","v":{"s":"Download on the"}},{"k":"app_store","v":{"s":"App Store"}},{"k":"get_it_on","v":{"s":"Get it on"}},{"k":"google_play","v":{"s":"Google Play"}},{"k":"merlin_allaboutbirds_org","v":{"s":"merlin.allaboutbirds.org ↗"}},{"k":"pair_each_mnemonic_below_with_the_actu","v":{"s":"Pair each mnemonic below with the actual recording in Merlin — the words make the rhythm memorable; the recording locks it in."}},{"k":"bird_call_trainer_modes","v":{"s":"Bird Call Trainer modes"}},{"k":"listen_identify_audio_first","v":{"s":"🎧 Listen & Identify (audio-first)"}},{"k":"mnemonic","v":{"s":"🎵 Mnemonic"}},{"k":"synthesized_rhythm_not_a_real_recordin","v":{"s":"Synthesized rhythm, not a real recording. Use Merlin Bird ID for actual audio."}},{"k":"rhythm","v":{"s":"Rhythm"}},{"k":"spectrogram","v":{"s":"Spectrogram"}},{"k":"playing_synthesized_rhythm_open_merlin","v":{"s":"🎶 Playing synthesized rhythm — open Merlin Bird ID for the real recording."}},{"k":"what_it_sounds_like","v":{"s":"What it sounds like: "}},{"k":"tip","v":{"s":"💡 Tip: "}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":" · Score: "}},{"k":"restart_with_new_questions","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart with new questions"}},{"k":"match_this_call_to_the_species","v":{"s":"Match this call to the species"}},{"k":"synthesized_rhythm_only_not_a_real_rec","v":{"s":"Synthesized rhythm only — not a real recording."}},{"k":"rhythm_pattern","v":{"s":"🎵 Rhythm pattern"}},{"k":"species_choices","v":{"s":"Species choices"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next question →"}},{"k":"your_answers","v":{"s":"Your answers"}},{"k":"new_quiz_with_fresh_questions","v":{"s":"🔄 New quiz with fresh questions"}},{"k":"review_the_mnemonics","v":{"s":"📖 Review the mnemonics"}},{"k":"score_2","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy"}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best"}},{"k":"reset_score","v":{"s":"🔄 Reset score"}},{"k":"listen_and_identify","v":{"s":"🎧 Listen and identify"}},{"k":"what_it_sounds_like_2","v":{"s":"What it sounds like: "}},{"k":"tip_2","v":{"s":"💡 Tip: "}},{"k":"next_call","v":{"s":"↻ Next call →"}},{"k":"why_audio_first_matters","v":{"s":"🦻 Why audio-first matters: "}},{"k":"in_real_birding_you_hear_birds_long_be","v":{"s":"In real birding you hear birds long before you see them. Most warblers in summer foliage are heard, not seen. Every birder you meet who can ID 50+ species by sound got there by repeated listen-and-guess practice. This drill is the same exercise — just with synthesized tones instead of recordings. For the real audio, "}},{"k":"merlin_bird_id_3","v":{"s":"Merlin Bird ID"}},{"k":"cornell_lab_is_free","v":{"s":" (Cornell Lab) is free."}},{"k":"habitat_match_2","v":{"s":"Habitat Match"}},{"k":"birds_and_habitats_are_matched_pairs","v":{"s":"Birds and habitats are matched pairs"}},{"k":"a_bird_s_body_is_shaped_to_fit_a_speci","v":{"s":"A bird's body is shaped to fit a specific habitat — feet, bill, body shape, behavior. Once you know a species' habitat, you can predict where to look for it. Once you know a habitat, you can predict which species you'll find there. Real birders use both directions of this reasoning."}},{"k":"habitat_match_modes","v":{"s":"Habitat Match modes"}},{"k