},{"k":"menu_97","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"4_major_bird_atlas_survey_projects_mai","v":{"s":"4 major bird atlas + survey projects — Maine + national breeding bird atlases, BBS, eBird."}},{"k":"benefit_5","v":{"s":"✓ Benefit: "}},{"k":"maine_bird_population_changes_2","v":{"s":"📊 Maine Bird Population Changes"}},{"k":"menu_98","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"how_10_maine_birds_have_changed_over_t","v":{"s":"How 10 Maine birds have changed over the past century — recoveries + losses + climate-driven shifts."}},{"k":"lesson_3","v":{"s":"📚 Lesson: "}},{"k":"bird_voice_types_categorize_sounds","v":{"s":"🎵 Bird Voice Types — Categorize Sounds"}},{"k":"menu_99","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"12_categories_of_bird_sounds_whistled_","v":{"s":"12 categories of bird sounds — whistled, buzzed, repeated phrase, jumble, trill, mournful, liquid, insect-like, mechanical, cackle, caw, hoot."}},{"k":"detailed_birding_calendar_2","v":{"s":"📆 Detailed Birding Calendar"}},{"k":"menu_100","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"week_by_week_maine_bird_calendar_with__2","v":{"s":"Week-by-week Maine bird calendar with bird activity + recommended birder actions."}},{"k":"activity_2","v":{"s":"🐦 Activity: "}},{"k":"action_3","v":{"s":"✓ Action: "}},{"k":"bird_art_aesthetic_history_2","v":{"s":"🎨 Bird Art + Aesthetic History"}},{"k":"menu_101","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"7_eras_of_bird_visual_representation_f","v":{"s":"7 eras of bird visual representation — from Indigenous art through Audubon through modern AI."}},{"k":"maine_29","v":{"s":"📍 Maine: "}},{"k":"birding_philosophy_meaning_2","v":{"s":"💭 Birding Philosophy + Meaning"}},{"k":"menu_102","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"8_deeper_reflections_on_what_birding_c","v":{"s":"8 deeper reflections on what birding can mean. The long game, birds as teachers, belonging, citizen science, indicators, active hope, community, intergenerational."}},{"k":"lesson_4","v":{"s":"📚 Lesson: "}},{"k":"maine_birds_in_numbers_2","v":{"s":"📊 Maine Birds in Numbers"}},{"k":"menu_103","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"15_statistics_about_maine_s_birds_bird","v":{"s":"15 statistics about Maine's birds + birding."}},{"k":"essential_birding_faq_2","v":{"s":"❓ Essential Birding FAQ"}},{"k":"menu_104","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"10_essential_questions_about_maine_bir","v":{"s":"10 essential questions about Maine birding answered."}},{"k":"birding_milestones_2","v":{"s":"🎯 Birding Milestones"}},{"k":"menu_105","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"14_birding_milestones_from_your_very_f","v":{"s":"14 birding milestones from your very first bird to lifetime achievement. Goals + benchmarks + tips for each stage."}},{"k":"stage","v":{"s":"📅 Stage: "}},{"k":"tip_13","v":{"s":"💡 Tip: "}},{"k":"birding_wisdom_quotes_2","v":{"s":"💬 Birding Wisdom + Quotes"}},{"k":"menu_106","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"10_quotes_about_birds_birding_from_nat","v":{"s":"10 quotes about birds + birding from naturalists, poets, scientists."}},{"k":"soarer_low_load_high_ar","v":{"s":"🦅 Soarer (low load, high AR)"}},{"k":"albatross_like_glides_on_thermals_mini","v":{"s":"Albatross-like. Glides on thermals, minimal flapping."}},{"k":"flapper_medium_load","v":{"s":"🐦 Flapper (medium load)"}},{"k":"songbird_like_active_wing_motion_moder","v":{"s":"Songbird-like. Active wing motion, moderate efficiency."}},{"k":"diver_high_load","v":{"s":"🦆 Diver (high load)"}},{"k":"duck_loon_like_heavy_fast_flight_body_","v":{"s":"Duck/loon-like. Heavy fast flight, body-density adapted."}},{"k":"general_purpose","v":{"s":"🪶 General-purpose"}},{"k":"mixed_strategy_crow_like_or_hawk_like","v":{"s":"Mixed strategy. Crow-like or hawk-like."}},{"k":"hypothesis_what_body_wing_combinations","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: What body+wing combinations enable soaring?"}},{"k":"how_do_wing_loading_aspect_ratio_shape","v":{"s":"How do wing loading + aspect ratio shape flight strategy?"}},{"k":"robin_call_description","v":{"s":"Liquid, melodic phrases sung at dawn and dusk. Each phrase rises slightly, then drops. The song of the spring suburb."}},{"k":"robin_call_tip","v":{"s":"The default \"songbird at dawn\" sound across most of North America."}},{"k":"chickadee_call_description","v":{"s":"Cheerful chatter all year; whistled two-note song mostly in spring. More \"dees\" at the end = higher alarm level."}},{"k":"chickadee_call_tip","v":{"s":"The number of \"dees\" literally encodes how much they think you're a threat. Researchers verified this."}},{"k":"cardinal_call_description","v":{"s":"Clear whistled phrases, surprisingly loud. Both males AND females sing — unusual among songbirds. Sharp \"chip!\" call when alarmed."}},{"k":"cardinal_call_tip","v":{"s":"If you hear a clear whistle and can't see the bird, scan the dense interior of a shrub at eye level."}},{"k":"towhee_call_description","v":{"s":"Three-syllable: a couple short notes, then a long buzzy trill. Sharp \"chewink!\" call when disturbed."}},{"k":"towhee_call_tip","v":{"s":"You'll often hear them rustling in leaf litter before you hear the song. Two-footed backwards-scratch hop is the giveaway."}},{"k":"wts_call_description","v":{"s":"High clear whistles in a distinctive pattern: 1 long note, 1 medium, then 3 quick triplets. The melancholy soundtrack of northern forests."}},{"k":"wts_call_tip","v":{"s":"One of the most evocative bird sounds in northeastern North America. If you hear it, you're likely in good northern woods."}},{"k":"titmouse_call_description","v":{"s":"Loud, ringing whistle on one pitch repeated 4-8 times. Sometimes \"Pidi-pidi-pidi\". A relative of chickadees with a small gray crest."}},{"k":"titmouse_call_tip","v":{"s":"Often the loudest sound in winter woods. You'll hear it before you see the bird."}},{"k":"barredowl_call_description","v":{"s":"8-note hooted question with the final \"you-all\" trailing down. The signature owl of eastern forests. Often calls in daylight."}},{"k":"barredowl_call_tip","v":{"s":"The most likely owl you'll hear in Maine forests. Active in daylight more than other owl species."}},{"k":"mourningdove_call_description","v":{"s":"Soft, mournful cooing. Often mistaken for an owl. The quiet sound of suburban summer mornings."}},{"k":"mourningdove_call_tip","v":{"s":"A common mistake: people hear mourning doves and think they're hearing owls. Owls hoot at night; doves call in daylight."}},{"k":"rwbb_call_description","v":{"s":"Loud, raspy proclamation from cattail tops. Males sing constantly through marsh breeding season. Females give softer \"chek\" calls."}},{"k":"rwbb_call_tip","v":{"s":"One of the first migrants to return to Maine in early March. Their song = spring is officially here."}},{"k":"vireo_call_description","v":{"s":"Persistent two-note phrases, repeated all day, even in summer heat. Recorded singing 22,000+ songs in a single day."}},{"k":"vireo_call_tip","v":{"s":"The bird that just won't shut up. If you're in summer woods and hear endless two-note phrases, that's vireo."}},{"k":"kingfisher_call_description","v":{"s":"Loud, dry, machine-like rattle given in flight along streams and ponds. Distinctive — sounds nothing like a \"song.\""}},{"k":"kingfisher_call_tip","v":{"s":"You'll often hear them flying along a stream before you see them. Look up — they fly purposeful straight lines."}},{"k":"jay_call_description","v":{"s":"Harsh nasal scream most often. Also a musical \"queedle-queedle.\" Famous for mimicking hawk calls perfectly — sometimes uses this to scare other birds away from feeders."}},{"k":"jay_call_tip","v":{"s":"If you think you heard a hawk but the songbirds didn't scatter, it was probably a Blue Jay messing with you."}},{"k":"yellowthroat_call_description","v":{"s":"Three-syllable phrases repeated. Bright, bouncy. Males have black masks; very secretive in cattails and reeds."}},{"k":"yellowthroat_call_tip","v":{"s":"You'll hear them constantly in summer marshes; seeing them is the harder challenge."}},{"k":"pileated_call_description","v":{"s":"Slow, deep \"wuk-wuk-wuk\" calls; drum is hollow and resonant — can be heard a half-mile away."}},{"k":"pileated_call_tip","v":{"s":"The crow-sized woodpecker. Drumming is unmistakably big. Different from smaller woodpecker drumming, which is faster."}},{"k":"loon_call_description","v":{"s":"Four distinct call types: wail (long note), tremolo (laughing trill), yodel (males only), and hoot. Together they make Maine summer evenings."}},{"k":"loon_call_tip","v":{"s":"Maine's state bird is the chickadee, but the loon is on the state quarter. Hearing a loon at sunset on a north-Maine lake is a core Maine experience."}},{"k":"forest_description","v":{"s":"Mixed deciduous + evergreen Northeastern forest. Mid-summer. Listen for layered birdsong: chickadees high, vireo persistent, distant woodpecker drumming."}},{"k":"marsh_description","v":{"s":"Freshwater wetland in summer. Cattails, lily pads, slow water. Listen for red-winged blackbirds calling from cattail tops, the kingfisher's rattle, frogs, and the occasional heron's harsh squawk."}},{"k":"backyard_description","v":{"s":"Suburban backyard in late summer. Bird feeder, tomato garden, deck. Listen for the constant chatter of feeder regulars: chickadees, finches, the occasional cardinal serenade, and the loud squawks of jays."}},{"k":"coast_description","v":{"s":"Maine rocky coast in summer. Granite ledges, kelp, surf. Look for puffins on offshore islands (rare from shore), eiders rafting in the swell, gulls everywhere, an eagle on a high snag."}},{"k":"mountain_description","v":{"s":"Western Maine high country in fall. Spruce-fir forest meeting alpine zone. Listen for the deep \"cronk\" of a raven, the trill of juncos, the wind in the conifers."}},{"k":"field_station","v":{"s":"Field station"}},{"k":"start_with_observation","v":{"s":"Start with observation, then sharpen the ID"}},{"k":"pick_a_habitat_scan_for_movement","v":{"s":"Pick a habitat, scan for movement, spot a bird in the scene, then use field marks to verify what you saw. Merlin and eBird stay available as real-world companions after the practice round."}},{"k":"start_i_spy","v":{"s":"Start I-Spy"}},{"k":"train_field_marks","v":{"s":"Train field marks"}},{"k":"birdlab_status_summary","v":{"s":"BirdLab status summary"}},{"k":"lifers_logged","v":{"s":"Lifers logged"}},{"k":"habitats_to_scan","v":{"s":"Habitats to scan"}},{"k":"scene_birds","v":{"s":"Scene birds"}},{"k":"path_explored","v":{"s":"Path explored"}},{"k":"i_spy_mission","v":{"s":"I-Spy mission"}},{"k":"i_spy_status_summary","v":{"s":"I-Spy status summary"}},{"k":"hints_left","v":{"s":"Hints left"}},{"k":"access_mode","v":{"s":"Access mode"}},{"k":"current_streak","v":{"s":"Current streak"}},{"k":"i_spy_prompt_scan","v":{"s":"Scan the habitat for movement, posture, and shape before using a hint."}},{"k":"i_spy_prompt_complete","v":{"s":"Habitat complete. Try another habitat or re-run on a harder hint budget."}},{"k":"i_spy_prompt_keep","v":{"s":"Keep scanning. The remaining birds are still moving in the scene."}},{"k":"find_a_module","v":{"s":"Find a BirdLab module"}},{"k":"search_modules_placeholder","v":{"s":"Search calls, migration, feathers, Maine..."}},{"k":"clear_module_search","v":{"s":"Clear module search"}},{"k":"no_matching_modules","v":{"s":"No matching modules"}},{"k":"try_broader_module_search","v":{"s":"Try a broader bird, habitat, skill, or science term."}},{"k":"show_all_modules","v":{"s":"Show all modules"}},{"k":"color_bill","v":{"s":"bill black"}},{"k":"color_breast","v":{"s":"breast warm rust"}},{"k":"color_legs","v":{"s":"legs yellow"}},{"k":"color_vague","v":{"s":"\"brown bird\""}},{"k":"cross_closed","v":{"s":"Bill closed"}},{"k":"cross_closed_note","v":{"s":"The tips cross past each other. The bird slides the closed bill under a cone scale."}},{"k":"cross_open","v":{"s":"Bill opening"}},{"k":"cross_open_note","v":{"s":"Opening drives the tips sideways in opposite directions, levering the scale up. Then the tongue takes the seed."}},{"k":"cross_red","v":{"s":"Red: plain dark wing, no bars"}},{"k":"cross_ww","v":{"s":"White-winged: two white wing bars"}},{"k":"dh_downy","v":{"s":"Downy Woodpecker"}},{"k":"dh_downy_note","v":{"s":"Bill about a third of the head — short and stubby, like a tack."}},{"k":"dh_hairy","v":{"s":"Hairy Woodpecker"}},{"k":"dh_hairy_note","v":{"s":"Bill as long as the head is wide, or longer — a chisel."}},{"k":"dh_note","v":{"s":"The plumage is near enough identical, so size alone will not settle it at distance. The red bar under each bill is its length; the blue bar under each head is the head width. Compare those two, not the whole bird."}},{"k":"dh_title","v":{"s":"Downy or Hairy? Measure the bill against the head"}},{"k":"egg_img","v":{"s":"Egg illustration showing colour and pattern"}},{"k":"fish_grasping_osprey","v":{"s":"Fish-grasping (Osprey)"}},{"k":"foil_alt","v":{"s":"Cross-section of a wing. The upper surface is curved and the lower is nearly flat. Air splitting at the front travels further over the top than underneath, and an arrow marks lift pointing upward."}},{"k":"foil_fast","v":{"s":"longer path over the top"}},{"k":"foil_lift","v":{"s":"lift"}},{"k":"foil_note","v":{"s":"Curvature is drawn larger than life so it is visible — a real wing is far subtler. Flatter sections (falcons, auks) trade lift for speed; deeper ones (hawks, vultures) lift more at low speed."}},{"k":"foil_slow","v":{"s":"shorter path underneath"}},{"k":"foil_title","v":{"s":"Cut through the wing — this curve is the lift"}},{"k":"foot_see_drawn","v":{"s":"Every foot below is drawn in the Beak & Feet Lab — open it to see the toes, webs, spines and claws described here"}},{"k":"foot_types_click_to_learn_how_each_one","v":{"s":"foot types — click to learn how each one moves"}},{"k":"fv_empid_warn","v":{"s":"The Empidonax flycatchers overlap heavily, and silent birds often cannot be identified to species by sight at all — even experienced birders leave many as \"Empidonax sp.\" and call them on voice instead. Treat this drawing as showing the tendency, not a guarantee."}},{"k":"fv_empid_warn_title","v":{"s":"⚠ Be careful with this one. "}},{"k":"fv_plate_note","v":{"s":"This group sorts on what surrounds the eye: nothing at all, a bold complete ring, or spectacles — a ring joined to the bill by a short line. Then check the bill: flycatchers have a broad flat one, vireos a thicker one with a hooked tip."}},{"k":"fv_plate_title","v":{"s":"Start at the eye, then the bill"}},{"k":"gamebird_walking","v":{"s":"Gamebird (walking)"}},{"k":"glossary_search_label","v":{"s":"Search birding glossary"}},{"k":"glossary_see_topography","v":{"s":"Body-part terms (lore, malar, nape, rump...) are drawn on a labelled bird in the Bird Topography Lab"}},{"k":"gull_breeding","v":{"s":"Adult breeding"}},{"k":"gull_first","v":{"s":"First winter"}},{"k":"gull_stage_note","v":{"s":"The brown streaky bird and the clean grey-and-white one on the same beach are often the same species at different ages. Large gulls pass through several intermediate plumages not drawn here, so a bird that matches none of these three is probably a middle year rather than a different species."}},{"k":"gull_stage_title","v":{"s":"Same bird, {y} years apart — this is why gulls are hard"}},{"k":"gull_winter","v":{"s":"Adult winter"}},{"k":"irrupt_chart_alt","v":{"s":"Schematic chart. A regular winter visitor shows bars of similar height every winter. An irruptive visitor shows near-zero bars most winters and three very tall bars. A row below marks the northern seed crop as good or failed, and the failed crops line up with the tall bars."}},{"k":"irrupt_chart_note","v":{"s":"A regular visitor turns up in similar numbers every winter. An irruptive one is absent, absent, absent — then floods south in one winter. The bottom row is why: when the northern seed or cone crop fails (✗), the birds have to leave. Schematic, to show the shape of the pattern — not counts for any one species."}},{"k":"irrupt_chart_title","v":{"s":"What \"irruptive\" means — ten winters, same feeder"}},{"k":"irrupt_row_crop","v":{"s":"N. crop"}},{"k":"irrupt_row_irruptive","v":{"s":"Irruptive"}},{"k":"irrupt_row_regular","v":{"s":"Regular"}},{"k":"journal_behavior","v":{"s":"Behavior"}},{"k":"journal_behavior_v","v":{"s":"Double-scratching in leaf litter, tail pumped"}},{"k":"journal_date","v":{"s":"Date"}},{"k":"journal_date_v","v":{"s":"14 May, 7:20 am"}},{"k":"journal_note","v":{"s":"The species line ends in a question mark on purpose — an honest \"sparrow?\" plus what you actually saw is worth more than a confident wrong name."}},{"k":"journal_place","v":{"s":"Place"}},{"k":"journal_place_v","v":{"s":"Alder edge, back field"}},{"k":"journal_species","v":{"s":"Species"}},{"k":"journal_species_v","v":{"s":"Sparrow? streaked breast, central spot"}},{"k":"journal_weather","v":{"s":"Weather"}},{"k":"journal_weather_v","v":{"s":"Overcast, still, 11C"}},{"k":"lobed_swimmer","v":{"s":"Lobed (swimming)"}},{"k":"posture_sp","v":{"s":"Sandpiper"}},{"k":"posture_sp_n","v":{"s":"Body near level, carried high on long legs."}},{"k":"posture_sr","v":{"s":"Soaring hawk"}},{"k":"posture_sr_n","v":{"s":"Draw the wing line first — the body is a short stroke."}},{"k":"posture_wp","v":{"s":"Woodpecker"}},{"k":"posture_wp_n","v":{"s":"Upright against the trunk, braced on a stiff tail."}},{"k":"quiz_silhouette_img","v":{"s":"Silhouette of the bird you are being asked to identify"}},{"k":"sa_alt","v":{"s":"Timeline from March to May. Each species is a bar spanning its arrival window, sorted earliest first. Blackbirds, robins and grackles arrive in March; loons and the first flycatchers in April; most warblers, thrushes and flycatchers cluster in mid May."}},{"k":"sa_apr","v":{"s":"APR"}},{"k":"sa_mar","v":{"s":"MAR"}},{"k":"sa_may","v":{"s":"MAY"}},{"k":"sa_note","v":{"s":"Sorted by arrival, not alphabetically, so the wave is visible: a thin early trickle of blackbirds and robins, then a broad pile-up in mid May when most insect-eaters arrive at once. Bars show the window each species is expected in, not a single date — arrival shifts with the season and with the weather."}},{"k":"sa_title","v":{"s":"The spring wave, in order of arrival"}},{"k":"sb_bill","v":{"s":"Bill:"}},{"k":"sb_down","v":{"s":"down-curved"}},{"k":"sb_headwide","v":{"s":"head width"}},{"k":"sb_legs","v":{"s":"Legs:"}},{"k":"sb_long","v":{"s":"long"}},{"k":"sb_med","v":{"s":"medium"}},{"k":"sb_note","v":{"s":"Every shorebird here is drawn on the same body at the same scale, so bill length, bill curve and leg length can be compared directly. Those three plus leg colour settle most of this family; plumage changes with the season, but the proportions do not."}},{"k":"sb_short","v":{"s":"short"}},{"k":"sb_straight","v":{"s":"straight"}},{"k":"sb_title","v":{"s":"Drawn to one scale — compare, do not memorise"}},{"k":"sb_up","v":{"s":"slightly upturned"}},{"k":"shape_img","v":{"s":"Bird body-shape sketch"}},{"k":"sketch_d1","v":{"s":"Bill"}},{"k":"sketch_d2","v":{"s":"Eye"}},{"k":"sketch_d3","v":{"s":"Wing pattern"}},{"k":"sketch_d4","v":{"s":"Tail shape"}},{"k":"sketch_d5","v":{"s":"Feet"}},{"k":"sketch_detail_alt","v":{"s":"A pencil sketch of a perched bird with five numbered callouts: bill, eye, wing pattern, tail shape, feet."}},{"k":"sketch_note1","v":{"s":"Head about half the body. Set the gap between them before anything else."}},{"k":"sketch_note2","v":{"s":"Connect the ovals. The dashed line is the body angle — that angle is a field mark."}},{"k":"sketch_note3","v":{"s":"Now the details, in order. Stop here and you already have a usable sketch."}},{"k":"sketch_note4","v":{"s":"Wing line, then plumage marks last. About 1-2 minutes total."}},{"k":"sketch_step1","v":{"s":"Two ovals"}},{"k":"sketch_step2","v":{"s":"Join + posture line"}},{"k":"sketch_step3","v":{"s":"Bill, eye, tail, legs"}},{"k":"sketch_step4","v":{"s":"Wing + marks"}},{"k":"sp_head_note","v":{"s":"Sparrows are told apart by the face. Compare the crown, the eyebrow, the line through the eye and the throat against the next bird before looking at anything else."}},{"k":"sp_head_title","v":{"s":"Head pattern"}},{"k":"spread_dist","v":{"s":"Distraction display"}},{"k":"spread_dist_note","v":{"s":"ONE wing splayed and dragging, the bird stumbling AWAY from the nest. The other wing stays folded."}},{"k":"spread_mantle","v":{"s":"Mantling"}},{"k":"spread_mantle_note","v":{"s":"Wings arched FORWARD and down like a tent, head low. There is prey underneath, being hidden from thieves."}},{"k":"spread_sun","v":{"s":"Sun-bathing"}},{"k":"spread_sun_note","v":{"s":"Wings open FLAT and turned to the sun, tail fanned, feathers raised. Nothing underneath the bird."}},{"k":"spread_wing_intro","v":{"s":"Sun-bathing and mantling are both defined below as a \"spread-wing posture\", which is not enough to separate them in the field. What actually differs is where the wings go and what is underneath the bird."}},{"k":"spread_wing_title","v":{"s":"Three \"wings out\" postures — how to tell them apart"}},{"k":"wax_bohemian","v":{"s":"Bohemian Waxwing"}},{"k":"wax_bohemian_note","v":{"s":"Rusty undertail. Greyer body, white + yellow wing marks."}},{"k":"wax_cedar","v":{"s":"Cedar Waxwing"}},{"k":"wax_cedar_note","v":{"s":"White undertail. Warmer brown, yellow belly, plain wing."}},{"k":"wax_key","v":{"s":"Check the undertail first — rusty means Bohemian, white means Cedar. It is the one mark that holds up at distance and in poor winter light."}},{"k":"wax_tag_rusty","v":{"s":"rusty undertail"}},{"k":"wax_tag_white","v":{"s":"white undertail"}},{"k":"wb_plate_note","v":{"s":"Every warbler here is drawn on the same body, so what differs on screen is what differs in the field: throat, face, wing bars, sides and rump. Females and autumn birds are duller — the shape and the wing bars survive, the colours often do not."}},{"k":"wb_plate_title","v":{"s":"Breeding male"}},{"k":"wf_female","v":{"s":"♀ Female"}},{"k":"wf_male","v":{"s":"♂ Male"}},{"k":"wf_note","v":{"s":"Both sexes are drawn because the female is the harder call — most are brown, and they are separated on head shape, bill shape and where the pale patches sit rather than on colour. Look at the outline before the plumage."}},{"k":"wing3d_aspect","v":{"s":"Aspect ratio: "}},{"k":"wing3d_aspect_note","v":{"s":"Span divided by average width. High means long and thin — cheap to fly far, useless in dense cover. Low means short and broad — costly over distance, but it turns."}},{"k":"wing3d_failed","v":{"s":"3D view unavailable on this device or network. Every wing is a button above and every fact about it is in the cards below — nothing here needs the picture."}},{"k":"wing3d_label","v":{"s":"Wing shape, 3D. Interactive. Arrow keys rotate, plus and minus zoom, zero resets. Every wing here is also a button, and every fact about it is in the card below."}},{"k":"wing3d_loading","v":{"s":"Loading the wing model…"}},{"k":"wing3d_look","v":{"s":"Look for: "}},{"k":"wing3d_look_droop","v":{"s":"Front on: the wings are not a flat plank. They fall away from the shoulder, and how much they do that differs by wing."}},{"k":"wing3d_look_slots_no","v":{"s":"This wing has no slotted fingers — the outer edge stays one clean surface. Switch to the soaring wing to compare."}},{"k":"wing3d_look_slots_yes","v":{"s":"Look from below: the outer feathers separate into upswept fingers. Those slots are what let a soaring bird stay up at low speed."}},{"k":"wing3d_look_span","v":{"s":"Look along the span from the wingtip to see how much of the wing is length and how much is width."}},{"k":"wing3d_preset_above","v":{"s":"⬒ From above — see the planform"}},{"k":"wing3d_preset_front","v":{"s":"◡ Front on — see the droop"}},{"k":"wing3d_title","v":{"s":"Turn the wing over — planform, sweep and slots in 3D"}},{"k":"wp_plate_note","v":{"s":"Woodpeckers sort first on what the back is doing — a white ladder stripe, zebra bars, solid black, or brown spots — and only then on which patch of the head is red. Bills are drawn to scale against the head."}},{"k":"wp_plate_title","v":{"s":"Back pattern + head marks"}}],"id":492}},{"k":"brainatlas","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_treatment_comparison_already_has_two_modalities_r","v":{"s":"Treatment comparison already has two modalities. Remove one before adding another."}},{"k":"sr_choose_two_treatment_modalities_before_viewing_th","v":{"s":"Choose two treatment modalities before viewing the comparison."}},{"k":"sr_comparison_already_has_two_classes_remove_one_bef","v":{"s":"Comparison already has two classes. Remove one before adding another."}},{"k":"sr_choose_two_medication_classes_before_viewing_the","v":{"s":"Choose two medication classes before viewing the comparison."}},{"k":"sr_interactive_3d_brain_anatomy_model_ready_drag_to","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D brain anatomy model ready. Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom, or select a structure."}},{"k":"sr_cutaway_visible_side_flipped","v":{"s":"Cutaway visible side flipped."}},{"k":"sr_that_comparison_is_not_available_in_the_loaded_mo","v":{"s":"That comparison is not available in the loaded model."}},{"k":"sr_clinical_lesion_explorer_closed","v":{"s":"Clinical lesion explorer closed."}},{"k":"sr_saved_study_set_cleared","v":{"s":"Saved study set cleared."}},{"k":"sr_save_at_least_one_structure_before_starting_a_stu","v":{"s":"Save at least one structure before starting a study-set quiz."}},{"k":"sr_saved_structure_quiz_ended","v":{"s":"Saved structure quiz ended."}},{"k":"sr_compare_mode_opened_choose_two_structures_from_se","v":{"s":"Compare mode opened. Choose two structures from search or directly from the model."}},{"k":"sr_compare_mode_closed","v":{"s":"Compare mode closed."}},{"k":"sr_pathway_playback_started_each_structure_will_be_h","v":{"s":"Pathway playback started. Each structure will be highlighted in sequence."}},{"k":"sr_pathway_playback_paused","v":{"s":"Pathway playback paused."}},{"k":"sr_guided_pathway_closed","v":{"s":"Guided pathway closed."}},{"k":"sr_speech_pronunciation_is_not_available_in_this_bro","v":{"s":"Speech pronunciation is not available in this browser."}},{"k":"sr_speech_pronunciation_could_not_start","v":{"s":"Speech pronunciation could not start."}},{"k":"sr_3d_structure_selection_cleared","v":{"s":"3D structure selection cleared."}},{"k":"sr_find_it_in_3d_challenge_closed","v":{"s":"Find It in 3D challenge closed."}},{"k":"sr_quick_scan_selected_safety_context_remains_visibl","v":{"s":"Quick scan selected. Safety context remains visible."}},{"k":"sr_full_reference_selected_mechanisms_and_examples_a","v":{"s":"Full reference selected. Mechanisms and examples are now visible."}},{"k":"sr_medication_class_comparison_cleared","v":{"s":"Medication class comparison cleared."}},{"k":"sr_quick_treatment_scan_selected_safety_context_rema","v":{"s":"Quick treatment scan selected. Safety context remains visible."}},{"k":"sr_full_treatment_reference_selected_circuit_targets","v":{"s":"Full treatment reference selected. Circuit targets and clinical status are now visible."}},{"k":"sr_treatment_filters_cleared_all_modalities_are_visi","v":{"s":"Treatment filters cleared. All modalities are visible."}},{"k":"sr_treatment_comparison_cleared","v":{"s":"Treatment comparison cleared."}},{"k":"none_selected","v":{"s":"None selected"}},{"k":"now_studying_region_detail","v":{"s":"Now studying selected region details, function, related conditions, and damage patterns."}},{"k":"group_atlas","v":{"s":"Atlas"}},{"k":"group_atlas_note","v":{"s":"Lobes, language, body maps, orientation, nerves, and vessels"}},{"k":"group_systems","v":{"s":"Systems"}},{"k":"group_systems_note","v":{"s":"Cells, synapses, chemistry, memory, emotion, and movement loops"}},{"k":"group_simulations","v":{"s":"Simulations"}},{"k":"group_simulations_note","v":{"s":"Stimulation, sleep, rhythms, and wiring"}},{"k":"pick_a_region_takeaway","v":{"s":"Pick a region to see its student takeaway."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_selected","v":{"s":"Ask students: What evidence from the function or damage pattern helps you identify this region?"}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_nt","v":{"s":"Ask students to change one neurotransmitter at a time, then name what changed and what stayed uncertain."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_neuron","v":{"s":"Ask students to trace one signal from dendrite to axon terminal using one sentence per step."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_synapse","v":{"s":"Ask students to explain how strengthening or pruning a synapse could change learning over time."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_stim","v":{"s":"Ask students to predict the patient response first, then revise their reasoning after feedback."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_sleep","v":{"s":"Ask students to compare two sleep stages and connect each to memory, attention, or recovery."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_eeg","v":{"s":"Ask students to match a brain-wave pattern to a state, then explain the clue they used."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_cross","v":{"s":"Ask students to predict what the patient can say versus what the left hand can pick, then explain why the corpus callosum matters."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_atlas","v":{"s":"Ask students to choose one region, name its everyday job, and predict what might change if it were damaged."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_nt","v":{"s":"Neurotransmitter view showing synaptic release, receptor binding, and reuptake. Active scenario: "}},{"k":"canvas_summary_neuron","v":{"s":"Neuron anatomy view showing dendrites, soma, axon hillock, myelin, nodes, terminals, and a spike-cycle decoder for threshold, sodium influx, potassium efflux, refractory recovery, and all-or-nothing firing."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_synapse","v":{"s":"Synapse and development view showing how neural connections can strengthen, weaken, or be pruned through experience."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_stim","v":{"s":"Stimulation Lab view showing a Penfield-style response map for motor movement, sensory tingling, visual flashes, auditory sounds, language disruption, memory-emotion responses, autonomic body-state changes, and quiet association cortex while the current electrode target pulses."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_sleep","v":{"s":"Sleep-stage view showing an animated overnight hypnogram, repeated 90-minute cycles, early-night N3 slow-wave sleep, later-night REM periods, N2 spindle sleep, and a sleep-architecture decoder for recovery, memory, emotion, attention, and fragmented sleep."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_eeg","v":{"s":"EEG rhythm view comparing delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma waves across different brain states."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_cross","v":{"s":"Cross-lateral wiring view showing motor, sensory, and visual crossing, language lateralization, corpus callosum transfer between hemispheres, and a split-brain fixation task where left visual field -> right hemisphere information cannot be verbally named but the left hand can pick the object."}},{"k":"route_badge_atlas","v":{"s":"Atlas"}},{"k":"route_lobes_title","v":{"s":"Map the lobes"}},{"k":"route_lobes_copy","v":{"s":"Start with the side view and connect each lobe to everyday function."}},{"k":"route_badge_deep","v":{"s":"Deep"}},{"k":"route_deep_title","v":{"s":"Find hidden systems"}},{"k":"route_deep_copy","v":{"s":"Trace memory, emotion, homeostasis, and midline structures."}},{"k":"route_badge_cells","v":{"s":"Cells"}},{"k":"route_signals_title","v":{"s":"Follow a signal"}},{"k":"route_signals_copy","v":{"s":"Zoom from neuron anatomy into firing, myelin, and synaptic flow."}},{"k":"route_badge_rhythms","v":{"s":"Rhythms"}},{"k":"route_rhythms_title","v":{"s":"Read brain waves"}},{"k":"route_rhythms_copy","v":{"s":"Compare delta through gamma and connect waves to sleep and attention."}},{"k":"route_badge_wiring","v":{"s":"Wiring"}},{"k":"route_crossing_title","v":{"s":"Trace left vs right"}},{"k":"route_crossing_copy","v":{"s":"See why one hemisphere affects the opposite side of the body and why the corpus callosum lets the halves share information."}},{"k":"neural_systems_lab","v":{"s":"Neural systems lab"}},{"k":"brain_atlas_modes","v":{"s":"Brain atlas modes"}},{"k":"atlas_view","v":{"s":"Atlas view"}},{"k":"neurochemistry_lab","v":{"s":"Neurochemistry"}},{"k":"hide_function_match","v":{"s":"Hide Function Match"}},{"k":"function_match_2","v":{"s":"Function Match"}},{"k":"quiz_active","v":{"s":"Quiz active"}},{"k":"start_quiz","v":{"s":"Start quiz"}},{"k":"view_group_picker","v":{"s":"Brain Atlas view groups"}},{"k":"brain_atlas_progress_summary","v":{"s":"Brain atlas progress summary"}},{"k":"views_explored","v":{"s":"Views explored"}},{"k":"current_targets","v":{"s":"Current targets"}},{"k":"regions_in_this_view","v":{"s":"regions in this view"}},{"k":"quiz_score","v":{"s":"Quiz score"}},{"k":"damage_pattern_practice","v":{"s":"damage-pattern practice"}},{"k":"selected","v":{"s":"Selected"}},{"k":"detail_panel_ready","v":{"s":"detail panel ready"}},{"k":"pick_a_region","v":{"s":"pick a region on the atlas"}},{"k":"view_choices","v":{"s":"view choices"}},{"k":"selected_region","v":{"s":"Selected region"}},{"k":"select_a_region_or_switch_views","v":{"s":"Select a region on the atlas, or switch views to compare systems."}},{"k":"canvas_summary","v":{"s":"Canvas summary"}},{"k":"teacher_move","v":{"s":"Teacher move"}},{"k":"detail_view","v":{"s":"Detail view"}},{"k":"advanced_detail_note","v":{"s":"Advanced anatomy, pathways, and clinical context are visible."}},{"k":"plain_detail_note","v":{"s":"Plain view keeps the high-value student takeaways visible first."}},{"k":"detail_density","v":{"s":"Detail density"}},{"k":"plain_view","v":{"s":"Plain view"}},{"k":"advanced","v":{"s":"Advanced"}},{"k":"student_takeaway","v":{"s":"Student takeaway"}},{"k":"region_directory","v":{"s":"Region directory"}},{"k":"study_step_select","v":{"s":"1. Select"}},{"k":"study_step_select_note","v":{"s":"Pick a region from the atlas or list."}},{"k":"study_step_connect","v":{"s":"2. Connect"}},{"k":"study_step_connect_note","v":{"s":"Read function, blood supply, and conditions."}},{"k":"study_step_practice","v":{"s":"3. Practice"}},{"k":"study_step_practice_note","v":{"s":"Use quiz or Function Match when ready."}},{"k":"side_view_showing_all_four_lobes_cereb","v":{"s":"Side view showing all four lobes, cerebellum, and brainstem"}},{"k":"brainstem","v":{"s":"Brainstem"}},{"k":"broca_s_area","v":{"s":"Broca's Area"}},{"k":"wernicke_s_area","v":{"s":"Wernicke's Area"}},{"k":"insular_cortex_insula","v":{"s":"Insular Cortex (Insula)"}},{"k":"angular_gyrus","v":{"s":"Angular Gyrus"}},{"k":"supramarginal_gyrus","v":{"s":"Supramarginal Gyrus"}},{"k":"ventricular_system","v":{"s":"Ventricular System"}},{"k":"pineal_gland","v":{"s":"Pineal Gland"}},{"k":"fornix","v":{"s":"Fornix"}},{"k":"mammillary_bodies","v":{"s":"Mammillary Bodies"}},{"k":"septum_pellucidum","v":{"s":"Septum Pellucidum"}},{"k":"supplementary_motor_area","v":{"s":"Supplementary Motor Area"}},{"k":"pituitary_gland","v":{"s":"Pituitary Gland"}},{"k":"circle_of_willis","v":{"s":"Circle of Willis"}},{"k":"neurotransmitters","v":{"s":"⚡ Neurotransmitters"}},{"k":"complete_reference_synthesis_receptors","v":{"s":"Complete reference: synthesis, receptors, pathways, functions, pharmacology"}},{"k":"dopamine_da","v":{"s":"Dopamine (DA)"}},{"k":"serotonin_5_ht","v":{"s":"Serotonin (5-HT)"}},{"k":"norepinephrine_ne","v":{"s":"Norepinephrine (NE)"}},{"k":"acetylcholine_ach","v":{"s":"Acetylcholine (ACh)"}},{"k":"gaba_aminobutyric_acid","v":{"s":"GABA (γ-Aminobutyric Acid)"}},{"k":"glutamate_glu","v":{"s":"Glutamate (Glu)"}},{"k":"glycine","v":{"s":"Glycine"}},{"k":"histamine","v":{"s":"Histamine"}},{"k":"endorphins_endorphin","v":{"s":"Endorphins (β-Endorphin)"}},{"k":"substance_p","v":{"s":"Substance P"}},{"k":"oxytocin","v":{"s":"Oxytocin"}},{"k":"vasopressin_adh","v":{"s":"Vasopressin (ADH)"}},{"k":"melatonin","v":{"s":"Melatonin"}},{"k":"nitric_oxide_no","v":{"s":"Nitric Oxide (NO)"}},{"k":"anandamide_aea","v":{"s":"Anandamide (AEA)"}},{"k":"atp_adenosine","v":{"s":"ATP / Adenosine"}},{"k":"neuropeptide_y_npy","v":{"s":"Neuropeptide Y (NPY)"}},{"k":"epinephrine_adrenaline","v":{"s":"Epinephrine (Adrenaline)"}},{"k":"action_potential","v":{"s":"⚡ Action Potential"}},{"k":"watch_a_neuron_fire_ion_channels_depol","v":{"s":"Watch a neuron fire — ion channels, depolarization, and signal propagation"}},{"k":"dendrites","v":{"s":"Dendrites"}},{"k":"cell_body_soma","v":{"s":"Cell Body (Soma)"}},{"k":"axon_hillock","v":{"s":"Axon Hillock"}},{"k":"myelin_sheath","v":{"s":"Myelin Sheath"}},{"k":"nodes_of_ranvier","v":{"s":"Nodes of Ranvier"}},{"k":"axon_terminal","v":{"s":"Axon Terminal"}},{"k":"synapse_development","v":{"s":"🔗 Synapse & Development"}},{"k":"how_synapses_form_get_pruned_and_matur","v":{"s":"How synapses form, get pruned, and mature across the lifespan"}},{"k":"synaptogenesis_synapse_formation","v":{"s":"Synaptogenesis (synapse formation)"}},{"k":"synaptic_pruning","v":{"s":"Synaptic pruning"}},{"k":"critical_sensitive_periods","v":{"s":"Critical & sensitive periods"}},{"k":"myelination_development","v":{"s":"Myelination (development)"}},{"k":"neuroplasticity_lifelong","v":{"s":"Neuroplasticity (lifelong)"}},{"k":"adolescent_remodeling","v":{"s":"Adolescent remodeling"}},{"k":"stimulation_lab","v":{"s":"⚡ Stimulation Lab"}},{"k":"predict_what_stimulating_each_region_d","v":{"s":"Predict what stimulating each region does (how Penfield mapped the brain)"}},{"k":"sleep_stages","v":{"s":"💤 Sleep Stages"}},{"k":"hypnogram_visualization_sleep_architec","v":{"s":"Hypnogram visualization — sleep architecture across a full night"}},{"k":"wakefulness","v":{"s":"Wakefulness"}},{"k":"stage_n1_light_sleep","v":{"s":"Stage N1 (Light Sleep)"}},{"k":"stage_n2_core_sleep","v":{"s":"Stage N2 (Core Sleep)"}},{"k":"stage_n3_deep_sws","v":{"s":"Stage N3 (Deep/SWS)"}},{"k":"rem_sleep","v":{"s":"REM Sleep"}},{"k":"eeg_waves","v":{"s":"📈 EEG Waves"}},{"k":"real_time_brain_wave_patterns_frequenc","v":{"s":"Real-time brain wave patterns — frequency bands and clinical significance"}},{"k":"delta_waves_0_5_4_hz","v":{"s":"Delta Waves (0.5–4 Hz)"}},{"k":"theta_waves_4_8_hz","v":{"s":"Theta Waves (4–8 Hz)"}},{"k":"alpha_waves_8_13_hz","v":{"s":"Alpha Waves (8–13 Hz)"}},{"k":"beta_waves_13_30_hz","v":{"s":"Beta Waves (13–30 Hz)"}},{"k":"gamma_waves_30_100_hz","v":{"s":"Gamma Waves (30–100 Hz)"}},{"k":"cross_lateralization","v":{"s":"🔀 Cross-Lateralization"}},{"k":"how_each_hemisphere_controls_the_oppos","v":{"s":"How each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body and shares information through the corpus callosum"}},{"k":"motor_decussation_pyramids","v":{"s":"Motor Decussation (Pyramids)"}},{"k":"sensory_decussation_medial_lemniscus","v":{"s":"Sensory Decussation (Medial Lemniscus)"}},{"k":"optic_chiasm_visual_crossing","v":{"s":"Optic Chiasm (Visual Crossing)"}},{"k":"corpus_callosum","v":{"s":"Corpus Callosum"}},{"k":"language_lateralization","v":{"s":"Language Lateralization"}},{"k":"handedness_motor_dominance","v":{"s":"Handedness & Motor Dominance"}},{"k":"split_brain_phenomenon","v":{"s":"Split-Brain Phenomenon"}},{"k":"normal","v":{"s":"Normal"}},{"k":"baseline_neurotransmission_neurotransm","v":{"s":"Baseline neurotransmission: neurotransmitters are released, bind receptors, and are cleared by reuptake and enzymatic degradation."}},{"k":"selective_serotonin_reuptake_inhibitor","v":{"s":"Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitor (e.g., Fluoxetine/Prozac): blocks the serotonin transporter (SERT), increasing 5-HT concentration in the synaptic cleft. Used for depression and anxiety."}},{"k":"serotonin_norepinephrine_reuptake_inhi","v":{"s":"Serotonin-Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitor (e.g., Venlafaxine/Effexor): blocks both SERT and NET, increasing serotonin AND norepinephrine in the cleft. Used for depression, anxiety, and chronic pain."}},{"k":"benzodiazepine","v":{"s":"Benzodiazepine"}},{"k":"positive_allosteric_modulator_of_gaba_","v":{"s":"Positive allosteric modulator of GABA-A receptors (e.g., Diazepam/Valium): increases Cl⁻ channel opening frequency, enhancing inhibitory neurotransmission. Used for anxiety, seizures, and insomnia. Risk: dependence."}},{"k":"cocaine","v":{"s":"Cocaine"}},{"k":"blocks_dat_net_and_sert_reuptake_trans","v":{"s":"Blocks DAT, NET, and SERT reuptake transporters non-selectively. Massive dopamine accumulation in mesolimbic pathway → intense euphoria. Highly addictive. Cardiotoxic: causes vasoconstriction, arrhythmias, MI, and stroke."}},{"k":"opioid","v":{"s":"Opioid"}},{"k":"mu_receptor_agonist_e_g_morphine_fenta","v":{"s":"Mu-receptor agonist (e.g., Morphine, Fentanyl): activates endogenous opioid receptors → analgesia, euphoria, respiratory depression. Hijacks VTA reward circuit. Tolerance and physical dependence develop rapidly. Overdose: fatal respiratory arrest."}},{"k":"alcohol","v":{"s":"Alcohol"}},{"k":"dual_mechanism_enhances_gaba_a_recepto","v":{"s":"Dual mechanism: enhances GABA-A receptor activity (sedation) AND blocks NMDA glutamate receptors (reduces excitation). Results in CNS depression, impaired judgment, ataxia. Chronic use: neuroadaptation, tolerance, and life-threatening withdrawal seizures."}},{"k":"awake","v":{"s":"Awake"}},{"k":"awake_2","v":{"s":"Awake"}},{"k":"resting","v":{"s":"Resting"}},{"k":"sleeping","v":{"s":"Sleeping"}},{"k":"studying","v":{"s":"Studying"}},{"k":"exercise","v":{"s":"Exercise"}},{"k":"meditating","v":{"s":"Meditating"}},{"k":"delta_0_5_4_hz","v":{"s":"Delta (0.5–4 Hz)"}},{"k":"theta_4_8_hz","v":{"s":"Theta (4–8 Hz)"}},{"k":"alpha_8_13_hz","v":{"s":"Alpha (8–13 Hz)"}},{"k":"beta_13_30_hz","v":{"s":"Beta (13–30 Hz)"}},{"k":"gamma_30_100_hz","v":{"s":"Gamma (30–100 Hz)"}},{"k":"motor_corticospinal","v":{"s":"Motor (corticospinal)"}},{"k":"sensory_dcml","v":{"s":"Sensory (DCML)"}},{"k":"visual_optic","v":{"s":"Visual (optic)"}},{"k":"language_left_dominant","v":{"s":"Language (left-dominant)"}},{"k":"motor_cortex_stimulation_produces_move","v":{"s":"Motor-cortex stimulation produces MOVEMENT on the opposite side of the body. Penfield mapped the motor homunculus exactly this way in awake patients."}},{"k":"the_somatosensory_strip_just_behind_th","v":{"s":"The somatosensory strip (just behind the motor strip) produces a SENSATION on the opposite side, not a movement."}},{"k":"v1_stimulation_causes_phosphenes_simpl","v":{"s":"V1 stimulation causes phosphenes (simple flashes or spots of light), not formed images. This is the basis of experimental visual prostheses."}},{"k":"auditory_cortex_stimulation_produces_s","v":{"s":"Auditory-cortex stimulation produces simple sounds (buzzing or ringing), not words or music."}},{"k":"stimulating_broca_area_causes_speech_a","v":{"s":"Stimulating Broca area causes speech ARREST with intact comprehension, the brief mirror of Broca aphasia."}},{"k":"wernicke_area_stimulation_disrupts_lan","v":{"s":"Wernicke-area stimulation disrupts language COMPREHENSION and produces jargon, unlike the clean arrest from Broca area."}},{"k":"penfield_famously_evoked_experiential_","v":{"s":"Penfield famously evoked experiential responses (vivid memories, songs, deja vu) from temporal-lobe stimulation, a clue to how memory is organized."}},{"k":"amygdala_stimulation_can_trigger_fear_","v":{"s":"Amygdala stimulation can trigger fear and autonomic arousal, consistent with its role in threat processing."}},{"k":"the_hypothalamus_drives_autonomic_and_","v":{"s":"The hypothalamus drives autonomic and homeostatic responses (temperature, hunger, heart rate), not sensation or movement."}},{"k":"much_of_association_cortex_including_p","v":{"s":"Much of association cortex (including prefrontal) is electrically quiet: stimulation produces no dramatic response. Not every brain area maps to one obvious output."}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"brain_atlas","v":{"s":"🧠 Brain Atlas"}},{"k":"monoamine_model_anxious_hyperaroused_p","v":{"s":"Monoamine model: anxious / hyperaroused profile"}},{"k":"in_the_simplified_monoamine_framing_lo","v":{"s":"In the simplified monoamine framing, low GABA relative to norepi + glutamate is associated with racing thoughts, restlessness, hypervigilance. Real anxiety has many causes (receptor sensitivity, cortisol axis, context) — this is one toy mapping."}},{"k":"monoamine_model_agitated_profile","v":{"s":"Monoamine model: agitated profile"}},{"k":"high_arousal_low_mood_in_the_toy_mappi","v":{"s":"High arousal + low mood in the toy mapping. Patterns labeled \"agitated\" in clinical settings can come from many sources including medication side-effects, withdrawal, and mixed mood states."}},{"k":"monoamine_model_well_regulated_profile","v":{"s":"Monoamine model: well-regulated profile"}},{"k":"dopamine_serotonin_in_mid_range_with_m","v":{"s":"Dopamine + serotonin in mid-range with modest arousal — the toy mapping calls this on-task / engaged. Real attention regulation depends on prefrontal-striatal circuits, not just transmitter levels."}},{"k":"monoamine_model_low_energy_profile","v":{"s":"Monoamine model: low-energy profile"}},{"k":"low_da_ne_5_ht_in_the_toy_mapping_is_h","v":{"s":"Low DA + NE + 5-HT in the toy mapping is historically associated with anhedonia and fatigue. The \"chemical imbalance\" theory of depression is contested in current research (see Moncrieff et al., 2022); receptor sensitivity, network dynamics, and life context matter as much."}},{"k":"monoamine_model_baseline_neutral","v":{"s":"Monoamine model: baseline / neutral"}},{"k":"no_single_system_dominates_most_everyd","v":{"s":"No single system dominates. Most everyday states live here in the toy mapping."}},{"k":"dopamine","v":{"s":"Dopamine"}},{"k":"serotonin","v":{"s":"Serotonin"}},{"k":"glutamate","v":{"s":"Glutamate"}},{"k":"norepinephrine","v":{"s":"Norepinephrine"}},{"k":"neurotransmitter_inquiry_predict_the_s","v":{"s":"🔬 Neurotransmitter Inquiry — Explore the State"}},{"k":"set_five_neurotransmitter_levels_predi","v":{"s":"Set five neurotransmitter levels, then observe how the modeled state changes. The result updates live; record a hypothesis or pattern you notice. This is not a clinical interpretation."}},{"k":"arousal","v":{"s":"Arousal"}},{"k":"mood","v":{"s":"Mood"}},{"k":"attention","v":{"s":"Attention"}},{"k":"anxiety_idx","v":{"s":"Anxiety idx"}},{"k":"log_this_profile","v":{"s":"📋 Log this profile"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_which_two_neurotransmi","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (which two neurotransmitters most strongly trade off in shaping affect?)"}},{"k":"e_g_gaba_and_glutamate_are_functional_","v":{"s":"e.g., GABA and glutamate are functional opposites (inhibitory vs excitatory)..."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_open_questions","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions"}},{"k":"open_questions_no_answer_key","v":{"s":"Open questions (no answer key)"}},{"k":"gaba_and_glutamate_are_inhibitory_exci","v":{"s":"GABA and glutamate are inhibitory/excitatory — what does that mean at the synapse, not just at the system level?"}},{"k":"dopamine_is_often_called_the_reward_ch","v":{"s":"Dopamine is often called the \"reward\" chemical. Is that the right frame? (See Schultz reward-prediction-error work.)"}},{"k":"why_is_the_low_serotonin_depression_cl","v":{"s":"Why is the \"low serotonin → depression\" claim contested in current research?"}},{"k":"what_states_would_you_not_expect_to_se","v":{"s":"What states would you NOT expect to see in this five-axis space, and what would they require?"}},{"k":"i_can_explain_why_this_nt_profile_is_a","v":{"s":"I can explain why this NT profile is associated with this functional state."}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words..."}},{"k":"inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a","v":{"s":"Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. Mapping from neurotransmitter levels to felt states is a teaching heuristic, NOT a clinical model. The \"chemical imbalance\" theory of mood disorders is contested; receptor sensitivity, network dynamics, and lifecycle/context matter at least as much."}},{"k":"lateral_view_the_4_lobes_cerebellum_br","v":{"s":"Lateral view — the 4 lobes, cerebellum, brainstem"}},{"k":"the_classic_side_view_frontal_planning","v":{"s":"The classic side view. Frontal (planning + Broca), parietal (sensation + space), temporal (language + memory), occipital (vision). Each lobe maps to specific clinical syndromes."}},{"k":"medial_sagittal_midline_structures","v":{"s":"Medial sagittal — midline structures"}},{"k":"cut_the_brain_in_half_corpus_callosum_","v":{"s":"Cut the brain in half: corpus callosum, thalamus, hypothalamus, cingulate, hippocampus. The deep structures the lateral view hides — most of the limbic system lives here."}},{"k":"superior_view_top_down","v":{"s":"Superior view — top-down"}},{"k":"look_down_at_the_brain_the_longitudina","v":{"s":"Look down at the brain. The longitudinal fissure splits L and R hemispheres; the central sulcus separates motor (front) from somatosensory (back). Asymmetry reveals."}},{"k":"inferior_view_bottom_up","v":{"s":"Inferior view — bottom-up"}},{"k":"look_up_from_underneath_cranial_nerves","v":{"s":"Look up from underneath. Cranial nerves I–XII exit here. The Circle of Willis (basilar + vertebral + ICA) supplies the brain — strokes happen at these branch points."}},{"k":"neurotransmitters_chemistry_of_mind","v":{"s":"Neurotransmitters — chemistry of mind"}},{"k":"glutamate_excite_gaba_inhibit_dopamine","v":{"s":"Glutamate (excite), GABA (inhibit), dopamine (reward + motor), serotonin (mood + GI), norepinephrine (alertness), acetylcholine (memory + autonomic). Drug targets all live here."}},{"k":"neuron_anatomy_single_cell","v":{"s":"Neuron anatomy — single cell"}},{"k":"dendrites_input_soma_axon_output_termi","v":{"s":"Dendrites (input) → soma → axon (output) → terminal. Action potential travels at 10–120 m/s; myelin makes it faster. Synapses can be excitatory or inhibitory."}},{"k":"sleep_stages_n1_n2_n3_rem","v":{"s":"Sleep stages — N1, N2, N3, REM"}},{"k":"cycles_every_90_min_n3_deep_for_memory","v":{"s":"Cycles every ~90 min. N3 (deep) for memory consolidation; REM for emotional processing + procedural memory. Adolescents need 8–10 hrs; chronic deprivation impairs everything cognitive."}},{"k":"eeg_waves_brain_rhythms","v":{"s":"EEG waves — brain rhythms"}},{"k":"delta_deep_sleep_theta_drowsy_memory_a","v":{"s":"Delta (deep sleep) → Theta (drowsy + memory) → Alpha (relaxed wakefulness) → Beta (active thinking) → Gamma (focused attention). Frequency rises with arousal level."}},{"k":"cross_lateral_wiring_left_vs_right","v":{"s":"Cross-lateral wiring — left vs right"}},{"k":"each_hemisphere_controls_the_opposite_","v":{"s":"Each hemisphere controls the OPPOSITE side of the body, while the corpus callosum transfers information between hemispheres. Split-brain studies make that bridge visible: what one hemisphere sees may not be verbally reportable unless information crosses."}},{"k":"search_regions_functions_conditions","v":{"s":"🔍 Search regions, functions, conditions..."}},{"k":"search_brain_regions_functions_and_con","v":{"s":"Search brain regions, functions, and conditions"}},{"k":"frontal_lobe","v":{"s":"Frontal lobe"}},{"k":"parietal_lobe","v":{"s":"Parietal lobe"}},{"k":"temporal_lobe","v":{"s":"Temporal lobe"}},{"k":"occipital_lobe","v":{"s":"Occipital lobe"}},{"k":"hippocampus","v":{"s":"Hippocampus"}},{"k":"amygdala","v":{"s":"Amygdala"}},{"k":"cerebellum","v":{"s":"Cerebellum"}},{"k":"brainstem_2","v":{"s":"Brainstem"}},{"k":"function_match","v":{"s":"Function Match"}},{"k":"12_function_clinical_syndrome_vignette","v":{"s":"12 function / clinical-syndrome vignettes — pick the brain region."}},{"k":"for_each_clinical_vignette_pick_the_br","v":{"s":"For each clinical vignette, pick the brain region most likely involved. Coaching cites the canonical case (HM, Phineas Gage, etc.) and what distinguishes the correct region from look-alikes."}},{"k":"start_vignette_1_of_12","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start — vignette 1 of 12"}},{"k":"vignette","v":{"s":"Vignette "}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_brain_region","v":{"s":"Pick the brain region"}},{"k":"all_12_vignettes_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 12 vignettes complete"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_vignette","v":{"s":"➡️ Next vignette"}},{"k":"tolerance_receptor_desensitization","v":{"s":"⚠️ Tolerance & Receptor Desensitization"}},{"k":"with_repeated_exposure_postsynaptic_re","v":{"s":"With repeated exposure, postsynaptic receptors undergo downregulation — the cell reduces receptor density or sensitivity (internalization) to compensate for excess stimulation. This means higher doses are needed to achieve the same effect, driving the cycle of tolerance and dependence. Abrupt cessation can cause withdrawal as the nervous system has adapted to the drug’s presence."}},{"k":"how_neurons_fire_the_action_potential","v":{"s":"⚡ How Neurons Fire: The Action Potential"}},{"k":"ion_chemistry","v":{"s":"🧪 Ion Chemistry"}},{"k":"at_rest_the_neuron_is_polarized_at_70m","v":{"s":"At rest, the neuron is polarized at -70mV. Na⁺ (sodium) is concentrated outside; K⁺ (potassium) inside. The Na⁺/K⁺ pump maintains this gradient using ATP energy (3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in)."}},{"k":"all_or_nothing_firing","v":{"s":"⚡ All-or-Nothing Firing"}},{"k":"when_stimulation_reaches_threshold_55m","v":{"s":"When stimulation reaches threshold (-55mV), voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open and the neuron fires at FULL strength. There is no “half” signal — it either fires completely or not at all. Stronger stimuli increase firing RATE, not intensity."}},{"k":"recovery_cycle","v":{"s":"🔄 Recovery Cycle"}},{"k":"after_firing_na_channels_close_k_chann","v":{"s":"After firing: Na⁺ channels close, K⁺ channels open → repolarization. The membrane briefly overshoots to -80mV (hyperpolarization), creating a refractory period where the neuron cannot fire again. This ensures one-way signal travel."}},{"k":"saltatory_conduction_signals_jump_betw","v":{"s":"Saltatory conduction: signals “jump” between Nodes of Ranvier along the myelinated axon, increasing speed from ~2 m/s to ~120 m/s."}},{"k":"stimulation_lab_2","v":{"s":"⚡ Stimulation Lab"}},{"k":"wilder_penfield_mapped_the_brain_by_ge","v":{"s":"Wilder Penfield mapped the brain by gently stimulating awake patients during surgery. Predict what the patient would experience."}},{"k":"electrode_on","v":{"s":"Electrode on: "}},{"k":"what_does_the_awake_patient_experience","v":{"s":"What does the awake patient experience?"}},{"k":"predict_the_effect_of_stimulation","v":{"s":"Predict the effect of stimulation"}},{"k":"next_stimulation","v":{"s":"Next stimulation"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"patient_simulator_ai","v":{"s":"🧑‍⚕️ Patient Simulator (AI)"}},{"k":"a_simulated_patient_ai_reacts_to_a_hid","v":{"s":"A simulated patient (AI) reacts to a hidden stimulation. Read what they say, then guess which region was stimulated. The patient never names the region."}},{"k":"stimulate_the_patient","v":{"s":"⚡ Stimulate the patient"}},{"k":"the_patient_is_responding","v":{"s":"⏳ The patient is responding..."}},{"k":"the_patient_says","v":{"s":"The patient says"}},{"k":"which_region_was_stimulated","v":{"s":"Which region was stimulated?"}},{"k":"guess_the_stimulated_region","v":{"s":"Guess the stimulated region"}},{"k":"new_patient","v":{"s":"New patient"}},{"k":"new_patient_2","v":{"s":"New patient →"}},{"k":"brain_quiz","v":{"s":"🧠 Brain Quiz"}},{"k":"what_happens_when_this_region_is_damag","v":{"s":"What happens when this region is damaged?"}},{"k":"if_damaged","v":{"s":"🏥 If Damaged: "}},{"k":"conditions","v":{"s":"⚠ Conditions: "}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next Question"}},{"k":"next_question_2","v":{"s":"Next Question →"}},{"k":"brainwave_visualizer","v":{"s":"Brainwave Visualizer"}},{"k":"delta","v":{"s":"Δ Delta"}},{"k":"deep_sleep_healing_unconscious_process","v":{"s":"Deep sleep, healing, unconscious processes. Highest amplitude, slowest frequency."}},{"k":"theta","v":{"s":"Θ Theta"}},{"k":"light_sleep_meditation_creativity_memo","v":{"s":"Light sleep, meditation, creativity, memory consolidation. \"Twilight\" state between waking and sleeping."}},{"k":"alpha","v":{"s":"α Alpha"}},{"k":"relaxed_alertness_calm_focus_mindfulne","v":{"s":"Relaxed alertness, calm focus, mindfulness. Prominent over occipital cortex; attenuates when the eyes open (Berger effect)."}},{"k":"beta","v":{"s":"β Beta"}},{"k":"active_thinking_problem_solving_focuse","v":{"s":"Active thinking, problem-solving, focused concentration. Dominant during normal waking consciousness."}},{"k":"gamma","v":{"s":"γ Gamma"}},{"k":"higher_cognitive_processing_peak_aware","v":{"s":"Higher cognitive processing, peak awareness, information binding across brain regions. Fastest brainwave."}},{"k":"eeg_waveform_animation","v":{"s":"EEG waveform animation"}},{"k":"delta_2","v":{"s":"Δ Delta"}},{"k":"deep_sleep_healing_unconscious_process_2","v":{"s":"Deep sleep, healing, unconscious processes. Highest amplitude, slowest frequency."}},{"k":"theta_2","v":{"s":"Θ Theta"}},{"k":"light_sleep_meditation_creativity_memo_2","v":{"s":"Light sleep, meditation, creativity, memory consolidation. Twilight state."}},{"k":"alpha_2","v":{"s":"α Alpha"}},{"k":"relaxed_alertness_calm_focus_mindfulne_2","v":{"s":"Relaxed alertness, calm focus, mindfulness. Prominent over occipital cortex; attenuates with eye opening."}},{"k":"beta_2","v":{"s":"β Beta"}},{"k":"active_thinking_problem_solving_focuse_2","v":{"s":"Active thinking, problem-solving, focused concentration. Normal waking consciousness."}},{"k":"gamma_2","v":{"s":"γ Gamma"}},{"k":"higher_cognitive_processing_peak_aware_2","v":{"s":"Higher cognitive processing, peak awareness, cross-region information binding."}},{"k":"frequency","v":{"s":"Frequency"}},{"k":"amplitude","v":{"s":"Amplitude"}},{"k":"mental_states","v":{"s":"Mental States"}},{"k":"eeg_location","v":{"s":"EEG Location"}},{"k":"deep_sleep_healing_highest_amplitude_s","v":{"s":"Deep sleep, healing. Highest amplitude, slowest frequency. Dominant in infants and during deep non-REM sleep."}},{"k":"light_sleep_meditation_creativity_the_","v":{"s":"Light sleep, meditation, creativity. The twilight state between waking and sleeping. Linked to memory consolidation."}},{"k":"relaxed_alertness_calm_focus_prominent","v":{"s":"Relaxed alertness, calm focus. Prominent over occipital cortex during relaxed, eyes-closed wakefulness. Blocked by opening eyes (Berger effect)."}},{"k":"active_thinking_problem_solving_domina","v":{"s":"Active thinking, problem-solving. Dominant during normal waking consciousness. Excess linked to anxiety."}},{"k":"peak_cognitive_processing_cross_region","v":{"s":"Peak cognitive processing, cross-region binding. Fastest brainwave. Associated with advanced meditation and heightened perception."}},{"k":"close_region_detail_panel","v":{"s":"Close region detail panel"}},{"k":"function","v":{"s":"Function"}},{"k":"brodmann_areas","v":{"s":"Brodmann Areas"}},{"k":"blood_supply","v":{"s":"Blood Supply"}},{"k":"category","v":{"s":"⚗️ Category"}},{"k":"synthesis_pathway","v":{"s":"🧬 Synthesis Pathway"}},{"k":"receptor_subtypes","v":{"s":"🎯 Receptor Subtypes"}},{"k":"neural_pathways","v":{"s":"🛤️ Neural Pathways"}},{"k":"pharmacology","v":{"s":"💊 Pharmacology"}},{"k":"associated_conditions","v":{"s":"⚠ Associated Conditions"}},{"k":"if_damaged_2","v":{"s":"🏥 If Damaged"}},{"k":"plain","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"grade_5","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"high_school","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"explain_at_my_level","v":{"s":"✨ Explain at my level"}},{"k":"reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"no_regions_match_your_search","v":{"s":"No regions match your search."}},{"k":"cranial_nerves_willis","v":{"s":"Cranial Nerves & Willis"}},{"k":"cranial_nerves_willis_desc","v":{"s":"Inferior brain map of cranial nerve exits and the Circle of Willis"}},{"k":"cn_i_olfactory","v":{"s":"CN I - Olfactory Bulbs/Tracts"}},{"k":"cn_ii_chiasm","v":{"s":"CN II / Optic Chiasm"}},{"k":"cn_iii_oculomotor","v":{"s":"CN III - Oculomotor"}},{"k":"cn_v_trigeminal","v":{"s":"CN V - Trigeminal"}},{"k":"cn_vi_abducens","v":{"s":"CN VI - Abducens"}},{"k":"cn_vii_viii_facial_vestibular","v":{"s":"CN VII/VIII - Facial & Vestibulocochlear"}},{"k":"cn_ix_x_xi_lower_cranial","v":{"s":"CN IX/X/XI - Lower Cranial Nerves"}},{"k":"cn_xii_hypoglossal","v":{"s":"CN XII - Hypoglossal"}},{"k":"circle_willis_ring","v":{"s":"Circle of Willis Ring"}},{"k":"acomm_aca","v":{"s":"AComm / ACA"}},{"k":"pcomm_ica","v":{"s":"PComm / Internal Carotid"}},{"k":"basilar_vertebral","v":{"s":"Basilar / Vertebral Arteries"}},{"k":"bedside_clue_decoder","v":{"s":"Bedside Clue Decoder"}},{"k":"stroke_territories","v":{"s":"Stroke Territories"}},{"k":"stroke_territories_desc","v":{"s":"ACA, MCA, PCA, lacunar, watershed, and posterior-circulation stroke localization"}},{"k":"aca_stroke_territory","v":{"s":"ACA Territory"}},{"k":"mca_superior_stroke","v":{"s":"MCA Superior Division"}},{"k":"mca_inferior_stroke","v":{"s":"MCA Inferior Division"}},{"k":"pca_stroke_territory","v":{"s":"PCA Territory"}},{"k":"deep_lacunar_stroke","v":{"s":"Deep Lacunar Territory"}},{"k":"watershed_stroke","v":{"s":"Watershed / Border Zone"}},{"k":"posterior_circulation_stroke","v":{"s":"Posterior Circulation"}},{"k":"stroke_case_decoder","v":{"s":"Stroke Case Decoder"}},{"k":"cerebellum_clinic","v":{"s":"Cerebellum Clinic"}},{"k":"cerebellum_clinic_desc","v":{"s":"Cerebellar functional zones, artery territories, and ipsilateral ataxia clues"}},{"k":"cerebellum_vermis","v":{"s":"Vermis / Truncal Zone"}},{"k":"cerebellum_hemisphere","v":{"s":"Cerebellar Hemispheres"}},{"k":"cerebellum_flocculonodular","v":{"s":"Flocculonodular / Vestibular Zone"}},{"k":"cerebellum_deep_nuclei","v":{"s":"Deep Cerebellar Nuclei"}},{"k":"cerebellum_pica","v":{"s":"PICA Territory"}},{"k":"cerebellum_aica","v":{"s":"AICA Territory"}},{"k":"cerebellum_sca","v":{"s":"SCA Territory"}},{"k":"cerebellar_case_decoder","v":{"s":"Cerebellar Case Decoder"}},{"k":"brainstem_cross_section","v":{"s":"Brainstem Cross-Section"}},{"k":"brainstem_cross_section_desc","v":{"s":"Midbrain, pons, medulla, long tracts, cranial nerve nuclei, and crossed findings"}},{"k":"midbrain_cross_section","v":{"s":"Midbrain Level"}},{"k":"pons_cross_section","v":{"s":"Pons Level"}},{"k":"medulla_cross_section","v":{"s":"Medulla Level"}},{"k":"corticospinal_brainstem","v":{"s":"Corticospinal Tract"}},{"k":"medial_lemniscus_brainstem","v":{"s":"Medial Lemniscus"}},{"k":"spinothalamic_brainstem","v":{"s":"Spinothalamic Tract"}},{"k":"cranial_nuclei_brainstem","v":{"s":"Cranial Nerve Nuclei"}},{"k":"crossed_findings_decoder","v":{"s":"Crossed Findings Decoder"}},{"k":"csf_flow_hydrocephalus","v":{"s":"CSF Flow & Hydrocephalus"}},{"k":"csf_flow_hydrocephalus_desc","v":{"s":"CSF production, ventricular flow, reabsorption, and hydrocephalus localization"}},{"k":"choroid_plexus_csf","v":{"s":"Choroid Plexus"}},{"k":"lateral_ventricles_csf","v":{"s":"Lateral Ventricles"}},{"k":"third_ventricle_csf","v":{"s":"Third Ventricle"}},{"k":"cerebral_aqueduct_csf","v":{"s":"Cerebral Aqueduct"}},{"k":"fourth_ventricle_csf","v":{"s":"Fourth Ventricle"}},{"k":"subarachnoid_space_csf","v":{"s":"Subarachnoid Space"}},{"k":"arachnoid_granulations_csf","v":{"s":"Arachnoid Granulations"}},{"k":"hydrocephalus_decoder_csf","v":{"s":"Hydrocephalus Decoder"}},{"k":"motor_sensory_homunculus","v":{"s":"Motor/Sensory Homunculus"}},{"k":"homunculus_map_motor_sensory_cortex","v":{"s":"Penfield-style body map across primary motor and primary somatosensory cortex"}},{"k":"primary_motor_cortex_precentral","v":{"s":"Primary Motor Cortex (precentral gyrus)"}},{"k":"primary_somatosensory_cortex_postcentral","v":{"s":"Primary Somatosensory Cortex (postcentral gyrus)"}},{"k":"homunculus_leg_foot","v":{"s":"Leg & Foot Area"}},{"k":"homunculus_hand_area","v":{"s":"Hand Area"}},{"k":"homunculus_face_lips","v":{"s":"Face & Lips Area"}},{"k":"homunculus_tongue_area","v":{"s":"Tongue Area"}},{"k":"visual_pathway","v":{"s":"Visual Pathway"}},{"k":"visual_pathway_map_desc","v":{"s":"Retina to V1 map with classic visual-field cuts"}},{"k":"visual_retina","v":{"s":"Retina"}},{"k":"visual_optic_nerve","v":{"s":"Optic Nerve"}},{"k":"visual_optic_chiasm","v":{"s":"Optic Chiasm"}},{"k":"visual_optic_tract","v":{"s":"Optic Tract"}},{"k":"visual_lgn","v":{"s":"Lateral Geniculate Nucleus (LGN)"}},{"k":"visual_meyer_loop","v":{"s":"Meyer Loop (temporal radiation)"}},{"k":"visual_parietal_radiation","v":{"s":"Parietal Optic Radiation"}},{"k":"visual_primary_visual_cortex","v":{"s":"Primary Visual Cortex (V1)"}},{"k":"visual_field_cut_decoder","v":{"s":"Field-Cut Decoder"}},{"k":"language_network","v":{"s":"Language Network"}},{"k":"language_network_desc","v":{"s":"Dominant-hemisphere speech, comprehension, reading, and repetition circuit"}},{"k":"language_auditory_word_input","v":{"s":"Auditory Word Input"}},{"k":"language_wernicke_area","v":{"s":"Wernicke Area"}},{"k":"language_angular_gyrus","v":{"s":"Angular Gyrus"}},{"k":"language_supramarginal_gyrus","v":{"s":"Supramarginal Gyrus"}},{"k":"language_arcuate_fasciculus","v":{"s":"Arcuate Fasciculus"}},{"k":"language_broca_area","v":{"s":"Broca Area"}},{"k":"language_motor_speech_cortex","v":{"s":"Motor Speech Cortex"}},{"k":"language_visual_word_input","v":{"s":"Visual Word Input"}},{"k":"language_semantic_network","v":{"s":"Semantic Network"}},{"k":"language_aphasia_clue_cards","v":{"s":"Aphasia Clue Cards"}},{"k":"basal_ganglia_loop","v":{"s":"Basal Ganglia Loop"}},{"k":"basal_ganglia_loop_desc","v":{"s":"Direct and indirect movement-selection loops with dopamine modulation"}},{"k":"bg_motor_cortex","v":{"s":"Motor Cortex"}},{"k":"bg_striatum_d1","v":{"s":"Striatum D1 (Direct / GO)"}},{"k":"bg_striatum_d2","v":{"s":"Striatum D2 (Indirect / NO-GO)"}},{"k":"bg_gpe","v":{"s":"Globus Pallidus Externus (GPe)"}},{"k":"bg_stn","v":{"s":"Subthalamic Nucleus (STN)"}},{"k":"bg_gpi_snr","v":{"s":"GPi / SNr Output Brake"}},{"k":"bg_thalamus","v":{"s":"Thalamus (VA/VL)"}},{"k":"bg_snc_dopamine","v":{"s":"Substantia Nigra pars compacta (Dopamine)"}},{"k":"bg_direct_pathway","v":{"s":"Direct Pathway: GO"}},{"k":"bg_indirect_pathway","v":{"s":"Indirect Pathway: NO-GO"}},{"k":"bg_movement_disorder_decoder","v":{"s":"Movement Disorder Decoder"}},{"k":"limbic_papez_loop","v":{"s":"Limbic / Papez Loop"}},{"k":"limbic_papez_loop_desc","v":{"s":"Memory-emotion circuit linking hippocampus, cingulate, amygdala, and hypothalamus"}},{"k":"limbic_hippocampus","v":{"s":"Hippocampus"}},{"k":"limbic_fornix","v":{"s":"Fornix"}},{"k":"limbic_mammillary","v":{"s":"Mammillary Bodies"}},{"k":"limbic_mammillothalamic","v":{"s":"Mammillothalamic Tract"}},{"k":"limbic_anterior_thalamus","v":{"s":"Anterior Thalamic Nucleus"}},{"k":"limbic_cingulate","v":{"s":"Cingulate Cortex"}},{"k":"limbic_cingulum","v":{"s":"Cingulum Bundle"}},{"k":"limbic_entorhinal","v":{"s":"Entorhinal / Parahippocampal Cortex"}},{"k":"limbic_amygdala","v":{"s":"Amygdala"}},{"k":"limbic_hypothalamus","v":{"s":"Hypothalamus"}},{"k":"limbic_prefrontal","v":{"s":"Medial Prefrontal Regulation"}},{"k":"limbic_papez_path","v":{"s":"Papez Memory Loop"}},{"k":"limbic_emotion_output","v":{"s":"Amygdala Emotion Output"}},{"k":"limbic_memory_emotion_decoder","v":{"s":"Memory-Emotion Decoder"}},{"k":"spike_cycle_decoder","v":{"s":"Spike Cycle Decoder"}},{"k":"prenatal_brain_development","v":{"s":"Prenatal Brain Development"}},{"k":"prenatal_brain_development_desc","v":{"s":"Follow the overlapping stages that shape the brain from neural tube to a folded, connected newborn brain."}},{"k":"prenatal_neural_tube","v":{"s":"Weeks 3-4 — Neural tube"}},{"k":"prenatal_brain_regions","v":{"s":"Weeks 5-8 — Brain regions emerge"}},{"k":"prenatal_neuron_production","v":{"s":"Weeks 9-12 — Neuron production"}},{"k":"prenatal_cortical_migration","v":{"s":"Weeks 13-20 — Migration builds cortex"}},{"k":"prenatal_connections","v":{"s":"Weeks 21-27 — Connections organize"}},{"k":"prenatal_folding_networks","v":{"s":"Weeks 28-40 — Folding and networks"}},{"k":"stim_motor_map","v":{"s":"Motor Response Zone"}},{"k":"stim_sensory_map","v":{"s":"Sensory Response Zone"}},{"k":"stim_visual_map","v":{"s":"Visual Flash Zone"}},{"k":"stim_auditory_map","v":{"s":"Auditory Sound Zone"}},{"k":"stim_language_map","v":{"s":"Language Disruption Zone"}},{"k":"stim_memory_emotion_map","v":{"s":"Memory and Emotion Zone"}},{"k":"stim_autonomic_quiet_map","v":{"s":"Autonomic or Quiet Zone"}},{"k":"sleep_architecture_decoder","v":{"s":"Sleep Architecture Decoder"}},{"k":"split_brain_fixation_task","v":{"s":"Split-Brain Fixation Task"}},{"k":"eeg_resting_readout","v":{"s":"Resting readout"}},{"k":"eeg_resting_dominant","v":{"s":"Alpha leads; beta stays modest"}},{"k":"eeg_resting_pattern","v":{"s":"Relaxed wakefulness often shows a posterior alpha rhythm that fades with eye opening or effort."}},{"k":"eeg_resting_caution","v":{"s":"Asymmetry or absent reactivity matters more than one pretty alpha trace."}},{"k":"eeg_sleeping_readout","v":{"s":"Sleep readout"}},{"k":"eeg_sleeping_dominant","v":{"s":"Delta/theta rise; beta quiets"}},{"k":"eeg_sleeping_pattern","v":{"s":"Deep sleep shifts toward high-amplitude slow activity, while lighter sleep adds theta and stage-specific features."}},{"k":"eeg_sleeping_caution","v":{"s":"Delta is healthy in N3 sleep but concerning if it dominates an awake adult."}},{"k":"eeg_studying_readout","v":{"s":"Studying readout"}},{"k":"eeg_studying_dominant","v":{"s":"Beta/gamma increase with focus"}},{"k":"eeg_studying_pattern","v":{"s":"Sustained attention tends to raise faster, lower-amplitude activity while alpha becomes less dominant."}},{"k":"eeg_studying_caution","v":{"s":"More fast activity is not automatically better; stress, caffeine, and artifact can look similar."}},{"k":"eeg_exercise_readout","v":{"s":"Exercise readout"}},{"k":"eeg_exercise_dominant","v":{"s":"Arousal rises; artifact risk rises"}},{"k":"eeg_exercise_pattern","v":{"s":"Movement and alertness can increase beta-like activity, while muscle artifact can contaminate high-frequency channels."}},{"k":"eeg_exercise_caution","v":{"s":"Always separate brain rhythm from muscle, blink, and movement artifact."}},{"k":"eeg_meditating_readout","v":{"s":"Meditating readout"}},{"k":"eeg_meditating_dominant","v":{"s":"Alpha/theta settle into synchrony"}},{"k":"eeg_meditating_pattern","v":{"s":"Relaxed attention can strengthen alpha and theta while reducing high-arousal beta dominance."}},{"k":"eeg_meditating_caution","v":{"s":"A calm-looking rhythm is a state clue, not a diagnosis or a personality readout."}},{"k":"eeg_activity_mode_set","v":{"s":"EEG activity mode: "}},{"k":"diagram_in_view","v":{"s":"Brain Atlas diagram is now in view."}},{"k":"region_directory_in_view","v":{"s":"Brain Atlas region directory is now in view."}},{"k":"region_detail_in_view","v":{"s":"Selected region detail is now in view."}},{"k":"diagram_zoom_set","v":{"s":"Diagram zoom set to"}},{"k":"fullscreen_view_changed","v":{"s":"Fullscreen diagram"}},{"k":"now_studying_prenatal_stage","v":{"s":"Now studying one prenatal milestone and the overlapping processes around it."}},{"k":"group_clinical","v":{"s":"Clinical"}},{"k":"group_clinical_note","v":{"s":"Stroke territories, cerebellar signs, brainstem tracts, CSF flow, and case clues"}},{"k":"group_development","v":{"s":"Development"}},{"k":"group_development_note","v":{"s":"Brain growth before birth"}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_prenatal_selected","v":{"s":"Ask students to connect this milestone to the stages immediately before and after it, then name one process that overlaps both."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_prenatal","v":{"s":"Ask students to move the week slider, compare two milestones, and explain why brain development is a continuous overlapping process."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_homunculus","v":{"s":"Ask students to choose one body part, then predict whether a nearby lesion would change movement, sensation, or both."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_visual_pathway","v":{"s":"Ask students to cover one pathway segment and predict the exact visual-field cut before revealing the card."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_language_network","v":{"s":"Ask students to trace one spoken or written word from input to comprehension to speech output, then predict the aphasia clue."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_basal_ganglia","v":{"s":"Ask students to trace the GO and NO-GO loops, then predict how dopamine loss changes movement."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_limbic_papez","v":{"s":"Ask students to trace one memory signal around Papez circuit, then explain how amygdala output changes the body."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_cranial_willis","v":{"s":"Ask students to pick one nerve or vessel, then name the bedside clue that would localize a lesion there."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_stroke_territories","v":{"s":"Give one stroke clue, then ask students to localize the likely vascular territory before checking the case cards."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_cerebellum_clinic","v":{"s":"Ask students whether the sign is midline, limb, vestibular, or artery-pattern before naming the cerebellar target."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_brainstem_cross","v":{"s":"Ask students to identify the ipsilateral cranial nerve sign first, then pair it with the crossed body tract finding."}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_csf_hydro","v":{"s":"Ask students to trace one CSF drop from production to reabsorption, then predict which ventricles enlarge when a blockage occurs."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_prenatal","v":{"s":"Prenatal brain-development timeline from gestational week 3 to 40, showing neural-tube closure, early brain regions, neuron production, cortical migration, long-range wiring, folding, and growing networks. Timings are approximate and overlap."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_homunculus","v":{"s":"Motor and sensory homunculus view showing primary motor cortex, primary somatosensory cortex, the central sulcus, and enlarged hand, face, and tongue representations."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_visual_pathway","v":{"s":"Visual pathway view showing retina, optic nerve, optic chiasm, optic tract, LGN, optic radiations, V1, a field-cut decoder, and classic patterns such as bitemporal hemianopia, homonymous hemianopia, quadrantanopia, and macular sparing."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_language_network","v":{"s":"Language network view showing auditory and visual word input, Wernicke comprehension, angular and supramarginal integration, arcuate fasciculus repetition, Broca speech planning, motor speech output, and classic aphasia clues."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_basal_ganglia","v":{"s":"Basal ganglia loop view showing direct GO and indirect NO-GO pathways, GPi/SNr thalamic braking, STN excitation, dopamine modulation from substantia nigra pars compacta, and a movement-disorder decoder for Parkinson disease, Huntington chorea, STN hemiballismus, and dyskinesia."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_limbic_papez","v":{"s":"Limbic and Papez loop view showing hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, mammillothalamic tract, anterior thalamus, cingulate, cingulum, entorhinal cortex, amygdala, hypothalamus, prefrontal regulation, and a memory-emotion decoder for new amnesia, confabulation, fear tagging, and poor top-down regulation."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_cranial_willis","v":{"s":"Cranial nerves and Circle of Willis view showing inferior brain anatomy, CN I, II, III, V, VI, VII/VIII, IX/X/XI, XII, AComm/ACA, PComm/ICA, basilar/vertebral arteries, and a bedside clue decoder for pupil-involving CN III palsy, bitemporal field loss, bulbar symptoms, and thunderclap headache."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_stroke_territories","v":{"s":"Stroke territories view showing ACA, MCA superior, MCA inferior, PCA, deep lacunar, watershed, and posterior circulation maps with case cards for leg-predominant weakness, Broca aphasia with face-arm weakness, fluent aphasia or neglect, and homonymous visual-field loss."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_cerebellum_clinic","v":{"s":"Cerebellum clinic view showing vermis, cerebellar hemispheres, flocculonodular vestibular zone, deep nuclei, PICA, AICA, SCA, and case cards for wide gait, finger overshoot, vertigo with nystagmus, and hoarse ataxia."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_brainstem_cross","v":{"s":"Brainstem cross-section view showing midbrain, pons, medulla, corticospinal tract, medial lemniscus, spinothalamic tract, cranial nerve nuclei, and a crossed-findings decoder for localizing ipsilateral cranial nerve signs with contralateral body deficits."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_csf_hydro","v":{"s":"CSF flow and hydrocephalus view showing choroid plexus production, lateral ventricles, third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, arachnoid granulation reabsorption, and a hydrocephalus decoder for obstructive, communicating, normal-pressure, and raised-pressure patterns."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_eeg_active","v":{"s":"EEG rhythm view comparing delta, theta, alpha, beta, and gamma waves across different brain states. Active state: "}},{"k":"route_badge_base","v":{"s":"Base"}},{"k":"route_cranial_willis_title","v":{"s":"Map nerves and vessels"}},{"k":"route_cranial_willis_copy","v":{"s":"Use the underside map to connect nerve exits, blood supply, and classic lesion clues."}},{"k":"route_badge_stroke","v":{"s":"Stroke"}},{"k":"route_stroke_title","v":{"s":"Localize a stroke"}},{"k":"route_stroke_copy","v":{"s":"Match deficit patterns to ACA, MCA, PCA, lacunar, watershed, and posterior circulation territories."}},{"k":"route_badge_cerebellum","v":{"s":"Cerebellum"}},{"k":"route_cerebellum_title","v":{"s":"Read ataxia clues"}},{"k":"route_cerebellum_copy","v":{"s":"Separate vermis, limb hemispheres, vestibular signs, and PICA/AICA/SCA patterns."}},{"k":"route_badge_brainstem","v":{"s":"Brainstem"}},{"k":"route_brainstem_title","v":{"s":"Decode crossed findings"}},{"k":"route_brainstem_copy","v":{"s":"Pair ipsilateral cranial nerve signs with contralateral body tract findings."}},{"k":"route_badge_csf","v":{"s":"CSF"}},{"k":"route_csf_title","v":{"s":"Trace CSF flow"}},{"k":"route_csf_copy","v":{"s":"Follow ventricular flow, aqueduct bottlenecks, reabsorption, and hydrocephalus patterns."}},{"k":"route_badge_map","v":{"s":"Map"}},{"k":"route_homunculus_title","v":{"s":"Map the body"}},{"k":"route_homunculus_copy","v":{"s":"Compare motor output and sensory input across the cortical homunculus."}},{"k":"route_badge_vision","v":{"s":"Vision"}},{"k":"route_visual_pathway_title","v":{"s":"Trace vision"}},{"k":"route_visual_pathway_copy","v":{"s":"Follow retinal fibers through the chiasm and match each lesion to its field cut."}},{"k":"route_badge_language","v":{"s":"Language"}},{"k":"route_language_network_title","v":{"s":"Map language"}},{"k":"route_language_network_copy","v":{"s":"Trace hearing, reading, comprehension, repetition, and speech output across the dominant-hemisphere network."}},{"k":"route_badge_movement","v":{"s":"Movement"}},{"k":"route_basal_ganglia_title","v":{"s":"Balance GO and NO-GO"}},{"k":"route_basal_ganglia_copy","v":{"s":"Trace direct, indirect, and dopamine pathways that select movement."}},{"k":"route_badge_memory","v":{"s":"Memory"}},{"k":"route_limbic_papez_title","v":{"s":"Link memory and emotion"}},{"k":"route_limbic_papez_copy","v":{"s":"Trace Papez circuit and compare it with amygdala-driven body responses."}},{"k":"route_badge_development","v":{"s":"Development"}},{"k":"route_prenatal_title","v":{"s":"Grow a brain before birth"}},{"k":"route_prenatal_copy","v":{"s":"Slide from neural-tube closure through neuron migration, cortical folding, and expanding networks."}},{"k":"stem_learning_lab","v":{"s":"STEM learning lab"}},{"k":"view_large_diagram","v":{"s":"View the large Brain Atlas diagram"}},{"k":"view_diagram","v":{"s":"View diagram"}},{"k":"section_navigation","v":{"s":"Brain Atlas section navigation"}},{"k":"jump_to","v":{"s":"Jump to"}},{"k":"diagram","v":{"s":"Diagram"}},{"k":"regions","v":{"s":"Regions"}},{"k":"view_selected_region_detail","v":{"s":"View selected region detail for"}},{"k":"detail","v":{"s":"Detail"}},{"k":"expand_overview","v":{"s":"Expand overview"}},{"k":"collapse_overview","v":{"s":"Collapse overview"}},{"k":"hide_medication_guide","v":{"s":"Hide medication guide"}},{"k":"medication_guide","v":{"s":"Medication guide"}},{"k":"hide_treatment_guide","v":{"s":"Hide treatment guide"}},{"k":"treatment_guide","v":{"s":"Treatment guide"}},{"k":"guided_exploration","v":{"s":"Guided exploration"}},{"k":"learning_paths","v":{"s":"Learning paths"}},{"k":"learning_paths_note","v":{"s":"Choose a path or scroll sideways to preview every diagram."}},{"k":"paths","v":{"s":"paths"}},{"k":"available_learning_paths","v":{"s":"Available learning paths"}},{"k":"open_diagram","v":{"s":"Open diagram"}},{"k":"neurotransmitter_levels_chart","v":{"s":"Neurotransmitter levels chart"}},{"k":"explanation_label","v":{"s":"Explain your prediction"}},{"k":"diagram_library","v":{"s":"Diagram library"}},{"k":"views_count","v":{"s":"views"}},{"k":"homunculus_motor_sensory_body_map","v":{"s":"Homunculus — motor/sensory body map"}},{"k":"homunculus_map_hint","v":{"s":"A Penfield-style map of the precentral motor strip and postcentral sensory strip. Hands, lips, face, and tongue look oversized because they get more cortical real estate."}},{"k":"visual_pathway_field_cuts","v":{"s":"Visual pathway — field cuts"}},{"k":"visual_pathway_hint","v":{"s":"Follow light from retina to optic nerve, chiasm, tract, LGN, radiations, and V1. The mini cards show why each lesion creates a different visual-field pattern."}},{"k":"language_network_broca_wernicke","v":{"s":"Language network - Broca/Wernicke"}},{"k":"language_network_hint","v":{"s":"A dominant-hemisphere map linking auditory and visual word input to Wernicke comprehension, arcuate fasciculus repetition, Broca speech planning, and motor speech output."}},{"k":"prenatal_timeline_title","v":{"s":"Prenatal brain development - week by week"}},{"k":"prenatal_timeline_hint","v":{"s":"Use the gestational-week slider to connect neural-tube closure, early brain regions, neuron production, cortical migration, long-range wiring, and third-trimester folding. Timings are approximate and processes overlap."}},{"k":"synapse_development_lifespan_map","v":{"s":"Synapse & development - lifespan map"}},{"k":"synapse_development_lifespan_hint","v":{"s":"Compare early synapse overproduction, sensory pruning, later prefrontal pruning, myelination, lifelong plasticity, and adolescent remodeling on one uncluttered development map."}},{"k":"stimulation_lab_penfield_map","v":{"s":"Stimulation Lab - Penfield response map"}},{"k":"stimulation_lab_penfield_hint","v":{"s":"Use the pulsing electrode target to predict whether stimulation should cause movement, tingling, flashes, sounds, language disruption, memory-emotion responses, body-state changes, or no obvious report."}},{"k":"stroke_territories_clinical_map","v":{"s":"Stroke territories - clinical map"}},{"k":"stroke_territories_hint","v":{"s":"Match the deficit pattern to the vascular map: leg greater than arm for ACA, face/arm plus language or neglect for MCA, visual-field loss for PCA, pure motor or sensory signs for lacunar stroke, and crossed or vestibular clues for posterior circulation."}},{"k":"cerebellum_clinic_map","v":{"s":"Cerebellum clinic - ataxia map"}},{"k":"cerebellum_clinic_hint","v":{"s":"Use same-side coordination signs to separate vermis gait instability, limb hemisphere dysmetria, vestibular nystagmus, and PICA/AICA/SCA artery patterns."}},{"k":"brainstem_cross_findings_map","v":{"s":"Brainstem - crossed findings"}},{"k":"brainstem_cross_hint","v":{"s":"Localize the brainstem by pairing ipsilateral cranial nerve signs with contralateral body tract findings: CN III for midbrain, CN V-VIII for pons, and bulbar/tongue signs for medulla."}},{"k":"csf_flow_hydrocephalus_map","v":{"s":"CSF flow - hydrocephalus map"}},{"k":"csf_flow_hydrocephalus_hint","v":{"s":"Follow CSF from choroid plexus through the lateral ventricles, third ventricle, aqueduct, fourth ventricle, subarachnoid space, and arachnoid granulations. The decoder separates obstructive, communicating, normal-pressure, and raised-pressure patterns."}},{"k":"cranial_willis_base_map","v":{"s":"Cranial nerves & Willis - base map"}},{"k":"cranial_willis_hint","v":{"s":"An underside view that separates cranial nerve exits from the arterial ring. It highlights CN III near PComm, lower cranial nerves at the medulla, and basilar/vertebral brainstem supply."}},{"k":"basal_ganglia_loop_go_no_go","v":{"s":"Basal ganglia loop - GO / NO-GO"}},{"k":"basal_ganglia_loop_hint","v":{"s":"Direct pathway releases selected movement; indirect pathway suppresses competing movement. Dopamine nudges D1 up and D2 down, so loss of dopamine makes movement harder to start."}},{"k":"limbic_papez_memory_emotion","v":{"s":"Limbic / Papez loop - memory + emotion"}},{"k":"limbic_papez_hint","v":{"s":"Papez circuit links hippocampus, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, cingulate, and entorhinal cortex for memory context. Amygdala output tags memories with emotion and drives hypothalamic body responses."}},{"k":"educational_reference","v":{"s":"Educational reference"}},{"k":"psychopharmacology_guide","v":{"s":"Psychopharmacology guide"}},{"k":"psychopharmacology_guide_intro","v":{"s":"Compare major medication classes, connect mechanisms to brain systems, and identify the kinds of monitoring or warnings that make each class clinically distinct."}},{"k":"close_psychopharmacology_guide","v":{"s":"Close psychopharmacology guide"}},{"k":"close","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"medication_safety_boundary","v":{"s":"Medication safety boundary"}},{"k":"medication_safety_boundary_note","v":{"s":"This is a learning aid, not a prescribing, dosing, combination, or taper guide. Do not start, stop, share, or change a prescribed medicine without a licensed clinician. Product-specific warnings and interactions can change; check the current FDA Medication Guide or label."}},{"k":"choose_learning_depth","v":{"s":"Choose learning depth"}},{"k":"full_reference_help","v":{"s":"Full reference adds mechanisms and medication examples to the key uses, brain systems, and safety context."}},{"k":"quick_scan_help","v":{"s":"Quick scan keeps the key uses, brain systems, and safety context visible with less text."}},{"k":"psychopharmacology_learning_depth","v":{"s":"Psychopharmacology learning depth"}},{"k":"quick_scan","v":{"s":"Quick scan"}},{"k":"full_reference","v":{"s":"Full reference"}},{"k":"search_medication_classes","v":{"s":"Search medication classes"}},{"k":"search_by_class_mechanism_or_warning","v":{"s":"Search class, mechanism, brain system, or warning..."}},{"k":"clear_medication_search","v":{"s":"Clear medication search"}},{"k":"no_medication_classes_match","v":{"s":"No medication classes match this search. Try a mechanism such as dopamine, GABA, serotonin, or norepinephrine."}},{"k":"psychopharm_sources_note","v":{"s":"Class summaries are educational overviews. Individual medicines differ; use current product labeling for decisions."}},{"k":"authoritative_medication_sources","v":{"s":"Authoritative medication sources"}},{"k":"treatment_reference","v":{"s":"Mechanism-first treatment reference"}},{"k":"brain_stimulation_treatments","v":{"s":"Brain stimulation & somatic treatments"}},{"k":"brain_stimulation_intro","v":{"s":"Compare how magnetic, electrical, nerve-based, implanted, and acoustic approaches interact with brain circuits. Targets, evidence, and regulatory status vary by device and condition."}},{"k":"close_treatment_guide","v":{"s":"Close treatment guide"}},{"k":"treatment_safety_boundary","v":{"s":"Treatment safety boundary"}},{"k":"treatment_safety_boundary_note","v":{"s":"This is a learning aid, not treatment selection, device setup, or protocol guidance. These procedures require trained clinical teams and individualized screening. Do not use or purchase a stimulation device based on this guide; confirm current device labeling and status with the FDA and a qualified clinician."}},{"k":"tms_term_note_title","v":{"s":"About “TCMS” terminology: "}},{"k":"tms_term_note","v":{"s":"Current brain-treatment sources generally use TMS, rTMS, deep TMS, or TBS. “TcMS” is ambiguous and can also refer to transcutaneous magnetic stimulation outside the brain, so this guide uses the more specific current terms."}},{"k":"stim_full_reference_help","v":{"s":"Full reference adds circuit targets and clinical status while keeping mechanism and safety visible."}},{"k":"stim_quick_scan_help","v":{"s":"Quick scan focuses on energy delivery, circuit effects, and safety with less text."}},{"k":"treatment_learning_depth","v":{"s":"Treatment guide learning depth"}},{"k":"explore_by_approach","v":{"s":"Explore by approach"}},{"k":"explore_by_approach_help","v":{"s":"Choose one delivery family. Search can narrow the selected family further."}},{"k":"treatment_family_filter","v":{"s":"Filter treatments by approach"}},{"k":"search_treatments","v":{"s":"Search treatments"}},{"k":"search_treatments_placeholder","v":{"s":"Search modality, energy, circuit, status, or safety..."}},{"k":"clear_treatment_search","v":{"s":"Clear treatment search"}},{"k":"build_treatment_comparison","v":{"s":"Build a treatment comparison"}},{"k":"treatments_selected_for_comparison","v":{"s":"Treatments selected for comparison"}},{"k":"view_treatment_comparison","v":{"s":"View comparison"}},{"k":"quick_comparison_paths","v":{"s":"Quick comparison paths"}},{"k":"quick_comparison_help","v":{"s":"Start with a common learning contrast, then inspect the same evidence and safety fields side by side."}},{"k":"treatment_modalities","v":{"s":"Brain stimulation and somatic treatment modalities"}},{"k":"no_treatments_match_filters","v":{"s":"No treatment modalities match this approach and search. Try another approach or clear the filters."}},{"k":"clear_treatment_filters","v":{"s":"Clear treatment filters"}},{"k":"compare_treatments","v":{"s":"Compare treatment modalities"}},{"k":"clear_treatment_comparison","v":{"s":"Clear comparison"}},{"k":"pathway_comparison","v":{"s":"How each route reaches a network"}},{"k":"pathway_comparison_help","v":{"s":"A conceptual learning route—not device placement or treatment-protocol guidance."}},{"k":"treatment_comparison_matrix","v":{"s":"Treatment modality comparison matrix"}},{"k":"learning_field","v":{"s":"Learning field"}},{"k":"treatment_sources_note","v":{"s":"Modality summaries are educational overviews. Device design, indication, evidence, and regulatory status can differ; verify current labeling."}},{"k":"authoritative_treatment_sources","v":{"s":"Authoritative treatment sources"}},{"k":"find_a_milestone","v":{"s":"Find a milestone"}},{"k":"find_a_region","v":{"s":"Find a region"}},{"k":"search_prenatal_milestones","v":{"s":"Search prenatal milestones..."}},{"k":"search_prenatal_development","v":{"s":"Search prenatal development milestones"}},{"k":"clear_region_search","v":{"s":"Clear region search"}},{"k":"clear_search","v":{"s":"Clear search"}},{"k":"eeg_activity_mode","v":{"s":"EEG activity mode"}},{"k":"eeg_state_readout","v":{"s":"EEG state readout"}},{"k":"eeg_interpretation_caution","v":{"s":"Interpretation caution"}},{"k":"interactive_diagram","v":{"s":"Interactive diagram"}},{"k":"view_position","v":{"s":"View"}},{"k":"diagram_zoom_controls","v":{"s":"Diagram zoom controls"}},{"k":"zoom_out_diagram","v":{"s":"Zoom out diagram"}},{"k":"reset_diagram_zoom","v":{"s":"Reset diagram zoom"}},{"k":"zoom_in_diagram","v":{"s":"Zoom in diagram"}},{"k":"fullscreen_diagram_navigation","v":{"s":"Fullscreen diagram navigation"}},{"k":"previous_diagram","v":{"s":"Previous diagram"}},{"k":"next_diagram","v":{"s":"Next diagram"}},{"k":"toggle_fullscreen_diagram","v":{"s":"Toggle full screen for the brain atlas diagram"}},{"k":"full_screen","v":{"s":"Full screen"}},{"k":"exit_full_screen","v":{"s":"Exit full screen"}},{"k":"diagram_label_controls","v":{"s":"Diagram label controls"}},{"k":"diagram_label_controls_help","v":{"s":"Choose how many anatomy callouts appear and scale text across every diagram."}},{"k":"diagram_label_auto_help","v":{"s":"Label spacing is protected for this specialty diagram. You can still enlarge its text."}},{"k":"label_density","v":{"s":"Density"}},{"k":"automatic_label_layout_active","v":{"s":"Automatic label layout is active"}},{"k":"auto_layout","v":{"s":"Auto layout"}},{"k":"protected_spacing","v":{"s":"Protected spacing"}},{"k":"label_size","v":{"s":"Text size"}},{"k":"diagram_status","v":{"s":"Diagram status"}},{"k":"view_group","v":{"s":"View:"}},{"k":"focus_region","v":{"s":"Focus:"}},{"k":"diagram_enlarged_scroll_hint","v":{"s":"Diagram enlarged - scroll to explore every label."}},{"k":"quiz_status_hint","v":{"s":"Quiz is active - use the diagram and evidence to decide."}},{"k":"canvas_interaction_hint","v":{"s":"Select a label on the diagram or choose a region below."}},{"k":"prenatal_timeline_controls","v":{"s":"Prenatal development timeline controls"}},{"k":"gestational_week","v":{"s":"Gestational week"}},{"k":"prenatal_milestones","v":{"s":"Prenatal development milestones"}},{"k":"back_to_region_directory","v":{"s":"Back to region directory"}},{"k":"region_list","v":{"s":"Region list"}},{"k":"quick_milestone","v":{"s":"Milestone"}},{"k":"quick_also_happening","v":{"s":"Also happening"}},{"k":"quick_continues_next","v":{"s":"Continues next"}},{"k":"quick_function","v":{"s":"Function"}},{"k":"quick_watch_for","v":{"s":"Watch for"}},{"k":"quick_if_damaged","v":{"s":"If damaged"}},{"k":"damage_depends_on_circuit","v":{"s":"Effects depend on the connected circuit and task being tested."}},{"k":"what_is_forming","v":{"s":"What is forming"}},{"k":"also_happening","v":{"s":"Also happening"}},{"k":"development_continues","v":{"s":"Development continues"}},{"k":"explore_the_diagram","v":{"s":"Explore the diagram"}},{"k":"available_regions","v":{"s":"Available regions"}},{"k":"try_a_broader_search","v":{"s":"Try a broader term, search by function, or reset the directory."}},{"k":"open_region_details","v":{"s":"Open region details for"}},{"k":"neuromyths_view","v":{"s":"🧠 Neuromyths & Neurodiversity"}},{"k":"neuromyths_view_desc","v":{"s":"Popular brain claims checked against the evidence: what is debunked, what is oversimplified, what is real but small, and what is promising but not proven"}},{"k":"myth_learning_styles","v":{"s":"Learning styles (VAK matching)"}},{"k":"myth_left_right_brain","v":{"s":"Left-brained vs right-brained people"}},{"k":"myth_ten_percent","v":{"s":"We only use 10% of our brain"}},{"k":"myth_first_three_years","v":{"s":"The window closes at age 3"}},{"k":"myth_brain_training","v":{"s":"Brain-training apps raise intelligence"}},{"k":"myth_adhd_brain","v":{"s":"ADHD brain differences"}},{"k":"myth_autism_brain","v":{"s":"The \"autistic brain\""}},{"k":"myth_retina_screening","v":{"s":"An eye photo can diagnose ADHD"}},{"k":"group_evidence","v":{"s":"Evidence"}},{"k":"group_evidence_note","v":{"s":"Neuromyths, neurodiversity, and what the research actually supports"}},{"k":"teacher_prompt_neuromyths","v":{"s":"Ask students to pick one claim they have heard, find the evidence card, and explain in one sentence why belief and evidence can disagree."}},{"k":"canvas_summary_neuromyths","v":{"s":"Neuromyths view showing a bar chart of how many surveyed teachers endorsed learning styles, hemispheric dominance, the ten-percent myth, and brain-training claims, next to verdict chips for eight claims: debunked, oversimplified, real but small, or promising but not proven."}},{"k":"quick_the_claim","v":{"s":"The claim"}},{"k":"quick_the_evidence","v":{"s":"The evidence"}},{"k":"quick_instead","v":{"s":"Instead"}},{"k":"what_the_evidence_says","v":{"s":"What the evidence says"}},{"k":"evidence_verdict","v":{"s":"Evidence verdict"}},{"k":"the_claim","v":{"s":"The claim"}},{"k":"instead_try","v":{"s":"Instead, try"}},{"k":"source","v":{"s":"Source"}},{"k":"verdict_debunked","v":{"s":"Debunked"}},{"k":"verdict_debunked_meaning","v":{"s":"Tested directly and not supported. Still widely taught, which is why it is worth naming."}},{"k":"verdict_oversimplified","v":{"s":"Oversimplified"}},{"k":"verdict_oversimplified_meaning","v":{"s":"Built on a real finding, then stretched far past what the evidence shows."}},{"k":"verdict_real_small","v":{"s":"Real, but small and not diagnostic"}},{"k":"verdict_real_small_meaning","v":{"s":"Group-level differences replicate, but they are small, overlap almost completely between groups, and cannot classify an individual."}},{"k":"verdict_promising","v":{"s":"Promising, not proven"}},{"k":"verdict_promising_meaning","v":{"s":"An early result worth following, but not yet validated in new populations or real-world use."}},{"k":"neuromyths_panel_title","v":{"s":"The brain science, honestly"}},{"k":"neuromyths_panel_intro","v":{"s":"Surveys of teachers in several countries find that most endorse at least a few neuromyths, and the myths tend to spread through well-meant training rather than bad intent. Each card here pairs a popular claim with the evidence and a classroom alternative."}},{"k":"neuromyths_panel_note","v":{"s":"Nothing here is a diagnosis or a treatment claim. Where a condition is discussed, differences are described at the group level only, and support decisions belong with the student, family, and clinical team."}},{"k":"region_selected_generic","v":{"s":"Region selected. Details are below the diagram."}},{"k":"region_selected_suffix","v":{"s":"selected. Details are below the diagram."}},{"k":"headline_check_title","v":{"s":"Headline check"}},{"k":"headline_check_intro","v":{"s":"These headlines are invented for practice, but each one echoes a claim you will meet. Sort it into the verdict the evidence supports. This is practice, not a grade."}},{"k":"headline_check_choose","v":{"s":"Which verdict fits this headline?"}},{"k":"headline_fits","v":{"s":"Yes, that verdict fits."}},{"k":"headline_different","v":{"s":"The evidence points to a different verdict."}},{"k":"headline_verdict_is","v":{"s":"The evidence verdict is"}},{"k":"headline_open_card","v":{"s":"Open the card"}},{"k":"next_headline","v":{"s":"Next headline →"}},{"k":"view_opened","v":{"s":"view opened."}},{"k":"see_it_in_the_atlas","v":{"s":"See it in the atlas"}},{"k":"open_view_named","v":{"s":"Open"}},{"k":"honestly","v":{"s":"The claim you may have heard"}},{"k":"honestly_note","v":{"s":"Opens the evidence card: what the claim says, what the research supports, and what to do instead."}},{"k":"evidence_card_opened","v":{"s":"evidence card opened."}},{"k":"myth_chemical_imbalance","v":{"s":"Depression is a chemical imbalance"}},{"k":"share_this_card","v":{"s":"Share this card"}},{"k":"copy_card_text","v":{"s":"Copy the claim, the evidence and the source"}},{"k":"card_copied","v":{"s":"Card copied."}},{"k":"card_copy_failed","v":{"s":"Copy failed. Select the card text and press Control C."}},{"k":"copy_footer","v":{"s":"From the AlloFlow Brain Atlas, Neuromyths and Neurodiversity. Research findings described at the group level. Not a diagnosis and not medical advice."}},{"k":"plain_brocas_idea","v":{"s":"Contributes to putting words together and producing speech."}},{"k":"plain_brocas_example","v":{"s":"You turn an idea into a spoken sentence when you answer a question out loud."}},{"k":"plain_brocas_connection","v":{"s":"Producing words and understanding them are different contributions within one language network."}},{"k":"plain_brocas_question","v":{"s":"Why might someone understand a question and still find it hard to say the answer?"}},{"k":"plain_brocas_explanation","v":{"s":"Understanding words and producing them draw on different contributions. After damage near this area, speech can become effortful while understanding stays much better."}},{"k":"plain_wernickes_idea","v":{"s":"Contributes to understanding words that are heard or read."}},{"k":"plain_wernickes_example","v":{"s":"You follow what a friend is saying while they explain the rules of a game."}},{"k":"plain_wernickes_connection","v":{"s":"Areas that help with understanding and areas that help with speaking are linked by nerve-fiber pathways."}},{"k":"plain_wernickes_question","v":{"s":"How is understanding a sentence different from saying one?"}},{"k":"plain_wernickes_explanation","v":{"s":"Understanding draws on making sense of incoming words. Saying a sentence adds choosing and forming the words. Both draw on a wider network."}},{"k":"plain_supramarginal_idea","v":{"s":"Contributes to working with the sounds inside words, and to information from touch."}},{"k":"plain_supramarginal_example","v":{"s":"You repeat a new name back to someone right after hearing it."}},{"k":"plain_supramarginal_connection","v":{"s":"Repeating a word links hearing it to saying it along a shared pathway."}},{"k":"plain_supramarginal_question","v":{"s":"What has to happen between hearing a name and repeating it?"}},{"k":"plain_supramarginal_explanation","v":{"s":"The sounds are held briefly and then used to produce speech. Pathways connecting understanding and speaking areas contribute to this."}},{"k":"plain_angular_gyrus_idea","v":{"s":"Helps bring together information of different kinds, which supports reading."}},{"k":"plain_angular_gyrus_example","v":{"s":"You see a written word and connect it with its sound and its meaning."}},{"k":"plain_angular_gyrus_connection","v":{"s":"Reading combines seeing, sound, and meaning across several brain areas."}},{"k":"plain_angular_gyrus_question","v":{"s":"Why does reading need more than seeing the letters?"}},{"k":"plain_angular_gyrus_explanation","v":{"s":"Seeing letters provides visual information. Connecting that information with sounds and meaning draws on additional areas working together."}},{"k":"plain_insular_idea","v":{"s":"Contributes to noticing signals from inside your body, including taste."}},{"k":"plain_insular_example","v":{"s":"You notice your heart beating faster after running up a flight of stairs."}},{"k":"plain_insular_connection","v":{"s":"Noticing a body signal and deciding what it means involve several brain areas together."}},{"k":"plain_insular_question","v":{"s":"Why is noticing a body signal not the same as knowing what it means?"}},{"k":"plain_insular_explanation","v":{"s":"A fast heartbeat can follow exercise, excitement, or worry. Working out which one it is draws on the situation and on wider brain networks."}},{"k":"plain_insular_note","v":{"s":"This area is one contributor to noticing body signals and feelings. No single region produces a feeling on its own."}},{"k":"word_speech","v":{"s":"Speech"}},{"k":"word_speech_definition","v":{"s":"Spoken language. Producing speech involves choosing words and forming their sounds."}},{"k":"word_comprehension","v":{"s":"Comprehension"}},{"k":"word_comprehension_definition","v":{"s":"Understanding the meaning of words that are heard or read."}},{"k":"word_integration","v":{"s":"Bringing information together"}},{"k":"word_integration_definition","v":{"s":"Combining information of different kinds, such as what you see and what you hear, so it can be used together."}},{"k":"check_brocas_question","v":{"s":"A learner knows exactly what they want to say but the words come out slowly, one at a time. Which contribution does this example point to?"}},{"k":"check_brocas_choice_0","v":{"s":"Putting words together and producing speech"}},{"k":"check_brocas_choice_1","v":{"s":"Understanding what other people say"}},{"k":"check_brocas_choice_2","v":{"s":"Noticing the loudness of a voice"}},{"k":"check_brocas_feedback_0","v":{"s":"The difficulty in this example is producing the words, not understanding them. Producing speech draws on a wider language network as well."}},{"k":"check_brocas_feedback_1","v":{"s":"The learner already knows what they want to say, so understanding is not the difficulty described here."}},{"k":"check_brocas_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Loudness is one feature of sound. This example is about getting words out."}},{"k":"check_wernickes_question","v":{"s":"A learner listens to instructions in a language they know, but the meaning does not come through. Which contribution does this example point to?"}},{"k":"check_wernickes_choice_0","v":{"s":"Understanding the meaning of the words"}},{"k":"check_wernickes_choice_1","v":{"s":"Sending signals to move the mouth"}},{"k":"check_wernickes_choice_2","v":{"s":"Keeping balance while standing still"}},{"k":"check_wernickes_feedback_0","v":{"s":"The sounds arrive, but their meaning does not. Understanding language draws on this area within a wider network."}},{"k":"check_wernickes_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Moving the mouth helps produce speech. This example is about taking meaning from words that are heard."}},{"k":"check_wernickes_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Balance can matter while listening, but it does not explain the missing meaning."}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_question","v":{"s":"A learner hears a new word and repeats it back straight away. What has to happen in between?"}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_choice_0","v":{"s":"The sounds are held briefly and then used to speak"}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_choice_1","v":{"s":"The word is stored permanently before it can be said"}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_choice_2","v":{"s":"The word is turned into a picture first"}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Repeating links hearing to speaking. Pathways between the understanding and speaking areas contribute to this."}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_feedback_1","v":{"s":"A word can be repeated without being remembered later. Long-term memory is a different contribution."}},{"k":"check_supramarginal_feedback_2","v":{"s":"A picture can help with meaning, but repeating a sound does not require one."}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_question","v":{"s":"A learner reads a written word and knows both how it sounds and what it means. Which contribution is highlighted?"}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_choice_0","v":{"s":"Bringing different kinds of information together"}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_choice_1","v":{"s":"Only detecting the brightness of the page"}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_choice_2","v":{"s":"Keeping breathing going while reading"}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Reading connects what is seen with sound and meaning. Several areas contribute to that combination."}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Brightness is one visual feature. It does not explain connecting a word with its sound and meaning."}},{"k":"check_angular_gyrus_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Breathing continues during reading, but it does not explain the reading itself."}},{"k":"check_insular_question","v":{"s":"A learner notices their heart beating faster before a performance. Which contribution does this example point to?"}},{"k":"check_insular_choice_0","v":{"s":"Noticing a signal from inside the body"}},{"k":"check_insular_choice_1","v":{"s":"Producing the feeling entirely in one brain area"}},{"k":"check_insular_choice_2","v":{"s":"Sending the signal that moves the hands"}},{"k":"check_insular_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Noticing the heartbeat is a body signal. What the learner makes of it also depends on the situation and on wider networks."}},{"k":"check_insular_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Feelings involve many brain areas working together. No single area produces one on its own."}},{"k":"check_insular_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Hand movements need movement signals. This example is about noticing what the body is doing."}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_idea","v":{"s":"Contributes to noticing when something is not going as expected, so effort can be adjusted."}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_example","v":{"s":"You notice you have taken a wrong turn and slow down to check the map."}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_connection","v":{"s":"Noticing a problem and deciding what to do about it draw on connected areas working together."}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_question","v":{"s":"Why is noticing a mistake useful before you can fix it?"}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_explanation","v":{"s":"Noticing that something has gone off track is what prompts a change of approach. Choosing the new approach draws on planning areas as well."}},{"k":"plain_cingulate_note","v":{"s":"The front and back parts of this region take part in different things, and it is one contributor to each of them rather than the source."}},{"k":"plain_basal_ganglia_idea","v":{"s":"A group of structures deep in the brain that help adjust movements and support habits."}},{"k":"plain_basal_ganglia_example","v":{"s":"After a lot of practice, tying a shoelace takes much less deliberate thought."}},{"k":"plain_basal_ganglia_connection","v":{"s":"Adjusting a movement is one contribution; planning it and carrying it out involve other areas too."}},{"k":"plain_basal_ganglia_question","v":{"s":"Why can a practised action feel easier than a new one?"}},{"k":"plain_basal_ganglia_explanation","v":{"s":"With practice, an action can be produced with less step-by-step deliberation. Several brain systems change over that time, not one."}},{"k":"plain_fornix_idea","v":{"s":"A bundle of nerve fibers that carries information out of the hippocampus."}},{"k":"plain_fornix_example","v":{"s":"Information about a new experience travels along this route toward other memory structures."}},{"k":"plain_fornix_connection","v":{"s":"A bundle of fibers is a connection between areas rather than an area that processes on its own."}},{"k":"plain_fornix_question","v":{"s":"How is a fiber bundle different from a region that processes information?"}},{"k":"plain_fornix_explanation","v":{"s":"A bundle carries signals between places. The processing happens at the structures it connects."}},{"k":"plain_mammillary_idea","v":{"s":"A pair of small structures that form one stop on a memory circuit."}},{"k":"plain_mammillary_example","v":{"s":"Information from the hippocampus passes through here on its way toward the thalamus."}},{"k":"plain_mammillary_connection","v":{"s":"Memory for events depends on a loop of connected structures rather than on one place."}},{"k":"plain_mammillary_question","v":{"s":"Why can damage at more than one place cause similar memory problems?"}},{"k":"plain_mammillary_explanation","v":{"s":"These structures form a connected circuit. A break at different points along it can interrupt the same flow of information."}},{"k":"plain_septum_pell_idea","v":{"s":"A thin membrane between the two large fluid-filled spaces in the middle of the brain."}},{"k":"plain_septum_pell_example","v":{"s":"On a scan it appears as a thin line separating the two lateral ventricles."}},{"k":"plain_septum_pell_connection","v":{"s":"A labeled part is not always an area of nerve cells; some are membranes and some are spaces."}},{"k":"plain_septum_pell_question","v":{"s":"Why is it useful to know whether a labeled part is tissue, a membrane, or a space?"}},{"k":"plain_septum_pell_explanation","v":{"s":"Different kinds of structure do different things. Naming which kind it is keeps a divider or a fluid space from being read as a processing area."}},{"k":"plain_pineal_brain_idea","v":{"s":"A small gland that releases melatonin, a signal that follows the daily cycle of light and dark."}},{"k":"plain_pineal_brain_example","v":{"s":"As evening light fades, melatonin release rises, which is one of the signals linked with sleep timing."}},{"k":"plain_pineal_brain_connection","v":{"s":"Sleep timing depends on light, routine and other body signals together, not on one gland alone."}},{"k":"plain_pineal_brain_question","v":{"s":"Why is melatonin better described as a timing signal than as a sleep switch?"}},{"k":"plain_pineal_brain_explanation","v":{"s":"Melatonin rises with darkness and forms part of the body timing information. Falling asleep also depends on how tired you are, on routine, and on the surroundings."}},{"k":"word_habit","v":{"s":"Habit"}},{"k":"word_habit_definition","v":{"s":"An action that becomes easier and needs less step-by-step thought after repeated practice."}},{"k":"word_tract","v":{"s":"Nerve-fiber bundle"}},{"k":"word_tract_definition","v":{"s":"A group of nerve fibers that carries signals between brain areas. The processing happens at the areas it connects."}},{"k":"word_membrane","v":{"s":"Membrane"}},{"k":"word_membrane_definition","v":{"s":"A thin sheet of tissue. Here, one that separates two spaces rather than processing signals."}},{"k":"word_gland","v":{"s":"Gland"}},{"k":"word_gland_definition","v":{"s":"A structure that releases a substance. An endocrine gland releases hormones into the blood."}},{"k":"check_cingulate_question","v":{"s":"Halfway through a route a learner realises the directions no longer match the street signs, and slows down. Which contribution does this example point to?"}},{"k":"check_cingulate_choice_0","v":{"s":"Noticing that something is not going as expected"}},{"k":"check_cingulate_choice_1","v":{"s":"Storing the street names for next week"}},{"k":"check_cingulate_choice_2","v":{"s":"Sending the signals that move the legs"}},{"k":"check_cingulate_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Noticing the mismatch is what prompts the change of pace. Choosing what to do next draws on planning areas as well."}},{"k":"check_cingulate_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Remembering the names later is a different contribution. The example is about noticing a mismatch now."}},{"k":"check_cingulate_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Movement signals keep the learner walking. They do not explain noticing that the route is wrong."}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_question","v":{"s":"A learner ties a shoelace without thinking through each step, though it took full attention a year ago. Which contribution does this example point to?"}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_choice_0","v":{"s":"Supporting an action that practice has made easier"}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_choice_1","v":{"s":"Seeing the color of the lace"}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_choice_2","v":{"s":"Understanding the word shoelace"}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Practice changes how much deliberate thought the action needs. Several brain systems change over that time, not one."}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Color is visual information. It does not explain why the action now needs less thought."}},{"k":"check_basal_ganglia_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Understanding the word is a language contribution. The example is about the practised action itself."}},{"k":"check_fornix_question","v":{"s":"A learner sees this structure labeled as a bundle of nerve fibers. What does that tell them about it?"}},{"k":"check_fornix_choice_0","v":{"s":"It carries signals between structures rather than processing them itself"}},{"k":"check_fornix_choice_1","v":{"s":"It stores memories inside the bundle"}},{"k":"check_fornix_choice_2","v":{"s":"It is a fluid-filled space like a ventricle"}},{"k":"check_fornix_feedback_0","v":{"s":"A bundle is a route between places. The processing happens at the structures it connects."}},{"k":"check_fornix_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Memory involves connected structures. The bundle is the route between them, not a store."}},{"k":"check_fornix_feedback_2","v":{"s":"A ventricle is a space containing fluid. A fiber bundle is tissue that carries signals."}},{"k":"check_mammillary_question","v":{"s":"Damage at several different points along one memory circuit can cause similar difficulties. Why?"}},{"k":"check_mammillary_choice_0","v":{"s":"The structures are connected, so a break anywhere interrupts the same flow"}},{"k":"check_mammillary_choice_1","v":{"s":"Every structure does exactly the same job"}},{"k":"check_mammillary_choice_2","v":{"s":"Memory is stored in only one of them"}},{"k":"check_mammillary_feedback_0","v":{"s":"The circuit passes information from one structure to the next, so an interruption at different points can have a similar effect."}},{"k":"check_mammillary_feedback_1","v":{"s":"The structures contribute in different ways. What they share is being part of one connected route."}},{"k":"check_mammillary_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Memory for events draws on a connected circuit rather than a single store."}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_question","v":{"s":"A learner sees this thin membrane labeled between two ventricles. Which statement fits best?"}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_choice_0","v":{"s":"It separates two fluid-filled spaces rather than processing signals"}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_choice_1","v":{"s":"It is the area that plans movements"}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_choice_2","v":{"s":"It is a bundle of fibers linking the hemispheres"}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Naming what kind of structure it is keeps a divider from being read as a processing area."}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Planning draws on nerve-cell networks in the cerebrum. This label marks a thin membrane."}},{"k":"check_septum_pell_feedback_2","v":{"s":"That describes the corpus callosum. This membrane separates spaces instead of carrying signals across."}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_question","v":{"s":"Melatonin release rises as evening light fades. Which description fits that best?"}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_choice_0","v":{"s":"A timing signal that follows the light and dark cycle"}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_choice_1","v":{"s":"A switch that decides on its own when sleep begins"}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_choice_2","v":{"s":"A signal that carries visual detail to the cortex"}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_feedback_0","v":{"s":"Melatonin forms part of the body timing information. Falling asleep also depends on tiredness, routine and surroundings."}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_feedback_1","v":{"s":"Sleep timing depends on several things together. One signal does not decide it alone."}},{"k":"check_pineal_brain_feedback_2","v":{"s":"Visual detail travels along the visual pathway. Melatonin is a hormone released into the blood."}},{"k":"revisit_announced","v":{"s":"Card opened with its check ready to try again."}},{"k":"views_opened","v":{"s":"Views opened"}},{"k":"views_opened_note","v":{"s":"opened, which is not the same as practised"}},{"k":"checks_answered","v":{"s":"Checks answered"}},{"k":"checks_to_revisit","v":{"s":"to look at again"}},{"k":"checks_answered_note","v":{"s":"understanding checks on region cards"}},{"k":"look_again_at","v":{"s":"Look again at"}},{"k":"quiz_answers","v":{"s":"Quiz answers"}},{"k":"damage_pattern_practice_note","v":{"s":"damage-pattern practice, not a grade"}},{"k":"saved_quiz_started","v":{"s":"Saved structure round started. Find the structure that"}},{"k":"saved_quiz_name_is","v":{"s":"The structure is"}},{"k":"saved_quiz_next_clue","v":{"s":"Next structure. Find the one that"}},{"k":"saved_quiz_hinted_note","v":{"s":"of these were answered after the name was shown, so those were locating practice rather than recall. This is a record of the round, not a score."}},{"k":"saved_quiz_no_hint_note","v":{"s":"Answered from the clue alone. This is a record of the round, not a score."}},{"k":"saved_quiz_goal","v":{"s":"Practice goal: connect what a structure does to where it sits. Showing the name turns the round into locating practice instead, which the summary records."}},{"k":"saved_quiz_find_the_structure","v":{"s":"Find the structure that"}},{"k":"saved_quiz_show_name","v":{"s":"Show the name"}},{"k":"mode_3d_selected","v":{"s":"3D brain anatomy mode selected. The model is loading."}},{"k":"mode_2d_selected","v":{"s":"2D brain diagram mode selected."}},{"k":"selection_carried","v":{"s":"Your selection came with you."}},{"k":"selection_no_counterpart","v":{"s":"This one has no matching structure in the other view, so nothing is selected here."}},{"k":"show_in_2d","v":{"s":"Show this in the 2D diagram"}},{"k":"show_in_3d","v":{"s":"Show this in the 3D model"}},{"k":"no_3d_counterpart","v":{"s":"No single structure in the 3D model matches this region, so the model will open without a selection."}},{"k":"3d_not_loaded_yet","v":{"s":"The 3D model has not been opened yet in this session, so its structures are not listed. Opening it will load them."}},{"k":"d3_brain_atlas_display_mode","v":{"s":"Brain atlas display mode"}},{"k":"d3_find_and_reveal_a_3d_brain_structure","v":{"s":"Find and reveal a 3D brain structure"}},{"k":"d3_3d_anatomy_controls","v":{"s":"3D anatomy controls"}},{"k":"d3_visible_anatomy_layer","v":{"s":"Visible anatomy layer"}},{"k":"d3_selection_focus_style","v":{"s":"Selection focus style"}},{"k":"d3_3d_anatomical_cutaway_plane","v":{"s":"3D anatomical cutaway plane"}},{"k":"d3_guided_3d_pathway","v":{"s":"Guided 3D pathway"}},{"k":"d3_3d_learning_tools","v":{"s":"3D learning tools"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_cases","v":{"s":"Clinical cases"}},{"k":"d3_3d_camera_presets","v":{"s":"3D camera presets"}},{"k":"d3_applied_anatomy","v":{"s":"Applied anatomy"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_lesion_explorer","v":{"s":"Clinical lesion explorer"}},{"k":"d3_close_clinical_lesion_explorer","v":{"s":"Close clinical lesion explorer"}},{"k":"d3_close","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_teaching_cases","v":{"s":"Clinical teaching cases"}},{"k":"d3_pattern","v":{"s":"Pattern"}},{"k":"d3_explore","v":{"s":"Explore"}},{"k":"d3_keep_in_mind","v":{"s":"Keep in mind"}},{"k":"d3_teaching_association_only_not_a_diagnosis_or","v":{"s":"Teaching association only—not a diagnosis or a substitute for clinical evaluation. Symptoms can reflect distributed networks and many possible causes."}},{"k":"d3_saved_study_set","v":{"s":"Saved study set"}},{"k":"d3_close_saved_study_set","v":{"s":"Close saved study set"}},{"k":"d3_start_custom_quiz","v":{"s":"Start custom quiz"}},{"k":"d3_clear_set","v":{"s":"Clear set"}},{"k":"d3_no_structures_saved_yet_select_anatomy_in","v":{"s":"No structures saved yet. Select anatomy in the model, then choose Save to study set in its learning card."}},{"k":"d3_study_round_complete","v":{"s":"Study round complete"}},{"k":"d3_back_to_atlas","v":{"s":"Back to atlas"}},{"k":"d3_retry_structures_needing_practice","v":{"s":"Retry structures needing practice"}},{"k":"d3_find_a_saved_structure","v":{"s":"Find a saved structure"}},{"k":"d3_end_quiz","v":{"s":"End quiz"}},{"k":"d3_accessible_saved_structure_answers","v":{"s":"Accessible saved structure answers"}},{"k":"d3_3d_anatomy_comparison_selection","v":{"s":"3D anatomy comparison selection"}},{"k":"d3_suggested_structure_comparisons","v":{"s":"Suggested structure comparisons"}},{"k":"d3_pathway_playback_controls","v":{"s":"Pathway playback controls"}},{"k":"d3_pathway_playback_speed","v":{"s":"Pathway playback speed"}},{"k":"d3_cut_depth","v":{"s":"Cut depth"}},{"k":"d3_drag_the_plane_through_the_model_to","v":{"s":"Drag the plane through the model to expose deep anatomy. Flip side changes which half remains visible; search and challenges automatically restore the whole model before revealing an answer."}},{"k":"d3_orientation","v":{"s":"Orientation"}},{"k":"d3_depth_zone","v":{"s":"Depth zone"}},{"k":"d3_visible_side","v":{"s":"Visible side"}},{"k":"d3_structures_intersecting_the_current_cutaway","v":{"s":"Structures intersecting the current cutaway"}},{"k":"d3_find_it_in_3d","v":{"s":"Find It in 3D"}},{"k":"d3_rotate_the_model_and_select_the_structure","v":{"s":"Rotate the model and select the structure that best matches the clue. Hints reduce the available points, but retrying is always encouraged."}},{"k":"d3_3d_challenge_progress","v":{"s":"3D challenge progress"}},{"k":"d3_score","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"d3_mastered","v":{"s":"Mastered"}},{"k":"d3_streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"d3_3d_challenge_difficulty","v":{"s":"3D challenge difficulty"}},{"k":"d3_show_answer","v":{"s":"Show answer"}},{"k":"d3_next_structure","v":{"s":"Next structure →"}},{"k":"d3_accessible_answer_choices_for_the_3d_challenge","v":{"s":"Accessible answer choices for the 3D challenge"}},{"k":"d3_structure_comparison","v":{"s":"Structure comparison"}},{"k":"d3_guided_learning_selected_anatomy","v":{"s":"Guided learning · selected anatomy"}},{"k":"psych_medication_classes_selected_for_comparison","v":{"s":"Medication classes selected for comparison"}},{"k":"psych_medication_class_comparison_matrix","v":{"s":"Medication class comparison matrix"}},{"k":"d3_search_is_paused_during_your_saved_structure","v":{"s":"Search is paused during your saved-structure quiz"}},{"k":"d3_search_is_paused_during_find_it_in","v":{"s":"Search is paused during Find It in 3D"}},{"k":"d3_choose_comparison_a_and_b_by_anatomy","v":{"s":"Choose comparison A and B by anatomy or function"}},{"k":"d3_search_grouped_anatomy_or_function_such_as","v":{"s":"Search grouped anatomy or function, such as hippocampus or memory"}},{"k":"d3_building_the_3d_structure_index","v":{"s":"Building the 3D structure index..."}},{"k":"d3_search_3d_brain_structures_by_anatomy_or","v":{"s":"Search 3D brain structures by anatomy or function"}},{"k":"d3_clear_3d_structure_search","v":{"s":"Clear 3D structure search"}},{"k":"d3_matching_3d_brain_structures","v":{"s":"Matching 3D brain structures"}},{"k":"d3_no_matching_structure","v":{"s":"No matching structure"}},{"k":"d3_try_a_broader_anatomical_name_or_a","v":{"s":"Try a broader anatomical name or a function such as memory, vision, movement, or balance."}},{"k":"d3_use_the_model_or_accessible_answer_list","v":{"s":"Use the model or accessible answer list to identify the target."}},{"k":"d3_search_is_paused_so_the_structure_name","v":{"s":"Search is paused so the structure name is not revealed."}},{"k":"d3_choose_two_structures_a_third_choice_replaces","v":{"s":"Choose two structures; a third choice replaces the oldest."}},{"k":"d3_search_by_anatomy_or_function_then_reveal","v":{"s":"Search by anatomy or function, then reveal the result."}},{"k":"d3_the_searchable_index_appears_as_soon_as","v":{"s":"The searchable index appears as soon as the model is ready."}},{"k":"d3_end_compare","v":{"s":"End compare"}},{"k":"d3_end_challenge","v":{"s":"End challenge"}},{"k":"d3_connect_a_symptom_pattern_to_a_teaching","v":{"s":"Connect a symptom pattern to a teaching pathway and one representative structure."}},{"k":"d3_save_structures_from_their_guide_cards_revisit","v":{"s":"Save structures from their guide cards, revisit them, then practice identifying only your selections."}},{"k":"d3_round_finished_repeat_the_set_whenever_you","v":{"s":"Round finished. Repeat the set whenever you want more practice."}},{"k":"d3_practice_whole_set_again","v":{"s":"Practice whole set again"}},{"k":"d3_choose_it_directly_in_the_model_or","v":{"s":"Choose it directly in the model or use the accessible answer list."}},{"k":"d3_finish_round","v":{"s":"Finish round"}},{"k":"d3_next_structure_2","v":{"s":"Next structure"}},{"k":"d3_compare_anatomy","v":{"s":"Compare anatomy"}},{"k":"d3_choose_two_structures_from_search_or_the","v":{"s":"Choose two structures from search or the model."}},{"k":"d3_choose_a_structure","v":{"s":"Choose a structure"}},{"k":"d3_search_or_select_the_model","v":{"s":"Search or select the model"}},{"k":"d3_suggested_pairs","v":{"s":"Suggested pairs"}},{"k":"d3_guided_pathway","v":{"s":"Guided pathway"}},{"k":"d3_previous_pathway_step","v":{"s":"Previous pathway step"}},{"k":"d3_next_pathway_step","v":{"s":"Next pathway step"}},{"k":"d3_slow","v":{"s":"Slow"}},{"k":"d3_standard","v":{"s":"Standard"}},{"k":"d3_fast","v":{"s":"Fast"}},{"k":"d3_anatomical_cutaway","v":{"s":"Anatomical cutaway"}},{"k":"d3_show_first_side","v":{"s":"Show first side"}},{"k":"d3_flip_side","v":{"s":"Flip side"}},{"k":"d3_close_2","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"d3_opposite_half","v":{"s":"Opposite half"}},{"k":"d3_first_half","v":{"s":"First half"}},{"k":"d3_structures_in_this_section","v":{"s":"Structures in this section"}},{"k":"d3_move_the_plane_to_identify_intersecting_anatomy","v":{"s":"Move the plane to identify intersecting anatomy"}},{"k":"d3_structure_name","v":{"s":"Structure name"}},{"k":"d3_function_clue","v":{"s":"Function clue"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_clue","v":{"s":"Clinical clue"}},{"k":"d3_another_hint","v":{"s":"Another hint"}},{"k":"d3_use_model_free_answer_choices","v":{"s":"Use model-free answer choices"}},{"k":"d3_open_model_source_and_attribution","v":{"s":"Open model source and attribution"}},{"k":"d3_choose_one_more_structure_to_compare_function","v":{"s":"Choose one more structure to compare function, connections, and clinical relevance."}},{"k":"d3_read_across_matching_categories_to_find_shared","v":{"s":"Read across matching categories to find shared roles and meaningful differences."}},{"k":"d3_use_hear_name_for_an_audio_pronunciation","v":{"s":"Use Hear name for an audio pronunciation."}},{"k":"d3_recenter","v":{"s":"Recenter"}},{"k":"d3_save_to_study_set","v":{"s":"Save to study set"}},{"k":"d3_hear_name","v":{"s":"Hear name"}},{"k":"d3_open_related_2d_diagram","v":{"s":"Open related 2D diagram"}},{"k":"d3_open_current_2d_view","v":{"s":"Open current 2D view"}},{"k":"d3_clear_selected_3d_structure","v":{"s":"Clear selected 3D structure"}},{"k":"d3_clear","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"d3_what_it_contributes","v":{"s":"What it contributes"}},{"k":"d3_connected_systems","v":{"s":"Connected systems"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_lens","v":{"s":"Clinical lens"}},{"k":"d3_a_matching_teaching_card_is_open_below","v":{"s":"A matching teaching card is open below for deeper study."}},{"k":"d3_this_model_label_has_no_one_to","v":{"s":"This model label has no one-to-one card in this orientation; use the 2D atlas and neighboring anatomy for context."}},{"k":"p_shortcuts","v":{"s":"Brain Atlas. Keyboard shortcuts: 1 through {count} switch views, Q toggles quiz, / focuses search, Escape closes region detail."}},{"k":"p_choose_a_side_for","v":{"s":"Choose a side for {name}"}},{"k":"p_reveal_on_side","v":{"s":"Reveal {name} on the {side} side"}},{"k":"p_reveal_side","v":{"s":"Reveal {side} side"}},{"k":"p_study_set_count","v":{"s":"Study set ({count})"}},{"k":"p_your_collection_count","v":{"s":"Your collection · {count}/24"}},{"k":"p_show_in_the_model","v":{"s":"Show {name} in the model"}},{"k":"p_remove_from_study_set","v":{"s":"Remove {name} from study set"}},{"k":"p_identified_on_the_first_try","v":{"s":"{correct} of {total} identified on the first try."}},{"k":"p_practice_again","v":{"s":"Practice again: {list}"}},{"k":"p_custom_quiz_progress","v":{"s":"Custom quiz · {index} of {total}"}},{"k":"p_remove_from_comparison","v":{"s":"Remove {name} from comparison"}},{"k":"p_sequence","v":{"s":"{name} sequence"}},{"k":"p_controls","v":{"s":"{name} controls"}},{"k":"p_percent_through_plane","v":{"s":"{percent} percent through the {axis} plane"}},{"k":"p_slice_orientation_map","v":{"s":"Slice orientation map. {direction}, depth {percent} percent. Click or use arrow keys to reposition."}},{"k":"p_gestational_week","v":{"s":"Gestational week {week}, nearest milestone {milestone}"}},{"k":"p_of_two_selected","v":{"s":"{count} of 2 selected"}},{"k":"p_recenter_camera_on","v":{"s":"Recenter the 3D camera on {name}"}},{"k":"p_hear_the_name_pronounced","v":{"s":"Hear the name {name} pronounced"}},{"k":"p_function_label","v":{"s":"Function: "}},{"k":"p_reading_level_selected","v":{"s":"Reading level: {name} (selected)"}},{"k":"p_reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level: {name}"}},{"k":"p_generate_explanation_for","v":{"s":"Generate AI explanation for {region} at {level} level"}},{"k":"p_shown_count","v":{"s":"{count} shown"}},{"k":"fm_why_1","v":{"s":"Broca's aphasia (left frontal lobe, BA 44/45). Non-fluent + intact comprehension + frustrated awareness is the canonical triad. Adjacent motor cortex damage explains the right hemiparesis (corticospinal tract crosses)."}},{"k":"fm_why_2","v":{"s":"Wernicke's aphasia (left posterior superior temporal gyrus, BA 22). Fluent + nonsensical + comprehension-impaired + unaware is the canonical contrast to Broca's. No hemiparesis because motor cortex is far away."}},{"k":"fm_why_3","v":{"s":"Henry Molaison (HM) case — the most-cited case in cognitive neuroscience. Bilateral hippocampal removal = profound anterograde amnesia. Distinguishes declarative (lost) from procedural (preserved) memory systems. Implicit memory works through different circuits."}},{"k":"fm_why_4","v":{"s":"Bilateral amygdala damage (Klüver-Bucy syndrome features). Fear-recognition + appropriate-caution-around-strangers depend on amygdala fast-track threat processing. Patient SR (Bechara et al.) is the canonical case."}},{"k":"fm_why_5","v":{"s":"Phineas Gage (1848) — the founding case of frontal-lobe / executive-function neuroscience. Prefrontal damage with intact cognition + memory shows that personality + planning + impulse control can change personality, planning, and impulse control while sparing memory and language."}},{"k":"fm_why_6","v":{"s":"Bilateral occipital lobe damage (V1) = cortical blindness. Pupillary reflex is intact because it bypasses cortex (subcortical pathway via pretectum). Anton syndrome (denial of blindness) suggests damage extends to association areas that would normally signal the deficit."}},{"k":"fm_why_7","v":{"s":"Classic cerebellar syndrome. Coordination + timing + motor learning deficits without weakness. PICA (posterior inferior cerebellar artery) supplies the cerebellum. Note: cerebellar damage causes IPSILATERAL signs (same side), unlike cerebral cortex damage."}},{"k":"fm_why_8","v":{"s":"Right-parietal damage = hemispatial neglect. Spatial-attention systems are right-lateralized, so right damage produces left neglect (much more common than the reverse). Distinguishes from hemianopia (visual-field cut) — neglect is attentional, not visual."}},{"k":"fm_why_9","v":{"s":"Prosopagnosia (face blindness). The fusiform face area (FFA) in the right ventral temporal cortex is specialized for face recognition. Voice + context recognition are unaffected because they use different cortical pathways. Distinguishes face-specific from general visual agnosia."}},{"k":"fm_why_10","v":{"s":"Midbrain damage affecting the reticular activating system (RAS) = coma. Pupils are unresponsive because cranial nerves III + sympathetic innervation pass through midbrain. Vital centers (medulla) determine whether spontaneous breathing continues. Brainstem death = legal death criterion."}},{"k":"fm_why_11","v":{"s":"PTSD = amygdala hyperactivity + reduced prefrontal regulation. Trauma-conditioned cues activate the amygdala's fear circuit, bypassing slower cortical evaluation. Treatment (exposure therapy, EMDR, propranolol) targets re-consolidation of the fear-conditioned trace."}},{"k":"fm_why_12","v":{"s":"Earliest Alzheimer's pathology starts in the entorhinal cortex + hippocampus (medial temporal lobe). Spatial memory + new-route learning depend on hippocampal place cells (Nobel Prize 2014: O'Keefe + Mosers). Anterograde memory deficit before other cognitive symptoms is the typical AD progression."}},{"k":"p_view_3d_anatomy","v":{"s":"{name} — 3D Anatomy"}},{"k":"p_141_structures","v":{"s":"141 structures"}},{"k":"p_milestone_count","v":{"s":"{count} milestones"}},{"k":"p_target_count","v":{"s":"{count} targets"}},{"k":"p_correct_highlighted","v":{"s":"Correct—{name} is highlighted. Continue when ready."}},{"k":"p_that_was_try_again","v":{"s":"That was {name}. Try again in the model or answer list."}},{"k":"p_slice_direction_hint","v":{"s":"{start} → {end}. Select anywhere on the map to move the synchronized 3D cut plane."}},{"k":"p_intersect_this_plane","v":{"s":"{total} intersect this plane · showing {shown}"}},{"k":"p_challenge_round","v":{"s":"Active learning challenge · Round {round} of {total}"}},{"k":"p_hint_level","v":{"s":"Hint {level}: "}},{"k":"p_correct_one_point","v":{"s":"Correct · +{points} point. "}},{"k":"p_correct_points","v":{"s":"Correct · +{points} points. "}},{"k":"p_answer_revealed","v":{"s":"Answer revealed: {name}. "}},{"k":"p_not_the_target","v":{"s":"{name} is not the target. Try again. "}},{"k":"d3_rotate_change_layers_or_request_a_hint","v":{"s":"Rotate, change layers, or request a hint."}},{"k":"p_depth_opposite_side","v":{"s":"Depth {percent}% · opposite side visible"}},{"k":"p_depth_first_side","v":{"s":"Depth {percent}% · first side visible"}},{"k":"d3_function","v":{"s":"Function"}},{"k":"d3_connections","v":{"s":"Connections"}},{"k":"d3_clinical_lens_2","v":{"s":"Clinical lens"}},{"k":"fm_def_frontal","v":{"s":"Executive function, planning, motor cortex, Broca's area (speech production), personality, impulse control. Damage = Phineas Gage / motor aphasia / hemiparesis."}},{"k":"fm_def_parietal","v":{"s":"Somatosensory cortex, spatial awareness, visuomotor integration. Right-hemisphere damage = hemispatial neglect; left = Gerstmann syndrome."}},{"k":"fm_def_temporal","v":{"s":"Auditory cortex, Wernicke's area (language comprehension), face recognition (fusiform), encloses the hippocampus + amygdala."}},{"k":"fm_def_occipital","v":{"s":"Primary visual cortex (V1) + association areas (V2-V5). Color, motion, depth, object recognition. Damage = cortical blindness, hemianopia, visual agnosia."}},{"k":"fm_def_hippocampus","v":{"s":"Memory formation + consolidation; spatial memory (London-taxi-driver studies). Bilateral damage = anterograde amnesia (HM / Henry Molaison case)."}},{"k":"fm_def_amygdala","v":{"s":"Emotion processing, fear recognition + conditioning, threat detection. Damage = Klüver-Bucy / fear-blindness; hyperactivity = PTSD, anxiety disorders."}},{"k":"fm_def_cerebellum","v":{"s":"Motor coordination, balance, motor learning, timing. Damage = ipsilateral ataxia, dysmetria, intention tremor, scanning speech (NOT contralateral like cerebrum)."}},{"k":"fm_def_brainstem","v":{"s":"Cranial nerve nuclei (III-XII), reticular activating system (consciousness), vital centers (cardiac, respiratory). Damage = coma, cranial palsies, vital instability."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_1","v":{"s":"Patient produces telegraphic, non-fluent speech (\"water... want...\") with intact comprehension. Aware of the deficit and frustrated by it. Right hemiparesis on exam."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_2","v":{"s":"Patient produces fluent, well-articulated but nonsensical \"word salad\" with poor comprehension of others. Unaware of the deficit. No motor weakness."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_3","v":{"s":"After surgical removal of bilateral medial temporal lobes for intractable epilepsy, patient cannot form ANY new long-term memories, though working memory + procedural learning are preserved. Pre-surgery memories intact."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_4","v":{"s":"Patient has flattened emotional response. Cannot recognize fear in others' faces. Approaches strangers without normal caution. No motor or sensory deficits."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_5","v":{"s":"After damage from a railroad accident, patient becomes impulsive, socially inappropriate, abandons long-term plans. Personality changed. Memory + speech + motor function intact."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_6","v":{"s":"Patient develops cortical blindness after a stroke but their pupillary light reflex is intact. They sometimes deny being blind (Anton syndrome). Eyes are anatomically normal."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_7","v":{"s":"Patient has wide-based gait, intention tremor (worsens as the hand approaches a target), dysmetria (overshoot/undershoot on finger-to-nose test), and scanning speech. Strength normal. Damage from PICA stroke."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_8","v":{"s":"Patient ignores the entire left side of space. Eats food only off the right half of their plate, dresses only the right side, draws clocks with all numbers crammed on the right. No visual-field cut."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_9","v":{"s":"Patient cannot recognize familiar faces (including own family) but can recognize them by voice or context. Other visual recognition intact. Lesion in fusiform gyrus."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_10","v":{"s":"Patient becomes comatose after a midbrain lesion. Pupils unresponsive. Spontaneous breathing intact but irregular. Patient does not arouse to noxious stimuli."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_11","v":{"s":"Combat veteran shows hyper-vigilance, exaggerated startle response, and intense fear responses to neutral cues that resemble combat (a car backfire, bright flashes). Imaging shows hyperactivity here."}},{"k":"fm_scenario_12","v":{"s":"Patient with mild cognitive impairment cannot remember a 4-item shopping list 5 minutes later, gets lost in their own neighborhood, and has trouble learning new routes. Pre-clinical Alzheimer's suspected."}}],"id":493}},{"k":"bridgelab","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_vehicle_starting_automated_drive_across_bridge_sp","v":{"s":"Vehicle starting automated drive across bridge span"}},{"k":"a11y_top_bridge_designs_by_cost","v":{"s":"Top bridge designs by cost"}},{"k":"initializing_bridge_lab","v":{"s":"🌉 Initializing Bridge Lab..."}},{"k":"stress_test","v":{"s":"Stress Test"}},{"k":"bridge_types","v":{"s":"Bridge Types"}},{"k":"materials","v":{"s":"Materials"}},{"k":"forces","v":{"s":"Forces"}},{"k":"case_studies","v":{"s":"Case Studies"}},{"k":"design_cycle","v":{"s":"Design Cycle"}},{"k":"quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"Print"}},{"k":"inquiry","v":{"s":"Inquiry"}},{"k":"bridge_engineering_sections","v":{"s":"Bridge Engineering sections"}},{"k":"red_tension","v":{"s":"Red = tension"}},{"k":"blue_compression","v":{"s":"blue = compression"}},{"k":"thickness_shows_magnitude_forces_calcu","v":{"s":", thickness shows magnitude. Forces calculated using the deep-beam approximation (real analysis would use method of joints or matrix structural analysis)."}},{"k":"tension","v":{"s":"Tension"}},{"k":"compression","v":{"s":"Compression"}},{"k":"thicker_larger_force","v":{"s":"Thicker = larger force"}},{"k":"supports_pin_left_roller_right","v":{"s":"Supports: pin (left) ▲  ·  roller (right) ▲⊙"}},{"k":"uniform_load","v":{"s":"Uniform load"}},{"k":"moving_vehicle","v":{"s":"Moving vehicle"}},{"k":"moving_load_analysis","v":{"s":"Moving-load analysis: "}},{"k":"real_bridges_are_designed_by_sliding_t","v":{"s":"real bridges are designed by sliding the worst possible vehicle across every point on the span and recording the maximum force in each member. The \"influence line\" of a member is how its force varies with the load's position. Each member has its own worst case — usually NOT in the same place as the worst case for any other member."}},{"k":"uniform_mode_treats_the_load_as_equall","v":{"s":"Uniform mode treats the load as equally distributed across all top joints — useful for studying the bridge under its own weight + typical dead loads."}},{"k":"warren","v":{"s":"Warren"}},{"k":"pratt","v":{"s":"Pratt"}},{"k":"howe","v":{"s":"Howe"}},{"k":"design_name_e_g_marie_s_pedestrian_bri","v":{"s":"Design name (e.g., \"Marie's pedestrian bridge over Back Cove\")"}},{"k":"why_this_material_what_are_the_constra","v":{"s":"Why this material? What are the constraints? What would you improve?"}},{"k":"real_engineering_is_not_just_does_it_w","v":{"s":"Real engineering is not just \"does it work?\" — it is \"what's the cheapest design that works?\" This optimizer sweeps every combination of material + cross-section, keeps only those that pass your target safety factor for BOTH yield AND buckling, and ranks them by material cost. Span, height, number of bays, and load remain at your current settings."}},{"k":"target_safety_factor","v":{"s":"Target safety factor"}},{"k":"target_safety_factor_2","v":{"s":"Target safety factor"}},{"k":"1_5_very_thin_margin","v":{"s":"1.5 (very thin margin)"}},{"k":"2_0_code_minimum","v":{"s":"2.0 (code minimum)"}},{"k":"4_0_conservative","v":{"s":"4.0+ (conservative)"}},{"k":"6_0_brooklyn_bridge","v":{"s":"6.0 (Brooklyn Bridge)"}},{"k":"cost_estimate_is_material_only_doesn_t","v":{"s":"Cost estimate is material-only — doesn't include labor, fabrication, transport, fastening, or maintenance over the bridge's lifetime. Real bridge economics also factor in durability (steel maintenance vs concrete) + replacement cycle. But raw material cost is the starting line."}},{"k":"click_the_winner","v":{"s":"Click the winner: "}},{"k":"apply_this_design_to_my_bridge","v":{"s":"Apply this design to my bridge"}},{"k":"exact_analysis_method_of_joints","v":{"s":"✓ Exact analysis (Method of Joints). "}},{"k":"forces_are_computed_from_2d_pin_jointe","v":{"s":"Forces are computed from 2D pin-jointed equilibrium: for each joint, sum of forces in x and sum of forces in y both equal zero. "}},{"k":"deep_beam_approximation","v":{"s":"⚠ Deep-beam approximation. "}},{"k":"k_trusses_use_the_deep_beam_approximat","v":{"s":"K-trusses use the deep-beam approximation: the truss is treated like a single beam carrying the same total load. Maximum chord force ≈ M / h, where M is the bending moment and h is the truss height. The qualitative pattern (max chord at center, max diagonal force at ends) is correct, but per-member forces are not exact. Warren, Pratt, and Howe trusses use the exact Method of Joints solver."}},{"k":"suspension_bridge_cable_shape","v":{"s":"Suspension bridge cable shape"}},{"k":"tower","v":{"s":"Tower"}},{"k":"tower_2","v":{"s":"Tower"}},{"k":"anchor","v":{"s":"Anchor"}},{"k":"anchor_2","v":{"s":"Anchor"}},{"k":"suspension_bridges_work_differently_fr","v":{"s":"Suspension bridges work differently from trusses. The deck hangs from cables; cables hang in a curve between towers; towers transfer the load down. The cables are in PURE TENSION, the towers in PURE COMPRESSION. Anchorages resist the horizontal pull of the cables — they're typically massive concrete blocks buried in bedrock at each end of the bridge."}},{"k":"span_m","v":{"s":"Span (m)"}},{"k":"sag_m","v":{"s":"Sag (m)"}},{"k":"deck_load_kn_m","v":{"s":"Deck load (kN/m)"}},{"k":"horizontal_h","v":{"s":"Horizontal H"}},{"k":"vertical_v","v":{"s":"Vertical V"}},{"k":"max_tension_t","v":{"s":"Max tension T"}},{"k":"cable_length","v":{"s":"Cable length"}},{"k":"angle_at_support","v":{"s":"Angle at support"}},{"k":"sag_span_ratio","v":{"s":"Sag/span ratio"}},{"k":"why_sag_matters","v":{"s":"Why sag matters: "}},{"k":"shallower_sag_less_drop_in_the_cable_m","v":{"s":"Shallower sag (less drop in the cable) means the cable is more horizontal, which means MORE horizontal tension is needed to support the same deck weight. Cable tension scales as 1/sag — halving the sag doubles the cable force. Real suspension bridges use sag/span ratios around 1:8 to 1:12 to balance cable cost (more sag = lower force = thinner cable) against tower height (more sag = taller towers needed to clear the deck above water/road). Golden Gate sag ratio: 1:11. Akashi-Kaikyō: 1:10."}},{"k":"catenary_vs_parabola","v":{"s":"Catenary vs parabola: "}},{"k":"a_free_cable_under_its_own_weight_hang","v":{"s":"A free cable under its own weight hangs in a CATENARY (cosh) shape. A cable supporting a uniformly distributed load (like a bridge deck) hangs in a PARABOLA. Real suspension cables are close to parabolic because the deck mass dominates the cable mass. The tool uses the parabolic approximation. Galileo first proposed a parabola; Huygens (1646, age 17) proved it was wrong for a free chain; Bernoulli + Leibniz + Huygens worked out the true catenary in 1691."}},{"k":"bridge_type_depends_on_span_environmen","v":{"s":"Bridge type depends on span, environment, material, and budget. Engineers pick the right form for the constraints — not a favorite."}},{"k":"arch_bridge_thrust_diagram","v":{"s":"Arch bridge thrust diagram"}},{"k":"abutment","v":{"s":"Abutment"}},{"k":"abutment_2","v":{"s":"Abutment"}},{"k":"h_out","v":{"s":"H (out)"}},{"k":"arches_do_the_opposite_of_suspension_b","v":{"s":"Arches do the opposite of suspension bridges: a load on top pushes the curve OUTWARD into the abutments rather than pulling them inward. The entire arch is in compression — there is no tension in a true arch under design loads. This is why stone arches work (stone is excellent in compression). The horizontal thrust at the supports is the design challenge — abutments must resist it without sliding or tipping."}},{"k":"span_m_2","v":{"s":"Span (m)"}},{"k":"rise_m","v":{"s":"Rise (m)"}},{"k":"deck_load_kn_m_2","v":{"s":"Deck load (kN/m)"}},{"k":"horizontal_thrust_h","v":{"s":"Horizontal thrust H"}},{"k":"vertical_reaction_v","v":{"s":"Vertical reaction V"}},{"k":"max_compression","v":{"s":"Max compression"}},{"k":"min_compression","v":{"s":"Min compression"}},{"k":"rise_span_ratio","v":{"s":"Rise/span ratio"}},{"k":"why_arches_stand_for_centuries","v":{"s":"Why arches stand for centuries: "}},{"k":"compression_is_what_stone_brick_and_co","v":{"s":"Compression is what stone, brick, and concrete handle best. Roman arch bridges from 100 BCE are still in use because: (1) they are entirely in compression, no tension anywhere; (2) the abutments resist the horizontal thrust through sheer mass; (3) compressive forces have no fatigue limit in the way that bending or tension do — stone under steady compression effectively lasts forever."}},{"k":"why_high_thrust_matters","v":{"s":"Why high thrust matters: "}},{"k":"flatter_arches_smaller_rise_need_much_","v":{"s":"Flatter arches (smaller rise) need much more horizontal thrust. Roman arches were typically semicircular (rise = half the span) for that reason — the thrust is moderate and easily resisted. Modern shallow steel arches need enormous engineered abutments anchored into rock, or tied-arch designs where horizontal tie rods at deck level absorb the thrust (so the abutments only resist vertical load). Sydney Harbour Bridge and New River Gorge are tied-arch designs."}},{"k":"beam_bending_diagram","v":{"s":"Beam bending diagram"}},{"k":"most_modern_bridges_over_short_spans_8","v":{"s":"Most modern bridges over short spans (< 80 m) are simply beams sitting on two supports. The internal forces are very different from trusses: members carry BENDING + SHEAR (not just tension/compression along their axis). The bottom of a loaded beam stretches; the top compresses. This is the simplest structural analysis, the foundation of every other one."}},{"k":"span_m_3","v":{"s":"Span (m)"}},{"k":"depth_mm","v":{"s":"Depth (mm)"}},{"k":"width_mm","v":{"s":"Width (mm)"}},{"k":"point_load_kn","v":{"s":"Point load (kN)"}},{"k":"load_position_0_1","v":{"s":"Load position (0..1)"}},{"k":"material","v":{"s":"Material"}},{"k":"r_a_left","v":{"s":"R_A (left)"}},{"k":"r_b_right","v":{"s":"R_B (right)"}},{"k":"max_moment_m","v":{"s":"Max moment M"}},{"k":"max_shear_v","v":{"s":"Max shear V"}},{"k":"bending_stress","v":{"s":"Bending stress σ"}},{"k":"shear_stress","v":{"s":"Shear stress τ"}},{"k":"section_mod_s","v":{"s":"Section mod. S"}},{"k":"deflection","v":{"s":"Deflection δ"}},{"k":"why_i_beams","v":{"s":"Why I-beams: "}},{"k":"bending_stress_is_highest_at_the_top_b","v":{"s":"Bending stress is highest at the top + bottom (far from the neutral axis), zero at the center. So material in the middle of the cross-section is wasted. The I-shape concentrates material as top + bottom flanges where it carries the bending, leaving a thin web in the middle that handles only shear. Same depth gets 3-5× more bending capacity than a solid rectangle of equal weight. This insight (Galileo, 1638) is the foundation of every steel beam ever made."}},{"k":"cable_stayed_bridge_diagram","v":{"s":"Cable-stayed bridge diagram"}},{"k":"tower_3","v":{"s":"Tower"}},{"k":"stays_in_tension","v":{"s":"Stays in tension"}},{"k":"a_cable_stayed_bridge_uses_cables_runn","v":{"s":"A cable-stayed bridge uses cables running directly from a tower (or pylons) to the deck — unlike a suspension bridge, which has a main draped cable + vertical hangers. Each stay is in tension; the tower is in compression. Faster + cheaper to build than a suspension bridge of comparable span, and noticeably stiffer in wind. The Millau Viaduct, Russky Bridge, + Sundial Bridge are all cable-stayed."}},{"k":"main_span_m","v":{"s":"Main span (m)"}},{"k":"tower_height_m","v":{"s":"Tower height (m)"}},{"k":"stays_per_side","v":{"s":"Stays per side"}},{"k":"deck_load_kn_m_3","v":{"s":"Deck load (kN/m)"}},{"k":"max_stay_force_longest","v":{"s":"Max stay force (longest)"}},{"k":"min_stay_force_steepest","v":{"s":"Min stay force (steepest)"}},{"k":"tributary_per_stay","v":{"s":"Tributary per stay"}},{"k":"tower_compression","v":{"s":"Tower compression"}},{"k":"avg_stay_angle_to_horiz","v":{"s":"Avg stay angle (to horiz.)"}},{"k":"cable_stayed_vs_suspension_the_trade","v":{"s":"Cable-stayed vs suspension — the trade: "}},{"k":"a_suspension_bridge_has_one_main_cable","v":{"s":"A SUSPENSION bridge has ONE main cable carrying everything; very long spans possible but the deck is more flexible. A CABLE-STAYED bridge has many independent stays each handling its tributary load; stiffer in wind, easier to build (no main-cable spinning + no separate anchorages — the tower handles compression directly), but maximum economic span is ~1,100 m (vs ~2,000 m for suspension). Most new long bridges built since 1990 are cable-stayed."}},{"k":"why_longer_stays_carry_more_force","v":{"s":"Why longer stays carry MORE force: "}},{"k":"a_stay_near_the_tower_is_steep_mostly_","v":{"s":"A stay near the tower is steep (mostly vertical). It carries its tributary load mostly along its axis with a small horizontal component. A stay farther from the tower is shallow (mostly horizontal). To support the same vertical load with a low angle, it needs a HUGE total tension force. Hence the longest stays at the far ends of the span are the most heavily loaded — usually visibly thicker on real bridges."}},{"k":"strength","v":{"s":"Strength"}},{"k":"weakness","v":{"s":"Weakness"}},{"k":"interactive_catenary_cable_analyzer","v":{"s":"⌣ Interactive catenary cable analyzer"}},{"k":"interactive_arch_thrust_analyzer","v":{"s":"∩ Interactive arch thrust analyzer"}},{"k":"interactive_beam_bending_analyzer","v":{"s":"═ Interactive beam bending analyzer"}},{"k":"interactive_cable_stayed_analyzer","v":{"s":"✦ Interactive cable-stayed analyzer"}},{"k":"railway_bridge_loading_is_different","v":{"s":"Railway bridge loading is different"}},{"k":"fatigue_is_the_dominant_concern","v":{"s":"Fatigue is the dominant concern"}},{"k":"iconic_tragic_railway_bridges","v":{"s":"Iconic + tragic railway bridges"}},{"k":"high_speed_rail_bridges","v":{"s":"High-speed rail bridges"}},{"k":"modern_railway_bridge_engineering","v":{"s":"Modern railway bridge engineering"}},{"k":"maine_railway_bridges","v":{"s":"Maine railway bridges"}},{"k":"maglev_the_future","v":{"s":"Maglev + the future"}},{"k":"railway_bridges_freight_engineering","v":{"s":"🚂 Railway bridges + freight engineering"}},{"k":"railway_bridges_are_a_distinct_discipl","v":{"s":"Railway bridges are a distinct discipline from highway bridges. Loads are heavier, concentrated, and repeated. Fatigue dominates design. Speeds for modern high-speed rail require dynamic considerations highway engineers rarely see. Railway-bridge practice has a smaller academic footprint than highway practice but the engineering depth is at least equivalent. For passenger + freight rail revival, every region's railway bridges are infrastructure-renewal frontline."}},{"k":"what_it_is","v":{"s":"What it is"}},{"k":"how_it_works_in_practice","v":{"s":"How it works in practice"}},{"k":"honest_limit","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"cut_and_cover_tunnel","v":{"s":"Cut-and-cover tunnel"}},{"k":"tunnel_boring_machine_tbm","v":{"s":"Tunnel Boring Machine (TBM)"}},{"k":"new_austrian_tunneling_method_natm","v":{"s":"New Austrian Tunneling Method (NATM)"}},{"k":"immersed_tube_tunnel","v":{"s":"Immersed-tube tunnel"}},{"k":"subsea_bored_tunnel","v":{"s":"Subsea bored tunnel"}},{"k":"ventilation_fire_safety","v":{"s":"Ventilation + fire safety"}},{"k":"channel_tunnel_the_big_one","v":{"s":"Channel Tunnel — the big one"}},{"k":"when_not_to_tunnel","v":{"s":"When NOT to tunnel"}},{"k":"tunnels_underwater_bridges","v":{"s":"🚇 Tunnels + underwater bridges"}},{"k":"tunnels_are_the_siblings_of_bridges_in","v":{"s":"Tunnels are the siblings of bridges in transportation engineering — used to cross when going OVER won't work. They share many fundamentals (forces, fatigue, foundations) + have their own demanding specifics (ventilation, fire safety, hydrostatic pressure, ground behavior). Understanding tunnels alongside bridges gives the full picture of how engineers move people + freight across geographical obstacles."}},{"k":"how_it_works","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"forces_design_logic","v":{"s":"Forces + design logic"}},{"k":"honest_limits","v":{"s":"Honest limits"}},{"k":"bascule_drawbridge","v":{"s":"Bascule (drawbridge)"}},{"k":"swing_bridge","v":{"s":"Swing bridge"}},{"k":"vertical_lift_bridge","v":{"s":"Vertical lift bridge"}},{"k":"retractable_rolling_bridge","v":{"s":"Retractable (rolling) bridge"}},{"k":"transporter_bridge","v":{"s":"Transporter bridge"}},{"k":"submersible_submerged_floating_bridges","v":{"s":"Submersible (submerged-floating) bridges"}},{"k":"floating_pontoon_bridge","v":{"s":"Floating (pontoon) bridge"}},{"k":"movable_unusual_bridge_types","v":{"s":"🚧 Movable + unusual bridge types"}},{"k":"most_bridges_are_fixed_structures_but_","v":{"s":"Most bridges are fixed structures. But where waterborne traffic must pass + a low fixed bridge would block tall ships, engineers have developed a whole family of movable + unusual bridge types. Each one is a specific solution to a specific geometric constraint, with its own forces, failure modes, and historical contexts."}},{"k":"how_it_works_2","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"forces_structural_logic","v":{"s":"Forces + structural logic"}},{"k":"failure_modes_history","v":{"s":"Failure modes + history"}},{"k":"slope_grade","v":{"s":"Slope + grade"}},{"k":"clear_width_passing_zones","v":{"s":"Clear width + passing zones"}},{"k":"surface_materials","v":{"s":"Surface materials"}},{"k":"wayfinding_sensory_cues","v":{"s":"Wayfinding + sensory cues"}},{"k":"handrails_guardrails","v":{"s":"Handrails + guardrails"}},{"k":"lighting_visibility","v":{"s":"Lighting + visibility"}},{"k":"rest_stops_seating","v":{"s":"Rest stops + seating"}},{"k":"case_study_the_high_line","v":{"s":"Case study: The High Line"}},{"k":"pedestrian_accessible_bridge_design","v":{"s":"♿ Pedestrian + accessible bridge design"}},{"k":"a_bridge_that_excludes_people_is_half_","v":{"s":"A bridge that excludes people is half-built. Modern pedestrian + accessible bridge design is governed by the ADA Accessibility Guidelines, PROWAG (Public Right-of-Way Accessibility Guidelines, 2023), AASHTO's Guide for the Development of Bicycle Facilities, and (in many states) more stringent local codes. The goal is not \"minimum compliance.\" The goal is a bridge ANYONE can use, comfortably, in all weather."}},{"k":"spec","v":{"s":"Spec"}},{"k":"common_gotcha","v":{"s":"Common gotcha"}},{"k":"the_framing_that_matters","v":{"s":"The framing that matters: "}},{"k":"accessibility_is_not_a_special_needs_a","v":{"s":"Accessibility is not a special-needs accommodation. About 1 in 4 US adults has a disability (CDC 2024). Add older adults, pregnant women, parents with strollers, people pulling luggage, kids on scooters, bike commuters, and the temporary injuries everyone has — and \"accessible\" design serves a clear MAJORITY of users. The \"able-bodied adult on foot\" the older codes were written for is the minority case, not the standard case."}},{"k":"test_with_the_people_who_depend_on_it","v":{"s":"Test with the people who depend on it: "}},{"k":"no_accessibility_design_should_ship_wi","v":{"s":"No accessibility design should ship without USER TESTING by people who actually use mobility devices, white canes, hearing aids, AAC devices. A design reviewed only by able-bodied engineers + architects will always miss something. The disability community has been clear for decades: \"Nothing about us without us.\" Apply it to bridge design."}},{"k":"every_material_has_tradeoffs_strength_","v":{"s":"Every material has tradeoffs. Strength, weight, cost, durability, environmental impact. The right material depends on the structure and the context."}},{"k":"yield_strength","v":{"s":"Yield strength: "}},{"k":"force_per_unit_area_at_which_a_materia","v":{"s":"Force per unit area at which a material starts to deform permanently. Above this, the material is damaged even after the load is removed. In MPa (megapascals = N/mm²)."}},{"k":"modulus_of_elasticity_e","v":{"s":"Modulus of elasticity (E): "}},{"k":"how_stiff_the_material_is_higher_e_les","v":{"s":"How stiff the material is. Higher E = less deformation under the same force. Stress = E * strain. In GPa (gigapascals = 1000 MPa)."}},{"k":"mass_per_unit_volume_determines_self_w","v":{"s":"Mass per unit volume. Determines self-weight of the structure. A heavy material requires bigger members to support its own weight before any external load."}},{"k":"how_much_a_material_deforms_before_bre","v":{"s":"How much a material deforms before breaking. Ductile (steel) gives warning. Brittle (cast iron, stone) breaks suddenly."}},{"k":"concrete_patch_repair","v":{"s":"Concrete patch repair"}},{"k":"cfrp_carbon_fiber_strengthening","v":{"s":"CFRP (carbon-fiber) strengthening"}},{"k":"steel_concrete_jacketing","v":{"s":"Steel + concrete jacketing"}},{"k":"deck_replacement","v":{"s":"Deck replacement"}},{"k":"cathodic_protection","v":{"s":"Cathodic protection"}},{"k":"scour_countermeasures","v":{"s":"Scour countermeasures"}},{"k":"full_replacement","v":{"s":"Full replacement"}},{"k":"about_90_of_bridge_engineering_work_is","v":{"s":"About 90% of bridge engineering work is on EXISTING bridges, not new ones. Repair + retrofit extend life at a fraction of replacement cost. The right intervention depends on what's deteriorating + how badly."}},{"k":"how_it_works_3","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"when_to_use_it","v":{"s":"When to use it"}},{"k":"limits_honest_caveats","v":{"s":"Limits + honest caveats"}},{"k":"the_us_infrastructure_picture","v":{"s":"The US infrastructure picture: "}},{"k":"about_1_in_13_us_bridges_46_000_of_619","v":{"s":"About 1 in 13 US bridges (~46,000 of 619,000) is in \"poor\" condition according to FHWA. Average bridge age is ~44 years; many were built in the 1950s-1970s federal highway expansion + are now reaching the end of their original design life. The 2021 Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act allocated $40 billion specifically for bridge repair + replacement — the largest US bridge investment since 1956. Done well: substantial life-extension via repair + retrofit. Done poorly: replace-everything cycles that miss the opportunity to keep historic structures functioning longer."}},{"k":"seismic_retrofit_success_the_californi","v":{"s":"Seismic retrofit success — the California program: "}},{"k":"after_the_1971_san_fernando_1989_loma_","v":{"s":"After the 1971 San Fernando + 1989 Loma Prieta earthquakes, California undertook a massive bridge retrofit program. CFRP wrapping + steel jacketing + isolation bearings + restrainer cables added to thousands of pre-1971 bridges. The 1994 Northridge + 2014 South Napa earthquakes both showed that retrofitted bridges performed far better than non-retrofitted ones — many would have collapsed had the retrofit work not been done. One of the most successful infrastructure investments in US history."}},{"k":"concrete_is_great_in_compression_but_u","v":{"s":"Concrete is great in COMPRESSION but useless in TENSION — it cracks at about 3 MPa. Reinforced concrete adds steel rebar to handle tension, but the concrete still cracks (tiny cracks at \"service\" loads that don't affect strength but let water + chlorides reach the rebar over decades). PRESTRESSED concrete is different: high-strength steel tendons are tensioned + permanently compress the concrete BEFORE any external load. The concrete never sees tension under service loads → no cracks → much longer life."}},{"k":"prestressed_beam_stress_diagram","v":{"s":"Prestressed beam stress diagram"}},{"k":"external_load_causes_bending","v":{"s":"External load (causes bending)"}},{"k":"precompression_from_tendons_mpa","v":{"s":"Precompression from tendons (MPa)"}},{"k":"bending_stress_from_external_load_mpa","v":{"s":"Bending stress from external load (MPa)"}},{"k":"two_methods_of_prestressing","v":{"s":"Two methods of prestressing: "}},{"k":"tendons_are_tensioned_before_concrete_","v":{"s":"Tendons are tensioned BEFORE concrete is poured + before it cures. Once concrete hardens + bonds to the tendons, anchors are released; the tendons pull inward through friction. Used for FACTORY-MADE beams (highway girders, double-tees, hollow-core planks)."}},{"k":"concrete_is_poured_first_with_hollow_d","v":{"s":"Concrete is poured first WITH HOLLOW DUCTS through it. After curing, tendons are threaded through, tensioned hydraulically, and anchored at the ends. Used for IN-PLACE construction (bridges, slabs, dams, nuclear reactor containment)."}},{"k":"eug_ne_freyssinet_france_1928_gustave_","v":{"s":"Eugène Freyssinet (France, 1928) + Gustave Magnel (Belgium, 1940s) developed prestressed concrete. The technique required high-strength steel wire (developed 1900-30) — earlier \"low-carbon\" steel relaxed under sustained load + lost the precompression. Now standard worldwide for medium-span bridges (30-200 m). The Confederation Bridge (Canada, 12.9 km of prestressed concrete) + most US highway overpasses are prestressed."}},{"k":"why_this_matters","v":{"s":"Why this matters: "}},{"k":"a_reinforced_concrete_beam_cracks_unde","v":{"s":"A reinforced concrete beam cracks under service load → the cracks are tiny + don't reduce strength, but they let water + chloride ions reach the rebar → rebar corrosion is the #1 cause of premature concrete bridge deterioration. A prestressed beam stays in compression under service loads → no cracks → no chloride ingress → much longer life. Prestressed bridges often last 100+ years; reinforced concrete bridges often need major repair at 50-70."}},{"k":"honest_caveat_when_prestressing_fails","v":{"s":"Honest caveat — when prestressing fails: "}},{"k":"the_morandi_bridge_genoa_italy_collaps","v":{"s":"The Morandi Bridge (Genoa, Italy) collapsed in August 2018, killing 43. Built 1967 as a prestressed cable-stayed design with concrete-encased stays. Decades of corrosion ate the steel wires inside the concrete encasement — invisible from outside. The encasement (the very feature meant to protect the steel) prevented inspection from detecting the deterioration. Modern designs use replaceable stays + non-encased prestressing where possible."}},{"k":"solid_rock_bedrock","v":{"s":"Solid rock (bedrock)"}},{"k":"granite_basalt_sound_limestone_the_str","v":{"s":"Granite, basalt, sound limestone. The strongest natural foundation. Allowable bearing 5-15 MPa (and much higher with engineered anchors)."}},{"k":"dense_sand_gravel","v":{"s":"Dense sand + gravel"}},{"k":"glacial_outwash_well_graded_compacted_","v":{"s":"Glacial outwash, well-graded compacted gravel. Allowable bearing typically 200-600 kPa."}},{"k":"stiff_clay","v":{"s":"Stiff clay"}},{"k":"cohesive_fine_grained_allowable_bearin","v":{"s":"Cohesive, fine-grained. Allowable bearing typically 100-300 kPa for stiff clay; much lower for soft clay."}},{"k":"soft_clay_silt","v":{"s":"Soft clay / silt"}},{"k":"marine_deposits_lake_bed_recently_depo","v":{"s":"Marine deposits, lake bed, recently-deposited mud. Allowable bearing 25-100 kPa."}},{"k":"loose_fill_made_ground","v":{"s":"Loose fill / made ground"}},{"k":"man_made_fill_demolition_rubble_uncont","v":{"s":"Man-made fill, demolition rubble, uncontrolled placement. Highly variable."}},{"k":"underwater_river_bed_ocean_floor","v":{"s":"Underwater (river bed, ocean floor)"}},{"k":"saturated_sand_mud_or_gravel_bearing_d","v":{"s":"Saturated sand, mud, or gravel. Bearing depends on the specific material."}},{"k":"spread_footing","v":{"s":"Spread footing"}},{"k":"mat_foundation","v":{"s":"Mat foundation"}},{"k":"driven_piles","v":{"s":"Driven piles"}},{"k":"drilled_shafts_caissons","v":{"s":"Drilled shafts (caissons)"}},{"k":"spread_caisson","v":{"s":"Spread caisson"}},{"k":"every_bridge_stands_on_something_the_s","v":{"s":"Every bridge stands on something. The strongest member designed by the best engineer fails if the FOUNDATION does. The 1907 Quebec Bridge, 1967 Silver Bridge, 1987 Schoharie Creek, and many others are partly foundation/anchorage failures. Soil mechanics + foundation engineering are as important as the superstructure design."}},{"k":"good_for","v":{"s":"Good for"}},{"k":"watch_out_for","v":{"s":"Watch out for"}},{"k":"your_current_bridge_s_required_footing","v":{"s":"Your current bridge's required footing"}},{"k":"reaction_at_each_support","v":{"s":"Reaction at each support: "}},{"k":"on","v":{"s":" · On "}},{"k":"or_a_square_footing_of","v":{"s":" (or a square footing of ≈ "}},{"k":"on_each_side","v":{"s":" on each side)."}},{"k":"why_scour_kills_bridges","v":{"s":"Why scour kills bridges: "}},{"k":"flowing_water_erodes_soil_from_around_","v":{"s":"Flowing water erodes soil from around bridge piers. As scour deepens, the foundation loses bearing area. Eventually it tips or the pier sinks. Schoharie Creek (1987, NY) collapsed during a flood from scour. The 2001 Hatchie River bridge (Tennessee) failure also. About 60% of US bridge failures involve scour. Modern bridges include scour-monitoring instruments + countermeasures (riprap, sheet piles, deeper foundations). The 1995 Federal Highway Administration requires every bridge over water to have a scour evaluation."}},{"k":"treated_wood_has_higher_ec_sustainably","v":{"s":"Treated wood has higher EC; sustainably-managed forest wood has near-zero net EC over its lifetime (CO₂ sequestered while growing)."}},{"k":"lowest_embodied_carbon_of_any_building","v":{"s":"Lowest embodied carbon of any building material if quarried near use. But quarrying disturbs ecosystems + transport costs energy."}},{"k":"cast_iron_is_brittle_has_been_largely_","v":{"s":"Cast iron is brittle + has been largely phased out for structural use. Lower embodied carbon than steel but worse mechanical properties."}},{"k":"new_steel_production_is_carbon_intensi","v":{"s":"New steel production is carbon-intensive (most via blast furnace + coking coal). Recycled steel via electric-arc furnace cuts emissions by 70%+. \"Green steel\" via hydrogen is being deployed (HYBRIT in Sweden, ArcelorMittal in Spain)."}},{"k":"concrete_itself_has_low_embodied_carbo","v":{"s":"Concrete itself has low embodied carbon per kg, but bridges use VAST amounts. Cement production alone is ~8% of global CO₂ emissions. Supplementary cementitious materials (fly ash, slag, calcined clay) cut this by 30-50%."}},{"k":"high_embodied_carbon_per_kg_difficult_","v":{"s":"High embodied carbon per kg + difficult end-of-life. But weight savings can reduce total material. Best for retrofits + specialty applications, not yet competitive for primary structure."}},{"k":"modern_bridge_design_considers_environ","v":{"s":"Modern bridge design considers ENVIRONMENTAL cost alongside dollar cost. Cement is ~8% of global CO₂ emissions; steel ~7%. A typical bridge uses thousands of tons of these materials. Sustainable design = lower embodied carbon + longer life + end-of-life recyclability."}},{"k":"mass","v":{"s":"Mass"}},{"k":"embodied_co","v":{"s":"Embodied CO₂"}},{"k":"person_years_of_emissions","v":{"s":"= person-years of emissions"}},{"k":"ec_factor","v":{"s":"EC factor"}},{"k":"typical_durability","v":{"s":"Typical durability: "}},{"k":"sustainable_design_strategies","v":{"s":"Sustainable design strategies: "}},{"k":"optimize_for_the_lifetime","v":{"s":"Optimize for the LIFETIME: "}},{"k":"a_bridge_that_lasts_150_years_has_lowe","v":{"s":"A bridge that lasts 150 years has lower per-year carbon than one rebuilt at 60. Long-term durability planning matters."}},{"k":"use_less_material","v":{"s":"Use less material: "}},{"k":"high_strength_steel_advanced_cross_sec","v":{"s":"High-strength steel + advanced cross-sections + optimized geometry can cut material 10-30% with no loss of capacity. The cost-optimizer in the Build tab is doing this."}},{"k":"recycled_content","v":{"s":"Recycled content: "}},{"k":"specify_minimum_recycled_steel_content","v":{"s":"Specify minimum recycled steel content (typically 70-90% achievable). Replace 30-50% of cement with fly ash, slag, or calcined clay."}},{"k":"design_for_disassembly","v":{"s":"Design for disassembly: "}},{"k":"bolted_modular_connections_enable_salv","v":{"s":"Bolted + modular connections enable salvage at end of life. Older welded + rusted bridges get scrapped; modular ones get repurposed."}},{"k":"local_sourcing","v":{"s":"Local sourcing: "}},{"k":"transport_adds_embodied_carbon_sourcin","v":{"s":"Transport adds embodied carbon. Sourcing materials within 500 km can cut total emissions 15-25%."}},{"k":"why_this_matters_now","v":{"s":"Why this matters now: "}},{"k":"infrastructure_construction_accounts_f","v":{"s":"Infrastructure construction accounts for ~25-30% of global emissions. The 1971 National Bridge Inspection Standards (post-Silver-Bridge) created a database showing about 1 in 13 US bridges is in poor condition; thousands need replacement in the next 20 years. The Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act (2021) funds bridge replacement at scale. Done well: lower-carbon materials + longer service life. Done poorly: same emissions, repeating the same mistakes."}},{"k":"s_n_curve_stress_vs_cycles_to_failure","v":{"s":"S-N curve (stress vs cycles to failure)"}},{"k":"cycles_to_failure_log_scale","v":{"s":"Cycles to failure (log scale)"}},{"k":"stress_amplitude","v":{"s":"Stress amplitude"}},{"k":"a_bridge_that_handles_its_design_load_","v":{"s":"A bridge that handles its design load just fine on day one can still fail decades later from FATIGUE: tiny cracks that grow microscopically with every load cycle, until one final cycle propagates a crack to failure. Every truck crossing, every wind gust, every temperature swing is a cycle. A heavily-trafficked highway bridge sees 5,000-50,000 cycles per day; over 50 years that's ~10⁸-10⁹ cycles."}},{"k":"stress_amplitude_mpa","v":{"s":"Stress amplitude (MPa)"}},{"k":"cycles_per_day","v":{"s":"Cycles per day"}},{"k":"endurance_limit_steel_only","v":{"s":"Endurance limit (steel only): "}},{"k":"some_steels_have_a_special_property_be","v":{"s":"Some steels have a special property — below a stress threshold (~100 MPa for typical structural steel), fatigue cracks essentially don't grow. This \"endurance limit\" lets steel bridges last centuries IF designed conservatively below it. Aluminum and most non-ferrous metals have NO endurance limit — they always fatigue eventually."}},{"k":"silver_bridge_1967_fatigue_corrosion","v":{"s":"Silver Bridge 1967 — fatigue + corrosion: "}},{"k":"one_eyebar_of_the_silver_bridge_ohio_r","v":{"s":"One eyebar of the Silver Bridge (Ohio River) had a tiny 2.5 mm defect from manufacturing 40 years earlier. Stress-corrosion-cracking grew it to critical size over decades of traffic cycles. On December 15 1967 it snapped during rush hour; the bridge had no redundancy + the entire structure collapsed into the river. 46 dead. Led directly to the 1971 National Bridge Inspection Standards — every public bridge in the US now gets inspected every 2 years."}},{"k":"when_why_bridges_end","v":{"s":"When + why bridges end"}},{"k":"explosive_controlled_demolition","v":{"s":"Explosive (controlled) demolition"}},{"k":"mechanical_saw_cut_demolition","v":{"s":"Mechanical + saw-cut demolition"}},{"k":"piece_by_piece_decking_removal","v":{"s":"Piece-by-piece + decking removal"}},{"k":"recycling_material_recovery","v":{"s":"Recycling + material recovery"}},{"k":"environmental_community_impact","v":{"s":"Environmental + community impact"}},{"k":"famous_demolitions","v":{"s":"Famous demolitions"}},{"k":"demolition_as_engineering_education","v":{"s":"Demolition as engineering education"}},{"k":"bridge_demolition_decommissioning","v":{"s":"💣 Bridge demolition + decommissioning"}},{"k":"every_bridge_eventually_retires_the_ho","v":{"s":"Every bridge eventually retires. The how + why is its own engineering discipline — explosive demolition for unconstrained sites, mechanical saw-cut work in dense urban settings, piece-by-piece retirement of bridges that must stay in service during their own replacement. Environmental recycling, community impact, historical significance, + research opportunities all factor in. A bridge's last project is rarely simpler than its first."}},{"k":"what_happens","v":{"s":"What happens"}},{"k":"how_it_works_4","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"honest_limit_2","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"visual_inspection_nbis","v":{"s":"Visual inspection (NBIS)"}},{"k":"hands_on_access","v":{"s":"Hands-on access"}},{"k":"drone_based_inspection","v":{"s":"Drone-based inspection"}},{"k":"strain_gauges_accelerometers","v":{"s":"Strain gauges + accelerometers"}},{"k":"distributed_fiber_optic_sensing","v":{"s":"Distributed fiber-optic sensing"}},{"k":"ground_penetrating_radar_ultrasonics","v":{"s":"Ground-penetrating radar + ultrasonics"}},{"k":"when_shm_fails_the_genoa_case","v":{"s":"When SHM fails — the Genoa case"}},{"k":"ai_machine_learning_in_inspection","v":{"s":"AI + machine learning in inspection"}},{"k":"bridge_inspection_structural_health_mo","v":{"s":"🔍 Bridge inspection + Structural Health Monitoring (SHM)"}},{"k":"a_bridge_is_not_designed_once_left_alo","v":{"s":"A bridge is not designed once + left alone. It is inspected on a defined cycle, and increasingly, it is monitored continuously through sensors, drones, and AI-assisted analysis. The shift from \"inspect every 2 years\" to \"monitor in real time\" is one of the major operational changes in infrastructure engineering of the past 30 years. The technology is genuinely powerful + has clear limits."}},{"k":"technique_how_it_works","v":{"s":"Technique + how it works"}},{"k":"honest_limit_3","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"the_institutional_reality","v":{"s":"The institutional reality: "}},{"k":"the_technical_tools_for_bridge_monitor","v":{"s":"The technical tools for bridge monitoring have advanced enormously in the past 20 years. The institutional capacity to USE them — funding for sensor installation, trained personnel for data analysis, clear chains of responsibility, political will to close + repair structures when warning signs appear — has advanced less. Most major bridge tragedies of the past 50 years (Silver, Mianus, I-35W, Morandi/Genoa, Pittsburgh Fern Hollow) have been institutional failures as much as technical ones."}},{"k":"permafrost_thaw","v":{"s":"Permafrost + thaw"}},{"k":"ice_loads_ice_impact","v":{"s":"Ice loads + ice impact"}},{"k":"sustained_high_wind_sites","v":{"s":"Sustained high-wind sites"}},{"k":"high_seismic_zones","v":{"s":"High-seismic zones"}},{"k":"coastal_salt_corrosion","v":{"s":"Coastal + salt corrosion"}},{"k":"wildfire_exposed_crossings","v":{"s":"Wildfire-exposed crossings"}},{"k":"remote_indigenous_territory_crossings","v":{"s":"Remote + Indigenous-territory crossings"}},{"k":"bridges_in_extreme_environments","v":{"s":"🌡️ Bridges in extreme environments"}},{"k":"most_bridge_engineering_education_assu","v":{"s":"Most bridge-engineering education assumes a temperate climate, stable soil, modest wind, and a willing nearby supplier. Real bridges get built in conditions that violate all of those assumptions. Permafrost. Sea ice. Sustained high winds. Earthquake faults. Salt spray. Wildfire-prone forests. Remote sites a thousand kilometers from the nearest steel mill. Each one demands specific design responses + an honest reckoning with the limits."}},{"k":"the_challenge","v":{"s":"The challenge"}},{"k":"engineering_responses","v":{"s":"Engineering responses"}},{"k":"honest_limits_2","v":{"s":"Honest limits"}},{"k":"rivets","v":{"s":"Rivets"}},{"k":"high_strength_bolts","v":{"s":"High-strength bolts"}},{"k":"welds","v":{"s":"Welds"}},{"k":"five_types_of_force_matter_for_structu","v":{"s":"Five types of force matter for structural engineering. Every part of every bridge experiences some combination of these."}},{"k":"materials_that_handle_it_well","v":{"s":"Materials that handle it well"}},{"k":"watch_out_for_2","v":{"s":"Watch out for"}},{"k":"in_seismic_regions_pacific_rim_much_of","v":{"s":"In seismic regions (Pacific Rim, much of the western US, Japan, Chile, New Zealand), bridges must survive earthquakes. The ground SHAKES horizontally and vertically; the bridge's mass becomes a hammer driven against itself by inertia. Modern seismic design uses three main strategies: STRENGTH (don't break), DUCTILITY (deform plastically without breaking), and ISOLATION (decouple the bridge from the ground motion)."}},{"k":"earthquake_magnitude_m_w","v":{"s":"Earthquake magnitude (M_w)"}},{"k":"distance_from_epicenter_km","v":{"s":"Distance from epicenter (km)"}},{"k":"bridge_weight_kn","v":{"s":"Bridge weight (kN)"}},{"k":"peak_ground_acceleration_pga","v":{"s":"Peak ground acceleration (PGA)"}},{"k":"mmi_intensity","v":{"s":"MMI intensity"}},{"k":"lateral_force_on_bridge","v":{"s":"Lateral force on bridge"}},{"k":"energy_release","v":{"s":"Energy release"}},{"k":"three_seismic_design_strategies","v":{"s":"Three seismic design strategies: "}},{"k":"design_the_structure_to_elastically_re","v":{"s":"Design the structure to elastically resist seismic forces. Works for moderate quakes but becomes uneconomic for major ones — a strength-only bridge would need to be extraordinarily massive."}},{"k":"ductility_the_modern_approach","v":{"s":"Ductility (the modern approach): "}},{"k":"design_specific_elements_to_yield_defo","v":{"s":"Design specific elements to YIELD + DEFORM plastically during major quakes — absorbing energy without rupturing. Plastic hinges in columns are common. Steel works wonderfully here; brittle materials (cast iron, plain concrete) do NOT. The bridge sustains damage but doesn't collapse."}},{"k":"base_isolation","v":{"s":"Base isolation: "}},{"k":"place_the_bridge_superstructure_on_lea","v":{"s":"Place the bridge superstructure on lead-rubber bearings or sliding bearings. The ground shakes; the bearings absorb most of the motion; the bridge above moves much less. Most aggressive new approach. Common in Japanese + Chilean + California bridges."}},{"k":"akashi_kaiky_1995_kobe_earthquake_mid_","v":{"s":"Akashi-Kaikyō (1995 Kobe earthquake mid-construction): "}},{"k":"the_akashi_kaiky_bridge_was_being_buil","v":{"s":"The Akashi-Kaikyō Bridge was being built when the M6.9 Hyogoken-Nanbu (Kobe) earthquake hit on January 17, 1995. The two towers moved 1 METER farther apart. Engineers redesigned the deck mid-construction to accommodate the new geometry. The bridge survived + opened in 1998. A real-world case study in seismic resilience under extreme + unpredictable conditions."}},{"k":"cypress_street_viaduct_1989_loma_priet","v":{"s":"Cypress Street Viaduct (1989 Loma Prieta): "}},{"k":"a_double_decker_freeway_in_oakland_bui","v":{"s":"A double-decker freeway in Oakland built in the 1950s lacked the ductile detailing required by modern codes. When the M6.9 Loma Prieta earthquake hit, columns sheared, the upper deck collapsed onto the lower deck. 42 people died — the deadliest individual structural failure of the quake. The disaster accelerated retrofitting + replacement of pre-1971 California bridges. Many similar viaducts have since been demolished + replaced (or extensively retrofitted with steel jackets around columns + base isolators)."}},{"k":"strain_gauges","v":{"s":"Strain gauges"}},{"k":"fiber_bragg_gratings_fbg","v":{"s":"Fiber Bragg gratings (FBG)"}},{"k":"accelerometers","v":{"s":"Accelerometers"}},{"k":"gps_displacement_sensors","v":{"s":"GPS displacement sensors"}},{"k":"corrosion_sensors","v":{"s":"Corrosion sensors"}},{"k":"temperature_sensors","v":{"s":"Temperature sensors"}},{"k":"modern_bridges_are_increasingly_instru","v":{"s":"Modern bridges are increasingly INSTRUMENTED — equipped with hundreds or thousands of sensors that report condition continuously. \"Structural Health Monitoring\" (SHM) catches early signs of trouble that human inspection would miss. Sensors don't replace inspectors; they augment them with 24/7 measurements of forces, motions, temperatures, corrosion that visible inspection can't reach."}},{"k":"what_it_measures","v":{"s":"What it measures"}},{"k":"how_it_works_5","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"real_world_examples","v":{"s":"Real-world examples"}},{"k":"why_it_matters","v":{"s":"Why it matters"}},{"k":"from_data_to_decisions","v":{"s":"From data to decisions: "}},{"k":"raw_sensor_data_is_useless_without_ana","v":{"s":"Raw sensor data is useless without analysis. Modern SHM systems use machine-learning models trained on the bridge's \"normal\" behavior to flag anomalies — e.g., a particular vibration mode shifting frequency, suggesting a connection is loosening. The data pipeline: sensors → edge computing on bridge → cloud database → AI analysis → maintenance team alerts. Several startups (Resensys, Bridge Diagnostics, others) sell SHM-as-a-service."}},{"k":"i_35w_mississippi_river_bridge_collaps","v":{"s":"I-35W Mississippi River Bridge collapse (2007): "}},{"k":"the_minneapolis_bridge_collapsed_durin","v":{"s":"The Minneapolis bridge collapsed during evening rush hour, killing 13 + injuring 145. Investigation showed a design flaw (undersized gusset plates) combined with construction loads (resurfacing materials piled on the deck). The replacement bridge (St. Anthony Falls Bridge, 2008) was instrumented with 323 sensors during construction — strain gauges, accelerometers, temperature sensors, corrosion sensors. It is one of the most heavily-monitored bridges in the world. Lessons from the collapse drove federal investment in SHM technology across US bridges."}},{"k":"the_members_are_usually_not_what_fails","v":{"s":"The members are usually not what fails — the connections between them are. Hyatt Regency (1981), Silver Bridge (1967), Tay Bridge (1879) were all connection failures, not material failures. Modern bridge engineering pays as much attention to joints as to members."}},{"k":"history","v":{"s":"History"}},{"k":"how_it_works_6","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"how_it_fails","v":{"s":"How it fails"}},{"k":"still_used","v":{"s":"Still used"}},{"k":"the_hyatt_regency_lesson","v":{"s":"The Hyatt Regency lesson: "}},{"k":"the_1981_walkway_collapse_was_a_connec","v":{"s":"The 1981 walkway collapse was a connection failure. A late design change replaced one long rod (suspending both walkways) with two shorter rods (each walkway hung from the next). The change DOUBLED the load on the upper connection. No one re-engineered. 114 dead, 216 injured. Engineer of record carries final responsibility — both lead engineers lost their licenses. Now required reading in engineering ethics curricula."}},{"k":"why_bridges_are_seismic_hard","v":{"s":"Why bridges are seismic-hard"}},{"k":"how_ground_motion_works","v":{"s":"How ground motion works"}},{"k":"loma_prieta_caltrans_response","v":{"s":"Loma Prieta + Caltrans response"}},{"k":"base_isolation_2","v":{"s":"Base isolation"}},{"k":"energy_dissipation_dampers","v":{"s":"Energy dissipation + dampers"}},{"k":"capacity_design_fuses","v":{"s":"Capacity design (fuses)"}},{"k":"restrainer_cables_seat_extensions","v":{"s":"Restrainer cables + seat extensions"}},{"k":"liquefaction_foundation_engineering","v":{"s":"Liquefaction + foundation engineering"}},{"k":"codes_their_limits","v":{"s":"Codes + their limits"}},{"k":"seismic_engineering_for_bridges","v":{"s":"🌎 Seismic engineering for bridges"}},{"k":"bridges_in_seismic_zones_are_designed_","v":{"s":"Bridges in seismic zones are designed to survive earthquakes that would destroy ordinary buildings. The discipline developed mostly after the 1971 San Fernando earthquake + matured after Loma Prieta 1989 + Kobe 1995 made the problem unmissable. Modern seismic bridge engineering combines base isolation, energy dissipation, capacity design, and foundation engineering — all calibrated to specific seismic-hazard predictions for the site."}},{"k":"how_it_works_7","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"honest_limit_4","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"a_maine_note","v":{"s":"A Maine note: "}},{"k":"maine_is_in_a_low_seismic_hazard_zone_","v":{"s":"Maine is in a LOW seismic-hazard zone but not zero. The 1755 Cape Ann earthquake (estimated M 6.0-6.3) shook Boston + Maine; magnetic-anomaly + paleoseismic studies suggest similar events recur every few centuries. Maine + New England bridges use the AASHTO Seismic Zone 1 provisions (minimal seismic design). The Bridge to Treasure Island in Portland + the Casco Bay Bridge are designed for the local Zone 1 hazard. If you want to see major seismic engineering up close, the Penobscot Narrows Bridge (1996) + the recent I-295 over the Kennebec used some seismic-resistant detailing but at much lower intensity than California or Cascadia subduction zone designs would require."}},{"k":"why_bridges_need_wind_testing","v":{"s":"Why bridges need wind testing"}},{"k":"a_field_forged_by_failure","v":{"s":"A field forged by failure"}},{"k":"section_model_tests","v":{"s":"Section model tests"}},{"k":"full_bridge_aeroelastic_models","v":{"s":"Full-bridge aeroelastic models"}},{"k":"computational_les_validation","v":{"s":"Computational + LES validation"}},{"k":"aerodynamic_countermeasures","v":{"s":"Aerodynamic countermeasures"}},{"k":"test_facilities_around_the_world","v":{"s":"Test facilities around the world"}},{"k":"when_local_bridges_need_testing","v":{"s":"When local bridges need testing"}},{"k":"wind_tunnel_testing_of_long_span_bridg","v":{"s":"🌪️ Wind tunnel testing of long-span bridges"}},{"k":"a_long_span_bridge_that_has_never_been","v":{"s":"A long-span bridge that has never been in a wind tunnel is a long-span bridge that has skipped a fundamental design check. The discipline was forged in the wake of the 1940 Tacoma Narrows collapse + has matured into one of the most distinctive specialties in civil engineering. Every major suspension + cable-stayed bridge built since the 1970s has been wind-tunnel tested before construction."}},{"k":"what_it_is_how_it_works","v":{"s":"What it is + how it works"}},{"k":"honest_limit_5","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"a_classroom_demonstration","v":{"s":"A classroom demonstration: "}},{"k":"students_can_build_a_simple_section_mo","v":{"s":"Students can build a simple section-model wind tunnel from a box fan, a length of cardboard for an airfoil-like deck cross-section, and rubber bands as springs. Hang the cross-section on rubber bands in the airflow + slowly increase the fan speed. Bluff-shaped sections (rectangular cardboard) flutter at relatively low wind speeds; streamlined sections (curved + tapered) remain stable at higher speeds. This is exactly the same principle that distinguished the original Tacoma deck from modern long-span bridge cross-sections. The Khan Academy + Bridge Design + Engineering YouTube channels have demonstrations."}},{"k":"1_anchorages_first","v":{"s":"1. Anchorages first"}},{"k":"2_towers_saddles","v":{"s":"2. Towers + saddles"}},{"k":"3_catwalks_pilot_ropes","v":{"s":"3. Catwalks + pilot ropes"}},{"k":"4_cable_spinning_the_magic","v":{"s":"4. Cable spinning (the magic)"}},{"k":"5_aerial_spun_vs_ppws","v":{"s":"5. Aerial Spun vs PPWS"}},{"k":"6_hangers_suspenders","v":{"s":"6. Hangers + suspenders"}},{"k":"7_deck_erection","v":{"s":"7. Deck erection"}},{"k":"8_final_tensioning_acceptance","v":{"s":"8. Final tensioning + acceptance"}},{"k":"how_a_long_span_suspension_bridge_is_a","v":{"s":"🏗️ How a long-span suspension bridge is actually built"}},{"k":"you_can_hold_a_piece_of_cable_in_your_","v":{"s":"You can hold a piece of cable in your hand: it is a few millimeters of high-tensile steel wire. A long-span suspension bridge's main cable contains tens of thousands of such wires, bundled, compressed, wrapped, and tensioned to carry 50,000+ tons. The CONSTRUCTION SEQUENCE is one of the most remarkable choreographies in modern engineering — each step depending on the previous + impossible without it."}},{"k":"what_happens_2","v":{"s":"What happens"}},{"k":"physics_structural_logic","v":{"s":"Physics + structural logic"}},{"k":"the_roebling_lineage","v":{"s":"The Roebling lineage: "}},{"k":"john_a_roebling_invented_the_aerial_sp","v":{"s":"John A. Roebling invented the aerial-spinning method while building the 1855 Niagara Falls railroad suspension bridge — the technique remains essentially unchanged 170 years later. He died in 1869 from injuries sustained during early construction of the Brooklyn Bridge; his son Washington took over + suffered decompression sickness (\"caisson disease\") supervising the pier sinking. His wife Emily Roebling effectively managed the project for the next decade. The Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883 + still carries traffic today."}},{"k":"aerodynamic_flutter_demo","v":{"s":"💨 Aerodynamic flutter demo"}},{"k":"move_the_wind_speed_slider_watch_how_t","v":{"s":"Move the wind speed slider. Watch how the deck behaves. The open H-shaped cross-section of the original Tacoma deck caused alternating lift forces, and the bridge tore itself apart at moderate wind speeds. Modern bridges use closed or aerodynamic cross-sections that don't flutter."}},{"k":"tacoma_narrows_flutter_animation","v":{"s":"Tacoma Narrows flutter animation"}},{"k":"wind","v":{"s":"Wind →"}},{"k":"wind_speed","v":{"s":"Wind speed"}},{"k":"wind_speed_in_miles_per_hour","v":{"s":"Wind speed in miles per hour"}},{"k":"0_mph","v":{"s":"0 mph"}},{"k":"80_mph","v":{"s":"80 mph"}},{"k":"resonant_flutter","v":{"s":"Resonant flutter: "}},{"k":"the_deck_has_entered_an_aeroelastic_fl","v":{"s":"The deck has entered an aeroelastic flutter mode. Each oscillation creates lift forces that amplify the next oscillation — a positive feedback loop. Unrestrained, it grows until structural failure. The historical Tacoma Narrows collapsed at ~42 mph in 1940."}},{"k":"below_the_critical_flutter_speed_moder","v":{"s":"Below the critical flutter speed. Modern bridges have streamlined or trussed cross-sections that prevent flutter at any wind speed they're likely to encounter. Wind tunnel testing is now mandatory for major bridges."}},{"k":"pedestrian_synchronization_demo","v":{"s":"🚶 Pedestrian synchronization demo"}},{"k":"move_the_pedestrian_count_slider_each_","v":{"s":"Move the pedestrian-count slider. Each walker on the bridge applies a small lateral push at ~1 Hz (half their step rate). At low density, the pushes are randomly phased + cancel out. Above the critical density (~160 on the London north span), walkers UNCONSCIOUSLY synchronize their steps with the swaying bridge, amplifying the motion."}},{"k":"millennium_bridge_pedestrian_synchroni","v":{"s":"Millennium Bridge pedestrian synchronization animation"}},{"k":"pedestrians_on_the_bridge","v":{"s":"Pedestrians on the bridge"}},{"k":"number_of_pedestrians","v":{"s":"Number of pedestrians"}},{"k":"0_people","v":{"s":"0 people"}},{"k":"600_people","v":{"s":"600 people"}},{"k":"locked_synchronization","v":{"s":"Locked synchronization: "}},{"k":"pedestrians_are_now_unconsciously_phas","v":{"s":"Pedestrians are now unconsciously phase-locked with the bridge's natural lateral mode. Each step adds to the swaying. The bridge wobbles violently. At 8-10 mm visible amplitude, panicked pedestrians grip the railing + slow down — actually making it worse by sustaining synchronization. London opening day reached ~70 mm amplitude."}},{"k":"synchronization_beginning","v":{"s":"Synchronization beginning: "}},{"k":"bridge_motion_is_becoming_perceptible_","v":{"s":"Bridge motion is becoming perceptible. Some pedestrians are unconsciously adjusting their gait. If more people arrive or stay, locked synchronization will develop."}},{"k":"below_the_critical_pedestrian_density_","v":{"s":"Below the critical pedestrian density. Random gait phasing cancels out laterally. Modern pedestrian bridges include tuned mass dampers + lateral viscous dampers to suppress this even at high density — the Millennium retrofit added ~87 dampers total."}},{"k":"engineering_is_learned_at_least_as_muc","v":{"s":"Engineering is learned at least as much from failures as from successes. The collapse of Tacoma Narrows changed how every bridge in the world is designed; the success of Brooklyn proved suspension bridges could last centuries."}},{"k":"roman_stone_arch","v":{"s":"Roman stone arch"}},{"k":"mesopotamian_earliest_bridges","v":{"s":"Mesopotamian + earliest bridges"}},{"k":"inca_rope_suspension_bridges","v":{"s":"Inca rope suspension bridges"}},{"k":"chinese_ancient_bridges","v":{"s":"Chinese ancient bridges"}},{"k":"islamic_golden_age_bridges","v":{"s":"Islamic Golden Age bridges"}},{"k":"medieval_european_bridges","v":{"s":"Medieval European bridges"}},{"k":"mughal_india_bridges_waterworks","v":{"s":"Mughal India bridges + waterworks"}},{"k":"what_the_ancients_knew","v":{"s":"What the ancients knew"}},{"k":"ancient_traditional_bridge_engineering","v":{"s":"🏛️ Ancient + traditional bridge engineering"}},{"k":"modern_bridge_engineering_is_the_inher","v":{"s":"Modern bridge engineering is the inheritor of at least 4000 years of trial-and-error + cultural transmission. Several pre-modern bridge traditions produced structures that still carry traffic, span rivers, + serve communities today — sometimes outperforming their 20th-century successors. Honoring these traditions is intellectual honesty AND practical: ancient engineering has lessons modern engineering is rediscovering."}},{"k":"what_was_built","v":{"s":"What was built"}},{"k":"how_it_worked","v":{"s":"How it worked"}},{"k":"legacy_lessons","v":{"s":"Legacy + lessons"}},{"k":"bailey_bridge","v":{"s":"Bailey bridge"}},{"k":"military_pontoon_ribbon_bridge","v":{"s":"Military pontoon (ribbon) bridge"}},{"k":"medium_girder_bridge_mgb","v":{"s":"Medium Girder Bridge (MGB)"}},{"k":"floating_log_improvised_bridges","v":{"s":"Floating-log + improvised bridges"}},{"k":"puc_vr_pont_utilisation_civile_civil_u","v":{"s":"PUC-VR (Pont à Utilisation Civile / civil-use pontoon)"}},{"k":"tactical_floating_causeway_inls","v":{"s":"Tactical Floating Causeway / INLS"}},{"k":"what_emergency_bridges_teach_engineers","v":{"s":"What emergency bridges teach engineers"}},{"k":"military_emergency_bridges","v":{"s":"⚒️ Military + emergency bridges"}},{"k":"most_of_the_world_s_bridge_engineering","v":{"s":"Most of the world's bridge engineering research between 1900 + 1990 happened in military contexts. The Bailey, Medium Girder, and ribbon-pontoon systems were designed to keep armies supplied across destroyed rivers — and now keep civilian communities connected after floods, earthquakes, hurricanes, and infrastructure failures. The modular + accelerated-construction techniques that dominate modern civilian bridge work descend directly from this lineage."}},{"k":"technical_design","v":{"s":"Technical design"}},{"k":"operational_use","v":{"s":"Operational use"}},{"k":"honest_limits_ethics","v":{"s":"Honest limits + ethics"}},{"k":"salginatobel_bridge","v":{"s":"Salginatobel Bridge"}},{"k":"forth_bridge","v":{"s":"Forth Bridge"}},{"k":"akashi_kaiky_bridge","v":{"s":"Akashi Kaikyō Bridge"}},{"k":"sundial_bridge","v":{"s":"Sundial Bridge"}},{"k":"menai_suspension_bridge","v":{"s":"Menai Suspension Bridge"}},{"k":"erasmusbrug","v":{"s":"Erasmusbrug"}},{"k":"the_iron_bridge","v":{"s":"The Iron Bridge"}},{"k":"bridge_aesthetics_iconic_designs_throu","v":{"s":"🌉 Bridge aesthetics — iconic designs through history"}},{"k":"a_bridge_can_be_ugly_and_still_work_a_","v":{"s":"A bridge can be ugly and still work. A bridge can be beautiful and still fail. The most-loved bridges in history tend to do BOTH: they earn their beauty by making their structural logic legible, by serving for generations, and by carrying meaning beyond their crossing function."}},{"k":"a_working_definition_of_bridge_beauty","v":{"s":"A working definition of bridge beauty: "}},{"k":"a_bridge_is_beautiful_when_its_form_ma","v":{"s":"A bridge is beautiful when its form makes its forces visible, when it suits its place, when it serves its community for generations, and when it ages without shame. None of those are decoration. They are all structural, social, and ethical decisions made at the design table."}},{"k":"the_honest_critique","v":{"s":"The honest critique: "}},{"k":"some_iconic_bridges_win_awards_for_the","v":{"s":"Some \"iconic\" bridges win awards for the architect and burden the community with maintenance costs the original budget did not contain (several Calatrava bridges have faced lawsuits over this). The reverse is also true: many beloved century-old bridges were not considered beautiful when new (the Eiffel Tower was originally hated by Parisian artists). Time, use, and care are part of what makes a bridge become iconic — not just the original sketch."}},{"k":"engineering_is_iterative_you_design_te","v":{"s":"Engineering is iterative. You design, test, fail, learn, redesign. Failure is information, not defeat. Every working bridge in the world is the descendant of many failures."}},{"k":"what_span_do_you_need_to_cross_what_lo","v":{"s":"What span do you need to cross? What load (pedestrians? cars? trains?)? What materials are available locally? What's your budget?"}},{"k":"sketch_3_different_bridge_types_you_co","v":{"s":"Sketch 3 different bridge types you could use. Don't commit yet."}},{"k":"pick_the_most_promising_use_the_stress","v":{"s":"Pick the most promising. Use the Stress Test tab to size it. Calculate cost. Identify what could go wrong (wind, ice, overload, fatigue, foundation problems)."}},{"k":"in_the_simulation_build_it_in_real_lif","v":{"s":"In the simulation, build it. In real life, a physical model from cardboard, popsicle sticks, or 3D-printed parts."}},{"k":"apply_loads_in_the_simulation_for_phys","v":{"s":"Apply loads in the simulation. For physical models: weights, books, water. Where does it fail first? Why?"}},{"k":"use_what_you_saw_to_redesign_add_mater","v":{"s":"Use what you saw to redesign. Add material where stress is highest; reduce where stress is low. Iterate."}},{"k":"where_the_money_comes_from","v":{"s":"Where the money comes from"}},{"k":"federal_funding_mechanics","v":{"s":"Federal funding mechanics"}},{"k":"toll_bridges_revenue","v":{"s":"Toll bridges + revenue"}},{"k":"procurement_models","v":{"s":"Procurement models"}},{"k":"environmental_justice_community_impact","v":{"s":"Environmental justice + community impact"}},{"k":"life_cycle_cost_analysis","v":{"s":"Life-cycle cost analysis"}},{"k":"why_bridges_collapse_from_neglect","v":{"s":"Why bridges collapse from neglect"}},{"k":"who_decides_who_benefits","v":{"s":"Who decides + who benefits"}},{"k":"bridge_financing_politics_procurement","v":{"s":"💰 Bridge financing, politics, + procurement"}},{"k":"every_bridge_has_an_engineering_story_","v":{"s":"Every bridge has an engineering story + a money story. Where the dollars come from, who decides what gets built, how contracts are awarded, who benefits from the project, who pays the maintenance — these questions shape what bridges actually exist + which communities get to use them. Engineering does not happen in a political vacuum + students who understand only the technical side will struggle to advocate for the bridges their communities need."}},{"k":"how_it_works_8","v":{"s":"How it works"}},{"k":"honest_limit_6","v":{"s":"Honest limit"}},{"k":"for_maine_students_families","v":{"s":"For Maine students + families: "}},{"k":"maine_spends_about_700m_year_on_transp","v":{"s":"Maine spends about $700M/year on transportation infrastructure across all modes; bridges are a major slice of that. The Maine DOT publishes its annual Work Plan + 3-Year Transportation Improvement Program online (maine.gov/mdot). Major recent projects: the Penobscot Narrows Bridge replacement (2006, $85M), the Memorial Bridge / Sarah Mildred Long Bridge replacements in Portsmouth-Kittery (~$170M each), ongoing Maine Turnpike widenings + bridge replacements. Maine resisted highway tolling outside the Turnpike + has relied on federal + state gas-tax funding plus IIJA money. Students who care about Maine infrastructure can engage at MPO public meetings (Portland Area Comprehensive Transportation System, Greater Portland Council of Governments) + by writing to the Maine DOT Commissioner."}},{"k":"why_abc_exists","v":{"s":"Why ABC exists"}},{"k":"conventional_cast_in_place_bridge_cons","v":{"s":"Conventional cast-in-place bridge construction takes months to years and closes a road or rail line for the entire duration. Long closures wreck communities (longer commutes, lost business, blocked emergency response, increased emissions from detours). The Federal Highway Administration (FHWA) coined the term \"Accelerated Bridge Construction\" (ABC) in the mid-2000s to capture techniques that compress the on-site closure to days or sometimes hours. The motivating insight: most of the bridge can be built OFF-site, in parallel with traffic continuing on-site, and then installed in a single short closure window."}},{"k":"prefabricated_bridge_elements_pbes","v":{"s":"Prefabricated bridge elements (PBES)"}},{"k":"prefabricated_bridge_elements_systems_","v":{"s":"Prefabricated Bridge Elements + Systems (PBES) are the building blocks of ABC. Common elements: prestressed precast deck panels, full-depth precast deck slabs, precast concrete pier caps, precast piers + columns, prefabricated steel girders with deck panels already attached, modular abutment systems, even entire prefabricated short-span bridges. They are manufactured in factory conditions (controlled temperature + humidity, full quality control, tight tolerances), then shipped to site on flatbed trucks or rail cars. Field work becomes assembly + connection rather than form-pouring-curing."}},{"k":"self_propelled_modular_transporters_sp","v":{"s":"Self-propelled modular transporters (SPMTs)"}},{"k":"spmts_are_massive_multi_axle_hydraulic","v":{"s":"SPMTs are massive multi-axle hydraulically-leveled platforms that can carry entire bridge spans — sometimes thousands of tons — and roll them into place along a highway closure. Each axle has its own steering + hydraulic suspension, so the SPMT can crab sideways, rotate in place, and self-level over uneven ground. Multiple SPMT trailers can be linked together. The Massena Bridge replacement (Massachusetts, 2014) used SPMTs to roll a 416-ton bridge span ~5 miles from a staging area + lower it onto its piers in a single 55-hour weekend closure. Total bridge replacement: 4 days. Conventional construction would have taken 6+ months of partial closure."}},{"k":"lateral_slide_in_construction","v":{"s":"Lateral slide-in construction"}},{"k":"instead_of_using_spmts_the_new_bridge_","v":{"s":"Instead of using SPMTs, the new bridge is built alongside the existing bridge on temporary supports. During a short closure, the old bridge is demolished + the new bridge is slid sideways into its final position on hydraulic jacks or rollers, typically 20-40 feet. The Wells Avenue Bridge replacement (Newton, MA, 2009) was one of the first US slide-ins; the Pulaski Skyway in NJ used a slide-in for one span in 2014. The Massachusetts I-93 fast-14 project (2011) replaced 14 bridges in 14 weekends using slide-in + other ABC techniques, saving an estimated 4+ years of conventional construction time."}},{"k":"geosynthetic_reinforced_soil_grs_ibs","v":{"s":"Geosynthetic Reinforced Soil (GRS-IBS)"}},{"k":"grs_ibs_geosynthetic_reinforced_soil_i","v":{"s":"GRS-IBS (Geosynthetic Reinforced Soil — Integrated Bridge System) replaces conventional reinforced-concrete abutments + piles with alternating layers of compacted granular fill + geosynthetic reinforcement (essentially industrial-strength plastic mesh). The result is a stable abutment that supports the bridge directly, with no pile foundations needed. GRS abutments can be built in days using standard earthwork crews. They cost 25-60% less than conventional abutments, eliminate the joint between superstructure + approach (a major maintenance headache), and are particularly well-suited to short-span single-lane bridges. FHWA reports 200+ GRS-IBS bridges built in the US since 2005, with very low maintenance costs."}},{"k":"standard_abc_bridge_designs","v":{"s":"Standard ABC bridge designs"}},{"k":"several_states_have_developed_pre_engi","v":{"s":"Several states have developed pre-engineered standard bridges for the most common short-span applications. The Vermont Agency of Transportation's ABC standard plans cover spans from 25-80 feet, with detailed precast deck + abutment specs that any local contractor can build. The Federal Lands Highway Program has similar plans for remote sites. The advantage: instead of paying for a custom design ($100K+ engineering), a small-town bridge replacement can use a standard plan ($5-10K design fee) + standard precast pieces. Lead time drops from 18 months to 6 months for a typical rural bridge."}},{"k":"success_cases","v":{"s":"Success cases"}},{"k":"a_massachusetts_fast_14_i_93_2011_repl","v":{"s":"(a) Massachusetts Fast-14 (I-93, 2011): replaced 14 bridges over 14 consecutive weekend closures. Each weekend: Friday 11 PM close, Monday 5 AM open. Total project: 10 weeks vs estimated 4 years conventional. Massive media coverage; widely cited as the proof-of-concept event for US ABC. (b) Utah Riverdale Road bridge over I-84 (2007): first US use of SPMTs to slide an entire bridge into place. (c) Iowa Lower Mud River bridge (2010): demonstrator GRS-IBS bridge built in 23 working days at 30% lower cost. (d) Florida 17th Street Causeway Bridge approaches (2009-2011): SPMT-installed bridge segments with overnight closures. ABC has shipped at scale in the US for nearly 20 years now."}},{"k":"what_abc_cannot_do_well","v":{"s":"What ABC cannot do well"}},{"k":"abc_is_not_a_universal_answer_limitati","v":{"s":"ABC is NOT a universal answer. Limitations: (a) Cost. The direct construction cost is higher. For rural bridges with low traffic, the user-cost savings may not justify the premium. (b) Lead time. Precast elements need to be ordered + manufactured. ABC saves on-site time but adds front-end time. Emergency bridge replacement (after a hurricane or earthquake) may not have time for prefab. (c) Geometry. Many old bridges have unusual skews, super-elevations, or non-standard cross-sections that don't match factory-standard products. Heavy customization erodes the ABC cost advantage. (d) Maintenance access. Some ABC connections (UHPC closure pours, grouted shear keys) are difficult to inspect + maintain in service. The connection details matter as much as the elements."}},{"k":"accelerated_bridge_construction_abc_pr","v":{"s":"🏭 Accelerated Bridge Construction (ABC) + prefab"}},{"k":"modern_bridges_do_not_have_to_take_yea","v":{"s":"Modern bridges do not have to take years to build. Accelerated Bridge Construction — using prefabricated factory-made elements installed during short on-site closures — has matured into a mainstream practice in the past 20 years. A bridge that would have closed a road for 6 months can now be replaced in a single weekend. The cost premium is real; the user-cost savings are typically larger."}},{"k":"honest_limit_7","v":{"s":"Honest limit: "}},{"k":"the_big_idea","v":{"s":"The big idea"}},{"k":"you_cannot_solve_the_exact_equations_o","v":{"s":"You cannot solve the exact equations of elasticity for a real bridge — the geometry is too complicated. So you do something clever: cut the bridge into thousands or millions of small simple pieces (the \"elements\"), each of which you CAN solve. Triangles and tetrahedra are the most common. Each element has nodes at its corners, and the displacement inside the element is interpolated linearly (or quadratically) from the node displacements. Force balance at each node gives one equation per degree of freedom. The result is a giant system of linear equations: [K]{u} = {F}, where K is the global stiffness matrix, u is the unknown nodal displacements, and F is the applied loads. Solve it (numerically, on a computer) and you have approximate displacement everywhere — and from there, approximate strain and stress."}},{"k":"how_fea_was_invented","v":{"s":"How FEA was invented"}},{"k":"the_conceptual_foundations_come_from_r","v":{"s":"The conceptual foundations come from Richard Courant's variational methods (1943) and earlier matrix structural analysis (the Slope-Deflection method, 1915). The modern form of FEA was developed in the 1950s-60s primarily for AIRCRAFT analysis (Boeing 707, supersonic transport designs). Key figures: Ray Clough at Berkeley (who coined the term \"finite element method\" in 1960), John Argyris at Imperial College London + Stuttgart, Olek Zienkiewicz at Swansea (who wrote the foundational textbook in 1967, still cited). Bridge engineering adopted FEA in the 1970s once mainframe computers became affordable. By 2000, every major bridge in the world was designed using FEA software (ANSYS, ABAQUS, SAP2000, MIDAS, LUSAS)."}},{"k":"the_mesh_matters","v":{"s":"The mesh matters"}},{"k":"meshing_is_the_engineering_judgment_in","v":{"s":"Meshing is the engineering judgment in FEA. Bad meshes give bad answers — sometimes catastrophically wrong, sometimes subtly wrong, both dangerous. (a) Element shape: very thin \"sliver\" triangles introduce numerical error. Aspect ratios should be modest (ideally <5:1, never >20:1). (b) Mesh density: needs to be fine where stress changes quickly (corners, holes, contact zones) and can be coarse where stress is uniform. (c) Singularities: at sharp re-entrant corners (90° internal corners with zero fillet radius), stress is theoretically infinite. Refining the mesh forever produces ever-larger stress, not convergence. This is a real trap — students sometimes refine indefinitely chasing a number that has no physical meaning."}},{"k":"boundary_conditions_the_model","v":{"s":"Boundary conditions = the model"}},{"k":"a_bridge_model_has_fixed_supports_pinn","v":{"s":"A bridge model has fixed supports (pinned, roller, fixed), applied loads (point, distributed, gravity), and possibly contact / friction / pre-stress. Most FEA errors trace back to boundary conditions. Common mistakes: (a) Over-constraining: clamping every direction at one end forces the model to resist temperature expansion that wouldn't exist in reality. Result: huge artificial thermal stress. (b) Under-constraining: forgetting to fix translation in one direction. The solver fails or gives a rigid-body answer. (c) Applying loads at single nodes: a \"point load\" at one node creates an infinite stress spike (the singularity above). Better: distribute over a small area. (d) Wrong load combinations: dead + live + wind + thermal + seismic, in the wrong combination, can over- or under-predict the critical case."}},{"k":"element_types_you_will_meet","v":{"s":"Element types you will meet"}},{"k":"1_beam_elements_1d_line_elements_with_","v":{"s":"(1) BEAM elements: 1D line elements with bending + axial + sometimes torsion. Use for truss members, beams, frames. Fast. Best for slender members where length >> cross-section. (2) SHELL elements: 2D surface elements with bending. Use for plates, decks, walls, the body of a box girder. Captures both in-plane and out-of-plane behavior. (3) SOLID elements: full 3D bricks or tetrahedra. Use for chunky parts: bearings, pylon bases, weld details, foundations. Most accurate, most expensive. (4) CONTACT elements: model surfaces sliding or separating. Used for bearings, bolted joints, expansion joints. (5) SPRING / LINK elements: model stiffness without geometry — e.g., soil under a footing, cable in a stay."}},{"k":"linear_vs_nonlinear","v":{"s":"Linear vs nonlinear"}},{"k":"a_linear_fea_assumes_a_small_displacem","v":{"s":"A LINEAR FEA assumes (a) small displacements, (b) elastic material (stress proportional to strain, no permanent deformation), (c) boundary conditions don't change during loading. Doubles the load, doubles the displacement and stress everywhere. Most bridge-design work is linear, by code. NONLINEAR FEA relaxes these. Material nonlinearity (plastic yielding, concrete cracking). Geometric nonlinearity (large deflections — e.g., cable-stayed bridges where cable sag changes as load increases). Contact nonlinearity (bearings opening + closing). Time-dependent nonlinearity (concrete creep, steel relaxation). Nonlinear analysis is honest about post-yield behavior but is slower, sensitive to solver settings, and harder to interpret."}},{"k":"how_to_trust_an_fea_result","v":{"s":"How to trust an FEA result"}},{"k":"professional_verification_1_sanity_che","v":{"s":"Professional verification: (1) Sanity check magnitudes — does the maximum stress match a hand calculation within ~20%? (2) Reaction sum — do the support reactions equal the applied loads (force balance)? (3) Symmetry check — if loading + geometry are symmetric, the answer must be symmetric. (4) Mesh convergence — halve the element size; does the answer move <5%? (5) Energy check — is strain energy a smooth function of load (not jumping)? (6) Independent re-analysis — run the model in a second software package. (7) Physical correlation — load test the actual structure; sensors at critical points should match FEA within ~10%."}},{"k":"what_fea_cannot_tell_you","v":{"s":"What FEA cannot tell you"}},{"k":"a_whether_your_design_is_good_fea_tell","v":{"s":"(a) Whether your design is GOOD. FEA tells you stress; you decide if it is acceptable. (b) Whether your materials behave as modeled. Real concrete varies in strength batch-to-batch by 15-25%. Real welds have unknown internal defects. FEA assumes idealized homogeneous material. (c) What will happen at the limit. FEA does not know how the bridge fails — that comes from material science, fracture mechanics, and engineering experience. (d) Long-term behavior. Creep, fatigue, corrosion, weather, traffic patterns, climate change — FEA alone does not capture these; you bolt extra analyses on top. (e) The \"unknown unknown.\" FEA cannot model failure modes you didn't think to include. Tacoma Narrows would not have shown flutter in 1940s FEA because the aerodynamic-elastic coupling wasn't in the model. Every famous bridge collapse has involved a failure mode the designers did not think to analyze."}},{"k":"finite_element_analysis_how_modern_bri","v":{"s":"🧠 Finite Element Analysis — how modern bridges actually get analyzed"}},{"k":"every_bridge_built_today_is_analyzed_w","v":{"s":"Every bridge built today is analyzed with FEA somewhere in its design. It is the workhorse method, the reason engineers can design structures the hand-calculation generation could not. It is also the easiest tool in engineering to use badly. Both are worth understanding."}},{"k":"what_we_should_not_overstate","v":{"s":"What we should not overstate: "}},{"k":"try_it_free","v":{"s":"Try it free: "}},{"k":"calculix_open_source_fea_runs_on_any_l","v":{"s":"CalculiX (open-source FEA, runs on any laptop), Onshape with SimScale plugin (browser-based, free for students), LISA-FEA (free Windows tool with great tutorials for beginners). You can model a simple truss bridge in any of these in an afternoon. Start with the 1D beam elements, hand-check every result, build up from there."}},{"k":"your_answer","v":{"s":"Your answer: "}},{"k":"retake_quiz","v":{"s":"Retake quiz"}},{"k":"load_bearing_discovery","v":{"s":"🔬 Load-bearing discovery"}},{"k":"span_length","v":{"s":"Span length: "}},{"k":"span_length_in_meters","v":{"s":"Span length in meters"}},{"k":"cross_section_area","v":{"s":"Cross-section area: "}},{"k":"cross_section_area_in_square_millimete","v":{"s":"Cross-section area in square millimeters"}},{"k":"log_observation","v":{"s":"📋 Log observation"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_free_text_no_right_ans","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (free text — no right answer):"}},{"k":"what_single_change_is_the_most_efficie","v":{"s":"What single change is the most efficient way to move a FAILED bridge into SAFE? Does doubling span require doubling area? Type your own theory."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_me_questions_to_think_a","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show me questions to think about (no answers)"}},{"k":"open_questions_investigate_by_manipula","v":{"s":"Open questions — investigate by manipulating:"}},{"k":"hold_material_and_area_fixed_sweep_spa","v":{"s":"Hold material and area fixed. Sweep span from 6 to 60 m. At what span does the state flip from SAFE to MARGINAL? Now change material — does the flip-point move?"}},{"k":"pick_a_span_where_the_bridge_fails_wha","v":{"s":"Pick a span where the bridge FAILS. What is cheaper: doubling the cross-section area, or switching to a stronger material? Test both and compare."}},{"k":"some_materials_have_very_high_strength","v":{"s":"Some materials have very high strength but are heavy or expensive in real life. The simulation does not penalize this. What would change about your hypotheses if cost mattered?"}},{"k":"log_4_5_safe_configurations_look_at_th","v":{"s":"Log 4-5 SAFE configurations. Look at the safety factor column. What range do they share? Is there a single threshold or a band?"}},{"k":"in_real_engineering_the_rule_of_thumb_","v":{"s":"In real engineering, the rule of thumb is safety factor ≥ 1.5 for steel structures. Why might the simulation show MARGINAL at sf 1-2 but SAFE only at sf ≥ 2?"}},{"k":"no_answers_will_be_revealed_investigat","v":{"s":"No answers will be revealed. Investigate."}},{"k":"i_think_i_understand_the_trade_offs_le","v":{"s":"I think I understand the trade-offs — let me explain them in my own words"}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words_how_do_span_","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words: how do span, cross-section area, and material strength interact to determine load-bearing capacity? Which one matters most? What is the trade-off a real engineer faces?"}},{"k":"saved_notice_nobody_checked_your_answe","v":{"s":"✓ Saved. Notice — nobody checked your answer. That is what learner-driven inquiry looks like."}},{"k":"design_note_no_numeric_safety_factor_t","v":{"s":"Design note: no numeric safety-factor target, no reveal button, no quiz validation. Safety is shown as a discrete 3-state marker (SAFE / MARGINAL / FAILS), not a continuous gradient — by design, to discourage optimization-gaming behavior. The point is the inquiry, not the number."}},{"k":"bridge_design_specification_sheet","v":{"s":"🖨 Bridge design specification sheet. "}},{"k":"a_one_page_artifact_for_engineering_po","v":{"s":"A one-page artifact for engineering portfolios, design competitions, classroom presentations, or maker-faire submissions. Contains the design parameters, the stress analysis result, the material properties, and your design notes."}},{"k":"print_save_as_pdf","v":{"s":"🖨 Print / Save as PDF"}},{"k":"ngss_ms_ets1_hs_ets1_hs_ps2","v":{"s":"NGSS MS-ETS1 · HS-ETS1 · HS-PS2"}},{"k":"design_parameters","v":{"s":"Design parameters"}},{"k":"stress_analysis_deep_beam_approximatio","v":{"s":"Stress analysis (deep-beam approximation)"}},{"k":"quantities","v":{"s":"Quantities"}},{"k":"design_notes","v":{"s":"Design notes"}},{"k":"honest_limit_of_this_analysis","v":{"s":"Honest limit of this analysis: "}},{"k":"a_deep_beam_approximation_real_bridge_","v":{"s":"A deep-beam approximation. Real bridge design uses method of joints, method of sections, or matrix structural analysis to get exact member forces, then checks every individual member for yield, buckling, fatigue, and connection limits. This tool is for learning, not for actually building a bridge."}},{"k":"sources_ngss_lead_states_2013_hibbeler","v":{"s":"Sources: NGSS Lead States (2013) · Hibbeler, R.C. (2017), Structural Analysis · AASHTO LRFD Bridge Design Specifications · Petroski, H. (1994), Design Paradigms (Tacoma Narrows + Tay Bridge + Hyatt). Printed from AlloFlow STEAM Lab."}},{"k":"bridge_engineering_lab","v":{"s":"Bridge Engineering Lab"}},{"k":"engineering_design_structural_mechanic","v":{"s":"Engineering Design · Structural Mechanics · NGSS HS-ETS1 + HS-PS2"}},{"k":"beam_desc","v":{"s":"The simplest bridge. A horizontal beam supported at each end. The beam is in compression on top and tension on bottom under load."}},{"k":"truss_desc","v":{"s":"A framework of triangles. Triangles can't change shape without breaking a member, so the truss is rigid. Loads are carried as tension or compression along the members — no bending."}},{"k":"arch_desc","v":{"s":"A curve that pushes the load outward and downward into the abutments. The entire arch is in compression."}},{"k":"suspension_desc","v":{"s":"The deck hangs from cables, which hang in a curve between two tall towers. The cables are in tension; the towers are in compression. Anchorages at each end resist the cable tension."}},{"k":"cable_stayed_desc","v":{"s":"Like a suspension bridge but the cables run directly from the towers to the deck (no main suspended cable). More cables, less material, stiffer than a suspension bridge."}},{"k":"wood_desc","v":{"s":"Renewable, easy to work, light. Strong in tension and compression along the grain. Weak across the grain. Sensitive to moisture, fire, insects."}},{"k":"stone_desc","v":{"s":"Very strong in compression, weak in tension. Heavy. Lasts millennia if well-built. Roman arch bridges from 100 BCE still stand."}},{"k":"iron_desc","v":{"s":"The first metal used at scale for bridges. Strong in compression, brittle and weak in tension. Many 19th-century failures."}},{"k":"steel_desc","v":{"s":"Strong in BOTH tension and compression. Ductile (bends before breaking, giving warning). The workhorse of modern bridge engineering."}},{"k":"concrete_desc","v":{"s":"Concrete: strong in compression, weak in tension. Reinforced with steel rebar to carry tension. Pre-stressed concrete uses tensioned cables for even more strength."}},{"k":"composite_desc","v":{"s":"Fibers (carbon, glass, aramid) in a polymer matrix. Light, strong, corrosion-proof. Currently used in repair / strengthening more than new structures."}},{"k":"route_stress","v":{"s":"Stress test"}},{"k":"route_stress_hint","v":{"s":"Change span, loads, and truss style."}},{"k":"route_materials","v":{"s":"Materials"}},{"k":"route_materials_hint","v":{"s":"Compare strength, cost, and density."}},{"k":"route_forces","v":{"s":"Forces"}},{"k":"route_forces_hint","v":{"s":"Read tension, compression, shear, and torsion."}},{"k":"route_cycle","v":{"s":"Design cycle"}},{"k":"route_cycle_hint","v":{"s":"Plan, test, revise, and document."}},{"k":"brief_safe","v":{"s":"Ready for testing"}},{"k":"brief_marginal","v":{"s":"Needs revision"}},{"k":"brief_failed","v":{"s":"Redesign needed"}},{"k":"design_brief","v":{"s":"Design brief"}},{"k":"design_brief_copy","v":{"s":"Start by stress-testing a bridge, then use material and force references only when you need evidence for a redesign."}},{"k":"open_route","v":{"s":"Open"}},{"k":"design_readiness","v":{"s":"Design readiness"}},{"k":"bridge_readiness_visual","v":{"s":"Bridge readiness visual"}},{"k":"status_span","v":{"s":"Span"}},{"k":"status_load","v":{"s":"Load"}},{"k":"status_material","v":{"s":"Material"}},{"k":"status_stress","v":{"s":"Stress"}},{"k":"status_mass","v":{"s":"Mass"}},{"k":"status_cost","v":{"s":"Material cost"}},{"k":"hide_reference_tabs","v":{"s":"Hide reference tabs"}},{"k":"show_reference_tabs","v":{"s":"Show reference tabs"}},{"k":"thickness_moj","v":{"s":", thickness shows magnitude. Forces are solved exactly by the method of joints — every member force satisfies equilibrium at both of its ends."}},{"k":"gl_unavailable","v":{"s":"3D view unavailable on this device — the elevation below shows the same analysis."}},{"k":"gl_loading","v":{"s":"Loading 3D view…"}},{"k":"gl_hint","v":{"s":"Drag — orbit  ·  Scroll — zoom"}},{"k":"design_name","v":{"s":"Bridge design name"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Bridge design explanation"}},{"k":"check_stability","v":{"s":"Stability (buckling)"}},{"k":"check_strength","v":{"s":"Strength (yield)"}},{"k":"gl_buckling","v":{"s":"Buckling"}},{"k":"gl_buckling_d","v":{"s":"When a long thin piece under squeeze suddenly snaps sideways, far below its crushing strength. Press a spaghetti noodle from both ends to feel it."}},{"k":"gl_compression","v":{"s":"Compression"}},{"k":"gl_compression_d","v":{"s":"A squeezing force, like pressing on a soda can. Shown in blue."}},{"k":"gl_governing","v":{"s":"Governing"}},{"k":"gl_governing_d","v":{"s":"Engineer-speak for \"the one that decides\". The governing member is the weakest link; the governing check is whichever fails first."}},{"k":"gl_hint2","v":{"s":"Drag or arrow keys — orbit · Scroll or +/− — zoom"}},{"k":"gl_kn","v":{"s":"kN (kilonewton)"}},{"k":"gl_kn_d","v":{"s":"A unit of force. 1 kN is roughly the weight of a washing machine; a car weighs about 15 kN."}},{"k":"gl_member","v":{"s":"Member / chord / diagonal"}},{"k":"gl_member_d","v":{"s":"Any single piece is a member. The long top and bottom pieces are chords; the zig-zags are diagonals."}},{"k":"gl_mpa","v":{"s":"MPa (megapascal)"}},{"k":"gl_mpa_d","v":{"s":"Pressure: force concentrated on an area. The same push hurts more through a thumbtack than a book cover; that is higher MPa."}},{"k":"gl_sf","v":{"s":"Safety factor"}},{"k":"gl_sf_d","v":{"s":"How many times stronger than needed. 1.0 = barely holds; 2.0 = holds twice the load. Real bridge codes demand 2–6."}},{"k":"gl_slender","v":{"s":"Slenderness"}},{"k":"gl_slender_d","v":{"s":"Length compared to thickness. A pencil is stubby; an uncooked spaghetti noodle is slender. Slender pieces buckle easily."}},{"k":"gl_span","v":{"s":"Span"}},{"k":"gl_span_d","v":{"s":"The open distance the bridge must cross without support underneath."}},{"k":"gl_tension","v":{"s":"Tension"}},{"k":"gl_tension_d","v":{"s":"A pulling force, like a tug-of-war rope. Shown in red."}},{"k":"gl_truss","v":{"s":"Truss"}},{"k":"gl_truss_d","v":{"s":"A frame of triangles. Triangles cannot change shape without changing a side length, which makes them stiff."}},{"k":"gl_yield","v":{"s":"Yield"}},{"k":"gl_yield_d","v":{"s":"The point where a material stops springing back and permanently bends or tears. Passing it is a strength failure."}},{"k":"glossary_title","v":{"s":"The words on this page, in plain language"}},{"k":"hint_angle","v":{"s":"About 45–60° is the sweet spot: steep enough to be short, shallow enough to share the load."}},{"k":"hint_bays","v":{"s":"How many triangles the truss is divided into. More bays = shorter, sturdier pieces, but more joints to build."}},{"k":"hint_bracing","v":{"s":"Cross-ties that stop the top chord flexing sideways. Fewer braces = longer free lengths = easier to buckle. This slider decides most verdicts here."}},{"k":"hint_chord","v":{"s":"The biggest pull or squeeze in the long top/bottom pieces. (1 kN is about the weight of a washing machine.)"}},{"k":"hint_cost","v":{"s":"The engineering game: the CHEAPEST design that is still SAFE wins."}},{"k":"hint_diag","v":{"s":"The biggest force in the zig-zag pieces. Diagonals work hardest near the supports."}},{"k":"hint_height","v":{"s":"A taller truss spreads the load better, like a wider stance. Watch the safety factor rise as you raise it."}},{"k":"hint_length","v":{"s":"All the pieces laid end to end. More length = more material = more cost."}},{"k":"hint_load","v":{"s":"The weight pressing down at each top joint. Imagine trucks parked all along the deck."}},{"k":"hint_mass","v":{"s":"What the bare structure weighs. A bridge must carry itself before it carries anything else."}},{"k":"hint_pcr","v":{"s":"The push at which the weakest strut snaps sideways, like a spaghetti noodle pressed from both ends."}},{"k":"hint_reaction","v":{"s":"How hard each end pushes back up. The two ends together carry the whole load."}},{"k":"hint_slender","v":{"s":"Length divided by thickness. Long and thin = wobbly. Real design codes stop at about 200."}},{"k":"hint_span","v":{"s":"The distance between the two supports. Longer spans are MUCH harder: forces grow fast with span."}},{"k":"hint_stress","v":{"s":"Force squeezed into each bit of material. Must stay well BELOW the yield number beside it."}},{"k":"hint_vehiclepos","v":{"s":"Slide the truck across the bridge and watch the forces chase it."}},{"k":"hint_weakest","v":{"s":"The piece closest to buckling. Find it in the picture above; the whole bridge is only as safe as this one piece."}},{"k":"hint_yield","v":{"s":"The stress where this material starts to permanently bend or tear. Your ceiling."}},{"k":"intro_colors","v":{"s":"In every picture: red pieces are being STRETCHED (tension), blue pieces are being SQUEEZED (compression), and thicker means working harder."}},{"k":"intro_dismiss","v":{"s":"Got it — hide this"}},{"k":"intro_step1","v":{"s":"Read the verdict card below the picture: is this bridge SAFE, MARGINAL, or FAILED, and why?"}},{"k":"intro_step2","v":{"s":"Drag any slider and watch the verdict, the colors, and the numbers respond instantly. Breaking it on purpose is a great way to learn."}},{"k":"intro_step3","v":{"s":"Your goal: a ✓ SAFE bridge for the LOWEST material cost. That trade-off is the whole job of a structural engineer."}},{"k":"intro_title","v":{"s":"Start here"}},{"k":"label_bays","v":{"s":"Bays (triangle sections)"}},{"k":"label_pcr","v":{"s":"Buckling load (P_cr)"}},{"k":"label_reaction","v":{"s":"Push-back at each support"}},{"k":"label_weakest","v":{"s":"Weakest strut"}},{"k":"verdict_failed_buckle_plain","v":{"s":"A long, thin piece would snap SIDEWAYS before the material itself ever failed. That is buckling, and it happens suddenly, with no warning creak."}},{"k":"verdict_failed_yield_plain","v":{"s":"The material itself would be crushed or torn apart. That is a yield failure: too much force through too little material."}},{"k":"verdict_marginal_plain","v":{"s":"It holds, but with less spare strength than real bridge codes demand (a safety factor of at least 2). An engineer would strengthen it before letting traffic on."}},{"k":"verdict_safe_plain","v":{"s":"Two different checks, both passed: the material is strong enough (yield), and no piece is so long and thin that it would snap sideways (buckling)."}},{"k":"bridge_side_elevation_scroll_region","v":{"s":"Bridge side elevation horizontal scroll region"}}],"id":494}},{"k":"calculus","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_explanation_ready","v":{"s":"Explanation ready."}},{"k":"a11y_back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"a11y_calculus_reasoning_pathway","v":{"s":"Calculus reasoning pathway"}},{"k":"a11y_calculus_tool_sections","v":{"s":"Calculus Tool sections"}},{"k":"a11y_overestimate","v":{"s":"OVERestimate"}},{"k":"a11y_underestimate","v":{"s":"UNDERestimate"}},{"k":"a11y_exact_estimate","v":{"s":"Exact estimate"}},{"k":"a11y_reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"a11y_integral_estimate_input","v":{"s":"Integral estimate input"}},{"k":"a11y_compare_estimate_with_exact_integral","v":{"s":"Compare estimate with exact integral"}},{"k":"a11y_optional_integral_estimate_calibration_note","v":{"s":"Optional integral estimate calibration note"}},{"k":"a11y_try_another_estimate","v":{"s":"Try another estimate"}},{"k":"a11y_calculus_error_versus_rectangle_count_chart","v":{"s":"Calculus error versus rectangle count chart"}},{"k":"a11y_antiderivative_x_cubed_coefficient","v":{"s":"Antiderivative x-cubed coefficient"}},{"k":"a11y_antiderivative_x_squared_coefficient","v":{"s":"Antiderivative x-squared coefficient"}},{"k":"a11y_antiderivative_x_coefficient","v":{"s":"Antiderivative x coefficient"}},{"k":"a11y_check","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"a11y_clear_and_try_again","v":{"s":"Clear and try again"}},{"k":"a11y_snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"a11y_tangent_point_x0","v":{"s":"Tangent point x0"}},{"k":"a11y_secant_gap_h","v":{"s":"Secant gap h"}},{"k":"a11y_derivative_x_coefficient","v":{"s":"Derivative x coefficient"}},{"k":"a11y_derivative_constant","v":{"s":"Derivative constant"}},{"k":"a11y_try_again","v":{"s":"Try again"}},{"k":"a11y_exact_integral_value","v":{"s":"Exact integral value"}},{"k":"a11y_mission_1_progress","v":{"s":"Mission 1 progress"}},{"k":"a11y_riemann_sum_error_at_n_equals_4","v":{"s":"Riemann-sum error at n equals 4"}},{"k":"a11y_got_it","v":{"s":"Got it"}},{"k":"a11y_hypothesis_committed_now_measure","v":{"s":"Hypothesis committed; now measure"}},{"k":"a11y_riemann_sum_error_at_n_equals_8","v":{"s":"Riemann-sum error at n equals 8"}},{"k":"a11y_estimated_error_at_n_equals_16","v":{"s":"Estimated error at n equals 16"}},{"k":"a11y_estimate_committed","v":{"s":"Estimate committed"}},{"k":"a11y_measured_error_at_n_equals_16","v":{"s":"Measured error at n equals 16"}},{"k":"a11y_load_f_x_x_squared_on_0_to_3_with_6_subdivision","v":{"s":"Load f(x) = x squared on 0 to 3 with 6 subdivisions"}},{"k":"a11y_hypothesis_committed_collect_evidence","v":{"s":"Hypothesis committed; collect evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"a11y_next_step","v":{"s":"Next Step"}},{"k":"a11y_see_answer","v":{"s":"See answer"}},{"k":"a11y_load_f_x_x_squared_with_x_zero_equal_to_1","v":{"s":"Load f(x) = x squared with x zero equal to 1"}},{"k":"a11y_got_all_three","v":{"s":"Got all three"}},{"k":"a11y_check_answer","v":{"s":"Check answer"}},{"k":"a11y_derivative_at_x_equals_1_for_2x_squared","v":{"s":"Derivative at x equals 1 for 2x squared"}},{"k":"a11y_verify","v":{"s":"Verify"}},{"k":"a11y_load_v_t_2t_t_0_3","v":{"s":"Load v(t) = 2t, t  [0,3]"}},{"k":"a11y_triangle_area_in_meters","v":{"s":"Triangle area in meters"}},{"k":"a11y_definite_integral_value_in_meters","v":{"s":"Definite integral value in meters"}},{"k":"a11y_estimated_distance_in_5_seconds_in_meters","v":{"s":"Estimated distance in 5 seconds in meters"}},{"k":"a11y_commit_distance_estimate_and_test_it","v":{"s":"Commit distance estimate and test it"}},{"k":"a11y_calculus_visualization_view","v":{"s":"Calculus visualization view"}},{"k":"a11y_visualization_controls","v":{"s":"Visualization controls"}},{"k":"a11y_reset_interaction_resume_auto_animation","v":{"s":"Reset interaction; resume auto-animation"}},{"k":"a11y_toggle_fullscreen","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen"}},{"k":"a11y_logged_derivative_evidence","v":{"s":"Logged derivative evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_working_explanation_from_live_derivative_eviden","v":{"s":"Working explanation from live derivative evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_based_derivative_behavior_explanation","v":{"s":"Evidence-based derivative behavior explanation"}},{"k":"a11y_reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"left","v":{"s":"Left"}},{"k":"midpoint","v":{"s":"Midpoint"}},{"k":"right","v":{"s":"Right"}},{"k":"trapezoid","v":{"s":"Trapezoid"}},{"k":"simpson","v":{"s":"Simpson"}},{"k":"rectangles_n","v":{"s":"Rectangles (n)"}},{"k":"coeff_a","v":{"s":"Coeff a"}},{"k":"signed_area_title","v":{"s":"Signed integral and total area"}},{"k":"above_axis_area","v":{"s":"Above the axis: "}},{"k":"below_axis_area","v":{"s":"Below the axis (magnitude): "}},{"k":"total_geometric_area","v":{"s":"Total geometric area: "}},{"k":"signed_net_equation","v":{"s":"Net integral = above − below: "}},{"k":"signed_area_reason","v":{"s":"The integral subtracts the region below the axis. Total geometric area adds both magnitudes. Displayed values are rounded."}}],"id":495}},{"k":"cell","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_choose_a_prediction_or_select_start_without_predi","v":{"s":"Choose a prediction, or select Start without prediction."}},{"k":"sr_microdissection_evidence_recorded","v":{"s":"Microdissection evidence recorded"}},{"k":"a11y_back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"a11y_search_modes","v":{"s":"Search modes"}},{"k":"a11y_continuous_light_exposure","v":{"s":"Continuous light exposure"}},{"k":"a11y_reset_unavailable_mission_targets_and_resume_th","v":{"s":"Reset unavailable mission targets and resume the simulation"}},{"k":"a11y_microscope_zoom_level","v":{"s":"Microscope zoom level"}},{"k":"a11y_reset_microscope_view","v":{"s":"Reset microscope view"}},{"k":"a11y_simulation_speed","v":{"s":"Simulation speed"}},{"k":"a11y_choose_the_explanation_best_supported_by_missio","v":{"s":"Choose the explanation best supported by mission evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_show_all_cell_types_in_petri_dish","v":{"s":"Show all cell types in petri dish"}},{"k":"a11y_clear_all_cell_types_from_petri_dish","v":{"s":"Clear all cell types from petri dish"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_type_visibility_filters","v":{"s":"Cell type visibility filters"}},{"k":"a11y_next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"a11y_close_badges","v":{"s":"Close badges"}},{"k":"a11y_close_ai_tutor","v":{"s":"Close AI tutor"}},{"k":"a11y_ask_the_cell_biology_ai_tutor","v":{"s":"Ask the cell biology AI tutor"}},{"k":"a11y_toggle_badges_panel","v":{"s":"Toggle badges panel"}},{"k":"a11y_ai_tutor","v":{"s":"AI Tutor"}},{"k":"a11y_snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_type","v":{"s":"Cell type"}},{"k":"a11y_study_progress","v":{"s":"Study progress"}},{"k":"a11y_mastery_progress","v":{"s":"Mastery progress"}},{"k":"a11y_portable_cell_progress_json","v":{"s":"Portable cell progress JSON"}},{"k":"a11y_progress_across_cell_types","v":{"s":"Progress across cell types"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_comparison_and_guided_pathways","v":{"s":"Cell comparison and guided pathways"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_diagram_detail_zoom","v":{"s":"Cell diagram detail zoom"}},{"k":"a11y_optical_section_depth","v":{"s":"Optical section depth"}},{"k":"a11y_structure_directory","v":{"s":"Structure directory"}},{"k":"a11y_search_structure_directory","v":{"s":"Search structure directory"}},{"k":"a11y_organelle_concept_check","v":{"s":"Organelle concept check"}},{"k":"a11y_concept_check_answers","v":{"s":"Concept check answers"}},{"k":"a11y_adaptive_organelle_check","v":{"s":"Adaptive organelle check"}},{"k":"a11y_adaptive_check_answers","v":{"s":"Adaptive check answers"}},{"k":"a11y_text_descriptions_of_cell_structures","v":{"s":"Text descriptions of cell structures"}},{"k":"a11y_organelles_tap_to_inspect","v":{"s":"Organelles — tap to inspect"}},{"k":"a11y_ultrastructure_features_to_notice","v":{"s":"Ultrastructure features to notice"}},{"k":"a11y_microdissection_protocol_progress","v":{"s":"Microdissection protocol progress"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_specimen_type","v":{"s":"Cell specimen type"}},{"k":"a11y_cell_structures_to_target","v":{"s":"Cell structures to target"}},{"k":"a11y_microdissection_instrument","v":{"s":"Microdissection instrument"}},{"k":"a11y_microscopy_label","v":{"s":"Microscopy label"}},{"k":"a11y_scale_journey_destinations","v":{"s":"Scale Journey destinations"}},{"k":"a11y_cellular_process_diagrams","v":{"s":"Cellular process diagrams"}},{"k":"a11y_focused_pathway_stage","v":{"s":"Focused pathway stage"}},{"k":"a11y_search_organism_encyclopedia","v":{"s":"Search organism encyclopedia"}},{"k":"a11y_filter_organisms_by_group","v":{"s":"Filter organisms by group"}},{"k":"a11y_choose_an_organism","v":{"s":"Choose an organism"}},{"k":"a11y_organism_a","v":{"s":"Organism A"}},{"k":"a11y_organism_b","v":{"s":"Organism B"}},{"k":"a11y_search_glossary_terms","v":{"s":"Search glossary terms"}},{"k":"a11y_library_shelves","v":{"s":"Library shelves"}},{"k":"a11y_tables_on_this_shelf","v":{"s":"Tables on this shelf"}},{"k":"a11y_osmosis_hypothesis","v":{"s":"Osmosis hypothesis"}},{"k":"a11y_osmosis_explanation","v":{"s":"Osmosis explanation"}},{"k":"a11y_close_flashcard","v":{"s":"Close flashcard"}},{"k":"amoeba","v":{"s":"Amoeba"}},{"k":"paramecium","v":{"s":"Paramecium"}},{"k":"euglena","v":{"s":"Euglena"}},{"k":"white_blood_cell","v":{"s":"White Blood Cell"}},{"k":"bacterium","v":{"s":"Bacterium"}},{"k":"plant_cell","v":{"s":"Plant Cell"}},{"k":"diatom","v":{"s":"Diatom"}},{"k":"volvox","v":{"s":"Volvox"}},{"k":"stentor","v":{"s":"Stentor"}},{"k":"tardigrade","v":{"s":"Tardigrade"}},{"k":"microscopic","v":{"s":"Microscopic "}},{"k":"spirillum","v":{"s":"Spirillum"}},{"k":"interactive_desc","v":{"s":"Watch, play, or quiz"}},{"k":"browse_desc","v":{"s":"Encyclopedia + filter + compare"}},{"k":"knowledge_desc","v":{"s":"History, biologists, labs, diseases, ecology"}},{"k":"reference_desc","v":{"s":"Glossary + finale"}},{"k":"firstObserve_desc","v":{"s":"Select your first organism"}},{"k":"allOrganisms_desc","v":{"s":"Observe all 11 organisms"}},{"k":"quizStreak3_desc","v":{"s":"Get 3 quiz answers correct in a row"}},{"k":"quizStreak5_desc","v":{"s":"5 quiz answers correct in a row"}},{"k":"quizMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Answer 15 quiz questions correctly"}},{"k":"playMode_desc","v":{"s":"Play as any organism"}},{"k":"discoverer10_desc","v":{"s":"Discover 10 organism facts"}},{"k":"foodCollector_desc","v":{"s":"Collect 20 food particles"}},{"k":"anatomyExplorer_desc","v":{"s":"Click 10 different organelle labels"}},{"k":"centurion_desc","v":{"s":"Earn 100+ total XP in Cell Sim"}},{"k":"interior_caveat","v":{"s":"Schematic, not to scale: organelle sizes and numbers are simplified (a real cell has hundreds of mitochondria), and this is one 2-D slice of a 3-D cell. Cells also specialize — this is a “typical” one."}},{"k":"explored","v":{"s":"Explored"}},{"k":"organelles","v":{"s":"organelles in this cell"}},{"k":"found_in","v":{"s":"Found in"}},{"k":"tap_organelle","v":{"s":"Tap an organelle in the cell (or a button above) to see what it does — and which cells have it."}}],"id":496}},{"k":"cephalopodlab","v":{"o":[{"k":"2016","v":{"s":", 2016."}},{"k":"sr_3d_engine_ready","v":{"s":"3D engine ready"}},{"k":"sr_surfaced_ready_to_dive_again","v":{"s":"Surfaced. Ready to dive again."}},{"k":"sr_sea_otter_spotted_on_surface","v":{"s":"Sea otter spotted on surface"}},{"k":"sr_sperm_whale_approaching_from_depth","v":{"s":"Sperm whale approaching from depth"}},{"k":"sr_anglerfish_lure_spotted","v":{"s":"Anglerfish lure spotted"}},{"k":"sr_giant_squid_hunting","v":{"s":"Giant squid hunting"}},{"k":"sr_barracuda_incoming","v":{"s":"Barracuda incoming"}},{"k":"sr_reef_shark_approaching","v