")"}},{"k":"filter_glossary_terms","v":{"s":"Filter glossary terms"}},{"k":"sources_bibliography","v":{"s":"📜 Sources + bibliography"}},{"k":"cephalopod_lab_content_is_anchored_in_","v":{"s":"Cephalopod Lab content is anchored in peer-reviewed marine biology + behavioral science. Primary sources:"}},{"k":"hanlon_messenger_cephalopod_behaviour_","v":{"s":"Hanlon & Messenger, \"Cephalopod Behaviour\" (Cambridge, 2018)"}},{"k":"godfrey_smith_other_minds_fsg_2016","v":{"s":"Godfrey-Smith, \"Other Minds\" (FSG, 2016)"}},{"k":"roper_sweeney_nauen_fao_cephalopod_cat","v":{"s":"Roper, Sweeney & Nauen, FAO Cephalopod Catalog (1984)"}},{"k":"finn_tregenza_norman_2009_current_biol","v":{"s":"Finn, Tregenza & Norman (2009) Current Biology"}},{"k":"norman_finn_2001_bull_mar_sci","v":{"s":"Norman & Finn (2001) Bull. Mar. Sci."}},{"k":"hanlon_2007_current_biology","v":{"s":"Hanlon (2007) Current Biology"}},{"k":"wang_ragsdale_2018_2022","v":{"s":"Wang & Ragsdale (2018, 2022)"}},{"k":"doubleday_et_al_2016_current_biology","v":{"s":"Doubleday et al. (2016) Current Biology"}},{"k":"birch_burn_schnell_et_al_2021_lse_revi","v":{"s":"Birch, Burn, Schnell et al. (2021) LSE review"}},{"k":"cambridge_declaration_on_consciousness_2","v":{"s":"Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness (2012)"}},{"k":"roger_hanlon_s_marine_biological_labor","v":{"s":"Roger Hanlon's Marine Biological Laboratory website"}},{"k":"be_a_cephalopod_scientist","v":{"s":"🔬 Be a cephalopod scientist"}},{"k":"cephalopod_biology_is_one_of_the_few_s","v":{"s":"Cephalopod biology is one of the few scientific fields where amateur observations regularly contribute to peer-reviewed papers. New species are still being discovered. Behavior is poorly documented for most species. "}},{"k":"if_you_see_a_cephalopod_doing_somethin","v":{"s":"If you see a cephalopod doing something interesting, report it."}},{"k":"inaturalist_cephalopod_project","v":{"s":"iNaturalist Cephalopod Project"}},{"k":"noaa_marine_wildlife_reports","v":{"s":"NOAA Marine Wildlife Reports"}},{"k":"cephresearch_org_sightings_log","v":{"s":"CephResearch.org sightings log"}},{"k":"tonmo_com_community","v":{"s":"TONMO.com community"}},{"k":"australian_museum_cephrecord","v":{"s":"Australian Museum CephRecord"}},{"k":"iseahorse_org_broader_marine_focus","v":{"s":"iSeahorse.org (broader marine focus)"}},{"k":"how","v":{"s":"🔗 How: "}},{"k":"pro_tip_for_students","v":{"s":"💡 Pro tip for students"}},{"k":"if_you_live_near_tidepools_a_snorkel_a","v":{"s":"If you live near tidepools, a snorkel + a phone in a waterproof case is enough to start. Cephalopods are notoriously hard to find — most sightings come from patient observation, not lucky encounters. The Pacific Northwest has Giant Pacific octopuses; the Mediterranean has common octopuses; the Indo-Pacific has the highest cephalopod diversity on Earth."}},{"k":"field_notes_you_ve_unlocked","v":{"s":"📔 Field notes you've unlocked"}},{"k":"real_biology_trivia_unlocked_through_h","v":{"s":"Real biology trivia unlocked through Hunter Sim attempts. Each note maps to documented research."}},{"k":"you_haven_t_unlocked_any_field_notes_y","v":{"s":"You haven't unlocked any field notes yet. Head to the "}},{"k":"hunter_sim_3","v":{"s":"Hunter Sim"}},{"k":"tab_and_attempt_a_hunt_every_attempt_u","v":{"s":" tab and attempt a hunt — every attempt unlocks a real biology fact."}},{"k":"cephalopod_lab_2","v":{"s":"Cephalopod Lab"}},{"k":"abandon_day_confirmation_title","v":{"s":"Abandon this day?"}},{"k":"abandon_day_confirmation_message","v":{"s":"Your current day progress will be lost."}},{"k":"cancel","v":{"s":"Cancel"}},{"k":"confirm_abandon_day","v":{"s":"Abandon day"}},{"k":"evidence_based_cephalopod_biology","v":{"s":"Evidence-based cephalopod biology: dynamic skin, distributed neural control, hunting strategies, diverse life histories, and deep evolutionary history."}},{"k":"find_a_section","v":{"s":"Find a section"}},{"k":"search_sections_placeholder","v":{"s":"Search camouflage, anatomy, ethics, lessons..."}},{"k":"clear_section_search","v":{"s":"Clear section search"}},{"k":"no_matching_sections","v":{"s":"No matching sections. Try a broader biology, activity, or teaching term."}},{"k":"welcome_evidence_based_intro","v":{"s":"Octopuses, squid, cuttlefish, and nautiluses are marine mollusks with unusual nervous systems, dynamic skin, and diverse life histories. Explore what evidence supports, where groups differ, and which popular claims are only metaphors."}},{"k":"one_brain_distributed_control","v":{"s":"One brain, distributed control"}},{"k":"octopus_distributed_nervous_system_correction","v":{"s":"An octopus has one central brain plus large nerve cords and ganglia in its arms, where much sensory and motor processing occurs. \"Nine brains\" is a metaphor, not anatomy. Octopuses also have one systemic and two branchial hearts; oxygenated hemocyanin appears blue."}},{"k":"dynamic_light_sensitive_skin","v":{"s":"Dynamic, light-sensitive skin"}},{"k":"cephalopod_skin_opsin_correction","v":{"s":"Nerves control pigment-filled chromatophores while iridophores and leucophores reflect light. Skin opsins are associated with local light responses, but current evidence does not show image-forming skin vision or full color vision through the skin."}},{"k":"mimicry_tool_use_correction","v":{"s":"Mimic octopuses shift posture, movement, and pattern in ways resembling several other animals. Veined octopuses carry coconut or shell halves as portable shelter — an important example of invertebrate tool use, but not the only one."}},{"k":"short_lives_with_exceptions","v":{"s":"Mostly short lives, with exceptions"}},{"k":"cephalopod_lifespan_exceptions","v":{"s":"Many coleoids live months to a few years and reproduce once. Nautiluses can live for decades and reproduce repeatedly, and some deep-sea cephalopods also depart from the once-and-die pattern."}},{"k":"fast_responses_mixed_outcomes","v":{"s":"Fast responses, mixed outcomes"}},{"k":"global_abundance_trend_caution","v":{"s":"A 2016 analysis found broad increases in cephalopod catch-rate indices over six decades, but that does not make every species a climate winner. Temperature, oxygen, acidification, fishing, and prey changes can produce different regional outcomes."}},{"k":"ancient_lineage_still_evolving","v":{"s":"Ancient lineage, still evolving"}},{"k":"nautilus_living_fossil_correction","v":{"s":"Cephalopod lineages extend back more than 500 million years. Living nautiluses retain an external shell, but they have continued evolving and diversifying; \"living fossil\" is shorthand, not evidence of an unchanged animal."}},{"k":"compare_field_guide_species_evidence","v":{"s":"Compare all field-guide species across body plan, habitat, camouflage, movement, and hunting strategy. Relative simulation profiles are labeled as teaching models rather than scientific rankings."}},{"k":"relative_teaching_profiles","v":{"s":"Relative teaching profiles: "}},{"k":"profile_scores_not_standardized_measurements","v":{"s":"These bars tune comparisons inside this lab; they are not standardized scientific measurements. Cognition especially depends on the task, context, age, and evidence available for each species."}},{"k":"wcag_2_2_aa_changes_apply_on_next_dive","v":{"s":"WCAG 2.2 AA. Changes apply on next Dive. Captions mirror every audio cue (predator alerts, hunt success, ink, achievements). Reduced motion dims animation amplitudes. All settings persist on this device only — no cloud, no FERPA surface."}},{"k":"3d_hunt_canvas_fallback","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D octopus hunt. Use WASD to crawl, A and D to rotate, Space to jet, I for ink defense, and G to grab shelters."}},{"k":"tip_tab_to_or_click_the_canvas_for_keyboard","v":{"s":"Tip: Tab to or click the canvas so keyboard input is captured."}},{"k":"counter_illumination_accessible_summary","v":{"s":"Counter-illumination simulation"}},{"k":"octopus_anatomy_accessible_summary","v":{"s":"Octopus anatomy diagram"}},{"k":"evolutionary_timeline_accessible_summary","v":{"s":"Cephalopod evolutionary timeline from 538 million years ago to present"}},{"k":"comparative_cognition_matrix_caption","v":{"s":"Comparative cognition evidence matrix"}},{"k":"camouflage_visualization_accessible_summary","v":{"s":"Camouflage visualization"}},{"k":"camouflage_observations_caption","v":{"s":"Camouflage observations"}},{"k":"explain_your_reasoning","v":{"s":"Explain your reasoning"}},{"k":"taxonomy_table_caption","v":{"s":"Cephalopod taxonomy from kingdom to family"}},{"k":"anat3d_failed","v":{"s":"3D is unavailable on this device or network, so the flat diagram is shown. Every structure is also a button below — nothing here needs the 3D view."}},{"k":"anat3d_hint","v":{"s":"Schematic on purpose — a body plan you can walk around, not a scale model of one species. X-ray shows the organs in place; skin view shows the animal."}},{"k":"anat3d_label","v":{"s":"Octopus anatomy, 3D. Interactive. Arrow keys rotate, plus and minus zoom, zero resets. Every structure here is also a button in the All regions list below."}},{"k":"anat3d_loading","v":{"s":"Loading the 3D body plan…"}},{"k":"body_cuttlefish","v":{"s":"Cuttlefish"}},{"k":"body_cuttlefish_note","v":{"s":"Eight arms plus two tentacles, a broad mantle with a fin down each side, and the gas-filled cuttlebone inside it (turn off X-ray to hide it)."}},{"k":"body_octopus","v":{"s":"Octopus"}},{"k":"body_octopus_note","v":{"s":"Eight arms, a rounded mantle, and no hard part anywhere but the beak."}},{"k":"body_plan_picker","v":{"s":"Body plan"}},{"k":"body_squid","v":{"s":"Squid"}},{"k":"body_squid_note","v":{"s":"Eight arms plus two longer feeding tentacles, on a tapered mantle with fins at the far end."}},{"k":"label_all","v":{"s":"🏷️ Label everything"}},{"k":"reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset the view"}},{"k":"rotate_and_click","v":{"s":"🔍 Drag to rotate · click a structure"}},{"k":"rotate_left","v":{"s":"Rotate left"}},{"k":"rotate_right","v":{"s":"Rotate right"}},{"k":"same_organs_note","v":{"s":"The organs do not change between these three — two gills, three hearts, one brain ring. Only the body around them does."}},{"k":"tilt_down","v":{"s":"Tilt down"}},{"k":"tilt_up","v":{"s":"Tilt up"}},{"k":"xray_off","v":{"s":"🐙 Skin view"}},{"k":"xray_on","v":{"s":"👁️ X-ray: organs visible"}},{"k":"zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}}],"id":497}},{"k":"chem_balance","v":{"o":[{"k":"ud83cudfb2_random","v":{"s":"🎲 Random: "}},{"k":"water","v":{"s":"Water"}},{"k":"table_salt","v":{"s":"Table Salt"}},{"k":"magnesium_oxide","v":{"s":"Magnesium Oxide"}},{"k":"iron_oxide","v":{"s":"Iron Oxide"}},{"k":"combustion","v":{"s":"Combustion"}},{"k":"photosynthesis","v":{"s":"Photosynthesis"}},{"k":"acid_base","v":{"s":"Acid + Base"}},{"k":"ammonia","v":{"s":"Ammonia"}},{"k":"thermite","v":{"s":"Thermite"}},{"k":"ethanol_combustion","v":{"s":"Ethanol Combustion"}},{"k":"calcium_carbonate","v":{"s":"Calcium Carbonate"}},{"k":"glucose_combustion","v":{"s":"Glucose Combustion"}}],"id":498}},{"k":"chembalance","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_speed_challenge_started_there_is_no_time_limit_an","v":{"s":"Speed challenge started. There is no time limit, and you can pause at any time."}},{"k":"sr_speed_challenge_timer_resumed","v":{"s":"Speed challenge timer resumed."}},{"k":"sr_speed_challenge_timer_paused","v":{"s":"Speed challenge timer paused."}},{"k":"sr_next_chemistry_challenge_question","v":{"s":"Next chemistry challenge question."}},{"k":"sr_chemistry_challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Chemistry challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_element_battle_won","v":{"s":"Element battle won."}},{"k":"sr_element_battle_complete","v":{"s":"Element battle complete."}},{"k":"sr_next_element_battle_round","v":{"s":"Next element battle round."}},{"k":"sr_opening_titration_lab","v":{"s":"Opening Titration Lab"}},{"k":"a11y_molar_mass_result","v":{"s":"Molar mass result"}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"chemistry_lab","v":{"s":"⚗️ Chemistry Lab"}},{"k":"chem_v3","v":{"s":"CHEM v3"}},{"k":"ai","v":{"s":"🤖 AI"}},{"k":"hub","v":{"s":"🏠 Hub"}},{"k":"search_38_sub_tools","v":{"s":"Search 38 sub-tools..."}},{"k":"no_matches_try_a_different_keyword","v":{"s":"No matches. Try a different keyword."}},{"k":"balance_atoms_in_atoms_out","v":{"s":"Balance — atoms in = atoms out"}},{"k":"lavoisier_1789_conservation_of_mass_tr","v":{"s":"Lavoisier 1789: conservation of mass. Trick is the coefficient (NOT the subscript). 2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O. Balance metals first, then non-metals, then hydrogen, then oxygen."}},{"k":"reaction_types_the_5_patterns","v":{"s":"Reaction Types — the 5 patterns"}},{"k":"synthesis_a_b_ab_decomposition_ab_a_b_","v":{"s":"Synthesis (A+B→AB), decomposition (AB→A+B), single replace (A+BC→AC+B), double replace (AB+CD→AD+CB), combustion (CxHy + O₂ → CO₂ + H₂O). 95% of high-school equations fit one of these."}},{"k":"stoichiometry_mole_math","v":{"s":"Stoichiometry — mole math"}},{"k":"1_mole_6_022_10_particles_avogadro_mol","v":{"s":"1 mole = 6.022×10²³ particles (Avogadro). Molar mass = sum of atomic masses. Grams → moles → moles → grams. The bridge between balanced equations and lab quantities."}},{"k":"molecular_ball_and_stick_geometry","v":{"s":"Molecular — ball-and-stick geometry"}},{"k":"vsepr_predicts_shape_linear_trigonal_p","v":{"s":"VSEPR predicts shape: linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal pyramidal, bent. Lone pairs occupy more space than bonded pairs. CH₄ is a perfect tetrahedron, NH₃ is a slightly squashed one."}},{"k":"lab_safety_ghs_symbols_emergency_respo","v":{"s":"Lab Safety — GHS symbols + emergency response"}},{"k":"9_ghs_pictograms_explosive_flammable_o","v":{"s":"9 GHS pictograms: explosive, flammable, oxidizer, gas pressure, corrosive, toxic, irritant, environmental hazard, health hazard. Acid into water (never reverse) — splashes are violent."}},{"k":"challenge_graded_quiz_across_3_tiers","v":{"s":"Challenge — graded quiz across 3 tiers"}},{"k":"beginner_naming_counting_atoms_interme","v":{"s":"Beginner = naming + counting atoms; Intermediate = balancing; Advanced = mole math + limiting reagent. AP Chem topics 1–6. NGSS HS-PS1."}},{"k":"element_battle_retrieval_as_combat","v":{"s":"Element Battle — retrieval as combat"}},{"k":"speed_builds_automaticity_once_balanci","v":{"s":"Speed builds automaticity. Once balancing + naming are automatic, your working memory is free for higher-order reasoning like predicting product formation and equilibrium shifts."}},{"k":"learn_concepts_by_grade_band","v":{"s":"Learn — concepts by grade band"}},{"k":"k_2_matter_is_stuff_3_5_matter_cycles_","v":{"s":"K-2: matter is stuff. 3-5: matter cycles. MS: atoms + simple compounds. HS: full periodic-table reasoning. AP: thermodynamics + kinetics + equilibrium. The vertical alignment runs through every grade."}},{"k":"chemistry_badges","v":{"s":"🏅 Chemistry Badges"}},{"k":"ai_chemistry_tutor","v":{"s":"🤖 AI Chemistry Tutor"}},{"k":"ask_a_chemistry_question","v":{"s":"Ask a chemistry question..."}},{"k":"ask_the_chemistry_tutor","v":{"s":"Ask the chemistry tutor"}},{"k":"switch_preset","v":{"s":"Switch Preset"}},{"k":"reactants","v":{"s":"Reactants"}},{"k":"products","v":{"s":"Products"}},{"k":"balanced","v":{"s":"✅ Balanced!"}},{"k":"subtract","v":{"s":"Subtract"}},{"k":"balanced_2","v":{"s":"✓ balanced"}},{"k":"check","v":{"s":"⚖️ Check"}},{"k":"hints","v":{"s":"Hints"}},{"k":"hints_2","v":{"s":"💡 Hints"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"🔄 Reset"}},{"k":"random","v":{"s":"Random"}},{"k":"random_2","v":{"s":"🎲 Random"}},{"k":"balance_one_element_at_a_time_start_wi","v":{"s":"• Balance one element at a time • Start with the most complex compound • Save O or H for last"}},{"k":"classify_this_reaction","v":{"s":"🧩 Classify This Reaction"}},{"k":"start","v":{"s":"Start"}},{"k":"start_2","v":{"s":"▶ Start"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"next_2","v":{"s":"➡ Next"}},{"k":"enter_chemical_formula","v":{"s":"🧮 Enter Chemical Formula"}},{"