ted chemistry tools and reviewed reference content still work."}},{"k":"ai_verify_hint","v":{"s":"AI-generated guidance is a study hint, not a source of record. Verify calculations and safety decisions against the reviewed reference content, instructor, SDS, or local plan."}},{"k":"basic_ph","v":{"s":"🔵 Basic pH (7.5 < pH ≤ 11)"}},{"k":"block_width_key","v":{"s":"Block width = electron capacity"}},{"k":"blocks_definition","v":{"s":"electron-filling regions"}},{"k":"blocks_term","v":{"s":"Blocks"}},{"k":"challenge_difficulty","v":{"s":"Challenge difficulty"}},{"k":"chemistry_reference_overview","v":{"s":"Chemistry Lab Reference Hub"}},{"k":"chemistry_sections","v":{"s":"Chemistry Lab sections"}},{"k":"choose_an_element","v":{"s":"Choose an element"}},{"k":"competency_progress","v":{"s":"Competency progress"}},{"k":"d_block_capacity","v":{"s":"5 orbitals × 2 e⁻ = 10 columns"}},{"k":"d_block_label","v":{"s":"d-block"}},{"k":"d_block_location","v":{"s":"Groups 3–12, periods 4–7"}},{"k":"d_block_members","v":{"s":"This is the central transition-metal region of the table."}},{"k":"decrease_coefficient_for","v":{"s":"Decrease coefficient for"}},{"k":"electron_configuration_map","v":{"s":"Electron-configuration map"}},{"k":"element_choices","v":{"s":"Element choices"}},{"k":"element_pages","v":{"s":"Element pages"}},{"k":"enable_sounds","v":{"s":"Enable chemistry sounds"}},{"k":"equation_coefficients","v":{"s":"Equation coefficients"}},{"k":"equation_presets","v":{"s":"Equation presets"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"pH and hydronium explanation"}},{"k":"explore_all_118_elements","v":{"s":"Explore all 118 elements"}},{"k":"f_block_capacity","v":{"s":"7 orbitals × 2 e⁻ = 14 columns"}},{"k":"f_block_label","v":{"s":"f-block"}},{"k":"f_block_location","v":{"s":"Two rows from periods 6 and 7"}},{"k":"f_block_members","v":{"s":"These are the lanthanide and actinide series shown below the main table."}},{"k":"f_block_pulled_below","v":{"s":"The f block is pulled below the main table from periods 6 and 7."}},{"k":"four_block_guide","v":{"s":"Guide to the four periodic-table blocks"}},{"k":"groups_13_18","v":{"s":"Groups 13–18"}},{"k":"groups_1_2","v":{"s":"Groups 1–2"}},{"k":"groups_3_12","v":{"s":"Groups 3–12"}},{"k":"groups_definition","v":{"s":"vertical columns"}},{"k":"groups_term","v":{"s":"Groups"}},{"k":"highlight","v":{"s":"Highlight"}},{"k":"highlight_relationship","v":{"s":"Highlight a periodic relationship"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"pH and hydronium hypothesis"}},{"k":"increase_coefficient_for","v":{"s":"Increase coefficient for"}},{"k":"interactive_atlas","v":{"s":"Interactive atlas"}},{"k":"learning_path_title","v":{"s":"Suggested learning path"}},{"k":"log_hydronium_activity","v":{"s":"log10 hydronium activity"}},{"k":"log_hydronium_short","v":{"s":"log₁₀ a(H₃O⁺)"}},{"k":"model_boundary","v":{"s":"Model boundary"}},{"k":"mute_sounds","v":{"s":"Mute chemistry sounds"}},{"k":"near_neutral_ph","v":{"s":"🟢 Near-neutral visual band (6.5 ≤ pH ≤ 7.5)"}},{"k":"no_elements_found","v":{"s":"No elements match that search."}},{"k":"open_full_element_profile","v":{"s":"Open full profile →"}},{"k":"p_block_capacity","v":{"s":"3 orbitals × 2 e⁻ = 6 columns"}},{"k":"p_block_label","v":{"s":"p-block"}},{"k":"p_block_location","v":{"s":"Groups 13–18, periods 2–7"}},{"k":"p_block_members","v":{"s":"Includes metals, metalloids, and nonmetals; helium is the exception."}},{"k":"period_axis","v":{"s":"Period"}},{"k":"periodic_atlas_caption","v":{"s":"The detached rows preserve atomic-number order without making the main table too wide. This convention places La and Ac before each 14-cell f-filling region."}},{"k":"periodic_atlas_instructions","v":{"s":"Choose an element, then highlight its group, period, or electron-filling block. In the map, Left and Right Arrow move by atomic number; Home and End jump to the first and last elements."}},{"k":"periodic_block_legend","v":{"s":"Periodic table block legend"}},{"k":"periodic_blocks_big_idea","v":{"s":"The letters s, p, d, and f name electron subshells. A wider block means that subshell has more orbitals and can hold more electrons."}},{"k":"periodic_blocks_big_idea_label","v":{"s":"The big idea"}},{"k":"periodic_blocks_caption","v":{"s":"The f block belongs to periods 6 and 7 but is shown below to keep the table compact. Helium sits over Group 18 because of its chemical behavior, while its 1s² electron configuration places it in the s block."}},{"k":"periodic_blocks_intro","v":{"s":"Each block groups elements by the type of electron subshell being filled in their ground-state electron configurations."}},{"k":"periodic_map_scroll_hint","v":{"s":"The shapes follow the table’s 18 groups and 7 periods."}},{"k":"periods_definition","v":{"s":"horizontal rows"}},{"k":"periods_term","v":{"s":"Periods"}},{"k":"ph","v":{"s":"pH"}},{"k":"ph_activity_intro","v":{"s":"Move the hydronium-activity exponent and observe the logarithmic pH response. A one-unit exponent change changes hydronium activity tenfold."}},{"k":"ph_explanation_activity","v":{"s":"Explain the relationship among hydronium activity, powers of ten, and pH."}},{"k":"ph_hypothesis_activity","v":{"s":"How does changing log₁₀ a(H₃O⁺) by one unit change pH and hydronium activity?"}},{"k":"ph_model_caveat","v":{"s":"This activity-based model represents dilute aqueous solutions at 25 °C. Exact neutrality is pH 7.00 at 25 °C. The colored regions describe pH, not acid or base strength, and real pH values are not universally limited to 0–14."}},{"k":"ph_prompt_direction","v":{"s":"Why does pH move in the opposite direction from hydronium activity?"}},{"k":"ph_prompt_logarithm","v":{"s":"Explain why a logarithmic scale is useful across fourteen powers of ten."}},{"k":"ph_prompt_predict","v":{"s":"Predict the pH before moving to an adjacent exponent."}},{"k":"ph_prompt_tenfold","v":{"s":"Compare adjacent exponents. How many times larger is the hydronium activity?"}},{"k":"ph_region","v":{"s":"pH region"}},{"k":"practice_next","v":{"s":"Practice next"}},{"k":"practice_next_aria","v":{"s":"Practice next: pick my next equation, retrying ones I missed first"}},{"k":"practice_next_hint","v":{"s":"Picks an unsolved equation — ones you missed come back first."}},{"k":"preset_difficulty","v":{"s":"Equation preset difficulty"}},{"k":"preset_missed","v":{"s":"missed"}},{"k":"preset_solved","v":{"s":"solved"}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"progress_explainer","v":{"s":"These counts use unique completed items, not clicks or XP."}},{"k":"s_block_capacity","v":{"s":"1 orbital × 2 e⁻ = 2 columns"}},{"k":"s_block_label","v":{"s":"s-block"}},{"k":"s_block_location","v":{"s":"Groups 1–2, plus helium"}},{"k":"s_block_members","v":{"s":"Includes hydrogen, alkali metals, alkaline-earth metals, and helium."}},{"k":"safety_sections","v":{"s":"Safety sections"}},{"k":"scroll_ph_observations","v":{"s":"Scrollable pH observations"}},{"k":"scroll_to_explore","v":{"s":"Scroll to explore →"}},{"k":"scrollable_periodic_table","v":{"s":"Scrollable 18-column periodic table"}},{"k":"search_elements","v":{"s":"Search by name, symbol, or atomic number..."}},{"k":"search_elements_label","v":{"s":"Search elements by name, symbol, or atomic number"}},{"k":"search_sub_tools_count","v":{"s":"Search {count} sub-tools..."}},{"k":"select_equation_preset","v":{"s":"Select equation preset"}},{"k":"sodium_block_example","v":{"s":"Sodium (Na) sits in period 3, group 1 → s-block → its ground-state electron configuration ends in 3s¹."}},{"k":"sound_off","v":{"s":"Muted"}},{"k":"sound_on","v":{"s":"Sound"}},{"k":"sub_tools_elements_facts","v":{"s":"{count} sub-tools · 118 elements · Reviewed learning resources"}},{"k":"try_an_example","v":{"s":"Try an example"}},{"k":"very_acidic_ph","v":{"s":"🔴 Very acidic pH (pH < 3)"}},{"k":"very_basic_ph","v":{"s":"🟣 Very basic pH (pH > 11)"}},{"k":"what_this_workspace_includes","v":{"s":"📚 What This Workspace Includes"}},{"k":"where_blocks_sit","v":{"s":"Where the blocks sit"}},{"k":"mass_conserved","v":{"s":"✓ Mass is conserved — every atom that goes in comes out (Lavoisier, 1789)."}},{"k":"mass_never_lost","v":{"s":"Real reactions never create or destroy mass — fix the coefficients."}},{"k":"mass_not_conserved","v":{"s":"Not balanced yet: as written, the equation would create or destroy"}},{"k":"mass_products","v":{"s":"Product mass"}},{"k":"mass_reactants","v":{"s":"Reactant mass"}},{"k":"particle_view_note","v":{"s":"Clusters show atoms per molecule, not true shapes"}},{"k":"particle_view_sr","v":{"s":"Particle view of the equation as written"}},{"k":"particle_view_sr_rule","v":{"s":"Coefficients count whole molecules; subscripts count atoms inside each molecule."}},{"k":"particle_view_title","v":{"s":"Particle view — what the coefficients really count"}},{"k":"reaction_pattern_sr","v":{"s":"Particle diagram of the pattern"}},{"k":"reaction_pattern_sr_note","v":{"s":"Each circle is an atom; touching circles are one molecule."}},{"k":"subscript_why_body","v":{"s":"A subscript is part of what the substance IS: H₂O is water, H₂O₂ is hydrogen peroxide — a bleach. Change a subscript and you have changed the chemical, not balanced it. A coefficient only says how many copies of that molecule take part, so coefficients are the only numbers balancing is allowed to touch."}},{"k":"subscript_why_summary","v":{"s":"Why can’t I just change the small numbers?"}}],"id":499}},{"k":"circuit","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_circuit_cleared","v":{"s":"Circuit cleared."}},{"k":"sr_opening_circuit_shelf_advanced_simulator","v":{"s":"Opening Circuit Shelf advanced simulator."}},{"k":"a11y_voltage","v":{"s":"Voltage"}},{"k":"a11y_resistance","v":{"s":"Resistance"}},{"k":"u2705_challenge_complete_you_hit","v":{"s":"✅ Challenge complete! You hit "}},{"k":"ud83cudfaf_target","v":{"s":"🎯 Target: "}},{"k":"mode","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"resistance","v":{"s":"Resistance"}},{"k":"current","v":{"s":"Current"}},{"k":"power","v":{"s":"Power"}},{"k":"get_2a_current","v":{"s":"Get 2A current"}},{"k":"get_05a_current","v":{"s":"Get 0.5A current"}},{"k":"total_r_200u03a9","v":{"s":"Total R = 200Ω"}},{"k":"power_24w","v":{"s":"Power = 24W"}},{"k":"total_r_50u03a9","v":{"s":"Total R = 50Ω"}},{"k":"led_basic_desc","v":{"s":"Basic LED with current-limiting resistor"}},{"k":"voltage_divider_desc","v":{"s":"Two resistors sharing voltage"}},{"k":"parallel_bulbs_desc","v":{"s":"Three bulbs in parallel - remove one, others stay lit"}},{"k":"ammeter_test_desc","v":{"s":"Series ammeter reads total current"}},{"k":"christmas_lights_desc","v":{"s":"Series LEDs - if one fails, all go dark!"}},{"k":"firstCircuit_desc","v":{"s":"Build your first circuit"}},{"k":"seriesMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Build a 3+ component series circuit"}},{"k":"parallelPro_desc","v":{"s":"Build a 3+ component parallel circuit"}},{"k":"switchWizard_desc","v":{"s":"Toggle a switch 5 times"}},{"k":"allComponents_desc","v":{"s":"Use all 7 component types"}},{"k":"challengeChamp_desc","v":{"s":"Complete 5 circuit challenges"}},{"k":"quizAce_desc","v":{"s":"Score 5+ on Ohm's Law quiz"}},{"k":"quizStreak_desc","v":{"s":"Get 3 quiz answers in a row"}},{"k":"shortCircuitSurvivor_desc","v":{"s":"Trigger a short circuit"}},{"k":"presetExplorer_desc","v":{"s":"Load 3 different preset circuits"}},{"k":"s1_explanation","v":{"s":"Current I = V/R = 1.5 V ÷ 5 Ω = 0.3 A. Power = V·I = 0.45 W — a medium glow for a small bulb. The bulb conducts because the circuit is closed, and V=IR splits the energy across the only resistor."}},{"k":"s2_explanation","v":{"s":"In series, resistances add: 10 Ω total. Current drops to 0.15 A. Each bulb gets only 0.75 V (half the battery) and 0.11 W — about ¼ the power of s1. Series circuits split voltage; that's why old Christmas-light strings dimmed when you added bulbs."}},{"k":"s3_explanation","v":{"s":"In parallel, each bulb sees the full 1.5 V. Each draws 0.3 A independently and glows just as brightly as in s1 — but now the battery delivers 0.6 A total and drains twice as fast. Parallel splits current; voltage stays."}},{"k":"s4_explanation","v":{"s":"Total R = 11 Ω, so I ≈ 0.136 A. Voltage across the bulb is I·R_bulb = 1.36 V; voltage across the small resistor is just 0.14 V. The bulb gets most of the voltage because it has most of the resistance — a key insight: voltage divides in proportion to resistance."}},{"k":"meter_status_ammeter_short","v":{"s":"Unsafe placement: short-circuit path"}},{"k":"meter_status_voltmeter_open","v":{"s":"Incorrect placement: circuit is nearly open"}},{"k":"meter_status_correct","v":{"s":"Measurement placement is correct"}},{"k":"meter_guidance_ammeter_short","v":{"s":"An ammeter has nearly zero resistance. Across parallel nodes it bypasses the load and draws extremely high current. Move it into the series path."}},{"k":"meter_guidance_voltmeter_open","v":{"s":"A voltmeter has extremely high resistance. In series it nearly stops current. Connect it across the component whose voltage difference you want."}},{"k":"meter_guidance_ammeter_ok","v":{"s":"The ammeter is in series, so circuit current passes through it."}},{"k":"meter_guidance_voltmeter_ok","v":{"s":"The voltmeter is in parallel, so it compares electric potential across the branch while drawing negligible current."}},{"k":"comp_bulb","v":{"s":"Bulb"}},{"k":"switch_on","v":{"s":"ON"}},{"k":"switch_off","v":{"s":"OFF"}},{"k":"comp_led","v":{"s":"LED"}},{"k":"comp_ammeter","v":{"s":"Ammeter"}},{"k":"comp_voltmeter","v":{"s":"Voltmeter"}},{"k":"comp_cap_abbrev","v":{"s":"Cap"}},{"k":"intro_g_low","v":{"s":"Build a circuit! Connect parts to make electricity flow and light up bulbs."}},{"k":"intro_g_mid","v":{"s":"Build series and parallel circuits. Add resistors, bulbs, and switches. Watch how changing voltage affects current!"}},{"k":"intro_g_high","v":{"s":"Explore Ohm's Law (V=IR) by building circuits. Compare series vs parallel. Measure with ammeter and voltmeter."}},{"k":"intro_g_adv","v":{"s":"Analyze DC circuits using Ohm's Law, Kirchhoff's Laws, and power dissipation. Series, parallel, and mixed configurations."}},{"k":"state_short_risk","v":{"s":"Short risk"}},{"k":"state_short_risk_note","v":{"s":"Add resistance before current spikes."}},{"k":"state_ready_to_build","v":{"s":"Ready to build"}},{"k":"state_open_path","v":{"s":"Open path"}},{"k":"state_ready_note","v":{"s":"Start with a load, then close the loop."}},{"k":"state_open_note","v":{"s":"Close switches or add a real load."}},{"k":"state_current_flowing","v":{"s":"Current flowing"}},{"k":"state_current_flowing_note","v":{"s":"Use the meters and readouts to test the model."}},{"k":"stat_parts","v":{"s":"Parts"}},{"k":"stat_voltage","v":{"s":"Voltage"}},{"k":"stat_voltage_hint","v":{"s":"battery source"}},{"k":"stat_current","v":{"s":"Current"}},{"k":"stat_current_too_high","v":{"s":"too high"}},{"k":"stat_current_live_flow","v":{"s":"live flow"}},{"k":"badges","v":{"s":"Badges"}},{"k":"route_load_starter","v":{"s":"Load Starter Circuit"}},{"k":"route_load_starter_note","v":{"s":"LED + resistor baseline"}},{"k":"route_open_presets","v":{"s":"Open Presets"}},{"k":"route_open_presets_note","v":{"s":"Voltage dividers, meters, short demo"}},{"k":"route_advanced_sim","v":{"s":"Advanced Simulator"}},{"k":"route_advanced_sim_note","v":{"s":"CircuitJS meters and real-world challenges"}},{"k":"route_try_target","v":{"s":"Try a Target"}},{"k":"route_show_laws","v":{"s":"Show Laws"}},{"k":"route_ask_tutor","v":{"s":"Ask Tutor"}},{"k":"route_kirchhoff_support","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff support"}},{"k":"route_circuit_hint","v":{"s":"Get a circuit hint"}},{"k":"electronics_bench","v":{"s":"Electronics bench"}},{"k":"bench_headline","v":{"s":"Build the loop, then prove the numbers"}},{"k":"aria_bench_visual","v":{"s":"Circuit bench visual showing battery, wire loop, load, and live meter readouts"}},{"k":"add_resistance","v":{"s":"Add resistance"}},{"k":"svg_series_caption","v":{"s":"Series: same current through every part"}},{"k":"svg_parallel_caption","v":{"s":"Parallel: branches share voltage"}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"circuit_builder","v":{"s":"Circuit Builder"}},{"k":"interactive_badge","v":{"s":"INTERACTIVE"}},{"k":"short_circuit_banner","v":{"s":"SHORT CIRCUIT!"}},{"k":"ai_tutor","v":{"s":"AI Tutor"}},{"k":"field_signal_light_speed","v":{"s":"field signal ≈ light speed"}},{"k":"meter_reading_short","v":{"s":"short"}},{"k":"short_circuit_label","v":{"s":"SHORT CIRCUIT"}},{"k":"short_circuit_detail","v":{"s":"near-zero resistance • current surge"}},{"k":"add_components_below","v":{"s":"Add components below"}},{"k":"comp_resistor","v":{"s":"Resistor"}},{"k":"comp_switch","v":{"s":"Switch"}},{"k":"comp_capacitor","v":{"s":"Capacitor"}},{"k":"comp_clear","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"aria_voltage_slider","v":{"s":"Voltage slider"}},{"k":"aria_toggle_switch","v":{"s":"Toggle Switch"}},{"k":"btn_close","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"btn_open","v":{"s":"Open"}},{"k":"aria_cycle_led_color","v":{"s":"Cycle LED Color"}},{"k":"aria_remove_component","v":{"s":"Remove Component"}},{"k":"readout_mode","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"readout_resistance","v":{"s":"Resistance"}},{"k":"readout_power","v":{"s":"Power"}},{"k":"meter_safety_coach","v":{"s":"Meter Safety Coach"}},{"k":"fix_placement","v":{"s":"Fix placement"}},{"k":"connected_correctly","v":{"s":"Connected correctly"}},{"k":"ammeter_rule","v":{"s":"Ammeter rule"}},{"k":"ammeter_rule_desc","v":{"s":"Series connection; very low internal resistance."}},{"k":"voltmeter_rule","v":{"s":"Voltmeter rule"}},{"k":"voltmeter_rule_desc","v":{"s":"Parallel connection; very high internal resistance."}},{"k":"ohm_iv_title","v":{"s":"Ohm's law: I–V characteristic"}},{"k":"ohm_iv_desc","v":{"s":"For a fixed resistance, current rises in a straight line with voltage (slope = 1/R). Steeper = lower resistance."}},{"k":"axis_voltage","v":{"s":"Voltage (V) →"}},{"k":"mental_model_checks","v":{"s":"Mental-model checks"}},{"k":"mmc_electrons_bold","v":{"s":"Electrons crawl; the signal races. "}},{"k":"mmc_electrons_body","v":{"s":"The glowing dots move fast for visibility, but real electrons drift at only about 0.1 mm/s. The electric field that pushes them travels near light speed, so every bulb lights essentially the instant you connect the battery."}},{"k":"mmc_series_bold","v":{"s":"Current is not used up. "}},{"k":"mmc_parallel_bold","v":{"s":"A parallel branch LOWERS total resistance. "}},{"k":"mmc_battery_bold","v":{"s":"The battery sets voltage, not current. "}},{"k":"per_component_analysis","v":{"s":"Per-Component Analysis"}},{"k":"connect_ammeters_series","v":{"s":"connect ammeters in series"}},{"k":"connect_voltmeters_parallel","v":{"s":"connect voltmeters in parallel"}},{"k":"short_branch","v":{"s":"short branch (~0 Ω)"}},{"k":"status_closed","v":{"s":"Closed"}},{"k":"status_open","v":{"s":"Open"}},{"k":"status_lit","v":{"s":"Lit"}},{"k":"status_off","v":{"s":"Off"}},{"k":"formula_series_note","v":{"s":"Series: same current through all"}},{"k":"formula_parallel_note","v":{"s":"Parallel: same voltage across all"}},{"k":"eq_hearing_aid","v":{"s":"a hearing-aid battery load"}},{"k":"eq_led_nightlight","v":{"s":"a small LED night-light"}},{"k":"eq_phone_charger","v":{"s":"a phone fast-charger"}},{"k":"eq_desk_lamp","v":{"s":"a bright desk lamp"}},{"k":"eq_laptop","v":{"s":"a laptop under load"}},{"k":"eq_appliance","v":{"s":"a household appliance"}},{"k":"energy_budget_title","v":{"s":"Energy budget — where the power goes"}},{"k":"energy_budget_intro","v":{"s":"The battery pours out "}},{"k":"no_dissipating_load","v":{"s":"no dissipating load"}},{"k":"dissipate_series_note","v":{"s":"Biggest resistor dissipates the most (P = I²R, same I)"}},{"k":"dissipate_parallel_note","v":{"s":"Smallest resistor dissipates the most (P = V²/R, same V)"}},{"k":"paradox_title","v":{"s":"The paradox: electrons crawl, the signal races"}},{"k":"paradox_body","v":{"s":"The blue dots in the schematic move fast so you can see them — but real electrons barely creep. So why does the bulb light instantly? Because flipping the switch launches an electric field down the wire at nearly light speed, nudging every electron at once."}},{"k":"aria_paradox_svg","v":{"s":"Two wires. In the top wire the electric field pulse races across almost instantly. In the bottom wire individual electrons drift very slowly."}},{"k":"lane_field_label","v":{"s":"Electric field / signal"}},{"k":"lane_electrons_label","v":{"s":"Actual electrons"}},{"k":"signal_crosses_label","v":{"s":"Signal crosses 1 m in"}},{"k":"electron_crosses_label","v":{"s":"One electron crosses 1 m in"}},{"k":"kvl_title","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL) Verification"}},{"k":"kvl_desc","v":{"s":"The sum of voltage drops around any closed loop equals the source voltage. The same current flows through every series component, so this isn't a lucky coincidence — Ohm's law forces it to balance."}},{"k":"must_balance","v":{"s":"must balance: ΣV = I×ΣR = V"}},{"k":"circuit_open_title","v":{"s":"CIRCUIT OPEN"}},{"k":"circuit_open_desc","v":{"s":"A switch is open - no current flows. Close all switches to complete the circuit."}},{"k":"short_circuit_detected_title","v":{"s":"SHORT CIRCUIT DETECTED"}},{"k":"short_circuit_detected_desc","v":{"s":"Total resistance is below 1Ω! In real life, this could damage components or cause a fire. Add more resistance."}},{"k":"circuit_presets","v":{"s":"Circuit Presets"}},{"k":"aria_load_preset","v":{"s":"Load Preset"}},{"k":"circuit_challenges_title","v":{"s":"Circuit Challenges"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next Question"}},{"k":"ohm_law_quiz","v":{"s":"Ohm's Law Quiz"}},{"k":"quiz_correct","v":{"s":"Correct!"}},{"k":"ai_circuit_tutor","v":{"s":"AI Circuit Tutor"}},{"k":"ai_question_label","v":{"s":"Question for the AI Circuit Tutor"}},{"k":"placeholder_ask","v":{"s":"Ask about circuits, Ohm's Law, components..."}},{"k":"aria_ai_thinking","v":{"s":"AI is thinking"}},{"k":"aria_ask_ai_tutor","v":{"s":"Ask the AI tutor"}},{"k":"thinking_ellipsis","v":{"s":"Thinking..."}},{"k":"ask_btn","v":{"s":"Ask"}},{"k":"ai_is_thinking","v":{"s":"AI is thinking..."}},{"k":"qa_ohms_law","v":{"s":"What is Ohm's Law?"}},{"k":"qa_series_parallel","v":{"s":"Series vs parallel?"}},{"k":"qa_short_circuit","v":{"s":"What is a short circuit?"}},{"k":"qa_capacitors","v":{"s":"How do capacitors work?"}},{"k":"qa_ammeter","v":{"s":"What does an ammeter measure?"}},{"k":"aria_ask_question","v":{"s":"Ask question"}},{"k":"aria_kirchhoff_laws","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff Laws"}},{"k":"kirchhoff_laws_title","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff's Laws"}},{"k":"kcl_title","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff's Current Law (KCL)"}},{"k":"kcl_desc","v":{"s":"The total current entering a junction equals the total current leaving that junction."}},{"k":"your_circuit_label","v":{"s":"Your circuit:"}},{"k":"kvl_law_title","v":{"s":"Kirchhoff's Voltage Law (KVL)"}},{"k":"kvl_law_desc","v":{"s":"The sum of all voltage drops around any closed loop equals the source voltage (EMF)."}},{"k":"key_relationships","v":{"s":"Key Relationships"}},{"k":"oscilloscope_label","v":{"s":"Oscilloscope"}},{"k":"aria_oscilloscope","v":{"s":"Circuit oscilloscope visualization showing voltage, current, and capacitor charge traces"}},{"k":"physics_resistor_how","v":{"s":"Electrons collide with atoms in the resistive material, converting electrical energy into heat. The more collisions (higher resistance), the less current flows."}},{"k":"physics_resistor_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a narrow section of pipe - it restricts water flow and creates pressure difference."}},{"k":"comp_light_bulb","v":{"s":"Light Bulb"}},{"k":"physics_bulb_how","v":{"s":"Current heats a thin wire (filament) to ~2,500°C until it glows white-hot. The filament's resistance increases with temperature."}},{"k":"physics_bulb_analogy","v":{"s":"Like rubbing your hands together fast - friction (resistance) creates heat and light."}},{"k":"physics_switch_how","v":{"s":"A physical gap in the conductor. When closed, electrons flow freely. When open, the air gap has near-infinite resistance, stopping current completely."}},{"k":"physics_switch_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a drawbridge - when up, nothing crosses. When down, traffic flows."}},{"k":"physics_led_how","v":{"s":"A semiconductor diode that emits photons when electrons drop from a high energy band to a low one. Different materials produce different colors."}},{"k":"physics_led_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a one-way door with a light - electrons can only go one direction, and they release light as they pass."}},{"k":"physics_ammeter_how","v":{"s":"Measures current by detecting the magnetic field created by flowing electrons. Connected in series so all current passes through it. Has very low internal resistance."}},{"k":"physics_ammeter_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a turnstile counting how many people pass per second."}},{"k":"physics_voltmeter_how","v":{"s":"Measures potential difference (voltage) between two points. Connected in parallel with very high internal resistance so it doesn't affect the circuit."}},{"k":"physics_voltmeter_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a pressure gauge on a water pipe - measures the push without blocking flow."}},{"k":"physics_capacitor_how","v":{"s":"Two metal plates separated by an insulator. Electrons accumulate on one plate and leave the other, storing energy in an electric field. Releases energy quickly when discharged."}},{"k":"physics_capacitor_analogy","v":{"s":"Like a water tank - it fills up slowly and can release all its stored water at once."}},{"k":"analogy_label","v":{"s":"Analogy: "}},{"k":"aria_read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read aloud"}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read aloud"}},{"k":"tap_component_hint","v":{"s":"Tap a component above to learn how it works inside!"}},{"k":"real_world_circuits_title","v":{"s":"Real-World Circuits"}},{"k":"app_flashlight_name","v":{"s":"Flashlight"}},{"k":"circuit_type_series","v":{"s":"Series"}},{"k":"app_flashlight_desc","v":{"s":"Battery + switch + bulb in series. Switch breaks circuit to turn off."}},{"k":"app_flashlight_comps","v":{"s":"Switch, Bulb"}},{"k":"app_phone_charger_name","v":{"s":"Phone Charger"}},{"k":"circuit_type_series_parallel","v":{"s":"Series + Parallel"}},{"k":"app_phone_charger_desc","v":{"s":"Transformer reduces 120V to 5V. Capacitors smooth the current for steady charging."}},{"k":"app_phone_charger_comps","v":{"s":"Resistor, Capacitor"}},{"k":"app_car_headlights_name","v":{"s":"Car Headlights"}},{"k":"circuit_type_parallel","v":{"s":"Parallel"}},{"k":"app_car_headlights_desc","v":{"s":"Headlights wired in parallel so if one burns out, the other stays on."}},{"k":"app_car_headlights_comps","v":{"s":"Bulb, Switch"}},{"k":"app_guitar_pedal_name","v":{"s":"Guitar Pedal"}},{"k":"app_guitar_pedal_desc","v":{"s":"Resistors and capacitors filter frequencies to create distortion or reverb effects."}},{"k":"app_guitar_pedal_comps","v":{"s":"Resistor, Capacitor"}},{"k":"app_traffic_light_name","v":{"s":"Traffic Light"}},{"k":"app_traffic_light_desc","v":{"s":"Three LED groups in parallel, controlled by a timer circuit switching between them."}},{"k":"app_traffic_light_comps","v":{"s":"LED, Switch"}},{"k":"app_heart_monitor_name","v":{"s":"Heart Monitor"}},{"k":"app_heart_monitor_desc","v":{"s":"Amplifies tiny electrical signals from heart muscle. Resistors set gain, capacitors filter noise."}},{"k":"app_heart_monitor_comps","v":{"s":"Resistor, Ammeter"}},{"k":"key_parts_label","v":{"s":"Key parts: "}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"read_aloud_btn","v":{"s":"Read Aloud"}},{"k":"footer_ohm","v":{"s":"Ohm's Law: V = IR"}},{"k":"footer_power","v":{"s":"Power: P = IV"}},{"k":"reference_tab_build","v":{"s":"Build"}},{"k":"reference_tab_reference","v":{"s":"Reference"}},{"k":"aria_workspace","v":{"s":"Circuit Builder workspace"}},{"k":"reference_library_title","v":{"s":"Circuit Reference Library"}},{"k":"reference_library_subtitle","v":{"s":"Interactive references — pick a topic below to explore."}},{"k":"close_section","v":{