tracts bees."}},{"k":"radish","v":{"s":"Radish"}},{"k":"fastest_crop_trap_crop_for_flea_beetle","v":{"s":"Fastest crop. Trap crop for flea beetles. Loosens soil."}},{"k":"borage","v":{"s":"Borage"}},{"k":"top_bee_attractor_deep_taproots_mine_m","v":{"s":"Top bee attractor. Deep taproots mine minerals from subsoil. Edible flowers. Self-seeds."}},{"k":"comfrey","v":{"s":"Comfrey"}},{"k":"dynamic_accumulator_deep_roots_pull_nu","v":{"s":"Dynamic accumulator — deep roots pull nutrients up. Cut-and-drop mulch. Permaculture staple."}},{"k":"buckwheat","v":{"s":"Buckwheat"}},{"k":"fast_cover_crop_suppresses_weeds_attra","v":{"s":"Fast cover crop. Suppresses weeds, attracts hoverflies, phosphorus accumulator. Till in as green manure."}},{"k":"yarrow","v":{"s":"Yarrow"}},{"k":"native_perennial_attracts_ladybugs_lac","v":{"s":"Native perennial. Attracts ladybugs, lacewings, parasitic wasps. Medicinal. Drought-proof."}},{"k":"bee_hotel","v":{"s":"Bee Hotel"}},{"k":"nesting_habitat_for_solitary_bees_maso","v":{"s":"Nesting habitat for solitary bees (mason bees, leafcutter bees). Boosts pollination in a 3-cell radius. Solitary bees pollinate 95% of wild plants!"}},{"k":"compost_bin","v":{"s":"Compost Bin"}},{"k":"converts_organic_matter_into_rich_humu","v":{"s":"Converts organic matter into rich humus. Boosts nitrogen and soil health in adjacent cells. The ultimate closed-loop nutrient cycle."}},{"k":"rain_barrel","v":{"s":"Rain Barrel"}},{"k":"captures_rainwater_for_irrigation_redu","v":{"s":"Captures rainwater for irrigation. Reduces moisture decay rate for the whole garden by 20%. Free water from the sky!"}},{"k":"strawberry_2","v":{"s":"Strawberry"}},{"k":"perennial_first_year_yield_is_low_doub","v":{"s":"Perennial. First-year yield is low; doubles in year 2. Runners spread to fill gaps. Great ground cover."}},{"k":"asparagus_2","v":{"s":"Asparagus"}},{"k":"perennial_don_t_harvest_for_3_years_th","v":{"s":"Perennial. Don't harvest for 3 years! Then produces for 20+ years. Deep roots improve soil structure."}},{"k":"blueberry_2","v":{"s":"Blueberry"}},{"k":"perennial_needs_acidic_soil_ph_4_5_5_5","v":{"s":"Perennial. Needs ACIDIC soil (pH 4.5-5.5). Takes 3 years to mature. Partner with azaleas."}},{"k":"garlic_2","v":{"s":"Garlic"}},{"k":"plant_in_fall_harvest_in_spring_strong","v":{"s":"Plant in fall, harvest in spring. Strong pest repellent. Deters aphids, spider mites, Japanese beetles."}},{"k":"rhubarb","v":{"s":"Rhubarb"}},{"k":"perennial_don_t_harvest_year_1_leaves_","v":{"s":"Perennial. Don't harvest year 1. Leaves are toxic (oxalic acid) — only eat the stalks! Produces for 10+ years."}},{"k":"basil_repels_tomato_hornworm_and_white","v":{"s":"Basil repels tomato hornworm and whiteflies"}},{"k":"marigold_roots_kill_soil_nematodes","v":{"s":"Marigold roots kill soil nematodes"}},{"k":"tomato_shade_protects_carrots_in_summe","v":{"s":"Tomato shade protects carrots in summer"}},{"k":"nasturtium_lures_aphids_away_from_toma","v":{"s":"Nasturtium lures aphids away from tomatoes"}},{"k":"same_family_share_blight_and_beetle_pe","v":{"s":"Same family — share blight and beetle pests!"}},{"k":"mature_dill_stunts_tomato_growth","v":{"s":"Mature dill stunts tomato growth"}},{"k":"beans_fix_nitrogen_corn_provides_a_tre","v":{"s":"Beans fix nitrogen; corn provides a trellis"}},{"k":"squash_shades_soil_corn_blocks_wind","v":{"s":"Squash shades soil; corn blocks wind"}},{"k":"living_mulch_nitrogen_thriving_squash","v":{"s":"Living mulch + nitrogen = thriving squash"}},{"k":"onion_sulfur_compounds_inhibit_bean_rh","v":{"s":"Onion sulfur compounds inhibit bean rhizobium"}},{"k":"onion_repels_carrot_fly_carrots_repel_","v":{"s":"Onion repels carrot fly; carrots repel onion fly"}},{"k":"dill_cross_pollinates_and_stunts_carro","v":{"s":"Dill cross-pollinates and stunts carrot roots"}},{"k":"radish_loosens_soil_for_shallow_lettuc","v":{"s":"Radish loosens soil for shallow lettuce roots"}},{"k":"sunflower_provides_afternoon_shade_for","v":{"s":"Sunflower provides afternoon shade for lettuce"}},{"k":"dill_attracts_predatory_wasps_that_eat","v":{"s":"Dill attracts predatory wasps that eat cucumber beetles"}},{"k":"sunflower_attracts_pollinators_for_cuc","v":{"s":"Sunflower attracts pollinators for cucumber flowers"}},{"k":"basil_deters_aphids_and_improves_peppe","v":{"s":"Basil deters aphids and improves pepper flavor"}},{"k":"rosemary_repels_cabbage_moth_larvae","v":{"s":"Rosemary repels cabbage moth larvae"}},{"k":"nasturtium_traps_cabbage_aphids","v":{"s":"Nasturtium traps cabbage aphids"}},{"k":"peas_fix_nitrogen_that_carrots_need","v":{"s":"Peas fix nitrogen that carrots need"}},{"k":"allium_family_inhibits_legume_nitrogen","v":{"s":"Allium family inhibits legume nitrogen fixation"}},{"k":"beans_fix_nitrogen_potatoes_provide_gr","v":{"s":"Beans fix nitrogen; potatoes provide ground cover"}},{"k":"allelopathic_sunflower_chemicals_stunt","v":{"s":"Allelopathic sunflower chemicals stunt potatoes"}},{"k":"marigold_attracts_beneficial_insects_t","v":{"s":"Marigold attracts beneficial insects to bean flowers"}},{"k":"mediterranean_herbs_thrive_together_at","v":{"s":"Mediterranean herbs thrive together; attract pollinators"}},{"k":"mint_deters_flea_beetles_on_brassicas","v":{"s":"Mint deters flea beetles on brassicas"}},{"k":"clover_fixes_nitrogen_and_prevents_ero","v":{"s":"Clover fixes nitrogen and prevents erosion"}},{"k":"living_mulch_retains_moisture_around_t","v":{"s":"Living mulch retains moisture around tomatoes"}},{"k":"borage_attracts_pollinators_and_repels","v":{"s":"Borage attracts pollinators and repels tomato hornworm"}},{"k":"borage_is_the_1_bee_attractor_essentia","v":{"s":"Borage is the #1 bee attractor — essential for squash pollination"}},{"k":"comfrey_deep_roots_mine_potassium_cut_","v":{"s":"Comfrey deep roots mine potassium — cut leaves make perfect tomato mulch"}},{"k":"comfrey_leaves_as_mulch_provide_potass","v":{"s":"Comfrey leaves as mulch provide potassium that potatoes crave"}},{"k":"buckwheat_attracts_hoverflies_whose_la","v":{"s":"Buckwheat attracts hoverflies whose larvae eat cucumber aphids"}},{"k":"fast_buckwheat_cover_suppresses_weeds_","v":{"s":"Fast buckwheat cover suppresses weeds around slow lettuce"}},{"k":"yarrow_attracts_parasitic_wasps_that_c","v":{"s":"Yarrow attracts parasitic wasps that control bean beetles"}},{"k":"yarrow_attracts_lacewings_that_devour_","v":{"s":"Yarrow attracts lacewings that devour cabbage aphids"}},{"k":"mint_invasion","v":{"s":"Mint Invasion!"}},{"k":"your_mint_has_spread_to_adjacent_plots","v":{"s":"Your mint has spread to adjacent plots! Mint sends underground runners that colonize nearby soil."}},{"k":"japanese_beetles","v":{"s":"Japanese Beetles!"}},{"k":"metallic_green_beetles_are_devouring_l","v":{"s":"Metallic green beetles are devouring leaves! They skeletonize foliage and damage fruit."}},{"k":"aphid_swarm","v":{"s":"Aphid Swarm!"}},{"k":"tiny_sap_sucking_insects_coat_the_stem","v":{"s":"Tiny sap-sucking insects coat the stems. They multiply explosively — one aphid can produce 80 offspring per week!"}},{"k":"cabbage_white_moths","v":{"s":"Cabbage White Moths!"}},{"k":"white_butterflies_are_laying_eggs_on_y","v":{"s":"White butterflies are laying eggs on your brassicas. The green caterpillars will devour leaves."}},{"k":"bindweed_invasion","v":{"s":"Bindweed Invasion!"}},{"k":"twisting_vines_are_strangling_your_pla","v":{"s":"Twisting vines are strangling your plants! Bindweed can grow 3-6 feet per season and regenerates from root fragments."}},{"k":"late_blight","v":{"s":"Late Blight!"}},{"k":"brown_patches_spread_across_leaves_thi","v":{"s":"Brown patches spread across leaves. This is the same disease that caused the Irish Potato Famine!"}},{"k":"spotted_lanternfly","v":{"s":"Spotted Lanternfly!"}},{"k":"these_invasive_insects_from_asia_suck_","v":{"s":"These invasive insects from Asia suck sap and excrete sticky honeydew that grows sooty mold."}},{"k":"first_harvest","v":{"s":"First Harvest"}},{"k":"harvest_your_first_crop","v":{"s":"Harvest your first crop"}},{"k":"companion_master","v":{"s":"Companion Master"}},{"k":"5_positive_companion_pairs_active","v":{"s":"5+ positive companion pairs active"}},{"k":"pollinator_paradise","v":{"s":"Pollinator Paradise"}},{"k":"plant_3_pollinator_attracting_species","v":{"s":"Plant 3+ pollinator-attracting species"}},{"k":"biodiversity_champion","v":{"s":"Biodiversity Champion"}},{"k":"10_different_species_planted","v":{"s":"10+ different species planted"}},{"k":"nitrogen_master","v":{"s":"Nitrogen Master"}},{"k":"keep_soil_nitrogen_above_60_for_10_day","v":{"s":"Keep soil nitrogen above 60 for 10+ days"}},{"k":"invasive_defender","v":{"s":"Invasive Defender"}},{"k":"successfully_manage_an_invasive_event","v":{"s":"Successfully manage an invasive event"}},{"k":"rotation_expert","v":{"s":"Rotation Expert"}},{"k":"complete_3_seasons_with_crop_rotation","v":{"s":"Complete 3 seasons with crop rotation"}},{"k":"organic_grower","v":{"s":"Organic Grower"}},{"k":"reach_harvest_without_any_plant_dying","v":{"s":"Reach harvest without any plant dying"}},{"k":"market_star","v":{"s":"Market Star"}},{"k":"earn_100_harvest_points_in_one_season","v":{"s":"Earn 100+ harvest points in one season"}},{"k":"three_sisters","v":{"s":"Three Sisters"}},{"k":"plant_corn_beans_squash_as_neighbors","v":{"s":"Plant corn + beans + squash as neighbors"}},{"k":"regenerative_gardener","v":{"s":"Regenerative Gardener"}},{"k":"plant_3_regenerative_species_comfrey_c","v":{"s":"Plant 3+ regenerative species (comfrey, clover, buckwheat, yarrow, borage)"}},{"k":"soil_healer","v":{"s":"Soil Healer"}},{"k":"maintain_nitrogen_above_50_with_only_l","v":{"s":"Maintain nitrogen above 50 with only legumes (no compost action)"}},{"k":"pollinator_rescue","v":{"s":"Pollinator Rescue"}},{"k":"a_local_farm_s_cucumber_and_squash_yie","v":{"s":"A local farm's cucumber and squash yields are crashing because pollinators have disappeared. Design a garden that attracts pollinators AND produces food."}},{"k":"harvest_3_pollinator_dependent_crops_c","v":{"s":"Harvest 3+ pollinator-dependent crops (cucumber, squash) with at least 3 pollinator plants nearby."}},{"k":"marigold_sunflower_lavender_borage_and","v":{"s":"Marigold, sunflower, lavender, borage, and nasturtium all attract bees and butterflies. Plant them NEAR your cucumbers and squash."}},{"k":"the_nitrogen_crisis","v":{"s":"The Nitrogen Crisis"}},{"k":"after_years_of_corn_monoculture_this_s","v":{"s":"After years of corn monoculture, this soil is depleted (nitrogen starts at 10). Restore soil fertility using ONLY biological nitrogen fixation — no compost allowed!"}},{"k":"raise_nitrogen_above_60_using_only_leg","v":{"s":"Raise nitrogen above 60 using only legumes (beans, peas, clover)."}},{"k":"legumes_have_symbiotic_bacteria_rhizob","v":{"s":"Legumes have symbiotic bacteria (Rhizobium) in their root nodules that convert atmospheric N₂ into plant-available ammonium. This is how nature makes fertilizer."}},{"k":"pest_gauntlet","v":{"s":"Pest Gauntlet"}},{"k":"design_a_garden_that_can_survive_a_ful","v":{"s":"Design a garden that can survive a full summer of pest pressure WITHOUT any weeding actions. Use only companion planting and beneficial insect attraction for defense."}},{"k":"keep_average_plant_health_above_60_thr","v":{"s":"Keep average plant health above 60% through summer with 8+ plants and no weeding."}},{"k":"dill_attracts_parasitic_wasps_nasturti","v":{"s":"Dill attracts parasitic wasps. Nasturtium is a trap crop. Marigold kills nematodes. Yarrow attracts ladybugs. Build your defense through biodiversity!"}},{"k":"four_season_rotation","v":{"s":"Four-Season Rotation"}},{"k":"practice_the_4_year_crop_rotation_syst","v":{"s":"Practice the 4-year crop rotation system: Year 1 legumes → Year 2 brassicas → Year 3 nightshades → Year 4 root crops. No family should repeat in the same plot."}},{"k":"complete_4_seasons_with_proper_family_","v":{"s":"Complete 4 seasons with proper family rotation in every cell. Zero rotation warnings."}},{"k":"season_1_beans_peas_clover_season_2_br","v":{"s":"Season 1: beans, peas, clover. Season 2: broccoli, radish. Season 3: tomato, pepper, potato. Season 4: carrot, onion. Each season, move families to new plots."}},{"k":"the_regenerative_garden","v":{"s":"The Regenerative Garden"}},{"k":"build_a_garden_that_improves_the_ecosy","v":{"s":"Build a garden that IMPROVES the ecosystem over time. Start with degraded soil and transform it into a thriving, self-sustaining food forest."}},{"k":"achieve_ecosystem_score_80_with_3_rege","v":{"s":"Achieve ecosystem score 80+ with 3+ regenerative plants, 5+ families, and nitrogen above 50."}},{"k":"comfrey_buckwheat_and_yarrow_heal_soil","v":{"s":"Comfrey, buckwheat, and yarrow heal soil. Clover and beans fix nitrogen. Borage attracts pollinators. A regenerative garden feeds itself."}},{"k":"three_sisters_in_the_community","v":{"s":"Three Sisters in the Community"}},{"k":"the_three_sisters_corn_beans_squash_is","v":{"s":"The Three Sisters (corn, beans, squash) is a 7,000-year-old agricultural system from Indigenous Mesoamerican and Haudenosaunee traditions. Plant them as neighbors and watch the synergy."}},{"k":"plant_corn_beans_and_squash_adjacent_t","v":{"s":"Plant corn, beans, and squash adjacent to each other and harvest all three."}},{"k":"corn_provides_a_trellis_for_beans_bean","v":{"s":"Corn provides a trellis for beans. Beans fix nitrogen for corn. Squash shades the soil and suppresses weeds. Together, they provide complete nutrition."}},{"k":"ladybug_colony","v":{"s":"Ladybug Colony!"}},{"k":"ladybugs_have_moved_in_a_single_ladybu","v":{"s":"Ladybugs have moved in! A single ladybug eats 50+ aphids per day."}},{"k":"spring_rain","v":{"s":"Spring Rain!"}},{"k":"a_gentle_soaking_rain_replenishes_the_","v":{"s":"A gentle soaking rain replenishes the soil."}},{"k":"mycorrhizal_bloom_2","v":{"s":"Mycorrhizal Bloom!"}},{"k":"beneficial_fungi_have_colonized_the_ro","v":{"s":"Beneficial fungi have colonized the root zone, extending root reach by 100×."}},{"k":"earthworm_surge","v":{"s":"Earthworm Surge!"}},{"k":"earthworm_populations_are_thriving_the","v":{"s":"Earthworm populations are thriving! They aerate soil and recycle nutrients."}},{"k":"pollinator_migration","v":{"s":"Pollinator Migration!"}},{"k":"monarch_butterflies_are_passing_throug","v":{"s":"Monarch butterflies are passing through! Pollinator-dependent crops get a boost."}},{"k":"patience_pays_off","v":{"s":"Patience Pays Off"}},{"k":"your_first_harvest_took_many_days_of_c","v":{"s":"Your first harvest took many days of care. Think about a goal in your life that takes patience. What helps you keep going when progress feels slow?"}},{"k":"dealing_with_setbacks","v":{"s":"Dealing with Setbacks"}},{"k":"pests_damaged_your_garden_how_did_you_","v":{"s":"Pests damaged your garden. How did you respond? When something goes wrong in your life, do you react quickly or take time to think about the best response?"}},{"k":"better_together","v":{"s":"Better Together"}},{"k":"you_discovered_that_some_plants_help_e","v":{"s":"You discovered that some plants help each other grow. Who in your life helps you grow? How do you help them?"}},{"k":"learning_from_the_past","v":{"s":"Learning from the Past"}},{"k":"planting_the_same_thing_in_the_same_pl","v":{"s":"Planting the same thing in the same place causes problems. Can you think of a time when doing the same thing over and over stopped working? What did you change?"}},{"k":"strength_in_diversity","v":{"s":"Strength in Diversity"}},{"k":"your_diverse_garden_attracted_more_hel","v":{"s":"Your diverse garden attracted more helpful insects and resisted pests better. How does diversity make communities stronger? What happens when everyone thinks and acts the same way?"}},{"k":"protecting_what_matters","v":{"s":"Protecting What Matters"}},{"k":"you_managed_an_invasive_species_that_t","v":{"s":"You managed an invasive species that threatened your garden. Is there ever a time in your life when you had to stand up to protect something you cared about?"}},{"k":"root_system","v":{"s":"Root System"}},{"k":"soil_chemistry","v":{"s":"Soil Chemistry"}},{"k":"cell_biology","v":{"s":"Cell Biology"}},{"k":"fungal_network","v":{"s":"Fungal Network"}},{"k":"zoom_into_the_invisible_world_beneath_","v":{"s":"Zoom into the invisible world beneath the garden"}},{"k":"back_to_garden","v":{"s":"← Back to Garden"}},{"k":"microscope_science_layers","v":{"s":"Microscope science layers"}},{"k":"root_nodules_nitrogen_fixation","v":{"s":"🔴 Root Nodules (Nitrogen Fixation)"}},{"k":"swollen_pink_red_nodules_on_the_roots_","v":{"s":"Swollen pink/red nodules on the roots contain billions of Rhizobium bacteria. These bacteria have an enzyme called nitrogenase that breaks the triple bond of atmospheric N₂ — one of the strongest chemical bonds in nature."}},{"k":"n_8h_8e_16atp_2nh_h_16adp","v":{"s":"N₂ + 8H⁺ + 8e⁻ + 16ATP → 2NH₃ + H₂ + 16ADP"}},{"k":"this_reaction_converts_inert_nitrogen_","v":{"s":"This reaction converts inert nitrogen gas into ammonia (NH₃), which plant roots can absorb. It's the biological equivalent of the industrial Haber-Bosch process — but at room temperature, using only sunlight energy."}},{"k":"water_transport_osmosis","v":{"s":"💧 Water Transport (Osmosis)"}},{"k":"root_hairs_increase_surface_area_by_10","v":{"s":"Root hairs increase surface area by 100×. Water enters by osmosis — moving from low solute concentration (soil) to high (root cells). The path: soil → root hair → cortex → xylem → stem → leaves."}},{"k":"soil_root_h_o_flows_into_root","v":{"s":"Ψ_soil > Ψ_root → H₂O flows into root"}},{"k":"water_potential_drives_the_flow_transp","v":{"s":"Water potential (Ψ) drives the flow. Transpiration from leaves creates negative pressure that pulls water up — like a straw. A large tree can move 100+ gallons per day this way."}},{"k":"the_npk_cycle","v":{"s":"🧪 The NPK Cycle"}},{"k":"nitrogen","v":{"s":"Nitrogen"}},{"k":"drives_leaf_growth","v":{"s":"Drives leaf growth"}},{"k":"phosphorus","v":{"s":"Phosphorus"}},{"k":"drives_roots_fruit","v":{"s":"Drives roots & fruit"}},{"k":"potassium","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"drives_health_water","v":{"s":"Drives health & water"}},{"k":"companion_chemistry_active","v":{"s":"🧬 Companion Chemistry (Active)"}},{"k":"allelopathy_chemical_warfare","v":{"s":"☠️ Allelopathy (Chemical Warfare)"}},{"k":"some_plants_release_chemicals_from_the","v":{"s":"Some plants release chemicals from their roots that inhibit competitors. Sunflowers release heliannuol. Black walnut releases juglone. Even decomposing brassica leaves release glucosinolates that can suppress the next crop."}},{"k":"root_exudates_soil_neighboring_root_up","v":{"s":"Root exudates → soil → neighboring root uptake → growth inhibition"}},{"k":"photosynthesis_chloroplasts","v":{"s":"☀️ Photosynthesis (Chloroplasts)"}},{"k":"inside_each_leaf_cell_hundreds_of_chlo","v":{"s":"Inside each leaf cell, hundreds of chloroplasts capture photons of sunlight. Chlorophyll molecules absorb red and blue light (reflecting green — that's why plants look green). The energy splits water molecules and drives carbon fixation:"}},{"k":"6co_6h_o_light_c_h_o_6o","v":{"s":"6CO₂ + 6H₂O + light → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂"}},{"k":"this_single_equation_sustains_nearly_a","v":{"s":"This single equation sustains nearly all life on Earth. The glucose (C₆H₁₂O₆) becomes the plant's energy and building material. The oxygen (O₂) is released — every breath you take was made by a plant."}},{"k":"cellular_respiration_mitochondria","v":{"s":"🔥 Cellular Respiration (Mitochondria)"}},{"k":"at_night_and_always_in_roots_mitochond","v":{"s":"At night (and always, in roots), mitochondria reverse the process — burning glucose to release energy for growth:"}},{"k":"c_h_o_6o_6co_6h_o_36_atp","v":{"s":"C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O + 36 ATP"}},{"k":"atp_is_the_universal_energy_currency_o","v":{"s":"ATP is the universal energy currency of life. Every cell process — growth, division, nutrient transport — spends ATP. A growing plant produces and consumes millions of ATP molecules per second."}},{"k":"cell_wall_turgor_pressure","v":{"s":"🧱 Cell Wall & Turgor Pressure"}},{"k":"plant_cells_have_a_rigid_cellulose_wal","v":{"s":"Plant cells have a rigid cellulose wall (animals don't). Water pressure inside the cell (turgor) pushes against the wall, keeping the plant upright. When a plant wilts, it's losing turgor pressure — the cells deflate like tiny balloons."}},{"k":"live_plant_processes_right_now","v":{"s":"📊 Live Plant Processes (right now)"}},{"k":"photosynthesis_rate","v":{"s":"☀️ Photosynthesis rate"}},{"k":"respiration_rate","v":{"s":"🔥 Respiration rate"}},{"k":"transpiration_water_loss_through_leave","v":{"s":"💨 Transpiration (water loss through leaves)"}},{"k":"the_wood_wide_web","v":{"s":"🍄 The Wood Wide Web"}},{"k":"mycorrhizal_networks","v":{"s":"🕸️ Mycorrhizal Networks"}},{"k":"beneath_your_garden_an_invisible_inter","v":{"s":"Beneath your garden, an invisible internet of fungal threads (hyphae) connects 90% of plant species. These mycorrhizal fungi form symbiotic partnerships with roots:"}},{"k":"plant","v":{"s":"Plant"}},{"k":"sugars_c_h_o","v":{"s":"← sugars (C₆H₁₂O₆)"}},{"k":"fungus","v":{"s":"Fungus"}},{"k":"phosphorus_po","v":{"s":"phosphorus (PO₄³⁻) →"}},{"k":"plant_2","v":{"s":"Plant"}},{"k":"the_fungus_extends_the_plant_s_root_sy","v":{"s":"The fungus extends the plant's root system by 100-1000×, accessing water and phosphorus the roots can't reach. In return, the plant shares 10-30% of its photosynthesized sugars. Both benefit — neither survives as well alone."}},{"k":"chemical_signaling","v":{"s":"📡 Chemical Signaling"}},{"k":"when_one_plant_is_attacked_by_pests_it","v":{"s":"When one plant is attacked by pests, it sends chemical alarm signals through the fungal network. Connected plants receive the warning and preemptively produce defensive compounds — before the pest even reaches them."}},{"k":"mother_trees_the_largest_oldest_plants","v":{"s":"\"Mother trees\" (the largest, oldest plants) share nutrients with seedlings through the network, especially shaded seedlings that can't photosynthesize enough on their own. The forest — and the garden — is a community, not a competition."}},{"k":"threats_to_the_network","v":{"s":"⚠️ Threats to the Network"}},{"k":"tilling_destroys_mycorrhizal_networks_","v":{"s":"Tilling destroys mycorrhizal networks (this is why no-till farming is gaining traction). Chemical fertilizers can suppress fungal growth — the plant gets \"lazy\" and stops feeding its fungal partner. Garlic mustard (an invasive) releases chemicals that specifically destroy mycorrhizal fungi, devastating the entire underground ecosystem."}},{"k":"growth","v":{"s":"Growth"}},{"k":"health","v":{"s":"Health"}},{"k":"companion","v":{"s":"Companion"}},{"k":"pests","v":{"s":"Pests"}},{"k":"write_your_reflection_here","v":{"s":"Write your reflection here..."}},{"k":"your_reflection","v":{"s":"Your reflection"}},{"k":"save_to_journal","v":{"s":"📝 Save to Journal"}},{"k":"skip","v":{"s":"Skip"}},{"k":"science","v":{"s":"🔬 Science: "}},{"k":"spring_perfect_for_cool_season_crops_l","v":{"s":"Spring: Perfect for cool-season crops (lettuce, peas, radish, broccoli). Plant nitrogen-fixers now to enrich soil for summer heavy feeders."}},{"k":"summer_peak_growth_for_warm_season_cro","v":{"s":"Summer: Peak growth for warm-season crops (tomatoes, peppers, squash). Watch moisture closely — evaporation is 2.5× higher. Pollinators are most active now!"}},{"k":"autumn_harvest_mature_crops_before_fro","v":{"s":"Autumn: Harvest mature crops before frost. Plant cover crops (clover) in empty plots to protect soil over winter. Cool-season crops can go in for a fall harvest."}},{"k":"winter_growth_stops_this_is_planning_s","v":{"s":"Winter: Growth stops. This is planning season! Review what worked, note crop rotation for spring, and let the soil rest. Compost decomposes slowly in cold."}},{"k":"some_pollinator_coverage","v":{"s":"Some pollinator coverage"}},{"k":"no_pollinator_plants_yields_suffering","v":{"s":"No pollinator plants — yields suffering"}},{"k":"overall_season_grade","v":{"s":"Overall Season Grade"}},{"k":"select_a_plant_then_click_a_cell_to_pl","v":{"s":"🌱 Select a plant, then click a cell to place it:"}},{"k":"fixes_nitrogen","v":{"s":"⚗️ Fixes nitrogen"}},{"k":"heavy_feeder","v":{"s":"⚗️ Heavy feeder"}},{"k":"needs_pollinators","v":{"s":"🐝 Needs pollinators"}},{"k":"attracts_pollinators","v":{"s":"🐝 Attracts pollinators"}},{"k":"regenerative","v":{"s":"♻️ Regenerative"}},{"k":"native_species","v":{"s":"🏔️ Native species"}},{"k":"keep_apart","v":{"s":"← keep apart"}},{"k":"good_neighbor","v":{"s":"good neighbor →"}},{"k":"good_neighbors","v":{"s":"✅ Good neighbors: "}},{"k":"keep_apart_2","v":{"s":"⚠️ Keep apart: "}},{"k":"ecosystem_health","v":{"s":"🌍 Ecosystem Health"}},{"k":"plant_families","v":{"s":"Plant Families"}},{"k":"pollinators","v":{"s":"🐝 Pollinators"}},{"k":"avg_health","v":{"s":"Avg Health"}},{"k":"low_diversity_try_planting_more_plant_","v":{"s":"⚠️ Low diversity — try planting more plant families for ecosystem resilience"}},{"k":"no_pollinator_plants_squash_and_cucumb","v":{"s":"⚠️ No pollinator plants — squash and cucumber yields will suffer"}},{"k":"high_pest_pressure_companion_plants_an","v":{"s":"🐛 High pest pressure — companion plants and weeding can help"}},{"k":"nitrogen_depleted_plant_legumes_beans_","v":{"s":"⚗️ Nitrogen depleted — plant legumes (beans, peas, clover) to fix nitrogen"}},{"k":"soil_is_dry_water_soon_to_prevent_wilt","v":{"s":"💧 Soil is dry — water soon to prevent wilting"}},{"k":"overwatered_roots_may_rot_let_soil_dra","v":{"s":"💧 Overwatered — roots may rot. Let soil drain."}},{"k":"your_garden_ecosystem_is_thriving_dive","v":{"s":"🌳 Your garden ecosystem is thriving! Diversity and companion planting are working."}},{"k":"regenerative_plants_are_healing_your_s","v":{"s":"♻️ Regenerative plants are healing your soil — comfrey, clover, and buckwheat build long-term fertility without synthetic inputs."}},{"k":"no_regenerative_plants_yet_try_comfrey","v":{"s":"♻️ No regenerative plants yet. Try comfrey, buckwheat, yarrow, or clover — they build soil health for future seasons."}},{"k":"try_regenerative_plants_comfrey_buckwh","v":{"s":"Try regenerative plants (comfrey, buckwheat, clover, yarrow) — they heal soil, attract beneficials, and build long-term garden health."}},{"k":"your_pollinator_dependent_crops_need_n","v":{"s":"Your pollinator-dependent crops need nearby flowers. Plant marigold, borage, lavender, or sunflower within 2 cells for full yield."}},{"k":"empty_soil_erodes_and_loses_nutrients_","v":{"s":"Empty soil erodes and loses nutrients. Cover crops (clover, buckwheat) protect soil even when you're not growing food — like nature's blanket."}},{"k":"mint_is_an_aggressive_spreader_in_real","v":{"s":"Mint is an aggressive spreader! In real gardens, always plant mint in containers. Here it may invade adjacent cells — monitor carefully."}},{"k":"regenerative_tip_instead_of_adding_fer","v":{"s":"Regenerative tip: Instead of adding fertilizer, plant legumes (beans, peas, clover). They partner with soil bacteria to pull nitrogen from the air — free and sustainable!"}},{"k":"sustainable_gardening_tips","v":{"s":"♻️ Sustainable Gardening Tips"}},{"k":"isometric_community_garden_view_click_","v":{"s":"Isometric community garden view. Click plots to plant or inspect."}},{"k":"watered","v":{"s":"💧 Watered!"}},{"k":"weeded","v":{"s":"🌿 Weeded!"}},{"k":"composted","v":{"s":"♻️ Composted!"}},{"k":"companion_planting_guide","v":{"s":"📖 Companion Planting Guide"}},{"k":"active","v":{"s":"✓ Active"}},{"k":"active_2","v":{"s":"⚠️ Active!"}},{"k":"garden_challenges_optional","v":{"s":"🎯 Garden Challenges (optional)"}},{"k":"close_challenge","v":{"s":"Close challenge"}},{"k":"start_growing_season","v":{"s":"Start growing season"}},{"k":"start_growing","v":{"s":"▶️ Start Growing"}},{"k":"clear_all","v":{"s":"🗑️ Clear All"}},{"k":"next_day","v":{"s":"⏩ Next Day"}},{"k":"5_days","v":{"s":"⏭️ +5 Days"}},{"k":"water_2","v":{"s":"💧 Water"}},{"k":"weed_2","v":{"s":"🧹 Weed"}},{"k":"compost_2","v":{"s":"🧱 Compost"}},{"k":"harvest_ready_crops","v":{"s":"Harvest ready crops"}},{"k":"harvest_ready","v":{"s":"🌾 Harvest Ready"}},{"k":"edit_garden","v":{"s":"✏️ Edit Garden"}},{"k":"soil_chemistry_2","v":{"s":"🧪 Soil Chemistry"}},{"k":"nitrogen_2","v":{"s":"Nitrogen"}},{"k":"phosphorus_2","v":{"s":"Phosphorus"}},{"k":"potassium_2","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"soil_ph_ideal_6_0_7_0_for_most_crops","v":{"s":"Soil pH (ideal 6.0-7.0 for most crops)"}},{"k":"organic_matter_higher_better_water_ret","v":{"s":"Organic matter % (higher = better water retention)"}},{"k":"add_lime_to_raise_ph_0_50","v":{"s":"Add lime to raise pH (+$0.50)"}},{"k":"lime","v":{"s":"⬆ Lime"}},{"k":"add_sulfur_to_lower_ph_0_50","v":{"s":"Add sulfur to lower pH (+$0.50)"}},{"k":"sulfur","v":{"s":"⬇ Sulfur"}},{"k":"farm_budget","v":{"s":"💰 Farm Budget"}},{"k":"pest_ecosystem","v":{"s":"🐛 Pest Ecosystem"}},{"k":"release_ladybugs_1_50","v":{"s":"Release ladybugs ($1.50)"}},{"k":"ladybugs","v":{"s":"🐞 Ladybugs"}},{"k":"neem_spray_1_00","v":{"s":"Neem spray ($1.00)"}},{"k":"neem","v":{"s":"🌿 Neem"}},{"k":"hand_pick_pests_free","v":{"s":"Hand-pick pests (free)"}},{"k":"pick","v":{"s":"✋ Pick"}},{"k":"row_covers_2_00","v":{"s":"Row covers ($2.00)"}},{"k":"covers","v":{"s":"🧵 Covers"}},{"k":"garden_advisor","v":{"s":"🧑‍🌾 Garden Advisor"}},{"k":"achievements","v":{"s":"🏆 Achievements"}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"community_garden_simulator","v":{"s":"🏡 Community Garden Simulator"}},{"k":"plan_plant_and_manage_a_diverse_garden","v":{"s":"Plan, plant, and manage a diverse garden ecosystem"}},{"k":"three_sisters_2","v":{"s":"🌽 Three Sisters"}},{"k":"community_garden","v":{"s":"🏡 Community Garden"}},{"k":"back_to_tools_2","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"companion_planting_lab","v":{"s":"🌱 Companion Planting Lab"}},{"k":"the_milpa_three_sisters_7_000_years_of","v":{"s":"The milpa / Three Sisters — 7,000+ years of agricultural science"}},{"k":"community_garden_2","v":{"s":"🏡 Community Garden"}},{"k":"cultural_context","v":{"s":"Cultural context"}},{"k":"origins","v":{"s":"📜 Origins"}},{"k":"compare","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"compare_2","v":{"s":"🔬 Compare"}},{"k":"soil_science","v":{"s":"Soil Science"}},{"k":"soil_science_2","v":{"s":"🧪 Soil Science"}},{"k":"cultural_origins_living_knowledge","v":{"s":"📜 Cultural Origins & Living Knowledge"}},{"k":"mesoamerican_origins_the_milpa","v":{"s":"Mesoamerican Origins: The Milpa"}},{"k":"the_companion_planting_of_corn_beans_a","v":{"s":"The companion planting of corn, beans, and squash — known as milpa in Mesoamerica — is one of humanity's oldest agricultural innovations. Archaeological evidence traces this system to over 7,000 years ago in present-day Mexico. Squash was domesticated ~10,000 years ago, maize ~9,000 years ago from the wild grass teosinte, and common beans ~7,000 years ago."}},{"k":"the_milpa_system_diffused_northward_ov","v":{"s":"The milpa system diffused northward over millennia, appearing in North America around 1070 CE. By the time European colonizers arrived around 1500, it was a foundational food system across Central and North America."}},{"k":"haudenosaunee_tradition_de_oh_h_ko","v":{"s":"Haudenosaunee Tradition: De-oh-há-ko"}},{"k":"the_haudenosaunee_iroquois_confederacy","v":{"s":"The Haudenosaunee (Iroquois Confederacy) call these plants De-oh-há-ko — \"they sustain us.\" The Three Sisters are spiritual beings and precious gifts, central to Haudenosaunee language, ceremony, songs, and cosmology. This is not merely a farming technique — it is a living relationship between people and plants."}},{"k":"this_knowledge_is_not_historical_artif","v":{"s":"This knowledge is not historical artifact. Milpa and Three Sisters gardens are actively cultivated today across the Americas, representing a continuous tradition of ecological wisdom."}},{"k":"learn_more","v":{"s":"Learn more:"}},{"k":"haudenosaunee_confederacy","v":{"s":"Haudenosaunee Confederacy"}},{"k":"smithsonian_nmai","v":{"s":"Smithsonian NMAI"}},{"k":"usda_three_sisters","v":{"s":"USDA: Three Sisters"}},{"k":"companion_planting_garden_visualizatio","v":{"s":"Companion planting garden visualization showing corn, beans, and squash growing together"}},{"k":"needs_meters","v":{"s":"🧪 Needs & Meters"}},{"k":"overall_soil_health","v":{"s":"Overall Soil Health"}},{"k":"monoculture_comparison","v":{"s":"🌽 Monoculture Comparison"}},{"k":"without_beans_nitrogen_depletes_withou","v":{"s":"⚠️ Without beans, nitrogen depletes. Without squash leaves, moisture drops and weeds take over."}},{"k":"the_science","v":{"s":"🔬 The Science"}},{"k":"nitrogen_fixation_2","v":{"s":"Nitrogen Fixation:"}},{"k":"rhizobium_bacteria_in_bean_root_nodule_2","v":{"s":" Rhizobium bacteria in bean root nodules convert atmospheric N₂ → NH₃ (ammonia) via nitrogenase enzyme. This biological process enriches soil without synthetic fertilizers."}},{"k":"living_mulch","v":{"s":"Living Mulch:"}},{"k":"squash_s_broad_leaves_create_ground_co","v":{"s":" Squash's broad leaves create ground cover that shades soil, reducing evapotranspiration by up to 50% and suppressing weed germination by blocking sunlight."}},{"k":"structural_symbiosis","v":{"s":"Structural Symbiosis:"}},{"k":"corn_stalks_serve_as_natural_trellises","v":{"s":" Corn stalks serve as natural trellises for bean vines. Bean vines, in turn, stabilize corn against wind shear."}},{"k":"nutritional_complementarity","v":{"s":"Nutritional Complementarity:"}},{"k":"corn_provides_methionine_rich_carbohyd","v":{"s":" Corn provides methionine-rich carbohydrates; beans provide lysine-rich protein. Together they form a complete amino acid profile — a balanced diet from one garden."}},{"k":"corn_3","v":{"s":"Corn"}},{"k":"beans_3","v":{"s":"Beans"}},{"k":"squash_3","v":{"s":"Squash"}},{"k":"grow","v":{"s":"▶ Grow!"}},{"k":"plant_all_three_seeds_to_begin","v":{"s":"Plant all three seeds to begin"}},{"k":"actions","v":{"s":"🎮 Actions"}},{"k":"water_3","v":{"s":"Water"}},{"k":"irrigation_replenishes_soil_moisture_o","v":{"s":"Irrigation replenishes soil moisture. Over-watering leaches nutrients."}},{"k":"compost_3","v":{"s":"Compost"}},{"k":"composting_adds_organic_nitrogen_and_b","v":{"s":"Composting adds organic nitrogen and beneficial microbes."}},{"k":"weed_3","v":{"s":"Weed"}},{"k":"weeding_removes_competition_for_light_","v":{"s":"Weeding removes competition for light, water, and nutrients."}},{"k":"inspect_2","v":{"s":"Inspect"}},{"k":"inspecting_reveals_plant_condition_and","v":{"s":"Inspecting reveals plant condition and early pest signs."}},{"k":"mulch_2","v":{"s":"Mulch"}},{"k":"mulching_regulates_soil_temperature_an","v":{"s":"Mulching regulates soil temperature and retains moisture."}},{"k":"companion_synergies","v":{"s":"🤝 Companion Synergies"}},{"k":"corn_beans","v":{"s":"Corn ↔ Beans"}},{"k":"structural_support_n_fixation","v":{"s":"Structural support & N-fixation"}},{"k":"beans_soil","v":{"s":"Beans → Soil"}},{"k":"rhizobium_nitrogen_enrichment","v":{"s":"Rhizobium nitrogen enrichment"}},{"k":"squash_all","v":{"s":"Squash → All"}},{"k":"living_mulch_pest_deterrent","v":{"s":"Living mulch & pest deterrent"}},{"k":"crop_yields","v":{"s":"🌾 Crop Yields"}},{"k":"corn_4","v":{"s":"Corn"}},{"k":"beans_4","v":{"s":"Beans"}},{"k":"squash_4","v":{"s":"Squash"}},{"k":"total_harvest","v":{"s":"Total Harvest"}},{"k":"health_score","v":{"s":"Health Score"}},{"k":"avg_synergy","v":{"s":"Avg Synergy"}},{"k":"season_score","v":{"s":"Season Score"}},{"k":"harvest_and_start_next_season","v":{"s":"Harvest and start next season"}},{"k":"harvest_start_next_season","v":{"s":"🌾 Harvest & Start Next Season →"}},{"k":"quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"quiz_2","v":{"s":"🧠 Quiz"}},{"k":"science_2","v":{"s":"Science"}},{"k":"science_3","v":{"s":"📚 Science"}},{"k":"the_three_sisters_science_of_companion","v":{"s":"🌾 The Three Sisters: Science of Companion Planting"}},{"k":"corn_structural_support","v":{"s":"Corn (Structural Support)"}},{"k":"grows_tall_stalks_6_10_ft_that_serve_a","v":{"s":"Grows tall stalks (6–10 ft) that serve as natural trellises for bean vines, providing vertical structure and replacing the need for artificial poles."}},{"k":"beans_nitrogen_fixation","v":{"s":"Beans (Nitrogen Fixation)"}},{"k":"rhizobium_bacteria_in_root_nodules_con","v":{"s":"Rhizobium bacteria in root nodules convert atmospheric N₂ into plant-usable ammonia. This biological nitrogen fixation enriches the soil without synthetic fertilizer."}},{"k":"squash_living_mulch","v":{"s":"Squash (Living Mulch)"}},{"k":"broad_leaves_shade_the_soil_reducing_w","v":{"s":"Broad leaves shade the soil, reducing water evaporation by up to 50%. Prickly stems deter animal pests. Acts as natural weed suppression through ground cover."}},{"k":"underground_chemistry","v":{"s":"🧪 Underground Chemistry"}},{"k":"mycorrhizal_network","v":{"s":"Mycorrhizal Network"}},{"k":"fungal_threads_extend_root_systems_100","v":{"s":"Fungal threads extend root systems 100–1000x, creating an underground wood wide web that trades soil minerals for plant sugars."}},{"k":"rhizobium_nodules","v":{"s":"Rhizobium Nodules"}},{"k":"specialized_bacteria_colonize_bean_roo","v":{"s":"Specialized bacteria colonize bean roots, forming visible pink nodules. Nitrogenase enzyme breaks the triple bond in N₂ gas, producing ammonia for all plants."}},{"k":"water_cycling","v":{"s":"Water Cycling"}},{"k":"squash_leaf_shade_reduces_soil_tempera","v":{"s":"Squash leaf shade reduces soil temperature by up to 10°F, cutting evaporation in half. Fallen leaves build organic matter, improving water retention."}},{"k":"nutrient_cycling","v":{"s":"Nutrient Cycling"}},{"k":"corn_is_a_heavy_nitrogen_feeder_beans_","v":{"s":"Corn is a heavy nitrogen feeder. Beans replace what corn takes. Squash returns organic matter. Together they maintain soil fertility without synthetic inputs."}},{"k":"cultural_heritage","v":{"s":"🏛️ Cultural Heritage"}},{"k":"the_three_sisters_de_oh_h_ko_meaning_t","v":{"s":"The Three Sisters (De-oh-há-ko, meaning “they sustain us” in Haudenosaunee) is a 7,000-year-old agricultural system originating in Mesoamerica. Indigenous agricultural science developed sophisticated polyculture techniques millennia before modern ecology."}},{"k":"together_corn_and_beans_provide_a_comp","v":{"s":"Together, corn and beans provide a complete protein — corn supplies methionine while beans supply lysine — forming the nutritional foundation of many Indigenous diets."}},{"k":"sustainable_farming_industrial_vs_rege","v":{"s":"⚖️ Sustainable Farming: Industrial vs Regenerative"}},{"k":"how_does_companion_planting_compare_to","v":{"s":"How does companion planting compare to industrial farming? The energy, water, and carbon differences are staggering."}},{"k":"energy_efficiency_eroi","v":{"s":"Energy Efficiency (EROI)"}},{"k":"biodiversity_score","v":{"s":"Biodiversity Score"}},{"k":"energy","v":{"s":"⚡ Energy"}},{"k":"water_4","v":{"s":"💧 Water"}},{"k":"chemicals","v":{"s":"🧪 Chemicals"}},{"k":"carbon","v":{"s":"🌍 Carbon"}},{"k":"pros","v":{"s":"✅ Pros"}},{"k":"cons","v":{"s":"⚠ Cons"}},{"k":"food_miles_carbon_calculator","v":{"s":"🚚 Food Miles & Carbon Calculator"}},{"k":"how_much_energy_does_your_food_travel_","v":{"s":"How much energy does your food travel? Homegrown food = zero food miles, zero transport emissions."}},{"k":"homegrown","v":{"s":" Homegrown"}},{"k":"grocery_store","v":{"s":" Grocery Store"}},{"k":"imported","v":{"s":" Imported"}},{"k":"a_4_8_garden_bed_can_save_500_lbs_co_y","v":{"s":"🌍 A 4×8 garden bed can save ~500 lbs CO₂/year compared to buying the same food imported!"}},{"k":"water_footprint_of_food","v":{"s":"💧 Water Footprint of Food"}},{"k":"virtual_water_the_total_water_needed_t","v":{"s":"Virtual water: the total water needed to produce food, from field to fork. Companion planting's living mulch cuts water use 40-60%!"}},{"k":"notice_plant_based_proteins_beans_43_g","v":{"s":"💡 Notice: plant-based proteins (beans: 43 gal/lb) use 43x LESS water than beef (1,847 gal/lb). The Three Sisters provide complete protein at a fraction of the water cost!"}},{"k":"12_permaculture_principles","v":{"s":"🌀 12 Permaculture Principles"}},{"k":"regenerative_practices","v":{"s":"🌱 Regenerative Practices"}},{"k":"next_scenario","v":{"s":"Next Scenario"}},{"k":"next_scenario_2","v":{"s":"Next Scenario →"}},{"k":"did_you_know","v":{"s":"Did You Know?"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"next_2","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"quick_reference_cards","v":{"s":"📋 Quick Reference Cards"}},{"k":"the_nitrogen_cycle","v":{"s":"♻️ The Nitrogen Cycle"}},{"k":"this_is_why_beans_are_so_important_the","v":{"s":"This is why beans are so important! They hack the nitrogen cycle through bacterial symbiosis."}},{"k":"this_is_what_bean_roots_do_free_fertil","v":{"s":"🌱 THIS is what bean roots do! Free fertilizer from thin air!"}},{"k":"composting_guide","v":{"s":"♻️ Composting Guide"}},{"k":"greens_nitrogen","v":{"s":"GREENS (Nitrogen)"}},{"k":"the_golden_ratio_3_parts_brown_carbon_","v":{"s":"The golden ratio: 3 parts Brown (carbon) to 1 part Green (nitrogen). Keep moist like a wrung-out sponge!"}},{"k":"greens_nitrogen_2","v":{"s":"🌿 GREENS (Nitrogen)"}},{"k":"browns_carbon","v":{"s":"🍂 BROWNS (Carbon)"}},{"k":"seasonal_planting_calendar","v":{"s":"📅 Seasonal Planting Calendar"}},{"k":"soil_ph_plant_preferences","v":{"s":"⚖️ Soil pH & Plant Preferences"}},{"k":"ph_measures_soil_acidity_1_14_most_veg","v":{"s":"pH measures soil acidity (1-14). Most veggies prefer 6.0-7.0. Test your soil!"}},{"k":"acidic","v":{"s":"← Acidic"}},{"k":"neutral","v":{"s":"Neutral"}},{"k":"alkaline","v":{"s":"Alkaline →"}},{"k":"plant_families_crop_rotation","v":{"s":"🌾 Plant Families & Crop Rotation"}},{"k":"never_plant_the_same_family_in_the_sam","v":{"s":"Never plant the same family in the same spot two years in a row! Rotate to prevent disease buildup and nutrient depletion."}},{"k":"soil_types_guide","v":{"s":"🪨 Soil Types Guide"}},{"k":"garden_pests_allies","v":{"s":"🐞 Garden Pests & Allies"}},{"k":"pests_2","v":{"s":"Pests ("}},{"k":"allies","v":{"s":"Allies ("}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"companion_planting_lab_2","v":{"s":"Companion Planting Lab"}},{"k":"snapshot_2","v":{"s":"📸 Snapshot"}},{"k":"three_sisters_thrive","v":{"s":"🌽🫘🎃 Three Sisters thrive"}},{"k":"balanced_trio_beans_fix_nitrogen_corn_","v":{"s":"Balanced trio. Beans fix nitrogen, corn provides poles, squash shades soil."}},{"k":"overcrowded","v":{"s":"⚠️ Overcrowded"}},{"k":"competition_for_light_water_nutrients","v":{"s":"Competition for light, water, nutrients."}},{"k":"no_nitrogen_fixation","v":{"s":"🟠 No nitrogen fixation"}},{"k":"too_few_beans_soil_nitrogen_depletes","v":{"s":"Too few beans — soil nitrogen depletes."}},{"k":"unbalanced_monoculture_risk","v":{"s":"🟡 Unbalanced — monoculture risk"}},{"k":"one_species_dominates_synergy_reduced","v":{"s":"One species dominates. Synergy reduced."}},{"k":"three_sisters_synergy_discovery","v":{"s":"🌽 Three-sisters synergy discovery"}},{"k":"sliders_for_corn_bean_squash_density_d","v":{"s":"Sliders for corn, bean, squash density. Discrete 4-state synergy. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_what_ratio_makes_the_three_","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: What ratio makes the Three Sisters mutually beneficial?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"haudenosaunee_traditional_ratios_rough","v":{"s":"Haudenosaunee traditional ratios: roughly equal. Investigate why."}},{"k":"beans_fix_nitrogen_via_rhizobium_what_","v":{"s":"Beans fix nitrogen via Rhizobium. What happens at near-zero beans?"}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain 