y copied — paste it into your notes"}},{"k":"copy_glossary","v":{"s":"📋 Copy"}},{"k":"close_glossary","v":{"s":"Close glossary"}},{"k":"quiz_failed_offline_hint","v":{"s":"Quiz generation failed — try the Challenge scenarios in the reference shelf (they work offline)"}},{"k":"scenario_complete","v":{"s":"🏁 Full deck answered! "}},{"k":"scenario_complete_2","v":{"s":" correct, best streak "}},{"k":"scenario_complete_3","v":{"s":". Scenarios repeat — can you beat your streak?"}},{"k":"demand_flatter","v":{"s":" — flatter, more ELASTIC: buyers respond sharply to price (luxuries, goods with substitutes)."}},{"k":"demand_steeper","v":{"s":" — steeper, more INELASTIC: buyers barely change quantity when price moves (necessities like gasoline or medicine)."}},{"k":"supply_flatter","v":{"s":" — flatter, more ELASTIC: producers can easily ramp output up or down (manufactured goods)."}},{"k":"supply_steeper","v":{"s":" — steeper, more INELASTIC: output is hard to change quickly (housing, farmland, concert seats)."}},{"k":"tax_incidence_1","v":{"s":" — a \"tax wedge\" opens between what buyers pay and sellers keep, and some mutually beneficial trades stop happening (DEADWEIGHT LOSS). Right now buyers bear ~"}},{"k":"tax_incidence_2","v":{"s":"% of it and sellers ~"}},{"k":"tax_incidence_3","v":{"s":"% — the more INELASTIC (steeper) side always bears more. Tilt the elasticity sliders and watch the split move."}},{"k":"elastic_flat","v":{"s":"elastic (flat)"}},{"k":"inelastic_steep","v":{"s":"inelastic (steep)"}},{"k":"moderate","v":{"s":"moderate"}},{"k":"demand_elasticity","v":{"s":"Demand elasticity — slope "}},{"k":"demand_elasticity_2","v":{"s":"Demand curve slope (elasticity)"}},{"k":"supply_elasticity","v":{"s":"Supply elasticity — slope "}},{"k":"supply_elasticity_2","v":{"s":"Supply curve slope (elasticity)"}},{"k":"slope_note","v":{"s":"📚 Flat = elastic: people react strongly to price (luxuries, substitutes). Steep = inelastic: they can't easily change behavior (gasoline, medicine). Watch who bears a tax as you tilt the curves."}},{"k":"reset_graph","v":{"s":"♻ Reset Graph"}},{"k":"cash_flow_warning","v":{"s":"⚠️ Your cash is negative — you are spending more than you earn. Try cheaper housing, a lower investment %, or paying down debt before it compounds."}},{"k":"emergency_fund","v":{"s":"🛟 Emergency fund: "}},{"k":"months_of_expenses","v":{"s":"months of expenses in cash"}},{"k":"emergency_fund_target","v":{"s":"advisors suggest keeping 3–6 months"}},{"k":"flow_housing","v":{"s":"Housing"}},{"k":"flow_invested","v":{"s":"Moved into investments (still yours!)"}},{"k":"flow_growth","v":{"s":"Portfolio growth (inside investments)"}},{"k":"flow_debt_interest","v":{"s":"Debt interest added (10% APR)"}},{"k":"last_year_flow","v":{"s":"🧾 Last year's money flow"}},{"k":"flow_net","v":{"s":"Net cash change"}},{"k":"flow_footnote","v":{"s":"* not part of cash — growth compounds inside your portfolio; interest compounds inside your debt."}},{"k":"index_hold","v":{"s":"Index (hold)"}},{"k":"index_lesson","v":{"s":"📚 The buy-and-hold index is beating your trading. Most active traders underperform simply holding everything — this is why index funds are the default advice."}},{"k":"concentration_warning","v":{"s":"⚠️ Over 70% of your stock value is in one company. Diversification cushions single-company shocks — spread your bets."}},{"k":"biz_cash_warning","v":{"s":"⚠️ Your business is losing money — cash is negative. Check your unit economics: does price cover unit cost AND your share of fixed costs? Raising price, cutting costs, or building reputation are your levers."}},{"k":"below_cost_warning","v":{"s":"⚠️ Price is below unit cost — you lose money on EVERY sale."}},{"k":"high_price_hint","v":{"s":"📚 Price is far above suggested — expect demand to fall (price elasticity)."}},{"k":"goal_cool_inflation","v":{"s":"❄️ Cool inflation"}},{"k":"goal_boost_growth","v":{"s":"📈 Boost growth"}},{"k":"goal_cut_unemployment","v":{"s":"💼 Cut unemployment"}},{"k":"shock_oil","v":{"s":"Oil price shock"}},{"k":"shock_oil_lesson","v":{"s":"Supply shocks raise prices AND cut output at the same time — stagflation pressure, like the 1973 OPEC embargo."}},{"k":"shock_tech","v":{"s":"Tech productivity boom"}},{"k":"shock_tech_lesson","v":{"s":"Productivity growth is the rare free lunch: more output per worker lifts GDP without stoking inflation."}},{"k":"shock_financial","v":{"s":"Financial crisis"}},{"k":"shock_financial_lesson","v":{"s":"Credit crunches destroy demand: GDP, prices, and employment all fall together, like 2008."}},{"k":"shock_trade_war","v":{"s":"Trade war escalation"}},{"k":"shock_trade_war_lesson","v":{"s":"Tariffs raise import prices at home and invite retaliation against your exporters abroad."}},{"k":"shock_consumer_boom","v":{"s":"Consumer confidence surge"}},{"k":"shock_consumer_boom_lesson","v":{"s":"Expectations move economies: when households feel secure they spend, and spending is someone else's income."}},{"k":"report_spend_up","v":{"s":"🏛️ Government spending "}},{"k":"report_spend_up_2","v":{"s":"% of GDP added demand (Keynesian multiplier) — but deficits must eventually be financed."}},{"k":"report_spend_down","v":{"s":"🏛️ Austerity: spending cut "}},{"k":"report_spend_down_2","v":{"s":"% of GDP removed demand from the economy."}},{"k":"report_tax_up","v":{"s":"💸 Tax increase of "}},{"k":"report_tax_up_2","v":{"s":"% left households less to spend, cooling demand."}},{"k":"report_tax_down","v":{"s":"💸 Tax cut of "}},{"k":"report_tax_down_2","v":{"s":"% left households more to spend, boosting demand."}},{"k":"report_rate_high","v":{"s":"🏦 Interest rates above the ~3% neutral rate made borrowing expensive — investment and hiring slowed (monetary brake)."}},{"k":"report_rate_low","v":{"s":"🏦 Interest rates below neutral made borrowing cheap — credit-fueled demand rose (monetary gas pedal)."}},{"k":"report_okun","v":{"s":"📊 Okun's law: growth of "}},{"k":"report_okun_2","v":{"s":"% vs the ~2% trend moved unemployment to "}},{"k":"goal_check_inflation","v":{"s":"Goal check — inflation: "}},{"k":"goal_missed_inflation","v":{"s":"(moved the wrong way — which lever raises the cost of borrowing?)"}},{"k":"goal_check_growth","v":{"s":"Goal check — GDP growth: "}},{"k":"goal_missed_growth","v":{"s":"(what adds demand — spending, tax cuts, or cheaper credit?)"}},{"k":"goal_check_unemployment","v":{"s":"Goal check — unemployment: "}},{"k":"goal_missed_unemployment","v":{"s":"(Okun's law: unemployment falls when growth beats the ~2% trend)"}},{"k":"policy_goal_label","v":{"s":"🎯 Policy goal (pick one, predict, then advance):"}},{"k":"central_bank","v":{"s":"🏦 Central Bank (Monetary Policy)"}},{"k":"policy_interest_rate","v":{"s":"Policy Interest Rate: "}},{"k":"policy_interest_rate_2","v":{"s":"Policy interest rate, percent"}},{"k":"real_rate_note","v":{"s":"📚 Real rate = nominal − inflation = "}},{"k":"real_rate_negative","v":{"s":"Negative real rates mean borrowers win and savers lose — very stimulative."}},{"k":"real_rate_positive","v":{"s":"Positive real rates reward saving and slow borrowing — restrictive."}},{"k":"congress_fiscal","v":{"s":"🏛️ Congress (Fiscal Policy)"}},{"k":"spending_change","v":{"s":"Spending change: "}},{"k":"pct_of_gdp","v":{"s":"% of GDP"}},{"k":"spending_change_2","v":{"s":"Government spending change, percent of GDP"}},{"k":"tax_change","v":{"s":"Tax change: "}},{"k":"tax_change_2","v":{"s":"Tax change, percent"}},{"k":"deficit_note","v":{"s":"📚 Spending more while taxing less = deficit spending. It stimulates now, but the debt is a claim on future taxpayers."}},{"k":"advance_year","v":{"s":"📅 Advance One Year ("}},{"k":"year_report","v":{"s":"📋 Year "}},{"k":"year_report_2","v":{"s":" in Review"}},{"k":"shock_label","v":{"s":"Shock: "}},{"k":"economy_reset","v":{"s":"♻ Economy reset to 2025 baseline"}},{"k":"reset_economy","v":{"s":"♻ Reset Economy"}},{"k":"macro_model_disclaimer","v":{"s":"Toy model with textbook-Keynesian signs plus Okun's-law unemployment — NOT a forecast. Real economies depend on expectations, credibility, and global conditions, and economists genuinely disagree about these coefficients (see Schools of Thought in the reference shelf)."}},{"k":"know_thyself","v":{"s":"Know Thyself"}},{"k":"completed_investor_profile","v":{"s":"Completed the investor profile quiz"}},{"k":"stress_tested","v":{"s":"Stress-Tested"}},{"k":"ran_retirement_sim","v":{"s":"Ran a retirement simulation"}},{"k":"asset_allocation","v":{"s":"Asset Allocation"}},{"k":"rebalancing","v":{"s":"Rebalancing"}},{"k":"expense_ratio","v":{"s":"Expense Ratio"}},{"k":"sequence_risk","v":{"s":"Sequence-of-Returns Risk"}},{"k":"advisor_question","v":{"s":"Ask an economics question"}},{"k":"market_theme","v":{"s":"Market theme"}},{"k":"iv_profile_conservative","v":{"s":"Conservative"}},{"k":"iv_profile_balanced","v":{"s":"Balanced"}},{"k":"iv_profile_aggressive","v":{"s":"Aggressive"}},{"k":"iv_q_drop","v":{"s":"The market drops 30% in one year. What do you do?"}},{"k":"iv_q_drop_a","v":{"s":"Sell everything before it gets worse"}},{"k":"iv_q_drop_b","v":{"s":"Hold on and wait it out"}},{"k":"iv_q_drop_c","v":{"s":"Buy more while prices are low"}},{"k":"iv_q_when","v":{"s":"When will you actually need this money?"}},{"k":"iv_q_when_a","v":{"s":"In under 5 years"}},{"k":"iv_q_when_b","v":{"s":"In 5–15 years"}},{"k":"iv_q_when_c","v":{"s":"In 15+ years"}},{"k":"iv_q_pref","v":{"s":"Which portfolio sounds better to you?"}},{"k":"iv_q_pref_a","v":{"s":"Small steady gains, few surprises"}},{"k":"iv_q_pref_b","v":{"s":"A balance of growth and stability"}},{"k":"iv_q_pref_c","v":{"s":"Big swings if it means bigger growth"}},{"k":"iv_pa_intro","v":{"s":"Real investors don’t just pick stocks — they decide how to split money across asset types. Answer 3 questions to find your risk profile, then build your mix."}},{"k":"iv_profile_unlocked","v":{"s":"Investor profile unlocked!"}},{"k":"iv_pa_suggested","v":{"s":"Suggested mix"}},{"k":"iv_stocks","v":{"s":"stocks"}},{"k":"iv_bonds","v":{"s":"bonds"}},{"k":"iv_cash","v":{"s":"cash"}},{"k":"iv_pa_apply","v":{"s":"Apply"}},{"k":"iv_stocks_pct","v":{"s":"Stocks"}},{"k":"iv_stocks_pct_aria","v":{"s":"Percent in stocks"}},{"k":"iv_bonds_pct","v":{"s":"Bonds"}},{"k":"iv_bonds_pct_aria","v":{"s":"Percent in bonds"}},{"k":"iv_alloc_bar","v":{"s":"Allocation bar"}},{"k":"iv_expected_return","v":{"s":"Expected return"}},{"k":"iv_typical_year","v":{"s":"Typical year"}},{"k":"iv_bad_year","v":{"s":"Bad year, on $10K"}},{"k":"iv_pa_mismatch","v":{"s":"🧭 Your mix is quite far from your quiz profile. That’s allowed — but know why: more stocks = more growth and bigger drops; fewer = calmer ride, slower growth."}},{"k":"iv_rv_steady","v":{"s":"Steady Eddie"}},{"k":"iv_rv_wild","v":{"s":"Wild Ride"}},{"k":"iv_rv_mix","v":{"s":"50/50 mix, rebalanced"}},{"k":"iv_rv_intro","v":{"s":"All three investments below average exactly +7% per year. Watch what the ride does to the destination."}},{"k":"iv_rv_chart_aria","v":{"s":"Line chart of $10,000 over 10 years: steady +7% ends near $19,700; a wild ride averaging +7% ends near $14,900; a rebalanced 50/50 mix ends near $18,400."}},{"k":"iv_rv_year0","v":{"s":"Year 0"}},{"k":"iv_rv_ends","v":{"s":"ends"}},{"k":"iv_rv_worst_drop","v":{"s":"worst drop"}},{"k":"iv_rv_lesson","v":{"s":"📚 Same average, different endings: big losses hurt more than equal-sized gains help (volatility drag). Splitting money between the two and rebalancing every year recovers most of the gap — that’s what diversification buys, and why \"risk\" means more than \"some red days.\""}},{"k":"iv_mc_intro_retire","v":{"s":"200 simulated retirements: a $500,000 nest egg in your mix"}},{"k":"iv_mc_intro_retire2","v":{"s":"spending"}},{"k":"iv_mc_intro_retire3","v":{"s":"/yr. Will it last?"}},{"k":"iv_mc_intro","v":{"s":"The Compound Interest calculator draws ONE smooth line. Real markets deliver a range. Here are 200 simulated futures for $10,000 in your mix"}},{"k":"iv_mc_intro_contrib","v":{"s":"/yr added"}},{"k":"iv_mc_mode_aria","v":{"s":"Simulation mode"}},{"k":"iv_mc_mode_grow","v":{"s":"🌱 Growing"}},{"k":"iv_mc_mode_retire","v":{"s":"🏖️ Retiring"}},{"k":"iv_mc_years","v":{"s":"Years"}},{"k":"iv_mc_years_aria","v":{"s":"Simulation years"}},{"k":"iv_mc_spend","v":{"s":"Spending/yr"}},{"k":"iv_mc_spend_aria","v":{"s":"Yearly spending in retirement"}},{"k":"iv_mc_contrib","v":{"s":"Added/yr"}},{"k":"iv_mc_contrib_aria","v":{"s":"Yearly contribution"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee","v":{"s":"Fund fee"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee_aria","v":{"s":"Yearly fund fee percent"}},{"k":"iv_mc_rerolled","v":{"s":"New simulation run generated."}},{"k":"iv_mc_reroll","v":{"s":"🎲 Re-roll 200 futures"}},{"k":"iv_mc_chart_aria","v":{"s":"Fan chart of simulated outcomes."}},{"k":"iv_mc_chart_aria2","v":{"s":"After"}},{"k":"iv_mc_chart_aria3","v":{"s":"years, the middle 80% of runs end between"}},{"k":"iv_mc_chart_aria4","v":{"s":"and"}},{"k":"iv_mc_chart_aria5","v":{"s":"Median"}},{"k":"iv_mc_band","v":{"s":"middle 80% of runs"}},{"k":"iv_mc_median","v":{"s":"median run"}},{"k":"iv_mc_straight","v":{"s":"straight-line calc"}},{"k":"iv_mc_unlucky","v":{"s":"Unlucky (10th pct)"}},{"k":"iv_mc_median_end","v":{"s":"Median"}},{"k":"iv_mc_lucky","v":{"s":"Lucky (90th pct)"}},{"k":"iv_mc_alive1","v":{"s":"of 200 simulated retirements still had money after"}},{"k":"iv_mc_alive2","v":{"s":"years"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee_cost1","v":{"s":"Fees compound too: at"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee_cost2","v":{"s":"the straight-line path ends near"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee_cost3","v":{"s":"at 0.05% (a cheap index fund) it ends near"}},{"k":"iv_mc_fee_cost4","v":{"s":"a gap of"}},{"k":"iv_mc_lesson_retire","v":{"s":"📚 Sequence-of-returns risk, live: every run here has the SAME average return — the runs that went broke just met their bad years FIRST, while withdrawals kept draining the pot. This is why retirees hold more bonds, and why the \"4% rule\" is a guideline, not a guarantee."}},{"k":"iv_mc_lesson","v":{"s":"📚 The dashed line is what a constant-rate calculator promises — the median simulated run usually lands below it, because the average is pulled up by a few lucky runs. And two savers with the same average return can end in very different places: the ORDER of good and bad years matters (sequence-of-returns risk), especially near retirement."}},{"k":"iv_deep_dives","v":{"s":"🎓 Investing Deep-Dives"}},{"k":"iv_deep_dives_sub","v":{"s":"Beyond picking stocks: build a whole portfolio, see what risk really means, and explore the range of long-run outcomes."}},{"k":"iv_pa_title","v":{"s":"🧩 Portfolio Builder — risk profile & asset mix"}},{"k":"iv_rv_title","v":{"s":"🎢 Risk Visualizer — same average, different ride"}},{"k":"iv_mc_title","v":{"s":"🎲 Range of Outcomes — 200 simulated futures"}},{"k":"iv_disclaimer","v":{"s":"Illustrative teaching model: long-run US-style averages (stocks ≈10%/yr, bonds ≈4%, cash ≈1.5%, historical-style volatility), ignoring fees, taxes, and inflation. Not a prediction and not financial advice."}},{"k":"business_idea","v":{"s":"Business idea"}},{"k":"policy_outcomes_chart","v":{"s":"Policy outcomes chart for GDP, inflation, and unemployment"}},{"k":"policy_explanation","v":{"s":"Explain your policy prediction"}},{"k":"iv_drift_title","v":{"s":"🔄 If you never rebalance…"}},{"k":"iv_drift_now","v":{"s":"Now"}},{"k":"iv_drift_y10","v":{"s":"Year 10"}},{"k":"iv_drift_y20","v":{"s":"Year 20"}},{"k":"iv_drift_lesson1","v":{"s":"Stocks outgrow the rest, so your mix quietly drifts stock-heavy: a typical bad year worsens from"}},{"k":"iv_drift_lesson2","v":{"s":"today to"}},{"k":"iv_drift_lesson3","v":{"s":"at year 20. Rebalancing — selling a little of what grew, topping up the rest — keeps the risk you actually chose."}},{"k":"iv_rv_custom","v":{"s":"Your ride"}},{"k":"iv_rv_swing","v":{"s":"Build your own ride — yearly swing"}},{"k":"iv_rv_swing_aria","v":{"s":"Yearly swing percent for your custom ride"}},{"k":"iv_rv_swing_result1","v":{"s":"Alternating"}},{"k":"iv_rv_swing_result2","v":{"s":"still averages +7% — but the swings cost"}},{"k":"iv_rv_swing_result3","v":{"s":"over 10 years vs the steady line."}},{"k":"economic_timeline","v":{"s":"Economic timeline"}},{"k":"indicator_list","v":{"s":"Indicator list"}}],"id":518}},{"k":"ecosystem","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_prediction_committed_before_evidence_model_settin","v":{"s":"Prediction committed before evidence. Model settings are locked until this run is complete or the commitment is changed."}},{"k":"sr_prediction_unlocked_before_the_run_choose_and_com","v":{"s":"Prediction unlocked before the run. Choose and commit again when ready."}},{"k":"sr_current_graph_cleared_model_settings_and_predicti","v":{"s":"Current graph cleared. Model settings and prediction choices are ready for the next run."}},{"k":"sr_cer_evidence_removed","v":{"s":"CER evidence removed."}},{"k":"sr_complete_claim_evidence_and_reasoning_transferred","v":{"s":"Complete claim, evidence, and reasoning transferred to the experiment notebook."}},{"k":"sr_predict_observe_explain_workflow_complete","v":{"s":"Predict, observe, explain workflow complete."}},{"k":"sr_run_unavailable_choose_and_commit_a_prediction_fi","v":{"s":"Run unavailable. Choose and commit a prediction first."}},{"k":"sr_sandbox_reset_complete","v":{"s":"Sandbox reset complete."}},{"k":"sr_sandbox_reset_queued","v":{"s":"Sandbox reset queued."}},{"k":"sr_across_ecosystems_comparison_revealed","v":{"s":"Across-ecosystems comparison revealed."}},{"k":"sr_ai_conservation_reading_complete","v":{"s":"AI Conservation Reading complete."}},{"k":"a11y_display_profile","v":{"s":"Display profile"}},{"k":"a11y_choose_an_ecosystem_study_scenario","v":{"s":"Choose an ecosystem study scenario"}},{"k":"a11y_scenario_comparison_chart_legend","v":{"s":"Scenario comparison chart legend"}},{"k":"a11y_predator_level_relative_to_initial_prey_scale","v":{"s":"Predator level relative to initial prey scale"}},{"k":"a11y_model_parameter_range","v":{"s":"Model parameter range"}},{"k":"a11y_predict_observe_explain_workflow","v":{"s":"Predict, observe, explain workflow"}},{"k":"a11y_ecosystem_analysis_views","v":{"s":"Ecosystem analysis views"}},{"k":"a11y_jump_to_a_key_modeled_moment","v":{"s":"Jump to a key modeled moment"}},{"k":"a11y_cer_progress","v":{"s":"CER progress"}},{"k":"a11y_collected_cer_evidence","v":{"s":"Collected CER evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_rotate_3d_trajectory_view","v":{"s":"Rotate 3D trajectory view"}},{"k":"a11y_change_3d_trajectory_elevation","v":{"s":"Change 3D trajectory elevation"}},{"k":"a11y_move_through_the_3d_trajectory_over_modeled_tim","v":{"s":"Move through the 3D trajectory over modeled time"}},{"k":"a11y_outcome_map_with_initial_prey_increasing_left_t","v":{"s":"Outcome map with initial prey increasing left to right and initial predators increasing bottom to top"}},{"k":"a11y_outcome_map_legend","v":{"s":"Outcome map legend"}},{"k":"prey_birth","v":{"s":"Prey Birth"}},{"k":"pred_death","v":{"s":"Pred Death"}},{"k":"balance60_desc","v":{"s":"Prey:pred ratio 2:1–5:1 for 60 samples"}},{"k":"survive_crash_desc","v":{"s":"Prey drops below 10, then recovers above 25"}},{"k":"coexist_desc","v":{"s":"Both species above 5 for 100 samples"}},{"k":"apex_crash_desc","v":{"s":"Predators hit 0 while prey exceed 40"}},{"k":"quiz_ace_desc","v":{"s":"Correctly answer 3 questions in the quiz"}},{"k":"vocab_study_desc","v":{"s":"Study 3 key terminology definitions"}},{"k":"grayWolf_desc","v":{"s":"Locally extirpated in Maine for over a century. Reintroduction is the most dramatic conservation move possible: documented in Yellowstone to trigger a trophic cascade by reducing elk browse, letting streamside willows and aspen recover. (Whether wolves also reshaped stream channels themselves is still debated among scientists.)"}},{"k":"beaver_desc","v":{"s":"The defining engineer of New England wetlands. Their dams raise water tables, slow flooding, create wet meadows, and create firebreaks. Recovering from near-extinction in the 1800s fur trade."}},{"k":"moose_desc","v":{"s":"Maine's iconic megaherbivore and a key prey species for any returning wolf population. Numbers have declined sharply since 2014 due to winter ticks, an outbreak driven by milder winters under climate change."}},{"k":"deer_desc","v":{"s":"Hyperabundant in much of southern New England since predators were removed. Heavy browse pressure prevents oak and pine regeneration. In Maine the picture is mixed: dense in the south and along the coast, sparse in the deep north woods."}},{"k":"salmon_desc","v":{"s":"Critically endangered in the Gulf of Maine. The Penobscot River is the only Maine river with consistent returning runs. Dam removal has been the key conservation lever; in 2012 and 2013 the Veazie and Great Works dams came down through the Penobscot River Restoration Project."}},{"k":"brookTrout_desc","v":{"s":"Maine state fish. Native to cold, clean streams. The Northeast holds the largest remaining wild brook trout populations in the continental United States. Climate warming and habitat fragmentation are the central threats."}},{"k":"firstSim_desc","v":{"s":"Run your first graph simulation"}},{"k":"presetExplorer_desc","v":{"s":"Try all 4 presets"}},{"k":"balanceKeeper_desc","v":{"s":"Achieve a stable prey:pred ratio between 2:1 and 4:1"}},{"k":"extinctionWitness_desc","v":{"s":"Witness a population crash to below 5"}},{"k":"boomObserver_desc","v":{"s":"See prey population exceed 150"}},{"k":"phasePortrait_desc","v":{"s":"View the phase portrait"}},{"k":"graphToggler_desc","v":{"s":"Switch between Population and Environment graph views"}},{"k":"quizMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Answer 5 quiz questions correctly"}},{"k":"aiScholar_desc","v":{"s":"Use AI tutor 3 times"}},{"k":"nightOwl_desc","v":{"s":"Observe the simulation during nighttime"}},{"k":"eventSurvivor_desc","v":{"s":"Trigger 3 different events"}},{"k":"sandboxBuilder_desc","v":{"s":"Place 10 entities in sandbox mode"}},{"k":"speedDemon_desc","v":{"s":"Run simulation at 3x speed for 30 seconds"}},{"k":"carryCapExplorer_desc","v":{"s":"Change carrying capacity 3 times"}},{"k":"wolfReintroducer_desc","v":{"s":"Successfully reintroduce wolves to Maine in the Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"beaverEngineer_desc","v":{"s":"Bring beaver population above 75 in the Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"salmonChampion_desc","v":{"s":"Bring Atlantic salmon population above 50 in the Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"troutDefender_desc","v":{"s":"Bring brook trout population above 70 in the Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"cascadeMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Trigger a full trophic cascade (wolves up, deer down, habitat recovers) in the Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"directorRank_desc","v":{"s":"Complete a 10-year Conservation Manager campaign on Director difficulty"}},{"k":"apprentice_desc","v":{"s":"32 hours / year, gentler events. Good for first runs."}},{"k":"manager_desc","v":{"s":"24 hours / year, standard events. Default."}},{"k":"director_desc","v":{"s":"18 hours / year, harsher events. Tight constraint."}},{"k":"aria_pop_traj_pre","v":{"s":"Predator and prey population trajectories over "}},{"k":"aria_modeled_time_steps","v":{"s":" modeled time steps."}},{"k":"axis_time_steps","v":{"s":"Time Steps"}},{"k":"axis_population","v":{"s":"Population"}},{"k":"aria_phase_portrait_pre","v":{"s":"Phase portrait of predator abundance versus prey abundance across "}},{"k":"axis_prey_population","v":{"s":"Prey Population"}},{"k":"axis_predator_population","v":{"s":"Predator Population"}},{"k":"cycles_label","v":{"s":"Cycles: "}},{"k":"peak_prey_label","v":{"s":"Peak Prey: "}},{"k":"peak_pred_label","v":{"s":"Peak Pred: "}},{"k":"run_sim_env_data","v":{"s":"Run the canvas simulation to see environment data."}},{"k":"aria_veg_day_pre","v":{"s":"Vegetation-health and day-phase indices over "}},{"k":"aria_live_samples","v":{"s":" live samples."}},{"k":"axis_time","v":{"s":"Time"}},{"k":"axis_level_0_1","v":{"s":"Level (0–1)"}},{"k":"last_10s","v":{"s":"Last ~10s"}},{"k":"prey_colon","v":{"s":"Prey: "}},{"k":"predators_colon","v":{"s":"Predators: "}},{"k":"paused_caps","v":{"s":"PAUSED"}},{"k":"preset_balanced","v":{"s":"Balanced"}},{"k":"preset_extinction","v":{"s":"Extinction"}},{"k":"preset_boom","v":{"s":"Boom"}},{"k":"preset_equilibrium","v":{"s":"Equilibrium"}},{"k":"place_rabbit","v":{"s":"Place Rabbit"}},{"k":"place_fox","v":{"s":"Place Fox"}},{"k":"place_tree","v":{"s":"Place Tree"}},{"k":"erase","v":{"s":"Erase"}},{"k":"move","v":{"s":"Move"}},{"k":"drought","v":{"s":"Drought"}},{"k":"disease","v":{"s":"Disease"}},{"k":"food_boom","v":{"s":"Food Boom"}},{"k":"migration","v":{"s":"Migration"}},{"k":"wildfire","v":{"s":"Wildfire"}},{"k":"explore","v":{"s":"Explore"}},{"k":"route_explore_hint","v":{"s":"Tune predator-prey dynamics."}},{"k":"sandbox","v":{"s":"Sandbox"}},{"k":"route_sandbox_hint","v":{"s":"Build a food web by hand."}},{"k":"conserve","v":{"s":"Conserve"}},{"k":"route_conserve_hint","v":{"s":"Run the Maine campaign."}},{"k":"inquiry","v":{"s":"Inquiry"}},{"k":"route_inquiry_hint","v":{"s":"Sweep variables and observe."}},{"k":"conservation","v":{"s":"Conservation"}},{"k":"quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"badges","v":{"s":"Badges"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"title","v":{"s":"Ecosystem Simulator"}},{"k":"live","v":{"s":"LIVE"}},{"k":"field_station","v":{"s":"Field station"}},{"k":"field_brief_lead","v":{"s":"Pick a route, watch the live population system, and use the graph or campaign when you need evidence."}},{"k":"biome","v":{"s":"Biome"}},{"k":"mode","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"paused","v":{"s":"Paused"}},{"k":"live_status","v":{"s":"Live"}},{"k":"research","v":{"s":"Research"}},{"k":"change_biome","v":{"s":"Change Biome"}},{"k":"aria_explorer_sections","v":{"s":"Ecosystem Explorer sections"}},{"k":"hero_explore_title","v":{"s":"Explore the food web"}},{"k":"hero_explore_hint","v":{"s":"Click any species to see what it eats and what eats it. Trophic-level cascades become obvious — remove a top predator and watch the system reorganize."}},{"k":"hero_sandbox_title","v":{"s":"Sandbox — your ecosystem"}},{"k":"hero_sandbox_hint","v":{"s":"Drop in producers, consumers, and predators; watch population dynamics emerge. Lotka-Volterra cycles appear when you have one predator + one prey + nothing else."}},{"k":"hero_conserve_title","v":{"s":"Conservation Manager — Maine scenario"}},{"k":"hero_conserve_hint","v":{"s":"Explore a Maine-inspired 10-year teaching scenario. Population, habitat, and public-support values are 0-100 indices, and hand-authored cascade rules are not forecasts."}},{"k":"hero_inquiry_title","v":{"s":"Inquiry — predator-prey dynamics"}},{"k":"hero_inquiry_hint","v":{"s":"Adjust predator birth, prey lifespan, and resource scarcity. Watch which discrete regime the system settles into. No score, no reveal, no answer dump — just slider sweep and observation."}},{"k":"hero_quiz_title","v":{"s":"Quiz — foundational concepts"}},{"k":"hero_quiz_hint","v":{"s":"Six questions cover predator-prey lag, model purpose, predation, carrying capacity, primary producers, and trophic levels."}},{"k":"hero_badges_title","v":{"s":"Badges — what you have learned"}},{"k":"hero_badges_hint","v":{"s":"Achievements track which ecological concepts you have demonstrated, not just visited. Trophic-cascade badge requires you to actually trigger one in the sandbox."}},{"k":"goal_label","v":{"s":"Goal: "}},{"k":"explore_goal_body","v":{"s":"compare settings that produce cycles, damping, or collapse in these teaching models. Predator peaks often lag prey peaks, but the amount of lag depends on the parameters. The animated canvas uses separate stochastic rules; the graph below uses a deterministic logistic predator-prey equation."}},{"k":"aria_eco_sim_prefix","v":{"s":"Ecosystem simulation. "}},{"k":"aria_biome_initial_prey","v":{"s":" biome. Initial prey: "}},{"k":"aria_initial_predators","v":{"s":", initial predators: "}},{"k":"paused_dot","v":{"s":"Paused."}},{"k":"running_dot","v":{"s":"Running."}},{"k":"start_suffix","v":{"s":" start"}},{"k":"pred_colon","v":{"s":"Pred: "}},{"k":"watch_evolve","v":{"s":"Watch the ecosystem evolve!"}},{"k":"resume","v":{"s":"Resume"}},{"k":"pause","v":{"s":"Pause"}},{"k":"speed_label","v":{"s":"Speed:"}},{"k":"aria_sim_speed","v":{"s":"Simulation speed"}},{"k":"event_history","v":{"s":"Event History"}},{"k":"secs_ago","v":{"s":"s ago"}},{"k":"mins_ago","v":{"s":"m ago"}},{"k":"total_events_label","v":{"s":"Total events: "}},{"k":"unique_label","v":{"s":"Unique: "}},{"k":"carrying_capacity_k","v":{"s":"Carrying Capacity (K)"}},{"k":"aria_carrying_capacity","v":{"s":"Carrying capacity"}},{"k":"live_pop_graph","v":{"s":"Live Population Graph"}},{"k":"populations","v":{"s":"Populations"}},{"k":"environment","v":{"s":"Environment"}},{"k":"aria_live_pop_hist_pre","v":{"s":"Live predator and prey population history over "}},{"k":"aria_samples","v":{"s":" samples."}},{"k":"canvas_generating_data","v":{"s":"Canvas simulation is generating live data..."}},{"k":"prey","v":{"s":"Prey"}},{"k":"predators","v":{"s":"Predators"}},{"k":"carrying_cap","v":{"s":"Carrying Cap"}},{"k":"vegetation","v":{"s":"Vegetation"}},{"k":"day_night","v":{"s":"Day/Night"}},{"k":"peak_prey","v":{"s":"Peak Prey"}},{"k":"peak_pred","v":{"s":"Peak Pred"}},{"k":"ratio","v":{"s":"Ratio"}},{"k":"samples","v":{"s":"Samples"}},{"k":"food_web","v":{"s":"Food Web"}},{"k":"sun","v":{"s":"Sun"}},{"k":"plants","v":{"s":"Plants"}},{"k":"herbivores","v":{"s":"Herbivores"}},{"k":"decomposers","v":{"s":"Decomposers"}},{"k":"explore_model_desc","v":{"s":"Explore a logistic extension of the Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model. Adjust initial populations and coefficients to compare cycles, density dependence, and extinction in a discrete Euler approximation."}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read aloud"}},{"k":"aria_apply_pre","v":{"s":"Apply "}},{"k":"aria_preset_suffix","v":{"s":" preset"}},{"k":"prey_start","v":{"s":"Prey Start"}},{"k":"aria_prey_start_pop","v":{"s":"Prey start population"}},{"k":"aria_pred_start_pop","v":{"s":"Predator start population"}},{"k":"prey_growth_r","v":{"s":"Prey intrinsic growth (r)"}},{"k":"aria_prey_growth_r","v":{"s":"prey intrinsic growth rate r"}},{"k":"pred_mortality_d","v":{"s":"Predator mortality (d)"}},{"k":"aria_pred_mortality_d","v":{"s":"predator mortality coefficient d"}},{"k":"predation_coeff_a","v":{"s":"Predation coefficient (a)"}},{"k":"aria_predation_coeff_a","v":{"s":"predation coefficient a"}},{"k":"pred_conversion_b","v":{"s":"Predator conversion (b)"}},{"k":"aria_pred_conversion_b","v":{"s":"predator conversion coefficient b"}},{"k":"teaching_model_note","v":{"s":"Teaching model: Euler updates use a 0.1 time step; populations stay continuous internally and are rounded for display. It omits age structure, space, seasons, stochasticity, handling time, disease, and changing K; parameter values are illustrative rather than fitted to rabbits and foxes."}},{"k":"run_graph_sim","v":{"s":"Run Graph Simulation"}},{"k":"logistic_pp_approx","v":{"s":"Logistic Predator-Prey Approximation"}},{"k":"steps_label","v":{"s":"Steps"}},{"k":"phase_portrait","v":{"s":"Phase Portrait"}},{"k":"start","v":{"s":"Start"}},{"k":"end","v":{"s":"End"}},{"k":"challenges_suffix","v":{"s":" challenges"}},{"k":"completed_excl","v":{"s":"Completed!"}},{"k":"take_snapshot","v":{"s":"Take Snapshot"}},{"k":"export_csv","v":{"s":"Export CSV"}},{"k":"ai_ecology_tutor","v":{"s":"AI Ecology Tutor"}},{"k":"q_what_lotka_volterra","v":{"s":"What is Lotka-Volterra?"}},{"k":"q_why_oscillate","v":{"s":"Why do populations oscillate?"}},{"k":"q_what_carrying_capacity","v":{"s":"What is carrying capacity?"}},{"k":"q_explain_food_webs","v":{"s":"Explain food webs"}},{"k":"thinking","v":{"s":"Thinking..."}},{"k":"sandbox_goal_body","v":{"s":"design experiments. Try the textbook Lotka-Volterra setup (1 predator + 1 prey, nothing else) and watch the cycle. Then break it: add a second predator (competitive exclusion), or remove the predator entirely (prey overshoots carrying capacity and crashes). Inject events to test resilience. Every entity placed is graded by Carrying Capacity (K) on the slider below."}},{"k":"aria_sandbox_prefix","v":{"s":"Ecosystem sandbox. Click to place prey (left) or predators (right). "}},{"k":"sandbox_mode","v":{"s":"Sandbox Mode"}},{"k":"click_place_entities","v":{"s":"Click to place entities"}},{"k":"entities_placed","v":{"s":"Entities Placed:"}},{"k":"sync_count","v":{"s":"Sync Count"}},{"k":"aria_sandbox_speed","v":{"s":"Sandbox simulation speed"}},{"k":"inject_events","v":{"s":"Inject Events"}},{"k":"trigger_event","v":{"s":"Trigger Event"}},{"k":"aria_carrying_capacity_lc","v":{"s":"carrying capacity"}},{"k":"sandbox_instructions","v":{"s":"Sandbox Instructions"}},{"k":"select_tool_intro","v":{"s":"Select a tool above, then click on the canvas to interact:"}},{"k":"instr_place","v":{"s":"Place Rabbit/Fox/Tree: Click anywhere to spawn"}},{"k":"instr_erase","v":{"s":"Erase: Click near an entity to remove it"}},{"k":"instr_move","v":{"s":"Move: Click and drag an entity to reposition it"}},{"k":"instr_tip","v":{"s":"Tip: Pause the simulation first for precise placement!"}},{"k":"experiment_ideas","v":{"s":"Experiment Ideas"}},{"k":"exp_idea_1","v":{"s":"Remove all predators and watch what happens to prey and vegetation"}},{"k":"exp_idea_2","v":{"s":"Create a \"wall\" of trees and see if it affects hunting patterns"}},{"k":"exp_idea_3","v":{"s":"Add many foxes at once, then trigger a Food Boom to save the rabbits"}},{"k":"exp_idea_4","v":{"s":"Pause, place prey in one corner and predators in the other, then resume"}},{"k":"exp_idea_5","v":{"s":"Trigger a Wildfire then immediately add trees to observe recovery"}},{"k":"tip_wolf_priority","v":{"s":"Build toward wolf reintroduction AND raise deer quota"}},{"k":"tip_wolf_text_pre","v":{"s":"Deer are hyperabundant ("}},{"k":"tip_wolf_text_post","v":{"s":" index) and wolves are absent. Without predators, deer degrade every habitat through browse pressure. Two parallel moves: raise the deer hunting quota for immediate relief, and start the long arc toward wolf reintroduction (Compensation fund + Public education to lift wolf support above 50, plus Habitat protection to lift wolf habitat above 60)."}},{"k":"tip_salmon_priority","v":{"s":"Stream restoration for salmon and trout"}},{"k":"tip_salmon_text_pre","v":{"s":"Atlantic salmon are critically low ("}},{"k":"tip_salmon_text_post","v":{"s":"). Stream restoration helps salmon, brook trout, and beaver in one move. Dam removal is a bigger lever but costs public support."}},{"k":"tip_hold_priority","v":{"s":"Hold steady and read the land"}},{"k":"tip_hold_text","v":{"s":"No single zone is in crisis. Use Year 1 to monitor and protect habitat; the cascade rules will compound your moves in later years."}},{"k":"ai_no_text_fallback","v":{"s":"The reader returned no text. Try again in a moment."}},{"k":"ai_offline","v":{"s":"The AI reader is offline right now. Try again in a moment."}},{"k":"ai_unavailable","v":{"s":"AI is not available in this context."}},{"k":"ai_reading_status","v":{"s":"AI conservation biologist is reading your ecosystem..."}},{"k":"ai_cons_reading","v":{"s":"AI Conservation Reading"}},{"k":"read_again","v":{"s":"Read again"}},{"k":"re_read","v":{"s":"Re-read"}},{"k":"dismiss_reading","v":{"s":"Dismiss reading"}},{"k":"ai_educator_disclaimer1","v":{"s":"AI conservation biology educator. "}},{"k":"ai_disclaimer2","v":{"s":"It is not a Wabanaki person, not a wildlife professional, and does not speak for any Wabanaki nation, agency, or organization."}},{"k":"ai_disclaimer3","v":{"s":" For authoritative voices on Maine conservation work, consult Penobscot Cultural and Historic Preservation Department, Passamaquoddy Cultural Heritage Museum, Wabanaki Public Health and Wellness, Maine Indian Basketmakers Alliance, Maine Department of Inland Fisheries and Wildlife, and the Atlantic Salmon Federation."}},{"k":"foodweb_cascade_caption","v":{"s":"Food web: cascade rules active in real time"}},{"k":"click_species_deepdive","v":{"s":"Click any species for deep-dive →"}},{"k":"aria_foodweb_diagram","v":{"s":"Food-web diagram of the 6 Maine species"}},{"k":"label_suppresses","v":{"s":"suppresses"}},{"k":"label_overbrowsing","v":{"s":"overbrowsing"}},{"k":"label_engineers_habitat","v":{"s":"engineers habitat"}},{"k":"label_marine_nutrients","v":{"s":"marine nutrients"}},{"k":"aria_open_deepdive_pre","v":{"s":"Open deep-dive for "}},{"k":"pop_prefix","v":{"s":"pop "}},{"k":"species_deepdive","v":{"s":"Species deep-dive"}},{"k":"close","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"ecology","v":{"s":"Ecology"}},{"k":"case_work","v":{"s":"Case work"}},{"k":"modern_context","v":{"s":"Modern context"}},{"k":"what_you_can_do","v":{"s":"What you can do for this species in the sim"}},{"k":"pop_trends_campaign","v":{"s":"Population trends across the campaign"}},{"k":"aria_trend_chart","v":{"s":"Population-index trend chart by species year-by-year"}},{"k":"event_quiet_year_name","v":{"s":"A Quiet Year"}},{"k":"event_quiet_year_desc","v":{"s":"No major event. Routine fieldwork, steady progress."}},{"k":"outcome_mastery_label","v":{"s":"Conservation Mastery"}},{"k":"outcome_mastery_desc","v":{"s":"You held the whole web together. Wolves are back. Beavers are reshaping streams. Salmon are running. Brook trout hold the headwaters. This is what landscape-scale recovery looks like."}},{"k":"outcome_skilled_label","v":{"s":"Skilled Conservation Manager"}},{"k":"outcome_skilled_desc","v":{"s":"You met most of the recovery targets. Some species are thriving, others are still climbing. The trajectory is good."}},{"k":"outcome_mixed_label","v":{"s":"Mixed Results"}},{"k":"outcome_mixed_desc","v":{"s":"A few species made meaningful gains. Others stalled or slipped. Conservation is rarely clean."}},{"k":"outcome_losing_label","v":{"s":"Losing Ground"}},{"k":"outcome_losing_desc","v":{"s":"The ecosystem is slipping. Populations are low, habitat is degraded, public support is fragile. This is how species disappear quietly."}},{"k":"cons_manager_maine","v":{"s":"Conservation Manager: Maine"}},{"k":"steward_intro","v":{"s":"Steward six species across 10 years of fieldwork."}},{"k":"setup_intro_1","v":{"s":"You are the lead manager in a Maine-inspired teaching scenario. Six species. Ten modeled years. The catch: "}},{"k":"setup_intro_connected","v":{"s":"these species are connected."}},{"k":"setup_intro_2","v":{"s":" The scenario links wolves, deer browse, beaver wetlands, migratory fish, and stream habitat through hand-authored rules. Population, habitat, and support values are relative indices, not animal counts; outcomes are not forecasts or management recommendations."}},{"k":"difficulty","v":{"s":"Difficulty"}},{"k":"begin_campaign","v":{"s":"Begin 10-year Conservation Campaign"}},{"k":"aria_deepdive","v":{"s":"Deep-dive"}},{"k":"population_colon","v":{"s":"Population: "}},{"k":"habitat_colon","v":{"s":"Habitat: "}},{"k":"public_support_colon","v":{"s":"Public support: "}},{"k":"targets_met_label","v":{"s":"Targets met: "}},{"k":"what_if_nothing","v":{"s":"What if you had done nothing for 10 years?"}},{"k":"your_campaign","v":{"s":"Your campaign"}},{"k":"avg_pop_prefix","v":{"s":"Avg pop "}},{"k":"targets_met_mid","v":{"s":" · Targets met "}},{"k":"pure_neglect","v":{"s":"Pure neglect"}},{"k":"deer_hit_mid","v":{"s":" · Deer hit "}},{"k":"wolves_mid","v":{"s":" · Wolves "}},{"k":"neglect_outperformed","v":{"s":"Pure neglect outperformed active management this run. Trophic cascade went poorly. Look at which interventions cost you ground."}},{"k":"moved_ahead","v":{"s":"You moved the ecosystem substantially ahead of where neglect would have left it. The gap is the conservation infrastructure you built."}},{"k":"held_the_line","v":{"s":"You roughly held the line against drift. Conservation that matches the do-nothing baseline still counts: stasis is the holding ground for everything you build later."}},{"k":"new_campaign","v":{"s":"New campaign"}},{"k":"replay_same","v":{"s":"Replay same conditions"}},{"k":"campaign_seed","v":{"s":"Campaign seed: "}},{"k":"year_prefix","v":{"s":"Year "}},{"k":"event_mid","v":{"s":" event: "}},{"k":"quiet_fallback","v":{"s":"quiet"}},{"k":"trophic_cascade_year","v":{"s":"Trophic cascade this year"}},{"k":"what_changed_year","v":{"s":"What changed this year"}},{"k":"delta_pop","v":{"s":"Pop"}},{"k":"delta_hab","v":{"s":"Hab"}},{"k":"delta_sup","v":{"s":"Sup"}},{"k":"see_final_outcome","v":{"s":"See final outcome →"}},{"k":"begin_year_pre","v":{"s":"Begin Year "}},{"k":"year1_priority_label","v":{"s":"Year 1 priority: "}},{"k":"dismiss_tip","v":{"s":"Dismiss tip"}},{"k":"year_label","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"field_hours","v":{"s":"Field hours"}},{"k":"manager","v":{"s":"Manager"}},{"k":"aria_ask_ai_read","v":{"s":"Ask AI conservation biologist to read your ecosystem state"}},{"k":"title_ai_reads_state","v":{"s":"AI conservation educator reads your current state"}},{"k":"reading_status","v":{"s":"Reading..."}},{"k":"read_ecosystem_ai","v":{"s":"Read the ecosystem (AI)"}},{"k":"aria_end_year","v":{"s":"End this year"}},{"k":"end_year","v":{"s":"End Year →"}},{"k":"aria_deepdive_for_pre","v":{"s":"Deep-dive for "}},{"k":"goal_prefix","v":{"s":"goal "}},{"k":"actions_suffix","v":{"s":" actions"}},{"k":"no_actions_yet","v":{"s":"No actions yet this year. Pick a species, pick a technique."}},{"k":"iq_boom_label","v":{"s":"Predator-weighted regime"}},{"k":"iq_boom_desc","v":{"s":"In this teaching rule, the predator-birth input outweighs prey-lifespan and scarcity terms."}},{"k":"iq_balanced_label","v":{"s":"Middle weighted regime"}},{"k":"iq_balanced_desc","v":{"s":"Neither arbitrary score crosses the classifier threshold; this does not demonstrate ecological equilibrium."}},{"k":"iq_collapse_label","v":{"s":"Scarcity-weighted regime"}},{"k":"iq_collapse_desc","v":{"s":"In this teaching rule, the scarcity score crosses its classifier threshold."}},{"k":"iq_discovery_title","v":{"s":"Predator-prey discovery"}},{"k":"iq_intro","v":{"s":"Sweep three conceptual sliders and compare an arbitrary weighted classifier. The labels are prompts for inquiry, not Lotka-Volterra solutions, population forecasts, or measured ecological rates."}},{"k":"iq_slider_pred_birth","v":{"s":"Predator birth rate"}},{"k":"iq_slider_prey_life","v":{"s":"Prey lifespan"}},{"k":"iq_slider_res_scarcity","v":{"s":"Resource scarcity"}},{"k":"log_observation","v":{"s":"Log observation"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"observations_logged_suffix","v":{"s":" observations logged"}},{"k":"th_pred_birth","v":{"s":"pred birth %"}},{"k":"th_prey_life","v":{"s":"prey life %"}},{"k":"th_res_scarcity","v":{"s":"resource scarcity %"}},{"k":"th_outcome","v":{"s":"outcome"}},{"k":"hypothesis_label","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (free text — no right answer):"}},{"k":"iq_hypo_placeholder","v":{"s":"Which slider matters MOST for triggering a collapse? Can you balance the system with predator birth at 80%? Type your own theory."}},{"k":"iq_stuck_btn","v":{"s":"I'm stuck — show me questions to think about (no answers)"}},{"k":"open_questions","v":{"s":"Open questions — investigate by manipulating:"}},{"k":"iq_q1","v":{"s":"Start with all three at 50%. What outcome do you get? Now raise predator birth slowly. At what point does the regime flip?"}},{"k":"iq_q2","v":{"s":"Set predator birth high (80%). Can you save the system by changing ONLY prey lifespan? What if you change only resource scarcity?"}},{"k":"iq_q3","v":{"s":"Try to find TWO different settings that both produce a balanced cycle. What do they have in common?"}},{"k":"iq_q4","v":{"s":"Log 4-5 observations of each regime (boom, balanced, collapse). Look for patterns in the table — are there single-variable thresholds, or do they interact?"}},{"k":"iq_q5","v":{"s":"Ecological transfer efficiency varies among systems; about 10% is only a rough teaching heuristic. What additional information would you need to connect energy flow to predator abundance?"}},{"k":"no_answers_revealed","v":{"s":"No answers will be revealed. Investigate."}},{"k":"iq_understand_label","v":{"s":"I think I understand the trade-offs — let me explain them in my own words"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Ecosystem trade-off explanation"}},{"k":"iq_explain_placeholder","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words: how do predator birth, prey lifespan, and resource scarcity interact? Why does balanced cycling require something specific? When do predators win? When does the system collapse?"}},{"k":"iq_saved_note","v":{"s":"Saved. Notice — nobody checked your answer. That is what learner-driven inquiry looks like."}},{"k":"iq_model_limit","v":{"s":"Model limit: two arbitrary weighted scores create three regime labels. This inquiry widget is not a population-dynamics model, does not calculate trophic energy transfer, and should not be interpreted as a forecast."}},{"k":"quiz_covers_title","v":{"s":"What this quiz covers"}},{"k":"quiz_covers_intro","v":{"s":"6 multiple-choice questions on foundational ecology concepts:"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_1","v":{"s":"Predator response after prey availability increases"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_2","v":{"s":"What the Lotka-Volterra family of models represents"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_3","v":{"s":"Predation as an ecological interaction"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_4","v":{"s":"Carrying capacity under specified conditions"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_5","v":{"s":"Primary producers"}},{"k":"quiz_topic_6","v":{"s":"Trophic levels in food chains and webs"}},{"k":"quiz_covers_footer","v":{"s":"Each question links back to behavior you can produce in Explore or Sandbox. If a concept feels abstract, swap to Sandbox and rebuild the scenario."}},{"k":"question_prefix","v":{"s":"Question "}},{"k":"of_mid","v":{"s":" of "}},{"k":"read_question","v":{"s":"Read question"}},{"k":"answer_prefix","v":{"s":"Answer "}},{"k":"correct_excl","v":{"s":"Correct!"}},{"k":"not_quite_excl","v":{"s":"Not quite!"}},{"k":"concept_prefix","v":{"s":"Concept: "}},{"k":"study_term","v":{"s":"Study Term (+5 RP)"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next Question"}},{"k":"quiz_progress","v":{"s":"Quiz Progress"}},{"k":"percent_correct_mid","v":{"s":"% correct ("}},{"k":"answer_to_track","v":{"s":"Answer questions to track your progress"}},{"k":"badges_earned","v":{"s":"Badges Earned: "}},{"k":"earned","v":{"s":"EARNED"}},{"k":"simulate","v":{"s":"Simulate"}},{"k":"step_prefix","v":{"s":"Step "}},{"k":"of_5_suffix","v":{"s":" of 5"}},{"k":"tut_step_1","v":{"s":"Welcome to the Ecosystem Simulator! Watch rabbits and foxes interact in the canvas above."}},{"k":"tut_step_2","v":{"s":"Choose a biome at the top to change the environment. Each has unique colors and ecology."}},{"k":"tut_step_3","v":{"s":"Trigger environmental events to test the ecosystem’s resilience! Try Drought or Wildfire."}},{"k":"tut_step_4","v":{"s":"Complete ecology challenges by maintaining specific population patterns. Earn RP!"}},{"k":"tut_step_5","v":{"s":"Run the graph simulation with Lotka–Volterra equations. Try all 4 presets!"}},{"k":"aria_dismiss_tutorial","v":{"s":"Dismiss Tutorial"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"start_exploring","v":{"s":"Start Exploring!"}},{"k":"canvas_keyboard_instructions","v":{"s":"With a sandbox tool selected, use the arrow keys to move the placement cursor, hold Shift for larger steps, press Enter or Space to apply the tool, and press Escape to hide the cursor."}},{"k":"clear_baseline","v":{"s":"Clear baseline"}},{"k":"comparison_label","v":{"s":"Run comparison: "}},{"k":"evidence_history","v":{"s":"Saved evidence history ("}},{"k":"kb_across","v":{"s":"across"}},{"k":"kb_applied","v":{"s":"Applied at "}},{"k":"kb_down","v":{"s":"down"}},{"k":"kb_dropped","v":{"s":"Dropped."}},{"k":"kb_nothing_here","v":{"s":"Nothing close enough to pick up here."}},{"k":"kb_picked_up","v":{"s":"Picked up. Use the arrow keys to move it, then press Enter to drop it."}},{"k":"notebook_intro","v":{"s":"Predict first, run the model, then explain what the evidence shows. Change one factor at a time when you compare runs."}},{"k":"notebook_title","v":{"s":"Prediction & evidence notebook"}},{"k":"pause_simulation","v":{"s":"Pause simulation"}},{"k":"prediction_label","v":{"s":"My prediction for the prey outcome"}},{"k":"reflection_label","v":{"s":"Reasoning or observation"}},{"k":"reflection_placeholder","v":{"s":"What changed? What evidence supports your explanation?"}},{"k":"resume_simulation","v":{"s":"Resume simulation"}},{"k":"save_evidence","v":{"s":"Save evidence"}},{"k":"set_baseline","v":{"s":"Set current run as baseline"}},{"k":"simulation_speed_suffix","v":{"s":"simulation speed"}}],"id":519}},{"k":"epidemic","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_advanced_the_epidemic_chart_by_one_day_automatic","v":{"s":"Advanced the epidemic chart by one day. Automatic playback is off while reduced motion is enabled."}},{"k":"sr_last_quarantine_zone_removed","v":{"s":"Last quarantine zone removed."}},{"k":"sr_response_strategy_committed_modeled_consequence_r","v":{"s":"Response strategy committed. Modeled consequence revealed. This practice scenario is ungraded."}},{"k":"sr_ai_public_health_reading_complete","v":{"s":"AI Public Health Reading complete."}},{"k":"sr_all_quarantine_zones_cleared","v":{"s":"All quarantine zones cleared."}},{"k":"a11y_inspect_epidemic_day","v":{"s":"Inspect epidemic day"}},{"k":"a11y_playback_speed","v":{"s":"Playback speed"}},{"k":"a11y_record_current_live_sir_setup","v":{"s":"Record current live SIR setup"}},{"k":"a11y_record_current_live_seir_setup","v":{"s":"Record current live SEIR setup"}},{"k":"a11y_record_current_live_vaccination_setup","v":{"s":"Record current live vaccination setup"}},{"k":"a11y_advance_outbreak_map_one_day_and_stay_paused","v":{"s":"Advance outbreak map one day and stay paused"}},{"k":"a11y_view_outbreak_map_day","v":{"s":"View outbreak map day"}},{"k":"a11y_undo_last_quarantine_zone","v":{"s":"Undo last quarantine zone"}},{"k":"a11y_matched_no_additional_response_baseline_compari","v":{"s":"Matched no-additional-response baseline comparison"}},{"k":"a11y_attack","v":{"s":"Attack!"}},{"k":"of_population","v":{"s":"% of population"}},{"k":"healthcare_capacity_20","v":{"s":"🏥 Healthcare capacity (~20%)"}},{"k":"epidemic_modeling_lab","v":{"s":"Epidemic Modeling Lab"}},{"k":"select_intervention_strategy","v":{"s":"Select intervention strategy"}},{"k":"sir_the_foundational_epidemic_model","v":{"s":"SIR — the foundational epidemic model"}},{"k":"three_compartments_susceptible_infecte","v":{"s":"Three compartments: Susceptible → Infected → Recovered. The simplest model that captures real epidemic dynamics. Peaks when S = 1/R₀."}},{"k":"seir_add_the_latent_exposed_phase","v":{"s":"SEIR — add the latent (exposed) phase"}},{"k":"diseases_with_incubation_covid_measles","v":{"s":"Diseases with incubation (COVID, measles, mumps) need an Exposed compartment. Latent period delays the epidemic peak and changes intervention timing."}},{"k":"r_basic_reproduction_number","v":{"s":"R₀ — basic reproduction number"}},{"k":"average_secondary_infections_per_prima","v":{"s":"Average secondary infections per primary case in a fully susceptible population. R₀ > 1 = outbreak; R₀ < 1 = dies out. Measles ~15, flu ~1.3, COVID ~2-3."}},{"k":"vaccination_herd_immunity","v":{"s":"Vaccination + herd immunity"}},{"k":"herd_immunity_threshold_1_1_r_measles_","v":{"s":"Herd-immunity threshold = 1 - 1/R₀. Measles needs ~94% coverage; flu only ~25%. The math is what makes vaccination a public-health superlever."}},{"k":"non_pharmaceutical_interventions","v":{"s":"Non-pharmaceutical interventions"}},{"k":"masks_distancing_school_closures_conta","v":{"s":"Masks, distancing, school closures, contact tracing — each lowers R effective. Stack them: 50% reduction × 50% → 75% total transmission cut."}},{"k":"outbreak_response_26_week_pho_campaign","v":{"s":"Outbreak Response — 26-week PHO campaign"}},{"k":"outbreak_map_spatial_spread","v":{"s":"Outbreak map — spatial spread"}},{"k":"disease_moves_through_networks_househo","v":{"s":"Disease moves through networks (households, schools, transit, workplaces). Travel networks dominate at country scale; close-contact at household scale."}},{"k":"contact_tracing","v":{"s":"Contact tracing"}},{"k":"identifies_and_isolates_contacts_of_co","v":{"s":"Identifies and isolates contacts of confirmed cases before they spread further. Most effective early when case count is low; breaks down when contacts > tracers."}},{"k":"pandemic_history","v":{"s":"Pandemic history"}},{"k":"1918_flu_2003_sars_2009_h1n1_2014_ebol","v":{"s":"1918 flu, 2003 SARS, 2009 H1N1, 2014 Ebola, 2019 COVID. Each shaped modern public health — and surfaced new gaps to fix before the next."}},{"k":"scenario_walk_through","v":{"s":"Scenario walk-through"}},{"k":"step_through_historical_and_hypothetic","v":{"s":"Step through historical and hypothetical outbreaks. Practice intervention sequencing under uncertainty — the actual job of public health."}},{"k":"daily_challenge","v":{"s":"Daily challenge"}},{"k":"a_new_outbreak_puzzle_every_session_pi","v":{"s":"A new outbreak puzzle every session: pick interventions, optimize timing, minimize deaths. Streak counter tracks daily wins."}},{"k":"battle_mode_head_to_head","v":{"s":"Battle mode — head-to-head"}},{"k":"two_players_race_to_contain_identical_","v":{"s":"Two players race to contain identical outbreaks with limited resources. Tests intervention literacy under speed pressure."}},{"k":"reference_glossary","v":{"s":"Reference + glossary"}},{"k":"r_r_effective_attack_rate_case_fatalit","v":{"s":"R₀, R-effective, attack rate, case fatality rate, generation interval — the glossary you keep coming back to as you read epi papers."}},{"k":"inquiry_when_does_an_outbreak_become_o","v":{"s":"Inquiry — when does an outbreak become observable?"}},{"k":"three_sliders_contact_frequency_interv","v":{"s":"Three sliders: contact frequency, intervention adoption, pathogen transmissibility. Discrete regime: contained / emerging / pandemic. No score, no reveal — just sweep and observe."}},{"k":"disease_presets","v":{"s":"Disease Presets"}},{"k":"apply_preset","v":{"s":"Apply Preset"}},{"k":"find_vaccination_and_behavior_settings","v":{"s":"find vaccination and behavior settings that keep the peak Infected curve below the hospital threshold, or that drive R-effective below 1 entirely. Pick a Disease Preset to load real R0 values; raise vaccination until the Herd Threshold readout flips to Achieved. The Effective R0 stat below the chart tells you whether the epidemic is still spreading or burning out."}},{"k":"parameters","v":{"s":"Parameters"}},{"k":"run_simulation","v":{"s":"Run Simulation"}},{"k":"run_simulation_2","v":{"s":"▶ Run Simulation"}},{"k":"sir_curves","v":{"s":"SIR Curves"}},{"k":"peak_infected","v":{"s":"Peak Infected"}},{"k":"total_infected","v":{"s":"Total Infected"}},{"k":"effective_r","v":{"s":"Effective R₀"}},{"k":"herd_threshold","v":{"s":"Herd Threshold"}},{"k":"particle_simulation","v":{"s":"Particle Simulation"}},{"k":"epidemic_visualization","v":{"s":"Epidemic visualization"}},{"k":"sir_equations","v":{"s":"SIR Equations"}},{"k":"ds_dt_s_i","v":{"s":"dS/dt = -β × S × I"}},{"k":"di_dt_s_i_i","v":{"s":"dI/dt = β × S × I - γ × I"}},{"k":"dr_dt_i","v":{"s":"dR/dt = γ × I"}},{"k":"seir_parameters","v":{"s":"SEIR Parameters"}},{"k":"run_seir_simulation","v":{"s":"Run SEIR Simulation"}},{"k":"run_seir_simulation_2","v":{"s":"▶ Run SEIR Simulation"}},{"k":"seir_curves","v":{"s":"SEIR Curves"}},{"k":"peak_infected_2","v":{"s":"Peak Infected"}},{"k":"total_infected_2","v":{"s":"Total Infected"}},{"k":"latent_period","v":{"s":"Latent Period"}},{"k":"herd_threshold_2","v":{"s":"Herd Threshold"}},{"k":"seir_equations","v":{"s":"SEIR Equations"}},{"k":"ds_dt_s_i_2","v":{"s":"dS/dt = -β × S × I"}},{"k":"de_dt_s_i_e","v":{"s":"dE/dt = β × S × I - σ × E"}},{"k":"di_dt_e_i","v":{"s":"dI/dt = σ × E - γ × I"}},{"k":"dr_dt_i_2","v":{"s":"dR/dt = γ × I"}},{"k":"r_explorer","v":{"s":"🌡️ R₀ Explorer"}},{"k":"add_to_comparison","v":{"s":"+ Add to Comparison"}},{"k":"clear","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"clear_2","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"r_danger_scale","v":{"s":"R₀ Danger Scale"}},{"k":"dies_out_1","v":{"s":"Dies out (<1)"}},{"k":"low_1_2","v":{"s":"Low (1-2)"}},{"k":"moderate_2_4","v":{"s":"Moderate (2-4)"}},{"k":"high_4_8","v":{"s":"High (4-8)"}},{"k":"extreme_8","v":{"s":"Extreme (>8)"}},{"k":"comparison_table","v":{"s":"Comparison Table"}},{"k":"extreme_8_2","v":{"s":"Extreme (>8)"}},{"k":"vaccination_strategy","v":{"s":"💉 Vaccination Strategy"}},{"k":"simulate_vaccination","v":{"s":"Simulate Vaccination"}},{"k":"simulate_vaccination_2","v":{"s":"💉 Simulate Vaccination"}},{"k":"herd_immunity_status","v":{"s":"Herd Immunity Status"}},{"k":"0_vaccinated","v":{"s":"0% Vaccinated"}},{"k":"without_vaccination","v":{"s":"Without Vaccination"}},{"k":"peak_infected_3","v":{"s":"Peak Infected"}},{"k":"peak_infected_4","v":{"s":"Peak Infected"}},{"k":"non_pharmaceutical_interventions_2","v":{"s":"😷 Non-Pharmaceutical Interventions"}},{"k":"select_interventions","v":{"s":"Select Interventions"}},{"k":"subtract","v":{"s":"Subtract"}},{"k":"simulate_with_npis","v":{"s":"Simulate with NPIs"}},{"k":"simulate_with_npis_2","v":{"s":"😷 Simulate with NPIs"}},{"k":"reduction","v":{"s":"β Reduction"}},{"k":"status","v":{"s":"Status"}},{"k":"without_interventions","v":{"s":"Without Interventions"}},{"k":"hospital_capacity","v":{"s":"🏥 Hospital Capacity"}},{"k":"initial_conditions_look_stable_use_wee","v":{"s":"Initial conditions look stable. Use Week 1 to read the system: which group infections climb fastest under your specific contact rates and seed events. Active intervention before you have signal can burn trust unnecessarily."}},{"k":"demographic_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Demographic deep-dive"}},{"k":"close","v":{"s":"✕ Close"}},{"k":"epidemiology","v":{"s":"📊 Epidemiology"}},{"k":"case_work","v":{"s":"📰 Case work"}},{"k":"maine_context","v":{"s":"🌍 Maine context"}},{"k":"infection_by_demographic_across_all_we","v":{"s":"📈 Infection % by demographic across all weeks"}},{"k":"week_by_week_infection_trend_chart_by_","v":{"s":"Week-by-week infection trend chart by demographic group"}},{"k":"ai_public_health_educator_is_reading_y","v":{"s":"⏳ AI public health educator is reading your county data..."}},{"k":"ai_public_health_reading","v":{"s":"AI Public Health Reading"}},{"k":"ai_public_health_reading_2","v":{"s":"AI Public Health Reading"}},{"k":"re_read","v":{"s":"↻ Re-read"}},{"k":"ai_public_health_educator","v":{"s":"AI public health educator. "}},{"k":"it_is_not_a_real_public_health_officer","v":{"s":"It is not a real Public Health Officer, not a Wabanaki person, and does not speak for any Wabanaki nation, public health agency, or named individual."}},{"k":"for_authoritative_public_health_voices","v":{"s":" For authoritative public health voices, consult Maine CDC, Wabanaki Public Health and Wellness, the US CDC, and your local public health department directly."}},{"k":"a_quiet_week","v":{"s":"A Quiet Week"}},{"k":"no_major_event_the_system_stayed_withi","v":{"s":"No major event. The system stayed within its normal noise."}},{"k":"pandemic_response_mastery","v":{"s":"Pandemic Response Mastery"}},{"k":"low_deaths_high_trust_zero_hospital_ov","v":{"s":"Low deaths, high trust, zero hospital overload. This is what a well-run public health response looks like, and it required real tradeoffs along the way."}},{"k":"solid_public_health_response","v":{"s":"Solid Public Health Response"}},{"k":"you_kept_the_curve_flattened_most_of_t","v":{"s":"You kept the curve flattened most of the time and maintained public engagement. Real-world responses look like this on the good days."}},{"k":"mixed_outcomes","v":{"s":"Mixed Outcomes"}},{"k":"significant_illness_some_hospital_stra","v":{"s":"Significant illness, some hospital strain, eroded trust. The system held together but at real cost."}},{"k":"system_strain","v":{"s":"System Strain"}},{"k":"high_deaths_or_sustained_hospital_over","v":{"s":"High deaths or sustained hospital overload. This is the trajectory communities and health systems work to avoid."}},{"k":"outbreak_response_26_week_pho_campaign_2","v":{"s":"Outbreak Response: 26-week PHO campaign"}},{"k":"a_novel_respiratory_pathogen_is_circul","v":{"s":"A novel respiratory pathogen is circulating in your county. You have 26 weeks of decisions. Four demographic groups respond differently. Feedback rules tie "}},{"k":"infection_hospital_strain_public_trust","v":{"s":"infection, hospital strain, public trust, and vaccine uptake"}},{"k":"together_this_is_pandemic_response_as_","v":{"s":" together. This is pandemic response as decision-making under uncertainty, not as a formula."}},{"k":"demographic_deep_dive_2","v":{"s":"📚 Demographic deep-dive →"}},{"k":"difficulty","v":{"s":"Difficulty"}},{"k":"begin_26_week_outbreak_response","v":{"s":"🏥 Begin 26-week Outbreak Response"}},{"k":"cumulative_deaths","v":{"s":"Cumulative deaths"}},{"k":"average_trust","v":{"s":"Average trust"}},{"k":"hospital_overload_weeks","v":{"s":"Hospital overload weeks"}},{"k":"what_if_a_pho_had_done_nothing_for_26_","v":{"s":"↔ What if a PHO had done nothing for 26 weeks?"}},{"k":"your_campaign","v":{"s":"Your campaign"}},{"k":"pure_neglect","v":{"s":"Pure neglect"}},{"k":"new_campaign","v":{"s":"↻ New campaign"}},{"k":"replay_same_conditions","v":{"s":"🔁 Replay same conditions"}},{"k":"campaign_seed","v":{"s":"Campaign seed: "}},{"k":"hospital_load","v":{"s":"Hospital load"}},{"k":"overload_weeks_total","v":{"s":"Overload weeks total"}},{"k":"feedback_rules_this_week","v":{"s":"🔄 Feedback rules this week"}},{"k":"what_changed_this_week","v":{"s":"What changed this week"}},{"k":"week_1_priority","v":{"s":"Week 1 priority: "}},{"k":"dismiss_tip","v":{"s":"Dismiss tip"}},{"k":"week","v":{"s":"Week"}},{"k":"hours_left","v":{"s":"Hours left"}},{"k":"avg_trust","v":{"s":"Avg trust"}},{"k":"total_infected_sum_across_groups","v":{"s":"Total infected (sum across groups)"}},{"k":"ask_ai_public_health_educator_to_read_","v":{"s":"Ask AI public health educator to read your county data"}},{"k":"ai_public_health_educator_reads_your_c","v":{"s":"AI public health educator reads your current state"}},{"k":"end_this_week","v":{"s":"End this week"}},{"k":"end_week","v":{"s":"End Week →"}},{"k":"active_interventions_or_mods","v":{"s":"🛠 Active interventions or mods: "}},{"k":"demographic_deep_dive_3","v":{"s":"Demographic deep-dive"}},{"k":"county_wide_interventions","v":{"s":"🛠 County-wide interventions"}},{"k":"no_interventions_yet_this_week_pick_fr","v":{"s":"No interventions yet this week. Pick from the county-wide row or use Targeted vaccination on a demographic."}},{"k":"outbreak_map","v":{"s":"🗺️ Outbreak Map"}},{"k":"scenario","v":{"s":"Scenario"}},{"k":"generate_map","v":{"s":"🗺️ Generate Map"}},{"k":"add_zone","v":{"s":"+ Add Zone"}},{"k":"add_zone_2","v":{"s":"+ Add Zone"}},{"k":"clear_3","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"clear_4","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"quarantine_zones_reduce_transmission_b","v":{"s":"Quarantine zones reduce transmission by 80% and speed recovery by 50% within the zone."}},{"k":"hospital_capacity_2","v":{"s":"🏥 Hospital Capacity"}},{"k":"hospitals_overwhelmed_mortality_increa","v":{"s":"⚠️ HOSPITALS OVERWHELMED — mortality increases!"}},{"k":"outbreak_timeline","v":{"s":"Outbreak Timeline"}},{"k":"contact_tracing_2","v":{"s":"🔗 Contact Tracing"}},{"k":"trace_the_infection_chain","v":{"s":"Trace the infection chain!"}},{"k":"click_on_people_connected_to_known_cas","v":{"s":"Click on people connected to known cases to test if they're infected."}},{"k":"start_tracing","v":{"s":"Start Tracing"}},{"k":"start_tracing_2","v":{"s":"🔗 Start Tracing"}},{"k":"all_found","v":{"s":"✅ All Found!"}},{"k":"new_network","v":{"s":"↺ New Network"}},{"k":"patient_zero","v":{"s":"🔴 Patient Zero"}},{"k":"unknown_clickable_if_connected","v":{"s":"🔵 Unknown (clickable if connected)"}},{"k":"tested_clear","v":{"s":"🟢 Tested Clear"}},{"k":"confirmed_infected","v":{"s":"🔴 Confirmed Infected"}},{"k":"historical_pandemics","v":{"s":"📜 Historical Pandemics"}},{"k":"view_pandemic","v":{"s":"View Pandemic"}},{"k":"deaths","v":{"s":"Deaths"}},{"k":"world_pop","v":{"s":"% World Pop"}},{"k":"r_estimate","v":{"s":"R₀ Estimate"}},{"k":"transmission","v":{"s":"Transmission"}},{"k":"key_facts","v":{"s":"Key Facts:"}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read Aloud"}},{"k":"read_aloud_2","v":{"s":"🔊 Read Aloud"}},{"k":"simulate","v":{"s":"Simulate"}},{"k":"simulate_2","v":{"s":"🔬 Simulate"}},{"k":"outbreak_scenarios","v":{"s":"🎭 Outbreak Scenarios"}},{"k":"ai_outbreak_scenario_generator","v":{"s":"AI Outbreak Scenario Generator"}},{"k":"gemini_will_create_a_unique_fictional_","v":{"s":"Gemini will create a unique fictional outbreak for you to respond to."}},{"k":"generate_scenario","v":{"s":"Generate Scenario"}},{"k":"generate_scenario_2","v":{"s":"🧠 Generate Scenario"}},{"k":"ai_not_available_requires_gemini_integ","v":{"s":"AI not available — requires Gemini integration."}},{"k":"generating_outbreak_scenario","v":{"s":"Generating outbreak scenario..."}},{"k":"origin","v":{"s":"Origin"}},{"k":"transmission_2","v":{"s":"Transmission"}},{"k":"symptoms","v":{"s":"Symptoms"}},{"k":"choose_your_response_strategy","v":{"s":"Choose Your Response Strategy"}},{"k":"choose_scenario","v":{"s":"Choose Scenario"}},{"k":"new_scenario","v":{"s":"New Scenario"}},{"k":"new_scenario_2","v":{"s":"🧠 New Scenario"}},{"k":"simulate_r","v":{"s":"Simulate R="}},{"k":"build_a_streak_of_correct_answers_unde","v":{"s":"build a streak of correct answers under Easy, Medium, or Hard tier. Hint costs nothing but breaks the streak; Skip resets the streak. AI Next pulls a fresh AI-generated question if you have exhausted the built-in pool. Streak is your fluency indicator; points reward harder tiers."}},{"k":"epidemiology_challenge","v":{"s":"🎯 Epidemiology Challenge"}},{"k":"ai_generating_question","v":{"s":"AI generating question..."}},{"k":"ai","v":{"s":"🧠 AI"}},{"k":"type_your_answer","v":{"s":"Type your answer..."}},{"k":"challenge_answer","v":{"s":"Challenge answer"}},{"k":"check","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"hint","v":{"s":"💡 Hint"}},{"k":"skip","v":{"s":"Skip ➡"}},{"k":"ai_next","v":{"s":"AI Next"}},{"k":"ai_next_2","v":{"s":"✨ AI Next"}},{"k":"outbreak_defense","v":{"s":"⚔️ Outbreak Defense"}},{"k":"a_virus_is_threatening_the_population","v":{"s":"A virus is threatening the population!"}},{"k":"start_battle","v":{"s":"⚔️ Start Battle"}},{"k":"ai_battle","v":{"s":"AI Battle"}},{"k":"ai_battle_2","v":{"s":"🧠 AI Battle"}},{"k":"you","v":{"s":"🛡️ You"}},{"k":"virus","v":{"s":"🦠 Virus"}},{"k":"play_again","v":{"s":"↺ Play Again"}},{"k":"ai_rematch","v":{"s":"AI Rematch"}},{"k":"ai_rematch_2","v":{"s":"✨ AI Rematch"}},{"k":"ai_generating_question_2","v":{"s":"AI generating question..."}},{"k":"ai_generated","v":{"s":"🧠 AI-GENERATED"}},{"k":"type_your_answer_2","v":{"s":"Type your answer..."}},{"k":"battle_answer","v":{"s":"Battle answer"}},{"k":"attack","v":{"s":"⚔️ Attack!"}},{"k":"hint_2","v":{"s":"Hint"}},{"k":"hint_3","v":{"s":"💡 Hint"}},{"k":"learn_epidemiology_concepts","v":{"s":"📚 Learn — Epidemiology Concepts"}},{"k":"try_it","v":{"s":"Try It"}},{"k":"try_it_2","v":{"s":"🔬 Try It"}},{"k":"read_aloud_3","v":{"s":"Read Aloud"}},{"k":"read_aloud_4","v":{"s":"🔊 Read Aloud"}},{"k":"contained","v":{"s":"🟢 Contained"}},{"k":"r_eff_1_outbreak_dies_out_before_becom","v":{"s":"R_eff < 1: outbreak dies out before becoming detectable. No pandemic."}},{"k":"emerging","v":{"s":"🟡 Emerging"}},{"k":"r_eff_just_above_1_outbreak_grows_slow","v":{"s":"R_eff just above 1: outbreak grows slowly. Visible to surveillance but not pandemic."}},{"k":"pandemic","v":{"s":"🔴 Pandemic"}},{"k":"r_eff_well_above_1_exponential_growth_","v":{"s":"R_eff well above 1: exponential growth. Pandemic scale within weeks-months."}},{"k":"epidemic_threshold_discovery","v":{"s":"🔬 Epidemic threshold discovery"}},{"k":"three_sliders_contact_frequency_interv_2","v":{"s":"Three sliders: contact frequency, intervention adoption, pathogen transmissibility. Sweep them. The outbreak will settle into one of three discrete regimes (no numeric score, no death-toll). There is no right answer — and no reveal. Log observations. Type what you discover about when an outbreak becomes a pandemic."}},{"k":"time","v":{"s":"time →"}},{"k":"sir_trajectories_susceptible_infected_","v":{"s":"SIR trajectories (Susceptible / Infected / Recovered). Axes unlabeled by design — focus on the shape, not the number."}},{"k":"contact_frequency","v":{"s":"Contact frequency (%)"}},{"k":"intervention_adoption","v":{"s":"Intervention adoption (%)"}},{"k":"transmissibility_r","v":{"s":"Transmissibility (R₀)"}},{"k":"log_observation","v":{"s":"📋 Log observation"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_free_text_no_right_ans","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (free text — no right answer):"}},{"k":"which_slider_is_the_most_efficient_lev","v":{"s":"Which slider is the most efficient lever to flip a pandemic into emerging or contained? Is there a single combined number that predicts the regime? Type your own theory."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_me_questions_to_think_a","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show me questions to think about (no answers)"}},{"k":"open_prompts_investigate_by_manipulati","v":{"s":"Open prompts — investigate by manipulating:"}},{"k":"hold_two_sliders_steady_move_the_third","v":{"s":"Hold two sliders steady. Move the third. Watch what happens. Repeat with each."}},{"k":"log_observations_from_each_of_the_thre","v":{"s":"Log observations from each of the three regimes. What patterns do you notice in the table?"}},{"k":"try_to_find_two_completely_different_s","v":{"s":"Try to find two completely different slider settings that produce the same regime. What do they share?"}},{"k":"notice_where_a_small_slider_change_fli","v":{"s":"Notice where a small slider change flips the regime versus where large changes do nothing. What might explain the difference?"}},{"k":"look_up_what_r_eff_means_in_real_epide","v":{"s":"Look up what R_eff means in real epidemiology. Compare it to what you observe here."}},{"k":"no_answers_no_specific_values_no_direc","v":{"s":"No answers, no specific values, no directions. Investigate."}},{"k":"i_think_i_understand_the_trade_offs_le","v":{"s":"I think I understand the trade-offs — let me explain them in my own words"}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words_what_is_the_","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words: what is the relationship between contact frequency, intervention adoption, transmissibility, and outbreak regime? What single equation could capture it? Why is the threshold at R_eff = 1?"}},{"k":"saved_notice_nobody_checked_your_answe","v":{"s":"✓ Saved. Notice — nobody checked your answer. That is what learner-driven inquiry looks like."}},{"k":"design_note_no_death_toll_counter_no_p","v":{"s":"Design note: no death-toll counter, no peak-infection chart, no quiz validation. Outcome is shown as a discrete 3-regime marker (contained / emerging / pandemic), not a continuous indicator — by design, to discourage optimization-gaming behavior. The point is the inquiry, not the number."}},{"k":"schoolAge_desc","v":{"s":"Highest transmission rate due to dense indoor contact in schools and after-school activities. Lowest severe-case rate per infection, but a major vector to working-age and elderly household members."}},{"k":"workingAge_desc","v":{"s":"Workplace, commute, and household exposure. Moderate severe-case rate, with sharp increase above 50. This is also your healthcare workforce."}},{"k":"elderly_desc","v":{"s":"Lower exposure overall (fewer daily contacts) but dramatically higher severe-case and death rate per infection. Long-term care facilities are extreme-risk environments."}},{"k":"healthcare_desc","v":{"s":"Healthcare workforce is roughly 1.3% of total population but they are also part of the working-age group via family. Burnout reduces hospital capacity. Trust in this group is what keeps boosters and PPE compliance high."}},{"k":"firstSim_desc","v":{"s":"Run your first SIR simulation"}},{"k":"seirMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Run an SEIR model simulation"}},{"k":"r0Explorer_desc","v":{"s":"Compare R₀ for 3+ diseases"}},{"k":"vaccHero_desc","v":{"s":"Achieve herd immunity with vaccination"}},{"k":"outbreakMapper_desc","v":{"s":"Run an outbreak map simulation"}},{"k":"flatCurve_desc","v":{"s":"Reduce peak infection below 20%"}},{"k":"herdImmunity_desc","v":{"s":"Achieve herd immunity threshold"}},{"k":"presetPro_desc","v":{"s":"Try all 6 disease presets"}},{"k":"particlePro_desc","v":{"s":"Run 3 particle simulations"}},{"k":"quizStreak3_desc","v":{"s":"Get 3 correct answers in a row"}},{"k":"quizStreak5_desc","v":{"s":"Get 5 correct answers in a row"}},{"k":"battleWin_desc","v":{"s":"Win an Outbreak Defense battle"}},{"k":"aiBattle_desc","v":{"s":"Win an AI-powered battle"}},{"k":"scholar_desc","v":{"s":"Read all 4 Learn topics"}},{"k":"npiMaster_desc","v":{"s":"Flatten the curve using interventions"}},{"k":"contactTracer_desc","v":{"s":"Successfully trace an infection chain"}},{"k":"historian_desc","v":{"s":"Explore all historical pandemics"}},{"k":"scenarioSolver_desc","v":{"s":"Complete an AI-generated outbreak scenario"}},{"k":"quarantine_desc","v":{"s":"Use quarantine zones on outbreak map"}},{"k":"hospitalMgr_desc","v":{"s":"Keep hospital capacity below 100%"}},{"k":"flattener_desc","v":{"s":"Keep peak infection below 10% in the Outbreak Response campaign"}},{"k":"trustBuilder_desc","v":{"s":"Hold average public trust above 70 across the campaign"}},{"k":"capacityKeeper_desc","v":{"s":"Complete the campaign with zero weeks of hospital overload"}},{"k":"equityPHO_desc","v":{"s":"Bring elderly vaccination above 80% without losing trust"}},{"k":"phoMastery_desc","v":{"s":"Complete the Outbreak Response campaign on State Director difficulty with low total cases"}},{"k":"apprentice_desc","v":{"s":"16 hrs/week, gentler events. For first runs."}},{"k":"pho_desc","v":{"s":"12 hrs/week, standard events. Default."}},{"k":"state_desc","v":{"s":"9 hrs/week, harsher events. Real constraint."}},{"k":"attack_rate","v":{"s":"Attack Rate"}},{"k":"initial_r_effective","v":{"s":"Initial R-effective"}},{"k":"severe_cases_hospitalised","v":{"s":"Severe cases (hospitalised)"}},{"k":"most_severe_cases_survive","v":{"s":"Most severe cases survive — hospital care is why."}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Epidemic regime explanation"}},{"k":"healthcare_capacity","v":{"s":"🏥 Hospital capacity ({pct}% infected at once)"}},{"k":"declining","v":{"s":"🟢 Declining"}},{"k":"evidence_based_growth_explanation","v":{"s":"Evidence-based outbreak growth explanation"}},{"k":"fast_growth","v":{"s":"🔴 Faster growth"}},{"k":"growing","v":{"s":"🟡 Growing"}},{"k":"r_eff_at_least_1_5","v":{"s":"R_eff >= 1.5: infections grow faster in this simplified model. This is not, by itself, a definition of a pandemic."}},{"k":"r_eff_at_or_below_1","v":{"s":"R_eff <= 1: infections do not sustain growth in this simplified model."}},{"k":"r_eff_between_1_and_1_5","v":{"s":"1 < R_eff < 1.5: infections grow in this simplified model. Growth alone does not establish geographic spread."}}],"id":520}},{"k":"evolab","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_capture_this_run_in_the_capstone_evidence_noteb","v":{"s":"Capture this {value1} run in the Capstone evidence notebook"}},{"k":"a11y_set_population_size_to","v":{"s":"Set population size to {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_selection_sandbox_population_creatures_mean_tra","v":{"s":"Selection sandbox population: {value1} creatures, mean trait {value2}, generation {value3}"}},{"k":"a11y_select_preset","v":{"s":"Select preset: {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_assign_clade_for","v":{"s":"Assign clade for {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_review_and_retry_the_missed_questions_only","v":{"s":"Review and retry the {value1} missed questions only"}},{"k":"a11y_two_trait_means_and_a_trait_overlap_proxy_over","v":{"s":"Two trait means and a trait-overlap proxy over generations. The proxy is currently {value1} percent."}},{"k":"a11y_two_populations_separated_by_a_barrier_left_mea","v":{"s":"Two populations separated by a barrier. Left mean {value1}, right mean {value2}. Trait-overlap proxy {value3} percent."}},{"k":"a11y_predator_and_prey_mean_speeds_over_generations","v":{"s":"Predator and prey mean speeds over generations. Currently predator {value1}, prey {value2}."}},{"k":"a11y_predator_prey_simulation_red_predators_chasing","v":{"s":"Predator-prey simulation. {value1} red predators chasing {value2} blue prey. Mean predator speed {value3}, prey {value4}."}},{"k":"a11y_mean_trait_over_rounds_approaching_the_camoufla","v":{"s":"Mean trait over {value1} rounds. Approaching the camouflage ideal trait of {value2}."}},{"k":"a11y_bar_chart_of_time_to_complete_each_round_rounds","v":{"s":"Bar chart of time to complete each round. {value1} rounds completed. Latest time: {value2} seconds."}},{"k":"a11y_hunting_field_with_camouflaged_prey_on_backgrou","v":{"s":"Hunting field with {value1} camouflaged prey on {value2} background. Click a prey, or press Enter or Space to hunt the prey farthest from the camouflage optimum."}},{"k":"a11y_set_environment_to","v":{"s":"Set environment to {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_remove_run_snapshot","v":{"s":"Remove run {value1} snapshot"}},{"k":"a11y_pick_scenario","v":{"s":"Pick scenario: {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_prediction","v":{"s":"Prediction {value1}: {value2}"}},{"k":"a11y_open_to_run_the_simulation","v":{"s":"Open {value1} to run the simulation"}},{"k":"a11y_reflection","v":{"s":"Reflection {value1}: {value2}"}},{"k":"a11y_open_module","v":{"s":"Open {value1} module"}},{"k":"a11y_open_covers","v":{"s":"Open {value1} (covers {value2})"}},{"k":"sr_module_explored","v":{"s":"Module explored: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_captured_simulator_run_for_the_capstone_evidence","v":{"s":"Captured simulator run {value1} for the Capstone evidence notebook."}},{"k":"sr_evolab_path_selected","v":{"s":"EvoLab path selected: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_ran_5_lineages_at_population_size_for_generations","v":{"s":"Ran 5 lineages at population size {value1} for {value2} generations. Fixed at A: {value3}, fixed at a: {value4}."}},{"k":"sr_generation_mean_trait_survivors_before_reproducti","v":{"s":"Generation {value1}. Mean trait {value2}, survivors before reproduction {value3} of {value4}."}},{"k":"sr_cladogram_graded_of_organisms_placed_correctly","v":{"s":"Cladogram graded. {value1} of {value2} organisms placed correctly."}},{"k":"sr_review_mode_missed_questions_to_retry","v":{"s":"Review mode. {value1} missed questions to retry."}},{"k":"sr_trait_divergence_milestone_reached_at_generation","v":{"s":"Trait-divergence milestone reached at generation {value1}. This model does not test reproductive isolation."}},{"k":"sr_generation_trait_overlap_proxy_percent","v":{"s":"Generation {value1}. Trait-overlap proxy {value2} percent."}},{"k":"sr_generation_predator_mean_speed_prey_mean_captures","v":{"s":"Generation {value1}. Predator mean speed {value2}, prey mean {value3}. {value4} captures."}},{"k":"sr_extinction_event_population_dropped_to_climate_ch","v":{"s":"Extinction event! Population dropped to {value1}. Climate changed faster than they could adapt."}},{"k":"sr_you_chose_the_bird_with_showiness_next_generation","v":{"s":"You chose the bird with showiness {value1}. Next generation mean: {value2}."}},{"k":"sr_round_complete_eaten_in_seconds_population_mean_t","v":{"s":"Round {value1} complete. {value2} eaten in {value3} seconds. Population mean trait now {value4}."}},{"k":"sr_evidence_verdict_selected","v":{"s":"Evidence verdict selected: {value1}."}},{"k":"sr_this_trajectory_is_already_captured_reset_the_mod","v":{"s":"This trajectory is already captured. Reset the model or run a fresh lineage batch for another trial."}},{"k":"sr_hardy_weinberg_reset_to_equilibrium","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg reset to equilibrium."}},{"k":"sr_mate_choice_begins_click_the_bird_you_find_most_a","v":{"s":"Mate choice begins. Click the bird you find most attractive each round."}},{"k":"sr_hunt_started_click_prey_to_select_against_them","v":{"s":"Hunt started. Click prey to select against them."}},{"k":"sr_capstone_project_reset","v":{"s":"Capstone Project reset."}},{"k":"sr_captured_simulator_run_removed_from_the_evidence","v":{"s":"Captured simulator run removed from the evidence notebook."}},{"k":"sr_all_captured_simulator_runs_cleared_your_interpre","v":{"s":"All captured simulator runs cleared. Your interpretation notes were kept."}},{"k":"sr_claim_starter_added_to_the_interpretation_noteboo","v":{"s":"Claim starter added to the interpretation notebook."}},{"k":"a11y_capstone_data_mission","v":{"s":"Capstone data mission"}},{"k":"a11y_capture_simulator_evidence_for_the_capstone_pro","v":{"s":"Capture simulator evidence for the Capstone project"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_comparison_coach","v":{"s":"Evidence comparison coach"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_building_progress","v":{"s":"Evidence-building progress"}},{"k":"a11y_clear_all_saved_run_snapshots","v":{"s":"Clear all saved run snapshots"}},{"k":"a11y_captured_simulator_runs_comparison_table","v":{"s":"Captured simulator runs comparison table"}},{"k":"a11y_distinct_planned_trials_captured","v":{"s":"Distinct planned trials captured"}},{"k":"a11y_scenario_data_mission","v":{"s":"Scenario data mission"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_replay","v":{"s":"Evidence replay"}},{"k":"a11y_trial_plan_made_before_testing","v":{"s":"Trial plan made before testing"}},{"k":"back_to_evolab_menu","v":{"s":"Back to EvoLab menu"}},{"k":"menu","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_discussion_questions_sta","v":{"s":"Teacher Notes — discussion questions, standards alignment, and extension activities"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher Notes — click to expand"}},{"k":"print_this_module_page_includes_teache","v":{"s":"Print this module page (includes Teacher Notes)"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"ngss_standards","v":{"s":"NGSS Standards"}},{"k":"discussion_questions","v":{"s":"Discussion Questions"}},{"k":"watch_for_these_misconceptions","v":{"s":"Watch for these misconceptions"}},{"k":"extension_activity","v":{"s":"Extension Activity"}},{"k":"predator_vision","v":{"s":"Predator Vision"}},{"k":"you_are_the_selection_pressure","v":{"s":"YOU are the selection pressure"}},{"k":"hunt_camouflaged_prey_by_clicking_them","v":{"s":"Hunt camouflaged prey by clicking them on a textured background. Each round, the survivors reproduce — and the population evolves to be HARDER for you to spot. By generation 10, you're hunting something almost invisible. The most viscerally interactive way to feel evolution happening."}},{"k":"mate_choice_lab","v":{"s":"Mate Choice Lab"}},{"k":"you_are_the_mate_chooser","v":{"s":"YOU are the mate-chooser"}},{"k":"pick_the_bird_you_find_most_attractive","v":{"s":"Pick the bird you find most attractive. The chosen mate reproduces — others don't. Over rounds, the population's \"showiness\" escalates because YOU keep picking the showiest. Watch peacock-style runaway sexual selection unfold under your own preferences. Toggle a predator cost to see how it stabilizes."}},{"k":"climate_pressure_lab","v":{"s":"Climate Pressure Lab"}},{"k":"you_are_the_climate","v":{"s":"YOU are the climate"}},{"k":"drag_the_temperature_slider_slowly_the","v":{"s":"Drag the temperature slider. Slowly: the population adapts. Fast: they go extinct. The most direct demonstration of why the RATE of climate change matters — not just the magnitude. Real organisms today face this exact challenge."}},{"k":"selection_sandbox","v":{"s":"Selection Sandbox"}},{"k":"watch_a_population_evolve_under_select","v":{"s":"Watch a population evolve under selection"}},{"k":"50_creatures_with_a_heritable_trait_se","v":{"s":"50 creatures with a heritable trait. Set the selection pressure (which trait values survive); each generation, the population shifts. Includes Maine snowshoe hare preset for climate-driven coat color selection."}},{"k":"gal_pagos_beak_lab","v":{"s":"Galápagos Beak Lab"}},{"k":"real_grant_data_observed_evolution","v":{"s":"Real Grant data — observed evolution"}},{"k":"finches_with_different_beak_depths_spe","v":{"s":"Finches with different beak depths specialize in different seed types. When drought hits, only the largest hard seeds remain — and only large-beaked birds can crack them. Reproduces the famous 1977 Grant study."}},{"k":"speciation_simulator","v":{"s":"Speciation Simulator"}},{"k":"watch_one_species_split_into_two","v":{"s":"Watch one species split into two"}},{"k":"a_single_ancestral_population_splits_i","v":{"s":"A single ancestral population splits in half across a geographic barrier. Each half evolves under its own pressure. Over generations they diverge until they can no longer interbreed — speciation. Reproduces the allopatric model behind Galápagos finches and Lake Victoria cichlids."}},{"k":"phylogenetic_tree_builder","v":{"s":"Phylogenetic Tree Builder"}},{"k":"build_a_cladogram_by_shared_traits","v":{"s":"Build a cladogram by shared traits"}},{"k":"drop_12_organisms_onto_a_branching_tre","v":{"s":"Place 13 organisms on the tips of a branching tree by following shared derived traits (synapomorphies): backbone, four limbs, amniotic egg, hair or feathers. Grade the tree, then toggle the evidence: skeletons and DNA disagree about exactly two branches."}},{"k":"allele_frequency_equilibrium","v":{"s":"Allele frequency equilibrium"}},{"k":"visualize_p_2pq_q_1_with_adjustable_al","v":{"s":"Visualize p² + 2pq + q² = 1 with adjustable allele frequencies. Toggle selection / mutation / drift / migration to see equilibrium break."}},{"k":"genetic_drift","v":{"s":"Genetic Drift"}},{"k":"random_allele_change_in_small_populati","v":{"s":"Random allele change in small populations"}},{"k":"50_50_starting_alleles_no_selection_ju","v":{"s":"50/50 starting alleles, no selection — just random sampling. Smaller populations drift hard; larger populations stay near 50/50. Demonstrates founder effects and bottlenecks."}},{"k":"common_ancestry","v":{"s":"Common Ancestry"}},{"k":"homologous_bones_across_species","v":{"s":"Homologous bones across species"}},{"k":"click_any_bone_in_the_human_arm_see_th","v":{"s":"Click any bone in the human arm — see the same bone in a bat wing, whale flipper, horse leg, and bird wing. Same skeleton, different jobs. Then sort fifteen real findings into the five lines of evidence."}},{"k":"antibiotic_resistance","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Resistance"}},{"k":"watch_bacteria_evolve_resistance","v":{"s":"Watch bacteria evolve resistance"}},{"k":"apply_antibiotic_pulses_to_a_bacterial","v":{"s":"Apply antibiotic pulses to a bacterial population. Resistant strains survive and reproduce. Real-world public health: this is why finishing your prescription matters."}},{"k":"coevolution_lab","v":{"s":"Coevolution Lab"}},{"k":"predator_prey_arms_race","v":{"s":"Predator-prey arms race"}},{"k":"two_populations_evolving_against_each_","v":{"s":"Two populations evolving against each other. Predators get faster; prey escape better; the cycle continues. Demonstrates the Red Queen hypothesis: you have to keep running just to stay in place."}},{"k":"discovery_timeline","v":{"s":"Discovery Timeline"}},{"k":"how_we_learned_what_we_know","v":{"s":"How we learned what we know"}},{"k":"the_250_year_history_of_evolution_scie","v":{"s":"The 290-year history of evolution science: Darwin, Wallace, Mendel, the Modern Synthesis, the often-overlooked figures (Margulis, Franklin, McClintock), and the field and lab studies that watched selection happen (Kettlewell, the Grants, Lenski)."}},{"k":"misconceptions_quiz","v":{"s":"Misconceptions Quiz"}},{"k":"12_common_evolution_errors","v":{"s":"12 common evolution errors"}},{"k":"targets_the_misunderstandings_just_a_t","v":{"s":"Targets the misunderstandings: \"just a theory,\" \"humans from monkeys,\" evolution-with-a-goal, survival-of-the-strongest. Each question gives a detailed evidence-based correction."}},{"k":"selection_sleuth","v":{"s":"Selection Sleuth"}},{"k":"10_vignettes_name_the_mechanism","v":{"s":"10 vignettes — name the mechanism"}},{"k":"for_each_scenario_identify_the_evoluti","v":{"s":"For each scenario, identify the evolutionary mechanism: natural selection (directional / stabilizing / disruptive), sexual selection, artificial selection, or genetic drift. Forces students past \"evolution = natural selection\" toward the full taxonomy of mechanisms."}},{"k":"homology_vs_analogy","v":{"s":"Homology vs Analogy"}},{"k":"10_trait_pairs_same_origin_or_same_fun","v":{"s":"10 trait pairs — same origin or same function"}},{"k":"for_each_pair_of_structures_decide_hom","v":{"s":"For each pair of structures, decide: homologous (same evolutionary origin, often different function — like human arm vs whale flipper) or analogous (same function, different origin — like bird wing vs butterfly wing). The signature distinction for inferring common descent."}},{"k":"capstone_project","v":{"s":"Capstone Project"}},{"k":"predict_run_reflect","v":{"s":"Predict, test, explain"}},{"k":"a_guided_4_step_research_project_pick_","v":{"s":"A guided investigation with a persistent evidence notebook. Pick a real-world scenario, predict the outcome, test it in a linked simulation, record data, and generate a print-ready lab report."}},{"k":"evolution_and_natural_selection_see_po","v":{"s":"Evolution and natural selection — see populations change, build the tree of life, and untangle the most common misconceptions."}},{"k":"core_simulators","v":{"s":"Core Simulators"}},{"k":"quick_labs","v":{"s":"Quick Labs"}},{"k":"teacher_resources","v":{"s":"Teacher Resources"}},{"k":"for_educators_planning_a_unit","v":{"s":"— for educators planning a unit"}},{"k":"5_day_curriculum_guide_sequenced_unit_","v":{"s":"5-Day Curriculum Guide — sequenced unit plan with warm-ups, labs, discussions, and exit tickets"}},{"k":"teacher","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher"}},{"k":"5_day_curriculum_guide","v":{"s":"5-Day Curriculum Guide"}},{"k":"sequenced_unit_plan","v":{"s":"Sequenced unit plan"}},{"k":"five_50_minute_periods_sequencing_all_","v":{"s":"Five 50-minute periods sequencing all modules into a coherent unit. Each day includes warm-up, lab activity, class discussion, and exit ticket. Click any module link inside the guide to launch it directly. Print-friendly."}},{"k":"module_map_visual_flowchart_of_how_all","v":{"s":"Module Map — visual flowchart of how all modules connect by conceptual scale"}},{"k":"teacher_2","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher"}},{"k":"module_map","v":{"s":"Module Map"}},{"k":"how_modules_connect","v":{"s":"How modules connect"}},{"k":"visual_flowchart_showing_the_11_studen","v":{"s":"Visual flowchart showing all 17 student modules organized by conceptual scale (alleles → populations → species → all of life). Includes Quick / Standard / Deep-dive learning paths."}},{"k":"standards_crosswalk_find_a_module_that","v":{"s":"Standards Crosswalk — find a module that addresses a specific NGSS standard"}},{"k":"teacher_3","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher"}},{"k":"standards_crosswalk","v":{"s":"Standards Crosswalk"}},{"k":"ngss_by_module_lookup","v":{"s":"NGSS-by-module lookup"}},{"k":"for_unit_planning_pick_an_ngss_perform","v":{"s":"For unit planning. Pick an NGSS performance expectation and see which modules address it. Includes AP Biology Big Idea cross-reference for AP Bio teachers."}},{"k":"stem_lab_tool_evolution_natural_select","v":{"s":"STEAM Lab tool · Evolution & natural selection · Maine examples sprinkled in"}},{"k":"homozygous_dominant","v":{"s":"Homozygous dominant"}},{"k":"heterozygous","v":{"s":"Heterozygous"}},{"k":"homozygous_recessive","v":{"s":"Homozygous recessive"}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_calculator","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg Calculator"}},{"k":"p_2pq_q_1","v":{"s":"p² + 2pq + q² = 1"}},{"k":"in_a_non_evolving_population_genotype_","v":{"s":"In a non-evolving population, genotype frequencies are predicted by allele frequencies."}},{"k":"your_job_turn_on_selection_mutation_or","v":{"s":"🎯 Your job: turn on selection, mutation, or migration and watch this equation BREAK."}},{"k":"how_to_use_this_calculator_click_to_to","v":{"s":"📜 How to use this calculator (click to toggle)"}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_equilibrium","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium"}},{"k":"describes_the_genotype_frequencies_you","v":{"s":" describes the genotype frequencies you should see in a population that is NOT evolving. If reality matches the equation, the population is stable. If reality drifts away from it, ONE of the five forces is acting:"}},{"k":"selection_against_aa","v":{"s":"Selection against aa"}},{"k":"aa_individuals_leave_fewer_offspring_a","v":{"s":": aa individuals leave fewer offspring. Allele q drops over generations."}},{"k":"mutation_rate","v":{"s":"Mutation rate"}},{"k":"a_randomly_flips_to_a_or_vice_versa_pe","v":{"s":": A randomly flips to a (or vice versa) per generation. Slow but constant."}},{"k":"migration","v":{"s":"Migration"}},{"k":"new_alleles_arrive_from_outside_the_po","v":{"s":": new alleles arrive from outside the population. Pushes p toward the migrant frequency."}},{"k":"non_random_mating","v":{"s":"Non-random mating"}},{"k":"not_modeled_here_and_small_population_","v":{"s":" (not modeled here) and small population size (genetic drift) are the other two."}},{"k":"try_this","v":{"s":"Try this: "}},{"k":"set_p_to_0_5_turn_selection_against_aa","v":{"s":"set p to 0.5, turn selection against aa up to 0.3, press Step a few times. Watch q crash. Now reset, turn migration on instead. See how the curve looks different."}},{"k":"p_a","v":{"s":"p (A)"}},{"k":"q_a","v":{"s":"q (a)"}},{"k":"generation","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"status","v":{"s":"Status"}},{"k":"1_allele_frequency_p","v":{"s":"1. Allele Frequency p"}},{"k":"p_is_the_frequency_of_allele_a_q_frequ","v":{"s":"p is the frequency of allele A. q (frequency of a) is automatically 1 - p."}},{"k":"2_selection_against_aa","v":{"s":"2. Selection Against aa"}},{"k":"how_much_aa_offspring_survive_less_tha","v":{"s":"How much aa offspring survive less than AA / Aa. 0 = no selection; 1 = aa is lethal."}},{"k":"3_mutation_rate_a_a","v":{"s":"3. Mutation Rate (A → a)"}},{"k":"rate_at_which_a_mutates_to_a_per_gener","v":{"s":"Rate at which A mutates to a per generation. Real biology: ~10⁻⁶ per gene; here exaggerated to be visible."}},{"k":"4_migration_in_allele_a_from_outside","v":{"s":"4. Migration In (allele A from outside)"}},{"k":"each_generation_this_fraction_of_the_p","v":{"s":"Each generation, this fraction of the population is replaced by immigrants with p = 0.5."}},{"k":"advance_one_generation_applying_any_ac","v":{"s":"Advance one generation, applying any active selection, mutation, or migration forces"}},{"k":"step_1_generation","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Generation"}},{"k":"advance_ten_generations","v":{"s":"Advance ten generations"}},{"k":"step_10","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Step 10"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"why_hardy_weinberg_matters","v":{"s":"📖 Why Hardy-Weinberg Matters"}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_is_the_no_evolution_bas","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg is the \"no-evolution baseline.\" If p² + 2pq + q² holds across generations, the population is NOT evolving. Real populations always violate at least one of the five HW assumptions: no selection, no mutation, no migration, infinite population (no drift), random mating."}},{"k":"the_point","v":{"s":"The point: "}},{"k":"evolution_any_change_in_allele_frequen","v":{"s":"evolution = any change in allele frequencies over time. Watching HW break is watching evolution happen."}},{"k":"try_these_experiments","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"no_forces","v":{"s":"No forces: "}},{"k":"set_p_to_anything_e_g_0_7_leave_select","v":{"s":"Set p to anything (e.g., 0.7), leave selection / mutation / migration at 0. Press Step 10. The bars don't move — equilibrium holds."}},{"k":"strong_selection","v":{"s":"Strong selection: "}},{"k":"set_p_0_5_selection_0_8_aa_is_80_letha","v":{"s":"Set p = 0.5, selection = 0.8 (aa is 80% lethal). Step 10. Watch the aa bar shrink and AA grow as a is purged from the population."}},{"k":"mutation_pressure","v":{"s":"Mutation pressure: "}},{"k":"set_p_1_0_all_a_mutation_0_05_step_50_","v":{"s":"Set p = 1.0 (all A), mutation = 0.05. Step 50. Watch p slowly drift down as A → a mutations accumulate."}},{"k":"migration_overwhelms_selection","v":{"s":"Migration overwhelms selection: "}},{"k":"set_p_0_1_selection_0_5_migration_0_2_","v":{"s":"Set p = 0.1, selection = 0.5, migration = 0.2. Step 20. The population can't purge a because immigrants keep restoring it."}},{"k":"next_up","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_assumes_infinite_popula","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg assumes infinite population size. In small populations, RANDOM sampling changes allele frequencies even without selection. The Genetic Drift lab shows you exactly how much."}},{"k":"genetic_drift_simulator","v":{"s":"Genetic Drift Simulator"}},{"k":"random_sampling_no_selection","v":{"s":"Random sampling, no selection"}},{"k":"set_a_population_size_then_run_5_paral","v":{"s":"Set a population size, then run 5 parallel lineages. They all start at p = 0.5 (50/50 allele frequencies) and evolve only by chance — no selection, no mutation. Smaller populations drift much harder."}},{"k":"population_n","v":{"s":"Population (N)"}},{"k":"generations","v":{"s":"Generations"}},{"k":"fixed_lineages","v":{"s":"Fixed Lineages"}},{"k":"allele_frequency_over_time","v":{"s":"Allele Frequency Over Time"}},{"k":"p_1_a_fixed","v":{"s":"p=1 (A fixed)"}},{"k":"p_0_a_fixed","v":{"s":"p=0 (a fixed)"}},{"k":"gen_0","v":{"s":"gen 0"}},{"k":"press_run_to_watch_5_lineages_drift_by","v":{"s":"Press Run to watch 5 lineages drift by chance"}},{"k":"population_size_n","v":{"s":"Population Size (N)"}},{"k":"smaller_populations_drift_harder_n_10_","v":{"s":"Smaller populations drift harder. N=10 → most lineages fix within 50 generations. N=1000 → rarely fixes."}},{"k":"generations_to_run","v":{"s":"Generations to Run"}},{"k":"run_5_lineages","v":{"s":"🎲 Run 5 Lineages"}},{"k":"clear","v":{"s":"↺ Clear"}},{"k":"why_drift_matters","v":{"s":"📖 Why Drift Matters"}},{"k":"founder_effect","v":{"s":"Founder effect: "}},{"k":"when_a_small_group_splits_off_from_a_l","v":{"s":"when a small group splits off from a larger population, their allele frequencies are random samples — and may differ wildly from the source. Many island populations show this."}},{"k":"when_a_population_crashes_the_survivor","v":{"s":"when a population crashes, the survivors' allele frequencies become the new starting point. Northern elephant seals went through a bottleneck of ~20 individuals in the 1890s — they have very low genetic diversity today as a result."}},{"k":"the_takeaway","v":{"s":"The takeaway: "}},{"k":"not_all_evolution_is_selection_random_","v":{"s":"not all evolution is selection. Random change is real, especially in small populations. Conservation biologists worry about this for endangered species — even with perfect protection, a tiny population loses genetic diversity by drift alone."}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_2","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"tiny_population_n_10","v":{"s":"Tiny population (N=10): "}},{"k":"run_5_lineages_over_100_generations_mo","v":{"s":"Run 5 lineages over 100 generations. Most lineages \"fix\" — alleles go to 0 or 1 by chance. Repeat the run and notice the END states are different each time even though all started identically."}},{"k":"big_population_n_1000","v":{"s":"Big population (N=1000): "}},{"k":"same_100_generations_lineages_stay_nea","v":{"s":"Same 100 generations. Lineages stay near p=0.5 — drift can't move them much because random sampling from many individuals smooths out."}},{"k":"long_term_tiny_population","v":{"s":"Long-term tiny population: "}},{"k":"set_n_10_and_generations_500_watch_how","v":{"s":"Set N=10 and generations=500. Watch how lineages settle on whichever allele was lucky early. This is the founder effect playing out."}},{"k":"compare_outcomes","v":{"s":"Compare outcomes: "}},{"k":"run_n_10_three_times_then_n_1000_three","v":{"s":"Run N=10 three times, then N=1000 three times. The N=10 outcomes vary wildly between runs; the N=1000 outcomes are nearly identical. Drift's power scales as 1/N."}},{"k":"next_up_2","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"drift_is_one_major_force_selection_is_","v":{"s":"Drift is one major force; SELECTION is the other. The Selection Sandbox lets you watch a 50-creature population evolve under your chosen pressure — stabilizing, directional, or disruptive."}},{"k":"coming_soon","v":{"s":"Coming soon"}},{"k":"what_it_will_do","v":{"s":"What it will do:"}},{"k":"stabilizing_birth_weight","v":{"s":"Stabilizing — Birth Weight"}},{"k":"extreme_trait_values_are_penalized_the","v":{"s":"Extreme trait values are penalized; the population converges on the optimum. Real example: human birth weight — too small or too large is risky, ~7 lbs is optimal."}},{"k":"directional_peppered_moth","v":{"s":"Directional — Peppered Moth"}},{"k":"selection_favors_one_extreme_industria","v":{"s":"Selection favors one extreme. Industrial pollution darkened tree bark; moth populations shifted from light to dark over decades (Biston betularia)."}},{"k":"disruptive_island_beak_depth","v":{"s":"Disruptive — Island Beak Depth"}},{"k":"both_extremes_outperform_the_middle_ra","v":{"s":"Both extremes outperform the middle. Rare in nature — happens when two distinct food sources favor different specialists, with intermediates losing both."}},{"k":"drift_only_no_selection","v":{"s":"Drift Only — No Selection"}},{"k":"no_fitness_pressure_watch_how_the_popu","v":{"s":"No fitness pressure. Watch how the population still changes over generations from random sampling (genetic drift). Smaller populations drift harder."}},{"k":"maine_snowshoe_hare_climate_change","v":{"s":"Maine Snowshoe Hare — Climate Change"}},{"k":"real_ongoing_research_as_maine_winters","v":{"s":"Real ongoing research: as Maine winters shorten, hares are still molting white in November but the snow arrives later. White-coated hares against bare ground are easy prey. Selection now favors hares that delay their molt."}},{"k":"trait_distribution","v":{"s":"Trait Distribution"}},{"k":"mean_trait_over_generations","v":{"s":"Mean Trait Over Generations"}},{"k":"selection_sandbox_2","v":{"s":"Selection Sandbox"}},{"k":"generation_2","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"mean_trait","v":{"s":"Mean Trait"}},{"k":"std_dev","v":{"s":"Std Dev"}},{"k":"last_survivors","v":{"s":"Last Survivors"}},{"k":"selection_mode","v":{"s":"Selection Mode"}},{"k":"selection_strength","v":{"s":"Selection Strength"}},{"k":"how_strongly_the_environment_selects_a","v":{"s":"How strongly the environment selects against unfit creatures. 0 = no selection (genetic drift only); 1 = full selection."}},{"k":"mutation_size","v":{"s":"Mutation Size"}},{"k":"standard_deviation_of_trait_change_bet","v":{"s":"Standard deviation of trait change between parent and offspring. Higher = more variation each generation."}},{"k":"step_1_generation_2","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Generation"}},{"k":"step_10_2","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Step 10"}},{"k":"reset_population","v":{"s":"↺ Reset Population"}},{"k":"what_you_re_seeing","v":{"s":"📖 What you're seeing"}},{"k":"each_generation_s_trait_distribution_t","v":{"s":"Each generation's trait distribution (the histogram) shows variation among individuals. Without variation, there's nothing for selection to act on."}},{"k":"creatures_with_traits_closer_to_the_pr","v":{"s":"Creatures with traits closer to the preset's \"ideal\" survive at higher rates. The fraction that dies before reproducing is the selection pressure."}},{"k":"survivors_offspring_inherit_the_parent","v":{"s":"Survivors' offspring inherit the parent's trait ± a small random change (mutation). Over generations, this shifts the population mean."}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_3","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"selection_vs_drift","v":{"s":"Selection vs drift: "}},{"k":"pick_the_directional_peppered_moth_pre","v":{"s":"Pick the directional (peppered moth) preset, set Selection Strength to 1.0, click Step 10. Watch the mean shift toward 0.85. Now set Selection to 0 and step another 30 generations — the mean wanders randomly without converging."}},{"k":"stabilizing_keeps_the_mean_stable","v":{"s":"Stabilizing keeps the mean stable: "}},{"k":"switch_to_the_stabilizing_birth_weight","v":{"s":"Switch to the stabilizing (birth weight) preset. The mean stays near 0.5 but the standard deviation SHRINKS over generations as extremes are pruned. Variation decreases without the mean moving."}},{"k":"disruptive_splits_the_population","v":{"s":"Disruptive splits the population: "}},{"k":"switch_to_disruptive_after_15_20_gener","v":{"s":"Switch to disruptive. After 15-20 generations the histogram becomes BIMODAL — two peaks at 0.2 and 0.8 with a valley at 0.5. This is how one species can begin to split into two."}},{"k":"mutation_matters","v":{"s":"Mutation matters: "}},{"k":"on_the_directional_preset_with_selecti","v":{"s":"On the directional preset with selection ON, set Mutation Size to 0 (no variation introduced). After convergence the population stops changing — selection has nothing left to act on. Then bump Mutation Size up — convergence resumes faster."}},{"k":"snowshoe_hare_scenario","v":{"s":"Snowshoe hare scenario: "}},{"k":"switch_to_the_snowshoe_hare_preset_the","v":{"s":"Switch to the snowshoe hare preset. The \"ideal\" trait is 0.68 (later molt) — selection now pushes hares away from their ancestral early-molt genes. This is real ongoing Maine evolution."}},{"k":"next_up_3","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"see_selection_happen_in_real_data_the_","v":{"s":"See selection happen in REAL data. The Galápagos Beak Lab reproduces the Grant 1977 drought study where finch beak depth shifted measurably in just one year."}},{"k":"small_9mm","v":{"s":"Small (<9mm)"}},{"k":"medium_9_10mm","v":{"s":"Medium (9-10mm)"}},{"k":"large_10_11mm","v":{"s":"Large (10-11mm)"}},{"k":"extra_large_11mm","v":{"s":"Extra-large (>11mm)"}},{"k":"small_soft_chamaesyce","v":{"s":"Small soft (chamaesyce)"}},{"k":"medium_cordia","v":{"s":"Medium (cordia)"}},{"k":"large_tribulus","v":{"s":"Large (tribulus)"}},{"k":"very_hard_palo_santo","v":{"s":"Very hard (palo santo)"}},{"k":"pre_drought_baseline","v":{"s":"Pre-drought baseline"}},{"k":"during_drought","v":{"s":"During drought"}},{"k":"after_drought_1_year","v":{"s":"After drought (1 year)"}},{"k":"wet_again_partial_reversion","v":{"s":"Wet again — partial reversion"}},{"k":"equilibrating","v":{"s":"Equilibrating"}},{"k":"population_by_beak_class","v":{"s":"Population by Beak Class"}},{"k":"available_seeds_this_year","v":{"s":"Available Seeds This Year"}},{"k":"mean_beak_depth_over_years","v":{"s":"Mean Beak Depth Over Years"}},{"k":"your_sim","v":{"s":"Your sim"}},{"k":"real_grant_data","v":{"s":"Real Grant data"}},{"k":"baseline_9_2mm","v":{"s":"baseline 9.2mm"}},{"k":"year_0","v":{"s":"Year 0"}},{"k":"gal_pagos_beak_lab_2","v":{"s":"Galápagos Beak Lab"}},{"k":"daphne_major_1977","v":{"s":"Daphne Major, 1977"}},{"k":"peter_rosemary_grant_tagged_every_medi","v":{"s":"Peter & Rosemary Grant tagged every medium ground finch on this Galápagos islet for 40+ years. In 1977 a severe drought killed 85% of small soft seeds. The next year, surviving birds had measurably larger beaks — observed evolution within ONE generation."}},{"k":"year","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"mean_beak","v":{"s":"Mean Beak"}},{"k":"last_year","v":{"s":"Last Year"}},{"k":"mode","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"run_1_year","v":{"s":"⏭ Run 1 Year"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"mutation_size_beak_variation_per_gener","v":{"s":"Mutation Size (Beak Variation per Generation)"}},{"k":"standard_deviation_of_offspring_beak_d","v":{"s":"Standard deviation of offspring beak depth around the parental mean. Higher = faster evolution but also faster reversal."}},{"k":"connecting_to_maine","v":{"s":"🦆 Connecting to Maine: "}},{"k":"maine_has_its_own_beak_specialist_finc","v":{"s":"Maine has its own beak-specialist finches. Purple finches (small conical bills) eat seeds and berries. Evening grosbeaks have unusually large bills for cracking sugar maple and box elder seeds — a Maine winter staple. White-winged crossbills have crossed mandible tips that pry open spruce cones. Same evolutionary principle as the Galápagos: bill shape adapts to the available food source."}},{"k":"what_the_grants_found","v":{"s":"📖 What the Grants Found"}},{"k":"1977_baseline","v":{"s":"1977 baseline: "}},{"k":"mean_medium_ground_finch_beak_depth_9_","v":{"s":"Mean medium-ground-finch beak depth ≈ 9.21mm. Population ~1,500 birds."}},{"k":"85_of_all_the_soft_seeds_died_off_only","v":{"s":"~85% of all the soft seeds died off. Only the largest, hardest seeds remained."}},{"k":"85_of_the_finch_population_died_and_th","v":{"s":"~85% of the finch population died — and the survivors were almost all large-beaked birds (the only ones who could crack the remaining seeds)."}},{"k":"1978_mean","v":{"s":"1978 mean: "}},{"k":"10_05mm_a_0_84mm_shift_in_one_generati","v":{"s":"≈10.05mm in this lab's teaching approximation of the record: a shift of roughly a millimetre within ONE generation. It is one of the first cases of natural selection measured directly, bird by bird, in a wild vertebrate population."}},{"k":"the_lesson","v":{"s":"The lesson: "}},{"k":"evolution_is_not_slow_given_strong_sel","v":{"s":"Evolution is not slow. Given strong selection, populations can shift dramatically within a single generation. The Grants' work is now a textbook example of natural selection in action."}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_4","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"reproduce_the_1977_study","v":{"s":"Reproduce the 1977 study: "}},{"k":"start_fresh_reset_trigger_drought_then","v":{"s":"Start fresh (Reset). Trigger Drought, then Run 1 Year. Look at the mean beak depth shift. Toggle \"Show Real Grant Data\" — your result should track the historical 1977 → 1978 jump (~0.5-1mm)."}},{"k":"drought_without_an_end","v":{"s":"Drought without an end: "}},{"k":"keep_drought_on_and_run_5_more_years_t","v":{"s":"Keep drought ON and run 5 more years. The population keeps shifting toward larger beaks until it hits the upper end of available variation — selection can't create new traits, only sort existing ones."}},{"k":"reversal_when_the_rains_return","v":{"s":"Reversal when the rains return: "}},{"k":"after_5_drought_years_turn_drought_off","v":{"s":"After 5 drought years, turn drought OFF and run 5 more years. The mean drifts back toward smaller, slowly: a drought-shifted population is mostly deep-beaked birds, and deep beaks take fewer of the tiny seeds that normal years are full of."}},{"k":"mutation_rate_matters","v":{"s":"Mutation rate matters: "}},{"k":"set_0_05_very_tight_inheritance_and_ru","v":{"s":"Set σ = 0.05 (very tight inheritance) and run 5 drought years. Compare with σ = 0.40 (loose inheritance). Lower mutation = slower response to selection because there's less variation to work with."}},{"k":"try_to_make_small_beaks_come_back","v":{"s":"Try to make small beaks come back: "}},{"k":"once_large_beaked_birds_dominate_turni","v":{"s":"Once large-beaked birds dominate, turning off drought is not enough — the small-beak gene pool may already be too thin to recover quickly. This is why evolution often doesn't fully reverse even when conditions revert."}},{"k":"next_up_4","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_watched_one_species_change_the_spe","v":{"s":"You watched one species CHANGE. The Trait Divergence Model shows what happens next — when an isolated population diverges far enough that it becomes a SECOND species. This is how the Galápagos got 14 finch species from one ancestor."}},{"k":"moose","v":{"s":"Moose"}},{"k":"maine_s_largest_land_animal_has_fur_mi","v":{"s":"Maine's largest land animal — has fur, milk glands."}},{"k":"white_tail_deer","v":{"s":"White-tail Deer"}},{"k":"common_maine_mammal_fur_live_birth_mam","v":{"s":"Common Maine mammal — fur, live birth, mammary glands."}},{"k":"black_bear","v":{"s":"Black Bear"}},{"k":"mainland_maine_apex_carnivore","v":{"s":"Mainland Maine apex carnivore."}},{"k":"cardinal","v":{"s":"Cardinal"}},{"k":"year_round_maine_resident_feathers_lay","v":{"s":"Year-round Maine resident — feathers, lays eggs."}},{"k":"box_turtle","v":{"s":"Box Turtle"}},{"k":"cold_blooded_scales_lays_leathery_eggs","v":{"s":"Cold-blooded, scales, lays leathery eggs."}},{"k":"garter_snake","v":{"s":"Garter Snake"}},{"k":"common_maine_snake_scaly_ectothermic","v":{"s":"Common Maine snake — scaly, ectothermic."}},{"k":"atlantic_salmon","v":{"s":"Atlantic Salmon"}},{"k":"anadromous_maine_s_endangered_native","v":{"s":"Anadromous — Maine's endangered native."}},{"k":"bullfrog","v":{"s":"Bullfrog"}},{"k":"moist_skin_lays_jelly_coated_eggs_in_w","v":{"s":"Moist skin, lays jelly-coated eggs in water."}},{"k":"maine_lobster","v":{"s":"Maine Lobster"}},{"k":"jointed_exoskeleton_10_legs_like_insec","v":{"s":"Jointed exoskeleton, 10 legs — like insects, not fish."}},{"k":"honeybee","v":{"s":"Honeybee"}},{"k":"insect_6_legs_exoskeleton","v":{"s":"Insect — 6 legs, exoskeleton."}},{"k":"crab","v":{"s":"Crab"}},{"k":"crustacean_close_cousin_to_lobster","v":{"s":"Crustacean — close cousin to lobster."}},{"k":"red_maple","v":{"s":"Red Maple"}},{"k":"maine_state_tree_photosynthesizes_no_n","v":{"s":"Maine state tree — photosynthesizes, no nervous system."}},{"k":"plants","v":{"s":"🌳 Plants"}},{"k":"photosynthesize_cell_walls_of_cellulos","v":{"s":"Photosynthesize. Cell walls of cellulose. No nervous system."}},{"k":"arthropods","v":{"s":"🦞 Arthropods"}},{"k":"jointed_exoskeleton_segmented_body_ins","v":{"s":"Jointed exoskeleton. Segmented body. Insects, crustaceans, spiders."}},{"k":"fish","v":{"s":"🐟 Fish"}},{"k":"aquatic_vertebrate_gills_scales_fins_c","v":{"s":"Aquatic vertebrate. Gills, scales, fins. Cold-blooded."}},{"k":"amphibians","v":{"s":"🐸 Amphibians"}},{"k":"moist_permeable_skin_lay_eggs_in_water","v":{"s":"Moist permeable skin. Lay eggs in water. Larval water → adult land."}},{"k":"reptiles","v":{"s":"🐢 Reptiles"}},{"k":"dry_scaly_skin_amniotic_eggs_or_live_b","v":{"s":"Dry scaly skin. Amniotic eggs (or live birth). Cold-blooded."}},{"k":"birds","v":{"s":"🐦 Birds"}},{"k":"feathers_modified_scales_warm_blooded_","v":{"s":"Feathers (modified scales). Warm-blooded. Lay hard-shelled eggs."}},{"k":"mammals","v":{"s":"🫎 Mammals"}},{"k":"hair_fur_mammary_glands_warm_blooded_m","v":{"s":"Hair/fur. Mammary glands. Warm-blooded. Mostly live birth."}},{"k":"actual_cladogram","v":{"s":"🌳 Actual Cladogram"}},{"k":"phylogenetic_tree_builder_2","v":{"s":"Phylogenetic Tree Builder"}},{"k":"build_the_tree_of_life","v":{"s":"Build the tree of life"}},{"k":"each_organism_belongs_to_one_of_seven_","v":{"s":"Each organism belongs to one of seven major clades. Use the dropdown to assign each one. The tool grades your placements against the canonical cladogram. Watch out — some organisms look like they'd group with one clade but their evolutionary history says otherwise."}},{"k":"assigned","v":{"s":"Placed"}},{"k":"correct","v":{"s":"Correct"}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"the_7_clades","v":{"s":"The 7 Clades"}},{"k":"12_organisms_pick_each_one_s_clade","v":{"s":"12 Organisms: pick one, then its tip"}},{"k":"correct_2","v":{"s":"Correct"}},{"k":"incorrect","v":{"s":"Incorrect"}},{"k":"select_clade","v":{"s":"— Select clade —"}},{"k":"why","v":{"s":"💡 Why: "}},{"k":"try_again","v":{"s":"↺ Start Over"}},{"k":"tree_thinking_the_cladogram_way","v":{"s":"📖 Tree-thinking, the cladogram way"}},{"k":"synapomorphies_are_everything","v":{"s":"Synapomorphies are everything: "}},{"k":"modern_phylogenetics_groups_species_by","v":{"s":"Modern phylogenetics groups species by SHARED DERIVED traits (synapomorphies), not by overall similarity. A whale and a fish both swim, but the whale's lungs, hair, and mammary glands tell us it's a mammal that returned to the water — its closest cousins are hippos, not herring."}},{"k":"why_lobster_fish","v":{"s":"Why lobster ≠ fish: "}},{"k":"lobsters_live_in_water_and_have_a_tail","v":{"s":"Lobsters live in water and have a tail-like abdomen, but they have an exoskeleton, jointed legs, and lack a vertebral column. Their closest relatives are CRABS, then more distantly INSECTS — both arthropods. Salmon (a true fish) shares a vertebral column with you, not with the lobster."}},{"k":"why_birds_are_reptiles","v":{"s":"Why birds are reptiles: "}},{"k":"cladistic_logic_says_a_clade_includes_","v":{"s":"Cladistic logic says a clade includes ALL descendants of an ancestor. Birds descended from theropod dinosaurs — and dinosaurs were reptiles — so technically birds belong inside the reptile clade. We split them out for convenience because feathers are so distinctive."}},{"k":"tree_thinking_matters_because","v":{"s":"Tree-thinking matters because: "}},{"k":"folk_taxonomy_groups_things_by_appeara","v":{"s":"Folk taxonomy groups things by appearance (\"looks like a fish\"). Cladistics groups by ancestry. The two often disagree, and the latter is what evolution explains."}},{"k":"glossary_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"📚 Glossary — click to expand"}},{"k":"clade","v":{"s":"Clade"}},{"k":"a_group_of_organisms_that_includes_a_c","v":{"s":"A group of organisms that includes a common ancestor AND all of its descendants. Mammals form a clade; \"fish\" usually does not (because tetrapods descended from fish but most people don't consider whales fish)."}},{"k":"synapomorphy","v":{"s":"Synapomorphy"}},{"k":"a_trait_shared_by_all_members_of_a_cla","v":{"s":"A trait shared by all members of a clade because they inherited it from their common ancestor — but NOT found in organisms outside the clade. Mammary glands are a synapomorphy of mammals; feathers are a synapomorphy of birds."}},{"k":"homologous_structures","v":{"s":"Homologous structures"}},{"k":"body_parts_that_share_a_common_evoluti","v":{"s":"Body parts that share a common evolutionary origin even if they look different — like the human arm, bat wing, and whale flipper. Same blueprint, modified for different uses."}},{"k":"analogous_structures","v":{"s":"Analogous structures"}},{"k":"body_parts_that_look_similar_and_do_si","v":{"s":"Body parts that LOOK similar and do similar jobs but evolved INDEPENDENTLY — like a bird wing and a butterfly wing. Convergent evolution, NOT shared ancestry."}},{"k":"vertebrate","v":{"s":"Vertebrate"}},{"k":"an_animal_with_a_backbone_vertebral_co","v":{"s":"An animal with a backbone (vertebral column). Includes fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds, and mammals."}},{"k":"tetrapod","v":{"s":"Tetrapod"}},{"k":"a_vertebrate_with_four_limbs_or_whose_","v":{"s":"A vertebrate with four limbs (or whose ancestors had four limbs — whales and snakes count even though they've lost theirs). Includes amphibians, reptiles, birds, and mammals."}},{"k":"amniote_amniotic_egg","v":{"s":"Amniote / amniotic egg"}},{"k":"a_vertebrate_that_lays_eggs_with_inter","v":{"s":"A vertebrate that lays eggs with internal membranes (amnion, chorion, allantois) — or whose ancestors did. The amniotic egg let vertebrates fully colonize land. Reptiles, birds, and mammals are all amniotes."}},{"k":"arthropod","v":{"s":"Arthropod"}},{"k":"an_animal_with_a_jointed_exoskeleton_a","v":{"s":"An animal with a jointed exoskeleton and segmented body. Includes insects, crustaceans (lobster, crab, shrimp), arachnids (spiders), and myriapods (centipedes). Most numerous animal phylum on Earth."}},{"k":"cladogram","v":{"s":"Cladogram"}},{"k":"a_branching_diagram_showing_inferred_e","v":{"s":"A branching diagram showing inferred evolutionary relationships among organisms. Each split (node) represents a common ancestor; each tip is a present-day species."}},{"k":"common_ancestor","v":{"s":"Common ancestor"}},{"k":"an_organism_or_population_from_which_t","v":{"s":"An organism (or population) from which two or more later lineages descended. Humans and chimps share a common ancestor that lived ~6 million years ago — that ancestor was neither a human nor a chimp, but a third thing that gave rise to both."}},{"k":"next_up_5","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"the_cladogram_is_a_tree_built_from_sha","v":{"s":"The cladogram is a tree built from shared traits. Common Ancestry Viewer shows you the most striking shared trait of all — the same five-bone forelimb skeleton in human, bat, whale, horse, and bird."}},{"k":"humerus","v":{"s":"Humerus"}},{"k":"radius","v":{"s":"Radius"}},{"k":"ulna","v":{"s":"Ulna"}},{"k":"carpals_wrist","v":{"s":"Carpals (wrist)"}},{"k":"metacarpals_palm","v":{"s":"Metacarpals (palm)"}},{"k":"phalanges_fingers_toes","v":{"s":"Phalanges (fingers/toes)"}},{"k":"how_this_bone_is_modified_across_speci","v":{"s":"How this bone is modified across species:"}},{"k":"common_ancestry_viewer","v":{"s":"Common Ancestry Viewer"}},{"k":"same_blueprint_different_jobs","v":{"s":"Same blueprint, different jobs"}},{"k":"five_tetrapod_forelimbs_look_closely_e","v":{"s":"Five tetrapod forelimbs. Look closely — every one has a humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals, and phalanges. Same bones, dramatically modified to fly, swim, gallop, or grasp. Click any bone to see its homologous match in all species."}},{"k":"why_this_is_evidence_of_common_descent","v":{"s":"📖 Why this is evidence of common descent"}},{"k":"the_argument","v":{"s":"The argument: "}},{"k":"a_horse_a_whale_a_bat_a_bird_and_a_hum","v":{"s":"A horse, a whale, a bat, a bird, and a human have completely different lifestyles. If each species had been designed independently, you'd expect each to have a totally different limb engineered for its job — like a fan blade vs a propeller vs a paddle vs a leg."}},{"k":"what_we_observe","v":{"s":"What we observe: "}},{"k":"instead_they_all_have_the_same_skeleta","v":{"s":"Instead, they all have the SAME skeletal blueprint, just heavily modified. The only natural explanation is that they all inherited that blueprint from a common ancestor — and modification by descent reshaped it for each lineage."}},{"k":"homologous_vs_analogous","v":{"s":"Homologous vs analogous: "}},{"k":"these_are_homologous_structures_same_o","v":{"s":"These are HOMOLOGOUS structures (same origin, different function). A bird wing and a butterfly wing are ANALOGOUS (same function, totally different origins) — the butterfly wing has no humerus or phalanges. Homology is the key signature of common descent."}},{"k":"vestigial_bonus","v":{"s":"Vestigial bonus: "}},{"k":"the_horse_s_ulna_and_the_whale_s_ulna_","v":{"s":"The horse's ulna and the whale's ulna are both vestigial (greatly reduced) — fossils of the ancestor still visible in the descendant's skeleton."}},{"k":"try_these_comparisons","v":{"s":"🔍 Try these comparisons"}},{"k":"find_the_5_bone_pattern","v":{"s":"Find the 5-bone pattern: "}},{"k":"click_each_of_the_6_bone_chips_in_turn","v":{"s":"Click each of the 6 bone chips in turn (humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals, phalanges). Notice that ALL five species have ALL six bone groups — even the bird and the whale."}},{"k":"spot_the_disappearing_bone","v":{"s":"Spot the disappearing bone: "}},{"k":"click_ulna_in_the_horse_it_s_tiny_fuse","v":{"s":"Click \"Ulna.\" In the horse it's tiny — fused into the radius. In the whale it's also reduced. Why? Both don't need a separate forearm-rotation bone; the radius alone is enough."}},{"k":"spot_the_runaway_bone","v":{"s":"Spot the runaway bone: "}},{"k":"click_metacarpals_in_humans_they_re_mo","v":{"s":"Click \"Metacarpals.\" In humans they're modest palm bones. In bats they're INSANELY long — the structural ribs of the wing membrane. In horses, four of five vanished and the remaining one became the cannon bone."}},{"k":"find_the_one_toe_lineage","v":{"s":"Find the \"one toe\" lineage: "}},{"k":"click_phalanges_compare_horse_vs_human","v":{"s":"Click \"Phalanges.\" Compare horse vs human. Horses reduced from 5 toes to 1 (the hoof). The other 4 are vestigial splint bones. This took ~50 million years."}},{"k":"spot_the_embedded_skeleton","v":{"s":"Spot the embedded skeleton: "}},{"k":"click_any_bone_notice_the_whale_flippe","v":{"s":"Click any bone — notice the whale flipper has the WHOLE arm hidden inside it. The flipper is anatomically still an arm with hand bones; it just got shorter, flatter, and wrapped in soft tissue."}},{"k":"next_up_6","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"common_descent_is_one_of_the_most_misu","v":{"s":"Common descent is one of the most-misunderstood ideas in biology. The Misconceptions Quiz tackles \"humans from monkeys,\" the \"just a theory\" trap, and 10 other common errors."}},{"k":"a_guess_that_hasn_t_been_proven_yet","v":{"s":"A guess that hasn't been proven yet"}},{"k":"a_well_tested_explanation_supported_by","v":{"s":"A well-tested explanation supported by extensive evidence"}},{"k":"something_less_reliable_than_a_law","v":{"s":"Something less reliable than a \"law\""}},{"k":"a_belief_that_hasn_t_been_observed","v":{"s":"A belief that hasn't been observed"}},{"k":"yes_modern_monkeys_are_our_direct_ance","v":{"s":"Yes, modern monkeys are our direct ancestors"}},{"k":"no_humans_and_modern_monkeys_share_a_c","v":{"s":"No, humans and modern monkeys share a common ancestor that lived ~25-30 million years ago"}},{"k":"no_humans_evolved_separately_from_any_","v":{"s":"No, humans evolved separately from any other primate"}},{"k":"only_some_humans_evolved_from_monkeys","v":{"s":"Only some humans evolved from monkeys"}},{"k":"yes_life_is_constantly_improving_towar","v":{"s":"Yes — life is constantly improving toward greater complexity"}},{"k":"yes_every_species_is_moving_toward_an_","v":{"s":"Yes — every species is moving toward an \"ideal\" form"}},{"k":"no_evolution_is_directionless_it_just_","v":{"s":"No — evolution is directionless; it just optimizes for survival in current conditions"}},{"k":"only_intelligent_species_have_evolutio","v":{"s":"Only intelligent species have evolutionary direction"}},{"k":"the_strongest_individual_wins","v":{"s":"The strongest individual wins"}},{"k":"the_fastest_individual_wins","v":{"s":"The fastest individual wins"}},{"k":"the_individual_that_produces_the_most_","v":{"s":"The individual that produces the most surviving offspring \"wins\""}},{"k":"the_smartest_individual_wins","v":{"s":"The smartest individual wins"}},{"k":"yes_acquired_traits_are_passed_to_offs","v":{"s":"Yes — acquired traits are passed to offspring"}},{"k":"no_only_changes_to_dna_can_be_inherite","v":{"s":"No — only changes to DNA can be inherited; muscles built in life don't change DNA"}},{"k":"sometimes_only_if_both_parents_lift_we","v":{"s":"Sometimes — only if both parents lift weights"}},{"k":"yes_but_only_after_many_generations_of","v":{"s":"Yes — but only after many generations of weightlifting"}},{"k":"modern_apes_are_old_human_ancestors_wh","v":{"s":"Modern apes are old human ancestors who somehow survived"}},{"k":"evolution_is_wrong_we_d_expect_the_anc","v":{"s":"Evolution is wrong — we'd expect the ancestors to be gone"}},{"k":"lineages_branch_when_one_population_ev","v":{"s":"Lineages branch — when one population evolves into something new, the original population can persist alongside as a separate species"}},{"k":"modern_apes_are_humans_who_haven_t_fin","v":{"s":"Modern apes are humans who haven't finished evolving yet"}},{"k":"yes_mutations_always_damage_the_organi","v":{"s":"Yes — mutations always damage the organism"}},{"k":"no_most_mutations_are_neutral_some_are","v":{"s":"No — most mutations are neutral; some are harmful; some are beneficial"}},{"k":"mutations_only_happen_in_cancer","v":{"s":"Mutations only happen in cancer"}},{"k":"mutations_are_always_beneficial","v":{"s":"Mutations are always beneficial"}},{"k":"yes_evolution_covers_everything_from_n","v":{"s":"Yes — evolution covers everything from non-life to humans"}},{"k":"no_evolution_explains_how_life_diversi","v":{"s":"No — evolution explains how life DIVERSIFIES once it exists; the origin of life itself is a separate field called abiogenesis"}},{"k":"yes_evolution_proved_life_came_from_no","v":{"s":"Yes — evolution proved life came from non-life"}},{"k":"no_evolution_disproves_the_origin_of_l","v":{"s":"No — evolution disproves the origin of life"}},{"k":"yes_every_aspect_of_evolution_is_purel","v":{"s":"Yes — every aspect of evolution is purely random"}},{"k":"no_mutations_are_random_but_selection_","v":{"s":"No — mutations are random, but selection is non-random (it favors traits that boost survival/reproduction)"}},{"k":"no_evolution_is_fully_directed_by_an_e","v":{"s":"No — evolution is fully directed by an external force"}},{"k":"mutations_are_non_random_but_selection","v":{"s":"Mutations are non-random but selection is random"}},{"k":"true_macroevolution_is_unproven_and_im","v":{"s":"True — macroevolution is unproven and impossible"}},{"k":"false_speciation_has_been_directly_obs","v":{"s":"False — speciation has been directly observed many times (apple maggot fly, London Underground mosquito, ring species, lab fruit flies, observed sympatric speciation in cichlid fish)"}},{"k":"true_but_evolution_is_still_real_for_s","v":{"s":"True — but evolution is still real for small changes"}},{"k":"false_but_only_on_geological_timescale","v":{"s":"False — but only on geological timescales"}},{"k":"yes_needs_drive_evolution","v":{"s":"Yes — needs drive evolution"}},{"k":"no_selection_happens_because_individua","v":{"s":"No — selection happens because individuals with longer necks happened to leave more offspring; no \"need\" causes mutations to appear"}},{"k":"yes_but_only_if_many_giraffes_need_it_","v":{"s":"Yes — but only if many giraffes need it at once"}},{"k":"no_giraffes_were_created_with_long_nec","v":{"s":"No — giraffes were created with long necks"}},{"k":"always_extremely_slow_millions_of_year","v":{"s":"Always extremely slow — millions of years for any change"}},{"k":"variable_strong_selection_can_produce_","v":{"s":"Variable — strong selection can produce visible change in a single generation; weak selection takes thousands or millions of generations"}},{"k":"always_extremely_fast_within_a_single_","v":{"s":"Always extremely fast — within a single lifetime"}},{"k":"it_depends_only_on_the_species_not_the","v":{"s":"It depends only on the species, not the environment"}},{"k":"loading_quiz","v":{"s":"Loading quiz…"}},{"k":"evolution_misconceptions_quiz","v":{"s":"Evolution Misconceptions Quiz"}},{"k":"spot_the_misconception","v":{"s":"Spot the misconception"}},{"k":"score_2","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"resistance_fraction_over_time","v":{"s":"📊 Resistance Fraction Over Time"}},{"k":"resistant","v":{"s":"% resistant"}},{"k":"antibiotic_on","v":{"s":"antibiotic on"}},{"k":"tick_0","v":{"s":"tick 0"}},{"k":"antibiotic_resistance_lab","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Resistance Lab"}},{"k":"evolution_on_a_human_timescale","v":{"s":"Evolution on a human timescale"}},{"k":"bacteria_divide_every_20_30_minutes_ap","v":{"s":"Bacteria divide every 20-30 minutes. Apply an antibiotic, kill 99% of them, and the 1% that survive — usually carrying a rare resistance mutation — repopulate. Within days you have a fully resistant strain. This is happening in real hospitals right now."}},{"k":"population","v":{"s":"Population"}},{"k":"resistant_2","v":{"s":"% Resistant"}},{"k":"tick","v":{"s":"Tick"}},{"k":"mode_2","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"step_1_tick","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Tick"}},{"k":"step_10_3","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Step 10"}},{"k":"reset_dish","v":{"s":"↺ Reset Dish"}},{"k":"antibiotic_dose","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Dose"}},{"k":"higher_dose_kills_more_susceptible_bac","v":{"s":"Higher dose kills more susceptible bacteria each tick. But it also selects more strongly for resistance."}},{"k":"mutation_rate_2","v":{"s":"Mutation Rate"}},{"k":"how_much_offspring_resistance_varies_f","v":{"s":"How much offspring resistance varies from parent. Real bacterial mutation is much rarer; here exaggerated to be visible."}},{"k":"starting_resistance","v":{"s":"Starting Resistance"}},{"k":"fraction_of_the_initial_population_tha","v":{"s":"Fraction of the initial population that's already resistant. Even 1-2% is enough to seed full resistance under treatment."}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_5","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"watch_resistance_evolve","v":{"s":"Watch resistance evolve: "}},{"k":"reset_apply_antibiotic_auto_run_for_30","v":{"s":"Reset. Apply Antibiotic. Auto-Run for ~30 ticks. Watch the population crash, then rebuild from the few resistant survivors. By tick 30 the dish is mostly red."}},{"k":"finish_your_prescription","v":{"s":"\"Finish your prescription\": "}},{"k":"apply_antibiotic_for_10_ticks_populati","v":{"s":"Apply Antibiotic for 10 ticks (population shrinks but isn't gone). Turn antibiotic OFF. The remaining bacteria — already partly resistant — repopulate fast. Restart the antibiotic; it's less effective now. This is exactly why doctors say \"finish all the pills.\""}},{"k":"dose_makes_the_poison","v":{"s":"Dose makes the poison: "}},{"k":"reset_set_dose_to_0_4_low_apply_antibi","v":{"s":"Reset. Set Dose to 0.4 (low). Apply antibiotic and Auto-Run. Notice resistance rises slowly. Now Reset and try Dose = 1.0 (max). Resistance evolves FASTER under stronger selection — counterintuitive but true."}},{"k":"stop_using_it_altogether","v":{"s":"Stop using it altogether: "}},{"k":"after_resistance_reaches_100_turn_anti","v":{"s":"After resistance reaches ~100%, turn the antibiotic OFF and Auto-Run 15+ ticks with Cost of Resistance at 0: resistance stays. Reset, repeat with the cost at 0.05 or more: now resistance slowly erodes as susceptible cells out-reproduce resistant ones. Real bacteria show both patterns, and compensatory mutations can cancel a cost over time."}},{"k":"mutation_rate_matters_2","v":{"s":"Mutation rate matters: "}},{"k":"reset_set_mutation_rate_to_0_apply_ant","v":{"s":"Reset. Set Mutation Rate to 0. Apply Antibiotic. Population goes extinct — no resistance can evolve from scratch. Now set Mutation Rate to 0.15. Resistance evolves nearly instantly. Real-world: bacteria with higher mutation rates evolve resistance faster."}},{"k":"real_world_public_health","v":{"s":"📖 Real-world public health"}},{"k":"methicillin_resistant_staphylococcus_a","v":{"s":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. First seen in 1961, two years after methicillin was introduced. Now common in hospitals worldwide. Annually causes ~10,000+ deaths in the US alone."}},{"k":"multidrug_resistant_tuberculosis_mdr_t","v":{"s":"Multidrug-resistant tuberculosis (MDR-TB) and extensively drug-resistant (XDR-TB) emerged because TB requires 6-9 months of treatment — patients who stop early select for resistance."}},{"k":"c_difficile","v":{"s":"C. difficile: "}},{"k":"often_emerges_after_antibiotic_treatme","v":{"s":"Often emerges AFTER antibiotic treatment for something else. The antibiotic kills off normal gut bacteria, leaving room for resistant C. diff to take over. ~12,800 US deaths/year."}},{"k":"why_this_matters","v":{"s":"Why this matters: "}},{"k":"the_cdc_estimates_antibiotic_resistant","v":{"s":"The CDC estimates antibiotic-resistant infections kill ~35,000 Americans per year. WHO calls antibiotic resistance one of the top 10 public health threats. Every prescription not finished, every antibiotic taken for a viral infection, every drop in livestock feed adds selection pressure."}},{"k":"what_you_can_do","v":{"s":"What you can do: "}},{"k":"finish_prescriptions_even_if_you_feel_","v":{"s":"Finish prescriptions even if you feel better. Don't demand antibiotics for viral infections (they don't work on viruses). Don't share or save antibiotics. The selection pressure you create today shapes the resistance landscape your kids will inherit."}},{"k":"next_up_7","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_ve_seen_evolution_happening_in_day","v":{"s":"You've seen evolution happening in days. The Misconceptions Quiz tackles the deeper conceptual errors people still make about how all of this works."}},{"k":"trait_means_compatibility_over_time","v":{"s":"Trait Means + Compatibility Over Time"}},{"k":"left_mean","v":{"s":"Left mean"}},{"k":"right_mean","v":{"s":"Right mean"}},{"k":"compatibility","v":{"s":"Compatibility"}},{"k":"speciation_threshold_30","v":{"s":"speciation threshold (30%)"}},{"k":"speciation_simulator_2","v":{"s":"Speciation Simulator"}},{"k":"how_one_species_becomes_two","v":{"s":"How one species becomes two"}},{"k":"a_geographic_barrier_splits_an_ancestr","v":{"s":"A geographic barrier splits an ancestral population. Each half evolves under its own pressure. When trait distributions diverge enough that the two can no longer interbreed (compatibility < 30%), they're separate species. This is the textbook allopatric model behind Galápagos finches, Lake Victoria cichlids, and ring species."}},{"k":"generation_3","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"left_mean_2","v":{"s":"Left Mean"}},{"k":"right_mean_2","v":{"s":"Right Mean"}},{"k":"compatibility_2","v":{"s":"Compatibility"}},{"k":"left_side_ideal_trait","v":{"s":"Left side: ideal trait"}},{"k":"what_trait_value_selection_favors_on_t","v":{"s":"What trait value selection favors on the left (cool/wet) side. Move toward 0 to push left population away from right."}},{"k":"right_side_ideal_trait","v":{"s":"Right side: ideal trait"}},{"k":"what_trait_value_selection_favors_on_t_2","v":{"s":"What trait value selection favors on the right (hot/dry) side. The greater the gap from left ideal, the faster speciation."}},{"k":"selection_strength_both_sides","v":{"s":"Selection Strength (both sides)"}},{"k":"how_strongly_each_side_selects_toward_","v":{"s":"How strongly each side selects toward its ideal. With selection off, drift alone may still cause speciation in small populations — eventually."}},{"k":"mutation_size_2","v":{"s":"Mutation Size"}},{"k":"higher_mutation_faster_response_to_sel","v":{"s":"Higher mutation = faster response to selection AND more variance maintained = somewhat slower full speciation."}},{"k":"step_1_generation_3","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Generation"}},{"k":"step_10_4","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Step 10"}},{"k":"reset_3","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_6","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"default_fast_speciation","v":{"s":"Default fast divergence: "}},{"k":"left_ideal_0_3_right_0_7_selection_0_7","v":{"s":"Left ideal = 0.3, right = 0.7, selection = 0.7. Auto-run. Speciation usually triggers within 15-25 generations."}},{"k":"same_ideal_no_divergence","v":{"s":"Same ideal, no divergence: "}},{"k":"set_both_ideals_to_0_5_same_selection_","v":{"s":"Set BOTH ideals to 0.5 (same selection pressure on both sides). Auto-run for 50 generations — populations stay compatible. Speciation needs DIFFERENT pressures."}},{"k":"drift_only_speciation","v":{"s":"Drift-only divergence: "}},{"k":"set_selection_to_0_on_both_sides_auto_","v":{"s":"Set selection to 0 on both sides. Auto-run for 80+ generations. Even without selection, populations drift apart by chance — this is exactly how some island species formed."}},{"k":"reverse_a_near_speciation","v":{"s":"Reverse trait divergence: "}},{"k":"run_until_compatibility_40_set_both_id","v":{"s":"Run until compatibility ≈ 40%. Set BOTH ideals to 0.5 and resume. The populations partially merge back — but only if you stop in time. Past the threshold, divergence accumulates faster than mixing can reverse."}},{"k":"slow_vs_fast_speciation","v":{"s":"Slow vs fast divergence: "}},{"k":"compare_selection_1_0_strong_vs_0_3_we","v":{"s":"Compare selection = 1.0 (strong) vs 0.3 (weak). Stronger selection causes faster trait divergence and faster speciation."}},{"k":"real_world_speciation_events","v":{"s":"📖 Real-world speciation events"}},{"k":"gal_pagos_finches","v":{"s":"Galápagos finches: "}},{"k":"a_single_ancestral_finch_species_reach","v":{"s":"A single ancestral finch species reached the Galápagos ~2 million years ago. With ~14 islands offering different food sources, the population spread, and isolated subpopulations evolved into 14 distinct species — different beak shapes for different niches. This is adaptive radiation."}},{"k":"lake_victoria_cichlids","v":{"s":"Lake Victoria cichlids: "}},{"k":"500_species_of_cichlid_fish_in_a_singl","v":{"s":"~500 species of cichlid fish in a single lake. The lake nearly dried up ~14,000 years ago — the survivors radiated explosively as the lake refilled. One of the fastest documented speciation events."}},{"k":"ring_species","v":{"s":"Ring species: "}},{"k":"the_ensatina_salamander_complex_in_cal","v":{"s":"The Ensatina salamander complex in California — populations form a \"ring\" around the Central Valley. Adjacent populations interbreed. End populations meeting at the southern junction can't. They're mid-speciation, caught in the act."}},{"k":"maine_relevance","v":{"s":"Maine relevance: "}},{"k":"the_american_eel_and_european_eel_spli","v":{"s":"The American eel and European eel split ~3 million years ago when their range fragmented across the Atlantic. Both still spawn in the Sargasso Sea (a remnant of their shared ancestry) but are now distinct species — partial allopatric speciation in progress."}},{"k":"next_up_8","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_ve_seen_one_species_become_two_now","v":{"s":"You've seen one species become two. Now scale up: the Phylogenetic Tree Builder shows the full branching pattern of life — every species today descended from a chain of speciation events going back billions of years."}},{"k":"speed_coevolution_over_time","v":{"s":"📊 Speed Coevolution Over Time"}},{"k":"predator_speed","v":{"s":"Predator speed"}},{"k":"prey_speed","v":{"s":"Prey speed"}},{"k":"coevolution_lab_2","v":{"s":"Coevolution Lab"}},{"k":"the_red_queen_runs_faster_every_year","v":{"s":"The Red Queen runs faster every year"}},{"k":"now_here_you_see_it_takes_all_the_runn","v":{"s":"\"Now, here, you see, it takes all the running you can do, to keep in the same place.\" — Lewis Carroll, Through the Looking-Glass. Predators select prey to be faster; prey selects predators to be faster. Both populations escalate together — neither getting ahead. Real examples: cheetahs vs gazelles, bats vs moths, plants vs caterpillars."}},{"k":"generation_4","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"predator_speed_2","v":{"s":"Predator Speed"}},{"k":"prey_speed_2","v":{"s":"Prey Speed"}},{"k":"hunt_pressure","v":{"s":"Hunt Pressure"}},{"k":"how_effective_predators_are_at_convert","v":{"s":"How effective predators are at converting speed advantage into kills. Higher = stronger selection on both sides."}},{"k":"mutation_size_3","v":{"s":"Mutation Size"}},{"k":"how_much_offspring_speed_varies_from_p","v":{"s":"How much offspring speed varies from parent. Higher = faster adaptation but messier dynamics."}},{"k":"cost_of_speed","v":{"s":"Cost of Speed"}},{"k":"step_1_generation_4","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Generation"}},{"k":"step_10_5","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Step 10"}},{"k":"reset_4","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_7","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"watch_the_arms_race","v":{"s":"Watch the arms race: "}},{"k":"default_settings_auto_run_for_30_gener","v":{"s":"Default settings, Auto-Run for 30 generations. Both lines climb together — neither gets meaningfully ahead. The GAP stays small even though absolute speeds rise dramatically."}},{"k":"cost_off_runaway","v":{"s":"Cost OFF — runaway: "}},{"k":"disable_cost_of_speed_auto_run_for_50_","v":{"s":"Disable Cost of Speed. Auto-Run for 50 generations. Both speeds climb toward the simulation cap (1.0) and sit near it. In nature, real costs (energy, predation visibility, joint stress) prevent this, which is why cheetahs aren't infinitely fast."}},{"k":"hunt_pressure_crash","v":{"s":"Hunt pressure crash: "}},{"k":"reset_set_hunt_pressure_to_1_0_max_wit","v":{"s":"Reset, set Hunt Pressure to 1.0 (max). Within ~10 generations one side often crashes — usually prey, since they're selected harder. Population recovery takes many generations."}},{"k":"hunt_pressure_off","v":{"s":"Hunt pressure off: "}},{"k":"reset_set_hunt_pressure_to_0_2_low_bot","v":{"s":"Reset, set Hunt Pressure to 0.2 (low). Both populations stay at low speed — without selection pressure, there's no arms race."}},{"k":"high_mutation","v":{"s":"High mutation: "}},{"k":"set_mutation_size_to_0_12_high_the_dyn","v":{"s":"Set Mutation Size to 0.12 (high). The dynamics get noisier but the arms race speeds up. Real mutation rates are lower; here exaggerated to be visible on the timescale of a classroom session."}},{"k":"real_world_coevolution","v":{"s":"📖 Real-world coevolution"}},{"k":"bats_vs_moths","v":{"s":"Bats vs moths: "}},{"k":"bats_evolved_echolocation_moths_evolve","v":{"s":"Bats evolved echolocation. Moths evolved ears tuned to detect bat calls — and SOMETIMES, jamming clicks that interfere with bat sonar. Bats then evolved frequency-shifting calls to defeat moth jamming. Layer after layer of tit-for-tat innovation."}},{"k":"cheetahs_vs_gazelles","v":{"s":"Cheetahs vs gazelles: "}},{"k":"cheetahs_reach_60_mph_but_their_hunts_","v":{"s":"Cheetahs reach 60+ mph but their hunts succeed only ~50% of the time — and that's with a 30% chance of injury per chase. Gazelles aren't much slower in absolute terms; the race is close."}},{"k":"plants_vs_herbivores","v":{"s":"Plants vs herbivores: "}},{"k":"plants_evolved_chemical_defenses_caffe","v":{"s":"Plants evolved chemical defenses (caffeine, capsaicin, alkaloids). Some insects evolved enzymes to detoxify those defenses. Plants evolved BETTER toxins. Caterpillars evolved BETTER detox. This is happening right now in milkweed-monarch interactions."}},{"k":"maine_relevance_2","v":{"s":"Maine relevance: "}},{"k":"white_tailed_deer_browse_pressure_on_m","v":{"s":"White-tailed deer browse pressure on Maine forests has driven plants like striped maple to invest more in chemical defenses (high tannins). The deer compensate by being less selective. Deer-tick coevolution is also active: ticks evolved to detect host CO2 plumes; deer evolved more vigorous grooming."}},{"k":"next_up_9","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_just_saw_two_species_evolving_agai","v":{"s":"You just saw two species evolving against each other. The Antibiotic Resistance Lab shows the SAME dynamic on a much faster timescale: bacteria vs antibiotics is just predator-prey at the molecular level."}},{"k":"carl_linnaeus","v":{"s":"Carl Linnaeus"}},{"k":"jean_baptiste_lamarck","v":{"s":"Jean-Baptiste Lamarck"}},{"k":"charles_darwin_alfred_russel_wallace","v":{"s":"Charles Darwin & Alfred Russel Wallace"}},{"k":"gregor_mendel","v":{"s":"Gregor Mendel"}},{"k":"august_weismann","v":{"s":"August Weismann"}},{"k":"de_vries_correns_tschermak","v":{"s":"de Vries · Correns · Tschermak"}},{"k":"fisher_haldane_wright","v":{"s":"Fisher · Haldane · Wright"}},{"k":"dobzhansky_mayr_simpson_stebbins","v":{"s":"Dobzhansky · Mayr · Simpson · Stebbins"}},{"k":"barbara_mcclintock","v":{"s":"Barbara McClintock"}},{"k":"franklin_wilkins_watson_crick","v":{"s":"Franklin · Wilkins · Watson · Crick"}},{"k":"motoo_kimura","v":{"s":"Motoo Kimura"}},{"k":"lynn_margulis","v":{"s":"Lynn Margulis"}},{"k":"eldredge_gould","v":{"s":"Eldredge & Gould"}},{"k":"human_genome_project","v":{"s":"Human Genome Project"}},{"k":"today","v":{"s":"Today"}},{"k":"foundations","v":{"s":"Foundations"}},{"k":"genetics","v":{"s":"Genetics"}},{"k":"synthesis","v":{"s":"Synthesis"}},{"k":"molecular","v":{"s":"Molecular"}},{"k":"modern","v":{"s":"Modern"}},{"k":"discovery_timeline_2","v":{"s":"Discovery Timeline"}},{"k":"how_we_learned_what_we_know_2","v":{"s":"How we learned what we know"}},{"k":"290_years_of_figuring_out_evolution_da","v":{"s":"290 years of figuring out evolution. Darwin gets the textbook spotlight, but he didn't do this alone — and many key figures (Wallace, Mendel, Margulis, McClintock, Franklin) were ignored or pushed aside in their time. Click any card to read more."}},{"k":"why_this_matters_2","v":{"s":"📚 Why this matters"}},{"k":"patterns_to_notice","v":{"s":"🤔 Patterns to notice"}},{"k":"multiple_discovery","v":{"s":"Multiple discovery: "}},{"k":"major_ideas_often_emerge_from_multiple","v":{"s":"Major ideas often emerge from multiple people simultaneously (Darwin & Wallace 1858, three rediscoverers of Mendel in 1900, Fisher/Haldane/Wright in the 1920s). When an idea's time comes, the necessary tools and questions are usually shared."}},{"k":"long_delays","v":{"s":"Long delays: "}},{"k":"mendel_waited_35_years_for_recognition","v":{"s":"Mendel waited 35 years for recognition. McClintock waited 35 years for the Nobel. Margulis was rejected 15 times. Franklin died before her contribution was acknowledged. Being right early is hard."}},{"k":"resistance_to_new_ideas","v":{"s":"Resistance to new ideas: "}},{"k":"each_major_step_darwin_s_natural_selec","v":{"s":"Each major step (Darwin's natural selection, Mendel's discrete inheritance, Margulis's endosymbiosis, Eldredge & Gould's punctuated equilibrium) was met with strong opposition before being accepted. Healthy skepticism is part of how science self-corrects — but it can also slow down ideas that turn out to be right."}},{"k":"underrepresented_contributions","v":{"s":"Underrepresented contributions: "}},{"k":"wallace_co_discovered_selection_but_da","v":{"s":"Wallace co-discovered selection but Darwin gets the credit. Franklin's data unlocked DNA but Watson and Crick got the Nobel. Margulis and McClintock did paradigm-shifting work despite being marginalized. The textbook story tends to compress messy history into \"hero scientists\" — the messier reality is more interesting and more honest."}},{"k":"next_up_10","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_ve_seen_the_people_who_built_the_t","v":{"s":"You've seen the people who built the theory. The Misconceptions Quiz tests how well the IDEAS landed — including ones the textbook story sometimes obscures."}},{"k":"temperature_tolerance_population","v":{"s":"Temperature, Tolerance, & Population"}},{"k":"temp","v":{"s":"Temp"}},{"k":"tolerance_mean","v":{"s":"Tolerance mean"}},{"k":"pop_size","v":{"s":"Pop size"}},{"k":"temperature_mean_tolerance_and_populat","v":{"s":"Temperature, mean tolerance, and population size over generations."}},{"k":"when_temperature_red_and_tolerance_mea","v":{"s":"When temperature (red) and tolerance mean (blue) move together, the population is adapting. When they DIVERGE, the population is in trouble."}},{"k":"climate_pressure_lab_2","v":{"s":"Climate Pressure Lab"}},{"k":"you_are_the_climate_2","v":{"s":"You ARE the climate"}},{"k":"drag_the_temperature_slider_each_gener","v":{"s":"Drag the temperature slider, or set a warming RATE and run a trial. Each generation, creatures whose tolerance trait matches the current temperature survive better. If temperature changes SLOWLY, the population can adapt by selection. If it changes too FAST, they go extinct. The lesson: the RATE of climate change matters more than the magnitude, and here the rate is a number you can report."}},{"k":"generation_5","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"population_2","v":{"s":"Population"}},{"k":"tolerance_lag","v":{"s":"Tolerance Lag"}},{"k":"stressed","v":{"s":"Stressed"}},{"k":"temperature_drag_this","v":{"s":"🌡️ Temperature (drag this!)"}},{"k":"slide_slowly_while_the_simulation_runs","v":{"s":"Slide slowly while the simulation runs to let the population catch up. Slide fast to force extinction."}},{"k":"mutation_size_offspring_trait_variatio","v":{"s":"Mutation Size (offspring trait variation)"}},{"k":"higher_mutation_rate_faster_adaptation","v":{"s":"Higher mutation rate = faster adaptation. Population can survive faster temperature changes when offspring vary more from parents."}},{"k":"step_1_generation_5","v":{"s":"⏭ Step 1 Generation"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_8","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"slow_gradual_warming_adaptation","v":{"s":"Slow gradual warming (adaptation): "}},{"k":"reset_auto_run_then_move_temp_slider_f","v":{"s":"Set the warming rate to 0.010 or less and run a trial. The mean tolerance tracks the temperature line, lagging slightly. The population survives: it ADAPTED."}},{"k":"rapid_temperature_jump_extinction","v":{"s":"Rapid temperature change (extinction): "}},{"k":"reset_pause_auto_run_drag_temp_from_0_","v":{"s":"Set the rate to 0.050 and run a trial, or drag the slider from 0.5 to 0.95 in one move and Auto-Run. Within a few generations the population crashes. Extinction."}},{"k":"find_the_survivable_rate","v":{"s":"Find the survivable rate: "}},{"k":"reset_try_moving_the_slider_at_differe","v":{"s":"Run trials at several rates until you have one that survived and one that went extinct. The boundary between them is this population's \"adaptive capacity\", and the Best rate survived card keeps your record."}},{"k":"mutation_matters_2","v":{"s":"Mutation matters: "}},{"k":"reset_set_mutation_size_to_0_10_high_t","v":{"s":"Set Mutation Size to 0.10 and rerun a rate that went extinct at σ = 0.02 (0.040 or faster). The population now survives almost every time: more variation gives selection more to work with each generation."}},{"k":"compare_with_predator_vision","v":{"s":"Compare with Predator Vision: "}},{"k":"both_labs_put_you_in_control_of_select","v":{"s":"Both labs put YOU in control of selection. Predator Vision: you applied a static pressure. Climate Pressure: you apply a CHANGING pressure. The dynamic introduces extinction as a possibility — selection pressure can outpace adaptation."}},{"k":"this_is_happening_right_now","v":{"s":"📖 This is happening right now"}},{"k":"pace_matters","v":{"s":"Pace matters: "}},{"k":"earth_s_temperature_is_currently_risin","v":{"s":"Earth's temperature is currently rising about 0.2°C per decade — about 10x faster than typical post-glacial warming. Many species can adapt to climate change, but many can't adapt this fast."}},{"k":"maine_examples","v":{"s":"Maine examples: "}},{"k":"the_gulf_of_maine_is_warming_faster_th","v":{"s":"The Gulf of Maine is warming faster than 99% of the world's oceans. American lobsters are migrating north (lobster industry shifting from Maine toward Canada). Maine moose are stressed by tick infestations made worse by warmer winters. Iconic Maine cod populations have collapsed partly from temperature shifts."}},{"k":"mass_extinctions","v":{"s":"Mass extinctions: "}},{"k":"five_of_the_six_major_mass_extinctions","v":{"s":"Five of the six major mass extinctions in Earth's history involved rapid climate change. The current \"Holocene/Anthropocene extinction\" is happening 100-1000x faster than the natural background rate. The driver is the same: rate of environmental change exceeding adaptive capacity."}},{"k":"generation_time_matters","v":{"s":"Generation time matters: "}},{"k":"bacteria_reproduce_in_minutes_they_ada","v":{"s":"Bacteria reproduce in minutes — they adapt to climate shifts essentially instantly. Whales reproduce every few years — they may not be able to keep up with century-scale change. Most large mammals and birds are in this \"too slow to adapt\" category."}},{"k":"next_up_11","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_ve_been_the_predator_camouflage_th","v":{"s":"You've been the predator (camouflage), the climate (extinction). Be the mate-chooser next — the third major selection pressure that drives evolution. Mate Choice Lab shows runaway sexual selection."}},{"k":"showiness_over_rounds","v":{"s":"Showiness Over Rounds"}},{"k":"mean","v":{"s":"Mean"}},{"k":"mean_and_max_showiness_over_rounds","v":{"s":"Mean and max showiness over rounds."}},{"k":"round_0","v":{"s":"round 0"}},{"k":"mate_choice_lab_2","v":{"s":"Mate Choice Lab"}},{"k":"you_pick_the_mate","v":{"s":"You pick the mate"}},{"k":"each_round_you_see_12_male_birds_with_","v":{"s":"Each round you see 12 male birds with varying tail showiness. You pick one to be the parent of the next generation. The others don't reproduce. Watch how YOUR choices drive the population. Without a predator cost, showiness escalates indefinitely (the famous \"runaway sexual selection\" model). With a cost, it stabilizes — like real peacocks who have to balance attractiveness against being eaten."}},{"k":"round","v":{"s":"Round"}},{"k":"mean_showiness","v":{"s":"Mean Showiness"}},{"k":"max_showiness","v":{"s":"Max Showiness"}},{"k":"cost","v":{"s":"Cost"}},{"k":"predator_cost","v":{"s":"🦅 Predator Cost"}},{"k":"start_choosing","v":{"s":"▶ Start Choosing"}},{"k":"your_choices_shaped_the_population","v":{"s":"Your choices shaped the population"}},{"k":"run_again","v":{"s":"↻ Run Again"}},{"k":"end_selection","v":{"s":"⏹️ End Selection"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"↺ Restart"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_9","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"runaway_selection","v":{"s":"Runaway selection: "}},{"k":"with_predator_cost_off_always_pick_the","v":{"s":"With Predator Cost OFF, pick the showiest bird for 12 rounds, then Fast-forward. The population climbs to and stays near maximum showiness. Nothing pushes back."}},{"k":"stabilizing_balance","v":{"s":"Stabilizing balance: "}},{"k":"restart_with_predator_cost_on_pick_the","v":{"s":"Restart with Predator Cost ON and leave it on. Pick the showiest surviving bird each round, then Fast-forward past round 20. The trait rises but settles around 0.6-0.7, because males above ~0.8 keep getting taken before they can mate. Real-world peacocks live here."}},{"k":"pick_something_different","v":{"s":"Pick something different: "}},{"k":"try_picking_the_least_showy_bird_each_","v":{"s":"Try picking the LEAST showy bird each round. Your quarter of the chicks pulls the average down, but the other females keep choosing showy fathers. Set choosiness to 0.9 and try again: now the population fights you."}},{"k":"mix_strategies","v":{"s":"Mix strategies: "}},{"k":"pick_the_showiest_for_3_rounds_then_sw","v":{"s":"Pick the showiest for 3 rounds, then switch to picking modestly showy birds. The trait dips, but the other females' preference keeps pushing it back up. Selection has memory, and here the memory is in the preference gene."}},{"k":"compare_with_predator_vision_2","v":{"s":"Compare with Predator Vision: "}},{"k":"in_predator_vision_lab_your_selection_","v":{"s":"In Predator Vision Lab, your selection drove camouflage (cryptic, hard-to-see). Here, it drives showiness (conspicuous, easy-to-see). Same MECHANISM (heritable variation + selection), opposite DIRECTION."}},{"k":"real_biology","v":{"s":"📖 Real biology"}},{"k":"male_peacocks_have_absurd_tail_trains_","v":{"s":"Male peacocks have absurd tail trains — 5 feet long, with up to 200 eyespots. The tail makes flying harder and attracts predators. Why do they have it? Female peahens prefer it. The tail evolved because females consistently picked the showiest males for thousands of generations."}},{"k":"birds_of_paradise","v":{"s":"Birds of paradise: "}},{"k":"40_species_in_new_guinea_all_descended","v":{"s":"~40 species in New Guinea, all descended from a crow-like ancestor. Each species has a different bizarre display: spinning ribbons, head shaking, capes that flip up, dance moves. All driven by female mate preference operating in isolation on different islands."}},{"k":"trinidad_guppies_have_vivid_spots_in_s","v":{"s":"Trinidad guppies have vivid spots in safe streams (mate choice wins) but dull spots in predator-rich streams (natural selection wins). Same species, different equilibria — exactly the dynamic the predator cost toggle models."}},{"k":"why_this_matters_3","v":{"s":"Why this matters: "}},{"k":"sexual_selection_is_one_of_the_major_e","v":{"s":"Sexual selection is one of the major engines of evolution. It drives speciation, ornamentation, song, courtship dances. It also explains why some traits seem maladaptive — they're not optimizing survival; they're optimizing reproduction."}},{"k":"next_up_12","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_ve_seen_sexual_selection_within_a_","v":{"s":"You've seen sexual selection within a population. The Coevolution Lab shows what happens when TWO populations evolve in response to each other — natural selection's answer to sexual selection."}},{"k":"forest_floor","v":{"s":"🌲 Forest Floor"}},{"k":"mottled_greens_and_browns_mid_range_tr","v":{"s":"Mottled greens and browns. Mid-range trait values blend in best."}},{"k":"sandy_beach","v":{"s":"🏖️ Sandy Beach"}},{"k":"light_tan_and_beige_pale_trait_values_","v":{"s":"Light tan and beige. Pale trait values disappear into the sand."}},{"k":"snowy_field","v":{"s":"❄️ Snowy Field"}},{"k":"white_on_white_whitest_creatures_are_n","v":{"s":"White on white. Whitest creatures are nearly invisible."}},{"k":"urban_concrete","v":{"s":"🏙️ Urban Concrete"}},{"k":"mid_grey_concrete_and_asphalt_mid_dark","v":{"s":"Mid-grey concrete and asphalt. Mid-dark traits blend best."}},{"k":"mean_trait_over_rounds","v":{"s":"📊 Mean Trait Over Rounds"}},{"k":"camouflage_ideal","v":{"s":"camouflage ideal"}},{"k":"round_0_2","v":{"s":"round 0"}},{"k":"time_to_eat_10_per_round","v":{"s":"⏱️ Time to Eat 10 (Per Round)"}},{"k":"bars_get_taller_slower_as_the_prey_bec","v":{"s":"Bars get TALLER (slower) as the prey become better camouflaged."}},{"k":"predator_vision_lab","v":{"s":"Predator Vision Lab"}},{"k":"click_prey_to_eat_them_each_round_the_","v":{"s":"Click prey to \"eat\" them. Each round, the survivors reproduce — and each generation looks slightly more like the background. By round 10, the prey will be almost invisible. The camouflage you're watching emerge is YOUR doing — you applied the selection pressure that produced it."}},{"k":"round_2","v":{"s":"Round"}},{"k":"eaten_this_round","v":{"s":"Eaten This Round"}},{"k":"total_hunted","v":{"s":"Total Hunted"}},{"k":"camouflage","v":{"s":"Camouflage"}},{"k":"round_times_will_appear_after_round_1","v":{"s":"⏱️ Round times will appear after Round 1."}},{"k":"choose_environment","v":{"s":"Choose Environment"}},{"k":"start_hunting","v":{"s":"▶ Start Hunting"}},{"k":"end_hunt","v":{"s":"⏹️ End Hunt"}},{"k":"restart_2","v":{"s":"↺ Restart"}},{"k":"hunt_complete","v":{"s":"Hunt complete"}},{"k":"hunt_again","v":{"s":"↻ Hunt Again"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_10","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"race_against_your_own_evolution","v":{"s":"Race against your own evolution: "}},{"k":"note_your_time_to_eat_10_in_round_1_co","v":{"s":"Note your time to eat 10 in Round 1. Compare to your time in Round 10. You'll be SLOWER even though you're more practiced — because the prey are evolving to evade YOU."}},{"k":"switch_environments_mid_evolution","v":{"s":"Switch environments mid-evolution: "}},{"k":"after_5_rounds_in_forest_switch_to_sno","v":{"s":"After 5 rounds in Forest, switch to Snow while the hunt is still running. The prey keep the colours they evolved for leaves, so they now stand out against snow and the camouflage score drops. They have to evolve again, from where they are."}},{"k":"hunt_strategically_vs_randomly","v":{"s":"Hunt strategically vs randomly: "}},{"k":"in_round_1_click_only_the_brightest_pr","v":{"s":"Run one hunt clicking only the most VISIBLE prey and save it as Run A; then hunt again clicking whatever is nearest your cursor and save it as Run B. The comparison table shows the camouflage gain of each. Selective hunting produces faster camouflage evolution."}},{"k":"compare_with_a_partner","v":{"s":"Compare with a partner: "}},{"k":"two_students_one_screen_take_turns_hun","v":{"s":"Two students, one screen — take turns hunting one round each. Notice how each person's \"predator profile\" subtly differs and shapes the prey differently."}},{"k":"check_the_chart","v":{"s":"Check the chart: "}},{"k":"the_red_line_on_the_trait_chart_approa","v":{"s":"The red line on the trait chart approaches the green dashed \"camouflage ideal\" line. That's natural selection in action — the population converging on the trait you're NOT selecting against."}},{"k":"this_is_real_biology","v":{"s":"📖 This is real biology"}},{"k":"peppered_moths_biston_betularia","v":{"s":"Peppered moths (Biston betularia): "}},{"k":"before_the_industrial_revolution_most_","v":{"s":"Before the Industrial Revolution, most peppered moths were light-colored and matched lichen-covered tree bark. Pollution killed lichen and blackened trees with soot. Within 50 years, dark moths dominated — bird predators selected against light moths against dark trees, exactly like you just selected against high-contrast prey."}},{"k":"snowshoe_hares","v":{"s":"Snowshoe hares: "}},{"k":"in_maine_where_you_live_snowshoe_hares","v":{"s":"In Maine, where you live, snowshoe hares molt to white in winter. With climate change shortening winters, hares that molt EARLY are now mismatched against bare ground — and predators (lynx, foxes, owls) eat them at higher rates. Real selection pressure happening right now."}},{"k":"cuttlefish_on_demand","v":{"s":"Cuttlefish on demand: "}},{"k":"cuttlefish_change_their_skin_color_in_","v":{"s":"Cuttlefish change their skin color in seconds to match their background — a non-evolutionary form of camouflage that they can do because of specialized cells. Compare with what you saw: peppered moths and hares need MULTIPLE GENERATIONS to camouflage; cuttlefish do it in seconds."}},{"k":"you_as_a_predator","v":{"s":"You as a predator: "}},{"k":"you_aren_t_a_natural_predator_for_thes","v":{"s":"You aren't a \"natural\" predator for these creatures, but the dynamic is identical. ANY consistent selection pressure (predators, weather, mate choice, food sources) drives populations to evolve. The pressure doesn't have to be intentional."}},{"k":"next_up_13","v":{"s":"Next up: "}},{"k":"you_just_felt_selection_happening_the_","v":{"s":"You just felt selection happening. The Selection Sandbox lets you adjust selection pressure as a slider rather than as your own clicks — see how the same dynamic plays out under different settings."}},{"k":"natural_directional","v":{"s":"Natural — directional"}},{"k":"natural_stabilizing","v":{"s":"Natural — stabilizing"}},{"k":"natural_disruptive","v":{"s":"Natural — disruptive"}},{"k":"sexual_selection","v":{"s":"Sexual selection"}},{"k":"artificial_selection","v":{"s":"Artificial selection"}},{"k":"genetic_drift_2","v":{"s":"Genetic drift"}},{"k":"back_to_evolab_menu_2","v":{"s":"← Back to EvoLab menu"}},{"k":"selection_sleuth_2","v":{"s":"🕵️ Selection Sleuth"}},{"k":"10_vignettes_for_each_identify_which_o","v":{"s":"10 vignettes. For each, identify which of six mechanisms is driving the evolutionary change. After picking, a coaching block names what makes this mechanism more likely than the others (and what would have to be different to make a different mechanism the right answer)."}},{"k":"the_six_mechanisms","v":{"s":"The six mechanisms"}},{"k":"start_vignette_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start — vignette 1 of 10"}},{"k":"back_to_evolab_menu_3","v":{"s":"← Back to EvoLab menu"}},{"k":"selection_sleuth_3","v":{"s":"🕵️ Selection Sleuth"}},{"k":"vignette","v":{"s":"Vignette "}},{"k":"score_3","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_mechanism","v":{"s":"Pick the mechanism"}},{"k":"all_10_vignettes_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 vignettes complete"}},{"k":"restart_3","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_vignette","v":{"s":"➡️ Next vignette"}},{"k":"homologous","v":{"s":"Homologous"}},{"k":"analogous","v":{"s":"Analogous"}},{"k":"back_to_evolab_menu_4","v":{"s":"← Back to EvoLab menu"}},{"k":"homology_vs_analogy_2","v":{"s":"🦴 Homology vs Analogy"}},{"k":"10_pairs_of_structures_for_each_decide","v":{"s":"10 pairs of structures. For each, decide: HOMOLOGOUS (shared evolutionary origin — same ancestral structure even if function differs) or ANALOGOUS (same function but independently evolved — convergent evolution). The single most important inferential reflex for reading evolutionary trees."}},{"k":"the_two_types","v":{"s":"The two types"}},{"k":"start_pair_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🦴 Start — pair 1 of 10"}},{"k":"back_to_evolab_menu_5","v":{"s":"← Back to EvoLab menu"}},{"k":"homology_vs_analogy_3","v":{"s":"🦴 Homology vs Analogy"}},{"k":"pair","v":{"s":"Pair "}},{"k":"score_4","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy_2","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak_2","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best_2","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_relationship_type","v":{"s":"Pick the relationship type"}},{"k":"all_10_pairs_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 pairs complete"}},{"k":"restart_4","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_pair","v":{"s":"➡️ Next pair"}},{"k":"pesticide_resistance_in_a_wheat_field","v":{"s":"Pesticide Resistance in a Wheat Field"}},{"k":"the_stranded_island_population","v":{"s":"The Stranded Island Population"}},{"k":"climate_change_in_maine","v":{"s":"Climate Change in Maine"}},{"k":"the_invasive_species_arms_race","v":{"s":"The Invasive Species Arms Race"}},{"k":"design_your_own_scenario","v":{"s":"Design Your Own Scenario"}},{"k":"step_1_pick_your_scenario","v":{"s":"Step 1: Pick your scenario"}},{"k":"each_scenario_is_a_real_world_evolutio","v":{"s":"Each scenario is a real-world evolutionary problem. Pick one that interests you. The Custom option is open-ended."}},{"k":"your_name_optional_for_the_report","v":{"s":"Your Name (optional, for the report)"}},{"k":"enter_your_name","v":{"s":"Enter your name…"}},{"k":"student_name_for_the_lab_report","v":{"s":"Student name for the lab report"}},{"k":"step_2_predict_the_outcome","v":{"s":"Step 2: Predict the outcome"}},{"k":"your_scenario","v":{"s":"🎯 Your scenario"}},{"k":"before_you_run_any_simulation_write_do","v":{"s":"Before you run any simulation: write down your predictions. Be specific. Use complete sentences. Aim for at least 2-3 sentences per prompt."}},{"k":"type_your_prediction","v":{"s":"Type your prediction…"}},{"k":"step_3_run_the_simulation","v":{"s":"Step 3: Run the simulation"}},{"k":"recommended_module","v":{"s":"🧪 Recommended module"}},{"k":"what_to_record","v":{"s":"📓 What to record"}},{"k":"the_starting_state_of_the_simulation_p","v":{"s":"The starting state of the simulation (population size, trait distribution)."}},{"k":"any_settings_you_adjusted_selection_st","v":{"s":"Any settings you adjusted (selection strength, mutation rate, etc.)."}},{"k":"the_state_after_running_for_the_recomm","v":{"s":"The state after running for the recommended number of generations."}},{"k":"any_patterns_or_surprises_you_noticed","v":{"s":"Any patterns or surprises you noticed."}},{"k":"take_screenshots_write_notes_you_ll_us","v":{"s":"Take screenshots, write notes — you'll use them in the next step."}},{"k":"i_ve_run_the_simulation_advance_to_the","v":{"s":"I've run the simulation; advance to the reflection step"}},{"k":"i_ve_run_it_continue","v":{"s":"✓ I've run it — continue"}},{"k":"step_4_reflect_on_your_findings","v":{"s":"Step 4: Evaluate the evidence"}},{"k":"compare_what_you_observed_to_what_you_","v":{"s":"Compare what you observed to what you predicted. Be honest if your prediction was wrong — that's how you learn."}},{"k":"type_your_reflection","v":{"s":"Type your reflection…"}},{"k":"capstone_complete_print_this_page_to_s","v":{"s":"Capstone complete! Print this page to submit your report."}},{"k":"evolab_capstone_project_lab_report","v":{"s":"🧬 EvoLab Capstone Project — Lab Report"}},{"k":"module_used","v":{"s":"Module used: "}},{"k":"1_problem_statement","v":{"s":"1. Problem Statement"}},{"k":"2_predictions_made_before_running_the_","v":{"s":"2. Predictions (made BEFORE running the sim)"}},{"k":"no_answer_recorded","v":{"s":"(no answer recorded)"}},{"k":"3_findings_reflection_after_running_th","v":{"s":"3. Findings & Reflection (after running the sim)"}},{"k":"no_answer_recorded_2","v":{"s":"(no answer recorded)"}},{"k":"generated_by_evolab_capstone_project_u","v":{"s":"Generated by EvoLab Capstone Project · Use the print button below to save or submit this report."}},{"k":"print_this_report","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print This Report"}},{"k":"start_a_new_project","v":{"s":"↻ Start a New Project"}},{"k":"capstone_project_2","v":{"s":"Capstone Project"}},{"k":"design_predict_run_reflect","v":{"s":"Predict, test, explain"}},{"k":"a_4_step_research_project_pick_a_real_","v":{"s":"A 5-step investigation. Pick a real-world scenario, predict what will happen, run the matching simulation, record evidence, and explain what the result means. The final step generates a print-ready lab report. Tip: a prediction that is not supported can teach you the most."}},{"k":"progress","v":{"s":"Progress"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"← Back"}},{"k":"allele_level_mechanics","v":{"s":"Allele-level mechanics"}},{"k":"how_allele_frequencies_change_the_math","v":{"s":"How allele frequencies change. The math under everything else."}},{"k":"genetic_drift_3","v":{"s":"Genetic Drift"}},{"k":"within_one_population","v":{"s":"Within one population"}},{"k":"how_a_single_species_changes_over_gene","v":{"s":"How survival, mate choice, and environmental change shift a population over generations."}},{"k":"selection_sandbox_3","v":{"s":"Selection Sandbox"}},{"k":"beak_lab","v":{"s":"Beak Lab"}},{"k":"antibiotic_resistance_2","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Resistance"}},{"k":"between_populations","v":{"s":"Between populations"}},{"k":"what_happens_when_isolated_populations","v":{"s":"What happens when isolated populations diverge, or two species coevolve."}},{"k":"speciation","v":{"s":"Speciation"}},{"k":"coevolution","v":{"s":"Coevolution"}},{"k":"across_all_of_life","v":{"s":"Across all of life"}},{"k":"macroevolutionary_patterns_common_ance","v":{"s":"Macroevolutionary patterns. Common ancestry. The tree of life."}},{"k":"phylo_tree_builder","v":{"s":"Phylo Tree Builder"}},{"k":"common_ancestry_2","v":{"s":"Common Ancestry"}},{"k":"context_cleanup","v":{"s":"Context, reasoning & synthesis"}},{"k":"history_of_the_science_and_the_most_co","v":{"s":"History, misconception repair, mechanism practice, and an evidence-based final investigation."}},{"k":"discovery_timeline_3","v":{"s":"Discovery Timeline"}},{"k":"misconceptions_quiz_2","v":{"s":"Misconceptions Quiz"}},{"k":"module_map_2","v":{"s":"Module Map"}},{"k":"how_the_modules_connect","v":{"s":"How the modules connect"}},{"k":"evolab_s_11_modules_are_organized_into","v":{"s":"EvoLab's 17 student modules are organized into 5 conceptual scales — from allele frequencies, to changing populations, to common ancestry and scientific synthesis. The recommended flow walks through every module, but you can jump anywhere anytime."}},{"k":"recommended_flow_the_next_up_path","v":{"s":"🧭 Recommended flow (the \"Next up\" path)"}},{"k":"you_don_t_have_to_follow_this_order_ea","v":{"s":"You don't have to follow this order. Each module is self-contained. But each module's ending suggestion points to the next one in this sequence."}},{"k":"quick_30_min","v":{"s":"🎯 Quick (30 min)"}},{"k":"for_introducing_evolution_in_one_perio","v":{"s":"For introducing evolution in one period:"}},{"k":"1_selection_sandbox_10_min","v":{"s":"1. 🧪 Selection Sandbox (10 min)"}},{"k":"2_beak_lab_10_min","v":{"s":"2. 🐦 Beak Lab (10 min)"}},{"k":"3_misconceptions_quiz_10_min","v":{"s":"3. ❓ Misconceptions Quiz (10 min)"}},{"k":"standard_5_days","v":{"s":"📚 Standard (5 days)"}},{"k":"see_the_5_day_curriculum_guide_walks_t","v":{"s":"See the 5-Day Curriculum Guide for a 10-module core sequence, with additional reasoning challenges and the Capstone available for extension or assessment."}},{"k":"open_curriculum_guide","v":{"s":"→ Open Curriculum Guide"}},{"k":"deep_dive_10_days","v":{"s":"🔬 Deep dive (10+ days)"}},{"k":"for_ap_biology_or_evolution_focused_el","v":{"s":"For AP Biology or evolution-focused electives:"}},{"k":"one_full_period_per_major_module","v":{"s":"· One full period per major module"}},{"k":"use_extension_activities_as_homework","v":{"s":"· Use Extension Activities as homework"}},{"k":"add_primary_literature_reading","v":{"s":"· Add primary literature reading"}},{"k":"capstone_original_research_proposal","v":{"s":"· Capstone: original research proposal"}},{"k":"communicate_scientific_information_abo","v":{"s":"Communicate scientific information about evidence for evolution"}},{"k":"construct_an_explanation_based_on_evid","v":{"s":"Construct an explanation based on evidence for natural selection"}},{"k":"apply_concepts_of_statistics_and_proba","v":{"s":"Apply concepts of statistics and probability to explain population frequency"}},{"k":"construct_an_explanation_for_how_natur","v":{"s":"Construct an explanation for how natural selection leads to adaptation"}},{"k":"evaluate_evidence_supporting_claims_ab","v":{"s":"Evaluate evidence supporting claims about distribution of traits"}},{"k":"create_or_revise_a_simulation_to_test_","v":{"s":"Create or revise a simulation to test a solution"}},{"k":"inheritance_and_gene_environment_effec","v":{"s":"Inheritance and gene-environment effects on traits"}},{"k":"apply_statistics_to_explain_trait_vari","v":{"s":"Apply statistics to explain trait variation"}},{"k":"design_solutions_for_human_activity_im","v":{"s":"Design solutions for human activity impacts on biodiversity"}},{"k":"analyze_and_interpret_patterns_in_the_","v":{"s":"Analyze and interpret patterns in the fossil record"}},{"k":"construct_explanations_from_anatomical","v":{"s":"Construct explanations from anatomical similarities"}},{"k":"nature_of_science_cross_cutting","v":{"s":"Nature of Science (cross-cutting)"}},{"k":"selection_sandbox_4","v":{"s":"Selection Sandbox"}},{"k":"beak_lab_2","v":{"s":"Beak Lab"}},{"k":"speciation_2","v":{"s":"Speciation"}},{"k":"coevolution_2","v":{"s":"Coevolution"}},{"k":"phylo_tree","v":{"s":"Phylo Tree"}},{"k":"genetic_drift_4","v":{"s":"Genetic Drift"}},{"k":"common_ancestry_3","v":{"s":"Common Ancestry"}},{"k":"antibiotic_lab","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Lab"}},{"k":"discovery_timeline_4","v":{"s":"Discovery Timeline"}},{"k":"misconceptions","v":{"s":"Misconceptions"}},{"k":"standards_crosswalk_2","v":{"s":"Standards Crosswalk"}},{"k":"ngss_standards_crosswalk","v":{"s":"NGSS Standards Crosswalk"}},{"k":"for_unit_planning_pick_a_next_generati","v":{"s":"For unit planning. Pick a Next Generation Science Standard you need to address; see which modules cover it. Each module has its own NGSS list inside its 🍎 Teacher Notes panel."}},{"k":"by_standard","v":{"s":"By Standard"}},{"k":"by_module","v":{"s":"By Module"}},{"k":"ap_biology_mapping","v":{"s":"🎓 AP Biology mapping"}},{"k":"big_idea_1_evolution","v":{"s":"Big Idea 1 (Evolution): "}},{"k":"most_modules_contribute_selection_sand","v":{"s":"Most modules contribute. Selection Sandbox, Beak Lab, Speciation, and Coevolution directly address Topics 1.A and 1.C. Hardy-Weinberg + Genetic Drift cover the quantitative work in Topic 1.B."}},{"k":"big_idea_2_cellular_processes","v":{"s":"Big Idea 2 (Cellular Processes): "}},{"k":"antibiotic_resistance_lab_connects_to_","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Resistance Lab connects to selection at the molecular level (Topic 2.A.4). Discovery Timeline gives historical context for Watson-Crick / Franklin / Margulis."}},{"k":"big_idea_3_genetics","v":{"s":"Big Idea 3 (Genetics): "}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_directly_addresses_topi","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg directly addresses Topic 3.C.1 (allele frequencies). Genetic Drift covers Topic 3.C.2 (random change). Phylo Tree Builder addresses Topic 3.D (information transfer between generations)."}},{"k":"big_idea_4_interactions","v":{"s":"Big Idea 4 (Interactions): "}},{"k":"coevolution_and_antibiotic_resistance_","v":{"s":"Coevolution and Antibiotic Resistance both fit Topic 4.B.3 (species interactions drive evolution)."}},{"k":"hardy_weinberg_calculator_2","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg Calculator"}},{"k":"genetic_drift_simulator_2","v":{"s":"Genetic Drift Simulator"}},{"k":"selection_sandbox_try_all_4_modes","v":{"s":"Selection Sandbox (try all 4 modes)"}},{"k":"gal_pagos_beak_lab_reproduce_1977_drou","v":{"s":"Galápagos Beak Lab (reproduce 1977 drought)"}},{"k":"speciation_simulator_3","v":{"s":"Speciation Simulator"}},{"k":"coevolution_lab_3","v":{"s":"Coevolution Lab"}},{"k":"phylogenetic_tree_builder_3","v":{"s":"Phylogenetic Tree Builder"}},{"k":"common_ancestry_viewer_2","v":{"s":"Common Ancestry Viewer"}},{"k":"antibiotic_resistance_lab_2","v":{"s":"Antibiotic Resistance Lab"}},{"k":"misconceptions_quiz_12_questions","v":{"s":"Misconceptions Quiz (12 questions)"}},{"k":"warm_up_5_min","v":{"s":"Warm-up (5 min)"}},{"k":"class_discussion_10_min","v":{"s":"Class discussion (10 min)"}},{"k":"exit_ticket_5_min","v":{"s":"Exit ticket (5 min)"}},{"k":"curriculum_guide_5_day_unit_plan","v":{"s":"Curriculum Guide — 5-Day Unit Plan"}},{"k":"drop_in_evolution_unit","v":{"s":"Drop-in evolution unit"}},{"k":"five_50_minute_periods_sequenced_from_","v":{"s":"Five 50-minute periods sequenced from \"what is an allele frequency\" to \"why does antibiotic resistance matter.\" Each day has a warm-up, lab activity, discussion, and exit ticket. Click any module link to launch it directly. Designed for high school biology; modular enough to compress to 3 days or expand to 8."}},{"k":"days","v":{"s":"Days"}},{"k":"lab_modules","v":{"s":"Lab Modules"}},{"k":"total_time","v":{"s":"Total Time"}},{"k":"print_this_curriculum_guide_for_offlin","v":{"s":"Print this curriculum guide for offline use"}},{"k":"print_this_guide","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print This Guide"}},{"k":"adapting_this_unit","v":{"s":"🧰 Adapting this unit"}},{"k":"compress_to_3_days","v":{"s":"Compress to 3 days: "}},{"k":"combine_days_1_2_foundation_selection_","v":{"s":"Combine Days 1+2 (foundation + selection in one period), keep Day 3 as-is, combine Days 4+5 (macro + application). Extend the discussion sections in each."}},{"k":"expand_to_8_10_days","v":{"s":"Expand to 8-10 days: "}},{"k":"spend_a_full_period_per_major_module_a","v":{"s":"Spend a full period per major module. Add a fossil-record day, an embryology / developmental-biology day, a human-evolution day. Use the \"Extension Activities\" in each module's Teacher Notes as homework."}},{"k":"middle_school_adaptation","v":{"s":"Middle school adaptation: "}},{"k":"skip_hardy_weinberg_math_heavy_replace","v":{"s":"Skip Hardy-Weinberg (math-heavy). Replace the misconceptions quiz with a teacher-led 5-question version. Spend more time on Common Ancestry and Phylo Tree (concrete, visual)."}},{"k":"ap_biology_extension","v":{"s":"AP Biology extension: "}},{"k":"each_module_s_teacher_notes_lists_ngss","v":{"s":"Each module's Teacher Notes lists NGSS standards. Cross-reference with AP Bio Unit 7 (Natural Selection). The Hardy-Weinberg + Genetic Drift labs map directly to AP's Big Idea 1."}},{"k":"open_each_module_before_class_to_verif","v":{"s":"Open each module before class to verify it loads correctly and to see what controls students will see. Each module has its own collapsible 🍎 Teacher Notes section with NGSS standards, common misconceptions, and extension activities."}},{"k":"prey_dominated","v":{"s":"🌿 Prey-dominated"}},{"k":"prey_camouflage_low_harshness_outpace_","v":{"s":"Prey camouflage + low harshness outpace predator vision. Prey population stable."}},{"k":"coevolutionary_balance","v":{"s":"⚖️ Coevolutionary balance"}},{"k":"both_pressures_roughly_equal_classic_r","v":{"s":"Both pressures roughly equal. Classic Red Queen arms race."}},{"k":"predator_dominated","v":{"s":"🦅 Predator-dominated"}},{"k":"predator_vision_environmental_stress_o","v":{"s":"Predator vision + environmental stress overwhelm prey defenses. Prey decline."}},{"k":"prey_camouflage","v":{"s":"Prey camouflage (%)"}},{"k":"predator_vision_2","v":{"s":"Predator vision (%)"}},{"k":"environment_harshness","v":{"s":"Environment harshness (%)"}},{"k":"hypothesis_free_text_when_does_the_env","v":{"s":"Hypothesis (free text): When does the environment tip the balance?"}},{"k":"explain_how_camouflage_predator_vision","v":{"s":"Explain how camouflage, predator vision, and environment co-determine population outcomes."}},{"k":"trait_divergence_model","v":{"s":"Trait Divergence Model"}},{"k":"explore_divergence_not_species_proof","v":{"s":"Explore divergence without treating one trait as proof of speciation"}},{"k":"trait_divergence_model_overview","v":{"s":"A geographic barrier separates two teaching populations and selection shifts one quantitative trait on each side. The overlap score is a visualization of trait divergence, not a measurement of reproductive isolation or proof of separate species."}},{"k":"hwe_expected_proportions_menu","v":{"s":"Calculate expected random-mating genotype proportions, then apply selection, one-way mutation, or migration to track how allele frequency p changes. Genetic drift is explored in its own lab."}},{"k":"drift_sampling_model_menu","v":{"s":"50/50 starting alleles, no selection — just random sampling. Smaller populations show larger fluctuations; larger populations usually stay closer to 50/50. Connects repeated drift to founder effects and bottlenecks."}},{"k":"antibiotic_exactly_as_prescribed","v":{"s":"Apply antibiotic pulses to a simplified bacterial population and observe selection on resistance. In real life, use antibiotics only when needed and take them exactly as prescribed."}},{"k":"hwe_expected_random_mating","v":{"s":"Under random mating, p², 2pq, and q² are the expected genotype proportions for the current allele frequencies."}},{"k":"hwe_track_allele_change","v":{"s":"Your job: apply a modeled force, track whether p changes, and watch the expected genotype proportions recalculate."}},{"k":"hwe_proportions_do_not_prove_stability","v":{"s":" gives expected genotype proportions after random mating. Matching those proportions at one time point does not by itself prove that allele frequencies are stable; testing evolution requires comparing allele frequencies across generations. The classic baseline assumes:"}},{"k":"one_way_mutation_model","v":{"s":": this simplified control models A → a only. Reverse mutation is not included."}},{"k":"selection_coefficient_relative_survival","v":{"s":"Relative reduction in aa survival compared with AA and Aa. 0 = equal survival in this model; 1 = no aa survivors."}},{"k":"one_way_mutation_rate_model_limit","v":{"s":"One-way A → a mutation probability per modeled generation. Real rates vary by organism and locus and are generally far below this deliberately exaggerated teaching range."}},{"k":"hwe_baseline_model_limit","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is an idealized baseline: random mating, a very large population, and no selection, mutation, or migration at the locus. The equation always sums to one algebraically; its biological use is predicting genotype proportions from p and q and testing whether allele frequencies remain constant across generations."}},{"k":"evolution_allele_frequency_change","v":{"s":"evolution at this scale means allele frequencies change across generations. This model shows that change directly in p; the bars remain Hardy-Weinberg expectations for each new p."}},{"k":"finite_population_drift_link","v":{"s":"The idealized Hardy-Weinberg baseline assumes a very large population. In finite populations, random sampling changes allele frequencies even without selection. The Genetic Drift lab explores that process."}},{"k":"long_term_drift_not_founder","v":{"s":"Set N=10 and generations=500. Watch lineages drift toward loss or fixation. This is repeated random sampling, not a founder event by itself."}},{"k":"drift_variance_scaling","v":{"s":"Run N=10 three times, then N=1000 three times. The N=10 outcomes vary widely; N=1000 trajectories usually fluctuate less. Per-generation sampling variance is p(1-p)/(2N), so typical fluctuation scales roughly with 1/√N."}},{"k":"antibiotic_model_limit","v":{"s":"Apply an antibiotic in this simplified model and susceptible cells are more likely to die while more-resistant survivors reproduce. The continuous resistance trait, mutation rate, growth, and timing are exaggerated for visibility; real resistance can also involve horizontal gene transfer and many drug-specific mechanisms."}},{"k":"antibiotic_dose_model_limit","v":{"s":"In this model, increasing dose lowers survival more strongly for susceptible cells. Real dose-response and resistance selection depend on the drug, organism, exposure, and prescribed regimen."}},{"k":"antibiotic_starting_resistance_limit","v":{"s":"Fraction of the initial modeled population above the resistance threshold. Outcomes vary stochastically; a small resistant minority can expand under selection but is not guaranteed to do so."}},{"k":"follow_prescribed_regimen","v":{"s":"Follow the prescribed regimen: "}},{"k":"interrupted_exposure_model_limit","v":{"s":"Pick the Complete course schedule and Auto-Run to tick 25; note the resistant share and population. Reset, pick Stopped early, repeat; then Pulsed. This compares interrupted exposure inside this model, not a universal treatment rule. Real patients should take antibiotics exactly as prescribed and ask their clinician before changing duration."}},{"k":"compare_dose_without_clinical_generalization","v":{"s":"Compare Dose = 0.4 with Dose = 1.0. In this model, stronger exposure often enriches resistant survivors faster, but it can also drive the small population extinct. Do not generalize this slider directly to clinical dosing."}},{"k":"mrsa_current_context","v":{"s":"Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus was identified soon after methicillin entered use and remains an important healthcare- and community-associated pathogen."}},{"k":"drug_resistant_tb_context","v":{"s":"Drug-resistant tuberculosis can be selected by inadequate or interrupted effective therapy and can also spread directly between people. TB treatment requires a disease-specific multidrug regimen taken exactly as directed."}},{"k":"amr_global_context","v":{"s":"Antimicrobial resistance causes substantial illness and death worldwide. Unnecessary or inappropriate antimicrobial use in people, animals, and agriculture adds selection pressure, while resistant organisms can also spread between hosts and environments."}},{"k":"antibiotic_exactly_as_directed_actions","v":{"s":"Use antibiotics only when prescribed, take them exactly as directed, and do not share or save them. Antibiotics do not treat viral infections. Ask the prescriber before changing or stopping a regimen."}},{"k":"trait_means_overlap_proxy_over_time","v":{"s":"Trait Means + Overlap Proxy Over Time"}},{"k":"trait_overlap_proxy","v":{"s":"Overlap proxy"}},{"k":"trait_divergence_milestone_30","v":{"s":"teaching milestone (30%)"}},{"k":"trait_divergence_model_2","v":{"s":"Trait Divergence Model"}},{"k":"from_separation_to_trait_divergence","v":{"s":"From separation to trait divergence"}},{"k":"trait_divergence_milestone_limit","v":{"s":"A geographic barrier splits an ancestral population. Each half evolves under its own pressure. This simplified model marks a teaching milestone when one trait's overlap proxy falls below 30%. Real speciation requires evidence of independently evolving lineages and reproductive isolation or other species criteria; this activity does not test mating."}},{"k":"trait_overlap_proxy_2","v":{"s":"Trait overlap proxy"}},{"k":"right_trait_optimum_divergence","v":{"s":"What trait value selection favors on the right (hot/dry) side. A larger gap usually produces faster divergence in the modeled trait."}},{"k":"selection_off_trait_drift_limit","v":{"s":"How strongly each side selects toward its ideal. With selection off, random sampling may still separate the modeled trait means. That alone does not establish speciation."}},{"k":"offspring_variation_model_limit","v":{"s":"Larger offspring variation can speed movement toward an optimum while also maintaining broader trait distributions. This slider is not a biological mutation-rate estimate."}},{"k":"default_trait_divergence_experiment","v":{"s":"Left ideal = 0.3, right = 0.7, selection = 0.7. Auto-run and note when the arbitrary overlap milestone is reached."}},{"k":"same_optimum_trait_experiment","v":{"s":"Set both ideals to 0.5. The trait means usually remain closer, although random sampling can still separate them."}},{"k":"drift_only_trait_divergence_limit","v":{"s":"Set selection to 0 on both sides. Auto-run for 80+ generations. Random sampling can separate trait means, but the model cannot show whether reproductive isolation evolves."}},{"k":"reverse_trait_divergence_no_mixing","v":{"s":"Run until the overlap proxy is about 40%, then set both ideals to 0.5 with gene flow still at 0. The means may converge independently, with nothing crossing the barrier; compare that with the Secondary contact experiment, where they mix."}},{"k":"selection_strength_trait_divergence","v":{"s":"Compare selection = 1.0 with 0.3. Stronger selection usually moves this modeled trait toward different optima faster; it does not by itself prove faster speciation."}},{"k":"hunt_most_visible_prey","v":{"s":"Hunt most-visible prey"}},{"k":"keyboard_hunt_model_note","v":{"s":"Keyboard mode removes the trait farthest from the camouflage optimum; it models the same selection direction without visual-search timing."}},{"k":"trait_divergence_model_3","v":{"s":"Trait Divergence Model"}},{"k":"pressure_model_prey_weighted_regime","v":{"s":"In this teaching rule, camouflage and lower harshness outweigh predator vision."}},{"k":"pressure_model_balanced_regime_limit","v":{"s":"The teaching scores are similar; real coevolution depends on inheritance, demography, behavior, and changing environments."}},{"k":"pressure_model_predator_weighted_regime","v":{"s":"In this teaching rule, predator vision and environmental harshness outweigh camouflage."}},{"k":"3_evidence_notebook","v":{"s":"3. Evidence Notebook"}},{"k":"4_findings_reflection_after_running_th","v":{"s":"4. Findings & Reflection (after running the sim)"}},{"k":"capstone_investigation","v":{"s":"Capstone investigation"}},{"k":"evidence_notebook","v":{"s":"📓 Interpretation notebook"}},{"k":"evidence_recorded_continue","v":{"s":"✓ Evidence recorded — continue"}},{"k":"in_progress_investigation","v":{"s":"In-progress Capstone investigation"}},{"k":"investigation_saved","v":{"s":"Investigation saved in this session"}},{"k":"no_answer_recorded_3","v":{"s":"(no answer recorded)"}},{"k":"record_numbers_not_just_impressions","v":{"s":"Captured values are filled automatically the first time. Add context, revise the interpretation, and note surprises or model limits in your own words."}},{"k":"record_required_evidence","v":{"s":"Record starting conditions + outcome"}},{"k":"resume_investigation","v":{"s":"Resume investigation →"}},{"k":"return_to_capstone_project","v":{"s":"Return to your in-progress Capstone Project"}},{"k":"return_to_project","v":{"s":"🎓 Return to project"}},{"k":"saved_during_lab_visits","v":{"s":"Saved during lab visits"}},{"k":"sr_module_completed","v":{"s":"Module completed: {value1}. {value2}"}},{"k":"sr_experiment_detected","v":{"s":"Experiment logged: {value1}"}},{"k":"read_aloud","v":{"s":"Read this aloud"}},{"k":"try_these_experiments_generic","v":{"s":"🧪 Try these experiments"}},{"k":"experiments_done","v":{"s":"done"}},{"k":"experiment_detected","v":{"s":"Detected by the simulator"}},{"k":"experiment_auto_detects","v":{"s":"The simulator will detect this one when you do it"}},{"k":"detected","v":{"s":"detected"}},{"k":"auto_detects","v":{"s":"auto-detects"}},{"k":"sr_prediction_saved","v":{"s":"Prediction saved: {value1}"}},{"k":"you_predicted","v":{"s":"You predicted: "}},{"k":"now_run_it_and_see","v":{"s":"Now run the simulation and see whether the population agrees with you."}},{"k":"change_prediction","v":{"s":"Change prediction"}},{"k":"prediction_held","v":{"s":"✓ Your prediction held"}},{"k":"prediction_did_not_hold","v":{"s":"↺ The population disagreed with your prediction"}},{"k":"predicted","v":{"s":"Predicted: "}},{"k":"observed","v":{"s":"Observed: "}},{"k":"predict_again","v":{"s":"Predict again for the next run"}},{"k":"predict_first","v":{"s":"🔮 Predict first"}},{"k":"check_yourself","v":{"s":"🧠 Check yourself"}},{"k":"sr_check_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"sr_check_not_yet","v":{"s":"Not quite. Read the feedback and try another option."}},{"k":"check_correct_heading","v":{"s":"✓ Right, and here is why"}},{"k":"check_wrong_heading","v":{"s":"✗ Why this feels right, and why it is not"}},{"k":"try_another_option","v":{"s":"Try another option"}},{"k":"check_hw_q","v":{"s":"With selection, mutation, and migration all at 0, p did not change at all. Why?"}},{"k":"check_hw_dominant","v":{"s":"The dominant allele had already taken over, so there was nothing left to change."}},{"k":"check_hw_dominant_fb","v":{"s":"Dominance is about which phenotype a heterozygote shows, not about which allele spreads. A dominant allele at p = 0.1 stays at p = 0.1 under these conditions. Dominance does not push allele frequencies anywhere by itself."}},{"k":"check_hw_nothing","v":{"s":"Nothing changed who reproduced, and this calculator assumes a very large population, so there was no drift either."}},{"k":"check_hw_nothing_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Hardy-Weinberg is the null model: no force, no change. It is useful precisely because any change you DO see in a real population points at a cause (selection, mutation, migration, or, in small populations, drift, which this infinite-population calculator leaves to the Genetic Drift lab)."}},{"k":"check_hw_hetero","v":{"s":"Heterozygotes are fitter, so p is held at its balance point."}},{"k":"check_hw_hetero_fb","v":{"s":"Heterozygote advantage would be a form of selection, and selection was set to 0. Also, that would hold p at one particular value; here p stayed wherever you set it, which is the signature of no force at all."}},{"k":"check_hw_random","v":{"s":"Random mating stops evolution."}},{"k":"check_hw_random_fb","v":{"s":"Random mating keeps genotype proportions at p², 2pq, q² for whatever p is, but it does not stop p from changing: add selection and p moves every generation even though mating stays random."}},{"k":"check_drift_q","v":{"s":"With N = 10, allele A was lost in some lineages and fixed in others, with NO selection. What does that show?"}},{"k":"check_drift_harmful","v":{"s":"A must have been harmful in the lineages where it was lost."}},{"k":"check_drift_harmful_fb","v":{"s":"Every lineage used identical rules and A had no effect on survival anywhere. The same allele fixed in one lineage and vanished in another purely because of which individuals happened to be sampled as parents. If harm explained loss, A could not also have fixed next door."}},{"k":"check_drift_chance","v":{"s":"Chance alone changes allele frequencies, and it matters most in small populations."}},{"k":"check_drift_chance_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Each generation samples 2N alleles from the last; sampling error is about p(1−p)/(2N) per generation, so ten individuals wander far while a thousand barely move. Drift is evolution with no direction, and it is why small endangered populations lose variation even when nothing is selecting against it."}},{"k":"check_drift_faster","v":{"s":"Small populations adapt faster, so they reach the best allele sooner."}},{"k":"check_drift_faster_fb","v":{"s":"There was no \"best\" allele: A and a were equivalent. Small populations change faster, but in RANDOM directions. Adaptation needs selection, and drift can even push a population away from an adaptive allele."}},{"k":"check_drift_biased","v":{"s":"The simulator's random numbers must be biased toward one allele."}},{"k":"check_drift_biased_fb","v":{"s":"Run it again: across many lineages about half fix at A and half lose it. Fixation and loss are equally likely from p = 0.5, which is exactly what unbiased sampling predicts."}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_q","v":{"s":"Under \"{value1}\" with selection strength {value2}, what will the trait histogram look like after 20 generations?"}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_shift","v":{"s":"The whole hump slides toward one side."}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_narrow","v":{"s":"It stays centred but gets narrower."}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_split","v":{"s":"It splits into two humps with a gap in the middle."}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_wander","v":{"s":"It wanders randomly with no consistent shape."}},{"k":"predict_sandbox_hint","v":{"s":"Step or Auto-Run to generation 20 and the histogram will be compared with your prediction."}},{"k":"explain_sandbox_shift","v":{"s":"Directional selection: one side of the distribution survives better every generation, so the whole hump slides that way until it sits on the fitness peak. The peppered moth and the snowshoe hare presets both do this."}},{"k":"explain_sandbox_narrow","v":{"s":"Stabilizing selection: both tails die more often than the middle, so the mean stays put while the spread shrinks. Human birth weight is the textbook case. Notice the mean did not need to move for selection to be acting."}},{"k":"explain_sandbox_split","v":{"s":"Disruptive selection: the middle is the worst place to be, so the population piles up at the two extremes. Two humps with a valley is the first step toward two specialists, though not by itself two species."}},{"k":"explain_sandbox_wander","v":{"s":"With selection off (or very weak), the only thing changing the population is random sampling of who happens to reproduce. That is genetic drift: real change, no direction. Smaller populations wander further; try N = 10 versus N = 200."}},{"k":"population_size","v":{"s":"Population Size"}},{"k":"applies_on_reset","v":{"s":"applies on Reset"}},{"k":"population_size_hint","v":{"s":"How many organisms the model refills to each generation. Smaller populations drift harder: with selection off, compare N = 10 against N = 200. Takes effect when you Reset the population."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_q","v":{"s":"Under stabilizing selection the mean barely moved. Was selection happening?"}},{"k":"check_sandbox_no","v":{"s":"No. If the mean does not move, nothing is being selected."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_no_fb","v":{"s":"Look at the standard deviation, not the mean. Under the birth-weight preset both tails die more often than the middle, so the spread shrinks while the average stays put. Selection was strong; it just pointed inward from both sides."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_yes","v":{"s":"Yes. Both extremes were dying more often, so the spread shrank even though the average stayed put."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Selection is about who survives to reproduce, and here the survivors were the middle every generation. The histogram narrows and the SD falls. \"Evolution\" is any change in the distribution, not only a moving average."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_weak","v":{"s":"Only weakly. Selection always moves the mean when it is strong."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_weak_fb","v":{"s":"Strength and direction are separate things. Set stabilizing strength to 1.0 and watch the survivor count drop hard each generation while the mean holds at 0.5. Strong selection can leave the average exactly where it is."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_mutation","v":{"s":"No. The population had stopped mutating."}},{"k":"check_sandbox_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"Mutation (σ) was unchanged the whole time; every chick still varied from its parent. The narrowing happened despite mutation constantly adding spread, which tells you how hard selection was pushing inward."}},{"k":"drift_and_population_size","v":{"s":"Drift and population size: "}},{"k":"drift_and_population_size_body","v":{"s":"Pick Drift Only, set Population Size to 10, Reset, and Auto-Run 20 generations. Then do the same at N = 200. The tiny population wanders far from 0.5; the large one barely moves. Same zero selection, different sampling noise."}},{"k":"grant_source","v":{"s":"Approximate teaching values after Boag & Grant 1981 (Science 214: 82-85) and Grant 1986. Check the paper for the exact means."}},{"k":"predict_beak_q","v":{"s":"If a drought removes the soft seeds for three years running, what happens to the population's MEAN beak depth?"}},{"k":"predict_beak_increase","v":{"s":"It increases: the average bird ends up with a deeper beak."}},{"k":"predict_beak_decrease","v":{"s":"It decreases: birds economise on beak growth when food is scarce."}},{"k":"predict_beak_same","v":{"s":"It stays about the same: only the population size changes."}},{"k":"predict_beak_hint","v":{"s":"Trigger Drought and run at least three years to test it."}},{"k":"explain_beak_increase","v":{"s":"With the soft seeds gone, only birds that could crack hard seeds ate enough to survive, and they were the deep-beaked ones. Their offspring inherited deeper beaks (parental mean ± σ). No bird's beak grew; the average moved because the small-beaked birds were missing from the parents."}},{"k":"explain_beak_decrease","v":{"s":"The mean fell. In this model that happens when tiny seeds dominate (El Niño years) or, more slowly, in normal years after a drought, because deep beaks handle tiny seeds badly. Check the Mode card: was the drought actually on for those years?"}},{"k":"explain_beak_same","v":{"s":"Little change usually means too few drought years, or a very tight inheritance σ so there was little variation for the drought to sort. Run more drought years and watch the survivors column."}},{"k":"check_beak_q","v":{"s":"During the drought the average beak got deeper. How?"}},{"k":"check_beak_grew","v":{"s":"Each finch grew a deeper beak to cope with the harder seeds."}},{"k":"check_beak_grew_fb","v":{"s":"A finch's beak depth is set when it grows up and does not change with diet. Look at the birds grid: each bird keeps its class. The average changed because the small-beaked birds were missing from next year's parents, not because any bird changed."}},{"k":"check_beak_survived","v":{"s":"Small-beaked birds starved; the deep-beaked survivors bred, and their chicks inherited deeper beaks."}},{"k":"check_beak_survived_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. The drought changed WHO survived, inheritance carried that into the next generation, and the average followed. This is exactly what the Grants measured on Daphne Major: survivors of 1977 had deeper beaks than the birds that died, and the 1978 chicks resembled their parents."}},{"k":"check_beak_mutation","v":{"s":"The drought caused mutations for deeper beaks."}},{"k":"check_beak_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"The variation was already there before the drought (the Year 0 population had beaks from about 8 to 10.5 mm). Offspring variation (σ) is random in both directions and did not change when the drought started. The drought selected among existing variants."}},{"k":"check_beak_migrated","v":{"s":"Deep-beaked birds flew in from other islands."}},{"k":"check_beak_migrated_fb","v":{"s":"No migration exists in this model (and Daphne Major is isolated enough that the Grants could rule it out by tagging every bird). The change came from within the island population."}},{"k":"moose_traits","v":{"s":"Fur, nurses its calf with milk, walks on two-toed hooves. Maine's largest land animal."}},{"k":"deer_traits","v":{"s":"Fur, live birth and milk, two-toed hooves, chews plant food with flat molars."}},{"k":"bear_traits","v":{"s":"Fur and milk, five clawed toes per foot, shearing cheek teeth (carnassials)."}},{"k":"cardinal_traits","v":{"s":"Feathers, scaly legs, lays hard-shelled eggs on land. Year-round Maine resident."}},{"k":"turtle_traits","v":{"s":"Dry scaly skin, a bony shell fused to the ribs, lays leathery eggs on land, ectothermic."}},{"k":"snake_traits","v":{"s":"Dry overlapping scales, forked tongue, flexible jaw; limbless, but its ancestors had four legs."}},{"k":"salmon_traits","v":{"s":"Backbone, gills, fins supported by bony rays, no limbs. Maine's endangered native."}},{"k":"frog_traits","v":{"s":"Four limbs, moist permeable skin, lays jelly-coated eggs in water."}},{"k":"lobster_traits","v":{"s":"Jointed exoskeleton, ten legs, two pairs of antennae. Lives in water but has no backbone."}},{"k":"bee_traits","v":{"s":"Jointed exoskeleton, six legs, three body segments, one pair of antennae."}},{"k":"crab_traits","v":{"s":"Jointed exoskeleton, ten legs, two pairs of antennae."}},{"k":"maple_traits","v":{"s":"Photosynthesises, cellulose cell walls, no nervous system. Maine's state tree."}},{"k":"trait_cellulose","v":{"s":"cellulose walls, photosynthesis"}},{"k":"trait_exoskeleton","v":{"s":"jointed exoskeleton"}},{"k":"trait_six_legs","v":{"s":"six legs, 3 body segments"}},{"k":"trait_two_antennae","v":{"s":"two pairs of antennae, 10 legs"}},{"k":"trait_backbone","v":{"s":"backbone"}},{"k":"trait_fin_rays","v":{"s":"fins with bony rays, gills"}},{"k":"trait_four_limbs","v":{"s":"four limbs (or limbed ancestors)"}},{"k":"trait_moist_skin","v":{"s":"moist skin, eggs in water"}},{"k":"trait_amniotic_egg","v":{"s":"amniotic egg (or live birth from it)"}},{"k":"trait_hair","v":{"s":"hair and milk"}},{"k":"trait_hooves","v":{"s":"two-toed hooves"}},{"k":"trait_claws","v":{"s":"claws, carnassial teeth"}},{"k":"trait_scales","v":{"s":"dry scales"}},{"k":"trait_feathers","v":{"s":"feathers"}},{"k":"trait_shell","v":{"s":"bony shell"}},{"k":"trait_forked_tongue","v":{"s":"forked tongue, flexible jaw"}},{"k":"tip_plants","v":{"s":"Plants"}},{"k":"tip_insects","v":{"s":"Insects"}},{"k":"tip_crustaceans","v":{"s":"Crustaceans"}},{"k":"tip_rayfinned","v":{"s":"Ray-finned fishes"}},{"k":"tip_amphibians","v":{"s":"Amphibians"}},{"k":"tip_ungulates","v":{"s":"Even-toed ungulates"}},{"k":"tip_carnivorans","v":{"s":"Carnivorans"}},{"k":"tip_turtles","v":{"s":"Turtles"}},{"k":"tip_squamates","v":{"s":"Squamates (snakes & lizards)"}},{"k":"tip_birds","v":{"s":"Birds"}},{"k":"node_reptiles_incl_birds","v":{"s":"Reptiles (incl. birds)"}},{"k":"syn_scales","v":{"s":"dry scales, water-tight skin"}},{"k":"syn_shell","v":{"s":"bony shell; anapsid skull (no temporal openings)"}},{"k":"node_diapsids","v":{"s":"Diapsids"}},{"k":"syn_diapsid","v":{"s":"two openings behind each eye socket"}},{"k":"syn_squamate","v":{"s":"forked tongue, flexible jaw joint"}},{"k":"syn_feathers","v":{"s":"feathers (via theropod dinosaurs)"}},{"k":"node_archelosauria","v":{"s":"Archelosauria"}},{"k":"syn_archelosauria","v":{"s":"shared DNA sequence changes (genome-scale studies, 2012 on)"}},{"k":"syn_shell_mol","v":{"s":"bony shell; skull openings lost secondarily"}},{"k":"node_life","v":{"s":"Life"}},{"k":"syn_plants","v":{"s":"cellulose cell walls, chloroplasts"}},{"k":"node_animals","v":{"s":"Animals"}},{"k":"syn_animals","v":{"s":"multicellular, eats other organisms, nerves"}},{"k":"node_arthropods","v":{"s":"Arthropods"}},{"k":"syn_arthropods","v":{"s":"jointed exoskeleton, segmented body"}},{"k":"syn_insects","v":{"s":"six legs, three body segments"}},{"k":"syn_crustaceans","v":{"s":"two pairs of antennae"}},{"k":"node_vertebrates","v":{"s":"Vertebrates"}},{"k":"syn_vertebrates","v":{"s":"backbone, skull"}},{"k":"syn_rayfinned","v":{"s":"fins supported by bony rays"}},{"k":"node_tetrapods","v":{"s":"Tetrapods"}},{"k":"syn_tetrapods","v":{"s":"four limbs with digits"}},{"k":"syn_amphibians","v":{"s":"moist skin, larvae in water"}},{"k":"node_amniotes","v":{"s":"Amniotes"}},{"k":"syn_amniotes","v":{"s":"amniotic egg (membranes around the embryo)"}},{"k":"node_mammals","v":{"s":"Mammals"}},{"k":"syn_mammals","v":{"s":"hair, milk"}},{"k":"syn_ungulates","v":{"s":"even-toed hooves"}},{"k":"syn_carnivorans","v":{"s":"carnassial shearing teeth"}},{"k":"sr_placed_on_tip","v":{"s":"{value1} placed on {value2}. {value3} of {value4} organisms placed."}},{"k":"evidence_molecular_note","v":{"s":"DNA hypothesis: genome-scale comparisons (e.g. Crawford et al. 2012; Chiari et al. 2012) place turtles as the sister group of birds + crocodiles; the turtle's solid skull is now read as a later loss of the openings. DNA also nests whales inside the even-toed ungulates, closest to hippos (Cetartiodactyla); Eocene whale fossils described in 2001 turned out to carry the even-toed \"double-pulley\" ankle bone, so the skeletons agreed once the right bones were found."}},{"k":"evidence_morph_note","v":{"s":"Skeletal hypothesis: for most of the 20th century turtles were placed outside the diapsids because their skulls lack the two temporal openings that lizards, snakes, crocodiles and birds share, and whales, with no ankle bones to compare, were kept as an order of their own. Toggle to DNA to see the same tips arranged by molecular evidence: two branches move."}},{"k":"missing_here","v":{"s":"← missing"}},{"k":"a11y_cladogram_summary","v":{"s":"Cladogram with {value1} tips, {value2} evidence. {value3}"}},{"k":"phylo_hero_v2","v":{"s":"Thirteen Maine organisms, one branching tree. Pick an organism, read its observable traits, then place it on the tip whose path of shared derived traits (synapomorphies) it matches: backbone? four limbs? amniotic egg? hair, or feathers? Every split on the tree is labelled with the trait that defines it. Grade your tree, then toggle the evidence: skeletons and DNA agree on almost everything, and disagree about exactly two branches."}},{"k":"predict_phylo_q","v":{"s":"Before you build: which two are the closer relatives?"}},{"k":"predict_phylo_lobster_salmon","v":{"s":"Lobster and salmon (both live in the sea)."}},{"k":"predict_phylo_lobster_bee","v":{"s":"Lobster and honeybee."}},{"k":"predict_phylo_salmon_frog","v":{"s":"Salmon and bullfrog (both slimy and aquatic)."}},{"k":"predict_phylo_hint","v":{"s":"Place all twelve and grade the tree to see the answer."}},{"k":"explain_phylo_right","v":{"s":"Lobster and bee share a jointed exoskeleton and a segmented body, inherited from a common arthropod ancestor. Salmon has a backbone, which lobsters never had. Habitat is not ancestry: the sea is full of unrelated things that happen to live there."}},{"k":"explain_phylo_lobster_salmon","v":{"s":"Where something lives says nothing about its ancestry. Lobster has a jointed exoskeleton and no backbone; salmon has a backbone and no exoskeleton. Lobster's closest relative here is the honeybee (both arthropods), and salmon's is the frog (both vertebrates). Grouping by habitat is the folk taxonomy that tree-thinking replaces."}},{"k":"explain_phylo_salmon_frog","v":{"s":"Salmon and frog ARE relatives (both vertebrates), so this is the second-best answer. But the question asked about the lobster pairs: lobster and bee share a jointed exoskeleton from a common arthropod ancestor, and that is a closer relationship than either has to any vertebrate."}},{"k":"evidence","v":{"s":"🔬 Evidence used to draw the tree"}},{"k":"evidence_type","v":{"s":"Evidence type"}},{"k":"sr_evidence_dna","v":{"s":"Tree redrawn from DNA evidence: turtles now sit next to birds, and whales sit inside the even-toed ungulates."}},{"k":"sr_evidence_skeletal","v":{"s":"Tree redrawn from skeletal evidence: turtles branch off before the other reptiles, and whales stand as their own branch of mammals."}},{"k":"skeletons_morphology","v":{"s":"🦴 Skeletons"}},{"k":"dna_molecular","v":{"s":"🧬 DNA"}},{"k":"the_tree","v":{"s":"🌳 The tree: tips are clades, labels on branches are synapomorphies"}},{"k":"click_a_tip_to_place","v":{"s":"Click a tip to place {value1}, or use the buttons below."}},{"k":"pick_an_organism_first","v":{"s":"Pick an organism below, then a tip."}},{"k":"place_organism_on","v":{"s":"Place {value1} on which tip?"}},{"k":"tips_to_choose","v":{"s":"Tips to choose from"}},{"k":"a11y_place_on","v":{"s":"Place {value1} on {value2}"}},{"k":"cancel","v":{"s":"Cancel"}},{"k":"trait_checklist_on","v":{"s":"Trait checklist ON"}},{"k":"show_trait_checklist","v":{"s":"Show trait checklist"}},{"k":"not_placed_yet","v":{"s":"not placed yet"}},{"k":"path_to_the_right_tip","v":{"s":"Path to the right tip: "}},{"k":"remove_from_tree","v":{"s":"remove from tree"}},{"k":"grade_my_tree","v":{"s":"Grade My Tree"}},{"k":"place_all_12_first","v":{"s":"Place all 12 first"}},{"k":"perfect_tree","v":{"s":"Perfect tree"}},{"k":"strong_tree","v":{"s":"Strong tree"}},{"k":"keep_going_tree","v":{"s":"Keep going"}},{"k":"tree_result_detail","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2} organisms sit on the right tip. Wrong placements show the path of synapomorphies that leads to the right one; fix them and grade again."}},{"k":"check_phylo_q","v":{"s":"On this tree the cardinal sits inside the reptile branch. Are birds reptiles?"}},{"k":"check_phylo_separate","v":{"s":"No. Birds are warm-blooded and feathered, so they are their own separate class."}},{"k":"check_phylo_separate_fb","v":{"s":"That is the old Linnaean habit: cut a group out because it looks different. A clade is an ancestor plus ALL its descendants. The reptile ancestor's descendants include birds (via theropod dinosaurs), so a \"reptiles\" that excludes birds is not a clade, just as \"fish\" that excludes tetrapods is not."}},{"k":"check_phylo_yes","v":{"s":"Yes, in tree terms: birds descend from the reptile common ancestor, so any clade containing all reptiles must contain birds."}},{"k":"check_phylo_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Feathers are a synapomorphy of birds, layered on top of the reptile synapomorphies (dry scales are still on a cardinal's legs; the amniotic egg is still in the nest). Being distinctive does not remove you from your ancestry; humans are still primates, mammals, tetrapods, and vertebrates."}},{"k":"check_phylo_mammals","v":{"s":"No. Birds evolved from mammals, since both are warm-blooded."}},{"k":"check_phylo_mammals_fb","v":{"s":"Warm-bloodedness arose independently in the two lineages (convergence), which is exactly why single traits are risky for building trees. Feathers on a scaly leg, egg-laying, and the skull and hip bones all place birds with dinosaurs and crocodiles, not with mammals."}},{"k":"check_phylo_flightless","v":{"s":"Only flightless birds like ostriches are reptiles."}},{"k":"check_phylo_flightless_fb","v":{"s":"Ancestry is not a matter of degree or of what an animal does today. Every bird, flying or not, descends from the same feathered theropod ancestors and so sits on the same branch."}},{"k":"bones_viewed_progress","v":{"s":"Bone groups compared: {value1} of {value2}"}},{"k":"check_ancestry_q","v":{"s":"A bat wing and a bird wing both fly, and both are forelimbs with the same bones. Are they homologous?"}},{"k":"check_ancestry_wings","v":{"s":"Yes, completely: a wing is a wing."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_wings_fb","v":{"s":"Half right. The LIMB is homologous (same humerus, radius, ulna, digits, inherited from a common tetrapod ancestor). But that ancestor did not fly. Bat and bird each turned the shared forelimb into a wing separately, one with a skin membrane over long fingers and one with feathers on a fused hand. The wing as a flying structure is convergent."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_nested","v":{"s":"The forelimb bones are homologous, but the wing itself evolved independently in each, so the flight surfaces are analogous."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_nested_fb","v":{"s":"Exactly. Homology is about shared ancestry of a structure, and it can hold at one level (the tetrapod forelimb) while a later modification (flight) arose separately. Click the phalanges: elongated fingers under a membrane in the bat, fused into a feather anchor in the bird. Same bones, different wings."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_class","v":{"s":"No, because one is a mammal and one is a bird."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_class_fb","v":{"s":"Being in different groups does not remove homology; mammals and birds are both tetrapods and share the whole forelimb plan from that ancestor. Your own arm is homologous with both wings, and you are a mammal too."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_function","v":{"s":"No, because homologous structures must do the same job."}},{"k":"check_ancestry_function_fb","v":{"s":"Backwards. Homology is about origin, not job: the human arm and whale flipper do completely different things and are the textbook homologous pair. Same job with different origins is analogy."}},{"k":"about_your_answer","v":{"s":"About your answer: "}},{"k":"predict_antibiotic_q","v":{"s":"Starting with {value1}% resistant, if you apply the antibiotic and Auto-Run for 20 ticks, what will the dish look like?"}},{"k":"predict_antibiotic_resistant","v":{"s":"The population crashes, then regrows, mostly resistant."}},{"k":"predict_antibiotic_extinct","v":{"s":"The antibiotic wipes the population out."}},{"k":"predict_antibiotic_unchanged","v":{"s":"The population shrinks but stays mostly susceptible."}},{"k":"predict_antibiotic_hint","v":{"s":"Apply Antibiotic, then Auto-Run to tick 20."}},{"k":"explain_antibiotic_resistant","v":{"s":"The drug killed susceptible cells preferentially, so the few resistant ones became most of the survivors and then most of the parents. Resistance did not appear because of the drug; it was already there (or arose by random mutation) and the drug removed everything else."}},{"k":"explain_antibiotic_extinct","v":{"s":"Nobody survived long enough to found a resistant lineage. That happens with zero starting resistance and low mutation, or a very strong dose on a small population. In real infections, extinction of the pathogen is the goal of a complete, correctly dosed course."}},{"k":"explain_antibiotic_unchanged","v":{"s":"The population is still mostly susceptible. Either the drug was off for most of the run, the dose was low, or you have not reached tick 20 with the drug active. Selection needs exposure to act."}},{"k":"cost_of_resistance","v":{"s":"Cost of Resistance"}},{"k":"no_cost_resistance_is_free","v":{"s":"None: resistance is free"}},{"k":"fewer_offspring_at_full_resistance","v":{"s":"fewer offspring at full resistance"}},{"k":"cost_of_resistance_hint","v":{"s":"Resistance often carries a growth cost (a pump or altered enzyme to maintain). With the drug off, cells keep dying and dividing (10% a tick) and 5% of a resistant cell's divisions lose the plasmid, so a cost of 0.30 or more rolls resistance back over about a hundred ticks; at zero cost it barely moves. Real bacteria show both, and compensatory mutations can erase a cost over time."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_q","v":{"s":"Where did the resistant bacteria come from?"}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_learned","v":{"s":"The bacteria sensed the antibiotic and built resistance to fight it."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_learned_fb","v":{"s":"Bacteria do not sense a drug and respond by redesigning themselves. Resistance in this model is a heritable number each cell is born with. The drug did not create resistant cells; it killed the susceptible ones and left the resistant minority to multiply."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_already","v":{"s":"A few were already resistant (or became so by random mutation); the drug killed the others, so the resistant ones took over."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_already_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Set Starting Resistance to 0 and Mutation to 0 and the drug simply wins: with no variation there is nothing to select. Resistance spreads because selection acts on variation that exists first. The same logic ran the finch drought and your predator hunt."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_stronger","v":{"s":"The surviving bacteria got stronger each time they were exposed."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_stronger_fb","v":{"s":"No individual cell changed its resistance during its life; a cell's r is fixed and only its offspring vary (by ± σ, in both directions). What rose was the POPULATION's share of high-r cells, tick by tick, because low-r cells kept dying."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_dose","v":{"s":"The dose was too weak to kill anything, so the population just grew."}},{"k":"check_antibiotic_dose_fb","v":{"s":"Look at the population line: it crashed when the drug came on. The dish did not just grow; it was replaced. A dose that kills 85% of susceptible cells per tick is strong; what it cannot do is kill cells that carry high resistance."}},{"k":"predict_speciation_q","v":{"s":"With the left ideal at {value1} and the right at {value2} (selection {value3}), where will the trait-overlap proxy be after 30 generations?"}},{"k":"predict_speciation_diverge","v":{"s":"Below the 30% teaching milestone: the two trait distributions barely overlap."}},{"k":"predict_speciation_partial","v":{"s":"Lower than now, but still above 30%."}},{"k":"predict_speciation_same","v":{"s":"About where it started: the two sides stay similar."}},{"k":"predict_speciation_hint","v":{"s":"Auto-Run to generation 30 to test it."}},{"k":"explain_speciation_diverge","v":{"s":"Each side was pulled toward its own ideal, and with gene flow at zero nothing pulled them back together, so the trait distributions separated until they barely overlap. Remember what the proxy is: trait overlap, not a mating test. Divergence like this is a precondition for speciation, not proof of it."}},{"k":"explain_speciation_partial","v":{"s":"The sides separated but still overlap. Weaker selection, closer ideals, or high offspring variation all slow divergence. Given more generations they would likely keep separating; given migration (not modelled here) they might not."}},{"k":"explain_speciation_same","v":{"s":"Little divergence. With the same ideal on both sides, or selection near 0, only random sampling separates the means, and that is slow at N = 30 per side. Set the ideals apart and watch the difference."}},{"k":"check_speciation_q","v":{"s":"The overlap proxy dropped below 30%. Are the two populations now two species?"}},{"k":"check_speciation_yes","v":{"s":"Yes. Low trait overlap means they can no longer interbreed."}},{"k":"check_speciation_yes_fb","v":{"s":"The proxy compares two TRAIT distributions; it never puts a left and a right individual together to see whether they mate or whether their offspring survive. Many real populations differ strongly in a trait and still interbreed freely. Reproductive isolation has to be tested, not inferred from one number."}},{"k":"check_speciation_notnec","v":{"s":"Not necessarily. The model measures trait divergence; speciation needs reproductive isolation, which this model does not test."}},{"k":"check_speciation_notnec_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Divergence in isolation is how many speciation events START, and the 30% line is an arbitrary teaching milestone, not a species boundary. To call them species you would need evidence about mating, hybrid fertility, or gene flow if the barrier came down."}},{"k":"check_speciation_barrier","v":{"s":"Yes. Once a barrier separates populations they are separate species by definition."}},{"k":"check_speciation_barrier_fb","v":{"s":"Separation is geography, not biology. Populations split by a river or a road for centuries often remain one species and merge back when reconnected. What matters is whether they have changed in ways that stop them interbreeding."}},{"k":"check_speciation_size","v":{"s":"No, because the two sides still have the same number of individuals."}},{"k":"check_speciation_size_fb","v":{"s":"Population size has nothing to do with species status (the model refills each side to 30 anyway). The relevant question is reproductive compatibility, which the model leaves untested."}},{"k":"check_coevo_q","v":{"s":"Both speed lines climb together and the gap between them stays small. Who is winning?"}},{"k":"check_coevo_pred","v":{"s":"The predators: they are faster than they were."}},{"k":"check_coevo_pred_fb","v":{"s":"They are faster, but so is their food. Look at the capture rate rather than the speed: it stays roughly level, because every predator gain is matched by a prey gain. Being faster than your grandparents is not the same as catching more dinner."}},{"k":"check_coevo_nobody","v":{"s":"Nobody: each side's improvement cancels the other's, so both run faster just to stay in place."}},{"k":"check_coevo_nobody_fb","v":{"s":"That is the Red Queen. Each population is the other's selective environment, so the target keeps moving. Fitness is relative: a predator only needs to be faster than the prey in front of it, and that prey is being selected to be faster too."}},{"k":"check_coevo_prey","v":{"s":"The prey: they keep escaping."}},{"k":"check_coevo_prey_fb","v":{"s":"Prey escape at about the same rate they always did; the predators improved in step. If prey were truly winning the capture rate would fall toward zero and the predators would crash."}},{"k":"check_coevo_worse","v":{"s":"Both are getting worse, since neither has gained an advantage."}},{"k":"check_coevo_worse_fb","v":{"s":"Both are objectively faster than at generation 0 (turn Cost of Speed on and you will see the real price of that). No advantage gained does not mean no change; it means the change was reciprocal."}},{"k":"timeline_entries_read","v":{"s":"Entries read: {value1} (open five to complete this module)"}},{"k":"check_timeline_q","v":{"s":"Darwin published in 1859 without knowing how traits were inherited. Does that weaken the theory of evolution?"}},{"k":"check_timeline_complete","v":{"s":"Yes. A theory has to be complete when it is first proposed."}},{"k":"check_timeline_complete_fb","v":{"s":"No scientific theory arrives complete. Newton had gravity without knowing what carried it; Darwin had selection without knowing what carried heredity. Theories are judged by whether new evidence keeps fitting, and for evolution it has, for 165 years."}},{"k":"check_timeline_converged","v":{"s":"No. Mendel's genetics (rediscovered in 1900), then DNA, supplied the mechanism Darwin lacked, and independent lines of evidence converged on the same picture."}},{"k":"check_timeline_converged_fb","v":{"s":"Right. The Modern Synthesis of the 1930s and 40s joined Darwin's selection to Mendel's particles of inheritance; Watson, Crick and Franklin's DNA structure explained how those particles copy and mutate. A gap that later evidence FILLS is a strength of a theory, not a weakness."}},{"k":"check_timeline_replaced","v":{"s":"Yes. Darwin's theory has since been replaced by genetics."}},{"k":"check_timeline_replaced_fb","v":{"s":"Genetics did not replace natural selection; it explained the inheritance step that selection depends on. Modern evolutionary biology is Darwin plus Mendel plus molecular biology, layered, not swapped."}},{"k":"check_timeline_secret","v":{"s":"No, because Darwin already knew about DNA."}},{"k":"check_timeline_secret_fb","v":{"s":"DNA's structure was worked out in 1953, 71 years after Darwin died. He proposed his own (wrong) heredity idea, pangenesis. The theory survived his mistake because its core claim did not depend on it."}},{"k":"sr_trial_survived","v":{"s":"Warming trial survived: {value1} per generation, {value2} generations, {value3} creatures alive."}},{"k":"sr_trial_extinct","v":{"s":"Warming trial ended in extinction at {value1} per generation after {value2} generations."}},{"k":"sr_new_best_rate","v":{"s":"New personal best: the population survived warming of {value1} per generation."}},{"k":"sr_trial_started","v":{"s":"Warming trial started: temperature rises {value1} per generation from 0.50 to 0.90."}},{"k":"predict_climate_q","v":{"s":"If the temperature rises {value1} per generation from 0.50 to 0.90 (about {value2} generations), what will the population do?"}},{"k":"predict_climate_track","v":{"s":"Survive: its mean tolerance tracks the temperature, lagging a little."}},{"k":"predict_climate_extinct","v":{"s":"Go extinct before the warming ends."}},{"k":"predict_climate_unchanged","v":{"s":"Survive without its tolerance changing much."}},{"k":"predict_climate_hint","v":{"s":"Press \"Run warming trial\" to test it at exactly this rate."}},{"k":"explain_climate_track","v":{"s":"Slow enough. Each generation only a slice of the population was outside its tolerance, so survivors existed to breed the next generation, and their offspring (with small random variation) sat a little closer to the new temperature. Repeat that for many generations and the mean tolerance follows the climate. No individual changed; the population did."}},{"k":"explain_climate_extinct","v":{"s":"Too fast. When the temperature jumps further in one generation than the spread of tolerances in the population, almost nobody survives to breed, and the few who do cannot restore the numbers. Adaptation needs survivors; the rate decided whether there were any."}},{"k":"explain_climate_unchanged","v":{"s":"The population survived but barely shifted. That happens when the rise is small relative to how tolerant individuals already are, or the trial ended early. Push the rate up and the shift becomes visible, until it stops being possible at all."}},{"k":"best_rate_survived","v":{"s":"Best rate survived"}},{"k":"per_generation_sigma","v":{"s":"per generation · σ "}},{"k":"run_a_warming_trial","v":{"s":"run a warming trial"}},{"k":"warming_trial","v":{"s":"🌡️ Warming trial: set the RATE, then run"}},{"k":"warming_trial_desc","v":{"s":"Auto-Run raises the temperature by this amount every generation, from 0.50 to 0.90, so every trial is comparable."}},{"k":"warming_per_generation","v":{"s":"Warming per generation"}},{"k":"about_n_generations_for_the_rise","v":{"s":"~{value1} generations for the 0.40 rise"}},{"k":"locked_during_trial","v":{"s":"locked during the trial"}},{"k":"warming_rate_hint","v":{"s":"Compare with how far the population can shift in one generation, which depends on Mutation Size. At σ 0.02 about 0.03 per generation is the edge; raise σ and the edge moves."}},{"k":"trial_running","v":{"s":"Trial running…"}},{"k":"run_warming_trial","v":{"s":"Run warming trial (resets, 0.50 → 0.90)"}},{"k":"trial_status_running","v":{"s":"Rate {value1}/gen · temperature {value2} · generation {value3} · {value4} alive"}},{"k":"trial_status_survived","v":{"s":"✓ Survived {value1}/gen: {value2} generations, {value3} alive, tolerance lag {value4} at the end."}},{"k":"trial_status_extinct","v":{"s":"✗ Extinct at {value1}/gen after {value2} generations. Try a slower rate, or more variation."}},{"k":"trials_this_session","v":{"s":"Trials this visit: "}},{"k":"check_climate_q","v":{"s":"Why does the population survive slow warming but go extinct under fast warming?"}},{"k":"check_climate_adjust","v":{"s":"Fast warming gives individuals no time to adjust their own tolerance."}},{"k":"check_climate_adjust_fb","v":{"s":"No individual adjusts. Each creature's tolerance is fixed at birth (look at one: it never changes colour). Slow warming works because every generation SOME individuals already sit near the new temperature, survive, and breed. Time helps the population, not the individual."}},{"k":"check_climate_survivors","v":{"s":"Each generation only creatures near the current temperature survive; slow change leaves survivors to breed, fast change leaves too few."}},{"k":"check_climate_survivors_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Adaptation is made of survivors. When the climate moves less per generation than the spread of tolerances, there are always some survivors and their offspring shift the mean. When it moves more than that spread, there is nobody left to breed, and selection has nothing to work with."}},{"k":"check_climate_mutations","v":{"s":"Fast warming stops mutations from happening."}},{"k":"check_climate_mutations_fb","v":{"s":"Mutation (the ± σ on each chick) happened at the same rate in every run. Mutation supplies variation, but it cannot rescue a population that has no survivors to pass anything on. Raising σ helps only because it widens the spread of tolerances among the survivors' offspring."}},{"k":"check_climate_chose","v":{"s":"The population chose to stop reproducing under stress."}},{"k":"check_climate_chose_fb","v":{"s":"Nothing chooses in this model. Reproduction always refills the population from whoever survived. Extinction happened because fewer than 5 survived the selection step, not because anyone stopped breeding."}},{"k":"sr_you_chose_showiness_next_mean_pref","v":{"s":"You chose the bird with showiness {value1}. Next generation: mean showiness {value2}, mean female preference {value3}."}},{"k":"sr_fast_forward_rounds","v":{"s":"Fast-forwarded {value1} rounds with every female choosing by her inherited preference. Round {value2}: mean showiness {value3}."}},{"k":"a11y_male_taken_by_predator","v":{"s":"Male with showiness {value1}, taken by a predator before mating. Not available."}},{"k":"taken_by_a_predator","v":{"s":"taken by a predator"}},{"k":"showiness_and_preference_over_rounds","v":{"s":"Showiness and preference over rounds"}},{"k":"mean_showiness_males","v":{"s":"Mean showiness (males)"}},{"k":"max","v":{"s":"Max"}},{"k":"mean_preference_females","v":{"s":"Mean preference (females)"}},{"k":"a11y_showiness_preference_chart","v":{"s":"Mean and max male showiness and mean female preference over {value1} rounds. Latest mean showiness {value2}, latest mean preference {value3}."}},{"k":"pref_line_caption","v":{"s":"Cyan is the females' inherited preference. Fisher's runaway says it should ride up with the ornament, because choosy mothers and showy fathers make chicks carrying both. In 24 birds over a few dozen rounds that pull is too weak to beat chance, so expect the cyan line to wander. The escalation you see is powered by the preference that already exists; set it with the choosiness slider."}},{"k":"mate_choice_hero_v2","v":{"s":"Each round you see 12 male birds with varying tail showiness. You are one of 12 females: pick a mate, and he fathers a quarter of the next generation. The other 11 females choose by an inherited PREFERENCE for showy tails: a choosy female compares four males and takes the showiest. Tail and preference both pass to the chicks. With no predator the ornament escalates. Turn the predator on and showy males start disappearing before they can mate; the escalation stalls where attraction and survival balance, which is where real peacocks live. Use Fast-forward to see where the population settles."}},{"k":"predict_mate_q","v":{"s":"With the predator cost as it is set now, and you picking the showiest surviving male every round, where will average showiness be when the run ends?"}},{"k":"predict_mate_runaway","v":{"s":"High: it climbs past 0.75 and keeps going."}},{"k":"predict_mate_plateau","v":{"s":"It rises, then stalls somewhere below 0.75."}},{"k":"predict_mate_flat","v":{"s":"It stays about where it started."}},{"k":"predict_mate_drop","v":{"s":"It falls."}},{"k":"predict_mate_hint","v":{"s":"Your answer should depend on whether the predator cost is on. Run it both ways, and use Fast-forward to see where each one settles."}},{"k":"explain_mate_runaway","v":{"s":"With no survival cost, nothing pushes back. Showy males get most of the matings and their sons inherit the ornament, so the average climbs toward the maximum and stays there. Real ornaments stop growing only when a cost appears."}},{"k":"explain_mate_plateau","v":{"s":"Showiness rose, then stalled. With the predator on, males above about 0.8 almost never survive to the display ground, so beyond a point extra showiness costs more matings (by death) than it wins. Sexual selection pushing up met natural selection pushing down; the population settled where they balance. If the predator was OFF and you still see this, the run was short: fast-forward and watch it keep climbing."}},{"k":"explain_mate_flat","v":{"s":"The average barely moved. That happens with very few rounds, mixed picks, or a predator so effective that showy males rarely survive to be chosen. Selection needs consistent direction to move a trait."}},{"k":"explain_mate_drop","v":{"s":"Showiness fell because dull males were the ones that mated, either by your picks or because the predator kept removing the showy ones. Mate choice can drive a trait in any direction; there is nothing special about \"more\"."}},{"k":"hands_on_rounds_done","v":{"s":"hands-on rounds done"}},{"k":"males","v":{"s":"males"}},{"k":"mean_preference","v":{"s":"Mean Preference"}},{"k":"females_0_indifferent_1_choosy","v":{"s":"females · 0 indifferent, 1 choosy"}},{"k":"taken_by_predators","v":{"s":"Taken by predators"}},{"k":"this_round","v":{"s":"this round"}},{"k":"female_choosiness_at_start","v":{"s":"Female choosiness at start"}},{"k":"choose_by_looks","v":{"s":"choose by looks"}},{"k":"locked_during_run","v":{"s":"locked during a run"}},{"k":"choosiness_hint","v":{"s":"The other 11 females' starting preference: the chance each one compares four males and takes the showiest instead of mating at random. Applies on Restart. At 0 only YOUR choices drive the ornament."}},{"k":"predator_cost_on_desc","v":{"s":"ON — before each round, a male survives with probability 1 − 1.6 × showiness². A male at 0.5 survives 60% of the time; above ~0.8 almost none reach the display ground. Sexual selection vs natural selection in tension."}},{"k":"predator_cost_off_desc","v":{"s":"OFF — no penalty for being showy. The ornament runs away on the strength of the preference already in the flock; a full Fisherian runaway, with the preference climbing too, needs far more than 24 birds (see the chart caption)."}},{"k":"a11y_fast_forward_rounds","v":{"s":"Fast-forward {value1} rounds with every female choosing by her inherited preference"}},{"k":"fast_forward_rounds","v":{"s":"Fast-forward {value1} rounds (females choose by preference)"}},{"k":"check_mate_q","v":{"s":"A peacock's tail makes it easier for predators to catch. Why hasn't natural selection removed it?"}},{"k":"check_mate_weak","v":{"s":"Sexual selection is weaker than natural selection, so the tail is slowly disappearing."}},{"k":"check_mate_weak_fb","v":{"s":"Sexual selection is not the weaker force; it built the tail in the first place. In your run, the ornament rose even with the predator on, until the two pressures balanced. The tail persists because mating success and survival are both part of fitness."}},{"k":"check_mate_balance","v":{"s":"A showier male loses some survival but gains more matings; the tail stops growing where those two effects cancel."}},{"k":"check_mate_balance_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Fitness is offspring left behind, not survival alone. A male eaten at 3 years old after siring twenty chicks out-reproduces a cautious male who lives to 10 with two. With the predator on, your population settled where the mating gain of extra showiness equalled its survival cost."}},{"k":"check_mate_choose","v":{"s":"Peacocks choose to grow big tails to impress females."}},{"k":"check_mate_choose_fb","v":{"s":"No individual chooses its tail. Tail size is inherited; the males in your grid were born with their showiness and could not change it. What changed across rounds was which males got to be fathers."}},{"k":"check_mate_predators","v":{"s":"Predators cannot see the tail, so it has no survival cost."}},{"k":"check_mate_predators_fb","v":{"s":"The cost is real: in your run the predator took the showiest males most often (survival = 1 − 1.6 × showiness²). The trait persists DESPITE the cost, which is exactly what makes it interesting."}},{"k":"what_this_model_leaves_out","v":{"s":"What this model leaves out: "}},{"k":"mate_model_limits","v":{"s":"Real preference and ornament are polygenic, condition-dependent, and shaped by \"good genes\" signalling as well as Fisherian feedback. Twelve birds and twelve rounds exaggerate every effect so it is visible in a class period."}},{"k":"sr_environment_changed_mid_hunt","v":{"s":"Environment changed to {value1} during the hunt. The population keeps its colours; camouflage is now measured against the new background."}},{"k":"blue_dashed_lines_mark_environment_changes","v":{"s":"Blue dashed lines mark environment changes. The green ideal line moved; the red population line had to chase it again."}},{"k":"predict_predator_q","v":{"s":"After 12 rounds of hunting the easiest-to-see prey, what will happen to the population's AVERAGE colour?"}},{"k":"predict_predator_toward","v":{"s":"It shifts toward the background colour (harder to see)."}},{"k":"predict_predator_same","v":{"s":"It stays about where it started; prey just get eaten at random."}},{"k":"predict_predator_away","v":{"s":"It shifts away from the background (easier to see)."}},{"k":"predict_predator_individual","v":{"s":"Each surviving prey gradually changes its own colour to match."}},{"k":"explain_predator_individual","v":{"s":"No individual prey changed colour. Every prey was born with a fixed colour and kept it. What changed was WHO survived to reproduce: the hard-to-see ones. Their offspring inherited that colour (plus small random variation), so the population average moved even though no individual did."}},{"k":"explain_predator_toward","v":{"s":"You removed the most visible prey each round, so the survivors that bred were, on average, closer to the background. Random variation in the offspring then gave you new visible prey to remove. Selection plus heritable variation moved the average."}},{"k":"explain_predator_away","v":{"s":"The average moved away from the background. That happens when the environment changed mid-hunt (the old camouflage is now the wrong colour) or when you hunted the well-camouflaged prey instead of the visible ones. The direction of selection sets the direction of change."}},{"k":"explain_predator_same","v":{"s":"The average barely moved. Random or very short hunting gives selection little to act on. Try more rounds and hunt the prey you can see most easily."}},{"k":"colour_distribution_start_vs_now","v":{"s":"📊 Colour distribution: start vs now"}},{"k":"start","v":{"s":"Start"}},{"k":"now","v":{"s":"Now"}},{"k":"dark","v":{"s":"dark"}},{"k":"light","v":{"s":"light"}},{"k":"histogram_caption_predator","v":{"s":"Grey bars are the colours the hunt started with; green bars are the living population now. The spread narrows and slides toward the ideal because you removed the visible tail every round."}},{"k":"env_switch_keeps_population","v":{"s":"Switching during a hunt keeps the population. The prey do not change colour; only the background does."}},{"k":"check_predator_q","v":{"s":"In this hunt, HOW did the prey become harder to see?"}},{"k":"check_predator_learned","v":{"s":"Each prey learned to hide better after watching others get eaten."}},{"k":"check_predator_learned_fb","v":{"s":"Nothing in this population learns. A prey's colour is fixed the moment it is born and never changes. It feels like learning because the group changes, but the group changes only because different individuals survive to breed."}},{"k":"check_predator_inherit","v":{"s":"Prey that happened to be harder to see were eaten less, so more of the next generation inherited their colour."}},{"k":"check_predator_inherit_fb","v":{"s":"Exactly. Variation existed before you clicked anything. You removed the visible individuals; the camouflaged ones bred; offspring resembled their parents. Repeat that and the average moves. That is natural selection: differential survival plus inheritance."}},{"k":"check_predator_purpose","v":{"s":"The prey mutated on purpose toward the background colour."}},{"k":"check_predator_purpose_fb","v":{"s":"Mutation in this model is random noise of ± 0.05 in EVERY direction, applied before you see the offspring. Some offspring are more visible than their parent, some less. You removed the visible ones. The direction came from you, not from the mutations."}},{"k":"check_predator_me","v":{"s":"I got worse at hunting as the rounds went on."}},{"k":"check_predator_me_fb","v":{"s":"Your round times rose even though you had more practice. If you were the thing changing, practice should have made you faster. The prey changed: the population converged on colours you could not pick out."}},{"k":"peppered_moths_and_the_soot","v":{"s":"Peppered Moths and the Soot"}},{"k":"guppies_in_two_streams","v":{"s":"Guppies in Two Streams"}},{"k":"daphne_major_drought","v":{"s":"Drought on Daphne Major"}},{"k":"trait_histogram_default_title","v":{"s":"📊 Trait distribution"}},{"k":"sr_challenge_met","v":{"s":"Challenge met: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_new_personal_best","v":{"s":"New personal best: {value1} {value2}{value3}"}},{"k":"challenges_heading","v":{"s":"🏅 Challenges"}},{"k":"met","v":{"s":"met"}},{"k":"challenge_met_label","v":{"s":"Challenge met"}},{"k":"challenge_open_label","v":{"s":"Challenge not yet met"}},{"k":"personal_best","v":{"s":"Personal best: "}},{"k":"no_record_yet","v":{"s":"No record yet: "}},{"k":"my_journal","v":{"s":"My journal"}},{"k":"sr_progress_reset","v":{"s":"EvoLab progress reset. Module completion, experiments, predictions and checks cleared."}},{"k":"my_learning_journal","v":{"s":"My Learning Journal"}},{"k":"evolution_field_journal","v":{"s":"Evolution field journal"}},{"k":"journal_of","v":{"s":"{value1}'s journal"}},{"k":"journal_intro","v":{"s":"Everything you have done in EvoLab, in one place: what you finished, what you predicted and whether the population agreed, the checks you passed, the experiments and challenges you logged. Print it or hand it to your teacher."}},{"k":"print_journal","v":{"s":"Print journal"}},{"k":"journal_summary_speech","v":{"s":"You have completed {value1} of {value2} modules, logged {value3} experiments and {value4} challenges, and {value5} of {value6} judged predictions held."}},{"k":"completed","v":{"s":"Completed"}},{"k":"experiments","v":{"s":"Experiments"}},{"k":"challenges","v":{"s":"Challenges"}},{"k":"predictions_held","v":{"s":"Predictions held"}},{"k":"checks_passed","v":{"s":"Checks passed"}},{"k":"modules_completed","v":{"s":"✅ Modules completed"}},{"k":"explored_not_finished","v":{"s":"Explored but not yet finished: "}},{"k":"no_modules_completed_yet","v":{"s":"Nothing completed yet. Finish a hunt, run 20 generations, grade a tree, or finish a quiz to earn a badge."}},{"k":"predictions_vs_observations","v":{"s":"🔮 Predictions vs what happened"}},{"k":"predictions_table_caption","v":{"s":"Your predictions by module, with the observed outcome"}},{"k":"module","v":{"s":"Module"}},{"k":"i_predicted","v":{"s":"I predicted"}},{"k":"observed_col","v":{"s":"Observed"}},{"k":"result","v":{"s":"Result"}},{"k":"not_run_yet","v":{"s":"not run yet"}},{"k":"held","v":{"s":"Held"}},{"k":"revised","v":{"s":"Revised"}},{"k":"no_predictions_yet","v":{"s":"No predictions yet. Each simulator asks for one before you run it."}},{"k":"checks_heading","v":{"s":"🧠 Check-yourself questions"}},{"k":"solved_in_tries","v":{"s":"solved in {value1} tr{value2}"}},{"k":"still_open","v":{"s":"still open"}},{"k":"my_answer","v":{"s":"My answer: "}},{"k":"no_checks_yet","v":{"s":"No checks answered yet. They appear after your first run in each simulator."}},{"k":"experiments_logged","v":{"s":"🧪 Experiments logged"}},{"k":"no_experiments_yet","v":{"s":"No experiments logged yet. Each simulator lists five; the tool detects most of them for you."}},{"k":"challenges_and_records","v":{"s":"🏅 Challenges and personal bests"}},{"k":"no_challenges_yet","v":{"s":"No challenges met yet. Each simulator lists one or two under its experiments."}},{"k":"capstone_heading","v":{"s":"🎓 Capstone investigation"}},{"k":"step_n_of_5","v":{"s":"step {value1} of 5"}},{"k":"runs_captured_n","v":{"s":"{value1} simulator run{value2} captured"}},{"k":"verdict","v":{"s":"verdict: "}},{"k":"claim","v":{"s":"Claim: "}},{"k":"open_capstone","v":{"s":"Open the Capstone →"}},{"k":"no_capstone_yet","v":{"s":"No Capstone started. Pick a scenario in the Capstone Project to begin an investigation."}},{"k":"reset_my_progress","v":{"s":"Reset my EvoLab progress…"}},{"k":"reset_confirm_q","v":{"s":"Clear all badges, experiments, predictions, checks and field notes on this device? The Capstone notebook is kept."}},{"k":"yes_reset","v":{"s":"Yes, reset"}},{"k":"ch_purge","v":{"s":"Purge the allele: "}},{"k":"ch_purge_body","v":{"s":"Start at p = 0.50 with selection at 1.0 (aa never breeds), mutation and migration at 0, and reach p ≥ 0.95 within 30 generations. Then keep stepping: the last 5% never quite goes, because a recessive allele hides in heterozygotes where selection cannot see it."}},{"k":"ch_melt","v":{"s":"Melt a fixed allele: "}},{"k":"ch_melt_body","v":{"s":"Set p = 1.0, selection 0, mutation 0.05 or more, and step until p falls below 0.5. Mutation pressure alone is slow but relentless."}},{"k":"predict_drift_q","v":{"s":"With N = {value1} for {value2} generations, how many of the 5 lineages will end at 0 or 1 (allele lost or fixed)?"}},{"k":"predict_drift_none","v":{"s":"None: all five stay somewhere in between."}},{"k":"predict_drift_some","v":{"s":"One to three of them."}},{"k":"predict_drift_most","v":{"s":"Four or all five."}},{"k":"predict_drift_hint","v":{"s":"Press Run 5 Lineages to test it at exactly these settings."}},{"k":"drift_observed_detail","v":{"s":"N = {value1}, {value2} generations: {value3} of 5 lineages fixed or lost the allele."}},{"k":"explain_drift_none","v":{"s":"No lineage reached 0 or 1. Sampling error per generation scales as p(1−p)/(2N), so with a large N (or few generations) the random walk simply has not travelled far enough to hit a wall."}},{"k":"explain_drift_some","v":{"s":"Some lineages decided and some did not, from identical starting points. That spread IS drift: the same rules, different luck. Increase generations or shrink N and more of them will reach a wall."}},{"k":"explain_drift_most","v":{"s":"Almost every lineage hit 0 or 1. Small populations lose variation fast, and once an allele is lost only mutation or migration can bring it back. This is why tiny, isolated populations worry conservation biologists even when nothing is selecting against them."}},{"k":"ch_allfixed","v":{"s":"Every lineage decided: "}},{"k":"ch_allfixed_body","v":{"s":"With N at 10 or less and 100 generations or fewer, get a run where all five lineages have fixed or lost the allele by the end."}},{"k":"ch_holdsteady","v":{"s":"Big numbers hold steady: "}},{"k":"ch_holdsteady_body","v":{"s":"With N at 1000 or more for 100+ generations, get a run where all five lineages finish between 0.30 and 0.70. Then try the same at N = 10 and count how many even stay off the edges."}},{"k":"ch_tight","v":{"s":"Tight middle: "}},{"k":"ch_tight_body","v":{"s":"Under the stabilizing preset, get the standard deviation below 0.08 within 15 generations. Selection strength and mutation size both matter."}},{"k":"ch_bimodal","v":{"s":"A clean split: "}},{"k":"ch_bimodal_body","v":{"s":"Under disruptive selection, reach a population with at least 40% in each extreme third and no more than 10% left in the middle."}},{"k":"ch_bigbeaks","v":{"s":"Deep beaks: "}},{"k":"ch_bigbeaks_body","v":{"s":"Push the population mean past 10.5 mm. How many drought years does it take, and does the inheritance σ change that?"}},{"k":"deepest_mean_beak","v":{"s":"deepest mean beak"}},{"k":"ch_recover","v":{"s":"Bring the small beaks back: "}},{"k":"ch_recover_body","v":{"s":"After three or more drought years, bring the mean back under 10.0 mm. Normal years revert slowly, El Niño years faster. Find out why the way back is never as quick as the shift."}},{"k":"ch_perfectfirst","v":{"s":"First time right: "}},{"k":"ch_perfectfirst_body","v":{"s":"Place all twelve correctly before your first grading. Start Over resets the attempt."}},{"k":"ch_nohints","v":{"s":"From observation alone: "}},{"k":"ch_nohints_body","v":{"s":"A perfect tree with the trait checklist switched off the whole way. Read each organism's note and reason down the tree yourself."}},{"k":"ch_perfect12","v":{"s":"Clean sweep: "}},{"k":"ch_perfect12_body","v":{"s":"All 12 correct on a single pass, no review round."}},{"k":"best_score","v":{"s":"best score"}},{"k":"ch_eradicate","v":{"s":"Eradicate the infection: "}},{"k":"ch_eradicate_body","v":{"s":"Drive the dish to zero bacteria while the drug is on. Which settings make that possible, and which make it hopeless?"}},{"k":"ch_rollback","v":{"s":"Roll resistance back: "}},{"k":"ch_rollback_body","v":{"s":"Let resistance reach 90%, then bring it back under 20% with the drug OFF. Practically impossible at zero cost; set a cost of 0.30 or more, auto-run, and be patient (about a hundred ticks)."}},{"k":"ch_milestone20","v":{"s":"Milestone in 20: "}},{"k":"ch_milestone20_body","v":{"s":"Reach the 30% overlap milestone within 20 generations. Which of the three sliders gets you there fastest?"}},{"k":"ch_holdtogether","v":{"s":"Same pressure, same shape: "}},{"k":"ch_holdtogether_body","v":{"s":"With both ideals within 0.05 of each other, run 40 generations and keep the overlap proxy above 70%. Drift will try to separate them anyway."}},{"k":"predict_coevo_q","v":{"s":"After 30 generations at hunt pressure {value1} with the speed cost {value2}, what will the two populations look like?"}},{"k":"predict_coevo_both","v":{"s":"Both get faster; the capture rate stays about where it started."}},{"k":"predict_coevo_pred","v":{"s":"Predators pull ahead: the capture rate climbs."}},{"k":"predict_coevo_prey","v":{"s":"Prey pull ahead: the capture rate falls."}},{"k":"predict_coevo_flat","v":{"s":"Neither changes much."}},{"k":"predict_coevo_hint","v":{"s":"Auto-Run to generation 30 to test it."}},{"k":"coevo_observed_detail","v":{"s":"Generation {value1}: predator speed {value2} → {value3}, prey speed {value4} → {value5}, capture rate {value6} → {value7}."}},{"k":"explain_coevo_both","v":{"s":"The Red Queen: each side is the other's environment, so every gain is answered. Both populations are objectively faster than at generation 0 and neither catches or escapes more than before. Fitness is relative."}},{"k":"explain_coevo_pred","v":{"s":"Predators gained on prey. That happens when prey are selected less harshly than predators for a while, or when a prey crash reseeded slow prey. Watch a few more generations: the prey usually claw back, because the ones caught are the slow ones."}},{"k":"explain_coevo_prey","v":{"s":"Prey gained on predators, so captures fell. Hungry predators are now under strong selection for speed; expect the gap to close again. A permanent prey lead would starve the predators out."}},{"k":"explain_coevo_flat","v":{"s":"Little changed. Low hunt pressure means few captures, so few slow prey die and few slow predators starve; with weak selection on both sides the arms race never starts."}},{"k":"ch_redqueen","v":{"s":"Run in place: "}},{"k":"ch_redqueen_body","v":{"s":"Over 40 generations, get both mean speeds up by 0.3 or more while the capture rate ends within 0.1 of where it started. Everyone faster, nobody ahead."}},{"k":"sr_sequence_checked","v":{"s":"Order checked: {value1} of {value2} in the right place."}},{"k":"who_came_first","v":{"s":"🧩 Who came first?"}},{"k":"sequence_instructions","v":{"s":"Put these six milestones in order, earliest at the top, then check. Years stay hidden until you do."}},{"k":"a11y_move_up","v":{"s":"Move {value1} up"}},{"k":"a11y_move_down","v":{"s":"Move {value1} down"}},{"k":"check_order","v":{"s":"✓ Check order"}},{"k":"shuffle_again","v":{"s":"🔀 Shuffle again"}},{"k":"sequence_all_right","v":{"s":"All six in order after {value1} attempt{value2}."}},{"k":"sequence_partial","v":{"s":"{value1} of 6 in the right place. Swap the ✗ rows and check again."}},{"k":"sequence_takeaway","v":{"s":"Notice the gap: Mendel published in 1866, eight years after Darwin and Wallace, and nobody connected the two until 1900. The mechanism of inheritance sat unread while the theory that needed it spread."}},{"k":"ch_perfectorder","v":{"s":"Perfect order, first try: "}},{"k":"ch_perfectorder_body","v":{"s":"Sequence all six milestones correctly on your first check after a shuffle."}},{"k":"fewest_attempts","v":{"s":"fewest attempts"}},{"k":"ch_survive03","v":{"s":"Fast climate, living population: "}},{"k":"ch_survive03_body","v":{"s":"Survive a warming trial at 0.030 per generation or faster. Hint: more offspring variation widens the spread selection can work with."}},{"k":"fastest_warming_survived","v":{"s":"fastest warming survived"}},{"k":"ch_survive03lowvar","v":{"s":"Hard mode: "}},{"k":"ch_survive03lowvar_body","v":{"s":"Survive 0.040 per generation with Mutation Size at 0.02 or less. About one trial in four makes it; expect extinctions first, that is the point."}},{"k":"ch_plateau","v":{"s":"Hold the balance: "}},{"k":"ch_plateau_body","v":{"s":"With the predator ON for the whole run, get past round 30 (use Fast-forward) with mean showiness risen but still under 0.70. That is the peacock's equilibrium."}},{"k":"highest_mean_showiness","v":{"s":"highest mean showiness"}},{"k":"ch_dull","v":{"s":"Against the tide: "}},{"k":"ch_dull_body","v":{"s":"Set choosiness to 0.65 or higher, then drive mean showiness below 0.15 with your picks alone. The other females will fight you every round."}},{"k":"this_hunt","v":{"s":"this hunt"}},{"k":"run_a","v":{"s":"Run A"}},{"k":"run_b","v":{"s":"Run B"}},{"k":"visual_search","v":{"s":"visual search"}},{"k":"keyboard_rule","v":{"s":"keyboard rule"}},{"k":"mixed","v":{"s":"mixed"}},{"k":"sr_saved_as_run","v":{"s":"Saved this hunt as Run {value1}: {value2}, {value3} rounds, camouflage gain {value4}."}},{"k":"compare_two_hunts","v":{"s":"🆚 Compare two hunts"}},{"k":"clear_saved_runs","v":{"s":"Clear saved runs"}},{"k":"saved_hunts_caption","v":{"s":"Saved hunts compared"}},{"k":"run","v":{"s":"Run"}},{"k":"environment","v":{"s":"Environment"}},{"k":"rounds","v":{"s":"Rounds"}},{"k":"camouflage_gain","v":{"s":"Camouflage gain"}},{"k":"final_camouflage","v":{"s":"Final camouflage"}},{"k":"round_time_first_last","v":{"s":"Time first → last"}},{"k":"hunt_mode","v":{"s":"Mode"}},{"k":"runs_tied","v":{"s":"The two hunts gained the same camouflage."}},{"k":"run_x_gained_more","v":{"s":"Run {value1} gained more camouflage ({value2} vs {value3}). What differed: environment, number of rounds, who was hunting, or how selectively?"}},{"k":"replace_run_a","v":{"s":"Replace Run A"}},{"k":"save_as_run_a","v":{"s":"Save as Run A"}},{"k":"replace_run_b","v":{"s":"Replace Run B"}},{"k":"save_as_run_b","v":{"s":"Save as Run B"}},{"k":"save_run_hint","v":{"s":"Save this hunt, then hunt again (a different background, a different strategy, or a partner at the controls) and save that one too. Both appear on the chart and in a comparison table."}},{"k":"ch_camo90in8","v":{"s":"Sharp eyes: "}},{"k":"ch_camo90in8_body","v":{"s":"Hunting by eye (mouse or touch, no keyboard rule), reach 90% camouflage by round 8. Pick the most visible prey every time; each random click slows the population down."}},{"k":"fewest_rounds_to_90","v":{"s":"fewest rounds to 90%, by eye"}},{"k":"ch_readapt","v":{"s":"Two backgrounds, one population: "}},{"k":"ch_readapt_body","v":{"s":"After at least 3 rounds, switch environments mid-hunt and bring camouflage back above 80% on the new background."}},{"k":"camouflage_after_12","v":{"s":"camouflage after 12 rounds"}},{"k":"ch_streak10","v":{"s":"Ten in a row: "}},{"k":"ch_streak10_body","v":{"s":"Name the mechanism correctly ten vignettes running. Ask first whether humans chose the breeders (artificial), whether mates did (sexual), whether survival did (natural), or whether nothing did (drift)."}},{"k":"best_streak","v":{"s":"best streak"}},{"k":"ch_streak10_h","v":{"s":"Ten in a row: "}},{"k":"ch_streak10_h_body","v":{"s":"Ten pairs correct running. The test is always the same: shared ORIGIN (homologous) or shared JOB (analogous)? Appearance is the trap."}},{"k":"note_saved","v":{"s":"Saved to your journal"}},{"k":"sr_note_saved","v":{"s":"Field note saved to your journal."}},{"k":"my_field_note","v":{"s":"📝 My field note"}},{"k":"field_note_prompt_default","v":{"s":"What surprised you here, and what would you test next?"}},{"k":"field_note_placeholder","v":{"s":"Write one or two sentences in your own words…"}},{"k":"unsaved_changes","v":{"s":"Unsaved"}},{"k":"save_note","v":{"s":"Save note"}},{"k":"suggested_next_challenge","v":{"s":"Suggested next challenge"}},{"k":"challenges_met_of","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2} challenges met"}},{"k":"take_it_on","v":{"s":"Take it on →"}},{"k":"journal_copied","v":{"s":"Journal copied as text."}},{"k":"journal_copy_failed","v":{"s":"Could not copy. Select the page text and press Ctrl+C."}},{"k":"copy_as_text","v":{"s":"Copy as text"}},{"k":"field_notes_heading","v":{"s":"📝 My field notes"}},{"k":"no_notes_yet","v":{"s":"No field notes yet. Every simulator has a note box near the bottom; one honest sentence is enough."}},{"k":"predict_hw_q","v":{"s":"From p = {value1} with selection {value2}, mutation {value3}, migration {value4}: where will p be after 10 more generations?"}},{"k":"predict_hw_up","v":{"s":"Higher (A becomes more common)."}},{"k":"predict_hw_same","v":{"s":"About the same (within 0.05)."}},{"k":"predict_hw_down","v":{"s":"Lower (A becomes rarer)."}},{"k":"predict_hw_hint","v":{"s":"Press Step 10 to test it."}},{"k":"hw_observed_detail","v":{"s":"p went from {value1} to {value2} over the last 10 generations."}},{"k":"explain_hw_up","v":{"s":"p rose. Selection against aa removes a alleles every generation (fastest while a is common, slower as it hides in heterozygotes), and migration from a pool richer in A adds more. Check which force you had on."}},{"k":"explain_hw_same","v":{"s":"p held within 0.05. With every force at zero this is Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium: random mating alone changes nothing. It can also happen when forces are weak or when opposing forces roughly cancel (selection removing a, mutation making it)."}},{"k":"explain_hw_down","v":{"s":"p fell. One-way mutation turns A into a, and migration from a pool poorer in A dilutes it; both work regardless of fitness. This is how a rare allele can persist, or grow, with no selection for it at all."}},{"k":"note_prompt_hw","v":{"s":"Which force moved p fastest, and which one could never finish the job? Why is a model where nothing changes useful?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_drift","v":{"s":"Two lineages with identical rules ended at 0 and 1. Explain that to someone who thinks evolution always has a reason."}},{"k":"note_prompt_sandbox","v":{"s":"Which preset surprised you, and how did the histogram change shape? Say it without the word \"evolve\"."}},{"k":"note_prompt_beak","v":{"s":"How did your drought compare with the Grants' record, and why might a teaching model exaggerate? What would you measure on the island if you were there?"}},{"k":"check_yourself_2","v":{"s":"🧠 Check yourself (2)"}},{"k":"check_fish_q","v":{"s":"The tip says \"Ray-finned fishes\", not \"Fish\". Why is plain \"fish\" not a clade?"}},{"k":"check_fish_tetrapods","v":{"s":"Because tetrapods descend from fish ancestors, so a group of every fish descendant would have to include frogs, birds, and us."}},{"k":"check_fish_tetrapods_fb","v":{"s":"Right. A lungfish is closer to a moose than to a salmon. \"Fish\" as people use it means \"vertebrate that is not a tetrapod\", a group defined by what it leaves out. Ray-finned fishes, by contrast, share a real ancestor with fin rays and include all of its descendants."}},{"k":"check_fish_water","v":{"s":"Because some fish live in fresh water and some in the sea."}},{"k":"check_fish_water_fb","v":{"s":"Habitat has nothing to do with clade membership; salmon themselves move between the two. The problem with \"fish\" is which descendants it excludes, not where its members swim."}},{"k":"check_fish_sharks","v":{"s":"Because sharks have no bones, so they cannot be related to bony fish."}},{"k":"check_fish_sharks_fb","v":{"s":"Sharks ARE related to bony fishes (all are vertebrates with jaws). Their cartilage skeleton makes them a separate branch, which is a reason \"fish\" is a grab-bag of several branches, but the deeper problem is that the bony-fish branch also contains every land vertebrate."}},{"k":"check_fish_toomany","v":{"s":"Because there are too many species of fish for one clade."}},{"k":"check_fish_toomany_fb","v":{"s":"Clades can be any size: beetles alone number hundreds of thousands of species and form a perfectly good clade. Size is irrelevant; completeness (ancestor plus ALL descendants) is what matters."}},{"k":"note_prompt_phylo","v":{"s":"Which organism was hardest to place, and which trait finally decided it? What did the DNA toggle change, and what did it not?"}},{"k":"check_fused_q","v":{"s":"Click through the radius and ulna. In which animal are they fused into one rigid bone, and why would that be favoured?"}},{"k":"check_fused_horse","v":{"s":"The horse: a running leg needs a stiff strut, not a rotating forearm."}},{"k":"check_fused_horse_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. A horse never turns its palm up; rotation is useless weight on a leg built for galloping, so fusion won. The bones are still there (the ulna remnant sits at the elbow), which is the fingerprint of shared ancestry: modification of what was inherited, not a fresh design."}},{"k":"check_fused_bat","v":{"s":"The bat: the wing must be rigid to fly."}},{"k":"check_fused_bat_fb","v":{"s":"The bat's ulna is reduced and partly fused, but the wing is anything but rigid: the long finger bones spread and fold the membrane every stroke. The fully fused, load-bearing strut belongs to the horse."}},{"k":"check_fused_human","v":{"s":"The human: our forearm is one solid bone."}},{"k":"check_fused_human_fb","v":{"s":"Turn your palm up, then down: that twist is the radius rolling over the ulna. Humans keep two separate bones precisely because we rotate the forearm constantly."}},{"k":"check_fused_whale","v":{"s":"The whale: a flipper is a paddle."}},{"k":"check_fused_whale_fb","v":{"s":"The whale's radius and ulna are shortened and flattened into a stiff platform, but they stay two bones; the flipper is stiffened by connective tissue around them. Fused into ONE bone describes the horse."}},{"k":"note_prompt_ancestry","v":{"s":"Pick one bone and describe its job in two different animals. What does the same bone doing different jobs tell you?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_antibiotic","v":{"s":"Where did the resistant bacteria come from, in your own words? What would you tell a friend who wants to stop a course early?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_speciation","v":{"s":"The overlap proxy fell. What would you need to observe in the field before calling these two species?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_coevo","v":{"s":"Who was winning the arms race at the end, and what evidence on the chart says so? Where else does \"run to stay in place\" apply?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_timeline","v":{"s":"Which discovery was ignored the longest, and what changed so people finally listened? Who on this timeline had you never heard of?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_climate","v":{"s":"What was the fastest warming your population survived, and what decided it? Name one real species you think is near that edge."}},{"k":"note_prompt_mate","v":{"s":"Where did showiness settle with the predator on, and why there? What would a female peahen \"want\" if she could want anything?"}},{"k":"note_prompt_predator","v":{"s":"What did the prey look like at the start and at the end? Where did the new colours come from, if no prey ever changed colour?"}},{"k":"two_rims_one_canyon","v":{"s":"Two Rims, One Canyon"}},{"k":"g_allele","v":{"s":"allele"}},{"k":"g_allele_def","v":{"s":"One of the alternative versions of a gene. A population's allele frequency (p) is the fraction of gene copies that are one version."}},{"k":"g_variation","v":{"s":"variation"}},{"k":"g_variation_def","v":{"s":"Differences among individuals in a population. Selection can only act on variation that already exists."}},{"k":"g_heritable","v":{"s":"heritable"}},{"k":"g_heritable_def","v":{"s":"Passed from parent to offspring through genes. A trait built during life (bigger muscles, a tan) is not heritable."}},{"k":"g_natsel","v":{"s":"natural selection"}},{"k":"g_natsel_def","v":{"s":"Individuals with some heritable traits survive and reproduce more than others in a given environment, so those traits become more common. No goal, no foresight."}},{"k":"g_fitness","v":{"s":"fitness"}},{"k":"g_fitness_def","v":{"s":"Reproductive success: how many surviving offspring an individual leaves compared with others. Not strength, speed, or cleverness."}},{"k":"g_directional","v":{"s":"directional selection"}},{"k":"g_directional_def","v":{"s":"One extreme of a trait is favoured, so the population mean shifts that way."}},{"k":"g_stabilizing","v":{"s":"stabilizing selection"}},{"k":"g_stabilizing_def","v":{"s":"The middle is favoured over both extremes; the mean holds while the spread shrinks."}},{"k":"g_disruptive","v":{"s":"disruptive selection"}},{"k":"g_disruptive_def","v":{"s":"Both extremes are favoured over the middle; the distribution splits toward two peaks."}},{"k":"g_sexsel","v":{"s":"sexual selection"}},{"k":"g_sexsel_def","v":{"s":"Selection through mating success (being chosen, or out-competing rivals for mates) rather than through survival."}},{"k":"g_drift","v":{"s":"genetic drift"}},{"k":"g_drift_def","v":{"s":"Change in allele frequency from the random sampling of who happens to reproduce. Strongest in small populations; has no direction."}},{"k":"g_mutation","v":{"s":"mutation"}},{"k":"g_mutation_def","v":{"s":"A random change in DNA. The ultimate source of new variation; not aimed at what would be useful."}},{"k":"g_geneflow","v":{"s":"gene flow (migration)"}},{"k":"g_geneflow_def","v":{"s":"Movement of alleles between populations when individuals move and breed."}},{"k":"g_bottleneck","v":{"s":"bottleneck"}},{"k":"g_bottleneck_def","v":{"s":"A sharp drop in population size that leaves a random, smaller sample of the original variation."}},{"k":"g_founder","v":{"s":"founder effect"}},{"k":"g_founder_def","v":{"s":"A new population started by a few individuals carries only the alleles they happened to bring."}},{"k":"g_adaptation","v":{"s":"adaptation"}},{"k":"g_adaptation_def","v":{"s":"A heritable trait that raises fitness in a particular environment, and the process that produces it. Adaptation is local: what fits one environment can fail in another."}},{"k":"g_population","v":{"s":"population"}},{"k":"g_population_def","v":{"s":"All the individuals of one species living and breeding in an area. Populations evolve; individuals do not."}},{"k":"g_generation","v":{"s":"generation"}},{"k":"g_generation_def","v":{"s":"The step from parents to offspring; the unit in which evolutionary change is counted."}},{"k":"g_hwe","v":{"s":"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium"}},{"k":"g_hwe_def","v":{"s":"The null model: with no selection, mutation, migration or drift, allele frequencies stay constant and genotype frequencies are p², 2pq and q²."}},{"k":"g_genophen","v":{"s":"genotype / phenotype"}},{"k":"g_genophen_def","v":{"s":"Genotype is the set of alleles an individual carries; phenotype is the observable trait. Selection acts on phenotypes."}},{"k":"g_speciation","v":{"s":"speciation"}},{"k":"g_speciation_def","v":{"s":"One species splitting into two, marked by reproductive isolation: the populations no longer exchange genes."}},{"k":"g_repiso","v":{"s":"reproductive isolation"}},{"k":"g_repiso_def","v":{"s":"When two populations cannot, or do not, interbreed successfully. The test that decides species status; trait differences alone do not."}},{"k":"g_coevo","v":{"s":"coevolution"}},{"k":"g_coevo_def","v":{"s":"Two species evolving in response to each other, each forming part of the other's environment."}},{"k":"g_redqueen","v":{"s":"Red Queen effect"}},{"k":"g_redqueen_def","v":{"s":"In an arms race both sides keep improving and neither gains a lasting advantage: run to stay in place."}},{"k":"g_clade","v":{"s":"clade"}},{"k":"g_clade_def","v":{"s":"An ancestor together with all of its descendants."}},{"k":"g_synapomorphy","v":{"s":"synapomorphy"}},{"k":"g_synapomorphy_def","v":{"s":"A shared derived trait inherited from a common ancestor, marking a clade."}},{"k":"g_homologous","v":{"s":"homologous"}},{"k":"g_homologous_def","v":{"s":"Structures with a common evolutionary origin, whatever job they do now."}},{"k":"g_analogous","v":{"s":"analogous"}},{"k":"g_analogous_def","v":{"s":"Structures with a similar job but separate origins; the product of convergent evolution."}},{"k":"g_commonancestor","v":{"s":"common ancestor"}},{"k":"g_commonancestor_def","v":{"s":"The population from which two or more later lineages descend."}},{"k":"g_resistance","v":{"s":"resistance"}},{"k":"g_resistance_def","v":{"s":"A heritable trait that lets an organism survive a treatment such as an antibiotic or pesticide. It spreads when the treatment removes everything else."}},{"k":"g_selpressure","v":{"s":"selection pressure"}},{"k":"g_selpressure_def","v":{"s":"Any factor in the environment that changes which individuals survive and reproduce."}},{"k":"g_camouflage","v":{"s":"camouflage"}},{"k":"g_camouflage_def","v":{"s":"Colouring or pattern that makes an organism harder to detect against its background. In this lab it emerges from selection; no individual changes."}},{"k":"g_theory","v":{"s":"theory (scientific)"}},{"k":"g_theory_def","v":{"s":"A well-tested explanation supported by many lines of evidence, such as the theory of evolution or the germ theory of disease. Not a guess."}},{"k":"key_terms_n","v":{"s":"Key terms ({value1})"}},{"k":"a11y_class_snapshot_card","v":{"s":"Class Snapshot — paste student journals to see completion, prediction hit rates, misconceptions, and field notes"}},{"k":"teacher_4","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher"}},{"k":"class_snapshot","v":{"s":"Class Snapshot"}},{"k":"from_pasted_journals","v":{"s":"From pasted student journals"}},{"k":"class_snapshot_card_desc","v":{"s":"Students copy their journal as text; paste them here to see who finished what, which predictions held, the most common wrong first answers on each check, and every field note by module. Runs in the page; nothing is uploaded."}},{"k":"student_n","v":{"s":"Student {value1}"}},{"k":"teacher_tool","v":{"s":"Teacher tool"}},{"k":"class_snapshot_title","v":{"s":"Class Snapshot from student journals"}},{"k":"class_snapshot_intro","v":{"s":"Ask students to open My Journal → Copy as text and paste it to you (LMS, email, a shared doc). Paste everything you received below. The snapshot reads the data line at the end of each journal and shows completion, which predictions held, the most common wrong first answers on each check (your misconception map), challenges, and field notes by module. Nothing is uploaded; this runs in the page."}},{"k":"paste_journals_here","v":{"s":"Paste student journals here (any number, any order)"}},{"k":"paste_placeholder","v":{"s":"Paste the copied journal text. Each one ends with a line starting EVOLAB-JOURNAL-DATA:"}},{"k":"journals_read_n","v":{"s":"{value1} journal{value2} read"}},{"k":"unreadable_n","v":{"s":"{value1} data line{value2} could not be read"}},{"k":"print_snapshot","v":{"s":"Print snapshot"}},{"k":"snapshot_empty","v":{"s":"Paste at least one journal to see the class picture, or press “Show me a sample class” above to see exactly what this page gives you, using six made-up students. Clear it before pasting the real thing."}},{"k":"roster_n","v":{"s":"👩‍🎓 Roster ({value1})"}},{"k":"roster_caption","v":{"s":"One row per student journal"}},{"k":"student","v":{"s":"Student"}},{"k":"notes","v":{"s":"Notes"}},{"k":"completion_by_module","v":{"s":"✅ Completion by module"}},{"k":"completion_caption","v":{"s":"Students who completed each module"}},{"k":"students_completed","v":{"s":"Students completed"}},{"k":"predictions_by_module","v":{"s":"🔮 Predictions by module"}},{"k":"predictions_caption","v":{"s":"Predictions made, judged, and held per module"}},{"k":"made","v":{"s":"Made"}},{"k":"judged","v":{"s":"Judged"}},{"k":"hit_rate","v":{"s":"Hit rate"}},{"k":"no_predictions_in_journals","v":{"s":"No predictions in these journals yet."}},{"k":"misconception_map","v":{"s":"🧠 Misconception map: check-yourself questions"}},{"k":"misconception_map_hint","v":{"s":"The most common wrong FIRST answer is the misconception your class walked in with. Reteach that, not the whole topic."}},{"k":"checks_caption","v":{"s":"Check questions with solve counts and the most common wrong first answer"}},{"k":"check","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"answered","v":{"s":"Answered"}},{"k":"solved","v":{"s":"Solved"}},{"k":"most_common_wrong_first","v":{"s":"Most common wrong first answer"}},{"k":"no_checks_in_journals","v":{"s":"No check questions answered in these journals yet."}},{"k":"challenges_met_heading","v":{"s":"🏅 Challenges met"}},{"k":"no_challenges_in_journals","v":{"s":"No challenges met yet."}},{"k":"field_notes_by_module","v":{"s":"📝 Field notes by module"}},{"k":"no_notes_in_journals","v":{"s":"No field notes in these journals yet."}},{"k":"milk_drinkers","v":{"s":"Milk Drinkers: How Fast Can a Helpful Allele Spread?"}},{"k":"clear_pasted","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"day1_theme","v":{"s":"Foundation: Allele Frequencies"}},{"k":"day1_ticket","v":{"s":"In one sentence each: define equilibrium, define drift, and give one example where drift overwhelmed selection in a real population."}},{"k":"day2_theme","v":{"s":"Selection in Action"}},{"k":"day2_ticket","v":{"s":"Describe in words (or sketch on paper) the trait distribution before vs after directional selection, then the same for stabilizing selection. Say what happens to the mean and to the spread in each."}},{"k":"day3_theme","v":{"s":"Between Populations: Speciation & Coevolution"}},{"k":"day3_ticket","v":{"s":"Pick a real coevolutionary pair (bats/moths, plants/herbivores, predator/prey, host/parasite) and describe one specific innovation and its counter-innovation."}},{"k":"day4_theme","v":{"s":"Macroevolution: The Tree of Life"}},{"k":"day4_ticket","v":{"s":"Choose 5 organisms and describe how you would arrange them on a cladogram. Name one synapomorphy (shared derived trait) that defines each clade you use."}},{"k":"day5_theme","v":{"s":"Application & Reflection"}},{"k":"day5_ticket","v":{"s":"Scenario: your friend feels better after 3 days of a 10-day antibiotic course and wants to save the rest for next time. Write a 2-sentence response to your friend, then 1 sentence explaining the evolutionary mechanism behind your advice."}},{"k":"exit_tickets","v":{"s":"Exit Tickets"}},{"k":"ticket_saved","v":{"s":"Saved to your journal"}},{"k":"sr_ticket_saved","v":{"s":"Day {value1} exit ticket saved."}},{"k":"day_n","v":{"s":"Day {value1}"}},{"k":"ticket_placeholder","v":{"s":"Answer in your own words. Complete sentences, no jargon you cannot explain."}},{"k":"how_sure","v":{"s":"How sure are you?"}},{"k":"conf_sure","v":{"s":"I could teach this"}},{"k":"conf_mostly","v":{"s":"Mostly sure"}},{"k":"conf_unsure","v":{"s":"Still shaky"}},{"k":"words","v":{"s":"words"}},{"k":"save_answer","v":{"s":"Save answer"}},{"k":"five_day_unit","v":{"s":"Five-day unit"}},{"k":"exit_tickets_intro","v":{"s":"One short written answer per day, in your own words, plus an honest confidence check. Your answers save to your journal; when you copy the journal for your teacher they come along."}},{"k":"exit_tickets_heading","v":{"s":"🎟️ Exit tickets"}},{"k":"no_answer_yet","v":{"s":"no written answer yet"}},{"k":"no_tickets_yet","v":{"s":"No exit tickets answered yet. Open Exit Tickets from the menu; there is one per day of the unit."}},{"k":"tickets","v":{"s":"Tickets"}},{"k":"cmp_completed","v":{"s":"Completed"}},{"k":"cmp_predictions","v":{"s":"Predictions"}},{"k":"cmp_challenges","v":{"s":"Challenges"}},{"k":"cmp_notes","v":{"s":"Field notes"}},{"k":"cmp_tickets","v":{"s":"Exit tickets"}},{"k":"cmp_check","v":{"s":"Check: "}},{"k":"compare_caption","v":{"s":"Two students side by side"}},{"k":"pick_two_students","v":{"s":"Pick two different students to see them side by side: what each finished, where their predictions held, and which check each one first got wrong."}},{"k":"compare_two_students","v":{"s":"🔍 Compare two students"}},{"k":"student_a","v":{"s":"Student A"}},{"k":"student_b","v":{"s":"Student B"}},{"k":"exit_tickets_by_day","v":{"s":"🎟️ Exit tickets by day"}},{"k":"confidence_tally","v":{"s":"confidence: {value1} could teach it, {value2} mostly sure, {value3} still shaky"}},{"k":"no_written_answer","v":{"s":"no written answer"}},{"k":"no_tickets_in_journals","v":{"s":"No exit tickets in these journals yet."}},{"k":"humpback_whale","v":{"s":"Humpback Whale"}},{"k":"whale_traits","v":{"s":"Nurses its calf with milk; hair only as whiskers on the snout; breathes through a blowhole; no hind limbs, though tiny internal pelvic bones remain. Summers in the Gulf of Maine."}},{"k":"trait_blowhole","v":{"s":"blowhole, tail flukes; hind limbs lost"}},{"k":"tip_cetaceans","v":{"s":"Whales & dolphins"}},{"k":"syn_cetaceans_morph","v":{"s":"blowhole, flukes; affinities unresolved from skeletons"}},{"k":"node_cetartiodactyla","v":{"s":"Cetartiodactyla"}},{"k":"syn_cetartiodactyla","v":{"s":"shared DNA changes; double-pulley ankle in fossil whales"}},{"k":"syn_cetaceans_mol","v":{"s":"blowhole, flukes; hind limbs lost"}},{"k":"n_organisms_pick_one","v":{"s":"{value1} organisms: pick one, then its tip"}},{"k":"place_all_n_first","v":{"s":"Place all {value1} first"}},{"k":"tickets_in_app_note","v":{"s":"Students can answer each day's exit ticket in-app (Exit Tickets on the menu) with a confidence check; answers land in their journal and in your Class Snapshot."}},{"k":"open_exit_tickets","v":{"s":"Open Exit Tickets →"}},{"k":"how_evolab_works","v":{"s":"How EvoLab works"}},{"k":"five_moves","v":{"s":"five moves, every lab"}},{"k":"how_1","v":{"s":"Predict"}},{"k":"how_1_d","v":{"s":"Commit to an outcome before you run. The lab compares afterwards."}},{"k":"how_2","v":{"s":"Run and experiment"}},{"k":"how_2_d","v":{"s":"Change one thing at a time. Most \"Try this\" cards tick themselves when you do them; challenges are the harder targets."}},{"k":"how_3","v":{"s":"Check yourself"}},{"k":"how_3_d","v":{"s":"One question after your run. Wrong answers get their own explanation; the tool remembers what you first thought."}},{"k":"how_4","v":{"s":"Write a field note"}},{"k":"how_4_d","v":{"s":"One or two honest sentences in your own words. This is where the learning sticks."}},{"k":"how_5","v":{"s":"Journal it"}},{"k":"how_5_d","v":{"s":"Everything lands in My Journal. Copy it as text for your teacher; badges are earned by finishing, not by visiting."}},{"k":"ideas_you_revised","v":{"s":"Ideas you revised"}},{"k":"revised_summary","v":{"s":"You changed your mind {value1} time{value2} after seeing the evidence{value3}. That is the skill."}},{"k":"first_thought","v":{"s":"First thought: "}},{"k":"now_label","v":{"s":"Now: "}},{"k":"still_open_revisit","v":{"s":"Still open. Revisit the check when you are ready."}},{"k":"bernard_kettlewell","v":{"s":"Bernard Kettlewell"}},{"k":"boag_and_the_grants","v":{"s":"Peter Boag, Peter & Rosemary Grant"}},{"k":"richard_lenski","v":{"s":"Richard Lenski"}},{"k":"observed_evolution","v":{"s":"Observed evolution"}},{"k":"finish_round_by_rule","v":{"s":"Finish round by the rule ({value1} left)"}},{"k":"bottleneck_event","v":{"s":"⏳ Bottleneck event"}},{"k":"bottleneck_on_desc","v":{"s":"ON: at generation {value1} the population drops to 10 for 5 generations, then returns to N. Watch the lineages jump inside the shaded band."}},{"k":"bottleneck_off_desc","v":{"s":"OFF: N is constant for the whole run. Turn it on to see how a brief crash leaves a permanent mark on allele frequencies, even in a huge population."}},{"k":"bottleneck_enabled","v":{"s":"Bottleneck on"}},{"k":"bottleneck_disabled","v":{"s":"Bottleneck off"}},{"k":"ch_bottleneck","v":{"s":"Scar of a bottleneck: "}},{"k":"ch_bottleneck_body","v":{"s":"With N at 500 or more and the bottleneck on, get at least one lineage to finish at or beyond 0.8 or 0.2. Cheetahs carry exactly this kind of scar."}},{"k":"exp_bottleneck","v":{"s":"Bottleneck in a big population: "}},{"k":"exp_bottleneck_body","v":{"s":"Set N = 1000, turn the Bottleneck on, and run. Lineages that were flat for 50 generations lurch inside the shaded band and then freeze at their new level: five generations of small numbers did what a thousand individuals could not."}},{"k":"custom_landscape","v":{"s":"Custom — Design your own"}},{"k":"custom_landscape_desc","v":{"s":"You choose the fitness landscape: where the peak sits (ideal) and how sharply fitness falls away from it (width). Move the peak mid-run and watch the population chase it."}},{"k":"fitness_peak_ideal","v":{"s":"Fitness peak (ideal trait)"}},{"k":"fitness_peak_hint","v":{"s":"The trait value with the highest survival. Slide it while Auto-Run is on and the population will follow, lagging behind."}},{"k":"landscape_width","v":{"s":"Landscape width"}},{"k":"harsh","v":{"s":"harsh"}},{"k":"forgiving","v":{"s":"forgiving"}},{"k":"landscape_width_hint","v":{"s":"How far from the peak fitness stays high. Narrow = strong selection and a tight population; wide = almost neutral."}},{"k":"run_5_years","v":{"s":"⏭⏭ Run 5 Years"}},{"k":"sp_human","v":{"s":"Human"}},{"k":"sp_bat","v":{"s":"Bat"}},{"k":"sp_whale","v":{"s":"Whale"}},{"k":"sp_horse","v":{"s":"Horse"}},{"k":"sp_bird","v":{"s":"Bird"}},{"k":"sr_detective_started","v":{"s":"Bone detective started: five clues."}},{"k":"sr_detective_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"sr_detective_wrong","v":{"s":"Not quite. That was the {value1}."}},{"k":"bone_detective","v":{"s":"🔎 Bone detective"}},{"k":"bone_detective_intro","v":{"s":"Five clues. Each describes how ONE bone group is modified in ONE of the five animals. Name the animal. The clues come from the bone notes above, so compare limbs first."}},{"k":"detective_score","v":{"s":"Score {value1} / {value2}"}},{"k":"start_detective","v":{"s":"Start the five clues"}},{"k":"clue_n_of","v":{"s":"Clue {value1} of {value2} · {value3}"}},{"k":"which_animal","v":{"s":"Which animal?"}},{"k":"detective_right","v":{"s":"✓ Right."}},{"k":"detective_wrong","v":{"s":"✗ That was the {value1}."}},{"k":"detective_hint","v":{"s":"Click the {value1} above to see it highlighted in every limb."}},{"k":"see_score","v":{"s":"See score →"}},{"k":"next_clue","v":{"s":"Next clue →"}},{"k":"detective_perfect","v":{"s":"Five for five."}},{"k":"detective_result","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2}. Same bones, five jobs; the clue is always what the job did to the bone."}},{"k":"new_clues","v":{"s":"New clues"}},{"k":"ch_detective5","v":{"s":"Bone detective, five for five: "}},{"k":"ch_detective5_body","v":{"s":"Name all five animals from their bone modifications on one run of the clues."}},{"k":"best_detective_score","v":{"s":"best score"}},{"k":"sched_manual","v":{"s":"Manual toggle"}},{"k":"sched_manual_d","v":{"s":"You switch the antibiotic on and off yourself."}},{"k":"sched_continuous","v":{"s":"Continuous"}},{"k":"sched_continuous_d","v":{"s":"Antibiotic on every tick."}},{"k":"sched_course","v":{"s":"Complete course (15 on)"}},{"k":"sched_course_d","v":{"s":"On for 15 ticks from when you start, then off."}},{"k":"sched_early","v":{"s":"Stopped early (5 on)"}},{"k":"sched_early_d","v":{"s":"On for 5 ticks, then off: the patient felt better and stopped."}},{"k":"sched_pulsed","v":{"s":"Pulsed (3 on / 3 off)"}},{"k":"sched_pulsed_d","v":{"s":"Repeating 3 ticks on, 3 ticks off: missed doses."}},{"k":"sr_schedule_chosen","v":{"s":"Dosing schedule: {value1}. Press Auto-Run to follow it."}},{"k":"dosing_schedule","v":{"s":"💊 Dosing schedule (followed during Auto-Run)"}},{"k":"dosing_schedule_short","v":{"s":"Dosing schedule"}},{"k":"schedule_status","v":{"s":"Schedule started at tick {value1}; antibiotic is {value2} now. Reset the dish and pick a different schedule to compare, then capture both."}},{"k":"next_open_checks","v":{"s":"Retry {value1} open check{value2}: {value3}"}},{"k":"next_pending_predictions","v":{"s":"Run {value1} prediction{value2} you made but have not tested: {value3}"}},{"k":"next_finish_explored","v":{"s":"Finish what you opened: {value1}"}},{"k":"next_tickets","v":{"s":"Answer exit ticket{value1} for day{value2} {value3}"}},{"k":"next_not_started","v":{"s":"Not started yet: {value1}"}},{"k":"next_steps","v":{"s":"➡️ Next steps"}},{"k":"go","v":{"s":"Go →"}},{"k":"gene_flow_migration","v":{"s":"Gene flow (migration)"}},{"k":"barrier_complete","v":{"s":"Barrier complete (0)"}},{"k":"cross_per_generation","v":{"s":"cross per generation"}},{"k":"gene_flow_hint","v":{"s":"The fraction of each side that crosses the barrier every generation. Even a little gene flow fights divergence; strong selection can hold it off. Try letting the sides diverge, then open the barrier."}},{"k":"ch_holdwithflow","v":{"s":"Divergence against gene flow: "}},{"k":"ch_holdwithflow_body","v":{"s":"With gene flow at 0.05 or more and selection at 0.70 or more, reach generation 30 with the overlap proxy still under 50%. Selection holding a line against migration is how real species stay distinct across a leaky barrier."}},{"k":"secondary_contact","v":{"s":"Secondary contact: "}},{"k":"secondary_contact_body","v":{"s":"Let the sides diverge below 50% overlap with gene flow at 0, then open the barrier to 0.05 or more and keep running. Watch the overlap climb back: gene flow undoes divergence unless selection is strong enough to hold it."}},{"k":"sr_crash_over","v":{"s":"Predators are back. Prey mean speed went from {value1} to {value2} during the crash."}},{"k":"predator_crash","v":{"s":"🦦 Predator crash"}},{"k":"crash_running","v":{"s":"No hunting until generation {value1}. Prey face only the cost of speed; predators re-seed slow. Watch the blue line."}},{"k":"crash_done","v":{"s":"Last crash: prey mean speed {value1} → {value2}. Keep running to watch the arms race restart from the predators' side."}},{"k":"crash_idle","v":{"s":"Remove the predators for 20 generations (a disease, a hunt, a lost habitat). With the cost of speed on, prey speed relaxes a little: a few hundredths, because selection can only work with the variation there is. With it off, nothing pulls speed back down."}},{"k":"sr_crash_started","v":{"s":"Predator crash: no hunting for {value1} generations."}},{"k":"crash_in_progress","v":{"s":"Crash in progress"}},{"k":"start_predator_crash","v":{"s":"Start a predator crash"}},{"k":"ch_relaxed","v":{"s":"Relaxed selection: "}},{"k":"ch_relaxed_body","v":{"s":"Build the arms race up first, then run a predator crash in which prey mean speed falls by 0.04 or more. Use it or lose it, slowly: real relaxed selection takes far longer than twenty generations, and sometimes the prey lose the race outright and stay slow."}},{"k":"exp_crash","v":{"s":"Predator crash: "}},{"k":"exp_crash_body","v":{"s":"Run 20 generations with the cost on, then Start a predator crash. For 20 generations nobody hunts: prey speed drifts DOWN a little (fast prey pay a cost and gain nothing; the drop is small because the population has little variation left), predators re-seed slow. When hunting resumes, watch which side climbs first."}},{"k":"lim_pv_1","v":{"s":"Prey behaviour: real prey hide, freeze, and choose backgrounds; these only drift."}},{"k":"lim_pv_2","v":{"s":"A modelled predator with a search image (the keyboard rule has none; by eye you supply your own, and the search-image card measures it), and more than one kind of predator."}},{"k":"lim_pv_3","v":{"s":"Sex and recombination: offspring are copies of one parent plus noise, and the mutation step (σ 0.05 per generation) is far larger than real colour genetics."}},{"k":"lim_pv_4","v":{"s":"Population size is pinned at 30, so drift is stronger than in a real moth population."}},{"k":"lim_pv_dont","v":{"s":"Do not read the number of rounds as years, or the camouflage score as a measurement of any real species."}},{"k":"lim_mc_1","v":{"s":"Costs to the female of being choosy, and direct benefits of a mate (territory, food, care)."}},{"k":"lim_mc_2","v":{"s":"\"Good genes\" signalling, where the ornament advertises health rather than just taste."}},{"k":"lim_mc_3","v":{"s":"Male-male competition; here females do all the selecting."}},{"k":"lim_mc_4","v":{"s":"Twelve pairs and a few dozen rounds: drift swamps the slow rise of preference that Fisher's theory predicts."}},{"k":"lim_mc_dont","v":{"s":"Do not conclude that preference does not evolve because the cyan line wandered; the population is too small and the run too short to show it."}},{"k":"lim_cp_1","v":{"s":"Plasticity is one dial and one cap for everyone: real individuals differ in how far they can acclimatise, the capacity can itself be heritable and evolve, and acclimation usually costs energy; none of that is here."}},{"k":"lim_cp_2","v":{"s":"Moving instead of adapting: range shifts toward cooler places are the commonest real response."}},{"k":"lim_cp_3","v":{"s":"Generation time: one tick is one generation whether the species is a fly or a whale."}},{"k":"lim_cp_4","v":{"s":"Gene flow is only the rescue kind (migrants always suit the current temperature); the swamping kind, where immigrants drag a local population away from its own optimum, is not here. Nor are the other stresses (drought, food, disease) that arrive with warming."}},{"k":"lim_cp_dont","v":{"s":"Do not map the model's rate limit (about 0.03 per generation at σ 0.02) onto any real species; the shape of the result, that rate and heritable variation decide survival, is the transferable part."}},{"k":"lim_ss_1","v":{"s":"Genetics: the trait is one continuous number, not alleles at loci, so there are no dominance effects or recombination."}},{"k":"lim_ss_2","v":{"s":"A changing environment: the fitness landscape stays where you put it."}},{"k":"lim_ss_3","v":{"s":"Trade-offs between traits; only one trait exists."}},{"k":"lim_ss_4","v":{"s":"Realistic selection strength: at 1.0 most of the population dies every generation, far harsher than most natural selection."}},{"k":"lim_ss_dont","v":{"s":"Do not treat the preset labels (peppered moth, birth weight, snowshoe hare) as datasets; they are named fitness landscapes shaped to resemble the real cases."}},{"k":"lim_bl_1","v":{"s":"Everything but beak depth and seed hardness: weather, disease, predators, and competition with other finch species."}},{"k":"lim_bl_2","v":{"s":"Immigration and emigration; Daphne Major is unusually isolated, which is why the Grants could count every bird."}},{"k":"lim_bl_3","v":{"s":"A fixed 60 birds instead of a population that crashed from over a thousand to under two hundred."}},{"k":"lim_bl_4","v":{"s":"Heritability is a dial you set (default 1); the Grants measured beak depth at roughly 0.7 to 0.9 in this species, and surviving adults here keep their own beaks unchanged, so the whole-flock mean moves a little more than the chicks alone."}},{"k":"lim_bl_dont","v":{"s":"Do not compare your millimetres directly with the overlay; it is a classroom approximation of the record and the model exaggerates the shift so it is visible in a period."}},{"k":"lim_sp_1","v":{"s":"Reproductive isolation itself: the simulated generations never mate across the line. The mating trial tests it, but under a rule the trial assumes (success falls with trait difference), not one the model discovered."}},{"k":"lim_sp_2","v":{"s":"Hybrid fitness, behavioural and timing barriers, and chromosomal changes, which decide most real speciation."}},{"k":"lim_sp_3","v":{"s":"More than one trait; real divergence involves many, often unlinked to the ecology you can see."}},{"k":"lim_sp_4","v":{"s":"Asymmetric or seasonal gene flow; the slider swaps individuals evenly in both directions."}},{"k":"lim_sp_dont","v":{"s":"Do not call the two sides species when the overlap proxy crosses the milestone; say the traits diverged and name what you would still need to measure."}},{"k":"lim_co_1","v":{"s":"Any trait but speed: stealth, armour, toxins, group defence, and habitat use are all missing."}},{"k":"lim_co_2","v":{"s":"Population dynamics: both sides refill to 30 each generation (prey lose 30% to other causes on top of predation), so booms and crashes are faked by re-seeding."}},{"k":"lim_co_3","v":{"s":"Refuges and patchy habitat, which let prey escape without being fast."}},{"k":"lim_co_4","v":{"s":"Learning within a lifetime, on either side."}},{"k":"lim_co_dont","v":{"s":"Do not conclude that arms races always balance; the Red Queen pattern here follows from the model's symmetric rules, and real pairs often settle very unevenly."}},{"k":"lim_hw_1","v":{"s":"Chance: the population is infinite, so there is no drift at all (see the Genetic Drift lab)."}},{"k":"lim_hw_2","v":{"s":"More than one gene, more than two alleles, and any linkage between them."}},{"k":"lim_hw_3","v":{"s":"Selection is at most two fixed numbers (against aa, and optionally against AA); fitness that depends on frequency, on sex, or on a changing environment is not here."}},{"k":"lim_hw_4","v":{"s":"Overlapping generations and age structure."}},{"k":"lim_hw_dont","v":{"s":"Do not read a smooth curve as a prediction for a real population; it is the expectation with all noise removed."}},{"k":"lim_gd_1","v":{"s":"Mutation and migration: nothing enters or leaves, and selection, when you turn it on, is one fixed advantage for A."}},{"k":"lim_gd_2","v":{"s":"Family structure: every generation is a fresh random draw of 2·Ne alleles (the Wright-Fisher model). Ne here comes only from the sex ratio and the bottleneck; unequal family sizes and overlapping generations, which are why measured Ne is often a tenth of the census, are ignored."}},{"k":"lim_gd_3","v":{"s":"A constant N except during the optional bottleneck; real populations fluctuate every year."}},{"k":"lim_gd_dont","v":{"s":"Do not conclude that drift is weak in large populations; it is slow, which over enough generations is a different thing."}},{"k":"lim_ca_1","v":{"s":"The developmental genetics underneath: the same gene toolkit patterns every one of these limbs, which is stronger evidence than the shapes alone."}},{"k":"lim_ca_2","v":{"s":"Fossils that connect them, such as the fish-to-tetrapod and land-mammal-to-whale series."}},{"k":"lim_ca_3","v":{"s":"Only five forelimbs, drawn schematically; bone counts and proportions are simplified."}},{"k":"lim_ca_dont","v":{"s":"Do not treat similarity alone as proof of ancestry; the Homology vs Analogy challenge shows why the ORIGIN of a structure, not its look, is what counts."}},{"k":"lim_ab_1","v":{"s":"Horizontal gene transfer is reduced to one slider: a plasmid jumps to a touching cell at a fixed rate. Real transfer also crosses species, carries several genes at once, and depends on contact, density and the plasmid's own cost."}},{"k":"lim_ab_2","v":{"s":"The immune system, which finishes most infections after the drug has thinned them."}},{"k":"lim_ab_3","v":{"s":"Pharmacokinetics: a real dose rises and falls between pills; here a tick is either on or off at one strength."}},{"k":"lim_ab_4","v":{"s":"A continuous resistance number; real resistance is often all-or-nothing, and the dish holds 150 cells, not billions."}},{"k":"lim_ab_dont","v":{"s":"Do not turn a schedule that looks good in this model into medical advice; take antibiotics exactly as prescribed and ask a clinician before changing anything."}},{"k":"lim_ph_1","v":{"s":"Time and branch lengths: the tree shows order of branching only."}},{"k":"lim_ph_2","v":{"s":"Uncertainty: real trees carry support values, and the two hypotheses here are simplified to two moving branches."}},{"k":"lim_ph_3","v":{"s":"Thirteen organisms; the real tree of life has millions of tips, and every tip here stands for a whole group."}},{"k":"lim_ph_dont","v":{"s":"Do not read left-to-right as \"less to more evolved\"; every tip is equally old, and any node can be rotated without changing the tree."}},{"k":"what_this_model_leaves_out_title","v":{"s":"What this model leaves out"}},{"k":"move_pv","v":{"s":"Rerun Predator Vision on the projector with the histogram showing. After each round ask two questions: which prey changed colour (none) and which prey are missing (the visible ones)."}},{"k":"move_mc","v":{"s":"Put the predator ON and fast-forward to round 40 together. Ask where showiness stopped and why a showier male would leave FEWER chicks there. Fitness counts chicks, not years."}},{"k":"move_cp","v":{"s":"Run one trial at 0.010 and one at 0.040 side by side. Freeze on the generation the fast one collapses and count the survivors; adaptation is made of survivors."}},{"k":"move_ss","v":{"s":"Show the stabilizing preset at strength 1.0 with the SD read aloud each generation. The mean holds while the spread shrinks; selection is about who breeds, not about the average moving."}},{"k":"move_bl","v":{"s":"Run one drought year and then look at the bird grid: same birds, fewer of them. The average changed because the small-beaked birds are missing, not because any beak grew."}},{"k":"move_sp","v":{"s":"Ask the class what the overlap proxy actually measures (trait overlap of two histograms). Then ask what a field biologist would have to observe before using the word species."}},{"k":"move_co","v":{"s":"Show the capture-rate line next to the two speed lines. Both sides are faster, nobody catches more: fitness is relative to the other side, not to the past."}},{"k":"move_hw","v":{"s":"Set every force to zero and step twenty generations: nothing moves. Name it the null model, then turn one force on at a time and ask what changed."}},{"k":"move_gd","v":{"s":"Run N = 10 three times and list the five endpoints each time. Identical rules, different fates; ask which lineage had the harmful allele (none did)."}},{"k":"move_ca","v":{"s":"Click the phalanges in every limb: same bones, five jobs. Then ask whether the wing is homologous with the flipper (yes) and whether the bat wing and bird wing are the same wing (no)."}},{"k":"move_ca2","v":{"s":"Have students turn their own forearm palm-up and palm-down while looking at the horse leg. Ask what a galloping leg gains by fusing the two bones."}},{"k":"move_ab","v":{"s":"Set starting resistance and mutation to zero and apply the drug: the dish dies. Then set starting resistance to 2%: the dish comes back resistant. The drug never made resistance; it revealed it."}},{"k":"move_dt","v":{"s":"Put Darwin (1859), Mendel (1866) and the rediscovery (1900) on the board and ask what each one lacked that the next supplied. A theory that gains mechanism later is a theory that predicted well."}},{"k":"move_ph","v":{"s":"Build the amniote branch together and ask where the cardinal goes. A clade is an ancestor plus ALL descendants; a reptile group without birds is a group with a hole in it."}},{"k":"move_ph2","v":{"s":"Ask which is closer to a salmon: a lungfish or a trout. Then ask which is closer to a lungfish: a salmon or a cow. Fish is a lifestyle, not a clade."}},{"k":"move_title_check","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2} first answered \"{value3}\" ({value4})"}},{"k":"move_generic","v":{"s":"Reopen the check as a class and argue the wrong answer out loud before revealing the feedback."}},{"k":"move_title_pred","v":{"s":"Predictions missed in {value1} ({value2} of {value3})"}},{"k":"move_pred_body","v":{"s":"Run the module live and make the prediction together before pressing Start. Then run it twice more: missed predictions are usually about noise, not about the mechanism."}},{"k":"move_title_ticket","v":{"s":"Day {value1}: {value2} of {value3} still shaky"}},{"k":"move_ticket_body","v":{"s":"Reopen {value1} with the shaky students at the controls, then have them rewrite the exit ticket in one sentence each."}},{"k":"reteach_plan","v":{"s":"🧭 Reteach plan"}},{"k":"no_reteach_needed","v":{"s":"Nothing stands out yet: no check has a third of the class starting wrong, no module has most predictions missing, and no exit-ticket day is 40% shaky. Paste more journals, or celebrate."}},{"k":"practice_these_terms","v":{"s":"Practice these terms"}},{"k":"yes","v":{"s":"yes"}},{"k":"no","v":{"s":"no"}},{"k":"table_copied_n","v":{"s":"Copied {value1} rows. Paste into a spreadsheet: each column lands in its own cell."}},{"k":"table_copy_failed","v":{"s":"Could not copy. Select the table and press Ctrl+C."}},{"k":"data_table","v":{"s":"Data table"}},{"k":"n_rows","v":{"s":"{value1} rows"}},{"k":"copy_for_spreadsheet","v":{"s":"Copy for Sheets / Excel"}},{"k":"data_table_hint","v":{"s":"Make your own graph, then check it against the one above."}},{"k":"showing_last_rows","v":{"s":"Showing the last {value1} of {value2} rows; the copy has all of them."}},{"k":"data_table_empty","v":{"s":"Run the model and the rows appear here."}},{"k":"sr_sprint_started","v":{"s":"Term sprint started: {value1} questions."}},{"k":"sr_sprint_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"sr_sprint_wrong","v":{"s":"Not quite. {value1}: {value2}"}},{"k":"best_sprint_module","v":{"s":"Best sprint on {value1} terms"}},{"k":"best_sprint","v":{"s":"Best term sprint"}},{"k":"sr_sprint_done","v":{"s":"Sprint finished: {value1} of {value2}."}},{"k":"term_sprint","v":{"s":"Term sprint"}},{"k":"best_so_far","v":{"s":"Best so far: {value1}/{value2}"}},{"k":"sprint_intro_scoped","v":{"s":"{value1} quick questions on this lab's terms: match a definition to its term, then a term to its definition. Recalling a word is what makes it stick."}},{"k":"sprint_intro_global","v":{"s":"Ten quick questions drawn from every term in EvoLab. Half ask for the term, half for the definition. A perfect ten is a challenge."}},{"k":"start_sprint","v":{"s":"Start sprint"}},{"k":"sprint_result","v":{"s":"Sprint result"}},{"k":"sprint_perfect","v":{"s":"Every one. Try the whole-glossary sprint from the menu next."}},{"k":"sprint_one_missed","v":{"s":"One to revisit. Read it below, then run again."}},{"k":"sprint_n_missed","v":{"s":"{value1} to revisit. Read them below, then run again."}},{"k":"terms_to_revisit","v":{"s":"Terms to revisit"}},{"k":"question_n_of","v":{"s":"Question {value1} of {value2}"}},{"k":"score_streak","v":{"s":"Score {value1} · streak {value2}"}},{"k":"which_term_fits","v":{"s":"Which term fits this definition?"}},{"k":"which_definition_fits","v":{"s":"Which definition fits this term?"}},{"k":"answer_choices","v":{"s":"Answer choices"}},{"k":"sprint_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"sprint_it_was","v":{"s":"It was {value1}: {value2}"}},{"k":"finish_sprint","v":{"s":"Finish"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"retrieval_practice","v":{"s":"Retrieval practice"}},{"k":"term_sprint_page_intro","v":{"s":"All {value1} EvoLab terms in one pool. Pulling a word out of memory is a better test than reading it again; a term you miss is listed with its definition at the end. Every lab's Key terms card has a shorter sprint on just that lab's words."}},{"k":"sprints_by_lab","v":{"s":"Sprints by lab"}},{"k":"next_term_sprint","v":{"s":"Run a term sprint: ten quick questions on the words you have been using"}},{"k":"snapshot_copied","v":{"s":"Snapshot copied as text."}},{"k":"snapshot_copy_failed","v":{"s":"Could not copy. Select the page text and press Ctrl+C."}},{"k":"copy_snapshot","v":{"s":"Copy as text"}},{"k":"dt_hw_title","v":{"s":"Allele and genotype frequencies by generation"}},{"k":"col_generation","v":{"s":"Generation"}},{"k":"dt_drift_title","v":{"s":"Allele frequency of each lineage by generation"}},{"k":"col_lineage","v":{"s":"Lineage"}},{"k":"dt_sandbox_title","v":{"s":"Mean, spread and survivors by generation"}},{"k":"col_mean_trait","v":{"s":"Mean trait"}},{"k":"col_sd","v":{"s":"SD"}},{"k":"col_survivors","v":{"s":"Survivors"}},{"k":"dt_beak_title","v":{"s":"Beak depth and survivors by year"}},{"k":"col_year","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"col_mean_beak","v":{"s":"Mean beak depth (mm)"}},{"k":"col_drought","v":{"s":"Drought year"}},{"k":"dt_antibiotic_title","v":{"s":"Population and resistant count by tick"}},{"k":"col_tick","v":{"s":"Tick"}},{"k":"col_population","v":{"s":"Population"}},{"k":"col_resistant","v":{"s":"Resistant to A"}},{"k":"col_antibiotic_on","v":{"s":"Antibiotic on"}},{"k":"dt_speciation_title","v":{"s":"Trait means and overlap by generation"}},{"k":"col_left_mean","v":{"s":"Left mean"}},{"k":"col_right_mean","v":{"s":"Right mean"}},{"k":"col_overlap","v":{"s":"Overlap proxy"}},{"k":"dt_coevo_title","v":{"s":"Speeds, gap and capture rate by generation"}},{"k":"col_predator_speed","v":{"s":"Predator speed"}},{"k":"col_prey_speed","v":{"s":"Prey speed"}},{"k":"col_gap","v":{"s":"Gap"}},{"k":"col_capture_rate","v":{"s":"Capture rate"}},{"k":"col_predator_crash","v":{"s":"Predator crash"}},{"k":"dt_climate_title","v":{"s":"Temperature, tolerance and population by generation"}},{"k":"col_temperature","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"col_mean_tolerance","v":{"s":"Mean tolerance"}},{"k":"dt_mate_title","v":{"s":"Showiness, preference and predation by round"}},{"k":"col_round","v":{"s":"Round"}},{"k":"col_mean_showiness","v":{"s":"Mean showiness"}},{"k":"col_max_showiness","v":{"s":"Max showiness"}},{"k":"col_mean_preference","v":{"s":"Mean preference"}},{"k":"col_eaten","v":{"s":"Eaten by predator"}},{"k":"dt_predator_title","v":{"s":"Mean colour and hunt time by round"}},{"k":"col_hunt_time","v":{"s":"Time to eat 10 (s)"}},{"k":"col_kills","v":{"s":"Kills"}},{"k":"sprint_run_again","v":{"s":"Run it again"}},{"k":"sprint_best","v":{"s":"Best"}},{"k":"regime_up","v":{"s":"Directional (toward high values)"}},{"k":"regime_down","v":{"s":"Directional (toward low values)"}},{"k":"regime_stabilizing","v":{"s":"Stabilizing"}},{"k":"regime_disruptive","v":{"s":"Disruptive"}},{"k":"regime_drift","v":{"s":"Drift only (no selection)"}},{"k":"regime_up_diag","v":{"s":"Directional (up) would have dragged the mean toward 1."}},{"k":"regime_down_diag","v":{"s":"Directional (down) would have dragged the mean toward 0."}},{"k":"regime_stab_diag","v":{"s":"Stabilizing keeps the mean where it started and pulls the spread in."}},{"k":"regime_disr_diag","v":{"s":"Disruptive hollows out the middle and leaves two humps."}},{"k":"regime_drift_diag","v":{"s":"Drift alone leaves the spread wide and the mean close to where it started."}},{"k":"tell_up","v":{"s":"The mean climbed from {value1} to {value2} while the spread collapsed from {value3} to {value4}: one end of the range was favoured. Directional selection toward high values."}},{"k":"tell_down","v":{"s":"The mean fell from {value1} to {value2} while the spread collapsed from {value3} to {value4}: one end of the range was favoured. Directional selection toward low values."}},{"k":"tell_stabilizing","v":{"s":"The mean barely moved ({value1} → {value2}) but the spread fell from {value3} to {value4}: the middle was favoured and both tails were removed. Stabilizing selection."}},{"k":"tell_disruptive","v":{"s":"Two humps and an empty middle, with the spread still wide ({value4}): both extremes were favoured over the middle. Disruptive selection."}},{"k":"tell_drift","v":{"s":"Nothing was favoured: the mean wandered only from {value1} to {value2} and the spread stayed wide ({value3} → {value4}). The changes are sampling noise. Drift only."}},{"k":"sr_mystery_right","v":{"s":"Correct. "}},{"k":"sr_mystery_wrong","v":{"s":"Not this time. "}},{"k":"mystery_regime","v":{"s":"Mystery regime: read the distribution"}},{"k":"mystery_intro","v":{"s":"A hidden fitness landscape ran for 15 generations on 60 individuals. Look at the start-vs-now histograms and the numbers, then name the regime. Two clues decide most cases: did the mean move, and did the spread shrink?"}},{"k":"mystery_score","v":{"s":"Streak {value1} · solved {value2}{value3}"}},{"k":"mystery_best","v":{"s":"best streak {value1}"}},{"k":"next_mystery","v":{"s":"Next mystery"}},{"k":"new_mystery","v":{"s":"New mystery"}},{"k":"mystery_hist_title","v":{"s":"📊 Start vs after 15 generations"}},{"k":"mystery_numbers","v":{"s":"Mean {value1} → {value2} · SD {value3} → {value4} · share in the middle fifth {value5}% → {value6}%"}},{"k":"name_the_regime","v":{"s":"Name the regime"}},{"k":"mystery_right","v":{"s":"Right: "}},{"k":"mystery_wrong","v":{"s":"You said {value1}. {value2} "}},{"k":"move_hw_chi","v":{"s":"Sample 500 newborns on the projector, then step 10 generations under selection and sample newborns again: both fit. Then sample the adults. The test sees only what happened before the count."}},{"k":"sr_sample_result","v":{"s":"Sampled {value1} {value2}: chi-square {value3}, {value4}."}},{"k":"sample_departs","v":{"s":"χ² = {value1} > 3.84: this sample departs from Hardy-Weinberg proportions (p < 0.05)."}},{"k":"sample_departs_adults","v":{"s":"Selection removed aa individuals before you counted, and the test caught it."}},{"k":"sample_departs_newborn","v":{"s":"These newborns came from exact Hardy-Weinberg proportions, so this is a false alarm: the 5% threshold lets one sample in twenty through."}},{"k":"sample_fits","v":{"s":"χ² = {value1} < 3.84: no detectable departure from Hardy-Weinberg proportions."}},{"k":"sample_fits_adults","v":{"s":"Selection WAS acting on these adults and this sample could not see it: too few individuals for an effect this size."}},{"k":"sample_fits_newborn","v":{"s":"That is what newborns under random mating look like. It is not proof that nothing is evolving; p can still be changing between generations."}},{"k":"sample_test_title","v":{"s":"🔬 Sample and test: is it in equilibrium?"}},{"k":"sample_test_intro","v":{"s":"A field biologist never sees p. They catch some individuals, count genotypes, and ask whether the counts fit p², 2pq, q². Draw a sample from this population and run the chi-square test biologists use."}},{"k":"sample_size","v":{"s":"Sample size"}},{"k":"sample_newborns","v":{"s":"Sample newborns"}},{"k":"sample_adults","v":{"s":"Sample adults after selection"}},{"k":"sample_ten_times","v":{"s":"Repeat the last kind of sample ten times"}},{"k":"sample_adults_hint","v":{"s":"With selection at 0 the adults are the newborns; raise s to sample the survivors."}},{"k":"sample_from","v":{"s":"Sampled {value1} {value2} at p = {value3}. From the sample, p̂ = {value4}."}},{"k":"adults_after_selection_s","v":{"s":"adults after selection (s = {value1})"}},{"k":"newborns","v":{"s":"newborns"}},{"k":"chi_table_caption","v":{"s":"Observed and expected genotype counts with chi-square terms"}},{"k":"col_genotype","v":{"s":"Genotype"}},{"k":"col_observed","v":{"s":"Observed"}},{"k":"col_expected","v":{"s":"Expected"}},{"k":"chi_df_note","v":{"s":"df = 1 (three classes, minus one for the total, minus one for the estimated p̂); critical value 3.84 at α = 0.05."}},{"k":"sample_tally","v":{"s":"Departed: newborn samples {value1} of {value2} · adult samples {value3} of {value4}"}},{"k":"check_chi_q","v":{"s":"A sample of 500 newborns fits Hardy-Weinberg proportions (χ² = 0.4). What does that tell you?"}},{"k":"check_chi_not_evolving","v":{"s":"The population is not evolving."}},{"k":"check_chi_not_evolving_fb","v":{"s":"p can be changing between generations while each generation's newborns still sit at p², 2pq, q². The test looks inside one generation, not across them."}},{"k":"check_chi_proportions","v":{"s":"Genotypes are close to random-mating proportions for the current p; whether p is changing between generations is a separate question."}},{"k":"check_chi_proportions_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. A goodness-of-fit test checks one generation's genotype counts against p̂², 2p̂q̂, q̂². To see evolution you compare p across generations, which needs repeated sampling."}},{"k":"check_chi_no_selection","v":{"s":"Selection cannot be acting on this gene."}},{"k":"check_chi_no_selection_fb","v":{"s":"Selection that acts after birth leaves the newborns untouched. Sample the adults after selection and test again; that is where the departure shows."}},{"k":"check_chi_no_drift","v":{"s":"The population is large enough that drift is absent."}},{"k":"check_chi_no_drift_fb","v":{"s":"A goodness-of-fit test says nothing about drift. Drift changes p between generations; this test looks within one."}},{"k":"ch_false_alarm","v":{"s":"Catch a false alarm: "}},{"k":"ch_false_alarm_body","v":{"s":"Sample NEWBORNS until one sample departs from Hardy-Weinberg proportions anyway. Nothing was wrong with the population; the test lets one false alarm in twenty through."}},{"k":"exp_sample_newborns","v":{"s":"Sample the newborns: "}},{"k":"exp_sample_newborns_body","v":{"s":"In Sample and test, draw a sample of newborns and read the chi-square table. Then press ×10 and watch the verdict wobble from sample to sample."}},{"k":"exp_catch_selection","v":{"s":"Catch selection: "}},{"k":"exp_catch_selection_body","v":{"s":"Set selection to 0.5 or more and sample 100 or 500 adults after selection. The survivors are short of aa, and a big sample says so."}},{"k":"exp_small_sample","v":{"s":"Miss it with a small sample: "}},{"k":"exp_small_sample_body","v":{"s":"Same selection, sample size 30, adults after selection. The same effect is usually invisible; absence of evidence is not evidence of absence."}},{"k":"ch_mystery5","v":{"s":"Five mysteries in a row: "}},{"k":"ch_mystery5_body","v":{"s":"In the Mystery regime game below, name five hidden regimes correctly in a row from the histograms alone."}},{"k":"move_gd_het","v":{"s":"Run N = 10 three times with the heterozygosity chart showing. Three different sets of lineages, one dashed curve every time: ask what is random here and what is not."}},{"k":"het_caption","v":{"s":"Theory: at Ne = {value1} the average heterozygosity halves every ≈ {value2} generations. Observed five-lineage average: {value3}"}},{"k":"het_crossed","v":{"s":"fell below 0.25 at generation {value1}."}},{"k":"het_not_crossed","v":{"s":"still above 0.25 after {value1} generations."}},{"k":"het_title","v":{"s":"📉 Variation lost on schedule: heterozygosity vs theory"}},{"k":"het_observed","v":{"s":"observed average of 5 lineages"}},{"k":"het_theory","v":{"s":"theory (1 − 1/2N) per generation"}},{"k":"a11y_het_chart","v":{"s":"Heterozygosity over {value1} generations: observed average of five lineages against the theoretical decay curve. {value2}"}},{"k":"het_note","v":{"s":"Run it again: the red line wobbles differently every time, around the same dashed curve. Which allele wins is a coin toss; how fast variation drains away is set by N."}},{"k":"exp_half_life","v":{"s":"Variation halves on schedule: "}},{"k":"exp_half_life_body","v":{"s":"Run N = 10 or 50 for 100+ generations and read the heterozygosity chart: the five-lineage average should cross 0.25 near 1.4 × N generations. Run it again; the lineages differ, the schedule does not."}},{"k":"check_het_q","v":{"s":"Each of the five lineages wandered differently, yet their average heterozygosity tracked one smooth curve. What does that tell you?"}},{"k":"check_het_steer","v":{"s":"The simulator secretly steers the average."}},{"k":"check_het_steer_fb","v":{"s":"No: each lineage is an independent set of coin tosses. Averages of many random processes are predictable even when each one is not; that is the basis of all statistics."}},{"k":"check_het_rate","v":{"s":"Chance is unpredictable for one lineage but has a predictable average: variation is lost at a rate set by N."}},{"k":"check_het_rate_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Heterozygosity falls by a factor (1 − 1/2N) each generation on average. Which allele wins is a coin toss; how fast variation drains away is not."}},{"k":"check_het_unfit","v":{"s":"Heterozygosity falls because heterozygotes are less fit."}},{"k":"check_het_unfit_fb","v":{"s":"There is no selection in this model at all. Heterozygosity falls because sampling pushes p toward 0 or 1, where 2p(1−p) is small."}},{"k":"check_het_mutating","v":{"s":"Small populations must be mutating faster."}},{"k":"check_het_mutating_fb","v":{"s":"No mutation here either. Smaller N means a bigger random jump each generation, so p reaches an edge sooner."}},{"k":"sr_mating_trial","v":{"s":"Mating trial at generation {value1}: {value2} of {value3} cross-side pairs mated, {value4} of {value5} within-side pairs. Isolation index {value6}."}},{"k":"mating_isolated","v":{"s":"Largely isolated under this rule. A field biologist would still ask whether the few hybrids are fertile, and whether the rule holds outside the model."}},{"k":"mating_partial","v":{"s":"Partly isolated: the sides still interbreed often. Trait divergence is running ahead of reproductive isolation."}},{"k":"mating_not","v":{"s":"Not isolated: the two sides mate about as readily across the divide as within it. Looking different is not the same as being different species."}},{"k":"mating_trial_title","v":{"s":"💞 Mating trial: test isolation instead of eyeballing it"}},{"k":"mating_trial_intro","v":{"s":"The overlap proxy compares histograms. A species question needs a mating test. This trial pairs 50 random cross-side couples and 50 within-side couples under one stated rule: a pair mates with probability exp(−(Δtrait / 0.15)²), i.e. mating success falls as the traits differ, as if the trait were a courtship signal. That rule is an assumption of the trial, not a discovery."}},{"k":"run_mating_trial","v":{"s":"Run mating trial"}},{"k":"mating_trial_hint","v":{"s":"Step at least one generation first."}},{"k":"cross_side_pairs","v":{"s":"Cross-side pairs mated"}},{"k":"within_side_pairs","v":{"s":"Within-side pairs mated"}},{"k":"isolation_index","v":{"s":"Isolation index"}},{"k":"isolation_formula","v":{"s":"Isolation index = 1 − cross/within = 1 − {value1}/{value2} = {value3}, measured at generation {value4}. 0 means the divide makes no difference; 1 means no cross-side pair mated."}},{"k":"ch_isolated80","v":{"s":"Isolation 0.8: "}},{"k":"ch_isolated80_body","v":{"s":"Reach an isolation index of 0.80 or more in a mating trial. Then try to get there WITH gene flow at 0.05."}},{"k":"exp_mating_trial","v":{"s":"Test it, do not eyeball it: "}},{"k":"exp_mating_trial_body","v":{"s":"After the milestone, run a mating trial. Compare the isolation index with the overlap proxy: they measure different things, and only one of them is about mating."}},{"k":"si_lighter","v":{"s":"the lighter end"}},{"k":"si_darker","v":{"s":"the darker end"}},{"k":"si_even","v":{"s":"the whole range about evenly"}},{"k":"si_strong","v":{"s":"That is a clear search image."}},{"k":"si_some","v":{"s":"That is a mild search image."}},{"k":"si_none","v":{"s":"No search image showing: you hunted about as a random picker would."}},{"k":"si_round","v":{"s":"Round {value1}: {value2} of your 9 consecutive kills were within 0.10 of the one before; random picks from this population would give about {value3}. Your kills averaged {value4} against a population mean of {value5}, so you were hunting {value6}."}},{"k":"si_why","v":{"s":"Runs of similar kills are a search image: once you find one prey type you keep finding that type. Real predators do the same, which makes the common form pay and lets rare forms slip through (apostatic selection). Bond and Kamil showed it with blue jays hunting virtual moths (Nature 415:609, 2002); it is one way a population stays polymorphic."}},{"k":"your_search_image","v":{"s":"Your search image"}},{"k":"si_history","v":{"s":"By-eye rounds so far: {value1}"}},{"k":"si_try","v":{"s":"Try it: next round, hunt on purpose for whichever colour you have NOT been taking, and watch the number drop."}},{"k":"exp_search_image","v":{"s":"Catch your own search image: "}},{"k":"exp_search_image_body","v":{"s":"Hunt a round by eye and read the search-image card. If your consecutive kills are far more alike than random picks would be, you formed one. Then break it on purpose next round."}},{"k":"lim_bl_5","v":{"s":"One assumed rule for small-seed handling (deeper beaks take fewer tiny seeds a year) is what makes El Niño years favour small beaks here; the real trade-off was measured in the field, not assumed."}},{"k":"move_bl_reverse","v":{"s":"Run drought, drought, then El Niño, El Niño on the projector with the seed panel showing. Ask which beak is best; the only honest answer is: best for which year?"}},{"k":"move_ab_hgt","v":{"s":"Run plasmid transfer with the drug OFF on the projector and ask who is reproducing more (nobody). Then switch the drug ON and ask again. Selection is the second run, not the first."}},{"k":"seeds_drought_desc","v":{"s":"In drought the soft seeds are gone. Only birds that can crack the remaining hard seeds eat enough to live."}},{"k":"seeds_elnino_desc","v":{"s":"After an El Niño the ground is carpeted with tiny seeds and the big hard ones are scarce. Small beaks handle tiny seeds fast; a deep beak is clumsy with them and goes hungry."}},{"k":"seeds_normal_desc","v":{"s":"Normal year: seeds of every size and enough for everyone, so there is little selection either way."}},{"k":"col_el_nino","v":{"s":"El Niño year"}},{"k":"el_nino_on","v":{"s":"El Niño ON"}},{"k":"el_nino_aftermath","v":{"s":"El Niño aftermath"}},{"k":"check_reverse_q","v":{"s":"After the El Niño years the average beak got smaller again. Which statement is right?"}},{"k":"check_reverse_harmful","v":{"s":"Deep beaks had become harmful, so the birds evolved back to normal."}},{"k":"check_reverse_harmful_fb","v":{"s":"There is no normal to go back to. A deep beak was the best beak in the drought and the worst one in a year of tiny seeds; fitness is always relative to this year's food."}},{"k":"check_reverse_tracks","v":{"s":"With tiny seeds everywhere, small-beaked birds fed better and left more chicks. Selection has no fixed direction; it tracks the environment."}},{"k":"check_reverse_tracks_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. The Grants watched selection push beaks up after 1977 and down after 1983. Direction is set by the seeds of the year, not by any goal."}},{"k":"check_reverse_memory","v":{"s":"The population remembered its original beak size and returned to it."}},{"k":"check_reverse_memory_fb","v":{"s":"Populations have no memory. The mean fell only because deep-beaked birds went hungry on tiny seeds and left fewer chicks; had the drought continued, it would have kept rising."}},{"k":"check_reverse_mutation","v":{"s":"The wet weather caused mutations for smaller beaks."}},{"k":"check_reverse_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"Offspring beaks vary around their parents' by the same σ in every year. The weather changed who survived, not what was born."}},{"k":"el_nino_1983","v":{"s":"1982-83 El Niño: "}},{"k":"el_nino_1983_body","v":{"s":"Months of rain carpeted the island with small soft seeds and smothered the hard-seeded plants. In the dry years that followed, small-beaked birds survived better and selection ran the other way (Gibbs & Grant 1987, Nature 327: 511-513). There is no best beak, only the best beak for this year's seeds."}},{"k":"exp_el_nino","v":{"s":"Selection reverses after El Niño: "}},{"k":"exp_el_nino_body","v":{"s":"After at least one drought year, switch on El Niño aftermath and run two or more years. The mean falls: with tiny seeds everywhere, the deep beaks are the clumsy ones now."}},{"k":"col_plasmid_transfers","v":{"s":"Plasmid transfers"}},{"k":"plasmid_transfer","v":{"s":"Plasmid Transfer (HGT)"}},{"k":"no_plasmid_transfer","v":{"s":"Off: genes pass only parent to child"}},{"k":"per_contact_per_tick","v":{"s":"per contact per tick"}},{"k":"plasmid_transfer_hint","v":{"s":"Real bacteria also pass resistance genes sideways, by horizontal gene transfer: a plasmid copies itself into a neighbouring cell. Here a susceptible cell touching a resistant one (within 30 px) picks up its resistance at this rate. Try it with the antibiotic OFF."}},{"k":"exp_plasmid_spread","v":{"s":"Resistance with no drug at all: "}},{"k":"exp_plasmid_spread_body","v":{"s":"Reset, leave the antibiotic OFF, set plasmid transfer to 0.10 or more and auto-run. Resistance spreads to half the dish without a single dose. Then ask whether that was selection."}},{"k":"check_hgt_q","v":{"s":"With the drug OFF and plasmid transfer ON, resistance spread through the dish. Was that natural selection?"}},{"k":"check_hgt_outcompeted","v":{"s":"Yes: the resistant cells out-competed the others."}},{"k":"check_hgt_outcompeted_fb","v":{"s":"With the drug off, a resistant cell survives and divides exactly like a susceptible one. Nothing out-competed anything; the gene moved sideways into cells that were already alive."}},{"k":"check_hgt_sideways","v":{"s":"No: the trait spread sideways between living cells. Selection needs differences in survival or reproduction, and there were none."}},{"k":"check_hgt_sideways_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Horizontal transfer changes what cells carry without anyone leaving more offspring. Selection starts the moment the drug arrives and the carriers survive it."}},{"k":"check_hgt_mutation","v":{"s":"Yes: the plasmid is a kind of mutation."}},{"k":"check_hgt_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"A mutation makes a new variant in one cell's DNA. Transfer copies an existing gene from one cell to another; no new variant appeared."}},{"k":"check_hgt_nodrug","v":{"s":"No, because bacteria cannot evolve without a drug present."}},{"k":"check_hgt_nodrug_fb","v":{"s":"They can: mutation, drift and selection on other traits go on with or without an antibiotic. The reason this was not selection is that nobody reproduced differently."}},{"k":"lim_ab_5","v":{"s":"Two fixed guesses, chosen so that a cost of resistance can be seen to act: with the drug off 10% of cells are replaced each tick, and 5% of a resistant cell's divisions lose the plasmid."}},{"k":"g_vestigial","v":{"s":"vestigial structure"}},{"k":"g_vestigial_def","v":{"s":"A reduced body part that has lost most or all of its original function, such as whale pelvic bones: a leftover of ancestry."}},{"k":"g_fossil_record","v":{"s":"fossil record"}},{"k":"g_fossil_record_def","v":{"s":"The preserved remains and traces of past life in datable rock layers; its order of appearance is itself a test of evolution."}},{"k":"g_biogeography","v":{"s":"biogeography"}},{"k":"g_biogeography_def","v":{"s":"The study of where species live. Island species resemble their nearest mainland relatives, not species in similar habitats elsewhere."}},{"k":"ev_line_fossil","v":{"s":"Fossils"}},{"k":"ev_line_fossil_d","v":{"s":"Remains in rock, in datable layers: what lived when, and forms between forms."}},{"k":"ev_line_anatomy","v":{"s":"Anatomy & development"}},{"k":"ev_line_anatomy_d","v":{"s":"Shared body plans, leftover structures, and shared embryonic programmes."}},{"k":"ev_line_molecular","v":{"s":"Molecules"}},{"k":"ev_line_molecular_d","v":{"s":"DNA and protein sequences, including shared mistakes."}},{"k":"ev_line_biogeo","v":{"s":"Biogeography"}},{"k":"ev_line_biogeo_d","v":{"s":"Where species live, and which relatives live nearby."}},{"k":"ev_line_observed","v":{"s":"Observed directly"}},{"k":"ev_line_observed_d","v":{"s":"Evolution measured while it happened, in the field or the lab."}},{"k":"ev_tiktaalik","v":{"s":"Tiktaalik, about 375 million years old: a fish with a flat head, a neck and wrist-like bones, found in Arctic rocks of the age and type where a fish-to-land transition had been predicted."}},{"k":"ev_tiktaalik_why","v":{"s":"A transitional form found where and when the theory said to look is a risky prediction that came true."}},{"k":"ev_whales","v":{"s":"A series of whale ancestors (Pakicetus, Ambulocetus, Rodhocetus, Basilosaurus) whose hind legs shrink over about ten million years."}},{"k":"ev_whales_why","v":{"s":"Fossils record intermediate stages between land mammals and modern whales, in the order the theory expects."}},{"k":"ev_layers","v":{"s":"The fossil record is layered: mammals never appear below the first amphibians, and no rabbit has ever turned up in Precambrian rock."}},{"k":"ev_layers_why","v":{"s":"The order of appearance matches the branching order. One badly out-of-place fossil would be a serious problem, and none has been found."}},{"k":"ev_forelimb","v":{"s":"Bat wing, whale flipper, horse leg and human arm are built from the same set of bones doing different jobs."}},{"k":"ev_forelimb_why","v":{"s":"Same parts, different uses (homology) is what descent with modification produces."}},{"k":"ev_pelvis","v":{"s":"Modern whales carry small pelvic bones, remnants of hind limbs, with no legs attached."}},{"k":"ev_pelvis_why","v":{"s":"Vestigial structures make sense as leftovers from legged ancestors, which the whale fossils confirm."}},{"k":"ev_arches","v":{"s":"Human, chicken and fish embryos all form pharyngeal arches early in development; in fish they become gills, in us parts of the jaw, ear and throat."}},{"k":"ev_arches_why","v":{"s":"Shared developmental programmes point to shared ancestry: the same early structures are reshaped for different ends."}},{"k":"ev_gulo","v":{"s":"Humans and other primates carry the same broken vitamin C gene (GULO), disabled by the same mutations, which is why we all need vitamin C in our food."}},{"k":"ev_gulo_why","v":{"s":"A shared broken gene with identical damage is inherited from a common ancestor, not reinvented."}},{"k":"ev_cytc","v":{"s":"Cytochrome c, a protein every aerobic organism uses, is identical in humans and chimpanzees, differs by one amino acid from a rhesus monkey's, and by about a dozen from a dog's."}},{"k":"ev_cytc_why","v":{"s":"Molecular differences pile up with time since a common ancestor, so they line up with the tree built from anatomy."}},{"k":"ev_code","v":{"s":"All known life uses the same genetic code, with the same amino acids assigned to the same codons, apart from a few small variations."}},{"k":"ev_code_why","v":{"s":"One inherited code across bacteria, plants and animals is what a single origin predicts."}},{"k":"ev_finches","v":{"s":"The Galápagos finches fill many roles, yet all resemble a mainland South American finch more than they resemble birds filling the same roles on other islands."}},{"k":"ev_finches_why","v":{"s":"Island species look like the nearest mainland species, not like species in matching habitats elsewhere: they descend from whoever arrived."}},{"k":"ev_marsupials","v":{"s":"Australia's native mammals are almost all marsupials; placental mammals fill the same roles on other continents."}},{"k":"ev_marsupials_why","v":{"s":"Isolation lets one lineage radiate into roles that unrelated lineages fill elsewhere."}},{"k":"ev_islands","v":{"s":"Remote oceanic islands have native bats and birds but no native land mammals, amphibians or freshwater fish."}},{"k":"ev_islands_why","v":{"s":"Only groups that can cross open ocean arrive; the pattern follows dispersal, not habitat."}},{"k":"ev_lenski","v":{"s":"In Lenski's long-term experiment, one of twelve E. coli lines evolved the ability to use citrate after about 31,000 generations, with frozen ancestors kept for comparison."}},{"k":"ev_lenski_why","v":{"s":"A new metabolic ability evolving, watched directly, with the ancestors still in the freezer to prove it."}},{"k":"ev_daphne","v":{"s":"On Daphne Major the mean beak depth of medium ground finches rose after the 1977 drought and fell after the 1983 El Niño."}},{"k":"ev_daphne_why","v":{"s":"Natural selection measured in the field, in both directions, on tagged birds."}},{"k":"ev_moths","v":{"s":"The dark form of the peppered moth rose to near 100% around sooty industrial cities and fell again after clean-air laws."}},{"k":"ev_moths_why","v":{"s":"A frequency change tracked over a century as the environment changed, and confirmed by field experiments."}},{"k":"sr_evidence_started","v":{"s":"Lines of evidence: {value1} cards to sort."}},{"k":"sr_evidence_right","v":{"s":"Correct. "}},{"k":"sr_evidence_wrong","v":{"s":"Not quite: that is {value1}. "}},{"k":"lines_of_evidence","v":{"s":"Lines of evidence: sort the cards"}},{"k":"evidence_intro","v":{"s":"Common ancestry is not one argument but several independent ones that agree. Fifteen real findings, five lines of evidence: read each card and say which line it belongs to."}},{"k":"sort_again","v":{"s":"Sort again"}},{"k":"restart_sort","v":{"s":"Restart"}},{"k":"start_sorting","v":{"s":"Start sorting"}},{"k":"evidence_perfect","v":{"s":"Every card in its line. Now say in one sentence why five independent lines agreeing matters more than any one of them."}},{"k":"evidence_missed_n","v":{"s":"{value1} to revisit. Each is listed with its line and the reason."}},{"k":"card_n_of","v":{"s":"Card {value1} of {value2}"}},{"k":"score_n","v":{"s":"Score {value1}"}},{"k":"which_line","v":{"s":"Which line of evidence is this?"}},{"k":"lines_of_evidence_choices","v":{"s":"Lines of evidence"}},{"k":"finish_sort","v":{"s":"Finish"}},{"k":"next_card","v":{"s":"Next card →"}},{"k":"ch_evidence15","v":{"s":"Every line, first pass: "}},{"k":"ch_evidence15_body","v":{"s":"Sort all fifteen evidence cards into the right line of evidence on one pass. Then explain why five independent lines agreeing is stronger than any one of them."}},{"k":"darwins_moth","v":{"s":"Darwin's predicted moth"}},{"k":"shubin_daeschler_jenkins","v":{"s":"Shubin · Daeschler · Jenkins"}},{"k":"prediction_confirmed","v":{"s":"Prediction confirmed"}},{"k":"cer_claim","v":{"s":"Claim"}},{"k":"cer_claim_p","v":{"s":"One sentence that answers the question. \"The mean beak got deeper because…\""}},{"k":"cer_evidence","v":{"s":"Evidence"}},{"k":"cer_evidence_p","v":{"s":"Numbers from YOUR run: a before and an after, a survivor count, a rate. Copy them from the data table."}},{"k":"cer_reasoning","v":{"s":"Reasoning"}},{"k":"cer_reasoning_p","v":{"s":"Why that evidence supports the claim: name the mechanism (variation, who survived, what was inherited) and one thing the model leaves out."}},{"k":"note_shape","v":{"s":"Note shape"}},{"k":"note_free","v":{"s":"Free note"}},{"k":"note_cer","v":{"s":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning"}},{"k":"note_cer_hint","v":{"s":"The shape scientists use to argue from data."}},{"k":"cer_has_number","v":{"s":"includes a number"}},{"k":"cer_needs_number","v":{"s":"add a number from your run"}},{"k":"cer_parts_done","v":{"s":"{value1} of 3 parts written"}},{"k":"migrants_short","v":{"s":"migrants"}},{"k":"assisted_gene_flow","v":{"s":"🧳 Migrants from a warmer region"}},{"k":"rescue_on_desc","v":{"s":"ON: two individuals already suited to the current temperature arrive each generation and breed. Gene flow from an adapted neighbour can rescue a population that could not adapt fast enough on its own; conservation biologists call moving them on purpose assisted gene flow. Rate challenges and the record ignore trials with migrants."}},{"k":"rescue_off_desc","v":{"s":"OFF: the population must adapt from its own variation. Turn on to see whether gene flow from an already-adapted neighbour rescues a rate that went extinct."}},{"k":"migrants_on","v":{"s":"Migrants on"}},{"k":"migrants_off","v":{"s":"Migrants off"}},{"k":"exp_rescue","v":{"s":"Rescue by gene flow: "}},{"k":"exp_rescue_body","v":{"s":"Take a rate that went extinct at σ = 0.02, turn Migrants on, and run it again. Two suited immigrants a generation are enough; then ask what happens to a locally adapted population when the immigrants are NOT suited to it."}},{"k":"move_hw_balance","v":{"s":"Ask for a prediction first: with μ = 0.02 and s = 0.5, where will p stop? Then step 40 generations and compare with √(μ/s). Ask why cystic fibrosis is still with us."}},{"k":"move_gd_ns","v":{"s":"Set s = 0.05 and run N = 10 five times, then N = 200 once. Write the two tallies on the board next to Kimura's numbers (73% and ~100%) and ask what changed: not the allele, only N."}},{"k":"balance_title","v":{"s":"⚖️ Mutation-selection balance"}},{"k":"balance_pred","v":{"s":"Mutation keeps making a (μ = {value1}) and selection keeps removing it (s = {value2}), but only from aa. They balance at q̂ = √(μ/s) = {value3}, so p settles at {value4}. Current p = {value5}{value6}"}},{"k":"balance_reached","v":{"s":"(at balance)."}},{"k":"balance_why","v":{"s":"This is why harmful recessive alleles never disappear: heterozygotes hide them from selection while mutation restocks them. With a real mutation rate of 10⁻⁵ and s = 1, q̂ ≈ 0.003 and about 1 person in 160 is a carrier."}},{"k":"exp_balance","v":{"s":"Mutation-selection balance: "}},{"k":"exp_balance_body","v":{"s":"Set selection to 0.50 and mutation to 0.020, then step until p stops moving. It stops at p = 0.80, exactly where q̂ = √(μ/s) says, however you start. Change μ or s and predict the new resting point before you step."}},{"k":"selection_for_a","v":{"s":"Selection for A (s)"}},{"k":"neutral_pure_drift","v":{"s":"Neutral: pure drift"}},{"k":"fitter_than_a","v":{"s":"fitter than a"}},{"k":"selection_for_a_hint","v":{"s":"Give allele A an advantage and see whether chance can still beat it. What decides is the product N × s: below about 1 drift dominates; above about 10 selection nearly always wins."}},{"k":"kimura_title","v":{"s":"🎯 Will the better allele win?"}},{"k":"kimura_pred","v":{"s":"Kimura's formula: with Ne = {value1} and s = {value2} (4·Ne·s = {value3}), a lineage starting at p = 0.5 fixes A with probability {value4}%; pure chance would give 50%."}},{"k":"kimura_tally","v":{"s":"Your runs at these settings: A won (p ≥ 0.95) in {value1} of {value2} decided lineages ({value3}%), {value4} still undecided, over {value5} run{value6}."}},{"k":"kimura_no_runs","v":{"s":"Run the lineages a few times at these settings and compare your tally with the prediction."}},{"k":"kimura_note","v":{"s":"The neutral challenges and the heterozygosity curve assume s = 0; with selection on they are switched off."}},{"k":"exp_selection_loses","v":{"s":"A favoured allele lost by chance: "}},{"k":"exp_selection_loses_body","v":{"s":"N = 10, selection for A at 0.05, run 5 lineages. Kimura predicts A wins about 73% of the time, so expect it to be LOST in one or two lineages despite being better. Selection changes the odds; in small populations it does not decide."}},{"k":"exp_selection_wins","v":{"s":"Big numbers let selection decide: "}},{"k":"exp_selection_wins_body","v":{"s":"Same s = 0.05, N = 200: 4Ns = 40 and every lineage should end with A near 1 (exact fixation at large N can take longer than 100 generations). The same advantage that was a coin toss at N = 10 is a certainty at N = 200."}},{"k":"check_ns_q","v":{"s":"With N = 10 and A favoured by 5%, A was lost in two of five lineages. Which is right?"}},{"k":"check_ns_tooweak","v":{"s":"Selection was too weak to matter, so this was pure drift."}},{"k":"check_ns_tooweak_fb","v":{"s":"It mattered: it moved the odds of A winning from 50% to about 73%. It just did not decide. Weak and decisive are different things."}},{"k":"check_ns_odds","v":{"s":"In a small population chance can overrule a real advantage; the advantage only changes the odds."}},{"k":"check_ns_odds_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Kimura's formula says what decides is N × s. The same 5% edge is a near-certainty at N = 200, because sampling noise shrinks as N grows."}},{"k":"check_ns_hidden","v":{"s":"The lineages that lost A must have had a hidden cost to A."}},{"k":"check_ns_hidden_fb","v":{"s":"Every lineage used the same rule: A was 5% fitter in all of them. Only the random draws differed."}},{"k":"check_ns_threshold","v":{"s":"Selection only works when s is above 10%."}},{"k":"check_ns_threshold_fb","v":{"s":"There is no threshold on s alone. A tiny s wins reliably in a huge population; a large s can lose in a tiny one. The product N × s is what counts."}},{"k":"g_heritability","v":{"s":"heritability (h²)"}},{"k":"g_heritability_def","v":{"s":"The share of the trait differences among individuals that is genetic and so passed on; the slope of offspring on parents. Selection moves the next generation by h² × S, so at h² = 0 it moves nothing."}},{"k":"lim_ss_5","v":{"s":"Lasting heritable variation: with no recombination to reshuffle genes, selection uses up genetic variance within a few generations and h² \"now\" falls. In the finch studies heritability stayed high across cohorts measured years apart."}},{"k":"move_ss_herit","v":{"s":"Moth preset, heritability 0, mutation 0, data table open on the projector. Step ten times, reading S and then the mean aloud each time. Ask who survived (the dark ones) and who was born (the same old spread). Then turn mutation on and wait for h² now to climb."}},{"k":"breeder_story_none","v":{"s":"Nothing here is heritable, so each generation's survivors pass none of their advantage on: S keeps adding up while R stays near zero. Selection happened every generation; evolution did not."}},{"k":"breeder_story_mut","v":{"s":"You started with nothing heritable. Every generation, mutation (σ = {value1}) adds a little genetic variation, so h² now is {value2}; once it passes about 0.1 the population starts to follow the survivors. Mutation is where heritable variation comes from."}},{"k":"breeder_story_full","v":{"s":"With h² = 1 every difference between creatures is genetic, so the population moves by the whole selection differential each generation, give or take the luck of who gets drawn to breed."}},{"k":"breeder_story_part","v":{"s":"Only the heritable part of a survivor's advantage reaches its offspring; the rest was environment, and environment is not inherited. h² now is {value1} against the {value2} you set: selection keeps removing the genetic variation it acts on, and mutation puts some back."}},{"k":"breeder_run","v":{"s":"Whole run, {value1} generations: selection pushed by ΣS = {value2}; with each generation's h², the equation predicts a total move of {value3}; the population moved {value4}."}},{"k":"breeder_title","v":{"s":"📐 The breeder's equation: R = h² × S"}},{"k":"breeder_intro","v":{"s":"S is the selection differential: how far the survivors' average sits above or below the whole population's. R is the response: how far the next generation actually moved. Heritability is the link between them."}},{"k":"breeder_last_s","v":{"s":"Last S"}},{"k":"breeder_h2_then","v":{"s":"h² that generation"}},{"k":"breeder_pred","v":{"s":"Predicted R"}},{"k":"breeder_obs","v":{"s":"Observed R"}},{"k":"breeder_noise","v":{"s":"Any single generation's R wobbles by about ±{value1} from the luck of the draw at N = {value2}, so judge the equation on the run totals, not on one row. Near the edges of the trait range (0 and 1) the observed move also falls short, because a trait cannot go past the edge."}},{"k":"breeder_real","v":{"s":"Real numbers: Boag and Grant measured the heritability of beak depth in Daphne Major's medium ground finches at roughly 0.7 to 0.8 by comparing chicks with their parents. After the 1977 drought the Grants used this equation, with the survivors' S, to predict how much deeper the 1978 chicks' beaks would be, and the chicks came in close to the prediction."}},{"k":"col_survivors_mean","v":{"s":"Survivors' mean"}},{"k":"col_sel_diff","v":{"s":"S"}},{"k":"col_h2","v":{"s":"h²"}},{"k":"col_response","v":{"s":"R"}},{"k":"dt_sandbox_note","v":{"s":"S = survivors' mean − mean before selection; R = next mean − mean before; h² = the heritable share of the variation that generation."}},{"k":"heritability_h2","v":{"s":"Heritability (h²)"}},{"k":"h2_now","v":{"s":"now"}},{"k":"heritability_hint","v":{"s":"How much of the differences between creatures is genetic. Survivors pass on only the heritable part of their advantage; at 0 it dies with them. Takes effect at once, and \"now\" shows the share that is still heritable after selection has used some up."}},{"k":"seeing_inherit_h2","v":{"s":"The Heritability dial sets how much of a parent's difference from the average is genetic; only that part reaches the offspring, and R = h² × S says how far the population moves."}},{"k":"check_herit_q","v":{"s":"Peppered-moth preset, heritability 0, no mutation. Every generation the survivors averaged about 0.3 above the population, yet the mean never moved. Why?"}},{"k":"check_herit_weak","v":{"s":"Selection was too weak; a stronger pressure would have moved it."}},{"k":"check_herit_weak_fb","v":{"s":"Selection was as strong as the dial goes: most of the population died each generation and the survivors sat 0.3 above the average, which is exactly what S measures. Strength was not the problem; inheritance was."}},{"k":"check_herit_yes","v":{"s":"The survivors' advantage was environmental, not genetic, so their offspring were drawn from the same old distribution."}},{"k":"check_herit_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Each survivor was lucky in its environment, not different in its genes, and luck is not passed on. R = h² × S, and h² was zero. That is the difference between selection and evolution: selection is who survives; evolution is what the next generation inherits."}},{"k":"check_herit_mut","v":{"s":"Without mutation there was no variation for selection to act on."}},{"k":"check_herit_mut_fb","v":{"s":"There was plenty of variation: the histogram was as wide as ever and the survivors differed from the rest by 0.3. What was missing was HERITABLE variation. Mutation matters for a different reason: turn it on and it slowly builds genetic variation, and the response begins."}},{"k":"check_herit_gens","v":{"s":"Ten generations is not enough; it would have moved eventually."}},{"k":"check_herit_gens_fb","v":{"s":"Run fifty. With h² = 0 and no mutation the population is redrawn from the same genes every generation, forever. Time cannot inherit what the genes do not carry."}},{"k":"ch_lowherit","v":{"s":"Peak on a low heritability budget: "}},{"k":"ch_lowherit_body","v":{"s":"With Heritability at 0.3 or less from the start, bring the peppered-moth mean within 0.05 of 0.85 by generation 10. It fails more often than it succeeds at the default settings; one other dial changes that."}},{"k":"exp_noinherit","v":{"s":"Selection without inheritance: "}},{"k":"exp_noinherit_body","v":{"s":"Peppered-moth preset, Heritability 0, Mutation 0, Reset, then Step 10. Open the data table: the survivors sit about 0.3 above the average every generation (S), and the mean does not move (R). Selection happened ten times; evolution did not happen once."}},{"k":"exp_mutrebuild","v":{"s":"Mutation rebuilds heritable variation: "}},{"k":"exp_mutrebuild_body","v":{"s":"Same setup with Mutation back at 0.04 and 20 generations. For a while nothing happens; then h² \"now\" climbs as mutation builds new genetic variation, and the response begins. Mutation is where heritable variation comes from."}},{"k":"exp_breeder","v":{"s":"Only the heritable part responds: "}},{"k":"exp_breeder_body","v":{"s":"Snowshoe hare preset, Heritability 0.5, ten or more generations. In the equation card, selection pushed by ΣS, but the population followed by roughly the heritable fraction. Then read h² now: selection has been eating the variation it uses."}},{"k":"g_inbreeding","v":{"s":"inbreeding coefficient (F)"}},{"k":"g_inbreeding_def","v":{"s":"The chance that an individual's two alleles are copies of one ancestral allele. Inbreeding turns heterozygotes into homozygotes (Aa = 2pq(1 − F)) without changing p; it exposes recessive alleles, which is why it can be harmful."}},{"k":"lim_hw_5","v":{"s":"Inbreeding that grows on its own: here F is a dial you set, while in a real small population F rises every generation (by about 1/2N), which is why inbreeding and drift arrive together."}},{"k":"move_hw_inbreed","v":{"s":"Set F = 0.5 with every force at 0 and step ten times on the projector while the class watches the bars. Ask what changed (Aa halved) and what did not (p). Then add selection against aa and ask why it now moves faster: selection can only remove what it can see."}},{"k":"sample_departs_inbred","v":{"s":"These newborns were produced with inbreeding F = {value1}: fewer heterozygotes than 2pq. The test detected non-random mating, which is real, but p did not change; only a later generation's p can show evolution."}},{"k":"sample_fits_inbred","v":{"s":"Inbreeding at F = {value1} hid in this sample. The statistic runs about n·F² = {value2}, under the 3.84 line; a sample needs n above 3.84/F² ≈ {value3} before the heterozygote deficit shows more often than not."}},{"k":"never_500","v":{"s":"never (500 generations)"}},{"k":"n_generations","v":{"s":"{value1} generations"}},{"k":"inbreed_lead","v":{"s":"F = {value1} is the chance that an individual's two alleles are copies of one ancestral allele. Heterozygotes are converted into homozygotes: Aa = 2pq(1 − F) = {value2} instead of {value3}, and aa = q² + Fpq = {value4} instead of q² = {value5}{value6}. p is still {value7}: inbreeding repackages the alleles without changing how many there are."}},{"k":"inbreed_purge","v":{"s":"Selection against aa (s = {value1}) can now see more of the a alleles. From q = {value2}, halving q takes {value3} under random mating and {value4} at F = {value5}."}},{"k":"inbreed_purge_hint","v":{"s":"Turn selection against aa on and this card will compare how fast q falls with and without inbreeding."}},{"k":"inbreed_real","v":{"s":"Inbreeding depression is this exposure at hundreds of genes at once. Children of first cousins (F ≈ 0.06) face roughly a 5% chance of a serious recessive condition against about 3% for the general population. The Florida panther fell to a few dozen animals by the early 1990s, with kinked tails, heart defects and failing sperm; eight female Texas cougars released in 1995 restored heterozygosity and the defects receded. That was migration undoing inbreeding, and slider 4 models it."}},{"k":"inbreed_title","v":{"s":"🧬 Inbreeding: same p, fewer heterozygotes"}},{"k":"hw_primer_inbreeding","v":{"s":" (inbreeding, F): converts heterozygotes into homozygotes without changing p. On its own it is not evolution, but it exposes recessives to selection. Small population size (genetic drift) is the fifth condition, left to the Genetic Drift lab."}},{"k":"bars_inbred","v":{"s":"Bars show expected genotype proportions with inbreeding F = {value1}: AA = p² + Fpq, Aa = 2pq(1 − F), aa = q² + Fpq; they are not sampled observed counts."}},{"k":"5_inbreeding_f","v":{"s":"5. Inbreeding (F)"}},{"k":"random_mating","v":{"s":"Random mating"}},{"k":"inbreeding_hint","v":{"s":"The chance that an individual's two alleles are copies of one ancestral allele: about 0.06 for children of first cousins, 0.25 for children of siblings, climbing toward 1 in self-fertilising plants. It changes genotype proportions, not p."}},{"k":"balance_pred_inbred","v":{"s":"Mutation keeps making a (μ = {value1}) and selection keeps removing it (s = {value2}), but only from aa, which inbreeding at F = {value3} makes commoner than q². They balance where q² + Fpq = μ/s: q̂ = {value4}, so p settles at {value5}; without inbreeding it would rest at q̂ = √(μ/s) = {value7}. Current p = {value6}{value8}"}},{"k":"check_inbreed_q","v":{"s":"F = 0.5 with selection, mutation and migration at 0, ten generations stepped. Heterozygotes fell from 50% to 25%, yet p stayed at 0.50. Is the population evolving?"}},{"k":"check_inbreed_yes","v":{"s":"Yes: genotype frequencies changed, and that is evolution."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Evolution at this scale is a change in allele frequencies. Inbreeding reshuffles the same alleles into different genotypes: the same p, differently packaged. Nothing was inherited differently."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_no","v":{"s":"No: p did not change. Inbreeding only changed how the alleles are packaged into genotypes."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_no_fb","v":{"s":"Right. Non-random mating on its own is not an evolutionary force: it changes genotype proportions, not allele frequencies. It matters because it exposes recessive alleles; add selection against aa and p moves faster than it did under random mating."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_drift","v":{"s":"Yes: with fewer heterozygotes, drift must be stronger."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_drift_fb","v":{"s":"This calculator has an infinite population, so there is no drift at all. Real inbred populations are often small, which adds drift, but that is a separate force; the slider by itself changed nothing about p."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_lost","v":{"s":"Yes: heterozygotes are being lost, so the a allele will disappear."}},{"k":"check_inbreed_lost_fb","v":{"s":"Count the alleles: aa rose by exactly as much as Aa fell (Fpq went into each homozygote class), so a is as common as ever. Only selection, mutation, migration or drift can change that."}},{"k":"ch_purge_rm","v":{"s":"Random mating only (F = 0)."}},{"k":"ch_finish_purge","v":{"s":"Finish the purge: "}},{"k":"ch_finish_purge_body","v":{"s":"Same start (p = 0.50, s = 1.0, no mutation or migration), but reach p ≥ 0.99 within 30 generations. Under random mating that takes 98; find the dial that drags the hidden a alleles into the open."}},{"k":"exp_inbreed_none","v":{"s":"Inbreeding alone leaves p alone: "}},{"k":"exp_inbreed_none_body","v":{"s":"Every force at 0, inbreeding F = 0.5, Step 10. The Aa bar halves and both homozygote bars grow, but p does not move and the status banner says so. Non-random mating changes genotypes, not alleles."}},{"k":"exp_inbreed_purge","v":{"s":"Inbreeding exposes the recessive: "}},{"k":"exp_inbreed_purge_body","v":{"s":"Set p = 0.90 (a is rare, hiding in carriers), selection 0.5, and Step 10 with F = 0. Reset to the same p and repeat with F = 0.25: the inbreeding card shows how many generations halving q takes each way. Selection can only remove what it can see."}},{"k":"exp_catch_inbreed","v":{"s":"Catch inbreeding in a sample: "}},{"k":"exp_catch_inbreed_body","v":{"s":"F = 0.3, no forces. Sample 30 newborns: the test usually passes them. Sample 100 or 500: the heterozygote deficit shows. The statistic is about n·F², so the sample decides what you can see."}},{"k":"g_ne","v":{"s":"effective population size (Ne)"}},{"k":"g_ne_def","v":{"s":"The size of an ideal, evenly breeding population that would lose variation as fast as the real one. Unequal sex ratios, unequal family sizes and past crashes all push Ne below the head count; Ne = 4·Nm·Nf / (Nm + Nf) for a skewed sex ratio."}},{"k":"move_gd_ne","v":{"s":"Set N = 1000 with one breeding male and run it next to N = 10 on the projector. Same lurching lineages. Ask the class which number the sampling step can see (the parents, not the herd), then work Ne = 4·Nm·Nf/(Nm+Nf) for a real colony."}},{"k":"ne_lead_skew","v":{"s":"Drift is set by how many individuals breed and how evenly, not by the head count. With {value1} breeding males and {value2} females, Ne = 4·Nm·Nf / (Nm + Nf) = {value3}: this population of {value4} drifts like an even-ratio population of {value3}. The rarer sex sets the pace, because half of every generation's genes come through it."}},{"k":"ne_lead_even","v":{"s":"With {value1} breeding males and {value1} females, Ne = 4·Nm·Nf / (Nm + Nf) = {value2}, the same as the census. Skew the sex ratio and watch Ne fall: the rarer sex sets the pace, because half of every generation's genes come through it."}},{"k":"ne_harmonic","v":{"s":"Over the last run the population drifted like one of Ne ≈ {value1}: the harmonic mean of the generation sizes, which the {value2} bottleneck generations at 10 dominate. One bad decade outweighs a century of plenty."}},{"k":"ne_inbreeding","v":{"s":"Inbreeding rises by about 1/(2Ne) each generation: at Ne = {value1} that is {value2}% a generation, so after 10 generations F ≈ {value3} and after 50 F ≈ {value4} (the Hardy-Weinberg lab's slider 5 shows what that does to genotypes)."}},{"k":"ne_real","v":{"s":"Northern elephant seals breed in harems where one bull can father most of a beach's pups, so their Ne sits far below the head count, and their 1890s crash to about twenty animals is still written in their DNA. Conservation biologists use a rule of thumb from this arithmetic: an Ne below about 50 risks inbreeding depression within a few generations, and below about 500 a population slowly loses the variation it would need to adapt."}},{"k":"ne_title","v":{"s":"👥 The size that matters: effective population size"}},{"k":"breeding_males","v":{"s":"Breeding males (of N)"}},{"k":"breeding_males_hint","v":{"s":"How many of the N breeders are male; the rest are female. An even ratio gives Ne = N. One harem bull among hundreds of females gives Ne ≈ 4, and the sampling step then draws 2·Ne alleles, so the census stops mattering."}},{"k":"check_ne_q","v":{"s":"A colony of 200 elephant seals has 10 breeding bulls and 190 breeding females. Which number sets the pace of drift?"}},{"k":"check_ne_census","v":{"s":"200: every animal in the colony counts."}},{"k":"check_ne_census_fb","v":{"s":"Head count is not what the sampling step sees. Half of every pup's genes come through one of only 10 bulls, so those 10 act as a tiny bottleneck every single generation. Ne = 4 × 10 × 190 / 200 = 38."}},{"k":"check_ne_ne","v":{"s":"38: the effective size, set mostly by the rarer sex."}},{"k":"check_ne_ne_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Ne = 4·Nm·Nf / (Nm + Nf) = 38, so this colony of 200 drifts like an even-ratio colony of 38 and loses heterozygosity at about 1/76 a generation. Skew the slider to 10 of 200 and run it to see the lineages behave that way."}},{"k":"check_ne_males","v":{"s":"10: only the bulls matter."}},{"k":"check_ne_males_fb","v":{"s":"The bulls set the pace but the females still carry half the genes: Ne is 38, not 10. The formula weights the rarer sex heavily without ignoring the other."}},{"k":"check_ne_half","v":{"s":"100: half the census, since only one sex breeds at a time."}},{"k":"check_ne_half_fb","v":{"s":"Both sexes breed every generation. The loss comes from the skew: 4·10·190/200 = 38. With 100 bulls and 100 cows it would be the full 200."}},{"k":"ch_netiny","v":{"s":"Drift like ten with a thousand: "}},{"k":"ch_netiny_body","v":{"s":"At N = 1000, choose a breeding sex ratio that brings Ne to 12 or below, then get all five lineages fixed at 0 or 1 within 100 generations. A thousand animals, and the sampling step never sees more than a dozen."}},{"k":"exp_harem","v":{"s":"Census size lies: "}},{"k":"exp_harem_body","v":{"s":"N = 200 or 1000 with ONE breeding male, s = 0, 50 generations. Every lineage is decided within the run, exactly as N = 10 would be, because Ne = 4·1·(N−1)/N is about 4 whatever the head count. Elephant seals live this every breeding season."}},{"k":"lim_ab_6","v":{"s":"Two drugs that share one dose dial and kill independently. Real combinations can help or hinder each other, one resistance mechanism can cover several drugs (cross-resistance), and the plasmid here carries resistance to drug A only."}},{"k":"move_ab_combo","v":{"s":"On the projector run A alone to a resistant dish, Reset, then A + B from the same start. Ask what the second drug did that a bigger dose of the first could not. Then put the calculator on screen with N = 10⁸ and work the TB numbers together."}},{"k":"sched_combo","v":{"s":"A + B together (15 on)"}},{"k":"sched_combo_d","v":{"s":"Both drugs on for 15 ticks from when you start, then off: combination therapy."}},{"k":"sched_sequential","v":{"s":"A then B (15 + 15)"}},{"k":"sched_sequential_d","v":{"s":"Drug A alone for 15 ticks, then drug B alone for 15: one drug at a time."}},{"k":"combo_verdict_present","v":{"s":"About {value1} cells resistant to the whole regimen are there before the first dose. The drugs will select them; resistance is not created by treatment, it is revealed by it."}},{"k":"combo_verdict_absent","v":{"s":"Fewer than one such cell is expected ({value1}), so only about {value2}% of patients start with any. For the rest, the regimen meets no cell that can survive it."}},{"k":"combo_under","v":{"s":"under 0.01"}},{"k":"combo_live","v":{"s":"In the dish now: {value1} of {value2} cells resistant to drug A, {value3} to drug B, {value4} to both. With {value5}% starting resistance to each drug, the expected number of double-resistant cells at the start is 150 × {value6}² = {value7}: {value8}"}},{"k":"combo_live_none","v":{"s":"usually none, which is why the combination clears the dish."}},{"k":"combo_live_some","v":{"s":"some, and the combination will find them."}},{"k":"combo_sequential","v":{"s":"One drug at a time is the trap. After drug A alone, the survivors are the N × f cells that resisted it; they regrow to N. Drug B alone then meets N × f B-resistant cells among THEM, resistance to both is assembled one step at a time, and an interrupted course can act the same way."}},{"k":"combo_tb","v":{"s":"Real numbers: a tuberculosis lung cavity holds about 10⁸ bacilli. Roughly 1 in 10⁶ is already resistant to isoniazid and 1 in 10⁸ to rifampicin, so a cavity holds about a hundred of the first and one of the second, but only 1 in 10¹⁴ resists both. That is why TB is never treated with a single drug, and why an interrupted course breeds multidrug-resistant TB."}},{"k":"combo_title","v":{"s":"🧪 Why doctors combine drugs"}},{"k":"combo_how","v":{"s":"Drug B kills by the same rule as drug A but tests a separate resistance trait, so a cell survives both only if it resists both. Switch Drug B on with the antibiotic, or pick the A + B or A then B schedule."}},{"k":"combo_calc_title","v":{"s":"Calculator: is the regimen already beaten?"}},{"k":"combo_calc_n","v":{"s":"Bacteria in the patient, N = 10^"}},{"k":"combo_calc_f","v":{"s":"Already resistant to any one drug, 1 in 10^"}},{"k":"combo_calc_k","v":{"s":"Drugs in the regimen"}},{"k":"combo_calc_result","v":{"s":"Cells already resistant to all {value1} drug{value2}: N × f^{value1} = {value3} × ({value4})^{value1} = {value5}"}},{"k":"col_resistant_b","v":{"s":"Resistant to B"}},{"k":"col_both_drugs","v":{"s":"Both"}},{"k":"col_drug_b_on","v":{"s":"Drug B on"}},{"k":"aria_drug_b_on","v":{"s":"Drug B on; press to switch it off"}},{"k":"aria_drug_b_off","v":{"s":"Apply drug B, a second antibiotic with its own resistance"}},{"k":"check_combo_q","v":{"s":"Drug A alone left the dish 90% resistant. Drug B alone would do the same. Yet A and B together cleared it. Why?"}},{"k":"check_combo_stronger","v":{"s":"Two drugs are simply twice as strong."}},{"k":"check_combo_stronger_fb","v":{"s":"Strength is not it. A cell resistant to A shrugs A off however much of it there is; doubling the dose of A would change nothing. B works because it asks a DIFFERENT question, one that cell cannot answer."}},{"k":"check_combo_both","v":{"s":"A cell must resist both at once, and with 2% resistant to each, double resistance is about 0.04%: none in a dish of 150."}},{"k":"check_combo_both_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Each drug alone had a few resistant cells to select from the start; together they had none, so there was nothing to select. That is the whole logic of combination therapy, and the calculator shows the same arithmetic at the scale of a real infection."}},{"k":"check_combo_targets","v":{"s":"Drug B kills the A-resistant cells specifically."}},{"k":"check_combo_targets_fb","v":{"s":"B knows nothing about A. It kills any cell with low resistance to B, and nearly all the A-resistant cells happen to be such cells because the two resistances are independent. Run A alone first and B alone second and B still kills them."}},{"k":"check_combo_mutation","v":{"s":"Mutation stopped once the second drug was added."}},{"k":"check_combo_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"Mutation went on exactly as before. It had no double-resistant cell to start from and too few survivors to build one before the dish was empty. Raise starting resistance to 20% and the combination fails: double-resistant cells are there from tick 0."}},{"k":"ch_eradicate_single","v":{"s":"Drug A on its own."}},{"k":"ch_combo5","v":{"s":"Two drugs, gone in five: "}},{"k":"ch_combo5_body","v":{"s":"From a fresh dish, switch on drug A and drug B together and empty it within 5 ticks. At the default 2% start it works about four times in five; the dose dial changes the odds."}},{"k":"exp_combo","v":{"s":"Two drugs at once: "}},{"k":"exp_combo_body","v":{"s":"Reset, switch on Drug B together with the antibiotic (or pick the A + B schedule) and Auto-Run. Drug A alone leaves a resistant dish more than eight times in ten; the same dose of two drugs usually clears it, because no cell was resistant to both."}},{"k":"exp_preexisting","v":{"s":"When the combination fails: "}},{"k":"exp_preexisting_body","v":{"s":"Set Starting Resistance to 20%, Reset, and run A + B for 20 ticks. Now about six cells resist both drugs from the start, and they take over. Combination therapy only works when double resistance is absent before the first dose; the calculator says when that is."}},{"k":"g_parsimony","v":{"s":"parsimony"}},{"k":"g_parsimony_def","v":{"s":"Prefer the tree that needs the fewest evolutionary changes to explain the characters. It is how trees are chosen from data; it is not a claim that evolution takes short cuts."}},{"k":"lim_ph_4","v":{"s":"Parsimony only, on binary characters: real studies also fit likelihood and Bayesian models of how DNA changes, and the step-counter's DNA site counts are illustrative rather than the published numbers."}},{"k":"move_ph_pars","v":{"s":"Put the step-counter on the projector with skeletons only and ask which row decides (the skull). Then load the genome sample and ask why one character lost to twelve. Finish by unticking the skull row so the class sees it cost Tree B exactly one step: a reversal, not an error."}},{"k":"pars_mammal","v":{"s":"Bear (mammal)"}},{"k":"pars_squamate","v":{"s":"Snake (squamate)"}},{"k":"pars_turtle","v":{"s":"Turtle"}},{"k":"pars_bird","v":{"s":"Cardinal (bird)"}},{"k":"pars_tree_a","v":{"s":"Tree A · skeletons: turtle outside the diapsids"}},{"k":"pars_tree_b","v":{"s":"Tree B · DNA: turtle beside the birds"}},{"k":"pars_c_scales","v":{"s":"Dry scales"}},{"k":"pars_c_feathers","v":{"s":"Feathers"}},{"k":"pars_c_shell","v":{"s":"Bony shell"}},{"k":"pars_c_tongue","v":{"s":"Forked tongue"}},{"k":"pars_c_skull","v":{"s":"Two openings behind each eye socket (diapsid skull)"}},{"k":"pars_c_amnion","v":{"s":"Amniotic egg"}},{"k":"pars_c_hair","v":{"s":"Hair and milk"}},{"k":"pars_verdict_tie","v":{"s":"Both trees need {value1} steps: the characters you included cannot tell them apart."}},{"k":"pars_verdict_a","v":{"s":"Tree A needs {value1} steps, Tree B {value2}: parsimony prefers the skeletal tree by {value3}. This is where turtles sat for most of the 20th century, on the strength of one skull character."}},{"k":"pars_verdict_b","v":{"s":"Tree B needs {value1} steps, Tree A {value2}: parsimony prefers the DNA tree by {value3}. Many independent sites outvote one skull character, and the turtle's solid skull is read as a later LOSS of the openings, which costs one step on Tree B."}},{"k":"pars_intro","v":{"s":"Trees are not read off organisms; they are inferred by counting. Parsimony keeps the tree that needs the fewest evolutionary changes to explain the characters. Tick characters on and off, then add DNA sites, and watch the totals."}},{"k":"pars_title","v":{"s":"🔢 Parsimony: count the steps"}},{"k":"pars_total","v":{"s":"{value1} steps"}},{"k":"pars_table_caption","v":{"s":"Character states for the four tips and the steps each character costs on each tree"}},{"k":"pars_col_character","v":{"s":"Character"}},{"k":"pars_col_a","v":{"s":"Steps on A"}},{"k":"pars_col_b","v":{"s":"Steps on B"}},{"k":"pars_include","v":{"s":"Include {value1}"}},{"k":"pars_decides","v":{"s":"decides"}},{"k":"pars_uninformative","v":{"s":"shared by all"}},{"k":"pars_dna_tb","v":{"s":"DNA sites shared by turtle and bird only × {value1}"}},{"k":"pars_dna_ts","v":{"s":"DNA sites shared by snake and bird only × {value1}"}},{"k":"pars_total_row","v":{"s":"Total steps"}},{"k":"pars_slider_tb","v":{"s":"DNA sites: turtle + bird"}},{"k":"pars_sites","v":{"s":"sites"}},{"k":"pars_slider_tb_hint","v":{"s":"Each site is a DNA position where turtle and bird share a change the others lack. On Tree B it costs one step; on Tree A two."}},{"k":"pars_slider_ts","v":{"s":"DNA sites: snake + bird"}},{"k":"pars_slider_ts_hint","v":{"s":"Sites where snake and bird share a change the turtle lacks: these back Tree A. Real data always carries some sites for each alternative, from chance and from ancestral variation sorting unevenly; parsimony weighs the majority."}},{"k":"pars_load_sample","v":{"s":"🧬 Load a genome sample (illustrative: 12 + 3)"}},{"k":"pars_skeletons_only","v":{"s":"🦴 Skeletons only"}},{"k":"pars_note","v":{"s":"Parsimony is a rule of evidence, not a claim that evolution is thrifty: the preferred tree is the one the data support best, and more data can change it. Real studies also use likelihood and Bayesian methods that model how DNA changes, and the site counts here are illustrative; the 2012 genome studies compared thousands of loci."}},{"k":"ch_fliptwo","v":{"s":"Flip the tree with two sites: "}},{"k":"ch_fliptwo_body","v":{"s":"In the step-counter, keep every skeletal character and no snake + bird sites. Find the smallest number of turtle + bird DNA sites that makes Tree B win outright (one site only ties it)."}},{"k":"parsimony_term","v":{"s":"Parsimony"}},{"k":"parsimony_def","v":{"s":"The rule for choosing between trees: prefer the one that needs the fewest evolutionary changes (steps) to explain the characters. A rule of evidence, not a claim that evolution takes short cuts."}},{"k":"check_yourself_3","v":{"s":"🧠 Check yourself (3)"}},{"k":"check_pars_q","v":{"s":"With the genome sample, Tree B needs fewer steps than Tree A. What does that show?"}},{"k":"check_pars_proof","v":{"s":"It proves turtles are closer to birds than to snakes."}},{"k":"check_pars_proof_fb","v":{"s":"Parsimony ranks hypotheses by how well the data support them; it does not prove one. Tree B is the best-supported tree given these characters, and new data could in principle reorder them. What made the 2012 result stick is that thousands of loci agreed."}},{"k":"check_pars_support","v":{"s":"Tree B is the hypothesis these data support best; adding data could change the ranking, which is why genome-scale studies mattered."}},{"k":"check_pars_support_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. A tree is an inference from counted evidence. The skull character still costs Tree B a step (the turtle lost its openings); the tree wins because many independent sites outweigh one reversal."}},{"k":"check_pars_thrifty","v":{"s":"Evolution takes the fewest steps, so the tree with fewer steps is the one that happened."}},{"k":"check_pars_thrifty_fb","v":{"s":"Evolution is not thrifty; reversals and convergence happen all the time (the turtle skull is one). Parsimony is a rule for weighing evidence: unexplained coincidences count against a tree."}},{"k":"check_pars_skull","v":{"s":"Nothing, because the skull character still supports Tree A."}},{"k":"check_pars_skull_fb","v":{"s":"One character does not outvote twelve independent ones. Untick the skull row and the DNA tree wins by even more; tick it back and it costs Tree B exactly one step."}},{"k":"lim_gd_4","v":{"s":"Migration as an island model: five equal islands, symmetric exchange, every migrant breeds. Real landscapes have stepping stones, one-way flows and migrants that never find a mate, which is why conservation counts effective migrants, not animals that crossed."}},{"k":"move_gd_corridor","v":{"s":"Run N = 50 for 100 generations at Nm = 0 and count the islands that fixed. Set Nm = 1 (2% of an island) and run again. Ask the class why 2% can matter so much, then write both rates on the board: drift ~1/2N, migration Nm/N. N cancels."}},{"k":"het_migrants_note","v":{"s":"The dashed curve assumes isolated islands; with migrants arriving, alleles lost on one island return from another, so the red line should stay above it."}},{"k":"corridor_lead","v":{"s":"Roads, farms and dams cut one population into islands, and each island then drifts on its own. A corridor, an overpass, or an animal moved by hand is a migrant. Drift pushes islands apart at a rate that shrinks with N; migration pulls them together at the rate m = Nm/N; so what decides the balance is the NUMBER of migrants a generation, Nm, not the fraction."}},{"k":"corridor_formula","v":{"s":"Wright's island model: with Nm = {value1} the islands settle at Fst ≈ 1/(1 + 4Nm) = {value2}, where Fst is the share of the variation that sits BETWEEN islands (0 = one well-mixed population, 1 = every island fixed on its own allele). It takes about N generations to get there."}},{"k":"corridor_none","v":{"s":"With Nm = 0 nothing pulls the islands together: each drifts to 0 or 1 on its own schedule and Fst climbs toward 1."}},{"k":"corridor_reading","v":{"s":"Last run: {value1} of 5 islands ended fixed or lost; final Fst = {value2} (one locus, five islands: expect scatter around the formula)."}},{"k":"corridor_no_run","v":{"s":"Run the lineages to measure Fst."}},{"k":"corridor_rule","v":{"s":"The conservation rule of thumb that falls out of this arithmetic is \"one migrant per generation\": with Nm = 1, Fst ≈ 0.2, enough exchange to stop islands losing alleles one by one, yet little enough for local adaptation to persist. A migrant only counts if it breeds."}},{"k":"corridor_real","v":{"s":"Isle Royale, Lake Superior: the island's wolves were cut off and inbred until a single male crossed the ice in 1997. Within a decade most of the pack carried his genes and inbreeding fell; when his contribution faded the population collapsed again, and in 2018 managers began moving wolves in on purpose. One migrant did what this slider does."}},{"k":"corridor_title","v":{"s":"🌉 Corridors: one migrant per generation"}},{"k":"migrants_nm","v":{"s":"Migrants between the five populations (Nm)"}},{"k":"nm_isolated","v":{"s":"Isolated islands"}},{"k":"nm_per_gen","v":{"s":"migrant(s) a generation each"}},{"k":"migrants_nm_hint","v":{"s":"Treat the five lineages as five islands and let this many breeding migrants a generation move between them (a corridor, a wildlife overpass, an animal moved by hand). Even one a generation changes everything; see the Corridors card."}},{"k":"check_corridor_q","v":{"s":"One migrant a generation is 2% of an island of 50, yet it keeps five islands from losing alleles one by one. Why does so little matter so much?"}},{"k":"check_corridor_stronger","v":{"s":"Because migration is always stronger than drift once any migrants arrive."}},{"k":"check_corridor_stronger_fb","v":{"s":"It is a balance, not a knockout: drift separates the islands at a rate near 1/2N a generation and migration pulls them together at m = Nm/N. Their ratio is what Fst ≈ 1/(1 + 4Nm) measures, and at Nm = 0.25 drift still wins most of the way."}},{"k":"check_corridor_number","v":{"s":"Because drift also weakens with N, so the number of migrants sets the balance: one a generation offsets drift in an island of any size."}},{"k":"check_corridor_number_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Both rates carry a 1/N, so N cancels and Nm is what is left. That is why the conservation rule is stated in migrants, not in percentages, and why moving a single breeding wolf to Isle Royale could rescue a whole population."}},{"k":"check_corridor_new","v":{"s":"Because each migrant brings a brand-new allele."}},{"k":"check_corridor_new_fb","v":{"s":"The migrant carries the same two alleles the islands started with. What it brings is a different FREQUENCY: an allele lost on one island is still common on another, and one breeding immigrant reintroduces it."}},{"k":"check_corridor_double","v":{"s":"Because one migrant doubles the effective population size."}},{"k":"check_corridor_double_fb","v":{"s":"Ne of each island barely moves; the migrant changes the connection between islands, not their size. Turn the slider up to 4 and the five behave like one population of 250; turn it to 0 and each is on its own."}},{"k":"ch_onefive","v":{"s":"Five islands, one population: "}},{"k":"ch_onefive_body","v":{"s":"With N = 50 or more and migrants switched on, run 200 generations and finish with all five lineages within 0.2 of each other and none fixed. One migrant a generation almost never manages it; four migrants, about one run in three."}},{"k":"exp_corridor","v":{"s":"One migrant per generation: "}},{"k":"exp_corridor_body","v":{"s":"N = 50, 100 generations, migrants at 0: most runs end with islands fixed or lost. Set migrants to 1 and run again: in nine runs out of ten no island loses the allele, and Fst settles near 0.2. Same islands, one breeding migrant a generation."}},{"k":"corridor_no_variation","v":{"s":"undefined, no variation left anywhere"}},{"k":"move_bl_h2","v":{"s":"Run one drought year at h² = 1 and one at 0.8 on the projector, data table open. Read S, the prediction and the chicks aloud for each. Ask where the missing 0.15 mm went: not into any bird, it was never in the genes."}},{"k":"chicks_last","v":{"s":"Year {value1}{value2}: the flock averaged {value3} mm before the year; the {value4} survivors averaged {value5} mm, so S = {value6} mm. With h² = {value7} the breeder's equation predicts chicks at {value3} + {value7} × {value6} = {value8} mm. The {value9} chicks hatched at {value10} mm."}},{"k":"drought_word","v":{"s":"drought"}},{"k":"chicks_hit","v":{"s":"Prediction and chicks agree within {value1} mm: the equation works because what the survivors pass on is the heritable share of their advantage, no more."}},{"k":"chicks_miss","v":{"s":"Off by {value1} mm this year; with a few dozen chicks the luck of who paired with whom shows. Run another year and compare again."}},{"k":"chicks_h2_full","v":{"s":"At h² = 1 the chicks inherit the whole of their parents' difference from the flock. Set h² to 0.8, the value the Grants measured from parent–chick comparisons, to run the model the way they ran their prediction."}},{"k":"chicks_h2_part","v":{"s":"At h² = {value1} only that share of the survivors' advantage reaches the chicks; the rest was a good territory, a lucky first year, or measurement, none of which is inherited. The survivors themselves stay in the flock unchanged, which is why the whole-flock mean moves a little more than the chicks."}},{"k":"chicks_grants","v":{"s":"What the Grants did: after the 1977 drought they knew the survivors' S from measuring every bird on Daphne Major, and they had h² for beak depth (around 0.8) from comparing chicks with their parents in earlier years. They predicted the 1978 chicks with this equation before those chicks were measured, and the chicks came in close. A prediction made before the data is the strongest test a model can pass."}},{"k":"chicks_title","v":{"s":"🐣 Predict the chicks: the Grants' test"}},{"k":"col_chicks_mean","v":{"s":"Chicks' mean"}},{"k":"dt_beak_note","v":{"s":"S = survivors' mean − the flock mean before the year; the chicks are predicted at before + h² × S."}},{"k":"beak_h2","v":{"s":"Heritability of beak depth (h²)"}},{"k":"beak_h2_grants","v":{"s":"the Grants' value"}},{"k":"beak_h2_all","v":{"s":"everything inherited"}},{"k":"beak_h2_hint","v":{"s":"How much of a pair's difference from the flock their chicks inherit. The Grants measured about 0.8 for beak depth by comparing chicks with their parents; at 1 the chicks copy their parents exactly (± σ). The challenges were set at 1."}},{"k":"check_yourself_h2","v":{"s":"🧠 Check yourself: the chicks"}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_q","v":{"s":"The 1977 survivors' beaks averaged about half a millimetre deeper than the flock before the drought. Why were the 1978 chicks not a full half-millimetre deeper?"}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_grow","v":{"s":"The chicks had not finished growing when they were measured."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_grow_fb","v":{"s":"The Grants measured beaks at full size. The shortfall is systematic and predictable, which growth would not be."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_herit","v":{"s":"Only the heritable share of the survivors' advantage is passed on: with h² near 0.8 the chicks come in about 0.4 mm deeper, and the rest of the survivors' edge was environment."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_herit_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. R = h² × S. Part of why a survivor had a deep beak was its genes, and part was a good first year; only the genes reach the chicks. Set the slider to 0.8 and the model's chicks fall short by the same fraction."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_rain","v":{"s":"The drought ended, so the chicks grew smaller beaks to match the new seeds."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_rain_fb","v":{"s":"Beaks do not grow to match the seeds available; the seeds decide who survives, not how anyone develops. The shortfall shows up even in the model, where nothing about the seeds reaches a chick's beak."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_chance","v":{"s":"Pure chance in which birds happened to pair up."}},{"k":"check_beak_h2_chance_fb","v":{"s":"Chance adds scatter of a few hundredths of a millimetre either way; it does not push the chicks short by a fixed fraction year after year. Heritability does."}},{"k":"exp_predict_chicks","v":{"s":"The Grants' test: "}},{"k":"exp_predict_chicks_body","v":{"s":"Set h² to 0.8, run a drought year, and read the Predict the chicks card: S from the survivors, the prediction before + 0.8 × S, and the chicks that actually hatched. This is the calculation the Grants published for 1978, made before the chicks were measured."}},{"k":"move_cp_plast","v":{"s":"Run 0.040 a generation at acclimation 0 and then at 0.6 on the projector, and freeze on the Tolerance Lag card at the end. Ask which population is better adapted (the one with the smaller lag, which is the one that nearly died) and which is better off right now. Both answers are right; that is the point."}},{"k":"accl_live","v":{"s":"Right now the flock's genetic tolerance sits {value1} below the temperature. Acclimation covers {value2} of that (each individual can shift up to {value3} within its lifetime), leaving {value4} exposed, and survival is judged on the exposed part only."}},{"k":"accl_off","v":{"s":"Acclimation is off: every individual is judged on the full gap between its inherited tolerance and the temperature. Turn it up to let individuals shift part of the way within their lifetimes, up to a cap."}},{"k":"accl_lesson","v":{"s":"Two things happen at once. Buying time: at σ = 0.02 a rise of 0.040 a generation is survived about one run in four without acclimation and nine in ten with acclimation at 0.6. Hiding the signal: acclimated individuals differ less in what selection can see, so the inherited tolerance evolves more slowly, and a survived trial ends with the genes further behind the climate (about 0.17 instead of 0.09 at 0.020 a generation). The cap is where the bill comes due: a lag can be hidden up to the cap and no further, and a population that fails with acclimation fails carrying a bigger hidden lag than one without."}},{"k":"accl_real","v":{"s":"Reef corals acclimatise to about a degree of extra heat by shuffling their algal partners and making heat-shock proteins; past that threshold whole reefs bleach at once, which is why bleaching arrives as a cliff rather than a slope. Biologists still debate whether plasticity mostly shields genes from selection or mostly keeps populations alive long enough for selection to work (the Baldwin effect); this model shows both at the same time."}},{"k":"accl_title","v":{"s":"🧥 Acclimation: buying time, hiding the signal"}},{"k":"acclimation_short","v":{"s":"acclimation"}},{"k":"acclimation_label","v":{"s":"Acclimation (plasticity within a lifetime)"}},{"k":"acclimation_none","v":{"s":"None: tolerance is fixed for life"}},{"k":"acclimation_value","v":{"s":"Each individual closes {value1}% of its gap, up to {value2}"}},{"k":"acclimation_hint","v":{"s":"How far an individual can shift its tolerance toward the current temperature within its own lifetime, capped at 0.08. Not inherited. Rate challenges and the record count only trials run with this at 0."}},{"k":"check_plast_q","v":{"s":"With acclimation at 0.6 the population came through a rise its genes could not have kept up with. Has it adapted?"}},{"k":"check_plast_yes","v":{"s":"Yes: it survived, so its tolerance must have evolved to match the climate."}},{"k":"check_plast_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Read the Tolerance Lag card: the inherited tolerance is further behind the climate than in a run without acclimation. Surviving and adapting are not the same thing; here the individuals coped and the genes fell behind."}},{"k":"check_plast_partly","v":{"s":"Partly: individuals coped within their lifetimes, that shielding slowed the genetic change, and the population is alive but carrying a hidden lag."}},{"k":"check_plast_partly_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Acclimation is not inherited, so what it covers is invisible to selection and the genetic response is smaller. The population is fine until the gap exceeds what acclimation can cover; then it fails from a worse position than a population that never had the cushion."}},{"k":"check_plast_no","v":{"s":"No: acclimation is not heritable, so nothing about the population changed."}},{"k":"check_plast_no_fb","v":{"s":"The genes did move, just less. Selection still saw the exposed part of each gap, and the survivors' inherited tolerance rose; compare the chart with a run at acclimation 0."}},{"k":"check_plast_evolved","v":{"s":"Yes, and the acclimation itself evolved as the adaptation."}},{"k":"check_plast_evolved_fb","v":{"s":"In this model plasticity is a dial you set, the same for every individual, and it never changes. In nature the capacity to acclimatise can itself be heritable and evolve; that is where this model stops, and the limits card says so."}},{"k":"exp_acclimation","v":{"s":"Acclimation buys time: "}},{"k":"exp_acclimation_body","v":{"s":"At σ = 0.02, run 0.040 a generation with acclimation at 0 (it fails about three runs in four), then at 0.6 (it survives nine in ten). Same genes, same climate; the individuals bent instead of breaking."}},{"k":"exp_hidden_lag","v":{"s":"Acclimation hides the lag: "}},{"k":"exp_hidden_lag_body","v":{"s":"Survive 0.020 a generation twice, once at acclimation 0 and once at 0.6, and compare the Tolerance Lag at the end: roughly 0.09 against 0.17. The cushioned population is alive with its genes further behind, which is the position a population fails from when the cushion runs out."}},{"k":"mq_l1","v":{"s":"\"Just a theory\""}},{"k":"mq_l2","v":{"s":"Humans from monkeys"}},{"k":"mq_l3","v":{"s":"Evolution has a goal"}},{"k":"mq_l4","v":{"s":"Fitness = strength"}},{"k":"mq_l5","v":{"s":"Acquired traits inherited"}},{"k":"mq_l6","v":{"s":"Why still apes"}},{"k":"mq_l7","v":{"s":"Mutations always bad"}},{"k":"mq_l8","v":{"s":"Evolution explains life's origin"}},{"k":"mq_l9","v":{"s":"Evolution is random"}},{"k":"mq_l10","v":{"s":"Macroevolution unobserved"}},{"k":"mq_l11","v":{"s":"Giraffes needed long necks"}},{"k":"mq_l12","v":{"s":"Evolution is always slow"}},{"k":"move_title_quiz","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2} still miss \"{value3}\" after a second pass of the quiz"}},{"k":"move_quiz","v":{"s":"A misconception that survives a retry needs the mechanism, not the answer. Put the question on the projector, have students argue FOR the wrong answer they chose first, then take it apart together with the module that covers it."}},{"k":"quiz_tvn_heading","v":{"s":"🪞 Misconceptions quiz: first pass vs latest"}},{"k":"quiz_tvn_summary","v":{"s":"{value1} student{value2} with a first pass, {value3} with two or more. Among retakers, {value4} of {value5} first-pass misconceptions were shed. A misconception many still miss after a retake is the one to reteach by mechanism."}},{"k":"quiz_tvn_caption","v":{"s":"Quiz items with first-pass misses and misses that survived a retake"}},{"k":"tvn_col_item","v":{"s":"Misconception"}},{"k":"quiz_col_first_miss","v":{"s":"Missed on first pass"}},{"k":"quiz_col_still","v":{"s":"Still missed after a retake"}},{"k":"no_quiz_in_journals","v":{"s":"No completed quiz passes in these journals yet."}},{"k":"sr_quiz_pass","v":{"s":"Pass {value1} recorded: {value2} of the {value3} misconceptions from your first pass are gone."}},{"k":"tvn_summary","v":{"s":"First pass {value1}/{value2}, this pass {value3}/{value2}. You shed {value4} of the {value5} misconceptions you started with{value6}{value7}."}},{"k":"tvn_still","v":{"s":"{value1} still with you"}},{"k":"tvn_slipped","v":{"s":"{value1} slipped"}},{"k":"tvn_shed","v":{"s":"shed"}},{"k":"tvn_still_here","v":{"s":"still here"}},{"k":"tvn_slipped_word","v":{"s":"slipped"}},{"k":"tvn_kept","v":{"s":"kept"}},{"k":"tvn_title","v":{"s":"🪞 Then vs now"}},{"k":"tvn_hint","v":{"s":"A misconception you shed is one you can now explain away; the ones still here are worth a field note in the module that covers them."}},{"k":"tvn_caption","v":{"s":"Each misconception on the first pass and on the latest pass"}},{"k":"tvn_col_first","v":{"s":"First pass"}},{"k":"tvn_col_now","v":{"s":"Now"}},{"k":"tvn_col_status","v":{"s":"Status"}},{"k":"quiz_pass_line","v":{"s":"Pass {value1} · first pass {value2}/{value3}"}},{"k":"g_hetadv","v":{"s":"heterozygote advantage"}},{"k":"g_hetadv_def","v":{"s":"When the carrier (Aa) out-survives both homozygotes, so selection holds both alleles in the population instead of removing one. The allele whose homozygote pays s rests at q̂ = t/(s + t); sickle cell in malarial regions is the classic case."}},{"k":"ch_tenforten","v":{"s":"Ten for ten: "}},{"k":"ch_tenforten_body","v":{"s":"Score 10 out of 10 on a whole-glossary sprint (the one on this page, not a single lab's). Both directions count: some questions give you the definition and ask for the term, some do the reverse."}},{"k":"sprint_best_record","v":{"s":"best whole-glossary score"}},{"k":"sprint_labs_done","v":{"s":"{value1} of {value2} labs drilled"}},{"k":"sprint_all_drilled","v":{"s":"Every lab sprinted, {value1} of them at full marks. Retrieval fades, so come back to the weakest ones in a few days."}},{"k":"sprint_some_left","v":{"s":"A lab with no score is one you have not drilled yet. Open it and use the sprint button on its key-terms card."}},{"k":"sprint_best_n","v":{"s":"best {value1} of {value2}"}},{"k":"sprint_not_yet","v":{"s":"not sprinted yet ({value1} terms)"}},{"k":"a11y_trait_histogram","v":{"s":"Trait histogram."}},{"k":"a11y_state_completed","v":{"s":"(completed)"}},{"k":"a11y_state_explored","v":{"s":"(explored, not yet completed)"}},{"k":"a11y_open_card","v":{"s":"Open {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_state_explored_short","v":{"s":"(explored)"}},{"k":"journal_copy_trimmed","v":{"s":"({value1} long answer{value2} shortened to keep this paste-able. The full text is still in the tool, and your teacher's snapshot reads it from the line below.)"}},{"k":"sample_s1","v":{"s":"Sample: Ada"}},{"k":"sample_note1","v":{"s":"The birds did not change. The small-beaked ones died, so the average moved without any single beak growing."}},{"k":"sample_ticket1","v":{"s":"Variation has to be there first, and it has to be inherited."}},{"k":"sample_s2","v":{"s":"Sample: Ben"}},{"k":"sample_note2","v":{"s":"I expected the helpful allele to win every time. In the small runs it was lost twice."}},{"k":"sample_ticket2","v":{"s":"Small populations are the ones where luck takes over."}},{"k":"sample_s3","v":{"s":"Sample: Chidi"}},{"k":"sample_ticket3","v":{"s":"Still not sure how the beaks knew to change."}},{"k":"sample_s4","v":{"s":"Sample: Dara"}},{"k":"sample_note3","v":{"s":"Two drugs worked because no cell was resistant to both before the first dose."}},{"k":"sample_ticket4","v":{"s":"Selection cannot make a trait; it can only keep what is already there."}},{"k":"sample_s5","v":{"s":"Sample: Esi"}},{"k":"sample_ticket5","v":{"s":"I think I mixed up drift and selection."}},{"k":"sample_s6","v":{"s":"Sample: Farrah"}},{"k":"sample_note4","v":{"s":"The allele stops falling because mutation keeps putting it back."}},{"k":"move_hw_het","v":{"s":"Set s = 0.8, t = 0.12 and start p at 0.2, then at 0.9: both runs stop at the same p. Ask what would make the allele disappear (only removing the carrier advantage, t = 0), then run that. The allele that was protected becomes the allele that is purged."}},{"k":"journals_duplicate_n","v":{"s":"{value1} duplicate{value2} skipped"}},{"k":"sr_sample_loaded","v":{"s":"A sample class of six journals was loaded so you can see what the snapshot shows."}},{"k":"show_sample_class","v":{"s":"Show me a sample class"}},{"k":"hetadv_lead","v":{"s":"AA pays {value1} (say, malaria), aa pays {value2} (sickle-cell disease), Aa pays nothing. Neither allele can win: when a is rare almost every copy sits in a protected heterozygote and it climbs; when a is common, aa deaths pull it back. They meet where the two losses balance: q̂ = t / (s + t) = {value4} for the allele whose homozygote pays s, so p̂ = s / (s + t) = {value3}{value5}. Current p = {value6}{value7}"}},{"k":"hetadv_inbreed_shift","v":{"s":"(with inbreeding F = {value1} the resting point moves to p = {value2}: inbreeding makes both homozygotes commoner, and at high F the balance can collapse to one allele)"}},{"k":"hetadv_lead_noaa","v":{"s":"AA pays {value1} and aa pays nothing, so this is plain selection against A: p falls toward 0. Give aa a cost too (slider 2) and the two selections trap the allele between them."}},{"k":"hetadv_real","v":{"s":"Sickle cell is the textbook case. In malarial West Africa, children with two normal alleles (AA) died of malaria often enough that their fitness was roughly 0.85 to 0.9 relative to carriers (AS), while sickle-cell disease (SS) had a fitness near 0.2 without modern care. Set t = 0.12 and s = 0.8 and the balance lands near q̂ = 0.13, close to the 10 to 20% HbS frequencies measured there. Where there is no malaria, t = 0 and the allele sinks toward the mutation floor, which is why it is rare in populations whose ancestors never lived with the disease."}},{"k":"hetadv_title","v":{"s":"⚖️ Heterozygote advantage: trapped between two losses"}},{"k":"a11y_genotype_bar","v":{"s":"{value1} ({value2}): {value3} percent"}},{"k":"primer_het","v":{"s":"Add selection against AA as well and the heterozygote is fittest: the allele is held at a balance instead of being lost (sickle cell and malaria)."}},{"k":"2b_selection_against_aa_het","v":{"s":"2b. Selection Against AA (heterozygote advantage)"}},{"k":"hetadv_off","v":{"s":"Off: AA as fit as Aa"}},{"k":"hetadv_hint","v":{"s":"Relative reduction in AA survival while Aa pays nothing. With slider 2 also on, the heterozygote is fittest and the a allele is held at q̂ = t/(s + t): sickle cell in a malarial region. Set t and s equal to hold p at 0.50."}},{"k":"check_balance_q","v":{"s":"Cystic fibrosis is caused by a recessive allele that was lethal in childhood before modern care, yet about 1 European in 25 carries it. Selection has had thousands of generations. Why is it still here?"}},{"k":"check_balance_time","v":{"s":"Selection has not had long enough yet; given more generations it would finish the job."}},{"k":"check_balance_time_fb","v":{"s":"Step this model as long as you like: p stops moving and stays there. The resting point is not a stage on the way to zero, it is where removal and replacement match. Time does not help when the two rates are equal."}},{"k":"check_balance_yes","v":{"s":"Selection only sees the allele in aa individuals, and mutation keeps remaking it; removal and replacement balance at q̂ = √(μ/s)."}},{"k":"check_balance_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. Almost every copy sits in a carrier where selection cannot touch it, so the allele is removed slowly, and mutation replaces it at a steady rate. Where those rates meet, q stops falling. Raise μ and the resting point rises; raise s and it falls, exactly as √(μ/s) predicts."}},{"k":"check_balance_carriers","v":{"s":"Carriers are healthy, so the allele is not selected against at all."}},{"k":"check_balance_carriers_fb","v":{"s":"It is selected against, just inefficiently: every aa individual removes two copies. Set mutation to 0 and keep stepping and q really does fall, only slower and slower. Mutation is what stops it reaching zero."}},{"k":"check_balance_advantage","v":{"s":"Carriers must have some hidden advantage, the way sickle-cell carriers resist malaria."}},{"k":"check_balance_advantage_fb","v":{"s":"That is a real possibility for some alleles and it is worth testing, but it is not needed here: this run has no advantage for carriers (slider 2b is at 0) and the allele still settles at a stable frequency. Mutation alone is enough to explain a low steady level."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_q","v":{"s":"Sickle-cell disease kills most aa children, yet the a allele stays near 10 to 15% in malarial regions. Why?"}},{"k":"check_hetadv_mutation","v":{"s":"Mutation keeps making new copies fast enough to replace the ones lost."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_mutation_fb","v":{"s":"Mutation is far too slow: with a real rate near 10⁻⁵ the mutation floor for a lethal recessive is about q = 0.003, not 0.1. Something is favouring the allele, not just restocking it."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_yes","v":{"s":"Carriers (Aa) survive malaria better than AA, so both homozygotes lose and the allele settles where the two losses balance."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_yes_fb","v":{"s":"Yes. This is heterozygote advantage, and the resting point is q̂ = t/(s + t) for the sickle allele. Take the malaria away (t = 0) and the same allele becomes a plain disease allele that selection slowly removes."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_late","v":{"s":"The disease strikes only after people have had their children."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_late_fb","v":{"s":"Untreated sickle-cell disease is mostly a disease of childhood. If it struck only after reproduction it would not be selected against at all, and the allele would drift, not sit at a fixed balance."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_hide","v":{"s":"Selection cannot remove a recessive allele, because it hides in carriers."}},{"k":"check_hetadv_hide_fb","v":{"s":"Hiding in carriers slows the LAST few percent, not the first ten: run s = 0.8 with t = 0 and q falls well below 0.05 within a few dozen generations. What holds it at 0.13 is the carrier advantage, not the hiding."}},{"k":"ch_meetmiddle","v":{"s":"Meet in the middle: "}},{"k":"ch_meetmiddle_body","v":{"s":"Start from p = 0.20 or 0.80 and, using selection against aa AND against AA, bring p to 0.50 and hold it there for 30 generations. There is exactly one relation between s and t that does it."}},{"k":"exp_balanced_poly","v":{"s":"Balanced polymorphism: "}},{"k":"exp_balanced_poly_body","v":{"s":"Sickle cell: set selection against aa to 0.80 and against AA to 0.12, start p anywhere, and Step 10 a few times. q stops at t/(s + t) = 0.13 (p at 0.87) whichever side it came from; neither allele can be lost while both homozygotes pay."}},{"k":"exp_malaria_gone","v":{"s":"Take the malaria away: "}},{"k":"exp_malaria_gone_body","v":{"s":"From the sickle-cell balance, set selection against AA back to 0 and keep stepping. With no advantage for carriers the allele is just a disease allele again, and p climbs past 0.95. Same allele, different environment, opposite fate."}},{"k":"a11y_drift_chart_empty","v":{"s":"Empty chart. Press Run to populate."}},{"k":"a11y_drift_chart","v":{"s":"Five colored lines showing allele-frequency trajectories. {value1} of 5 fixed at 0 or 1."}},{"k":"a11y_histogram_bucket","v":{"s":"Trait {value1}-{value2}: {value3} creatures"}},{"k":"a11y_no_history_yet","v":{"s":"No history yet."}},{"k":"a11y_mean_trait_chart","v":{"s":"Mean trait over {value1} generations. Latest mean: {value2}"}},{"k":"breeder_h2_noise_small","v":{"s":"The \"h² now\" reading is just as noisy: measuring heritability from {value1} creatures means comparing two small variances, so a true 0.5 reads anywhere from about 0.2 to 0.8. Raise the population to 200 before reading much into it; real studies measure heritability from hundreds of parent-offspring pairs, which is why it takes years of fieldwork."}},{"k":"breeder_h2_noise","v":{"s":"The \"h² now\" reading has its own scatter: at N = {value1} a true 0.5 reads roughly {value2}. It is an estimate from two measured variances, not a dial reading, which is why real heritability estimates need hundreds of parent-offspring pairs."}},{"k":"h2_noisy","v":{"s":"(± a lot at this N)"}},{"k":"a11y_beak_chart","v":{"s":"Mean beak depth chart over {value1} years"}},{"k":"a11y_beak_chart_grant","v":{"s":", with the Grants' 1977 drought record overlaid (approximate teaching values)"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_dna","v":{"s":"DNA"}},{"k":"a11y_evidence_skeletal","v":{"s":"skeletal"}},{"k":"a11y_placed_on","v":{"s":", placed on {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_not_placed","v":{"s":", not placed"}},{"k":"a11y_answer_correct","v":{"s":", correct"}},{"k":"a11y_answer_incorrect","v":{"s":", incorrect"}},{"k":"a11y_forelimb_skeleton","v":{"s":"{value1} forelimb skeleton. Click any bone to highlight it across all species."}},{"k":"a11y_choice_correct","v":{"s":"(correct answer)"}},{"k":"a11y_choice_incorrect","v":{"s":"(your incorrect answer)"}},{"k":"a11y_resistance_chart_empty","v":{"s":"Empty resistance chart."}},{"k":"a11y_resistance_chart","v":{"s":"Resistance fraction chart over {value1} generations. Currently {value2} percent resistant."}},{"k":"a11y_petri_dish","v":{"s":"Petri dish with {value1} bacteria. {value2} percent resistant. Antibiotic {value3}."}},{"k":"a11y_drug_active","v":{"s":"active"}},{"k":"a11y_drug_off","v":{"s":"off"}},{"k":"a11y_drug_b_active","v":{"s":"Drug B active."}},{"k":"a11y_state_expanded","v":{"s":"(expanded)"}},{"k":"a11y_state_collapsed","v":{"s":"(click to expand)"}},{"k":"a11y_climate_landscape","v":{"s":"Climate landscape with {value1} creatures. Temperature {value2}. Mean tolerance {value3}."}},{"k":"a11y_extinction_occurred","v":{"s":"EXTINCTION OCCURRED."}},{"k":"a11y_temperature_value","v":{"s":"Temperature {value1}"}},{"k":"a11y_lag_critical","v":{"s":", population in critical lag"}},{"k":"a11y_lag_stressed","v":{"s":", population stressed"}},{"k":"a11y_lag_matched","v":{"s":", population matched"}},{"k":"a11y_bird_choose","v":{"s":"Bird with showiness {value1}{value2}. Click to choose this mate."}},{"k":"a11y_your_last_pick","v":{"s":"(your last pick)"}},{"k":"a11y_bird_figure","v":{"s":"A bird with showiness {value1}{value2}, tail length {value3} pixels"}},{"k":"a11y_tail_eyespots","v":{"s":", {value1} tail eyespots"}},{"k":"sig_group","v":{"s":"{value1} run{value2} at {value3}: mean {value4}{value5}"}},{"k":"sig_no_spread","v":{"s":"no repeat, so no spread to measure"}},{"k":"sig_spread","v":{"s":"spread {value1}"}},{"k":"sig_no_noise","v":{"s":"Your repeat runs landed on identical values, which is unusual in a model with randomness: capture another repeat before trusting the comparison."}},{"k":"sig_overlap","v":{"s":"The two settings differ by {value1}, and repeat runs at one setting already vary by {value2}. The change is not yet distinguishable from the model's own scatter. More repeats at each setting would narrow the question; so would a bigger change."}},{"k":"sig_signal","v":{"s":"The two settings differ by {value1}, about {value2}× the widest spread among repeats ({value3}). That is a difference the model's scatter does not explain by itself, which is what makes it worth a claim."}},{"k":"sig_one_factor","v":{"s":"Exactly one setting differs between the groups ({value1}: {value2} → {value3}), so the difference can be attributed to it within this model."}},{"k":"sig_many_factors","v":{"s":"{value1} settings differ between the groups ({value2}), so the difference cannot be attributed to any one of them. Hold the others fixed and run again."}},{"k":"sig_no_factors","v":{"s":"The recorded factors are identical, so these groups differ only in settings the notebook does not track."}},{"k":"sig_title","v":{"s":"📏 Is the difference bigger than the noise?"}},{"k":"sig_note","v":{"s":"This is a rule of thumb, not a statistical test: with a handful of runs a p-value would claim more than the data can carry. Real studies use many more replicates and a test chosen for the design."}},{"k":"a11y_captured_evidence","v":{"s":"Captured simulator evidence"}},{"k":"a11y_captured_runs","v":{"s":"Captured simulator runs"}},{"k":"a11y_generate_report","v":{"s":"Generate the final report"}},{"k":"a11y_advance_step","v":{"s":"Advance to the next step"}}],"id":521}},{"k":"fireecology","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_ai_land_reading_complete","v":{"s":"AI Land Reading complete."}},{"k":"a11y_toggle_cultural_burn_and_suppression_comparison","v":{"s":"Toggle cultural-burn and suppression comparison"}},{"k":"a11y_chart_legend","v":{"s":"Chart legend"}},{"k":"a11y_simulation_events","v":{"s":"Simulation events"}},{"k":"a11y_predict_the_verdict_before_evaluating","v":{"s":"Predict the verdict before evaluating"}},{"k":"a11y_which_survival_strategy_is_this","v":{"s":"Which survival strategy is this?"}},{"k":"a11y_two_hillslopes_in_the_same_rainstorm_after_a_cu","v":{"s":"Two hillslopes in the same rainstorm. After a cultural burn the trees and roots stand, litter and open soil let the rain soak in, and the stream at the bottom runs clear. After a high-severity wildfire the trees are dead snags, a water-repellent layer sits under the surface, the rain runs off down the slope, and the stream is choked with ash and mud."}},{"k":"indigenous_knowledge","v":{"s":"Indigenous Knowledge"}},{"k":"fire_ecosystems","v":{"s":"Fire Ecosystems"}},{"k":"forest_simulator","v":{"s":"Forest Simulator"}},{"k":"cultural_mosaic","v":{"s":"Cultural Mosaic"}},{"k":"burn_planner","v":{"s":"Burn Planner"}},{"k":"fire_science","v":{"s":"Fire Science"}},{"k":"smoke_seeds","v":{"s":"Smoke & Seeds"}},{"k":"watersheds","v":{"s":"Watersheds"}},{"k":"case_studies","v":{"s":"Case Studies"}},{"k":"carbon_calculator","v":{"s":"Carbon Calculator"}},{"k":"beavers_fire","v":{"s":"Beavers & Fire"}},{"k":"firekeeper_challenge","v":{"s":"Firekeeper Challenge"}},{"k":"quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"fire_regime","v":{"s":"Fire Regime"}},{"k":"indigenous_fire_knowledge","v":{"s":"Indigenous fire knowledge"}},{"k":"cultural_burning_predates_colonization","v":{"s":"Cultural burning predates colonization by 65,000+ years across Australia, North America, and Africa. Center Indigenous practitioners as living authorities while comparing their place-based knowledge with contemporary fire ecology."}},{"k":"fire_adapted_ecosystems","v":{"s":"Fire-adapted ecosystems"}},{"k":"some_ecosystems_need_fire_to_function_","v":{"s":"Some ecosystems need fire to function. Compare the adaptations, return intervals, and risks that shape each landscape."}},{"k":"forest_simulator_2","v":{"s":"Forest simulator"}},{"k":"cellular_automaton_fire_spread_each_ce","v":{"s":"Test how cultural burning, prescribed burning, and suppression change fuel, biodiversity, and wildfire risk over decades."}},{"k":"cultural_mosaic_wabanaki_stewardship","v":{"s":"Cultural mosaic — Wabanaki stewardship"}},{"k":"a_real_indigenous_approach_is_patchwor","v":{"s":"A real Indigenous approach is patchwork, not uniform. Each habitat has its own fire-return interval: blueberry barren every 2 years, riparian almost never. Steward 7 zones over 8 years."}},{"k":"burn_planner_cultural_burn_safety","v":{"s":"Burn conditions - classroom comparison"}},{"k":"indigenous_modern_burn_planners_read_t","v":{"s":"Compare four weather and fuel inputs in a simplified classroom model; real burn decisions require a site-specific approved plan and qualified personnel."}},{"k":"fire_science_the_chemistry","v":{"s":"Fire science — the chemistry"}},{"k":"the_fire_triangle_fuel_oxygen_heat_com","v":{"s":"Investigate combustion, the fire triangle, heat transfer, and flashover."}},{"k":"smoke_seeds_2","v":{"s":"Smoke + seeds"}},{"k":"many_plants_need_smoke_chemicals_karri","v":{"s":"Explore karrikins, fire-followers, and seed banks that wait decades for the right signal."}},{"k":"fire_watersheds","v":{"s":"Fire + watersheds"}},{"k":"burn_scars_trigger_debris_flows_hydrop","v":{"s":"Connect burn severity to hydrophobic soils, runoff, debris flows, and recovery."}},{"k":"case_studies_2","v":{"s":"Case studies"}},{"k":"camp_fire_2018_black_saturday_2009_car","v":{"s":"Black Summer (2019-2020), the Camp Fire (2018), Yellowstone (1988), the Montecito debris flow (2018), and the Oregon Labor Day fires (2020). Use real events to examine weather, fuel, policy, and community impact."}},{"k":"wildfire_carbon_ledger","v":{"s":"Wildfire carbon ledger"}},{"k":"wildfire_is_carbon_neutral_over_decade","v":{"s":"Estimate how fire severity and regrowth change the carbon balance."}},{"k":"beavers_vs_fire","v":{"s":"Beavers vs fire"}},{"k":"beaver_wetlands_act_as_fire_breaks_wet","v":{"s":"Explore how wetland restoration can create living fire breaks and refuges."}},{"k":"firekeeper_challenge_100_year_game","v":{"s":"Firekeeper Challenge — 100-year game"}},{"k":"you_manage_a_forest_for_100_years_cult","v":{"s":"Make long-term stewardship decisions and adapt to uncertain events."}},{"k":"fire_ecology_quiz","v":{"s":"Fire ecology quiz"}},{"k":"multi_choice_items_spanning_ecology_ch","v":{"s":"Check understanding across ecology, chemistry, stewardship, watersheds, and policy."}},{"k":"fire_ecology_sections","v":{"s":"Fire Ecology sections"}},{"k":"back_to_all_nations","v":{"s":"Back to All Nations"}},{"k":"back_to_all_nations_2","v":{"s":"← Back to All Nations"}},{"k":"the_science","v":{"s":"🔬 The Science"}},{"k":"key_practices","v":{"s":"🛠️ Key Practices"}},{"k":"fire_managed_plants","v":{"s":"🌱 Fire-Managed Plants"}},{"k":"living_legacy","v":{"s":"✨ Living Legacy"}},{"k":"for_tens_of_thousands_of_years_indigen","v":{"s":"For tens of thousands of years, Indigenous peoples around the world have used fire as a sophisticated land management tool. These are not “primitive” practices — they represent deep ecological knowledge refined over hundreds of generations. Modern fire science is only now beginning to understand what Indigenous fire keepers have always known: "}},{"k":"fire_is_not_the_enemy_of_healthy_ecosy","v":{"s":"fire is not the enemy of healthy ecosystems — the absence of fire is."}},{"k":"studied","v":{"s":"✓ Studied"}},{"k":"back_to_all_ecosystems","v":{"s":"Back to All Ecosystems"}},{"k":"back_to_all_ecosystems_2","v":{"s":"← Back to All Ecosystems"}},{"k":"role_of_fire","v":{"s":"🔥 Role of Fire"}},{"k":"fire_adaptations","v":{"s":"🧬 Fire Adaptations"}},{"k":"without_fire","v":{"s":"⚠️ Without Fire"}},{"k":"many_of_earth_s_most_biodiverse_ecosys","v":{"s":"Many of Earth’s most biodiverse ecosystems are "}},{"k":"they_evolved_with_fire_and_cannot_surv","v":{"s":" — they evolved WITH fire and cannot survive without it. From Australian eucalyptus forests to American tallgrass prairies, fire is as essential as rain or sunlight."}},{"k":"explored","v":{"s":"✓ Explored"}},{"k":"fireecology_visualization","v":{"s":"Forest treatment visualization"}},{"k":"forest_visualization_showing_current_f","v":{"s":"Forest visualization showing current fuel load, canopy cover, and biodiversity"}},{"k":"moderate","v":{"s":"Moderate"}},{"k":"high","v":{"s":"High"}},{"k":"manage_a_forest_over_decades_choose_yo","v":{"s":"Manage a forest over decades. Choose your strategy each decade: "}},{"k":"cultural_burn","v":{"s":"Cultural Burn"}},{"k":"indigenous_method","v":{"s":" (Indigenous method), "}},{"k":"prescribed_burn","v":{"s":"Prescribed Burn"}},{"k":"western_forestry_or","v":{"s":" (Western forestry), or "}},{"k":"suppress","v":{"s":"Suppress"}},{"k":"fight_all_fire_watch_what_happens_over","v":{"s":" (fight all fire). Watch what happens over time."}},{"k":"keep_biodiversity_above_70_and_fuel_lo","v":{"s":"keep biodiversity above 70 and fuel load below 30 across 50+ years without triggering a catastrophic wildfire. Cultural burn every decade or two; suppression looks safe at first but stockpiles fuel until one bad year erases everything."}},{"k":"year","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"year_2","v":{"s":"📅 Year"}},{"k":"wildfire_risk","v":{"s":"⚠️ Wildfire Risk"}},{"k":"cultural_burn_10_yrs","v":{"s":"Cultural Burn (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"cultural_burn_10_yrs_2","v":{"s":"🔥 Cultural Burn (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"prescribed_burn_10_yrs","v":{"s":"Prescribed Burn (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"prescribed_burn_10_yrs_2","v":{"s":"📋 Prescribed Burn (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"suppress_fire_10_yrs","v":{"s":"Suppress Fire (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"suppress_fire_10_yrs_2","v":{"s":"⛔ Suppress Fire (+10 yrs)"}},{"k":"reset_forest","v":{"s":"Reset Forest"}},{"k":"reset_forest_2","v":{"s":"🔄 Reset Forest"}},{"k":"side_by_side_cultural_burn_vs_suppress","v":{"s":"↔️ Side-by-Side: Cultural Burn vs. Suppression"}},{"k":"click_to_advance_both_forests_10_years","v":{"s":"Click to advance both forests 10 years — one managed with cultural burning, one with total fire suppression."}},{"k":"advance_both_forests_10_years","v":{"s":"➡️ Advance Both Forests +10 Years"}},{"k":"forest_health_over_time","v":{"s":"📊 Forest Health Over Time"}},{"k":"biodiversity","v":{"s":"■ Biodiversity"}},{"k":"fuel_load","v":{"s":"■ Fuel Load"}},{"k":"event_log","v":{"s":"📜 Event Log"}},{"k":"go_excellent_conditions_for_cultural_b","v":{"s":"Four classroom ranges matched"}},{"k":"caution_proceed_with_extra_care","v":{"s":"Some classroom ranges matched"}},{"k":"no_go_conditions_unsafe_for_burning","v":{"s":"Few classroom ranges matched"}},{"k":"indigenous_fire_practitioners_read_the","v":{"s":"Indigenous fire practitioners read the land, sky, and wind to determine the right moment to burn. Modern prescribed burn planners use the same principles with instruments. Compare the four inputs below, then identify what additional local information and professional planning a real decision would require."}},{"k":"land_all_four_sliders_inside_their_ide","v":{"s":"compare how four inputs affect this classroom score. A high score is not authorization or evidence that a real burn is safe."}},{"k":"ideal_humidity_30_to_55_percent","v":{"s":"Ideal Humidity: 30 to 55 percent"}},{"k":"ideal_wind_3_to_12_mph","v":{"s":"Ideal Wind: 3 to 12 mph"}},{"k":"ideal_fuel_moisture_12_to_25_percent","v":{"s":"Ideal Fuel Moisture: 12 to 25 percent"}},{"k":"evaluate_burn_plan","v":{"s":"Compare Classroom Conditions"}},{"k":"evaluate_burn_plan_2","v":{"s":"🔥 Compare Classroom Conditions"}},{"k":"indigenous_wisdom","v":{"s":"Indigenous Wisdom: "}},{"k":"traditional_fire_practitioners_burn_on","v":{"s":"Cultural burning knowledge is place-based, relational, and held by specific communities. Weather readings are only part of that knowledge. Learn from local Indigenous fire practitioners and qualified fire professionals; this classroom activity cannot represent or authorize their practice."}},{"k":"back_to_concepts","v":{"s":"Back to Concepts"}},{"k":"back_to_concepts_2","v":{"s":"← Back to Concepts"}},{"k":"what_this_quiz_covers","v":{"s":"📜 What this quiz covers"}},{"k":"32_multi_choice_items_across_six_theme","v":{"s":"32 multi-choice items across six themes:"}},{"k":"indigenous_fire_stewardship_timelines_","v":{"s":"Indigenous fire stewardship (timelines, nations, practices)"}},{"k":"fire_adapted_ecosystem_mechanics_serot","v":{"s":"Fire-adapted ecosystem mechanics (serotiny, smoke-cued germination)"}},{"k":"combustion_chemistry_fire_triangle_fla","v":{"s":"Combustion chemistry (fire triangle, flashover, fuel moisture)"}},{"k":"watershed_and_soil_response_hydrophobi","v":{"s":"Watershed and soil response (hydrophobic soils, debris flows)"}},{"k":"suppression_policy_failures_and_their_","v":{"s":"Suppression policy failures and their case studies"}},{"k":"beavers_prescribed_burning_and_modern_","v":{"s":"Beavers, prescribed burning, and modern restoration tools"}},{"k":"streak_counters_track_consecutive_corr","v":{"s":"Streak counters track consecutive correct answers. The quiz loops, so you can keep going for mastery."}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"best_streak","v":{"s":"Best Streak"}},{"k":"select_answer","v":{"s":"Select Answer"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next Question"}},{"k":"next_question_2","v":{"s":"Next Question →"}},{"k":"ai_fire_ecology_tutor","v":{"s":"🤖 AI Fire Ecology Tutor"}},{"k":"ask_about_fire_ecology_indigenous_prac","v":{"s":"Ask about fire ecology, Indigenous practices, prescribed burning..."}},{"k":"smoke_chemicals_that_trigger_life","v":{"s":"🧪 Smoke Chemicals That Trigger Life"}},{"k":"fire_survival_strategies","v":{"s":"🌱 Fire Survival Strategies"}},{"k":"fire_dependent_species_gallery","v":{"s":"🌺 Fire-Dependent Species Gallery"}},{"k":"watershed_impact_cultural_burn_vs_wild","v":{"s":"📊 Watershed Impact: Cultural Burn vs. Wildfire"}},{"k":"metric","v":{"s":"Metric"}},{"