to 40,000 ft at the same speed. Lift drops because density drops."}},{"k":"compare_the_f_22_small_fast_wings_vs_t","v":{"s":"Compare the F-22 (small fast wings) vs the 747 (big slower wings) — both fly, but very differently."}},{"k":"lower_c_to_0_3_cruise_config_vs_raise_","v":{"s":"Lower Cₗ to 0.3 (cruise config) vs raise it to 1.5 (flaps extended for landing)."}},{"k":"normal_flow","v":{"s":"🟢 Normal flow"}},{"k":"air_remains_attached_lift_increases_pr","v":{"s":"Air remains attached. Lift increases predictably with AoA."}},{"k":"boundary_separation","v":{"s":"🟡 Boundary separation"}},{"k":"lift_still_rising_but_turbulent_flow_d","v":{"s":"Lift still rising but turbulent flow detaching. Buffet may begin."}},{"k":"full_stall","v":{"s":"🔴 Full stall"}},{"k":"lift_collapses_wing_no_longer_producin","v":{"s":"Lift collapses. Wing no longer producing required lift — nose drops."}},{"k":"back_to_menu_5","v":{"s":"← Back to menu"}},{"k":"stall_discovery_2","v":{"s":"⚠️ Stall discovery"}},{"k":"airspeed_kts","v":{"s":"Airspeed (kts)"}},{"k":"altitude_ft","v":{"s":"Altitude (ft)"}},{"k":"angle_of_attack","v":{"s":"Angle of attack (°)"}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset_3","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_free_text_no_right_answer_w","v":{"s":"Hypothesis (free text — no right answer): Which slider matters most for stall? Is airspeed alone sufficient to prevent it?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"hold_airspeed_steady_vary_aoa_when_doe","v":{"s":"Hold airspeed steady. Vary AoA. When does the state flip?"}},{"k":"at_a_fixed_aoa_vary_airspeed_does_the_","v":{"s":"At a fixed AoA, vary airspeed. Does the state change?"}},{"k":"find_two_combinations_that_produce_a_s","v":{"s":"Find two combinations that produce a stall. What do they share?"}},{"k":"real_pilots_are_taught_angle_of_attack","v":{"s":"Real pilots are taught \"angle of attack, not airspeed\" causes stall. Investigate why."}},{"k":"i_think_i_understand_explain_in_own_wo","v":{"s":"I think I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words_what_role_do","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words: what role do airspeed, altitude, and AoA each play?"}},{"k":"loading_skyschool","v":{"s":"Loading SkySchool..."}},{"k":"first_flight_desc","v":{"s":"Take off, climb to 3,000 ft, maintain level flight for 30 seconds, then descend and land."}},{"k":"cross_country_desc","v":{"s":"Fly from Portland (PWM) to JFK using heading and distance. No GPS — dead reckoning only!"}},{"k":"mountain_flying_desc","v":{"s":"Fly to Denver at 5,431 ft elevation. Learn how altitude affects aircraft performance."}},{"k":"energy_management_desc","v":{"s":"Trade speed for altitude and back. Demonstrate conservation of energy."}},{"k":"world_tour_desc","v":{"s":"Visit 5 airports across different continents. Learn geography as you fly!"}},{"k":"coastal_rescue_desc","v":{"s":"A boater is in distress 5nm offshore from PWM. Fly the rescue helicopter to the location, hover stably below 100 ft AGL, lower the hoist for 5 seconds, then return to PWM and land."}},{"k":"drone_survey_desc","v":{"s":"Fly a Part 107 drone survey of Casco Bay. Stay below 400 ft AGL, within visual line of sight, capture 4 photo waypoints, and return to launch."}},{"k":"drone_powerline_desc","v":{"s":"Real Part 107 work: fly low and slow along a 6-segment powerline, photograph each defect site for the utility crew, and return to launch. Tighter tolerance — 30 ft, 60 ft AGL, 5 kts."}},{"k":"americas_desc","v":{"s":"Fly from Canada to Argentina — name every capital!"}},{"k":"europe_desc","v":{"s":"Blast through 8 European capitals in 90 seconds!"}},{"k":"asia_desc","v":{"s":"From the Middle East to the Pacific — how many can you name?"}},{"k":"world_desc","v":{"s":"The ultimate challenge — 12 cities across every continent!"}},{"k":"capitals_desc","v":{"s":"Capitals only — can you get all 20?"}},{"k":"landmarks_desc","v":{"s":"Fly over the world's most famous natural wonders!"}},{"k":"lift_content","v":{"s":"Lift is generated by the shape of the wing (airfoil). Air moves faster over the curved top surface, creating lower pressure above the wing than below. This pressure difference pushes the wing UP. Lift increases with speed² — double your speed, quadruple your lift!"}},{"k":"drag_content","v":{"s":"Drag opposes motion through the air. There are two types: parasite drag (from the shape of the aircraft pushing through air) increases with speed², and induced drag (a byproduct of creating lift) decreases with speed. Together they create a 'drag bucket' — an optimal speed where total drag is minimized."}},{"k":"thrust_content","v":{"s":"Thrust is produced by engines — either propellers (which are spinning airfoils) or jet engines (which accelerate air backward, pushing the plane forward via Newton's Third Law). More thrust = more acceleration. In level flight, thrust exactly equals drag."}},{"k":"weight_content","v":{"s":"In straight, level, unaccelerated flight, four forces are in perfect balance: Lift = Weight, and Thrust = Drag. When you pitch up, you trade speed for altitude (kinetic energy → potential energy). When you pitch down, you trade altitude for speed. This is conservation of energy in action!"}},{"k":"bernoulli_content","v":{"s":"Daniel Bernoulli discovered that as a fluid (including air) speeds up, its pressure drops. This is why airplane wings work: the curved upper surface makes air travel faster, reducing pressure on top. The higher pressure underneath pushes the wing up. You can demonstrate this by blowing over a piece of paper — it rises!"}},{"k":"navigation_content","v":{"s":"Modern navigation uses GPS, but the fundamentals haven't changed: heading (compass direction), airspeed, altitude, and time. Great circle routes (the shortest path between two points on a sphere) appear curved on flat maps but are actually straight on a globe. That's why flights from the US to Europe often go over Greenland!"}},{"k":"skyschool_cockpit_briefing","v":{"s":"SkySchool Cockpit Briefing"}},{"k":"free_flight","v":{"s":"Free Flight"}},{"k":"file_route","v":{"s":"File Route"}},{"k":"open_learn_mode","v":{"s":"Open Learn mode"}},{"k":"open_pre_flight_checklist","v":{"s":"Open pre-flight checklist"}},{"k":"pre_flight","v":{"s":"Pre-Flight"}},{"k":"cockpit_briefing_mission","v":{"s":"Default mission: depart {dep}, build airspeed, rotate near {vr} knots, then follow the route or explore landmarks."}},{"k":"cockpit_briefing_mission_glider","v":{"s":"Default mission: release from the tow at 3,000 ft over {dep}, then trade altitude for speed and hunt for lift."}},{"k":"cockpit_briefing_mission_heli","v":{"s":"Default mission: lift off from {dep} on the collective, ease the cyclic forward for speed, then follow the route or explore landmarks."}},{"k":"cockpit_briefing_mission_drone","v":{"s":"Default mission: launch from {dep}, stay below 400 ft AGL and within visual line of sight, then follow the route or explore landmarks."}},{"k":"lesson_completed","v":{"s":"✓ Lesson completed"}},{"k":"mark_lesson_complete","v":{"s":"📚 Mark lesson complete"}},{"k":"exit_fullscreen","v":{"s":"⛶ Exit Fullscreen"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Stall factors explanation"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Stall factors hypothesis"}}],"id":524}},{"k":"fluency","v":{"o":[{"k":"fluency_drill_started","v":{"s":"Fluency drill started! "}},{"k":"probe_button","v":{"s":"Fluency Probe"}},{"k":"probe_button_aria","v":{"s":"Open the timed fluency probe in the Math panel"}},{"k":"panel_opened","v":{"s":"Fluency Probes is open in the Math panel. Set the operation and press Start."}},{"k":"mixed_blocks_note","v":{"s":"Note: fluency blocks are not part of the generated document. Run them from the Math panel's Fluency Probes mode."}},{"k":"stem_assessment_saved_to_resources","v":{"s":"STEM Assessment saved to resources ("}},{"k":"stem_assessment","v":{"s":"STEM Assessment: "}}],"id":525}},{"k":"forge","v":{"o":[{"k":"no_ai","v":{"s":"