y"}},{"k":"iep_goals_3","v":{"s":"IEP goals"}},{"k":"lessons_3","v":{"s":"Lessons"}},{"k":"trivia_2","v":{"s":"Trivia"}},{"k":"sessions_2","v":{"s":"Sessions"}},{"k":"settings_3","v":{"s":"Settings"}},{"k":"learn_2","v":{"s":"Learn"}},{"k":"visualization_heavy_no_quiz_pressure","v":{"s":"Visualization-heavy, no quiz pressure"}},{"k":"practice_5","v":{"s":"Practice"}},{"k":"skill_focused_scored","v":{"s":"Skill-focused, scored"}},{"k":"apply_2","v":{"s":"Apply"}},{"k":"real_world_contexts_and_games","v":{"s":"Real-world contexts and games"}},{"k":"teacher_2","v":{"s":"Teacher"}},{"k":"tools_for_instructors_and_ieps","v":{"s":"Tools for instructors and IEPs"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"fraction_lab","v":{"s":"🍕 Fraction Lab"}},{"k":"fraction_lab_mode","v":{"s":"Fraction Lab mode"}},{"k":"fraction_lab_sections","v":{"s":"Fraction Lab sections"}},{"k":"practice_pizza_bar_model_intuition","v":{"s":"Practice — pizza + bar model intuition"}},{"k":"common_core_fluency_automatic_recall_o","v":{"s":"Common Core fluency = automatic recall of basic facts in under 3 seconds. Pizza model for what-out-of-the-whole; bar model for comparison and operation."}},{"k":"compare_cross_multiply_or_benchmark","v":{"s":"Compare — cross-multiply or benchmark"}},{"k":"three_tools_same_denom_just_compare_to","v":{"s":"Three tools: same denom (just compare tops), cross-multiplication (a/b vs c/d → ad vs bc), or benchmark to ½ / ¾. Last one is fastest in your head."}},{"k":"operations_add_sub_mul_div_with_rules","v":{"s":"Operations — add/sub/mul/div with rules"}},{"k":"add_sub_common_denominator_first_multi","v":{"s":"Add/sub: common denominator first. Multiply: tops×tops, bottoms×bottoms. Divide: keep-change-flip (multiply by reciprocal). Always simplify to lowest terms."}},{"k":"equivalents_same_value_different_form","v":{"s":"Equivalents — same value, different form"}},{"k":"multiply_top_and_bottom_by_the_same_nu","v":{"s":"Multiply top AND bottom by the same number; value stays put. ½ = 2/4 = 50/100 = 0.5 = 50%. Equivalent fractions are the bridge between fractions, decimals, and percents."}},{"k":"converter_mixed_improper_decimal","v":{"s":"Converter — mixed ↔ improper ↔ decimal"}},{"k":"2_11_4_long_division_gives_a_terminati","v":{"s":"2¾ = 11/4. Long division gives a terminating decimal (denom = 2¹ × 5ⁿ) or a repeating one (any other prime in the denom). 1/3 = 0.333... forever."}},{"k":"wall_visual_proof_of_equivalence","v":{"s":"Wall — visual proof of equivalence"}},{"k":"stack_the_fraction_wall_1_whole_2_halv","v":{"s":"Stack the fraction wall: 1 whole = 2 halves = 4 quarters = 8 eighths. Same height = same value. Cuisenaire rods (1952) made this concrete; the wall is the digital descendant."}},{"k":"equals_exactly_1_whole","v":{"s":"🟢 Equals exactly 1 whole"}},{"k":"less_than_1","v":{"s":"🟡 Less than 1"}},{"k":"more_than_1_mixed_number","v":{"s":"🔴 More than 1 (mixed number)"}},{"k":"slider_mixer_discovery","v":{"s":"🎚 Slider Mixer discovery"}},{"k":"mix_n_copies_of_a_fraction_discover_wh","v":{"s":"Mix N copies of a fraction. Discover when they sum to a whole. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_when_do_n_copies_of_a_fract","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: When do N copies of a fraction equal exactly 1?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"what_relationship_between_count_and_de","v":{"s":"What relationship between count and denominator gives exactly 1?"}},{"k":"find_two_combinations_that_produce_und","v":{"s":"Find two combinations that produce \"under\". What do they share?"}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_how_count_num_den_determines_w","v":{"s":"Explain how count × (num/den) determines whether you reach 1."}},{"k":"design_note_discrete_3_state_outcome_n","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 3-state outcome; no exact-match score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"fraction_challenge","v":{"s":"🎯 Fraction Challenge"}},{"k":"generate_challenge","v":{"s":"Generate Challenge"}},{"k":"generate_challenge_2","v":{"s":"🎲 Generate Challenge"}},{"k":"your_answer","v":{"s":"Your answer..."}},{"k":"check_3","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"check_4","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"next_challenge","v":{"s":"Next Challenge"}},{"k":"next_challenge_2","v":{"s":"➡️ Next Challenge"}},{"k":"ai_tutor_2","v":{"s":"AI Tutor"}},{"k":"ai_tutor_3","v":{"s":"🤖 AI Tutor"}},{"k":"benchmarks_4","v":{"s":"Benchmarks"}},{"k":"benchmarks_5","v":{"s":"📋 Benchmarks"}},{"k":"benchmark_fractions","v":{"s":"📋 Benchmark Fractions"}},{"k":"fraction_6","v":{"s":"Fraction"}},{"k":"decimal_5","v":{"s":"Decimal"}},{"k":"percent_6","v":{"s":"Percent"}},{"k":"visual_4","v":{"s":"Visual"}},{"k":"1_6_tabs_n_new_challenge_b_benchmarks_","v":{"s":"⌨️ 1-6: tabs | N: new challenge | B: benchmarks | P: pie/bar | ?: AI tutor"}},{"k":"equivalent_fractions_same_value_differ","v":{"s":"🍰 Equivalent Fractions — Same value, different forms"}},{"k":"ai_question_label","v":{"s":"Ask a question about fractions"}},{"k":"challenge_answer_label","v":{"s":"Your answer"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Fraction equivalence explanation"}},{"k":"frac_a_den_label","v":{"s":"Fraction A denominator"}},{"k":"frac_a_num_label","v":{"s":"Fraction A numerator"}},{"k":"frac_b_den_label","v":{"s":"Fraction B denominator"}},{"k":"frac_b_num_label","v":{"s":"Fraction B numerator"}},{"k":"glossary_search_label","v":{"s":"Search fraction glossary"}},{"k":"hidden_guess_label","v":{"s":"Guess the hidden fraction"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Fraction equivalence hypothesis"}},{"k":"numberline_img_label","v":{"s":"Number line showing the current fraction"}},{"k":"pattern_guess_label","v":{"s":"Next fraction in the pattern"}},{"k":"wall_img_label","v":{"s":"Interactive fraction wall comparing equivalent fraction lengths"}},{"k":"build_a_fraction","v":{"s":"Build a fraction"}},{"k":"learning_path","v":{"s":"Follow a learning path"}},{"k":"build_fraction_heading","v":{"s":"Build a fraction — connect the picture and the number"}},{"k":"build_fraction_guidance","v":{"s":"Start with equal parts of one whole. Build a fraction, compare its size, then connect it to a decimal and percent. Work at your own pace."}},{"k":"equal_fractions_statement","v":{"s":"The fractions are equal."}},{"k":"is_larger","v":{"s":"is larger."}},{"k":"negative_magnitude_model","v":{"s":"Negative quantity: these wholes show its magnitude; see its position left of zero below."}},{"k":"more_wholes","v":{"s":"more whole units"}},{"k":"whole_units","v":{"s":"whole units"}},{"k":"compare_bar_view","v":{"s":"Bar models"}},{"k":"compare_pie_view","v":{"s":"Pie models"}},{"k":"compare_equal_wholes","v":{"s":"Each complete bar or circle represents one whole of the same size. Compare shaded amounts, not just the number of pieces."}},{"k":"compare_line_title","v":{"s":"Locate both fractions on the same scale"}},{"k":"compare_line_scroll","v":{"s":"Scrollable fraction comparison number line"}},{"k":"compare_line_direction","v":{"s":"Numbers increase from left to right."}},{"k":"compare_line_hint","v":{"s":"Scroll if needed. A uses a circle; B uses a diamond. The fraction farther right is larger. Matching positions mean equal values."}},{"k":"same_parts_explanation","v":{"s":"Explain with same-sized parts"}},{"k":"common_parts_reason","v":{"s":"Rename both fractions using the same denominator. Each part is now the same size, so compare how many parts each fraction contains."}},{"k":"cross_products_connection","v":{"s":"Cross-products compare counts of the same-sized parts too: "}},{"k":"compare_line_hint_fitted","v":{"s":"A uses a circle; B uses a diamond. The fraction farther right is larger. Matching positions mean equal values."}},{"k":"difference_label","v":{"s":"difference "}},{"k":"compare_equal_wholes_title","v":{"s":"Compare fractions using equal wholes"}},{"k":"compare_reasoning_intro","v":{"s":"Start with equal-sized wholes. Compare shaded amounts and number-line positions, then explain the relationship using same-sized parts."}},{"k":"quantity_zero_reason","v":{"s":"A is zero. Multiplying it, or dividing it by a nonzero number, gives zero again."}},{"k":"quantity_one_reason","v":{"s":"B equals 1, so the numerical value of A stays the same."}},{"k":"quantity_mul_small","v":{"s":"B is less than 1. Taking that fraction of a positive A gives less than A."}},{"k":"quantity_mul_large","v":{"s":"B is greater than 1. Taking more than one copy of a positive A gives more than A."}},{"k":"quantity_div_small","v":{"s":"B is between 0 and 1. Counting smaller-than-one groups gives a numerical quotient greater than A."}},{"k":"quantity_div_large","v":{"s":"B is greater than 1. Counting larger-than-one groups gives a numerical quotient smaller than a positive A."}},{"k":"quantity_smaller","v":{"s":"Smaller than A"}},{"k":"quantity_equal","v":{"s":"Equal to A"}},{"k":"quantity_larger","v":{"s":"Larger than A"}},{"k":"quantity_predict_toggle","v":{"s":"Predict before revealing the result"}},{"k":"quantity_predict_question","v":{"s":"Compare the numerical result with Fraction A. What do you expect, and how does B help you decide?"}},{"k":"quantity_predict_legend","v":{"s":"Predict the result compared with A"}},{"k":"quantity_check_prediction","v":{"s":"Check prediction"}},{"k":"quantity_reveal_model","v":{"s":"Reveal worked model"}},{"k":"quantity_prediction_confirmed","v":{"s":"Prediction confirmed. "}},{"k":"quantity_prediction_review","v":{"s":"Compare your prediction with the model. "}},{"k":"quantity_product_title","v":{"s":"Multiplication: an area made from fractional pieces"}},{"k":"quantity_product_dimensions","v":{"s":"Rectangle height A and width B: "}},{"k":"quantity_product_unit","v":{"s":"The dashed square is 1 square unit. Each small rectangle is "}},{"k":"quantity_square_unit_suffix","v":{"s":" of a square unit. The shaded rectangle can extend beyond one whole."}},{"k":"quantity_zero_product","v":{"s":"One side length is zero, so no area is shaded."}},{"k":"quantity_count_product","v":{"s":"Count the selected rows times the selected columns. Multiplying the denominators tells you the size of each small piece."}},{"k":"quantity_division_title","v":{"s":"Division: how many groups of B fit in A?"}},{"k":"quantity_available","v":{"s":"Available amount: "}},{"k":"quantity_group_size","v":{"s":"Size of one group: "}},{"k":"quantity_common_pieces","v":{"s":"Use equal-sized pieces: "}},{"k":"quantity_group_outline","v":{"s":"Each outlined bar below holds one group, not necessarily one whole unit. A full bar contains "}},{"k":"quantity_units_suffix","v":{"s":" units."}},{"k":"quantity_start_grouping","v":{"s":"Start grouping"}},{"k":"quantity_previous_group","v":{"s":"Previous group"}},{"k":"quantity_next_group","v":{"s":"Next group"}},{"k":"quantity_show_groups","v":{"s":"Show all groups"}},{"k":"quantity_full_groups","v":{"s":"Full groups counted: "}},{"k":"quantity_partial_added","v":{"s":"The partial group is included. "}},{"k":"quantity_amount_remaining","v":{"s":"Amount still ungrouped: "}},{"k":"quantity_group_number","v":{"s":"Group "}},{"k":"quantity_empty_group","v":{"s":"One-group outline (empty)"}},{"k":"quantity_more_full_groups","v":{"s":"additional full groups, each with the same amount. The count includes these groups."}},{"k":"quantity_partial_group","v":{"s":"Partial group"}},{"k":"quantity_remaining_is","v":{"s":" units remain after the full groups. This is "}},{"k":"quantity_of_one_group","v":{"s":" of one group."}},{"k":"quantity_remainder_explanation","v":{"s":"Compare leftover pieces with the pieces in a group, not with the pieces in a whole unit."}},{"k":"quantity_zero_groups","v":{"s":"There is no amount to group, so the quotient is zero."}},{"k":"quantity_inverse_check","v":{"s":"Check with multiplication: "}},{"k":"quantity_signed_input_help","v":{"s":"Use whole-number numerators from -20 to 20 and denominators from 1 to 20. Finish the highlighted inputs to update the model."}},{"k":"quantity_input_help","v":{"s":"Use whole-number numerators from 0 to 20 and denominators from 1 to 20. Finish the highlighted inputs to update the model."}},{"k":"quantity_wait_prediction","v":{"s":"Check a prediction or reveal the worked model to see the exact value."}},{"k":"nav_fraction_models","v":{"s":"Fraction models"}},{"k":"nav_build_draw_write","v":{"s":"Build, draw & write"}},{"k":"nav_fraction_wall","v":{"s":"Fraction wall"}},{"k":"nav_hands_on_tools","v":{"s":"Hands-on tools"}},{"k":"nav_help_about","v":{"s":"Help & about"}},{"k":"nav_calculator_patterns","v":{"s":"Calculator & patterns"}},{"k":"nav_practice_quizzes","v":{"s":"Practice & quizzes"}},{"k":"nav_multistep_problems","v":{"s":"Multi-step problems"}},{"k":"nav_teaching_guides","v":{"s":"Teaching guides"}},{"k":"nav_goals_progress","v":{"s":"Goals & progress"}},{"k":"nav_learn_hint","v":{"s":"Explore fractions with pictures and hands-on models."}},{"k":"nav_practice_hint","v":{"s":"Compare, calculate, or choose a quiz to check your understanding."}},{"k":"nav_apply_hint","v":{"s":"Use fractions in stories, everyday problems, and games."}},{"k":"nav_teacher_hint","v":{"s":"Find planning, assessment, and teaching resources."}},{"k":"nav_practice_score","v":{"s":"Practice score:"}},{"k":"nav_choose_focus","v":{"s":"Choose a focus"}},{"k":"nav_choose_activity","v":{"s":"Choose an activity"}},{"k":"nav_show_fewer","v":{"s":"Show fewer activities"}},{"k":"nav_show_all","v":{"s":"Show all activities"}},{"k":"nav_build_hint","v":{"s":"Set the number of equal parts in one whole, then choose how many to fill."}},{"k":"nav_compare_hint","v":{"s":"Set two fractions, compare equal-sized wholes, and explain which amount is greater."}},{"k":"nav_operations_hint","v":{"s":"Choose an operation and enter two fractions. Use the visual model and worked steps to explain the result."}},{"k":"nav_equivalents_hint","v":{"s":"Change the number of equal parts while keeping the same amount. Look for fractions with the same value."}},{"k":"nav_converter_hint","v":{"s":"Connect mixed numbers, improper fractions, decimals, and percents. Choose a conversion to see its steps."}},{"k":"nav_wall_hint","v":{"s":"Compare rows using the same whole. Look for pieces that line up to find equivalent fractions."}},{"k":"nav_cra_hint","v":{"s":"Move from objects to pictures to fraction notation: concrete, representational, and abstract (CRA)."}},{"k":"strip_instructions","v":{"s":"Select a strip section to fill that many parts. You can also use the sliders or number fields. With a keyboard, Tab to a section and press Enter or Space."}}],"id":527}},{"k":"func_graph","v":{"o":[{"k":"cell_sim","v":{"s":"Cell Sim"}}],"id":528}},{"k":"func_grapher","v":{"o":[{"k":"ud83dudcc8","v":{"s":"📈 "}},{"k":"correct_it","v":{"s":"✅ Correct! It\\"}},{"k":"that","v":{"s":"❌ That\\"}},{"k":"linear","v":{"s":"Linear"}},{"k":"quadratic","v":{"s":"Quadratic"}},{"k":"cubic","v":{"s":"Cubic"}},{"k":"trig","v":{"s":"Trig"}},{"k":"exponential","v":{"s":"Exponential"}},{"k":"absolute","v":{"s":"Absolute"}}],"id":529}},{"k":"funcgrapher","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_explanation_ready","v":{"s":"Explanation ready."}},{"k":"a11y_function_investigation_pathway","v":{"s":"Function investigation pathway"}},{"k":"a11y_function_families","v":{"s":"Function families"}},{"k":"a11y_function_graph_showing_the_configured_curve","v":{"s":"Function graph showing the configured curve"}},{"k":"a11y_function_zoo_comparison_of_six_common_function","v":{"s":"Function Zoo comparison of six common function shapes"}},{"k":"reflected_over_x_axis","v":{"s":"Reflected over x-axis"}},{"k":"square_root","v":{"s":"Square Root"}},{"k":"logarithm","v":{"s":"Logarithm"}},{"k":"rational","v":{"s":"Rational"}},{"k":"function_grapher_keyboard_shortcuts_1_","v":{"s":"Function Grapher. Keyboard shortcuts: 1 through 6 pick a function type, D derivative, A area, T table, L learn."}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"function_grapher","v":{"s":"📈 Function Grapher"}},{"k":"view","v":{"s":"🔍 View"}},{"k":"zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"zoom_in_2","v":{"s":"➕ Zoom In"}},{"k":"zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"zoom_out_2","v":{"s":"➖ Zoom Out"}},{"k":"pan_left","v":{"s":"Pan left"}},{"k":"pan_right","v":{"s":"Pan right"}},{"k":"pan_up","v":{"s":"Pan up"}},{"k":"pan_down","v":{"s":"Pan down"}},{"k":"reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset view"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"trace_x_position","v":{"s":"Trace x position"}},{"k":"table_of_values","v":{"s":"📋 Table of Values"}},{"k":"funcgrapher_data_table","v":{"s":"funcgrapher data table"}},{"k":"understanding_functions","v":{"s":"📖 Understanding Functions"}},{"k":"roots_x_intercepts","v":{"s":"🔴 Roots (x-intercepts): "}},{"k":"where_the_function_crosses_the_x_axis_","v":{"s":"Where the function crosses the x-axis. At these points, f(x) = 0. Solving for the roots is essential in algebra and calculus."}},{"k":"y_intercept","v":{"s":"🟢 Y-intercept: "}},{"k":"where_the_function_crosses_the_y_axis_","v":{"s":"Where the function crosses the y-axis. This is the value of f(0) — simply plug in x = 0."}},{"k":"slope_tangent_line","v":{"s":"📌 Slope & Tangent Line: "}},{"k":"the_slope_tells_you_how_steep_the_func","v":{"s":"The slope tells you how steep the function is at any point. The tangent line touches the curve at exactly one point. Use the trace slider to explore!"}},{"k":"derivative_f_x","v":{"s":"📉 Derivative f′(x): "}},{"k":"the_derivative_measures_the_rate_of_ch","v":{"s":"The derivative measures the rate of change. Toggle it on to see how the slope varies across the function. Where f′(x) = 0, the function has a local max or min."}},{"k":"area_under_the_curve","v":{"s":"∫ Area Under the Curve: "}},{"k":"the_shaded_area_represents_the_integra","v":{"s":"The shaded area represents the integral — the total \"accumulation\" of f(x). In real life, this could mean distance traveled, energy consumed, or probability."}},{"k":"parabolas","v":{"s":"∩ Parabolas: "}},{"k":"when_a_0_the_parabola_opens_up_when_a_","v":{"s":"When a > 0, the parabola opens up (⏣). When a < 0, it opens down (⏢). The vertex is the turning point. Try changing 'a' to see!"}},{"k":"trig_functions","v":{"s":"∼ Trig Functions: "}},{"k":"amplitude_a_controls_the_height_freque","v":{"s":"Amplitude (a) controls the height, frequency (b) controls how many cycles, and phase shift (c) slides the wave left/right. Period = 2π/b."}},{"k":"ai_explanation","v":{"s":"🧠 AI Explanation"}},{"k":"quick_presets","v":{"s":"⚡ Quick Presets"}},{"k":"y_2x_1","v":{"s":"y = 2x + 1"}},{"k":"slope_2_y_intercept_1","v":{"s":"Slope 2, y-intercept 1"}},{"k":"y_x","v":{"s":"y = x²"}},{"k":"standard_parabola","v":{"s":"Standard parabola"}},{"k":"y_x_4","v":{"s":"y = -x² + 4"}},{"k":"inverted_parabola_vertex_at_0_4","v":{"s":"Inverted parabola, vertex at (0,4)"}},{"k":"y_sin_x","v":{"s":"y = sin(x)"}},{"k":"standard_sine_wave","v":{"s":"Standard sine wave"}},{"k":"y_2sin_3x","v":{"s":"y = 2sin(3x)"}},{"k":"amplitude_2_period_2_3","v":{"s":"Amplitude=2, period=2π/3"}},{"k":"y_x_2","v":{"s":"y = x³"}},{"k":"cubic_with_inflection_at_origin","v":{"s":"Cubic with inflection at origin"}},{"k":"y_e_x","v":{"s":"y = e^x"}},{"k":"natural_exponential_growth","v":{"s":"Natural exponential growth"}},{"k":"y_x_3","v":{"s":"y = |x|"}},{"k":"v_shaped_absolute_value","v":{"s":"V-shaped absolute value"}},{"k":"f_x","v":{"s":"—— f(x)"}},{"k":"f_x_2","v":{"s":"- - - f′(x)"}},{"k":"g_x","v":{"s":"- ─ - g(x)"}},{"k":"roots","v":{"s":"🔴 Roots"}},{"k":"y_intercept_2","v":{"s":"🟢 y-intercept"}},{"k":"name_that_graph","v":{"s":"🎯 Name That Graph"}},{"k":"find_the_root","v":{"s":"📍 Find the Root"}},{"k":"y_intercept_3","v":{"s":"🟢 Y-Intercept?"}},{"k":"start_challenge","v":{"s":"Start Challenge"}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"snapshot_2","v":{"s":"📸 Snapshot"}},{"k":"plain","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"using_simple_everyday_words_and_short_","v":{"s":"using simple everyday words and short sentences, no jargon"}},{"k":"grade_5","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"for_a_5th_grade_student","v":{"s":"for a 5th grade student"}},{"k":"high_school","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"for_a_high_school_algebra_or_pre_calc_","v":{"s":"for a high school algebra or pre-calc student, including vocabulary like slope, intercept, vertex, period, amplitude"}},{"k":"explain_at_my_level","v":{"s":"✨ Explain at my level"}},{"k":"reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"click_explain_for_the_ai_tutor_to_desc","v":{"s":"Click “Explain” for the AI tutor to describe this function at your chosen reading level."}},{"k":"function_zoo_six_common_function_shape","v":{"s":"Function Zoo — Six common function shapes"}},{"k":"linear","v":{"s":"Linear"}},{"k":"quadratic","v":{"s":"Quadratic"}},{"k":"cubic","v":{"s":"Cubic"}},{"k":"exponential","v":{"s":"Exponential"}},{"k":"sine","v":{"s":"Sine"}},{"k":"tall_fast_intense","v":{"s":"🌊 Tall + Fast (intense)"}},{"k":"tall_wave","v":{"s":"🗻 Tall wave"}},{"k":"fast_cycle","v":{"s":"⚡ Fast cycle"}},{"k":"short_wave","v":{"s":"〰️ Short wave"}},{"k":"standard","v":{"s":"🟢 Standard"}},{"k":"wave_parameter_discovery","v":{"s":"🌊 Wave parameter discovery"}},{"k":"sliders_for_amplitude_frequency_phase_","v":{"s":"Sliders for amplitude, frequency, phase. Discrete 5-state classification. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_how_does_phase_shift_affect","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: How does phase shift affect the visible wave?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"find_two_settings_that_produce_visuall","v":{"s":"Find two settings that produce visually identical waves."}},{"k":"what_does_negative_amplitude_do","v":{"s":"What does negative amplitude do?"}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_how_each_parameter_shapes_a_si","v":{"s":"Explain how each parameter shapes a sine wave."}},{"k":"design_note_discrete_5_state_marker_no","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 5-state marker; no wave score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"true_or_false","v":{"s":"🧠 True or False?"}},{"k":"challenge_level","v":{"s":"Challenge level"}},{"k":"level","v":{"s":"Level:"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Function grapher hypothesis"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Function grapher explanation"}},{"k":"trace_undefined","v":{"s":"The function is undefined at this x."}},{"k":"trace_no_derivative","v":{"s":"no finite derivative at this point."}},{"k":"root_meaning_refined","v":{"s":"A root has f(x) = 0. The graph can cross or touch the x-axis there. Numerical searches can miss roots, so use algebra or the table to check."}},{"k":"tangent_meaning_refined","v":{"s":"A tangent describes the local slope where the derivative exists. It can cross the curve or meet it again. Corners and domain boundaries need special care."}},{"k":"integral_nonnegative_endpoint","v":{"s":"Choose a right endpoint at or above 0 for this overlay."}},{"k":"integral_undefined_point","v":{"s":"The expression is undefined at a point in this interval."}},{"k":"integral_pole","v":{"s":"The integral diverges at the vertical asymptote."}},{"k":"integral_domain_limit","v":{"s":"The interval includes values outside the real domain or an endpoint requiring an improper-integral calculation."}},{"k":"wave_observation_prompt","v":{"s":"Change one parameter at a time. Compare wave height, spacing, and horizontal shift, then record what you notice."}},{"k":"integral_numeric_range","v":{"s":"A sampled value is outside the numerical range. Try a smaller interval or coefficients."}},{"k":"wave_compare_evidence","v":{"s":"Compare two logged settings. Describe which parameter changed and how the wave responded."}},{"k":"wave_focus_height","v":{"s":"Height"}},{"k":"wave_height_question","v":{"s":"How does a change the height and orientation? Keep b and φ fixed."}},{"k":"wave_focus_spacing","v":{"s":"Spacing"}},{"k":"wave_spacing_question","v":{"s":"How does b change the distance between repeats? Keep a and φ fixed."}},{"k":"wave_focus_shift","v":{"s":"Shift"}},{"k":"wave_shift_question","v":{"s":"Which direction does the wave move as φ increases? Keep a and b fixed."}},{"k":"wave_saved_setting","v":{"s":"Saved setting"}},{"k":"wave_start_comparison","v":{"s":"Save a starting setting, then move one slider to compare the curves."}},{"k":"wave_same_settings","v":{"s":"The current parameters match the saved reference."}},{"k":"wave_one_change","v":{"s":"One parameter changed: "}},{"k":"wave_held_fixed","v":{"s":"Held fixed: "}},{"k":"wave_multiple_changes","v":{"s":"Several parameters changed. Restore the reference and change one slider to isolate its effect."}},{"k":"wave_investigation_title","v":{"s":"Wave investigation"}},{"k":"wave_investigation_intro","v":{"s":"Explore y = a sin(bx + φ). Save a setting and change one parameter; the dashed curve keeps your reference visible."}},{"k":"wave_focus_label","v":{"s":"Choose a wave investigation"}},{"k":"wave_plot_description","v":{"s":"Wave plot linked to a, b, and φ. Solid curve: "}},{"k":"wave_plot_window","v":{"s":"Fixed window: x from −2π to 2π radians, y from −3.5 to 3.5."}},{"k":"wave_at_zero","v":{"s":"At x = 0, y ≈ "}},{"k":"wave_vertical_multiplier","v":{"s":"Vertical multiplier a"}},{"k":"wave_inside_multiplier","v":{"s":"Inside multiplier b"}},{"k":"wave_phase_radians","v":{"s":"Phase φ (radians)"}},{"k":"wave_amplitude_measure","v":{"s":"Amplitude: "}},{"k":"wave_period_measure","v":{"s":"Period: "}},{"k":"wave_no_fundamental_period","v":{"s":"No fundamental period"}},{"k":"wave_shift_measure","v":{"s":"Horizontal shift: "}},{"k":"wave_shift_not_unique","v":{"s":"Not unique"}},{"k":"wave_constant_meaning","v":{"s":"This is a constant function: no oscillation, no fundamental period, and no unique horizontal shift."}},{"k":"wave_formula_meaning","v":{"s":"Amplitude is |a|, period is 2π/b, and one horizontal shift is −φ/b. Negative a reflects the wave across y = 0. A negative shift moves it left."}},{"k":"wave_long_period","v":{"s":"A full period extends beyond this window."}},{"k":"wave_already_saved","v":{"s":"Already saved as setting "}},{"k":"wave_save_setting","v":{"s":"Save setting"}},{"k":"wave_reset_investigation","v":{"s":"Reset investigation"}},{"k":"wave_compare_with","v":{"s":"Compare with"}},{"k":"wave_show_reference","v":{"s":"Show saved reference (dashed)"}},{"k":"wave_restore_reference","v":{"s":"Restore reference"}},{"k":"wave_saved_settings","v":{"s":"Saved settings"}},{"k":"wave_record_limit","v":{"s":"Keeps the latest eight distinct settings. Values shown are rounded."}},{"k":"wave_working_explanation","v":{"s":"What do you notice?"}},{"k":"wave_observation_placeholder","v":{"s":"Compare two settings. Which parameter changed, and what stayed the same?"}},{"k":"wave_explain_with_evidence","v":{"s":"Explain using saved settings"}},{"k":"wave_evidence_placeholder","v":{"s":"Name two saved settings. Use the graph and measurements to explain the effect you observed."}}],"id":530}},{"k":"galaxy","v":{"o":[{"k":"view_angles_title","v":{"s":"Viewing angle"}},{"k":"view_angles_help","v":{"s":"Explore the shape from above or across its edge. Your zoom and selected object stay in place."}},{"k":"appearance_soft_hint","v":{"s":"Dimmer · crisp stars"}},{"k":"appearance_balanced_hint","v":{"s":"Natural glow"}},{"k":"appearance_vivid_hint","v":{"s":"Brighter · soft halos"}},{"k":"appearance_custom","v":{"s":"Custom"}},{"k":"radio_ordered_unavailable","v":{"s":"Radio gas view. Ordered velocity and magnetic-field overlays are shown for spiral models only."}},{"k":"radio_motion_key_aria","v":{"s":"Velocity map shown. Blue approaches; red recedes."}},{"k":"radio_magnetic_key_aria","v":{"s":"Magnetic field shown. Line orientation shows field direction; ribbon colors distinguish Faraday depth."}},{"k":"radio_magnetic_hidden","v":{"s":"Magnetic field hidden"}},{"k":"radio_observation_key","v":{"s":"Radio observation key"}},{"k":"radio_overlay_compare_note","v":{"s":"Toggle the velocity map and magnetic field separately to compare the layers."}},{"k":"radio_overlays_title","v":{"s":"Radio overlays"}},{"k":"radio_magnetic_toggle","v":{"s":"Magnetic field"}},{"k":"radio_magnetic_toggle_hint","v":{"s":"Show or hide the field-direction ticks and colored ribbons."}},{"k":"radio_velocity_hidden_aria","v":{"s":"Radio observation key. Velocity map hidden. Line orientation shows magnetic-field direction; ribbon colors distinguish Faraday depth."}},{"k":"radio_velocity_hidden","v":{"s":"Velocity map hidden"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_toggle","v":{"s":"Velocity map"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_toggle_hint","v":{"s":"Compare the gas with or without its blue-to-red motion overlay."}},{"k":"focus_entered","v":{"s":"Focus view. Drag to orbit and scroll or pinch to zoom. Press H or Escape to show controls."}},{"k":"focus_exited","v":{"s":"Controls restored. Your view and appearance settings are unchanged."}},{"k":"caption_zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom +"}},{"k":"caption_zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom −"}},{"k":"caption_reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"caption_pause","v":{"s":"Pause"}},{"k":"caption_rotate","v":{"s":"Rotate"}},{"k":"caption_labels","v":{"s":"Labels"}},{"k":"caption_stop","v":{"s":"Stop"}},{"k":"caption_tour","v":{"s":"Tour"}},{"k":"caption_focus","v":{"s":"Focus"}},{"k":"focus_title","v":{"s":"Focus view — hide interface (H)"}},{"k":"caption_fullscreen","v":{"s":"Full"}},{"k":"focus_show_controls","v":{"s":"Show controls · Esc"}},{"k":"appearance_title","v":{"s":"Appearance"}},{"k":"appearance_reset_aria","v":{"s":"Reset brightness and glow"}},{"k":"appearance_reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"focus_launcher","v":{"s":"Focus view · hide interface"}},{"k":"appearance_help","v":{"s":"Adjust how the scene looks. The galaxy and its measurements stay the same."}},{"k":"appearance_presets","v":{"s":"Appearance presets"}},{"k":"appearance_soft","v":{"s":"Soft"}},{"k":"appearance_balanced","v":{"s":"Balanced"}},{"k":"appearance_vivid","v":{"s":"Vivid"}},{"k":"appearance_brightness","v":{"s":"Brightness"}},{"k":"appearance_brightness_hint","v":{"s":"Dimmer scene to brighter scene."}},{"k":"appearance_glow","v":{"s":"Star glow"}},{"k":"appearance_glow_hint","v":{"s":"Sharper points to softer halos."}},{"k":"appearance_glow_unavailable","v":{"s":"Glow is unavailable in this renderer."}},{"k":"appearance_glow_loading","v":{"s":"Available when the 3D view is ready."}},{"k":"observation_guide_expand","v":{"s":"Understand this view & record evidence"}},{"k":"otype","v":{"s":"O-type"}},{"k":"btype","v":{"s":"B-type"}},{"k":"atype","v":{"s":"A-type"}},{"k":"ftype","v":{"s":"F-type"}},{"k":"gtype","v":{"s":"G-type"}},{"k":"ktype","v":{"s":"K-type"}},{"k":"mtype","v":{"s":"M-type"}},{"k":"orion_nebula","v":{"s":"Orion Nebula"}},{"k":"eagle_nebula","v":{"s":"Eagle Nebula"}},{"k":"crab_nebula","v":{"s":"Crab Nebula"}},{"k":"carina_nebula","v":{"s":"Carina Nebula"}},{"k":"helix_nebula","v":{"s":"Helix Nebula"}},{"k":"the","v":{"s":"The "}},{"k":"ring_nebula","v":{"s":"Ring Nebula"}},{"k":"horsehead_nebula","v":{"s":"Horsehead Nebula"}},{"k":"lagoon_nebula","v":{"s":"Lagoon Nebula"}},{"k":"barred_spiral","v":{"s":"Barred Spiral"}},{"k":"grand_design_spiral","v":{"s":"Grand Design Spiral"}},{"k":"elliptical","v":{"s":"Elliptical"}},{"k":"irregular","v":{"s":"Irregular"}},{"k":"galactic_core","v":{"s":"Galactic Core"}},{"k":"orion_arm_us","v":{"s":"Orion Arm (Us)"}},{"k":"perseus_arm","v":{"s":"Perseus Arm"}},{"k":"sagittarius_arm","v":{"s":"Sagittarius Arm"}},{"k":"the_next_arm_inward_toward","v":{"s":"The next arm inward toward the galactic center."}},{"k":"overview","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"full_view_of_the_galaxy","v":{"s":"Full view of the galaxy from above."}},{"k":"galaxy_diameter","v":{"s":"Galaxy Diameter"}},{"k":"disk_thickness","v":{"s":"Disk Thickness"}},{"k":"central_bulge","v":{"s":"Central Bulge"}},{"k":"sun_to_center","v":{"s":"Sun to Center"}},{"k":"stars","v":{"s":"Stars"}},{"k":"cosmic_dawn","v":{"s":"Cosmic Dawn"}},{"k":"first_galaxies","v":{"s":"First Galaxies"}},{"k":"galaxy_assembly","v":{"s":"Galaxy Assembly"}},{"k":"milky_way_forms","v":{"s":"Milky Way Forms"}},{"k":"sun_is_born","v":{"s":"Sun Is Born"}},{"k":"mature_galaxy","v":{"s":"Mature Galaxy"}},{"k":"present_era","v":{"s":"Present Era"}},{"k":"right_now","v":{"s":"Right Now"}},{"k":"nebula","v":{"s":"Nebula"}},{"k":"a_vast_cloud_of_gas","v":{"s":"A vast cloud of gas & dust collapses under gravity."}},{"k":"protostar","v":{"s":"Protostar"}},{"k":"core_heats_up_from_gravitational","v":{"s":"Core heats up from gravitational compression. Not yet fusing."}},{"k":"main_sequence","v":{"s":"Main Sequence"}},{"k":"red_giant","v":{"s":"Red Giant"}},{"k":"core_contracts_outer_layers_expand","v":{"s":"Core contracts, outer layers expand. Helium fusion begins."}},{"k":"planetary_nebula","v":{"s":"Planetary Nebula"}},{"k":"outer_layers_shed_gently_into","v":{"s":"Outer layers shed gently into space."}},{"k":"white_dwarf","v":{"s":"White Dwarf"}},{"k":"dense_stellar_core_slowly_cools","v":{"s":"Dense stellar core slowly cools. Size of Earth, mass of Sun."}},{"k":"supernova","v":{"s":"Supernova"}},{"k":"neutron_star","v":{"s":"Neutron Star"}},{"k":"ultradense_remnant_a_teaspoon_weighs","v":{"s":"Ultra-dense remnant. A teaspoon weighs billions of tons."}},{"k":"black_hole","v":{"s":"Black Hole"}},{"k":"gravity_so_strong_nothing_escapes","v":{"s":"Gravity so strong nothing escapes. Spacetime itself warps."}},{"k":"spiral_arms","v":{"s":"Spiral Arms"}},{"k":"nebulae","v":{"s":"Nebulae"}},{"k":"background","v":{"s":"Background"}},{"k":"scale_grid","v":{"s":"Scale Grid"}},{"k":"labels","v":{"s":"Labels"}},{"k":"first_stars","v":{"s":"First stars"}},{"k":"galaxies_form","v":{"s":"Galaxies form"}},{"k":"milky_way","v":{"s":"Milky Way"}},{"k":"sun_born","v":{"s":"Sun born"}},{"k":"temperature","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"luminosity","v":{"s":"Luminosity"}},{"k":"mass","v":{"s":"Mass"}},{"k":"lifetime","v":{"s":"Lifetime"}},{"k":"example","v":{"s":"Example"}},{"k":"galaxy","v":{"s":"Galaxy"}},{"k":"otype_desc","v":{"s":"Extremely hot, blue, massive. Rarest type — short lives of only a few million years."}},{"k":"otype_why","v":{"s":"O-type stars produce most of a galaxy's ultraviolet light and ionize surrounding gas, creating the glowing emission nebulae we see. Their supernovae seed the universe with heavy elements like iron and gold."}},{"k":"btype_desc","v":{"s":"Blue-white giants. Often found in young OB associations and spiral arms."}},{"k":"btype_why","v":{"s":"B-type stars trace the spiral arms of galaxies because they are short-lived. Astronomers use them as markers for galactic structure and recent star formation."}},{"k":"atype_desc","v":{"s":"White stars with strong hydrogen absorption lines. Many are binary systems."}},{"k":"atype_why","v":{"s":"A-type stars like Sirius were among the first to have their spectra analyzed, helping astronomers develop the stellar classification system we use today."}},{"k":"ftype_desc","v":{"s":"Yellow-white. Transition zone where convection begins in the outer layer."}},{"k":"ftype_why","v":{"s":"F-type stars are interesting for exoplanet searches because they have habitable zones and lifespans long enough for complex life to potentially develop."}},{"k":"gtype_desc","v":{"s":"Our Sun is a G2V star! Yellow stars with lifespans of ~10 billion years."}},{"k":"gtype_why","v":{"s":"G-type stars like our Sun prove that modest stars can nurture life. Their 10-billion-year lifespan gives plenty of time for biological evolution."}},{"k":"ktype_desc","v":{"s":"Orange stars. Many have habitable zones — prime candidates for exoplanet searches."}},{"k":"ktype_why","v":{"s":"K-type stars are considered the best candidates for finding habitable exoplanets—they are stable, long-lived, and common enough to offer many opportunities."}},{"k":"mtype_desc","v":{"s":"Red dwarfs — 76% of all stars! Extremely long-lived (trillions of years)."}},{"k":"mtype_why","v":{"s":"M-type red dwarfs will be the last stars shining in the universe. Proxima Centauri b, a potentially habitable exoplanet, orbits one of these stars—our closest neighbor!"}},{"k":"orion_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"Stellar nursery 1,344 light-years away. Visible to the naked eye. Contains the Trapezium star cluster."}},{"k":"eagle_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"Home of the Pillars of Creation. Star-forming region 7,000 light-years from Earth."}},{"k":"crab_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"Supernova remnant from 1054 AD. Contains a pulsar spinning 30x per second."}},{"k":"carina_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"One of the largest nebulae. Contains Eta Carinae, a hypergiant 4 million times brighter than the Sun."}},{"k":"helix_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"The \"Eye of God.\" A planetary nebula — the outer shell of a dying Sun-like star."}},{"k":"ring_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"Classic planetary nebula in Lyra. The central white dwarf is visible at high zoom."}},{"k":"horsehead_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"Dark nebula silhouetted against the emission nebula IC 434. An iconic astronomical object."}},{"k":"lagoon_nebula_desc","v":{"s":"One of the brightest emission nebulae. Visible with binoculars in Sagittarius."}},{"k":"barred_spiral_desc","v":{"s":"Like our Milky Way. A central bar of stars with spiral arms winding outward. ~60% of spirals have bars."}},{"k":"grand_design_spiral_desc","v":{"s":"Prominent, well-defined spiral arms. Usually triggered by gravitational interaction with a companion galaxy."}},{"k":"elliptical_desc","v":{"s":"Smooth, featureless ellipsoidal shape. Contain old, red stars with little gas or dust. Formed from galaxy mergers."}},{"k":"irregular_desc","v":{"s":"No distinct shape. Rich in gas and dust with active star formation. Often satellites of larger galaxies."}},{"k":"observe_visible_label","v":{"s":"Visible"}},{"k":"observe_visible_desc","v":{"s":"Human-eye color shows stars, dust shadows, and the overall shape of the galaxy."}},{"k":"observe_visible_note","v":{"s":"Best for classifying galaxy shape and comparing star colors."}},{"k":"observe_infrared_label","v":{"s":"Infrared"}},{"k":"observe_infrared_desc","v":{"s":"Infrared light passes through dusty lanes and reveals warm star-forming regions."}},{"k":"observe_infrared_note","v":{"s":"Great for seeing through dust that blocks visible light."}},{"k":"observe_radio_label","v":{"s":"Radio"}},{"k":"observe_radio_desc","v":{"s":"Radio maps trace cold hydrogen gas that outlines spiral arms and future star birth."}},{"k":"observe_radio_note","v":{"s":"Hydrogen at 21 cm is one of the best maps of hidden galactic gas."}},{"k":"observe_xray_label","v":{"s":"X-ray"}},{"k":"observe_xray_desc","v":{"s":"X-rays highlight the hottest, most energetic regions near compact objects and young massive stars."}},{"k":"observe_xray_note","v":{"s":"Useful for black-hole accretion, neutron stars, and supernova remnants."}},{"k":"observe_gravity_label","v":{"s":"Gravity"}},{"k":"observe_gravity_desc","v":{"s":"A gravity view shows the invisible mass halo inferred from star motions."}},{"k":"observe_gravity_note","v":{"s":"This is evidence-based, not a photograph: motion reveals the dark matter halo."}},{"k":"instrument_visible_detector","v":{"s":"Optical composite"}},{"k":"instrument_visible_tracer","v":{"s":"Starlight + dust shadows"}},{"k":"instrument_infrared_detector","v":{"s":"Thermal infrared"}},{"k":"instrument_infrared_tracer","v":{"s":"Warm dust + embedded stars"}},{"k":"instrument_radio_detector","v":{"s":"H I spectral line"}},{"k":"instrument_radio_tracer","v":{"s":"Cold hydrogen + magnetic field"}},{"k":"instrument_xray_detector","v":{"s":"High-energy imaging"}},{"k":"instrument_xray_tracer","v":{"s":"Hot plasma + compact objects"}},{"k":"instrument_gravity_band","v":{"s":"Mass inference"}},{"k":"instrument_gravity_detector","v":{"s":"Kinematics + lensing"}},{"k":"instrument_gravity_tracer","v":{"s":"Dark halo + shear arclets"}},{"k":"instrument_elliptical_visible_tracer","v":{"s":"Old starlight + smooth profile"}},{"k":"instrument_elliptical_infrared_tracer","v":{"s":"Evolved stars + very little dust"}},{"k":"instrument_elliptical_radio_tracer","v":{"s":"Weak cold-gas reservoir"}},{"k":"instrument_elliptical_xray_tracer","v":{"s":"Hot halo gas + compact core"}},{"k":"instrument_elliptical_gravity_tracer","v":{"s":"Dark halo + pressure-supported stars"}},{"k":"guide_visible_question","v":{"s":"Where does visible starlight disappear, and what is blocking it?"}},{"k":"guide_visible_sees","v":{"s":"Star colors, galaxy shape, and dark dust silhouettes"}},{"k":"guide_visible_misses","v":{"s":"Cold gas and stars still buried inside dense clouds"}},{"k":"guide_visible_inference","v":{"s":"A dark lane is evidence of foreground dust absorbing light, not an empty gap."}},{"k":"guide_visible_change","v":{"s":"Starlight and sharp dust shadows return, making morphology easiest to classify."}},{"k":"mark_dust_lane","v":{"s":"Dust lane"}},{"k":"mark_dust_lane_detail","v":{"s":"absorbs visible starlight"}},{"k":"mark_blue_association","v":{"s":"Young blue stars"}},{"k":"mark_blue_association_detail","v":{"s":"trace recent star formation"}},{"k":"guide_infrared_question","v":{"s":"Which bright regions were hidden in the visible-light dust lanes?"}},{"k":"guide_infrared_sees","v":{"s":"Warm dust, embedded protostars, and the older stellar disk"}},{"k":"guide_infrared_misses","v":{"s":"The coldest gas and the highest-energy compact objects"}},{"k":"guide_infrared_inference","v":{"s":"Infrared light penetrates dust better, revealing star birth before clouds disperse."}},{"k":"guide_infrared_change","v":{"s":"Dark lanes become translucent while warm nurseries and embedded stars brighten."}},{"k":"mark_embedded_protostars","v":{"s":"Embedded protostars"}},{"k":"mark_embedded_protostars_detail","v":{"s":"warm knots inside clouds"}},{"k":"mark_old_stellar_disk","v":{"s":"Older stellar disk"}},{"k":"mark_old_stellar_disk_detail","v":{"s":"warm stars fill the disk"}},{"k":"mark_thermal_dust","v":{"s":"Thermal dust emission"}},{"k":"mark_thermal_dust_detail","v":{"s":"absorbed starlight re-radiated as heat"}},{"k":"guide_radio_question","v":{"s":"Does the cold gas extend beyond the bright stellar arms?"}},{"k":"guide_radio_sees","v":{"s":"Cold hydrogen, magnetic structure, and energetic remnant shells"}},{"k":"guide_radio_misses","v":{"s":"Ordinary starlight and fine optical dust silhouettes"}},{"k":"guide_radio_inference","v":{"s":"The 21 cm hydrogen signal maps the fuel reservoir from which future stars can form."}},{"k":"guide_radio_change","v":{"s":"Most starlight fades while extended gas lanes and remnant filaments strengthen."}},{"k":"mark_cold_hydrogen","v":{"s":"Cold hydrogen"}},{"k":"mark_cold_hydrogen_detail","v":{"s":"fuel for future stars"}},{"k":"mark_radio_remnants","v":{"s":"Remnant shells"}},{"k":"mark_radio_remnants_detail","v":{"s":"expanding magnetized debris"}},{"k":"mark_doppler_field","v":{"s":"Doppler velocity field"}},{"k":"mark_doppler_field_detail","v":{"s":"blue approaches; red recedes"}},{"k":"guide_xray_question","v":{"s":"Where is matter hottest or being accelerated most violently?"}},{"k":"guide_xray_sees","v":{"s":"Accreting compact objects, hot gas, and supernova remnants"}},{"k":"guide_xray_misses","v":{"s":"Cool stars, cold molecular clouds, and most of the stellar disk"}},{"k":"guide_xray_inference","v":{"s":"Strong X-rays reveal million-degree gas or matter accelerated near neutron stars and black holes."}},{"k":"guide_xray_change","v":{"s":"The cool disk recedes while the energetic core and shocked remnant shells dominate."}},{"k":"mark_accreting_core","v":{"s":"Energetic core"}},{"k":"mark_accreting_core_detail","v":{"s":"hot infalling matter"}},{"k":"mark_xray_remnants","v":{"s":"Hot remnants"}},{"k":"mark_xray_remnants_detail","v":{"s":"shock-heated plasma"}},{"k":"mark_xray_binaries","v":{"s":"Compact-object beacons"}},{"k":"mark_xray_binaries_detail","v":{"s":"flickering accretion and pulsars"}},{"k":"guide_gravity_question","v":{"s":"How can motions reveal mass that emits no light?"}},{"k":"guide_gravity_sees","v":{"s":"The inferred dark-matter halo and gravitational structure"}},{"k":"guide_gravity_misses","v":{"s":"This is not a photograph and does not show a new kind of light"}},{"k":"guide_gravity_inference","v":{"s":"Outer stars orbit too quickly for visible matter alone, so additional unseen mass is required."}},{"k":"guide_gravity_change","v":{"s":"Luminous detail is suppressed and the much larger inferred mass halo becomes the central evidence."}},{"k":"mark_dark_halo","v":{"s":"Dark-matter halo"}},{"k":"mark_dark_halo_detail","v":{"s":"inferred from orbital speeds"}},{"k":"mark_fast_outer_stars","v":{"s":"Fast outer stars"}},{"k":"mark_fast_outer_stars_detail","v":{"s":"gravity exceeds visible mass"}},{"k":"mark_weak_lensing","v":{"s":"Weak-lensing arclets"}},{"k":"mark_weak_lensing_detail","v":{"s":"background light sheared by halo mass"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_visible_question","v":{"s":"How smoothly does the light fade from the bright center to the outer halo?"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_visible_sees","v":{"s":"A smooth ellipsoidal profile dominated by old, warm-colored stars"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_visible_misses","v":{"s":"The dark halo and any very faint hot gas around the galaxy"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_visible_inference","v":{"s":"The lack of arms, dust lanes, and blue nurseries indicates little recent star formation."}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_visible_change","v":{"s":"Warm starlight returns as a smooth oval with a concentrated but resolved core."}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_old_stars","v":{"s":"Old warm stars"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_old_stars_detail","v":{"s":"dominate the smooth stellar halo"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_core","v":{"s":"Concentrated core"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_core_detail","v":{"s":"brightness rises without forming a disk"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_infrared_question","v":{"s":"Does infrared reveal hidden nurseries, or mostly the same old stellar population?"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_infrared_sees","v":{"s":"Long-lived cool stars and the galaxy's smooth stellar mass distribution"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_infrared_misses","v":{"s":"Only a small amount of warm dust because ellipticals are usually gas-poor"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_infrared_inference","v":{"s":"A similar visible and infrared shape supports an old population with little obscuring dust."}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_infrared_change","v":{"s":"The smooth old population remains while dust-dependent knots stay faint."}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_evolved_stars","v":{"s":"Evolved stars"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_evolved_stars_detail","v":{"s":"remain bright in infrared"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_low_dust","v":{"s":"Very little dust"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_low_dust_detail","v":{"s":"few hidden stellar nurseries"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_radio_question","v":{"s":"How weak is the cold-hydrogen reservoir compared with a spiral galaxy?"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_radio_sees","v":{"s":"A weak gas signal and occasional activity associated with the central black hole"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_radio_misses","v":{"s":"Most of the old stellar population that defines the visible shape"}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_radio_inference","v":{"s":"Little cold hydrogen means little raw material is available for new stars."}},{"k":"guide_elliptical_radio_change","v":{"s":"Ordinary starlight fades without revealing spiral gas lanes."}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_gas_poor","v":{"s":"Gas-poor body"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_gas_poor_detail","v":{"s":"weak cold-hydrogen signal"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_radio_core","v":{"s":"Possible active core"}},{"k":"mark_elliptical_radio_core_detail","v":{"s":"radio emission can trace black-hole activity"}},{"k":"doppler_approaching_star","v":{"s":"Approaching star"}},{"k":"doppler_no_motion","v":{"s":"No motion"}},{"k":"doppler_receding_galaxy","v":{"s":"Receding galaxy"}},{"k":"doppler_fast_quasar","v":{"s":"Fast quasar"}},{"k":"rst_m31_name","v":{"s":"Andromeda Galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m31_type","v":{"s":"Local Group spiral"}},{"k":"rst_m31_bridge","v":{"s":"Naked-eye smudge in dark skies; huge in binoculars."}},{"k":"rst_m31_story","v":{"s":"Use this to compare the simulated Milky Way disk with a real neighboring spiral galaxy."}},{"k":"rst_m31_lesson1","v":{"s":"Classify the shape: bulge, disk, spiral arms, and dust lanes."}},{"k":"rst_m31_lesson2","v":{"s":"Switch to infrared and look for dust-hidden structure."}},{"k":"rst_m31_lesson3","v":{"s":"Ask what evidence shows this galaxy is not inside the Milky Way."}},{"k":"rst_m51_name","v":{"s":"Whirlpool Galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m51_type","v":{"s":"Interacting grand-design spiral"}},{"k":"rst_m51_bridge","v":{"s":"A telescope/photography classic; spiral arms are easier in images than eyepieces."}},{"k":"rst_m51_story","v":{"s":"A dramatic case where interaction with a companion sharpens spiral structure."}},{"k":"rst_m51_lesson1","v":{"s":"Trace the spiral arms and find the companion galaxy."}},{"k":"rst_m51_lesson2","v":{"s":"Compare the bridge of material with the Galaxy Explorer interaction model."}},{"k":"rst_m51_lesson3","v":{"s":"Predict where star formation is strongest before changing surveys."}},{"k":"rst_m87_name","v":{"s":"Virgo A"}},{"k":"rst_m87_type","v":{"s":"Giant elliptical galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m87_bridge","v":{"s":"Home of the first imaged black-hole shadow, M87*."}},{"k":"rst_m87_story","v":{"s":"A smooth elliptical galaxy that anchors the Virgo Cluster and hosts a supermassive black hole."}},{"k":"rst_m87_lesson1","v":{"s":"Compare its smooth light to spiral galaxies."}},{"k":"rst_m87_lesson2","v":{"s":"Use the catalog overlay to notice the crowded Virgo Cluster field."}},{"k":"rst_m87_lesson3","v":{"s":"Connect the bright core to black-hole evidence, not a visible event horizon."}},{"k":"rst_m104_name","v":{"s":"Sombrero Galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m104_type","v":{"s":"Dust-lane galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m104_bridge","v":{"s":"A bright galaxy where dust lanes make structure visible."}},{"k":"rst_m104_story","v":{"s":"A striking real example of how dust can reveal a galaxy disk in silhouette."}},{"k":"rst_m104_lesson1","v":{"s":"Find the dark lane and infer the disk orientation."}},{"k":"rst_m104_lesson2","v":{"s":"Compare visible and infrared: what changes when dust is less opaque?"}},{"k":"rst_m104_lesson3","v":{"s":"Decide whether this looks more spiral-like or elliptical-like."}},{"k":"rst_m82_name","v":{"s":"Cigar Galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m82_type","v":{"s":"Starburst galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_m82_bridge","v":{"s":"Best understood with multiwavelength views of gas and dust."}},{"k":"rst_m82_story","v":{"s":"A galaxy-wide starburst: intense star formation is driving material out of the disk."}},{"k":"rst_m82_lesson1","v":{"s":"Look for the disturbed shape instead of neat spiral arms."}},{"k":"rst_m82_lesson2","v":{"s":"Ask how a nearby galaxy interaction could trigger star birth."}},{"k":"rst_m82_lesson3","v":{"s":"Use infrared to hunt for dusty star-forming regions."}},{"k":"rst_m1_name","v":{"s":"Crab Nebula"}},{"k":"rst_m1_type","v":{"s":"Supernova remnant"}},{"k":"rst_m1_bridge","v":{"s":"A real supernova remnant from the 1054 event."}},{"k":"rst_m1_story","v":{"s":"The afterglow of stellar death: a pulsar powers a tangled nebula of expanding debris."}},{"k":"rst_m1_lesson1","v":{"s":"Connect the filaments to expanding supernova ejecta."}},{"k":"rst_m1_lesson2","v":{"s":"Compare this remnant with Star Life supernova and neutron-star stages."}},{"k":"rst_m1_lesson3","v":{"s":"Ask what data would reveal the hidden pulsar."}},{"k":"rst_m42_name","v":{"s":"Orion Nebula"}},{"k":"rst_m42_type","v":{"s":"Stellar nursery"}},{"k":"rst_m42_bridge","v":{"s":"One of the best beginner telescope targets."}},{"k":"rst_m42_story","v":{"s":"A nearby star-forming cloud where hot young stars light the gas around them."}},{"k":"rst_m42_lesson1","v":{"s":"Find the bright core and surrounding gas wings."}},{"k":"rst_m42_lesson2","v":{"s":"Compare optical and infrared views to see through dust."}},{"k":"rst_m42_lesson3","v":{"s":"Ask why this is a star nursery rather than a galaxy."}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_name","v":{"s":"Pleiades"}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_type","v":{"s":"Open cluster"}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_bridge","v":{"s":"Gorgeous in binoculars; too wide for many telescopes."}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_story","v":{"s":"A young cluster showing how stars are born together and drift apart over time."}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_lesson1","v":{"s":"Count bright blue stars and infer a young age."}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_lesson2","v":{"s":"Notice why binoculars can be better than high magnification."}},{"k":"rst_pleiades_lesson3","v":{"s":"Ask how an open cluster differs from a globular cluster."}},{"k":"rst_carina_name","v":{"s":"Carina Nebula"}},{"k":"rst_carina_type","v":{"s":"Massive stellar nursery"}},{"k":"rst_carina_bridge","v":{"s":"A southern-sky showpiece made famous by Hubble and JWST imagery."}},{"k":"rst_carina_story","v":{"s":"A huge star-forming complex where massive stars sculpt dust pillars and glowing gas."}},{"k":"rst_carina_lesson1","v":{"s":"Look for bright cavities carved by young massive stars."}},{"k":"rst_carina_lesson2","v":{"s":"Switch surveys and compare where dust is visible versus transparent."}},{"k":"rst_carina_lesson3","v":{"s":"Ask why massive stars reshape their birth clouds so quickly."}},{"k":"rst_m16_name","v":{"s":"Eagle Nebula"}},{"k":"rst_m16_type","v":{"s":"Pillars of Creation field"}},{"k":"rst_m16_bridge","v":{"s":"The Hubble/JWST Pillars are a famous close-up inside this star-forming region."}},{"k":"rst_m16_story","v":{"s":"Dense columns of gas and dust are being eroded by newborn stars while new stars form inside them."}},{"k":"rst_m16_lesson1","v":{"s":"Search for dark columns and bright ionized edges."}},{"k":"rst_m16_lesson2","v":{"s":"Predict which wavelengths reveal embedded protostars."}},{"k":"rst_m16_lesson3","v":{"s":"Compare pillar erosion to stellar feedback in Star Life."}},{"k":"rst_stephan_name","v":{"s":"Stephan's Quintet"}},{"k":"rst_stephan_type","v":{"s":"Compact interacting galaxy group"}},{"k":"rst_stephan_bridge","v":{"s":"A JWST showcase for colliding galaxies, shock fronts, and tidal debris."}},{"k":"rst_stephan_story","v":{"s":"A small patch of sky where multiple galaxies are gravitationally disturbing one another."}},{"k":"rst_stephan_lesson1","v":{"s":"Identify which galaxies look distorted by interaction."}},{"k":"rst_stephan_lesson2","v":{"s":"Ask what a shock front would look like in non-visible wavelengths."}},{"k":"rst_stephan_lesson3","v":{"s":"Compare the group to M51: one companion versus several galaxies."}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_name","v":{"s":"Cartwheel Galaxy"}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_type","v":{"s":"Ring galaxy after collision"}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_bridge","v":{"s":"A JWST/Hubble-friendly example of a collision-generated ring wave."}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_story","v":{"s":"A smaller galaxy likely punched through the disk, sending a star-forming ring outward."}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_lesson1","v":{"s":"Find the ring and compare it to ordinary spiral arms."}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_lesson2","v":{"s":"Ask why collisions can trigger new stars instead of only destroying structure."}},{"k":"rst_cartwheel_lesson3","v":{"s":"Use the ring as evidence of a past encounter."}},{"k":"survey_optical_label","v":{"s":"Optical"}},{"k":"survey_optical_desc","v":{"s":"Visible-light plates reveal star color, dust lanes, and galaxy structure."}},{"k":"survey_near_infrared_label","v":{"s":"Near infrared"}},{"k":"survey_near_infrared_desc","v":{"s":"Infrared light cuts through dust and highlights cooler stars."}},{"k":"survey_mid_infrared_label","v":{"s":"Mid infrared"}},{"k":"survey_mid_infrared_desc","v":{"s":"Warm dust and star-forming regions become easier to spot."}},{"k":"catalog_simbad_label","v":{"s":"SIMBAD objects"}},{"k":"catalog_simbad_desc","v":{"s":"Scientific object IDs around the target."}},{"k":"catalog_clean_survey_label","v":{"s":"Clean survey"}},{"k":"catalog_clean_survey_desc","v":{"s":"Image-only mode for careful visual inspection."}},{"k":"orion_arm_us_desc","v":{"s":"Our Solar System is here, about 26,000 light-years from the center."}},{"k":"perseus_arm_desc","v":{"s":"The next spiral arm outward from us. Contains many young, hot stars."}},{"k":"quiz_sun_type","v":{"s":"What type of star is our Sun?"}},{"k":"quiz_mw_center","v":{"s":"What is at the center of the Milky Way?"}},{"k":"quiz_hottest_type","v":{"s":"Which star type is the hottest?"}},{"k":"quiz_our_arm","v":{"s":"Which spiral arm contains our Solar System?"}},{"k":"quiz_mtype_pct","v":{"s":"What percentage of stars are M-type red dwarfs?"}},{"k":"quiz_what_nebula","v":{"s":"What is a nebula?"}},{"k":"quiz_star_count","v":{"s":"How many stars are in the Milky Way?"}},{"k":"quiz_mw_type","v":{"s":"What type of galaxy is the Milky Way?"}},{"k":"quiz_closest_star","v":{"s":"Which star is closest to our Sun?"}},{"k":"quiz_hottest_color","v":{"s":"What color are the hottest stars?"}},{"k":"quiz_planetary_nebula","v":{"s":"What is a planetary nebula?"}},{"k":"quiz_mw_width","v":{"s":"How wide is the Milky Way?"}},{"k":"quiz_supernova_cause","v":{"s":"What causes a supernova?"}},{"k":"quiz_dark_matter","v":{"s":"What is dark matter?"}},{"k":"quiz_light_cross","v":{"s":"How long does it take light to cross the Milky Way?"}},{"k":"quiz_sun_fate","v":{"s":"What will our Sun become at the end of its life?"}},{"k":"quiz_after_red_giant","v":{"s":"What stage comes after a Red Supergiant for a massive star?"}},{"k":"quiz_solar_mass_life","v":{"s":"How long does a star with 1 solar mass live?"}},{"k":"quiz_what_protostar","v":{"s":"What is a protostar?"}},{"k":"quiz_final_fate","v":{"s":"What determines a star's final fate?"}},{"k":"scale_age","v":{"s":"Age"}},{"k":"cosmic_dawn_desc","v":{"s":"The first stars ignite, ending the cosmic dark ages. These massive Population III stars forge the first heavy elements."}},{"k":"first_galaxies_desc","v":{"s":"Protogalaxies begin to coalesce from dark matter halos. The first quasars blaze to life, powered by supermassive black holes."}},{"k":"galaxy_assembly_desc","v":{"s":"Galaxies collide and merge, building larger structures. Spiral arms begin to form as gas settles into rotating disks."}},{"k":"milky_way_forms_desc","v":{"s":"Our galaxy takes shape. The galactic bar forms, organizing the inner structure. Star formation peaks in the spiral arms."}},{"k":"sun_is_born_desc","v":{"s":"A cloud of gas collapses in the Orion Arm, forming our Sun and Solar System 4.6 billion years ago. Life will eventually arise on Earth."}},{"k":"mature_galaxy_desc","v":{"s":"The Milky Way settles into its current form with 100-400 billion stars. Star formation slows as gas reserves deplete."}},{"k":"present_era_desc","v":{"s":"We are here! Humanity looks outward. The universe continues expanding, and dark energy accelerates its growth."}},{"k":"right_now_desc","v":{"s":"The observable universe is 93 billion light-years across. We can see the cosmic microwave background—the afterglow of the Big Bang."}},{"k":"stage_brown_dwarf_name","v":{"s":"Brown Dwarf"}},{"k":"stage_brown_dwarf_desc","v":{"s":"Too small for sustained hydrogen fusion; it glows faintly while cooling."}},{"k":"stage_cooling_brown_dwarf_name","v":{"s":"Cooling Brown Dwarf"}},{"k":"stage_cooling_brown_dwarf_desc","v":{"s":"A substellar ember fading slowly over cosmic time."}},{"k":"stage_red_dwarf_name","v":{"s":"Red Dwarf"}},{"k":"stage_red_dwarf_desc","v":{"s":"Burns slowly for hundreds of billions of years."}},{"k":"stage_blue_dwarf_name","v":{"s":"Blue Dwarf"}},{"k":"stage_blue_dwarf_desc","v":{"s":"Theoretical phase where a red dwarf heats up as its opacity changes."}},{"k":"stage_black_dwarf_name","v":{"s":"Black Dwarf"}},{"k":"stage_black_dwarf_desc","v":{"s":"Theoretical future: a white dwarf cooled after far longer than the universe has existed."}},{"k":"stage_ms_desc_sunlike","v":{"s":"Hydrogen fusion ignites! Stable for billions of years."}},{"k":"stage_ms_desc_massive","v":{"s":"Hot and enormous. Burns through fuel in millions of years."}},{"k":"stage_red_supergiant_name","v":{"s":"Red Supergiant"}},{"k":"stage_red_supergiant_desc","v":{"s":"Expands to massive proportions, large enough to swallow Jupiter!"}},{"k":"stage_supernova_desc_core","v":{"s":"Core collapses! A catastrophic explosion outshining entire galaxies."}},{"k":"stage_ms_desc_ultramassive","v":{"s":"An ultra-hot blue giant blazing with intense radiation."}},{"k":"stage_blue_supergiant_name","v":{"s":"Blue Supergiant"}},{"k":"stage_blue_supergiant_desc","v":{"s":"Sheds immense mass through violent stellar winds."}},{"k":"stage_supernova_desc_hyper","v":{"s":"A hypernova explosion obliterates the star."}},{"k":"bh_status_camera_reset","v":{"s":"Camera reset to the starting view."}},{"k":"grid_ring_sun","v":{"s":"Sun’s orbit · ~26,000 ly"}},{"k":"grid_ring_edge","v":{"s":"Disk edge · ~50,000 ly"}},{"k":"inspect_galaxytype_type","v":{"s":"Galaxy shape"}},{"k":"fact_arm_pattern","v":{"s":"arm pattern"}},{"k":"fact_no_arms","v":{"s":"None"}},{"k":"fact_bar_present","v":{"s":"Central bar present"}},{"k":"fact_bar_absent","v":{"s":"No central bar"}},{"k":"fact_example","v":{"s":"Example"}},{"k":"fact_arms","v":{"s":"Arms"}},{"k":"fact_bar","v":{"s":"Bar"}},{"k":"inspect_galaxytype_evidence","v":{"s":"Astronomers classify galaxies by wide-field images, color, gas content, and the motion of stars and dust."}},{"k":"inspect_galaxytype_question","v":{"s":"Which visible features helped you classify this galaxy?"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_title","v":{"s":"Central black hole"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_type","v":{"s":"Galactic core"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_desc","v":{"s":"The Milky Way contains Sagittarius A*, a compact object of about four million solar masses at the galactic center."}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_fact1","v":{"s":"Invisible event horizon"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_fact2","v":{"s":"Bright accretion disk when gas falls in"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_fact3","v":{"s":"Nearby stars orbit at extreme speeds"}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_evidence","v":{"s":"The strongest evidence is motion: stars whip around an unseen, tiny, massive object in the core."}},{"k":"inspect_blackhole_question","v":{"s":"What evidence would convince you the mass is compact instead of spread out?"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_title","v":{"s":"Spiral arms"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_type","v":{"s":"Density wave"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_desc","v":{"s":"Spiral arms are traffic jams of stars, gas, and dust. Stars move through them while gas compresses and forms new stars."}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_fact1","v":{"s":"Young blue stars trace arms"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_fact2","v":{"s":"Gas clouds collect there"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_fact3","v":{"s":"Dust lanes outline the wave"}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_evidence","v":{"s":"Blue star clusters, emission nebulae, and radio maps of hydrogen reveal where arms are strongest."}},{"k":"inspect_spiralarms_question","v":{"s":"Why do blue stars mark recent star formation better than red stars?"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_title","v":{"s":"Gas clouds"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_type","v":{"s":"Star-forming material"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_desc","v":{"s":"Cold hydrogen and glowing ionized gas are the raw materials for new stars and nebulae."}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_fact1","v":{"s":"Compressed gas can collapse"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_fact2","v":{"s":"Massive stars ionize nearby gas"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_fact3","v":{"s":"Radio telescopes map hidden hydrogen"}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_evidence","v":{"s":"Hydrogen emission and radio wavelengths show gas that visible-light images can miss."}},{"k":"inspect_gasclouds_question","v":{"s":"Where would you look for the next generation of stars?"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_title","v":{"s":"Dust lanes"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_type","v":{"s":"Light-blocking grains"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_desc","v":{"s":"Dust is not empty darkness. Tiny grains absorb visible light and help cool gas clouds so stars can form."}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_fact1","v":{"s":"Blocks visible starlight"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_fact2","v":{"s":"Glows in infrared"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_fact3","v":{"s":"Outlines spiral structure"}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_evidence","v":{"s":"Compare visible and infrared views: dust hides stars in one wavelength and glows in another."}},{"k":"inspect_dustlanes_question","v":{"s":"Why can infrared telescopes see deeper through dusty regions?"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_title","v":{"s":"Dark matter halo"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_type","v":{"s":"Invisible gravity"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_desc","v":{"s":"Outer stars orbit too fast for visible matter alone. A large invisible halo must be adding gravity."}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_fact1","v":{"s":"Does not emit light"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_fact2","v":{"s":"Revealed by motion"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_fact3","v":{"s":"Dominates a galaxy mass budget"}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_evidence","v":{"s":"Rotation curves stay flat instead of falling, showing extra unseen mass around the galaxy."}},{"k":"inspect_darkmatter_question","v":{"s":"Why is motion better evidence here than a photograph?"}},{"k":"inspect_mass_varies","v":{"s":"Mass varies"}},{"k":"inspect_lifetime_varies","v":{"s":"Lifetime varies"}},{"k":"fact_temperature","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"fact_mass","v":{"s":"Mass"}},{"k":"fact_lifetime","v":{"s":"Lifetime"}},{"k":"inspect_star_evidence","v":{"s":"A spectrum reveals temperature, composition, motion, and class from the pattern of absorption lines."}},{"k":"inspect_star_question","v":{"s":"How would this star change the galaxy if many formed at once?"}},{"k":"inspect_nebula_type_fallback","v":{"s":"Nebula"}},{"k":"inspect_distance_varies","v":{"s":"Distance varies"}},{"k":"inspect_gas_dust_cloud","v":{"s":"Gas and dust cloud"}},{"k":"inspect_deepsky_object","v":{"s":"Deep-sky object"}},{"k":"fact_distance","v":{"s":"Distance"}},{"k":"fact_made_of","v":{"s":"Made of"}},{"k":"fact_type","v":{"s":"Type"}},{"k":"inspect_nebula_evidence","v":{"s":"Color, emission lines, shape, and nearby stars tell whether this is a nursery, remnant, or dying-star shell."}},{"k":"inspect_nebula_question","v":{"s":"Is this object making stars, showing a dead star, or blocking light?"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_shape","v":{"s":"Shape"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_arms","v":{"s":"Arms"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_gas","v":{"s":"Gas"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_dust","v":{"s":"Dust"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_core","v":{"s":"Core"}},{"k":"inspect_btn_dark_halo","v":{"s":"Dark halo"}},{"k":"mission_cartographer_title","v":{"s":"Map the Galaxy"}},{"k":"mission_step_toggle_layers","v":{"s":"Toggle 3 layers"}},{"k":"mission_step_inspect_structure","v":{"s":"Inspect a structure"}},{"k":"mission_step_warp_landmark","v":{"s":"Warp to a landmark"}},{"k":"mission_nursery_title","v":{"s":"Find Star Birth"}},{"k":"mission_step_show_gas","v":{"s":"Show gas or nebulae"}},{"k":"mission_step_inspect_nebula","v":{"s":"Inspect a nebula or gas cloud"}},{"k":"mission_step_click_star","v":{"s":"Click any star"}},{"k":"mission_darkmatter_title","v":{"s":"Prove the Invisible"}},{"k":"mission_step_try_rotation","v":{"s":"Try 2 rotation models"}},{"k":"mission_step_flat_curve","v":{"s":"Use the flat curve"}},{"k":"mission_step_inspect_darkhalo","v":{"s":"Inspect dark halo evidence"}},{"k":"mission_step_measure_star","v":{"s":"Measure a star orbit"}},{"k":"mission_multiwavelength_title","v":{"s":"Decode Hidden Light"}},{"k":"mission_step_try_filters","v":{"s":"Try 2 observing filters"}},{"k":"mission_step_radio_infrared","v":{"s":"Use radio or infrared"}},{"k":"mission_step_gravity_view","v":{"s":"Use gravity view"}},{"k":"mission_step_test_doppler","v":{"s":"Test Doppler shift"}},{"k":"mission_step_record_evidence","v":{"s":"Record an evidence note"}},{"k":"mission_stellardeath_title","v":{"s":"Track Stellar Death"}},{"k":"mission_step_trigger_supernova","v":{"s":"Trigger a supernova"}},{"k":"mission_step_inspect_core","v":{"s":"Inspect the core"}},{"k":"mission_step_open_starlife","v":{"s":"Open Star Life"}},{"k":"stellar_body","v":{"s":"Stellar Body"}},{"k":"layer_dust_lanes","v":{"s":"Dust Lanes"}},{"k":"layer_gas_clouds","v":{"s":"Gas Clouds"}},{"k":"summary_no_selection","v":{"s":"No object selected"}},{"k":"summary_motion_reduced","v":{"s":"Reduced motion; automatic movement disabled"}},{"k":"summary_motion_rotating","v":{"s":"Gentle automatic rotation active"}},{"k":"summary_motion_paused","v":{"s":"Automatic rotation paused"}},{"k":"aria_back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"header_title","v":{"s":"Galaxy Explorer"}},{"k":"mode_switcher_label","v":{"s":"Galaxy Explorer modes"}},{"k":"mode_galaxy","v":{"s":"Galaxy"}},{"k":"mode_black_hole","v":{"s":"Black Hole"}},{"k":"mode_real_sky","v":{"s":"Real Sky"}},{"k":"mode_star_life","v":{"s":"Star Life"}},{"k":"mode_quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"mode_metallicity","v":{"s":"Metallicity"}},{"k":"galaxy_shape_label","v":{"s":"Galaxy shape"}},{"k":"canvas_description","v":{"s":"Interactive three-dimensional galaxy model."}},{"k":"canvas_description_layers","v":{"s":"Visible structures can include stars, dust lanes, gas, nebulae, the galactic core, and the dark-matter halo."}},{"k":"canvas_keyboard_instructions","v":{"s":"When the galaxy canvas has focus, use the arrow keys to orbit, plus and minus to zoom, left and right brackets or Page Up and Page Down to move between stars, Escape to clear a selection, and R to reset. Equivalent on-screen buttons are available after the canvas."}},{"k":"motion_preference_on","v":{"s":"Reduced-motion preference detected. Automatic rotation and cinematic tours are disabled."}},{"k":"motion_preference_off","v":{"s":"The galaxy rotates gently by default. Use the pause rotation button to stop automatic motion."}},{"k":"canvas_status_ready","v":{"s":"Galaxy canvas ready for exploration."}},{"k":"fallback_why_cdn","v":{"s":"The 3-D engine is loaded from the internet and could not be fetched — often a school network or offline device."}},{"k":"fallback_why_nowebgl","v":{"s":"This browser reports no WebGL support, which the 3-D view needs."}},{"k":"fallback_why_context","v":{"s":"WebGL exists here but a drawing surface could not be created — usually too many 3-D views open at once, or hardware acceleration switched off."}},{"k":"fallback_why_init","v":{"s":"The 3-D scene could not finish building on this device."}},{"k":"fallback_canvas_aria","v":{"s":"Flat illustration of the selected galaxy shape, shown because the interactive 3-D view is unavailable."}},{"k":"fallback_title","v":{"s":"Showing a flat view — the interactive 3-D galaxy is unavailable"}},{"k":"fallback_still_works","v":{"s":"Everything else on this page still works: filters, the object inspector, star life, and the quiz."}},{"k":"retry_3d_mode","v":{"s":"Retry 3D Mode"}},{"k":"fallback_gpu_label","v":{"s":"Graphics reported by this browser:"}},{"k":"aria_galaxy_canvas","v":{"s":"Interactive galaxy simulation"}},{"k":"building_scene","v":{"s":"Building the galaxy…"}},{"k":"building_scene_sub","v":{"s":"Placing stars, dust lanes and nebulae. This can take a moment on a slow connection."}},{"k":"aria_camera_controls","v":{"s":"Galaxy camera controls"}},{"k":"camera_zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"camera_zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"camera_reset","v":{"s":"Reset camera"}},{"k":"camera_motion_disabled","v":{"s":"Automatic rotation disabled by reduced-motion preference"}},{"k":"camera_pause_rotation","v":{"s":"Pause auto-rotation"}},{"k":"camera_resume_rotation","v":{"s":"Resume auto-rotation"}},{"k":"camera_show_labels","v":{"s":"Show simulation labels"}},{"k":"camera_hide_labels","v":{"s":"Hide simulation labels"}},{"k":"camera_tour_disabled","v":{"s":"Cinematic tour disabled by reduced-motion preference"}},{"k":"camera_stop_tour","v":{"s":"Stop cinematic tour"}},{"k":"camera_start_tour","v":{"s":"Start cinematic tour"}},{"k":"camera_fullscreen","v":{"s":"Toggle fullscreen"}},{"k":"aria_spectral_legend","v":{"s":"Star colour key, hottest to coolest: O B A F G K M — blue is hottest, red is coolest."}},{"k":"legend_hot","v":{"s":"hot"}},{"k":"legend_cool","v":{"s":"cool"}},{"k":"infrared_legend_aria","v":{"s":"Infrared view: visible dust extinction decreases while warm dust thermal emission and embedded protostars brighten."}},{"k":"infrared_legend_title","v":{"s":"Infrared radiative transfer"}},{"k":"infrared_extinction_down","v":{"s":"Dust shadow ↓"}},{"k":"infrared_emission_up","v":{"s":"Thermal glow ↑"}},{"k":"infrared_legend_note","v":{"s":"Dust absorbs short-wavelength starlight and re-radiates that energy in the infrared."}},{"k":"xray_legend_aria","v":{"s":"X-ray plasma key. Brighter points mark compact high-energy sources. Nested violet, cyan, and white arcs map cooler through hotter supernova shock layers. The central bicone traces a nuclear hot-gas outflow."}},{"k":"xray_legend_title","v":{"s":"High-energy plasma map"}},{"k":"xray_beacon_key","v":{"s":"Beacon = compact high-energy source"}},{"k":"xray_shock_key","v":{"s":"Nested arcs = shock temperature layers"}},{"k":"xray_outflow_key","v":{"s":"Bicone = nuclear hot-gas outflow"}},{"k":"xray_legend_note","v":{"s":"Color and intensity encode plasma energy, not ordinary visible-light brightness."}},{"k":"radio_velocity_aria","v":{"s":"Radio observation key. The blue-to-red field shows approaching through receding hydrogen. Short line segments trace projected magnetic-field direction. Cyan and magenta ribbons separate Faraday-rotation depth."}},{"k":"radio_velocity_title","v":{"s":"21 cm velocity + magnetic field"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_toward","v":{"s":"← Approaching"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_away","v":{"s":"Receding →"}},{"k":"radio_field_direction","v":{"s":"Line orientation = magnetic-field direction"}},{"k":"radio_faraday_depth","v":{"s":"Ribbon color = Faraday depth"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_note","v":{"s":"Velocity color maps motion; aligned ticks and layered ribbons reveal magnetized gas along the same sightline."}},{"k":"gravity_legend_aria","v":{"s":"Gravity inference key. Orbital speed constrains enclosed mass, while aligned weak-lensing arclets trace projected halo mass. This is an evidence map, not a photograph."}},{"k":"gravity_legend_title","v":{"s":"Mass inference field"}},{"k":"gravity_legend_sub","v":{"s":"Two measurements, one invisible halo"}},{"k":"gravity_orbit_key","v":{"s":"Orbital speed → enclosed mass"}},{"k":"gravity_lensing_key","v":{"s":"Arclet shear → projected mass"}},{"k":"gravity_legend_note","v":{"s":"The luminous disk is observed; the larger halo is reconstructed from motion and lensing evidence."}},{"k":"measurement_title","v":{"s":"Galactocentric measurement"}},{"k":"model_flat_short","v":{"s":"flat observed model"}},{"k":"model_keplerian_short","v":{"s":"visible-matter model"}},{"k":"model_rigid_short","v":{"s":"rigid toy model"}},{"k":"measure_radius","v":{"s":"Radius"}},{"k":"measure_speed","v":{"s":"Orbital speed"}},{"k":"measure_period","v":{"s":"Orbital period"}},{"k":"measure_mass","v":{"s":"Mass inside orbit"}},{"k":"vector_velocity","v":{"s":"Motion is tangent"}},{"k":"vector_gravity","v":{"s":"Gravity points inward"}},{"k":"measurement_ruler_note","v":{"s":"The cyan ruler measures radius. Sideways motion plus an inward gravitational pull continuously bends the star onto the glowing orbit path. Change the model to test a new speed and period."}},{"k":"live_scale_aria","v":{"s":"Approximate field of view and current galactic scale regime"}},{"k":"scale_overlay_title","v":{"s":"Scale"}},{"k":"controls_title","v":{"s":"Galaxy controls"}},{"k":"controls_sub","v":{"s":"Adjust the view without losing sight of the simulation."}},{"k":"aria_control_groups","v":{"s":"Galaxy control groups"}},{"k":"observatory_filters_title","v":{"s":"Observatory Filters"}},{"k":"observation_guide_title","v":{"s":"Observe - compare - explain"}},{"k":"guide_strong_signal","v":{"s":"Strong signal: "}},{"k":"guide_blind_spot","v":{"s":"Blind spot: "}},{"k":"guide_inference_label","v":{"s":"Inference: "}},{"k":"weak_lensing_title","v":{"s":"Read the arclets beyond the disk"}},{"k":"weak_lensing_explain","v":{"s":"Background galaxies appear stretched tangentially around the halo. Their shared alignment maps foreground mass, including matter that emits no light."}},{"k":"radio_velocity_guide_title","v":{"s":"Read rotation from the color split"}},{"k":"radio_velocity_guide_body","v":{"s":"The 21 cm line shifts blue on the approaching side and red on the receding side. Mapping that shift across the disk reveals both rotation direction and orbital speed."}},{"k":"infrared_transfer_title","v":{"s":"Track absorbed energy"}},{"k":"infrared_transfer_body","v":{"s":"Regions that were dark in visible light can glow in infrared because dust grains absorb starlight, warm up, and emit that energy again at longer wavelengths."}},{"k":"xray_energy_title","v":{"s":"Separate heat from ordinary brightness"}},{"k":"xray_energy_body","v":{"s":"Flickering points trace accreting compact objects and pulsars; expanding arcs trace gas heated to millions of degrees by supernova shocks."}},{"k":"evidence_note_label","v":{"s":"Evidence note - what changed, and what does it suggest?"}},{"k":"evidence_note_placeholder","v":{"s":"I notice... This suggests... because..."}},{"k":"rendering_detail_title","v":{"s":"Rendering detail"}},{"k":"rendering_detail_sub","v":{"s":"Auto adapts to this device; Cinematic maximizes depth and particle detail."}},{"k":"science_overlay_title","v":{"s":"Science labels"}},{"k":"science_overlay_sub","v":{"s":"Connect visible features to the evidence they provide."}},{"k":"overlay_on","v":{"s":"On"}},{"k":"overlay_off","v":{"s":"Off"}},{"k":"hide_layer","v":{"s":"Hide"}},{"k":"show_layer","v":{"s":"Show"}},{"k":"layer_not_characteristic","v":{"s":"is not characteristic of elliptical galaxies"}},{"k":"layer_minimal","v":{"s":"minimal"}},{"k":"how_elliptical_moves_title","v":{"s":"How do elliptical stars move?"}},{"k":"how_galaxy_spin_title","v":{"s":"How does a galaxy spin?"}},{"k":"velocity_dispersion_badge","v":{"s":"velocity dispersion"}},{"k":"dark_matter_mystery_badge","v":{"s":"dark matter mystery"}},{"k":"elliptical_motion_intro","v":{"s":"Elliptical galaxies are pressure-supported: their stars criss-cross the galaxy on differently tilted 3-D orbits instead of sharing one rotating disk."}},{"k":"rotation_intro","v":{"s":"Pick a rotation model and watch the stars in the 3-D view above actually obey it. This one question — “how fast do outer stars orbit?” — led to one of the biggest discoveries in physics."}},{"k":"rot_keplerian","v":{"s":"Keplerian"}},{"k":"rotbtn_keplerian_hint","v":{"s":"what visible mass predicts"}},{"k":"rotbtn_flat_label","v":{"s":"Flat (observed)"}},{"k":"rotbtn_flat_hint","v":{"s":"what telescopes measure"}},{"k":"rotbtn_rigid_label","v":{"s":"Rigid disk"}},{"k":"rotbtn_rigid_hint","v":{"s":"toy model — spins like a DVD"}},{"k":"elliptical_orbits_aria","v":{"s":"Stars cross an elliptical galaxy on many differently tilted orbital planes. Their velocity dispersion supports the galaxy against gravity."}},{"k":"elliptical_orbit_caption","v":{"s":"many orbital planes · no shared stellar disk"}},{"k":"elliptical_shape_label","v":{"s":"Shape"}},{"k":"elliptical_shape_value","v":{"s":"Triaxial"}},{"k":"elliptical_motion_label","v":{"s":"Motion"}},{"k":"elliptical_motion_value","v":{"s":"3-D orbits"}},{"k":"elliptical_evidence_label","v":{"s":"Evidence"}},{"k":"elliptical_evidence_value","v":{"s":"Velocity spread"}},{"k":"aria_rotation_curve","v":{"s":"Rotation curve chart: orbital speed versus distance from the galactic center. The Keplerian prediction from visible matter falls off with distance, but the observed curve stays flat. The shaded gap between them is the evidence for dark matter."}},{"k":"chart_fast","v":{"s":"fast"}},{"k":"chart_slow","v":{"s":"slow"}},{"k":"chart_distance_axis","v":{"s":"distance from galactic center"}},{"k":"chart_orbital_speed","v":{"s":"orbital speed"}},{"k":"chart_observed_flat","v":{"s":"observed (flat)"}},{"k":"chart_visible_matter","v":{"s":"visible matter alone"}},{"k":"chart_dark_matter","v":{"s":"dark matter"}},{"k":"elliptical_motion_explain","v":{"s":"The galaxy stays together because stars move through it in many directions. Astronomers measure the spread of those speeds—velocity dispersion—to infer the mass needed to hold the system together."}},{"k":"rotation_explain_keplerian","v":{"s":"If starlight were all there is, gravity weakens with distance and outer stars should crawl — watch the galaxy's center above lap the outskirts and the disk shear apart. Our solar system really works this way: Mercury laps Neptune 700 times per Neptune-year."}},{"k":"rotation_explain_rigid","v":{"s":"A toy model — the whole disk turns together like a painted DVD. No real galaxy does this; it would need mass to keep growing with radius squared. Compare it with the other two models!"}},{"k":"rotation_explain_flat","v":{"s":"In the 1970s Vera Rubin measured real galaxies and found this: outer stars move just as fast as inner ones. Visible matter can't supply that much gravity — an invisible halo of dark matter (~85% of all matter in the universe) must be holding the galaxy together. Nobody yet knows what it is."}},{"k":"comparison_title","v":{"s":"Same star, three gravity models"}},{"k":"comparison_sub","v":{"s":"Radius stays fixed. Speed and orbital period change because each model assumes a different mass distribution."}},{"k":"comparison_caption","v":{"s":"Predicted orbital motion for the selected star under three galaxy rotation models"}},{"k":"comparison_model","v":{"s":"Model"}},{"k":"comparison_speed","v":{"s":"Speed"}},{"k":"comparison_period","v":{"s":"Period"}},{"k":"comparison_flat","v":{"s":"Flat observed"}},{"k":"comparison_visible","v":{"s":"Visible matter"}},{"k":"comparison_rigid","v":{"s":"Rigid toy"}},{"k":"comparison_evidence_flat","v":{"s":"Evidence: at large radii, the observed speed stays high. The enclosed mass must keep increasing even where the visible disk fades."}},{"k":"comparison_evidence_keplerian","v":{"s":"Prediction from visible matter alone: outer stars slow down and take much longer to orbit."}},{"k":"comparison_evidence_rigid","v":{"s":"Toy prediction: speed rises directly with radius, as if the entire galaxy were a solid object."}},{"k":"elliptical_motion_note","v":{"s":"Notice that no single orbital plane dominates. The smooth shape is a statistical pattern made by many crossing stellar paths."}},{"k":"rotation_density_wave_note","v":{"s":"Also notice: the glowing gas lanes hold still while stars stream through them — real spiral arms are density waves (cosmic traffic jams), not fixed pinwheels of stars."}},{"k":"stars_count_label","v":{"s":"Stars"}},{"k":"aria_number_of_stars","v":{"s":"Number of stars"}},{"k":"density_dense","v":{"s":"Dense"}},{"k":"density_normal","v":{"s":"Normal"}},{"k":"density_sparse","v":{"s":"Sparse"}},{"k":"cosmic_timelapse_title","v":{"s":"Cosmic Time-Lapse"}},{"k":"big_bang_label","v":{"s":"Big Bang"}},{"k":"aria_cosmic_age","v":{"s":"Cosmic age in billion years"}},{"k":"aria_toggle_timelapse","v":{"s":"Toggle cosmic time-lapse playback"}},{"k":"stop_btn","v":{"s":"Stop"}},{"k":"play_timelapse_btn","v":{"s":"Play Time-Lapse"}},{"k":"aria_trigger_supernova","v":{"s":"Trigger a random supernova in the galaxy view"}},{"k":"title_trigger_supernova","v":{"s":"Trigger a random supernova flash in the galaxy view"}},{"k":"supernova_unavailable","v":{"s":"Supernova effect is not available yet."}},{"k":"random_supernova_btn","v":{"s":"Random supernova"}},{"k":"milestone_now","v":{"s":"Now"}},{"k":"warp_blackhole_desc","v":{"s":"Sagittarius A* sits in this crowded core; stars orbit it so quickly that an unseen compact mass is required."}},{"k":"warp_core_short","v":{"s":"The crowded nucleus, home to Sagittarius A*."}},{"k":"warp_hint","v":{"s":"Warp to a landmark, then inspect the evidence card below the workspace."}},{"k":"accessible_summary_title","v":{"s":"Text alternative for the 3-D scene"}},{"k":"accessible_summary_heading","v":{"s":"Current simulation state"}},{"k":"summary_galaxy","v":{"s":"Galaxy"}},{"k":"summary_observation","v":{"s":"Observation"}},{"k":"summary_age","v":{"s":"Cosmic age"}},{"k":"summary_selection","v":{"s":"Selection"}},{"k":"summary_motion","v":{"s":"Motion"}},{"k":"summary_layers","v":{"s":"Visible layers"}},{"k":"summary_layers_none","v":{"s":"None"}},{"k":"accessible_summary_help","v":{"s":"Every camera and layer action has a keyboard-accessible button. Focus the canvas for arrow-key orbiting, plus and minus zoom, bracket-key star selection, Escape to clear, and R to reset."}},{"k":"explore_science_summary","v":{"s":"Explore the science behind the simulation"}},{"k":"hubble_off_sequence","v":{"s":"off the fork"}},{"k":"hubble_tuning_fork_title","v":{"s":"Hubble Tuning Fork — classified by shape"}},{"k":"aria_hubble_tuning_fork","v":{"s":"Hubble tuning fork: ellipticals on the handle, spirals on the top prong, barred spirals on the bottom prong, and irregulars set apart because they do not fit the sequence, with the current galaxy type highlighted"}},{"k":"you_are_here","v":{"s":"you are here"}},{"k":"hubble_scheme_note","v":{"s":"Edwin Hubble's 1936 scheme sorts galaxies by SHAPE — it is NOT a timeline. Galaxies do not evolve along the fork from one type to the next."}},{"k":"cosmo_redshift_title","v":{"s":"Cosmological redshift — farther = faster = redder"}},{"k":"aria_two_spectra","v":{"s":"Two spectra: a nearby galaxy's absorption lines, and a distant galaxy's same lines shifted toward the red end (redshift)."}},{"k":"spectrum_nearby_rest","v":{"s":"Nearby galaxy (rest frame)"}},{"k":"spectrum_distant_redshift","v":{"s":"Distant galaxy (redshifted)"}},{"k":"redshift_note","v":{"s":"Each dark line is the fingerprint of the same element. In a more distant galaxy those lines sit farther toward red — expanding space stretched the light on its way here (Hubble's law: recession speed ∝ distance)."}},{"k":"doppler_lab_title","v":{"s":"Doppler Shift Lab — toward = blue, away = red"}},{"k":"doppler_lab_desc","v":{"s":"Move the source along your line of sight. Negative radial velocity moves spectral lines toward blue; positive radial velocity moves them toward red."}},{"k":"aria_doppler_spectrum","v":{"s":"Doppler shift spectrum: absorption lines move left for blueshift and right for redshift."}},{"k":"spectrum_rest","v":{"s":"Rest spectrum"}},{"k":"spectrum_observed","v":{"s":"Observed spectrum"}},{"k":"doppler_compressed_blue","v":{"s":"compressed toward blue"}},{"k":"doppler_stretched_red","v":{"s":"stretched toward red"}},{"k":"doppler_same_wavelength","v":{"s":"same wavelength"}},{"k":"doppler_toward_us","v":{"s":"Toward us / blueshift"}},{"k":"doppler_radial_velocity","v":{"s":"Radial velocity"}},{"k":"doppler_away","v":{"s":"Away / redshift"}},{"k":"aria_doppler_velocity","v":{"s":"Doppler radial velocity in kilometers per second"}},{"k":"doppler_blueshift_label","v":{"s":"Blueshift: "}},{"k":"doppler_blueshift_body","v":{"s":"the source is moving toward us, so wave crests arrive closer together and wavelengths get shorter."}},{"k":"doppler_redshift_label","v":{"s":"Redshift: "}},{"k":"doppler_redshift_body","v":{"s":"the source is moving away, so wave crests arrive farther apart and wavelengths get longer."}},{"k":"myth_busters_title","v":{"s":"Cosmic myth-busters"}},{"k":"myth_model","v":{"s":"This 3-D view is a MODEL — no spacecraft has ever photographed the Milky Way from outside. We live inside the disk, which is why we see it edge-on as a band of light across the night sky."}},{"k":"myth_not_solar_system","v":{"s":"A galaxy is NOT a solar system. Our entire solar system is just one of ~100–400 billion star systems in the Milky Way."}},{"k":"myth_constellation","v":{"s":"Stars in a constellation only LOOK close together — they're often wildly different distances away, just along the same line of sight."}},{"k":"myth_expansion","v":{"s":"Cosmic expansion stretches SPACE ITSELF — galaxies aren't flying outward through space, and there's no center. The Big Bang happened everywhere at once, not at one spot."}},{"k":"mission_control_title","v":{"s":"Mission Control"}},{"k":"object_inspector_title","v":{"s":"Object Inspector"}},{"k":"evidence_label","v":{"s":"Evidence"}},{"k":"astronomer_note_label","v":{"s":"Astronomer Note"}},{"k":"why_it_matters_label","v":{"s":"Why It Matters"}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"realsky_mode_title","v":{"s":"Real Sky Survey Mode"}},{"k":"open_in_aladin","v":{"s":"Open in Aladin"}},{"k":"real_data_lesson_title","v":{"s":"Real Data Lesson Prompt"}},{"k":"targets_label","v":{"s":"Targets"}},{"k":"survey_light_label","v":{"s":"Survey Light"}},{"k":"catalog_overlay_label","v":{"s":"Catalog Overlay"}},{"k":"real_sky_atlas_label","v":{"s":"Interactive real-sky survey atlas"}},{"k":"realsky_atlas_unavailable","v":{"s":"Real-sky atlas unavailable"}},{"k":"realsky_connecting","v":{"s":"Connecting to real sky surveys"}},{"k":"realsky_loading","v":{"s":"Loading Aladin Lite, sky survey tiles, and catalog services."}},{"k":"retry_atlas","v":{"s":"Retry atlas"}},{"k":"open_external_atlas","v":{"s":"Open external atlas"}},{"k":"card_observe_title","v":{"s":"Observe it"}},{"k":"card_observe_body","v":{"s":"Move this target into Night Sky & Astronomy to compare real survey data with telescope expectations."}},{"k":"card_observe_action","v":{"s":"Astronomy"}},{"k":"card_time_title","v":{"s":"Place it in time"}},{"k":"card_time_body","v":{"s":"Jump to Universe with the matching real-data tour, evidence thread, and guided mission selected."}},{"k":"card_time_action","v":{"s":"Universe"}},{"k":"card_analyze_title","v":{"s":"Analyze data"}},{"k":"card_analyze_body","v":{"s":"Use Data Lab for the next step: spectra, brightness, color, classification, and student research questions."}},{"k":"card_analyze_action","v":{"s":"Data Lab"}},{"k":"quiz_generating_title","v":{"s":"Gemini is Generating Astrophysic Questions..."}},{"k":"quiz_generating_sub","v":{"s":"Parsing deep space databases..."}},{"k":"quiz_question_label","v":{"s":"Question"}},{"k":"aria_quiz_progress","v":{"s":"Quiz progress"}},{"k":"quiz_correct","v":{"s":"Correct! +10 XP"}},{"k":"quiz_answer_is","v":{"s":"The answer is: "}},{"k":"quiz_see_results","v":{"s":"See results"}},{"k":"quiz_next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"quiz_result_strong","v":{"s":"Strong grasp of stellar and galactic structure."}},{"k":"quiz_result_building","v":{"s":"Solid start — revisit the panels for the ideas that slipped."}},{"k":"quiz_result_explore","v":{"s":"Explore the simulation panels, then try again — the answers are all in there."}},{"k":"quiz_complete_title","v":{"s":"Quiz complete"}},{"k":"quiz_try_again","v":{"s":"Try again"}},{"k":"quiz_back_to_galaxy","v":{"s":"Back to the galaxy"}},{"k":"aria_blackhole_canvas","v":{"s":"Interactive model of a rotating black hole with an event horizon, photon ring, accretion disk, polar jets, and a tidal-forces object-drop experiment."}},{"k":"blackhole_lab_title","v":{"s":"Black Hole Lab"}},{"k":"blackhole_drag_hint","v":{"s":"Drag or use arrow keys to orbit - scroll or use plus and minus to zoom"}},{"k":"blackhole_drop_begin","v":{"s":"Drop an object to begin"}},{"k":"blackhole_keyboard_help","v":{"s":"Keyboard controls: use the arrow keys to orbit, plus and minus to zoom, and Home to reset the camera. Animation can be paused with the button after the canvas."}},{"k":"bh_badge_event_horizon","v":{"s":"Event horizon"}},{"k":"bh_badge_photon_ring","v":{"s":"Photon ring"}},{"k":"bh_badge_accretion_disk","v":{"s":"Accretion disk"}},{"k":"bh_badge_polar_jets","v":{"s":"Polar jets"}},{"k":"relativistic_controls_title","v":{"s":"Relativistic controls"}},{"k":"blackhole_description","v":{"s":"A teaching model near a rotating black hole. Distances are visual, not to scale."}},{"k":"bh_status_reduced_motion","v":{"s":"Animation paused to honor your reduced-motion preference."}},{"k":"bh_status_paused","v":{"s":"Simulation paused."}},{"k":"bh_status_running","v":{"s":"Simulation running."}},{"k":"bh_spin_label","v":{"s":"Spin: "}},{"k":"bh_spin_desc","v":{"s":"Higher spin speeds the inner disk and strengthens its bright approaching side."}},{"k":"bh_disk_label","v":{"s":"Disk brightness: "}},{"k":"bh_start_anim","v":{"s":"Start animation"}},{"k":"bh_pause_anim","v":{"s":"Pause animation"}},{"k":"tidal_forces_title","v":{"s":"Tidal forces experiment"}},{"k":"tidal_forces_desc","v":{"s":"Release an object and observe spaghettification: gravity pulls harder on its near side, stretching it radially while compressing it sideways."}},{"k":"two_views_title","v":{"s":"Two views of time and light"}},{"k":"bh_traveler_clock","v":{"s":"Traveler's local clock"}},{"k":"bh_steady","v":{"s":"steady"}},{"k":"bh_delayed_redshifted","v":{"s":"delayed + redshifted"}},{"k":"bh_observer_view_desc","v":{"s":"Illustrative observer view: the traveler experiences their own clock normally, while a distant observer receives increasingly delayed and redshifted light signals."}},{"k":"bh_object_label","v":{"s":"Object"}},{"k":"bh_obj_probe","v":{"s":"Space probe"}},{"k":"bh_obj_astronaut","v":{"s":"Astronaut model"}},{"k":"bh_obj_star","v":{"s":"Star"}},{"k":"bh_mass_label","v":{"s":"Black hole mass"}},{"k":"bh_mass_stellar","v":{"s":"Stellar-mass"}},{"k":"bh_mass_supermassive","v":{"s":"Supermassive"}},{"k":"bh_mass_note_stellar","v":{"s":"Stronger tidal gradient: disruption begins farther outside the horizon."}},{"k":"bh_mass_note_supermassive","v":{"s":"Gentler at the horizon: a compact object can cross before extreme stretching develops."}},{"k":"bh_drop_btn","v":{"s":"Drop object into black hole"}},{"k":"bh_what_seeing_title","v":{"s":"What you are seeing"}},{"k":"bh_li_event_horizon_label","v":{"s":"Event horizon:"}},{"k":"bh_li_event_horizon_text","v":{"s":" the boundary beyond which light cannot escape."}},{"k":"bh_li_photon_ring_label","v":{"s":"Photon ring:"}},{"k":"bh_li_photon_ring_text","v":{"s":" light bent into repeated paths around the shadow."}},{"k":"bh_li_doppler_label","v":{"s":"Doppler beaming:"}},{"k":"bh_li_doppler_text","v":{"s":" the disk side moving toward us appears brighter."}},{"k":"bh_li_jets_label","v":{"s":"Jets:"}},{"k":"bh_li_jets_text","v":{"s":" energized matter guided away from the disk along magnetic poles."}},{"k":"bh_evidence_title","v":{"s":"What is a black hole - and what might be inside?"}},{"k":"bh_evidence_desc","v":{"s":"The event horizon is an evidence boundary: outside effects can reach us; information from inside cannot."}},{"k":"bh_evidence_map_badge","v":{"s":"Evidence map"}},{"k":"bh_outside_title","v":{"s":"Outside - observable"}},{"k":"bh_outside_li1","v":{"s":"Bright shadow and photon-ring structure"}},{"k":"bh_outside_li2","v":{"s":"Fast stellar orbits, hot gas, and X-rays"}},{"k":"bh_outside_li3","v":{"s":"Gravitational waves from black-hole mergers"}},{"k":"bh_inside_title","v":{"s":"Inside - causally hidden"}},{"k":"bh_inside_li1","v":{"s":"General relativity predicts continued collapse"}},{"k":"bh_inside_li2","v":{"s":"Its singularity may mark the theory's limit"}},{"k":"bh_inside_li3","v":{"s":"No outside observer can receive an interior signal"}},{"k":"bh_supported_title","v":{"s":"Strongly supported"}},{"k":"bh_supported_desc","v":{"s":"Horizons, curved light paths, accretion, and mergers match observations and relativity."}},{"k":"bh_predicted_title","v":{"s":"Predicted, not directly seen"}},{"k":"bh_predicted_desc","v":{"s":"A classical singularity and extremely slow Hawking evaporation remain theoretical."}},{"k":"bh_speculative_title","v":{"s":"Speculative ideas"}},{"k":"bh_speculative_desc","v":{"s":"Quantum cores, fuzzballs, firewalls, wormholes, and white holes are hypotheses - not established destinations."}},{"k":"bh_lifecycle_title","v":{"s":"Black-hole life cycle"}},{"k":"aria_bh_lifecycle","v":{"s":"Possible stages in a black hole's history"}},{"k":"bh_stage_formation_title","v":{"s":"Formation"}},{"k":"bh_stage_formation_text","v":{"s":"Massive-star collapse or growth of an earlier compact seed."}},{"k":"bh_stage_feeding_title","v":{"s":"Active feeding"}},{"k":"bh_stage_feeding_text","v":{"s":"Hot accretion, flares, and sometimes enormous particle jets."}},{"k":"bh_stage_quiet_title","v":{"s":"Quiet phase"}},{"k":"bh_stage_quiet_text","v":{"s":"The disk can fade; gravity still reveals the hidden mass."}},{"k":"bh_stage_merger_title","v":{"s":"Merger"}},{"k":"bh_stage_merger_text","v":{"s":"Two black holes combine and send gravitational waves outward."}},{"k":"bh_stage_future_title","v":{"s":"Far future?"}},{"k":"bh_stage_future_text","v":{"s":"Hawking evaporation is predicted, but has not been observed."}},{"k":"bh_lifecycle_note","v":{"s":"A black hole is not necessarily active forever: its surroundings can brighten, quiet down, and brighten again as matter becomes available."}},{"k":"aria_star_canvas","v":{"s":"Animated star lifecycle visualization showing the selected mass and evolutionary stage, including red dwarf, main sequence, supernova, neutron star, and black hole outcomes."}},{"k":"star_mass_class_title","v":{"s":"Star Mass & Classification"}},{"k":"star_mass_class_sub","v":{"s":"Adjust mass to explore how different stars live and die"}},{"k":"aria_star_mass","v":{"s":"Star or brown dwarf mass in solar masses"}},{"k":"masscat_brown_dwarf","v":{"s":"Brown Dwarf"}},{"k":"masscat_red_dwarf","v":{"s":"Red Dwarf (M-type)"}},{"k":"masscat_orange_dwarf","v":{"s":"Orange Dwarf (K-type)"}},{"k":"masscat_sunlike","v":{"s":"Sun-like (G-type)"}},{"k":"masscat_hot_ms","v":{"s":"Hot Main-Sequence Star"}},{"k":"masscat_b_type","v":{"s":"Bright B-type Star"}},{"k":"masscat_massive","v":{"s":"Massive Star"}},{"k":"masscat_very_massive","v":{"s":"Very Massive Star"}},{"k":"masspath_browndwarf_label","v":{"s":"Brown-dwarf path"}},{"k":"masspath_reddwarf_label","v":{"s":"Red-dwarf future"}},{"k":"masspath_supernova_label","v":{"s":"Supernova path"}},{"k":"masspath_blackhole_label","v":{"s":"Black-hole path"}},{"k":"lifecycle_journey_title","v":{"s":"Stellar Lifecycle Journey"}},{"k":"path_gentle","v":{"s":"Gentle path"}},{"k":"path_violent","v":{"s":"Violent path"}},{"k":"branch_gentle_death","v":{"s":"Gentle death — outer layers drift away"}},{"k":"branch_violent_death","v":{"s":"Violent death — core collapse!"}},{"k":"branch_cooling","v":{"s":"Cooling phase — fades slowly"}},{"k":"dur_cools_over_time","v":{"s":"cools over time"}},{"k":"stagefact_nebula","v":{"s":"Cold molecular cloud - about 10-20 K, light-years across"}},{"k":"stagefact_protostar","v":{"s":"Contracting and heating - not yet fusing hydrogen"}},{"k":"stagefact_red_giant","v":{"s":"Cooler but far larger - roughly 3,500 K and ~100x the Sun’s radius"}},{"k":"stagefact_red_supergiant","v":{"s":"Roughly 3,500 K and hundreds of solar radii - fusing heavier elements"}},{"k":"stagefact_blue_supergiant","v":{"s":"Roughly 20,000-30,000 K - tens of thousands of solar luminosities"}},{"k":"stagefact_planetary_nebula","v":{"s":"Ejected shell drifting outward; the exposed core exceeds 100,000 K"}},{"k":"stagefact_white_dwarf","v":{"s":"Earth-sized and no longer fusing - it shines only by cooling"}},{"k":"stagefact_black_dwarf","v":{"s":"Theoretical: a white dwarf cooled to the temperature of space"}},{"k":"stagefact_blue_dwarf","v":{"s":"Theoretical: the universe is too young for any to exist yet"}},{"k":"stagefact_supernova","v":{"s":"For weeks it can outshine every other star in its galaxy combined"}},{"k":"stagefact_neutron_star","v":{"s":"About 20 km across and ~1.4 M☉ - a teaspoon would weigh a billion tonnes"}},{"k":"stagefact_black_hole","v":{"s":"Event horizon roughly 3 km across for every solar mass inside"}},{"k":"stage_from_progenitor","v":{"s":"from a "}},{"k":"stage_from_progenitor_tail","v":{"s":" star"}},{"k":"stage_progenitor_mass","v":{"s":"Started as a "}},{"k":"stage_progenitor_mass_tail","v":{"s":"star"}},{"k":"stage_lasts","v":{"s":"Duration: "}},{"k":"stage_will_form","v":{"s":"will form a "}},{"k":"announcer_focused_on","v":{"s":"Focused on {name}"}},{"k":"status_focused_on","v":{"s":"Focused on {name} · drag to orbit around it"}},{"k":"status_fullscreen_closed","v":{"s":"Fullscreen closed"}},{"k":"status_immersive_off","v":{"s":"Immersive view off"}},{"k":"status_immersive_on","v":{"s":"Immersive view on. Press Escape to leave it."}},{"k":"status_keyboard_focus","v":{"s":"Keyboard focus: {name} · [ and ] choose another star · Escape clears"}},{"k":"status_motion_paused","v":{"s":"Motion paused to honor your reduced-motion preference"}},{"k":"status_motion_still_paused","v":{"s":"Reduced-motion preference is off; animation remains paused until you resume it"}},{"k":"status_orbit_help","v":{"s":"Drag or use arrows to orbit · scroll, pinch, or plus and minus to zoom · brackets select stars"}},{"k":"status_overview_restored","v":{"s":"Overview restored"}},{"k":"status_returning_overview","v":{"s":"Returning to the overview"}},{"k":"status_star_generic","v":{"s":"Star"}},{"k":"status_tour_complete","v":{"s":"Grand Tour complete"}},{"k":"status_tour_reduced_motion","v":{"s":"Cinematic tour is paused by reduced-motion settings"}},{"k":"status_tour_stage","v":{"s":"Grand Tour · {stage}"}},{"k":"status_tour_stopped","v":{"s":"Cinematic tour stopped"}},{"k":"status_viewpoint_updated","v":{"s":"Viewpoint updated"}},{"k":"status_zoom","v":{"s":"Zoom {percent}%"}},{"k":"tour_arms","v":{"s":"Spiral-arm stellar nurseries"}},{"k":"tour_clumps","v":{"s":"Clumpy star-forming regions"}},{"k":"tour_companions_background","v":{"s":"Distant background galaxies"}},{"k":"tour_companions_dwarfs","v":{"s":"Nearby dwarf companions"}},{"k":"tour_companions_interacting","v":{"s":"Companion galaxies and tidal streams"}},{"k":"tour_core","v":{"s":"Luminous galactic core"}},{"k":"tour_densest_clump","v":{"s":"Densest star-forming clump"}},{"k":"tour_dm_halo","v":{"s":"Dark-matter halo perspective"}},{"k":"tour_edge_on","v":{"s":"Dust-lane edge-on view"}},{"k":"tour_edge_on_elliptical","v":{"s":"Edge-on: no thin disk, no dust lane"}},{"k":"tour_edge_on_irregular","v":{"s":"Edge-on: thick and disordered, no flat disk"}},{"k":"tour_halo_stars","v":{"s":"Smooth halo of old stars"}},{"k":"tour_overview","v":{"s":"Galactic overview"}},{"k":"tour_return","v":{"s":"Return to the grand overview"}},{"k":"status_chip_focused","v":{"s":"Focused on {name} · drag or use arrow keys to orbit"}},{"k":"status_chip_help","v":{"s":"Drag or use arrows to orbit · scroll, pinch, or +/- to zoom · [ ] selects stars"}},{"k":"status_nebula_generic","v":{"s":"Nebula"}},{"k":"canvas_description_age","v":{"s":"seen {age} billion years after the Big Bang."}},{"k":"dur_gyr","v":{"s":"~{n} Gyr"}},{"k":"dur_myr","v":{"s":"~{n} Myr"}},{"k":"dur_kyr","v":{"s":"~{n} kyr"}},{"k":"dur_red_dwarf","v":{"s":"~100 Gyr+"}},{"k":"lifespan_no_fusion","v":{"s":"No sustained hydrogen fusion"}},{"k":"lifespan_red_dwarf","v":{"s":"Lifespan: hundreds of billions to trillions of years"}},{"k":"lifespan_value","v":{"s":"Lifespan: {value}"}},{"k":"bh_name_astronaut","v":{"s":"Astronaut"}},{"k":"bh_name_star","v":{"s":"Star"}},{"k":"bh_name_probe","v":{"s":"Probe"}},{"k":"bh_status_ready","v":{"s":"{object} is ready to fall. Start animation to begin."}},{"k":"bh_status_released","v":{"s":"{object} released. Watch radial stretching and sideways compression increase toward the horizon."}},{"k":"bh_status_stretching","v":{"s":"Tidal forces are now visibly stretching the {object} radially and squeezing it sideways."}},{"k":"bh_status_approaching","v":{"s":"{object} is approaching the event horizon. Its light is fading from the distant observer view."}},{"k":"bh_status_crossed","v":{"s":"{object} crossed the event horizon. No signal from it can return."}},{"k":"bh_readout_outside","v":{"s":"{object} | {radii} horizon radii | tidal stretch {stretch}x"}},{"k":"bh_readout_inside","v":{"s":"{object} | inside horizon | tidal stretch {stretch}x"}},{"k":"bh_readout_complete","v":{"s":"Drop complete | object no longer visible"}},{"k":"bh_signal","v":{"s":"Distant received signal: {percent}%"}},{"k":"bh_status_context_lost","v":{"s":"The 3-D graphics context was interrupted. The simulation is paused while it recovers."}},{"k":"bh_status_recovered_paused","v":{"s":"The 3-D view recovered and remains paused."}},{"k":"bh_status_recovered_running","v":{"s":"The 3-D view recovered and is running."}},{"k":"bh_three_failed","v":{"s":"The 3-D library could not load. The labeled black-hole explanation remains available."}},{"k":"bh_no_webgl","v":{"s":"The interactive 3-D view is unavailable because WebGL could not start. The labeled explanation remains available."}},{"k":"exit_fullscreen_pill","v":{"s":"Exit fullscreen (Esc)"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_100_000_light_years","v":{"s":"~100,000 light-years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_100_000_years","v":{"s":"~100,000 years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_100_400_billion","v":{"s":"100-400 billion"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_100_400_thousand","v":{"s":"100-400 thousand"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_100_million_years","v":{"s":"~100 million years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_10_000_light_years","v":{"s":"~10,000 light-years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_10_000_years","v":{"s":"~10,000 years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_10_40_million","v":{"s":"10-40 million"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_10_billion_years","v":{"s":"~10 billion years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_000_light_years","v":{"s":"~1,000 light-years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_000_years","v":{"s":"~1,000 years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_4_trillion","v":{"s":"1-4 trillion"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_million_light_years","v":{"s":"~1 million light-years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_million_years","v":{"s":"~1 million years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_1_trillion_years","v":{"s":"~1 trillion years"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_binary_star_system","v":{"s":"A binary star system"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_black_hole","v":{"s":"A black hole"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_black_hole_evaporating","v":{"s":"A black hole evaporating"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_cloud_of_gas_and_dust","v":{"s":"A cloud of gas and dust"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_massive_star_exploding","v":{"s":"A massive star exploding"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_nebula_igniting","v":{"s":"A nebula igniting"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_nebula_where_new_planets_are_forming","v":{"s":"A nebula where new planets are forming"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_small_distant_spiral_galaxy","v":{"s":"A small, distant spiral galaxy"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_star_forming_from_a_collapsing_gas_cloud","v":{"s":"A star forming from a collapsing gas cloud"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_star_running_out_of_nuclear_fuel","v":{"s":"A star running out of nuclear fuel"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_a_type_of_dark_matter","v":{"s":"A type of dark matter"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_alpha_centauri_a","v":{"s":"Alpha Centauri A"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_antimatter_left_over_from_the_big_bang","v":{"s":"Antimatter left over from the Big Bang"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_barnards_star","v":{"s":"Barnards Star"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_barred_spiral","v":{"s":"Barred spiral"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_black_hole","v":{"s":"Black hole"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_black_holes","v":{"s":"Black holes"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_blue","v":{"s":"Blue"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_gas_around_a_planet","v":{"s":"Gas around a planet"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_giant_star","v":{"s":"Giant star"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_invisible_matter_detected_by_gravity","v":{"s":"Invisible matter detected by gravity"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_its_age","v":{"s":"Its age"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_its_color","v":{"s":"Its color"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_its_distance_from_earth","v":{"s":"Its distance from Earth"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_its_mass","v":{"s":"Its mass"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_neutron_star","v":{"s":"Neutron star"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_norma_arm","v":{"s":"Norma Arm"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_orion_arm","v":{"s":"Orion Arm"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_outer_layers_shed_by_a_dying_star","v":{"s":"Outer layers shed by a dying star"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_planetary_nebula","v":{"s":"Planetary nebula"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_protostar","v":{"s":"Protostar"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_proxima_centauri","v":{"s":"Proxima Centauri"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_red","v":{"s":"Red"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_red_dwarf","v":{"s":"Red dwarf"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_sirius","v":{"s":"Sirius"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_spiral","v":{"s":"Spiral"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_supermassive_black_hole","v":{"s":"Supermassive black hole"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_supernova","v":{"s":"Supernova"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_the_burnt_out_core_of_a_dead_star","v":{"s":"The burnt-out core of a dead star"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_the_dense_core_left_after_a_supernova_explos","v":{"s":"The dense core left after a supernova explosion"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_the_empty_space_between_galaxies","v":{"s":"The empty space between galaxies"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_two_galaxies_colliding","v":{"s":"Two galaxies colliding"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_white","v":{"s":"White"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_white_dwarf","v":{"s":"White dwarf"}},{"k":"quiz_opt_yellow","v":{"s":"Yellow"}},{"k":"quiz_select_answer","v":{"s":"Select answer: {option}"}},{"k":"inspect_spectral_class","v":{"s":"Spectral class {id}"}},{"k":"announce_galaxy_active","v":{"s":" galaxy active."}},{"k":"canvas_description_mode","v":{"s":" observing mode. "}},{"k":"announce_observe_active","v":{"s":" observing mode active."}},{"k":"scale_val_diameter","v":{"s":"~100,000 light-years"}},{"k":"scale_val_thickness","v":{"s":"~2,000 light-years"}},{"k":"scale_val_bulge","v":{"s":"~10,000 light-years"}},{"k":"scale_val_sun_distance","v":{"s":"~26,000 light-years"}},{"k":"scale_val_age","v":{"s":"~13.6 billion years"}},{"k":"masscat_brown_dwarf_plain","v":{"s":"Brown dwarf"}},{"k":"masscat_red_dwarf_plain","v":{"s":"Red dwarf"}},{"k":"masscat_orange_k","v":{"s":"Orange K-type star"}},{"k":"masscat_sunlike_g","v":{"s":"Sun-like G-type star"}},{"k":"masscat_yellow_f","v":{"s":"Yellow-white F-type star"}},{"k":"masscat_white_a","v":{"s":"White A-type star"}},{"k":"masscat_hot_b","v":{"s":"Hot B-type star"}},{"k":"masscat_very_massive_o","v":{"s":"Very massive O-type star"}},{"k":"masshint_below_fusion","v":{"s":"Below sustained hydrogen fusion"}},{"k":"masshint_trillions","v":{"s":"Lives for trillions of years"}},{"k":"masshint_stable","v":{"s":"Stable, long-lived main sequence star"}},{"k":"masshint_ten_billion","v":{"s":"Lives roughly 10 billion years"}},{"k":"masshint_hotter","v":{"s":"Hotter and shorter-lived than the Sun"}},{"k":"masshint_burns_bright","v":{"s":"Burns bright for tens to hundreds of millions of years"}},{"k":"masshint_neutron","v":{"s":"Core collapse can leave a neutron star"}},{"k":"masshint_black_hole","v":{"s":"Core collapse can form a black hole"}},{"k":"orient_face_on","v":{"s":"Face-on view"}},{"k":"orient_edge_on","v":{"s":"Edge-on view"}},{"k":"orient_angled","v":{"s":"Angled view"}},{"k":"blurb_brown_dwarf","v":{"s":"Brown dwarfs are sometimes called 'failed stars.' They glow faintly from leftover formation heat, but never sustain hydrogen fusion. They begin around 13 Jupiter masses, so Jupiter itself is far below the brown-dwarf range."}},{"k":"blurb_red_dwarf","v":{"s":"M-type red dwarfs are the most common true stars. They burn fuel so slowly that no red dwarf has had time to die since the universe began."}},{"k":"blurb_orange_dwarf","v":{"s":"K-type orange dwarfs are stable, long-lived stars. Astronomers like them for exoplanet studies because their habitable zones can last for many billions of years."}},{"k":"blurb_sunlike","v":{"s":"Stars near the Sun's mass live for billions of years. Our Sun is about halfway through its main-sequence life and will eventually become a red giant before shedding a planetary nebula."}},{"k":"blurb_larger_ms","v":{"s":"Larger main-sequence stars burn hotter and die sooner. A 2 M☉ star lives only about 1-2 billion years, much shorter than the Sun."}},{"k":"blurb_neutron_star","v":{"s":"Neutron stars are so dense that a sugar-cube-sized piece weighs about 1 billion tons! They can spin up to 716 times per second and have magnetic fields trillions of times stronger than Earth's."}},{"k":"blurb_black_hole","v":{"s":"Stellar black holes form when the collapsed cores of very massive stars become compact enough for gravity to trap light. Astronomers find them with X-ray binaries, stellar orbits, and gravitational waves."}},{"k":"origin_cosmic_ray","v":{"s":"Cosmic-ray collisions"}},{"k":"origin_dying_low_mass","v":{"s":"Dying low-mass stars"}},{"k":"origin_massive_supernovae","v":{"s":"Massive-star supernovae"}},{"k":"origin_exploding_wd","v":{"s":"Exploding white dwarfs"}},{"k":"origin_merging_ns","v":{"s":"Merging neutron stars"}},{"k":"nucleo_supernova","v":{"s":"💥 This explosion is forging oxygen, silicon, and calcium RIGHT NOW — glowing below."}},{"k":"nucleo_planetary","v":{"s":"The dying star's winds are scattering fresh carbon and nitrogen into space — glowing below."}},{"k":"nucleo_helium","v":{"s":"Deep inside, helium is fusing into carbon — the backbone atom of all known life."}},{"k":"nucleo_neutron_merger","v":{"s":"If two neutron stars collide, they forge gold, platinum, and uranium in seconds."}},{"k":"nucleo_white_dwarf","v":{"s":"If a companion star dumps gas onto it, a white dwarf can detonate — the source of much of the iron in your blood."}},{"k":"nucleo_brown_dwarf","v":{"s":"This brown dwarf is substellar: it glows from leftover heat, but it never sustains hydrogen fusion."}},{"k":"nucleo_main_sequence","v":{"s":"Right now this star fuses hydrogen into helium. Heavier elements come from later stellar stages, explosions, and compact-object mergers."}},{"k":"nucleo_intro","v":{"s":"Almost every atom heavier than helium was forged inside a star. Colors show where each element in your body — and your phone — came from."}},{"k":"nucleo_footnote","v":{"s":"Colors show each element's dominant source today — many have more than one. Gold's neutron-star origin was confirmed in 2017, when telescopes watched the glow of freshly forged heavy elements after gravitational-wave event GW170817."}},{"k":"body_calcium","v":{"s":"Calcium in your bones"}},{"k":"body_iron","v":{"s":"Iron in your blood"}},{"k":"body_oxygen","v":{"s":"Oxygen in every breath"}},{"k":"body_gold","v":{"s":"Gold in jewelry"}},{"k":"label_mass_range","v":{"s":"Mass Range"}},{"k":"label_supernova_remnant","v":{"s":"Supernova Remnant"}},{"k":"aria_gyr_after_big_bang","v":{"s":" billion years after the Big Bang"}},{"k":"narrate_init_first","v":{"s":"Galaxy Explorer loaded. A 3-D view of the Milky Way with "}},{"k":"quiz_score_label","v":{"s":"Correct answers"}},{"k":"quiz_streak_label","v":{"s":"Current streak"}},{"k":"cls_brown_dwarf","v":{"s":"Brown dwarf (substellar)"}},{"k":"cls_m_type","v":{"s":"M-type Red Dwarf"}},{"k":"cls_k_type","v":{"s":"K-type Orange"}},{"k":"cls_g_type","v":{"s":"G-type (Sun-like)"}},{"k":"cls_f_type","v":{"s":"F-type Yellow-White"}},{"k":"cls_a_type","v":{"s":"A-type White"}},{"k":"cls_b_type","v":{"s":"B-type Blue-White"}},{"k":"cls_o_type","v":{"s":"O-type Blue Giant"}},{"k":"size_intro","v":{"s":"🔭 If our Sun were a basketball, a"}},{"k":"size_join","v":{"s":"-type star would be "}},{"k":"size_o","v":{"s":"a hot tub (6–15x wider)"}},{"k":"size_b","v":{"s":"a beach ball (2–6x wider)"}},{"k":"size_a","v":{"s":"a soccer ball (1.4–2x wider)"}},{"k":"size_f","v":{"s":"a volleyball (slightly bigger)"}},{"k":"size_g","v":{"s":"another basketball (same size!)"}},{"k":"size_k","v":{"s":"a softball (a bit smaller)"}},{"k":"size_m","v":{"s":"a tennis ball or smaller"}},{"k":"size_default","v":{"s":"similar in size"}},{"k":"regime_nuclear","v":{"s":"Nuclear region"}},{"k":"regime_arm","v":{"s":"Spiral-arm detail"}},{"k":"regime_halo","v":{"s":"Halo context"}},{"k":"status_autorotate_on","v":{"s":"Gentle auto-rotation on"}},{"k":"status_autorotate_blocked","v":{"s":"Auto-rotation remains off because reduced motion is enabled"}},{"k":"status_autorotate_paused","v":{"s":"Auto-rotation paused"}},{"k":"label_solar_masses","v":{"s":" Solar Masses"}},{"k":"status_camera_repositioning","v":{"s":"Camera repositioning to this location."}},{"k":"regime_structure","v":{"s":"Galactic structure"}},{"k":"orient_angled_initial","v":{"s":"Angled view"}},{"k":"scale_initial_field","v":{"s":"~21 kpc field"}},{"k":"announce_rotation_active","v":{"s":" rotation active."}},{"k":"toast_supernova","v":{"s":"Supernova flash triggered"}},{"k":"narrate_supernova_terse","v":{"s":"Supernova flash"}},{"k":"narrate_supernova_prefix","v":{"s":"Random supernova: "}},{"k":"aria_instrument_readout","v":{"s":" instrument readout. "}},{"k":"aria_observing_mode","v":{"s":" observing mode. "}},{"k":"aria_rendering_detail","v":{"s":" galaxy rendering detail"}},{"k":"rot_desc_rigid","v":{"s":"the same angular speed, like a solid disk"}},{"k":"rot_desc_keplerian","v":{"s":"Keplerian speeds — inner stars lap the outer ones dramatically"}},{"k":"rot_desc_flat","v":{"s":"the observed flat-curve speeds — outer stars keep up, which only dark matter can explain"}},{"k":"narrate_rotation_prefix","v":{"s":"Rotation model: "}},{"k":"doppler_magnified","v":{"s":"Screen shift magnified for clarity; actual z = "}},{"k":"doppler_blueshift","v":{"s":"Blueshift: source moving toward us at "}},{"k":"doppler_redshift","v":{"s":"Redshift: source moving away at "}},{"k":"doppler_none","v":{"s":"No Doppler shift: source has zero radial velocity. "}},{"k":"doppler_kms","v":{"s":" kilometers per second."}},{"k":"narrate_realsky_first","v":{"s":"Opening the Real Sky Atlas. Compare the galaxy model with live telescope survey imagery."}},{"k":"narrate_realsky_repeat","v":{"s":"Opening the Real Sky Atlas."}},{"k":"narrate_init_stars","v":{"s":" stars. Drag or use arrow keys to orbit; scroll, pinch, or use plus and minus to zoom. Explore galaxy types, warp to locations, and travel through cosmic time."}},{"k":"narrate_ready","v":{"s":"Galaxy Explorer ready."}},{"k":"narrate_realsky_terse","v":{"s":"Real Sky Atlas"}},{"k":"realsky_marker_name","v":{"s":"Classroom target"}},{"k":"toast_starlife_snapshot","v":{"s":"📸 Star life snapshot saved!"}},{"k":"toast_snapshot","v":{"s":"📸 Snapshot saved!"}},{"k":"narrate_starlife_prefix","v":{"s":"Star Life: "}},{"k":"preset_blue_dwarf","v":{"s":"0.2 M☉ blue-dwarf phase"}},{"k":"preset_core_collapse","v":{"s":"12 M☉ core collapse"}},{"k":"narrate_solar_masses","v":{"s":" solar masses."}},{"k":"aria_select_stage","v":{"s":"Select lifecycle stage: "}},{"k":"narrate_stage_selected","v":{"s":" stage selected."}},{"k":"aria_set_mass","v":{"s":"Set mass to "}},{"k":"aria_set_mass_join","v":{"s":"-type star, "}},{"k":"source_exploding_wd_inline","v":{"s":"exploding white dwarfs"}},{"k":"source_ns_mergers_inline","v":{"s":"neutron-star mergers"}},{"k":"modedesc_galaxy","v":{"s":"Galaxy view. Explore the structure, stars, and nebulae of the Milky Way."}},{"k":"modedesc_blackhole","v":{"s":"Black Hole Lab. Orbit an event horizon and observe an accretion disk, photon ring, relativistic beaming, and polar jets."}},{"k":"modedesc_realsky","v":{"s":"Real Sky. Compare the model with live sky survey imagery and object catalogs."}},{"k":"modedesc_metalhunt","v":{"s":"Metallicity. Investigate how star chemistry records galaxy history."}},{"k":"modedesc_starlife","v":{"s":"Star Lifecycle. Adjust stellar mass to explore how stars are born, live, and die."}},{"k":"narrate_mode_active","v":{"s":" mode active."}},{"k":"aria_set_rotation","v":{"s":"Set rotation model: "}},{"k":"bh_status_no_webgl_runtime","v":{"s":"Black hole simulation unavailable because WebGL could not start. Read the adjacent explanation for the event horizon, photon ring, accretion disk, and jets."}},{"k":"narrate_rotation_join","v":{"s":". Inner and outer stars now orbit at "}},{"k":"narrate_supernova_tail","v":{"s":". The flash marks a massive star exploding and spreading heavy elements into space."}},{"k":"unit_kpc_field","v":{"s":" kpc field"}},{"k":"unit_billion_years","v":{"s":" billion years"}},{"k":"unit_solar_masses","v":{"s":" solar masses"}},{"k":"label_big_bang","v":{"s":"Big Bang"}},{"k":"source_massive_supernovae_inline","v":{"s":"massive supernovae"}},{"k":"narrate_layer_shown","v":{"s":"Showing {layer} layer."}},{"k":"narrate_layer_hidden","v":{"s":"Hiding {layer} layer."}},{"k":"narrate_type_switched","v":{"s":"Switched to {type} galaxy. {desc} Example: {example}."}},{"k":"narrate_warping_to","v":{"s":"Warping to {target}. "}},{"k":"narrate_warped_to","v":{"s":"Warped to {target}."}},{"k":"canvas_sun_reference","v":{"s":"Sun (1 M☉)"}},{"k":"canvas_zone_radiative","v":{"s":"Radiative"}},{"k":"canvas_zone_convective","v":{"s":"Convective"}},{"k":"canvas_zone_fully_convective","v":{"s":"Convective throughout"}},{"k":"canvas_earth_sized","v":{"s":"(Earth-sized)"}},{"k":"canvas_supernova_label","v":{"s":"SUPERNOVA"}},{"k":"nebtype_emission","v":{"s":"Emission"}},{"k":"nebtype_planetary","v":{"s":"Planetary"}},{"k":"nebtype_dark","v":{"s":"Dark"}},{"k":"dur_forever","v":{"s":"Forever"}},{"k":"collapse_bd_title","v":{"s":"No sustained stellar fusion"}},{"k":"collapse_bd_badge","v":{"s":"Substellar"}},{"k":"collapse_bd_desc","v":{"s":"A brown dwarf is below the hydrogen-fusion limit, so it cools and fades instead of becoming a white dwarf, neutron star, or black hole."}},{"k":"collapse_bd_final","v":{"s":"Cooling brown dwarf"}},{"k":"collapse_wd_title","v":{"s":"No core-collapse supernova"}},{"k":"collapse_wd_badge","v":{"s":"Gentle ending"}},{"k":"collapse_wd_desc","v":{"s":"This star will shed outer layers and cool as a white dwarf instead of forming a neutron star or black hole."}},{"k":"collapse_wd_final","v":{"s":"White dwarf"}},{"k":"collapse_ns_title","v":{"s":"Core collapse makes a neutron star"}},{"k":"collapse_ns_desc","v":{"s":"The iron core collapses, rebounds as a supernova shock, and leaves an ultra-dense neutron-star remnant."}},{"k":"collapse_ns_final","v":{"s":"Neutron star"}},{"k":"collapse_bh_title","v":{"s":"Core collapse can form a black hole"}},{"k":"collapse_bh_desc","v":{"s":"After the supernova, the remaining core is massive enough that gravity wins and an event horizon forms."}},{"k":"collapse_bh_final","v":{"s":"Black hole"}},{"k":"collapse_step_massive","v":{"s":"Massive star"}},{"k":"collapse_step_iron_core","v":{"s":"Iron core collapse"}},{"k":"collapse_step_supernova_shock","v":{"s":"Supernova shock"}},{"k":"stagehr_protostar_note","v":{"s":"sliding down the Hayashi track toward the main sequence"}},{"k":"stagehr_ms_note_bd","v":{"s":"a brown dwarf — below the sustained hydrogen-fusion limit"}},{"k":"stagehr_ms_note","v":{"s":"on the main sequence, where it spends ~90% of its life"}},{"k":"stagehr_red_giant_note","v":{"s":"climbing the giant branch — cooler but far more luminous"}},{"k":"stagehr_red_supergiant_note","v":{"s":"top right — enormous, cool, and doomed"}},{"k":"stagehr_blue_supergiant_note","v":{"s":"top left — hyper-luminous and shedding mass"}},{"k":"stagehr_blue_dwarf_note","v":{"s":"a theoretical late phase — no red dwarf has died yet"}},{"k":"stagehr_planetary_nebula_note","v":{"s":"the exposed core dashes hot and left before fading"}},{"k":"stagehr_white_dwarf_note","v":{"s":"bottom left — white-hot but only Earth-sized"}},{"k":"offchart_nebula","v":{"s":"A nebula isn't a star yet — pick a later stage to see your star appear on the map."}},{"k":"offchart_supernova","v":{"s":"A supernova briefly outshines this entire chart — off the top by a factor of 10,000!"}},{"k":"offchart_neutron_star","v":{"s":"A neutron star no longer fuses anything — it has left the H-R diagram forever."}},{"k":"offchart_black_hole","v":{"s":"A black hole emits no light at all — nothing to plot. The diagram only maps shining stars."}},{"k":"offchart_black_dwarf_bd","v":{"s":"A cooling brown dwarf is faint and substellar — it fades below the main-sequence map."}},{"k":"offchart_black_dwarf","v":{"s":"A black dwarf is a theoretical cooled white dwarf; the universe is not old enough for true black dwarfs yet."}},{"k":"hr_diagram_title","v":{"s":"H-R Diagram — the astronomer's map"}},{"k":"hr_diagram_intro","v":{"s":"Every star is one dot: temperature across (hot on the LEFT — astronomers' quirk), luminosity up. Stars aren't scattered randomly. Drag the mass slider and click lifecycle stages — the dashed line traces YOUR star's whole journey."}},{"k":"aria_hr_diagram","v":{"s":"Hertzsprung-Russell diagram: surface temperature decreasing left to right, luminosity increasing upward. Shows the main sequence band, giants, supergiants and white dwarf regions, the Sun, and the current star's evolutionary track with its active stage highlighted."}},{"k":"hr_main_sequence_label","v":{"s":"MAIN SEQUENCE (90% of stars)"}},{"k":"hr_giants_label","v":{"s":"Giants"}},{"k":"hr_supergiants_label","v":{"s":"Supergiants"}},{"k":"hr_white_dwarfs_label","v":{"s":"White Dwarfs"}},{"k":"hr_sun_label","v":{"s":"Sun"}},{"k":"hr_select_stage_prompt","v":{"s":"Select a lifecycle stage to plot your star."}},{"k":"harvard_classification_title","v":{"s":"Harvard Spectral Classification (OBAFGKM)"}},{"k":"brown_dwarf_substellar_label","v":{"s":"Brown dwarf (substellar)"}},{"k":"brown_dwarf_substellar_desc","v":{"s":"This object is below about 0.08 solar masses, so it never settles onto the hydrogen-burning main sequence. It glows from leftover heat and slowly cools instead."}},{"k":"did_you_know_title","v":{"s":"Did You Know?"}},{"k":"star_stuff_title","v":{"s":"You Are Star Stuff"}},{"k":"size_comparison_title","v":{"s":"Size Comparison"}},{"k":"size_smaller","v":{"s":"Your star is smaller and cooler than the Sun."}},{"k":"size_similar","v":{"s":"Your star is about the same size as the Sun."}},{"k":"size_larger","v":{"s":"Your star is clearly larger and hotter than the Sun."}},{"k":"size_giant","v":{"s":"Your star is a blue giant — vastly larger, hotter, and more luminous than the Sun."}},{"k":"size_luminosity_prefix","v":{"s":"About "}},{"k":"size_luminosity_suffix","v":{"s":" times the Sun’s luminosity."}},{"k":"aria_size_comparison","v":{"s":"Size comparison: the Sun beside a star of "}},{"k":"aria_size_comparison_tail","v":{"s":" solar masses, drawn at "}},{"k":"aria_size_comparison_radii","v":{"s":" solar radii."}},{"k":"size_sun_label","v":{"s":"Sun"}},{"k":"size_clipped_note","v":{"s":"Drawn at the panel limit — the real star is bigger than this box can show."}},{"k":"mh_pop3_label","v":{"s":"Population III (zero-metal)"}},{"k":"mh_pop3_desc","v":{"s":"The hypothetical first stars: hydrogen and helium only, and thought to be very massive. None has ever been observed."}},{"k":"mh_pop2_label","v":{"s":"Metal-poor (Population II)"}},{"k":"mh_pop2_desc","v":{"s":"Old halo and globular-cluster stars, formed before much enrichment. Long-lived and low in heavy elements."}},{"k":"mh_pop1_label","v":{"s":"Solar-metallicity (Population I)"}},{"k":"mh_pop1_desc","v":{"s":"Sun-like disk stars. Enough heavy elements for rocky planets to form."}},{"k":"mh_rich_label","v":{"s":"Metal-rich (super-solar)"}},{"k":"mh_rich_desc","v":{"s":"Young inner-disk stars born from gas that generations of earlier stars already enriched."}},{"k":"mh_check_lifetime","v":{"s":"Still on the main sequence?"}},{"k":"mh_check_lifetime_yes","v":{"s":"Yes — its hydrogen-burning lifetime is about "}},{"k":"mh_check_lifetime_yes_tail","v":{"s":" Gyr, longer than the age you set."}},{"k":"mh_check_lifetime_no","v":{"s":"No — a star this massive burns out in about "}},{"k":"mh_check_lifetime_no_tail","v":{"s":" Gyr, so at this age it would already be a remnant."}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry","v":{"s":"Does the chemistry match the era?"}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry_yes","v":{"s":"Yes — gas forming stars this long ago carried roughly this much heavy-element content (about "}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry_yes_tail","v":{"s":" Z☉)."}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry_high","v":{"s":"Unusual — that is far more enrichment than the young universe had produced by then (roughly "}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry_tail","v":{"s":" Z☉ expected)."}},{"k":"mh_check_chemistry_low","v":{"s":"Unusual — a star born this recently would normally inherit far more heavy elements (roughly "}},{"k":"mh_slider_metallicity","v":{"s":"Metallicity (Z☉)"}},{"k":"mh_slider_mass","v":{"s":"Mass (M☉)"}},{"k":"mh_slider_age","v":{"s":"Age (Gyr)"}},{"k":"mh_unit_gyr","v":{"s":" billion years"}},{"k":"mh_title","v":{"s":"Stellar metallicity discovery"}},{"k":"mh_intro","v":{"s":"A star’s heavy-element content (\"metallicity\") records the universe it was born into. Set a metallicity, a mass, and an age, then read what kind of star that describes — and whether such a star could exist."}},{"k":"mh_plausibility_title","v":{"s":"Could this star exist?"}},{"k":"mh_chart_enrichment_title","v":{"s":"Heavy elements across cosmic time"}},{"k":"mh_chart_lifetime_title","v":{"s":"Age against hydrogen-burning lifetime"}},{"k":"mh_chart_x_axis","v":{"s":"Gyr after the Big Bang (when the star formed)"}},{"k":"mh_chart_lifetime_marker","v":{"s":"burns out"}},{"k":"mh_chart_age_marker","v":{"s":"age you set"}},{"k":"mh_chart_enrichment_aria","v":{"s":"Chart of heavy-element content against cosmic time. Your star: "}},{"k":"mh_chart_enrichment_aria_at","v":{"s":"at "}},{"k":"mh_chart_enrichment_aria_tail","v":{"s":" Gyr after the Big Bang, where the model expects about "}},{"k":"mh_chart_lifetime_aria","v":{"s":"Logarithmic scale comparing the age you set, "}},{"k":"mh_chart_lifetime_aria_mid","v":{"s":" Gyr, with this mass’s main-sequence lifetime of about "}},{"k":"mh_plausibility_note","v":{"s":"A warning is not a wrong answer — real stars that break these patterns exist, and each one is a research question. Ask what could explain it."}},{"k":"mh_log_btn","v":{"s":"Log this combination"}},{"k":"mh_reset_btn","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"mh_log_caption","v":{"s":"Logged star combinations, most recent last"}},{"k":"mh_log_age_col","v":{"s":"Age (Gyr)"}},{"k":"mh_log_population_col","v":{"s":"Population"}},{"k":"mh_hypothesis_label","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis"}},{"k":"mh_hypothesis_placeholder","v":{"s":"What does metallicity tell us about a star’s history?"}},{"k":"mh_stuck_btn","v":{"s":"Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"mh_prompt1","v":{"s":"Old globular clusters have very low metallicity. What does that say about when they formed?"}},{"k":"mh_prompt2","v":{"s":"Rocky planets need heavy elements. Which population is the most planet-friendly, and why?"}},{"k":"mh_prompt3","v":{"s":"Where did the metals in a Population I star come from, if the Big Bang made almost none?"}},{"k":"mh_understood_label","v":{"s":"I understand — let me explain in my own words"}},{"k":"mh_explanation_label","v":{"s":"Your explanation"}},{"k":"mh_explanation_placeholder","v":{"s":"Explain how metallicity differed in the early universe compared with today."}},{"k":"mh_model_note","v":{"s":"This is a simplified teaching model: real enrichment histories vary by galaxy and by location within it. There is deliberately no score here — the point is the reasoning, not a right answer."}},{"k":"aria_brown_dwarf","v":{"s":"brown dwarf, below the hydrogen fusion limit"}},{"k":"aria_doppler_away","v":{"s":"moving away from you, light shifted red"}},{"k":"aria_doppler_none","v":{"s":"no measurable shift"}},{"k":"aria_doppler_toward","v":{"s":"moving toward you, light shifted blue"}},{"k":"aria_gyr_unit","v":{"s":"billion years after the Big Bang"}},{"k":"aria_solar_masses","v":{"s":"solar masses"}},{"k":"aria_stars_unit","v":{"s":"stars"}},{"k":"instrument_irregular_gravity_tracer","v":{"s":"Asymmetric mass + disturbed dark halo"}},{"k":"instrument_irregular_infrared_tracer","v":{"s":"Warm dust knots + embedded star birth"}},{"k":"instrument_irregular_radio_tracer","v":{"s":"Clumpy H I reservoir + extended gas tail"}},{"k":"instrument_irregular_visible_tracer","v":{"s":"Patchy young stars + disturbed body"}},{"k":"instrument_irregular_xray_tracer","v":{"s":"Supernova-heated gas + compact remnants"}},{"k":"laye