_view","v":{"s":"Toggle day / night view"}},{"k":"quiz_difficulty","v":{"s":"Quiz difficulty"}},{"k":"quiz_region","v":{"s":"Quiz region"}},{"k":"275_waterfalls_along_the_argentina_bra","v":{"s":"275 waterfalls along the Argentina–Brazil border"}},{"k":"about_this_far","v":{"s":"About this far, capital to capital:"}},{"k":"africa_s_highest_peak_at_5_895_m_a_fre","v":{"s":"Africa's highest peak at 5,895 m, and a free-standing volcano"}},{"k":"ai_off_use_practice_pack","v":{"s":"⚠️ AI is switched off in this build, so Generate will not work. Practice pack builds questions from the built-in country data instead."}},{"k":"antarctica_note","v":{"s":"Six buttons, not seven: Antarctica has no countries, so it is never the answer. Australia and the Pacific islands are grouped here as Oceania."}},{"k":"around_1_600_limestone_karst_islands_r","v":{"s":"Around 1,600 limestone karst islands rising straight out of the sea"}},{"k":"borobudur","v":{"s":"Borobudur"}},{"k":"chichen_itza","v":{"s":"Chichén Itzá"}},{"k":"confidence_pct","v":{"s":"Confidence (%)"}},{"k":"difficulty_title","v":{"s":"Find Country: Easy zooms to the continent, names countries on hover and shows the region; Medium shows the continent; Hard shows nothing. Capitals: Easy adds multiple choice. Points scale 5 / 10 / 15."}},{"k":"dist_hunt_intro","v":{"s":"Not a quiz — build a feel for how far \"far\" is. Slide to a distance, read which real journeys are about that long, and rate how confident you are. Log a few and compare them."}},{"k":"distance_guess_in_miles","v":{"s":"Distance guess in miles"}},{"k":"distance_in_miles","v":{"s":"Distance in miles..."}},{"k":"distance_km","v":{"s":"Distance (km)"}},{"k":"distance_sense","v":{"s":"Distance Sense"}},{"k":"distance_sense_calibrate_your_gut","v":{"s":"Distance Sense — calibrate your gut"}},{"k":"distance_sense_heading","v":{"s":"🎚️ Distance sense"}},{"k":"distance_unit","v":{"s":"Distance unit"}},{"k":"earth_s_highest_peak_at_29_032_feet_on","v":{"s":"Earth's highest peak at 29,032 feet, on the Nepal–China border"}},{"k":"easy_hints","v":{"s":"Easy — hints"}},{"k":"eleven_medieval_churches_carved_downwa","v":{"s":"Eleven medieval churches carved downward into solid rock"}},{"k":"estimate_capital_to_capital","v":{"s":"Estimate the straight-line distance from one capital city to the other (within 15% to score!)"}},{"k":"far_side_desc","v":{"s":"Most of the way round the planet. Nothing can be farther than 20,037 km — half of Earth’s 40,075 km circumference."}},{"k":"far_side_over_12000_km","v":{"s":"🌍 Far side of the world (over 12,000 km)"}},{"k":"flat_topped_sandstone_massif_above_cap","v":{"s":"Flat-topped sandstone massif above Cape Town, about 1,085 m high"}},{"k":"geography_activities","v":{"s":"Geography activities"}},{"k":"geography_explorer","v":{"s":"Geography Explorer"}},{"k":"globe_aria","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D globe of Earth with country outlines. Drag to rotate, scroll to zoom, click a country for its details below."}},{"k":"go","v":{"s":"Go"}},{"k":"great_mosque_of_djenne","v":{"s":"Great Mosque of Djenné"}},{"k":"ha_long_bay","v":{"s":"Ha Long Bay"}},{"k":"hard_no_hints","v":{"s":"Hard — no hints"}},{"k":"hero_capitals","v":{"s":"Spelling is forgiving: accents and punctuation are ignored. Some countries split their capital — South Africa has three (Pretoria, Cape Town, Bloemfontein), Bolivia two (Sucre, La Paz), and the Netherlands governs from The Hague while Amsterdam is the constitutional capital. Those alternates are accepted."}},{"k":"hero_continents","v":{"s":"The 7-continent model (US/UK): Africa, Antarctica, Asia, Australia, Europe, North America, South America. Many countries teach 6 (one America) or 5 (Eurasia). Here Australia and the Pacific islands are grouped as Oceania, and Antarctica has no countries. Russia and Turkey straddle two continents."}},{"k":"hero_dist_hunt","v":{"s":"No score here. Slide to a distance, see which real capital-to-capital journeys are about that far, and note how confident you feel. The farthest two places on Earth can be is 20,037 km — half the circumference."}},{"k":"hero_distance","v":{"s":"Capital to capital along the great circle (the haversine formula). New York to Tokyo is about 10,850 km over the Arctic — shorter than the flat-map straight line. Anchors: London–Paris 340 km, New York–Los Angeles 3,940 km, London–Sydney 17,000 km."}},{"k":"hero_find_country","v":{"s":"Click the country on the map. Easy zooms to its continent and names countries on hover; Hard gives no hints. The map is a Mercator projection, which stretches land near the poles — Greenland looks as big as Africa but is 14× smaller."}},{"k":"hero_globe","v":{"s":"Earth is an oblate spheroid — about 43 km wider across the equator than from pole to pole. The shortest path between two cities is a great-circle arc, not a straight line on a flat map, which is why flights bend over the Arctic."}},{"k":"hero_landmarks","v":{"s":"Eiffel Tower (Paris), Christ the Redeemer (Rio), Sydney Opera House, Petra (Jordan), Angkor Wat (Cambodia). A vivid place gives memory a second handle on the country (dual coding). Some landmarks sit on a border — either country is accepted."}},{"k":"hero_quiz_builder","v":{"s":"Describe a topic and get 10 short-answer questions pitched at your grade. Retrieval practice — pulling an answer from memory — beats re-reading for long-term recall (Karpicke & Roediger, 2008)."}},{"k":"hypothesis","v":{"s":"Hypothesis"}},{"k":"i_understand_explain","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in my own words"}},{"k":"iguazu_falls","v":{"s":"Iguazú Falls"}},{"k":"jump_to_a_country","v":{"s":"Jump to a country…"}},{"k":"jump_to_a_country_on_the_globe","v":{"s":"Jump to a country on the globe"}},{"k":"keyboard_mode","v":{"s":"⌨️ Keyboard mode"}},{"k":"keyboard_mode_help","v":{"s":"Click the map once to focus it · arrow keys move · + and − zoom · Enter answers with the country under the crosshair"}},{"k":"keyboard_mode_on","v":{"s":"⌨️ Keyboard mode: on"}},{"k":"keyboard_mode_title","v":{"s":"Answer without a mouse: focus the map, arrow keys move the crosshair, + and - zoom, Enter answers."}},{"k":"lalibela","v":{"s":"Lalibela"}},{"k":"largest_hot_desert_3_6_million_sq_mile_","v":{"s":"Largest hot desert, 3.6 million sq miles across about ten countries"}},{"k":"largest_river_by_water_flow_rivals_the","v":{"s":"Largest river by water flow; rivals the Nile for longest (the measurement is disputed)"}},{"k":"loading_country_outlines","v":{"s":"⏳ Loading country outlines…"}},{"k":"loading_map","v":{"s":"⏳ Loading map…"}},{"k":"log_this","v":{"s":"📋 Log this"}},{"k":"long_haul_5000_12000_km_2","v":{"s":"🟠 Long-haul (5,000–12,000 km)"}},{"k":"long_haul_desc","v":{"s":"Across an ocean, e.g. San Francisco to Tokyo — an 8–14 hour flight."}},{"k":"map_aria","v":{"s":"World map. Click the country named above, or use the Show me button if you cannot use the map."}},{"k":"maya_city_its_step_pyramid_casts_a_ser","v":{"s":"Maya city; at the equinoxes its step pyramid casts a serpent-shaped shadow"}},{"k":"medium_1000_5000_km_2","v":{"s":"🟡 Medium (1,000–5,000 km)"}},{"k":"medium_continent_only","v":{"s":"Medium — continent only"}},{"k":"medium_desc","v":{"s":"Across a continent, e.g. New York to Los Angeles — a 2–6 hour flight."}},{"k":"mount_kilimanjaro","v":{"s":"Mount Kilimanjaro"}},{"k":"no_hints_on_hard","v":{"s":"No hints on Hard"}},{"k":"outlines_failed","v":{"s":"⚠️ Country outlines could not be downloaded (offline?). The map cannot be clicked until they load."}},{"k":"over_a_million_wildebeest_migrate_acro","v":{"s":"Over a million wildebeest migrate across it every year"}},{"k":"practice_pack","v":{"s":"📚 Practice pack"}},{"k":"practice_pack_title","v":{"s":"Build 10 questions from the built-in country data. No AI, no internet."}},{"k":"prompt_antipode","v":{"s":"The antipode of a place is the point exactly opposite it, straight through the centre of Earth. Where is the antipode of your school?"}},{"k":"prompt_circumference","v":{"s":"Earth’s circumference is 40,075 km. If you kept going past the far side, would the distance keep growing?"}},{"k":"prompt_rhumb","v":{"s":"A great circle is the shortest path on a sphere; a rhumb line keeps one compass heading. Which do pilots fly, and why?"}},{"k":"reset_2","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"reset_find_title","v":{"s":"Clear the found list, score and streak"}},{"k":"retry","v":{"s":"Retry"}},{"k":"reveal_capital_title","v":{"s":"Show the answer (no points; the country goes to your review list)"}},{"k":"reveal_title","v":{"s":"Show where it is (no points; the country goes to your review list)"}},{"k":"sandstone_inselberg_sacred_to_the_anan","v":{"s":"Sandstone inselberg sacred to the Anangu; climbing it closed in 2019"}},{"k":"serengeti","v":{"s":"Serengeti"}},{"k":"short_hop_desc","v":{"s":"Same continent or neighboring countries — a few hours by train, or a short flight."}},{"k":"short_hop_under_1000_km","v":{"s":"🟢 Short hop (under 1,000 km)"}},{"k":"show","v":{"s":"👁️ Show"}},{"k":"show_me","v":{"s":"👁️ Show me"}},{"k":"squares_to_scale","v":{"s":"The two squares are drawn to one scale, so what you see is the real area ratio."}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"table_mountain","v":{"s":"Table Mountain"}},{"k":"tap_one_to_study_it_now","v":{"s":"tap one to study it now"}},{"k":"the_largest_mud_brick_building_in_the_","v":{"s":"The largest mud-brick building in the world, re-plastered by the whole town each year"}},{"k":"the_world_s_largest_buddhist_temple_bu","v":{"s":"The world's largest Buddhist temple, built in the 9th century"}},{"k":"to_review","v":{"s":"To review"}},{"k":"uluru","v":{"s":"Uluru"}},{"k":"world_s_largest_sheet_of_falling_water_","v":{"s":"World's largest sheet of falling water, on the Zambia–Zimbabwe border"}},{"k":"your_explanation","v":{"s":"Your explanation"}}],"id":533}},{"k":"geology","v":{"o":[{"k":"fp_on","v":{"s":"First-person mode on. W A S D or arrow keys to fly, Q and E for up and down, I J K L or drag to look, Escape to exit."}},{"k":"pick_hint","v":{"s":"Pick a material — in the 3D model or the list below — to see its scene-specific position, scale, conditions, formation, and age relationship."}},{"k":"depth","v":{"s":"Depth"}},{"k":"temp","v":{"s":"Temp"}},{"k":"pressure","v":{"s":"Pressure"}},{"k":"state","v":{"s":"State"}},{"k":"forms","v":{"s":"Forms by"}},{"k":"minerals","v":{"s":"Minerals"}},{"k":"dig_fossil","v":{"s":"Dig or click this layer to uncover one."}},{"k":"no_fossils","v":{"s":"No fossils — melting and metamorphism destroy them, so geologists read time from sedimentary layers."}},{"k":"comparing","v":{"s":"Pinned to compare ✓"}},{"k":"compare","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"compare_title","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"clear","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"dating_title","v":{"s":"Radiometric clock"}},{"k":"parent_left","v":{"s":"Parent isotope still left (drag to let it decay):"}},{"k":"ma","v":{"s":"million years"}},{"k":"halflives","v":{"s":"half-lives"}},{"k":"dating_method","v":{"s":"Measure the real parent:daughter ratio → that fixes how many half-lives passed → × the half-life gives the age."}},{"k":"cycle_title","v":{"s":"Rock cycle — what happens next?"}},{"k":"cycle_hint","v":{"s":"Apply a real process and follow this rock around the cycle. (It traces the rock — it doesn’t change the ground.)"}},{"k":"cycle_path","v":{"s":"Your path: "}},{"k":"prev_step","v":{"s":"Previous step"}},{"k":"next_step","v":{"s":"Next step"}},{"k":"to_present","v":{"s":"Skip to present — show the whole cross-section"}},{"k":"present","v":{"s":"Present"}},{"k":"erupting","v":{"s":"Eruption in progress"}},{"k":"fossils_found","v":{"s":"Fossils uncovered"}},{"k":"core_read","v":{"s":"A core reads top → bottom: youngest at the surface, oldest at depth."}},{"k":"quiz_title","v":{"s":"Test yourself — relative dating"}},{"k":"quiz_hide","v":{"s":"Hide"}},{"k":"quiz_start","v":{"s":"Start"}},{"k":"quiz_next","v":{"s":"Next question →"}},{"k":"no3d_title","v":{"s":"3D view unavailable"}},{"k":"no3d_body","v":{"s":"Your device could not start WebGL. Use the cross-section and rock list — they have the full content."}},{"k":"loading3d","v":{"s":"Loading the 3D engine…"}},{"k":"fp_exit","v":{"s":"Exit"}},{"k":"fp_enter","v":{"s":"Drop in"}},{"k":"exit_fullscreen","v":{"s":"Exit fullscreen"}},{"k":"fullscreen","v":{"s":"Fullscreen"}},{"k":"detail","v":{"s":"Voxel detail level"}},{"k":"detail_tip","v":{"s":"Higher detail = smaller, sharper voxels (heavier on weak devices)"}},{"k":"detail_low","v":{"s":"Detail: Low"}},{"k":"detail_std","v":{"s":"Detail: Standard"}},{"k":"detail_high","v":{"s":"Detail: High"}},{"k":"fp_keys","v":{"s":"WASD / arrows fly · Q E up·down · IJKL / drag look · Esc exit"}},{"k":"title","v":{"s":"Geology Explorer"}},{"k":"subtitle","v":{"s":"Dig a cross-section of the crust. Identify rocks, read the layers, find the pluton that cuts them."}},{"k":"scene","v":{"s":"Scene"}},{"k":"slice","v":{"s":"Slice"}},{"k":"excavate","v":{"s":"Excavate"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"play_history_tip","v":{"s":"Watch the cross-section build in the order it formed"}},{"k":"stop","v":{"s":"Stop"}},{"k":"play_history","v":{"s":"Play history"}},{"k":"water_tip","v":{"s":"Show the water table and which layers hold groundwater"}},{"k":"water","v":{"s":"Water table"}},{"k":"erupt_tip","v":{"s":"Watch a volcano erupt — magma reaches the surface and cools fast into basalt"}},{"k":"erupting_short","v":{"s":"Erupting…"}},{"k":"erupt","v":{"s":"Erupt"}},{"k":"tip","v":{"s":"Drag to orbit · click a block to identify"}},{"k":"teach","v":{"s":"Deeper sedimentary layers are older (superposition). The granite pluton is YOUNGER than the layers it cuts (cross-cutting), and it bakes a metamorphic rim (contact metamorphism). Heat + pressure rise with depth toward the magma — where the rock cycle restarts."}},{"k":"rocks","v":{"s":"Rock types"}},{"k":"layers","v":{"s":"Layers"}},{"k":"core_title","v":{"s":"Drill a core sample"}},{"k":"dating_simulation","v":{"s":"Practice simulation: adjust the isotope ratio to see how an age calculation works. This does not date the voxel automatically."}},{"k":"lesson_label","v":{"s":"Teacher / lesson guide"}},{"k":"lesson_title","v":{"s":"Geology Explorer lesson guide"}},{"k":"lesson_objective","v":{"s":"Current objective: "}},{"k":"lesson_goals","v":{"s":"Learning goals"}},{"k":"lesson_prompts","v":{"s":"Teacher prompts"}},{"k":"lesson_ready","v":{"s":"The scene checks are complete. Invite the student to submit the CER explanation."}},{"k":"lesson_next","v":{"s":"Next teacher move: let the student explore until the three scene checks are complete."}},{"k":"orientation_title","v":{"s":"How to read this model"}},{"k":"orientation_scale","v":{"s":"Scale"}},{"k":"orientation_direction","v":{"s":"Direction"}},{"k":"schematic_note","v":{"s":"Schematic model - not to scale. Colors are illustrative."}},{"k":"hint_label","v":{"s":"Stuck?"}},{"k":"hide_hint","v":{"s":"Hide hint"}},{"k":"show_hint","v":{"s":"Show hint"}},{"k":"rubric_title","v":{"s":"CER rubric"}},{"k":"rubric_ready","v":{"s":"Ready to submit for teacher review."}},{"k":"rubric_next","v":{"s":"Use the feedback above to strengthen the explanation."}},{"k":"fullscreen_title","v":{"s":"Fullscreen geology explorer"}},{"k":"next_move","v":{"s":"Recommended next move"}},{"k":"metric","v":{"o":[{"k":"world","v":{"s":"World"}},{"k":"materials","v":{"s":"Materials"}},{"k":"evidence","v":{"s":"Evidence"}}],"id":535}},{"k":"mode_group","v":{"s":"Investigation mode"}},{"k":"lesson_group","v":{"s":"Lesson tools"}},{"k":"lesson_hide","v":{"s":"Hide lesson guide"}},{"k":"lesson_open","v":{"s":"Lesson guide"}},{"k":"context_summary","v":{"s":"Read the science context"}},{"k":"cutaway","v":{"s":"Cutaway"}},{"k":"fp_reset_and_enter","v":{"s":"Reset and enter"}}],"id":534}},{"k":"geometryworld","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_lesson_progress_of_questions_complete","v":{"s":"Lesson progress: {value1} of {value2} questio