molecular_geometry","v":{"s":"🔺 VSEPR — molecular geometry"}},{"k":"valence_shell_electron_pair_repulsion_","v":{"s":"Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion theory predicts molecular shape from steric number (bonded + lone pairs around central atom). Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonded pairs, so they push bonds closer."}},{"k":"bond_types_electronegativity_differenc","v":{"s":"⚛︎ Bond types — electronegativity difference"}},{"k":"bond_character_ranges_from_purely_cova","v":{"s":"Bond character ranges from purely covalent (equal sharing) to ionic (full electron transfer). The boundary is fuzzy — most real bonds have partial ionic character."}},{"k":"intermolecular_forces_imf","v":{"s":"↔ Intermolecular forces (IMF)"}},{"k":"forces_between_molecules_determine_mel","v":{"s":"Forces BETWEEN molecules determine melting/boiling points, viscosity, solubility, and surface tension. Stronger IMF = higher BP."}},{"k":"present_in","v":{"s":"Present in: "}},{"k":"tip","v":{"s":"💡 Tip: "}},{"k":"for_polar_molecules_all_applicable_imf","v":{"s":"For polar molecules, ALL applicable IMFs add up. Water has dispersion + dipole + H-bonding — that's why its BP is so high (100°C) compared to similar-mass H₂S (−60°C)."}},{"k":"reaction_types_2","v":{"s":"🔄 Reaction types"}},{"k":"reactions_fall_into_recognizable_patte","v":{"s":"Reactions fall into recognizable patterns. Knowing the type helps predict products + balance equations."}},{"k":"common_molecule_library","v":{"s":"📚 Common molecule library"}},{"k":"search_formula_name_use","v":{"s":"Search formula / name / use..."}},{"k":"no_molecules_match","v":{"s":"No molecules match \""}},{"k":"acid_base_reference","v":{"s":"⚖ Acid / base reference"}},{"k":"strength_how_completely_an_acid_base_d","v":{"s":"Strength = how completely an acid/base dissociates in water. Strong = ~100% (Ka >> 1). Weak = partial (Ka < 1). pH = −log[H⁺]."}},{"k":"name","v":{"s":"Name"}},{"k":"formula","v":{"s":"Formula"}},{"k":"strength","v":{"s":"Strength"}},{"k":"notes","v":{"s":"Notes"}},{"k":"ph_scale_2","v":{"s":"💡 pH scale: "}},{"k":"ph_0_6_acidic_ph_7_neutral_ph_8_14_bas","v":{"s":"pH 0-6 acidic · pH 7 neutral · pH 8-14 basic. Each unit = 10× change in [H⁺]. Stomach acid pH 1.5, blood pH 7.4, bleach pH 12."}},{"k":"quantum_numbers_orbital_identity","v":{"s":"⚛︎ Quantum numbers — orbital identity"}},{"k":"each_electron_in_an_atom_is_described_","v":{"s":"Each electron in an atom is described by 4 quantum numbers (n, ℓ, mₗ, mₛ). The Pauli exclusion principle: no two electrons in an atom share all 4."}},{"k":"more_molecules_more_collisions_time_de","v":{"s":"More molecules = more collisions / time. Determined experimentally, not from coefficients."}},{"k":"higher_t_more_molecules_with_energy_ea","v":{"s":"Higher T → more molecules with energy ≥ Ea (Maxwell-Boltzmann tail)."}},{"k":"grinding_solid_reactant_exposes_more_a","v":{"s":"Grinding solid reactant exposes more atoms to collision. Powder vs chunk."}},{"k":"provides_alternate_pathway_not_consume","v":{"s":"Provides alternate pathway. Not consumed. Enzymes are catalysts (often >10⁶× faster)."}},{"k":"same_as_concentration_more_molecules_p","v":{"s":"Same as concentration: more molecules per unit volume."}},{"k":"like_dissolves_like_solvent_can_stabil","v":{"s":"\"Like dissolves like.\" Solvent can stabilize transition state."}},{"k":"some_rxns_need_photons_photosynthesis_","v":{"s":"Some rxns need photons (photosynthesis, photographic film, UV-driven)."}},{"k":"enthalpy_change","v":{"s":"Enthalpy change"}},{"k":"entropy_change","v":{"s":"Entropy change"}},{"k":"gibbs_free_energy","v":{"s":"Gibbs free energy"}},{"k":"temperature","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"activation_energy","v":{"s":"Activation energy"}},{"k":"equilibrium_constant","v":{"s":"Equilibrium constant"}},{"k":"polyethylene_pe","v":{"s":"Polyethylene (PE)"}},{"k":"polypropylene_pp","v":{"s":"Polypropylene (PP)"}},{"k":"polystyrene_ps","v":{"s":"Polystyrene (PS)"}},{"k":"kevlar","v":{"s":"Kevlar"}},{"k":"polylactic_acid_pla","v":{"s":"Polylactic acid (PLA)"}},{"k":"silicone","v":{"s":"Silicone"}},{"k":"rubber_natural","v":{"s":"Rubber (natural)"}},{"k":"proteins","v":{"s":"Proteins"}},{"k":"dna_rna","v":{"s":"DNA / RNA"}},{"k":"polysaccharides","v":{"s":"Polysaccharides"}},{"k":"lipids_fats","v":{"s":"Lipids (fats)"}},{"k":"periodic_trends_2","v":{"s":"📊 Periodic trends"}},{"k":"patterns_in_element_properties_that_fo","v":{"s":"Patterns in element properties that follow position on the periodic table. Driven by effective nuclear charge (Zeff) — the net pull on outermost electrons after inner electrons shield them."}},{"k":"across_period","v":{"s":"Across period: "}},{"k":"down_group","v":{"s":"Down group: "}},{"k":"diagonal_relationship","v":{"s":"💡 Diagonal relationship: "}},{"k":"li_mg_be_al_b_si_pairs_across_a_diagon","v":{"s":"Li-Mg, Be-Al, B-Si — pairs across a diagonal share similar properties because the increases in size + charge offset."}},{"k":"molarity_dilution_calculator","v":{"s":"🧮 Molarity + dilution calculator"}},{"k":"molarity_m_moles_of_solute_per_liter_o","v":{"s":"Molarity (M) = moles of solute per liter of solution. To prepare a target molarity: weigh out (M × V × MW) grams of solute, dissolve in less than the final volume, then dilute to the mark."}},{"k":"molarity_m_mol_l","v":{"s":"Molarity (M, mol/L)"}},{"k":"volume_l","v":{"s":"Volume (L)"}},{"k":"molecular_weight_g_mol","v":{"s":"Molecular weight (g/mol)"}},{"k":"58_44_nacl","v":{"s":"58.44 (NaCl)"}},{"k":"grams_of_solute_needed","v":{"s":"Grams of solute needed"}},{"k":"m_v_m_v_solve_for_whichever_is_unknown","v":{"s":"M₁V₁ = M₂V₂. Solve for whichever is unknown."}},{"k":"serial_dilution","v":{"s":"Serial dilution: "}},{"k":"for_very_low_concentrations_dilute_1_1","v":{"s":"For very low concentrations, dilute 1:10 (or 1:100) repeatedly. Each step is precise; cumulative error stays small."}},{"k":"watch_out","v":{"s":"Watch out: "}},{"k":"add_half_the_water_first_then_add_solu","v":{"s":"Add ~half the water FIRST, then add solute + stir until dissolved, THEN top up to the mark. Adding solute to full-volume water often gives wrong final volume due to volume changes during dissolution."}},{"k":"stoichiometry_recipe_math","v":{"s":"⚖ Stoichiometry — recipe math"}},{"k":"balanced_equation_mole_ratio_like_a_co","v":{"s":"Balanced equation → mole ratio. Like a cooking recipe, but for atoms. Coefficients tell you the ratio of reactant moles to product moles."}},{"k":"atoms_in_atoms_out_on_each_side_balanc","v":{"s":"Atoms in = atoms out, on each side. Balance metals first, then non-O/H atoms, then O and H last. Charge balanced for ionic equations."}},{"k":"from_coefficients_2_h_1_o_2_h_o_means_","v":{"s":"From coefficients: 2 H₂ + 1 O₂ → 2 H₂O means 2 mol H₂ : 1 mol O₂ : 2 mol H₂O."}},{"k":"mol_g_molar_mass_always_work_in_moles_","v":{"s":"mol = g / molar mass. Always work in moles when comparing across the equation."}},{"k":"for_each_reactant_moles_available_coef","v":{"s":"For each reactant: (moles available) / (coefficient). Smallest result = limiting reactant."}},{"k":"use_limiting_reactant_s_moles_product_","v":{"s":"Use limiting reactant's moles × (product coefficient / limiting coefficient) × product MW."}},{"k":"actual_theoretical_100_real_reactions_","v":{"s":"Actual / Theoretical × 100. Real reactions rarely hit 100% — losses to side reactions, incomplete rxn, transfer losses."}},{"k":"worked_example","v":{"s":"🔢 Worked example"}},{"k":"n_3_h_2_nh","v":{"s":"N₂ + 3 H₂ → 2 NH₃"}},{"k":"28_g_n_6_g_h_g_nh","v":{"s":"28 g N₂ + 6 g H₂ → ? g NH₃"}},{"k":"1_00_mol_n_3_00_mol_h","v":{"s":"1.00 mol N₂ + 3.00 mol H₂"}},{"k":"ratio_n_1_1_00_h_3_1_00_tie_neither_li","v":{"s":"Ratio: N₂/1 = 1.00; H₂/3 = 1.00 → tie, neither limits"}},{"k":"2_00_mol_nh_17_03_g_mol_34_06_g_theore","v":{"s":"→ 2.00 mol NH₃ × 17.03 g/mol = 34.06 g (theoretical)"}},{"k":"phases_of_matter_phase_diagrams","v":{"s":"🧊 Phases of matter + phase diagrams"}},{"k":"a_phase_diagram_plots_phase_boundaries","v":{"s":"A phase diagram plots phase boundaries on a P (pressure) vs T (temperature) graph. Lines = phase transitions. Triple point: all three phases coexist. Critical point: liquid + gas become indistinguishable (supercritical fluid)."}},{"k":"water_is_unusual","v":{"s":"💧 Water is unusual: "}},{"k":"ice_is_less_dense_than_liquid_water_ic","v":{"s":"Ice is LESS dense than liquid water (ice floats). Most substances: solid denser than liquid. Hydrogen bonding gives ice its open crystal structure."}},{"k":"sublimation","v":{"s":"🌬 Sublimation: "}},{"k":"solid_gas_without_going_through_liquid","v":{"s":"Solid → gas without going through liquid (CO₂ dry ice; iodine at room temp). Reverse: deposition."}},{"k":"chemical_equilibrium_le_chatelier","v":{"s":"⇌ Chemical equilibrium + Le Chatelier"}},{"k":"reversible_reactions_reach_dynamic_equ","v":{"s":"Reversible reactions reach dynamic equilibrium when forward rate = reverse rate. Concentrations stop changing (but reactions keep going both ways). Keq = product of [products]^coefficients / product of [reactants]^coefficients."}},{"k":"le_chatelier_s_principle","v":{"s":"Le Chatelier's principle"}},{"k":"if_a_stress_is_applied_to_a_system_at_","v":{"s":"If a stress is applied to a system at equilibrium, the system shifts to relieve that stress. Predict the direction of shift to make sense of how rxns respond to changes."}},{"k":"reaction_kinetics_what_controls_speed","v":{"s":"⏱ Reaction kinetics — what controls speed"}},{"k":"kinetics_how_fast_thermodynamics_wheth","v":{"s":"Kinetics = how fast. Thermodynamics = whether possible. A reaction can be very favorable (large negative ΔG) but slow (high Ea). Catalysts and conditions tune kinetics."}},{"k":"arrhenius_equation","v":{"s":"📐 Arrhenius equation"}},{"k":"k_a_exp_ea_rt","v":{"s":"k = A · exp(−Ea / RT)"}},{"k":"k_rate_constant_a_collision_frequency_","v":{"s":"k = rate constant · A = collision frequency factor · Ea = activation energy · R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) · T = absolute temperature (K). Plot ln(k) vs 1/T; slope = −Ea/R."}},{"k":"thermodynamics_what_s_spontaneous","v":{"s":"🔥 Thermodynamics — what's spontaneous"}},{"k":"spontaneity_whether_a_reaction_proceed","v":{"s":"Spontaneity = whether a reaction proceeds on its own (regardless of speed). Gibbs free energy ΔG tells us: negative = spontaneous; positive = not; zero = at equilibrium."}},{"k":"master_equation","v":{"s":"Master equation"}},{"k":"g_h_t_s","v":{"s":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS"}},{"k":"exothermic_h_0","v":{"s":"🔥 Exothermic (ΔH < 0): "}},{"k":"combustion_neutralization_condensation","v":{"s":"Combustion, neutralization, condensation. Heat released to surroundings."}},{"k":"endothermic_h_0","v":{"s":"🧊 Endothermic (ΔH > 0): "}},{"k":"photosynthesis_melting_evaporation_col","v":{"s":"Photosynthesis, melting, evaporation, cold packs. Heat absorbed from surroundings."}},{"k":"polymers_biopolymers","v":{"s":"🧬 Polymers + biopolymers"}},{"k":"long_chains_of_repeating_units_monomer","v":{"s":"Long chains of repeating units (monomers). Addition polymers form by C=C double bonds opening up. Condensation polymers form by losing water (or other small molecule) at each link."}},{"k":"synthetic_polymers","v":{"s":"Synthetic polymers"}},{"k":"biopolymers_biological_macromolecules","v":{"s":"Biopolymers (biological macromolecules)"}},{"k":"lab_safety_basics","v":{"s":"🦺 Lab safety basics"}},{"k":"most_lab_accidents_are_preventable_the","v":{"s":"Most lab accidents are preventable. The biggest factors: PPE, attention, and not mixing things that shouldn't mix. Always read the SDS (Safety Data Sheet) for each chemical before using it."}},{"k":"ionic_2","v":{"s":"Ionic"}},{"k":"covalent_polar_h_bonded","v":{"s":"Covalent (polar / H-bonded)"}},{"k":"covalent_nonpolar_molecular","v":{"s":"Covalent (nonpolar molecular)"}},{"k":"metallic_2","v":{"s":"Metallic"}},{"k":"network_covalent","v":{"s":"Network covalent"}},{"k":"bond_detective_2","v":{"s":"🔬 Bond detective"}},{"k":"three_unknown_compounds_study_the_data","v":{"s":"Three unknown compounds. Study the data, pick the most likely bond type, AND check which pieces of evidence convinced you. The \"Reveal\" button only enables once you've committed to both — no peeking, no hedging."}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_bond_type","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (bond type):"}},{"k":"which_evidence_convinced_you_at_least_","v":{"s":"Which evidence convinced you? (at least 1)"}},{"k":"the_reveal_is_locked_behind_your_commi","v":{"s":"The reveal is locked behind your commitment — it's the prediction, not the answer, that builds intuition."}},{"k":"white_crystalline_solid_held_together_","v":{"s":"White crystalline solid. Held together by strong + / − ion attractions."}},{"k":"water_s_permanent_dipoles_surround_na_","v":{"s":"Water’s permanent dipoles surround Na+ and Cl- ions (ion–dipole forces strong enough to break the ionic lattice)."}},{"k":"purple_black_solid_each_i_molecule_hel","v":{"s":"Purple-black solid. Each I₂ molecule held loosely by London dispersion only."}},{"k":"i_is_nonpolar_only_london_dispersion_a","v":{"s":"I₂ is nonpolar; only London dispersion attractions stabilize it in solution. Hexane is also nonpolar — like-dissolves-like via dispersion."}},{"k":"many_oh_groups_on_a_covalent_ring_pola","v":{"s":"Many –OH groups on a covalent ring. Polar, but not ionic."}},{"k":"sucrose_dissolves_because_its_oh_group","v":{"s":"Sucrose dissolves because its –OH groups form hydrogen bonds with water. Not ionic, so the mechanism is H-bonding, not ion–dipole."}},{"k":"water_h_o","v":{"s":"Water (H₂O)"}},{"k":"strongly_polar_h_bond_donor_acceptor","v":{"s":"strongly polar; H-bond donor + acceptor"}},{"k":"hexane_c_h","v":{"s":"Hexane (C₆H₁₄)"}},{"k":"nonpolar_london_dispersion_only","v":{"s":"nonpolar; London dispersion only"}},{"k":"ethanol_c_h_oh","v":{"s":"Ethanol (C₂H₅OH)"}},{"k":"amphiphilic_polar_oh_end_nonpolar_tail","v":{"s":"amphiphilic — polar OH end, nonpolar tail"}},{"k":"pick_a_solvent_to_see_its_polarity_dia","v":{"s":"(pick a solvent to see its polarity diagram)"}},{"k":"predicted_imf_match_strength","v":{"s":"Predicted IMF match strength:"}},{"k":"mystery_solvent_2","v":{"s":"🧪 Mystery solvent"}},{"k":"three_unknown_solutes_for_each_1_pick_","v":{"s":"Three unknown solutes. For each: (1) pick the best solvent, (2) rank the chemistry reasoning that justifies your pick, (3) live diagrams update with each click, and (4) wrong answers walk you through the polarity logic step by step — no answer dump."}},{"k":"1_pick_a_solvent","v":{"s":"1. Pick a solvent:"}},{"k":"2_rank_the_reasoning_click_in_priority","v":{"s":"2. Rank the reasoning (click in priority order, top → bottom; need ≥2):"}},{"k":"let_s_walk_through_it_step_by_step_no_","v":{"s":"Let’s walk through it step by step (no answer dump):"}},{"k":"is_the_solvent_you_picked_polar_or_non","v":{"s":"Is the solvent you picked polar or nonpolar?"}},{"k":"like_dissolves_like_do_polarities_matc","v":{"s":"\"Like dissolves like.\" Do polarities match?"}},{"k":"why_this_works","v":{"s":"Why this works: "}},{"k":"1_for_correct_solvent_1_if_reasoning_o","v":{"s":"(+1 for correct solvent, +1 if reasoning order matches expert). Live diagrams + Socratic walkthrough are the point — not the score."}},{"k":"chemistry_glossary","v":{"s":"📖 Chemistry glossary"}},{"k":"common_chemistry_terms_students_mix_up","v":{"s":"Common chemistry terms students mix up. Bookmarkable reference for vocabulary."}},{"k":"boyle_s_law","v":{"s":"Boyle's Law"}},{"k":"charles_s_law","v":{"s":"Charles's Law"}},{"k":"gay_lussac_s_law","v":{"s":"Gay-Lussac's Law"}},{"k":"avogadro_s_law","v":{"s":"Avogadro's Law"}},{"k":"combined_gas_law","v":{"s":"Combined Gas Law"}},{"k":"ideal_gas_law","v":{"s":"Ideal Gas Law"}},{"k":"dalton_s_law_of_partial_pressures","v":{"s":"Dalton's Law of Partial Pressures"}},{"k":"graham_s_law_of_effusion","v":{"s":"Graham's Law of Effusion"}},{"k":"henry_s_law","v":{"s":"Henry's Law"}},{"k":"van_der_waals_equation","v":{"s":"Van der Waals equation"}},{"k":"alkane","v":{"s":"Alkane"}},{"k":"alkene","v":{"s":"Alkene"}},{"k":"alkyne","v":{"s":"Alkyne"}},{"k":"alcohol","v":{"s":"Alcohol"}},{"k":"ether","v":{"s":"Ether"}},{"k":"aldehyde","v":{"s":"Aldehyde"}},{"k":"ketone","v":{"s":"Ketone"}},{"k":"carboxylic_acid","v":{"s":"Carboxylic acid"}},{"k":"ester","v":{"s":"Ester"}},{"k":"amine","v":{"s":"Amine"}},{"k":"amide","v":{"s":"Amide"}},{"k":"nitrile","v":{"s":"Nitrile"}},{"k":"thiol_mercaptan","v":{"s":"Thiol (mercaptan)"}},{"k":"aromatic_arene","v":{"s":"Aromatic (arene)"}},{"k":"uv_vis_spectroscopy","v":{"s":"UV-Vis spectroscopy"}},{"k":"ir_infrared_spectroscopy","v":{"s":"IR (infrared) spectroscopy"}},{"k":"h_nmr_proton_nmr","v":{"s":"¹H NMR (proton NMR)"}},{"k":"c_nmr","v":{"s":"¹³C NMR"}},{"k":"mass_spectrometry_ms","v":{"s":"Mass spectrometry (MS)"}},{"k":"x_ray_crystallography","v":{"s":"X-ray crystallography"}},{"k":"raman_spectroscopy","v":{"s":"Raman spectroscopy"}},{"k":"atomic_absorption_aas","v":{"s":"Atomic absorption (AAS)"}},{"k":"fluorescence_spectroscopy","v":{"s":"Fluorescence spectroscopy"}},{"k":"epr_electron_paramagnetic_resonance","v":{"s":"EPR (electron paramagnetic resonance)"}},{"k":"simple_cubic","v":{"s":"Simple cubic"}},{"k":"body_centered_cubic_bcc","v":{"s":"Body-centered cubic (BCC)"}},{"k":"face_centered_cubic_fcc_cubic_close_pa","v":{"s":"Face-centered cubic (FCC) / Cubic close-packed"}},{"k":"hexagonal_close_packed_hcp","v":{"s":"Hexagonal close-packed (HCP)"}},{"k":"diamond_cubic","v":{"s":"Diamond cubic"}},{"k":"sodium_chloride_rock_salt","v":{"s":"Sodium chloride (rock salt)"}},{"k":"cesium_chloride","v":{"s":"Cesium chloride"}},{"k":"zinc_blende_sphalerite","v":{"s":"Zinc blende (sphalerite)"}},{"k":"wurtzite","v":{"s":"Wurtzite"}},{"k":"fluorite","v":{"s":"Fluorite"}},{"k":"perovskite","v":{"s":"Perovskite"}},{"k":"graphite","v":{"s":"Graphite"}},{"k":"troposphere","v":{"s":"Troposphere"}},{"k":"stratosphere","v":{"s":"Stratosphere"}},{"k":"mesosphere","v":{"s":"Mesosphere"}},{"k":"thermosphere","v":{"s":"Thermosphere"}},{"k":"exosphere","v":{"s":"Exosphere"}},{"k":"alpha_decay","v":{"s":"Alpha decay (α)"}},{"k":"beta_minus_decay","v":{"s":"Beta-minus decay (β⁻)"}},{"k":"beta_plus_decay","v":{"s":"Beta-plus decay (β⁺)"}},{"k":"electron_capture","v":{"s":"Electron capture"}},{"k":"gamma_decay","v":{"s":"Gamma decay (γ)"}},{"k":"neutron_emission","v":{"s":"Neutron emission"}},{"k":"fission","v":{"s":"Fission"}},{"k":"fusion","v":{"s":"Fusion"}},{"k":"spontaneous_fission","v":{"s":"Spontaneous fission"}},{"k":"antoine_lavoisier","v":{"s":"Antoine Lavoisier"}},{"k":"john_dalton","v":{"s":"John Dalton"}},{"k":"amedeo_avogadro","v":{"s":"Amedeo Avogadro"}},{"k":"dmitri_mendeleev","v":{"s":"Dmitri Mendeleev"}},{"k":"marie_curie","v":{"s":"Marie Curie"}},{"k":"fritz_haber","v":{"s":"Fritz Haber"}},{"k":"linus_pauling","v":{"s":"Linus Pauling"}},{"k":"rosalind_franklin","v":{"s":"Rosalind Franklin"}},{"k":"dorothy_hodgkin","v":{"s":"Dorothy Hodgkin"}},{"k":"stephanie_kwolek","v":{"s":"Stephanie Kwolek"}},{"k":"frances_arnold","v":{"s":"Frances Arnold"}},{"k":"jennifer_doudna_emmanuelle_charpentier","v":{"s":"Jennifer Doudna & Emmanuelle Charpentier"}},{"k":"gas_laws_2","v":{"s":"💨 Gas laws"}},{"k":"quantitative_relationships_between_p_v","v":{"s":"Quantitative relationships between P, V, T, and n (moles) for gases. Use Kelvin for temperature."}},{"k":"colligative_properties","v":{"s":"🧂 Colligative properties"}},{"k":"properties_that_depend_on_the_number_o","v":{"s":"Properties that depend on the NUMBER of solute particles, not their identity. i = van't Hoff factor (1 for non-electrolyte, ~2 for NaCl, ~3 for CaCl₂)."}},{"k":"standard_reduction_potentials_e","v":{"s":"🔋 Standard reduction potentials (E°)"}},{"k":"higher_e_stronger_oxidizer_more_eager_","v":{"s":"Higher E° = stronger oxidizer (more eager to GAIN electrons). To predict a redox reaction: cell potential = E°(cathode) − E°(anode). Positive → spontaneous."}},{"k":"organic_functional_groups","v":{"s":"🧪 Organic functional groups"}},{"k":"recognize_functional_groups_to_predict","v":{"s":"Recognize functional groups to predict chemical behavior. R = \"rest of molecule\" (any carbon chain)."}},{"k":"spectroscopy_methods","v":{"s":"📡 Spectroscopy methods"}},{"k":"different_wavelengths_probe_different_","v":{"s":"Different wavelengths probe different molecular properties. Chemists combine multiple methods to determine structure of unknown compounds."}},{"k":"crystal_structures_2","v":{"s":"💎 Crystal structures"}},{"k":"how_atoms_ions_pack_in_solids_determin","v":{"s":"How atoms/ions pack in solids determines material properties (hardness, conductivity, melting point, optical behavior)."}},{"k":"biochemistry_classes_of_biomolecules","v":{"s":"🧬 Biochemistry — classes of biomolecules"}},{"k":"atmospheric_chemistry","v":{"s":"🌫 Atmospheric chemistry"}},{"k":"atmospheric_layers_bottom_to_top","v":{"s":"Atmospheric layers (bottom to top)"}},{"k":"atmospheric_composition_dry_air_by_vol","v":{"s":"Atmospheric composition (dry air, by volume)"}},{"k":"nuclear_chemistry","v":{"s":"☢ Nuclear chemistry"}},{"k":"decay_and_nuclear_processes","v":{"s":"Decay and nuclear processes"}},{"k":"notable_isotopes","v":{"s":"Notable isotopes"}},{"k":"electrochemistry_cell_types","v":{"s":"⚡ Electrochemistry — cell types"}},{"k":"chemistry_history_selected_figures","v":{"s":"🕰 Chemistry history — selected figures"}},{"k":"industrial_scale_chemicals","v":{"s":"🏗 Industrial-scale chemicals"}},{"k":"volumes_are_approximate_annual_world_p","v":{"s":"Volumes are approximate annual world production. Chemistry at industrial scale underlies modern civilization."}},{"k":"color_chemistry_pigments_dyes","v":{"s":"🎨 Color chemistry — pigments + dyes"}},{"k":"color_in_chemistry_usually_comes_from_","v":{"s":"Color in chemistry usually comes from absorption of specific visible wavelengths. Conjugated double-bond systems (like in chlorophyll, beta-carotene) absorb in the visible."}},{"k":"flavor_scent_molecules","v":{"s":"👃 Flavor + scent molecules"}},{"k":"olfactory_receptors_detect_specific_mo","v":{"s":"Olfactory receptors detect specific molecules. Humans have ~400 different olfactory receptors and can distinguish ~10,000 distinct smells (some claim trillions)."}},{"k":"water_2","v":{"s":"Water"}},{"k":"carbon_dioxide_2","v":{"s":"Carbon dioxide"}},{"k":"methane_2","v":{"s":"Methane"}},{"k":"ammonia_3","v":{"s":"Ammonia"}},{"k":"hydrochloric_acid_3","v":{"s":"Hydrochloric acid"}},{"k":"sulfuric_acid_3","v":{"s":"Sulfuric acid"}},{"k":"nitric_acid_3","v":{"s":"Nitric acid"}},{"k":"sodium_hydroxide_lye","v":{"s":"Sodium hydroxide (lye)"}},{"k":"calcium_hydroxide_slaked_lime","v":{"s":"Calcium hydroxide (slaked lime)"}},{"k":"sodium_chloride_2","v":{"s":"Sodium chloride"}},{"k":"calcium_carbonate_2","v":{"s":"Calcium carbonate"}},{"k":"sodium_bicarbonate","v":{"s":"Sodium bicarbonate"}},{"k":"hydrogen_peroxide","v":{"s":"Hydrogen peroxide"}},{"k":"ozone","v":{"s":"Ozone"}},{"k":"glucose_2","v":{"s":"Glucose"}},{"k":"sucrose_table_sugar","v":{"s":"Sucrose (table sugar)"}},{"k":"ethanol_2","v":{"s":"Ethanol"}},{"k":"methanol_2","v":{"s":"Methanol"}},{"k":"acetone","v":{"s":"Acetone"}},{"k":"acetic_acid_2","v":{"s":"Acetic acid"}},{"k":"formaldehyde","v":{"s":"Formaldehyde"}},{"k":"benzene","v":{"s":"Benzene"}},{"k":"caffeine_3","v":{"s":"Caffeine"}},{"k":"aspirin_3","v":{"s":"Aspirin"}},{"k":"penicillin_g","v":{"s":"Penicillin G"}},{"k":"glycerin_glycerol","v":{"s":"Glycerin (glycerol)"}},{"k":"urea_2","v":{"s":"Urea"}},{"k":"iron_iii_oxide_rust","v":{"s":"Iron(III) oxide (rust)"}},{"k":"calcium_phosphate","v":{"s":"Calcium phosphate"}},{"k":"silicon_dioxide","v":{"s":"Silicon dioxide"}},{"k":"aluminum_oxide_2","v":{"s":"Aluminum oxide"}},{"k":"titanium_dioxide","v":{"s":"Titanium dioxide"}},{"k":"sodium_fluoride","v":{"s":"Sodium fluoride"}},{"k":"solubility_rules_data","v":{"s":"◐ Solubility rules + data"}},{"k":"rules_of_thumb_for_ionic_compound_solu","v":{"s":"Rules of thumb for ionic compound solubility in water. Helpful for predicting precipitation reactions."}},{"k":"general_solubility_rules","v":{"s":"General solubility rules"}},{"k":"solubility_of_specific_compounds_in_wa","v":{"s":"Solubility of specific compounds in water"}},{"k":"common_chemical_compounds","v":{"s":"⌬ Common chemical compounds"}},{"k":"molecular_weights_in_g_mol_listed_in_r","v":{"s":"Molecular weights in g/mol. Listed in rough order of familiarity."}},{"k":"h_ph_scale_of_common_substances","v":{"s":"H⁺ pH scale of common substances"}},{"k":"ph_log_h_each_unit_10_change_in_h_ph_7","v":{"s":"pH = −log[H⁺]. Each unit = 10× change in [H⁺]. pH 7 = neutral; <7 = acidic; >7 = basic. Scale theoretically goes beyond 0-14 but rarely encountered."}},{"k":"food_nutrition_chemistry_context","v":{"s":"🥦 Food + nutrition (chemistry context)"}},{"k":"macronutrients_carbs_fats_proteins_eac","v":{"s":"Macronutrients: carbs, fats, proteins. Each gram: carbs ~4 cal, protein ~4 cal, fat ~9 cal, alcohol ~7 cal."}},{"k":"melting_boiling_points","v":{"s":"🌡 Melting + boiling points"}},{"k":"at_1_atm_materials_with_strong_intermo","v":{"s":"At 1 atm. Materials with strong intermolecular forces (ionic, network covalent) have higher melting/boiling points than those with weak forces (London)."}},{"k":"hydrogen_2","v":{"s":"Hydrogen"}},{"k":"helium","v":{"s":"Helium"}},{"k":"lithium","v":{"s":"Lithium"}},{"k":"beryllium","v":{"s":"Beryllium"}},{"k":"boron","v":{"s":"Boron"}},{"k":"carbon_2","v":{"s":"Carbon"}},{"k":"nitrogen_3","v":{"s":"Nitrogen"}},{"k":"oxygen_3","v":{"s":"Oxygen"}},{"k":"fluorine","v":{"s":"Fluorine"}},{"k":"neon","v":{"s":"Neon"}},{"k":"sodium_2","v":{"s":"Sodium"}},{"k":"magnesium","v":{"s":"Magnesium"}},{"k":"aluminum","v":{"s":"Aluminum"}},{"k":"silicon_2","v":{"s":"Silicon"}},{"k":"phosphorus_2","v":{"s":"Phosphorus"}},{"k":"sulfur_2","v":{"s":"Sulfur"}},{"k":"chlorine_2","v":{"s":"Chlorine"}},{"k":"argon","v":{"s":"Argon"}},{"k":"potassium_2","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"calcium_2","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"scandium","v":{"s":"Scandium"}},{"k":"titanium","v":{"s":"Titanium"}},{"k":"vanadium","v":{"s":"Vanadium"}},{"k":"chromium","v":{"s":"Chromium"}},{"k":"manganese","v":{"s":"Manganese"}},{"k":"iron_2","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"cobalt","v":{"s":"Cobalt"}},{"k":"nickel","v":{"s":"Nickel"}},{"k":"copper","v":{"s":"Copper"}},{"k":"zinc","v":{"s":"Zinc"}},{"k":"gallium","v":{"s":"Gallium"}},{"k":"germanium","v":{"s":"Germanium"}},{"k":"arsenic","v":{"s":"Arsenic"}},{"k":"selenium","v":{"s":"Selenium"}},{"k":"bromine","v":{"s":"Bromine"}},{"k":"krypton","v":{"s":"Krypton"}},{"k":"rubidium","v":{"s":"Rubidium"}},{"k":"strontium","v":{"s":"Strontium"}},{"k":"yttrium","v":{"s":"Yttrium"}},{"k":"zirconium","v":{"s":"Zirconium"}},{"k":"niobium","v":{"s":"Niobium"}},{"k":"molybdenum","v":{"s":"Molybdenum"}},{"k":"technetium","v":{"s":"Technetium"}},{"k":"ruthenium","v":{"s":"Ruthenium"}},{"k":"rhodium","v":{"s":"Rhodium"}},{"k":"palladium","v":{"s":"Palladium"}},{"k":"silver","v":{"s":"Silver"}},{"k":"cadmium","v":{"s":"Cadmium"}},{"k":"indium","v":{"s":"Indium"}},{"k":"antimony","v":{"s":"Antimony"}},{"k":"tellurium","v":{"s":"Tellurium"}},{"k":"iodine","v":{"s":"Iodine"}},{"k":"xenon","v":{"s":"Xenon"}},{"k":"cesium","v":{"s":"Cesium"}},{"k":"barium","v":{"s":"Barium"}},{"k":"lanthanum","v":{"s":"Lanthanum"}},{"k":"cerium","v":{"s":"Cerium"}},{"k":"praseodymium","v":{"s":"Praseodymium"}},{"k":"neodymium","v":{"s":"Neodymium"}},{"k":"promethium","v":{"s":"Promethium"}},{"k":"samarium","v":{"s":"Samarium"}},{"k":"europium","v":{"s":"Europium"}},{"k":"gadolinium","v":{"s":"Gadolinium"}},{"k":"terbium","v":{"s":"Terbium"}},{"k":"dysprosium","v":{"s":"Dysprosium"}},{"k":"holmium","v":{"s":"Holmium"}},{"k":"erbium","v":{"s":"Erbium"}},{"k":"thulium","v":{"s":"Thulium"}},{"k":"ytterbium","v":{"s":"Ytterbium"}},{"k":"lutetium","v":{"s":"Lutetium"}},{"k":"hafnium","v":{"s":"Hafnium"}},{"k":"tantalum","v":{"s":"Tantalum"}},{"k":"tungsten","v":{"s":"Tungsten"}},{"k":"rhenium","v":{"s":"Rhenium"}},{"k":"osmium","v":{"s":"Osmium"}},{"k":"iridium","v":{"s":"Iridium"}},{"k":"platinum","v":{"s":"Platinum"}},{"k":"gold","v":{"s":"Gold"}},{"k":"mercury","v":{"s":"Mercury"}},{"k":"thallium","v":{"s":"Thallium"}},{"k":"lead","v":{"s":"Lead"}},{"k":"bismuth","v":{"s":"Bismuth"}},{"k":"polonium","v":{"s":"Polonium"}},{"k":"astatine","v":{"s":"Astatine"}},{"k":"radon","v":{"s":"Radon"}},{"k":"francium","v":{"s":"Francium"}},{"k":"radium","v":{"s":"Radium"}},{"k":"actinium","v":{"s":"Actinium"}},{"k":"thorium","v":{"s":"Thorium"}},{"k":"uranium","v":{"s":"Uranium"}},{"k":"plutonium","v":{"s":"Plutonium"}},{"k":"quartz","v":{"s":"Quartz"}},{"k":"feldspar","v":{"s":"Feldspar"}},{"k":"calcite","v":{"s":"Calcite"}},{"k":"halite","v":{"s":"Halite"}},{"k":"gypsum","v":{"s":"Gypsum"}},{"k":"mica_muscovite","v":{"s":"Mica (muscovite)"}},{"k":"olivine","v":{"s":"Olivine"}},{"k":"pyrite","v":{"s":"Pyrite"}},{"k":"galena","v":{"s":"Galena"}},{"k":"magnetite","v":{"s":"Magnetite"}},{"k":"hematite","v":{"s":"Hematite"}},{"k":"talc","v":{"s":"Talc"}},{"k":"corundum","v":{"s":"Corundum"}},{"k":"diamond","v":{"s":"Diamond"}},{"k":"topaz","v":{"s":"Topaz"}},{"k":"beryl_emerald_aquamarine","v":{"s":"Beryl (emerald, aquamarine)"}},{"k":"garnet","v":{"s":"Garnet"}},{"k":"fluorite_2","v":{"s":"Fluorite"}},{"k":"apatite","v":{"s":"Apatite"}},{"k":"orthoclase","v":{"s":"Orthoclase"}},{"k":"aspirin_4","v":{"s":"Aspirin"}},{"k":"ibuprofen","v":{"s":"Ibuprofen"}},{"k":"acetaminophen_tylenol","v":{"s":"Acetaminophen (Tylenol)"}},{"k":"caffeine_4","v":{"s":"Caffeine"}},{"k":"penicillin_g_2","v":{"s":"Penicillin G"}},{"k":"amoxicillin","v":{"s":"Amoxicillin"}},{"k":"insulin","v":{"s":"Insulin"}},{"k":"metformin","v":{"s":"Metformin"}},{"k":"statins_e_g_atorvastatin","v":{"s":"Statins (e.g., atorvastatin)"}},{"k":"levothyroxine","v":{"s":"Levothyroxine"}},{"k":"sertraline_zoloft","v":{"s":"Sertraline (Zoloft)"}},{"k":"albuterol","v":{"s":"Albuterol"}},{"k":"lisinopril","v":{"s":"Lisinopril"}},{"k":"atorvastatin_lipitor","v":{"s":"Atorvastatin (Lipitor)"}},{"k":"omeprazole","v":{"s":"Omeprazole"}},{"k":"diphenhydramine_benadryl","v":{"s":"Diphenhydramine (Benadryl)"}},{"k":"loratadine_claritin","v":{"s":"Loratadine (Claritin)"}},{"k":"codeine","v":{"s":"Codeine"}},{"k":"morphine","v":{"s":"Morphine"}},{"k":"hydrocodone","v":{"s":"Hydrocodone"}},{"k":"diazepam_valium","v":{"s":"Diazepam (Valium)"}},{"k":"warfarin","v":{"s":"Warfarin"}},{"k":"furosemide_lasix","v":{"s":"Furosemide (Lasix)"}},{"k":"albuterol_salbutamol","v":{"s":"Albuterol (salbutamol)"}},{"k":"metoprolol","v":{"s":"Metoprolol"}},{"k":"periodic_table_90_elements","v":{"s":"🅻 Periodic table (90 elements)"}},{"k":"a_summary_of_the_90_naturally_occurrin","v":{"s":"A summary of the 90 naturally-occurring elements + selected synthetic ones. Full periodic table has 118 elements."}},{"k":"common_minerals","v":{"s":"💎 Common minerals"}},{"k":"mohs_scale_measures_relative_hardness_","v":{"s":"Mohs scale measures relative hardness (1 = softest, 10 = hardest). The scale steps are NOT linear: corundum (9) is ~4× harder than topaz (8), and diamond (10) is ~4× harder than corundum."}},{"k":"common_medications","v":{"s":"💊 Common medications"}},{"k":"for_chemistry_context_only_not_medical","v":{"s":"For chemistry context only — NOT medical advice. Always consult a doctor or pharmacist before taking any medication."}},{"k":"household_chemicals","v":{"s":"🧴 Household chemicals"}},{"k":"safety","v":{"s":"⚠ Safety: "}},{"k":"never_mix_bleach_with_ammonia_toxic_ch","v":{"s":"NEVER mix bleach with ammonia (toxic chloramine vapors) or acids (deadly chlorine gas). Store cleaners separately. Keep away from children."}},{"k":"recrystallization","v":{"s":"Recrystallization"}},{"k":"distillation_simple","v":{"s":"Distillation (simple)"}},{"k":"distillation_fractional","v":{"s":"Distillation (fractional)"}},{"k":"filtration_gravity","v":{"s":"Filtration (gravity)"}},{"k":"vacuum_filtration_b_chner","v":{"s":"Vacuum filtration (Büchner)"}},{"k":"liquid_liquid_extraction","v":{"s":"Liquid-liquid extraction"}},{"k":"chromatography_tlc","v":{"s":"Chromatography (TLC)"}},{"k":"chromatography_column","v":{"s":"Chromatography (column)"}},{"k":"chromatography_hplc","v":{"s":"Chromatography (HPLC)"}},{"k":"chromatography_gc","v":{"s":"Chromatography (GC)"}},{"k":"reflux","v":{"s":"Reflux"}},{"k":"titration","v":{"s":"Titration"}},{"k":"gel_electrophoresis","v":{"s":"Gel electrophoresis"}},{"k":"rotary_evaporator_rotovap","v":{"s":"Rotary evaporator (rotovap)"}},{"k":"schlenk_line","v":{"s":"Schlenk line"}},{"k":"soxhlet_extraction","v":{"s":"Soxhlet extraction"}},{"k":"average_time_to_market","v":{"s":"Average time to market"}},{"k":"10_15_years_from_initial_discovery_to_","v":{"s":"10-15 years from initial discovery to approval."}},{"k":"average_cost","v":{"s":"Average cost"}},{"k":"estimated_1_2_5_billion_per_approved_d","v":{"s":"Estimated $1-2.5 billion per approved drug (includes failures)."}},{"k":"attrition_rate","v":{"s":"Attrition rate"}},{"k":"10_of_candidates_entering_phase_i_make","v":{"s":"~10% of candidates entering Phase I make it to market. Most fail at Phase II for efficacy or Phase III for safety."}},{"k":"lipinski_s_rule_of_5","v":{"s":"Lipinski's Rule of 5"}},{"k":"drug_likeness_mw_500_logp_5_5_h_bond_d","v":{"s":"Drug-likeness: MW < 500, logP < 5, ≤ 5 H-bond donors, ≤ 10 H-bond acceptors. Predicts oral bioavailability."}},{"k":"generic_drugs","v":{"s":"Generic drugs"}},{"k":"available_after_patent_expires_typical","v":{"s":"Available after patent expires (typically 20 years from filing). Much cheaper but bioequivalent."}},{"k":"biologics","v":{"s":"Biologics"}},{"k":"made_by_living_cells_antibodies_vaccin","v":{"s":"Made by living cells (antibodies, vaccines). More complex, more expensive, \"biosimilars\" instead of generics."}},{"k":"reducing_sugar_amino_acid_brown_flavor","v":{"s":"Reducing sugar + amino acid → brown flavor compounds. Happens above ~140°C. Why bread crusts, seared steak, coffee are flavorful."}},{"k":"sugar_molecules_break_recombine_at_hig","v":{"s":"Sugar molecules break + recombine at high heat (>160°C). Creates hundreds of flavor compounds. Different from Maillard (no amino acids needed)."}},{"k":"starch_granules_absorb_water_swell_whe","v":{"s":"Starch granules absorb water + swell when heated >60°C. Thickens sauces, cooks pasta."}},{"k":"wheat_proteins_glutenin_gliadin_form_e","v":{"s":"Wheat proteins (glutenin + gliadin) form elastic network when hydrated. Develops with kneading. Captures CO₂ in bread."}},{"k":"stable_mixture_of_oil_water_with_emuls","v":{"s":"Stable mixture of oil + water with emulsifier (lecithin in egg yolk → mayonnaise; mustard or honey help too)."}},{"k":"heat_unfolds_proteins_texture_changes_","v":{"s":"Heat unfolds proteins → texture changes. Eggs cook (clear → white), meat firms."}},{"k":"microbes_convert_sugars_to_acids_alcoh","v":{"s":"Microbes convert sugars to acids, alcohols, gases. Sauerkraut (lactic), beer (alcohol + CO₂), yogurt (lactic from lactose)."}},{"k":"spicy_compound_in_chili_peppers_activa","v":{"s":"Spicy compound in chili peppers. Activates TRPV1 heat receptors. Soluble in fat + alcohol, not water — why milk soothes spicy mouth."}},{"k":"sodium_salt_of_glutamate_triggers_umam","v":{"s":"Sodium salt of glutamate. Triggers umami taste receptor. Naturally abundant in tomatoes, parmesan, fish sauce, mushrooms."}},{"k":"polyphenol_oxidase_browns_cut_apples_b","v":{"s":"Polyphenol oxidase browns cut apples + bananas. Lemon juice (acid) or refrigeration slows it."}},{"k":"co_dissolves_in_cold_water_under_press","v":{"s":"CO₂ dissolves in cold water under pressure. Open bottle → pressure drops → CO₂ escapes as bubbles + H₂CO₃ tang."}},{"k":"plant_cell_wall_polysaccharide_forms_g","v":{"s":"Plant cell wall polysaccharide. Forms gel with sugar + acid → jams."}},{"k":"fat_base_soap_glycerol_soap_molecules_","v":{"s":"Fat + base → soap + glycerol. Soap molecules are amphipathic (one end loves water, one loves oil)."}},{"k":"sodium_alginate_calcium_liquid_gel_cav","v":{"s":"Sodium alginate + calcium → liquid gel \"caviar\" or \"ravioli\". Modernist cooking."}},{"k":"wild_yeast_lactic_bacteria_slower_more","v":{"s":"Wild yeast + lactic bacteria. Slower, more sour than commercial yeast."}},{"k":"metals","v":{"s":"Metals"}},{"k":"ceramics","v":{"s":"Ceramics"}},{"k":"polymers_plastics","v":{"s":"Polymers (plastics)"}},{"k":"composites","v":{"s":"Composites"}},{"k":"semiconductors","v":{"s":"Semiconductors"}},{"k":"biomaterials","v":{"s":"Biomaterials"}},{"k":"nanomaterials","v":{"s":"Nanomaterials"}},{"k":"smart_materials","v":{"s":"Smart materials"}},{"k":"superconductors","v":{"s":"Superconductors"}},{"k":"metamaterials","v":{"s":"Metamaterials"}},{"k":"central_metal_atom_bonded_to_ligands_n","v":{"s":"Central metal atom bonded to \"ligands\" (neutral or anionic). Geometry (octahedral, tetrahedral, square planar) determined by metal + ligands."}},{"k":"ligands_split_metal_d_orbital_energies","v":{"s":"Ligands split metal d-orbital energies. Determines color, magnetic properties. High-spin vs low-spin complexes."}},{"k":"a_single_ligand_binds_metal_through_mu","v":{"s":"A single ligand binds metal through multiple atoms (EDTA is hexadentate). Very stable complexes."}},{"k":"lewis_acid_electron_pair_acceptor_e_g_","v":{"s":"Lewis acid = electron-pair acceptor (e.g., BF₃, Cu²⁺); Lewis base = electron-pair donor (e.g., NH₃, OH⁻)."}},{"k":"hard_acids_prefer_hard_bases_small_non","v":{"s":"Hard acids prefer hard bases (small, non-polarizable). Soft prefer soft (large, polarizable). Predicts reactivity."}},{"k":"f_block_similar_chemistry_within_each_","v":{"s":"f-block. Similar chemistry within each series (lanthanide contraction). Many radioactive (all actinides)."}},{"k":"d_block_multiple_oxidation_states_fe_f","v":{"s":"d-block. Multiple oxidation states (Fe²⁺/Fe³⁺), often colored complexes, catalytic activity."}},{"k":"metal_carbon_bonds_grignard_reagents_r","v":{"s":"Metal-carbon bonds. Grignard reagents (RMgX), ferrocene (Fe(C₅H₅)₂), catalysts (Wilkinson, Grubbs)."}},{"k":"acids_with_o_h_bonds_h_so_h_po_hno_hcl","v":{"s":"Acids with O–H bonds. H₂SO₄, H₃PO₄, HNO₃, HClO₄. More O atoms → stronger acid (typically)."}},{"k":"binary_compounds_with_h_ionic_nah_cova","v":{"s":"Binary compounds with H. Ionic (NaH), covalent (CH₄), metallic (PdH, used in H₂ storage)."}},{"k":"better_to_prevent_waste_than_treat_or_","v":{"s":"Better to prevent waste than treat or clean it up."}},{"k":"maximize_fraction_of_reactant_atoms_en","v":{"s":"Maximize fraction of reactant atoms ending up in product."}},{"k":"use_generate_substances_with_little_no","v":{"s":"Use + generate substances with little/no toxicity."}},{"k":"design_products_with_minimal_toxicity_","v":{"s":"Design products with minimal toxicity while preserving function."}},{"k":"avoid_solvents_when_possible_use_safer","v":{"s":"Avoid solvents when possible; use safer ones when not (water, supercritical CO₂)."}},{"k":"run_reactions_at_ambient_t_p_when_poss","v":{"s":"Run reactions at ambient T + P when possible."}},{"k":"use_biomass_agricultural_by_products_i","v":{"s":"Use biomass/agricultural by-products instead of petroleum."}},{"k":"avoid_protecting_groups_and_unnecessar","v":{"s":"Avoid protecting groups and unnecessary modifications."}},{"k":"catalytic_stoichiometric_reusable_less","v":{"s":"Catalytic > stoichiometric. Reusable; less waste."}},{"k":"products_should_break_down_to_innocuou","v":{"s":"Products should break down to innocuous substances at end of life."}},{"k":"inline_monitoring_to_prevent_pollution","v":{"s":"Inline monitoring to prevent pollution."}},{"k":"design_for_accident_prevention_minimiz","v":{"s":"Design for accident prevention — minimize releases, explosions, fires."}},{"k":"structural_constitutional_isomers","v":{"s":"Structural (constitutional) isomers"}},{"k":"different_connectivity_of_atoms_same_m","v":{"s":"Different connectivity of atoms. Same molecular formula."}},{"k":"chain_isomers","v":{"s":"Chain isomers"}},{"k":"different_carbon_chain_arrangements","v":{"s":"Different carbon chain arrangements."}},{"k":"positional_isomers","v":{"s":"Positional isomers"}},{"k":"same_functional_group_different_positi","v":{"s":"Same functional group, different position."}},{"k":"functional_group_isomers","v":{"s":"Functional group isomers"}},{"k":"same_molecular_formula_different_funct","v":{"s":"Same molecular formula, different functional group."}},{"k":"geometric_cis_trans_isomers","v":{"s":"Geometric (cis-trans) isomers"}},{"k":"different_arrangement_around_a_rigid_b","v":{"s":"Different arrangement around a rigid bond (e.g., double bond or ring)."}},{"k":"enantiomers_mirror_image_stereoisomers","v":{"s":"Enantiomers (mirror-image stereoisomers)"}},{"k":"non_superimposable_mirror_images_chira","v":{"s":"Non-superimposable mirror images. \"Chirality\"."}},{"k":"diastereomers","v":{"s":"Diastereomers"}},{"k":"stereoisomers_that_are_not_mirror_imag","v":{"s":"Stereoisomers that are NOT mirror images."}},{"k":"conformational_isomers","v":{"s":"Conformational isomers"}},{"k":"differ_by_rotation_about_single_bonds_","v":{"s":"Differ by rotation about single bonds — interconvert rapidly."}},{"k":"tautomers","v":{"s":"Tautomers"}},{"k":"constitutional_isomers_that_interconve","v":{"s":"Constitutional isomers that interconvert by H-atom migration."}},{"k":"optical_rotation","v":{"s":"Optical rotation"}},{"k":"chiral_molecules_rotate_polarized_ligh","v":{"s":"Chiral molecules rotate polarized light. (+) or (−) prefix."}},{"k":"lab_techniques_2","v":{"s":"🔬 Lab techniques"}},{"k":"drug_discovery_pipeline","v":{"s":"💊 Drug discovery pipeline"}},{"k":"stages","v":{"s":"Stages"}},{"k":"industry_essentials","v":{"s":"Industry essentials"}},{"k":"food_chemistry_2","v":{"s":"🍳 Food chemistry"}},{"k":"materials_classes","v":{"s":"🪨 Materials classes"}},{"k":"notable_materials","v":{"s":"Notable materials"}},{"k":"inorganic_chemistry_topics","v":{"s":"⚛ Inorganic chemistry topics"}},{"k":"environmental_pollutants","v":{"s":"🏭 Environmental pollutants"}},{"k":"12_principles_of_green_chemistry","v":{"s":"🌱 12 Principles of Green Chemistry"}},{"k":"articulated_by_anastas_warner_1998_gui","v":{"s":"Articulated by Anastas + Warner (1998). Guides chemists toward more sustainable methods."}},{"k":"vsepr_electron_pair_geometries","v":{"s":"∡ VSEPR — electron pair geometries"}},{"k":"lone_pairs_lp_take_more_space_than_bon","v":{"s":"Lone pairs (LP) take more space than bonding pairs → compress bond angles. VSEPR predicts molecular shape from total electron pairs."}},{"k":"isomers_2","v":{"s":"⇄ Isomers"}},{"k":"molecules_with_the_same_molecular_form","v":{"s":"Molecules with the same molecular formula but different arrangement. Isomers can have very different properties."}},{"k":"he_noble_gases_group_18","v":{"s":"He Noble gases (Group 18)"}},{"k":"full_outer_electron_shell_very_unreact","v":{"s":"Full outer electron shell → very unreactive. Discovered late (Ramsay et al., 1894-1898) because they don't form compounds easily."}},{"k":"bohr_model_caption_honest","v":{"s":"⚡ The Bohr model draws electrons on tidy “shells” that fill inside-out — a useful first picture, but electrons don’t actually circle like planets. See the ☰ Orbital clouds tab for where they really are (probability clouds, not orbits)."}},{"k":"orbital_clouds","v":{"s":"Orbital clouds"}},{"k":"orbital_clouds_title","v":{"s":"☁️ Orbital clouds — where electrons really are"}},{"k":"orbital_clouds_intro","v":{"s":"An atom is not a tiny solar system. Electrons don’t orbit the nucleus on tracks — they exist as a fuzzy cloud of probability (|ψ|²). The two colours are the wavefunction’s + and − phases; where they meet, the electron is never found (a node)."}},{"k":"orbital_caveat","v":{"s":"These are exact one-electron (hydrogen-like, Z = 1) orbitals. Real multi-electron atoms keep the same shapes but with shifted sizes and energies. Each subshell is a set (3 p’s, 5 d’s — including the differently-shaped d_z²); we show one representative orientation of each."}},{"k":"phase_plus","v":{"s":"ψ > 0 (one phase)"}},{"k":"phase_minus","v":{"s":"ψ < 0 (other phase)"}},{"k":"nucleus","v":{"s":"nucleus"}},{"k":"shape","v":{"s":"Shape"}},{"k":"most_likely_radius","v":{"s":"Most-likely radius"}},{"k":"radial_nodes","v":{"s":"Radial nodes"}},{"k":"angular_nodes","v":{"s":"Angular nodes"}},{"k":"radial_distribution","v":{"s":"Radial distribution P(r) = r²R²"}},{"k":"peak","v":{"s":"most likely"}},{"k":"node","v":{"s":"node (P = 0)"}},{"k":"camera_controls","v":{"s":"3D molecule camera controls. Single-click alternatives to dragging the model."}},{"k":"camera","v":{"s":"Camera"}},{"k":"front_view","v":{"s":"Front view"}},{"k":"front","v":{"s":"Front"}},{"k":"side_view","v":{"s":"Side view"}},{"k":"side","v":{"s":"Side"}},{"k":"top_view","v":{"s":"Top view"}},{"k":"top","v":{"s":"Top"}},{"k":"zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"atom_and_bond_controls","v":{"s":"Atom and bond controls"}},{"k":"controls_equivalent_help","v":{"s":"These keyboard-accessible controls provide the same actions as the small controls inside the workspace."}},{"k":"step","v":{"s":"Step"}},{"k":"of","v":{"s":"of"}},{"k":"search_molecule_library","v":{"s":"Search molecule library"}},{"k":"en_atom_a","v":{"s":"Atom A"}},{"k":"en_atom_b","v":{"s":"Atom B"}},{"k":"en_axis_label","v":{"s":"ΔEN (Pauling)"}},{"k":"en_cartoon_even","v":{"s":"Diagram: the shared electron cloud sits centred between the two atoms, so neither end carries a partial charge."}},{"k":"en_ionic","v":{"s":"Mostly ionic"}},{"k":"en_ionic_character","v":{"s":"Ionic character: "}},{"k":"en_nonpolar","v":{"s":"Nonpolar covalent"}},{"k":"en_pick_first","v":{"s":"Choose the first atom"}},{"k":"en_pick_second","v":{"s":"Choose the second atom"}},{"k":"en_polar","v":{"s":"Polar covalent"}},{"k":"en_shared_evenly","v":{"s":"shared evenly"}},{"k":"en_spectrum_title","v":{"s":"🎚 Build a bond: the character is a slider, not a switch"}},{"k":"en_why_17","v":{"s":"Why 1.7? "}},{"k":"en_why_17_body","v":{"s":"Put ΔEN = 1.7 into Pauling's formula and you get 51% ionic character. The famous cutoff is just the halfway mark, picked for convenience. Nothing snaps at 1.7: Al–Cl (1.55) and C–F (1.43) sit right below it and behave like strongly polar covalent bonds, not salts."}},{"k":"en_zone_ionic","v":{"s":"mostly ionic"}},{"k":"en_zone_nonpolar","v":{"s":"nonpolar"}},{"k":"en_zone_polar","v":{"s":"polar covalent"}},{"k":"kin_axis_x","v":{"s":"reaction coordinate →"}},{"k":"kin_axis_y","v":{"s":"potential energy (kJ/mol)"}},{"k":"kin_boltz","v":{"s":"Share of collisions with enough energy"}},{"k":"kin_bust","v":{"s":"Drag the catalyst slider as far as it goes. The hill collapses, the rate jumps by orders of magnitude, and the two ends of the curve do not budge. A catalyst changes how fast you get there, never where you end up, and never the equilibrium position."}},{"k":"kin_bust_label","v":{"s":"⚠ The one to watch: "}},{"k":"kin_dh","v":{"s":"Reaction enthalpy ΔH"}},{"k":"kin_drop","v":{"s":"Catalyst removes"}},{"k":"kin_ea","v":{"s":"Activation energy Ea"}},{"k":"kin_leg_cat","v":{"s":"with catalyst"}},{"k":"kin_leg_plain","v":{"s":"no catalyst"}},{"k":"kin_per10","v":{"s":"Effect of +10 K"}},{"k":"kin_per10_note","v":{"s":"the \"rate doubles per 10 °C\" rule of thumb only holds near Ea ≈ 50 kJ/mol"}},{"k":"kin_products","v":{"s":"products"}},{"k":"kin_profile_title","v":{"s":"⛰ The energy hill: move the sliders and watch the rate move"}},{"k":"kin_reactants","v":{"s":"reactants"}},{"k":"kin_reverse","v":{"s":"Reverse barrier"}},{"k":"kin_show_cat","v":{"s":"Show catalysed path"}},{"k":"kin_speedup","v":{"s":"Catalyst speed-up"}},{"k":"kin_speedup_note","v":{"s":"same reactants, same products, same ΔH"}},{"k":"kin_temp","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"kin_ts","v":{"s":"transition state ‡"}},{"k":"phase_axis_p","v":{"s":"Pressure (Pa, log scale)"}},{"k":"phase_axis_t","v":{"s":"Temperature (K)"}},{"k":"phase_choose_condition","v":{"s":"Choose a real-world condition to mark on the diagram"}},{"k":"phase_choose_substance","v":{"s":"Choose a substance"}},{"k":"phase_co2_fus_note","v":{"s":"CO₂'s triple point sits at 5.1 atm. At ordinary room pressure there is no liquid band at all, which is why dry ice sublimes instead of melting."}},{"k":"phase_critical","v":{"s":"critical point"}},{"k":"phase_diagram_title","v":{"s":"🗺 Where does it sit right now?"}},{"k":"phase_gas","v":{"s":"Gas"}},{"k":"phase_liquid","v":{"s":"Liquid"}},{"k":"phase_on_boundary","v":{"s":"right on the boundary"}},{"k":"phase_p_cart","v":{"s":"CO₂ cartridge, 20 °C"}},{"k":"phase_p_cart_n","v":{"s":"20 °C at about 57 atm. The cartridge sits ON the line, holding liquid and vapour together. That is why the pressure barely drops as it empties, until the last of the liquid is gone."}},{"k":"phase_p_co2frz","v":{"s":"Home freezer"}},{"k":"phase_p_co2frz_n","v":{"s":"−18 °C, 1 atm. Still a gas. CO₂ needs −78.5 °C before it condenses at this pressure."}},{"k":"phase_p_co2room","v":{"s":"Room, sea level"}},{"k":"phase_p_co2room_n","v":{"s":"25 °C, 1 atm. Gas, and nowhere near liquid."}},{"k":"phase_p_cooker","v":{"s":"Pressure cooker"}},{"k":"phase_p_cooker_n","v":{"s":"121 °C at 2 atm. Same line, higher up. Sealing the pot lets pressure build, which drags the boiling point up with it, so the water reaches 121 °C instead of 100 °C and food cooks far faster."}},{"k":"phase_p_decaf","v":{"s":"Decaffeinating coffee"}},{"k":"phase_p_decaf_n","v":{"s":"40 °C at 200 atm. Supercritical CO₂ seeps through beans like a gas but dissolves caffeine like a liquid, then simply evaporates away."}},{"k":"phase_p_dryice","v":{"s":"Dry ice in the open"}},{"k":"phase_p_dryice_n","v":{"s":"−78.5 °C, 1 atm. Exactly on the sublimation line: solid turning straight into gas, no puddle."}},{"k":"phase_p_everest","v":{"s":"Everest summit"}},{"k":"phase_p_everest_n","v":{"s":"71 °C at 0.33 atm. On the line again, but lower down it. Thin air means water boils at 71 °C, so a kettle up here physically cannot get hot enough to brew tea properly. The water is boiling and still too cool."}},{"k":"phase_p_extinct","v":{"s":"Fire extinguisher"}},{"k":"phase_p_extinct_n","v":{"s":"20 °C at 84 atm. Compressed past the line into pure liquid. Release it and it flashes to gas, chilling so hard it makes CO₂ snow."}},{"k":"phase_p_freeze","v":{"s":"Freeze-dryer"}},{"k":"phase_p_freeze_n","v":{"s":"−40 °C at 8 Pa. Below the triple-point pressure there is no liquid band, so ice goes straight to vapour. This is how freeze-dried food is made."}},{"k":"phase_p_freezer","v":{"s":"Home freezer"}},{"k":"phase_p_freezer_n","v":{"s":"−18 °C, 1 atm. Solid, comfortably."}},{"k":"phase_p_kettle","v":{"s":"Kettle boiling"}},{"k":"phase_p_kettle_n","v":{"s":"100 °C at 1 atm. Sitting exactly ON the vaporisation line, where liquid and vapour coexist. That is all \"boiling point\" means: the temperature where the line crosses your pressure. Now try Everest and the pressure cooker, which land on the same line at different heights."}},{"k":"phase_p_room","v":{"s":"Room, sea level"}},{"k":"phase_p_room_n","v":{"s":"25 °C, 1 atm. A glass of water."}},{"k":"phase_p_superc","v":{"s":"Supercritical steam"}},{"k":"phase_p_superc_n","v":{"s":"400 °C at 300 atm. Past the critical point liquid and gas stop being different things. Modern power-station boilers run here."}},{"k":"phase_prompt","v":{"s":"Pick a condition above and watch where it lands. Try the same substance at the same temperature but two different pressures."}},{"k":"phase_provenance","v":{"s":"Triple and critical points are measured values. The vaporisation curve is a Clausius–Clapeyron fit forced through three measured points (triple, a mid-range reference, critical), so it is exact at those three and within a few percent between them. The sublimation curve is a two-point fit. The melting line is drawn straight between two measured points, and the real one curves."}},{"k":"phase_provenance_label","v":{"s":"Where these lines come from: "}},{"k":"phase_solid","v":{"s":"Solid"}},{"k":"phase_sub_co2","v":{"s":"Carbon dioxide (CO₂)"}},{"k":"phase_sub_water","v":{"s":"Water (H₂O)"}},{"k":"phase_supercritical","v":{"s":"Supercritical fluid"}},{"k":"phase_supercritical_short","v":{"s":"Supercritical"}},{"k":"phase_triple","v":{"s":"triple point"}},{"k":"phase_water_fus_note","v":{"s":"Water's melting line leans LEFT: squeeze ice hard enough and it melts. Almost every other substance leans right. Push past a few thousand atmospheres and water stops making ordinary ice altogether and forms denser crystal types instead, which is where this straight line stops being a fair drawing."}},{"k":"th_axis_g","v":{"s":"ΔG (kJ/mol)"}},{"k":"th_axis_t","v":{"s":"Temperature (K)"}},{"k":"th_badge_no","v":{"s":"not spontaneous"}},{"k":"th_badge_yes","v":{"s":"spontaneous"}},{"k":"th_case_haber","v":{"s":"Ammonia synthesis"}},{"k":"th_case_haber_why","v":{"s":"Four gas molecules collapse into two, so disorder falls. Below the crossover the energy release wins. This is the squeeze at the heart of the Haber process: cold favours yield, but cold is also slow, so industry compromises and adds pressure."}},{"k":"th_case_lime","v":{"s":"Limestone to lime"}},{"k":"th_case_lime_why","v":{"s":"Uphill in energy, but it frees a gas out of a solid, so disorder climbs. Heat it past the crossover and the entropy term takes over. Real lime kilns run a little above this line."}},{"k":"th_case_ozone","v":{"s":"Making ozone from oxygen"}},{"k":"th_case_ozone_why","v":{"s":"Uphill in energy AND down in disorder. Heat cannot rescue it. The ozone layer exists because ultraviolet photons pay the bill, not because the reaction wants to happen."}},{"k":"th_case_perox","v":{"s":"Peroxide decomposing"}},{"k":"th_case_perox_why","v":{"s":"Downhill in energy and up in disorder at the same time. Nothing can stop it; only the missing catalyst keeps the bottle stable on the shelf."}},{"k":"th_flips_at","v":{"s":"Flips at T = ΔH/ΔS = "}},{"k":"th_map_intro","v":{"s":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS is a straight line in T whose slope is −ΔS. Below the axis means it happens on its own. Two sign combinations never cross the axis; the other two always do, and the crossing point is just T = ΔH / ΔS."}},{"k":"th_map_title","v":{"s":"🌡 Temperature decides. Drag it and watch two of the four lines change their mind."}},{"k":"th_never_flips","v":{"s":"Never flips: both terms point the same way."}},{"k":"th_not_spont","v":{"s":"ΔG > 0 · will not happen on its own"}},{"k":"th_preset_furnace","v":{"s":"Furnace"}},{"k":"th_preset_haber","v":{"s":"Ammonia crossover"}},{"k":"th_preset_ice","v":{"s":"Ice point"}},{"k":"th_preset_kiln","v":{"s":"Lime kiln crossover"}},{"k":"th_preset_room","v":{"s":"Room"}},{"k":"th_provenance","v":{"s":"ΔH and ΔS here are standard-state values measured at 298 K, and the straight lines assume they hold steady as the temperature climbs. They drift a little in reality, so treat a crossover temperature as a good estimate rather than an exact threshold. Spontaneous also does not mean fast: peroxide is spontaneous at every temperature on this chart and still sits in the bottle for months."}},{"k":"th_provenance_label","v":{"s":"Reading the fine print: "}},{"k":"th_q_always","v":{"s":"ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0 · spontaneous at every temperature"}},{"k":"th_q_high","v":{"s":"ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0 · spontaneous only when hot"}},{"k":"th_q_low","v":{"s":"ΔH < 0, ΔS < 0 · spontaneous only when cold"}},{"k":"th_q_never","v":{"s":"ΔH > 0, ΔS < 0 · never spontaneous"}},{"k":"th_spont","v":{"s":"ΔG < 0 · spontaneous"}},{"k":"th_temp_label","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"eq_add_h2","v":{"s":"+1 mol H₂"}},{"k":"eq_add_n2","v":{"s":"+1 mol N₂"}},{"k":"eq_add_nh3","v":{"s":"+1 mol NH₃"}},{"k":"eq_box_note","v":{"s":"1 drawn molecule ≈"}},{"k":"eq_box_sr","v":{"s":"Particle box"}},{"k":"eq_box_sr_2","v":{"s":"molecules drawn in proportion to the moles present; the box width shows the container volume"}},{"k":"eq_box_title","v":{"s":"Inside the container"}},{"k":"eq_catalyst","v":{"s":"Add a catalyst"}},{"k":"eq_catalyst_why","v":{"s":"A catalyst lowers the energy barrier for the forward AND reverse reactions equally, so it changes how fast equilibrium is reached — never where it sits."}},{"k":"eq_compress","v":{"s":"Compress (V ÷ 2)"}},{"k":"eq_conc_title","v":{"s":"Concentrations at equilibrium (mol/L)"}},{"k":"eq_cool","v":{"s":"Cool (−100 K)"}},{"k":"eq_dir_forward","v":{"s":"forward (→), making more NH₃"}},{"k":"eq_dir_none","v":{"s":"nowhere — it was already at equilibrium"}},{"k":"eq_dir_reverse","v":{"s":"in reverse (←), breaking NH₃ back down"}},{"k":"eq_exo_why","v":{"s":"This reaction is exothermic, so heat behaves like a product: adding heat pushes toward reactants, removing it pushes toward NH₃."}},{"k":"eq_exothermic","v":{"s":"exothermic"}},{"k":"eq_expand","v":{"s":"Expand (V × 2)"}},{"k":"eq_group_conc","v":{"s":"Change an amount"}},{"k":"eq_group_temp","v":{"s":"Change the temperature"}},{"k":"eq_group_trap","v":{"s":"Common traps"}},{"k":"eq_group_vol","v":{"s":"Change the volume (pressure)"}},{"k":"eq_heat","v":{"s":"Heat (+100 K)"}},{"k":"eq_idea1_b","v":{"s":"Adding, removing, compressing: Q changes, K does not, and the mixture chases K. Heat or cool it and K itself moves."}},{"k":"eq_idea1_t","v":{"s":"Only temperature moves K"}},{"k":"eq_idea2_b","v":{"s":"“Shifts right” never means “goes to completion”. Some of the added reactant is used up; the rest stays. Read the numbers, not just the arrow."}},{"k":"eq_idea2_t","v":{"s":"A shift is partial"}},{"k":"eq_idea3_b","v":{"s":"At equilibrium N₂ and H₂ are still becoming NH₃, and NH₃ is still splitting — at exactly the same rate. Flat concentrations, busy molecules."}},{"k":"eq_idea3_t","v":{"s":"Nothing has stopped"}},{"k":"eq_idea4_b","v":{"s":"Cooling raises the yield here, but near 300 K the reaction is far too slow to be useful. Industry accepts a smaller K at ~700 K, then adds a catalyst for speed and pressure for yield."}},{"k":"eq_idea4_t","v":{"s":"Why Haber runs hot anyway"}},{"k":"eq_inert","v":{"s":"Add argon (same V)"}},{"k":"eq_inert_why","v":{"s":"Argon takes no part in the reaction. At constant volume the concentrations of N₂, H₂ and NH₃ are exactly what they were, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts."}},{"k":"eq_k_unchanged","v":{"s":"and K has not moved"}},{"k":"eq_model_note","v":{"s":"Model: ideal gases; K(T) from the van ’t Hoff equation with ΔH° held constant. Real K values drift from this by up to a factor of ~2 at high temperature, and real plants run near 200 bar to buy yield back — this box runs at whatever pressure its moles, volume and temperature give."}},{"k":"eq_nh3_share","v":{"s":"NH₃ share"}},{"k":"eq_no_shift","v":{"s":"No shift: Q still equals K, so the composition does not change."}},{"k":"eq_now","v":{"s":"now"}},{"k":"eq_of_mixture","v":{"s":"of the mixture"}},{"k":"eq_q_vs_k","v":{"s":"Right after the stress"}},{"k":"eq_remove_nh3","v":{"s":"Remove half the NH₃"}},{"k":"eq_reset","v":{"s":"Reset: 1 mol N₂ + 3 mol H₂, 10 L, 700 K"}},{"k":"eq_shift_sentence","v":{"s":"The reaction runs"}},{"k":"eq_sim_title","v":{"s":"🧪 Stress the Haber process and watch it answer"}},{"k":"eq_status_idle","v":{"s":"The mixture below is at equilibrium. Pick a stress and watch which way it runs — and by how much."}},{"k":"eq_temp_why","v":{"s":"Temperature is the ONE stress that changes K itself"}},{"k":"gl_box_sr","v":{"s":"Particle box"}},{"k":"gl_box_sr_2","v":{"s":"molecules drawn for"}},{"k":"gl_box_sr_3","v":{"s":"in a container of"}},{"k":"gl_box_sr_4","v":{"s":"The trail behind each molecule is its speed: root-mean-square speed is"}},{"k":"gl_box_sr_5","v":{"s":"metres per second at"}},{"k":"gl_calculated","v":{"s":"calculated"}},{"k":"gl_gas_co2","v":{"s":"Carbon dioxide"}},{"k":"gl_gas_he","v":{"s":"Helium"}},{"k":"gl_gas_n2","v":{"s":"Nitrogen"}},{"k":"gl_gas_o2","v":{"s":"Oxygen"}},{"k":"gl_graham_body","v":{"s":"At one temperature every gas carries the same average kinetic energy — so the light molecules must move faster to match the heavy ones. Helium is seven times lighter than nitrogen and moves about 2.6 times quicker, which is exactly √7. That is Graham's law, and it is why the helium balloon is on the floor by morning while the air-filled one is not."}},{"k":"gl_graham_title","v":{"s":"🏃 Why helium escapes a balloon first"}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_body","v":{"s":"Heat a balloon from 25 °C to 50 °C. You doubled the number on the thermometer, so the balloon doubles in size?"}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_right","v":{"s":"Using K"}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_right_note","v":{"s":"The real answer: about 8% bigger."}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_title","v":{"s":"🌡 The mistake that eats the most exam marks"}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_why","v":{"s":"Celsius has its zero in the wrong place. Doubling a Celsius reading does not double anything physical, because 0 °C is not \"no thermal energy\" — it is just where water freezes. Kelvin starts at true zero, so in Kelvin the ratios mean what they say. Every gas law wants Kelvin."}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_wrong","v":{"s":"Using °C"}},{"k":"gl_kelvin_wrong_note","v":{"s":"Predicts the balloon doubles."}},{"k":"gl_molar_volume","v":{"s":"Molar volume"}},{"k":"gl_molar_volume_note","v":{"s":"At 0 °C and 1 bar every ideal gas gives 22.71 L/mol — helium and carbon dioxide alike, because the law never asks what the molecule is."}},{"k":"gl_moles","v":{"s":"Amount"}},{"k":"gl_pressure","v":{"s":"Pressure"}},{"k":"gl_pressure_is","v":{"s":"Pressure is not molecules shoving each other apart. It is how often, and how hard, they strike the walls. Squeeze the box and each molecule simply reaches a wall sooner — it never speeds up. Only temperature changes speed."}},{"k":"gl_real_body","v":{"s":"The ideal law assumes molecules take up no room and ignore each other. Both assumptions hold well in a roomy, warm container and fail in a cramped, cold one. Shrink the volume or drop the temperature and watch the gap open — it opens fastest for CO₂, whose molecules attract each other most."}},{"k":"gl_real_gap","v":{"s":"gap"}},{"k":"gl_real_ideal","v":{"s":"Ideal"}},{"k":"gl_real_title","v":{"s":"⚠ Where the ideal gas law stops telling the truth"}},{"k":"gl_real_vdw","v":{"s":"Van der Waals"}},{"k":"gl_rms","v":{"s":"average molecular speed"}},{"k":"gl_sim_title","v":{"s":"💨 Set any three. The fourth is not yours to choose."}},{"k":"gl_solve_for","v":{"s":"Solve for"}},{"k":"gl_temperature","v":{"s":"Temperature"}},{"k":"gl_volume","v":{"s":"Volume"}},{"k":"gl_vs_n2","v":{"s":"vs N₂"}},{"k":"gl_which_gas","v":{"s":"Which gas"}}],"id":549}},{"k":"money","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_enter","v":{"s":"ENTER"}},{"k":"us_dollar","v":{"s":"US Dollar"}},{"k":"penny","v":{"s":"Penny"}},{"k":"nickel","v":{"s":"Nickel"}},{"k":"dime","v":{"s":"Dime"}},{"k":"quarter","v":{"s":"Quarter"}},{"k":"half_dollar","v":{"s":"Half Dollar"}},{"k":"dollar_coin","v":{"s":"Dollar Coin"}},{"k":"1_bill","v":{"s":"$1 Bill"}},{"k":"5_bill","v":{"s":"$5 Bill"}},{"k":"10_bill","v":{"s":"$10 Bill"}},{"k":"20_bill","v":{"s":"$20 Bill"}},{"k":"50_bill","v":{"s":"$50 Bill"}},{"k":"100_bill","v":{"s":"$100 Bill"}},{"k":"euro","v":{"s":"Euro"}},{"k":"1_cent","v":{"s":"1 Cent"}},{"k":"2_cent","v":{"s":"2 Cent"}},{"k":"5_cent","v":{"s":"5 Cent"}},{"k":"10_cent","v":{"s":"10 Cent"}},{"k":"20_cent","v":{"s":"20 Cent"}},{"k":"50_cent","v":{"s":"50 Cent"}},{"k":"british_pound","v":{"s":"British Pound"}},{"k":"canadian_dollar","v":{"s":"Canadian Dollar"}},{"k":"loonie","v":{"s":"Loonie"}},{"k":"toonie","v":{"s":"Toonie"}},{"k":"japanese_yen","v":{"s":"Japanese Yen"}},{"k":"mexican_peso","v":{"s":"Mexican Peso"}},{"k":"australian_dollar","v":{"s":"Australian Dollar"}},{"k":"in