t_fraction","v":{"s":"Plot fraction"}},{"k":"plot","v":{"s":"→ Plot"}},{"k":"type_a_decimal_plot_it","v":{"s":"Type a decimal, plot it:"}},{"k":"decimal_value","v":{"s":"Decimal value"}},{"k":"plot_decimal","v":{"s":"Plot decimal"}},{"k":"plot_2","v":{"s":"→ Plot"}},{"k":"quick_conversions_click_to_plot","v":{"s":"📌 Quick conversions (click to plot):"}},{"k":"money_view","v":{"s":"💵 Money view"}},{"k":"a_quarter_is_0_25_1_4_of_a_dollar_a_di","v":{"s":"A quarter is $0.25 = 1/4 of a dollar. A dime is $0.10 = 1/10. Coins are decimal-fraction practice."}},{"k":"place_value_expansion","v":{"s":"🔍 Place-value expansion"}},{"k":"every_decimal_place_is_a_fraction_1st_","v":{"s":"Every decimal place IS a fraction. 1st place: tenths, 2nd: hundredths, 3rd: thousandths."}},{"k":"compare_two_values","v":{"s":"↔ Compare two values"}},{"k":"second_value","v":{"s":"Second value:"}},{"k":"second_value_2","v":{"s":"Second value"}},{"k":"line_range","v":{"s":"Line range:"}},{"k":"line_minimum","v":{"s":"Line minimum"}},{"k":"line_maximum","v":{"s":"Line maximum"}},{"k":"0_to_1","v":{"s":"0 to 1"}},{"k":"0_to_2","v":{"s":"0 to 2"}},{"k":"1_to_1","v":{"s":"-1 to 1"}},{"k":"reset_frac_dec_tab_to_defaults","v":{"s":"Reset Frac Dec tab to defaults"}},{"k":"reset_frac_dec","v":{"s":"↺ Reset Frac↔Dec"}},{"k":"why_fractions_and_decimals_are_the_sam","v":{"s":"💡 Why fractions and decimals are the same thing"}},{"k":"a_fraction_is_a_division_problem","v":{"s":"A fraction IS a division problem. "}},{"k":"3_4_means_3_divided_by_4_type_3_4_into","v":{"s":"3/4 means \"3 divided by 4.\" Type 3 ÷ 4 into any calculator: 0.75. The slash and ÷ mean the same thing."}},{"k":"a_decimal_is_a_fraction_with_a_hidden_","v":{"s":"A decimal IS a fraction with a hidden denominator. "}},{"k":"0_6_means_6_tenths_6_10_0_25_means_25_","v":{"s":"0.6 means \"6 tenths,\" 6/10. 0.25 means \"25 hundredths,\" 25/100, which simplifies to 1/4."}},{"k":"the_number_line_proves_it","v":{"s":"The number line proves it. "}},{"k":"the_point_at_1_4_is_the_same_point_as_","v":{"s":"The point at 1/4 is the same point as the point at 0.25. Same spot, two names."}},{"k":"some_terminate_some_repeat","v":{"s":"Some terminate, some repeat. "}},{"k":"1_2_0_5_stops_1_4_0_25_stops_1_3_0_333","v":{"s":"1/2 = 0.5 (stops). 1/4 = 0.25 (stops). 1/3 = 0.333… (repeats forever). The denominator decides: if it factors into only 2s and 5s, it terminates. Otherwise it repeats. Watch the ↻ symbol on the decimal card."}},{"k":"ai_number_line_tutor","v":{"s":"🤖 AI Number Line Tutor"}},{"k":"toggle_number_line_option","v":{"s":"Toggle number line option"}},{"k":"ask_about_number_lines","v":{"s":"Ask about number lines..."}},{"k":"ask_question","v":{"s":"Ask question"}},{"k":"explore","v":{"s":"Explore"}},{"k":"challenges","v":{"s":"Challenges"}},{"k":"skip_count","v":{"s":"Skip Count"}},{"k":"frac_dec","v":{"s":"Frac ↔ Dec"}},{"k":"compare","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"number_line","v":{"s":"📏 Number Line"}},{"k":"number_line_sections","v":{"s":"Number Line sections"}},{"k":"explore_the_visual_home_for_number_sen","v":{"s":"Explore — the visual home for number sense"}},{"k":"numbers_as_positions_on_a_line_not_jus","v":{"s":"Numbers as positions on a line, not just symbols. Negatives mirror across zero. Fractions sit BETWEEN integers; decimals are the same idea finer-grained. Common Core 2.MD.6, 3.NF.2, 6.NS.6."}},{"k":"challenges_estimate_locate_compare","v":{"s":"Challenges — estimate, locate, compare"}},{"k":"where_does_7_8_sit_what_about_2_4_esti","v":{"s":"Where does 7/8 sit? What about −2.4? Estimation builds magnitude sense. Research by Siegler and colleagues links number-line estimation precision to later math achievement."}},{"k":"skip_count_the_bridge_to_multiplicatio","v":{"s":"Skip Count — the bridge to multiplication"}},{"k":"2_4_6_8_the_2_times_table_walking_skip","v":{"s":"2, 4, 6, 8… = the 2 times table walking. Skip counting by 5s and 10s pre-builds money and time. Counting backward by 3s pre-builds subtraction and division. The line shows the rhythm."}},{"k":"fractions_decimals_same_point_two_name","v":{"s":"Fractions ↔ Decimals, same point, two names"}},{"k":"a_fraction_is_a_division_problem_a_dec","v":{"s":"A fraction is a division problem. A decimal is a fraction with a hidden denominator of 10, 100, 1000… 1/4 and 0.25 are the SAME spot on the line. Common Core 4.NF.6, 4.NF.7, 5.NBT.3."}},{"k":"compare_magnitude_discovery","v":{"s":"Compare — magnitude discovery"}},{"k":"two_fractions_four_possible_relationsh","v":{"s":"Two fractions, four possible relationships: equal, touching the ½ landmark, A smaller, or B smaller. Sweep the sliders, log what you notice, and write your own comparison rule."}},{"k":"equivalent_fractions","v":{"s":"🟰 Equivalent fractions"}},{"k":"one_is_the_landmark","v":{"s":"⭐ One is the ½ landmark"}},{"k":"a_b","v":{"s":"🔼 A < B"}},{"k":"a_b_2","v":{"s":"🔽 A > B"}},{"k":"magnitude_discovery","v":{"s":"🔄 Magnitude discovery"}},{"k":"adjust_the_numerators_and_denominators","v":{"s":"Adjust the numerators and denominators of two fractions. The widget tells you one of four discrete relationships. No score, no reveal — just sweep and notice."}},{"k":"a_num","v":{"s":"A num"}},{"k":"a_den","v":{"s":"A den"}},{"k":"b_num","v":{"s":"B num"}},{"k":"b_den","v":{"s":"B den"}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_free_text_when_do_two_fract","v":{"s":"Hypothesis (free text): When do two fractions equal each other? When is one near ½?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"find_two_fractions_with_different_nume","v":{"s":"Find two fractions with different numerators and denominators that are equal."}},{"k":"what_makes_a_fraction_close_to_what_ab","v":{"s":"What makes a fraction close to ½? What about close to 1?"}},{"k":"when_are_two_fractions_easy_to_compare","v":{"s":"When are two fractions easy to compare? When are they hard?"}},{"k":