b_menu","v":{"s":"Back to NutritionLab menu"}},{"k":"menu","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_discussion_questions_sta","v":{"s":"Teacher Notes — discussion questions, standards alignment, and extension activities"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher Notes — click to expand"}},{"k":"print_this_module_page_includes_teache","v":{"s":"Print this module page (includes Teacher Notes)"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"ngss_cte_standards","v":{"s":"NGSS / CTE Standards"}},{"k":"discussion_questions","v":{"s":"Discussion Questions"}},{"k":"watch_for_these_misconceptions","v":{"s":"Watch for these misconceptions"}},{"k":"extension_activity","v":{"s":"Extension Activity"}},{"k":"sources_cited","v":{"s":"Sources cited"}},{"k":"my_nutrition_kit","v":{"s":"My Nutrition Kit"}},{"k":"127_personal_evidence_based_tools","v":{"s":"127 personal, evidence-based tools"}},{"k":"build_your_own_nutrition_practice_meal","v":{"s":"Build your own nutrition practice: meal logs, hydration, goals, body-listening, cooking, cultural foods, sports fuel, mental-health x food, NEDA-aligned recovery support, Maine seasonal eating, and more. Physiology-first, never restriction. All data stays in your browser."}},{"k":"macronutrient_lab","v":{"s":"Macronutrient Lab"}},{"k":"build_a_plate_see_what_your_body_uses_","v":{"s":"Build a plate, see what your body uses it for"}},{"k":"click_foods_to_add_them_to_a_plate_wat","v":{"s":"Click foods to add them to a plate. Watch the carbs, protein, fat, and fiber totals update from real USDA values. Get a physiology readout: which body systems each meal supports — muscle repair, brain fuel, oxygen delivery, gut health. No calorie targets, no good/bad food labels."}},{"k":"micronutrient_atlas","v":{"s":"Micronutrient Atlas"}},{"k":"13_vitamins_15_minerals_omega_3","v":{"s":"13 vitamins, 15 minerals, omega-3"}},{"k":"click_any_nutrient_to_see_what_it_does","v":{"s":"Click any nutrient to see what it does, where to find it, and what happens when you don't get enough. Maine-relevant: vitamin D (winter sun problem), omega-3 (Maine fisheries), iron (high-need group is teens). Every entry sourced from NIH ODS Fact Sheets."}},{"k":"food_label_reader","v":{"s":"Food Label Reader"}},{"k":"decode_a_nutrition_facts_panel_like_a_","v":{"s":"Decode a Nutrition Facts panel like a pro"}},{"k":"real_style_nutrition_facts_panels_from","v":{"s":"Real-style Nutrition Facts panels from common foods. Decode serving-size traps, % Daily Value, added sugars, sodium. Progressive challenges from a simple granola bar up to a multi-serving frozen meal that lies about its real serving count."}},{"k":"energy_metabolism","v":{"s":"Energy & Metabolism"}},{"k":"atp_mitochondria_and_your_brain","v":{"s":"ATP, mitochondria, and your brain"}},{"k":"where_your_body_s_energy_actually_come","v":{"s":"Where your body's energy actually comes from. ATP cycle, why brains use ~20% of daily energy, what BMR / TDEE actually mean. Framed as physiology, not weight management."}},{"k":"digestion_walkthrough","v":{"s":"Digestion Walkthrough"}},{"k":"mouth_stomach_intestines","v":{"s":"Mouth → stomach → intestines"}},{"k":"where_each_macronutrient_is_broken_dow","v":{"s":"Where each macronutrient is broken down. Hormones (insulin, glucagon, leptin, ghrelin) and what they actually do."}},{"k":"common_nutrition_myths","v":{"s":"Common Nutrition Myths"}},{"k":"8_10_myths_debunked_with_citations","v":{"s":"8–10 myths debunked with citations"}},{"k":"detoxes_superfoods_gluten_free_for_non","v":{"s":"Detoxes, \"superfoods,\" gluten-free for non-celiac, vitamin megadosing, fat-free craze, organic = healthier. NIH and Harvard sourced."}},{"k":"food_mental_health","v":{"s":"Food & Mental Health"}},{"k":"real_research_careful_framing","v":{"s":"Real research, careful framing"}},{"k":"omega_3_depression_iron_adhd_style_fat","v":{"s":"Omega-3 + depression, iron + ADHD-style fatigue, sugar crashes, caffeine + adolescent anxiety, gut-brain axis. Contributing factors, not cures."}},{"k":"eating_disorder_awareness","v":{"s":"Eating Disorder Awareness"}},{"k":"neda_aligned_opt_in_content_warning","v":{"s":"NEDA-aligned · opt-in content warning"}},{"k":"spectrum_of_disordered_eating_signs_to","v":{"s":"Spectrum of disordered eating, signs to watch for, online content red flags + algorithm-awareness, how to support a friend, what to do if it's you, recovery paths. NEDA + Surgeon General sourced. Gated by content warning."}},{"k":"maine_food_reality","v":{"s":"Maine Food Reality"}},{"k":"winter_fisheries_food_deserts","v":{"s":"Winter, fisheries, food deserts"}},{"k":"maine_winters_and_vitamin_d_maine_fish","v":{"s":"Maine winters and vitamin D, Maine fisheries (omega-3 powerhouse), Aroostook + Washington food deserts, school meals + SNAP basics, Good Shepherd Food Bank."}},{"k":"career_pathways","v":{"s":"Career Pathways"}},{"k":"rdn_public_health_food_science","v":{"s":"RDN, public health, food science"}},{"k":"registered_dietitian_master_s_1200hr_i","v":{"s":"Registered Dietitian (Master's + 1200hr internship), public health nutritionist, food scientist, sports nutritionist, school nutrition director. UMaine + Husson programs."}},{"k":"build_a_maine_day","v":{"s":"Build a Maine Day"}},{"k":"pick_4_meals_see_what_your_day_adds_up","v":{"s":"Pick 4 meals → see what your day adds up to"}},{"k":"pick_one_food_for_breakfast_lunch_dinn","v":{"s":"Pick one food for breakfast, lunch, dinner, and snack from a Maine-realistic short list (Maine wild blueberries, Atlantic salmon, lobster roll, fortified cereal, etc.). Watch six nutrient bars (protein, fiber, vitamin D, omega-3, iron, calcium) update against approximate adolescent DRIs. Final summary names deficits + strengths and ties them to Maine context (winter vit D, fisheries omega-3, adolescent iron)."}},{"k":"deficiency_detective","v":{"s":"Deficiency Detective"}},{"k":"10_vignettes_name_the_missing_nutrient","v":{"s":"10 vignettes — name the missing nutrient"}},{"k":"10_clinical_vignettes_identify_which_o","v":{"s":"10 clinical vignettes; identify which of 6 nutrients (vitamin D, iron, B12, folate, calcium, iodine) is most likely missing. Vignettes target the canonical real-world deficiency cases: Maine winter runner with stress fractures, menstruating teen with ADHD-like inattention, strict vegan with neuropathy, preconception folate, lactose-intolerant teen with low bone density, PPI-induced B12 deficiency."}},{"k":"hydration_lab","v":{"s":"Hydration Lab"}},{"k":"water_your_body_and_the_science_of_sta","v":{"s":"Water, your body, and the science of staying hydrated"}},{"k":"daily_needs_calculator_nam_adequate_in","v":{"s":"Daily-needs calculator (NAM Adequate Intake by age/sex), self-check quiz with the 8-point urine-color scale (ACSM/Armstrong), beverage comparison (water vs sports drink vs soda — sodium, sugar, hydration efficacy), and a sweat-rate calculator for student athletes (NATA standard). Physiology-first framing: hydration as body function, never appetite suppression."}},{"k":"macro_inquiry","v":{"s":"Macro Inquiry"}},{"k":"predict_energy_state_from_macro_ratios","v":{"s":"Predict energy state from macro ratios"}},{"k":"move_four_sliders_carb_protein_fat_fib","v":{"s":"Move four sliders (carb / protein / fat / fiber). Predict how the macro pattern shifts the energy state — ketogenic, low-carb, balanced, high-carb, sugar-dominant. Includes a satiety estimate and a fiber-adjusted glycemic index proxy. Discrete states, no score, no reveal — you mark your own understanding."}},{"k":"soon","v":{"s":"Soon"}},{"k":"nutrition_science_from_a_physiology_fi","v":{"s":"Nutrition science from a physiology-first lens. What your body uses food for, why micronutrients matter, how to read a label, and the careers built on this knowledge. No calorie targets, no good/bad food binaries — just real biology."}},{"k":"a_note_on_how_this_tool_talks_about_fo","v":{"s":"A note on how this tool talks about food"}},{"k":"no_food_is_bad_no_body_type_is_right_t","v":{"s":"No food is \"bad.\" No body type is \"right.\" This tool covers nutrition science the way a registered dietitian or doctor would — focused on what nutrients do for your body and brain, not what to avoid. If you or a friend are struggling with eating, the NEDA helpline is "}},{"k":"text_neda_to_741741","v":{"s":" (text \"NEDA\" to 741741)."}},{"k":"core_simulators","v":{"s":"Core Simulators"}},{"k":"quick_labs","v":{"s":"Quick Labs"}},{"k":"all_10_modules_live_physiology_first_n","v":{"s":"All 10 modules live — physiology-first nutrition science from a school-psych lens. Explore in any order."}},{"k":"add_foods_to_see_how_your_meal_s_energ","v":{"s":"Add foods to see how your meal's energy is split between carbs, protein, and fat."}},{"k":"carbs","v":{"s":"Carbs"}},{"k":"protein","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"kcal_in_plate","v":{"s":"kcal in plate"}},{"k":"energy_split","v":{"s":"Energy split"}},{"k":"each_gram_of_carb_or_protein_gives_4_k","v":{"s":"Each gram of carb or protein gives ~4 kcal; each gram of fat gives ~9 kcal. The split varies normally across meals — there's no single \"right\" ratio."}},{"k":"carbs_2","v":{"s":"Carbs"}},{"k":"rda_min_for_adolescents_minimum_to_fue","v":{"s":"RDA min for adolescents — minimum to fuel the brain."}},{"k":"protein_2","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"roughly_0_85_g_kg_body_weight_varies_w","v":{"s":"Roughly 0.85 g/kg body weight; varies widely with body size."}},{"k":"25_35_of_daily_energy_dietary_fat_is_e","v":{"s":"25–35% of daily energy. Dietary fat is essential, not optional."}},{"k":"fiber","v":{"s":"Fiber"}},{"k":"most_us_teens_get_only_half_this_fiber","v":{"s":"Most US teens get only ~half this. Fiber feeds gut microbes."}},{"k":"how_this_plate_stacks_against_a_typica","v":{"s":"How this plate stacks against a typical day"}},{"k":"informational_only_not_a_goal_per_meal","v":{"s":"Informational only — not a goal per meal."}},{"k":"bar_chart_comparing_this_meal_s_nutrie","v":{"s":"Bar chart comparing this meal's nutrients to typical adolescent daily reference intakes."}},{"k":"what_these_reference_values_mean","v":{"s":"What these reference values mean"}},{"k":"reference_intakes_from_nih_usda_for_ag","v":{"s":"Reference Intakes from NIH / USDA for ages 14–18. Real needs vary by body size, sex, and activity. This is a science-literacy reference, not a meal target."}},{"k":"energy","v":{"s":"Energy"}},{"k":"carbs_3","v":{"s":"Carbs"}},{"k":"protein_3","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"fiber_2","v":{"s":"Fiber"}},{"k":"compare_two_foods_side_by_side","v":{"s":"⚖️ Compare two foods side-by-side"}},{"k":"no_good_bad_just_what_each_one_brings","v":{"s":"No good/bad — just what each one brings."}},{"k":"food_a","v":{"s":"◆ Food A"}},{"k":"food_a_2","v":{"s":"Food A"}},{"k":"food_b","v":{"s":"◇ Food B"}},{"k":"food_b_2","v":{"s":"Food B"}},{"k":"reading_the_chart","v":{"s":"Reading the chart: "}},{"k":"each_row_shows_one_nutrient_longer_bar","v":{"s":"Each row shows one nutrient. Longer bar = more of that nutrient per typical serving. Two foods can both be excellent for different reasons — there are no winners or losers here, just different physiological roles."}},{"k":"includes_protein_your_body_uses_these_","v":{"s":"Includes protein — your body uses these amino acids to build muscle, make enzymes, and repair tissue."}},{"k":"includes_omega_3s_epa_dha_harvard_rese","v":{"s":"Includes omega-3s (EPA/DHA) — Harvard research links these to brain health and lower inflammation. AHA recommends 2 fatty-fish meals per week."}},{"k":"includes_iron_supports_hemoglobin_oxyg","v":{"s":"Includes iron — supports hemoglobin (oxygen transport in blood) and steady energy. Especially important for menstruating teens."}},{"k":"includes_vitamin_c_supports_immune_fun","v":{"s":"Includes vitamin C — supports immune function, builds collagen, and helps your body absorb iron from plant foods."}},{"k":"includes_calcium_supports_bones_muscle","v":{"s":"Includes calcium — supports bones, muscle contraction, and nerve signaling. Adolescent years are peak bone-building years."}},{"k":"includes_vitamin_d_supports_calcium_ab","v":{"s":"Includes vitamin D — supports calcium absorption, bones, and immune function. Especially valuable in Maine winters."}},{"k":"includes_folate_b9_supports_dna_synthe","v":{"s":"Includes folate (B9) — supports DNA synthesis and red blood cell formation."}},{"k":"includes_b12_supports_nerve_function_a","v":{"s":"Includes B12 — supports nerve function and red blood cells. B12 only comes from animal foods (or fortified products) — vegans need a supplement."}},{"k":"includes_potassium_supports_heart_rhyt","v":{"s":"Includes potassium — supports heart rhythm, muscle contraction, and healthy blood pressure."}},{"k":"includes_magnesium_most_us_adults_unde","v":{"s":"Includes magnesium — most US adults under-consume; supports muscle / nerve function and energy production."}},{"k":"includes_plenty_of_fiber_feeds_gut_bac","v":{"s":"Includes plenty of fiber — feeds gut bacteria, slows blood-sugar spikes, supports steady energy."}},{"k":"includes_choline_many_americans_under_","v":{"s":"Includes choline — many Americans under-consume; supports brain development and liver function."}},{"k":"all_foods","v":{"s":"All foods"}},{"k":"fruit","v":{"s":"Fruit"}},{"k":"vegetables","v":{"s":"Vegetables"}},{"k":"protein_4","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"legumes","v":{"s":"Legumes"}},{"k":"grains","v":{"s":"Grains"}},{"k":"macronutrient_lab_2","v":{"s":"Macronutrient Lab"}},{"k":"build_a_plate_see_what_your_body_uses__2","v":{"s":"Build a plate. See what your body uses it for."}},{"k":"click_any_food_below_to_add_it_to_your","v":{"s":"Click any food below to add it to your plate. Watch the macronutrient totals update from real USDA values. The benefit readout shows which body systems your meal supports — muscle, brain, blood, bones, gut. There is no calorie target. There is no \"right\" plate. Different bodies need different things, and your needs change throughout the day."}},{"k":"filter_by_category","v":{"s":"Filter by category"}},{"k":"food_category","v":{"s":"Food category"}},{"k":"tap_a_food_to_add_it_to_your_plate","v":{"s":"Tap a food to add it to your plate"}},{"k":"clear_plate","v":{"s":"Clear plate"}},{"k":"your_plate_is_empty_add_foods_above_to","v":{"s":"Your plate is empty. Add foods above to begin."}},{"k":"remove","v":{"s":"✕ Remove"}},{"k":"carbs_4","v":{"s":"Carbs"}},{"k":"protein_5","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"fiber_3","v":{"s":"Fiber"}},{"k":"energy_2","v":{"s":"Energy"}},{"k":"what_your_body_uses_this_meal_for","v":{"s":"What your body uses this meal for"}},{"k":"add_a_few_more_items_to_see_what_nutri","v":{"s":"Add a few more items to see what nutrients this meal contributes."}},{"k":"vitamins_13","v":{"s":"Vitamins (13)"}},{"k":"minerals_15","v":{"s":"Minerals (15)"}},{"k":"essential_fatty_acids","v":{"s":"Essential Fatty Acids"}},{"k":"what_it_does","v":{"s":"What it does"}},{"k":"where_to_find_it","v":{"s":"Where to find it"}},{"k":"when_you_don_t_get_enough","v":{"s":"When you don't get enough"}},{"k":"recommended_intake","v":{"s":"Recommended intake"}},{"k":"micronutrient_atlas_2","v":{"s":"Micronutrient Atlas"}},{"k":"13_vitamins_15_minerals_and_the_essent","v":{"s":"13 vitamins, 15 minerals, and the essential fatty acids"}},{"k":"these_are_the_nutrients_your_body_need","v":{"s":"These are the nutrients your body needs in small amounts but cannot make on its own. Click any card to see what it does, where to find it, and what happens when you don't get enough. Sources cited inline from NIH Office of Dietary Supplements."}},{"k":"vitamin_d","v":{"s":"Vitamin D"}},{"k":"iron","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"magnesium","v":{"s":"Magnesium"}},{"k":"vitamin_b12","v":{"s":"Vitamin B12"}},{"k":"calcium","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"watch_these_in_maine","v":{"s":"Watch these in Maine"}},{"k":"nutrients_adolescents_most_often_under","v":{"s":"— nutrients adolescents most often under-consume"}},{"k":"function","v":{"s":"Function"}},{"k":"without_it","v":{"s":"Without it"}},{"k":"nutrient_categories","v":{"s":"Nutrient categories"}},{"k":"click_any_nutrient_above_to_see_its_fu","v":{"s":"Click any nutrient above to see its function, food sources, and what happens when you don't get enough."}},{"k":"granola_bar","v":{"s":"Granola Bar"}},{"k":"total_fat","v":{"s":"Total Fat"}},{"k":"saturated_fat","v":{"s":"Saturated Fat"}},{"k":"trans_fat","v":{"s":"Trans Fat"}},{"k":"cholesterol","v":{"s":"Cholesterol"}},{"k":"sodium","v":{"s":"Sodium"}},{"k":"total_carbs","v":{"s":"Total Carbs"}},{"k":"dietary_fiber","v":{"s":"Dietary Fiber"}},{"k":"total_sugars","v":{"s":"Total Sugars"}},{"k":"includes_6_g_added_sugars","v":{"s":"Includes 6 g Added Sugars"}},{"k":"protein_6","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"vitamin_d_2","v":{"s":"Vitamin D"}},{"k":"calcium_2","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"iron_2","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"potassium","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"potato_chips_small_bag","v":{"s":"Potato Chips (small bag)"}},{"k":"total_fat_2","v":{"s":"Total Fat"}},{"k":"saturated_fat_2","v":{"s":"Saturated Fat"}},{"k":"trans_fat_2","v":{"s":"Trans Fat"}},{"k":"cholesterol_2","v":{"s":"Cholesterol"}},{"k":"sodium_2","v":{"s":"Sodium"}},{"k":"total_carbs_2","v":{"s":"Total Carbs"}},{"k":"dietary_fiber_2","v":{"s":"Dietary Fiber"}},{"k":"total_sugars_2","v":{"s":"Total Sugars"}},{"k":"includes_0_g_added_sugars","v":{"s":"Includes 0 g Added Sugars"}},{"k":"protein_7","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"vitamin_d_3","v":{"s":"Vitamin D"}},{"k":"calcium_3","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"iron_3","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"potassium_2","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"frozen_family_lasagna","v":{"s":"Frozen \"Family\" Lasagna"}},{"k":"total_fat_3","v":{"s":"Total Fat"}},{"k":"saturated_fat_3","v":{"s":"Saturated Fat"}},{"k":"trans_fat_3","v":{"s":"Trans Fat"}},{"k":"cholesterol_3","v":{"s":"Cholesterol"}},{"k":"sodium_3","v":{"s":"Sodium"}},{"k":"total_carbs_3","v":{"s":"Total Carbs"}},{"k":"dietary_fiber_3","v":{"s":"Dietary Fiber"}},{"k":"total_sugars_3","v":{"s":"Total Sugars"}},{"k":"includes_0_g_added_sugars_2","v":{"s":"Includes 0 g Added Sugars"}},{"k":"protein_8","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"vitamin_d_4","v":{"s":"Vitamin D"}},{"k":"calcium_4","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"iron_4","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"potassium_3","v":{"s":"Potassium"}},{"k":"plain_vs_vanilla_greek_yogurt","v":{"s":"Plain vs Vanilla Greek Yogurt"}},{"k":"total_fat_4","v":{"s":"Total Fat"}},{"k":"total_carbs_4","v":{"s":"Total Carbs"}},{"k":"total_sugars_4","v":{"s":"Total Sugars"}},{"k":"includes_14_g_added_sugars","v":{"s":"Includes 14 g Added Sugars"}},{"k":"protein_9","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"total_fat_5","v":{"s":"Total Fat"}},{"k":"total_carbs_5","v":{"s":"Total Carbs"}},{"k":"total_sugars_5","v":{"s":"Total Sugars"}},{"k":"includes_0_g_added_sugars_3","v":{"s":"Includes 0 g Added Sugars"}},{"k":"protein_10","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"calcium_5","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"vitamin_b12_2","v":{"s":"Vitamin B12"}},{"k":"nutrition_facts","v":{"s":"Nutrition Facts"}},{"k":"serving_size","v":{"s":"Serving size"}},{"k":"amount_per_serving","v":{"s":"Amount per serving"}},{"k":"calories","v":{"s":"Calories"}},{"k":"daily_value","v":{"s":"% Daily Value*"}},{"k":"the_daily_value_dv_tells_you_how_much_","v":{"s":"* The % Daily Value (DV) tells you how much a nutrient in a serving of food contributes to a daily diet. 2,000 calories a day is used for general nutrition advice."}},{"k":"food_label_reader_2","v":{"s":"Food Label Reader"}},{"k":"read_a_nutrition_facts_panel_like_a_re","v":{"s":"Read a Nutrition Facts panel like a registered dietitian"}},{"k":"the_nutrition_facts_panel_is_required_","v":{"s":"The Nutrition Facts panel is required by the FDA on every packaged food. It tells the truth — but the manufacturer chooses how to slice that truth. Common tricks: dividing one bag into \"2 servings\" so per-serving numbers look smaller, putting \"0 g trans fat\" on something that's 49% saturated fat, calling something \"fat-free\" while it's loaded with added sugar. Once you can read these labels, you can't un-read them."}},{"k":"plain_version","v":{"s":"Plain version"}},{"k":"vanilla_flavored_version","v":{"s":"Vanilla flavored version"}},{"k":"decode_this_label","v":{"s":"Decode this label"}},{"k":"challenge_complete","v":{"s":"✓ Challenge complete"}},{"k":"you_decoded_this_label_correctly_move_","v":{"s":"You decoded this label correctly. Move to the next challenge."}},{"k":"decoder","v":{"s":"Decoder"}},{"k":"all_five_challenges_decoded","v":{"s":"✓ All five challenges decoded"}},{"k":"1_atp_mitochondria","v":{"s":"1. ATP & Mitochondria"}},{"k":"2_three_energy_systems","v":{"s":"2. Three Energy Systems"}},{"k":"3_where_energy_goes","v":{"s":"3. Where Energy Goes"}},{"k":"atp_your_cellular_energy_currency","v":{"s":"ATP — your cellular energy currency"}},{"k":"every_cell_holds_50g_of_atp_and_recycl","v":{"s":"Every cell holds ~50g of ATP and recycles it ~1,000× per day."}},{"k":"three_energy_systems_run_side_by_side","v":{"s":"Three energy systems run side by side"}},{"k":"pcr_glycolysis_oxidative_each_peaks_at","v":{"s":"PCr · glycolysis · oxidative — each peaks at a different duration."}},{"k":"where_your_energy_actually_goes_at_res","v":{"s":"Where your energy actually goes at rest"}},{"k":"brain_20_of_resting_energy_at_only_2_o","v":{"s":"Brain ≈ 20% of resting energy at only ~2% of body weight."}},{"k":"constantly_processing_nutrients_detoxi","v":{"s":"Constantly processing nutrients, detoxifying, making proteins."}},{"k":"even_at_rest_muscles_maintain_tone_and","v":{"s":"Even at rest, muscles maintain tone and run baseline metabolism."}},{"k":"neurons_fire_constantly_brain_uses_20_","v":{"s":"Neurons fire constantly. Brain uses ~20% of resting energy despite being ~2% of body weight."}},{"k":"beats_100_000_times_day_never_stops","v":{"s":"Beats ~100,000 times/day, never stops."}},{"k":"filter_your_entire_blood_volume_60_tim","v":{"s":"Filter your entire blood volume ~60 times/day."}},{"k":"skin_gut_immune_cells_fat_tissue_nervo","v":{"s":"Skin, gut, immune cells, fat tissue, nervous system."}},{"k":"slightly_below_bmr_brain_and_organs_ar","v":{"s":"Slightly below BMR. Brain and organs are still busy."}},{"k":"bmr_baseline_plus_a_tiny_bit_for_postu","v":{"s":"BMR baseline plus a tiny bit for posture."}},{"k":"most_accessible_activity_for_nearly_an","v":{"s":"Most accessible activity for nearly any body."}},{"k":"joint_friendly_sustained_exercise","v":{"s":"Joint-friendly, sustained exercise."}},{"k":"aerobic_system_in_full_use_oxidative_g","v":{"s":"Aerobic system in full use; oxidative + glycolysis."}},{"k":"whole_body_low_impact_high_energy","v":{"s":"Whole-body, low-impact, high-energy."}},{"k":"phosphocreatine_anaerobic_glycolysis_c","v":{"s":"Phosphocreatine + anaerobic glycolysis. Cannot sustain."}},{"k":"energy_metabolism_2","v":{"s":"Energy & Metabolism"}},{"k":"how_your_body_actually_makes_and_uses_","v":{"s":"How your body actually makes and uses energy"}},{"k":"this_module_covers_the_biology_of_ener","v":{"s":"This module covers the BIOLOGY of energy — molecules, cells, organ systems. Numbers like \"BMR\" and \"kcal/min\" are descriptive (this is what your body is doing), not prescriptive (this is your daily target). Different bodies need different amounts. Growth, illness, sleep quality, weather, and life stage all change the numbers. None of this is a goal to hit."}},{"k":"energy_metabolism_sections","v":{"s":"Energy metabolism sections"}},{"k":"atp_your_cellular_energy_currency_2","v":{"s":"ATP — your cellular energy currency"}},{"k":"every_cell_in_your_body_runs_on_a_tiny","v":{"s":"Every cell in your body runs on a tiny molecule called ATP (adenosine triphosphate). When ATP gives up one phosphate group, it becomes ADP and releases energy your cells use to do work — contract muscles, fire neurons, build proteins, push molecules across membranes."}},{"k":"releases_energy","v":{"s":"releases energy"}},{"k":"adp_pi","v":{"s":"ADP + Pi"}},{"k":"recharged_in_mitochondria","v":{"s":"recharged in mitochondria"}},{"k":"your_body_holds_only_50_g_of_atp_at_an","v":{"s":"Your body holds only ~50 g of ATP at any moment, but recycles it constantly: each ATP molecule cycles ATP → ADP → ATP about 1,000+ times per day. Total ATP turnover roughly equals your body weight per day. The recycling happens in tiny organelles called mitochondria, which use oxygen and food (glucose, fatty acids) to charge ADP back into ATP."}},{"k":"mitochondria_the_cellular_powerhouses","v":{"s":"Mitochondria — the cellular powerhouses"}},{"k":"mitochondria_are_tiny_organelles_insid","v":{"s":"Mitochondria are tiny organelles inside almost every cell of your body. They take glucose (from carbs) or fatty acids (from fats), combine them with oxygen, and release CO₂ + water — capturing the energy in ATP along the way."}},{"k":"glucose_6_o_6_co_6_h_o_30_32_atp","v":{"s":"Glucose + 6 O₂ → 6 CO₂ + 6 H₂O + ~30-32 ATP"}},{"k":"this_is_cellular_respiration_the_chemi","v":{"s":"(this is cellular respiration — the chemistry that powers you right now)"}},{"k":"cells_with_high_energy_demand_have","v":{"s":"Cells with high energy demand have "}},{"k":"lots_of_mitochondria","v":{"s":"lots of mitochondria"}},{"k":"heart_muscle_5_000_per_cell_liver_brai","v":{"s":": heart muscle (~5,000 per cell), liver, brain. Endurance training increases mitochondrial density in your muscles — that's a big part of why trained athletes can sustain effort longer."}},{"k":"three_energy_systems_run_side_by_side_2","v":{"s":"Three energy systems run side by side"}},{"k":"your_body_has_three_different_ways_to_","v":{"s":"Your body has three different ways to make ATP. They're all running constantly, but the dominant system depends on how hard and how long you're working."}},{"k":"phosphocreatine_pcr","v":{"s":"Phosphocreatine (PCr)"}},{"k":"anaerobic_glycolysis","v":{"s":"Anaerobic glycolysis"}},{"k":"oxidative_aerobic","v":{"s":"Oxidative (aerobic)"}},{"k":"atp_yield","v":{"s":"ATP yield: "}},{"k":"all_three_systems_run_all_the_time","v":{"s":"All three systems run all the time. "}},{"k":"they_overlap_a_1500m_race_shifts_from_","v":{"s":"They overlap. A 1500m race shifts from PCr → anaerobic → aerobic across the four minutes. Endurance training boosts the oxidative system; sprint training boosts the anaerobic. Both adapt with practice."}},{"k":"where_your_energy_actually_goes","v":{"s":"Where your energy actually goes"}},{"k":"even_when_you_re_resting_your_body_use","v":{"s":"Even when you're resting, your body uses energy. The minimum to keep your heart beating, brain firing, lungs breathing, kidneys filtering, and core temperature stable is called Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Most adolescents have a BMR somewhere between 1,200 and 1,800 kcal/day — the wide range is normal biological variation."}},{"k":"resting_energy_distribution_by_organ","v":{"s":"Resting energy distribution by organ"}},{"k":"these_percentages_describe_a_healthy_a","v":{"s":"These percentages describe a healthy adult at rest. Children and adolescents typically have a brain that uses an even larger share (up to 50% in young kids) because of growth and learning."}},{"k":"activity_adds_energy_use_on_top_of_bmr","v":{"s":"Activity adds energy use on top of BMR"}},{"k":"these_are_descriptive_averages_actual_","v":{"s":"These are descriptive averages — actual energy use varies with body size, fitness, and how hard you're going."}},{"k":"important_framing","v":{"s":"Important framing"}},{"k":"your_body_needs_at_least_your_bmr_wort","v":{"s":"Your body needs at least your BMR worth of energy to keep its essential systems working. Eating well below BMR for an extended time forces the body to slow metabolism, break down muscle, weaken immunity, and impair concentration. The goal isn't to \"minimize calories\" — it's to give your body what it needs to do its job. If anything in this module triggers worry about how much you're eating, please talk with a trusted adult, your doctor, or NEDA (1-800-931-2237)."}},{"k":"mouth","v":{"s":"Mouth"}},{"k":"ghrelin","v":{"s":"Ghrelin"}},{"k":"stomach","v":{"s":"Stomach"}},{"k":"gastrin","v":{"s":"Gastrin"}},{"k":"ghrelin_2","v":{"s":"Ghrelin"}},{"k":"small_intestine","v":{"s":"Small Intestine"}},{"k":"cck_cholecystokinin","v":{"s":"CCK (Cholecystokinin)"}},{"k":"secretin","v":{"s":"Secretin"}},{"k":"insulin","v":{"s":"Insulin"}},{"k":"large_intestine_colon","v":{"s":"Large Intestine (Colon)"}},{"k":"pyy_peptide_yy","v":{"s":"PYY (Peptide YY)"}},{"k":"leptin","v":{"s":"Leptin"}},{"k":"brain_integration","v":{"s":"Brain Integration"}},{"k":"insulin_glucagon_balance","v":{"s":"Insulin / Glucagon balance"}},{"k":"leptin_2","v":{"s":"Leptin"}},{"k":"digestion_walkthrough_2","v":{"s":"Digestion Walkthrough"}},{"k":"follow_your_food_through_your_body","v":{"s":"Follow your food through your body"}},{"k":"digestion_is_mechanical_chewing_churni","v":{"s":"Digestion is mechanical (chewing, churning, peristalsis) AND chemical (enzymes breaking molecules apart). Different parts of the journey handle different macronutrients. Hormones coordinate the whole process — from \"I'm hungry\" to \"I'm full\" — across hours and days."}},{"k":"digestion_stages","v":{"s":"Digestion stages"}},{"k":"time_spent","v":{"s":"Time spent: "}},{"k":"mechanical_action","v":{"s":"🔨 Mechanical action"}},{"k":"chemical_action","v":{"s":"⚗️ Chemical action"}},{"k":"what_happens_to_each_macronutrient_at_","v":{"s":"What happens to each macronutrient at this stage"}},{"k":"carbohydrates","v":{"s":"Carbohydrates"}},{"k":"protein_11","v":{"s":"Protein"}},{"k":"fiber_4","v":{"s":"Fiber"}},{"k":"key_hormones_at_this_stage","v":{"s":"🧪 Key hormones at this stage"}},{"k":"previous_stage","v":{"s":"← Previous stage"}},{"k":"next_stage","v":{"s":"Next stage →"}},{"k":"common_nutrition_myths_2","v":{"s":"Common Nutrition Myths"}},{"k":"the_most_googled_bad_nutrition_advice","v":{"s":"The most-Googled bad nutrition advice"}},{"k":"these_ten_claims_show_up_constantly_in","v":{"s":"These ten claims show up constantly in social media, advertising, and well-meaning advice from family. We picked them because each is a documented misconception with a clear evidence base. Where research is genuinely mixed, we say so explicitly. Sources cited inline (NIH, Harvard, CDC, FDA, AHA)."}},{"k":"the_claim","v":{"s":"The claim"}},{"k":"what_s_the_truth","v":{"s":"What's the truth?"}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next_myth","v":{"s":"Next myth →"}},{"k":"omega_3_fatty_acids_and_depression","v":{"s":"Omega-3 fatty acids and depression"}},{"k":"iron_and_adhd_style_fatigue","v":{"s":"Iron and ADHD-style fatigue"}},{"k":"blood_sugar_swings_and_mood","v":{"s":"Blood sugar swings and mood"}},{"k":"caffeine_and_adolescent_anxiety","v":{"s":"Caffeine and adolescent anxiety"}},{"k":"the_gut_brain_axis","v":{"s":"The gut-brain axis"}},{"k":"vitamin_d_and_mood_especially_in_maine","v":{"s":"Vitamin D and mood (especially in Maine)"}},{"k":"hydration_and_concentration","v":{"s":"Hydration and concentration"}},{"k":"food_mental_health_2","v":{"s":"Food & Mental Health"}},{"k":"how_food_affects_mood_attention_and_me","v":{"s":"How food affects mood, attention, and mental health"}},{"k":"there_are_real_well_studied_connection","v":{"s":"There are real, well-studied connections between nutrition and mental health. This module covers seven of them — each as a CONTRIBUTING FACTOR, not a cure. Diet alone does not treat depression, anxiety, or ADHD. But poor nutrition can make any of those harder to manage, and addressing nutrition is part of comprehensive care."}},{"k":"important","v":{"s":"⚠ Important: "}},{"k":"if_you_are_struggling_with_mental_heal","v":{"s":"If you are struggling with mental health, please reach out to a doctor, therapist, school counselor, or trusted adult. Crisis support is available 24/7: "}},{"k":"suicide_crisis_lifeline","v":{"s":" (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) · "}},{"k":"text_home_to_741741","v":{"s":"Text HOME to 741741"}},{"k":"crisis_text_line_this_module_is_educat","v":{"s":" (Crisis Text Line). This module is education, not treatment."}},{"k":"what_the_research_says","v":{"s":"📚 What the research says"}},{"k":"mechanism_how_it_works","v":{"s":"🔬 Mechanism (how it works)"}},{"k":"food_sources","v":{"s":"🥗 Food sources"}},{"k":"important_caution","v":{"s":"⚠ Important caution"}},{"k":"click_any_topic_above_to_see_what_rese","v":{"s":"Click any topic above to see what research says, the proposed mechanism, food sources, and important cautions."}},{"k":"a_reminder_before_you_leave_this_modul","v":{"s":"A reminder before you leave this module"}},{"k":"these_connections_are_real_but_partial","v":{"s":"These connections are real but partial. Mental-health symptoms have many contributors — genetics, sleep, exercise, social support, life events, brain chemistry — and nutrition is one piece. If something here applies to you or someone you love, the next step is talking with a professional, not a diet change. School counselor, family doctor, NAMI helpline (1-800-950-6264), or crisis line 988 are all good places to start."}},{"k":"help_is_available_right_now","v":{"s":"Help is available right now: "}},{"k":"neda_helpline","v":{"s":"NEDA helpline "}},{"k":"text","v":{"s":" · text "}},{"k":"988_crisis_lifeline_maine_211_or_talk_","v":{"s":" · 988 Crisis Lifeline · Maine 211 · or talk with a trusted adult."}},{"k":"eating_disorder_awareness_2","v":{"s":"Eating Disorder Awareness"}},{"k":"content_note_before_you_continue","v":{"s":"Content note before you continue"}},{"k":"this_module_discusses_eating_disorders","v":{"s":"This module discusses eating disorders, which are serious mental-health conditions. It covers:"}},{"k":"the_spectrum_of_disordered_eating","v":{"s":"The spectrum of disordered eating"}},{"k":"signs_to_watch_for_in_yourself_or_a_fr","v":{"s":"Signs to watch for in yourself or a friend"}},{"k":"how_to_support_someone_who_is_struggli","v":{"s":"How to support someone who is struggling"}},{"k":"how_and_when_to_talk_with_an_adult","v":{"s":"How and when to talk with an adult"}},{"k":"resources_including_helplines_that_can","v":{"s":"Resources, including helplines that can help right now"}},{"k":"what_this_module_does_not_include","v":{"s":"What this module does NOT include: "}},{"k":"specific_weights_calorie_numbers_bmi_n","v":{"s":"specific weights, calorie numbers, BMI numbers, descriptions of restrictive behaviors, \"before/after\" framing, or any content that could function as a how-to. The information is intentionally general — focused on awareness, support, and recovery."}},{"k":"if_reading_about_eating_concerns_is_ha","v":{"s":"If reading about eating concerns is hard for you right now, "}},{"k":"please_consider","v":{"s":"please consider:"}},{"k":"talking_with_a_trusted_adult_before_co","v":{"s":"Talking with a trusted adult before continuing"}},{"k":"skipping_this_module_and_exploring_oth","v":{"s":"Skipping this module and exploring others in NutritionLab"}},{"k":"calling_neda_right_now","v":{"s":"Calling NEDA right now: "}},{"k":"mon_thu_11am_9pm_fri_11am_5pm_et","v":{"s":" (Mon-Thu 11am-9pm, Fri 11am-5pm ET)"}},{"k":"texting","v":{"s":"Texting "}},{"k":"for_24_7_crisis_support","v":{"s":" for 24/7 crisis support"}},{"k":"i_understand_continue","v":{"s":"✓ I understand — continue"}},{"k":"take_me_back_to_the_menu","v":{"s":"← Take me back to the menu"}},{"k":"1_what_disordered_eating_is","v":{"s":"1. What disordered eating is"}},{"k":"2_signs_to_watch_for","v":{"s":"2. Signs to watch for"}},{"k":"3_online_content_red_flags","v":{"s":"3. Online content red flags"}},{"k":"4_supporting_a_friend","v":{"s":"4. Supporting a friend"}},{"k":"5_if_it_s_you","v":{"s":"5. If it's YOU"}},{"k":"6_recovery_is_real","v":{"s":"6. Recovery is real"}},{"k":"eating_disorder_awareness_3","v":{"s":"Eating Disorder Awareness"}},{"k":"module_sections","v":{"s":"Module sections"}},{"k":"disordered_eating_is_on_a_spectrum","v":{"s":"Disordered eating is on a spectrum"}},{"k":"most_people_s_relationship_with_food_h","v":{"s":"Most people's relationship with food has bumps. There's a difference between an OCCASIONAL hard day with food (which is part of being human) and a PATTERN that interferes with your life or your health."}},{"k":"eating_disorders_are_real_medical_cond","v":{"s":"Eating disorders are real medical conditions, not choices and not phases. Like depression or anxiety, they involve brain chemistry, genetics, life experiences, and culture. They are also TREATABLE — most people who get support recover."}},{"k":"eating_disorders_affect_people_of_ever","v":{"s":"Eating disorders affect people of every body size, gender, race, age, and background. The stereotype of \"thin teenage girl\" causes real harm — boys, athletes, kids of color, larger-bodied kids, and adults all struggle and are often missed by the people around them."}},{"k":"key_idea","v":{"s":"Key idea: "}},{"k":"concern_is_justified_when_someone_s_th","v":{"s":"Concern is justified when someone's thinking about food, body, or exercise starts taking up so much mental space that it interferes with school, friendships, hobbies, or health. The frequency, intensity, and disruption matter more than any specific behavior in isolation."}},{"k":"signs_that_someone_may_be_struggling","v":{"s":"Signs that someone may be struggling"}},{"k":"these_are_general_patterns_the_nationa","v":{"s":"These are GENERAL patterns the National Eating Disorders Association (NEDA) names as warning signs. None of these alone proves an eating disorder; the concerning pattern is a CLUSTER of changes happening together over time."}},{"k":"strong_frequent_preoccupation_with_foo","v":{"s":"Strong, frequent preoccupation with food, body, weight, or \"good vs bad\" foods"}},{"k":"withdrawing_from_social_activities_tha","v":{"s":"Withdrawing from social activities that involve food (lunch, parties, family meals)"}},{"k":"significant_mood_changes_connected_to_","v":{"s":"Significant mood changes connected to eating (extreme guilt, shame, or anxiety after meals)"}},{"k":"rigid_rituals_around_food_exercise_or_","v":{"s":"Rigid rituals around food, exercise, or daily structure that disrupt normal life"}},{"k":"frequent_comments_about_feeling_too_bi","v":{"s":"Frequent comments about feeling \"too big,\" \"too small,\" or wanting to change body shape"}},{"k":"fatigue_dizziness_brain_fog_hair_chang","v":{"s":"Fatigue, dizziness, brain fog, hair changes, sleep problems, or feeling cold often"}},{"k":"noticing_peers_influencers_family_maki","v":{"s":"Noticing peers / influencers / family making comments that reinforce harmful patterns"}},{"k":"body_changes_alone_are_not_a_reliable_","v":{"s":"Body changes alone are NOT a reliable sign. People in every body size can have eating disorders. The behavior, thinking patterns, and disruption to life matter more than appearance."}},{"k":"online_content_red_flags","v":{"s":"Online content red flags"}},{"k":"a_lot_of_what_shapes_how_people_think_","v":{"s":"A LOT of what shapes how people think about food and bodies today comes from social media — TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Snapchat. Some of this content is helpful (registered dietitians, intuitive eating coaches, body-diverse fitness creators). A lot of it is not. The 2023 Surgeon General Advisory on Social Media and Youth Mental Health flagged content that promotes disordered eating as a documented driver of body-image concerns and eating disorders in adolescents. Recognizing the patterns is itself protective."}},{"k":"how_algorithms_feed_this","v":{"s":"How algorithms feed this: "}},{"k":"recommendation_algorithms_the_for_you_","v":{"s":"Recommendation algorithms (the \"For You\" page, IG Reels, YouTube's \"Up Next\") track what you watch and feed you more of it. A single search for \"weight loss\" or lingering on a body-comparison reel can cascade into an increasingly extreme feed. The algorithm doesn't know what's good for you — it knows what holds your attention. Strong feelings (especially anxiety, comparison, or self-criticism) hold attention well, which is why harmful content surfaces."}},{"k":"patterns_to_watch_for_in_food_body_con","v":{"s":"🚩 Patterns to watch for in food / body content"}},{"k":"what_you_can_do_your_feed_is_yours_to_","v":{"s":"✓ What you can do — your feed is yours to shape"}},{"k":"use_platform_tools","v":{"s":"Use platform tools. "}},{"k":"on_tiktok_long_press_a_video_not_inter","v":{"s":"On TikTok, long-press a video → \"Not interested.\" On Instagram, three-dot menu → \"Not interested.\" On YouTube, three-dot menu → \"Don't recommend channel.\" Each tap retrains the algorithm. After a few days the feed shifts."}},{"k":"block_mute_and_unfollow_without_guilt","v":{"s":"Block, mute, and unfollow without guilt. "}},{"k":"you_don_t_owe_anyone_your_attention_re","v":{"s":"You don't owe anyone your attention. Removing harmful accounts is a self-care move, not rudeness."}},{"k":"curate_actively_in_the_other_direction","v":{"s":"Curate actively in the other direction. "}},{"k":"follow_creators_with_bodies_of_varied_","v":{"s":"Follow creators with bodies of varied sizes who DO NOT center food/weight conversations. Athletes, scientists, comedians, gardeners, artists. The algorithm starts feeding you more of who you watch."}},{"k":"report_content_that_promotes_eating_di","v":{"s":"Report content that promotes eating disorders. "}},{"k":"every_major_platform_has_a_category_fo","v":{"s":"Every major platform has a category for this. Reporting is anonymous; the creator doesn't see you reported them. NEDA tracks platform responses and pushes for stronger moderation."}},{"k":"take_breaks_when_you_notice_the_spiral","v":{"s":"Take breaks when you notice the spiral. "}},{"k":"if_you_ve_been_on_a_platform_for_30_mi","v":{"s":"If you've been on a platform for 30 minutes and feel worse about yourself than when you started, close it. Body comparison is one of the most well-documented harms of social media."}},{"k":"talk_about_what_you_see","v":{"s":"Talk about what you see. "}},{"k":"naming_a_pattern_out_loud_with_a_frien","v":{"s":"Naming a pattern out loud, with a friend or trusted adult, is one of the fastest ways to recognize it for what it is. \"That video felt weird, did you see it?\" is enough to start."}},{"k":"a_note_about_wellness_specifically","v":{"s":"A note about \"wellness\" specifically"}},{"k":"a_lot_of_harmful_food_content_today_is","v":{"s":"A lot of harmful food content today is packaged as \"wellness,\" \"biohacking,\" \"lifestyle,\" or \"high-performance habits.\" The aesthetic is calm, white-marble, soft-lit, and professional-looking. The substance — extreme restriction, \"earning\" food, treating bodies as projects — is the same diet culture older generations were sold. Looking polished doesn't make content true. The same red flags above apply."}},{"k":"where_to_learn_more_about_media_litera","v":{"s":"Where to learn more about media literacy"}},{"k":"common_sense_media","v":{"s":"Common Sense Media "}},{"k":"commonsensemedia_org_independent_revie","v":{"s":"(commonsensemedia.org) — independent reviews of platforms, apps, and content from a kid/family lens"}},{"k":"neda_social_media_body_image","v":{"s":"NEDA Social Media + Body Image "}},{"k":"nationaleatingdisorders_org_research_b","v":{"s":"(nationaleatingdisorders.org) — research-backed guidance and free educator toolkits"}},{"k":"u_s_surgeon_general_s_2023_advisory_on","v":{"s":"U.S. Surgeon General's 2023 Advisory on Social Media and Youth Mental Health "}},{"k":"hhs_gov_surgeongeneral_official_report","v":{"s":"(hhs.gov/surgeongeneral) — official report on documented harms and what platforms / families / schools can do"}},{"k":"your_school_counselor_or_school_psycho","v":{"s":"Your school counselor or school psychologist "}},{"k":"can_help_you_think_through_your_own_so","v":{"s":"can help you think through your own social media use without judgment."}},{"k":"how_to_support_a_friend","v":{"s":"How to support a friend"}},{"k":"things_that_help","v":{"s":"✓ Things that help"}},{"k":"listen_without_trying_to_fix_or_convin","v":{"s":"LISTEN without trying to fix or convince"}},{"k":"talk_about_who_they_are_interests_humo","v":{"s":"Talk about who they ARE — interests, humor, projects — not what they look like"}},{"k":"tell_them_you_care_about_them_full_sto","v":{"s":"Tell them you care about them, full stop"}},{"k":"encourage_them_to_talk_with_a_trusted_","v":{"s":"Encourage them to talk with a trusted adult: parent, school counselor, doctor, or older sibling"}},{"k":"offer_to_sit_with_them_while_they_call","v":{"s":"Offer to sit with them while they call a helpline or set up an appointment"}},{"k":"keep_including_them_in_social_plans_ev","v":{"s":"Keep including them in social plans, even if they say no the first time"}},{"k":"things_that_don_t_help_even_if_well_in","v":{"s":"× Things that don't help (even if well-intentioned)"}},{"k":"commenting_on_their_body_weight_or_app","v":{"s":"Commenting on their body, weight, or appearance — positive OR negative"}},{"k":"trying_to_argue_them_into_eating_or_ma","v":{"s":"Trying to argue them into eating, or making meals into a battle"}},{"k":"telling_them_just_eat_or_just_stop_eat","v":{"s":"Telling them \"just eat\" or \"just stop\" — eating disorders aren't a choice"}},{"k":"comparing_their_struggle_to_someone_wh","v":{"s":"Comparing their struggle to someone who has it \"worse\""}},{"k":"keeping_a_serious_concern_secret_becau","v":{"s":"Keeping a serious concern secret because you promised — safety overrides secrecy"}},{"k":"when_to_tell_an_adult","v":{"s":"When to tell an adult: "}},{"k":"if_a_friend_s_health_or_safety_is_at_r","v":{"s":"If a friend's health or safety is at risk — they've mentioned hurting themselves, are passing out, can't function in daily life, or you're seriously worried — tell a trusted adult right away. Telling someone who can help is not a betrayal; it's the most loyal thing a friend can do. NEDA, school counselors, and parents are all good places to start."}},{"k":"if_you_re_the_one_struggling","v":{"s":"If you're the one struggling"}},{"k":"first_nothing_you_are_experiencing_mak","v":{"s":"First: nothing you are experiencing makes you \"broken\" or \"weak.\" Eating disorders are medical conditions that affect millions of people. They are TREATABLE. Most people who get support do recover."}},{"k":"you_don_t_have_to_wait_for_things_to_g","v":{"s":"You don't have to wait for things to get worse to ask for help. You don't have to \"earn\" support by being sick enough. You just have to reach out."}},{"k":"where_to_start_in_order_of_urgency","v":{"s":"Where to start, in order of urgency"}},{"k":"if_you_re_in_crisis_right_now","v":{"s":"If you're in crisis right now "}},{"k":"thinking_about_hurting_yourself_can_t_","v":{"s":"(thinking about hurting yourself, can't keep food down, feeling unsafe): call "}},{"k":"or_text","v":{"s":" or text "}},{"k":"for_ed_specific_support_24_7","v":{"s":"For ED-specific support 24/7: "}},{"k":"neda_helpline_2","v":{"s":"NEDA helpline "}},{"k":"mon_fri_business_hours_or_text","v":{"s":" (Mon-Fri business hours) or text "}},{"k":"24_7_crisis_text_free_confidential_tra","v":{"s":" (24/7 crisis text). Free, confidential, trained."}},{"k":"in_school","v":{"s":"In school: "}},{"k":"school_counselor_or_school_psychologis","v":{"s":"school counselor or school psychologist. They are bound to follow up appropriately and can connect you to outside care."}},{"k":"a_trusted_adult","v":{"s":"A trusted adult: "}},{"k":"parent_older_sibling_coach_teacher_fam","v":{"s":"parent, older sibling, coach, teacher, family friend, family doctor. You don't need a script — \"I've been having a hard time and could use some help\" is enough."}},{"k":"maine_211_call_211_connects_to_local_m","v":{"s":"Maine 211 (call 211) connects to local mental-health services. Maine Behavioral Healthcare has crisis services statewide. NAMI Maine: "}},{"k":"on_the_helpline","v":{"s":"On the helpline: "}},{"k":"you_don_t_need_to_know_what_to_say_the","v":{"s":"You don't need to know what to say. They're trained to start the conversation. You can call back. You can hang up. You can't do it wrong."}},{"k":"recovery_is_real","v":{"s":"Recovery is real"}},{"k":"most_people_with_eating_disorders_reco","v":{"s":"Most people with eating disorders recover, especially when they get support EARLIER rather than later. Recovery isn't linear — it has hard days, setbacks, and slow improvement — but the trajectory is real and well-documented."}},{"k":"you_or_someone_you_love_can_come_throu","v":{"s":"You — or someone you love — can come through this."}},{"k":"seasons_vitamin_d","v":{"s":"Seasons + vitamin D"}},{"k":"fisheries_omega_3","v":{"s":"Fisheries + omega-3"}},{"k":"food_access","v":{"s":"Food access"}},{"k":"school_meals_snap","v":{"s":"School meals + SNAP"}},{"k":"local_food_wabanaki","v":{"s":"Local food + Wabanaki"}},{"k":"maine_winters_are_a_vitamin_d_problem","v":{"s":"Maine winters are a vitamin D problem"}},{"k":"at_43_47_n_oct_mar_sun_is_too_low_for_","v":{"s":"At 43–47°N, Oct–Mar sun is too low for skin to make D — no matter how long you’re outside."}},{"k":"cold_water_fisheries_omega_3_powerhous","v":{"s":"Cold-water fisheries → omega-3 powerhouse"}},{"k":"maine_lobster_herring_and_farm_raised_","v":{"s":"Maine lobster, herring, and farm-raised salmon are world-class EPA/DHA sources."}},{"k":"rural_distance_seasonal_employment_rea","v":{"s":"Rural distance + seasonal employment = real food-access issues"}},{"k":"aroostook_and_washington_counties_have","v":{"s":"Aroostook and Washington counties have documented food deserts and long drive times."}},{"k":"free_school_meals_snap_basics","v":{"s":"Free school meals + SNAP basics"}},{"k":"maine_is_the_second_state_to_permanent","v":{"s":"Maine is the second state to permanently fund free meals for all K-12 kids (2022)."}},{"k":"local_food_systems_wabanaki_food_sover","v":{"s":"Local food systems + Wabanaki food sovereignty"}},{"k":"csas_farmers_markets_and_tribal_food_s","v":{"s":"CSAs, farmers markets, and tribal food-sovereignty efforts shape what’s actually on Maine plates."}},{"k":"maine_food_reality_2","v":{"s":"Maine Food Reality"}},{"k":"nutrition_science_as_it_actually_plays","v":{"s":"Nutrition science as it actually plays out in Maine"}},{"k":"maine_s_geography_climate_history_and_","v":{"s":"Maine's geography, climate, history, and economy all shape what food looks like here. Long winters and northern latitude make vitamin D a documented public-health issue. World-class cold-water fisheries make Maine an omega-3 powerhouse. Rural distances and seasonal employment create real food-access challenges. Maine became the second state in the country to permanently fund free school meals for all kids. This module names the realities students live inside."}},{"k":"maine_food_reality_sections","v":{"s":"Maine Food Reality sections"}},{"k":"maine_winters_are_a_vitamin_d_problem_2","v":{"s":"Maine winters are a vitamin D problem"}},{"k":"maine_sits_at_roughly_43_47_n_latitude","v":{"s":"Maine sits at roughly 43-47°N latitude. From October through March, the sun angle is too low for human skin to make vitamin D from sunlight, no matter how much time you spend outside. Most Mainers are vitamin D insufficient through winter. The Maine CDC has flagged this as a documented public-health issue."}},{"k":"why_this_matters","v":{"s":"Why this matters: "}},{"k":"vitamin_d_supports_calcium_absorption_","v":{"s":"Vitamin D supports calcium absorption (bones), immune function, and possibly mood (some research links low D to seasonal affective disorder). Adolescents are in peak bone-building years — adequate D matters more than usual."}},{"k":"what_works_in_maine","v":{"s":"What works in Maine: "}},{"k":"fatty_fish_salmon_sardines_herring_egg","v":{"s":"Fatty fish (salmon, sardines, herring), egg yolks, fortified milk and plant milks, fortified cereals. Many Mainers benefit from a winter vitamin D supplement — but dose should be guided by a blood test (ask your doctor for a 25-hydroxy vitamin D test). Don't guess."}},{"k":"local_growing_season_is_short","v":{"s":"Local growing season is short"}},{"k":"maine_s_frost_free_growing_season_runs","v":{"s":"Maine's frost-free growing season runs roughly mid-May to early October — about 130 days statewide, shorter in Aroostook. Outside that window, fresh local produce is limited. Frozen local berries, canned tomatoes, root vegetables (potatoes, carrots, beets, parsnips, winter squash), and apples in cold storage are real Maine winter staples. Frozen vegetables retain nearly all their nutrients and are typically cheaper than out-of-season fresh."}},{"k":"maine_is_an_omega_3_powerhouse","v":{"s":"Maine is an omega-3 powerhouse"}},{"k":"maine_s_cold_north_atlantic_waters_sup","v":{"s":"Maine's cold North Atlantic waters support some of the richest fatty-fish populations in the world. Cold water means more omega-3 (EPA + DHA) per gram of fish — these fats serve as antifreeze in cold-water species. Maine fisheries land roughly $700M+ in seafood per year (DMR, varies year to year), making it Maine's most economically significant agricultural sector after wild blueberries."}},{"k":"maine_s_key_fatty_fish","v":{"s":"Maine's key fatty fish"}},{"k":"cooke_aquaculture_in_eastern_maine_hig","v":{"s":"Cooke Aquaculture in eastern Maine. Higher omega-3 than wild salmon due to feed."}},{"k":"maine_s_most_abundant_pelagic_fish_oft","v":{"s":"Maine's most abundant pelagic fish. Often canned as sardines."}},{"k":"strong_flavored_oily_fish_underused_in","v":{"s":"Strong-flavored oily fish; underused in American kitchens."}},{"k":"tiny_fish_lower_mercury_canned_sardine","v":{"s":"Tiny fish = lower mercury. Canned sardines are cheap and shelf-stable."}},{"k":"high_mercury_for_adolescents_limit_to_","v":{"s":"High mercury for adolescents — limit to occasional."}},{"k":"iconic_but_lower_omega_3_than_fatty_fi","v":{"s":"Iconic but lower omega-3 than fatty fish. Excellent protein, low fat."}},{"k":"aha_recommendation","v":{"s":"AHA recommendation: "}},{"k":"2_fatty_fish_meals_per_week_about_8_oz","v":{"s":"2 fatty-fish meals per week (about 8 oz total). For Mainers, this is achievable with mostly local seafood — and canned sardines or herring keep it affordable."}},{"k":"food_access_in_maine_is_uneven","v":{"s":"Food access in Maine is uneven"}},{"k":"the_usda_tracks_food_deserts_areas_whe","v":{"s":"The USDA tracks \"food deserts\" — areas where residents have limited access to affordable, nutritious food. In Maine, the most affected areas are Aroostook County (the largest county east of the Mississippi by area, with the lowest population density), Washington County (down east), and pockets of inland rural Penobscot, Piscataquis, and Somerset counties. Lewiston and parts of Portland also have urban food-access challenges."}},{"k":"what_food_access_actually_means","v":{"s":"What \"food access\" actually means"}},{"k":"in_rural_maine_the_nearest_grocery_sto","v":{"s":"In rural Maine, the nearest grocery store with fresh produce can be 30+ minutes away. Without a car, that's functionally inaccessible."}},{"k":"fresh_fruits_vegetables_and_lean_prote","v":{"s":"Fresh fruits, vegetables, and lean proteins cost more per calorie than ultra-processed pantry food. For families on tight budgets, the calorie math pushes toward the latter."}},{"k":"cooking_from_scratch_takes_time_workin","v":{"s":"Cooking from scratch takes time. Working two jobs, single parenting, or shift work all compress the time available for food prep."}},{"k":"how_to_cook_beans_how_to_use_frozen_ve","v":{"s":"How to cook beans, how to use frozen vegetables, what to do with cheap cuts of meat — these are learned skills, often passed down through families. When food culture is disrupted, the skills can fade."}},{"k":"important_framing_2","v":{"s":"Important framing: "}},{"k":"food_access_is_a_systemic_issue_not_an","v":{"s":"Food access is a SYSTEMIC issue, not an individual moral failing. People making food choices in food deserts are not \"lazy\" or \"uninformed\" — they're solving an optimization problem with constraints most middle-class shoppers don't face. Public-health nutrition focuses on changing the system (more grocery stores, better school meals, SNAP-eligibility expansions, school food gardens) rather than blaming individuals."}},{"k":"school_meals_maine_led_the_country","v":{"s":"School meals — Maine led the country"}},{"k":"in_2022_maine_became_the_second_state_","v":{"s":"In 2022, Maine became the second state in the U.S. (after California) to permanently fund free school breakfast and lunch for ALL public-school students, regardless of family income. The law (LD 1679, signed by Governor Mills) eliminated the means-tested application process and the social stigma that came with it. Other states have since followed."}},{"k":"why_universal_free_meals_matter","v":{"s":"Why universal free meals matter: "}},{"k":"research_shows_kids_who_eat_breakfast_","v":{"s":"Research shows kids who eat breakfast at school perform better academically, have fewer behavioral incidents, and have better attendance. Removing the application step means kids who qualify but were embarrassed to apply now eat. Maine's law also expanded local-food sourcing into school cafeterias."}},{"k":"snap_the_federal_food_assistance_progr","v":{"s":"SNAP — the federal food-assistance program"}},{"k":"snap_supplemental_nutrition_assistance","v":{"s":"SNAP (Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program, formerly food stamps) provides monthly food benefits to eligible low-income families. In Maine, ~13% of residents receive SNAP at any given time (USDA). Benefits are loaded onto an EBT card and used at most grocery stores, farmers markets, and some online retailers."}},{"k":"common_misconception","v":{"s":"Common misconception: "}},{"k":"snap_is_not_a_flat_amount_per_family_t","v":{"s":"SNAP is not a flat amount per family. The benefit is calculated based on income, household size, expenses, and other factors. Average benefit is roughly $6 per person per day in Maine — meaningful, but tight. SNAP can be used for groceries (including seeds and food-producing plants) but not for prepared hot food, alcohol, or non-food items."}},{"k":"good_shepherd_food_bank_maine_food_pan","v":{"s":"Good Shepherd Food Bank + Maine food-pantry network"}},{"k":"good_shepherd_food_bank_auburn_is_main","v":{"s":"Good Shepherd Food Bank (Auburn) is Maine's largest hunger-relief organization, distributing food through ~600 pantries and meal programs statewide. Other Maine food resources: Wayside Food Programs (Portland), Preble Street, Locker Project (school-based pantries), and a strong network of regional food hubs."}},{"k":"maine_211","v":{"s":"Maine 211: "}},{"k":"dial_211_or_visit_211maine_org_for_con","v":{"s":"Dial 211 (or visit 211maine.org) for connections to food pantries, SNAP application help, school meal info, and other services. Free, confidential, available 24/7."}},{"k":"maine_s_local_food_economy","v":{"s":"Maine's local food economy"}},{"k":"maine_has_more_farms_per_capita_than_m","v":{"s":"Maine has more farms per capita than most states and a vibrant local food economy: farmers markets in nearly every town, CSA (community-supported agriculture) shares from spring through fall, regional food hubs, an active aquaculture industry, and signature crops like wild blueberries (Maine produces about 99% of U.S. wild blueberries, mostly from Washington and Hancock counties)."}},{"k":"wabanaki_food_sovereignty","v":{"s":"Wabanaki food sovereignty"}},{"k":"the_wabanaki_confederacy_penobscot_pas","v":{"s":"The Wabanaki Confederacy (Penobscot, Passamaquoddy, Maliseet, Mi'kmaq) have stewarded land in present-day Maine for thousands of years. Traditional Wabanaki food systems include moose, deer, salmon, eels, wild rice, fiddleheads, sweet grass, blueberries, corn, beans, and squash (the Three Sisters companion-planting system)."}},{"k":"food_sovereignty_the_right_of_communit","v":{"s":"Food sovereignty — the right of communities to control their own food systems — is an active issue for Maine's tribes. Recent decades have seen Wabanaki-led restoration of native crops, traditional fishing rights, and food-system education programs."}},{"k":"wabanaki_public_health_wellness_wabana","v":{"s":"Wabanaki Public Health & Wellness (wabanakiphw.org), Penobscot Nation Department of Natural Resources, Passamaquoddy Tribe at Pleasant Point, Indian Township."}},{"k":"climate_change_is_reshaping_maine_food","v":{"s":"Climate change is reshaping Maine food"}},{"k":"the_gulf_of_maine_is_warming_faster_th","v":{"s":"The Gulf of Maine is warming faster than 99% of the world's ocean (Gulf of Maine Research Institute). Lobster populations are shifting north into Canadian waters; cod stocks remain depressed. On land, the growing season is lengthening (by ~15 days since 1900) but with more extreme weather. New crops (peaches, kiwi, longer-season corn) are now possible; some traditional crops face new challenges."}},{"k":"1_why_nutrition","v":{"s":"1. Why Nutrition"}},{"k":"2_rdn_ladder","v":{"s":"2. RDN Ladder"}},{"k":"3_career_types","v":{"s":"3. Career Types"}},{"k":"4_maine_programs","v":{"s":"4. Maine Programs"}},{"k":"bachelor_s_degree","v":{"s":"Bachelor's degree"}},{"k":"supervised_practice_dietetic_internshi","v":{"s":"Supervised Practice (Dietetic Internship)"}},{"k":"master_s_degree_new_2024_requirement","v":{"s":"Master's degree (NEW 2024 requirement)"}},{"k":"cdr_registration_examination","v":{"s":"CDR Registration Examination"}},{"k":"entry_level_rdn","v":{"s":"Entry-level RDN"}},{"k":"specialized_senior_rdn","v":{"s":"Specialized / Senior RDN"}},{"k":"clinical_rdn","v":{"s":"Clinical RDN"}},{"k":"hospital_icu_dialysis_long_term_care_d","v":{"s":"Hospital, ICU, dialysis, long-term care. Diagnose nutrition needs, design tube-feeding regimens, work alongside MDs and nurses."}},{"k":"community_nutritionist","v":{"s":"Community Nutritionist"}},{"k":"wic_programs_community_health_centers_","v":{"s":"WIC programs, community health centers, public housing nutrition education, SNAP-Ed. Direct patient education + community programming."}},{"k":"public_health_nutritionist","v":{"s":"Public Health Nutritionist"}},{"k":"state_federal_health_departments_usda_","v":{"s":"State / federal health departments, USDA, CDC. Population-level nutrition policy, school food standards, food labeling."}},{"k":"school_nutrition_director","v":{"s":"School Nutrition Director"}},{"k":"manage_k_12_school_food_service_progra","v":{"s":"Manage K-12 school food service programs. Menu planning, USDA compliance, local-food procurement, budget management."}},{"k":"sports_performance_rdn","v":{"s":"Sports / Performance RDN"}},{"k":"athletic_departments_professional_spor","v":{"s":"Athletic departments, professional sports teams, fitness centers. Performance nutrition, hydration, recovery, eating disorder awareness in athletes."}},{"k":"food_industry_r_d","v":{"s":"Food Industry / R&D"}},{"k":"food_companies_kraft_general_mills_mai","v":{"s":"Food companies (Kraft, General Mills, Maine-based companies like Stonewall Kitchen). Product formulation, nutrition labeling, marketing review."}},{"k":"pediatric_rdn","v":{"s":"Pediatric RDN"}},{"k":"children_s_hospitals_pediatric_clinics","v":{"s":"Children's hospitals, pediatric clinics, feeding-disorder programs. Specialized in growth, allergy, NICU nutrition, eating disorders."}},{"k":"eating_disorder_rdn","v":{"s":"Eating Disorder RDN"}},{"k":"specialized_clinics_residential_treatm","v":{"s":"Specialized clinics, residential treatment centers, outpatient practices. Often paired with therapist; works under medical team. Highly specialized training."}},{"k":"private_practice_counseling","v":{"s":"Private Practice / Counseling"}},{"k":"self_employed_or_small_group_build_cli","v":{"s":"Self-employed or small group. Build client base over time. Telehealth options. Insurance billing varies by state."}},{"k":"career_pathways_2","v":{"s":"Career Pathways"}},{"k":"nutrition_careers_clear_and_honest","v":{"s":"Nutrition careers — clear and honest"}},{"k":"the_registered_dietitian_nutritionist_","v":{"s":"The Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) credential is the gold standard. As of 2024, RDN requires a Master's degree, an accredited supervised-practice internship (1200 hours), and passing a national exam. There are also paths in public health, food science, sports, and school nutrition that don't require RDN but offer related careers. This module is honest about timelines, pay, and the recent 2024 Master's change."}},{"k":"career_pathway_sections","v":{"s":"Career Pathway sections"}},{"k":"typical_rdn_pay","v":{"s":"typical RDN pay"}},{"k":"school_supervised_practice","v":{"s":"school + supervised practice"}},{"k":"master_s_now_required","v":{"s":"Master's now required"}},{"k":"nutritionist_vs_registered_dietitian_n","v":{"s":"⚠ \"Nutritionist\" vs \"Registered Dietitian Nutritionist\""}},{"k":"in_many_states_and_online_anyone_can_c","v":{"s":"In many states (and online), anyone can call themselves a \"nutritionist.\" The term is unregulated. There are real certified nutrition professionals (CNS — Certified Nutrition Specialist), but there are also Instagram \"nutritionists\" with no formal training selling supplements and meal plans."}},{"k":"registered_dietitian_nutritionist_rdn_","v":{"s":"Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) is regulated. "}},{"k":"earning_the_credential_requires_accred","v":{"s":"Earning the credential requires accredited education, supervised practice, a national exam, continuing education, and (in Maine) state licensure. RDN = regulated profession. \"Nutritionist\" alone = often not. When choosing whose food advice to trust, the credential matters."}},{"k":"why_this_career_path","v":{"s":"Why this career path?"}},{"k":"the_good","v":{"s":"✓ The good"}},{"k":"real_evidence_based_way_to_help_people","v":{"s":"Real, evidence-based way to help people"}},{"k":"stable_demand_every_hospital_and_schoo","v":{"s":"Stable demand — every hospital and school needs nutrition staff"}},{"k":"diverse_settings_clinical_community_pu","v":{"s":"Diverse settings: clinical, community, public health, industry"}},{"k":"path_to_private_practice_flexibility_o","v":{"s":"Path to private practice + flexibility over time"}},{"k":"maine_has_a_tight_supportive_professio","v":{"s":"Maine has a tight, supportive professional community"}},{"k":"the_hard_parts","v":{"s":"⚠ The hard parts"}},{"k":"7_year_path_with_a_master_s_requiremen","v":{"s":"7+ year path with a Master's requirement"}},{"k":"internship_match_is_competitive_50_mat","v":{"s":"Internship match is competitive (~50% match rate)"}},{"k":"entry_pay_50_65k_is_modest_for_the_edu","v":{"s":"Entry pay ($50-65K) is modest for the education investment"}},{"k":"insurance_coverage_of_nutrition_counse","v":{"s":"Insurance coverage of nutrition counseling is uneven"}},{"k":"working_with_eating_disorders_requires","v":{"s":"Working with eating disorders requires emotional sustainability"}},{"k":"rdn_credential_ladder_2024_requirement","v":{"s":"RDN credential ladder — 2024 requirements"}},{"k":"how_to_get_there","v":{"s":"How to get there: "}},{"k":"maine_education_programs","v":{"s":"Maine education programs"}},{"k":"maine_has_a_small_but_solid_set_of_nut","v":{"s":"Maine has a small but solid set of nutrition programs. The two main pathways are at UMaine (Orono) and Husson (Bangor)."}},{"k":"university_of_maine_human_nutrition_fo","v":{"s":"University of Maine — Human Nutrition + Foods"}},{"k":"husson_university_dietetics","v":{"s":"Husson University — Dietetics"}},{"k":"university_of_southern_maine_public_he","v":{"s":"University of Southern Maine — Public Health"}},{"k":"university_of_new_england_health_scien","v":{"s":"University of New England — Health Sciences"}},{"k":"other_places_to_look","v":{"s":"Other places to look"}},{"k":"maine_academy_of_nutrition_and_dieteti","v":{"s":"Maine Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics "}},{"k":"eatrightmaine_org_state_professional_o","v":{"s":"(eatrightmaine.org) — state professional organization. Job board, mentorship, networking events."}},{"k":"commission_on_dietetic_registration","v":{"s":"Commission on Dietetic Registration "}},{"k":"cdrnet_org_official_credentialing_body","v":{"s":"(cdrnet.org) — official credentialing body. Practice exam information."}},{"k":"eatrightpro_org_acend_accreditation_co","v":{"s":"(eatrightpro.org/acend) — accreditation council. Lists every accredited DPD and DI program nationwide."}},{"k":"careercenter_maine","v":{"s":"CareerCenter Maine "}},{"k":"mainecareercenter_gov_entry_level_open","v":{"s":"(mainecareercenter.gov) — entry-level openings, often through WIC, hospitals, and school food programs."}},{"k":"maine_wild_blueberries_on_oatmeal","v":{"s":"Maine wild blueberries on oatmeal"}},{"k":"maine_wild_blueberries_pack_33_more_an","v":{"s":"Maine wild blueberries pack 33% more antioxidants than cultivated."}},{"k":"two_eggs_any_style","v":{"s":"Two eggs, any style"}},{"k":"eggs_are_one_of_the_few_natural_vitami","v":{"s":"Eggs are one of the few natural vitamin-D foods."}},{"k":"greek_yogurt_with_honey","v":{"s":"Greek yogurt with honey"}},{"k":"calcium_standout","v":{"s":"Calcium standout."}},{"k":"whole_grain_toast_with_butter","v":{"s":"Whole-grain toast with butter"}},{"k":"quick_carbs_fiber_low_everything_else","v":{"s":"Quick carbs + fiber, low everything else."}},{"k":"fortified_cereal_with_milk","v":{"s":"Fortified cereal with milk"}},{"k":"fortified_iron_vit_d_calcium_nutrient_","v":{"s":"Fortified iron + vit D + calcium — nutrient-dense for the price."}},{"k":"lobster_roll_maine_summer","v":{"s":"Lobster roll (Maine summer)"}},{"k":"maine_lobster_protein_dense_vit_d_negl","v":{"s":"Maine lobster — protein-dense; vit D negligible."}},{"k":"tuna_sandwich_canned_light","v":{"s":"Tuna sandwich (canned light)"}},{"k":"canned_light_tuna_solid_omega_3_low_me","v":{"s":"Canned light tuna — solid omega-3, low mercury."}},{"k":"turkey_sandwich_on_whole_wheat","v":{"s":"Turkey sandwich on whole wheat"}},{"k":"balanced_protein_fiber","v":{"s":"Balanced protein + fiber."}},{"k":"mixed_greens_with_chickpeas_feta","v":{"s":"Mixed greens with chickpeas & feta"}},{"k":"iron_fiber_standout_add_citrus_for_iro","v":{"s":"Iron + fiber standout. Add citrus for iron absorption."}},{"k":"cheese_pizza_school_slice","v":{"s":"Cheese pizza (school slice)"}},{"k":"calcium_from_cheese_modest_fiber_iron","v":{"s":"Calcium from cheese; modest fiber/iron."}},{"k":"baked_atlantic_salmon_rice_greens","v":{"s":"Baked Atlantic salmon, rice, greens"}},{"k":"maine_fisheries_powerhouse_vit_d_omega","v":{"s":"Maine fisheries powerhouse — vit D + omega-3 in one plate."}},{"k":"spaghetti_with_marinara","v":{"s":"Spaghetti with marinara"}},{"k":"carb_forward_modest_iron_from_sauce_pa","v":{"s":"Carb-forward; modest iron from sauce + pasta."}},{"k":"roasted_chicken_sweet_potato_peas","v":{"s":"Roasted chicken, sweet potato, peas"}},{"k":"balanced_protein_complex_carbs_fiber","v":{"s":"Balanced protein + complex carbs + fiber."}},{"k":"beef_chili_with_beans","v":{"s":"Beef chili with beans"}},{"k":"iron_fiber_standout_beef_beans_togethe","v":{"s":"Iron + fiber standout. Beef + beans together stack well."}},{"k":"black_bean_tacos_with_cheese","v":{"s":"Black bean tacos with cheese"}},{"k":"plant_based_protein_huge_fiber_calcium","v":{"s":"Plant-based protein + huge fiber + calcium from cheese."}},{"k":"apple","v":{"s":"Apple"}},{"k":"fiber_only_standout_minimal_everything","v":{"s":"Fiber-only standout; minimal everything else."}},{"k":"cheese_stick","v":{"s":"Cheese stick"}},{"k":"calcium_dense_protein_dense","v":{"s":"Calcium-dense, protein-dense."}},{"k":"trail_mix_almonds_cranberries","v":{"s":"Trail mix (almonds + cranberries)"}},{"k":"healthy_fats_fiber_plant_iron","v":{"s":"Healthy fats, fiber, plant iron."}},{"k":"carrot_sticks_with_hummus","v":{"s":"Carrot sticks with hummus"}},{"k":"plant_fiber_protein","v":{"s":"Plant fiber + protein."}},{"k":"maine_maple_ice_cream","v":{"s":"Maine maple ice cream"}},{"k":"treat_calcium_present_low_everything_e","v":{"s":"Treat — calcium present, low everything else."}},{"k":"build_a_maine_day_summary","v":{"s":"Build a Maine Day — summary"}},{"k":"your_day_in_numbers","v":{"s":"Your day, in numbers"}},{"k":"six_nutrients_tracked_against_approxim","v":{"s":"Six nutrients tracked against approximate adolescent DRIs. The point is not to hit 100% on every bar — it is to see what a real day actually adds up to and what tends to be over- or under-represented."}},{"k":"nutrient_totals","v":{"s":"Nutrient totals"}},{"k":"maine_context","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine context"}},{"k":"standout_strengths","v":{"s":"✅ Standout strengths"}},{"k":"below_50_of_dri","v":{"s":"⚠️ Below 50% of DRI"}},{"k":"reminder_this_is_one_day_nutrient_need","v":{"s":"Reminder: this is one day. Nutrient needs balance over a week, not within a day. Persistent deficits across multiple days are what matter."}},{"k":"what_you_ate_today","v":{"s":"What you ate today"}},{"k":"build_another_day","v":{"s":"🔄 Build another day"}},{"k":"build_a_maine_day_2","v":{"s":"Build a Maine Day"}},{"k":"what_does_a_balanced_maine_day_actuall","v":{"s":"What does a balanced Maine day actually look like?"}},{"k":"pick_one_food_for_each_meal_from_a_mai","v":{"s":"Pick one food for each meal from a Maine-realistic short list. Watch the six nutrient bars update as you go. The bars are tied to approximate adolescent DRIs — but the point is not to \"win\" by hitting 100% on each one. It is to see what a real day adds up to and what tends to be over- or under-represented."}},{"k":"live_nutrient_totals","v":{"s":"Live nutrient totals"}},{"k":"chosen","v":{"s":"✓ chosen"}},{"k":"vitamin_d_5","v":{"s":"Vitamin D"}},{"k":"iron_5","v":{"s":"Iron"}},{"k":"folate","v":{"s":"Folate"}},{"k":"calcium_6","v":{"s":"Calcium"}},{"k":"iodine","v":{"s":"Iodine"}},{"k":"deficiency_detective_2","v":{"s":"Deficiency Detective"}},{"k":"10_clinical_vignettes_name_the_missing","v":{"s":"10 clinical vignettes — name the missing nutrient"}},{"k":"for_each_scenario_identify_which_of_6_","v":{"s":"For each scenario, identify which of 6 common nutrients is most likely deficient. Coaching after each pick names what makes this nutrient more likely than the others (and what would have to be different to make a different answer right)."}},{"k":"this_is_not_a_diagnostic_tool","v":{"s":"⚠️ This is not a diagnostic tool. "}},{"k":"real_deficiencies_require_lab_tests_no","v":{"s":"Real deficiencies require lab tests, not vignettes. The game teaches pattern recognition for screening; treatment + diagnosis stay with a clinician."}},{"k":"the_six_nutrients","v":{"s":"The six nutrients"}},{"k":"start_vignette_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start — vignette 1 of 10"}},{"k":"deficiency_detective_3","v":{"s":"Deficiency Detective"}},{"k":"vignette","v":{"s":"Vignette "}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_most_likely_missing_nutrient","v":{"s":"Pick the most likely missing nutrient"}},{"k":"all_10_vignettes_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 vignettes complete"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_vignette","v":{"s":"➡️ Next vignette"}},{"k":"very_pale","v":{"s":"Very pale"}},{"k":"often_clear_or_nearly_so_fine_if_you_h","v":{"s":"Often clear or nearly so. Fine if you have not been pushing fluids; overdrinking water has its own risks."}},{"k":"pale_yellow","v":{"s":"Pale yellow"}},{"k":"the_classic_well_hydrated_target_color","v":{"s":"The classic \"well-hydrated\" target color most clinicians teach."}},{"k":"light_yellow","v":{"s":"Light yellow"}},{"k":"still_in_the_green_zone_body_has_enoug","v":{"s":"Still in the green zone. Body has enough water for routine function."}},{"k":"yellow","v":{"s":"Yellow"}},{"k":"drink_water_in_the_next_hour_not_an_em","v":{"s":"Drink water in the next hour. Not an emergency, but you are trending toward thirsty."}},{"k":"dark_yellow","v":{"s":"Dark yellow"}},{"k":"about_1_2_body_weight_fluid_deficit_at","v":{"s":"About 1-2% body-weight fluid deficit. Attention, mood, short-term memory measurably drop here. Drink now."}},{"k":"amber","v":{"s":"Amber"}},{"k":"definitely_behind_sip_water_steadily_o","v":{"s":"Definitely behind. Sip water steadily over the next 1-2 hours. Avoid soda or caffeine right now."}},{"k":"if_this_is_post_exercise_or_after_illn","v":{"s":"If this is post-exercise or after illness, rehydrate with water + a salty snack or oral rehydration solution."}},{"k":"dark_brown","v":{"s":"Dark brown"}},{"k":"or_possibly_a_sign_of_something_else_m","v":{"s":"Or possibly a sign of something else (medication, liver, blood in urine). If you cannot rehydrate or it persists, see a clinician."}},{"k":"water","v":{"s":"Water"}},{"k":"sports_drink_typical","v":{"s":"Sports drink (typical)"}},{"k":"soda_cola","v":{"s":"Soda (cola)"}},{"k":"milk_1","v":{"s":"Milk (1%)"}},{"k":"ketogenic","v":{"s":"Ketogenic"}},{"k":"carbs_10_of_macros_body_shifts_to_fat_","v":{"s":"Carbs <10% of macros. Body shifts to fat oxidation; ketone production likely after several days."}},{"k":"carbs_10_30_stable_blood_sugar_likely_","v":{"s":"Carbs 10–30%. Stable blood sugar likely; common Mediterranean/paleo range."}},{"k":"balanced","v":{"s":"Balanced"}},{"k":"carbs_30_55_within_the_broader_balance","v":{"s":"Carbs 30–55%. Within the broader balanced range; the full Dietary Guidelines for Americans AMDR for carbs is 45–65%, so the upper end of this bucket and the start of \"high-carb\" both still fall inside DGA. Sustainable for most people."}},{"k":"carbs_55_75_the_55_65_segment_is_squar","v":{"s":"Carbs 55–75%. The 55–65% segment is squarely within the DGA AMDR. The 65–75% segment is the endurance-athlete or traditional Asian-rice-based pattern; fine with fiber + protein."}},{"k":"carbs_75_with_low_fiber_high_glycemic_","v":{"s":"Carbs >75% with low fiber → high glycemic load. Common in heavily-processed snack-dominated days, but well above the US average (NHANES puts US carb intake at ~46–50%)."}},{"k":"macro_inquiry_predict_the_energy_state","v":{"s":"Macro Inquiry — Predict the Energy State"}},{"k":"macro_inquiry_widget","v":{"s":"🔬 Macro Inquiry Widget"}},{"k":"set_carbohydrate_protein_fat_grams_plu","v":{"s":"Set carbohydrate, protein, fat (grams) plus fiber. Predict the energy state before reading it. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"total_macros","v":{"s":"total macros"}},{"k":"satiety_index","v":{"s":"Satiety index"}},{"k":"fiber_adj_glycemic_proxy","v":{"s":"Fiber-adj glycemic proxy"}},{"k":"carbs_g","v":{"s":"Carbs (g)"}},{"k":"protein_g","v":{"s":"Protein (g)"}},{"k":"fat_g","v":{"s":"Fat (g)"}},{"k":"fiber_g","v":{"s":"Fiber (g)"}},{"k":"log_this_macro_profile","v":{"s":"📋 Log this macro profile"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_which_macro_most_affec","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (which macro most affects satiety? Glycemic load? They're different.)"}},{"k":"e_g_fiber_blunts_the_glycemic_load_alm","v":{"s":"e.g., fiber blunts the glycemic load almost 1-for-1 with carb increase..."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_open_questions","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions"}},{"k":"open_questions_no_answer_key","v":{"s":"Open questions (no answer key)"}},{"k":"why_might_a_high_carb_meal_feel_differ","v":{"s":"Why might a \"high-carb\" meal feel different at 200g of oats vs 200g of white rice + sugar?"}},{"k":"how_does_fat_slow_glycemic_response_tr","v":{"s":"How does fat slow glycemic response? Try 80g carbs with 5g fat vs 30g fat."}},{"k":"a_100g_protein_day_what_trade_offs_wou","v":{"s":"A 100g protein day — what trade-offs would you need to make in the other macros to keep total calories sensible?"}},{"k":"when_could_ketogenic_help_or_harm_cons","v":{"s":"When could ketogenic help or harm? Consider: ketogenic-diet therapy for refractory epilepsy is well-established (decades of RCTs); nutrient gaps, hypoglycemia risk on diabetes meds, social/sustainability cost, and contested questions about long-term kidney/cardiovascular impact are all open. (For CKD specifically, high-protein patterns are the more-supported concern; isolated ketosis-vs-CKD evidence is mixed.)"}},{"k":"i_can_explain_why_this_macro_split_yie","v":{"s":"I can explain why this macro split yields this energy state."}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words..."}},{"k":"inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a","v":{"s":"Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. Satiety + GI proxies are illustrative; real glycemic response varies with food matrix, cooking, and individual metabolism. Not medical advice."}},{"k":"daily_needs","v":{"s":"Daily Needs"}},{"k":"status_check","v":{"s":"Status Check"}},{"k":"beverage_compare","v":{"s":"Beverage Compare"}},{"k":"sweat_rate","v":{"s":"Sweat Rate"}},{"k":"hydration_lab_2","v":{"s":"Hydration Lab"}},{"k":"hydration_is_body_function_not_body_sh","v":{"s":"Hydration is body function, not body shape."}},{"k":"roughly_60_of_your_body_weight_is_wate","v":{"s":"Roughly 60% of your body weight is water. Every cell uses it. The brain runs about 75% water and the first thing that drops with mild dehydration (1-2% body-weight fluid loss) is attention, short-term memory, and mood. This lab covers four things: how much fluid you actually need, how to read your own body for status, which drinks help and which do not, and how to handle hydration as a student athlete. Hydration is never a substitute for eating. If you find yourself using water to feel full or skip meals, talk to a trusted adult and call NEDA at 1-800-931-2237."}},{"k":"hydration_lab_sections","v":{"s":"Hydration Lab sections"}},{"k":"how_much_fluid_do_i_need_today","v":{"s":"How much fluid do I need today?"}},{"k":"tell_us_your_age_band_sex_assigned_at_","v":{"s":"Tell us your age band, sex assigned at birth (the reference values are biological, not gender), and how active you are. The result is a fluid-intake estimate, not a daily quota you must hit. Body size, climate, illness, and pregnancy all shift this. Listen to thirst."}},{"k":"age_band","v":{"s":"Age band"}},{"k":"ages_4_8","v":{"s":"Ages 4-8"}},{"k":"ages_9_13","v":{"s":"Ages 9-13"}},{"k":"ages_14_18","v":{"s":"Ages 14-18"}},{"k":"ages_19","v":{"s":"Ages 19+"}},{"k":"reference_sex_assigned_at_birth","v":{"s":"Reference (sex assigned at birth)"}},{"k":"female_reference","v":{"s":"Female reference"}},{"k":"male_reference","v":{"s":"Male reference"}},{"k":"activity_level_today","v":{"s":"Activity level today"}},{"k":"mostly_sitting","v":{"s":"Mostly sitting"}},{"k":"light_walking_easy_pe","v":{"s":"Light (walking, easy PE)"}},{"k":"moderate_1_hr_practice","v":{"s":"Moderate (1 hr practice)"}},{"k":"vigorous_long_practice_heat","v":{"s":"Vigorous (long practice / heat)"}},{"k":"sources_institute_of_medicine_2005_die","v":{"s":"Sources: Institute of Medicine (2005) Dietary Reference Intakes for Water, still cited by AAP and NIH ODS. Activity adder from NATA 2017 Position Statement on Fluid Replacement."}},{"k":"the_8_point_urine_color_check","v":{"s":"The 8-point urine-color check"}},{"k":"look_at_your_urine_the_next_time_you_u","v":{"s":"Look at your urine the next time you use the bathroom. Pick the color closest to what you saw. The Armstrong 8-point scale is widely used in athletic training and clinical hydration assessment."}},{"k":"urine_color","v":{"s":"Urine color"}},{"k":"other_body_signals_worth_tracking","v":{"s":"Other body signals worth tracking"}},{"k":"thirst_is_a_useful_late_signal_not_an_","v":{"s":"Thirst is a useful late signal, not an early one — by the time you feel thirsty you are usually already at 1-2% deficit."}},{"k":"lightheaded_standing_up_heart_pounding","v":{"s":"Lightheaded standing up, heart pounding at rest, headache without other cause: classic mild-moderate dehydration in clinical guides."}},{"k":"foggy_attention_slower_math_irritabili","v":{"s":"Foggy attention, slower math, irritability: documented in studies as effects of mild dehydration in adolescents."}},{"k":"dry_lips_and_dry_mouth_are_real_signal","v":{"s":"Dry lips and dry mouth are real signals — but skin \"tenting\" (slow snap-back) only shows up at moderate-to-severe dehydration."}},{"k":"persistent_dark_urine_despite_drinking","v":{"s":"Persistent dark urine despite drinking water? See a clinician — could be liver, medication, or blood-related."}},{"k":"if_you_find_yourself_using_water_to_fe","v":{"s":"If you find yourself using water to feel \"full\" or override hunger, that is a body-cue mismatch worth talking about with a trusted adult. NEDA helpline: 1-800-931-2237."}},{"k":"what_is_in_8_oz_of","v":{"s":"What is in 8 oz of..."}},{"k":"sodium_and_added_sugar_matter_for_hydr","v":{"s":"Sodium and added sugar matter for hydration efficacy AND for daily nutrient targets. AAP guidance: added sugar under 25 g/day for ages 2-18; sodium under ~2,300 mg/day for adolescents."}},{"k":"sodium_4","v":{"s":"Sodium"}},{"k":"sugar","v":{"s":"Sugar"}},{"k":"caffeine","v":{"s":"Caffeine"}},{"k":"when_it_fits","v":{"s":"When it fits: "}},{"k":"a_note_on_energy_drinks","v":{"s":"A note on energy drinks"}},{"k":"aap_says_outright_that_energy_drinks_m","v":{"s":"AAP says outright that energy drinks (Monster, Red Bull, etc.) \"should not be consumed by children or adolescents.\" High caffeine doses, heart-rhythm effects, and added stimulants are the reason. They are not in this comparison because they should not be on this list."}},{"k":"how_fast_do_you_sweat_nata_standard","v":{"s":"How fast do you sweat? (NATA standard)"}},{"k":"for_student_athletes_weigh_yourself_be","v":{"s":"For student athletes. Weigh yourself before practice, weigh yourself after, log the fluid you drank during, and the duration. The math: 1 kg of weight lost = 1 L of sweat. NATA recommends replacing 100-150% of fluid lost over the next 2-6 hours."}},{"k":"weight_before_kg","v":{"s":"Weight before (kg)"}},{"k":"e_g_65_0","v":{"s":"e.g. 65.0"}},{"k":"weight_after_kg","v":{"s":"Weight after (kg)"}},{"k":"e_g_64_2","v":{"s":"e.g. 64.2"}},{"k":"fluid_drunk_during_ml","v":{"s":"Fluid drunk during (mL)"}},{"k":"e_g_500","v":{"s":"e.g. 500"}},{"k":"duration_minutes","v":{"s":"Duration (minutes)"}},{"k":"e_g_90","v":{"s":"e.g. 90"}},{"k":"fluid_lost","v":{"s":"Fluid lost"}},{"k":"sweat_rate_2","v":{"s":"Sweat rate"}},{"k":"replace_target","v":{"s":"Replace target"}},{"k":"replace_target_uses_nata_s_125_recomme","v":{"s":"Replace target uses NATA's 125% recommendation (midpoint of the 100-150% guideline) to account for ongoing post-exercise sweat and urine loss."}},{"k":"sources_nata_2017_position_statement_o","v":{"s":"Sources: NATA 2017 Position Statement on Fluid Replacement; ACSM Position Stand on Exercise and Fluid Replacement (2007)."}},{"k":"my_nutrition_kit_2","v":{"s":"My Nutrition Kit"}},{"k":"practice_path","v":{"s":"Practice path"}},{"k":"practice_path_title","v":{"s":"Start with a useful, low-pressure action"}},{"k":"practice_path_copy","v":{"s":"The full library is still below. These four routes keep the first move concrete: build a private kit, decode a label, check hydration, or explore macro patterns."}},{"k":"modules_seen","v":{"s":"Modules seen"}},{"k":"core_modules","v":{"s":"Core modules"}},{"k":"frame","v":{"s":"Frame"}},{"k":"physiology_first","v":{"s":"Physiology-first"}},{"k":"explain_macro_split_energy_state","v":{"s":"Explain why this macro split yields this energy state"}},{"k":"bm_back_to_plate","v":{"s":"Back to the Macronutrient Lab"}},{"k":"bm_candidate_note","v":{"s":"These are candidates, not a diagnosis. Symptoms have many causes; a doctor can test for a nutrient shortfall."}},{"k":"bm_card_desc","v":{"s":"Pick any nutrient and watch the organs that use it glow on a glass 3D figure, with a stream tracing the route from your gut. Click an organ to see everything it depends on. Then predict before you look: which region is iron's main address?"}},{"k":"bm_card_subtitle","v":{"s":"3D — where each nutrient goes to work"}},{"k":"bm_card_title","v":{"s":"Nutrient Body Map"}},{"k":"bm_clear_region","v":{"s":"Clear region selection"}},{"k":"bm_compare_footnote","v":{"s":"Sharing an address does not make two nutrients interchangeable — they do different jobs once they arrive. Overlap tells you where a shortfall of either would be felt."}},{"k":"bm_compare_legend","v":{"s":"On the figure these burn brightest; a region only one of them serves sits dim."}},{"k":"bm_compare_none","v":{"s":"None"}},{"k":"bm_compare_only","v":{"s":"only"}},{"k":"bm_compare_only_2","v":{"s":"only"}},{"k":"bm_compare_second","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"bm_compare_shared","v":{"s":"Shared addresses"}},{"k":"bm_compare_title","v":{"s":"Two nutrients, one body"}},{"k":"bm_compare_why","v":{"s":"Why this pair is worth knowing"}},{"k":"bm_compare_with","v":{"s":"with"}},{"k":"bm_controls","v":{"s":"3D view controls"}},{"k":"bm_desc_line","v":{"s":"Glowing organs use the selected nutrient; dots trace the route from the gut. Drag or use the buttons to turn the figure. Click an organ to select it."}},{"k":"bm_energy_copy","v":{"s":"This is your body doing nothing at all: lying still, awake, not digesting a meal. Most of the fuel you eat is spent here, not on exercise. The bigger an organ glows on the figure, the larger its share."}},{"k":"bm_energy_headline","v":{"s":"The number worth remembering"}},{"k":"bm_energy_headline_copy","v":{"s":"Your brain is about 2% of your body weight and spends about 20% of your resting energy — and a teenage brain, still building connections, spends a larger share than an adult one. Thinking is expensive, and it is paid for out of what you eat."}},{"k":"bm_energy_open_module","v":{"s":"Open Energy & Metabolism"}},{"k":"bm_energy_other","v":{"s":"🫧 Everything else"}},{"k":"bm_energy_other_note","v":{"s":"Skin, gut, immune cells, bone, blood and fat tissue. Individually small, together a fifth of the bill."}},{"k":"bm_energy_source","v":{"s":"Healthy-adult averages from Wang et al. (2010) and Brody, Nutritional Biochemistry. Real shares vary with body composition, age and health — these describe the pattern, not a person."}},{"k":"bm_energy_title","v":{"s":"Where your resting energy goes"}},{"k":"bm_explored","v":{"s":"explored"}},{"k":"bm_from_atlas","v":{"s":"From the Micronutrient Atlas"}},{"k":"bm_group_fats","v":{"s":"Essential fats"}},{"k":"bm_group_macros","v":{"s":"Macronutrients + water"}},{"k":"bm_group_minerals","v":{"s":"Minerals"}},{"k":"bm_group_vitamins","v":{"s":"Vitamins"}},{"k":"bm_host_old","v":{"s":"The 3D figure needs a newer host module than this build has. The region list below carries the same map."}},{"k":"bm_in_plain_words","v":{"s":"In plain words"}},{"k":"bm_intro_copy","v":{"s":"Pick a nutrient and the body regions that use it glow, while a stream traces the route from your gut. Click an organ in the figure (or in the list) to see everything it depends on. Drag to turn the figure, or use the buttons and arrow keys. Nothing here is a target or a score: this is a map of what your body does with food."}},{"k":"bm_intro_title","v":{"s":"Every nutrient has an address"}},{"k":"bm_label_all","v":{"s":"🏷️ Label all"}},{"k":"bm_labels_on","v":{"s":"🏷️ Labels on"}},{"k":"bm_legend_energy","v":{"s":"Resting energy"}},{"k":"bm_legend_nutrients","v":{"s":"nutrients"}},{"k":"bm_legend_organs","v":{"s":"organs"}},{"k":"bm_legend_shared","v":{"s":"shared"}},{"k":"bm_loading","v":{"s":"Loading the 3D figure…"}},{"k":"bm_mode_compare","v":{"s":"Compare two"}},{"k":"bm_mode_compare_hint","v":{"s":"Do these two overlap?"}},{"k":"bm_mode_energy","v":{"s":"Resting energy"}},{"k":"bm_mode_energy_hint","v":{"s":"Which organs spend your fuel?"}},{"k":"bm_mode_group","v":{"s":"What the figure shows"}},{"k":"bm_mode_nutrient","v":{"s":"One nutrient"}},{"k":"bm_mode_nutrient_hint","v":{"s":"Where does this nutrient go?"}},{"k":"bm_my_plate","v":{"s":"My plate"}},{"k":"bm_no_webgl","v":{"s":"The 3D figure could not start on this device, usually because WebGL is unavailable or blocked. The region list below carries the same map."}},{"k":"bm_open_atlas","v":{"s":"Open the full Atlas card"}},{"k":"bm_pick_heading","v":{"s":"Pick a nutrient"}},{"k":"bm_pick_heading_first","v":{"s":"Pick the first nutrient"}},{"k":"bm_pick_prompt","v":{"s":"Pick a nutrient above, or click a region of the figure, to see what your body does with it."}},{"k":"bm_plate_copy_a","v":{"s":"nutrients from your plate reach"}},{"k":"bm_plate_copy_b","v":{"s":"body regions. Click any nutrient to trace it on its own."}},{"k":"bm_plate_empty","v":{"s":"Your plate is empty. Build one in the Macronutrient Lab, then come back."}},{"k":"bm_plate_title","v":{"s":"Where your plate goes"}},{"k":"bm_predict_all","v":{"s":"All of its addresses:"}},{"k":"bm_predict_copy_a","v":{"s":"Before looking at the figure:"}},{"k":"bm_predict_copy_b","v":{"s":"is delivered to several body regions. Pick ONE region you think is a main address, then check the map."}},{"k":"bm_predict_done","v":{"s":"predictions made"}},{"k":"bm_predict_group","v":{"s":"Prediction choices"}},{"k":"bm_predict_hit","v":{"s":"Yes — that is one of its addresses."}},{"k":"bm_predict_miss","v":{"s":"Not a main address for this one — look at what lit up instead."}},{"k":"bm_predict_next","v":{"s":"Next nutrient →"}},{"k":"bm_predict_title","v":{"s":"Where does it go? Predict, then look"}},{"k":"bm_region","v":{"s":"Body region"}},{"k":"bm_region_many","v":{"s":"regions"}},{"k":"bm_region_needs","v":{"s":"Nutrients it depends on"}},{"k":"bm_region_one","v":{"s":"region"}},{"k":"bm_region_short","v":{"s":"When supply runs short"}},{"k":"bm_region_uses","v":{"s":"What it does with nutrients"}},{"k":"bm_regions_heading","v":{"s":"Body regions — click one to ask what it needs"}},{"k":"bm_reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset view"}},{"k":"bm_rotate_left","v":{"s":"Rotate left"}},{"k":"bm_rotate_right","v":{"s":"Rotate right"}},{"k":"bm_step_1","v":{"s":"Pick a nutrient"}},{"k":"bm_step_2","v":{"s":"Watch where it goes"}},{"k":"bm_step_3","v":{"s":"Click an organ to ask what it needs"}},{"k":"bm_tilt_down","v":{"s":"Tilt down"}},{"k":"bm_tilt_up","v":{"s":"Tilt up"}},{"k":"bm_title_2","v":{"s":"Nutrient Body Map"}},{"k":"bm_viewer_aria","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D body map. Arrow keys rotate and tilt, plus and minus zoom, 0 resets the view."}},{"k":"bm_viewer_energy","v":{"s":"Glass figure — resting energy"}},{"k":"bm_viewer_none","v":{"s":"Glass figure"}},{"k":"bm_viewer_plate","v":{"s":"Glass figure — your plate"}},{"k":"bm_where_it_works","v":{"s":"Where it works — click a region"}},{"k":"bm_works_in","v":{"s":"Works in"}},{"k":"bm_zoom_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"bm_zoom_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"energy_to_body_button","v":{"s":"Open the body map →"}},{"k":"energy_to_body_copy","v":{"s":"The 3D body map can light each organ by how much resting energy it spends."}},{"k":"energy_to_body_title","v":{"s":"🫀 See these shares on the body"}},{"k":"glossary_title","v":{"s":"📖 Words you will meet in this lab"}},{"k":"hint_carbs","v":{"s":"Main fuel for brain and muscles"}},{"k":"hint_energy","v":{"s":"How much fuel the plate holds, not a target"}},{"k":"hint_fat","v":{"s":"Cell membranes, hormones, carries vitamins A, D, E, K"}},{"k":"hint_fiber","v":{"s":"Feeds gut microbes, steadies blood sugar"}},{"k":"hint_protein","v":{"s":"Building blocks: muscle, enzymes, immune cells"}},{"k":"journey_now","v":{"s":"Now:"}},{"k":"plate_to_body_button","v":{"s":"Open the body map →"}},{"k":"plate_to_body_many","v":{"s":"nutrients on this plate reach"}},{"k":"plate_to_body_one","v":{"s":"nutrient on this plate reaches"}},{"k":"plate_to_body_regions","v":{"s":"body regions. Watch them light up on the 3D figure."}},{"k":"plate_to_body_title","v":{"s":"🫀 See where this plate goes"}},{"k":"show_on_3d_body","v":{"s":"🫀 Show on the 3D body"}},{"k":"start_here_copy","v":{"s":"New to the lab? Build a plate, follow it into the body in 3D, then decode a real label. Each step takes about five minutes and none of them keeps score."}},{"k":"start_here_dismiss","v":{"s":"Got it"}},{"k":"start_here_dismiss_aria","v":{"s":"Hide the start-here guide"}},{"k":"start_here_title","v":{"s":"Start here — a three-step first lap"}},{"k":"start_step_1","v":{"s":"Build a plate"}},{"k":"start_step_1_hint","v":{"s":"Add foods; watch carbs, protein, fat and fiber add up"}},{"k":"start_step_2","v":{"s":"Follow it into the body"}},{"k":"start_step_2_hint","v":{"s":"See which organs use each nutrient, in 3D"}},{"k":"start_step_3","v":{"s":"Decode a label"}},{"k":"start_step_3_hint","v":{"s":"Serving-size tricks, % Daily Value, added sugars"}},{"k":"where_it_works","v":{"s":"Where it works in the body"}}],"id":556}},{"k":"off","v":{"s":"OFF"}},{"k":"on","v":{"s":"ON"}},{"k":"optics","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_the_wave_profile_csv_could_not_be_downloaded_in_t","v":{"s":"The wave profile CSV could not be downloaded in this environment."}},{"k":"sr_the_trial_csv_could_not_be_downloaded_in_this_env","v":{"s":"The trial CSV could not be downloaded in this environment."}},{"k":"sr_the_lab_note_could_not_be_downloaded_in_this_envi","v":{"s":"The lab note could not be downloaded in this environment."}},{"k":"a11y_wavelength","v":{"s":"Wavelength"}},{"k":"a11y_aperture","v":{"s":"Aperture"}},{"k":"a11y_log_frequency","v":{"s":"log₁₀(frequency)"}},{"k":"optics_lab","v":{"s":"Optics Lab"}},{"k":"ap_physics_2_ray_diagrams_snell_s_law_","v":{"s":"AP Physics 2: ray diagrams, Snell's law, mirrors, lenses, interference, diffraction, polarization. Side-by-side sims + calculators."}},{"k":"optics_lab_navigation","v":{"s":"Optics Lab navigation"}},{"k":"home","v":{"s":"🏠 Home"}},{"k":"sample_problems","v":{"s":"Sample problems"}},{"k":"reflection","v":{"s":"🪞 Reflection"}},{"k":"mirrors_ray_diagrams","v":{"s":"Mirrors + ray diagrams"}},{"k":"refraction","v":{"s":"🌊 Refraction"}},{"k":"snell_s_law_tir","v":{"s":"Snell's law + TIR"}},{"k":"lenses","v":{"s":"🔍 Lenses"}},{"k":"thin_lens_image_formation","v":{"s":"Thin lens + image formation"}},{"k":"interference","v":{"s":"✨ Interference"}},{"k":"double_slit_fringes","v":{"s":"Double-slit fringes"}},{"k":"diffraction","v":{"s":"〰 Diffraction"}},{"k":"single_slit_grating","v":{"s":"Single-slit + grating"}},{"k":"polarization","v":{"s":"↕ Polarization"}},{"k":"malus_s_law","v":{"s":"Malus's law"}},{"k":"phenomena","v":{"s":"🌈 Phenomena"}},{"k":"rainbows_mirages_after_images_color_po","v":{"s":"Rainbows · mirages · after-images · color · polarized sky"}},{"k":"calculators","v":{"s":"🧮 Calculators"}},{"k":"14_interactive_calculators_visualizers","v":{"s":"14 interactive calculators + visualizers"}},{"k":"encyclopedia","v":{"s":"📚 Encyclopedia"}},{"k":"120_optical_phenomena","v":{"s":"120+ optical phenomena"}},{"k":"scientists","v":{"s":"👨‍🔬 Scientists"}},{"k":"50_optical_scientists_from_antiquity_t","v":{"s":"50+ optical scientists from antiquity to today"}},{"k":"history","v":{"s":"📜 History"}},{"k":"100_optics_milestones","v":{"s":"100+ optics milestones"}},{"k":"instruments","v":{"s":"🔭 Instruments"}},{"k":"30_telescopes_microscopes_cameras_lase","v":{"s":"30+ telescopes, microscopes, cameras, lasers, displays"}},{"k":"lab_kits","v":{"s":"🧪 Lab Kits"}},{"k":"20_hands_on_experiments","v":{"s":"20+ hands-on experiments"}},{"k":"worked_problems","v":{"s":"📝 Worked Problems"}},{"k":"30_step_by_step_ap_problems","v":{"s":"30+ step-by-step AP problems"}},{"k":"careers","v":{"s":"💼 Careers"}},{"k":"40_optics_career_paths","v":{"s":"40+ optics career paths"}},{"k":"deep_dives","v":{"s":"🎓 Deep Dives"}},{"k":"lasers_vision_color_astronomy_more","v":{"s":"Lasers, vision, color, astronomy + more"}},{"k":"glossary","v":{"s":"📖 Glossary+"}},{"k":"120_optics_terms_with_examples","v":{"s":"120+ optics terms with examples"}},{"k":"reference","v":{"s":"📊 Reference"}},{"k":"trivia_records_quotes_safety_data_teac","v":{"s":"Trivia · records · quotes · safety · data · teaching tips"}},{"k":"sleuth","v":{"s":"🕵️ Sleuth"}},{"k":"predict_image_from_setup","v":{"s":"Predict image from setup"}},{"k":"quiz","v":{"s":"📝 Quiz"}},{"k":"ap_exam_practice","v":{"s":"AP exam practice"}},{"k":"mastery","v":{"s":"🏅 Mastery"}},{"k":"concept_progress_which_questions_you_h","v":{"s":"Concept progress + which questions you have nailed"}},{"k":"inquiry","v":{"s":"🔬 Inquiry"}},{"k":"snell_s_law_sandbox_predict_tir_disper","v":{"s":"Snell's law sandbox — predict TIR + dispersion"}},{"k":"reflection_plane_curved_mirrors","v":{"s":"🪞 Reflection — plane + curved mirrors"}},{"k":"refraction_snell_s_law_total_internal_","v":{"s":"🌊 Refraction — Snell's law + total internal reflection"}},{"k":"lenses_converging_diverging_thin_lens_","v":{"s":"🔍 Lenses — converging + diverging, thin lens equation"}},{"k":"interference_young_s_double_slit","v":{"s":"✨ Interference — Young's double-slit"}},{"k":"diffraction_single_slit_grating","v":{"s":"〰 Diffraction — single slit + grating"}},{"k":"polarization_malus_s_law","v":{"s":"↕ Polarization — Malus's law"}},{"k":"total_internal_reflection","v":{"s":"Total Internal Reflection"}},{"k":"angle_exceeds_critical_angle_no_light_","v":{"s":"Angle exceeds critical angle. No light transmitted — all reflects back. Basis of fiber optics, diamond brilliance, prism periscopes."}},{"k":"near_critical","v":{"s":"Near critical"}},{"k":"approaching_tir_boundary_refracted_ray","v":{"s":"Approaching TIR boundary. Refracted ray runs nearly along the surface; transmitted intensity dropping."}},{"k":"bend_toward_normal","v":{"s":"Bend toward normal"}},{"k":"light_entering_denser_medium_n_n_refra","v":{"s":"Light entering denser medium (n₁ < n₂). Refracted ray bends TOWARD the normal — slower light in dense medium."}},{"k":"bend_away_from_normal","v":{"s":"Bend away from normal"}},{"k":"light_entering_less_dense_medium_refra","v":{"s":"Light entering less-dense medium. Refracted ray bends AWAY from normal — faster light in light medium."}},{"k":"grazing_incidence","v":{"s":"Grazing incidence"}},{"k":"steep_angle_from_normal_much_energy_re","v":{"s":"Steep angle from normal. Much energy reflected (Fresnel rises), little transmitted."}},{"k":"snell_s_law_inquiry_refraction_tir_dis","v":{"s":"🔬 Snell's Law Inquiry — Refraction, TIR, Dispersion"}},{"k":"set_n_n_incidence_angle_and_wavelength","v":{"s":"Set n₁, n₂, incidence angle, and wavelength, then observe where the model transitions from refraction to total internal reflection. The result updates live; record a hypothesis or pattern you notice."}},{"k":"refracted","v":{"s":"θ₂ (refracted)"}},{"k":"critical_angle","v":{"s":"Critical angle"}},{"k":"n_toy","v":{"s":"n₂(λ) toy"}},{"k":"n_medium_1","v":{"s":"n₁ (medium 1)"}},{"k":"n_medium_2","v":{"s":"n₂ (medium 2)"}},{"k":"incidence_angle","v":{"s":"Incidence angle θ₁"}},{"k":"wavelength","v":{"s":"Wavelength λ"}},{"k":"log_this_configuration","v":{"s":"📋 Log this configuration"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_where_exactly_does_tir","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (where exactly does TIR start, and why does diamond TIR more aggressively than glass?)"}},{"k":"e_g_diamond_has_critical_angle_24_vs_g","v":{"s":"e.g., diamond has critical angle ~24° vs glass ~42°, so any steeper angle TIRs..."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_open_questions","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions"}},{"k":"open_questions_no_answer_key","v":{"s":"Open questions (no answer key)"}},{"k":"why_is_there_no_critical_angle_when_go","v":{"s":"Why is there NO critical angle when going from less-dense to more-dense?"}},{"k":"set_water_air_n_1_33_n_1_00_what_s_the","v":{"s":"Set water→air (n₁=1.33 n₂=1.00). What's the critical angle? Why do fish see the world compressed into a cone above?"}},{"k":"in_a_rainbow_why_does_red_bend_less_th","v":{"s":"In a rainbow, why does red bend less than blue? Connect to the wavelength slider."}},{"k":"how_does_a_prism_separate_white_light_","v":{"s":"How does a prism separate white light if all wavelengths obey the same Snell's law?"}},{"k":"i_can_explain_why_this_n_n_combination","v":{"s":"I can explain why this n₁/n₂/θ combination yields this refraction state."}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words..."}},{"k":"inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a","v":{"s":"Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. Dispersion model is illustrative (Cauchy-like first-order); real dispersion curves are material-specific (Sellmeier coefficients)."}},{"k":"concept_locked_in","v":{"s":"Concept locked in"}},{"k":"focus_choose_bench","v":{"s":"Choose a light bench"}},{"k":"focus_choose_bench_copy","v":{"s":"Start with a core simulation, then open the reference library when you need deeper AP support."}},{"k":"focus_reflection","v":{"s":"Mirror bench"}},{"k":"focus_reflection_copy","v":{"s":"Move objects around curved mirrors and compare ray diagrams with the mirror equation."}},{"k":"focus_refraction","v":{"s":"Snell lab"}},{"k":"focus_refraction_copy","v":{"s":"Change media and angle to see bending, critical angle, and total internal reflection."}},{"k":"focus_lenses","v":{"s":"Lens bench"}},{"k":"focus_lenses_copy","v":{"s":"Test converging and diverging lenses while the thin-lens math updates beside the model."}},{"k":"focus_interference","v":{"s":"Wave pattern bench"}},{"k":"focus_interference_copy","v":{"s":"Tune slit spacing, wavelength, and screen distance to build fringe intuition."}},{"k":"focus_diffraction","v":{"s":"Diffraction bench"}},{"k":"focus_diffraction_copy","v":{"s":"Compare single-slit and grating patterns without opening the whole reference library."}},{"k":"focus_polarization","v":{"s":"Polarizer bench"}},{"k":"focus_polarization_copy","v":{"s":"Rotate polarizers and connect the brightness changes to Malus law."}},{"k":"focus_quiz","v":{"s":"AP practice"}},{"k":"focus_quiz_copy","v":{"s":"Check readiness with topic-aware questions and keep mastered items in your study queue."}},{"k":"focus_mastery","v":{"s":"Mastery tracker"}},{"k":"focus_mastery_copy","v":{"s":"See which optics concepts are already solid and which deserve another pass."}},{"k":"focus_inquiry","v":{"s":"Inquiry sandbox"}},{"k":"focus_inquiry_copy","v":{"s":"Run prediction-first refraction experiments when you are ready for open-ended exploration."}},{"k":"focus_library","v":{"s":"Reference library"}},{"k":"focus_library_copy","v":{"s":"Use the expanded optics library for phenomena, calculators, scientists, history, instruments, and worked AP examples."}},{"k":"route_reflection","v":{"s":"Mirrors"}},{"k":"route_reflection_copy","v":{"s":"Trace reflected rays and image type."}},{"k":"route_refraction","v":{"s":"Refraction"}},{"k":"route_refraction_copy","v":{"s":"Bend light across materials."}},{"k":"route_lenses","v":{"s":"Lenses"}},{"k":"route_lenses_copy","v":{"s":"Form real and virtual images."}},{"k":"route_interference","v":{"s":"Interference"}},{"k":"route_interference_copy","v":{"s":"Build bright and dark fringes."}},{"k":"route_polarization","v":{"s":"Polarizers"}},{"k":"route_polarization_copy","v":{"s":"Rotate axes and test brightness."}},{"k":"light_bench","v":{"s":"Light bench"}},{"k":"status_station","v":{"s":"Station"}},{"k":"status_mastery","v":{"s":"AP mastery"}},{"k":"status_runs","v":{"s":"Practice runs"}},{"k":"status_library","v":{"s":"Library"}},{"k":"expanded","v":{"s":"Expanded"}},{"k":"core","v":{"s":"Core"}},{"k":"hide_reference_tabs","v":{"s":"Hide reference tabs"}},{"k":"show_reference_tabs","v":{"s":"Show reference tabs"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"TIR hypothesis"}},{"k":"back_to_benches","v":{"s":"← Bench home"}},{"k":"filter_reference_library","v":{"s":"Filter reference library"}},{"k":"library_all","v":{"s":"All"}},{"k":"libr