(geo-specialty)"}},{"k":"science_writer_geo_focus","v":{"s":"Science writer (geo-focus)"}},{"k":"geoarchaeologist","v":{"s":"Geoarchaeologist"}},{"k":"tectonic_geomorphologist","v":{"s":"Tectonic geomorphologist"}},{"k":"hazards_risk_modeler","v":{"s":"Hazards risk modeler"}},{"k":"geothermal_engineer","v":{"s":"Geothermal engineer"}},{"k":"carbon_sequestration_scientist","v":{"s":"Carbon sequestration scientist"}},{"k":"mount_katahdin_2","v":{"s":"Mount Katahdin"}},{"k":"acadia_national_park","v":{"s":"Acadia National Park"}},{"k":"mt_mica_paris","v":{"s":"Mt Mica (Paris)"}},{"k":"mt_apatite_auburn","v":{"s":"Mt Apatite (Auburn)"}},{"k":"plumbago_mine","v":{"s":"Plumbago Mine"}},{"k":"stonington_granite","v":{"s":"Stonington Granite"}},{"k":"acadia_schoodic_peninsula","v":{"s":"Acadia Schoodic Peninsula"}},{"k":"bay_of_fundy_approach","v":{"s":"Bay of Fundy approach"}},{"k":"roque_bluffs","v":{"s":"Roque Bluffs"}},{"k":"mt_battie","v":{"s":"Mt Battie"}},{"k":"quoddy_head","v":{"s":"Quoddy Head"}},{"k":"penobscot_bay_drumlins","v":{"s":"Penobscot Bay drumlins"}},{"k":"maine_moose_megunticook_fault","v":{"s":"Maine moose-Megunticook fault"}},{"k":"boothbay_region_pluton","v":{"s":"Boothbay region pluton"}},{"k":"mt_blue_state_park","v":{"s":"Mt Blue State Park"}},{"k":"saddleback_mountain","v":{"s":"Saddleback Mountain"}},{"k":"mt_abraham","v":{"s":"Mt Abraham"}},{"k":"old_man_of_the_mountain_nh_near_me","v":{"s":"Old Man of the Mountain (NH, near ME)"}},{"k":"mt_washington_nh","v":{"s":"Mt Washington (NH)"}},{"k":"cathedral_pines","v":{"s":"Cathedral Pines"}},{"k":"aroostook_county_limestone","v":{"s":"Aroostook County limestone"}},{"k":"maine_state_mineral_tourmaline","v":{"s":"Maine state mineral: Tourmaline"}},{"k":"pemaquid_point","v":{"s":"Pemaquid Point"}},{"k":"reid_state_park","v":{"s":"Reid State Park"}},{"k":"popham_beach","v":{"s":"Popham Beach"}},{"k":"ferry_beach","v":{"s":"Ferry Beach"}},{"k":"wells_reserve_at_laudholm","v":{"s":"Wells Reserve at Laudholm"}},{"k":"casco_bay_islands","v":{"s":"Casco Bay islands"}},{"k":"mount_cutler","v":{"s":"Mount Cutler"}},{"k":"the_notch_mahoosuc","v":{"s":"The Notch (Mahoosuc)"}},{"k":"pacific_2","v":{"s":"Pacific"}},{"k":"nazca_2","v":{"s":"Nazca"}},{"k":"s_american","v":{"s":"S. American"}},{"k":"african_2","v":{"s":"African"}},{"k":"eurasian_2","v":{"s":"Eurasian"}},{"k":"indian","v":{"s":"Indian"}},{"k":"n_american","v":{"s":"N. American"}},{"k":"hadean","v":{"s":"Hadean"}},{"k":"the_hadean_eon_4_6_4_0_ga_earth_formed","v":{"s":"The Hadean Eon (4.6-4.0 Ga). Earth formed from solar nebula accretion. The surface was a magma ocean bombarded by meteorites. The Moon formed from a giant impact. No tectonic plates existed."}},{"k":"archean","v":{"s":"Archean"}},{"k":"the_archean_eon_4_0_2_5_ga_earth_s_cru","v":{"s":"The Archean Eon (4.0-2.5 Ga). Earth's crust solidified. Small protocontinents called cratons formed the first stable landmasses. Plate tectonics may have begun. Oceans covered most of the surface."}},{"k":"rodinia","v":{"s":"Rodinia"}},{"k":"the_mesoproterozoic_era_rodinia_was_ea","v":{"s":"The Mesoproterozoic Era. Rodinia was Earth's first supercontinent, assembling ~1.1 Ga. All major cratons were joined. Its breakup (~750 Ma) triggered Snowball Earth glaciations and eventually led to the Cambrian explosion of life."}},{"k":"pangaea","v":{"s":"Pangaea"}},{"k":"the_late_paleozoic_carboniferous_permi","v":{"s":"The late Paleozoic (Carboniferous-Permian). Pangaea (\"All Earth\") was the most recent supercontinent. It stretched from pole to pole. The Tethys Sea separated its eastern margins. Coal swamps covered equatorial regions."}},{"k":"breakup","v":{"s":"Breakup"}},{"k":"the_triassic_jurassic_boundary_pangaea","v":{"s":"The Triassic-Jurassic boundary. Pangaea began rifting ~200 Ma. The central Atlantic opened as North America separated from Africa. Dinosaurs dominated both halves. Laurasia = N. America + Eurasia. Gondwana = S. America + Africa + India + Antarctica + Australia."}},{"k":"cretaceous","v":{"s":"Cretaceous"}},{"k":"the_cretaceous_period_south_america_se","v":{"s":"The Cretaceous Period. South America separated from Africa. India detached from Antarctica and began its northward journey at ~15 cm/year. The Atlantic Ocean widened significantly. Sea levels were 200m higher than today. Dinosaurs still ruled."}},{"k":"cenozoic","v":{"s":"Cenozoic"}},{"k":"the_early_cenozoic_eocene_india_s_coll","v":{"s":"The early Cenozoic (Eocene). India's collision with Eurasia created the Himalayas and Tibetan Plateau. Australia separated from Antarctica. The Alps formed as Africa pushed into Europe. Mammals diversified after the dinosaur extinction."}},{"k":"modern","v":{"s":"Modern"}},{"k":"the_present_day_seven_major_tectonic_p","v":{"s":"The present day. Seven major tectonic plates move at 2-10 cm/year. The Atlantic continues to widen. The Pacific Ring of Fire hosts 75% of the world's volcanoes. The Himalayas still rise ~5mm/year as India pushes into Eurasia."}},{"k":"plate_tectonics_keyboard_shortcuts_1_t","v":{"s":"Plate Tectonics. Keyboard shortcuts: 1 through 4 switch tabs, L toggles labels, C toggles convection currents."}},{"k":"plate_tectonics","v":{"s":"Plate Tectonics"}},{"k":"interactive_earth","v":{"s":"Interactive Earth"}},{"k":"interactive_sim","v":{"s":"Interactive Sim"}},{"k":"run_the_simulation_lab_timeline_and_qu","v":{"s":"Run the simulation, lab, timeline, and quiz"}},{"k":"world_catalogs","v":{"s":"World Catalogs"}},{"k":"plates_faults_volcanoes_mountains_tsun","v":{"s":"Plates, faults, volcanoes, mountains, tsunamis, hotspots"}},{"k":"earth_history","v":{"s":"Earth History"}},{"k":"rocks_fossils_ages_and_the_deep_past","v":{"s":"Rocks, fossils, ages, and the deep past"}},{"k":"people_and_places","v":{"s":"People and Places"}},{"k":"history_of_geology_biographies_maine_s","v":{"s":"History of geology, biographies, Maine sites, careers"}},{"k":"reference_and_activities","v":{"s":"Reference and Activities"}},{"k":"glossary_and_classroom_activities","v":{"s":"Glossary and classroom activities"}},{"k":"hub","v":{"s":"🏠 Hub"}},{"k":"search_tools","v":{"s":"Search tools..."}},{"k":"no_matches","v":{"s":"No matches."}},{"k":"drag_plates_toward_each_other_converge","v":{"s":"drag plates toward each other (convergent), apart (divergent), or sideways past each other (transform), and watch which boundary produces volcanoes, trenches, mid-ocean ridges, or earthquakes. Convection currents show why plates move; toggle them off (C key) to focus on surface features."}},{"k":"interactive_plate_tectonics_cross_sect","v":{"s":"Interactive plate tectonics cross-section visualization"}},{"k":"speed","v":{"s":"⏱ Speed:"}},{"k":"simulation_speed_multiplier","v":{"s":"Simulation speed multiplier"}},{"k":"labels","v":{"s":"Labels"}},{"k":"labels_2","v":{"s":"🏷 Labels"}},{"k":"currents","v":{"s":"Currents"}},{"k":"currents_2","v":{"s":"🌀 Currents"}},{"k":"erupt","v":{"s":"🌋 Erupt!"}},{"k":"plain","v":{"s":"Plain"}},{"k":"using_simple_everyday_words_and_short_","v":{"s":"using simple everyday words and short sentences"}},{"k":"grade_5","v":{"s":"Grade 5"}},{"k":"for_a_5th_grade_student_brief_and_frie","v":{"s":"for a 5th grade student, brief and friendly"}},{"k":"high_school","v":{"s":"High School"}},{"k":"for_a_high_school_earth_science_studen","v":{"s":"for a high school earth-science student, accurate but accessible"}},{"k":"ai_tectonics_tutor","v":{"s":"AI tectonics tutor"}},{"k":"explain_at_my_level","v":{"s":"✨ Explain at my level"}},{"k":"reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level"}},{"k":"click_explain_for_the_ai_tutor_to_desc","v":{"s":"Click “Explain” for the AI tutor to describe the current simulation at your chosen reading level."}},{"k":"feel_the_richter_scale_it_is_logarithm","v":{"s":"feel the Richter scale. It is logarithmic: each whole number is 10x more shaking and about 32x more energy. A 7.0 is not twice a 3.5, it is over 100,000x more energy. Slide through magnitudes and watch the damage tier flip from green to amber to red. P-waves arrive first, S-waves second, surface waves do most of the damage."}},{"k":"earthquake_magnitude_simulator","v":{"s":"Earthquake Magnitude Simulator"}},{"k":"earthquake_magnitude_richter_scale","v":{"s":"Earthquake magnitude (Richter scale)"}},{"k":"minor","v":{"s":"Minor"}},{"k":"barely_felt","v":{"s":"Barely felt"}},{"k":"light","v":{"s":"Light"}},{"k":"rattling_minor_damage","v":{"s":"Rattling, minor damage"}},{"k":"strong","v":{"s":"Strong"}},{"k":"structural_damage","v":{"s":"Structural damage"}},{"k":"compression_fastest","v":{"s":"Compression (fastest)"}},{"k":"shear_medium","v":{"s":"Shear (medium)"}},{"k":"surface","v":{"s":"Surface"}},{"k":"rolling_slowest_most_damage","v":{"s":"Rolling (slowest, most damage)"}},{"k":"interactive_plate_tectonics_seismograp","v":{"s":"Interactive plate tectonics seismograph visualization"}},{"k":"continental_drift_timeline","v":{"s":"Continental Drift Timeline"}},{"k":"timeline_era","v":{"s":"timeline era"}},{"k":"switch_to_simulation_tab_to_see_plate_","v":{"s":"💡 Switch to Simulation tab to see plate positions, or use Time-Lapse to animate through all eras."}},{"k":"earth_through_time","v":{"s":"Earth Through Time"}},{"k":"interactive_plate_tectonics_earth_cros","v":{"s":"Interactive plate tectonics Earth cross-section visualization"}},{"k":"drag_the_timeline_slider_to_see_how_ea","v":{"s":"💡 Drag the timeline slider to see how Earth's continents have shifted over billions of years"}},{"k":"hadean_2","v":{"s":"Hadean"}},{"k":"molten_magma_ocean","v":{"s":"Molten magma ocean"}},{"k":"archean_2","v":{"s":"Archean"}},{"k":"vaalbara","v":{"s":"Vaalbara"}},{"k":"kenorland","v":{"s":"Kenorland"}},{"k":"rodinia_2","v":{"s":"Rodinia"}},{"k":"rodinia_3","v":{"s":"Rodinia"}},{"k":"pangaea_2","v":{"s":"Pangaea"}},{"k":"pangaea_3","v":{"s":"Pangaea"}},{"k":"breakup_2","v":{"s":"Breakup"}},{"k":"laurasia","v":{"s":"Laurasia"}},{"k":"gondwana","v":{"s":"Gondwana"}},{"k":"cretaceous_2