,"v":{"s":"4m long, 170 kg, fast bipedal omnivore. 'Ostrich mimic.'"}},{"k":"recently_shown_to_have_full_feathered_","v":{"s":"Recently shown to have full feathered plumage. Could likely run 50+ km/h."}},{"k":"deinonychus","v":{"s":"Deinonychus"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_115_108_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (115-108 Ma)"}},{"k":"western_us","v":{"s":"Western US"}},{"k":"3_5m_long_70_100_kg_feathered_theropod","v":{"s":"3.5m long, 70-100 kg, feathered theropod with sickle claw. Inspired the 'raptors' in Jurassic Park."}},{"k":"john_ostrom_s_1969_study_revolutionize","v":{"s":"John Ostrom's 1969 study revolutionized dinosaur science by showing they were warm-blooded + bird-like."}},{"k":"microraptor","v":{"s":"Microraptor"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_120_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (120 Ma)"}},{"k":"china_liaoning","v":{"s":"China (Liaoning)"}},{"k":"1m_long_1_kg_feathered_theropod_with_4","v":{"s":"1m long, 1 kg, feathered theropod with 4 wings. Likely glided."}},{"k":"liaoning_fossils_preserve_feather_colo","v":{"s":"Liaoning fossils preserve feather color (black with iridescent sheen) via melanosome analysis."}},{"k":"argentinosaurus","v":{"s":"Argentinosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_96_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (96 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"35m_long_70_tonnes_herbivore_one_of_th","v":{"s":"35m long, 70+ tonnes, herbivore. One of the largest land animals ever."}},{"k":"known_from_incomplete_remains_mass_est","v":{"s":"Known from incomplete remains; mass estimates range from 60-100 tonnes."}},{"k":"carnotaurus","v":{"s":"Carnotaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_72_71_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (72-71 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina_2","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"8m_long_1_5_tonnes_bipedal_predator_wi","v":{"s":"8m long, 1.5 tonnes, bipedal predator with bull-like horns. Tiny arms."}},{"k":"only_abelisaurid_with_complete_skull_s","v":{"s":"Only abelisaurid with complete skull. Suggests South American theropod evolution diverged."}},{"k":"giganotosaurus","v":{"s":"Giganotosaurus"}},{"k":"mid_cretaceous_98_97_ma","v":{"s":"Mid-Cretaceous (98-97 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina_3","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"13m_long_8_tonnes_bipedal_predator_pos","v":{"s":"13m long, 8 tonnes, bipedal predator. Possibly larger than T. rex."}},{"k":"discovered_1993_by_amateur_fossil_hunt","v":{"s":"Discovered 1993 by amateur fossil hunter Rubén Carolini. Patagonian discoveries continue to reshape dinosaur scale."}},{"k":"therizinosaurus","v":{"s":"Therizinosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_70_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (70 Ma)"}},{"k":"mongolia","v":{"s":"Mongolia"}},{"k":"10m_long_5_tonnes_herbivorous_theropod","v":{"s":"10m long, 5 tonnes, herbivorous theropod with 1m claws. Bizarre body."}},{"k":"long_claws_may_have_been_for_feeding_p","v":{"s":"Long claws may have been for feeding (pulling branches), not predation."}},{"k":"mononykus","v":{"s":"Mononykus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_73_68_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (73-68 Ma)"}},{"k":"mongolia_2","v":{"s":"Mongolia"}},{"k":"1m_long_3_5_kg_tiny_theropod_with_sing","v":{"s":"1m long, 3.5 kg, tiny theropod with single short forelimb claw. Possible insect eater."}},{"k":"convergent_with_anteaters_unique_anato","v":{"s":"Convergent with anteaters. Unique anatomy in dinosaurs."}},{"k":"coelophysis","v":{"s":"Coelophysis"}},{"k":"late_triassic_216_203_ma","v":{"s":"Late Triassic (216-203 Ma)"}},{"k":"southwestern_us_ghost_ranch_nm","v":{"s":"Southwestern US (Ghost Ranch, NM)"}},{"k":"3m_long_25_kg_bipedal_predator_ghost_r","v":{"s":"3m long, 25 kg, bipedal predator. Ghost Ranch quarry yielded thousands."}},{"k":"some_preserved_with_juvenile_bones_in_","v":{"s":"Some preserved with juvenile bones in gut - once thought cannibalism but now likely lizards."}},{"k":"plateosaurus","v":{"s":"Plateosaurus"}},{"k":"late_triassic_214_204_ma","v":{"s":"Late Triassic (214-204 Ma)"}},{"k":"central_europe","v":{"s":"Central Europe"}},{"k":"8m_long_4_tonnes_early_herbivore_one_o","v":{"s":"8m long, 4 tonnes, early herbivore. One of the first big dinosaurs."}},{"k":"trossingen_germany_quarry_yielded_50_s","v":{"s":"Trossingen, Germany quarry yielded 50+ specimens - possible drought victims at watering hole."}},{"k":"eoraptor","v":{"s":"Eoraptor"}},{"k":"late_triassic_231_ma","v":{"s":"Late Triassic (231 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina_4","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"1m_long_10_kg_bipedal_early_dinosaur_a","v":{"s":"1m long, 10 kg, bipedal early dinosaur. Among earliest known dinosaurs."}},{"k":"discovered_1991_in_valle_de_la_luna_re","v":{"s":"Discovered 1991 in Valle de la Luna. Reshaped dinosaur origins research."}},{"k":"herrerasaurus","v":{"s":"Herrerasaurus"}},{"k":"late_triassic_231_ma_2","v":{"s":"Late Triassic (231 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina_5","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"3_6m_long_200_kg_bipedal_early_predato","v":{"s":"3-6m long, 200 kg, bipedal early predator."}},{"k":"contemporaneous_with_eoraptor_sometime","v":{"s":"Contemporaneous with Eoraptor. Sometimes considered a separate dinosaur lineage."}},{"k":"dilophosaurus","v":{"s":"Dilophosaurus"}},{"k":"early_jurassic_193_ma","v":{"s":"Early Jurassic (193 Ma)"}},{"k":"southwestern_us","v":{"s":"Southwestern US"}},{"k":"7m_long_400_kg_bipedal_predator_with_d","v":{"s":"7m long, 400 kg, bipedal predator with double crest on head."}},{"k":"jurassic_park_depicted_spitting_frill_","v":{"s":"Jurassic Park depicted spitting + frill - no fossil evidence for either. Real animal was much larger."}},{"k":"cryolophosaurus","v":{"s":"Cryolophosaurus"}},{"k":"early_jurassic_194_ma","v":{"s":"Early Jurassic (194 Ma)"}},{"k":"antarctica_3","v":{"s":"Antarctica"}},{"k":"6m_long_500_kg_bipedal_predator_with_e","v":{"s":"6m long, 500 kg, bipedal predator with Elvis-like head crest."}},{"k":"first_dinosaur_found_in_antarctica_199","v":{"s":"First dinosaur found in Antarctica (1991). Suggests early Jurassic Antarctic forests."}},{"k":"mamenchisaurus","v":{"s":"Mamenchisaurus"}},{"k":"late_jurassic_160_145_ma","v":{"s":"Late Jurassic (160-145 Ma)"}},{"k":"china","v":{"s":"China"}},{"k":"35m_long_30_tonnes_herbivore_with_neck","v":{"s":"35m long, 30 tonnes, herbivore with neck nearly half its body length."}},{"k":"largest_known_neck_of_any_dinosaur_cer","v":{"s":"Largest known neck of any dinosaur. Cervical vertebrae extremely elongate."}},{"k":"shantungosaurus","v":{"s":"Shantungosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_75_65_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (75-65 Ma)"}},{"k":"china_2","v":{"s":"China"}},{"k":"15m_long_16_tonnes_largest_hadrosaur_w","v":{"s":"15m long, 16 tonnes, largest hadrosaur. Wide duck-like beak."}},{"k":"among_largest_ornithischian_dinosaurs_","v":{"s":"Among largest ornithischian dinosaurs. Found in Shandong, China."}},{"k":"microceratus","v":{"s":"Microceratus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_70_ma_2","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (70 Ma)"}},{"k":"mongolia_3","v":{"s":"Mongolia"}},{"k":"0_6m_long_3_kg_smallest_ceratopsian_bi","v":{"s":"0.6m long, 3 kg, smallest ceratopsian. Bipedal."}},{"k":"reminds_us_that_ceratopsians_weren_t_a","v":{"s":"Reminds us that ceratopsians weren't all giants. Many small species existed."}},{"k":"pachyrhinosaurus","v":{"s":"Pachyrhinosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_73_68_ma_2","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (73-68 Ma)"}},{"k":"north_america_ak_to_ab","v":{"s":"North America (AK to AB)"}},{"k":"8m_long_4_tonnes_ceratopsian_with_mass","v":{"s":"8m long, 4 tonnes, ceratopsian with massive nose boss instead of horn."}},{"k":"pipestone_creek_bonebed_ab_yielded_100","v":{"s":"Pipestone Creek bonebed (AB) yielded 100+ individuals - herd died crossing flooded river."}},{"k":"styracosaurus","v":{"s":"Styracosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_75_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (75 Ma)"}},{"k":"western_n_america_3","v":{"s":"Western N America"}},{"k":"5_5m_long_3_tonnes_ceratopsian_with_6_","v":{"s":"5.5m long, 3 tonnes, ceratopsian with 6 long horns on frill. Spectacular display."}},{"k":"likely_lived_in_herds_frill_probably_f","v":{"s":"Likely lived in herds. Frill probably for display + species recognition."}},{"k":"maiasaura","v":{"s":"Maiasaura"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_76_74_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (76-74 Ma)"}},{"k":"montana_egg_mountain","v":{"s":"Montana (Egg Mountain)"}},{"k":"9m_long_4_tonnes_hadrosaur_good_mother","v":{"s":"9m long, 4 tonnes, hadrosaur. 'Good mother lizard' - nest sites found with babies."}},{"k":"first_dinosaur_known_to_have_cared_for","v":{"s":"First dinosaur known to have cared for young in nests. Jack Horner's 1979 discovery."}},{"k":"hypsilophodon","v":{"s":"Hypsilophodon"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_130_125_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (130-125 Ma)"}},{"k":"isle_of_wight_uk","v":{"s":"Isle of Wight, UK"}},{"k":"2m_long_20_kg_small_bipedal_herbivore_","v":{"s":"2m long, 20 kg, small bipedal herbivore. Once thought arboreal."}},{"k":"most_complete_european_cretaceous_dino","v":{"s":"Most complete European Cretaceous dinosaur known. Multiple specimens found."}},{"k":"gallimimus","v":{"s":"Gallimimus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_74_70_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (74-70 Ma)"}},{"k":"mongolia_4","v":{"s":"Mongolia"}},{"k":"6m_long_440_kg_ostrich_mimic_theropod_","v":{"s":"6m long, 440 kg, ostrich-mimic theropod. Likely omnivorous + fast."}},{"k":"featured_in_jurassic_park_stampede_sce","v":{"s":"Featured in Jurassic Park stampede scene. Estimated max speed 50 km/h."}},{"k":"ouranosaurus","v":{"s":"Ouranosaurus"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_125_112_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (125-112 Ma)"}},{"k":"niger_sahara","v":{"s":"Niger (Sahara)"}},{"k":"7m_long_1_6_tonnes_sail_backed_herbivo","v":{"s":"7m long, 1.6 tonnes, sail-backed herbivore. Iguanodontid."}},{"k":"sail_may_have_been_for_thermoregulatio","v":{"s":"Sail may have been for thermoregulation in hot North African environment."}},{"k":"suchomimus","v":{"s":"Suchomimus"}},{"k":"mid_cretaceous_125_112_ma","v":{"s":"Mid-Cretaceous (125-112 Ma)"}},{"k":"niger","v":{"s":"Niger"}},{"k":"11m_long_3_tonnes_spinosaurid_predator","v":{"s":"11m long, 3 tonnes, spinosaurid predator. Croc-like skull."}},{"k":"discovered_by_paul_sereno_1997_likely_","v":{"s":"Discovered by Paul Sereno 1997. Likely fish-eater."}},{"k":"carcharodontosaurus","v":{"s":"Carcharodontosaurus"}},{"k":"mid_cretaceous_99_94_ma","v":{"s":"Mid-Cretaceous (99-94 Ma)"}},{"k":"northern_africa","v":{"s":"Northern Africa"}},{"k":"13m_long_7_tonnes_allosaur_relative_ap","v":{"s":"13m long, 7 tonnes, allosaur-relative apex predator. Shark-toothed lizard."}},{"k":"first_specimens_destroyed_at_munich_du","v":{"s":"First specimens destroyed at Munich during WWII. Re-discovered 1990s in Morocco."}},{"k":"mapusaurus","v":{"s":"Mapusaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_96_93_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (96-93 Ma)"}},{"k":"argentina_6","v":{"s":"Argentina"}},{"k":"13m_long_6_tonnes_allosaur_relative_ap","v":{"s":"13m long, 6 tonnes, allosaur-relative apex predator. May have hunted in groups."}},{"k":"multiple_individuals_found_together_fi","v":{"s":"Multiple individuals found together - first evidence of pack-hunting in carcharodontosaurs."}},{"k":"yutyrannus","v":{"s":"Yutyrannus"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_125_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (125 Ma)"}},{"k":"liaoning_china","v":{"s":"Liaoning, China"}},{"k":"9m_long_1_4_tonnes_large_tyrannosauroi","v":{"s":"9m long, 1.4 tonnes, large tyrannosauroid with feathers."}},{"k":"largest_known_feathered_dinosaur_suppo","v":{"s":"Largest known feathered dinosaur. Supports view that T. rex relatives were feathered."}},{"k":"sinosauropteryx","v":{"s":"Sinosauropteryx"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_125_ma_2","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (125 Ma)"}},{"k":"liaoning","v":{"s":"Liaoning"}},{"k":"1m_long_5_kg_first_feathered_dinosaur_","v":{"s":"1m long, 5 kg, first feathered dinosaur discovered (1996). Counter-shaded coloring."}},{"k":"melanosome_analysis_showed_orange_and_","v":{"s":"Melanosome analysis showed orange-and-white striped tail. Helped prove feathers came first."}},{"k":"confuciusornis","v":{"s":"Confuciusornis"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_125_120_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (125-120 Ma)"}},{"k":"liaoning_2","v":{"s":"Liaoning"}},{"k":"0_3m_long_1_5_kg_early_bird_with_primi","v":{"s":"0.3m long, 1.5 kg, early bird with primitive jaw. Beaks; no teeth."}},{"k":"mass_mortality_deposits_show_flocking_","v":{"s":"Mass-mortality deposits show flocking behavior. Some specimens show pigmented feathers."}},{"k":"anchiornis","v":{"s":"Anchiornis"}},{"k":"late_jurassic_160_155_ma","v":{"s":"Late Jurassic (160-155 Ma)"}},{"k":"liaoning_3","v":{"s":"Liaoning"}},{"k":"0_35m_long_0_7_kg_four_winged_feathere","v":{"s":"0.35m long, 0.7 kg, four-winged feathered theropod. Possibly nocturnal."}},{"k":"color_reconstruction_2010_showed_black","v":{"s":"Color reconstruction (2010) showed black-white-orange iridescence. Pre-dates Archaeopteryx."}},{"k":"archaeopteryx_2","v":{"s":"Archaeopteryx"}},{"k":"late_jurassic_150_ma","v":{"s":"Late Jurassic (150 Ma)"}},{"k":"solnhofen_germany","v":{"s":"Solnhofen, Germany"}},{"k":"0_5m_long_1_kg_transitional_bird_with_","v":{"s":"0.5m long, 1 kg, transitional bird with teeth + claws + feathers."}},{"k":"discovered_1861_2_years_after_darwin_h","v":{"s":"Discovered 1861, 2 years after Darwin. Hugely supported evolution."}},{"k":"sauropelta","v":{"s":"Sauropelta"}},{"k":"early_cretaceous_108_105_ma","v":{"s":"Early Cretaceous (108-105 Ma)"}},{"k":"western_n_america_4","v":{"s":"Western N America"}},{"k":"5m_long_1_5_tonnes_nodosaurid_armored_","v":{"s":"5m long, 1.5 tonnes, nodosaurid armored herbivore. Spikes on neck."}},{"k":"body_armor_preserved_in_mummy_specimen","v":{"s":"Body armor preserved in mummy specimens. Spikes likely for defense + display."}},{"k":"nodosaurus","v":{"s":"Nodosaurus"}},{"k":"mid_cretaceous_98_95_ma","v":{"s":"Mid-Cretaceous (98-95 Ma)"}},{"k":"kansas_wyoming","v":{"s":"Kansas + Wyoming"}},{"k":"6m_long_1_5_tonnes_armored_herbivore_n","v":{"s":"6m long, 1.5 tonnes, armored herbivore. No tail club."}},{"k":"borealopelta_nodosaur_mummy_suncor_min","v":{"s":"Borealopelta nodosaur mummy (Suncor mine, AB 2011) preserved skin, scales, even gut contents."}},{"k":"edmontonia","v":{"s":"Edmontonia"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_76_67_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (76-67 Ma)"}},{"k":"western_n_america_5","v":{"s":"Western N America"}},{"k":"7m_long_3_tonnes_large_nodosaur_with_s","v":{"s":"7m long, 3 tonnes, large nodosaur with shoulder spikes."}},{"k":"heavy_armor_spikes_show_high_predation","v":{"s":"Heavy armor + spikes show high predation pressure from contemporary tyrannosaurs."}},{"k":"hadrosaurus","v":{"s":"Hadrosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_80_78_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (80-78 Ma)"}},{"k":"new_jersey","v":{"s":"New Jersey"}},{"k":"8m_long_3_tonnes_duck_billed_herbivore","v":{"s":"8m long, 3 tonnes, duck-billed herbivore. First nearly-complete US dinosaur skeleton (1858)."}},{"k":"discovery_by_william_parker_foulke_lau","v":{"s":"Discovery by William Parker Foulke launched American paleontology. NJ state dinosaur."}},{"k":"edmontosaurus","v":{"s":"Edmontosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_73_66_ma","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (73-66 Ma)"}},{"k":"western_n_america_6","v":{"s":"Western N America"}},{"k":"12m_long_4_tonnes_large_hadrosaur_with","v":{"s":"12m long, 4 tonnes, large hadrosaur with broad bill. May have lived in massive herds."}},{"k":"trackways_show_herding_behavior_common","v":{"s":"Trackways show herding behavior. Common in late Cretaceous floral assemblages."}},{"k":"therizinosaurus_2","v":{"s":"Therizinosaurus"}},{"k":"late_cretaceous_70_ma_3","v":{"s":"Late Cretaceous (70 Ma)"}},{"k":"mongolia_5","v":{"s":"Mongolia"}},{"k":"10m_long_5_tonnes_herbivorous_theropod_2","v":{"s":"10m long, 5 tonnes, herbivorous theropod with 1m claws."}},{"k":"claws_among_the_longest_in_nature_almo","v":{"s":"Claws among the longest in nature. Almost certainly for stripping vegetation, not hunting."}},{"k":"mass_extinctions","v":{"s":"Mass Extinctions"}},{"k":"earth_has_seen_multiple_mass_extinctio","v":{"s":"Earth has seen multiple mass extinction events, each followed by long recovery + evolution of new forms. The current Anthropocene extinction may be the sixth."}},{"k":"end_ordovician_444_ma","v":{"s":"End-Ordovician (444 Ma)"}},{"k":"85_of_marine_species_died","v":{"s":"85% of marine species died"}},{"k":"possible_causes_gamma_ray_burst_glacia","v":{"s":"Possible causes: gamma-ray burst, glaciation, sea-level drop. Geographic isolation of Gondwana drove ice-house climate."}},{"k":"what_died","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"loss_of_nearly_all_graptolites_brachio","v":{"s":"Loss of nearly all graptolites, brachiopods, conodonts. First major animal extinction."}},{"k":"recovery_took_30_million_years_new_sil","v":{"s":"Recovery took ~30 million years; new Silurian biota emerged different from before."}},{"k":"late_devonian_372_359_ma","v":{"s":"Late Devonian (372-359 Ma)"}},{"k":"75_of_species_died_mostly_marine","v":{"s":"75% of species died, mostly marine"}},{"k":"two_pulses_kellwasser_hangenberg_event","v":{"s":"Two pulses (Kellwasser + Hangenberg events). Possible causes: glaciation, marine anoxia from spreading land plants."}},{"k":"what_died_2","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"end_of_reef_building_stromatoporoids_p","v":{"s":"End of reef-building stromatoporoids. Placoderm fish hit hard. End of major Paleozoic reef ecosystem."}},{"k":"mississippian_aftermath_saw_return_of_","v":{"s":"Mississippian aftermath saw return of crinoid forests + early sharks."}},{"k":"end_permian_252_ma","v":{"s":"End-Permian (252 Ma)"}},{"k":"96_of_marine_70_of_land_species_died_t","v":{"s":"96% of marine + 70% of land species died - the Great Dying"}},{"k":"causes_siberian_traps_volcanism_10_mil","v":{"s":"Causes: Siberian Traps volcanism (10 million km³ of basalt over 1 million years) released massive CO2 + SO2. Ocean acidification + warming + anoxia followed."}},{"k":"what_died_3","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"trilobites_rugose_corals_eurypterids_a","v":{"s":"Trilobites + rugose corals + eurypterids all extinct. Lystrosaurus dominated land afterward."}},{"k":"recovery_took_10_million_years_longest","v":{"s":"Recovery took 10+ million years - longest of any mass extinction. Triassic life genuinely new."}},{"k":"end_triassic_201_ma","v":{"s":"End-Triassic (201 Ma)"}},{"k":"76_of_species_died","v":{"s":"76% of species died"}},{"k":"causes_camp_volcanism_central_atlantic","v":{"s":"Causes: CAMP volcanism (Central Atlantic Magmatic Province as Pangaea broke apart) released massive CO2. Ocean acidification + warming."}},{"k":"what_died_4","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"conodonts_gone_many_archosaur_lines_ex","v":{"s":"Conodonts gone. Many archosaur lines extinct. Cleared niches for dinosaur radiation."}},{"k":"dinosaurs_took_advantage_of_empty_nich","v":{"s":"Dinosaurs took advantage of empty niches in Jurassic to become Earth's dominant fauna."}},{"k":"end_cretaceous_66_ma","v":{"s":"End-Cretaceous (66 Ma)"}},{"k":"75_of_species_died_including_all_non_a","v":{"s":"75% of species died including all non-avian dinosaurs"}},{"k":"causes_chicxulub_asteroid_impact_10_km","v":{"s":"Causes: Chicxulub asteroid impact (10 km, Yucatán) + Deccan Traps volcanism. Impact threw debris worldwide; fires + cold + acid rain."}},{"k":"what_died_5","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"all_non_avian_dinosaurs_marine_reptile","v":{"s":"All non-avian dinosaurs, marine reptiles, ammonites, pterosaurs gone. Mammals + birds survived + radiated."}},{"k":"tanis_fossil_site_nd_2019_preserves_th","v":{"s":"Tanis fossil site (ND, 2019) preserves the very day of impact - debris-encrusted fish."}},{"k":"eocene_oligocene_34_ma","v":{"s":"Eocene-Oligocene (34 Ma)"}},{"k":"30_of_species_less_catastrophic","v":{"s":"30% of species, less catastrophic"}},{"k":"causes_antarctic_glaciation_began_ocea","v":{"s":"Causes: Antarctic glaciation began. Ocean cooling. Greenhouse-to-icehouse transition."}},{"k":"what_died_6","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"tropical_forests_retreated_many_tropic","v":{"s":"Tropical forests retreated. Many tropical mammals went extinct. Hyaenodonts declined."}},{"k":"cooler_open_habitats_led_to_evolution_","v":{"s":"Cooler open habitats led to evolution of grasslands + horse-like mammals + new lineages."}},{"k":"pleistocene_megafauna_12_000_years_ago","v":{"s":"Pleistocene Megafauna (~12,000 years ago)"}},{"k":"50_of_large_mammals_globally","v":{"s":"~50% of large mammals globally"}},{"k":"causes_climate_change_human_hunting_se","v":{"s":"Causes: climate change + human hunting. Selective for largest species."}},{"k":"what_died_7","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"mammoths_mastodons_giant_ground_sloths","v":{"s":"Mammoths, mastodons, giant ground sloths, sabre-tooth cats, dire wolves, North American camels + horses gone."}},{"k":"anthropocene_context_likely_first_exti","v":{"s":"Anthropocene context - likely first extinction substantially driven by humans."}},{"k":"modern_anthropocene","v":{"s":"Modern (Anthropocene)"}},{"k":"estimated_1000x_background_extinction_","v":{"s":"Estimated 1000x background extinction rate"}},{"k":"causes_habitat_loss_climate_change_inv","v":{"s":"Causes: habitat loss, climate change, invasive species, pollution, overhunting. All human-driven."}},{"k":"what_died_8","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"numerous_species_lost_in_past_century_","v":{"s":"Numerous species lost in past century: passenger pigeon, thylacine, Yangtze river dolphin, golden toad. Many more critically endangered."}},{"k":"possibly_earth_s_sixth_mass_extinction","v":{"s":"Possibly Earth's sixth mass extinction. International conservation efforts (CBD, IUCN) attempt to slow rate."}},{"k":"end_capitanian_260_ma_middle_permian","v":{"s":"End-Capitanian (260 Ma, Middle Permian)"}},{"k":"65_of_marine_species","v":{"s":"65% of marine species"}},{"k":"causes_emeishan_traps_volcanism_china_","v":{"s":"Causes: Emeishan Traps volcanism (China). Pre-cursor to greater End-Permian."}},{"k":"what_died_9","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"brachiopod_trilobite_reduction_conodon","v":{"s":"Brachiopod + trilobite reduction. Conodonts severely hit."}},{"k":"often_grouped_with_end_permian_smaller","v":{"s":"Often grouped with End-Permian; smaller separate event."}},{"k":"toarcian_event_183_ma_early_jurassic","v":{"s":"Toarcian Event (183 Ma, Early Jurassic)"}},{"k":"local_marine_extinction","v":{"s":"Local marine extinction"}},{"k":"causes_karoo_ferrar_volcanic_province_","v":{"s":"Causes: Karoo-Ferrar volcanic province. Ocean anoxia + warming."}},{"k":"what_died_10","v":{"s":"What died: "}},{"k":"some_marine_species_lost_less_severe_t","v":{"s":"Some marine species lost. Less severe than mass extinctions."}},{"k":"useful_analog_for_understanding_modern","v":{"s":"Useful analog for understanding modern marine deoxygenation."}},{"k":"geological_time_periods","v":{"s":"Geological Time Periods"}},{"k":"20_eras_epochs_from_hadean_4_5_ga_to_m","v":{"s":"20 eras + epochs from Hadean (4.5 Ga) to modern Anthropocene. Each tells a story of changing climate, plate configuration, and life."}},{"k":"hadean_eon_4567_4000_ma","v":{"s":"Hadean Eon (4567-4000 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"first_eon_earth_forms_from_solar_nebul","v":{"s":"First eon. Earth forms from solar nebula, moon-forming impact, magma ocean cools."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"no_fossils_zircons_from_jack_hills_aus","v":{"s":"No fossils. Zircons from Jack Hills, Australia date 4.4 Ga and suggest water + crust formed early."}},{"k":"key_events","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"moon_forming_impact_4_5_ga_late_heavy_","v":{"s":"Moon-forming impact ~4.5 Ga. Late Heavy Bombardment 4.1-3.8 Ga."}},{"k":"archean_eon_4000_2500_ma","v":{"s":"Archean Eon (4000-2500 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_2","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"second_eon_first_life_oceans_continent","v":{"s":"Second eon. First life. Oceans + continents form."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_2","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"stromatolites_cyanobacterial_mats_from","v":{"s":"Stromatolites (cyanobacterial mats) from 3.5 Ga. Methanogens earliest life."}},{"k":"key_events_2","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"earth_atmosphere_was_reducing_no_o2_fi","v":{"s":"Earth atmosphere was reducing (no O2). First continents (cratons) formed."}},{"k":"proterozoic_eon_2500_541_ma","v":{"s":"Proterozoic Eon (2500-541 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_3","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"third_eon_great_oxygenation_multicellu","v":{"s":"Third eon. Great Oxygenation. Multicellular life."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_3","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"banded_iron_formations_acritarchs_edia","v":{"s":"Banded iron formations. Acritarchs. Ediacaran fauna at end (580-541 Ma)."}},{"k":"key_events_3","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"snowball_earth_glaciations_720_635_ma_","v":{"s":"Snowball Earth glaciations ~720-635 Ma. Boring Billion (1800-800 Ma) - relatively stable era."}},{"k":"cambrian_period_541_485_ma","v":{"s":"Cambrian Period (541-485 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_4","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"start_of_phanerozoic_cambrian_explosio","v":{"s":"Start of Phanerozoic. Cambrian Explosion."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_4","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"first_abundant_animal_fossils_with_har","v":{"s":"First abundant animal fossils with hard parts. Trilobites, brachiopods, sponges. Burgess Shale."}},{"k":"key_events_4","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"cambrian_explosion_most_modern_animal_","v":{"s":"Cambrian Explosion: most modern animal phyla appeared in 20 million years."}},{"k":"ordovician_period_485_444_ma","v":{"s":"Ordovician Period (485-444 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_5","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"marine_biodiversity_flourishes_first_l","v":{"s":"Marine biodiversity flourishes. First land plants."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_5","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"graptolites_diverse_trilobites_brachio","v":{"s":"Graptolites. Diverse trilobites + brachiopods. Earliest fish (jawless). First land plants (liverworts)."}},{"k":"key_events_5","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"ended_with_the_end_ordovician_mass_ext","v":{"s":"Ended with the End-Ordovician mass extinction."}},{"k":"silurian_period_444_419_ma","v":{"s":"Silurian Period (444-419 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_6","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"recovery_diversification","v":{"s":"Recovery + diversification."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_6","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"jawed_fish_appeared_vascular_plants_on","v":{"s":"Jawed fish appeared. Vascular plants on land. Eurypterids dominant."}},{"k":"key_events_6","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"first_coral_reefs_of_modern_type_atmos","v":{"s":"First coral reefs of modern type. Atmospheric O2 close to modern."}},{"k":"devonian_period_419_359_ma","v":{"s":"Devonian Period (419-359 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_7","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"age_of_fishes_first_tetrapods","v":{"s":"Age of Fishes. First tetrapods."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_7","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"placoderms_lobe_finned_fish_tiktaalik_","v":{"s":"Placoderms, lobe-finned fish (Tiktaalik). First insects. First seeds. Coal-forming forests start."}},{"k":"key_events_7","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"tiktaalik_375_ma_was_a_critical_fish_t","v":{"s":"Tiktaalik (375 Ma) was a critical fish-tetrapod transition. Late Devonian mass extinction."}},{"k":"carboniferous_period_359_299_ma","v":{"s":"Carboniferous Period (359-299 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_8","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"coal_forests_giant_arthropods","v":{"s":"Coal forests. Giant arthropods."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_8","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"lepidodendron_sigillaria_lycopsid_tree","v":{"s":"Lepidodendron, Sigillaria (lycopsid trees up to 50m). Meganeura dragonflies with 75 cm wingspan."}},{"k":"key_events_8","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"high_o2_levels_30_allowed_giant_arthro","v":{"s":"High O2 levels (~30%) allowed giant arthropods. Coal deposits worldwide originate here."}},{"k":"permian_period_299_252_ma","v":{"s":"Permian Period (299-252 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_9","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"pangaea_assembled_reptiles_dominant","v":{"s":"Pangaea assembled. Reptiles dominant."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_9","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"dimetrodon_therapsids_proto_mammals_gl","v":{"s":"Dimetrodon. Therapsids (proto-mammals). Glossopteris flora in Gondwana."}},{"k":"key_events_9","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"ended_with_end_permian_mass_extinction","v":{"s":"Ended with End-Permian mass extinction. Pangaea was complete."}},{"k":"triassic_period_252_201_ma","v":{"s":"Triassic Period (252-201 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_10","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"recovery_dinosaur_origins","v":{"s":"Recovery + dinosaur origins."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_10","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"first_dinosaurs_eoraptor_herrerasaurus","v":{"s":"First dinosaurs (Eoraptor, Herrerasaurus). First true mammals. Conifers + ginkgos."}},{"k":"key_events_10","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"pangaea_began_to_break_up_end_triassic","v":{"s":"Pangaea began to break up. End-Triassic extinction cleared niches for dinosaurs."}},{"k":"jurassic_period_201_145_ma","v":{"s":"Jurassic Period (201-145 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_11","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"dinosaur_diversification_first_birds","v":{"s":"Dinosaur diversification. First birds."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_11","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"allosaurus_stegosaurus_apatosaurus_bra","v":{"s":"Allosaurus, Stegosaurus, Apatosaurus, Brachiosaurus. Archaeopteryx. Ammonites diverse."}},{"k":"key_events_11","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"tethys_sea_narrowed_atlantic_ocean_beg","v":{"s":"Tethys Sea narrowed. Atlantic Ocean began to open."}},{"k":"cretaceous_period_145_66_ma","v":{"s":"Cretaceous Period (145-66 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_12","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"dinosaur_peak_flowers_evolve_modern_co","v":{"s":"Dinosaur peak. Flowers evolve. Modern continents take shape."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_12","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"t_rex_triceratops_velociraptor_first_f","v":{"s":"T. rex, Triceratops, Velociraptor. First flowering plants. First modern birds."}},{"k":"key_events_12","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"ended_with_k_pg_mass_extinction_astero","v":{"s":"Ended with K-Pg mass extinction (asteroid impact + Deccan Traps)."}},{"k":"paleocene_epoch_66_56_ma","v":{"s":"Paleocene Epoch (66-56 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_13","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"recovery_after_dinosaurs_mammals_radia","v":{"s":"Recovery after dinosaurs. Mammals radiate."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_13","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"early_primates_condylarths_hyaenodonts","v":{"s":"Early primates, condylarths, hyaenodonts. Tropical forests extended to high latitudes."}},{"k":"key_events_13","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"paleocene_eocene_thermal_maximum_petm_","v":{"s":"Paleocene-Eocene Thermal Maximum (PETM, 56 Ma): rapid global warming. Useful analog for modern climate change."}},{"k":"eocene_epoch_56_34_ma","v":{"s":"Eocene Epoch (56-34 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_14","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"subtropical_forests_global_first_whale","v":{"s":"Subtropical forests global. First whales (Pakicetus to Basilosaurus)."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_14","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"first_horses_eohippus_bats_whales_croc","v":{"s":"First horses (Eohippus), bats, whales. Crocodiles in Arctic."}},{"k":"key_events_14","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"cooling_began_at_end_antarctic_glaciat","v":{"s":"Cooling began at end. Antarctic glaciation started ~34 Ma."}},{"k":"oligocene_epoch_34_23_ma","v":{"s":"Oligocene Epoch (34-23 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_15","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"grasslands_expand_open_ecosystems","v":{"s":"Grasslands expand. Open ecosystems."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_15","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"early_horses_radiate_anthropoid_primat","v":{"s":"Early horses radiate. Anthropoid primates. First baleen whales."}},{"k":"key_events_15","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"cooling_continued_antarctica_became_ic","v":{"s":"Cooling continued. Antarctica became ice-covered. Mid-Ocean Ridge geometry stabilized."}},{"k":"miocene_epoch_23_5_3_ma","v":{"s":"Miocene Epoch (23-5.3 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_16","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"apes_radiate_mediterranean_salinity_cr","v":{"s":"Apes radiate. Mediterranean salinity crisis."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_16","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"mastodons_first_hominins_ardipithecus_","v":{"s":"Mastodons. First hominins (Ardipithecus, Sahelanthropus). Modern grasslands."}},{"k":"key_events_16","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"5_96_5_33_ma_messinian_salinity_crisis","v":{"s":"5.96-5.33 Ma: Messinian Salinity Crisis - Mediterranean dried up, recovered with Atlantic flood."}},{"k":"pliocene_epoch_5_3_2_6_ma","v":{"s":"Pliocene Epoch (5.3-2.6 Ma)"}},{"k":"what_happened_17","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"modern_fauna_takes_shape_human_ancesto","v":{"s":"Modern fauna takes shape. Human ancestors walking upright."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_17","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"australopithecus_lucy_3_2_ma_north_sou","v":{"s":"Australopithecus (Lucy, 3.2 Ma). North + South American Great Faunal Interchange."}},{"k":"key_events_17","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"panama_isthmus_closed_3_ma_atlantic_pa","v":{"s":"Panama isthmus closed ~3 Ma. Atlantic-Pacific cooled differently. Glaciation began Arctic."}},{"k":"pleistocene_epoch_2_6_ma_11_700_years_","v":{"s":"Pleistocene Epoch (2.6 Ma - 11,700 years ago)"}},{"k":"what_happened_18","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"ice_ages_megafauna_human_migration_glo","v":{"s":"Ice Ages. Megafauna. Human migration globally."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_18","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"mammoths_mastodons_dire_wolves_saberto","v":{"s":"Mammoths, mastodons, dire wolves, sabertooths. Homo erectus, Neanderthals, modern humans."}},{"k":"key_events_18","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"20_glacial_interglacial_cycles_last_gl","v":{"s":"20+ glacial-interglacial cycles. Last glacial maximum ~26,000 years ago."}},{"k":"holocene_epoch_11_700_years_ago_presen","v":{"s":"Holocene Epoch (11,700 years ago - present)"}},{"k":"what_happened_19","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"modern_interglacial_agriculture_civili","v":{"s":"Modern interglacial. Agriculture + civilizations."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_19","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"pleistocene_megafauna_extinction_12_00","v":{"s":"Pleistocene megafauna extinction (~12,000 years ago). All modern human cultures."}},{"k":"key_events_19","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"anthropocene_proposed_as_new_epoch_inf","v":{"s":"Anthropocene proposed as new epoch (informal, 1950 base). Modern climate change accelerating."}},{"k":"anthropocene_proposed_1950","v":{"s":"Anthropocene (proposed, 1950+)"}},{"k":"what_happened_20","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"human_dominated_epoch_sixth_mass_extin","v":{"s":"Human-dominated epoch. Sixth mass extinction."}},{"k":"life_of_the_period_20","v":{"s":"Life of the period: "}},{"k":"plutonium_markers_in_sediments_globall","v":{"s":"Plutonium markers in sediments globally. Plastic in oceans. Microplastics universal."}},{"k":"key_events_20","v":{"s":"Key events: "}},{"k":"stratigraphic_golden_spike_still_under","v":{"s":"Stratigraphic golden spike still under formal review. Defined by atomic-bomb radionuclide markers."}},{"k":"national_parks_geology","v":{"s":"National Parks Geology"}},{"k":"20_us_national_parks_with_their_geolog","v":{"s":"20 US national parks with their geological highlights. Visit these to see plate tectonics in action - from mid-ocean basalts (Hawaii) to active subduction zones (Olympic) to ancient stable cratons (Acadia)."}},{"k":"grand_canyon_np_arizona","v":{"s":"Grand Canyon NP (Arizona)"}},{"k":"1_5_miles_deep_277_miles_long_carved_b","v":{"s":"1.5 miles deep, 277 miles long. Carved by Colorado River over 5-6 million years."}},{"k":"layers_span_1_84_billion_years_vishnu_","v":{"s":"Layers span 1.84 billion years (Vishnu Schist basement) to 270 Ma (Kaibab Limestone rim). Major unconformities mark missing time."}},{"k":"key_features","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"major_unconformity_at_tonto_group_base","v":{"s":"Major unconformity at Tonto Group base = 1.2 billion years of missing record (Great Unconformity)."}},{"k":"visit_south_rim_year_round_north_rim_c","v":{"s":"Visit: South Rim year-round; North Rim closed in winter. Backcountry permits required for below-rim travel."}},{"k":"yellowstone_np_wyoming_montana_idaho","v":{"s":"Yellowstone NP (Wyoming/Montana/Idaho)"}},{"k":"first_us_national_park_1872_sits_on_to","v":{"s":"First US national park (1872). Sits on top of a continental hotspot."}},{"k":"three_caldera_eruptions_2_1_1_3_0_6_ma","v":{"s":"Three caldera eruptions (2.1, 1.3, 0.6 Ma) ejected up to 1000 km³ of material each."}},{"k":"key_features_2","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"currently_3000_5000_quakes_year_mostly","v":{"s":"Currently ~3000-5000 quakes/year (mostly tiny). Geyser system unique on Earth. Old Faithful predictable to ~10 min."}},{"k":"visit_geyser_basins_along_firehole_riv","v":{"s":"Visit: Geyser basins along Firehole River. Yellowstone Volcano Observatory monitors for unrest."}},{"k":"crater_lake_np_oregon","v":{"s":"Crater Lake NP (Oregon)"}},{"k":"deepest_lake_in_us_594m_caldera_of_mt_","v":{"s":"Deepest lake in US (594m). Caldera of Mt Mazama, which erupted catastrophically 7700 years ago."}},{"k":"klamath_tribe_oral_history_records_the","v":{"s":"Klamath Tribe oral history records the eruption - confirmed by tephra dating + radiocarbon."}},{"k":"key_features_3","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"lake_formed_4_5_centuries_after_erupti","v":{"s":"Lake formed 4-5 centuries after eruption when caldera filled with snowmelt + rain."}},{"k":"visit_drive_rim_drive_33_miles_for_vie","v":{"s":"Visit: Drive Rim Drive (33 miles) for views; trails descend to lake."}},{"k":"hawaii_volcanoes_np_hawaii_island","v":{"s":"Hawaii Volcanoes NP (Hawaii Island)"}},{"k":"k_lauea_mauna_loa_active_shield_volcan","v":{"s":"Kīlauea + Mauna Loa active shield volcanoes. Kīlauea erupted continuously 1983-2018."}},{"k":"hawaiian_emperor_seamount_chain_record","v":{"s":"Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain records 80 Ma of Pacific Plate motion."}},{"k":"key_features_4","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"volcanoes_added_200_sq_miles_to_island","v":{"s":"Volcanoes added 200 sq miles to island in 1983-2018 phase. Lava enters ocean creating new land."}},{"k":"visit_crater_rim_drive_chain_of_crater","v":{"s":"Visit: Crater Rim Drive, Chain of Craters Road. Volcano House lodge overlooks summit caldera."}},{"k":"glacier_np_montana","v":{"s":"Glacier NP (Montana)"}},{"k":"sperry_grinnell_jackson_glaciers_retre","v":{"s":"Sperry + Grinnell + Jackson glaciers retreating fast. Of 150 in 1850, ~25 remain."}},{"k":"belt_supergroup_sedimentary_rocks_1_5_","v":{"s":"Belt Supergroup sedimentary rocks 1.5 billion years old, oldest exposed in US national parks."}},{"k":"key_features_5","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"lewis_overthrust_placed_older_rocks_at","v":{"s":"Lewis Overthrust placed older rocks atop younger rocks during Cordilleran orogeny."}},{"k":"visit_going_to_the_sun_road_open_july_","v":{"s":"Visit: Going-to-the-Sun Road (open July-October). Many glaciers near vanishing by 2030."}},{"k":"olympic_np_washington","v":{"s":"Olympic NP (Washington)"}},{"k":"rugged_coast_temperate_rainforest_alpi","v":{"s":"Rugged coast + temperate rainforest + alpine glaciers in single park."}},{"k":"active_accretionary_wedge_juan_de_fuca","v":{"s":"Active accretionary wedge: Juan de Fuca subducting under N America has scraped seafloor sediment onto continental edge."}},{"k":"key_features_6","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"tsunami_hazard_1700_cascadia_earthquak","v":{"s":"Tsunami hazard: 1700 Cascadia earthquake produced ghost forests visible at Copalis River + other Olympic Peninsula sites."}},{"k":"visit_hoh_rainforest_hurricane_ridge_b","v":{"s":"Visit: Hoh Rainforest, Hurricane Ridge, beaches. Avoid storm-surge risk areas during winter swells."}},{"k":"acadia_np_maine","v":{"s":"Acadia NP (Maine)"}},{"k":"granite_diabase_coast_carved_by_wiscon","v":{"s":"Granite + diabase coast carved by Wisconsin ice sheet."}},{"k":"cadillac_mountain_1530_ft_is_highest_p","v":{"s":"Cadillac Mountain (1530 ft) is highest point on US Atlantic coast - first place to see sunrise in US for half the year."}},{"k":"key_features_7","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"fjords_drumlins_glacial_erratics_visib","v":{"s":"Fjords + drumlins + glacial erratics visible everywhere."}},{"k":"visit_park_loop_road_sand_beach_thunde","v":{"s":"Visit: Park Loop Road, Sand Beach, Thunder Hole. Cadillac Summit Road by reservation only in summer."}},{"k":"shenandoah_np_virginia","v":{"s":"Shenandoah NP (Virginia)"}},{"k":"blue_ridge_mountains_precambrian_grani","v":{"s":"Blue Ridge Mountains - Precambrian granite + Paleozoic sedimentary cover."}},{"k":"catoctin_formation_lavas_erupted_570_m","v":{"s":"Catoctin Formation lavas erupted ~570 Ma when Rodinia was breaking up."}},{"k":"key_features_8","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"iron_rich_gneiss_colors_old_rag_mounta","v":{"s":"Iron-rich gneiss colors Old Rag Mountain. Hawksbill (4051 ft) is highest peak."}},{"k":"visit_skyline_drive_105_miles_excellen","v":{"s":"Visit: Skyline Drive (105 miles). Excellent fall foliage. Hawksbill summit accessible by short hike."}},{"k":"rocky_mountain_np_colorado","v":{"s":"Rocky Mountain NP (Colorado)"}},{"k":"continental_divide_76_peaks_12_000_ft_","v":{"s":"Continental Divide. 76 peaks >12,000 ft. Granite + gneiss basement."}},{"k":"laramide_orogeny_built_the_rockies_70_","v":{"s":"Laramide orogeny built the Rockies 70-40 Ma."}},{"k":"key_features_9","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"stunning_glacial_cirques_bear_lake_dre","v":{"s":"Stunning glacial cirques (Bear Lake, Dream Lake). Subalpine + alpine tundra ecosystems."}},{"k":"visit_trail_ridge_road_highest_paved_r","v":{"s":"Visit: Trail Ridge Road (highest paved road in US national parks, peak 12,183 ft). Limited window May-October."}},{"k":"big_bend_np_texas","v":{"s":"Big Bend NP (Texas)"}},{"k":"where_texas_mexico_meet_cretaceous_lim","v":{"s":"Where Texas + Mexico meet. Cretaceous limestone + Tertiary volcanic rocks."}},{"k":"t_rex_fossils_cretaceous_marine_reefs_","v":{"s":"T. rex fossils + Cretaceous marine reefs both exposed in same park."}},{"k":"key_features_10","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"chisos_mountains_rise_from_desert_floo","v":{"s":"Chisos Mountains rise from desert floor. Window Trail offers Rio Grande overlook."}},{"k":"visit_santa_elena_canyon_by_canoe_remo","v":{"s":"Visit: Santa Elena Canyon by canoe. Remote - fill gas tank in Marathon or Terlingua."}},{"k":"death_valley_np_california_nevada","v":{"s":"Death Valley NP (California/Nevada)"}},{"k":"lowest_elevation_in_n_america_282_ft_a","v":{"s":"Lowest elevation in N America (-282 ft at Badwater). Hottest place on Earth on record."}},{"k":"basin_range_fault_zone_salt_flats_allu","v":{"s":"Basin + Range fault zone. Salt flats, alluvial fans, badlands, sand dunes."}},{"k":"key_features_11","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"pleistocene_lake_manly_filled_valley_6","v":{"s":"Pleistocene Lake Manly filled valley 600 ft deep 11,000 years ago."}},{"k":"visit_badwater_basin_zabriskie_point_m","v":{"s":"Visit: Badwater Basin, Zabriskie Point, Mesquite Flat Dunes. Summer heat is dangerous - limit activity 10am-6pm."}},{"k":"joshua_tree_np_california","v":{"s":"Joshua Tree NP (California)"}},{"k":"where_mojave_colorado_deserts_meet_gra","v":{"s":"Where Mojave + Colorado deserts meet. Granite + gneiss rock formations."}},{"k":"granite_is_mesozoic_plutonic_rock_sphe","v":{"s":"Granite is Mesozoic plutonic rock; spheroidal weathering creates iconic rounded boulders."}},{"k":"key_features_12","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"joshua_trees_yucca_brevifolia_ranged_e","v":{"s":"Joshua trees (Yucca brevifolia) ranged elsewhere during ice ages; now confined."}},{"k":"visit_skull_rock_hidden_valley_keys_vi","v":{"s":"Visit: Skull Rock, Hidden Valley, Keys View. Climbing destination - 8000+ established routes."}},{"k":"petrified_forest_np_arizona","v":{"s":"Petrified Forest NP (Arizona)"}},{"k":"permian_triassic_chinle_formation_petr","v":{"s":"Permian + Triassic Chinle Formation. Petrified wood of Araucarioxylon trees."}},{"k":"trees_flowed_in_floodwaters_into_oxbow","v":{"s":"Trees flowed in floodwaters into oxbow lakes 225 Ma, buried by ash + silt that permineralized them."}},{"k":"key_features_13","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"painted_desert_section_nearby_colored_","v":{"s":"Painted Desert section nearby; colored badlands from oxidized soils."}},{"k":"visit_28_mile_loop_drive_through_park_","v":{"s":"Visit: 28-mile loop drive through park. Do not take petrified wood - felony with steep fine."}},{"k":"carlsbad_caverns_np_new_mexico","v":{"s":"Carlsbad Caverns NP (New Mexico)"}},{"k":"limestone_cave_system_1583_ft_deep_30_","v":{"s":"Limestone cave system 1583 ft deep, 30+ miles surveyed. Permian reef-aged."}},{"k":"cave_formed_by_sulfuric_acid_weatherin","v":{"s":"Cave formed by sulfuric acid weathering of Permian Capitan Reef limestone (not carbonic acid like most caves)."}},{"k":"key_features_14","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"mexican_free_tail_bat_colony_400_000_e","v":{"s":"Mexican Free-Tail bat colony (~400,000) emerges nightly during summer."}},{"k":"visit_big_room_walk_1_25_mile_or_full_","v":{"s":"Visit: Big Room walk (1.25 mile) or full Natural Entrance route (1.25 mile + elevator out). Lechuguilla Cave nearby is closed to public."}},{"k":"mammoth_cave_np_kentucky","v":{"s":"Mammoth Cave NP (Kentucky)"}},{"k":"world_s_longest_cave_system_420_miles_","v":{"s":"World's longest cave system (>420 miles mapped). Mississippian limestone."}},{"k":"cave_continues_to_be_mapped_every_year","v":{"s":"Cave continues to be mapped - every year adds new passages."}},{"k":"key_features_15","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"bedrock_is_carboniferous_mississippian","v":{"s":"Bedrock is Carboniferous (Mississippian) limestone deposited in shallow tropical sea."}},{"k":"visit_multiple_guided_tours_from_1_hou","v":{"s":"Visit: Multiple guided tours from 1-hour to 4-hour 'Wild Cave' adventure. Reserve in advance."}},{"k":"bryce_canyon_np_utah","v":{"s":"Bryce Canyon NP (Utah)"}},{"k":"hoodoo_amphitheaters_tertiary_claron_f","v":{"s":"Hoodoo amphitheaters. Tertiary Claron Formation limestone."}},{"k":"freeze_thaw_weathering_carves_the_hood","v":{"s":"Freeze-thaw weathering carves the hoodoos."}},{"k":"key_features_16","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"trail_down_the_rim_navajo_trail_queens","v":{"s":"Trail down the rim (Navajo Trail, Queens Garden) gives close-up views."}},{"k":"visit_sunrise_sunset_points_at_dawn_du","v":{"s":"Visit: Sunrise + Sunset Points at dawn/dusk for color contrast."}},{"k":"zion_np_utah","v":{"s":"Zion NP (Utah)"}},{"k":"vertical_navajo_sandstone_cliffs_carve","v":{"s":"Vertical Navajo Sandstone cliffs. Carved by Virgin River."}},{"k":"navajo_sandstone_is_200_ma_jurassic_er","v":{"s":"Navajo Sandstone is 200 Ma Jurassic erg (ancient sand sea). Cross-bedding preserves wind direction."}},{"k":"key_features_17","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"angels_landing_trail_features_1500_ft_","v":{"s":"Angels Landing trail features 1500 ft chain-aided final ascent."}},{"k":"visit_riverside_walk_emerald_pools_the","v":{"s":"Visit: Riverside Walk, Emerald Pools, The Narrows (slot canyon hike - check flash flood forecast)."}},{"k":"arches_np_utah","v":{"s":"Arches NP (Utah)"}},{"k":"2000_sandstone_arches_salt_tectonics_s","v":{"s":"2000+ sandstone arches. Salt tectonics shaped the landscape."}},{"k":"subsurface_paradox_salt_forms_diapirs_","v":{"s":"Subsurface Paradox salt forms diapirs that fractured overlying Entrada Sandstone."}},{"k":"key_features_18","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"delicate_arch_symbol_of_utah_license_p","v":{"s":"Delicate Arch - symbol of Utah license plates."}},{"k":"visit_park_avenue_delicate_arch_trail_","v":{"s":"Visit: Park Avenue, Delicate Arch trail (best near sunset). Timed entry required May-October."}},{"k":"canyonlands_np_utah","v":{"s":"Canyonlands NP (Utah)"}},{"k":"confluence_of_colorado_green_rivers_th","v":{"s":"Confluence of Colorado + Green Rivers. Three districts."}},{"k":"island_in_the_sky_section_gives_panora","v":{"s":"Island in the Sky section gives panoramic overlooks. The Maze + Needles districts more remote."}},{"k":"key_features_19","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"mesa_verde_era_pueblo_ruins_anasazi_he","v":{"s":"Mesa Verde-era pueblo ruins (Anasazi Heritage)."}},{"k":"visit_island_in_the_sky_for_driving_ne","v":{"s":"Visit: Island in the Sky for driving; Needles for hiking; Maze for true wilderness experience."}},{"k":"capitol_reef_np_utah","v":{"s":"Capitol Reef NP (Utah)"}},{"k":"waterpocket_fold_100_mile_long_monocli","v":{"s":"Waterpocket Fold - 100-mile-long monocline. Hidden gem of the Mighty 5."}},{"k":"monocline_formed_during_laramide_oroge","v":{"s":"Monocline formed during Laramide orogeny ~70-40 Ma."}},{"k":"key_features_20","v":{"s":"Key features: "}},{"k":"fruita_orchards_still_produce_cherries","v":{"s":"Fruita orchards still produce cherries, apples, peaches - pick-your-own seasonally."}},{"k":"visit_scenic_drive_capitol_gorge_petro","v":{"s":"Visit: Scenic Drive + Capitol Gorge. Petroglyphs near visitor center."}},{"k":"seafloor_features","v":{"s":"Seafloor Features"}},{"k":"20_major_seafloor_features_including_r","v":{"s":"20 major seafloor features including ridges, trenches, fracture zones, hotspot tracks, and submarine volcanoes. Most of Earth's surface is under water; this is where plate tectonics is most active."}},{"k":"mid_atlantic_ridge_2","v":{"s":"Mid-Atlantic Ridge"}},{"k":"10_000_km_long_divergent_boundary_runn","v":{"s":"10,000 km long divergent boundary running pole-to-pole through Atlantic Ocean."}},{"k":"spreading_at_2_5_cm_yr_iceland_is_the_","v":{"s":"Spreading at ~2.5 cm/yr. Iceland is the only place this ridge emerges above water."}},{"k":"discovered_mapped_by_marie_tharp_bruce","v":{"s":"Discovered + mapped by Marie Tharp + Bruce Heezen (1957). Central rift valley visible."}},{"k":"tour_iceland_s_ingvellir_national_park","v":{"s":"Tour: Iceland's Þingvellir National Park lets you walk between the European + N American plates."}},{"k":"east_pacific_rise_2","v":{"s":"East Pacific Rise"}},{"k":"spreading_at_15_cm_yr_fastest_on_earth","v":{"s":"Spreading at ~15 cm/yr - fastest on Earth. Causes high-volume volcanic activity."}},{"k":"stretches_from_antarctica_to_gulf_of_c","v":{"s":"Stretches from Antarctica to Gulf of California."}},{"k":"easily_mapped_via_paleomagnetic_stripe","v":{"s":"Easily mapped via paleomagnetic stripes. Hydrothermal vents discovered here (Galapagos Rift, 1977)."}},{"k":"black_smokers_tube_worms_first_seen_he","v":{"s":"Black smokers + tube worms first seen here from Alvin submersible 1977."}},{"k":"mariana_trench_2","v":{"s":"Mariana Trench"}},{"k":"deepest_known_point_on_earth_challenge","v":{"s":"Deepest known point on Earth: Challenger Deep, 10,994 m below sea level."}},{"k":"pacific_plate_subducting_under_mariana","v":{"s":"Pacific Plate subducting under Mariana microplate. Trench just east of Mariana Islands."}},{"k":"pressure_at_bottom_1100_atm_yet_life_e","v":{"s":"Pressure at bottom: 1100 atm. Yet, life exists - amphipods, tiny snailfish, microbial mats."}},{"k":"visit_james_cameron_descended_solo_201","v":{"s":"Visit: James Cameron descended solo 2012; ongoing submersible research."}},{"k":"puerto_rico_trench_2","v":{"s":"Puerto Rico Trench"}},{"k":"deepest_part_of_atlantic_ocean_8_376_m","v":{"s":"Deepest part of Atlantic Ocean (8,376 m). Subduction of N Am plate under Caribbean."}},{"k":"tsunami_hazard_for_caribbean_basin_his","v":{"s":"Tsunami hazard for Caribbean basin - historically active."}},{"k":"largest_negative_gravity_anomaly_on_ea","v":{"s":"Largest negative gravity anomaly on Earth (sub-trench mass deficit)."}},{"k":"visit_submersible_expeditions_rare_mos","v":{"s":"Visit: Submersible expeditions rare; mostly remote sensing studies."}},{"k":"hawaiian_emperor_seamount_chain","v":{"s":"Hawaiian-Emperor Seamount Chain"}},{"k":"6000_km_long_volcanic_chain_records_80","v":{"s":"6000 km long volcanic chain. Records 80 million years of Pacific Plate motion."}},{"k":"bend_at_47_ma_where_pacific_plate_moti","v":{"s":"Bend at 47 Ma where Pacific Plate motion direction changed (cause debated)."}},{"k":"mauna_kea_on_hawaii_island_rises_10_00","v":{"s":"Mauna Kea on Hawaii Island rises 10,000 m from seafloor - tallest mountain on Earth by some measures."}},{"k":"visit_hawaii_volcanoes_np_papah_naumok","v":{"s":"Visit: Hawaii Volcanoes NP; Papahānaumokuākea Marine National Monument farther NW."}},{"k":"iceland_faeroes_ridge","v":{"s":"Iceland-Faeroes Ridge"}},{"k":"submarine_extension_of_mid_atlantic_ri","v":{"s":"Submarine extension of Mid-Atlantic Ridge running from Iceland toward Greenland-Iceland boundary."}},{"k":"controls_deep_water_flow_from_norwegia","v":{"s":"Controls deep water flow from Norwegian-Greenland Seas to Atlantic abyssal."}},{"k":"carbon_rich_north_atlantic_deep_water_","v":{"s":"Carbon-rich North Atlantic Deep Water flows over this ridge into the world ocean."}},{"k":"climate_critical_for_thermohaline_circ","v":{"s":"Climate-critical for thermohaline circulation."}},{"k":"walvis_ridge","v":{"s":"Walvis Ridge"}},{"k":"hotspot_track_running_from_africa_to_t","v":{"s":"Hotspot track running from Africa to Tristan da Cunha. ~500 km long."}},{"k":"records_130_ma_of_african_plate_motion","v":{"s":"Records ~130 Ma of African Plate motion over Tristan hotspot."}},{"k":"started_with_etendeka_traps_eruption_i","v":{"s":"Started with Etendeka traps eruption in NW Namibia 132 Ma."}},{"k":"submarine_pre_tristan_portion_shows_pr","v":{