rystal size?"}},{"k":"stratigraphic_column","v":{"s":"Stratigraphic column"}},{"k":"diagram_interpret_a_vertical_stratigra","v":{"s":"Diagram + interpret a vertical stratigraphic column from local outcrop."}},{"k":"use_walther_s_law_facies_vertically_re","v":{"s":"Use Walther's Law: facies vertically reflect facies laterally."}},{"k":"discussion_depositional_environment","v":{"s":"Discussion: depositional environment?"}},{"k":"local_fault_map","v":{"s":"Local fault map"}},{"k":"research_map_local_faults_using_state_","v":{"s":"Research + map local faults using state geological survey data."}},{"k":"most_us_states_have_geological_surveys","v":{"s":"Most US states have geological surveys with fault data."}},{"k":"discussion_is_your_area_at_earthquake_","v":{"s":"Discussion: is your area at earthquake risk?"}},{"k":"mineral_collection","v":{"s":"Mineral collection"}},{"k":"assemble_representative_mineral_collec","v":{"s":"Assemble representative mineral collection of your state."}},{"k":"buy_or_collect_with_permission","v":{"s":"Buy or collect with permission."}},{"k":"discussion_which_minerals_are_mined_lo","v":{"s":"Discussion: which minerals are mined locally + why?"}},{"k":"climate_reconstruction","v":{"s":"Climate reconstruction"}},{"k":"use_tree_rings_or_sediment_cores_to_re","v":{"s":"Use tree rings or sediment cores to reconstruct climate."}},{"k":"palicci_tree_ring_data_or_local_pond_m","v":{"s":"PALICCI tree ring data or local pond mud cores."}},{"k":"discussion_how_does_climate_change_his","v":{"s":"Discussion: how does climate change historically?"}},{"k":"topography_puzzle","v":{"s":"Topography puzzle"}},{"k":"print_contour_lines_fold_to_make_3d_mo","v":{"s":"Print contour lines; fold to make 3D model."}},{"k":"show_how_contour_lines_reveal_terrain","v":{"s":"Show how contour lines reveal terrain."}},{"k":"discussion_where_are_steepest_slopes_f","v":{"s":"Discussion: where are steepest slopes + flattest areas?"}},{"k":"hutton_s_site_visit","v":{"s":"Hutton's site visit"}},{"k":"find_a_local_unconformity_explore_deep","v":{"s":"Find a local unconformity + explore deep time concept."}},{"k":"roadcut_railroad_cut_creek_bank_often_","v":{"s":"Roadcut + railroad cut + creek bank often expose unconformities."}},{"k":"discussion_1_mm_of_unconformity_how_ma","v":{"s":"Discussion: 1 mm of unconformity = how many million years missing?"}},{"k":"plate_motion_in_dollar_bills","v":{"s":"Plate motion in dollar bills"}},{"k":"use_1_bills_as_plates_push_pull_slide","v":{"s":"Use $1 bills as plates + push, pull, slide."}},{"k":"demonstrate_convergent_divergent_trans","v":{"s":"Demonstrate convergent, divergent, transform."}},{"k":"discussion_what_is_wrong_with_this_mod","v":{"s":"Discussion: what is wrong with this model? Real boundaries are wider + more complex."}},{"k":"tectonic_essay","v":{"s":"Tectonic essay"}},{"k":"choose_a_current_earthquake_or_volcani","v":{"s":"Choose a current earthquake or volcanic event + write 1000-word analysis."}},{"k":"use_usgs_smithsonian_global_volcanism_","v":{"s":"Use USGS + Smithsonian Global Volcanism Program data."}},{"k":"discussion_causes_consequences_public_","v":{"s":"Discussion: causes, consequences, public response."}},{"k":"major_geological_events_recap","v":{"s":"Major Geological Events Recap"}},{"k":"20_of_the_most_important_geological_ev","v":{"s":"20 of the most important geological events in human + Earth history. From the Great Oxygenation Event 2.4 billion years ago to the 2023 Turkey-Syria doublet earthquake."}},{"k":"toba_eruption_74_000_yr_ago","v":{"s":"Toba eruption (74,000 yr ago)"}},{"k":"indonesian_super_eruption","v":{"s":"Indonesian super-eruption."}},{"k":"vei_8_may_have_caused_genetic_bottlene","v":{"s":"VEI 8. May have caused genetic bottleneck in human ancestors."}},{"k":"largest_known_eruption_in_last_100_000","v":{"s":"Largest known eruption in last 100,000 years."}},{"k":"younger_dryas_12_900_years_ago","v":{"s":"Younger Dryas (12,900 years ago)"}},{"k":"sudden_return_to_glacial_conditions_du_2","v":{"s":"Sudden return to glacial conditions during deglaciation."}},{"k":"possible_impact_trigger_or_n_atlantic_","v":{"s":"Possible impact trigger; or N Atlantic freshwater pulse."}},{"k":"lasted_1_300_years","v":{"s":"Lasted ~1,300 years."}},{"k":"snowball_earth_720_635_ma_2","v":{"s":"Snowball Earth (720-635 Ma)"}},{"k":"equatorial_glaciation_evidence_found","v":{"s":"Equatorial glaciation evidence found."}},{"k":"cryogenian_period_multiple_events","v":{"s":"Cryogenian period. Multiple events."}},{"k":"recovery_via_volcanic_co2","v":{"s":"Recovery via volcanic CO2."}},{"k":"goe_great_oxygenation_event_2_4_ga","v":{"s":"GOE (Great Oxygenation Event, 2.4 Ga)"}},{"k":"cyanobacteria_produce_o2_atmosphere_tr","v":{"s":"Cyanobacteria produce O2; atmosphere transforms."}},{"k":"massive_iron_oxide_deposits_bifs_resul","v":{"s":"Massive iron oxide deposits (BIFs) result."}},{"k":"set_stage_for_eukaryotic_multicellular","v":{"s":"Set stage for eukaryotic + multicellular life."}},{"k":"cambrian_explosion_541_ma","v":{"s":"Cambrian Explosion (541 Ma)"}},{"k":"rapid_diversification_of_animal_life","v":{"s":"Rapid diversification of animal life."}},{"k":"most_modern_phyla_appear_in_20_25_my","v":{"s":"Most modern phyla appear in 20-25 My."}},{"k":"mystery_remains_why_this_happened_then","v":{"s":"Mystery remains why this happened then."}},{"k":"tonga_hunga_eruption_jan_2022","v":{"s":"Tonga Hunga eruption (Jan 2022)"}},{"k":"largest_atmospheric_eruption_recorded_","v":{"s":"Largest atmospheric eruption recorded by modern instruments."}},{"k":"plume_reached_58_km","v":{"s":"Plume reached 58 km."}},{"k":"pressure_waves_circled_earth_multiple_","v":{"s":"Pressure waves circled Earth multiple times."}},{"k":"2004_sumatra_andaman_quake","v":{"s":"2004 Sumatra-Andaman quake"}},{"k":"m9_1_tsunami","v":{"s":"M9.1 + tsunami."}},{"k":"230_000_deaths_across_14_countries","v":{"s":"230,000 deaths across 14 countries."}},{"k":"spurred_indian_ocean_tsunami_warning_s","v":{"s":"Spurred Indian Ocean tsunami warning system."}},{"k":"2011_tohoku_quake_tsunami","v":{"s":"2011 Tohoku quake + tsunami"}},{"k":"m9_1_tsunami_fukushima_nuclear_disaste","v":{"s":"M9.1 + tsunami + Fukushima nuclear disaster."}},{"k":"19_759_deaths_16_9_trillion_damage","v":{"s":"19,759 deaths; ¥16.9 trillion damage."}},{"k":"reshaped_japanese_energy_nuclear_polic","v":{"s":"Reshaped Japanese energy + nuclear policy."}},{"k":"2010_haiti_earthquake","v":{"s":"2010 Haiti earthquake"}},{"k":"m7_0_230_000_deaths","v":{"s":"M7.0 + 230,000 deaths."}},{"k":"strike_slip_on_enriquillo_plantain_gar","v":{"s":"Strike-slip on Enriquillo-Plantain Garden Fault."}},{"k":"relief_failure_case_study","v":{"s":"Relief failure case study."}},{"k":"2023_turkey_syria_doublet","v":{"s":"2023 Turkey-Syria doublet"}},{"k":"m7_8_m7_5_within_9_hours","v":{"s":"M7.8 + M7.5 within 9 hours."}},{"k":"59_259_deaths_14m_displaced","v":{"s":"59,259 deaths; 14M displaced."}},{"k":"east_anatolian_fault_rupture_sequence","v":{"s":"East Anatolian Fault rupture sequence."}},{"k":"1815_tambora","v":{"s":"1815 Tambora"}},{"k":"year_without_a_summer_1816","v":{"s":"Year Without a Summer 1816."}},{"k":"crop_failures_mary_shelley_frankenstei","v":{"s":"Crop failures + Mary Shelley + Frankenstein."}},{"k":"largest_known_eruption_since_1500_ad","v":{"s":"Largest known eruption since 1500 AD."}},{"k":"1883_krakatoa","v":{"s":"1883 Krakatoa"}},{"k":"loudest_sound_in_history","v":{"s":"Loudest sound in history."}},{"k":"36_000_deaths_from_tsunami","v":{"s":"36,000 deaths from tsunami."}},{"k":"vivid_global_sunsets_for_2_years","v":{"s":"Vivid global sunsets for 2 years."}},{"k":"1980_mt_st_helens","v":{"s":"1980 Mt St Helens"}},{"k":"lateral_blast_eruption","v":{"s":"Lateral blast eruption."}},{"k":"57_deaths_600_km_of_forest_flattened","v":{"s":"57 deaths; 600 km² of forest flattened."}},{"k":"reshaped_us_volcano_monitoring","v":{"s":"Reshaped US volcano monitoring."}},{"k":"1991_pinatubo","v":{"s":"1991 Pinatubo"}},{"k":"successful_prediction_evacuation","v":{"s":"Successful prediction + evacuation."}},{"k":"200_000_saved_global_cooling_for_2_yea","v":{"s":"200,000 saved; global cooling for 2 years."}},{"k":"volcanology_s_greatest_triumph","v":{"s":"Volcanology's greatest triumph."}},{"k":"1700_cascadia_m9_2","v":{"s":"1700 Cascadia M9"}},{"k":"pacific_nw_megaquake","v":{"s":"Pacific NW megaquake."}},{"k":"recorded_by_japanese_tsunami_records_g","v":{"s":"Recorded by Japanese tsunami records + ghost forests + oral history."}},{"k":"confirmed_1980s_by_brian_atwater","v":{"s":"Confirmed 1980s by Brian Atwater."}},{"k":"1755_lisbon","v":{"s":"1755 Lisbon"}},{"k":"m8_7_tsunami_fire","v":{"s":"M8.7 + tsunami + fire."}},{"k":"30_000_60_000_deaths","v":{"s":"30,000-60,000 deaths."}},{"k":"enlightenment_philosophical_reflection","v":{"s":"Enlightenment philosophical reflections (Voltaire)."}},{"k":"1906_san_francisco","v":{"s":"1906 San Francisco"}},{"k":"m7_9_3_day_firestorm","v":{"s":"M7.9 + 3-day firestorm."}},{"k":"3_000_deaths_80_of_sf_destroyed","v":{"s":"3,000 deaths; 80% of SF destroyed."}},{"k":"reid_s_elastic_rebound_theory","v":{"s":"Reid's elastic rebound theory."}},{"k":"1923_great_kanto","v":{"s":"1923 Great Kanto"}},{"k":"m7_9_firestorm","v":{"s":"M7.9 + firestorm."}},{"k":"142_000_deaths_most_from_fire","v":{"s":"142,000 deaths (most from fire)."}},{"k":"drove_japanese_seismic_engineering_rev","v":{"s":"Drove Japanese seismic engineering revolution."}},{"k":"1960_chile_m9_5","v":{"s":"1960 Chile M9.5"}},{"k":"largest_quake_ever_recorded","v":{"s":"Largest quake ever recorded."}},{"k":"5_700_deaths_chile_global_tsunami","v":{"s":"5,700 deaths Chile + global tsunami."}},{"k":"sparked_pacific_tsunami_warning_system","v":{"s":"Sparked Pacific Tsunami Warning System."}},{"k":"1964_alaska_m9_2","v":{"s":"1964 Alaska M9.2"}},{"k":"good_friday_earthquake","v":{"s":"Good Friday earthquake."}},{"k":"139_deaths_coast_uplifted_11m","v":{"s":"139 deaths; coast uplifted 11m."}},{"k":"plafker_confirmed_subduction_mechanism","v":{"s":"Plafker confirmed subduction mechanism."}},{"k":"quick_review_questions","v":{"s":"Quick-Review Questions"}},{"k":"60_quick_review_questions_covering_key","v":{"s":"60 quick review questions covering key plate tectonic concepts. Use as flash cards: read the question, try to answer in your head, then check below."}},{"k":"review_question_1","v":{"s":"Review question 1"}},{"k":"concept_plate_boundaries","v":{"s":"Concept: plate boundaries"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_divergent_boundary","v":{"s":"Question: What is a divergent boundary?"}},{"k":"answer_a_divergent_plate_boundary_is_w","v":{"s":"Answer: A divergent plate boundary is where two plates are moving away from each other, often forming a mid-ocean ridge or continental rift. Examples include the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the East African Rift."}},{"k":"review_question_2","v":{"s":"Review question 2"}},{"k":"concept_earthquake_magnitude","v":{"s":"Concept: earthquake magnitude"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_richter_scale","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Richter scale?"}},{"k":"answer_the_richter_scale_was_the_origi","v":{"s":"Answer: The Richter scale was the original 1935 magnitude scale developed by Charles Richter. It is logarithmic, so M5 is 10x larger than M4. It works well for small to medium quakes but saturates above M7. Modern seismology uses moment magnitude (Mw) for large events."}},{"k":"review_question_3","v":{"s":"Review question 3"}},{"k":"concept_volcanic_types","v":{"s":"Concept: volcanic types"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_stratovolcano","v":{"s":"Question: What is a stratovolcano?"}},{"k":"answer_a_stratovolcano_is_a_steep_side","v":{"s":"Answer: A stratovolcano is a steep-sided conical volcano built from alternating layers of lava and ash. Examples include Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens, and Vesuvius. They tend to produce explosive eruptions."}},{"k":"review_question_4","v":{"s":"Review question 4"}},{"k":"concept_rock_cycle","v":{"s":"Concept: rock cycle"}},{"k":"question_what_is_metamorphic_rock_form","v":{"s":"Question: What is metamorphic rock formation?"}},{"k":"answer_metamorphic_rocks_form_when_exi","v":{"s":"Answer: Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are transformed by heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids. Examples include marble (from limestone), slate (from shale), and gneiss (from granite)."}},{"k":"review_question_5","v":{"s":"Review question 5"}},{"k":"concept_geological_time","v":{"s":"Concept: geological time"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_cenozoic_era","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Cenozoic Era?"}},{"k":"answer_the_cenozoic_era_began_66_milli","v":{"s":"Answer: The Cenozoic Era began 66 million years ago and continues to present. It is divided into Paleogene, Neogene, and Quaternary periods. Mammals diversified during this era."}},{"k":"review_question_6","v":{"s":"Review question 6"}},{"k":"concept_fossil_correlation","v":{"s":"Concept: fossil correlation"}},{"k":"question_what_is_an_index_fossil","v":{"s":"Question: What is an index fossil?"}},{"k":"answer_an_index_fossil_is_a_fossil_spe","v":{"s":"Answer: An index fossil is a fossil species that lived during a specific, limited time period and was geographically widespread. Examples include trilobites, ammonites, and graptolites. They help correlate rocks of the same age."}},{"k":"review_question_7","v":{"s":"Review question 7"}},{"k":"concept_subduction_zones","v":{"s":"Concept: subduction zones"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_wadati_benioff_zone","v":{"s":"Question: What is a Wadati-Benioff zone?"}},{"k":"answer_a_wadati_benioff_zone_is_the_in","v":{"s":"Answer: A Wadati-Benioff zone is the inclined plane of earthquakes that marks a subducting slab. Earthquakes occur as the slab bends, cracks, and undergoes phase changes. Some Wadati-Benioff zones extend to 700 km depth."}},{"k":"review_question_8","v":{"s":"Review question 8"}},{"k":"concept_mid_ocean_ridges","v":{"s":"Concept: mid-ocean ridges"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_east_pacific_rise","v":{"s":"Question: What is the East Pacific Rise?"}},{"k":"answer_the_east_pacific_rise_is_the_fa","v":{"s":"Answer: The East Pacific Rise is the fastest-spreading mid-ocean ridge on Earth, with rates up to 16 cm/yr. It is the divergent boundary between the Pacific and Nazca plates."}},{"k":"review_question_9","v":{"s":"Review question 9"}},{"k":"concept_hotspots","v":{"s":"Concept: hotspots"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_hawaiian_emperor_","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Hawaiian-Emperor chain?"}},{"k":"answer_the_hawaiian_emperor_seamount_c","v":{"s":"Answer: The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain is a 6000 km volcanic chain that records 80 million years of Pacific Plate motion over the Hawaiian hotspot. The bend at 47 Ma marks a change in Pacific Plate motion direction."}},{"k":"review_question_10","v":{"s":"Review question 10"}},{"k":"concept_transform_faults","v":{"s":"Concept: transform faults"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_san_andreas_fault","v":{"s":"Question: What is the San Andreas Fault?"}},{"k":"answer_the_san_andreas_fault_is_a_tran","v":{"s":"Answer: The San Andreas Fault is a transform plate boundary between the Pacific and North American plates. It is right-lateral strike-slip. The 1906 San Francisco quake (M7.9) was on this fault."}},{"k":"review_question_11","v":{"s":"Review question 11"}},{"k":"concept_plate_boundaries_2","v":{"s":"Concept: plate boundaries"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_divergent_boundary_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is a divergent boundary?"}},{"k":"answer_a_divergent_plate_boundary_is_w_2","v":{"s":"Answer: A divergent plate boundary is where two plates are moving away from each other, often forming a mid-ocean ridge or continental rift. Examples include the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the East African Rift."}},{"k":"review_question_12","v":{"s":"Review question 12"}},{"k":"concept_earthquake_magnitude_2","v":{"s":"Concept: earthquake magnitude"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_richter_scale_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Richter scale?"}},{"k":"answer_the_richter_scale_was_the_origi_2","v":{"s":"Answer: The Richter scale was the original 1935 magnitude scale developed by Charles Richter. It is logarithmic, so M5 is 10x larger than M4. It works well for small to medium quakes but saturates above M7. Modern seismology uses moment magnitude (Mw) for large events."}},{"k":"review_question_13","v":{"s":"Review question 13"}},{"k":"concept_volcanic_types_2","v":{"s":"Concept: volcanic types"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_stratovolcano_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is a stratovolcano?"}},{"k":"answer_a_stratovolcano_is_a_steep_side_2","v":{"s":"Answer: A stratovolcano is a steep-sided conical volcano built from alternating layers of lava and ash. Examples include Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens, and Vesuvius. They tend to produce explosive eruptions."}},{"k":"review_question_14","v":{"s":"Review question 14"}},{"k":"concept_rock_cycle_2","v":{"s":"Concept: rock cycle"}},{"k":"question_what_is_metamorphic_rock_form_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is metamorphic rock formation?"}},{"k":"answer_metamorphic_rocks_form_when_exi_2","v":{"s":"Answer: Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are transformed by heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids. Examples include marble (from limestone), slate (from shale), and gneiss (from granite)."}},{"k":"review_question_15","v":{"s":"Review question 15"}},{"k":"concept_geological_time_2","v":{"s":"Concept: geological time"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_cenozoic_era_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Cenozoic Era?"}},{"k":"answer_the_cenozoic_era_began_66_milli_2","v":{"s":"Answer: The Cenozoic Era began 66 million years ago and continues to present. It is divided into Paleogene, Neogene, and Quaternary periods. Mammals diversified during this era."}},{"k":"review_question_16","v":{"s":"Review question 16"}},{"k":"concept_fossil_correlation_2","v":{"s":"Concept: fossil correlation"}},{"k":"question_what_is_an_index_fossil_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is an index fossil?"}},{"k":"answer_an_index_fossil_is_a_fossil_spe_2","v":{"s":"Answer: An index fossil is a fossil species that lived during a specific, limited time period and was geographically widespread. Examples include trilobites, ammonites, and graptolites. They help correlate rocks of the same age."}},{"k":"review_question_17","v":{"s":"Review question 17"}},{"k":"concept_subduction_zones_2","v":{"s":"Concept: subduction zones"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_wadati_benioff_zone_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is a Wadati-Benioff zone?"}},{"k":"answer_a_wadati_benioff_zone_is_the_in_2","v":{"s":"Answer: A Wadati-Benioff zone is the inclined plane of earthquakes that marks a subducting slab. Earthquakes occur as the slab bends, cracks, and undergoes phase changes. Some Wadati-Benioff zones extend to 700 km depth."}},{"k":"review_question_18","v":{"s":"Review question 18"}},{"k":"concept_mid_ocean_ridges_2","v":{"s":"Concept: mid-ocean ridges"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_east_pacific_rise_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is the East Pacific Rise?"}},{"k":"answer_the_east_pacific_rise_is_the_fa_2","v":{"s":"Answer: The East Pacific Rise is the fastest-spreading mid-ocean ridge on Earth, with rates up to 16 cm/yr. It is the divergent boundary between the Pacific and Nazca plates."}},{"k":"review_question_19","v":{"s":"Review question 19"}},{"k":"concept_hotspots_2","v":{"s":"Concept: hotspots"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_hawaiian_emperor__2","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Hawaiian-Emperor chain?"}},{"k":"answer_the_hawaiian_emperor_seamount_c_2","v":{"s":"Answer: The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain is a 6000 km volcanic chain that records 80 million years of Pacific Plate motion over the Hawaiian hotspot. The bend at 47 Ma marks a change in Pacific Plate motion direction."}},{"k":"review_question_20","v":{"s":"Review question 20"}},{"k":"concept_transform_faults_2","v":{"s":"Concept: transform faults"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_san_andreas_fault_2","v":{"s":"Question: What is the San Andreas Fault?"}},{"k":"answer_the_san_andreas_fault_is_a_tran_2","v":{"s":"Answer: The San Andreas Fault is a transform plate boundary between the Pacific and North American plates. It is right-lateral strike-slip. The 1906 San Francisco quake (M7.9) was on this fault."}},{"k":"review_question_21","v":{"s":"Review question 21"}},{"k":"concept_plate_boundaries_3","v":{"s":"Concept: plate boundaries"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_divergent_boundary_3","v":{"s":"Question: What is a divergent boundary?"}},{"k":"answer_a_divergent_plate_boundary_is_w_3","v":{"s":"Answer: A divergent plate boundary is where two plates are moving away from each other, often forming a mid-ocean ridge or continental rift. Examples include the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the East African Rift."}},{"k":"review_question_22","v":{"s":"Review question 22"}},{"k":"concept_earthquake_magnitude_3","v":{"s":"Concept: earthquake magnitude"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_richter_scale_3","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Richter scale?"}},{"k":"answer_the_richter_scale_was_the_origi_3","v":{"s":"Answer: The Richter scale was the original 1935 magnitude scale developed by Charles Richter. It is logarithmic, so M5 is 10x larger than M4. It works well for small to medium quakes but saturates above M7. Modern seismology uses moment magnitude (Mw) for large events."}},{"k":"review_question_23","v":{"s":"Review question 23"}},{"k":"concept_volcanic_types_3","v":{"s":"Concept: volcanic types"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_stratovolcano_3","v":{"s":"Question: What is a stratovolcano?"}},{"k":"answer_a_stratovolcano_is_a_steep_side_3","v":{"s":"Answer: A stratovolcano is a steep-sided conical volcano built from alternating layers of lava and ash. Examples include Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens, and Vesuvius. They tend to produce explosive eruptions."}},{"k":"review_question_24","v":{"s":"Review question 24"}},{"k":"concept_rock_cycle_3","v":{"s":"Concept: rock cycle"}},{"k":"question_what_is_metamorphic_rock_form_3","v":{"s