 Rise?"}},{"k":"answer_the_east_pacific_rise_is_the_fa_5","v":{"s":"Answer: The East Pacific Rise is the fastest-spreading mid-ocean ridge on Earth, with rates up to 16 cm/yr. It is the divergent boundary between the Pacific and Nazca plates."}},{"k":"review_question_49","v":{"s":"Review question 49"}},{"k":"concept_hotspots_5","v":{"s":"Concept: hotspots"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_hawaiian_emperor__5","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Hawaiian-Emperor chain?"}},{"k":"answer_the_hawaiian_emperor_seamount_c_5","v":{"s":"Answer: The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain is a 6000 km volcanic chain that records 80 million years of Pacific Plate motion over the Hawaiian hotspot. The bend at 47 Ma marks a change in Pacific Plate motion direction."}},{"k":"review_question_50","v":{"s":"Review question 50"}},{"k":"concept_transform_faults_5","v":{"s":"Concept: transform faults"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_san_andreas_fault_5","v":{"s":"Question: What is the San Andreas Fault?"}},{"k":"answer_the_san_andreas_fault_is_a_tran_5","v":{"s":"Answer: The San Andreas Fault is a transform plate boundary between the Pacific and North American plates. It is right-lateral strike-slip. The 1906 San Francisco quake (M7.9) was on this fault."}},{"k":"review_question_51","v":{"s":"Review question 51"}},{"k":"concept_plate_boundaries_6","v":{"s":"Concept: plate boundaries"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_divergent_boundary_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is a divergent boundary?"}},{"k":"answer_a_divergent_plate_boundary_is_w_6","v":{"s":"Answer: A divergent plate boundary is where two plates are moving away from each other, often forming a mid-ocean ridge or continental rift. Examples include the Mid-Atlantic Ridge and the East African Rift."}},{"k":"review_question_52","v":{"s":"Review question 52"}},{"k":"concept_earthquake_magnitude_6","v":{"s":"Concept: earthquake magnitude"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_richter_scale_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Richter scale?"}},{"k":"answer_the_richter_scale_was_the_origi_6","v":{"s":"Answer: The Richter scale was the original 1935 magnitude scale developed by Charles Richter. It is logarithmic, so M5 is 10x larger than M4. It works well for small to medium quakes but saturates above M7. Modern seismology uses moment magnitude (Mw) for large events."}},{"k":"review_question_53","v":{"s":"Review question 53"}},{"k":"concept_volcanic_types_6","v":{"s":"Concept: volcanic types"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_stratovolcano_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is a stratovolcano?"}},{"k":"answer_a_stratovolcano_is_a_steep_side_6","v":{"s":"Answer: A stratovolcano is a steep-sided conical volcano built from alternating layers of lava and ash. Examples include Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens, and Vesuvius. They tend to produce explosive eruptions."}},{"k":"review_question_54","v":{"s":"Review question 54"}},{"k":"concept_rock_cycle_6","v":{"s":"Concept: rock cycle"}},{"k":"question_what_is_metamorphic_rock_form_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is metamorphic rock formation?"}},{"k":"answer_metamorphic_rocks_form_when_exi_6","v":{"s":"Answer: Metamorphic rocks form when existing rocks are transformed by heat, pressure, or chemically active fluids. Examples include marble (from limestone), slate (from shale), and gneiss (from granite)."}},{"k":"review_question_55","v":{"s":"Review question 55"}},{"k":"concept_geological_time_6","v":{"s":"Concept: geological time"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_cenozoic_era_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Cenozoic Era?"}},{"k":"answer_the_cenozoic_era_began_66_milli_6","v":{"s":"Answer: The Cenozoic Era began 66 million years ago and continues to present. It is divided into Paleogene, Neogene, and Quaternary periods. Mammals diversified during this era."}},{"k":"review_question_56","v":{"s":"Review question 56"}},{"k":"concept_fossil_correlation_6","v":{"s":"Concept: fossil correlation"}},{"k":"question_what_is_an_index_fossil_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is an index fossil?"}},{"k":"answer_an_index_fossil_is_a_fossil_spe_6","v":{"s":"Answer: An index fossil is a fossil species that lived during a specific, limited time period and was geographically widespread. Examples include trilobites, ammonites, and graptolites. They help correlate rocks of the same age."}},{"k":"review_question_57","v":{"s":"Review question 57"}},{"k":"concept_subduction_zones_6","v":{"s":"Concept: subduction zones"}},{"k":"question_what_is_a_wadati_benioff_zone_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is a Wadati-Benioff zone?"}},{"k":"answer_a_wadati_benioff_zone_is_the_in_6","v":{"s":"Answer: A Wadati-Benioff zone is the inclined plane of earthquakes that marks a subducting slab. Earthquakes occur as the slab bends, cracks, and undergoes phase changes. Some Wadati-Benioff zones extend to 700 km depth."}},{"k":"review_question_58","v":{"s":"Review question 58"}},{"k":"concept_mid_ocean_ridges_6","v":{"s":"Concept: mid-ocean ridges"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_east_pacific_rise_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is the East Pacific Rise?"}},{"k":"answer_the_east_pacific_rise_is_the_fa_6","v":{"s":"Answer: The East Pacific Rise is the fastest-spreading mid-ocean ridge on Earth, with rates up to 16 cm/yr. It is the divergent boundary between the Pacific and Nazca plates."}},{"k":"review_question_59","v":{"s":"Review question 59"}},{"k":"concept_hotspots_6","v":{"s":"Concept: hotspots"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_hawaiian_emperor__6","v":{"s":"Question: What is the Hawaiian-Emperor chain?"}},{"k":"answer_the_hawaiian_emperor_seamount_c_6","v":{"s":"Answer: The Hawaiian-Emperor seamount chain is a 6000 km volcanic chain that records 80 million years of Pacific Plate motion over the Hawaiian hotspot. The bend at 47 Ma marks a change in Pacific Plate motion direction."}},{"k":"review_question_60","v":{"s":"Review question 60"}},{"k":"concept_transform_faults_6","v":{"s":"Concept: transform faults"}},{"k":"question_what_is_the_san_andreas_fault_6","v":{"s":"Question: What is the San Andreas Fault?"}},{"k":"answer_the_san_andreas_fault_is_a_tran_6","v":{"s":"Answer: The San Andreas Fault is a transform plate boundary between the Pacific and North American plates. It is right-lateral strike-slip. The 1906 San Francisco quake (M7.9) was on this fault."}},{"k":"core_geological_concepts","v":{"s":"Core Geological Concepts"}},{"k":"40_foundational_geology_concepts_expla","v":{"s":"40 foundational geology concepts explained. Use as a reference + study guide. Each concept has definition, detail, significance, and source of knowledge."}},{"k":"plate_tectonics_2","v":{"s":"Plate tectonics"}},{"k":"the_theory_that_earth_s_outer_shell_is","v":{"s":"The theory that Earth's outer shell is divided into rigid plates that move over the underlying mantle."}},{"k":"three_boundary_types_divergent_apart_c","v":{"s":"Three boundary types: divergent (apart), convergent (together), transform (sliding past)."}},{"k":"unified_geology_in_the_1960s_explains_","v":{"s":"Unified geology in the 1960s; explains earthquakes, volcanoes, mountains, ocean basins."}},{"k":"confirmed_by_paleomagnetism_seafloor_a","v":{"s":"Confirmed by paleomagnetism, seafloor age + magnetic stripes, GPS measurements."}},{"k":"continental_drift_2","v":{"s":"Continental drift"}},{"k":"wegener_s_1912_hypothesis_that_contine","v":{"s":"Wegener's 1912 hypothesis that continents move over Earth's surface."}},{"k":"wegener_identified_continental_fit_fos","v":{"s":"Wegener identified continental fit + fossil + paleoclimate evidence."}},{"k":"initially_rejected_because_no_plausibl","v":{"s":"Initially rejected because no plausible mechanism. Confirmed when plate tectonics provided the mechanism (~1960s)."}},{"k":"wegener_died_in_1930_without_vindicati","v":{"s":"Wegener died in 1930 without vindication; he was right but ahead of his time."}},{"k":"seafloor_spreading","v":{"s":"Seafloor spreading"}},{"k":"new_oceanic_crust_forms_at_mid_ocean_r","v":{"s":"New oceanic crust forms at mid-ocean ridges and moves away from them."}},{"k":"harry_hess_proposed_1962_vine_matthews","v":{"s":"Harry Hess proposed (1962); Vine + Matthews provided magnetic-stripe evidence (1963)."}},{"k":"crust_gets_older_as_you_move_away_from","v":{"s":"Crust gets older as you move away from a ridge. Oldest oceanic crust ~200 Ma."}},{"k":"drives_motion_of_plates_carrying_ocean","v":{"s":"Drives motion of plates carrying oceanic lithosphere."}},{"k":"subduction","v":{"s":"Subduction"}},{"k":"process_by_which_one_tectonic_plate_di","v":{"s":"Process by which one tectonic plate dives beneath another into the mantle."}},{"k":"occurs_at_oceanic_oceanic_oceanic_cont","v":{"s":"Occurs at oceanic-oceanic + oceanic-continental boundaries. Produces trenches, volcanic arcs, and deep earthquakes."}},{"k":"recycles_oceanic_crust_back_into_mantl","v":{"s":"Recycles oceanic crust back into mantle. Triggers explosive volcanism via mantle wedge melting."}},{"k":"produces_earth_s_largest_earthquakes_m","v":{"s":"Produces Earth's largest earthquakes (M9+)."}},{"k":"mantle_convection","v":{"s":"Mantle convection"}},{"k":"hot_mantle_material_rises_while_cool_m","v":{"s":"Hot mantle material rises while cool material sinks; drives plate motion."}},{"k":"arthur_holmes_proposed_1929_now_well_e","v":{"s":"Arthur Holmes proposed (1929); now well-established."}},{"k":"plumes_rise_as_hot_dense_slabs_sink_mu","v":{"s":"Plumes rise as hot, dense slabs sink. Multiple convection cells in the mantle."}},{"k":"computer_modeling_reveals_complex_flow","v":{"s":"Computer modeling reveals complex flow patterns."}},{"k":"hotspots","v":{"s":"Hotspots"}},{"k":"localized_regions_of_mantle_upwelling_","v":{"s":"Localized regions of mantle upwelling that produce volcanism."}},{"k":"j_tuzo_wilson_proposed_concept_1963_ja","v":{"s":"J. Tuzo Wilson proposed concept (1963); Jason Morgan formalized (1971)."}},{"k":"roughly_stationary_plate_moves_over_th","v":{"s":"Roughly stationary; plate moves over them creating volcanic chains."}},{"k":"examples_hawaiian_emperor_yellowstone_","v":{"s":"Examples: Hawaiian-Emperor, Yellowstone, Iceland."}},{"k":"wilson_cycle","v":{"s":"Wilson Cycle"}},{"k":"theory_that_ocean_basins_open_close_in","v":{"s":"Theory that ocean basins open + close in cycles of ~500 million years."}},{"k":"named_for_j_tuzo_wilson_pangaea_pangae","v":{"s":"Named for J. Tuzo Wilson. Pangaea-Pangaea cycle."}},{"k":"rifting_spreading_subduction_begins_cl","v":{"s":"Rifting → Spreading → Subduction begins → Closure → Collision → New rifting."}},{"k":"tracks_earth_s_continents_over_hundred","v":{"s":"Tracks Earth's continents over hundreds of millions of years."}},{"k":"elastic_rebound_theory","v":{"s":"Elastic rebound theory"}},{"k":"faults_accumulate_elastic_strain_until","v":{"s":"Faults accumulate elastic strain until rapidly released as earthquake."}},{"k":"henry_fielding_reid_proposed_after_190","v":{"s":"Henry Fielding Reid proposed after 1906 SF quake."}},{"k":"tested_confirmed_by_seismology_geodesy","v":{"s":"Tested + confirmed by seismology + geodesy globally."}},{"k":"foundation_for_earthquake_cycle_resear","v":{"s":"Foundation for earthquake-cycle research."}},{"k":"magnitude_vs_intensity_2","v":{"s":"Magnitude vs intensity"}},{"k":"magnitude_measures_earthquake_size_int","v":{"s":"Magnitude measures earthquake size; intensity measures shaking effects."}},{"k":"magnitude_is_one_number_per_quake_inte","v":{"s":"Magnitude is one number per quake; intensity varies by location."}},{"k":"richter_1935_moment_magnitude_hanks_ka","v":{"s":"Richter (1935) → Moment Magnitude (Hanks + Kanamori 1979) for size. Modified Mercalli for intensity."}},{"k":"distinct_concepts_do_not_confuse","v":{"s":"Distinct concepts; do not confuse."}},{"k":"mantle_plume","v":{"s":"Mantle plume"}},{"k":"column_of_hot_mantle_rising_from_deep_","v":{"s":"Column of hot mantle rising from deep within Earth."}},{"k":"continuous_from_core_mantle_boundary_i","v":{"s":"Continuous from core-mantle boundary in some models; shallow in others."}},{"k":"generates_hotspot_volcanism_possibly_2","v":{"s":"Generates hotspot volcanism. Possibly 20+ plumes globally."}},{"k":"some_plumes_lips_erupt_enormous_flood_","v":{"s":"Some plumes (LIPs) erupt enormous flood basalts."}},{"k":"asthenosphere","v":{"s":"Asthenosphere"}},{"k":"partially_molten_plastic_layer_of_uppe","v":{"s":"Partially molten, plastic layer of upper mantle (~100-350 km depth)."}},{"k":"allows_plates_to_move_over_it_like_raf","v":{"s":"Allows plates to move over it like rafts on warm tar."}},{"k":"defined_by_low_seismic_velocity_zone_l","v":{"s":"Defined by low seismic velocity zone (LVZ)."}},{"k":"critical_to_plate_tectonic_mechanism","v":{"s":"Critical to plate tectonic mechanism."}},{"k":"lithosphere","v":{"s":"Lithosphere"}},{"k":"rigid_outer_shell_of_earth_including_c","v":{"s":"Rigid outer shell of Earth, including crust + uppermost mantle."}},{"k":"0_100_km_depth_approximately_tectonic_","v":{"s":"0-100 km depth approximately. Tectonic plates."}},{"k":"cools_thickens_as_it_moves_away_from_r","v":{"s":"Cools + thickens as it moves away from ridge crests."}},{"k":"made_up_of_solid_rock_brittle_to_80_km","v":{"s":"Made up of solid rock; brittle to ~80 km depth."}},{"k":"mid_ocean_ridge","v":{"s":"Mid-ocean ridge"}},{"k":"underwater_mountain_range_where_new_oc","v":{"s":"Underwater mountain range where new oceanic crust forms."}},{"k":"65_000_km_of_ridges_globally_longest_m","v":{"s":"65,000 km of ridges globally; longest mountain range on Earth."}},{"k":"magma_rises_cools_to_form_basaltic_cru","v":{"s":"Magma rises + cools to form basaltic crust + lithosphere."}},{"k":"marie_tharp_bruce_heezen_first_mapped_","v":{"s":"Marie Tharp + Bruce Heezen first mapped in 1957."}},{"k":"trench","v":{"s":"Trench"}},{"k":"deep_narrow_depression_in_seafloor_at_","v":{"s":"Deep, narrow depression in seafloor at subduction zone."}},{"k":"mariana_tonga_peru_chile_aleutian_amon","v":{"s":"Mariana, Tonga, Peru-Chile, Aleutian among deepest."}},{"k":"forms_where_one_plate_dives_beneath_an","v":{"s":"Forms where one plate dives beneath another."}},{"k":"plate_accelerates_downward_dragging_su","v":{"s":"Plate accelerates downward, dragging surface along."}},{"k":"transform_fault","v":{"s":"Transform fault"}},{"k":"plate_boundary_where_plates_slide_past","v":{"s":"Plate boundary where plates slide past each other horizontally."}},{"k":"san_andreas_north_anatolian_alpine_nz_","v":{"s":"San Andreas, North Anatolian, Alpine NZ are examples."}},{"k":"produces_shallow_earthquakes_no_creati","v":{"s":"Produces shallow earthquakes; no creation or destruction of plate."}},{"k":"wilson_1965_defined_the_concept_helped","v":{"s":"Wilson (1965) defined the concept; helped complete plate tectonics."}},{"k":"faulting","v":{"s":"Faulting"}},{"k":"slip_on_a_fracture_in_rock","v":{"s":"Slip on a fracture in rock."}},{"k":"strike_slip_normal_reverse_thrust_type","v":{"s":"Strike-slip, normal, reverse, thrust types."}},{"k":"earthquakes_are_slip_events_on_faults","v":{"s":"Earthquakes are slip events on faults."}},{"k":"most_faults_are_old_some_are_active","v":{"s":"Most faults are old; some are active."}},{"k":"volcanic_arc","v":{"s":"Volcanic arc"}},{"k":"curving_chain_of_volcanoes_parallel_to","v":{"s":"Curving chain of volcanoes parallel to a subduction zone."}},{"k":"aleutians_cascades_andes_japan_indones","v":{"s":"Aleutians, Cascades, Andes, Japan, Indonesia are examples."}},{"k":"subducted_plate_releases_water_water_l","v":{"s":"Subducted plate releases water; water lowers melting point of overlying mantle; magma rises."}},{"k":"produces_explosive_andesitic_dacitic_v","v":{"s":"Produces explosive andesitic + dacitic volcanism."}},{"k":"igneous_rock","v":{"s":"Igneous rock"}},{"k":"rock_formed_by_cooling_of_molten_mater","v":{"s":"Rock formed by cooling of molten material (magma or lava)."}},{"k":"intrusive_cooled_below_surface_vs_extr","v":{"s":"Intrusive (cooled below surface) vs extrusive (cooled at surface)."}},{"k":"grain_size_depends_on_cooling_rate_min","v":{"s":"Grain size depends on cooling rate. Mineral content reflects magma chemistry."}},{"k":"examples_granite_intrusive_basalt_extr","v":{"s":"Examples: granite (intrusive), basalt (extrusive)."}},{"k":"sedimentary_rock","v":{"s":"Sedimentary rock"}},{"k":"rock_formed_by_accumulation_lithificat","v":{"s":"Rock formed by accumulation + lithification of sediment."}},{"k":"clastic_from_grains_chemical_precipita","v":{"s":"Clastic (from grains), chemical (precipitated), biological (organic)."}},{"k":"records_environmental_conditions_of_de","v":{"s":"Records environmental conditions of deposition."}},{"k":"examples_sandstone_clastic_limestone_c","v":{"s":"Examples: sandstone (clastic), limestone (chemical/biological), coal (organic)."}},{"k":"metamorphic_rock","v":{"s":"Metamorphic rock"}},{"k":"rock_transformed_by_heat_pressure_or_f","v":{"s":"Rock transformed by heat, pressure, or fluids without melting."}},{"k":"contact_thermal_regional_orogenic_shoc","v":{"s":"Contact (thermal), regional (orogenic), shock (impact)."}},{"k":"mineral_assemblages_indicate_p_t_condi","v":{"s":"Mineral assemblages indicate P-T conditions of metamorphism."}},{"k":"examples_marble_from_limestone_slate_f","v":{"s":"Examples: marble (from limestone), slate (from shale), gneiss (from granite)."}},{"k":"rock_cycle","v":{"s":"Rock cycle"}},{"k":"continuous_transformation_between_igne","v":{"s":"Continuous transformation between igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic rocks."}},{"k":"all_rocks_can_be_transformed_into_any_","v":{"s":"All rocks can be transformed into any other type."}},{"k":"driven_by_plate_tectonics_surface_proc","v":{"s":"Driven by plate tectonics + surface processes."}},{"k":"first_articulated_by_hutton_1788","v":{"s":"First articulated by Hutton (1788)."}},{"k":"magma_differentiation","v":{"s":"Magma differentiation"}},{"k":"chemical_evolution_of_magma_during_coo","v":{"s":"Chemical evolution of magma during cooling + crystallization."}},{"k":"bowen_s_reaction_series_describes_orde","v":{"s":"Bowen's reaction series describes order of mineral crystallization."}},{"k":"olivine_ca_plagioclase_crystallize_fir","v":{"s":"Olivine + Ca-plagioclase crystallize first; quartz + K-feldspar last."}},{"k":"explains_diversity_of_igneous_rocks_fr","v":{"s":"Explains diversity of igneous rocks from single parent magma."}},{"k":"mineral_identification","v":{"s":"Mineral identification"}},{"k":"process_of_determining_mineral_species","v":{"s":"Process of determining mineral species."}},{"k":"tests_color_streak_luster_hardness_cle","v":{"s":"Tests: color, streak, luster, hardness, cleavage, density."}},{"k":"mohs_hardness_scale_talc_1_to_diamond_","v":{"s":"Mohs hardness scale: talc (1) to diamond (10)."}},{"k":"some_minerals_diagnostic_by_single_pro","v":{"s":"Some minerals diagnostic by single property; most need multiple."}},{"k":"stratigraphy","v":{"s":"Stratigraphy"}},{"k":"study_of_rock_layers_their_sequence","v":{"s":"Study of rock layers + their sequence."}},{"k":"steno_s_principles_superposition_origi","v":{"s":"Steno's principles: superposition, original horizontality, lateral continuity."}},{"k":"index_fossils_correlate_widely_separat","v":{"s":"Index fossils correlate widely-separated outcrops."}},{"k":"foundation_of_geological_history","v":{"s":"Foundation of geological history."}},{"k":"geological_time_scale","v":{"s":"Geological time scale"}},{"k":"hierarchical_division_of_earth_history","v":{"s":"Hierarchical division of Earth history into eons, eras, periods, epochs, ages."}},{"k":"constructed_by_relative_dating_radiome","v":{"s":"Constructed by relative dating + radiometric ages."}},{"k":"international_commission_on_stratigrap","v":{"s":"International Commission on Stratigraphy maintains the official scale."}},{"k":"continuously_updated_as_research_progr","v":{"s":"Continuously updated as research progresses."}},{"k":"radiometric_dating","v":{"s":"Radiometric dating"}},{"k":"determining_rock_age_by_measuring_radi","v":{"s":"Determining rock age by measu