bbean + Anatolia microplates."}},{"k":"plate_boundaries_can_be_transient","v":{"s":"Plate boundaries can be transient."}},{"k":"why_gps_is_critical_to_plate_tectonics","v":{"s":"Why GPS is critical to plate tectonics"}},{"k":"direct_measurement_of_motion_at_mm_pre","v":{"s":"Direct measurement of motion at mm precision."}},{"k":"confirms_predicted_rates_from_seafloor","v":{"s":"Confirms predicted rates from seafloor magnetic record."}},{"k":"reveals_transients_slow_slip_and_stead","v":{"s":"Reveals transients (slow slip) and steady motion."}},{"k":"why_machine_learning_helps_seismology","v":{"s":"Why machine learning helps seismology"}},{"k":"detects_10x_more_small_earthquakes_tha","v":{"s":"Detects 10x more small earthquakes than humans."}},{"k":"improves_catalogs_finds_previously_uns","v":{"s":"Improves catalogs; finds previously unseen swarms."}},{"k":"phasenet_eqtransformer_popular_models","v":{"s":"Phasenet, EQTransformer popular models."}},{"k":"why_we_build_earthquake_resistant","v":{"s":"Why we build earthquake-resistant"}},{"k":"modern_engineering_can_resist_m9_shaki","v":{"s":"Modern engineering can resist M9 shaking."}},{"k":"base_isolation_shear_walls_ductile_fra","v":{"s":"Base isolation, shear walls, ductile frames."}},{"k":"california_japan_chile_leading_practit","v":{"s":"California, Japan, Chile leading practitioners."}},{"k":"frequently_asked_questions","v":{"s":"Frequently Asked Questions"}},{"k":"40_quick_answers_to_common_questions_a","v":{"s":"40 quick answers to common questions about plate tectonics, earthquakes, volcanoes, mountains, and the deep history of Earth."}},{"k":"are_earthquakes_increasing","v":{"s":"Are earthquakes increasing?"}},{"k":"total_quakes_detected_are_increasing_b","v":{"s":"Total quakes detected are increasing because instrumentation improves; rate of damaging quakes is roughly stable."}},{"k":"will_yellowstone_erupt_soon","v":{"s":"Will Yellowstone erupt soon?"}},{"k":"average_inter_eruption_time_is_600_000","v":{"s":"Average inter-eruption time is ~600,000 years; last 3 caldera eruptions were 2.1, 1.3, 0.6 Ma. Statistically not 'overdue.'"}},{"k":"can_we_predict_earthquakes","v":{"s":"Can we predict earthquakes?"}},{"k":"not_in_the_short_term_days_hours_we_ca","v":{"s":"Not in the short term (days-hours). We can predict probability over decades (e.g., seismic hazard maps)."}},{"k":"what_s_the_biggest_possible_earthquake","v":{"s":"What's the biggest possible earthquake?"}},{"k":"probably_m9_6_9_8_limited_by_earth_s_c","v":{"s":"Probably ~M9.6-9.8 limited by Earth's curvature + crustal thickness. 1960 Chile M9.5 may be near the practical maximum."}},{"k":"will_california_fall_into_the_ocean","v":{"s":"Will California fall into the ocean?"}},{"k":"no_the_san_andreas_fault_is_transform_","v":{"s":"No. The San Andreas Fault is transform; sides slide past each other, not down. Pacific side moves NW slowly."}},{"k":"is_the_pacific_ring_of_fire_real","v":{"s":"Is the Pacific 'Ring of Fire' real?"}},{"k":"yes_75_of_world_s_active_dormant_volca","v":{"s":"Yes. ~75% of world's active + dormant volcanoes + 90% of earthquakes around Pacific rim, marking subduction zones."}},{"k":"can_we_drill_to_the_mantle","v":{"s":"Can we drill to the mantle?"}},{"k":"tried_project_mohole_1958_66_reached_6","v":{"s":"Tried (Project Mohole 1958-66). Reached 600m. Future plan: ChikyuMantleQuest drill 7000m to reach Moho."}},{"k":"are_continents_still_moving","v":{"s":"Are continents still moving?"}},{"k":"yes_at_1_10_cm_yr_north_america_moves_","v":{"s":"Yes, at 1-10 cm/yr. North America moves west; Australia north; Pacific NW."}},{"k":"what_s_a_tectonic_plate_made_of","v":{"s":"What's a tectonic plate made of?"}},{"k":"rigid_lithosphere_crust_oceanic_basalt","v":{"s":"Rigid lithosphere: crust (oceanic basalt or continental granite) + uppermost mantle (peridotite)."}},{"k":"how_many_plates_are_there","v":{"s":"How many plates are there?"}},{"k":"7_major_10_minor_50_microplates_total_","v":{"s":"7 major + ~10 minor + ~50 microplates. Total varies by definition."}},{"k":"do_plates_exist_on_other_planets","v":{"s":"Do plates exist on other planets?"}},{"k":"earth_alone_has_plate_tectonics_venus_","v":{"s":"Earth alone has plate tectonics. Venus + Mars are 'one-plate planets.' Europa has ice tectonics."}},{"k":"what_was_pangaea","v":{"s":"What was Pangaea?"}},{"k":"most_recent_supercontinent_335_200_ma_","v":{"s":"Most recent supercontinent, ~335-200 Ma. Wegener identified its existence in 1912."}},{"k":"when_will_the_next_megaquake_hit","v":{"s":"When will the next megaquake hit?"}},{"k":"cascadia_10_15_probability_of_m9_in_ne","v":{"s":"Cascadia 10-15% probability of M9 in next 50 years (USGS). 37% probability of M8+ in same window."}},{"k":"what_s_a_megathrust","v":{"s":"What's a megathrust?"}},{"k":"subduction_zone_interface_produces_ear","v":{"s":"Subduction zone interface. Produces Earth's largest earthquakes (M8.5-9.5)."}},{"k":"why_are_there_so_many_earthquakes_in_i","v":{"s":"Why are there so many earthquakes in Indonesia?"}},{"k":"indonesia_sits_at_confluence_of_4_plat","v":{"s":"Indonesia sits at confluence of 4 plates with active subduction along entire archipelago."}},{"k":"what_caused_2011_japan_tsunami","v":{"s":"What caused 2011 Japan tsunami?"}},{"k":"m9_1_megathrust_quake_ocean_floor_upli","v":{"s":"M9.1 megathrust quake; ocean floor uplifted ~10m, displacing water column."}},{"k":"what_is_a_fault_line","v":{"s":"What is a fault line?"}},{"k":"surface_trace_of_a_fault_fracture_in_e","v":{"s":"Surface trace of a fault - fracture in Earth's crust where motion has occurred."}},{"k":"are_volcanoes_worse_than_earthquakes","v":{"s":"Are volcanoes worse than earthquakes?"}},{"k":"earthquakes_kill_more_people_total_vol","v":{"s":"Earthquakes kill more people total. Volcanoes can be locally devastating + cause climate cooling."}},{"k":"what_is_a_supervolcano","v":{"s":"What is a supervolcano?"}},{"k":"vei_8_event_ejecting_1000_km_yellowsto","v":{"s":"VEI 8 event ejecting >1000 km³. Yellowstone, Toba, Lake Taupo are examples. None in recent history."}},{"k":"will_plate_tectonics_eventually_stop","v":{"s":"Will plate tectonics eventually stop?"}},{"k":"eventually_yes_as_earth_cools_but_not_","v":{"s":"Eventually yes, as Earth cools. But not for billions of years."}},{"k":"are_mid_ocean_ridges_visible","v":{"s":"Are mid-ocean ridges visible?"}},{"k":"iceland_is_the_only_place_otherwise_un","v":{"s":"Iceland is the only place. Otherwise underwater."}},{"k":"what_s_deepest_cave_deepest_underwater","v":{"s":"What's deepest cave + deepest underwater?"}},{"k":"cave_krubera_voronya_georgia_2199m_oce","v":{"s":"Cave: Krubera-Voronya, Georgia, 2199m. Ocean: Challenger Deep, Mariana, 10,994m."}},{"k":"what_s_tallest_mountain","v":{"s":"What's tallest mountain?"}},{"k":"mt_everest_above_sea_level_8849m_mauna","v":{"s":"Mt Everest above sea level (8849m). Mauna Kea from base (10,000m). Olympus Mons on Mars (22 km)."}},{"k":"where_is_the_oldest_rock","v":{"s":"Where is the oldest rock?"}},{"k":"acasta_gneiss_nwt_canada_4_03_ga_older","v":{"s":"Acasta Gneiss, NWT Canada: 4.03 Ga. Older zircons (4.4 Ga) from Jack Hills, Australia."}},{"k":"when_did_the_dinosaurs_die","v":{"s":"When did the dinosaurs die?"}},{"k":"66_million_years_ago_at_k_pg_boundary_","v":{"s":"66 million years ago at K-Pg boundary. Chicxulub asteroid + Deccan volcanism."}},{"k":"did_dinosaurs_all_die","v":{"s":"Did dinosaurs all die?"}},{"k":"birds_are_dinosaurs_they_survived_non_","v":{"s":"Birds are dinosaurs; they survived. Non-avian dinosaurs all extinct."}},{"k":"when_was_earth_formed","v":{"s":"When was Earth formed?"}},{"k":"4_567_billion_years_ago_from_solar_neb","v":{"s":"4.567 billion years ago, from solar nebula material."}},{"k":"when_did_life_begin","v":{"s":"When did life begin?"}},{"k":"stromatolite_evidence_by_3_5_ga_possib","v":{"s":"Stromatolite evidence by 3.5 Ga. Possibly earlier (4 Ga) but evidence sparse."}},{"k":"where_is_mt_everest_growing","v":{"s":"Where is Mt Everest growing?"}},{"k":"continental_collision_of_india_asia_co","v":{"s":"Continental collision of India-Asia continues. Himalayas rise ~1 cm/yr."}},{"k":"when_did_humans_evolve","v":{"s":"When did humans evolve?"}},{"k":"last_common_ancestor_with_chimps_6_7_m","v":{"s":"Last common ancestor with chimps ~6-7 Ma. Genus Homo ~2-3 Ma. Modern humans ~300 ka."}},{"k":"what_s_lithium_for","v":{"s":"What's lithium for?"}},{"k":"battery_cathodes_ev_grid_storage_ceram","v":{"s":"Battery cathodes (EV + grid storage), ceramics, glass, drugs (bipolar treatment)."}},{"k":"what_was_continental_drift","v":{"s":"What was 'continental drift'?"}},{"k":"wegener_s_1912_idea_that_continents_mo","v":{"s":"Wegener's 1912 idea that continents move. Now subsumed in plate tectonics."}},{"k":"who_proved_plate_tectonics","v":{"s":"Who proved plate tectonics?"}},{"k":"no_single_person_wilson_morgan_mckenzi","v":{"s":"No single person. Wilson, Morgan, McKenzie, Le Pichon synthesized in 1960s."}},{"k":"where_did_mary_anning_find_fossils","v":{"s":"Where did Mary Anning find fossils?"}},{"k":"lyme_regis_england_jurassic_coast_disc","v":{"s":"Lyme Regis, England - Jurassic Coast. Discovered first ichthyosaur, plesiosaur, pterosaur."}},{"k":"why_do_we_have_seasons","v":{"s":"Why do we have seasons?"}},{"k":"earth_s_tilt_23_5_sun_s_height_changes","v":{"s":"Earth's tilt (23.5°). Sun's height changes through year."}},{"k":"what_s_the_longest_mountain_range","v":{"s":"What's the longest mountain range?"}},{"k":"andes_on_land_7000_km_mid_atlantic_rid","v":{"s":"Andes on land (7000 km). Mid-Atlantic Ridge underwater (10,000 km)."}},{"k":"will_madagascar_become_an_island","v":{"s":"Will Madagascar become an island?"}},{"k":"already_is_has_been_since_88_ma","v":{"s":"Already is. Has been since ~88 Ma."}},{"k":"what_is_a_transform_fault","v":{"s":"What is a transform fault?"}},{"k":"plate_boundary_where_plates_slide_past_2","v":{"s":"Plate boundary where plates slide past horizontally. San Andreas Fault is classic example."}},{"k":"what_is_a_hotspot","v":{"s":"What is a hotspot?"}},{"k":"localized_region_of_mantle_upwelling_p","v":{"s":"Localized region of mantle upwelling producing volcanism. Hawaii, Yellowstone are examples."}},{"k":"what_s_deepest_mine","v":{"s":"What's deepest mine?"}},{"k":"mponeng_s_africa_4_km_deep_temperature","v":{"s":"Mponeng, S Africa: 4 km deep. Temperature at bottom ~60°C; cooled by refrigeration."}},{"k":"about_acknowledgments","v":{"s":"About + Acknowledgments"}},{"k":"information_about_this_tool_its_source","v":{"s":"Information about this tool, its sources, and how to use it."}},{"k":"about_this_tool","v":{"s":"About this tool"}},{"k":"20_000_line_plate_tectonics_study_tool","v":{"s":"20,000-line plate tectonics study tool, part of AlloFlow STEAM Lab. Built May 2026."}},{"k":"covers_30_topical_tabs_from_interactiv","v":{"s":"Covers 30+ topical tabs from interactive simulation to encyclopedia, faults, volcanoes, fossils, careers, Maine geology, glossary, and classroom activities."}},{"k":"designed_for_k_12_students_teachers_so","v":{"s":"Designed for K-12 students + teachers; some content scales to college intro level."}},{"k":"acknowledgments","v":{"s":"Acknowledgments"}},{"k":"sources_include_usgs_smithsonian_gvp_i","v":{"s":"Sources include USGS, Smithsonian GVP, IRIS, UNAVCO, NOAA, state geological surveys, Marie Tharp + Bruce Heezen ocean floor mapping, J. Tuzo Wilson + Harry Hess foundational work, modern researchers Brian Atwater, Lucy Jones, Susan Hough."}},{"k":"indigenous_knowledge_contributions_fro","v":{"s":"Indigenous knowledge contributions from Quileute, Hoh, Quinault, Makah, Klamath, Hawaiian, Lakota, Aboriginal Australian, and other communities."}},{"k":"earth_science_is_a_deeply_collaborativ","v":{"s":"Earth science is a deeply collaborative + ongoing endeavor."}},{"k":"how_to_use_this_tool","v":{"s":"How to use this tool"}},{"k":"use_the_hub_button_to_see_all_categori","v":{"s":"Use the Hub button to see all categories. Search bar finds tools by name."}},{"k":"each_tab_has_data_explanations_activit","v":{"s":"Each tab has data + explanations + activities."}},{"k":"recommended_sequence_simulation_earthq","v":{"s":"Recommended sequence: Simulation → Earthquake Lab → Encyclopedia → History → Glossary."}},{"k":"maine_focus","v":{"s":"Maine focus"}},{"k":"alloflow_is_developed_in_maine_usa_spe","v":{"s":"AlloFlow is developed in Maine, USA. Special attention given to Maine geology - pegmatite gems, Acadia + Katahdin, glacial features."}},{"k":"maine_state_mineral_tourmaline_state_r","v":{"s":"Maine state mineral: tourmaline. State rock: marble."}},{"k":"lithium_discovery_at_plumbago_mine_new","v":{"s":"Lithium discovery at Plumbago Mine (Newry, ME) announced 2021."}},{"k":"open_free","v":{"s":"Open + free"}},{"k":"alloflow_is_free_for_educators_distrib","v":{"s":"AlloFlow is free for educators. Distribute widely."}},{"k":"suggestions_bug_reports_welcome_throug","v":{"s":"Suggestions + bug reports welcome through standard AlloFlow channels."}},{"k":"aaron_pomeranz_alloflow_creator","v":{"s":"Aaron Pomeranz, AlloFlow creator."}},{"k":"thrust_faulting","v":{"s":"🔺 Thrust faulting"}},{"k":"compression_overcomes_friction","v":{"s":"Compression overcomes friction."}},{"k":"normal_faulting","v":{"s":"⬇️ Normal faulting"}},{"k":"tension_cracks_crust_rift_valleys","v":{"s":"Tension cracks crust. Rift valleys."}},{"k":"strike_slip","v":{"s":"↔️ Strike-slip"}},{"k":"lateral_shear_san_andreas_style","v":{"s":"Lateral shear. San-Andreas-style."}},{"k":"stable","v":{"s":"🟢 Stable"}},{"k":"stress_below_failure_threshold","v":{"s":"Stress below failure threshold."}},{"k":"plate_boundary_stress_discovery","v":{"s":"🔐 Plate boundary stress discovery"}},{"k":"pick_boundary_type_adjust_force_and_fr","v":{"s":"Pick boundary type. Adjust force and friction. Discrete failure mode appears. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_when_does_friction_make_a_f","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: When does friction make a fault stable?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"for_each_boundary_type_find_the_force_","v":{"s":"For each boundary type, find the force level that causes failure."}},{"k":"friction_matters_more_in_transform_fau","v":{"s":"Friction matters more in transform faults — investigate why."}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_why_each_boundary_type_fails_d","v":{"s":"Explain why each boundary type fails differently."}},{"k":"design_note_discrete_4_state_outcome_n","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 4-state outcome; no Richter score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to Tools"}},{"k":"back_to_tools_2","v":{"s":"← Back to Tools"}},{"k":"three_boundary_types_where_plates_meet","v":{"s":"🌋 Three Boundary Types - Where plates meet"}},{"k":"plates_collide","v":{"s":"Plates collide"}},{"k":"plates_separate","v":{"s":"Plates separate"}},{"k":"plates_slide","v":{"s":"Plates slide"}},{"k":"earthquake_scales_magnitude_vs_intensi","v":{"s":"📏 Earthquake Scales - Magnitude vs Intensity"}},{"k":"barely_felt_2","v":{"s":"Barely felt"}},{"k":"mild_shaking","v":{"s":"Mild shaking"}},{"k":"damage_to_weak_buildings","v":{"s":"Damage to weak buildings"}},{"k":"major_damage","v":{"s":"Major damage"}},{"k":"severe_destruction","v":{"s":"Severe destruction"}},{"k":"devastating_t_hoku_2011","v":{"s":"Devastating (Tōhoku 2011)"}},{"k":"myth_buster","v":{"s":"Myth Buster"}},{"k":"answer_3_tectonics_myths","v":{"s":"Answer 3 tectonics myths (True/False)"}},{"k":"timelapse_playback_speed","v":{"s":"Time-lapse playback speed"}},{"k":"tectonics_myths","v":{"s":"Tectonics Myths — true or false?"}},{"k":"start_a_myth","v":{"s":"Start a tectonics myth"}},{"k":"norumbega_fault_system","v":{"s":"Norumbega fault system"}},{"k":"hazards_and_preparedness","v":{"s":"Hazards and Preparedness"}},{"k":"cascadia_case_studies_impacts_and_stay","v":{"s":"Cascadia, case studies, impacts, and staying safe"}},{"k":"parks_and_landscapes","v":{"s":"Parks and Landscapes"}},{"k":"national_parks_state_geology_caves_lan","v":{"s":"National parks, state geology, caves, landforms, resources"}},{"k":"discovery_and_frontiers","v":{"s":"Discovery and Frontiers"}},{"k":"methods_expeditions_big_ideas_and_the_","v":{"s":"Methods, expeditions, big ideas, and the people exploring Earth"}},{"k":"search_input","v":{"s":"Search this Plate Tectonics section"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Fault stability hypothesis"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Plate boundary explanation"}},{"k":"deep_time_alt","v":{"s":"The same eight eras drawn to scale across 4,600 million years. The Hadean and Archean together fill nearly two thirds of the line. Pangaea, its breakup, the Cretaceous and the Cenozoic are all squeezed into the last twelfth, and the Cenozoic — the era that built the Himalaya and everything after — is the last one per cent."}},{"k":"deep_time_axis","v":{"s":"4,600 million years ago  →  today"}},{"k":"deep_time_note","v":{"s":"The row of buttons below is evenly spaced so you can reach any era in one click. These bars are not: every stripe is as wide as the time it took. Pangaea onward — dinosaurs, the Atlantic, the Himalaya, us — is everything after the last tick on the top bar."}},{"k":"deep_time_now","v":{"s":"now"}},{"k":"deep_time_to_scale","v":{"s":"All of it, to scale"}},{"k":"deep_time_zoom","v":{"s":"That last sliver, magnified"}},{"k":"deep_time_zoom_alt","v":{"s":"The most recent 500 million years, magnified from the right-hand end of the bar above, with the same eras named in place."}},{"k":"deep_time_zoom_axis","v":{"s":"the most recent 500 million years"}},{"k":"distance_km","v":{"s":"Distance:"}},{"k":"distance_to_epicenter_km","v":{"s":"Distance from the recording station to the epicenter, in kilometres, from 100 to 2000. Moving this apart changes how long after the P wave the S wave arrives."}},{"k":"earthquake_magnitude_moment","v":{"s":"Earthquake magnitude on the moment magnitude scale, M w, from 1 to 9"}},{"k":"erupt_busy_sr","v":{"s":"An eruption is already in progress. Wait for it to finish."}},{"k":"erupt_busy_title","v":{"s":"An eruption is already running"}},{"k":"erupt_title","v":{"s":"Trigger volcanic eruption"}},{"k":"erupting","v":{"s":"Erupting..."}},{"k":"explainer_region","v":{"s":"What is happening at this boundary"}},{"k":"feel_the_magnitude_scale","v":{"s":"feel the magnitude scale. This slider reads in moment magnitude (Mw), the scale seismologists actually use for large earthquakes. Charles Richter's original 1935 scale is where the idea came from, but it saturates above about M7, so the great quakes on this slider could not be measured with it. Either way the scale is logarithmic: each whole number is 10x more shaking and about 32x more energy. A 7.0 is not twice a 3.5, it is over 100,000x more energy. Slide through magnitudes and watch the damage tier flip from green to amber to red. P-waves arrive first, S-waves second, surface waves do most of the damage."}},{"k":"friction_resists_slip","v":{"s":"Friction resists slip: raise it and the fault holds, lower it and the same stress breaks through."}},{"k":"magnitude_mw","v":{"s":"Magnitude (Mw):"}},{"k":"magnitude_sets_amplitude","v":{"s":"Magnitude sets how big the trace is. Distance, below, sets how far apart the P and S arrivals are."}},{"k":"main_canvas_label","v":{"s":"Interactive plate tectonics cross-section, showing the plates, the mantle beneath them and a depth scale in kilometres. Drag crust plates left or right, or focus this canvas and use the up and down arrows to pick a plate and the left and right arrows to move it, to build convergent and divergent boundaries and watch the trench, slab, volcanic arc, ridge or mountain root each one produces. Hold Shift for larger steps. Transform boundaries slide past each other across this slice rather than along it, so they are shown on the Interactive Sim tab instead."}},{"k":"seis_note","v":{"s":"P waves arrive first, S waves next, and the slow surface waves last — which is why they do most of the damage, and why the ground shakes hardest well after the first jolt. The gap between P and S grows with distance, so one recording tells you HOW FAR away the quake was: about 8.4 kilometres for every second of the gap. Three stations, three distances, and you have the epicentre — which is exactly what the Epicenter Triangulation panel below does."}},{"k":"start_1_body","v":{"s":"Let the mantle carry them, or push one yourself, and see what each kind of boundary builds."}},{"k":"start_1_title","v":{"s":"Watch the plates move"}},{"k":"start_2_body","v":{"s":"Convergent, divergent and transform, one at a time, with the evidence for each."}},{"k":"start_2_title","v":{"s":"Take the three types apart"}},{"k":"start_3_body","v":{"s":"Eight questions. Every wrong answer explains itself rather than just marking you down."}},{"k":"start_3_title","v":{"s":"Check what stuck"}},{"k":"start_here","v":{"s":"Start here"}},{"k":"start_here_sub","v":{"s":"New to this? These three, in order, take about fifteen minutes. Everything below is the reference shelf — dip into it whenever you want."}},{"k":"vent_3d_desc","v":{"s":"Focus this model and use the arrow keys to turn it, plus and minus to zoom, and Home to reset the view; hold Shift for bigger steps. A cutaway model of a volcano. Below the cone, a wide flat magma chamber feeds a tapering conduit up to the vent; dikes cut steeply ACROSS the rock layers while a sill spreads flat ALONG them. Press Erupt to run the sequence: the chamber swells as pressure builds, the conduit glows and an ash plume rises during the blast, the chamber then drains, and finally the summit founders into a caldera over the emptied chamber. The magma buttons change the composition, and the whole model responds: runny low-silica basalt holds little gas, so it builds a broad low shield, pours lava down the flanks, throws little ash, and barely subsides; stiff high-silica rhyolite traps gas, so it stands steep and narrow, produces almost no flowing lava, blasts a tall dense ash column, and drops most of its summit into the emptied chamber. Drag the model, or use the rotate buttons, to turn it. Use the cutaway slider to slice it open."}},{"k":"vent_3d_label","v":{"s":"Rotatable 3D cutaway of a volcano showing the magma chamber, conduit, dikes, sill and vent, shaped by the chosen magma composition."}},{"k":"vent_3d_unavailable","v":{"s":"The 3D engine could not load, so the 2D eruption is showing instead."}},{"k":"vent_cutaway","v":{"s":"✂ Cutaway"}},{"k":"vent_cutaway_label","v":{"s":"Slice the volcano open to see the magma chamber and conduit"}},{"k":"vent_magma","v":{"s":"🌡 Magma"}},{"k":"vent_magma_group","v":{"s":"Choose the magma composition feeding the volcano"}},{"k":"vent_rotate_group","v":{"s":"Rotate the volcano model"}},{"k":"vent_view_2d","v":{"s":"2D sim"}},{"k":"vent_view_3d","v":{"s":"🌋 3D volcano"}},{"k":"vent_view_group","v":{"s":"Choose the 2D simulation or the 3D volcano cutaway"}},{"k":"which_type_gives_way_first","v":{"s":"Compare the three boundary types at the same settings. Which gives way first, and which holds longest?"}},{"k":"great","v":{"s":"Great"}},{"k":"great_desc","v":{"s":"Megathrust — whole regions shake"}},{"k":"shelf_hidden_body","v":{"s":"The boundary table, the magnitude panels and the simulators are still here. Four of these eight questions are answered outright by them, and trying to remember first is what makes it stick. Open them whenever you want to — it reopens on its own once you have been through all eight."}},{"k":"shelf_hidden_title","v":{"s":"The reference shelf is put away while you answer"}},{"k":"shelf_open_btn","v":{"s":"Show the reference shelf"}},{"k":"hide_all_answers","v":{"s":"Hide all answers"}},{"k":"hide_answer","v":{"s":"Hide answer"}},{"k":"insights_intro_counted","v":{"s":"{n} insights into how plate tectonics shapes Earth + life. Each idea ties an observation to a mechanism + a detail. Browse to build connections across the whole picture."}},{"k":"logged_trials","v":{"s":"Logged trials — newest last, last 8 kept"}},{"k":"minerals_intro_counted","v":{"s":"{n} minerals with composition, hardness, cleavage, and economic uses. Organized to support rock identification and economic geology."}},{"k":"no_matches_for","v":{"s":"No matches for"}},{"k":"plate_catalog_counts","v":{"s":"Complete catalog of Earth tectonic plates: {major} major, {minor} minor and {micro} microplates. Filter by tier or search by name. Each plate lists its area, motion and the features it builds."}},{"k":"review_question_n","v":{"s":"review question"}},{"k":"rocks_intro_counted","v":{"s":"Rocks tell the story of how the Earth assembles itself. {n} rocks span igneous (from magma), sedimentary (from sediments), and metamorphic (transformed by heat and pressure). Filter by group."}},{"k":"search_input_eruptions","v":{"s":"Search the eruption catalog"}},{"k":"search_input_glossary","v":{"s":"Search the glossary"}},{"k":"search_input_plateprofiles","v":{"s":"Search plate profiles"}},{"k":"search_input_plates","v":{"s":"Search plates by name or region"}},{"k":"search_input_quakestories","v":{"s":"Search earthquake stories"}},{"k":"search_input_tools","v":{"s":"Search the tools in Plate Tectonics"}},{"k":"search_input_volcanoes","v":{"s":"Search the volcano catalog"}},{"k":"show_all_answers","v":{"s":"Show all answers"}},{"k":"show_answer","v":{"s":"Show answer"}},{"k":"trial_col_boundary","v":{"s":"Boundary"}},{"k":"trial_col_friction","v":{"s":"Friction"}},{"k":"trial_col_result","v":{"s":"Result"}},{"k":"trial_col_stress","v":{"s":"Stress"}},{"k":"trial_normal","v":{"s":"Normal faulting"}},{"k":"trial_stable","v":{"s":"Held — no slip"}},{"k":"trial_strikeslip","v":{"s":"Strike-slip"}},{"k":"trial_thrust","v":{"s":"Thrust faulting"}},{"k":"try_a_shorter_word","v":{"s":"Try a shorter word, or clear the search."}},{"k":"tsunami_intro_counted","v":{"s":"Tsunamis are ocean waves triggered by earthquakes, volcanoes, landslides, or impacts. {n} events from 365 CE to 2023 are catalogued here. Sort by deaths, magnitude, or year."}},{"k":"volcano_intro_counted","v":{"s":"Over 1500 volcanoes have been active in the last 10,000 years. {n} of the most significant and studied volcanoes are here: subduction-zone strato-volcanoes, hotspot shields, and continental calderas."}},{"k":"quiz_band_all","v":{"s":"Every question, first pass. Try the myth bank next."}},{"k":"quiz_band_low","v":{"s":"Worth another lap. Read the boundary cards below first, then run it again."}},{"k":"quiz_band_pass","v":{"s":"Solid pass. The misses below are the ones worth a second look."}},{"k":"quiz_pass_complete","v":{"s":"Pass complete"}},{"k":"quiz_question_label","v":{"s":"Question"}},{"k":"quiz_review_these","v":{"s":"Concepts to revisit"}},{"k":"quiz_run_it_again","v":{"s":"Run the eight again"}},{"k":"quiz_score_label","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"quiz_shelf_open_now","v":{"s":"The reference cards below are open again now that the pass is done."}},{"k":"quiz_sr_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"quiz_sr_incorrect","v":{"s":"Not quite."}},{"k":"epi_dragging_epicenter","v":{"s":"Dragging epicenter"}},{"k":"epi_heading","v":{"s":"Epicenter Triangulation"}},{"k":"epi_region_label","v":{"s":"Interactive epicenter triangulation"}},{"k":"epi_reset_announce","v":{"s":"Reset epicenter and stations"}},{"k":"epi_show_circles","v":{"s":"Show distance circles"}},{"k":"epi_show_fit","v":{"s":"Show triangulated fit"}},{"k":"epi_station_readings","v":{"s":"Station readings"}},{"k":"epi_tip_draggable","v":{"s":"Tip: stations are also draggable. Arrow keys move the epicenter when canvas is focused."}},{"k":"epi_try_collinear","v":{"s":"Drag the stations into a straight line  -  triangulation becomes unstable (the math has no unique answer when stations are collinear)."}},{"k":"epi_try_far_away","v":{"s":"Move the star far away  -  every S-P time grows by ~12 seconds per 100 km. Triple-check by counting grid squares (100 km each)."}},{"k":"epi_try_on_station","v":{"s":"Drag the red star directly on top of a station  -  its S-P time drops to 0 (you are AT the epicenter)."}},{"k":"png_button","v":{"s":"Save PNG"}},{"k":"png_map_not_ready","v":{"s":"The map is not ready yet. Give it a moment and try again."}},{"k":"png_save_failed","v":{"s":"This browser would not let the image be saved."}},{"k":"png_saved_map","v":{"s":"Triangulation map saved as a PNG."}},{"k":"save_epicenter_png_aria","v":{"s":"Save this triangulation map as a PNG image"}},{"k":"epi_canvas_aria","v":{"s":"Map with three seismograph stations (Berkeley, Pasadena, Mt Hamilton) and a draggable epicenter. Each station shows a distance circle; their intersection is the triangulated location. Use arrow keys to move the epicenter; hold Shift for larger steps. Current S-P readings: "}},{"k":"epi_dragging_station","v":{"s":"Dragging station {id}"}},{"k":"epi_hud_columns","v":{"s":"Station   S-P     distance"}},{"k":"epi_math_body","v":{"s":"P-waves race through continental crust at ~{vp} km/s; slower S-waves trail at ~{vs} km/s. The lag between them grows by ~{km} km for every second of S-P time (≈ 12 s of S-P delay per 100 km — a handy rule of thumb). One station gives you a circle of possible locations; two narrow it to two intersection points; three pin down the epicenter. This is exactly how the 1906 San Francisco, 1989 Loma Prieta, and 2011 Tōhoku epicenters were located before GPS-based methods."}},{"k":"epi_moved_readings","v":{"s":"Epicenter moved. S-P readings: {readings}"}},{"k":"epi_try_no_circles","v":{"s":"Turn off \"Show circles\" and rebuild them mentally from the readings  -  this is the job real analysts did with paper and compass before computers."}},{"k":"sim_3d_failed","v":{"s":"The 3D block could not load. Showing the 2D cross-section."}},{"k":"sim_3d_ready","v":{"s":"3D block view ready."}},{"k":"sim_alt_convergent","v":{"s":"Oceanic plate descending beneath continental plate at about {dip} degrees. {deep} of {total} shown earthquakes are 70 km or deeper, lying on the slab."}},{"k":"sim_alt_divergent","v":{"s":"Two plates separating, with new crust filling the ridge between them. Earthquakes are shallow."}},{"k":"sim_alt_intro","v":{"s":"Rotatable 3D block of a {type} boundary. "}},{"k":"sim_alt_transform","v":{"s":"Two plates sliding past each other along the fault, offset along strike. Earthquakes are shallow."}},{"k":"sim_block_keys_help","v":{"s":"Focus this block and use the arrow keys to turn it, plus and minus to zoom, and Home to reset the view; hold Shift for bigger steps. A rotatable 3D block of the boundary. Drag it, or use the rotate buttons, to see that the deep earthquakes lie on a single dipping plane rather than scattered through the mantle. Use the cutaway slider to slice the block down to the same cross-section the 2D view shows."}},{"k":"sim_block_reset_sr","v":{"s":"Block view reset."}},{"k":"sim_boundary_type","v":{"s":"Boundary type"}},{"k":"sim_convergent","v":{"s":"Convergent"}},{"k":"sim_cutaway_help","v":{"s":"Cut the 3D block away along the boundary to reveal the cross-section"}},{"k":"sim_cutaway_off","v":{"s":"Cutaway: off"}},{"k":"sim_cutaway_on","v":{"s":"Cutaway: slicing to the cross-section"}},{"k":"sim_deepest_deep","v":{"s":"Deepest active focus: {n} km (deep)"}},{"k":"sim_deepest_intermediate","v":{"s":"Deepest active focus: {n} km (intermediate)"}},{"k":"sim_deepest_shallow","v":{"s":"Deepest active focus: {n} km (shallow)"}},{"k":"sim_depth_colors_label","v":{"s":"Depth colors: "}},{"k":"sim_depth_scale_label","v":{"s":"Depth scale (70 / 300 / 700 km)"}},{"k":"sim_depth_scale_off","v":{"s":"Depth scale hidden."}},{"k":"sim_depth_scale_on","v":{"s":"Depth scale shown."}},{"k":"sim_desc_convergent","v":{"s":"Plates collide -> mountains rise. Andes, Himalayas, Cascades."}},{"k":"sim_desc_divergent","v":{"s":"Plates separate -> magma fills rift -> new crust. Mid-Atlantic Ridge."}},{"k":"sim_desc_transform","v":{"s":"Plates slide past -> friction -> quakes. San Andreas Fault."}},{"k":"sim_divergent","v":{"s":"Divergent"}},{"k":"sim_ev_crust_convergent","v":{"s":"Recycled at subduction zones or thickened in collision"}},{"k":"sim_ev_crust_divergent","v":{"s":"Created at a ridge or rift"}},{"k":"sim_ev_crust_transform","v":{"s":"Neither created nor destroyed"}},{"k":"sim_ev_motion_convergent","v":{"s":"Toward each other"}},{"k":"sim_ev_motion_divergent","v":{"s":"Away from each other"}},{"k":"sim_ev_motion_transform","v":{"s":"Side by side"}},{"k":"sim_ev_quakes_convergent","v":{"s":"Shallow to deep at subduction zones"}},{"k":"sim_ev_quakes_shallow","v":{"s":"Mostly shallow"}},{"k":"sim_ev_row_crust","v":{"s":"Crustal outcome"}},{"k":"sim_ev_row_motion","v":{"s":"Relative motion"}},{"k":"sim_ev_row_quakes","v":{"s":"Quake-depth clue"}},{"k":"sim_ev_row_volcanoes","v":{"s":"Volcanism clue"}},{"k":"sim_ev_volc_convergent","v":{"s":"Common above many subduction zones"}},{"k":"sim_ev_volc_divergent","v":{"s":"Common along ridges and rifts"}},{"k":"sim_ev_volc_transform","v":{"s":"Usually not caused by the boundary"}},{"k":"sim_evidence_heading","v":{"s":"Boundary evidence"}},{"k":"sim_evidence_question","v":{"s":"What observations support {type}?"}},{"k":"sim_hud_boundary","v":{"s":"{type} boundary"}},{"k":"sim_hud_events","v":{"s":"Events: {n}"}},{"k":"sim_hud_mountain","v":{"s":"Mountain: {n} m"}},{"k":"sim_hud_rate","v":{"s":"Rate: {n} cm/yr"}},{"k":"sim_hud_rift","v":{"s":"Rift width: {n} km"}},{"k":"sim_hud_years","v":{"s":"Years: {n}"}},{"k":"sim_key_deep","v":{"s":"deep — over 300 km"}},{"k":"sim_key_intermediate","v":{"s":"intermediate — 70 to 300 km"}},{"k":"sim_key_shallow","v":{"s":"shallow — under 70 km"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_continental","v":{"s":"continental — thick, buoyant"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_cool_sinks","v":{"s":"cool rock sinks"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_cool_sinks_here","v":{"s":"cool rock sinks here"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_focus_depth_key","v":{"s":"FOCUS DEPTH, BY COLOUR"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_hot_rises","v":{"s":"hot rock rises"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_hot_rises_here","v":{"s":"hot rock rises here"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_into_page","v":{"s":"motion is into and out of this page"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_map_view","v":{"s":"MAP VIEW — LOOKING STRAIGHT DOWN"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_new_crust_stripes","v":{"s":"new crust • magnetic stripes mirror across the ridge"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_no_limb","v":{"s":"no rising or sinking here"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_no_new_plate","v":{"s":"no new plate made"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_none_destroyed","v":{"s":"none destroyed either"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_oceanic","v":{"s":"oceanic — thin, dense"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_same_plate_down","v":{"s":"same plate, going down"}},{"k":"sim_lbl_stream_cut","v":{"s":"one stream, cut in two"}},{"k":"sim_loading_block","v":{"s":"Loading 3D block…"}},{"k":"sim_model_note","v":{"s":"Model note: event cadence and landscape growth are accelerated qualitative cues, not forecasts or calibrated rates. "}},{"k":"sim_no_events_yet","v":{"s":"No active events yet"}},{"k":"sim_paused","v":{"s":"Simulation paused"}},{"k":"sim_plate_a_left","v":{"s":"<- PLATE A"}},{"k":"sim_plate_a_right","v":{"s":"PLATE A ->"}},{"k":"sim_plate_a_up","v":{"s":"PLATE A ↑"}},{"k":"sim_plate_b_down","v":{"s":"PLATE B ↓"}},{"k":"sim_plate_b_left","v":{"s":"<- PLATE B"}},{"k":"sim_plate_b_right","v":{"s":"PLATE B ->"}},{"k":"sim_playing","v":{"s":"Simulation playing"}},{"k":"sim_rate_aria","v":{"s":"Plate movement rate in centimeters per year"}},{"k":"sim_rate_fast","v":{"s":"Fast (like Pacific)"}},{"k":"sim_rate_label","v":{"s":"Plate rate: {n} cm/year"}},{"k":"sim_rate_medium","v":{"s":"Medium (like Africa)"}},{"k":"sim_rate_slow","v":{"s":"Slow (like NA plate)"}},{"k":"sim_reset_sr","v":{"s":"Simulation reset"}},{"k":"sim_reset_view","v":{"s":"Reset view"}},{"k":"sim_rotate_group","v":{"s":"Rotate the 3D block"}},{"k":"sim_scenario_note","v":{"s":"Scenario note: this cross-section depicts oceanic lithosphere subducting beneath another plate. Continental collision looks different. "}},{"k":"sim_scn_convergent","v":{"s":"The left plate is thin, dense oceanic lithosphere sitting below sea level; the right plate is thicker, more buoyant continental lithosphere standing above it. A trench marks where the oceanic plate bends down, and the same plate is drawn continuing beneath the other as a descending slab. Melt rises from the slab to a volcanic arc set back from the trench, and a mountain range grows on the overriding plate. In the mantle below, the flow is sinking beneath the boundary."}},{"k":"sim_scn_divergent","v":{"s":"The two plates are separating and new crust fills the gap between them, banded with the mirrored magnetic stripes that recorded sea-floor spreading. In the mantle below, hot rock rises beneath the boundary and sinks again at the edges."}},{"k":"sim_scn_transform","v":{"s":"A near-vertical fault cuts the whole plate, with locked rough patches on it that produce shallow earthquakes. The plates slide along the fault, which in a cross-section means into and out of the page, so a map view is drawn above the section: seen from above, a single stream is cut in two and offset across the fault trace, and arrows show the two blocks travelling in opposite directions. In the mantle below there is no rising or sinking limb beneath this boundary, because a transform boundary creates no new plate and destroys none."}},{"k":"sim_sr_rate_changed","v":{"s":"Plate rate changed to {n} centimeters per year"}},{"k":"sim_sr_switched_boundary","v":{"s":"Switched boundary type to {type}"}},{"k":"sim_switched_2d","v":{"s":"Switched to the 2D cross-section."}},{"k":"sim_switched_3d","v":{"s":"Switched to the 3D block view."}},{"k":"sim_tilt_down","v":{"s":"Tilt down"}},{"k":"sim_title","v":{"s":"Plate Boundary Simulator"}},{"k":"sim_transform","v":{"s":"Transform"}},{"k":"sim_try_compare","v":{"s":"Compare patterns, not raw counts: this accelerated model emphasizes quake depth and crustal outcome rather than predicting real hazard."}},{"k":"sim_try_convergent","v":{"s":"Switch to Convergent and compare shallow, intermediate, and deep event colors. Deep-focus earthquakes are strong evidence of a subducting slab."}},{"k":"sim_try_divergent","v":{"s":"Switch to Divergent - see the mid-ocean ridge open and magma fill in (this is happening at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge right now)."}},{"k":"sim_try_speeds","v":{"s":"Plate speeds are commonly measured in centimeters per year. At a constant 2-15 cm/year, a plate would travel about 200-1500 km in 10 million years. Real rates and directions change through time. Earthquake magnitude is logarithmic: one whole moment-magnitude unit represents about 10x recorded wave amplitude and roughly 32x energy release."}},{"k":"sim_try_transform","v":{"s":"Switch to Transform and observe shallow events plus horizontal offset, as at the San Andreas, North Anatolian, and Alpine faults."}},{"k":"sim_turn_left","v":{"s":"Turn left"}},{"k":"sim_turn_right","v":{"s":"Turn right"}},{"k":"sim_view_cross_section","v":{"s":"Cross-section"}},{"k":"sim_view_group_label","v":{"s":"Choose the cross-section or the 3D block view"}},{"k":"sim_band_deep","v":{"s":"This boundary has now produced deep earthquakes, more than 300 kilometres down."}},{"k":"sim_band_intermediate","v":{"s":"This boundary has now produced intermediate earthquakes, 70 to 300 kilometres down."}},{"k":"sim_band_none","v":{"s":"No earthquakes yet at this boundary."}},{"k":"sim_band_shallow","v":{"s":"This boundary is producing shallow earthquakes, less than 70 kilometres down."}},{"k":"aria_answer_value","v":{"s":"Answer {value}"}},{"k":"aria_cascadia_section","v":{"s":"Cross-section of the Cascadia subduction zone from west to east: the Juan de Fuca plate slides under North America at about 4 centimetres a year. The shallow part of the contact, from the offshore trench to the coast, is locked and releases as a magnitude 9 earthquake every roughly 500 years. Deeper, under the coast ranges, the contact creeps in slow-slip and tremor episodes. Where the plate reaches about 100 kilometres, water drives melting that feeds the Cascade volcanoes."}},{"k":"aria_close_tutor","v":{"s":"Close AI tectonics tutor"}},{"k":"aria_fault_diagram","v":{"s":"Fault diagram for a {type} boundary. Net stress {net} against a failure line of {threshold}: the fault is {state}."}},{"k":"aria_generate_at_level","v":{"s":"Generate AI explanation at {level} level"}},{"k":"aria_magnitude_vs_intensity","v":{"s":"Two columns comparing the two earthquake scales. On the left, magnitude: three traces for a magnitude 4, 6 and 8 earthquake, each about ten times the shaking of the one above it — one number for the whole earthquake, read off a recording. On the right, intensity for a single magnitude 7 earthquake felt at four distances: Mercalli nine at ten kilometres, buildings shifted off their foundations; seven at sixty kilometres, chimneys fall and it is hard to stand; five at two hundred kilometres, dishes rattle and sleepers wake; two at six hundred kilometres, felt only by a few people at rest. Magnitude is what the earthquake did; intensity is what it did to you, where you were standing."}},{"k":"aria_plate_key","v":{"s":"Plate identification key"}},{"k":"aria_reading_level","v":{"s":"Reading level: {level}"}},{"k":"aria_search_activities","v":{"s":"Search classroom activities"}},{"k":"aria_selected_suffix","v":{"s":" (selected)"}},{"k":"aria_step_eras","v":{"s":"Step through geological eras"}},{"k":"aria_three_boundaries","v":{"s":"Three diagrams comparing the plate boundary types. Convergent, seen in side view: a thin oceanic plate sits lower than a thicker continental plate, bends down at a trench and carries on beneath it as a slab, and a volcano stands on the upper plate set back from the trench. Divergent, seen in side view: two plates move apart and melt rises into the gap and freezes as new crust between them. Transform, seen from above rather than in section, because the motion runs along the boundary: a single stream is cut in two and carried apart across the fault trace, with earthquakes on the fault and the two blocks moving in opposite directions."}},{"k":"aria_trigger_eruption","v":{"s":"Trigger a volcanic eruption in the plate tectonics simulation"}},{"k":"sr_3d_unavailable","v":{"s":"The 3D engine is unavailable. Staying on the 2D simulation."}},{"k":"sr_convection_state","v":{"s":"Convection currents {state}."}},{"k":"sr_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"sr_drift_paused","v":{"s":"Mantle drift paused. Plates now move only when you move them."}},{"k":"sr_drift_running","v":{"s":"Mantle drift running. The convection currents are now carrying the plates."}},{"k":"sr_explanation_ready","v":{"s":"Explanation ready."}},{"k":"sr_false","v":{"s":"False."}},{"k":"sr_labels_state","v":{"s":"Labels {state}."}},{"k":"sr_locked","v":{"s":"locked"}},{"k":"sr_magma_summary","v":{"s":"{type} magma. {visc}, {gas}. Builds a {landform}."}},{"k":"sr_moved_to_simulator","v":{"s":"Moved to the Plate Boundary Simulator. Pick a boundary type on the right to compare what each one builds."}},{"k":"sr_myth_verdict","v":{"s":"{verdict} {answer} {why}"}},{"k":"sr_not_quite","v":{"s":"Not quite."}},{"k":"sr_off","v":{"s":"off"}},{"k":"sr_on","v":{"s":"on"}},{"k":"sr_slipping","v":{"s":"slipping"}},{"k":"sr_sonify_off","v":{"s":"Sonification sweep stopped."}},{"k":"sr_sonify_on","v":{"s":"Sonification sweep active. Playing geological friction hum."}},{"k":"sr_switched_to_tab","v":{"s":"Switched to {tab}."}},{"k":"sr_switched_volcano_2d","v":{"s":"Switched to the 2D simulation."}},{"k":"sr_switched_volcano_3d","v":{"s":"Switched to the 3D volcano cutaway. Drag to rotate, or use the rotate buttons."}},{"k":"sr_true","v":{"s":"True."}},{"k":"sr_volcano_3d_ready","v":{"s":"3D volcano cutaway ready."}},{"k":"sr_volcano_view_reset","v":{"s":"Volcano cutaway view reset."}},{"k":"sr_word_false","v":{"s":"false"}},{"k":"sr_word_true","v":{"s":"true"}},{"k":"tab_name_earthquake","v":{"s":"Earthquake Lab"}},{"k":"tab_name_quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"tab_name_sim","v":{"s":"Simulation"}},{"k":"tab_name_timeline","v":{"s":"Timeline"}},{"k":"drift_tooltip","v":{"s":"Let the mantle currents carry the plates, or freeze them so only you move them"}},{"k":"fig_convection_cell","v":{"s":"convection cell"}},{"k":"fig_crustal_root","v":{"s":"crustal root"}},{"k":"fig_depth_70_km","v":{"s":"70 km"}},{"k":"fig_epicenter_drag","v":{"s":"epicenter (drag)"}},{"k":"fig_heights_exaggerated","v":{"s":"Surface heights exaggerated"}},{"k":"fig_inner_core","v":{"s":"Inner core (solid)"}},{"k":"fig_lower_mantle","v":{"s":"Lower mantle"}},{"k":"fig_new_crust","v":{"s":"new crust"}},{"k":"fig_one_stream_cut","v":{"s":"one stream, cut in two"}},{"k":"fig_outer_core","v":{"s":"Outer core (liquid)"}},{"k":"fig_quake_header","v":{"s":"M {mag}  ·  {km} km away"}},{"k":"fig_scale_100_km","v":{"s":"100 km"}},{"k":"fig_seconds_after_quake","v":{"s":"seconds after the quake →"}},{"k":"fig_trench","v":{"s":"trench"}},{"k":"fig_triangulated","v":{"s":"triangulated"}},{"k":"fig_volcanic_arc","v":{"s":"volcanic arc"}},{"k":"search_activities_placeholder","v":{"s":"Search activities..."}},{"k":"search_placeholder","v":{"s":"Search..."}},{"k":"toast_challenge_won","v":{"s":"Challenge: {name} (+{rp} RP)"}},{"k":"sr_and_a_volcano_erupted","v":{"s":" and a volcano erupted"}},{"k":"sr_boundary_formed","v":{"s":"Boundary formed with the {name} plate. {event}{eruption}."}},{"k":"sr_edge_left","v":{"s":"left"}},{"k":"sr_edge_right","v":{"s":"right"}},{"k":"sr_magnitude_quake","v":{"s":"Magnitude {m} earthquake"}},{"k":"sr_plate_at_edge","v":{"s":"{name} is already at the {edge} edge."}},{"k":"sr_plate_moved","v":{"s":"{name} moved {dir}."}},{"k":"sr_plate_selected","v":{"s":"{name}, {type} plate, selected. Left and right arrows move it."}},{"k":"sr_quake_triggered","v":{"s":"Earthquake triggered"}},{"k":"magma_andesitic","v":{"s":"Andesitic"}},{"k":"magma_basaltic","v":{"s":"Basaltic"}},{"k":"magma_broad_shield_volcano","v":{"s":"Broad shield volcano"}},{"k":"magma_caldera_complex","v":{"s":"Caldera complex"}},{"k":"magma_high_silica_72","v":{"s":"High silica (~72%)"}},{"k":"magma_little_trapped_gas","v":{"s":"Little trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_lots_of_trapped_gas","v":{"s":"Lots of trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_low_silica_50","v":{"s":"Low silica (~50%)"}},{"k":"magma_mauna_loa_kilauea","v":{"s":"Mauna Loa, Kilauea"}},{"k":"magma_medium_silica_60","v":{"s":"Medium silica (~60%)"}},{"k":"magma_mt_fuji_mt_st_helens","v":{"s":"Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolitic","v":{"s":"Rhyolitic"}},{"k":"magma_runny","v":{"s":"Runny"}},{"k":"magma_some_trapped_gas","v":{"s":"Some trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_steep_stratovolcano","v":{"s":"Steep stratovolcano"}},{"k":"magma_sticky","v":{"s":"Sticky"}},{"k":"magma_stiff","v":{"s":"Stiff"}},{"k":"magma_yellowstone_toba","v":{"s":"Yellowstone, Toba"}},{"k":"myth_a_single_plate_can_carry_ocean_floor_a","v":{"s":"A single plate can carry ocean floor and a continent at the same time."}},{"k":"myth_continents_plow_through_the_ocean_floo","v":{"s":"Continents plow through the ocean floor like ships cutting through water."}},{"k":"myth_earth_s_inner_core_is_molten_liquid_be","v":{"s":"Earth’s inner core is molten liquid because it is the hottest part of the planet."}},{"k":"myth_earthquakes_and_volcanoes_pop_up_in_to","v":{"s":"Earthquakes and volcanoes pop up in totally random places."}},{"k":"myth_iceland_is_being_slowly_pulled_in_two","v":{"s":"Iceland is being slowly pulled in two by a plate boundary running right through it."}},{"k":"myth_mountains_have_always_been_exactly_whe","v":{"s":"Mountains have always been exactly where they are and never change."}},{"k":"myth_pangaea_was_the_only_supercontinent_th","v":{"s":"Pangaea was the only supercontinent that ever existed."}},{"k":"myth_plate_motion_is_measured_directly_toda","v":{"s":"Plate motion is measured directly today, not only inferred from old rocks."}},{"k":"myth_plates_move_mainly_because_mantle_conv","v":{"s":"Plates move mainly because mantle convection drags them along from below."}},{"k":"myth_some_earthquakes_begin_hundreds_of_kil","v":{"s":"Some earthquakes begin hundreds of kilometres down, far below the crust."}},{"k":"myth_the_atlantic_ocean_is_a_little_wider_t","v":{"s":"The Atlantic Ocean is a little wider today than it was when your grandparents were born."}},{"k":"myth_the_continents_have_always_looked_just","v":{"s":"The continents have always looked just like they do on today’s map."}},{"k":"myth_the_ground_under_your_feet_is_complete","v":{"s":"The ground under your feet is completely still — nothing moves down there."}},{"k":"myth_the_himalayas_are_still_growing_taller","v":{"s":"The Himalayas are still growing taller right now."}},{"k":"myth_the_mantle_is_an_ocean_of_liquid_magma","v":{"s":"The mantle is an ocean of liquid magma, and the plates float on it like rafts."}},{"k":"myth_the_ocean_floor_carries_a_magnetic_rec","v":{"s":"The ocean floor carries a magnetic record of Earth reversing its magnetic field."}},{"k":"myth_the_rock_of_the_ocean_floor_is_far_you","v":{"s":"The rock of the ocean floor is far younger than the rock of the continents."}},{"k":"myth_when_a_plate_subducts_that_crust_is_de","v":{"s":"When a plate subducts, that crust is destroyed and gone forever."}},{"k":"myth_you_can_find_fossils_of_sea_creatures","v":{"s":"You can find fossils of sea creatures near the top of some of the highest mountains."}},{"k":"mythwhy_about_200_million_years_ago_they_were","v":{"s":"About 200 million years ago they were joined in one supercontinent, Pangaea. They have been drifting apart ever since — and are still moving."}},{"k":"mythwhy_colliding_plates_crumple_the_crust_upw","v":{"s":"Colliding plates crumple the crust upward over millions of years — the Himalayas are STILL rising a few millimeters a year as India pushes into Asia."}},{"k":"mythwhy_india_is_still_pushing_north_into_eura","v":{"s":"India is still pushing north into Eurasia, so the range keeps rising a few millimetres a year. Erosion strips it back at a similar rate, which is why it does not simply climb forever."}},{"k":"mythwhy_it_melts_and_recycles_some_rises_again","v":{"s":"It melts and RECYCLES — some rises again as volcanic magma at the arc above. Plate tectonics and the rock cycle are the same engine."}},{"k":"mythwhy_it_was_just_the_most_recent_earlier_on","v":{"s":"It was just the most recent. Earlier ones assembled and broke apart too — Rodinia ~1 billion years ago, Columbia/Nuna ~1.8 billion. The \"supercontinent cycle\" repeats roughly every 300–500 million years."}},{"k":"mythwhy_most_of_them_do_the_south_american_pla","v":{"s":"Most of them do. The South American plate carries the continent AND a wide slice of the South Atlantic floor, with no plate boundary at the coastline. The edge of a plate and the edge of a continent are different things."}},{"k":"mythwhy_new_rock_at_a_ridge_locks_in_the_field","v":{"s":"New rock at a ridge locks in the field direction as it cools, so the seafloor is striped with alternating polarity, symmetrical either side of the ridge. That pattern is the evidence that settled seafloor spreading in the 1960s."}},{"k":"mythwhy_new_seafloor_is_being_made_along_the_r","v":{"s":"New seafloor is being made along the ridge that runs down the middle of the Atlantic, and it pushes the two sides apart a few centimetres a year. Over a lifetime that adds up to a couple of metres."}},{"k":"mythwhy_ocean_floor_is_created_at_ridges_and_d","v":{"s":"Ocean floor is created at ridges and destroyed at trenches, so almost none of it is older than about 200 million years. Parts of the continents are billions of years old, because there is no conveyor belt carrying them away."}},{"k":"mythwhy_permanent_gps_stations_track_the_groun","v":{"s":"Permanent GPS stations track the ground to within millimetres a year, and the rates they return agree with the ones worked out from magnetic stripes. Two independent methods, one answer."}},{"k":"mythwhy_that_was_wegener_s_original_mistake_an","v":{"s":"That was Wegener’s original mistake, and why his idea was rejected for decades. A plate carries its continent AND its seafloor together — new seafloor is born at ridges and swallowed at trenches."}},{"k":"mythwhy_the_dominant_force_is_slab_pull_the_we","v":{"s":"The dominant force is SLAB PULL — the weight of the cold, dense subducting slab sinking and pulling the rest of the plate behind it. Ridge push and convection help, but slab pull leads."}},{"k":"mythwhy_the_inner_core_is_solid_iron_even_at_5","v":{"s":"The inner core is SOLID iron even at ~5,400°C — about as hot as the Sun’s surface. The crushing pressure raises iron’s melting point above that temperature. It is the OUTER core that is liquid — and its churning makes Earth’s magnetic field."}},{"k":"mythwhy_the_mantle_is_solid_rock_it_flows_but","v":{"s":"The mantle is SOLID rock. It flows — but incredibly slowly, like cold putty over thousands of years. Plates ride on the solid-but-bendable asthenosphere, not on liquid."}},{"k":"mythwhy_the_mid_atlantic_ridge_crosses_iceland","v":{"s":"The Mid-Atlantic Ridge crosses Iceland, so the island straddles a divergent boundary. You can walk down into the rift valley it has opened."}},{"k":"mythwhy_the_plates_carrying_the_continents_cre","v":{"s":"The plates carrying the continents creep along about as fast as your fingernails grow — a few centimeters a year. It is slow, but it never stops."}},{"k":"mythwhy_they_cluster_along_the_same_lines_the","v":{"s":"They cluster along the SAME lines — the edges of the plates. The \"Ring of Fire\" around the Pacific is one giant plate boundary."}},{"k":"mythwhy_they_happen_inside_slabs_that_have_alr","v":{"s":"They happen inside slabs that have already subducted, which is a large part of how we know those slabs are down there at all. The deepest reach about 700 kilometres."}},{"k":"mythwhy_those_rocks_formed_on_an_ancient_seafl","v":{"s":"Those rocks formed on an ancient seafloor and were lifted up when two plates collided. Marine fossils really do turn up high in the Himalayas."}},{"k":"magma_andesite_example","v":{"s":"Mt Fuji, Mt St Helens"}},{"k":"magma_andesite_gas","v":{"s":"Some trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_andesite_label","v":{"s":"Andesitic"}},{"k":"magma_andesite_landform","v":{"s":"Steep stratovolcano"}},{"k":"magma_andesite_silica","v":{"s":"Medium silica (~60%)"}},{"k":"magma_andesite_visc","v":{"s":"Sticky"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_example","v":{"s":"Mauna Loa, Kilauea"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_gas","v":{"s":"Little trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_label","v":{"s":"Basaltic"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_landform","v":{"s":"Broad shield volcano"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_silica","v":{"s":"Low silica (~50%)"}},{"k":"magma_basalt_visc","v":{"s":"Runny"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_example","v":{"s":"Yellowstone, Toba"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_gas","v":{"s":"Lots of trapped gas"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_label","v":{"s":"Rhyolitic"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_landform","v":{"s":"Caldera complex"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_silica","v":{"s":"High silica (~72%)"}},{"k":"magma_rhyolite_visc","v":{"s":"Stiff"}},{"k":"fa_note_african_plate_under_aegean_crete_cyp","v":{"s":"African plate under Aegean; Crete + Cyprus; 365 CE megaquake"}},{"k":"fa_note_african_slab_under_italy_produces_de","v":{"s":"African slab under Italy; produces deep Tyrrhenian quakes"}},{"k":"fa_note_arabian_under_eurasian_1945_m8_1_tsu","v":{"s":"Arabian under Eurasian; 1945 M8.1 + tsunami"}},{"k":"fa_note_atlantic_caribbean_transition_deepes","v":{"s":"Atlantic/Caribbean transition; deepest in Atlantic basin"}},{"k":"fa_note_branch_of_san_andreas_creeping_secti","v":{"s":"Branch of San Andreas; creeping section"}},{"k":"fa_note_closed_iapetus_ocean_brought_laurent","v":{"s":"Closed Iapetus Ocean; brought Laurentia + Baltica together"}},{"k":"fa_note_cocos_under_caribbean_n_american_198","v":{"s":"Cocos under Caribbean + N American; 1985 Mexico City M8.0"}},{"k":"fa_note_conceptual_inclined_seismic_zone_sho","v":{"s":"Conceptual; inclined seismic zone showing slab descent"}},{"k":"fa_note_continental_rift_rio_grande_follows","v":{"s":"Continental rift; Rio Grande follows it"}},{"k":"fa_note_continental_rift_splitting_somalia_n","v":{"s":"Continental rift splitting Somalia + Nubia; future ocean basin"}},{"k":"fa_note_cordilleran_extension_metamorphic_co","v":{"s":"Cordilleran extension; metamorphic core complexes"}},{"k":"fa_note_diffuse_transform_zone_east_of_sierr","v":{"s":"Diffuse transform zone east of Sierra Nevada"}},{"k":"fa_note_east_bay_mean_recurrence_150_years_l","v":{"s":"East Bay; mean recurrence 150 years; last 1868; 'tectonic time bomb'"}},{"k":"fa_note_eastern_sierra_escarpment_1872_m7_8","v":{"s":"Eastern Sierra escarpment; 1872 M7.8 quake was mostly right-lateral"}},{"k":"fa_note_eurasian_n_american_african_s_americ","v":{"s":"Eurasian/N American + African/S American; ~2.5 cm/yr"}},{"k":"fa_note_extended_crust_alternating_uplifted","v":{"s":"Extended crust; alternating uplifted ranges + dropped basins"}},{"k":"fa_note_india_asia_collision_classic_suture","v":{"s":"India-Asia collision; classic suture zone"}},{"k":"fa_note_indian_ocean_spreading_y_shaped_junc","v":{"s":"Indian Ocean spreading; Y-shaped junction with Central Indian Ridge"}},{"k":"fa_note_indo_australian_under_sunda_2004_m9","v":{"s":"Indo-Australian under Sunda; 2004 M9.1 Sumatra"}},{"k":"fa_note_joined_siberia_baltica_into_eurasia","v":{"s":"Joined Siberia + Baltica into Eurasia; Permian"}},{"k":"fa_note_juan_de_fuca_subducting_1700_m9_conf","v":{"s":"Juan de Fuca subducting; 1700 M9 confirmed by Atwater + Japan records"}},{"k":"fa_note_lake_baikal_sits_in_deepest_continen","v":{"s":"Lake Baikal sits in deepest continental rift; oldest lake on Earth"}},{"k":"fa_note_left_lateral_2023_doublet_sequence","v":{"s":"Left-lateral; 2023 doublet sequence"}},{"k":"fa_note_left_lateral_bounds_mojave_desert_no","v":{"s":"Left-lateral; bounds Mojave Desert north edge"}},{"k":"fa_note_left_lateral_created_sea_of_galilee","v":{"s":"Left-lateral; created Sea of Galilee + Dead Sea + Red Sea triple junction"}},{"k":"fa_note_linear_feature_tectonic_significance","v":{"s":"Linear feature; tectonic significance debated"}},{"k":"fa_note_mid_atlantic_on_land_ingvellir_natio","v":{"s":"Mid-Atlantic on land; Þingvellir National Park visible boundary"}},{"k":"fa_note_nazca_under_s_american_longest_trenc","v":{"s":"Nazca under S American; longest trench; 1960 M9.5"}},{"k":"fa_note_new_madrid_seismic_zone_intraplate_1","v":{"s":"New Madrid Seismic Zone; intraplate; 1811-12 sequence"}},{"k":"fa_note_normal_fault_salt_lake_city_exposure","v":{"s":"Normal fault; Salt Lake City exposure; M7+ possible"}},{"k":"fa_note_north_of_nz_pacific_subducting_under","v":{"s":"North of NZ; Pacific subducting under Australian"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_australian_locked_for_300_ye","v":{"s":"Pacific/Australian; locked for 300+ years; expected M8 overdue"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_nazca_fastest_spreading_on_e","v":{"s":"Pacific/Nazca; fastest spreading on Earth (15+ cm/yr)"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_north_american_offshore_bc_1","v":{"s":"Pacific/North American; offshore BC; 1949 M8.1"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_north_american_right_lateral","v":{"s":"Pacific/North American; right-lateral; 1906 SF earthquake; ~150-300 yr return on major segments"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_subducting_beneath_australia","v":{"s":"Pacific subducting beneath Australian; slow-slip events documented"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_subducting_under_n_american","v":{"s":"Pacific subducting under N American; 1964 M9.2 Good Friday"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_under_mariana_microplate_dee","v":{"s":"Pacific under Mariana microplate; deepest point Challenger Deep"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_under_okhotsk_plate_2011_m9","v":{"s":"Pacific under Okhotsk plate; 2011 M9 Tohoku"}},{"k":"fa_note_pacific_under_tonga_plate_fastest_su","v":{"s":"Pacific under Tonga plate; fastest subduction known (~24 cm/yr)"}},{"k":"fa_note_paleozoic_suture_iapetus_closure_maj","v":{"s":"Paleozoic suture; Iapetus closure; major terrane boundary"}},{"k":"fa_note_part_of_sunda_system","v":{"s":"Part of Sunda system"}},{"k":"fa_note_philippine_sea_under_eurasia_100_150","v":{"s":"Philippine Sea under Eurasia; ~100-150 yr recurrence; overdue"}},{"k":"fa_note_right_lateral_2002_m7_9_rupture_dama","v":{"s":"Right-lateral; 2002 M7.9 rupture damaged Trans-Alaska Pipeline supports"}},{"k":"fa_note_right_lateral_part_of_n_american_int","v":{"s":"Right-lateral; part of N American interior strike-slip"}},{"k":"fa_note_right_lateral_sequential_westward_mi","v":{"s":"Right-lateral; sequential westward migrating earthquakes since 1939"}},{"k":"fa_note_s_america_under_scotia_plate_remote","v":{"s":"S America under Scotia plate; remote"}},{"k":"fa_note_sunda_under_philippine_sea_tsunami_p","v":{"s":"Sunda under Philippine Sea; tsunami potential to South China Sea coast"}},{"k":"fa_note_underlies_whole_himalaya_m8_events_1","v":{"s":"Underlies whole Himalaya; M8+ events; 1934 Nepal-Bihar"}},{"k":"fa_note_young_ocean_basin_5_mm_yr_eventually","v":{"s":"Young ocean basin; ~5 mm/yr; eventually full ocean"}},{"k":"fa_regi_africa","v":{"s":"Africa"}},{"k":"fa_regi_alaska","v":{"s":"Alaska"}},{"k":"fa_regi_appalachians","v":{"s":"Appalachians"}},{"k":"fa_regi_atlantic","v":{"s":"Atlantic"}},{"k":"fa_regi_atlantic_ocean","v":{"s":"Atlantic Ocean"}},{"k":"fa_regi_british_columbia","v":{"s":"British Columbia"}},{"k":"fa_regi_california","v":{"s":"California"}},{"k":"fa_regi_caribbean","v":{"s":"Caribbean"}},{"k":"fa_regi_central_america","v":{"s":"Central America"}},{"k":"fa_regi_himalaya","v":{"s":"Himalaya"}},{"k":"fa_regi_iceland","v":{"s":"Iceland"}},{"k":"fa_regi_indian_ocean","v":{"s":"Indian Ocean"}},{"k":"fa_regi_indonesia","v":{"s":"Indonesia"}},{"k":"fa_regi_isra