"sr_reveal_about_100_000_sorts","v":{"s":"Reveal: about 100,000 sorts."}},{"k":"sr_alloy_reset_to_historical_gutenberg_era_target","v":{"s":"Alloy reset to historical Gutenberg-era target."}},{"k":"sr_reformation_spread_animation_playing","v":{"s":"Reformation spread animation playing"}},{"k":"sr_analysis_revealed","v":{"s":"Analysis revealed"}},{"k":"sr_new_ai_generated_typeface_sample_loaded","v":{"s":"New AI-generated typeface sample loaded."}},{"k":"sr_new_ai_generated_quote_loaded","v":{"s":"New AI-generated quote loaded."}},{"k":"sr_question_loaded_click_ask_to_submit","v":{"s":"Question loaded. Click Ask to submit."}},{"k":"sr_not_quite_see_the_explanation","v":{"s":"Not quite — see the explanation."}},{"k":"sr_choice_recorded","v":{"s":"Choice recorded."}},{"k":"sr_journey_reset","v":{"s":"Journey reset."}},{"k":"sr_not_quite","v":{"s":"Not quite."}},{"k":"sr_correct","v":{"s":"Correct."}},{"k":"a11y_printingpress_shop_dashboard","v":{"s":"PrintingPress shop dashboard"}},{"k":"back_to_printingpress_menu","v":{"s":"Back to PrintingPress menu"}},{"k":"menu","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"print_this_module_as_a_classroom_hando","v":{"s":"Print this module as a classroom handout"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"right_answer","v":{"s":"Right answer: "}},{"k":"try_again","v":{"s":"Try again"}},{"k":"retry","v":{"s":"Retry"}},{"k":"try_again_2","v":{"s":"↺ Try again"}},{"k":"sources","v":{"s":"📖 Sources"}},{"k":"festina_lente","v":{"s":"festina lente"}},{"k":"this_tool_models_the_press_historicall","v":{"s":"This tool models the press historically and pedagogically. For hands-on letterpress, visit the "}},{"k":"international_printing_museum","v":{"s":"International Printing Museum"}},{"k":"or_a_local_letterpress_studio_letterpr","v":{"s":" or a local letterpress studio. Letterpress is alive in 2026."}},{"k":"continue_with","v":{"s":"↪ Continue with…"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_for_this_module","v":{"s":"Teacher Notes for this module"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher Notes — click to expand"}},{"k":"print_this_module_page","v":{"s":"Print this module page"}},{"k":"print_2","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"aligned_standards","v":{"s":"Aligned standards"}},{"k":"discussion_questions","v":{"s":"Discussion questions"}},{"k":"common_misconceptions","v":{"s":"Common misconceptions"}},{"k":"extension_activity","v":{"s":"Extension activity"}},{"k":"the_press_mechanism","v":{"s":"The Press Mechanism"}},{"k":"pull_the_bar_watch_the_screw_turn_the_","v":{"s":"Pull the bar, watch the screw turn, the platen drop, the type ink the paper. Mechanical advantage, force, and historical accuracy. The wow tile."}},{"k":"set_your_own_type","v":{"s":"Set Your Own Type"}},{"k":"drag_mirror_reversed_letter_blocks_int","v":{"s":"Drag mirror-reversed letter blocks into a composing stick to spell a phrase. Feel why typesetting took hours and why the apprenticeship was years."}},{"k":"casting_type","v":{"s":"Casting Type"}},{"k":"punch_matrix_cast_the_three_step_proce","v":{"s":"Punch → matrix → cast. The three-step process that made identical reusable type possible. Type-metal alloy: lead + tin + antimony, and why each."}},{"k":"print_run_economics","v":{"s":"Print Run Economics"}},{"k":"slider_driven_cost_calculator_hand_cop","v":{"s":"Slider-driven cost calculator: hand-copying a Bible vs printing it. The cost-per-book collapse from 1440 to 1500 to 1600."}},{"k":"before_after","v":{"s":"Before & After"}},{"k":"the_world_the_press_changed_literacy_r","v":{"s":"The world the press changed: literacy rates, Luther's 95 Theses, the scientific revolution, vernacular publishing, newspapers, the modern public."}},{"k":"typography_today","v":{"s":"Typography Today"}},{"k":"why_your_laptop_has_uppercase_and_lowe","v":{"s":"Why your laptop has uppercase and lowercase. Where leading and em-dash come from. The vocabulary of digital type is the vocabulary of a 1450 print shop."}},{"k":"the_people_behind_the_press","v":{"s":"The People Behind the Press"}},{"k":"gutenberg_fust_the_financier_who_sued_","v":{"s":"Gutenberg, Fust the financier who sued him, Schöffer the apprentice who improved it, Aldus Manutius and pocket books, and the women printers history forgot."}},{"k":"build_a_broadside","v":{"s":"Build a Broadside"}},{"k":"compose_a_single_page_printable_a_poem","v":{"s":"Compose a single-page printable: a poem, a manifesto, an announcement. Choose your type, set your layout, print as PDF."}},{"k":"the_same_fears","v":{"s":"The Same Fears"}},{"k":"every_new_information_technology_arriv","v":{"s":"Every new information technology arrives with the same warnings. The 1450 print critics and the 2026 internet/AI critics often share an argument. Some critics were right. Some were wrong. The grown-up question is: how do you tell which is which in your own time?"}},{"k":"a_day_in_the_shop","v":{"s":"A Day in the Shop"}},{"k":"it_is_1455_in_a_mainz_print_shop_choos","v":{"s":"It is 1455 in a Mainz print shop. Choose a role — apprentice, compositor, pressman, or master printer — and walk through four decisions across a working day. Your choices shape who you become. Pure role-play history."}},{"k":"the_globe_goes_pressed","v":{"s":"The Globe Goes Pressed"}},{"k":"gutenberg_was_not_first_korea_cast_mov","v":{"s":"Gutenberg was not first. Korea cast movable metal type in 1377 (Jikji). China had movable clay type by 1040. Japan printed in 770. The world had been printing for 700 years when a German goldsmith perfected the screw press."}},{"k":"paper_making","v":{"s":"Paper Making"}},{"k":"no_press_without_paper_rag_paper_suppl","v":{"s":"No press without paper. Rag paper supply chain from cotton/linen waste → pulp → sheet → press. Why paper price collapse before 1450 made Gutenberg even possible. Interactive sheet-forming sim."}},{"k":"type_foundry_workshop","v":{"s":"Type Foundry Workshop"}},{"k":"design_your_own_typeface_adjust_x_heig","v":{"s":"Design your own typeface. Adjust x-height, weight, contrast, serifs. Watch how each decision affects readability + cost + period style. Goes deeper than the casting module."}},{"k":"bookbinding_lab","v":{"s":"Bookbinding Lab"}},{"k":"printing_made_the_pages_but_a_stack_of","v":{"s":"Printing made the pages — but a stack of paper is not a book. Fold (folio, quarto, octavo), gather, sew, bind. Interactive simulator of the bindery work that followed every print run."}},{"k":"censorship_the_index","v":{"s":"Censorship & The Index"}},{"k":"when_books_spread_fast_authorities_tri","v":{"s":"When books spread fast, authorities tried to control them. Imprimatur (license to print), 1559 Index of Forbidden Books, banned authors, samizdat smuggling. The 1500-year cat-and-mouse between print and power."}},{"k":"the_first_news_wave","v":{"s":"The First News Wave"}},{"k":"newspapers_did_not_exist_for_150_years","v":{"s":"Newspapers did not exist for 150 years after Gutenberg. The first periodicals (1605 Strasbourg, 1622 London) invented how to publish news weekly. Walk through 4 historic front pages and the choices each editor made."}},{"k":"apprentice_s_journey","v":{"s":"Apprentice's Journey"}},{"k":"take_a_7_year_journey_from_14_year_old","v":{"s":"Take a 7-year journey from 14-year-old apprentice to journeyman printer. Choose your master, navigate the workshop hierarchy, accumulate skills, face career-defining decisions. Multi-year role-play sim."}},{"k":"maine_printers_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Maine Printers Deep Dive"}},{"k":"beyond_the_falmouth_gazette_the_printe","v":{"s":"Beyond the Falmouth Gazette. The printers of Portland, Bangor, Augusta, Belfast — newspaper presses, book printers, broadside makers across 250 years of Maine print history. Local connection."}},{"k":"music_printing","v":{"s":"Music Printing"}},{"k":"ottaviano_petrucci_printed_the_first_p","v":{"s":"Ottaviano Petrucci printed the first polyphonic music in 1501 — a feat requiring three separate impressions per page. How sheet music got from medieval manuscript to your music stand."}},{"k":"maps_atlases","v":{"s":"Maps + Atlases"}},{"k":"cartographic_printing_copperplate_engr","v":{"s":"Cartographic printing — copperplate engraving, the 1570 Ortelius atlas (first modern atlas), Mercator projection (1569). How printed maps changed worldview."}},{"k":"children_s_books","v":{"s":"Children's Books"}},{"k":"hornbooks_1500s_reading_primers_chapbo","v":{"s":"Hornbooks (1500s reading primers) → chapbooks (penny stories) → Newbery + Caldecott era. A 500-year history of how children's books became their own form."}},{"k":"scientific_publishing","v":{"s":"Scientific Publishing"}},{"k":"royal_society_philosophical_transactio","v":{"s":"Royal Society Philosophical Transactions (1665) was the first scientific journal. How peer review + preprint servers + open access reshape what counts as scientific knowledge."}},{"k":"letterpress_today","v":{"s":"Letterpress Today"}},{"k":"the_21st_century_letterpress_revival_h","v":{"s":"The 21st-century letterpress revival. Hatch Show Print (Nashville since 1879). Wedding invitations + art prints + craft brewery labels. Letterpress is alive."}},{"k":"press_variants_through_time","v":{"s":"Press Variants Through Time"}},{"k":"gutenberg_s_wooden_screw_press_1450_st","v":{"s":"Gutenberg's wooden screw press (1450) → Stanhope iron press (1800) → Adana tabletop (1922) → Heidelberg Windmill (1923) → digital. Every major press, what it did better."}},{"k":"the_reformation_print_war","v":{"s":"The Reformation Print War"}},{"k":"1517_1555_pamphlet_war_between_luther_","v":{"s":"1517-1555: pamphlet war between Luther + Rome that the Reformers won partly because they printed faster + cheaper. The first information war."}},{"k":"color_printing_history","v":{"s":"Color Printing History"}},{"k":"hand_coloring_1450_chromolithography_1","v":{"s":"Hand-coloring (1450-) → chromolithography (1837-) → photogravure (1880s) → CMYK offset (1950s) → digital RGB. How color got into print."}},{"k":"books_that_changed_the_world","v":{"s":"Books That Changed The World"}},{"k":"15_specific_books_that_altered_history","v":{"s":"15 specific books that altered history: from the Gutenberg Bible to Origin of Species to Silent Spring. Each one's story."}},{"k":"print_revolution","v":{"s":"Print + Revolution"}},{"k":"how_print_made_the_english_civil_war_t","v":{"s":"How print made the English Civil War, the American Revolution, the French Revolution, the 1848 European revolutions. Pamphlets + newspapers as political infrastructure."}},{"k":"the_book_as_object","v":{"s":"The Book As Object"}},{"k":"incunabula_illuminated_manuscripts_fir","v":{"s":"Incunabula, illuminated manuscripts, first editions, rare books. How to read a book as a physical artifact + how to start collecting."}},{"k":"ink_press_materials","v":{"s":"Ink + Press Materials"}},{"k":"lampblack_linseed_oil_varnish_how_prin","v":{"s":"Lampblack + linseed oil + varnish. How printing ink was made for 500 years, why oil-based ink was Gutenberg's breakthrough, and modern formulations."}},{"k":"translation_translators","v":{"s":"Translation + Translators"}},{"k":"tyndale_luther_the_king_james_committe","v":{"s":"Tyndale + Luther + the King James committee + modern Bible translation + literary translation today. How language crosses through print."}},{"k":"print_propaganda","v":{"s":"Print + Propaganda"}},{"k":"wwi_posters_nazi_propaganda_soviet_pos","v":{"s":"WWI posters, Nazi propaganda, Soviet posters, civil rights pamphlets, modern political ads. Print as instrument of persuasion + manipulation."}},{"k":"comics_graphic_novels","v":{"s":"Comics + Graphic Novels"}},{"k":"yellow_kid_1896_comic_strips_superhero","v":{"s":"Yellow Kid (1896) → comic strips → superhero comics → underground comix → graphic novels. The text-image hybrid that became its own form."}},{"k":"the_future_of_print","v":{"s":"The Future of Print"}},{"k":"ai_generated_print_3d_printing_e_ink_e","v":{"s":"AI-generated print, 3D printing, e-ink + e-paper, print-on-demand, programmable matter. What might \"printing\" mean in 50 years."}},{"k":"famous_print_shops_tour","v":{"s":"Famous Print Shops Tour"}},{"k":"12_historic_print_shops_in_detail_gute","v":{"s":"12 historic print shops in detail: Gutenberg's Mainz workshop, the Aldine Press, Plantin Antwerp, Estienne Paris, Imprimerie Royale, Kelmscott, more. Each with founding date, output, surviving evidence."}},{"k":"print_religion_beyond_bibles","v":{"s":"Print + Religion Beyond Bibles"}},{"k":"hymnals_devotional_tracts_theological_","v":{"s":"Hymnals, devotional tracts, theological treatises, Catechisms, Sunday-school papers. The Bible was only part of religious print — denominational publishing built modern religious infrastructure."}},{"k":"print_education","v":{"s":"Print + Education"}},{"k":"mcguffey_readers_university_press_hist","v":{"s":"McGuffey Readers, university press history, K-12 textbook market, modern open educational resources. How books built modern schooling."}},{"k":"print_money_security_printing","v":{"s":"Print + Money (Security Printing)"}},{"k":"banknotes_government_bonds_postage_sta","v":{"s":"Banknotes, government bonds, postage stamps, passport printing. The high-stakes specialty of security printing — anti-counterfeiting features + their long arms race."}},{"k":"the_library_through_history","v":{"s":"The Library Through History"}},{"k":"alexandria_monastery_libraries_univers","v":{"s":"Alexandria → monastery libraries → universities → Carnegie public libraries → digital archives. The institutional partner of print."}},{"k":"the_modern_magazine","v":{"s":"The Modern Magazine"}},{"k":"saturday_evening_post_new_yorker_vogue","v":{"s":"Saturday Evening Post → New Yorker → Vogue → Wired → digital. The magazine as cultural institution."}},{"k":"encyclopedias","v":{"s":"Encyclopedias"}},{"k":"pliny_s_natural_history_diderot_britan","v":{"s":"Pliny's Natural History → Diderot → Britannica → World Book → Wikipedia. The 2000-year project of compiling all knowledge."}},{"k":"dictionaries","v":{"s":"Dictionaries"}},{"k":"johnson_s_1755_dictionary_webster_oed_","v":{"s":"Johnson's 1755 dictionary → Webster → OED → Merriam-Webster online. How language gets standardized into print."}},{"k":"cookbooks","v":{"s":"Cookbooks"}},{"k":"apicius_ancient_rome_mrs_beeton_1861_j","v":{"s":"Apicius (ancient Rome) → Mrs. Beeton (1861) → Julia Child → modern celebrity chef cookbooks. Food + print history."}},{"k":"self_help_how_to","v":{"s":"Self-Help + How-To"}},{"k":"how_to_self_help_a_200_year_publishing","v":{"s":"How-to + self-help: a 200-year publishing tradition. Almanacs → home-improvement → diet books → modern self-help bestsellers."}},{"k":"paper_sustainability","v":{"s":"Paper Sustainability"}},{"k":"forest_management_recycled_paper_the_e","v":{"s":"Forest management, recycled paper, the environmental impact of print. Why Maine's paper industry matters + where it's going."}},{"k":"the_newspaper_comic_strip","v":{"s":"The Newspaper Comic Strip"}},{"k":"peanuts_garfield_calvin_hobbes_modern_","v":{"s":"Peanuts → Garfield → Calvin & Hobbes → modern. The shrinking-but-enduring newspaper comic strip."}},{"k":"print_war","v":{"s":"Print + War"}},{"k":"war_correspondents_war_posters_soldier","v":{"s":"War correspondents, war posters, soldier newspapers, war photography. Print at the front + the home front."}},{"k":"famous_bookbinders_their_work","v":{"s":"Famous Bookbinders + Their Work"}},{"k":"le_gascon_padeloup_cobden_sanderson_sa","v":{"s":"Le Gascon, Padeloup, Cobden-Sanderson, Sangorski + Sutcliffe, Philip Smith. The art-binding tradition."}},{"k":"maine_authors_their_publishers","v":{"s":"Maine Authors + Their Publishers"}},{"k":"hawthorne_longfellow_stowe_millay_king","v":{"s":"Hawthorne, Longfellow, Stowe, Millay, King. The Maine literary tradition + how it has been published."}},{"k":"print_tourism","v":{"s":"Print + Tourism"}},{"k":"guidebooks_baedeker_murray_lonely_plan","v":{"s":"Guidebooks (Baedeker, Murray, Lonely Planet), brochures, postcards. The travel-information industry."}},{"k":"print_healthcare","v":{"s":"Print + Healthcare"}},{"k":"medical_reference_patient_education_ph","v":{"s":"Medical reference, patient education, pharmaceutical inserts. The specialized world of medical publishing."}},{"k":"font_identification_quiz","v":{"s":"Font Identification Quiz"}},{"k":"look_at_type_samples_identify_the_type","v":{"s":"Look at type samples. Identify the typeface. Build typography vision through 12 progressively harder rounds."}},{"k":"postage_direct_mail","v":{"s":"Postage + Direct Mail"}},{"k":"postage_stamps_direct_mail_advertising","v":{"s":"Postage stamps, direct-mail advertising, junk mail. The 19th-c. shift that made print + the mail interlock."}},{"k":"print_sports","v":{"s":"Print + Sports"}},{"k":"sports_journalism_baseball_cards_progr","v":{"s":"Sports journalism, baseball cards, programs, sports magazines, fan publications. Print + sports culture."}},{"k":"print_architecture","v":{"s":"Print + Architecture"}},{"k":"architectural_plans_blueprints_monogra","v":{"s":"Architectural plans, blueprints, monographs, design magazines. How architects communicate visually."}},{"k":"print_law","v":{"s":"Print + Law"}},{"k":"legal_codes_court_reports_contracts_st","v":{"s":"Legal codes, court reports, contracts, statutes. The specialized print of the legal profession."}},{"k":"romance_novels","v":{"s":"Romance Novels"}},{"k":"harlequin_mills_boon_modern_romance_bi","v":{"s":"Harlequin, Mills + Boon, modern romance. Biggest US fiction category by units; understudied."}},{"k":"sci_fi_fantasy","v":{"s":"Sci-Fi + Fantasy"}},{"k":"pulp_era_tolkien_modern_hugo_nebula_aw","v":{"s":"Pulp era → Tolkien → modern. Hugo + Nebula awards. The genre history."}},{"k":"mystery_crime_fiction","v":{"s":"Mystery + Crime Fiction"}},{"k":"poe_conan_doyle_christie_hammett_moder","v":{"s":"Poe + Conan Doyle → Christie + Hammett → modern. The 180-year crime-fiction tradition."}},{"k":"manga_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Manga Deep Dive"}},{"k":"japanese_manga_history_formats_major_t","v":{"s":"Japanese manga: history, formats, major titles, global influence, Maine connections."}},{"k":"photography_books","v":{"s":"Photography Books"}},{"k":"photo_books_as_art_form_robert_frank_a","v":{"s":"Photo-books as art form. Robert Frank, Annie Leibovitz, Sebastião Salgado. The photo-book renaissance."}},{"k":"zines_fanzines","v":{"s":"Zines + Fanzines"}},{"k":"sci_fi_fanzines_punk_zines_modern_diy_","v":{"s":"Sci-fi fanzines → punk zines → modern. DIY publishing tradition + the riot grrrl + early-90s zine boom."}},{"k":"famous_forgeries_fakes","v":{"s":"Famous Forgeries + Fakes"}},{"k":"vinland_map_mark_hofmann_hitler_diarie","v":{"s":"Vinland Map, Mark Hofmann, Hitler Diaries, Konrad Kujau. The history of book + manuscript forgery + how forgers got caught."}},{"k":"manuscripts_calligraphy","v":{"s":"Manuscripts + Calligraphy"}},{"k":"pre_print_writing_tradition_scriptoria","v":{"s":"Pre-print writing tradition. Scriptoria, illuminated manuscripts, scribal arts. The Lindisfarne Gospels + Book of Kells."}},{"k":"bookplates_marginalia","v":{"s":"Bookplates + Marginalia"}},{"k":"ex_libris_bookplates_owners_inscriptio","v":{"s":"Ex libris bookplates, owners' inscriptions, marginalia. The personal histories preserved in books."}},{"k":"the_used_book_trade","v":{"s":"The Used Book Trade"}},{"k":"powell_s_strand_secondhand_bookstores_","v":{"s":"Powell's, Strand, secondhand bookstores. The economy of pre-owned books. Maine used bookstores."}},{"k":"the_library_card_catalog","v":{"s":"The Library Card Catalog"}},{"k":"the_100_year_reference_system_that_die","v":{"s":"The 100+ year reference system that died. Dewey + Library of Congress classification. Why catalog cards mattered."}},{"k":"diaries_journals","v":{"s":"Diaries + Journals"}},{"k":"pepys_anne_frank_modern_bullet_journal","v":{"s":"Pepys, Anne Frank, modern bullet journals. The 400-year diary tradition + its modern descendants."}},{"k":"tarot_oracle_decks","v":{"s":"Tarot + Oracle Decks"}},{"k":"tarot_cards_as_printed_objects_viscont","v":{"s":"Tarot cards as printed objects — Visconti decks → Rider-Waite (1909) → modern indie decks. Major printed-card subculture."}},{"k":"pulp_fiction_era","v":{"s":"Pulp Fiction Era"}},{"k":"black_mask_astounding_weird_tales_the_","v":{"s":"Black Mask, Astounding, Weird Tales. The 1920s-50s magazines that birthed sci-fi + hardboiled + many other genres."}},{"k":"pamphlets_tracts","v":{"s":"Pamphlets + Tracts"}},{"k":"political_religious_pamphlet_tradition","v":{"s":"Political + religious pamphlet tradition. Reformation + American Revolution + abolitionist + civil rights pamphlets."}},{"k":"greeting_cards_ephemera","v":{"s":"Greeting Cards + Ephemera"}},{"k":"hallmark_american_greetings_papyrus_th","v":{"s":"Hallmark, American Greetings, Papyrus. The greeting card industry + the ephemera-collecting tradition."}},{"k":"the_memoir_tradition","v":{"s":"The Memoir Tradition"}},{"k":"augustine_s_confessions_to_modern_cele","v":{"s":"Augustine's Confessions to modern celebrity memoir. The autobiography + memoir publishing tradition."}},{"k":"print_sound","v":{"s":"Print + Sound"}},{"k":"record_liner_notes_audiobook_industry_","v":{"s":"Record liner notes, audiobook industry, podcast transcripts. Where print + audio intersect."}},{"k":"the_printed_sermon","v":{"s":"The Printed Sermon"}},{"k":"spurgeon_edwards_mlk_the_printed_sermo","v":{"s":"Spurgeon, Edwards, MLK. The printed-sermon tradition — preaching that traveled beyond the church."}},{"k":"spelling_word_puzzles","v":{"s":"Spelling + Word Puzzles"}},{"k":"spelling_bees_crosswords_nyt_since_194","v":{"s":"Spelling bees, crosswords (NYT since 1942), Wordle (2021+). The puzzle + word-game tradition."}},{"k":"print_cinema","v":{"s":"Print + Cinema"}},{"k":"screenplays_film_novelizations_fan_mag","v":{"s":"Screenplays, film novelizations, fan magazines, movie posters. Where print + film meet."}},{"k":"print_astronomy","v":{"s":"Print + Astronomy"}},{"k":"star_catalogs_observatory_bulletins_po","v":{"s":"Star catalogs, observatory bulletins, popular astronomy. From Hipparchus to Sky & Telescope."}},{"k":"beauty_fashion_magazines","v":{"s":"Beauty + Fashion Magazines"}},{"k":"harper_s_bazaar_1867_vogue_1892_elle_c","v":{"s":"Harper's Bazaar (1867), Vogue (1892), Elle, Cosmopolitan. The fashion-magazine universe."}},{"k":"travel_literature","v":{"s":"Travel Literature"}},{"k":"beyond_guidebooks_bryson_iyer_theroux_","v":{"s":"Beyond guidebooks — Bryson, Iyer, Theroux, Lonely Planet travel-literature. The literary-travel tradition."}},{"k":"print_mathematics","v":{"s":"Print + Mathematics"}},{"k":"euclid_s_elements_300_bce_modern_math_","v":{"s":"Euclid's Elements (~300 BCE) → modern math journals. The publishing infrastructure of mathematics."}},{"k":"print_advertising_history","v":{"s":"Print Advertising History"}},{"k":"mad_men_the_print_advertising_traditio","v":{"s":"Mad Men + the print advertising tradition. From 1700s shop signs to 21st-c. magazine ads."}},{"k":"webcomics_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Webcomics Deep Dive"}},{"k":"xkcd_penny_arcade_achewood_order_of_th","v":{"s":"xkcd, Penny Arcade, Achewood, Order of the Stick, modern webcomics. The post-newspaper era of comics."}},{"k":"the_picture_book_craft","v":{"s":"The Picture Book Craft"}},{"k":"how_picture_books_are_actually_made_pa","v":{"s":"How picture books are actually made. Page-by-page design, dummies, art process. The craft."}},{"k":"poetry_magazines","v":{"s":"Poetry Magazines"}},{"k":"poetry_chicago_1912_the_new_yorker_par","v":{"s":"Poetry (Chicago, 1912+), The New Yorker, Paris Review. Where poems get published."}},{"k":"pulp_cover_art","v":{"s":"Pulp Cover Art"}},{"k":"the_bold_lurid_covers_that_defined_pul","v":{"s":"The bold lurid covers that defined pulp era. Frank R. Paul, Margaret Brundage, Virgil Finlay. A serious illustration tradition."}},{"k":"print_radio","v":{"s":"Print + Radio"}},{"k":"radio_scripts_radio_program_guides_the","v":{"s":"Radio scripts, radio program guides, the radio-magazine industry, BBC + NPR print presence."}},{"k":"print_gardening","v":{"s":"Print + Gardening"}},{"k":"seed_catalogs_burpee_park_gardening_ma","v":{"s":"Seed catalogs (Burpee + Park), gardening manuals, gardening magazines. A long-running niche publishing category."}},{"k":"forensic_document_examination","v":{"s":"Forensic Document Examination"}},{"k":"handwriting_analysis_document_dating_f","v":{"s":"Handwriting analysis, document dating, forgery detection. The specialty that catches forgers + supports investigations."}},{"k":"the_library_bindery","v":{"s":"The Library Bindery"}},{"k":"how_libraries_rebind_books_for_circula","v":{"s":"How libraries rebind books for circulation. Library binding standards, conservation, rebinding economics."}},{"k":"the_fine_press_tradition","v":{"s":"The Fine Press Tradition"}},{"k":"kelmscott_doves_officina_bodoni_modern","v":{"s":"Kelmscott → Doves → Officina Bodoni → modern. Limited-edition handpress book art across 130 years."}},{"k":"cookbook_subcultures","v":{"s":"Cookbook Subcultures"}},{"k":"community_cookbooks_vegan_cookbooks_bb","v":{"s":"Community cookbooks, vegan cookbooks, BBQ books, regional cuisines. Beyond the celebrity cookbook mainstream."}},{"k":"print_natural_history","v":{"s":"Print + Natural History"}},{"k":"audubon_to_modern_field_guides_the_ill","v":{"s":"Audubon to modern field guides. The illustrated natural-history tradition. Peterson Field Guides, Sibley, Kaufman."}},{"k":"about_this_tool","v":{"s":"About This Tool"}},{"k":"behind_the_scenes_how_this_tool_grew_f","v":{"s":"Behind the scenes: how this tool grew from 13 modules + 9,000 lines to 80+ modules + 20,000+ lines. Credits + history."}},{"k":"print_glossary","v":{"s":"Print Glossary"}},{"k":"120_printing_typography_book_terms_def","v":{"s":"120+ printing + typography + book terms defined. Quick reference for the vocabulary used throughout the tool."}},{"k":"cumulative_quiz","v":{"s":"Cumulative Quiz"}},{"k":"15_questions_across_all_10_modules_mis","v":{"s":"15 questions across all 10 modules. Missed answers link you back to the module you need to review."}},{"k":"ask_the_printer_ai","v":{"s":"Ask the Printer (AI)"}},{"k":"ask_any_printing_press_question_the_ai","v":{"s":"Ask any printing-press question; the AI returns a sourced answer. Educational only."}},{"k":"resources","v":{"s":"Resources"}},{"k":"every_org_cited_in_this_tool_plus_muse","v":{"s":"Every org cited in this tool, plus museum and primary-source links."}},{"k":"start_here","v":{"s":"Start here"}},{"k":"modules","v":{"s":"Modules"}},{"k":"practice_and_ask","v":{"s":"Practice and ask"}},{"k":"resources_2","v":{"s":"Resources"}},{"k":"you_toured_the_press","v":{"s":"You toured the press"}},{"k":"every_module_visited_take_the_cumulati","v":{"s":"Every module visited. Take the cumulative quiz to earn the Master Printer badge, or revisit any module that piqued your curiosity."}},{"k":"your_tier","v":{"s":"Your tier"}},{"k":"try_next","v":{"s":"Try next: "}},{"k":"open","v":{"s":"Open →"}},{"k":"printingpress","v":{"s":"📜 PrintingPress"}},{"k":"the_gutenberg_press_changed_who_could_","v":{"s":"The Gutenberg press changed who could read, what could be known, and how fast an idea could travel. Pull the bar. Set the type. Then see the world it built."}},{"k":"interdisciplinary_engineering_material","v":{"s":"Interdisciplinary: engineering, materials science, history, economics, typography, civics. Sources cited inline."}},{"k":"interactive_simulations","v":{"s":" interactive simulations"}},{"k":"cumulative_quiz_items","v":{"s":" cumulative-quiz items"}},{"k":"1450_screw_press","v":{"s":"~1450 screw press"}},{"k":"badges_earned","v":{"s":"🏅 Badges earned"}},{"k":"teacher_quick_start_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🎓 Teacher quick-start (click to expand)"}},{"k":"three_pre_built_sequences_depending_on","v":{"s":"Three pre-built sequences depending on how much class time you have. None require setup; everything in PrintingPress runs in the browser."}},{"k":"crew_warm_up","v":{"s":"Crew warm-up"}},{"k":"single_class_period","v":{"s":"Single class period"}},{"k":"full_unit","v":{"s":"Full unit"}},{"k":"standards_anchors","v":{"s":"Standards anchors"}},{"k":"ccss_ela_rh_6_8_2","v":{"s":"CCSS-ELA RH.6-8.2:"}},{"k":"determine_the_central_ideas_of_a_prima","v":{"s":" Determine the central ideas of a primary source (use the Reformation theses + Falmouth Gazette as period sources)."}},{"k":"ccss_ela_rh_6_8_9","v":{"s":"CCSS-ELA RH.6-8.9:"}},{"k":"analyze_the_relationship_between_a_pri","v":{"s":" Analyze the relationship between a primary source and the secondary account (compare 1450 critic quotes to modern echoes in The Same Fears)."}},{"k":"maine_social_studies_ss_m_4","v":{"s":"Maine Social Studies SS.M.4:"}},{"k":"trace_the_development_of_a_maine_insti","v":{"s":" Trace the development of a Maine institution; Falmouth Gazette (1785) traces to the modern Portland Press Herald, a 240-year chain."}},{"k":"ngss_ms_ets1_2","v":{"s":"NGSS MS-ETS1-2:"}},{"k":"evaluate_competing_design_solutions_us","v":{"s":" Evaluate competing design solutions using systematic criteria (alloy designer in Casting Type)."}},{"k":"ccss_math_7_rp_8_f","v":{"s":"CCSS-Math 7.RP, 8.F:"}},{"k":"proportional_reasoning_and_functions_p","v":{"s":" Proportional reasoning and functions (pamphlet reach calculator + mechanical-advantage screw math)."}},{"k":"map_to_your_subject","v":{"s":"Map to your subject"}},{"k":"built_by_a_portland_school_psychologis","v":{"s":"Built by a Portland school psychologist for King Middle, EL Education aligned. Every module has a Teacher Notes block at the bottom with discussion prompts and \"what students often ask.\" No student data is collected; everything runs locally in the browser."}},{"k":"what_should_the_press_print","v":{"s":"🪶 What should the press print?"}},{"k":"up_to_14_characters","v":{"s":"Up to 14 characters"}},{"k":"fiat_lux","v":{"s":"↺ FIAT LUX"}},{"k":"your_phrase_appears_mirror_reversed_on","v":{"s":"Your phrase appears mirror-reversed on the type forme and reads correctly on the paper after pressing. "}},{"k":"try_your_name_the_date_or_any_short_ph","v":{"s":"Try your name, the date, or any short phrase."}},{"k":"crossbeam","v":{"s":"Crossbeam"}},{"k":"frame","v":{"s":"Frame"}},{"k":"screw","v":{"s":"Screw"}},{"k":"bar_lever","v":{"s":"Bar (lever)"}},{"k":"platen","v":{"s":"Platen"}},{"k":"type_forme","v":{"s":"Type forme"}},{"k":"ink_balls","v":{"s":"Ink balls"}},{"k":"start_a_hands_free_guided_tour_through","v":{"s":"Start a hands-free guided tour through the full press cycle"}},{"k":"guided_tour","v":{"s":"▶ Guided Tour"}},{"k":"stop_tour","v":{"s":"⏸ Stop tour"}},{"k":"reset_run","v":{"s":"↺ Reset run"}},{"k":"a_1450_print_shop_has_a_screw_press_wi","v":{"s":"A 1450 print shop has a screw press with a 60 cm bar and 5 mm screw pitch. The journeyman pulls with about 15 kg of force. Approximately how much force lands on the platen?"}},{"k":"a_printer_pulls_the_bar_but_the_print_","v":{"s":"A printer pulls the bar but the print comes out faint and patchy on the right side. What is the most likely cause?"}},{"k":"a_print_shop_was_a_workshop_workshops_","v":{"s":"A print shop was a workshop. Workshops have hazards. The romance of the press has tended to obscure the labor reality, but the historical record is clear: every role had specific occupational risks that printers wrote about, joked about, and sometimes died from."}},{"k":"type_alloy_is_mostly_lead_compositors_","v":{"s":"Type alloy is mostly lead. Compositors picked up sorts thousands of times a day; casters worked over molten metal. Chronic low-dose lead exposure produced the \"printer's palsy\" that 19th-century occupational physicians documented. Modern letterpress shops use polymer plates or photopolymer instead."}},{"k":"boiling_linseed_oil_to_make_varnish_wa","v":{"s":"Boiling linseed oil to make varnish was the most dangerous step. Hot oil can ignite; once burning, it is hard to extinguish. Shops cooked ink outdoors when possible. Several documented 1500s shop fires started this way."}},{"k":"pulling_the_bar_250_times_a_day_every_","v":{"s":"Pulling the bar 250 times a day, every working day, year after year, wore out shoulders and elbows. By age 50 many pressmen had lost the strength for full-day work. The Linotype operator inherited the same kind of injury in a different shape (keyboard) 400 years later."}},{"k":"reading_mirror_reversed_type_by_candle","v":{"s":"Reading mirror-reversed type by candle or oil-lamp light, for 10+ hour days, ruined eyes. Reading glasses became common among compositors a century before they were common in the general population."}},{"k":"cleaning_solvents_turpentine_lye_solut","v":{"s":"Cleaning solvents (turpentine, lye solutions) for cleaning type and inking balls produced respiratory and skin issues. Apprentices got the worst of this because cleaning was usually their job."}},{"k":"paper_bales_lead_pigs_raw_type_metal_f","v":{"s":"Paper bales, lead pigs (raw type metal), finished books, locked formes all weighed substantial amounts. Back injuries were common. The apprentice carried everything."}},{"k":"modern_occupational_safety_scholarship","v":{"s":"Modern occupational-safety scholarship credits printers as one of the first trades to systematically document workplace hazards. The 19th-century reformers who pushed for child-labor laws, ventilation standards, and shop inspections often started by looking at print shops because the hazards were so visible."}},{"k":"manuscript_ink_was_water_based_and_bea","v":{"s":"Manuscript ink was water-based and beaded off metal type. Gutenberg adapted oil-painting varnish into a printer’s ink that clung to the type face, transferred cleanly to paper, and dried without bleeding. The recipe is short. Getting it right took years."}},{"k":"lampblack","v":{"s":"Lampblack"}},{"k":"soot_scraped_from_the_inside_of_an_oil","v":{"s":"Soot scraped from the inside of an oil lamp. Almost pure carbon. The black you see on a 1455 Bible page is lampblack."}},{"k":"linseed_oil_boiled_to_varnish","v":{"s":"Linseed oil (boiled to varnish)"}},{"k":"linseed_boiled_slowly_for_hours_become","v":{"s":"Linseed boiled slowly for hours becomes a sticky varnish. This is the breakthrough. It holds the pigment, clings to metal, and oxidizes to dry on paper instead of soaking through."}},{"k":"walnut_oil_rosin_a_touch_of_turpentine","v":{"s":"Walnut oil / rosin / a touch of turpentine"}},{"k":"tunes_the_drying_rate_and_consistency_","v":{"s":"Tunes the drying rate and consistency for the season. Hot summer ink is mixed differently from cold winter ink. This is artisan judgment, not a fixed formula."}},{"k":"the_process","v":{"s":"The process"}},{"k":"a_1450_print_shop_kept_its_ink_recipe_","v":{"s":"A 1450 print shop kept its ink recipe close. Some shops were known for blacker blacks; others for faster drying. The recipe survived in apprentice notebooks, not published manuals. Most modern letterpress ink is still oil-based, with synthetic pigments swapped in for lampblack."}},{"k":"gutenberg_museum_mainz","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Museum (Mainz)"}},{"k":"british_library_gutenberg_bible","v":{"s":"British Library — Gutenberg Bible"}},{"k":"international_printing_museum_carson_c","v":{"s":"International Printing Museum (Carson, CA) — Working Gutenberg replica"}},{"k":"smithsonian_graphic_arts_collection","v":{"s":"Smithsonian — Graphic Arts Collection"}},{"k":"adrian_johns_the_nature_of_the_book_pr","v":{"s":"Adrian Johns, \"The Nature of the Book: Print and Knowledge in the Making\" (1998)"}},{"k":"phrase_1_in_print","v":{"s":"Phrase 1: IN PRINT"}},{"k":"pick_mirror_reversed_letters_from_the_","v":{"s":"Pick mirror-reversed letters from the case and drop them in the composing stick — right-to-left, just as a 1450 compositor would."}},{"k":"phrase_2_first_proof_with_fi_ligature","v":{"s":"Phrase 2: FIRST PROOF (with fi ligature)"}},{"k":"this_challenge_includes_a_fi_ligature_","v":{"s":"This challenge includes a \"fi\" ligature — a single sort that prints both letters joined. Look for the special joined sort in the case."}},{"k":"phrase_3_falmouth_1785_maine","v":{"s":"Phrase 3: FALMOUTH (1785 Maine)"}},{"k":"compose_falmouth_the_way_thomas_b_wait","v":{"s":"Compose FALMOUTH the way Thomas B. Wait and Benjamin Titcomb would have set it for the first issue of the Falmouth Gazette in January 1785 — the first newspaper printed in what would become Maine. (Falmouth is now Portland, walking distance from King Middle School.)"}},{"k":"phrase_4_common_sense_1776_paine","v":{"s":"Phrase 4: COMMON SENSE (1776 Paine)"}},{"k":"compose_the_title_of_the_pamphlet_that","v":{"s":"Compose the title of the pamphlet that sold about 150,000 copies in colonial America in 1776 — the highest-leverage broadside in American history. Thomas Paine wrote it in three months; printers across the colonies set it in days. Per capita, one of the best-selling political works ever published."}},{"k":"phrase_5_ad_fontes_renaissance_motto","v":{"s":"Phrase 5: AD FONTES (Renaissance motto)"}},{"k":"compose_ad_fontes_latin_for_to_the_sou","v":{"s":"Compose AD FONTES — Latin for \"to the sources\" — the Renaissance scholar's rallying cry. When Erasmus and his fellow humanists pushed printers to publish ancient Greek and Hebrew texts in their original languages, this was the call. Movable type made it possible: scholars could finally lay ten copies of the same passage side by side and catch errors that a thousand years of hand-copying had introduced."}},{"k":"a_letter_is_missing_the_compositor_dro","v":{"s":"A letter is missing. The compositor dropped the second E in FOREVER. A 1450 corrector would mark a caret (∧) where the letter goes and write \"e\" in the margin."}},{"k":"letters_out_of_order_the_became_teh_be","v":{"s":"Letters out of order. THE became TEH because the compositor reached for sorts in the wrong order. The mark is a tilde-like swoop over the swapped letters, sometimes called a transposition mark."}},{"k":"a_duplicated_word_the_compositor_set_t","v":{"s":"A duplicated word. The compositor set the last sort twice. The corrector would draw a deletion mark through the second TYPE and write \"dele\" in the margin (short for delete)."}},{"k":"letters_out_of_order_people_became_peo","v":{"s":"Letters out of order. PEOPLE became PEOPEL. A corrector would mark the swapped L and E with a transposition swoop, the same mark as in TEH above."}},{"k":"a_duplicated_word_ten_was_set_twice_th","v":{"s":"A duplicated word. TEN was set twice. The corrector would strike through the second TEN with a deletion mark, the same as the duplicated TYPE above."}},{"k":"try_it_read_the_type","v":{"s":"🪞 Try it: read the type"}},{"k":"below_is_a_word_of_type_mirror_reverse","v":{"s":"Below is a word of type, mirror-reversed (as it would sit in the composing stick). What would it print as on the paper? Type the readable version."}},{"k":"type_the_readable_word","v":{"s":"Type the readable word…"}},{"k":"check","v":{"s":"Check"}},{"k":"new_word","v":{"s":"↻ New word"}},{"k":"generate_a_new_ai_word","v":{"s":"Generate a new AI word"}},{"k":"ai_generated_word","v":{"s":"🤖 AI-generated word"}},{"k":"proofreader_s_eye","v":{"s":"🔍 Proofreader’s eye"}},{"k":"ai_generated_round","v":{"s":"🤖 AI-generated round"}},{"k":"a_corrector_in_1450_caught_every_types","v":{"s":"A corrector in 1450 caught every typesetting error before the run. One word in this printed line is wrong. Click it."}},{"k":"what_it_should_say","v":{"s":"What it should say: "}},{"k":"corrector_s_margin_mark","v":{"s":"Corrector’s margin mark: "}},{"k":"new_proof","v":{"s":"↻ New proof"}},{"k":"try_this_one_again","v":{"s":"Try this one again"}},{"k":"even_the_most_careful_1450_1900_shops_","v":{"s":"Even the most careful 1450-1900 shops shipped errors. Some of them changed history; some of them got shops fined or shut down. The lesson is not \"correctors were lazy.\" It is that a fast-moving system needs institutional quality control beyond any single human eye."}},{"k":"the_wicked_bible","v":{"s":"The \"Wicked Bible\""}},{"k":"the_murderer_s_bible","v":{"s":"The \"Murderer's Bible\""}},{"k":"the_sheppard_s_bible","v":{"s":"The Sheppard's Bible"}},{"k":"the_bay_psalm_book","v":{"s":"The Bay Psalm Book"}},{"k":"the_error","v":{"s":"The error"}},{"k":"what_happened","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"the_shops_that_survived_these_embarras","v":{"s":"The shops that survived these embarrassments built bigger correction systems: dedicated correctors instead of shared ones, multiple proof passes, errata sheets distributed after the fact, eventually printed lists of corrections at the end of each book. Modern publishing (and modern software releases) inherited every one of these institutional fixes."}},{"k":"rst_proof","v":{"s":"RST PROOF"}},{"k":"the_fi_in_red_is_one_ligature_sort_a_s","v":{"s":"The \"fi\" in red is one ligature sort — a single piece of type with both letters joined."}},{"k":"your_pace","v":{"s":"Your pace, "}},{"k":"characters_per_hour","v":{"s":" characters per hour."}},{"k":"1_000_c_h_1450_compositor","v":{"s":"↑ 1,000 c/h (1450 compositor)"}},{"k":"12_000_c_h_modern_typist","v":{"s":"12,000 c/h (modern typist) ↑"}},{"k":"click_a_sort_below_to_pick_it_up_then_","v":{"s":"Click a sort below to pick it up, then click a slot to drop it. Click a filled slot to remove it."}},{"k":"in_a_real_1450_case","v":{"s":"In a real 1450 case: "}},{"k":"every_letter_had_a_fixed_compartment_v","v":{"s":"every letter had a fixed compartment. Vowels (a, e, i, o, u) and common consonants (n, r, s, t) sat near the center for fastest reach. Rare letters (q, x, z) lived at the edges. A skilled compositor knew the case by feel — picking sorts without looking."}},{"k":"why_some_compartments_were_bigger_lett","v":{"s":"📊 Why some compartments were bigger (letter frequency)"}},{"k":"set_now_you_understand_why_apprentices","v":{"s":"Set! Now you understand why apprenticeships were 7 years."}},{"k":"a_skilled_compositor_in_1450_could_set","v":{"s":"A skilled compositor in 1450 could set ~1,000 characters per hour, mirror-reversed, from memory of where every letter in the case sat. Modern keyboard typists do ~12,000 characters per hour. The compositor was a craft profession that required years of training, and a press with a single bad compositor produced unreadable books."}},{"k":"set_with_the_fi_ligature_in_place","v":{"s":"Set — with the fi-ligature in place."}},{"k":"a_real_1450_case_held_about_150_differ","v":{"s":"A real 1450 case held about 150 different sorts, not just 26 letters. Dozens of ligatures (fi, fl, ff, ffi, ffl, ct, st) lived alongside the letters, plus accented letters, fractions, and punctuation. A compositor who reached for the wrong drawer cost the shop a whole proof pull."}},{"k":"set_the_way_wait_and_titcomb_set_it_in","v":{"s":"Set the way Wait and Titcomb set it in January 1785."}},{"k":"the_first_issue_of_the_falmouth_gazett","v":{"s":"The first issue of the Falmouth Gazette ran four pages and sold for sixpence. Within fifteen years there were nine newspapers in what would become Maine. The press in Falmouth (now Portland) was a few hundred yards from the wharves where the paper, type, and ink arrived from Boston by sloop."}},{"k":"set_the_highest_leverage_pamphlet_in_c","v":{"s":"Set the highest-leverage pamphlet in colonial America."}},{"k":"common_sense_was_reprinted_by_dozens_o","v":{"s":"Common Sense was reprinted by dozens of printers across the thirteen colonies in 1776. Total run is estimated at 150,000 copies in a population of about 2.5 million. Per capita, that is the equivalent of around 20 million copies in the United States today, and it was set by hand in shops the size of a large bedroom."}},{"k":"bene_factum_erasmus_would_have_said_it","v":{"s":"BENE FACTUM. Erasmus would have said it that way."}},{"k":"ad_fontes_back_to_the_sources_was_the_","v":{"s":"AD FONTES, \"back to the sources,\" was the rallying cry that drove a huge share of the printed output of the 1500s. Scholars wanted Greek New Testaments, Hebrew Old Testaments, and classical Latin from before a thousand years of hand-copying had introduced errors. Print made comparison possible: ten copies of the same passage on ten desks at once."}},{"k":"type_forme_mirrored","v":{"s":"Type forme (mirrored)"}},{"k":"paper_readable","v":{"s":"Paper (readable)"}},{"k":"press","v":{"s":"🪢 Press!"}},{"k":"press_again","v":{"s":"↺ Press again"}},{"k":"compose_new","v":{"s":"🆕 Compose new"}},{"k":"imposition_puzzle_folio_4_pages","v":{"s":"📑 Imposition puzzle (folio, 4 pages)"}},{"k":"a_folio_is_one_sheet_of_paper_folded_o","v":{"s":"A \"folio\" is one sheet of paper folded once — 4 pages total. To make the folded book read in order (1, 2, 3, 4), the pages need to be on the sheet in specific positions. Pick a page number from the palette and click an empty slot. When you think you have it, check the fold."}},{"k":"pages","v":{"s":"Pages"}},{"k":"all_placed_hit_check_the_fold","v":{"s":"All placed. Hit “Check the fold.”"}},{"k":"outer_side_of_sheet","v":{"s":"Outer side of sheet"}},{"k":"inner_side_of_sheet","v":{"s":"Inner side of sheet"}},{"k":"check_the_fold","v":{"s":"📖 Check the fold"}},{"k":"clear","v":{"s":"↻ Clear"}},{"k":"why_this_works_when_you_fold_the_sheet","v":{"s":"Why this works: when you fold the sheet in half, the outer-right page (1) lands on top of the outer-left page (4). Page 1 is the cover; page 4 is the back. Opening the folded sheet reveals the inner side, with page 2 facing page 3. Read the book — 1, 2, 3, 4."}},{"k":"fold_it","v":{"s":"📖 Fold it"}},{"k":"open_it_back_up","v":{"s":"↩ Open it back up"}},{"k":"try_again_3","v":{"s":"↻ Try again"}},{"k":"bigger_formats_quarto_8_pages_per_shee","v":{"s":"Bigger formats (quarto, 8 pages per sheet; octavo, 16 pages) follow the same kind of logic but get progressively harder. In a real 1450 shop, the imposer was a senior craftsman; getting it wrong meant printing the run upside-down or out of order. The schematics for octavo imposition look like geometry puzzles — and they are."}},{"k":"you_just_set_8_to_10_characters_a_1450","v":{"s":"You just set 8 to 10 characters. A 1450 master compositor set roughly 1,000 characters per hour, ten hours per day, six days per week, for forty years."}},{"k":"per_hour","v":{"s":"Per hour"}},{"k":"per_10_hr_day","v":{"s":"Per 10-hr day"}},{"k":"per_6_day_week","v":{"s":"Per 6-day week"}},{"k":"per_50_week_year","v":{"s":"Per 50-week year"}},{"k":"per_40_year_career","v":{"s":"Per 40-year career"}},{"k":"a_120_million_character_career_is_roug","v":{"s":"A 120-million-character career is roughly 600 King James Bibles, set one mirror-reversed sort at a time, by hand, by feel of the nick. Most compositors did this for less wage than a modern barista. The economic case for the press was that one of these workers, with a press to back them, could do hundreds of times what a scribe could do alone."}},{"k":"for_comparison_a_fast_modern_keyboard_","v":{"s":"For comparison: a fast modern keyboard typist (12,000 chars/hour) writing 4 hours a day for 40 years produces about 380 million characters in their typing lifetime. The press-shop compositor was within an order of magnitude of this, four hundred years before computers existed."}},{"k":"a_compositor_in_1450_reads_from_a_lati","v":{"s":"A compositor in 1450 reads from a Latin manuscript and sets type. The text reads \"Confitemini Domino\" (Give thanks to the Lord). Which way through the composing stick does she set the letters?"}},{"k":"gutenberg_museum_mainz_composing_stick","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Museum (Mainz) — Composing stick demonstrations"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_printing_innovatio","v":{"s":"Library of Congress — \"Printing Innovations\" (typesetting history)"}},{"k":"robert_bringhurst_the_elements_of_typo","v":{"s":"Robert Bringhurst, \"The Elements of Typographic Style\" (4th ed., 2013)"}},{"k":"briar_press_letterpress_community_glos","v":{"s":"Briar Press — Letterpress community + glossary"}},{"k":"failed_cast","v":{"s":"Failed cast"}},{"k":"without_antimony_the_lead_tin_alloy_sh","v":{"s":"Without antimony, the lead-tin alloy shrinks as it cools. Cast type comes out smaller than the matrix with rounded edges. Letters are unreadable. This is exactly why pre-Gutenberg attempts at metal type failed."}},{"k":"brittle_chips_on_first_press","v":{"s":"Brittle, chips on first press"}},{"k":"too_much_antimony_makes_the_alloy_hard","v":{"s":"Too much antimony makes the alloy hard but brittle. Type chips or shatters under press pressure within a few impressions. Workshop disaster."}},{"k":"expensive_and_impractical","v":{"s":"Expensive and impractical"}},{"k":"mold_fill_problem","v":{"s":"Mold fill problem"}},{"k":"insufficient_tin_means_the_melting_poi","v":{"s":"Insufficient tin means the melting point is too high and the alloy does not flow well into the matrix. Cast letters come out incomplete around fine details."}},{"k":"too_soft","v":{"s":"Too soft"}},{"k":"mostly_lead_the_type_deforms_after_onl","v":{"s":"Mostly lead. The type deforms after only a few hundred impressions. Letters get fuzzy then unrecognizable. Workshop costs spike from constant recasting."}},{"k":"excellent_at_or_near_the_historical_ta","v":{"s":"Excellent — at or near the historical target"}},{"k":"this_is_approximately_the_lead_tin_ant","v":{"s":"This is approximately the lead-tin-antimony ratio that 1450 print shops settled on after generations of trial and error. Crisp casts, durable type, reasonable cost."}},{"k":"acceptable","v":{"s":"Acceptable"}},{"k":"workable_print_runs_would_be_slower_th","v":{"s":"Workable. Print runs would be slower than ideal and recasting more frequent, but the shop could function."}},{"k":"marginal","v":{"s":"Marginal"}},{"k":"the_cast_type_would_work_for_limited_r","v":{"s":"The cast type would work for limited runs but the workshop would lose money to recasting and lost impressions."}},{"k":"walk_through_the_steps","v":{"s":"▶ Walk through the steps"}},{"k":"stop","v":{"s":"⏸ Stop"}},{"k":"a_skilled_punch_cutter_carves_a_single","v":{"s":"A skilled punch-cutter carves a single letter, mirror-reversed, into the end of a hardened steel rod. This is the \"punch.\" It takes hours per letter. Each font needs ~250 punches (uppercase, lowercase, numerals, punctuation, ligatures, accents)."}},{"k":"steel_punch_mirror_cut","v":{"s":"Steel punch (mirror-cut)"}},{"k":"the_steel_punch_is_hammered_into_a_sof","v":{"s":"The steel punch is hammered into a softer copper bar, leaving a clean letter-shaped impression. This copper bar is the \"matrix.\" One matrix can be used to cast thousands of identical type sorts."}},{"k":"copper_matrix_struck_impression","v":{"s":"Copper matrix (struck impression)"}},{"k":"a_hand_held_casting_device_clamps_the_","v":{"s":"A hand-held casting device clamps the matrix at the bottom. Molten type-metal alloy is poured in. It cools in seconds, you knock out the new sort, repeat. A skilled caster could make ~4,000 sorts in a day."}},{"k":"hand_mold_matrix_molten_alloy","v":{"s":"Hand mold + matrix + molten alloy"}},{"k":"now","v":{"s":"◉ Now"}},{"k":"jikji","v":{"s":"Jikji"}},{"k":"buddhist_monks_heungdeok_temple_korea","v":{"s":"Buddhist monks, Heungdeok Temple, Korea"}},{"k":"the_oldest_extant_book_printed_with_me","v":{"s":"The oldest extant book printed with metal movable type. 78 years before Gutenberg. Subject: a Buddhist Zen anthology. Surviving copy in the Bibliothèque nationale de France. The casting process was different from Gutenberg's — a sand-mold technique, not the steel-punch-into-copper-matrix workflow that scaled."}},{"k":"gutenberg_bible","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Bible"}},{"k":"johannes_gutenberg_mainz_germany","v":{"s":"Johannes Gutenberg, Mainz, Germany"}},{"k":"180_copies_of_a_1_282_page_latin_bible","v":{"s":"~180 copies of a 1,282-page Latin Bible. Subject: the Vulgate. ~49 copies survive (12 on vellum). The punch-matrix-cast workflow that scaled into a continent-wide industry within 50 years."}},{"k":"so_why_did_one_scale_and_the_other_did","v":{"s":"So why did one scale and the other did not?"}},{"k":"alphabet_size","v":{"s":"Alphabet size:"}},{"k":"korean_hanja_used_in_1377_6_000_charac","v":{"s":"Korean Hanja used in 1377: ~6,000+ characters. Western Latin alphabet: ~250 glyphs (including caps, lowercase, ligatures, punctuation). The punch-cutting cost amortizes per book printed — a 250-glyph set pays off after ~50-100 books, a 6,000-glyph set never quite does."}},{"k":"mold_technique","v":{"s":"Mold technique:"}},{"k":"jikji_used_sand_mold_casting_less_repe","v":{"s":"Jikji used sand-mold casting (less repeatable). Gutenberg used the hand-mold + copper matrix (very repeatable). Repeatability matters when you need 50,000 identical sorts."}},{"k":"economic_context","v":{"s":"Economic context:"}},{"k":"korea_s_joseon_dynasty_restricted_hang","v":{"s":"Korea's Joseon dynasty restricted Hangul (the simpler alphabet of ~24 letters, designed by King Sejong in 1443) to limited use. Hanja remained the scholarly script. Western Europe had no such restriction — the Latin alphabet was the de-facto script everywhere print spread."}},{"k":"honest_summary","v":{"s":"Honest summary:"}},{"k":"korea_invented_metal_movable_type_gute","v":{"s":"Korea invented metal movable type. Gutenberg invented (or perfected) the SYSTEM — alphabet economics + repeatable mold + screw press + oil-based ink + paper supply — that made it scale. Both achievements are real; neither cancels the other."}},{"k":"one_piece_of_metal_type_about_the_size","v":{"s":"One piece of metal type, about the size of a fingernail, is more engineered than it looks. Every named part exists for a reason: alignment, stacking, picking up by feel, even running ink. Before designing the alloy, look at what you are casting."}},{"k":"labeled_axonometric_diagram_of_a_singl","v":{"s":"Labeled axonometric diagram of a single piece of metal type"}},{"k":"mirror_letter","v":{"s":"(mirror letter)"}},{"k":"shoulder","v":{"s":"Shoulder"}},{"k":"shank","v":{"s":"Shank"}},{"k":"nick","v":{"s":"Nick"}},{"k":"foot","v":{"s":"Foot"}},{"k":"24_mm","v":{"s":"~ 24 mm"}},{"k":"3_5_mm","v":{"s":"~ 3-5 mm"}},{"k":"shoulder_2","v":{"s":"Shoulder"}},{"k":"shank_body","v":{"s":"Shank (body)"}},{"k":"nick_2","v":{"s":"Nick"}},{"k":"foot_2","v":{"s":"Foot"}},{"k":"a_1450_print_shop_kept_maybe_100_000_s","v":{"s":"A 1450 print shop kept maybe 100,000 sorts in inventory. Every one was identical in size and height to within a fraction of a millimeter. That dimensional precision, multiplied by 100,000, is the real Gutenberg achievement — not any one sort, but the system that made them all the same."}},{"k":"a_compositor_picked_up_a_sort_and_knew","v":{"s":"A compositor picked up a sort and knew which way was up without looking. They felt for the nick groove near the foot on the front. Try the same skill below: in each row, only one of the four sorts is oriented correctly. Click it."}},{"k":"correct","v":{"s":"✓ correct"}},{"k":"next_sort","v":{"s":"Next sort →"}},{"k":"play_again","v":{"s":"↻ Play again"}},{"k":"a_42_line_gutenberg_bible_has_about_1_","v":{"s":"A 42-line Gutenberg Bible has about 1,290 pages, set two pages at a time in a forme. Each page averages roughly 2,500 characters. Question: about how many sorts (individual pieces of cast metal type) do you think Gutenberg's shop needed in inventory at one time?"}},{"k":"your_guess","v":{"s":"Your guess: "}},{"k":"reveal_the_answer","v":{"s":"🔍 Reveal the answer"}},{"k":"approximate_answer","v":{"s":"Approximate answer"}},{"k":"100_000","v":{"s":"~ 100,000"}},{"k":"individual_cast_sorts_in_working_inven","v":{"s":"individual cast sorts in working inventory"}},{"k":"your_guess_of","v":{"s":"Your guess of "}},{"k":"why_so_many","v":{"s":"Why so many?"}},{"k":"a_two_page_forme_might_use_5_000_sorts","v":{"s":" A two-page forme might use 5,000 sorts. But once those sorts are locked in the forme, they cannot be reused until the run prints AND someone (often the apprentice) distributes them back to their compartments. With multiple formes in various stages of composing, pressing, and distribution, you need roughly 5 to 10 times the per-forme count just to keep all the presses running. Some sorts (E, T, A) need many more copies than others (Q, X, Z), so the inventory is unevenly distributed across the case."}},{"k":"drag_the_sliders_to_mix_your_own_type_","v":{"s":"Drag the sliders to mix your own type-metal alloy. The quality of the cast — dimensional accuracy, hardness, mold fill, durability — responds in real time. Try going to extremes to see what fails, then dial back to the historical Gutenberg-era target."}},{"k":"always_sums_to_100_adjusting_one_slide","v":{"s":"Always sums to 100%. Adjusting one slider rebalances the others."}},{"k":"reset_to_historical_54_28_18","v":{"s":"↺ Reset to historical (54/28/18)"}},{"k":"cast_preview_one_sort","v":{"s":"Cast preview (one sort)"}},{"k":"a_16th_century_type_caster_pours_an_al","v":{"s":"A 16th-century type-caster pours an alloy of pure lead and tin (no antimony). The cast sorts come out smaller than the matrix and with rounded letter edges. What is the materials-science explanation?"}},{"k":"why_do_you_need_250_punches_to_make_a_","v":{"s":"Why do you need ~250 punches to make a single typeface, even though the alphabet has only 26 letters?"}},{"k":"smithsonian_how_movable_type_was_made","v":{"s":"Smithsonian — How Movable Type Was Made"}},{"k":"briar_press_type_casting_glossary","v":{"s":"Briar Press — Type-casting glossary"}},{"k":"theodore_low_de_vinne_the_practice_of_","v":{"s":"Theodore Low De Vinne, \"The Practice of Typography\" (1900)"}},{"k":"cheongju_early_printing_museum_korea_j","v":{"s":"Cheongju Early Printing Museum (Korea) — Jikji history"}},{"k":"how_many_copies","v":{"s":"How many copies? "}},{"k":"by_hand_scribes","v":{"s":"By hand (scribes)"}},{"k":"1_scribe_per_bible_1_year_per_copy","v":{"s":"1 scribe per Bible, ~1 year per copy. "}},{"k":"by_press_gutenberg_era","v":{"s":"By press (Gutenberg-era)"}},{"k":"12_workers_3_years_for_a_print_run_plu","v":{"s":"~12 workers, ~3 years for a print run, plus 2-year setup floor."}},{"k":"speedup_factor","v":{"s":"Speedup factor"}},{"k":"the_press_is_roughly","v":{"s":"The press is roughly "}},{"k":"approximate_per_book_cost_in_2026_usd_","v":{"s":"Approximate per-book cost in 2026 USD-equivalent. Estimates from economic history scholarship (Eltjo Buringh, Jan Luiten van Zanden — book production data 800-1800)."}},{"k":"a_roughly_5_000_fold_cost_reduction_in","v":{"s":"A roughly 5,000-fold cost reduction in 150 years. For comparison, computing power per dollar reduced by a similar factor over a comparable timespan in the late 20th century."}},{"k":"a_printed_pamphlet_did_not_stop_at_the","v":{"s":"A printed pamphlet did not stop at the buyer. It was lent, read aloud in taverns and town squares, posted on church doors, copied by hand, and re-printed in other cities. The reach multiplier is what made Luther’s 95 Theses reach Rome inside three months. Try the math."}},{"k":"copies_printed","v":{"s":"Copies printed: "}},{"k":"luther_s_95_theses_ran_300_000_copies_","v":{"s":"Luther’s 95 Theses ran ~300,000 copies in three years. A typical Reformation pamphlet ran 500–1,500."}},{"k":"readers_per_copy","v":{"s":"Readers per copy: "}},{"k":"in_1500_literacy_was_rare_so_reading_a","v":{"s":"In 1500 literacy was rare, so reading aloud was normal. One copy in a tavern might reach 10–20 hearers per evening."}},{"k":"passing_rounds","v":{"s":"Passing rounds: "}},{"k":"each_round_the_pamphlet_finds_new_audi","v":{"s":"Each round, the pamphlet finds new audiences — read aloud somewhere else, lent to a neighbor, reprinted in another town."}},{"k":"estimated_total_reach","v":{"s":"Estimated total reach"}},{"k":"maine_population_today","v":{"s":"Maine population today"}},{"k":"viral_social_post_2026","v":{"s":"Viral social post 2026"}},{"k":"real_history_luther_s_95_theses_oct_15","v":{"s":"Real history: Luther’s 95 Theses (Oct 1517) reached Rome by Dec 1517. Distance ~750 miles. The mechanism was exactly this multiplier — print + read-aloud + reprint, in dozens of cities, all in three months. No army or institution could keep up with the spread."}},{"k":"a_1455_monastery_wants_50_copies_of_a_","v":{"s":"A 1455 monastery wants 50 copies of a particular Latin commentary. Hand-copying (5 scribes, 10 years) vs printing (rent a press for 6 months). Which approach makes economic sense?"}},{"k":"eltjo_buringh_jan_luiten_van_zanden_ch","v":{"s":"Eltjo Buringh & Jan Luiten van Zanden — \"Charting the Rise of the West\" (book production data 800-1800)"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_economic_history_o","v":{"s":"Library of Congress — Economic history of printing"}},{"k":"elizabeth_eisenstein_the_printing_pres","v":{"s":"Elizabeth Eisenstein, \"The Printing Press as an Agent of Change\" (1980)"}},{"k":"jikji_korea","v":{"s":"Jikji (Korea)"}},{"k":"gutenberg_bible_42_line_bible","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Bible (42-line Bible)"}},{"k":"aldine_press_founded","v":{"s":"Aldine Press founded"}},{"k":"the_95_theses","v":{"s":"The 95 Theses"}},{"k":"de_revolutionibus_de_humani_corporis_f","v":{"s":"De revolutionibus + De humani corporis fabrica"}},{"k":"don_quixote_part_1","v":{"s":"Don Quixote, Part 1"}},{"k":"king_james_bible_authorized_version","v":{"s":"King James Bible (Authorized Version)"}},{"k":"philosophical_transactions","v":{"s":"Philosophical Transactions"}},{"k":"publick_occurrences","v":{"s":"Publick Occurrences"}},{"k":"common_sense","v":{"s":"Common Sense"}},{"k":"falmouth_gazette_and_weekly_advertiser","v":{"s":"Falmouth Gazette and Weekly Advertiser"}},{"k":"cherokee_phoenix_cherokee_syllabary_in","v":{"s":"Cherokee Phoenix (Cherokee syllabary in print)"}},{"k":"steam_powered_cylinder_press","v":{"s":"Steam-powered cylinder press"}},{"k":"maine_becomes_a_state_missouri_comprom","v":{"s":"Maine becomes a state (Missouri Compromise)"}},{"k":"uncle_tom_s_cabin","v":{"s":"Uncle Tom's Cabin"}},{"k":"the_narrative_most_u_s_textbooks_tell_","v":{"s":"The narrative most U.S. textbooks tell is \"Gutenberg invented printing in 1450.\" That is incomplete. Gutenberg invented the system that scaled printing for European alphabets in Europe. Printing as a concept and a practice is older and broader. The honest version names where else printing happened, and why those traditions did not produce the same explosive scale."}},{"k":"what_happened_2","v":{"s":"What happened: "}},{"k":"why_it_did_not_scale_like_mainz","v":{"s":"Why it did not scale like Mainz: "}},{"k":"the_right_framing_gutenberg_solved_a_s","v":{"s":"The right framing: Gutenberg solved a specific problem for a specific writing system at a specific moment, and that solution scaled fast. He did not invent the human idea of duplicating texts. Crediting him as \"the\" inventor of printing erases hundreds of years of innovation in other parts of the world. The honest sentence is \"Gutenberg developed the system that made movable-type printing economically viable for European alphabets.\""}},{"k":"the_falmouth_gazette_1785_was_the_firs","v":{"s":"The Falmouth Gazette (1785) was the first newspaper printed in what would become Maine. But the land had a sophisticated information culture for thousands of years before colonial printers arrived. Naming this honestly is part of the local-history thread."}},{"k":"the_wabanaki_confederacy_penobscot_pas","v":{"s":"The Wabanaki Confederacy: Penobscot, Passamaquoddy, Maliseet, Mi'kmaq, and Abenaki nations. Together they had occupied what is now Maine, the Maritimes, and northern New England for at least 10,000 years."}},{"k":"oral_traditions_backed_by_trained_memo","v":{"s":"Oral traditions backed by trained memory keepers. Stories, treaty histories, place-names, plant knowledge, navigation, and seasonal cycles were carried by individuals who specialized in remembering. Oral does not mean unreliable; specialist memorizers were checked against each other and against material artifacts."}},{"k":"wampum_belts_shell_beads_strung_in_pat","v":{"s":"Wampum belts (shell beads strung in patterns) recorded treaties, alliances, and historical events. A wampum keeper could read a belt the way a European could read a manuscript. Some surviving wampum belts are 400+ years old and still legible to trained readers. Maine museums and tribal cultural centers hold examples."}},{"k":"mi_kmaq_and_other_wabanaki_nations_use","v":{"s":"Mi'kmaq and other Wabanaki nations used inscribed birchbark for some communications, especially religious and historical material. Some scrolls survive in museum collections; the inscription technique predates colonial contact."}},{"k":"colonial_missionary_presses_began_prin","v":{"s":"Colonial missionary presses began printing in Wabanaki languages in the 1600s. The Eliot Indian Bible (1663, Massachusett-language) is the most famous example, set in Cambridge MA by John Eliot with native-language consultants. Print-in-Wabanaki-languages and oral-tradition-in-Wabanaki-languages coexisted for centuries; both continue today."}},{"k":"for_maine_students_the_abbe_museum_bar","v":{"s":"For Maine students: the Abbe Museum (Bar Harbor), the Maine Indian Basketmakers Alliance, and tribal cultural centers in Indian Township and Pleasant Point are working sources today. The Penobscot Nation Library (Indian Island) holds materials many state archives do not. Treat these as living institutions, not just historical references."}},{"k":"approximate_cumulative_book_production","v":{"s":"Approximate cumulative book production in Europe (Buringh & van Zanden, \"Charting the Rise of the West\"):"}},{"k":"the_gutenberg_bible_was_first_but_the_","v":{"s":"The Gutenberg Bible was first. But the next 38 years produced an extraordinary variety of \"firsts\" as printers tried out what the new technology could do beyond the Bible."}},{"k":"gutenberg_bible_2","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Bible"}},{"k":"mainz_psalter","v":{"s":"Mainz Psalter"}},{"k":"catholicon","v":{"s":"Catholicon"}},{"k":"der_edelstein","v":{"s":"Der Edelstein"}},{"k":"cicero_de_oratore","v":{"s":"Cicero, De Oratore"}},{"k":"mentelin_bible","v":{"s":"Mentelin Bible"}},{"k":"augustine_de_civitate_dei","v":{"s":"Augustine, De Civitate Dei"}},{"k":"recuyell_of_the_historyes_of_troye","v":{"s":"Recuyell of the Historyes of Troye"}},{"k":"dante_divine_comedy","v":{"s":"Dante, Divine Comedy"}},{"k":"mainz_mainzer_pilgerfahrt","v":{"s":"Mainz Mainzer Pilgerfahrt"}},{"k":"nuremberg_chronicle","v":{"s":"Nuremberg Chronicle"}},{"k":"within_40_years_of_the_gutenberg_bible","v":{"s":"Within 40 years of the Gutenberg Bible, every major category of book we still print had at least one printed exemplar: scripture, scholarship, classics, dictionaries, vernacular literature, illustrated books, reference, travel writing, popular illustration. The press did not just produce more books; it discovered what books could be."}},{"k":"each_marker_is_a_moment_when_print_ena","v":{"s":"Each marker is a moment when print enabled something that would otherwise have stayed local, slow, or impossible. Click to learn what changed."}},{"k":"close_detail","v":{"s":"Close detail"}},{"k":"primary_source_digital_facsimile","v":{"s":"→ Primary source / digital facsimile"}},{"k":"click_any_marker_above_to_read_about_t","v":{"s":"Click any marker above to read about the event."}},{"k":"falmouth_gazette_1785","v":{"s":"Falmouth Gazette (1785) "}},{"k":"was_published_in_what_is_now_portland_","v":{"s":"was published in what is now Portland, less than two miles from King Middle. It helped build the case for separating Maine from Massachusetts (achieved 1820). "}},{"k":"uncle_tom_s_cabin_1851","v":{"s":"Uncle Tom's Cabin (1851) "}},{"k":"was_written_by_harriet_beecher_stowe_i","v":{"s":"was written by Harriet Beecher Stowe in Brunswick, Maine — a 25-mile drive from Portland — while her husband taught at Bowdoin College. 300,000 copies in its first US year, the most-printed novel of the 19th century. The press in Mainz reached the kitchen table in Maine."}},{"k":"wittenberg","v":{"s":"Wittenberg"}},{"k":"leipzig","v":{"s":"Leipzig"}},{"k":"nuremberg","v":{"s":"Nuremberg"}},{"k":"basel","v":{"s":"Basel"}},{"k":"mainz","v":{"s":"Mainz"}},{"k":"augsburg","v":{"s":"Augsburg"}},{"k":"strasbourg","v":{"s":"Strasbourg"}},{"k":"cologne","v":{"s":"Cologne"}},{"k":"vienna","v":{"s":"Vienna"}},{"k":"antwerp","v":{"s":"Antwerp"}},{"k":"paris","v":{"s":"Paris"}},{"k":"geneva","v":{"s":"Geneva"}},{"k":"london","v":{"s":"London"}},{"k":"lyon","v":{"s":"Lyon"}},{"k":"krakow","v":{"s":"Krakow"}},{"k":"watch_the_95_theses_spread_oct_1517_se","v":{"s":"🗺️ Watch the 95 Theses spread (Oct 1517 → Sept 1518)"}},{"k":"each_dot_is_a_city_where_a_printed_edi","v":{"s":"Each dot is a city where a printed edition of the Theses or an early Luther pamphlet appeared. Click play to watch the spread by month. By comparison, Wycliffe's manuscript-era writings (1380s) took decades to reach a fraction of these cities."}},{"k":"simplified_map_of_europe_showing_citie","v":{"s":"Simplified map of Europe showing cities where the 95 Theses appeared, by month"}},{"k":"pause","v":{"s":"⏸ Pause"}},{"k":"replay","v":{"s":"↺ Replay"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"what_practice_is_luther_directly_criti","v":{"s":"What practice is Luther directly criticizing in this thesis?"}},{"k":"the_sale_of_indulgences_a_church_pract","v":{"s":"The sale of indulgences — a Church practice where the faithful could pay money to reduce time their loved ones spent in purgatory. The \"coin clinks in the chest\" refers to the iron collection chests indulgence-sellers carried. Luther argues this is a \"human folly,\" meaning a made-up tradition with no scriptural basis. This thesis became one of the most-quoted in the spread."}},{"k":"how_would_this_thesis_have_read_to_a_f","v":{"s":"How would this thesis have read to a faithful 1517 Catholic, and what makes it explosive?"}},{"k":"explosive_because_luther_is_saying_not","v":{"s":"Explosive because Luther is saying not only that the indulgence-buyers are mistaken — but that they AND the priests selling indulgences will be damned. It directly attacks the religious authority of clergy who profit from the practice. This is the kind of language that turned an academic dispute into a movement that authorities could not easily suppress."}},{"k":"why_is_naming_crassus_the_famously_wea","v":{"s":"Why is naming Crassus (the famously wealthy Roman) such a powerful rhetorical move in 1517?"}},{"k":"crassus_was_a_latin_shorthand_for_ulti","v":{"s":"Crassus was a Latin shorthand for ultimate wealth — every educated reader would recognize the reference. Luther is comparing the pope to a notorious pagan plutocrat and asking why a man with that level of resources is taking money from the poor to build his church. The class critique is sharp; the historical-classical reference makes it impossible to dismiss as ignorant. This kind of educated polemic is what print specifically enabled."}},{"k":"read_a_primary_source","v":{"s":"📜 Read a primary source"}},{"k":"excerpts_from_luther_s_95_theses_trans","v":{"s":"Excerpts from Luther's 95 Theses, translated from the Latin originals printed in 1517. Read the thesis, think about the comprehension question, then reveal the analysis."}},{"k":"reveal_the_analysis","v":{"s":"🔍 Reveal the analysis"}},{"k":"analysis","v":{"s":"Analysis"}},{"k":"a_relay_of_runners_reliable_but_slow_i","v":{"s":"A relay of runners. Reliable but slow. Imperial dispatches priority."}},{"k":"horse_relay_station_network_the_mongol","v":{"s":"Horse + relay station network. The Mongol yam system reached ~250 km/day; medieval Europe ran slower."}},{"k":"hanseatic_league_routes_plus_inland_co","v":{"s":"Hanseatic League routes plus inland courier. Weather-dependent."}},{"k":"couriers_carrying_printed_pamphlets_th","v":{"s":"Couriers carrying printed pamphlets that could be copied at each stop. Print multiplied at each node."}},{"k":"chappe_semaphore_towers_across_france_","v":{"s":"Chappe semaphore towers across France: signals from tower to tower at light-speed-of-sight, weather permitting."}},{"k":"submarine_cables_under_the_channel_ove","v":{"s":"Submarine cables under the Channel + overland wire. Reuters built a global news service on this."}},{"k":"distance_is_irrelevant_the_london_vien","v":{"s":"Distance is irrelevant. The London → Vienna packet arrives faster than you can blink."}},{"k":"bar_width_relative_speed_log_scale_eac","v":{"s":"Bar width = relative speed (log scale; each tick is roughly 10× faster than the one above). Print was a step change — about 2× faster than horse-only, plus the ability to multiply copies at each relay stop. The bigger change came later (telegraph, internet). But print is when the curve first bent up."}},{"k":"a_medium_s_reach_is_one_thing_its_dura","v":{"s":"A medium's reach is one thing. Its durability is another. Print made ideas harder to erase by multiplying physical copies. The trade-off: each new generation of media keeps changing the math. Born-digital content is the most durable in principle and the most fragile in practice."}},{"k":"pre_print_european_manuscripts_1100_14","v":{"s":"Pre-print European manuscripts (1100-1450)"}},{"k":"single_copies_vulnerable_to_fire_mold_","v":{"s":"Single copies, vulnerable to fire, mold, neglect. Most pre-print European texts are lost. Estimates vary by genre."}},{"k":"gutenberg_bibles_1455_180_printed","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Bibles (1455, ~180 printed)"}},{"k":"49_of_180_known_to_survive_in_some_for","v":{"s":"~49 of ~180 known to survive in some form. Sacred status protected them; cathedrals and universities preserved copies."}},{"k":"early_printed_books_1450_1500_incunabu","v":{"s":"Early printed books (1450-1500, \"incunabula\")"}},{"k":"bibliographers_track_30_000_known_incu","v":{"s":"Bibliographers track ~30,000 known incunabula editions; surviving examples are reasonably well catalogued in research libraries."}},{"k":"19th_century_newspapers_1800_1900","v":{"s":"19th-century newspapers (1800-1900)"}},{"k":"cheap_acidic_paper_degrades_microfilm_","v":{"s":"Cheap acidic paper degrades. Microfilm and digitization captured a fraction; many small-town titles are partially or fully lost."}},{"k":"1990s_websites_now_30_years_old","v":{"s":"1990s websites (now ~30 years old)"}},{"k":"link_rot_server_shutdowns_format_obsol","v":{"s":"Link rot, server shutdowns, format obsolescence. Internet Archive recovers a portion; much is gone or unreachable. The \"Eternal September\" generation of personal sites is largely vanished."}},{"k":"born_digital_today_properly_archived","v":{"s":"Born-digital today, properly archived"}},{"k":"in_principle_bit_perfect_copies_distri","v":{"s":"In principle: bit-perfect copies, distributed redundancy, format migration. In practice: only if institutions actively curate. Most personal data is not curated."}},{"k":"figures_are_best_effort_estimates_from","v":{"s":"Figures are best-effort estimates from bibliographic and archival scholarship; \"survival\" includes any copy, fragment, or facsimile that can be examined today. The point is order-of-magnitude, not the second decimal. The takeaway: each new medium re-opens the durability question, and the answer is usually \"depends on whether anyone bothers to preserve it.\""}},{"k":"about_180_gutenberg_bibles_were_printe","v":{"s":"About 180 Gutenberg Bibles were printed in 1454-1455. Approximately 49 survive today in some form (complete or substantial fragment). Most are in research libraries; some are owned by universities; a handful are in private collections. The list below catalogues the publicly held copies you can see or request to view."}},{"k":"most_research_libraries_that_hold_a_gu","v":{"s":"Most research libraries that hold a Gutenberg Bible will let qualified scholars (and sometimes interested students with an appointment) view it. The British Library and Library of Congress have full digital facsimiles online for free, page by page. Closest to King Middle: Harvard's Houghton Library and the Morgan Library in NYC are a Northeast Corridor trip away."}},{"k":"crew_reflection_what_about_you_would_s","v":{"s":"Crew reflection · What about you would survive?"}},{"k":"a_gutenberg_bible_survives_because_cat","v":{"s":"A Gutenberg Bible survives because cathedrals kept it. A 1450 apprentice's notebook does not survive because no one preserved it. Your generation is producing more text and images than any previous one. Most of it is on platforms that did not exist 20 years ago and may not exist in 20 years."}},{"k":"which_of_your_creations_would_you_want","v":{"s":"Which of your creations would you want to survive 600 years?"}},{"k":"schoolwork_photos_texts_to_friends_soc","v":{"s":" Schoolwork, photos, texts to friends, social posts, a piece of writing nobody has seen yet?"}},{"k":"where_does_that_thing_live_right_now","v":{"s":"Where does that thing live right now?"}},{"k":"on_your_phone_in_the_cloud_on_someone_","v":{"s":" On your phone, in the cloud, on someone else's server, on paper?"}},{"k":"who_would_have_to_keep_caring_about_it","v":{"s":"Who would have to keep caring about it for it to last?"}},{"k":"you_your_family_your_school_a_company_","v":{"s":" You, your family, your school, a company, an archive?"}},{"k":"what_would_your_generation_lose_if_clo","v":{"s":"What would your generation lose if cloud platforms shut down tomorrow?"}},{"k":"be_specific_what_goes_what_stays","v":{"s":" Be specific. What goes; what stays?"}},{"k":"print_spread_from_mainz_outward_in_wav","v":{"s":"Print spread from Mainz outward in waves. Each major center developed a specialty matched to its trade, religion, and politics. By 1500 these four cities defined what European printing looked like."}},{"k":"print_spread_across_europe_with_a_spee","v":{"s":"Print spread across Europe with a speed that looks miraculous unless you know the specific event that started it. In 1462, Mainz was caught in a local feudal conflict (the Diocesan War, also called the Mainz Archbishopric Feud) between two rival claimants to the archbishop's seat. The city was sacked and burned. The print shops were ruined."}},{"k":"but_the_printers_journeymen_and_appren","v":{"s":"But the printers, journeymen, and apprentices were not killed. They left. They carried Mainz-trained skills (punch-cutting, matrix-making, casting, composing, press operation, inkmaking) with them to whatever city would take them in. Within a decade, that diaspora produced presses in 60+ European cities. The spread that the standard story tells as \"the press caught on\" was specifically a refugee crisis turning into a technology transfer."}},{"k":"where_they_went_1462_1475","v":{"s":"Where they went · 1462–1475"}},{"k":"a_technology_that_might_have_stayed_lo","v":{"s":"A technology that might have stayed locked in Mainz for a generation went international in a decade because of a local war that scattered the people who knew how. Counterfactual: without the 1462 sack, the technology probably still spreads, but slower, and the Reformation 55 years later might find a thinner network of presses to spread Luther's arguments. Local accidents shape global outcomes."}},{"k":"each_milestone_collapses_one_bottlenec","v":{"s":"Each milestone collapses one bottleneck. The screw press scaled up the impression rate; the iron press eliminated frame failure; steam power broke human-arm throughput; Linotype broke composing-by-hand; desktop publishing put the shop on a desk; AI is trying to break composing entirely. The arc is six centuries of unbottlenecking the same job."}},{"k":"gutenberg_movable_type_screw_press","v":{"s":"Gutenberg movable-type screw press"}},{"k":"the_starting_point_you_have_been_study","v":{"s":"The starting point you have been studying. Wooden screw press, hand-set metal type, oil-based ink, two-person crew."}},{"k":"stanhope_iron_press","v":{"s":"Stanhope iron press"}},{"k":"earl_stanhope_replaced_the_wooden_fram","v":{"s":"Earl Stanhope replaced the wooden frame with cast iron. The press could handle larger sheets and last for decades without splitting. Same throughput, much higher reliability and page size."}},{"k":"koenig_steam_powered_press","v":{"s":"Koenig steam-powered press"}},{"k":"the_times_of_london_adopted_friedrich_","v":{"s":"The Times of London adopted Friedrich Koenig's steam-powered cylinder press. Daily newspapers became economically viable for the first time. Print broke free of human-arm throughput."}},{"k":"mergenthaler_linotype","v":{"s":"Mergenthaler Linotype"}},{"k":"a_keyboard_operated_machine_cast_a_who","v":{"s":"A keyboard-operated machine cast a whole line of type at once from molten metal (\"a line o' type\"). Newspapers exploded. The compositor's job changed from setting individual sorts to operating a keyboard. Same role, new bottleneck."}},{"k":"desktop_publishing_apple_laserwriter_p","v":{"s":"Desktop publishing (Apple LaserWriter + PageMaker)"}},{"k":"a_laser_printer_plus_page_layout_softw","v":{"s":"A laser printer plus page-layout software put what used to be a print shop into anyone's home. The 600-year arc from physical sorts to virtual type closes here. The terms (font, leading, kerning) come along untouched."}},{"k":"generative_ai_for_text_layout","v":{"s":"Generative AI for text + layout"}},{"k":"the_current_frontier_the_role_of_the_h","v":{"s":"The current frontier. The role of the human composer is shifting from setting type to setting prompts and judging output. The 1450 critic's questions (Who is responsible for accuracy? Does this rot reading? Who controls what spreads?) are getting asked again."}},{"k":"notice_the_vocabulary_the_1450_words_f","v":{"s":"Notice the vocabulary. The 1450 words (font, kerning, leading, justify, italic, em-dash) survive every transition. The job description keeps changing; the language stays the same. Whatever comes after AI will probably still use these words."}},{"k":"same_job_very_different_machines_the_s","v":{"s":"Same job, very different machines. The silhouettes below show what each generation's press looked like in profile. Notice how the press grows, mechanizes, and eventually disappears into a kitchen-appliance form factor."}},{"k":"wooden_screw_press","v":{"s":"Wooden screw press"}},{"k":"stanhope_iron_press_2","v":{"s":"Stanhope iron press"}},{"k":"koenig_steam_cylinder","v":{"s":"Koenig steam cylinder"}},{"k":"linotype_machine","v":{"s":"Linotype machine"}},{"k":"desktop_printer_press","v":{"s":"Desktop printer / press"}},{"k":"the_1450_press_was_wood_and_your_two_a","v":{"s":"The 1450 press was wood and your two arms. The 2026 desktop printer is plastic, electronics, and a USB cable. Both put ink on paper. The shape of the work moved off your back and into a kilowatt."}},{"k":"the_1450_type_case_was_organized_by_fr","v":{"s":"The 1450 type case was organized by frequency: the most-used letters (E, T, A, O, I, N) sat in the easiest compartments to reach. When the Linotype arrived in 1886, designers had a fresh chance to lay out a keyboard from scratch. They did not pick alphabetical. They picked frequency."}},{"k":"linotype_keyboard_lowercase_section","v":{"s":"Linotype keyboard, lowercase section"}},{"k":"the_gold_top_row_is_etaoin_shrdlu_the_","v":{"s":"The gold top row is \"ETAOIN SHRDLU\" — the 12 most-frequent English letters, in frequency order."}},{"k":"a_skilled_linotype_operator_did_not_ty","v":{"s":"A skilled Linotype operator did not \"type\" the way you do on QWERTY. They worked the keyboard with a left-hand right-hand split, hitting the most common letters from the top row, just like a compositor used to reach for E first in the case."}},{"k":"the_phrase_etaoin_shrdlu_became_newspa","v":{"s":"The phrase \"etaoin shrdlu\" became newspaper slang. When a Linotype operator made a mistake mid-line, the standard recovery was to run a finger down the first two columns to fill out the line, then throw away the slug. Sometimes the operator forgot to throw it away, and \"etaoin shrdlu\" appeared mysteriously in the next morning's paper. The New York Times collected dozens of these accidents in the 1960s."}},{"k":"qwerty_designed_for_typewriters_in_187","v":{"s":"QWERTY (designed for typewriters in 1873) was not frequency-ordered; it was designed to slow typists down so the keys would not jam. ETAOIN SHRDLU represents a counterfactual: if the dominant keyboard had descended from Linotype rather than typewriter, your laptop today would feel completely different under your fingers."}},{"k":"you_did_not_stop_using_the_press_the_p","v":{"s":"You did not stop using the press. The press changed shape and moved into your pocket. Four examples of where 1450 technology lives in your daily life."}},{"k":"printing_is_the_wonder_of_all_wonders_","v":{"s":"Printing is the wonder of all wonders. It is the gift of God, by which old things may be made new and new things made known."}},{"k":"desiderius_erasmus_1500","v":{"s":"— Desiderius Erasmus, ~1500"}},{"k":"dutch_humanist_scholar_writing_within_","v":{"s":"Dutch humanist scholar, writing within a generation of Gutenberg, on what the printed book meant to a person living through its arrival."}},{"k":"elizabeth_eisenstein_the_printing_pres_2","v":{"s":"Elizabeth Eisenstein, \"The Printing Press as an Agent of Change\" (1980)"}},{"k":"andrew_pettegree_the_book_in_the_renai","v":{"s":"Andrew Pettegree, \"The Book in the Renaissance\" (2010)"}},{"k":"eltjo_buringh_jan_luiten_van_zanden_bo","v":{"s":"Eltjo Buringh & Jan Luiten van Zanden — book production data"}},{"k":"british_library_the_gutenberg_bible","v":{"s":"British Library — The Gutenberg Bible"}},{"k":"leading_line_spacing","v":{"s":"Leading (line spacing)"}},{"k":"tight","v":{"s":"Tight"}},{"k":"when_the_leading_is_too_tight_lines_of","v":{"s":"When the leading is too tight, lines of text feel cramped and the eye loses its place between them."}},{"k":"open_2","v":{"s":"Open"}},{"k":"when_leading_is_open_the_text_breathes","v":{"s":"When leading is open, the text breathes and the eye finds the next line easily."}},{"k":"lead_strips_between_rows_of_type_contr","v":{"s":"Lead strips between rows of type controlled this. Today: CSS line-height."}},{"k":"kerning_letter_pair_spacing","v":{"s":"Kerning (letter-pair spacing)"}},{"k":"no_kerning","v":{"s":"No kerning"}},{"k":"kerned","v":{"s":"Kerned"}},{"k":"diagonal_letter_pairs_av_va_to_we_leav","v":{"s":"Diagonal letter pairs (AV, VA, To, We) leave visible gaps. Kerning closes them."}},{"k":"em_dash_and_en_dash","v":{"s":"Em-dash and En-dash"}},{"k":"em_dash_m_wide","v":{"s":" (em-dash, M-wide)"}},{"k":"en_dash_n_wide","v":{"s":" (en-dash, n-wide)"}},{"k":"hyphen","v":{"s":"- (hyphen)"}},{"k":"width_references_come_from_physical_ty","v":{"s":"Width references come from physical type sizes used as rulers in print shops."}},{"k":"italic_aldus_manutius_1500","v":{"s":"Italic (Aldus Manutius, ~1500)"}},{"k":"roman","v":{"s":"Roman"}},{"k":"aut_viam_inveniam_aut_faciam","v":{"s":"Aut viam inveniam aut faciam."}},{"k":"italic","v":{"s":"Italic"}},{"k":"aut_viam_inveniam_aut_faciam_2","v":{"s":"Aut viam inveniam aut faciam."}},{"k":"italic_was_invented_for_space_not_emph","v":{"s":"Italic was invented for SPACE, not emphasis — to fit more text per line in pocket-sized books."}},{"k":"justified_text_every_line_the_same_wid","v":{"s":"Justified text (every line the same width)"}},{"k":"when_the_right_edge_of_a_paragraph_is_","v":{"s":"When the right edge of a paragraph is allowed to fall where it falls, the result is called ragged-right. Each line ends with a natural word break."}},{"k":"justified","v":{"s":"Justified"}},{"k":"when_the_right_edge_of_a_paragraph_is__2","v":{"s":"When the right edge of a paragraph is forced to align, the compositor inserts thin metal spacers between words so every line ends at the same position."}},{"k":"compositors_used_metal_quads_between_w","v":{"s":"Compositors used metal \"quads\" between words. The spacing was a craft skill."}},{"k":"same_letter_six_different_times_in_his","v":{"s":"Same letter. Six different times in history. The lowercase a is one of the most-changed letters in Latin type. Compare the closed double-storey \"a\" of medieval Blackletter with the open geometric \"a\" of Helvetica."}},{"k":"blackletter","v":{"s":"Blackletter"}},{"k":"roman_2","v":{"s":"Roman"}},{"k":"italic_2","v":{"s":"Italic"}},{"k":"caslon","v":{"s":"Caslon"}},{"k":"bodoni","v":{"s":"Bodoni"}},{"k":"helvetica","v":{"s":"Helvetica"}},{"k":"designers_call_this_the_letter_s_skele","v":{"s":"Designers call this the letter's \"skeleton\" plus its \"skin.\" The skeleton (basic structure) survives across centuries; the skin (proportions, contrast, terminals) marks the era. A type historian can usually date an unattributed book within 50 years just by looking at the lowercase a."}},{"k":"every_typeface_carries_the_visual_pers","v":{"s":"Every typeface carries the visual personality of its era. Blackletter is medieval. Aldine italic is Renaissance scholarship. Bodoni is the Enlightenment cult of contrast. Helvetica is post-war Swiss neutrality. The samples below render in approximations of each typeface (depends on what your system has installed)."}},{"k":"blackletter_textura","v":{"s":"Blackletter / Textura"}},{"k":"roman_jenson","v":{"s":"Roman (Jenson)"}},{"k":"italic_aldine","v":{"s":"Italic (Aldine)"}},{"k":"garamond","v":{"s":"Garamond"}},{"k":"caslon_2","v":{"s":"Caslon"}},{"k":"baskerville","v":{"s":"Baskerville"}},{"k":"bodoni_2","v":{"s":"Bodoni"}},{"k":"times_new_roman","v":{"s":"Times New Roman"}},{"k":"helvetica_2","v":{"s":"Helvetica"}},{"k":"the_quick_brown_fox_jumps_over_the_laz","v":{"s":"The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog"}},{"k":"type_your_name_a_word_or_a_short_phras","v":{"s":"Type your name, a word, or a short phrase. See it set in five typefaces from across the printed centuries. Each one was the default of its era. Your name in Blackletter would have looked like a 1455 contract. In Helvetica it would look like a 1980s passport."}},{"k":"your_name_or_word","v":{"s":"Your name or word"}},{"k":"type_your_name","v":{"s":"Type your name…"}},{"k":"blackletter_2","v":{"s":"Blackletter"}},{"k":"garamond_2","v":{"s":"Garamond"}},{"k":"caslon_3","v":{"s":"Caslon"}},{"k":"bodoni_3","v":{"s":"Bodoni"}},{"k":"helvetica_3","v":{"s":"Helvetica"}},{"k":"if_the_typefaces_look_similar_on_your_","v":{"s":"If the typefaces look similar on your screen, your system may not have all of them installed. The fallbacks try to keep the right family (serif/sans/blackletter), but the exact letterforms vary."}},{"k":"look_at_the_special_character_set_in_a","v":{"s":"Look at the special-character set in any word processor or in Unicode. Many of these marks were physical sorts in a 1450 print shop, and they survived because compositors kept setting them and readers kept understanding them."}},{"k":"pilcrow","v":{"s":"Pilcrow"}},{"k":"section_sign","v":{"s":"Section sign"}},{"k":"dagger_obelus","v":{"s":"Dagger (obelus)"}},{"k":"double_dagger","v":{"s":"Double dagger"}},{"k":"asterism","v":{"s":"Asterism"}},{"k":"floral_heart_aldus_leaf","v":{"s":"Floral heart (aldus leaf)"}},{"k":"manicule_pointing_hand","v":{"s":"Manicule (pointing hand)"}},{"k":"ampersand","v":{"s":"Ampersand"}},{"k":"ligature","v":{"s":"Æ ligature"}},{"k":"in_1450","v":{"s":"In 1450"}},{"k":"in_2026","v":{"s":"In 2026"}},{"k":"open_a_word_processor_right_now_and_tr","v":{"s":"Open a word processor right now and try Insert > Special Character. Roughly a quarter of what is in there is the contents of a 1450 type case."}},{"k":"a_1500_printer_chose_blackletter_for_a","v":{"s":"A 1500 printer chose Blackletter for a Bible to signal sacred weight, Garamond for a Renaissance philosopher to signal humanist clarity, italic for a pocket book to fit more text per page. Every typeface choice was a claim about what the text was for."}},{"k":"you_make_the_same_kind_of_claim_every_","v":{"s":"You make the same kind of claim every time you open a document and pick a font. Most students never think about this. Pause and think about it now:"}},{"k":"what_font_do_you_actually_use","v":{"s":"What font do you actually use"}},{"k":"for_your_school_papers_your_texts_your","v":{"s":" for your school papers, your texts, your slides? If you say \"the default,\" whose default is that?"}},{"k":"if_you_had_to_pick_a_font_that_represe","v":{"s":"If you had to pick a font that represents you"}},{"k":"your_personality_your_sense_of_humor_t","v":{"s":" (your personality, your sense of humor, the way you carry yourself), which would you pick? Why?"}},{"k":"which_font_signals_i_am_being_serious","v":{"s":"Which font signals \"I am being serious\""}},{"k":"a_compositor_combined_craft_mirror_rea","v":{"s":"A compositor combined craft (mirror-reading, fine motor work) with aesthetics (layout, spacing, type choice). That blend is still its own family of careers."}},{"k":"graphic_designer_typographer","v":{"s":"Graphic designer / typographer"}},{"k":"direct_lineage_same_job_software_repla","v":{"s":"Direct lineage. Same job, software replaces metal. Type pairings, kerning, grid layout all descend from 1450 composing practice."}},{"k":"front_end_web_developer","v":{"s":"Front-end web developer"}},{"k":"css_grid_line_height_em_rem_units_kern","v":{"s":"CSS grid, line-height, em/rem units, kerning — the vocabulary of layout is literally compositor vocabulary."}},{"k":"book_designer","v":{"s":"Book designer"}},{"k":"the_most_direct_heir_of_the_compositor","v":{"s":"The most direct heir of the compositor’s craft. Chooses typefaces, sets margins, plans the page. Working Maine publishers still hire for this."}},{"k":"a_pressman_ran_a_heavy_dangerous_machi","v":{"s":"A pressman ran a heavy, dangerous machine that demanded both strength and a finicky sense for registration, ink flow, and material behavior. That cluster lives in modern manufacturing."}},{"k":"industrial_press_operator","v":{"s":"Industrial press operator"}},{"k":"the_literal_heir_bigger_faster_machine","v":{"s":"The literal heir. Bigger, faster machines; same job description: feed material, hold registration, manage wear, hit the schedule."}},{"k":"cnc_machinist","v":{"s":"CNC machinist"}},{"k":"skilled_machine_operator_read_prints_r","v":{"s":"Skilled-machine operator. Read prints, run programs, hold tolerances, change tooling. Top earners in Maine’s manufacturing sector."}},{"k":"3d_printer_additive_manufacturing_tech","v":{"s":"3D-printer / additive manufacturing technician"}},{"k":"newer_machine_same_role_load_material_","v":{"s":"Newer machine, same role: load material, monitor a run, troubleshoot defects, calibrate. The pressman’s skill cluster on 21st-century hardware."}},{"k":"a_master_printer_balanced_risk_which_c","v":{"s":"A master printer balanced risk — which commissions to accept, when to hire, when to refuse the dangerous job. That is the small-business decision space, then and now."}},{"k":"small_business_owner","v":{"s":"Small business owner"}},{"k":"same_tradeoffs_at_smaller_scale_who_to","v":{"s":"Same tradeoffs at smaller scale: who to trust, what jobs to accept, when to invest, when to say no. The most common business form in the state."}},{"k":"publisher_or_editor_in_chief","v":{"s":"Publisher or editor-in-chief"}},{"k":"direct_lineage_decides_what_gets_print","v":{"s":"Direct lineage. Decides what gets printed (or aired), takes the legal and reputational risk, hires the talent, sells the audience."}},{"k":"studio_or_production_manager","v":{"s":"Studio or production manager"}},{"k":"strategic_decisions_which_projects_to_","v":{"s":"Strategic decisions — which projects to take, which to decline, how to deploy a team of specialists. The master’s job in modern dress."}},{"k":"modern_echoes_of_your_role","v":{"s":"🔗 Modern echoes of your role"}},{"k":"you_inhabited_a_1450_role_for_a_few_mi","v":{"s":"You inhabited a 1450 role for a few minutes. The skills cluster you just practiced — the patience, the craft, the judgment under pressure — is still recruited for. Maine in 2026 has openings in every column above."}},{"k":"try_a_different_role","v":{"s":"🔄 Try a different role"}},{"k":"back_to_menu","v":{"s":"← Back to menu"}},{"k":"closing_reflection","v":{"s":"Closing reflection"}},{"k":"you_finished_the_tool_pick_one_of_thes","v":{"s":"You finished the tool. Pick one of these prompts and answer it in your head before you close the tab. Or write it down. Or bring it to your Crew this week:"}},{"k":"what_is_one_thing_you_understand_about","v":{"s":"What is one thing you understand about technology change that you didn't before?"}},{"k":"pick_a_1450_fear_from_the_same_fears_i","v":{"s":"Pick a 1450 fear from The Same Fears. Is it your fear today, or do you disagree?"}},{"k":"what_would_you_print_first_if_you_ran_","v":{"s":"What would you print first if you ran a shop?"}},{"k":"print_certificate","v":{"s":"🪶 Print Certificate"}},{"k":"retake_quiz","v":{"s":"Retake quiz"}},{"k":"back_to_menu_2","v":{"s":"← Back to menu"}},{"k":"review_these_specific_gaps","v":{"s":"📍 Review these specific gaps"}},{"k":"module","v":{"s":"Module"}},{"k":"review","v":{"s":"Review →"}},{"k":"your_answer","v":{"s":"Your answer: "}},{"k":"right_answer_2","v":{"s":"Right answer: "}},{"k":"question","v":{"s":"Question "}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":" · score "}},{"k":"try_again_4","v":{"s":"↺ Try again"}},{"k":"ai_tutor_is_not_available_in_this_buil","v":{"s":"AI tutor is not available in this build. Try the modules or Resources tab."}},{"k":"type_any_printing_press_question_the_a","v":{"s":"Type any printing-press question. The AI grounds its answer in the same sources cited throughout this lab. "}},{"k":"educational_only","v":{"s":"Educational only."}},{"k":"how_to_get_a_richer_answer_click_to_ex","v":{"s":"💡 How to get a richer answer (click to expand)"}},{"k":"a_short_broad_question_gets_a_short_br","v":{"s":"A short, broad question gets a short, broad answer. A specific question with a frame gets a thoughtful one. Three patterns that work well:"}},{"k":"weaker","v":{"s":"Weaker"}},{"k":"stronger","v":{"s":"Stronger"}},{"k":"why_it_works","v":{"s":"Why it works: "}},{"k":"this_is_a_real_skill_called_prompt_eng","v":{"s":"This is a real skill called \"prompt engineering\" that adults are now paid to do. Practice it here, and you have a skill that transfers everywhere you talk to an AI for the rest of your life."}},{"k":"or_click_a_starter_question","v":{"s":"Or click a starter question:"}},{"k":"your_question","v":{"s":"Your question:"}},{"k":"e_g_how_did_aldus_manutius_decide_what","v":{"s":"e.g. \"How did Aldus Manutius decide what books to print first?\" or \"What was a 1450 type-caster's daily routine?\""}},{"k":"clear_2","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"clear_history","v":{"s":"Clear history"}},{"k":"see_a_working_press","v":{"s":"See a working press"}},{"k":"international_printing_museum_carson_c_2","v":{"s":"International Printing Museum (Carson, CA) — Working Gutenberg replica + tours"}},{"k":"gutenberg_museum_mainz_germany_origina","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Museum (Mainz, Germany) — Original Bibles + working presses"}},{"k":"smithsonian_graphic_arts_collection_2","v":{"s":"Smithsonian — Graphic Arts Collection"}},{"k":"briar_press_find_a_letterpress_shop_ne","v":{"s":"Briar Press — Find a letterpress shop near you"}},{"k":"primary_sources_online","v":{"s":"Primary sources online"}},{"k":"british_library_gutenberg_bible_full_d","v":{"s":"British Library — Gutenberg Bible (full digital facsimile)"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_pre_1800_american_","v":{"s":"Library of Congress — Pre-1800 American Imprints"}},{"k":"bodleian_library_broadside_ballads","v":{"s":"Bodleian Library — Broadside Ballads"}},{"k":"cheongju_early_printing_museum_korea_j_2","v":{"s":"Cheongju Early Printing Museum (Korea) — Jikji (1377, oldest extant metal movable-type book)"}},{"k":"scholarship","v":{"s":"Scholarship"}},{"k":"elizabeth_eisenstein_the_printing_pres_3","v":{"s":"Elizabeth Eisenstein, \"The Printing Press as an Agent of Change\" (1980) — the foundational scholarly account"}},{"k":"adrian_johns_the_nature_of_the_book_pr_3","v":{"s":"Adrian Johns, \"The Nature of the Book: Print and Knowledge in the Making\" (1998)"}},{"k":"andrew_pettegree_the_book_in_the_renai_2","v":{"s":"Andrew Pettegree, \"The Book in the Renaissance\" (2010)"}},{"k":"eltjo_buringh_jan_luiten_van_zanden_bo_2","v":{"s":"Eltjo Buringh & Jan Luiten van Zanden — Book production data 800-1800"}},{"k":"typography","v":{"s":"Typography"}},{"k":"robert_bringhurst_the_elements_of_typo_3","v":{"s":"Robert Bringhurst, \"The Elements of Typographic Style\" (4th ed., 2013) — the standard reference"}},{"k":"marshall_mcluhan_the_gutenberg_galaxy__2","v":{"s":"Marshall McLuhan, \"The Gutenberg Galaxy\" (1962)"}},{"k":"type_cooper_typography_history_and_edu","v":{"s":"Type@Cooper — Typography history and education"}},{"k":"recovering_women_printers","v":{"s":"Recovering women printers"}},{"k":"susan_broomhall_work_on_women_in_early","v":{"s":"Susan Broomhall — Work on women in early modern French print"}},{"k":"helwi_blom_recent_scholarship_on_women","v":{"s":"Helwi Blom — Recent scholarship on women printers in early modern Europe"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_charlotte_guillard","v":{"s":"Library of Congress — Charlotte Guillard biography"}},{"k":"maine_archives_places_to_visit","v":{"s":"Maine archives & places to visit"}},{"k":"maine_historical_society_portland_falm","v":{"s":"Maine Historical Society (Portland) — Falmouth Gazette issues, 19th-c Maine newspapers, exhibits open to school groups"}},{"k":"wadsworth_longfellow_house_portland_lo","v":{"s":"Wadsworth-Longfellow House (Portland) — Longfellow family papers, early Portland press connections; walking distance from King Middle"}},{"k":"bowdoin_college_library_special_collec","v":{"s":"Bowdoin College Library Special Collections (Brunswick) — early American imprints, Maine pamphlets, periodicals"}},{"k":"bates_college_edmund_s_muskie_archives","v":{"s":"Bates College Edmund S. Muskie Archives (Lewiston) — political papers, Maine journalism history"}},{"k":"maine_state_archives_augusta_colonial_","v":{"s":"Maine State Archives (Augusta) — colonial-era documents, original Maine constitutional materials (1820)"}},{"k":"colby_college_special_collections_wate","v":{"s":"Colby College Special Collections (Waterville) — Maine literature manuscripts; Edwin Arlington Robinson papers"}},{"k":"penobscot_marine_museum_searsport_hist","v":{"s":"Penobscot Marine Museum (Searsport) — historic broadsides relating to coastal Maine shipping and ports"}},{"k":"university_of_maine_fogler_library_oro","v":{"s":"University of Maine Fogler Library (Orono) — extensive Maine periodicals collection; the largest run of pre-1900 Maine newspapers in the state"}},{"k":"portland_public_library_portland_room_","v":{"s":"Portland Public Library — Portland Room — Portland-specific materials including 19th- and 20th-c newspapers on microfilm"}},{"k":"every_museum_library_and_book_cited_in","v":{"s":"Every museum, library, and book cited in this lab. If you are within driving distance of one of the working-press museums, go. Holding a 30-pound platen and pulling the bar yourself is a different category of understanding than reading about it."}},{"k":"period_vocabulary_glossary","v":{"s":"📖 Period vocabulary glossary"}},{"k":"every_period_term_used_in_this_lab_pri","v":{"s":"Every period term used in this lab. Print this page and you have a one-sheet reference for the whole tool. Alphabetized for fast scan."}},{"k":"use_your_browser_s_print_menu_cmd_ctrl","v":{"s":"Use your browser's print menu (Cmd/Ctrl + P) on this Resources page to capture the glossary as a one-sheet handout."}},{"k":"next_steps_if_this_lab_caught_you","v":{"s":"🛤️ Next steps if this lab caught you"}},{"k":"you_finished_the_tool_the_most_rewardi","v":{"s":"You finished the tool. The most rewarding learning happens off the screen. Pick one or two from this list and actually do them."}},{"k":"international_printing_museum_carson_c_3","v":{"s":"International Printing Museum (Carson, CA) runs Gutenberg demonstrations on real reconstructions. Gutenberg Museum (Mainz) has originals. Neither requires special arrangement."}},{"k":"linotype_the_film_2012_on_streaming_co","v":{"s":"\"Linotype: The Film\" (2012, on streaming) covers the Mergenthaler Linotype, the machine that changed newspapers for 80 years. Wonderfully nerdy. The machines still run at hobbyist shops."}},{"k":"pull_any_hardback_off_a_shelf_look_at_","v":{"s":"Pull any hardback off a shelf. Look at the title page, the colophon (back of the title page), the type, the leading. You now know what every choice means."}},{"k":"briar_press_maintains_a_directory_of_w","v":{"s":"Briar Press maintains a directory of working letterpress shops worldwide. Many offer workshops. In Maine: try AS220 Industries (Providence, drivable) or look up Down East letterpress practitioners."}},{"k":"pick_a_sign_a_book_an_app_a_logo_try_t","v":{"s":"Pick a sign, a book, an app, a logo. Try to identify the typeface. Use Fonts In Use (fontsinuse.com) or the Identifont question-based tool. Keep doing this. Within a month you will start seeing typefaces everywhere."}},{"k":"tobias_frere_jones_erik_spiekermann_je","v":{"s":"Tobias Frere-Jones, Erik Spiekermann, Jessica Hische, Tracy Jenkins. They share work-in-progress sketches, talk about historical references, and post the kind of behind-the-scenes craft that schools rarely show."}},{"k":"open_glyphr_studio_free_browser_based_","v":{"s":"Open Glyphr Studio (free, browser-based) or FontForge (free, desktop). Design the lowercase \"a\" for your own typeface. You will fail your first 10 attempts. You will learn more about type than from any book."}},{"k":"take_the_broadside_you_composed_in_the","v":{"s":"Take the broadside you composed in the Build a Broadside module, print it on heavy paper (cardstock), and post it somewhere public with permission. Watch what happens. This is what a 1450 broadside was for."}},{"k":"the_british_library_has_the_entire_gut","v":{"s":"The British Library has the entire Gutenberg Bible online as a high-resolution facsimile. Spend 15 minutes scrolling. Try to read a line of Blackletter. Notice what you can and cannot decode."}},{"k":"a_skill_built_by_holding_a_30_pound_pl","v":{"s":"A skill built by holding a 30-pound platen is qualitatively different from a skill built by reading about one. Even a single hands-on session at a working press shifts everything you understood from this lab."}},{"k":"cite_this_tool","v":{"s":"📝 Cite this tool"}},{"k":"for_lesson_plans_student_bibliographie","v":{"s":"For lesson plans, student bibliographies, or teaching portfolios:"}},{"k":"printingpress_an_interactive_printing_","v":{"s":"PrintingPress: an interactive printing-press history and simulation tool. AlloFlow STEAM Lab (Pomeranz, A., "}},{"k":"retrieved","v":{"s":"). Retrieved "}},{"k":"from_alloflow_project","v":{"s":" from https://github.com/Apomera/AlloFlow."}},{"k":"korea","v":{"s":"Korea"}},{"k":"china","v":{"s":"China"}},{"k":"japan","v":{"s":"Japan"}},{"k":"the_islamic_world","v":{"s":"The Islamic World"}},{"k":"india","v":{"s":"India"}},{"k":"africa","v":{"s":"Africa"}},{"k":"pre_columbian_americas","v":{"s":"Pre-Columbian Americas"}},{"k":"wax_casting_similar_to_gutenberg_was_a","v":{"s":"Wax casting (similar to Gutenberg) was already mature in Korea. The Korean process: carve type characters in beech wood → press into a wax bowl to create matrices → pour molten bronze into the matrix cavities → finish and stack into a forme (composing tray). The technical sophistication was already there decades before any plausible knowledge could have reached Europe."}},{"k":"several_reasons_none_flattering_to_the","v":{"s":"Several reasons, none flattering to the Eurocentric historical record: (1) the Korean tradition stayed largely within state + religious use rather than seeding a commercial print boom; (2) Korean printing did not coincide with the Reformation, scientific revolution, or rise of vernacular publishing as Gutenberg's did; (3) Western historiography written in the 1700-1900s simply did not include Korea in the global narrative; (4) the Jikji itself spent most of the modern era in a single Paris collection rather than in active scholarship. Recognition started shifting in 1972 when UNESCO's Year of the Book brought Park Byeong-seon's research on the Jikji to global attention."}},{"k":"it_is_fair_to_say_korean_printing_exis","v":{"s":"It is fair to say Korean printing existed first; it is not fair to say it would have done what Gutenberg's did. The Korean tradition served different needs (state, temple, scholarly) in a different social structure. Gutenberg arrived at a moment when European cities had merchant classes, growing literacy, a Latin-reading church-and-university audience, and the social fuel for an information revolution. Same technology in different soil produces different harvests. Korean printing was a parallel invention, not a path not taken."}},{"k":"wang_zhen_1290_1333_was_a_chinese_loca","v":{"s":"Wang Zhen (~1290-1333) was a Chinese local magistrate who applied movable type to an agricultural treatise, the Nong Shu (Book of Agriculture). He developed: (1) more durable wooden type (vs Bi Sheng's clay), (2) a rotating round composing table — type sorted into wedge-shaped compartments around a vertical axle, so the compositor could spin to find characters faster, (3) a systematic character classification scheme by rhyme group. The composing table was a workflow innovation Western printers would not match for centuries."}},{"k":"the_standard_answer_chinese_script_nee","v":{"s":"The standard answer: Chinese script needs thousands of characters (Latin needs only 26 letters + punctuation), so movable type is much more expensive to cast and harder to compose. Real, but partial."}},{"k":"other_factors_1_china_already_had_a_ma","v":{"s":"Other factors: (1) China already had a mature woodblock industry producing books cheaply; (2) Chinese scholarly culture revered handwriting + calligraphy in ways that resisted mechanical reproduction; (3) imperial centralization meant the state controlled what got printed (no Reformation, no merchant pamphlets); (4) no equivalent of Latin-as-elite-vernacular meant no widespread literate demand bridging social classes. The technology existed; the social fuel did not."}},{"k":"ukiyo_e_pictures_of_the_floating_world","v":{"s":"Ukiyo-e (\"pictures of the floating world\") were full-color woodblock prints made by teams: designer drew the master image, carvers cut a separate block for each color, printer registered each block onto the same paper in sequence. A single complex Hokusai print might use 15-20 blocks. The technical sophistication was unmatched globally until photogravure in the 1880s."}},{"k":"katsushika_hokusai_s_the_great_wave_of","v":{"s":"Katsushika Hokusai's \"The Great Wave off Kanagawa\" (1830-1832) was made with around 8 blocks, printed in editions of about 5,000-8,000 over several years. Sold cheaply (about the price of a bowl of noodles), it was mass-market entertainment, not high art. When Japanese ukiyo-e arrived in Europe in the 1850s-60s, they shocked artists like Whistler, Monet, and Van Gogh — sparking japonisme and reshaping Western painting."}},{"k":"i_brahim_m_teferrika_1670_1747_was_a_h","v":{"s":"İbrahim Müteferrika (~1670-1747) was a Hungarian-born Ottoman convert + scholar who finally won permission from Sultan Ahmed III in 1727 to open the first Turkish-language printing press. The fatwa permitting it was carefully narrow: he could print on history, geography, dictionaries, and sciences — but not religious texts. The press printed 17 titles before Müteferrika's death; output was modest. Print expansion really accelerated only in the 19th century."}},{"k":"this_is_a_hotly_debated_claim_often_de","v":{"s":"This is a hotly debated claim, often deployed politically. The honest scholarly answer: print delay was one factor among many in Islamic technological + economic shifts from 1500-1800, but it is reductive to treat it as the cause. The Islamic world had complex book culture (handwritten manuscripts were excellent + numerous), regional differences (printing happened earlier in some places), and economic dynamics that involved trade routes, taxation, urbanization, and political organization. The print question is interesting; the \"Islamic world declined because of it\" framing usually oversimplifies."}},{"k":"the_first_printing_press_in_india_was_","v":{"s":"The first printing press in India was set up by the Jesuit mission at Goa in 1556 — originally intended for Ethiopia but kept at Goa when that mission was delayed. By 1557 it was printing in Portuguese. Indian-language printing followed: Tamil script type was cast (in metal) by a Spanish Jesuit named João de Faria in 1577 — the first non-Latin-script printing in Asia. Tamil works followed within years."}},{"k":"by_1900_india_had_hundreds_of_presses_","v":{"s":"By 1900 India had hundreds of presses publishing in dozens of languages — Bengali was the literary powerhouse, but Tamil, Marathi, Gujarati, Hindi, and Urdu had vibrant newspaper + book cultures. Indian printing took on its own character: bilingual editions, devotional pamphlets in vast quantities, the rise of mass-circulation newspapers in the 1880s-1920s tied to the nationalist movement. By the 1947 independence, print was central to political organizing — Gandhi's Harijan, Tilak's Kesari, Nehru's many works were all critical political infrastructure."}},{"k":"napoleon_s_1798_invasion_of_egypt_brou","v":{"s":"Napoleon's 1798 invasion of Egypt brought the first major Arabic-script printing press to North Africa (the Imprimerie de l'Armée d'Orient). The press was originally for French propaganda + military use but its existence accelerated Egyptian + Ottoman adoption of print. Muhammad Ali Pasha (Egyptian ruler 1805-1848) established the Bulaq Press in 1820 — Egypt's first government press. By 1850, Cairo + Beirut had become Arabic-language publishing centers; Arabic newspapers + journals proliferated from 1860s onward."}},{"k":"west_africa_had_a_deep_arabic_script_m","v":{"s":"West Africa had a deep Arabic-script manuscript tradition centered on Timbuktu (Mali) and Djenné. The Mali Empire + Songhai Empire (1200s-1500s) supported scholarly culture: Sankore + Djinguereber mosques in Timbuktu functioned as universities. Tens of thousands of Arabic-language manuscripts in mathematics, astronomy, jurisprudence, medicine, history. Many still survive in private family libraries; major preservation effort began in the 2000s after Mali political instability threatened the collections. This is \"writing without print\" but not without scholarship."}},{"k":"the_aztec_mexica_empire_maintained_ext","v":{"s":"The Aztec (Mexica) empire maintained extensive book traditions: tribute records, historical chronicles, religious texts, divinatory almanacs, land deeds. After conquest, some traditions continued under Spanish supervision — the Florentine Codex (1545-1590), compiled by Franciscan friar Bernardino de Sahagún with Nahua scholars, is a 12-volume ethnographic encyclopedia in Nahuatl + Spanish. It is the most extensive surviving documentation of pre-conquest Mesoamerican civilization. Sahagún's collaborators (named in the text) deserve as much credit as he does."}},{"k":"bishop_diego_de_landa_yucat_n_1562_ord","v":{"s":"Bishop Diego de Landa (Yucatán, 1562) ordered the burning of Maya codices at Maní — perhaps 5,000 or more books destroyed in a single event. The Aztec library at Texcoco was burned earlier (1530s) by Bishop Juan de Zumárraga. Inca quipu collections were repeatedly burned through the 1500s-1600s."}},{"k":"landa_later_wrote_his_own_ethnographic","v":{"s":"Landa later wrote his own ethnographic account (\"Relación de las cosas de Yucatán\", 1566), which became the main scholarly source for early Maya studies — meaning a primary record of Maya civilization comes filtered through the man who destroyed its books."}},{"k":"pick_a_region","v":{"s":"Pick a region:"}},{"k":"global_print_history_at_a_glance","v":{"s":"Global print history at a glance"}},{"k":"each_dot_is_one_event_in_print_history","v":{"s":"Each dot is one event in print history, colored by region. The Gutenberg Bible (1455) is one mark among many."}},{"k":"jikji_entry_unesco_memory_of_the_world","v":{"s":"Jikji entry, UNESCO Memory of the World register (2001)"}},{"k":"tsien_tsuen_hsuin_paper_and_printing_i","v":{"s":"Tsien Tsuen-Hsuin, \"Paper and Printing\" in Joseph Needham, Science and Civilisation in China vol. 5 part 1 (1985)"}},{"k":"christopher_reed_gutenberg_in_shanghai","v":{"s":"Christopher Reed, Gutenberg in Shanghai: Chinese Print Capitalism, 1876-1937 (2004)"}},{"k":"peter_burke_a_social_history_of_knowle","v":{"s":"Peter Burke, A Social History of Knowledge: from Gutenberg to Diderot (2000) — global comparative framing"}},{"k":"encyclopedia_britannica_m_teferrika_ib","v":{"s":"Encyclopedia Britannica, \"Müteferrika, Ibrahim\""}},{"k":"british_library_codex_dresden_facsimil","v":{"s":"British Library Codex Dresden facsimile + scholarship"}},{"k":"ge_ez_script_scholarship_via_beta_ma_f","v":{"s":"Ge'ez script scholarship via Beta Maṣāḥǝft project, Universität Hamburg"}},{"k":"overview","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"history","v":{"s":"History"}},{"k":"the_rag_trade","v":{"s":"The rag trade"}},{"k":"the_process_2","v":{"s":"The process"}},{"k":"calculator","v":{"s":"Calculator"}},{"k":"paper_grades","v":{"s":"Paper grades"}},{"k":"economics","v":{"s":"Economics"}},{"k":"collect_rags","v":{"s":"Collect rags"}},{"k":"rag_pickers_rag_and_bone_men_collect_w","v":{"s":"Rag pickers + rag-and-bone-men collect worn-out linen, hemp, and cotton textiles from households, hospitals, and ships. Linen is best — long, strong fibers. Wool is useless (felts when beaten). Quality is sorted at collection — white linen separately from coarse hemp."}},{"k":"retting_boiling","v":{"s":"Retting + boiling"}},{"k":"rags_are_sorted_cut_and_soaked_in_lime","v":{"s":"Rags are sorted, cut, and soaked in lime water for days to weeks (\"retting\") to break down sizing + soften fibers. Then boiled in lye for several hours to remove dirt + lignin. The boiling smell of a 15th-century paper mill was famously terrible — half the reason mills were built downstream of towns."}},{"k":"beating_to_pulp","v":{"s":"Beating to pulp"}},{"k":"the_retted_rags_are_beaten_in_a_water_","v":{"s":"The retted rags are beaten in a water-powered stamper mill — heavy wooden hammers tipped with iron, dropping into a stone trough of rag-and-water. Beating cycles: 12-30 hours depending on paper grade. The fibers separate + fibrillate, and the slurry becomes \"pulp\" or \"stuff.\""}},{"k":"forming_the_sheet","v":{"s":"Forming the sheet"}},{"k":"the_vatman_dips_a_wire_mesh_mould_a_wo","v":{"s":"The vatman dips a wire-mesh mould (a wooden frame with a metal-wire screen) into the pulp vat, lifts it level, shakes it side-to-side (\"the shake\") to interlock fibers, and tilts to drain water. The pulp settles into a thin even sheet on the wire. Skill takes years — uneven shake = uneven paper."}},{"k":"couching_pressing","v":{"s":"Couching + pressing"}},{"k":"the_coucher_carefully_transfers_couche","v":{"s":"The coucher carefully transfers (couches) the wet sheet off the mould onto a felt blanket. Sheets + felts are stacked into a \"post\" of ~144 sheets, then squeezed in a screw press to drive out water. The post emerges as wet but cohesive paper."}},{"k":"loft_drying_sizing","v":{"s":"Loft drying + sizing"}},{"k":"sheets_are_hung_individually_in_a_loft","v":{"s":"Sheets are hung individually in a loft to air-dry for days. Then dipped in animal-glue sizing (made from boiled bone/hide scraps) to make paper non-absorbent enough for writing/printing. Final pressing + finishing produces the marketable sheet."}},{"k":"white_linen_premium","v":{"s":"White linen (premium)"}},{"k":"pure_white_linen_rags_bedsheets_shirts","v":{"s":"Pure white linen rags — bedsheets, shirts. Best fiber, highest yield, premium paper."}},{"k":"mixed_linen_cotton","v":{"s":"Mixed linen + cotton"}},{"k":"standard_mix_most_paper_of_the_era_sol","v":{"s":"Standard mix — most paper of the era. Solid commercial grade."}},{"k":"cotton_heavy_mix","v":{"s":"Cotton-heavy mix"}},{"k":"more_cotton_shorter_fibers_newspaper_g","v":{"s":"More cotton, shorter fibers. Newspaper-grade if you had to compare to modern."}},{"k":"rough_hemp_canvas","v":{"s":"Rough hemp + canvas"}},{"k":"coarse_fibers_dark_color_wrapping_pape","v":{"s":"Coarse fibers, dark color. Wrapping paper, cheapest grades."}},{"k":"chancery_12_17","v":{"s":"Chancery (12×17\")"}},{"k":"royal_19_24","v":{"s":"Royal (19×24\")"}},{"k":"imperial_22_30","v":{"s":"Imperial (22×30\")"}},{"k":"most_histories_of_the_print_revolution","v":{"s":"Most histories of the print revolution focus on the press itself — the screw, the type, the ink. They underrate the supply chain. Paper had to travel a 1,300-year route from Cai Lun (China, 105 CE) through Samarkand, Baghdad, Cordoba, Fabriano in Italy (1276), Nuremberg (1390), and finally to Mainz before Gutenberg could afford to print 180 copies of a 1,272-page Bible. Every step of that journey is a story."}},{"k":"1_water_powered_stampers_mediterranean","v":{"s":"1. Water-powered stampers. Mediterranean Italian mountain streams could drive heavy wooden hammers. This replaced hand-beating with a 24-hour mechanical process. Output: roughly 5x what hand-beating could produce."}},{"k":"2_animal_glue_gelatin_sizing_made_pape","v":{"s":"2. Animal-glue (gelatin) sizing. Made paper non-absorbent enough for writing with iron-gall ink and for printing with oil-based ink. Critical — earlier Arabic papers used starch sizing which absorbed too much ink."}},{"k":"3_watermarks_wire_designs_sewn_onto_th","v":{"s":"3. Watermarks. Wire designs sewn onto the mould left a faint translucent image in each sheet. Originally a mill's quality + provenance signature. Today the principal way historians date and authenticate undated period documents."}},{"k":"a_1500_european_town_of_10_000_people_","v":{"s":"A 1500 European town of 10,000 people produced perhaps 20-40 tonnes of recyclable rag per year. Bremen, Hamburg, Nuremberg, Venice, Lyon all had organized rag merchants by 1450. Larger paper mills sent buyers as far as 200+ miles from the mill, especially during periods of paper-price spikes. The poorer classes who turned over rags for paper were unknowingly funding the Reformation, the scientific revolution, the rise of the newspaper."}},{"k":"rag_piles_paper_mills_were_repeatedly_","v":{"s":"Rag piles + paper mills were repeatedly identified as cholera + typhus vectors during 19th-century epidemics. The 1832 cholera outbreak in Europe spread along rag-collection routes; the 1854 London Broad Street outbreak had multiple sources, but rag mills were among the suspects (later research confirmed water, not rags)."}},{"k":"by_1880s_most_european_states_required","v":{"s":"By 1880s most European states required boiling or chemical disinfection of rag shipments. The \"rag trade\" survived as a regulated industry until mechanical wood-pulp papermaking (1860s onward) eventually displaced rag paper for most commercial use."}},{"k":"maine_had_multiple_rag_based_paper_mil","v":{"s":"Maine had multiple rag-based paper mills in the 1800s — Yarmouth Paper Mill, Eldridge Mill (Pittsfield), and several along the Kennebec. Maine's shift from rag to wood-pulp papermaking in the late 1800s — driven by the abundance of spruce + the technology of Hugh Burgess and J. C. Mengel — is one chapter in why central Maine became a paper-mill economy that lasted into the 21st century. Modern Sappi (Westbrook + Skowhegan) descended from these wood-pulp pioneers."}},{"k":"previous_stage","v":{"s":"← Previous stage"}},{"k":"10_lb","v":{"s":"10 lb"}},{"k":"500_lb","v":{"s":"500 lb"}},{"k":"rag_quality","v":{"s":"Rag quality:"}},{"k":"target_sheet_size","v":{"s":"Target sheet size:"}},{"k":"paper_yield","v":{"s":"Paper yield"}},{"k":"sheets_produced","v":{"s":"Sheets produced"}},{"k":"paper_quality","v":{"s":"Paper quality"}},{"k":"labor_needed","v":{"s":"Labor needed"}},{"k":"beat_form_dry_size","v":{"s":"beat + form + dry + size"}},{"k":"what_this_output_could_print","v":{"s":"What this output could print:"}},{"k":"a_watermark_is_a_faint_translucent_des","v":{"s":"A watermark is a faint translucent design pressed into paper while still wet. Made by sewing a wire shape onto the wire mould — fibers settle thinner where the wire raises them, leaving a \"shadow\" visible when held to light. Watermarks served as: (1) the mill's mark of ownership, (2) a quality guarantee (rep depended on the mark), and (3) the buyer's way of telling grades apart at-a-glance. Today, watermarks are still the principal way historians date and authenticate period documents — different mills used different marks in different years, and the catalog (e.g., Briquet's Les Filigranes, 1907) is the standard reference."}},{"k":"year","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"paper_sheet_cost_1_chancery","v":{"s":"Paper sheet cost (1 chancery)"}},{"k":"comparable_parchment_cost","v":{"s":"Comparable parchment cost"}},{"k":"paper_as_of_parchment","v":{"s":"Paper as % of parchment"}},{"k":"approximate_florentine_prices_denarii_","v":{"s":"Approximate Florentine prices, denarii (1/240 lira). Exact prices varied across cities + over time. Source: Carlo Cipolla, \"Money in Sixteenth-Century Florence\" + comparative studies."}},{"k":"rag_paper_held_the_market_for_600_year","v":{"s":"Rag paper held the market for ~600 years (1276 Fabriano to ~1880). The next paper revolution was wood-pulp papermaking — invented in stages by Friedrich Gottlob Keller (Germany, 1844, ground wood) and Hugh Burgess + Charles Watt (Britain + USA, 1854, chemical pulping). Wood pulp was vastly cheaper than rag, kicked off the mass-circulation newspaper era of 1870-1920, and made paper price collapse another order of magnitude. Maine's paper-mill industry (Sappi, Verso) descends from this wood-pulp tradition."}},{"k":"dard_hunter_papermaking_the_history_an","v":{"s":"Dard Hunter, Papermaking: The History and Technique of an Ancient Craft (1943, reprint 1978)"}},{"k":"tsien_tsuen_hsuin_paper_and_printing_i_2","v":{"s":"Tsien Tsuen-Hsuin, \"Paper and Printing\" in Joseph Needham, Science and Civilisation in China vol. 5 part 1 (1985)"}},{"k":"carlo_cipolla_money_in_sixteenth_centu","v":{"s":"Carlo Cipolla, Money in Sixteenth-Century Florence (1989)"}},{"k":"briquet_les_filigranes_standard_waterm","v":{"s":"Briquet, Les Filigranes — standard watermark catalog (1907, still in use)"}},{"k":"robert_l_mott_hand_papermaking_magazin","v":{"s":"Robert L. Mott, Hand Papermaking Magazine archives"}},{"k":"overview_2","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"history_2","v":{"s":"History"}},{"k":"folding","v":{"s":"Folding"}},{"k":"gathering","v":{"s":"Gathering"}},{"k":"sewing","v":{"s":"Sewing"}},{"k":"binding","v":{"s":"Binding"}},{"k":"decoration","v":{"s":"Decoration"}},{"k":"calculator_2","v":{"s":"Calculator"}},{"k":"folio_2","v":{"s":"Folio (2°)"}},{"k":"quarto_4","v":{"s":"Quarto (4°)"}},{"k":"octavo_8","v":{"s":"Octavo (8°)"}},{"k":"duodecimo_12","v":{"s":"Duodecimo (12°)"}},{"k":"sextodecimo_16","v":{"s":"Sextodecimo (16°)"}},{"k":"full_leather","v":{"s":"Full leather"}},{"k":"calf_goat_or_sheep_skin_covering_entir","v":{"s":"Calf, goat, or sheep skin covering entire book. Often gilded, embossed, gold-lettered. Top of the bookbinding craft — used for Bibles, legal volumes, libraries."}},{"k":"half_leather","v":{"s":"Half leather"}},{"k":"leather_spine_corners_paper_or_cloth_c","v":{"s":"Leather spine + corners, paper or cloth covered boards. The 1800s-1900s middle-class standard. Affordable but durable."}},{"k":"cloth_publisher","v":{"s":"Cloth (publisher)"}},{"k":"bookcloth_calico_or_buckram_over_hardb","v":{"s":"Bookcloth (calico or buckram) over hardboard. Mass production after ~1820. Modern hardcover descends from this."}},{"k":"paper_boards","v":{"s":"Paper boards"}},{"k":"paper_covered_hardboard_or_pulpboard_s","v":{"s":"Paper-covered hardboard or pulpboard. School texts, cheap novels. Wears out fast."}},{"k":"paperback_wraps","v":{"s":"Paperback (wraps)"}},{"k":"paper_wrapper_glued_or_stitched_1500s_","v":{"s":"Paper wrapper glued or stitched. 1500s pamphlets and 20th-century mass-market paperbacks both fit here."}},{"k":"hand_bookbinding_often_called_fine_bin","v":{"s":"Hand bookbinding (often called \"fine binding\") never died. Practitioners working in the 1500-style sewn-board-leather tradition exist today at conservation labs (Library of Congress, British Library), at private artisan studios, and in book arts programs (North Bennet Street School in Boston, the Center for Book Arts in New York, Penland in North Carolina). A high-end hand binding still takes weeks + costs $300-$3,000 per book. Industrial case-bound + perfect-bound books are produced by the million on automated lines for $0.50-$3 each. Both descend from the medieval codex."}},{"k":"fold_type","v":{"s":"Fold type:"}},{"k":"1_sheet","v":{"s":"1 sheet"}},{"k":"aldus_manutius_of_venice_1452_1515_is_","v":{"s":"Aldus Manutius of Venice (1452-1515) is credited with popularizing the octavo edition for serious literature. Before Aldus, classical Latin + Greek texts were always printed in folio or quarto — large, expensive, library-bound. Starting around 1501, Aldus printed classical texts in compact octavo editions priced for individual scholars + traveling clerics. The Aldine octavo is the direct ancestor of the modern paperback. Cost: roughly 1/8 of an equivalent folio. Result: an explosion in personal libraries among the educated middle class."}},{"k":"a_second_order_checking_device_the_cat","v":{"s":"A second order-checking device: the catchword. At the bottom of every right-hand page, the printer set the first word of the next page. The binder (or reader) flipping pages could verify the next page matched the catchword — and would catch a missing page or signature instantly. Catchwords were standard from ~1450-1800 then gradually dropped. Some scholarly + rare-book reprints still include them as period authenticity."}},{"k":"even_with_signatures_catchwords_misbin","v":{"s":"Even with signatures + catchwords, misbinding happens. Several copies of the Gutenberg Bible (1455) have known binding errors — pages out of order, missing leaves, duplicate sheets — all from binders working too fast 500 years ago. These errors are now prized: they help scholars trace which copy was bound where and when. The same is true for many famous editions: misbound copies of Shakespeare's First Folio (1623), Newton's Principia (1687), and Darwin's Origin of Species (1859) all exist + are catalogued."}},{"k":"a_skilled_bookbinder_can_sew_a_40_sign","v":{"s":"A skilled bookbinder can sew a 40-signature book on raised cords in 2-4 hours. Modern hand-binders typically allow a full day for sewing alone on a quality book. Pamphlets stab-sewn take 5-10 minutes. Industrial Smyth-sewn machines (the standard for quality modern hardcovers) sew 30-50 books per hour. Adhesive perfect-binding lines run 2,000-10,000 books per hour. The technology has gotten dramatically faster + the books proportionally less durable."}},{"k":"binding_type","v":{"s":"Binding type:"}},{"k":"cost_relative","v":{"s":"Cost (relative)"}},{"k":"durability","v":{"s":"Durability"}},{"k":"labor_days","v":{"s":"Labor days"}},{"k":"32_pamphlet","v":{"s":"32 (pamphlet)"}},{"k":"1500_bible","v":{"s":"1500 (Bible)"}},{"k":"sheets_needed","v":{"s":"Sheets needed"}},{"k":"labor_days_2","v":{"s":"Labor days"}},{"k":"per_copy","v":{"s":"per copy"}},{"k":"relative_cost","v":{"s":"Relative cost"}},{"k":"expected_life","v":{"s":"Expected life"}},{"k":"with_care","v":{"s":"with care"}},{"k":"cost_is_relative_a_full_leather_bindin","v":{"s":"Cost is relative — a full-leather binding on a 1500-page folio runs about 100× a paperback on a 32-page pamphlet. Labor scales with both binding choice + page count. Expected life with normal handling reflects what conservation experience says: a properly-bound full-leather book from 1500 can still be read in 2026, while a paperback from 2000 may already be falling apart."}},{"k":"bernard_c_middleton_a_history_of_engli","v":{"s":"Bernard C. Middleton, A History of English Craft Bookbinding Technique (1996)"}},{"k":"david_pearson_english_bookbinding_styl","v":{"s":"David Pearson, English Bookbinding Styles 1450-1800 (2005)"}},{"k":"the_center_for_book_arts_nyc_tutorials","v":{"s":"The Center for Book Arts (NYC) tutorials + scholarship"}},{"k":"american_academy_of_bookbinding_worksh","v":{"s":"American Academy of Bookbinding workshops"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_conservation_divis","v":{"s":"Library of Congress Conservation Division — bookbinding resources"}},{"k":"overview_3","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"history_3","v":{"s":"History"}},{"k":"the_index","v":{"s":"The Index"}},{"k":"banned_books","v":{"s":"Banned books"}},{"k":"smuggling","v":{"s":"Smuggling"}},{"k":"should_you_print","v":{"s":"Should you print?"}},{"k":"era_guesser","v":{"s":"Era guesser"}},{"k":"modern_parallels","v":{"s":"Modern parallels"}},{"k":"a_33_year_old_augustinian_monk_named_m","v":{"s":"A 33-year-old Augustinian monk named Martin Luther has nailed 95 theses to the door of Castle Church, then sent you a manuscript expanding the argument. He is requesting 500 copies for distribution to bishops + universities. The Pope has not yet responded."}},{"k":"print_500_copies_as_requested","v":{"s":"Print 500 copies as requested"}},{"k":"refuse_the_manuscript","v":{"s":"Refuse the manuscript"}},{"k":"print_50_copies_privately_no_shop_name","v":{"s":"Print 50 copies privately, no shop name"}},{"k":"copernicus_has_sent_you_the_manuscript","v":{"s":"Copernicus has sent you the manuscript of \"De Revolutionibus Orbium Coelestium\" — a treatise arguing the Earth orbits the Sun. This contradicts the Aristotelian + biblical model in use everywhere. He has just died (he received the first printed copy on his deathbed). His friend Andreas Osiander has added an unauthorized preface calling the heliocentric model \"a mere mathematical convenience.\""}},{"k":"print_as_osiander_prepared_with_the_di","v":{"s":"Print as Osiander prepared, with the disclaimer preface"}},{"k":"restore_copernicus_s_original_preface_","v":{"s":"Restore Copernicus's original preface (no disclaimer)"}},{"k":"refuse_too_risky","v":{"s":"Refuse — too risky"}},{"k":"galileo_galilei_has_been_sentenced_by_","v":{"s":"Galileo Galilei has been sentenced by the Roman Inquisition to house arrest. His Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems is on the Index. A Dutch agent approaches you with a smuggled copy of Galileo's next manuscript — \"Two New Sciences\" — covering mechanics + materials. The Inquisition has not yet condemned it. Galileo himself sent it via underground channels."}},{"k":"print_it_openly_in_leiden_protestant_n","v":{"s":"Print it openly in Leiden (Protestant Netherlands)"}},{"k":"print_without_galileo_s_name_hidden_im","v":{"s":"Print without Galileo's name, hidden imprint"}},{"k":"refuse_inquisition_might_extend_reach","v":{"s":"Refuse — Inquisition might extend reach"}},{"k":"jean_jacques_rousseau_has_sent_you_mil","v":{"s":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau has sent you \"Émile\" — a treatise on education that includes the Profession of Faith of the Savoyard Vicar, arguing for natural religion outside the Catholic Church. Geneva is Protestant + relatively tolerant, but other European states have condemned the book. The Sorbonne has issued a warrant against Rousseau."}},{"k":"print_ship_europe_wide","v":{"s":"Print + ship Europe-wide"}},{"k":"print_for_geneva_only","v":{"s":"Print for Geneva only"}},{"k":"refuse_the_commission","v":{"s":"Refuse the commission"}},{"k":"a_27_year_old_exiled_prussian_named_ka","v":{"s":"A 27-year-old exiled Prussian named Karl Marx has approached you with a manuscript co-written with Friedrich Engels: \"The Holy Family.\" It critiques the Young Hegelian philosophers. Marx is already known to Prussian authorities + monitored by their intelligence service. Selling the book in German states is risky."}},{"k":"print_distribute_via_the_network_of_ge","v":{"s":"Print + distribute via the network of German political exile booksellers"}},{"k":"print_distribute_belgium_france_only","v":{"s":"Print, distribute Belgium + France only"}},{"k":"refuse_too_political","v":{"s":"Refuse — too political"}},{"k":"the_catcher_in_the_rye_j_d_salinger","v":{"s":"The Catcher in the Rye (J.D. Salinger)"}},{"k":"the_communist_manifesto_marx_engels","v":{"s":"The Communist Manifesto (Marx + Engels)"}},{"k":"galileo_s_dialogue_concerning_the_two_","v":{"s":"Galileo's Dialogue Concerning the Two Chief World Systems"}},{"k":"common_sense_thomas_paine","v":{"s":"Common Sense (Thomas Paine)"}},{"k":"animal_farm_george_orwell","v":{"s":"Animal Farm (George Orwell)"}},{"k":"tyndale_s_english_new_testament_1526","v":{"s":"Tyndale's English New Testament (1526)"}},{"k":"ulysses_james_joyce","v":{"s":"Ulysses (James Joyce)"}},{"k":"origin_of_species_charles_darwin","v":{"s":"Origin of Species (Charles Darwin)"}},{"k":"maus_art_spiegelman","v":{"s":"Maus (Art Spiegelman)"}},{"k":"1984_george_orwell","v":{"s":"1984 (George Orwell)"}},{"k":"by_1900_the_index_was_being_widely_ign","v":{"s":"By 1900 the Index was being widely ignored even by Catholic intellectuals. The 20th century made it unsustainable: too much rapid scholarly + scientific publishing for any committee to evaluate, growing acceptance that condemned books often were the books worth reading, increasing irrelevance of state enforcement."}},{"k":"in_1966_pope_paul_vi_formally_abolishe","v":{"s":"In 1966 Pope Paul VI formally abolished the Index. The Vatican stated that Catholics still had a moral obligation to avoid harmful reading, but the formal apparatus was gone. Today the Index is studied as a primary source — a 407-year map of what authorities most feared."}},{"k":"the_soci_t_typographique_de_neuch_tel_","v":{"s":"The Société typographique de Neuchâtel (STN) was a Swiss publishing house that operated 1769-1789, specializing in pirated + forbidden editions for the French market. Their internal records — saved nearly complete because they happened to survive — became the most-studied censorship-era publishing archive in the world. Princeton historian Robert Darnton spent decades documenting their work, especially in The Business of Enlightenment (1979) + The Forbidden Best-Sellers of Pre-Revolutionary France (1995). What did the STN sell? Rousseau, Voltaire, Diderot — the Enlightenment canon — plus a substantial volume of clandestine erotica + political libels. The mix tells us what French readers actually paid money for under censorship: serious philosophy + sex + scandal, in roughly equal measure."}},{"k":"the_soviet_samizdat_era_1956_1986_prod","v":{"s":"The Soviet samizdat era (~1956-1986) produced one of the most extraordinary clandestine literary movements in history. Banned works — Solzhenitsyn, Brodsky, Bulgakov, foreign authors like Orwell + Pasternak — were typed by hand on typewriters, often 5+ carbon copies per typing session. A reader received the book, was given a deadline (often 24-48 hours), then passed it on. A single original book might pass through hundreds of readers over months. Tamizdat (printed abroad and smuggled in) was the parallel pipeline. The accumulated samizdat literature kept entire bodies of work alive that the Soviet state had committed to erasing. After the Soviet fall, samizdat editions became collectors' items + primary historical sources."}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"what_actually_happened_in_history","v":{"s":"📖 What actually happened in history"}},{"k":"try_a_different_choice","v":{"s":"↺ Try a different choice"}},{"k":"reason_for_banning","v":{"s":"Reason for banning: "}},{"k":"when_where_was_this_banned","v":{"s":"When/where was this banned?"}},{"k":"right_answer_3","v":{"s":"Right answer: "}},{"k":"next_book","v":{"s":"Next book →"}},{"k":"start_over","v":{"s":"↺ Start over"}},{"k":"authorities_still_want_to_control_what","v":{"s":"Authorities still want to control what people read. Underground networks still find ways around it. Bans still often backfire (Streisand effect). The arguments for + against censorship rhyme strikingly across centuries."}},{"k":"scale_is_unrecognizable_billions_of_pi","v":{"s":"Scale is unrecognizable — billions of pieces of content per day vs hundreds of books per year. Speed of distribution is global + instant. Algorithmic suppression is invisible in ways print bans weren't. AI-generated content creates new questions (who is \"the author\" of an AI-generated text that gets banned?). Cross-border enforcement is harder + easier simultaneously."}},{"k":"index_librorum_prohibitorum_1559_1948_","v":{"s":"Index Librorum Prohibitorum (1559-1948 editions) — primary source"}},{"k":"robert_darnton_the_business_of_enlight","v":{"s":"Robert Darnton, The Business of Enlightenment: A Publishing History of the Encyclopédie 1775-1800 (1979)"}},{"k":"robert_darnton_the_forbidden_best_sell","v":{"s":"Robert Darnton, The Forbidden Best-Sellers of Pre-Revolutionary France (1995)"}},{"k":"american_library_association_state_of_","v":{"s":"American Library Association State of America's Libraries report (annual)"}},{"k":"pen_america_banned_books_list","v":{"s":"PEN America Banned Books List"}},{"k":"index_on_censorship_uk_quarterly_found","v":{"s":"Index on Censorship (UK quarterly, founded 1972)"}},{"k":"overview_4","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"why_it_took_150yr","v":{"s":"Why it took 150yr"}},{"k":"the_first_papers","v":{"s":"The first papers"}},{"k":"editor_decisions","v":{"s":"Editor decisions"}},{"k":"reading_old_papers","v":{"s":"Reading old papers"}},{"k":"the_news_business","v":{"s":"The news business"}},{"k":"modern_echo","v":{"s":"Modern echo"}},{"k":"relation_aller_f_rnemmen_und_gedenckw_","v":{"s":"Relation aller Fürnemmen und gedenckwürdigen Historien"}},{"k":"courante_uyt_italien_duytslandt_c","v":{"s":"Courante uyt Italien, Duytslandt, &c."}},{"k":"weekly_newes_from_italy_germanie_hunga","v":{"s":"Weekly Newes from Italy, Germanie, Hungaria, Bohemia, the Palatinate, France and the Low Countreys"}},{"k":"boston_news_letter","v":{"s":"Boston News-Letter"}},{"k":"king_george_ii_grants_an_audience_to_t","v":{"s":"King George II grants an audience to the Russian ambassador (3 weeks old)"}},{"k":"war_of_the_austrian_succession_french_","v":{"s":"War of the Austrian Succession: French defeat in Bohemia (4 weeks old)"}},{"k":"highway_robbery_on_the_road_to_bath_3_","v":{"s":"Highway robbery on the road to Bath (3 days old, dramatic detail)"}},{"k":"new_east_india_company_stock_subscript","v":{"s":"New East India Company stock subscription opening Monday (immediate commercial interest)"}},{"k":"bastille_storming_lead_prominent_type_","v":{"s":"Bastille storming lead, prominent type, \"From Paris\" dateline"}},{"k":"russian_tariff_lead_commercial_readers","v":{"s":"Russian tariff lead — commercial readers will pay closest attention"}},{"k":"equal_coverage_both_above_the_fold_no_","v":{"s":"Equal coverage, both above the fold, no clear lead"}},{"k":"hold_bastille_story_for_next_issue_ver","v":{"s":"Hold Bastille story for next issue — verify the news first"}},{"k":"drop_your_price_to_1_cent_shift_to_mas","v":{"s":"Drop your price to 1 cent + shift to mass-circulation content (crime, gossip, sensation)"}},{"k":"stay_at_6_cents_emphasize_political_an","v":{"s":"Stay at 6 cents + emphasize political analysis + foreign correspondence for elite readers"}},{"k":"drop_to_3_cents_keep_current_content_m","v":{"s":"Drop to 3 cents + keep current content mix"}},{"k":"wait_watch_see_if_penny_press_is_a_fad","v":{"s":"Wait + watch — see if penny press is a fad"}},{"k":"destruction_of_the_war_ship_maine_was_","v":{"s":"\"DESTRUCTION OF THE WAR SHIP MAINE WAS THE WORK OF AN ENEMY\" — bold, banner type"}},{"k":"uss_maine_destroyed_in_harbor_investig","v":{"s":"\"USS Maine Destroyed in Harbor; Investigation Pending\""}},{"k":"spanish_treachery_suspected_in_battles","v":{"s":"\"Spanish Treachery Suspected in Battleship Disaster\""}},{"k":"uss_maine_lost_in_havana_restrained_un","v":{"s":"\"USS Maine Lost in Havana\" — restrained until facts known"}},{"k":"cut_foreign_local_readers_care_more_ab","v":{"s":"Cut foreign — local readers care more about city hall. Wire services can cover foreign."}},{"k":"cut_metro_local_tv_new_digital_news_co","v":{"s":"Cut metro — local TV + new digital news cover city hall. Differentiate via foreign."}},{"k":"cut_both_by_half_try_to_keep_skeleton_","v":{"s":"Cut both by half + try to keep skeleton coverage"}},{"k":"refuse_to_cut_journalists_cut_from_pro","v":{"s":"Refuse to cut journalists — cut from production + marketing instead"}},{"k":"johann_carolus_had_been_writing_handwr","v":{"s":"Johann Carolus had been writing handwritten newsletters by hand for a small subscription audience (wealthy merchants + diplomats) since the 1590s. By 1604 he had so many subscribers that hand-copying was unsustainable. He bought a press, started printing the newsletter as the Relation, and petitioned the Strasbourg city council in late 1605 for a printing monopoly. The petition (which survives) is the founding document of the newspaper as a form."}},{"k":"within_20_years_similar_papers_had_app","v":{"s":"Within 20 years similar papers had appeared in Wolfenbüttel (1609), Amsterdam (1618), London (1622), Cologne (1620), Berlin (1626), Hamburg (1630), Stockholm (1645), and Boston (1704). The form spread fast once one printer had proven it worked."}},{"k":"sample_headlines_from_this_paper","v":{"s":"— Sample headlines from this paper —"}},{"k":"interesting","v":{"s":"🔍 Interesting: "}},{"k":"previous_era","v":{"s":"← Previous era"}},{"k":"next_era","v":{"s":"Next era →"}},{"k":"you_chose_2","v":{"s":"You chose: "}},{"k":"what_history_actually_did","v":{"s":"📜 What history actually did"}},{"k":"try_a_different_choice_2","v":{"s":"↺ Try a different choice"}},{"k":"per_the_northwestern_local_news_initia","v":{"s":"Per the Northwestern Local News Initiative + UNC Hussman School: the US lost more than 2,500 daily + weekly newspapers between 2005 and 2024 — roughly one-third of US local newsrooms. About 70 million Americans now live in \"news deserts\" or \"limited-news areas\" — counties with no local newspaper. Maine has lost smaller weeklies in many rural counties; the surviving papers have cut newsroom staff dramatically. The civic + democratic consequences of this collapse are still being measured."}},{"k":"across_every_era_of_news_disruption_ce","v":{"s":"Across every era of news disruption, certain things stay true. Audiences want information about what is happening + what it means. Bad-faith actors try to game the news system. Good journalism is expensive + hard to fund. Editorial judgment matters more than any technology. The same skills that mattered in 1605 — verifying sources, weighing significance, writing clearly under deadline, balancing access with independence — still matter in 2026. The medium changes; the craft doesn't."}},{"k":"wolfgang_behringer_communications_revo","v":{"s":"Wolfgang Behringer, \"Communications Revolutions: A Historiographical Concept\" (2006)"}},{"k":"michael_schudson_discovering_the_news_","v":{"s":"Michael Schudson, Discovering the News: A Social History of American Newspapers (1978)"}},{"k":"tarleton_gillespie_custodians_of_the_i","v":{"s":"Tarleton Gillespie, Custodians of the Internet (2018)"}},{"k":"penelope_muse_abernathy_the_expanding_","v":{"s":"Penelope Muse Abernathy, The Expanding News Desert (UNC Hussman, 2018-)"}},{"k":"pew_research_center_news_industry_data","v":{"s":"Pew Research Center News Industry data"}},{"k":"heinrich_schmidt","v":{"s":"Heinrich Schmidt"}},{"k":"anna_r_gerin","v":{"s":"Anna Rügerin"}},{"k":"aldus_manutius_3","v":{"s":"Aldus Manutius"}},{"k":"klaus_a_17_year_old_senior_apprentice_","v":{"s":"Klaus, a 17-year-old senior apprentice, has been short-changing you on lunch + giving you the worst chores. You discover he is also stealing small amounts of paper to sell. What do you do?"}},{"k":"tell_the_master_directly","v":{"s":"Tell the master directly"}},{"k":"confront_klaus_privately","v":{"s":"Confront Klaus privately"}},{"k":"endure_quietly_eat_less_work_harder","v":{"s":"Endure quietly. Eat less + work harder."}},{"k":"quietly_take_a_small_share_of_the_pape","v":{"s":"Quietly take a small share of the paper sales"}},{"k":"a_wealthy_customer_a_city_notary_asks_","v":{"s":"A wealthy customer (a city notary) asks you to bring extra ink to his manuscript shop \"off the books\" — he tips you a florin to do so. The master would punish you if he knew."}},{"k":"take_the_tip_bring_the_ink_quietly","v":{"s":"Take the tip + bring the ink quietly"}},{"k":"refuse_explain_you_cannot_do_this","v":{"s":"Refuse — explain you cannot do this"}},{"k":"tell_the_master_about_the_offer","v":{"s":"Tell the master about the offer"}},{"k":"you_set_the_type_for_a_200_page_latin_","v":{"s":"You set the type for a 200-page Latin grammar. After the first 50 pages are printed, you discover a typo on page 38 — \"amat\" should be \"amet.\" Reprinting 50 pages costs the master 2 florins in paper + 2 days of labor. The error is small (a tense difference) + might be missed in proofing."}},{"k":"confess_immediately_accept_the_cost","v":{"s":"Confess immediately + accept the cost"}},{"k":"hide_the_typo_hope_no_one_notices","v":{"s":"Hide the typo + hope no one notices"}},{"k":"fix_in_remaining_copies_say_nothing_ab","v":{"s":"Fix in remaining copies, say nothing about the printed 50"}},{"k":"anna_a_17_year_old_baker_s_daughter_si","v":{"s":"Anna, a 17-year-old baker's daughter, signals interest in walking with you. But apprentices are forbidden to court or marry by guild rules. If caught, you lose 6 months of progress."}},{"k":"court_her_secretly","v":{"s":"Court her secretly"}},{"k":"tell_her_you_cannot_until_your_apprent","v":{"s":"Tell her you cannot until your apprenticeship ends"}},{"k":"pursue_openly_accept_consequences","v":{"s":"Pursue openly + accept consequences"}},{"k":"a_wealthier_rival_print_shop_in_the_sa","v":{"s":"A wealthier rival print shop in the same city offers to poach you — they will pay you a small wage (your master pays you nothing yet) + complete your training. Leaving breaches your contract; the rival shop swears the master won't find out."}},{"k":"leave_for_the_rival","v":{"s":"Leave for the rival"}},{"k":"stay_loyal_to_your_master","v":{"s":"Stay loyal to your master"}},{"k":"tell_your_master_negotiate_for_actual_","v":{"s":"Tell your master + negotiate for actual wages"}},{"k":"your_master_is_taking_on_a_greek_latin","v":{"s":"Your master is taking on a Greek-Latin edition (rare + prestigious). He needs help with Greek typesetting. Greek type is brutal — different letterforms, more ligatures, longer hours. Mastering it would set you apart. Or you could relax in the evenings with the other apprentices at the tavern."}},{"k":"volunteer_for_the_greek_work_extra_unp","v":{"s":"Volunteer for the Greek work — extra unpaid hours"}},{"k":"maintain_social_ties_go_to_the_tavern","v":{"s":"Maintain social ties — go to the tavern"}},{"k":"take_on_some_greek_work_but_not_all_ev","v":{"s":"Take on some Greek work — but not all evenings"}},{"k":"a_florentine_scholar_commissions_a_400","v":{"s":"A Florentine scholar commissions a 400-page Latin edition — 4-month project, premium pay. The master wants to assign you as lead compositor. You've never done a commission this size. Mistakes will be expensive + visible. Decline + the work goes to the senior apprentice."}},{"k":"accept_the_commission","v":{"s":"Accept the commission"}},{"k":"decline_suggest_the_senior_apprentice","v":{"s":"Decline + suggest the senior apprentice"}},{"k":"accept_ask_the_master_for_ongoing_guid","v":{"s":"Accept + ask the master for ongoing guidance"}},{"k":"the_local_printers_guild_holds_an_annu","v":{"s":"The local printers' guild holds an annual dinner. Your master takes you for the first time. Drinks flow. A master from a rival shop praises your Greek work + offers to introduce you to a Frankfurt printer for future contacts. He also asks you to share a few details about your master's commissions. The \"few details\" are not damaging but they are guild gossip you should not share."}},{"k":"share_the_details_it_s_just_gossip","v":{"s":"Share the details — it's just gossip"}},{"k":"politely_refuse_to_share","v":{"s":"Politely refuse to share"}},{"k":"talk_only_about_your_own_greek_work_no","v":{"s":"Talk only about your own Greek work, not the master's commissions"}},{"k":"your_master_gives_you_the_choice_of_wh","v":{"s":"Your master gives you the choice of which area to deepen for your final two years."}},{"k":"composition_typography_will_become_a_c","v":{"s":"Composition + typography (will become a compositor for life)"}},{"k":"presswork_ink_will_become_a_pressman_h","v":{"s":"Presswork + ink (will become a pressman, hands-on, hard physical labor)"}},{"k":"business_arithmetic_will_become_a_shop","v":{"s":"Business + arithmetic (will become a shop foreman or own a shop)"}},{"k":"a_cologne_customer_wants_a_heretical_t","v":{"s":"A Cologne customer wants a heretical theological tract printed (denying purgatory). The master is hesitant — the work pays well but could attract church inquiry. He asks your opinion."}},{"k":"take_the_commission_money_is_money","v":{"s":"\"Take the commission — money is money\""}},{"k":"decline_too_risky","v":{"s":"\"Decline — too risky\""}},{"k":"take_it_but_use_a_fake_imprint","v":{"s":"\"Take it but use a fake imprint\""}},{"k":"you_must_produce_a_journeyman_piece_a_","v":{"s":"You must produce a journeyman piece — a printed work that demonstrates your full skill. What do you choose?"}},{"k":"a_small_psalter_psalm_book_in_latin_wi","v":{"s":"A small psalter (psalm book) in Latin with red-and-black initials"}},{"k":"a_small_greek_latin_edition_of_an_aeso","v":{"s":"A small Greek-Latin edition of an Aesop fable"}},{"k":"a_vernacular_german_ballad_with_woodcu","v":{"s":"A vernacular German ballad with woodcut illustration"}},{"k":"a_single_broadside_political_poem_agai","v":{"s":"A single broadside political poem against a corrupt local official"}},{"k":"yes_i_will_wander_europe","v":{"s":"\"Yes — I will wander Europe\""}},{"k":"no_stay_home_take_a_local_journeyman_j","v":{"s":"\"No — stay home, take a local journeyman job\""}},{"k":"you_qualify_as_a_journeyman_what_now_t","v":{"s":"You qualify as a journeyman. What now? (This decision shapes your career.)"}},{"k":"stay_with_your_master_as_a_paid_journe","v":{"s":"Stay with your master as a paid journeyman"}},{"k":"travel_as_a_wandering_journeyman_acros","v":{"s":"Travel as a wandering journeyman across Europe"}},{"k":"open_your_own_shop_immediately","v":{"s":"Open your own shop immediately"}},{"k":"marry_the_daughter_of_a_master_printer","v":{"s":"Marry the daughter of a master printer + inherit a shop"}},{"k":"you_become_a_senior_journeyman_then_fo","v":{"s":"You become a senior journeyman, then foreman of your master's shop"}},{"k":"eight_years_after_qualifying_you_are_t","v":{"s":"Eight years after qualifying you are the trusted foreman. Your master makes you a partial partner. When he dies in 1502 you inherit a share + keep the shop running with his widow until she remarries. Comfortable, respected, never famous. Your name is in one surviving guild ledger."}},{"k":"your_wanderjahre_take_you_across_europ","v":{"s":"Your wanderjahre take you across Europe — and shape your career"}},{"k":"you_work_in_augsburg_nuremberg_frankfu","v":{"s":"You work in Augsburg, Nuremberg, Frankfurt, Lyon, Venice over 3 years. You see many shops + meet many printers. When you finally settle (at 24), you have unmatched practical knowledge. You open a small shop in your home city + specialize in the unusual genre you discovered abroad."}},{"k":"you_open_your_own_shop_at_21_a_long_ha","v":{"s":"You open your own shop at 21 — a long, hard climb"}},{"k":"the_first_3_years_are_brutal_you_sleep","v":{"s":"The first 3 years are brutal. You sleep at the shop. You make business mistakes that nearly bankrupt you. But by year 5 you have a customer base. By year 10 you are profitable. Your shop runs for 35 years. You teach 8 apprentices yourself. One of them becomes more famous than you."}},{"k":"you_marry_into_ownership_and_prove_you","v":{"s":"You marry into ownership — and prove yourself fast"}},{"k":"you_marry_lena_daughter_of_master_prin","v":{"s":"You marry Lena, daughter of master printer Konrad Müller. Within 2 years his health fails + you take over the shop. The trade grumbles about how you got there. Within 5 years your work speaks for itself + the grumbling fades. You run a successful shop for 30 years + your children inherit."}},{"k":"each_master_offers_different_training_","v":{"s":"Each master offers different training, different working conditions, different opportunities. Read carefully. Once you choose you cannot change."}},{"k":"starting_bonus","v":{"s":"Starting bonus: "}},{"k":"apprentice","v":{"s":"Apprentice"}},{"k":"master","v":{"s":"Master"}},{"k":"skills","v":{"s":"Skills"}},{"k":"state","v":{"s":"State"}},{"k":"decision","v":{"s":"Decision"}},{"k":"outcome","v":{"s":"Outcome"}},{"k":"decisions_made_year_ends_time_advances","v":{"s":"Decisions made. Year ends. Time advances."}},{"k":"see_your_career_outcome","v":{"s":"See your career outcome →"}},{"k":"final_stats","v":{"s":"Final stats"}},{"k":"historical_context","v":{"s":"Historical context: "}},{"k":"your_master_throughout","v":{"s":"Your master throughout"}},{"k":"start_a_new_journey_with_a_different_m","v":{"s":"↺ Start a new journey with a different master"}},{"k":"you_did_not_complete_the_final_decisio","v":{"s":"You did not complete the final decision in Year 7. Use the back button to return + finish."}},{"k":"about_apprenticeship_in_1480","v":{"s":"📜 About apprenticeship in 1480"}},{"k":"a_typical_printer_s_apprenticeship_las","v":{"s":"A typical printer's apprenticeship lasted 7 years (varied by city + guild). Apprentices started 12-16, lived with the master, received no wages but bed + board + training. Master could whip, fine, or extend contracts for disobedience. Marriage forbidden until journeyman status. After 7 years apprentices presented their journeyman piece + were tested by the guild. Successful apprentices became journeymen — wage-earning craftsmen who could travel + work — and eventually masters with their own shops."}},{"k":"start_over_will_lose_progress","v":{"s":"↺ Start over (will lose progress)"}},{"k":"sigfrid_steinberg_five_hundred_years_o","v":{"s":"Sigfrid Steinberg, Five Hundred Years of Printing (1955)"}},{"k":"elizabeth_eisenstein_the_printing_pres_4","v":{"s":"Elizabeth Eisenstein, The Printing Press as an Agent of Change (1979)"}},{"k":"brian_richardson_printing_writers_and_","v":{"s":"Brian Richardson, Printing, Writers and Readers in Renaissance Italy (1999)"}},{"k":"sheila_edmunds_the_apprentice_in_16th_","v":{"s":"Sheila Edmunds, \"The Apprentice in 16th-Century Cologne\" — Renaissance Quarterly"}},{"k":"overview_5","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"letter_anatomy","v":{"s":"Letter anatomy"}},{"k":"type_history","v":{"s":"Type history"}},{"k":"type_designer","v":{"s":"Type designer"}},{"k":"famous_faces","v":{"s":"Famous faces"}},{"k":"name_that_face","v":{"s":"Name that face"}},{"k":"modern_type","v":{"s":"Modern type"}},{"k":"jenson_nicolas_jenson_venice_1470","v":{"s":"Jenson (Nicolas Jenson, Venice 1470)"}},{"k":"garamond_claude_garamond_paris_1530s","v":{"s":"Garamond (Claude Garamond, Paris ~1530s)"}},{"k":"caslon_william_caslon_london_1722","v":{"s":"Caslon (William Caslon, London 1722)"}},{"k":"baskerville_john_baskerville_birmingha","v":{"s":"Baskerville (John Baskerville, Birmingham 1757)"}},{"k":"bodoni_giambattista_bodoni_parma_1790s","v":{"s":"Bodoni (Giambattista Bodoni, Parma 1790s)"}},{"k":"clarendon_robert_besley_london_1845","v":{"s":"Clarendon (Robert Besley, London 1845)"}},{"k":"times_new_roman_stanley_morison_victor","v":{"s":"Times New Roman (Stanley Morison + Victor Lardent, 1932)"}},{"k":"futura_paul_renner_1927","v":{"s":"Futura (Paul Renner, 1927)"}},{"k":"helvetica_max_miedinger_eduard_hoffman","v":{"s":"Helvetica (Max Miedinger + Eduard Hoffmann, Switzerland 1957)"}},{"k":"comic_sans_vincent_connare_microsoft_1","v":{"s":"Comic Sans (Vincent Connare, Microsoft 1994)"}},{"k":"helvetica_5","v":{"s":"Helvetica"}},{"k":"garamond_3","v":{"s":"Garamond"}},{"k":"bodoni_5","v":{"s":"Bodoni"}},{"k":"futura","v":{"s":"Futura"}},{"k":"comic_sans","v":{"s":"Comic Sans"}},{"k":"times_new_roman_2","v":{"s":"Times New Roman"}},{"k":"caslon_5","v":{"s":"Caslon"}},{"k":"helvetica_6","v":{"s":"Helvetica"}},{"k":"geometric_sans_futura_avenir_gotham","v":{"s":"Geometric sans (Futura, Avenir, Gotham)"}},{"k":"neo_grotesque_sans_helvetica_univers","v":{"s":"Neo-grotesque sans (Helvetica, Univers)"}},{"k":"slab_serif_clarendon_rockwell","v":{"s":"Slab-serif (Clarendon, Rockwell)"}},{"k":"didone_modern_bodoni_didot","v":{"s":"Didone / Modern (Bodoni, Didot)"}},{"k":"transitional_baskerville_times","v":{"s":"Transitional (Baskerville, Times)"}},{"k":"old_style_garamond_caslon_jenson","v":{"s":"Old Style (Garamond, Caslon, Jenson)"}},{"k":"humanist_serif_centaur_hoefler_text","v":{"s":"Humanist serif (Centaur, Hoefler Text)"}},{"k":"custom_unclassified","v":{"s":"Custom / unclassified"}},{"k":"ascender_t","v":{"s":"↑ ascender (t)"}},{"k":"descender_y_p","v":{"s":"↑ descender (y, p)"}},{"k":"x_height_e","v":{"s":"↑ x-height (e)"}},{"k":"anatomy_the_word_type","v":{"s":"Anatomy: the word \"type\""}},{"k":"closest_historical_classification","v":{"s":"Closest historical classification: "}},{"k":"tall_readable_at_small_sizes_low_elega","v":{"s":"Tall = readable at small sizes; low = elegant at large sizes"}},{"k":"heavy_stroke_vs_delicate_stroke","v":{"s":"Heavy stroke vs delicate stroke"}},{"k":"difference_between_thick_thin_strokes","v":{"s":"Difference between thick + thin strokes"}},{"k":"horizontal_extent_of_letters","v":{"s":"Horizontal extent of letters"}},{"k":"serif_style","v":{"s":"Serif style:"}},{"k":"none_sans_serif","v":{"s":"None (sans-serif)"}},{"k":"slab_heavy_rectangle","v":{"s":"Slab (heavy rectangle)"}},{"k":"bracketed_curved","v":{"s":"Bracketed (curved)"}},{"k":"hairline_thin","v":{"s":"Hairline (thin)"}},{"k":"wedge_angled","v":{"s":"Wedge (angled)"}},{"k":"or_load_a_historical_preset","v":{"s":"Or load a historical preset:"}},{"k":"garamond_like_1530","v":{"s":"Garamond-like (1530)"}},{"k":"bodoni_like_1798","v":{"s":"Bodoni-like (1798)"}},{"k":"clarendon_like_1845","v":{"s":"Clarendon-like (1845)"}},{"k":"futura_like_1927","v":{"s":"Futura-like (1927)"}},{"k":"helvetica_like_1957","v":{"s":"Helvetica-like (1957)"}},{"k":"how_to_recognize_it","v":{"s":"How to recognize it"}},{"k":"modern_uses","v":{"s":"Modern uses"}},{"k":"description","v":{"s":"Description"}},{"k":"right_answer_4","v":{"s":"Right answer: "}},{"k":"next_2","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"start_over_2","v":{"s":"↺ Start over"}},{"k":"robert_bringhurst_the_elements_of_typo_4","v":{"s":"Robert Bringhurst, The Elements of Typographic Style (1992, multiple editions) — standard reference"}},{"k":"stephen_coles_the_anatomy_of_type_2012","v":{"s":"Stephen Coles, The Anatomy of Type (2012)"}},{"k":"phil_baines_andrew_haslam_type_typogra","v":{"s":"Phil Baines + Andrew Haslam, Type & Typography (2002, 2nd ed 2005)"}},{"k":"klim_type_foundry_resources","v":{"s":"Klim Type Foundry resources"}},{"k":"i_love_typography_online_type_design_p","v":{"s":"I Love Typography (online type design publication)"}},{"k":"type_cooper_at_the_cooper_union_type_d","v":{"s":"Type@Cooper at the Cooper Union — type-design education"}},{"k":"overview_6","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"falmouth_gazette","v":{"s":"Falmouth Gazette"}},{"k":"newspapers","v":{"s":"Newspapers"}},{"k":"book_printers","v":{"s":"Book printers"}},{"k":"broadsides","v":{"s":"Broadsides"}},{"k":"modern_maine","v":{"s":"Modern Maine"}},{"k":"falmouth_gazette_weekly_advertiser","v":{"s":"Falmouth Gazette + Weekly Advertiser"}},{"k":"eastern_argus","v":{"s":"Eastern Argus"}},{"k":"portland_daily_press","v":{"s":"Portland Daily Press"}},{"k":"bangor_daily_news","v":{"s":"Bangor Daily News"}},{"k":"aroostook_republican_caribou","v":{"s":"Aroostook Republican (Caribou)"}},{"k":"single_year_survival_was_actually_comm","v":{"s":"Single-year survival was actually common for early American newspapers. The Gazette's subscribers were too few (Falmouth had ~3,000 residents); advertising revenue was inadequate; the partnership of Titcomb + Wait dissolved over financial pressure. Wait continued in Portland printing under various titles for decades; Titcomb left the trade. The next sustained Portland paper would be the Gazette of Maine (1790), followed by many others. The institutional lineage that eventually became today's Portland Press Herald began at this scale."}},{"k":"interesting_2","v":{"s":"🔍 Interesting: "}},{"k":"maine_historical_society_portland","v":{"s":"Maine Historical Society, Portland"}},{"k":"maine_memory_network_digitized_maine_a","v":{"s":"Maine Memory Network — digitized Maine archives"}},{"k":"maine_state_archives_augusta","v":{"s":"Maine State Archives, Augusta"}},{"k":"bangor_daily_news_history_pages","v":{"s":"Bangor Daily News history pages"}},{"k":"maine_trust_for_local_news","v":{"s":"Maine Trust for Local News"}},{"k":"overview_7","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"petrucci_1501","v":{"s":"Petrucci (1501)"}},{"k":"methods_compared","v":{"s":"Methods compared"}},{"k":"hymn_books","v":{"s":"Hymn books"}},{"k":"modern_sheet_music","v":{"s":"Modern sheet music"}},{"k":"triple_impression_printing_was_3_the_l","v":{"s":"Triple-impression printing was 3× the labor + 3× the registration challenges of normal text printing. Petrucci's books were beautiful + expensive — accessible only to wealthy patrons + institutional libraries. Within decades simpler methods (single-impression with notes integrated into staff-line sorts) emerged + dominated commercial music printing. Petrucci's legacy: he proved music could be printed; the specific method gave way to cheaper alternatives."}},{"k":"stanley_boorman_ottaviano_petrucci_a_c","v":{"s":"Stanley Boorman, Ottaviano Petrucci: A Catalogue Raisonné (2005)"}},{"k":"richard_crawford_america_s_musical_lif","v":{"s":"Richard Crawford, America's Musical Life: A History (2001)"}},{"k":"imslp_petrucci_music_library","v":{"s":"IMSLP / Petrucci Music Library"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_music_division_res","v":{"s":"Library of Congress Music Division resources"}},{"k":"overview_8","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"engraving_methods","v":{"s":"Engraving methods"}},{"k":"ortelius_1570","v":{"s":"Ortelius (1570)"}},{"k":"mercator_1569","v":{"s":"Mercator (1569)"}},{"k":"modern_maps","v":{"s":"Modern maps"}},{"k":"jerry_brotton_a_history_of_the_world_i","v":{"s":"Jerry Brotton, A History of the World in Twelve Maps (2012)"}},{"k":"edward_tufte_envisioning_information_1","v":{"s":"Edward Tufte, Envisioning Information (1990) — cartographic design"}},{"k":"openstreetmap_project","v":{"s":"OpenStreetMap project"}},{"k":"usgs_national_map","v":{"s":"USGS National Map"}},{"k":"overview_9","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"hornbooks","v":{"s":"Hornbooks"}},{"k":"chapbooks","v":{"s":"Chapbooks"}},{"k":"newbery_golden","v":{"s":"Newbery + golden"}},{"k":"caldecott_era","v":{"s":"Caldecott era"}},{"k":"modern","v":{"s":"Modern"}},{"k":"a_1650_hornbook_was_made_by_printing_t","v":{"s":"A 1650 hornbook was made by: printing the lesson sheet on paper or parchment; mounting it on a wooden paddle (oak or beech); cutting a thin sheet of transparent cow horn to size; nailing the horn over the print with small brass tacks. The result was durable enough to last a child's entire primary education. By the 1700s mass-produced hornbooks were sold for a few pence in market towns; by the early 1800s the form was largely replaced by printed primers like the New England Primer."}},{"k":"for_350_years_1500_1850_chapbooks_were","v":{"s":"For ~350 years (1500-1850), chapbooks were the only books many people could afford. They circulated fairy tales + ballads across continents. They were beloved by Wordsworth + Coleridge + Robert Louis Stevenson as childhood reading. Many authors' first published works appeared in chapbook form. They are the direct ancestor of mass-market paperback fiction."}},{"k":"cooperative_children_s_book_center_uw_","v":{"s":"Cooperative Children's Book Center (UW-Madison)"}},{"k":"we_need_diverse_books","v":{"s":"We Need Diverse Books"}},{"k":"american_library_association_newbery_c","v":{"s":"American Library Association — Newbery + Caldecott"}},{"k":"maine_reads_maine_state_library_childr","v":{"s":"Maine Reads / Maine State Library children's lit collection"}},{"k":"overview_10","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"royal_society_1665","v":{"s":"Royal Society 1665"}},{"k":"peer_review","v":{"s":"Peer review"}},{"k":"open_access","v":{"s":"Open access"}},{"k":"preprints_ai","v":{"s":"Preprints + AI"}},{"k":"philosophical_transactions_has_publish","v":{"s":"Philosophical Transactions has published continuously since 1665 — one of the longest continuously-published periodicals of any kind. Split into Philosophical Transactions A (mathematical + physical sciences) + Philosophical Transactions B (biological sciences) in 1887. Both are still publishing in 2026. Notable papers across the centuries: Newton on optics (1671), Maxwell on electromagnetism (1865), Watson + Crick on DNA structure (1953 — actually Nature, but Roy Soc journals had similar pieces)."}},{"k":"standard_concerns_peer_review_is_slow_","v":{"s":"Standard concerns: peer review is slow (months to years), unevenly rigorous (different reviewers reach different conclusions), biased (toward established figures + Western institutions + native English-speakers), uncompensated (reviewers work free). Reform efforts include paid peer review (some journals), automated triage (using AI to identify obviously-flawed papers before human review), and registered reports (review the methodology BEFORE the experiment runs, then commit to publishing regardless of result)."}},{"k":"royal_society_publishing_philosophical","v":{"s":"Royal Society Publishing — Philosophical Transactions archive"}},{"k":"plos_public_library_of_science","v":{"s":"PLOS (Public Library of Science)"}},{"k":"retraction_watch","v":{"s":"Retraction Watch"}},{"k":"overview_11","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"hatch_show_print","v":{"s":"Hatch Show Print"}},{"k":"letterpress_studios","v":{"s":"Letterpress studios"}},{"k":"how_to_learn","v":{"s":"How to learn"}},{"k":"maine_letterpress","v":{"s":"Maine letterpress"}},{"k":"most_working_letterpress_shops_will_we","v":{"s":"Most working letterpress shops will welcome visitors who call ahead. Many offer single-day workshops. The cost is modest; the experience is transformative — you leave knowing what it feels like to set type, ink a press, pull a print. The Falmouth Gazette's ghost runs through these shops; the technology has not changed in any fundamental way for 500 years."}},{"k":"hatch_show_print_2","v":{"s":"Hatch Show Print"}},{"k":"briar_press_community_forums","v":{"s":"Briar Press community + forums"}},{"k":"center_for_book_arts_nyc","v":{"s":"Center for Book Arts (NYC)"}},{"k":"haystack_mountain_school_of_crafts","v":{"s":"Haystack Mountain School of Crafts"}},{"k":"penland_school_of_craft","v":{"s":"Penland School of Craft"}},{"k":"gutenberg_wooden_screw_press","v":{"s":"Gutenberg wooden screw press"}},{"k":"blaeu_dutch_press","v":{"s":"Blaeu Dutch press"}},{"k":"stanhope_iron_press_3","v":{"s":"Stanhope iron press"}},{"k":"columbian_albion_press","v":{"s":"Columbian / Albion press"}},{"k":"steam_powered_koenig_press","v":{"s":"Steam-powered Koenig press"}},{"k":"hoe_rotary_press","v":{"s":"Hoe rotary press"}},{"k":"adana_tabletop_letterpress","v":{"s":"Adana tabletop letterpress"}},{"k":"heidelberg_windmill","v":{"s":"Heidelberg Windmill"}},{"k":"linotype_hot_metal_composition","v":{"s":"Linotype hot-metal composition"}},{"k":"offset_lithography","v":{"s":"Offset lithography"}},{"k":"digital_inkjet_press","v":{"s":"Digital + inkjet press"}},{"k":"innovation","v":{"s":"🔧 Innovation: "}},{"k":"trade_off","v":{"s":"⚖ Trade-off: "}},{"k":"older_press","v":{"s":"← Older press"}},{"k":"newer_press","v":{"s":"Newer press →"}},{"k":"sigfrid_steinberg_five_hundred_years_o_2","v":{"s":"Sigfrid Steinberg, Five Hundred Years of Printing (1955, revised 1996)"}},{"k":"frank_romano_history_of_the_linotype_c","v":{"s":"Frank Romano, History of the Linotype Company (2014)"}},{"k":"james_moran_printing_presses_history_a","v":{"s":"James Moran, Printing Presses: History and Development from the Fifteenth Century to Modern Times (1973)"}},{"k":"the_international_printing_museum_cars","v":{"s":"The International Printing Museum (Carson, CA)"}},{"k":"overview_12","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"timeline","v":{"s":"Timeline"}},{"k":"why_luther_s_side_won_the_print_war","v":{"s":"Why Luther's side won the print war"}},{"k":"catholic_response","v":{"s":"Catholic response"}},{"k":"modern_echo_2","v":{"s":"Modern echo"}},{"k":"northern_europe_germany_scandinavia_en","v":{"s":"Northern Europe — Germany, Scandinavia, England, Scotland, Netherlands — stayed Protestant. Southern + Mediterranean Europe — Italy, Spain, Portugal, France (after 1685 expulsion of Huguenots), Austria — stayed Catholic. Central Europe — Poland, Hungary, Bohemia — became Catholic again via Counter-Reformation success. The division held for the next 400 years + persists to this day in church membership + cultural patterns."}},{"k":"be_careful_with_the_analogy_the_reform","v":{"s":"Be careful with the analogy. The Reformation was driven by genuine theological + political grievances, not just by new technology. Print accelerated trends already in motion. The same caveat applies to digital + AI: technology amplifies; it rarely creates the underlying forces. The Reformation print war is instructive about how information technology interacts with institutional change — not about whether technology by itself is the cause of change."}},{"k":"andrew_pettegree_brand_luther_how_an_u","v":{"s":"Andrew Pettegree, Brand Luther: How an Unheralded Monk Turned His Small Town into a Center of Publishing (2015)"}},{"k":"mark_edwards_jr_printing_propaganda_an","v":{"s":"Mark Edwards Jr., Printing, Propaganda, and Martin Luther (1994)"}},{"k":"diarmaid_macculloch_the_reformation_a_","v":{"s":"Diarmaid MacCulloch, The Reformation: A History (2003)"}},{"k":"robert_scribner_for_the_sake_of_simple","v":{"s":"Robert Scribner, For the Sake of Simple Folk: Popular Propaganda for the German Reformation (1981)"}},{"k":"overview_13","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"chromolithography","v":{"s":"Chromolithography"}},{"k":"photogravure_halftone","v":{"s":"Photogravure + halftone"}},{"k":"cmyk_offset","v":{"s":"CMYK offset"}},{"k":"digital_rgb","v":{"s":"Digital RGB"}},{"k":"bamber_gascoigne_how_to_identify_print","v":{"s":"Bamber Gascoigne, How to Identify Prints (1986, multiple editions) — color print methods reference"}},{"k":"michael_twyman_a_history_of_chromolith","v":{"s":"Michael Twyman, A History of Chromolithography (2013)"}},{"k":"international_color_consortium_icc_sta","v":{"s":"International Color Consortium (ICC) standards"}},{"k":"pantone_color_systems","v":{"s":"Pantone color systems"}},{"k":"gutenberg_bible_1455","v":{"s":"Gutenberg Bible (1455)"}},{"k":"luther_s_german_bible_1534","v":{"s":"Luther's German Bible (1534)"}},{"k":"don_quixote_1605_part_i","v":{"s":"Don Quixote (1605, Part I)"}},{"k":"galileo_s_dialogue_1632","v":{"s":"Galileo's Dialogue (1632)"}},{"k":"king_james_bible_1611","v":{"s":"King James Bible (1611)"}},{"k":"newton_s_principia_mathematica_1687","v":{"s":"Newton's Principia Mathematica (1687)"}},{"k":"common_sense_1776_2","v":{"s":"Common Sense (1776)"}},{"k":"wealth_of_nations_1776","v":{"s":"Wealth of Nations (1776)"}},{"k":"origin_of_species_1859","v":{"s":"Origin of Species (1859)"}},{"k":"uncle_tom_s_cabin_1852","v":{"s":"Uncle Tom's Cabin (1852)"}},{"k":"das_kapital_1867","v":{"s":"Das Kapital (1867)"}},{"k":"the_feminine_mystique_1963","v":{"s":"The Feminine Mystique (1963)"}},{"k":"silent_spring_1962","v":{"s":"Silent Spring (1962)"}},{"k":"diary_of_a_young_girl_anne_frank_1947","v":{"s":"Diary of a Young Girl (Anne Frank, 1947)"}},{"k":"the_color_purple_1982","v":{"s":"The Color Purple (1982)"}},{"k":"previous_2","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next_3","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"encyclopedia_britannica_articles_on_ea","v":{"s":"Encyclopedia Britannica articles on each book"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_books_that_shaped_","v":{"s":"Library of Congress, Books That Shaped America"}},{"k":"modern_library_100_best_lists","v":{"s":"Modern Library 100 Best Lists"}},{"k":"overview_14","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"english_civil_war_1642","v":{"s":"English Civil War (1642)"}},{"k":"american_revolution_1776","v":{"s":"American Revolution (1776)"}},{"k":"french_revolution_1789","v":{"s":"French Revolution (1789)"}},{"k":"1848_revolutions","v":{"s":"1848 Revolutions"}},{"k":"the_pattern","v":{"s":"The pattern"}},{"k":"important_caveat_print_made_revolution","v":{"s":"Important caveat: print made revolutions possible at scales not previously possible, but did not cause them. Underlying political grievances drove the revolutions. Without those grievances, even massive print infrastructure would have produced no revolution. With those grievances + without print, revolutions would have happened but looked very different — smaller, slower, more localized. Print is a powerful enabling technology + a poor explanation for political change on its own."}},{"k":"bernard_bailyn_the_ideological_origins","v":{"s":"Bernard Bailyn, The Ideological Origins of the American Revolution (1967)"}},{"k":"robert_darnton_the_literary_undergroun","v":{"s":"Robert Darnton, The Literary Underground of the Old Regime (1982)"}},{"k":"jeremy_popkin_revolutionary_news_the_p","v":{"s":"Jeremy Popkin, Revolutionary News: The Press in France, 1789-1799 (1990)"}},{"k":"joad_raymond_ed_the_cambridge_companio","v":{"s":"Joad Raymond, ed., The Cambridge Companion to British Civil War Print (2015)"}},{"k":"george_thomason_collection_british_lib","v":{"s":"George Thomason collection — British Library"}},{"k":"overview_15","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"incunabula","v":{"s":"Incunabula"}},{"k":"first_editions","v":{"s":"First editions"}},{"k":"reading_a_book","v":{"s":"Reading a book"}},{"k":"how_to_collect","v":{"s":"How to collect"}},{"k":"rare_book_libraries","v":{"s":"Rare-book libraries"}},{"k":"most_rare_book_libraries_are_open_to_a","v":{"s":"Most rare-book libraries are open to any serious researcher. Steps: (1) check the library website for collection scope + access procedures, (2) make an appointment + describe what you want to see, (3) bring photo ID + relevant credentials, (4) follow handling rules (often: pencil only, weights to hold pages open, no eating/drinking). Library staff are usually delighted to share their collections with visitors who have serious interest."}},{"k":"allen_patricia_ahearn_collected_books_","v":{"s":"Allen + Patricia Ahearn, Collected Books: The Guide to Identification + Values (multiple editions)"}},{"k":"incunabula_short_title_catalogue","v":{"s":"Incunabula Short Title Catalogue"}},{"k":"antiquarian_booksellers_association_of","v":{"s":"Antiquarian Booksellers Association of America (ABAA)"}},{"k":"boston_antiquarian_book_fair","v":{"s":"Boston Antiquarian Book Fair"}},{"k":"overview_16","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"period_recipe","v":{"s":"Period recipe"}},{"k":"gutenberg_s_ink","v":{"s":"Gutenberg's ink"}},{"k":"colored_inks","v":{"s":"Colored inks"}},{"k":"modern_inks","v":{"s":"Modern inks"}},{"k":"tools_supplies","v":{"s":"Tools + supplies"}},{"k":"joseph_moxon_mechanick_exercises_on_th","v":{"s":"Joseph Moxon, Mechanick Exercises on the Whole Art of Printing (1683-84) — primary source on period inks"}},{"k":"adam_smyth_ed_a_history_of_the_book_in","v":{"s":"Adam Smyth, ed., A History of the Book in 100 Books (2014)"}},{"k":"library_of_congress_conservation_notes","v":{"s":"Library of Congress conservation notes on Gutenberg Bible ink"}},{"k":"overview_17","v":{"s":"Overview"}},{"k":"bible_translation","v":{"s":"Bible translation"}},{"k":"william_tyndale","v":{"s":"William Tyndale"}},{"k":"king_james_bible","v":{"s":"King James Bible"}},{"k":"literary_translation","v":{"s":"Literary translation"}},{"k":"modern_translation","v":{"s":"Modern translation"}},{"k":"david_daniell_william_tyndale_a_biogra","v":{"s":"David Daniell, William Tyndale: A Biography (1994)"}},{"k":"adam_nicolson_god_s_secretaries_the_ma","v":{"s":"Adam Nicolson, God's Secretaries: The Making of the King James Bible (2003)"}},{"k":"edith_grossman_why_translation_matters","v":{"s":"Edith Grossman, Why Translation Matters (2010)"}},{"k":"wycliffe_bible_translators","v":{"s":"Wycliffe Bible Translators"}},{"k":"american_translators_association","v":{"s":"American Translators Association"}},{"k":"overview_18","v":{"s":"Overview