lination_today","v":{"s":"Solar declination today: "}},{"k":"earth_s_axis_tilt_projected_onto_the_s","v":{"s":"(Earth's axis tilt projected onto the sun-Earth line)"}},{"k":"daily_energy_clear_sky","v":{"s":"Daily energy (clear sky): "}},{"k":"note_real_world_output_is_lower_becaus","v":{"s":"Note: real-world output is lower because of clouds + soiling + system losses. Annual capacity factor in Maine: 16–20%."}},{"k":"how_it_works_step_by_step","v":{"s":"How it works — step by step"}},{"k":"photons_arrive","v":{"s":"Photons arrive"}},{"k":"sunlight_is_a_stream_of_photons_each_c","v":{"s":" — sunlight is a stream of photons, each carrying a tiny packet of energy."}},{"k":"photon_hits_silicon","v":{"s":"Photon hits silicon"}},{"k":"if_the_photon_has_enough_energy_to_cro","v":{"s":" — if the photon has enough energy to cross silicon’s "}},{"k":"band_gap","v":{"s":"band gap"}},{"k":"1_12_ev_it_knocks_an_outer_shell_elect","v":{"s":" (~1.12 eV), it knocks an outer-shell electron loose."}},{"k":"p_n_junction_sorts_the_charges","v":{"s":"P-N junction sorts the charges"}},{"k":"the_panel_is_two_layers_of_silicon_dop","v":{"s":" — the panel is two layers of silicon doped with different impurities (boron / phosphorus). The electric field at their boundary pushes electrons one way, “holes” the other."}},{"k":"electrons_flow_as_dc_current","v":{"s":"Electrons flow as DC current"}},{"k":"they_travel_through_the_external_circu","v":{"s":" — they travel through the external circuit (your house, an inverter, the grid)."}},{"k":"inverter_converts_dc_ac","v":{"s":"Inverter converts DC → AC"}},{"k":"grid_most_appliances_run_on_alternatin","v":{"s":" — grid + most appliances run on alternating current."}},{"k":"try_it_solar_pv_power_calculator","v":{"s":"🔬 Try it: solar PV power calculator"}},{"k":"power_irradiance_area_efficiency_cos_t","v":{"s":"Power = Irradiance × Area × Efficiency × cos(tilt error). Drag the sliders to see how each factor changes output."}},{"k":"sunlight_intensity_irradiance","v":{"s":"Sunlight intensity (irradiance)"}},{"k":"1000_w_m_at_solar_noon_on_a_clear_summ","v":{"s":"~1000 W/m² at solar noon on a clear summer day. ~200 W/m² on overcast days."}},{"k":"total_panel_area","v":{"s":"Total panel area"}},{"k":"a_typical_home_rooftop_array_20_40_m_a","v":{"s":"A typical home rooftop array: 20–40 m². A megawatt utility array: ~5,000 m²."}},{"k":"module_efficiency","v":{"s":"Module efficiency"}},{"k":"commercial_panels_18_23_lab_record_mul","v":{"s":"Commercial panels: 18–23%. Lab record (multi-junction): 47%. Cheap thin-film: 10–12%."}},{"k":"tilt_error_from_optimal","v":{"s":"Tilt error from optimal"}},{"k":"0_perfectly_aimed_at_the_sun_90_parall","v":{"s":"0° = perfectly aimed at the sun. 90° = parallel to sunlight (zero output). Trackers cut this to near-zero."}},{"k":"a_home_consumes_1_kw_continuous_on_ave","v":{"s":"A home consumes ~1 kW continuous on average. So this array could power roughly "}},{"k":"at_this_instant_annual_energy_depends_","v":{"s":" at this instant. Annual energy depends on capacity factor (typically 15–27%)."}},{"k":"series_vs_parallel_wiring","v":{"s":"🔌 Series vs parallel wiring"}},{"k":"voltages_add_current_stays_the_same_a_","v":{"s":" voltages add, current stays the same. A weak / shaded panel limits the entire string."}},{"k":"currents_add_voltage_stays_the_same_sh","v":{"s":" currents add, voltage stays the same. Shading one panel only loses that panel’s output. Most modern arrays use module-level optimizers / microinverters to get the best of both."}},{"k":"maine_reality","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"why_wind_power_scales_with_v","v":{"s":"Why wind power scales with v³"}},{"k":"kinetic_energy_of_moving_air_m_v_the_m","v":{"s":"Kinetic energy of moving air = ½ m v². The mass passing through a rotor per second is ρ·A·v (density × area × speed). Multiply: "}},{"k":"p_a_v","v":{"s":"P = ½·ρ·A·v³"}},{"k":"so_wind_power_scales_with_the_cube_of_","v":{"s":". So wind power scales with the CUBE of wind speed."}},{"k":"betz_s_law","v":{"s":"Betz’s law:"}},{"k":"the_absolute_maximum_power_any_rotor_c","v":{"s":" the absolute maximum power any rotor can extract is "}},{"k":"of_the_wind_s_kinetic_energy_if_you_to","v":{"s":" of the wind’s kinetic energy. If you took 100%, the air behind would be motionless and no more air could flow through. Real turbines hit Cp ≈ 0.35–0.45."}},{"k":"try_it_wind_turbine_power","v":{"s":"🔬 Try it: wind turbine power"}},{"k":"wind_speed","v":{"s":"Wind speed"}},{"k":"cut_in_3_m_s_rated_12_15_m_s_cut_out_2","v":{"s":"Cut-in ~3 m/s. Rated ~12–15 m/s. Cut-out ~25 m/s (turbine pitches blades to feather and stops). 1 m/s ≈ 2.24 mph."}},{"k":"rotor_radius","v":{"s":"Rotor radius"}},{"k":"small_wind_2_5_m_modern_utility_onshor","v":{"s":"Small wind: 2–5 m. Modern utility onshore: 50–80 m. Offshore giants: 100–120 m+ (Haliade-X: 110 m)."}},{"k":"power_coefficient_cp","v":{"s":"Power coefficient (Cp)"}},{"k":"real_turbines_35_45_betz_limit_59_3_hi","v":{"s":"Real turbines: 35–45%. Betz limit: 59.3%. Higher Cp = better blade aerodynamics + control."}},{"k":"swept_area","v":{"s":"Swept area: "}},{"k":"doubling_the_rotor_radius_quadruples_t","v":{"s":". Doubling the rotor radius QUADRUPLES the swept area — that’s why turbines keep getting bigger."}},{"k":"onshore_vs_offshore","v":{"s":"🗼 Onshore vs offshore"}},{"k":"cheaper_to_install_maintain_land_confl","v":{"s":" cheaper to install + maintain. Land conflicts (viewsheds, wildlife). Capacity factor 35–45%."}},{"k":"steadier_wind_larger_rotors_saltwater_","v":{"s":" steadier wind + larger rotors. Saltwater corrosion + installation cost. Capacity factor 45–55%. Deep water (Gulf of Maine) requires "}},{"k":"floating_platforms","v":{"s":"floating platforms"}},{"k":"a_maine_specialty","v":{"s":" — a Maine specialty."}},{"k":"maine_reality_2","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"the_hydro_power_formula","v":{"s":"The hydro power formula"}},{"k":"p_g_h_q","v":{"s":"P = ρ × g × h × Q × η"}},{"k":"rho","v":{"s":"ρ (rho)"}},{"k":"water_density_1000_kg_m","v":{"s":" = water density = 1000 kg/m³"}},{"k":"gravity_9_81_m_s","v":{"s":" = gravity = 9.81 m/s²"}},{"k":"head_vertical_drop_in_meters","v":{"s":" = head = vertical drop in meters"}},{"k":"flow_rate_in_m_s","v":{"s":" = flow rate in m³/s"}},{"k":"eta","v":{"s":"η (eta)"}},{"k":"turbine_efficiency_typically_0_85_0_95","v":{"s":" = turbine efficiency, typically 0.85–0.95"}},{"k":"notice_both_head_and_flow_appear_once_","v":{"s":"Notice both head and flow appear ONCE — power is linear in each. Doubling either doubles the power; doubling both quadruples it."}},{"k":"try_it_hydro_plant_calculator","v":{"s":"🔬 Try it: hydro plant calculator"}},{"k":"head_vertical_drop","v":{"s":"Head (vertical drop)"}},{"k":"run_of_river_dam_5_20_m_medium_dam_30_","v":{"s":"Run-of-river dam: 5–20 m. Medium dam: 30–100 m. High alpine plant: 500–1800 m."}},{"k":"flow_rate","v":{"s":"Flow rate"}},{"k":"mountain_stream_0_5_5_m_s_mid_sized_ri","v":{"s":"Mountain stream: 0.5–5 m³/s. Mid-sized river: 50–100 m³/s. Mississippi: ~17,000 m³/s."}},{"k":"turbine_efficiency","v":{"s":"Turbine efficiency (η)"}},{"k":"modern_turbines_90_95_older_or_poorly_","v":{"s":"Modern turbines: 90–95%. Older or poorly matched: 75–85%."}},{"k":"for_comparison_hoover_dam_2_080_mw_a_t","v":{"s":"For comparison: Hoover Dam = 2,080 MW. A typical Maine hydro: 1–20 MW. A microhydro on a farm stream: 5–50 kW."}},{"k":"three_turbine_families_pick_by_site","v":{"s":"Three turbine families — pick by site"}},{"k":"maine_reality_3","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"too_cool_for_electricity_use_a_ground_","v":{"s":"Too cool for electricity — use a ground-source heat pump for direct heating"}},{"k":"binary_cycle_90_180_c","v":{"s":"Binary cycle (~90–180°C)"}},{"k":"flash_steam_180_235_c","v":{"s":"Flash steam (180–235°C)"}},{"k":"dry_steam_235_c","v":{"s":"Dry steam (≥ 235°C)"}},{"k":"where_does_the_heat_come_from","v":{"s":"Where does the heat come from?"}},{"k":"two_sources_leftover_heat_from_earth_s","v":{"s":"Two sources: leftover heat from Earth’s formation 4.5 billion years ago, and ongoing radioactive decay of uranium, thorium, and potassium-40 in the mantle. The result: temperature climbs roughly "}},{"k":"25_30_c_per_kilometer","v":{"s":"25–30°C per kilometer"}},{"k":"as_you_go_down_in_volcanic_regions_ice","v":{"s":" as you go down. In volcanic regions (Iceland, Yellowstone, Italy) the gradient is much steeper — 60–200°C/km."}},{"k":"try_it_how_deep_before_you_reach_steam","v":{"s":"🔬 Try it: how deep before you reach steam?"}},{"k":"surface_temperature","v":{"s":"Surface temperature"}},{"k":"maine_annual_avg_7_c_death_valley_summ","v":{"s":"Maine annual avg ~7°C. Death Valley summer ~45°C. Antarctic interior − 50°C."}},{"k":"geothermal_gradient","v":{"s":"Geothermal gradient"}},{"k":"25_30_c_km_is_normal_continental_crust","v":{"s":"25–30°C/km is normal continental crust. 60–100 in volcanic zones. 200+ at Iceland’s rift."}},{"k":"drill_depth","v":{"s":"Drill depth"}},{"k":"most_us_oil_wells_1_3_km_deepest_geoth","v":{"s":"Most US oil wells: 1–3 km. Deepest geothermal: ~5 km. Kola Superdeep (research): 12.3 km."}},{"k":"best_matched_plant_type_at_this_temper","v":{"s":"Best matched plant type at this temperature:"}},{"k":"four_plant_designs","v":{"s":"Four plant designs"}},{"k":"maine_reality_4","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"parabolic_trough","v":{"s":"Parabolic trough"}},{"k":"power_tower_heliostat","v":{"s":"Power tower (heliostat)"}},{"k":"parabolic_dish_stirling_engine","v":{"s":"Parabolic dish + Stirling engine"}},{"k":"linear_fresnel","v":{"s":"Linear Fresnel"}},{"k":"csp_vs_pv_the_big_difference","v":{"s":"CSP vs PV — the big difference"}},{"k":"pv_photovoltaic","v":{"s":"PV (Photovoltaic):"}},{"k":"photons_electrons_directly_instantly_c","v":{"s":" photons → electrons directly, instantly. Cheaper. But you can’t store electrons in bulk — need separate batteries."}},{"k":"csp_concentrated_solar_power","v":{"s":"CSP (Concentrated Solar Power):"}},{"k":"photons_heat_steam_electricity_more_ex","v":{"s":" photons → heat → steam → electricity. More expensive. But heat is CHEAP to store in molten salt at 565°C — a CSP plant can run 6–10 hours after sunset on stored heat."}},{"k":"four_csp_designs","v":{"s":"Four CSP designs"}},{"k":"why_csp_works_in_deserts_but_not_maine","v":{"s":"Why CSP works in deserts but not Maine"}},{"k":"csp_needs","v":{"s":"CSP needs "}},{"k":"sunlight_mirrors_can_t_focus_diffuse_c","v":{"s":" sunlight — mirrors can’t focus diffuse cloudy light. The economic threshold is ~2000 kWh/m²/year of direct normal irradiance (DNI). Maine averages 1000–1400 kWh/m²/year DNI, well below the cutoff. Spain, Morocco, Nevada, Arizona, Australian outback get 2200–2800 — ideal CSP territory."}},{"k":"wave_vs_tidal_different_physics","v":{"s":"Wave vs tidal — different physics"}},{"k":"wave_power","v":{"s":"Wave power"}},{"k":"comes_from_wind_transferring_energy_to","v":{"s":" comes from wind transferring energy to the ocean surface (waves are wind’s downstream effect). Wave heights are stochastic and depend on storms thousands of km away."}},{"k":"tidal_power","v":{"s":"Tidal power"}},{"k":"comes_from_gravitational_pull_of_the_m","v":{"s":" comes from gravitational pull of the Moon and Sun. Predictable to the minute, "}},{"k":"decades_in_advance","v":{"s":"decades in advance"}},{"k":"that_predictability_is_incredibly_valu","v":{"s":". That predictability is incredibly valuable for grid operators."}},{"k":"four_marine_energy_designs","v":{"s":"Four marine-energy designs"}},{"k":"maine_reality_5","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"three_pathways_from_organic_matter_to_","v":{"s":"Three pathways from organic matter to electricity"}},{"k":"caveat","v":{"s":"⚠ Caveat: "}},{"k":"is_biomass_really_renewable","v":{"s":"Is biomass really renewable?"}},{"k":"it_depends_on_the_timescale_burning_a_","v":{"s":"It depends on the timescale. Burning a tree releases CO₂ today; that carbon was pulled from the atmosphere over decades. If the next tree grows back at the same rate, the cycle is closed — "}},{"k":"carbon_neutral_but_if_you_cut_faster_t","v":{"s":" carbon-neutral. But if you cut faster than regrowth, it’s a net emitter for decades."}},{"k":"the_strongest_renewable_case_is_anaero","v":{"s":"The strongest renewable case is anaerobic digestion of waste streams"}},{"k":"dairy_manure_food_waste_sewage_landfil","v":{"s":" — dairy manure, food waste, sewage, landfill gas. That methane was going to be released anyway; capturing and burning it converts CH₂ into CO₂ (much weaker greenhouse gas) AND yields useful electricity + heat."}},{"k":"maine_reality_6","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine reality"}},{"k":"the_intermittency_problem_and_why_stor","v":{"s":"The intermittency problem — and why storage solves it"}},{"k":"sun_and_wind_don_t_generate_on_demand_","v":{"s":"Sun and wind don’t generate on demand. Solar peaks at noon; demand peaks 6–10 PM. Wind is windy when it’s windy. Without storage, a 100% wind-and-solar grid has hours of surplus and hours of nothing."}},{"k":"storage_moves_energy_across_time_absor","v":{"s":"Storage moves energy across time — absorb the midday solar surplus, release it at the evening peak. Different storage tech handles different durations: batteries for hours, pumped hydro for days, hydrogen for seasons."}},{"k":"five_storage_technologies_compared","v":{"s":"Five storage technologies compared"}},{"k":"pros","v":{"s":"✓ Pros: "}},{"k":"cons","v":{"s":"⚠ Cons: "}},{"k":"which_storage_do_you_actually_need","v":{"s":"Which storage do you actually need?"}},{"k":"hours_1_6_h","v":{"s":"Hours (1–6 h):"}},{"k":"lithium_ion_batteries_daily_solar_shif","v":{"s":" Lithium-ion batteries. Daily solar shifting, peak shaving, frequency regulation."}},{"k":"days_10_100_h","v":{"s":"Days (10–100 h):"}},{"k":"pumped_hydro_flow_batteries_compressed","v":{"s":" Pumped hydro, flow batteries, compressed air."}},{"k":"green_hydrogen_only_economic_option_fo","v":{"s":" Green hydrogen. Only economic option for storing summer solar to use in winter."}},{"k":"heat_for_buildings","v":{"s":"Heat for buildings:"}},{"k":"hot_water_tanks_ice_storage_building_t","v":{"s":" Hot-water tanks, ice storage, building thermal mass. Cheap, mature, underused."}},{"k":"try_again","v":{"s":"🔄 Try again"}},{"k":"back_to_menu","v":{"s":"← Back to menu"}},{"k":"what_this_quiz_covers_click_to_toggle","v":{"s":"📜 What this quiz covers (click to toggle)"}},{"k":"pass_at_70_earns_the_quiz_passed_badge","v":{"s":"Pass at 70% earns the Quiz Passed badge; 90% earns Quiz Ace."}},{"k":"solar_pv_2","v":{"s":"Solar PV:"}},{"k":"irradiance_efficiency_tilt_capacity_fa","v":{"s":" irradiance, efficiency, tilt, capacity factor."}},{"k":"v_law_betz_limit_capacity_factor_by_cl","v":{"s":" v³ law, Betz limit, capacity factor by class."}},{"k":"head_flow_turbine_types_pumped_storage","v":{"s":" head + flow, turbine types, pumped storage."}},{"k":"thermal_gradient_flash_vs_binary_vs_gs","v":{"s":" thermal gradient, flash vs binary vs GSHP."}},{"k":"wave_tidal_2","v":{"s":"Wave / Tidal:"}},{"k":"wave_power_formula_tidal_vs_water_dens","v":{"s":" wave power formula, tidal vs water density."}},{"k":"combustion_anaerobic_digestion_carbon_","v":{"s":" combustion, anaerobic digestion, carbon accounting."}},{"k":"storage_grid","v":{"s":"Storage + Grid:"}},{"k":"li_ion_vs_flow_vs_pumped_vs_hydrogen_c","v":{"s":" Li-ion vs flow vs pumped vs hydrogen, capacity factor, dispatchability."}},{"k":"question","v":{"s":"Question "}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"  ·  Score: "}},{"k":"rooftop_solar_pv","v":{"s":"Rooftop solar PV"}},{"k":"utility_solar_farm","v":{"s":"Utility solar farm"}},{"k":"onshore_wind","v":{"s":"Onshore wind"}},{"k":"offshore_wind","v":{"s":"Offshore wind"}},{"k":"hydropower_2","v":{"s":"Hydropower"}},{"k":"geothermal_2","v":{"s":"Geothermal"}},{"k":"wave_tidal_3","v":{"s":"Wave / Tidal"}},{"k":"biomass_biogas_2","v":{"s":"Biomass / biogas"}},{"k":"10_location_profiles_pick_the_best_ren","v":{"s":"10 location profiles — pick the best renewable"}},{"k":"for_each_location_pick_the_best_renewa","v":{"s":"For each location, pick the best renewable energy source from 8 options. Vignettes mix Maine-specific (Aroostook wind, Bath tidal, paper-mill biomass) and global (Iceland geothermal, Mojave utility PV, North Sea offshore wind, PNW hydro). Coaching cites the resource match + the trade-offs against alternatives."}},{"k":"the_8_renewable_sources","v":{"s":"The 8 renewable sources"}},{"k":"start_site_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start — site 1 of 10"}},{"k":"site","v":{"s":"Site "}},{"k":"score_2","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_best_renewable_for_this_site","v":{"s":"Pick the best renewable for this site"}},{"k":"all_10_sites_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 sites complete"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_site","v":{"s":"➡️ Next site"}},{"k":"every_organization_cited_in_this_tool_","v":{"s":"Every organization cited in this tool. NREL is the single best free resource for capacity factors and cost trends; the Annual Technology Baseline (ATB) is updated yearly."}},{"k":"first_what_is_capacity_factor_and_why_","v":{"s":"First — what is \"capacity factor\" and why does it matter?"}},{"k":"capacity_factor_2","v":{"s":"Capacity factor"}},{"k":"actual_annual_energy_output_what_the_p","v":{"s":" = actual annual energy output ÷ what the plant would produce at 100% rated power 24/7/365."}},{"k":"example_a_1_mw_solar_farm_in_maine_gen","v":{"s":"Example: a 1 MW solar farm in Maine generates ~1.7 GWh per year. If it ran flat-out 24/7 it would produce 8.76 GWh. So its capacity factor is 1.7 / 8.76 ≈ "}},{"k":"this_is_why_1_gw_of_solar_and_1_gw_of_","v":{"s":"This is why \"1 GW of solar\" and \"1 GW of nuclear\" are NOT equivalent — the nuclear plant runs near 90% CF, the solar farm near 20%. To match annual energy from 1 GW nuclear, you need ~4–5 GW of solar (plus storage to match dispatchability)."}},{"k":"side_by_side_comparison","v":{"s":"Side-by-side comparison"}},{"k":"comparison_table_of_renewable_and_refe","v":{"s":"Comparison table of renewable and reference electricity sources"}},{"k":"sources_nrel_annual_technology_baselin","v":{"s":"Sources: NREL Annual Technology Baseline 2024 (LCOE, capacity factor); IPCC AR6 WG3 Annex III (lifecycle CO₂ medians); Macknick 2012 + NREL (water); Hertwich 2015 + DOE (land use). The two reference rows (red) are NOT renewables — included for honest comparison."}},{"k":"important_caveats","v":{"s":"Important caveats"}},{"k":"solar_pv_3","v":{"s":"Solar PV"}},{"k":"wind_2","v":{"s":"Wind"}},{"k":"hydro","v":{"s":"Hydro"}},{"k":"geothermal_3","v":{"s":"Geothermal"}},{"k":"gas_combined_cycle","v":{"s":"Gas (combined-cycle)"}},{"k":"nuclear","v":{"s":"Nuclear"}},{"k":"capacity_factor_typical_of_nameplate_a","v":{"s":"Capacity factor — typical % of nameplate actually delivered"}},{"k":"a_1_mw_solar_farm_and_a_1_mw_nuclear_p","v":{"s":"A 1 MW solar farm and a 1 MW nuclear plant have the same nameplate — but very different annual output. This is why nameplate MW alone is misleading."}},{"k":"design_an_electricity_mix_for_a_region","v":{"s":"Design an electricity mix for a region. Slide each source. The remainder fills with coal (the dirtiest baseline). See lifecycle CO₂, reliability, and how much storage you would need."}},{"k":"your_mix_at_a_glance","v":{"s":"📈 Your mix at a glance"}},{"k":"lifecycle_co","v":{"s":"Lifecycle CO₂"}},{"k":"reliability_score","v":{"s":"Reliability score"}},{"k":"higher_more_dispatchable","v":{"s":"Higher = more dispatchable"}},{"k":"renewable_share","v":{"s":"Renewable share"}},{"k":"solar_wind_hydro_geo","v":{"s":"Solar + wind + hydro + geo"}},{"k":"load_2024_us_grid","v":{"s":"Load: 2024 US grid"}},{"k":"load_2050_net_zero","v":{"s":"Load: 2050 net-zero"}},{"k":"load_denmark_mix","v":{"s":"Load: Denmark mix"}},{"k":"load_iceland","v":{"s":"Load: Iceland"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"24_hour_grid_balance","v":{"s":"⚡ 24-hour grid balance"}},{"k":"stacked_supply_baseload_wind_solar_vs_","v":{"s":"Stacked supply (baseload + wind + solar) vs hourly demand for a residential grid. Adjust the storage slider to see how batteries smooth the gap."}},{"k":"battery_capacity","v":{"s":"🔋 Battery capacity"}},{"k":"battery_capacity_in_hours_of_peak_dema","v":{"s":"Battery capacity in hours of peak demand"}},{"k":"0_h_no_storage_4_h_typical_tesla_power","v":{"s":"0 h = no storage. 4 h ≈ typical Tesla Powerwall stack. 8+ h needed for high-renewable grids."}},{"k":"baseload","v":{"s":"Baseload"}},{"k":"wind_3","v":{"s":"Wind"}},{"k":"solar","v":{"s":"Solar"}},{"k":"demand","v":{"s":"Demand"}},{"k":"daily_supply_demand","v":{"s":"Daily supply / demand"}},{"k":"hours_of_unmet_demand","v":{"s":"Hours of unmet demand"}},{"k":"hours_of_curtailed_surplus","v":{"s":"Hours of curtailed surplus"}},{"k":"battery_throughput_day","v":{"s":"Battery throughput / day"}},{"k":"peak_demand_hours","v":{"s":"× peak-demand-hours"}},{"k":"what_to_notice","v":{"s":"What to notice: "}},{"k":"rough_estimate_for_a_maine_rooftop_rea","v":{"s":"Rough estimate for a Maine rooftop. Real quotes vary by orientation, shading, and contractor. Numbers below assume CMP territory + south-facing roof + 2024–25 incentive levels. Always get 3 quotes."}},{"k":"usable_south_facing_roof_area","v":{"s":"🏠 Usable south-facing roof area"}},{"k":"a_typical_maine_2_story_has_30_60_m_of","v":{"s":"A typical Maine 2-story has 30–60 m² of usable south slope. 1 m² ≈ 10.8 ft²."}},{"k":"average_monthly_electric_bill","v":{"s":"💵 Average monthly electric bill"}},{"k":"maine_residential_average_130_mo_2024_","v":{"s":"Maine residential average ~$130/mo (2024). Heat-pump homes run $200–400/mo in winter."}},{"k":"federal_investment_tax_credit","v":{"s":"🇺🇸 Federal Investment Tax Credit"}},{"k":"30_through_2032_under_current_law_26_i","v":{"s":"30% through 2032 under current law. 26% if installed before 2022 or if law changes."}},{"k":"estimated_outcome","v":{"s":"📊 Estimated outcome"}},{"k":"annual_generation","v":{"s":"Annual generation"}},{"k":"annual_savings","v":{"s":"Annual savings"}},{"k":"after_incentive_cost","v":{"s":"After-incentive cost"}},{"k":"payback_time","v":{"s":"Payback time"}},{"k":"25_yr_panel_lifespan","v":{"s":"25-yr panel lifespan"}},{"k":"25_yr_net_savings","v":{"s":"25-yr net savings"}},{"k":"excludes_electricity_rate_inflation_ty","v":{"s":"Excludes electricity-rate inflation (typically +)"}},{"k":"co_avoided_yr","v":{"s":"CO₂ avoided / yr"}},{"k":"maine_specific_notes","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine-specific notes"}},{"k":"cold_sun_high_efficiency_maine_winter_","v":{"s":"Cold + sun = high efficiency. Maine winter sun is short but the cold actually boosts panel output."}},{"k":"net_metering_rules_in_maine_have_shift","v":{"s":"Net metering rules in Maine have shifted multiple times. Current law gives ~retail rate credit; check the PUC for the current arrangement before signing."}},{"k":"snow_on_panels_tilt_30_lets_snow_slide","v":{"s":"Snow on panels: tilt ≥ 30° lets snow slide off. Bifacial panels can pick up reflected light from snow on the ground."}},{"k":"pair_with_a_heat_pump_for_biggest_impa","v":{"s":"Pair with a heat pump for biggest impact: summer solar runs the heat pump in shoulder seasons; you also free up oil dollars."}},{"k":"efficiency_maine_offers_0_loans_up_to_","v":{"s":"Efficiency Maine offers 0% loans up to $15,000 for solar + heat pump combos."}},{"k":"adding_an_ev","v":{"s":"🔗 Adding an EV?"}},{"k":"layer_in_ev_charging_math_from_alloflo","v":{"s":" Layer in EV charging math from AlloFlow "}},{"k":"a_typical_maine_commute_30_mi_day_adds","v":{"s":". A typical Maine commute (~30 mi/day) adds ~7–10 kWh/day to your home load — significant for solar sizing, and EVs unlock V2G later."}},{"k":"portland_metro","v":{"s":"Portland Metro"}},{"k":"dense_urban_suburb_lots_of_roofs_but_m","v":{"s":"Dense urban + suburb. Lots of roofs, but more competing firms. Average household electric bill $145/mo."}},{"k":"midcoast_maine","v":{"s":"Midcoast Maine"}},{"k":"mix_of_year_round_homes_summer_only_ca","v":{"s":"Mix of year-round homes + summer-only camps. Premium roofs (slate, complex). Tourist economy seasonality. Average bill $160/mo."}},{"k":"rural_northern_maine","v":{"s":"Rural Northern Maine"}},{"k":"low_population_density_long_drives_bet","v":{"s":"Low population density, long drives between jobs. Lower price ceiling but grid-resilience + winter demand high. Average bill $175/mo."}},{"k":"tier_1_maxeon_rec","v":{"s":"Tier-1 (Maxeon / REC)"}},{"k":"premium_panels_higher_upfront_cost_25_","v":{"s":"Premium panels. Higher upfront cost, 25-yr power warranty, low warranty-claim rate. Customers notice the brand."}},{"k":"tier_2_mid_chinese","v":{"s":"Tier-2 (mid-Chinese)"}},{"k":"lower_cost_panels_12_yr_warranty_highe","v":{"s":"Lower cost panels. 12-yr warranty + higher claim rate. Margin advantage if quality holds; reputation risk if it does not."}},{"k":"residential_price_sensitive","v":{"s":"Residential · price-sensitive"}},{"k":"residential_quality_driven","v":{"s":"Residential · quality-driven"}},{"k":"small_commercial","v":{"s":"Small commercial"}},{"k":"municipal","v":{"s":"Municipal"}},{"k":"congress_passed_a_step_down_the_30_itc","v":{"s":"Congress passed a step-down: the 30% ITC drops to 26% starting next year. Customer demand softens; expect ~10% fewer leads next year."}},{"k":"panels_stuck_at_port_for_6_weeks_half_","v":{"s":"Panels stuck at port for 6 weeks. Half this year's installs slip into next year. Cash hit but no permanent damage."}},{"k":"three_feet_of_snow_in_march_pushed_ins","v":{"s":"Three feet of snow in March pushed installs back. Labor hours up 20%, no extra revenue."}},{"k":"a_big_out_of_state_firm_entered_your_m","v":{"s":"A big out-of-state firm entered your market with aggressive pricing. Win-rate drops 15% this year only."}},{"k":"your_last_six_customers_loved_you_thre","v":{"s":"Your last six customers loved you. Three referrals come in unprompted; satisfaction-boosted leads next year."}},{"k":"panel_inverter_prices_up_8_mid_year_co","v":{"s":"Panel + inverter prices up 8% mid-year. COGS rises until you sign new supplier contracts next year."}},{"k":"state_rolled_out_a_one_page_interconne","v":{"s":"State rolled out a one-page interconnection form. Permit costs drop $200 per install; labor 0.5 day shorter."}},{"k":"three_apprentices_from_emcc_finish_the","v":{"s":"Three apprentices from EMCC finish their hours this quarter. You can hire any of them at the lower trainee rate."}},{"k":"how_the_sim_works","v":{"s":"📜 How the sim works"}},{"k":"4_years_4_decisions_per_year_survive_a","v":{"s":"4 years. 4 decisions per year. Survive AND grow to 100+ installs to win."}},{"k":"each_year_you_decide","v":{"s":"🎯 Each year you decide"}},{"k":"which_leads_to_bid_on","v":{"s":"Which leads to bid on"}},{"k":"bid_price_per_watt","v":{"s":"Bid price per watt"}},{"k":"tier_1_vs_tier_2_panel_supplier","v":{"s":"Tier-1 vs tier-2 panel supplier"}},{"k":"hire_fire_installers_inspectors_sales","v":{"s":"Hire / fire installers, inspectors, sales"}},{"k":"failure_modes","v":{"s":"⚠ Failure modes"}},{"k":"cash_0_for_two_years_in_a_row_bankrupt","v":{"s":"Cash < $0 for two years in a row → bankruptcy"}},{"k":"customer_satisfaction_50_reputation_co","v":{"s":"Customer satisfaction < 50 → reputation collapse, leads dry up"}},{"k":"year_4_ends_fewer_than_100_cumulative_","v":{"s":"Year 4 ends + fewer than 100 cumulative installs → short of target"}},{"k":"numbers_are_maine_realistic_panel_cost","v":{"s":"Numbers are Maine-realistic: panel cost ~$1.50 to $2.20/W wholesale, installs sold at $3 to $4/W, typical firm does 50 to 200 installs/yr. Carries some randomness (events, win-rolls) so two runs are not identical."}},{"k":"pick_your_service_area","v":{"s":"🗺 Pick your service area"}},{"k":"event_this_year","v":{"s":"Event this year"}},{"k":"you_hit_the_100_install_target_without","v":{"s":"You hit the 100+ install target without going bankrupt or losing reputation. Real-world equivalent: a small Maine firm running profitable, employing local labor, building grid-scale capacity one rooftop at a time."}},{"k":"bankruptcy_cash_hit_0_two_years_runnin","v":{"s":"💸 Bankruptcy · cash hit $0 two years running"}},{"k":"two_consecutive_negative_cash_years_cl","v":{"s":"Two consecutive negative-cash years closed the firm. Common causes: under-bidding leads, over-hiring before revenue ramps, or eating warranty claims from a low-quality supplier. Try again with tighter bids or a smaller workforce."}},{"k":"reputation_collapse_customer_satisfact","v":{"s":"📉 Reputation collapse · customer satisfaction fell below 50"}},{"k":"word_got_around_leads_dried_up_often_c","v":{"s":"Word got around. Leads dried up. Often caused by tier-2 panels failing under warranty, or over-loaded installers cutting corners. The math: customer-sat compounds across years. Stay above 60 to compound positively."}},{"k":"you_stayed_solvent_and_kept_your_reput","v":{"s":"You stayed solvent and kept your reputation, but did not reach 100 cumulative installs. The firm exists, but did not scale enough to call it a strong outcome. Try a bigger workforce or a higher-volume service area."}},{"k":"year_by_year","v":{"s":"📋 Year-by-year"}},{"k":"run_a_new_campaign","v":{"s":"🔁 Run a new campaign"}},{"k":"incoming_leads_pick_which_to_bid_on","v":{"s":"📞 Incoming leads — pick which to bid on"}},{"k":"bid_price","v":{"s":"💵 Bid price"}},{"k":"charge_per_watt_competitors_bid_2_80_3","v":{"s":"Charge per watt. Competitors bid $2.80–$3.60/W. Low = more wins, lower margin. High = fewer wins, fatter margin."}},{"k":"bid_price_per_watt_2","v":{"s":"Bid price per watt"}},{"k":"panel_supplier","v":{"s":"🏭 Panel supplier"}},{"k":"workforce","v":{"s":"👷 Workforce"}},{"k":"installers_do_the_work_inspectors_hand","v":{"s":"Installers do the work. Inspectors handle QA. Sales staff expands your bid bandwidth. Each annual headcount has a salary. Hiring costs $3K–$5K up front."}},{"k":"recent_events","v":{"s":"📋 Recent events"}},{"k":"pick_at_least_one_lead_to_bid_on_befor","v":{"s":"⚠ Pick at least one lead to bid on before advancing the year."}},{"k":"terms_used_throughout_this_lab_and_thr","v":{"s":"Terms used throughout this lab — and throughout any conversation about energy. Skim before reading the news; come back when something is fuzzy."}},{"k":"seven_misconceptions_you_will_hear_in_","v":{"s":"Seven misconceptions you will hear in news, comment threads, or family arguments. Each correction is sourced — bring receipts."}},{"k":"myth","v":{"s":"❌ Myth: "}},{"k":"what_s_actually_true","v":{"s":"✓ What's actually true: "}},{"k":"a_note_on_scope","v":{"s":"⚠ A note on scope"}},{"k":"nuclear_fission","v":{"s":"Nuclear fission"}},{"k":"capacity_factor_3","v":{"s":"Capacity factor: "}},{"k":"lifecycle_co_2","v":{"s":"Lifecycle CO₂: "}},{"k":"pros_2","v":{"s":"✓ Pros"}},{"k":"cons_2","v":{"s":"⚠ Cons"}},{"k":"reactor_designs","v":{"s":"Reactor designs"}},{"k":"what_about_fusion","v":{"s":"🔬 What about fusion?"}},{"k":"pick_a_scenario_write_4_8_sentences_de","v":{"s":"Pick a scenario. Write 4–8 sentences describing how you would design the electricity system. "}},{"k":"ai_critiques_your_reasoning","v":{"s":"AI critiques your reasoning"}},{"k":"against_an_engineering_rubric_and_the_","v":{"s":" against an engineering rubric and the same ground-truth facts you read in the source modules."}},{"k":"pick_a_scenario","v":{"s":"📋 Pick a scenario"}},{"k":"hint","v":{"s":"💡 Hint: "}},{"k":"your_design_4_8_sentences","v":{"s":"✏️ Your design (4–8 sentences)"}},{"k":"walk_through_your_system_which_sources","v":{"s":"Walk through your system. Which sources? Why? What storage? What backup? Any failure modes you would address?"}},{"k":"your_renewable_energy_system_design","v":{"s":"Your renewable energy system design"}},{"k":"characters_aim_for_300_800","v":{"s":" characters. Aim for ~300–800."}},{"k":"critique","v":{"s":"🎓 Critique"}},{"k":"solar_pv_cell","v":{"s":"Solar PV cell"}},{"k":"wind_turbine","v":{"s":"Wind turbine"}},{"k":"hydro_dam","v":{"s":"Hydro dam"}},{"k":"geothermal_flash_plant","v":{"s":"Geothermal flash plant"}},{"k":"csp_power_tower","v":{"s":"CSP power tower"}},{"k":"ground_source_heat_pump","v":{"s":"Ground-source heat pump"}},{"k":"wave_owc","v":{"s":"Wave OWC"}},{"k":"anaerobic_digester","v":{"s":"Anaerobic digester"}},{"k":"pumped_hydro_storage","v":{"s":"Pumped hydro storage"}},{"k":"cross_section_of_a_silicon_photovoltai","v":{"s":"Cross-section of a silicon photovoltaic cell"}},{"k":"photons_arrive_from_above_hit_silicon_","v":{"s":"Photons arrive from above, hit silicon, knock electrons loose. The P-N junction sorts charges. Electrons flow through an external circuit as DC current."}},{"k":"sunlight","v":{"s":"Sunlight"}},{"k":"n_type_silicon_extra_electrons","v":{"s":"N-type silicon (extra electrons)"}},{"k":"p_n_junction","v":{"s":"P-N junction"}},{"k":"p_type_silicon_electron_holes","v":{"s":"P-type silicon (electron \"holes\")"}},{"k":"external_circuit_lamp_home","v":{"s":"External circuit (lamp, home)"}},{"k":"1_photon_arrives_2_knocks_electron_loo","v":{"s":"1. Photon arrives  →  2. Knocks electron loose  →  3. P-N junction sorts charges  →  4. Electrons flow as DC current"}},{"k":"wind_turbine_cutaway","v":{"s":"Wind turbine cutaway"}},{"k":"wind_spins_the_rotor_blades_which_turn","v":{"s":"Wind spins the rotor blades, which turn a low-speed shaft connected to a gearbox, which spins a high-speed shaft into the generator. The generator produces electricity. The yaw drive points the turbine into the wind. Cables carry power down through the tower to the grid."}},{"k":"tower_80_120_m_tall","v":{"s":"Tower (80–120 m tall)"}},{"k":"gear","v":{"s":"Gear"}},{"k":"generator","v":{"s":"Generator"}},{"k":"wind_4","v":{"s":"Wind →"}},{"k":"power_cable","v":{"s":"Power cable"}},{"k":"to_grid","v":{"s":"to grid"}},{"k":"1_blades_capture_kinetic_energy","v":{"s":"1. Blades capture kinetic energy"}},{"k":"2_hub_low_speed_shaft","v":{"s":"2. Hub + low-speed shaft"}},{"k":"3_gearbox_80","v":{"s":"3. Gearbox (× ~80)"}},{"k":"4_generator","v":{"s":"4. Generator"}},{"k":"5_yaw_drive_aims_at_wind","v":{"s":"5. Yaw drive aims at wind"}},{"k":"power_scales_with_v_cube_of_wind_speed","v":{"s":"Power scales with v³ (cube of wind speed). Betz limit: max 59.3% extractable."}},{"k":"flash_steam_geothermal_power_plant","v":{"s":"Flash-steam geothermal power plant"}},{"k":"hot_pressurized_water_is_pumped_up_the","v":{"s":"Hot pressurized water is pumped up the production well. Pressure drops in the flash tank, instantly vaporizing most of it to steam. The steam spins a turbine. After condensing, the cooled water is reinjected to the reservoir."}},{"k":"surface","v":{"s":"Surface"}},{"k":"hot_rock_reservoir_180_300_c","v":{"s":"Hot rock reservoir (180–300 °C)"}},{"k":"hot_water","v":{"s":"Hot water"}},{"k":"flash","v":{"s":"Flash"}},{"k":"steam","v":{"s":"Steam"}},{"k":"turbine","v":{"s":"Turbine"}},{"k":"generator_2","v":{"s":"Generator"}},{"k":"to_grid_2","v":{"s":"To grid"}},{"k":"condenser","v":{"s":"Condenser"}},{"k":"cool_water","v":{"s":"Cool water"}},{"k":"1_production_well","v":{"s":"1. Production well →"}},{"k":"2_flash_to_steam","v":{"s":"2. Flash to steam"}},{"k":"3_spin_turbine","v":{"s":"3. Spin turbine"}},{"k":"4_condense_reinject","v":{"s":"4. Condense + reinject"}},{"k":"flash_steam_plant","v":{"s":"Flash-steam plant"}},{"k":"ground_source_heat_pump_cycle","v":{"s":"Ground-source heat pump cycle"}},{"k":"a_refrigeration_cycle_that_moves_heat_","v":{"s":"A refrigeration cycle that moves heat between the constant-temperature ground and a home. In winter, evaporator absorbs heat from the ground loop; compressor raises temperature; condenser releases heat to the home; expansion valve resets the cycle. COP 3 to 5 means 3 to 5 units of heat moved per unit of electricity used."}},{"k":"home","v":{"s":"Home"}},{"k":"ground_10_c","v":{"s":"Ground (~10°C)"}},{"k":"ground_loop_water_antifreeze","v":{"s":"Ground loop (water + antifreeze)"}},{"k":"heat_pump_unit","v":{"s":"Heat pump unit"}},{"k":"warm_air","v":{"s":"Warm air"}},{"k":"to_home","v":{"s":"to home"}},{"k":"electricity_in","v":{"s":"⚡ Electricity in"}},{"k":"heat_pump_moves_heat_it_does_not_gener","v":{"s":"Heat pump MOVES heat — it does not generate it"}},{"k":"cop_3_5_every_1_unit_of_electricity_mo","v":{"s":"COP 3–5: every 1 unit of electricity moves 3–5 units of heat into the home"}},{"k":"hydroelectric_dam_cross_section","v":{"s":"Hydroelectric dam cross-section"}},{"k":"a_reservoir_behind_a_concrete_dam_stor","v":{"s":"A reservoir behind a concrete dam stores water at high elevation. Water flows down through the penstock, spins a turbine, drives a generator, and exits the tailrace. Power equals density times gravity times head times flow times efficiency."}},{"k":"reservoir","v":{"s":"Reservoir"}},{"k":"potential_energy","v":{"s":"(potential energy)"}},{"k":"head_h","v":{"s":"Head (h)"}},{"k":"penstock","v":{"s":"Penstock"}},{"k":"turbine_2","v":{"s":"Turbine"}},{"k":"generator_3","v":{"s":"Generator"}},{"k":"to_grid_3","v":{"s":"To grid"}},{"k":"tailrace_low_elevation","v":{"s":"Tailrace (low elevation)"}},{"k":"hydro_power_p_g_h_q","v":{"s":"Hydro power: P = ρ · g · h · Q · η"}},{"k":"1_reservoir_holds_water_at_high_elevat","v":{"s":"1. Reservoir holds water at high elevation (potential energy)"}},{"k":"2_penstock_pipes_water_down_gains_kine","v":{"s":"2. Penstock pipes water down — gains kinetic energy"}},{"k":"3_turbine_generator_convert_to_electri","v":{"s":"3. Turbine + generator convert to electricity"}},{"k":"doubling_either_head_or_flow_doubles_t","v":{"s":"Doubling either head OR flow doubles the power output"}},{"k":"concentrated_solar_power_tower_with_mo","v":{"s":"Concentrated Solar Power tower with molten salt storage"}},{"k":"a_field_of_mirrors_called_heliostats_f","v":{"s":"A field of mirrors called heliostats focuses sunlight onto a central tower receiver. Molten salt circulates through the receiver, heating to 565 degrees Celsius. Hot salt is stored in an insulated tank. Demand-side, hot salt flows through a heat exchanger to make steam, which spins a turbine and generator. Cold salt returns to a second tank to be reheated."}},{"k":"heliostat_field_1000s_of_mirrors","v":{"s":"Heliostat field (1000s of mirrors)"}},{"k":"receiver","v":{"s":"Receiver"}},{"k":"hot_salt","v":{"s":"Hot salt"}},{"k":"cold_salt","v":{"s":"Cold salt"}},{"k":"heat","v":{"s":"Heat"}},{"k":"turbine_3","v":{"s":"Turbine"}},{"k":"steam_2","v":{"s":"Steam"}},{"k":"to_grid_4","v":{"s":"To grid"}},{"k":"csp_power_tower_with_thermal_energy_st","v":{"s":"CSP power tower with thermal energy storage"}},{"k":"hot_salt_can_run_the_turbine_for_6_10_","v":{"s":"Hot salt can run the turbine for 6–10 hours after sunset"}},{"k":"oscillating_water_column_wave_energy_c","v":{"s":"Oscillating water column wave-energy converter"}},{"k":"a_partly_submerged_chamber_traps_a_col","v":{"s":"A partly submerged chamber traps a column of air above the rising and falling sea surface. As waves push the water up and down inside the chamber, the trapped air is forced in and out through a Wells turbine — which spins the same direction regardless of which way the air flows. The turbine drives a generator."}},{"k":"water_rises_falls","v":{"s":"Water rises/falls"}},{"k":"air_column","v":{"s":"Air column"}},{"k":"wells","v":{"s":"Wells"}},{"k":"air","v":{"s":"Air ↕"}},{"k":"generator_4","v":{"s":"Generator"}},{"k":"to_grid_5","v":{"s":"To grid"}},{"k":"incoming_wave","v":{"s":"Incoming wave →"}},{"k":"oscillating_water_column_owc","v":{"s":"Oscillating water column (OWC)"}},{"k":"wells_turbine_spins_the_same_direction","v":{"s":"Wells turbine spins the SAME direction whether air flows in OR out — clever bidirectional design"}},{"k":"anaerobic_digester_biogas_system","v":{"s":"Anaerobic digester biogas system"}},{"k":"food_waste_manure_or_sewage_enters_a_s","v":{"s":"Food waste, manure, or sewage enters a sealed tank where bacteria break it down without oxygen. The tank produces biogas — about 60 percent methane — which is burned in a generator or upgraded to pipeline-grade renewable natural gas. The leftover digestate is high-quality fertilizer."}},{"k":"feedstock","v":{"s":"Feedstock"}},{"k":"sealed_digester_tank","v":{"s":"Sealed digester tank"}},{"k":"no_oxygen","v":{"s":"(no oxygen)"}},{"k":"bacteria_35_55_c","v":{"s":"Bacteria + 35–55 °C"}},{"k":"biogas_60_ch","v":{"s":"Biogas (~60% CH₄)"}},{"k":"electricity_heat","v":{"s":"Electricity + heat"}},{"k":"digestate","v":{"s":"Digestate"}},{"k":"anaerobic_digestion_capture_methane_th","v":{"s":"Anaerobic digestion: capture methane that would otherwise leak"}},{"k":"1_feedstock_in_food_waste_manure_sewag","v":{"s":"1. Feedstock in (food waste, manure, sewage)"}},{"k":"2_bacteria_digest_in_sealed_warm_tank","v":{"s":"2. Bacteria digest in sealed warm tank"}},{"k":"3_biogas_generator_or_upgrade_to_pipel","v":{"s":"3. Biogas → generator OR upgrade to pipeline gas"}},{"k":"4_digestate_out_as_fertilizer","v":{"s":"4. Digestate out as fertilizer"}},{"k":"methane_is_84_worse_than_co_over_20_yr","v":{"s":"Methane is ~84× worse than CO₂ over 20 yr — burning it is a NET WIN"}},{"k":"pumped_hydro_energy_storage","v":{"s":"Pumped hydro energy storage"}},{"k":"pumped_hydro_stores_electricity_by_pum","v":{"s":"Pumped hydro stores electricity by pumping water from a low reservoir up to a high reservoir when power is cheap. When power is needed, the water flows back down through a reversible pump-turbine that drives a generator. Round-trip efficiency 75 to 85 percent. About 95 percent of the world installed grid storage is pumped hydro."}},{"k":"upper_reservoir","v":{"s":"Upper reservoir"}},{"k":"lower_reservoir","v":{"s":"Lower reservoir"}},{"k":"reversible","v":{"s":"Reversible"}},{"k":"motor","v":{"s":"Motor /"}},{"k":"cheap_solar_noon","v":{"s":"(cheap solar @ noon)"}},{"k":"pump_water_up","v":{"s":"Pump water UP"}},{"k":"evening_peak_demand","v":{"s":"(evening peak demand)"}},{"k":"water_flows_down","v":{"s":"Water flows DOWN"}},{"k":"grid","v":{"s":"Grid"}},{"k":"pumped_hydro_storage_the_original_grid","v":{"s":"Pumped hydro storage — the original grid battery"}},{"k":"round_trip_efficiency_75_85_95_of_the_","v":{"s":"Round-trip efficiency 75–85%. ~95% of the world's installed grid storage."}},{"k":"labeled_cross_sections_of_how_four_com","v":{"s":"Labeled cross-sections of how four common renewable systems actually work. Switch tabs to compare designs."}},{"k":"schematic_diagrams","v":{"s":"Schematic diagrams"}},{"k":"these_are_simplified_schematics_for_le","v":{"s":"These are simplified schematics for learning. Real plants have additional safety, control, and efficiency systems (cooling, lubrication, transformers, switchgear, protection relays, SCADA). Use these to build intuition; consult engineering references for design."}},{"k":"the_bedrock_fact_about_electricity","v":{"s":"The bedrock fact about electricity"}},{"k":"generation_must_equal_consumption_ever","v":{"s":"Generation must equal consumption every single second."}},{"k":"electricity_in_the_grid_travels_at_the","v":{"s":" Electricity in the grid travels at ~⅔ the speed of light and there is essentially no buffer. If a power plant trips offline, frequency drops within seconds; if a city loses load, frequency rises. Grid operators run a perpetual real-time balancing act."}},{"k":"for_most_of_grid_history_balancing_mea","v":{"s":"For most of grid history, \"balancing\" meant adjusting a few large dispatchable plants. As wind + solar grow, the grid is becoming a million-piece orchestra instead of a string quartet — and "}},{"k":"smart_grid_technology","v":{"s":"smart-grid technology"}},{"k":"is_the_conductor","v":{"s":" is the conductor."}},{"k":"what_it_is","v":{"s":"What it is: "}},{"k":"who_runs_it","v":{"s":"Who runs it: "}},{"k":"why_it_matters","v":{"s":"Why it matters: "}},{"k":"big_takeaway","v":{"s":"🎯 Big takeaway"}},{"k":"a_high_renewable_future_depends_just_a","v":{"s":"A high-renewable future depends just as much on smart-grid software, transmission, and demand-side flexibility as it does on the next solar farm or wind turbine. The \"boring\" infrastructure pieces are where most of the engineering happens."}},{"k":"renewable_energy_spans_every_level_of_","v":{"s":"Renewable energy spans every level of training: high-school cert programs, 2-year community college, apprenticeships, 4-year engineering degrees, graduate research, policy work. Salaries from BLS OEWS 2024 medians; growth projections from BLS 2022–2032 outlook. Local Maine pipelines highlighted."}},{"k":"salary","v":{"s":"💵 Salary: "}},{"k":"outlook","v":{"s":"📈 Outlook: "}},{"k":"how_to_get_there","v":{"s":"🎓 How to get there: "}},{"k":"where","v":{"s":"📍 Where: "}},{"k":"maine_training_pipeline","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine training pipeline"}},{"k":"pair_with","v":{"s":"🔗 Pair with"}},{"k":"allobot_sage","v":{"s":"AlloBot Sage"}},{"k":"uses_retrieval_practice_combat_to_dril","v":{"s":" uses retrieval-practice combat to drill terminology + facts from this tool — useful for kids who want career-skill \"credits\" before transcripts catch up."}},{"k":"heat_pumps_don_t_generate_heat_they_mo","v":{"s":"Heat pumps don't generate heat — they MOVE it"}},{"k":"the_cop_magic_trick","v":{"s":"⚡ The COP magic trick"}},{"k":"four_flavors_of_heat_pump","v":{"s":"Four flavors of heat pump"}},{"k":"best_for","v":{"s":"Best for: "}},{"k":"common_myths","v":{"s":"🧐 Common myths"}},{"k":"truth","v":{"s":"✓ Truth: "}},{"k":"pairing_with_renewables","v":{"s":"🔌 Pairing with renewables"}},{"k":"maine_the_heat_pump_capital","v":{"s":"🌲 Maine: the heat-pump capital"}},{"k":"16_famous_renewable_installations_acro","v":{"s":"16 famous renewable installations across all 8 sources. Capacities are nameplate (rated max). Filter to compare across one source or browse all."}},{"k":"filter_plants_by_source_type","v":{"s":"Filter plants by source type"}},{"k":"no_plants_in_this_filter","v":{"s":"No plants in this filter."}},{"k":"hydrogen_101","v":{"s":"Hydrogen 101"}},{"k":"hydrogen_h_is_an_energy","v":{"s":"Hydrogen (H₂) is an energy "}},{"k":"not_an_energy","v":{"s":", not an energy "}},{"k":"you_spend_energy_to_make_it_you_get_en","v":{"s":". You spend energy to make it; you get energy back when you burn it or run it through a fuel cell. Whether that's clean depends entirely on HOW you made it — hence the color codes."}},{"k":"critical_fact","v":{"s":"Critical fact: "}},{"k":"95_of_hydrogen_produced_today_is_gray_","v":{"s":"~95% of hydrogen produced today is \"gray\" — made from natural gas with NO carbon capture. The \"hydrogen economy\" pitch usually means switching to GREEN hydrogen, which is a much smaller current reality."}},{"k":"the_color_taxonomy","v":{"s":"🎨 The color taxonomy"}},{"k":"verdict","v":{"s":"⚖ Verdict: "}},{"k":"where_hydrogen_actually_makes_sense","v":{"s":"🎯 Where hydrogen actually makes sense"}},{"k":"five_things_worth_remembering","v":{"s":"💡 Five things worth remembering"}},{"k":"the_energy_transition_is_technical_and","v":{"s":"The energy transition is technical AND political"}},{"k":"every_other_module_in_this_lab_focuses","v":{"s":"Every other module in this lab focuses on the engineering — the physics of how each source generates electricity. This page asks the harder questions: "}},{"k":"who_pays_who_profits_who_loses_who_dec","v":{"s":"who pays, who profits, who loses, who decides."}},{"k":"these_outcomes_are_not_natural_consequ","v":{"s":" These outcomes are not natural consequences of physics. They are choices, made by people, with very different stakes for different communities."}},{"k":"the_transition_can_be_designed_to_redu","v":{"s":"The transition CAN be designed to reduce the historical injustices of the fossil economy. Or it can repeat them. Both paths are technically possible."}},{"k":"what_it_is_2","v":{"s":"⓵ What it is: "}},{"k":"who_is_affected","v":{"s":"⓶ Who is affected: "}},{"k":"what_action_looks_like","v":{"s":"⓷ What action looks like: "}},{"k":"cross_link_this_is_connected_to","v":{"s":"🌐 Cross-link: this is connected to"}},{"k":"climate_explorer_2","v":{"s":"Climate Explorer"}},{"k":"the_climate_justice_map_and_the_design","v":{"s":" — the climate-justice map and the design-a-renewable-mix sim go deeper on policy + impact."}},{"k":"civic_action_rights_dissent","v":{"s":"Civic Action / Rights & Dissent"}},{"k":"when_these_issues_escalate_to_policy_c","v":{"s":" — when these issues escalate to policy, civic engagement is how decisions get made."}},{"k":"fire_ecology","v":{"s":"Fire Ecology"}},{"k":"indigenous_fire_stewardship_is_a_model","v":{"s":" — Indigenous fire stewardship is a model for how Indigenous-led environmental management actually works."}},{"k":"this_is_a_resource_page_for_educators_","v":{"s":"This is a resource page for educators. NGSS alignment per source, discussion prompts, hands-on activities, and pacing suggestions for unit planning."}},{"k":"ngss_standards_alignment","v":{"s":"📐 NGSS standards alignment"}},{"k":"ngss_standards_alignment_per_module","v":{"s":"NGSS standards alignment per module"}},{"k":"module","v":{"s":"Module"}},{"k":"standards","v":{"s":"Standards"}},{"k":"discussion_prompts","v":{"s":"💬 Discussion prompts"}},{"k":"hands_on_activities","v":{"s":"🛠️ Hands-on activities"}},{"k":"open_resource","v":{"s":"→ Open resource"}},{"k":"suggested_pacing","v":{"s":"🗓️ Suggested pacing"}},{"k":"more_teacher_resources","v":{"s":"📚 More teacher resources"}},{"k":"need_project","v":{"s":"NEED Project"}},{"k":"free_k_12_energy_curriculum_teacher_pd","v":{"s":" — free K-12 energy curriculum + teacher PD"}},{"k":"turbine_kits_lesson_plans_annual_chall","v":{"s":" — turbine kits, lesson plans, annual challenge co