en with perfect mirrors:"}},{"k":"always_do_a_shoulder_check_head_turn_b","v":{"s":"• ALWAYS do a shoulder check (head turn) before changing lanes or merging."}},{"k":"the_dutch_reach_when_opening_your_door","v":{"s":"• The \"Dutch reach\" when opening your door checks for cyclists in your blind spot."}},{"k":"motorcycles_and_bicycles_can_hide_in_b","v":{"s":"• Motorcycles and bicycles can hide in blind spots that even cars cannot."}},{"k":"at_highway_speed_a_car_in_your_blind_s","v":{"s":"• At highway speed, a car in your blind spot for 2 seconds has traveled 176 feet."}},{"k":"dry_2","v":{"s":"☀️ Dry"}},{"k":"rain_2","v":{"s":"🌧️ Rain"}},{"k":"snow_2","v":{"s":"❄️ Snow"}},{"k":"ice_2","v":{"s":"🧊 Ice"}},{"k":"menu_69","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"weather_impact_chart_3","v":{"s":"Weather Impact Chart"}},{"k":"same_car_same_speed_very_different_out","v":{"s":"Same car, same speed, VERY different outcomes."}},{"k":"wind_chill_test_speed_in_miles_per_hou","v":{"s":"Wind chill test speed in miles per hour"}},{"k":"key_takeaway","v":{"s":"💡 Key Takeaway"}},{"k":"than_on_dry_pavement_that_is_the_diffe","v":{"s":" than on dry pavement. That is the difference between stopping safely and a collision. "}},{"k":"the_physics_is_non_negotiable_the_only","v":{"s":"The physics is non-negotiable — the only variable you control is speed."}},{"k":"classroom_basics","v":{"s":"Classroom Basics"}},{"k":"learn_the_theory_first_physics_signs_a","v":{"s":"Learn the theory first: physics, signs, and stopping distance."}},{"k":"permit_prep","v":{"s":"Permit Prep"}},{"k":"take_the_practice_permit_test_until_yo","v":{"s":"Take the practice permit test until you score 80%+."}},{"k":"first_drive_2","v":{"s":"First Drive"}},{"k":"residential_at_25_mph_get_comfortable_","v":{"s":"Residential at 25 mph. Get comfortable with steering, braking, and signals."}},{"k":"maneuvers","v":{"s":"Maneuvers"}},{"k":"parallel_parking_3_point_turn_and_stra","v":{"s":"Parallel parking, 3-point turn, and straight backing. Road test maneuvers."}},{"k":"all_conditions","v":{"s":"All Conditions"}},{"k":"drive_in_rain_snow_fog_and_night_build","v":{"s":"Drive in rain, snow, fog, and night. Build confidence in every condition."}},{"k":"highway_skills","v":{"s":"Highway Skills"}},{"k":"highway_merge_lane_changes_and_maintai","v":{"s":"Highway merge, lane changes, and maintaining following distance at speed."}},{"k":"hazard_awareness","v":{"s":"Hazard Awareness"}},{"k":"test_your_hazard_perception_reaction_t","v":{"s":"Test your hazard perception reaction time. Target: 8/10."}},{"k":"emergency_response_2","v":{"s":"Emergency Response"}},{"k":"yield_to_emergency_vehicles_learn_brak","v":{"s":"Yield to emergency vehicles. Learn brake failure, blowout, and stuck accelerator responses."}},{"k":"advanced_physics","v":{"s":"Advanced Physics"}},{"k":"understand_the_forces_see_why_speed_ki","v":{"s":"Understand the forces. See why speed kills. Optimize your MPG."}},{"k":"road_test_ready","v":{"s":"Road Test Ready"}},{"k":"know_the_rubric_free_explore_until_eve","v":{"s":"Know the rubric. Free explore until every maneuver is automatic. Get your certificate."}},{"k":"menu_70","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"your_learning_path","v":{"s":"Your Learning Path"}},{"k":"start","v":{"s":"Start →"}},{"k":"rearview_both_side_mirrors_before_movi","v":{"s":"Rearview + both side mirrors before moving."}},{"k":"must_be_on_before_engine_starts","v":{"s":"Must be on before engine starts."}},{"k":"release_before_driving_forgetting_it_a","v":{"s":"Release before driving. Forgetting it = automatic fail in some states."}},{"k":"no_jackrabbit_starts_gradual_controlle","v":{"s":"No jackrabbit starts. Gradual, controlled."}},{"k":"stop_without_lurching_begin_braking_we","v":{"s":"Stop without lurching. Begin braking well before the stop line."}},{"k":"hand_over_hand_or_push_pull_no_palming","v":{"s":"Hand-over-hand or push-pull. No palming, no single-hand steering at speed."}},{"k":"within_5_mph_of_the_limit_too_fast_fai","v":{"s":"Within 5 mph of the limit. Too fast = fail. Too slow (>10 under) = points off."}},{"k":"complete_stop_behind_the_line_rolling_","v":{"s":"COMPLETE stop behind the line. Rolling stop = critical error; expect major points off or automatic failure."}},{"k":"visible_head_movement_to_check_all_dir","v":{"s":"Visible head movement to check all directions. Examiner must SEE you look."}},{"k":"yield_correctly_taking_right_of_way_wh","v":{"s":"Yield correctly. Taking right-of-way when it isn't yours = critical error."}},{"k":"do_not_enter_on_yellow_if_you_can_stop","v":{"s":"Do not enter on yellow if you can stop safely. NEVER enter on red."}},{"k":"center_of_your_lane_not_riding_the_lin","v":{"s":"Center of your lane. Not riding the line or drifting."}},{"k":"signal_mirror_blind_spot_check_smooth_","v":{"s":"Signal -> mirror -> blind spot check -> smooth move. Each step must be visible."}},{"k":"100_feet_before_turning_or_changing_la","v":{"s":"100 feet before turning or changing lanes. Signal BEFORE braking."}},{"k":"right_turns_from_right_lane_into_right","v":{"s":"Right turns from right lane into right lane. Left turns from left lane."}},{"k":"brake_before_the_turn_not_during_smoot","v":{"s":"Brake BEFORE the turn, not during. Smooth speed through the arc."}},{"k":"always_look_for_pedestrians_in_crosswa","v":{"s":"Always look for pedestrians in crosswalks when turning. Yield."}},{"k":"within_12_inches_of_curb_3_attempts_al","v":{"s":"Parallel, no more than 18 inches from the curb, with 2 feet to cars ahead and behind. Set the parking brake and select Park."}},{"k":"wheels_turned_correctly_uphill_away_fr","v":{"s":"Wheels turned correctly: uphill=away from curb, downhill=toward curb."}},{"k":"any_collision_immediate_fail","v":{"s":"Any collision = immediate fail."}},{"k":"running_a_red_light_or_stop_sign","v":{"s":"Running a red light or stop sign."}},{"k":"anything_the_examiner_considers_unsafe","v":{"s":"Anything the examiner considers unsafe enough to grab the wheel or brake."}},{"k":"if_the_examiner_has_to_verbally_correc","v":{"s":"If the examiner has to verbally correct you to prevent danger = fail."}},{"k":"menu_71","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"maine_road_test_rubric","v":{"s":"Maine Road Test Rubric"}},{"k":"what_the_examiner_actually_grades_chec","v":{"s":"What the examiner ACTUALLY grades. ⭐ = checked, ⭐⭐ = weighted, ⭐⭐⭐ = critical, ❌ = auto-fail."}},{"k":"menu_72","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"reaction_time_trainer_2","v":{"s":"Reaction Time Trainer"}},{"k":"measure_your_actual_reaction_speed_the","v":{"s":"Measure your ACTUAL reaction speed. The average driver is 1.5 seconds. How fast are you?"}},{"k":"click_tap_to_start","v":{"s":"Click / Tap to Start"}},{"k":"wait_for_green_then_click_as_fast_as_y","v":{"s":"Wait for GREEN, then click as fast as you can."}},{"k":"wait","v":{"s":"🔴 WAIT..."}},{"k":"go_click_now","v":{"s":"🟢 GO! CLICK NOW!"}},{"k":"click_tap_as_fast_as_you_can","v":{"s":"Click / tap as FAST as you can!"}},{"k":"too_early","v":{"s":"TOO EARLY!"}},{"k":"you_clicked_before_the_light_turned_gr","v":{"s":"You clicked before the light turned green. Click to try again."}},{"k":"click_to_try_again","v":{"s":"Click to try again"}},{"k":"your_results","v":{"s":"Your Results"}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best"}},{"k":"average","v":{"s":"Average"}},{"k":"attempts","v":{"s":"Attempts"}},{"k":"fatigue_alcohol_or_phone_use_can_doubl","v":{"s":"Fatigue, alcohol, or phone use can slow recognition and response. NHTSA lists reaction-time impairment at .08 BAC; your safest fix is more following distance and no impaired driving."}},{"k":"menu_73","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"night_vision_math_3","v":{"s":"Night Vision Math"}},{"k":"can_you_stop_within_what_your_headligh","v":{"s":"Can you stop within what your headlights reveal? This is how night crashes happen."}},{"k":"night_driving_speed_in_miles_per_hour","v":{"s":"Night driving speed in miles per hour"}},{"k":"low_350_ft","v":{"s":"💡 Low (350 ft)"}},{"k":"high_500_ft","v":{"s":"🔆 High (500 ft)"}},{"k":"the_rule","v":{"s":"🔬 The Rule"}},{"k":"low_beams_illuminate_350_ft_high_beams","v":{"s":"• Low beams illuminate ~350 ft. High beams ~500 ft."}},{"k":"rule_never_drive_faster_than_you_can_s","v":{"s":"• Rule: NEVER drive faster than you can stop within your headlight range."}},{"k":"dim_high_beams_within_500_ft_of_oncomi","v":{"s":"• Maine §2067: dim high beams within 500 ft of oncoming traffic or 300 ft when following another vehicle."}},{"k":"alcohol_shrinks_your_visual_field_by_u","v":{"s":"• Alcohol shrinks your visual field by up to 25% at night — tunnel vision + slow reaction = deadly."}},{"k":"budget_reality_check","v":{"s":"Budget Reality Check"}},{"k":"what_to_look_for","v":{"s":"What to Look For"}},{"k":"best_first_cars","v":{"s":"Best First Cars"}},{"k":"the_inspection","v":{"s":"The Inspection"}},{"k":"negotiation","v":{"s":"Negotiation"}},{"k":"after_purchase","v":{"s":"After Purchase"}},{"k":"menu_74","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"first_car_buying_guide","v":{"s":"First Car Buying Guide"}},{"k":"everything_they_don_t_teach_you_in_dri","v":{"s":"Everything they don't teach you in driver's ed — the money, the negotiation, the traps."}},{"k":"engine_block","v":{"s":"Engine Block"}},{"k":"converts_fuel_into_mechanical_motion_v","v":{"s":"Converts fuel into mechanical motion via controlled explosions (internal combustion). Runs at 700-6000+ RPM."}},{"k":"alternator","v":{"s":"Alternator"}},{"k":"generates_electricity_from_the_engine_","v":{"s":"Generates electricity from the engine to charge the battery and power all electrical systems while driving."}},{"k":"radiator","v":{"s":"Radiator"}},{"k":"cools_the_engine_by_circulating_coolan","v":{"s":"Cools the engine by circulating coolant. Sits at the front to catch airflow. Overheating = head gasket failure."}},{"k":"starter_motor","v":{"s":"Starter Motor"}},{"k":"small_electric_motor_that_cranks_the_e","v":{"s":"Small electric motor that cranks the engine when you turn the key. Uses battery power for ~2 seconds."}},{"k":"transmission","v":{"s":"Transmission"}},{"k":"changes_gear_ratios_to_match_engine_rp","v":{"s":"Changes gear ratios to match engine RPM to wheel speed. Automatic: fluid-coupled. Manual: clutch + gears."}},{"k":"differential","v":{"s":"Differential"}},{"k":"allows_wheels_to_spin_at_different_spe","v":{"s":"Allows wheels to spin at different speeds in turns (outer wheel goes further). Essential for cornering."}},{"k":"driveshaft","v":{"s":"Driveshaft"}},{"k":"transfers_power_from_transmission_to_t","v":{"s":"Transfers power from transmission to the wheels. FWD: half-shafts. RWD: single driveshaft + axle. AWD: both."}},{"k":"disc_brakes","v":{"s":"Disc Brakes"}},{"k":"caliper_squeezes_pads_against_a_spinni","v":{"s":"Caliper squeezes pads against a spinning rotor. Friction converts kinetic energy → heat. Front brakes do ~70% of the work."}},{"k":"brake_fluid","v":{"s":"Brake Fluid"}},{"k":"hydraulic_fluid_transmits_pedal_force_","v":{"s":"Hydraulic fluid transmits pedal force to calipers. Incompressible = instant response. Absorbs moisture over time."}},{"k":"abs_module","v":{"s":"ABS Module"}},{"k":"pulses_brakes_15x_second_to_prevent_wh","v":{"s":"Pulses brakes 15x/second to prevent wheel lockup. Lets you steer while braking hard. Pedal will vibrate — that is normal."}},{"k":"parking_brake","v":{"s":"Parking Brake"}},{"k":"mechanical_cable_brake_on_rear_wheels_","v":{"s":"Mechanical (cable) brake on rear wheels. Use when parked on hills. Some vehicles: electronic button instead of lever."}},{"k":"power_steering","v":{"s":"Power Steering"}},{"k":"hydraulic_or_electric_assist_that_redu","v":{"s":"Hydraulic or electric assist that reduces steering effort. Without it, turning at low speed requires enormous force."}},{"k":"absorb_bumps_and_keep_tires_in_contact","v":{"s":"Absorb bumps and keep tires in contact with the road. Worn shocks = bouncy ride + longer braking distance."}},{"k":"tie_rods","v":{"s":"Tie Rods"}},{"k":"connect_steering_wheel_to_the_wheels_w","v":{"s":"Connect steering wheel to the wheels. Worn tie rods = loose/wandering steering. Critical safety component."}},{"k":"wheel_alignment","v":{"s":"Wheel Alignment"}},{"k":"angles_of_the_wheels_relative_to_the_c","v":{"s":"Angles of the wheels relative to the car. Misalignment = uneven tire wear + pulling to one side."}},{"k":"battery","v":{"s":"Battery"}},{"k":"12v_lead_acid_gas_cars_or_high_voltage","v":{"s":"12V lead-acid (gas cars) or high-voltage lithium (EVs). Provides power for starting + electronics. Average life: 3-5 years."}},{"k":"fuse_box","v":{"s":"Fuse Box"}},{"k":"protects_circuits_from_overload_blown_","v":{"s":"Protects circuits from overload. Blown fuse = dead circuit (lights, radio, etc.). Replace with SAME amperage fuse only."}},{"k":"ecu_computer","v":{"s":"ECU (Computer)"}},{"k":"engine_control_unit_the_car_s_brain_co","v":{"s":"Engine Control Unit — the car's brain. Controls fuel injection, timing, emissions, transmission. Reads 100+ sensors."}},{"k":"airbags","v":{"s":"Airbags"}},{"k":"inflate_in_30_milliseconds_during_a_cr","v":{"s":"Inflate in ~30 milliseconds during a crash. Front, side, curtain, knee. SRS light on = system malfunction — get checked."}},{"k":"crumple_zones","v":{"s":"Crumple Zones"}},{"k":"engineered_to_crush_in_a_controlled_wa","v":{"s":"Engineered to crush in a controlled way, absorbing impact energy before it reaches the passenger cell."}},{"k":"seatbelts","v":{"s":"Seatbelts"}},{"k":"pre_tensioners_tighten_on_impact_load_","v":{"s":"Pre-tensioners tighten on impact. Load limiters prevent chest injury. 3-point belt reduces fatality risk by ~45%."}},{"k":"traction_control","v":{"s":"Traction Control"}},{"k":"reduces_engine_power_or_brakes_individ","v":{"s":"Reduces engine power or brakes individual wheels when slip is detected. Keeps you in control on wet/icy roads."}},{"k":"blind_spot_monitor","v":{"s":"Blind Spot Monitor"}},{"k":"radar_sensors_detect_vehicles_in_your_","v":{"s":"Radar sensors detect vehicles in your blind spots. Warning light in the side mirror. Does NOT replace shoulder checks."}},{"k":"tire_tread","v":{"s":"Tire Tread"}},{"k":"channels_water_away_from_the_contact_p","v":{"s":"Channels water away from the contact patch. Below 2/32\" = unsafe. Penny test: see all of Lincoln's head = replace."}},{"k":"tire_sidewall","v":{"s":"Tire Sidewall"}},{"k":"shows_size_p205_55r16_max_pressure_spe","v":{"s":"Shows size (P205/55R16), max pressure, speed rating, and manufacture date (DOT code). Never exceed max PSI."}},{"k":"tpms_sensor","v":{"s":"TPMS Sensor"}},{"k":"inside_each_wheel_measures_air_pressur","v":{"s":"Inside each wheel, measures air pressure. Light comes on if 25%+ below recommended. Check ALL 4 tires when it lights."}},{"k":"lug_nuts","v":{"s":"Lug Nuts"}},{"k":"hold_the_wheel_on_torque_spec_matters_","v":{"s":"Hold the wheel on. Torque spec matters — too loose = wheel falls off. Too tight = warped rotor. Use a torque wrench."}},{"k":"menu_75","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"know_your_car_3","v":{"s":"Know Your Car"}},{"k":"every_major_system_and_part_what_it_do","v":{"s":"Every major system and part — what it does, why it matters, and what breaks."}},{"k":"loading_roadready","v":{"s":"Loading RoadReady..."}},{"k":"roadready_error","v":{"s":"🚗 RoadReady Error"}},{"k":"residential_desc","v":{"s":"Quiet Maine neighborhood. Watch for kids, dogs, parked cars, driveways. Classic first lesson."}},{"k":"suburban_desc","v":{"s":"Two-lane each way with traffic lights, turn lanes, and shopping plazas. Typical US driving."}},{"k":"highway_desc","v":{"s":"I-295 Portland. Practice merging, lane changes, and keeping 3-second following distance at speed."}},{"k":"roundabout_desc","v":{"s":"Yield to traffic already in the circle. Enter on a gap. No stopping inside. Common in Maine new roads."}},{"k":"rural_desc","v":{"s":"Curvy Maine back road. Watch for moose, deer, cyclists, and blind curves."}},{"k":"parking_desc","v":{"s":"A guided top-down road-test maneuver. Reverse into a curb space, check clearance, then secure the car."}},{"k":"night_desc","v":{"s":"Suburban roads after dark. Headlights reach ~350 ft with lows, 500 ft with highs. Do not overdrive them."}},{"k":"fog_desc","v":{"s":"Maine coastal fog — thick, wet, visibility 100 ft. Low beams only. Slow WAY down."}},{"k":"rain_desc","v":{"s":"Wet pavement. Friction drops ~40%. Hydroplaning risk above 45 mph. Headlights on when wipers are in constant use or visibility is reduced."}},{"k":"snow_desc","v":{"s":"Snow-covered road. Friction drops to ~0.2. Gentle inputs only. Moose warning active. The test."}},{"k":"construction_desc","v":{"s":"Lane closure with flagger and cones. Posted construction-speed fines can double. Workers present — slow down early."}},{"k":"school_zone_desc","v":{"s":"Active school zone. Crosswalks, crossing guard, buses. Maine §2074: 15 mph during recess, opening/closing windows, flashing signs, or local active times."}},{"k":"downtown_desc","v":{"s":"Dense urban grid. One-way streets, heavy pedestrians, tight turns, taxis. Portland Old Port feel."}},{"k":"dawn_desc","v":{"s":"Low-angle sun glare. The most dangerous time for moose + deer. Visibility is tricky."}},{"k":"drag_content","v":{"s":"Every car pushes air out of the way as it moves. The force it takes to do that is DRAG. The key insight: drag grows with the square of speed. Double your speed and drag becomes 4× stronger. At 30 mph, drag is small. At 70 mph, it is the dominant force working against you. This is why most cars get their best MPG around 45-55 mph and lose efficiency sharply above that. Engineers spend huge effort shrinking 'Cd × A' — the drag coefficient times frontal area. A sleek EV might have Cd × A ≈ 0.53 m². A boxy pickup is ≈ 1.35 m² — almost three times more drag at the same speed."}},{"k":"rolling_content","v":{"s":"Tires are flexible. They squish at the contact patch, unsquish at the back — and some energy is lost to heat every rotation. That loss is called ROLLING RESISTANCE. Unlike drag, it barely changes with speed. It depends on: tire inflation (underinflated = huge penalty, lose ~0.3% MPG per 1 PSI low), tire compound (low-rolling-resistance LRR tires save ~4-8% fuel), road surface (asphalt < concrete < gravel), and vehicle weight. Below about 40 mph, rolling resistance is the DOMINANT force slowing you down. Above 40 mph, aerodynamic drag takes over."}},{"k":"stopping_content","v":{"s":"When you need to stop, two things happen. First, REACTION TIME (typically 1.5 seconds for an alert driver). During that time the car keeps moving at full speed — at 60 mph, that is 132 feet before the brakes even touch. Second, BRAKING DISTANCE — the tires slowing you down. Braking distance grows with v² (speed squared). The physics: KE = ½mv². Kinetic energy scales with the square of speed. At double the speed, you have 4× the energy to dissipate into heat through the brakes. This is why 'just a little faster' is so dangerous. At 30 mph total stopping distance is ≈110 ft. At 60 mph it is ≈300 ft. At 80 mph it is ≈475 ft — longer than a football field, end zones included."}},{"k":"kinetic_content","v":{"s":"A moving car stores energy — kinetic energy. KE = ½mv². Notice the v-squared again. At 30 mph a car has some kinetic energy. At 60 mph it has FOUR TIMES that. At 90 mph it has NINE TIMES. Every bit of that has to go somewhere in a crash — into crumple zones, airbags, and unfortunately, into people. This is the single biggest reason speed limits exist. Modern crumple zones are designed for ~35 mph impacts. Above that, the occupants start to see forces that human bodies cannot survive. Seatbelts, airbags, and crumple zones together cut fatality risk by ~75% at legal speeds — but they cannot fight physics forever."}},{"k":"mpg_content","v":{"s":"Miles per gallon (MPG) is a ratio — distance divided by fuel used. Higher = better. But MPG hides something important: the relationship is NON-LINEAR. Going from 15 to 20 MPG saves more fuel than going from 35 to 40 MPG, even though both are a 5 MPG improvement. That is why gallons-per-100-miles (GPM) is sometimes a better metric. Europe uses L/100km for exactly this reason. The things that move your MPG up or down are: speed (sweet spot 45-55 mph), aggression (jackrabbit starts = -15 to -40%), tire pressure (low = -3 to -10%), AC use (-5 to -10% at low speed, negligible at highway), roof racks (-10 to -25%), cargo weight (-1% per 100 lbs), and engine condition."}},{"k":"friction_content","v":{"s":"Your tires are the only thing connecting you to the road. The FRICTION COEFFICIENT (μ, 'mu') between tire and road determines: how hard you can brake, how sharply you can turn, and how fast you can accelerate without slipping. Dry pavement: μ ≈ 0.7. Wet: ≈ 0.4. Snow: ≈ 0.2. Ice: ≈ 0.1. That is a SEVEN-fold difference between dry and ice. The friction circle: your tires have a total grip budget. If you use 100% for braking, you have 0% left for steering — that is why locked wheels go straight. ABS exists to keep some grip reserved for steering during hard braking. This is also why you should brake BEFORE a turn, not during — you want all your grip available for cornering."}},{"k":"hypermiling_content","v":{"s":"Hypermiling is the art of squeezing every mile from a gallon. Proven techniques: (1) Gentle accel — imagine an egg between your foot and the pedal. (2) Anticipate — read the road 15 seconds ahead so you brake less. (3) Coast to stops — fuel cut-off activates when you lift off the gas, meaning coasting in gear uses ZERO fuel on modern cars. (4) Pulse and glide — gently accelerate, then coast. Works best under 45 mph. (5) Highway cruise at 55-60 mph, not 75 (drag is brutal). (6) Tires at door-sticker PSI. (7) Remove the roof box when not using it. (8) Don't idle — modern engines restart on <0.5 s of fuel, so 30+ sec of idling wastes more than restarting. Combined, these techniques can boost real-world MPG by 20-40% over aggressive driving. Free money."}},{"k":"inertia_content","v":{"s":"Every driving decision is physics. 1st Law (inertia): a body in motion stays in motion. That is why you need brakes — to fight your car's inertia. It is also why loose objects become missiles in a crash. 2nd Law (F = ma): force = mass × acceleration. A heavier car takes more force to stop. 3rd Law (action/reaction): when your engine pushes on the tires, the road pushes back — that reaction force is what actually moves the car forward. Without friction (ice), the wheels spin but the car goes nowhere, because there is no reaction force. Understanding these three laws means you can predict how your car will behave in any situation before it surprises you."}},{"k":"night_content","v":{"s":"At night your headlights illuminate about 350 feet with low beams, ~500 feet with high beams. Your braking distance at 60 mph is ~240 ft. Add reaction distance — you are already using your ENTIRE headlight range just to stop in time. At 75 mph, you physically cannot stop within your headlight range with low beams. This is called 'overdriving your headlights' and it is how single-vehicle-at-night crashes happen. Rule: drive slow enough that you could stop within the distance you can see. Use high beams only when clear; Maine §2067 requires dimming within 500 ft of oncoming traffic and within 300 ft when following another vehicle."}},{"k":"winter_content","v":{"s":"Maine winters test every skill you have. Four rules: (1) Look far ahead. Bad things happen slowly if you see them coming. Tight focus = surprise = crashes. (2) Gentle inputs. Smooth acceleration, smooth braking, smooth steering. Jerky = slip. (3) Increase following distance to 6-10 seconds on snow. Regular 3 seconds is nowhere near enough when friction drops to 0.2. (4) When in doubt, slow down more. Maine-specific: moose warnings are real — May through October at dawn/dusk, scan treelines. Black ice on bridges FIRST. Take reasonable measures so snow and ice cannot fall from your car; safest practice is clearing the roof, windows, lights, and hood before driving. Keep your gas tank above half (prevents fuel line freeze, and a half tank means real emergency reserves if you get stuck)."}},{"k":"rightOfWay_summary","v":{"s":"Who goes first when paths conflict. Follow the ladder top to bottom."}},{"k":"laneDiscipline_summary","v":{"s":"Where to drive, when to change lanes, and why."}},{"k":"signaling_summary","v":{"s":"Give other drivers enough time to react."}},{"k":"passing_summary","v":{"s":"Rules for getting around a slower vehicle safely."}},{"k":"merging_summary","v":{"s":"How to join traffic safely."}},{"k":"pedestrian_summary","v":{"s":"Pedestrians are vulnerable; crosswalks and special-right-of-way cases demand full attention."}},{"k":"emergency_summary","v":{"s":"What to do when you see lights, hear sirens, or encounter a scene."}},{"k":"railroad_summary","v":{"s":"Trains can't stop. You always lose the weight contest."}},{"k":"speedAndFollowing_summary","v":{"s":"Speed is never \"fixed\" — it's conditional on everything around you."}},{"k":"load_progress_file","v":{"s":"Load RoadReady progress file"}},{"k":"cinematic_drive_path_replay_label","v":{"s":"Cinematic drive-path replay from start to end"}},{"k":"restriction_extensions","v":{"s":"Restriction extensions"}},{"k":"restriction_consequence","v":{"s":"Restriction consequence"}},{"k":"three_foot_passing_diagram_label","v":{"s":"Diagram showing a car passing a cyclist with at least three feet of clearance"}},{"k":"right_hook_diagram_label","v":{"s":"Diagram showing a car turning right across a cyclist traveling straight in the bike lane"}},{"k":"stopping_distance_table_label","v":{"s":"Typical stopping distance by road condition"}},{"k":"favorite_scenarios_chart_label","v":{"s":"Favorite scenarios by drive count"}},{"k":"distracted_distance_diagram_label","v":{"s":"Distance traveled while looking away"}},{"k":"custom_car_preview_label","v":{"s":"Customized car preview"}},{"k":"custom_car_color","v":{"s":"Custom car color:"}},{"k":"vehicle_science_comparison_table_label","v":{"s":"Vehicle science comparison"}},{"k":"ideal_tire_model_note","v":{"s":"Ideal-tire model: braking distance here depends on speed and friction only. Real heavy vehicles stop longer (brake fade, tire loading) — see Vehicle Comparison."}},{"k":"braking_diagram_label","v":{"s":"Braking performance diagram"}},{"k":"braking_experiment_log","v":{"s":"Braking experiment log"}},{"k":"braking_hypothesis_label","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: Why does kinetic energy grow with speed squared but only linearly with mass?"}},{"k":"braking_explanation_label","v":{"s":"Explain why speed dominates stopping distance and what surprised you about grade."}},{"k":"fuel_cost_comparison","v":{"s":"Fuel cost comparison by vehicle"}},{"k":"mpg_by_speed_chart_label","v":{"s":"Miles per gallon by speed chart"}},{"k":"crash_replay_chart_label","v":{"s":"Crash replay speed and steering chart"}},{"k":"force_diagram_canvas_label","v":{"s":"Live vehicle force diagram"}},{"k":"blind_spot_diagram_canvas_label","v":{"s":"Top-down blind-spot diagram: your car is centered with forward vision ahead, rearview and side-mirror coverage behind, and red blind spots immediately behind the left and right sides where other vehicles can hide"}},{"k":"ice_vis_looks_clear","v":{"s":"looks clear!"}},{"k":"weather_impact_comparison","v":{"s":"Weather impact comparison"}},{"k":"calm_drive","v":{"s":"Calm Drive"}},{"k":"quieter_engine_and_ambient_layers_no","v":{"s":"— quieter engine/ambient layers; no celebration bursts; safety cues stay on"}},{"k":"choose_another_scenario","v":{"s":"Choose another scenario"}},{"k":"optional_coach_acknowledgement","v":{"s":"Optional Coach Acknowledgement"}},{"k":"safe_practice_achievement","v":{"s":"SAFE PRACTICE ACHIEVEMENT"}},{"k":"this_recognizes_that","v":{"s":"This recognizes that"}},{"k":"ride_along_automatic_scope","v":{"s":"— automatic steering, braking, and narration on road scenarios; parking and roundabout courses stay hands-on"}},{"k":"simple_reaction_trainer_context","v":{"s":"This measures only a simple tap response. Real driving adds perception and decision time; never substitute this score for the simulator's 1.5-second planning allowance."}}],"id":570}},{"k":"rock_cycle","v":{"o":[{"k":"spec_note_granite","v":{"s":"Coarse interlocking quartz, feldspar and mica crystals."}},{"k":"spec_note_basalt","v":{"s":"Dark, fine-grained; often pitted with gas vesicles."}},{"k":"spec_note_sandstone","v":{"s":"Cemented quartz sand grains with visible bedding."}},{"k":"spec_note_limestone","v":{"s":"Calcite (CaCO₃); often fossil-rich; fizzes in acid."}},{"k":"spec_note_shale","v":{"s":"Compacted clay; splits into thin sheets."}},{"k":"spec_note_slate","v":{"s":"Low-grade; flat cleavage planes, dull sheen."}},{"k":"spec_note_marble","v":{"s":"Recrystallized calcite; sugary, unlayered."}},{"k":"spec_note_gneiss","v":{"s":"High-grade; alternating light and dark mineral bands."}},{"k":"canvas_badge","v":{"s":"Rock Cycle"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Granite or Rhyolite"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Partial melting, then crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"650–750 °C (water-bearing), 15–30 km deep"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"10,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"Quartz and feldspar melt into a sticky, silica-rich (felsic) magma. Cooling slowly at depth regrows large crystals — granite. Erupted and chilled, the same melt becomes fine-grained rhyolite."}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Crystal size tells you the cooling rate: coarse = slow and deep, fine = fast and erupted."}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Heating toward the solidus"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"First melt on crystal edges"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Felsic magma body forms"}},{"k":"tx_granite_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Crystals nucleate and grow"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Gneiss"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"High-grade regional metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"600–700 °C, 20–30 km deep"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Minerals recrystallize and segregate into alternating light (quartz/feldspar) and dark (biotite/amphibole) bands. Nothing melts — the rock stays solid throughout."}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Look for gneissic banding: stripes that swirl around, not flat sedimentary layers."}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Burial and heating"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Minerals begin to align"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Light/dark segregation"}},{"k":"tx_granite_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Gneissic banding locked in"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Sandstone and Shale"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Hydrolysis, transport, lithification"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Surface temperatures and rainfall"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Feldspar reacts with weak acid in rainwater and breaks down to clay; quartz is too tough to dissolve and survives as sand. The two travel different distances and settle separately — sand near the source, clay far out in quiet water."}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"One granite yields TWO sedimentary rocks: quartz sand → sandstone, clay → shale."}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Rain and frost open cracks"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Feldspar → clay, quartz freed"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Rivers sort sand from clay"}},{"k":"tx_granite_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Burial, compaction, cementation"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Basalt or Gabbro"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Melting, then crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"1000–1250 °C"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Days (lava) to millions of years (pluton)"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"Basalt melts to a runny, iron- and magnesium-rich (mafic) magma. Erupted, it chills fast into fine-grained basalt; trapped at depth, the same melt cools slowly into coarse gabbro."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Same magma, two rocks — the only difference is cooling rate."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Mafic rock reaches melting point"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Low-viscosity magma pools"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Ascent or ponding"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Fast chill or slow crystal growth"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Greenschist, then Amphibolite"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Prograde regional metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"400–600 °C, 10–25 km deep"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"New minerals grow that are stable at the higher temperature: chlorite and epidote give greenschist its green colour, and at higher grade hornblende takes over to form amphibolite."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"The colour change is the data — green means chlorite, black-and-white speckle means hornblende + plagioclase."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Burial in a subduction zone"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Chlorite and epidote grow"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Greenschist stage"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Hornblende replaces chlorite"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Mudstone (iron-rich)"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Chemical weathering, transport, lithification"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Warm, wet surface conditions"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Olivine and pyroxene weather quickly to clay minerals, and the iron they release oxidizes to rust-red iron oxide. In the tropics this leaves deep red soils (laterite) that later harden into iron-rich mudstone."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"The red colour is oxidized iron — direct evidence that oxygen and water did the work."}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Water attacks olivine"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Clay forms, iron released"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Iron oxidizes rust-red"}},{"k":"tx_basalt_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Deposition and compaction"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Rhyolite or Granite"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Crustal melting, then crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"850–1100 °C, deep burial"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"A quartz-rich sandstone must be buried very deep before it melts. The result is a silica-rich melt that crystallizes into granite at depth or rhyolite if it erupts."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Quartz has a high melting point, so this route needs more heat than melting basalt does."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Deep burial"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Quartz cement breaks down"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Silica-rich melt forms"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Quartzite"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Contact or regional metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"300–500 °C"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"10,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Quartz grains recrystallize and fuse together, sealing the pore space. Because quartz grains are blocky rather than flat, they cannot line up — so quartzite has no foliation."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Break it: sandstone fractures AROUND its grains, quartzite fractures straight THROUGH them."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Heating of buried sand"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Pore space closes"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Grain boundaries fuse"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Interlocking quartz mosaic"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Sandstone (recycled)"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Erosion, transport, re-lithification"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Surface conditions"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"The cement dissolves and releases the sand grains, but quartz itself is chemically tough and physically hard, so the grains simply travel and are buried again. Each cycle rounds them a little more."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"Well-rounded, highly sorted quartz grains have been recycled many times."}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Cement dissolves"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Grains released"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Rounded during transport"}},{"k":"tx_sandstone_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Re-cemented as new sandstone"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Igneous rock (model outcome)"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Melting, then crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"Above ~900 °C"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"In the standard rock cycle any rock can melt and re-crystallize as igneous rock, and that is the pathway shown here."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Use a silicate rock such as granite or basalt for a cleaner example of the melting pathway."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_caveat","v":{"s":"Real limestone is the awkward case: heated at shallow depth, calcite tends to break down and release CO₂ (decarbonation) rather than melt. Genuine carbonate magmas (carbonatites) exist but are rare and come from the mantle, not from melting a limestone bed."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Strong heating"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Calcite becomes unstable"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Melt or CO₂ release"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Marble"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Contact or regional metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"300–600 °C"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"10,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Calcite crystals dissolve and regrow larger and interlocking. Fossils and bedding are erased in the process, which is why marble looks uniform and sugary."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Lost fossils are the tell — if you can still see shells, it is limestone, not marble."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Burial or nearby intrusion"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Calcite starts recrystallizing"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Fossils and bedding erased"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Interlocking calcite mosaic"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Limestone or Travertine"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Carbonate dissolution and re-precipitation"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Rainwater with dissolved CO₂"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Rain plus CO₂ makes weak carbonic acid, which dissolves calcite outright instead of grinding it into grains. The calcium is carried away in solution and re-precipitates elsewhere — as cave formations, travertine, or new marine limestone."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"This is why limestone regions form caves, sinkholes and karst instead of sandy beaches."}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Acidic rainwater contact"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Calcite dissolves"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Ca²⁺ carried in solution"}},{"k":"tx_limestone_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Re-precipitates as new carbonate"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Granite (S-type)"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Melting of sedimentary crust"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"650–800 °C, deep crust"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"Clay-rich rock buried into the deep crust melts to a silica- and aluminium-rich magma. Geologists call the granite it forms \"S-type\" because its source was Sedimentary."}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"S-type granites carry aluminium-rich minerals inherited from the original clay."}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Deep burial of clay beds"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Water-bearing minerals break down"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Aluminous melt forms"}},{"k":"tx_shale_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Crystallization as S-type granite"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Slate → Phyllite → Schist → Gneiss"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Prograde regional metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"200 °C (slate) rising to 700 °C (gneiss)"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"This is the classic metamorphic series. Flat clay minerals rotate to sit perpendicular to the squeeze, then regrow larger at each step: slate (too small to see) → phyllite (silky sheen) → schist (visible mica flakes) → gneiss (segregated bands)."}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Grain size IS the thermometer — the coarser the mica, the higher the grade it reached."}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Clay flakes rotate — slate"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Mica grows — phyllite sheen"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Visible flakes — schist"}},{"k":"tx_shale_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Bands segregate — gneiss"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Mudstone or Shale"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Erosion, transport, lithification"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Surface conditions"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Clay is already the end product of chemical weathering, so it does not break down further — it just splits apart, washes into quiet water and settles again. Clay particles are so fine they only settle where currents are nearly still."}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"Shale means quiet water: deep sea floor, lake bottom, or floodplain."}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Layers split and flake"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Clay suspended in water"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Settles in still water"}},{"k":"tx_shale_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Compaction to new shale"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Granite (S-type)"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Melting of metamorphosed mud"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"650–800 °C, deep crust"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"Slate carries the same clay-derived chemistry as the shale it came from, so melting it yields the same aluminium-rich, S-type granitic magma."}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"The melt \"forgets\" the foliation entirely — igneous crystals grow in random orientations."}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Deep burial"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Mica breaks down"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Aluminous melt forms"}},{"k":"tx_slate_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Random crystal growth"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Phyllite, then Schist"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Increasing metamorphic grade"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"400–600 °C"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Turning up heat and pressure on slate grows its microscopic micas into larger flakes. First they are just big enough to give a silky sheen (phyllite), then big enough to see and pick out individually (schist)."}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Run a finger over it: slate is dull and flat, phyllite is silky, schist sparkles."}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Grade increases"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Micas coarsen — silky sheen"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Phyllite stage"}},{"k":"tx_slate_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Visible mica flakes — schist"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Shale or Mudstone"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Erosion, transport, lithification"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Surface conditions"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Slate splits readily along its cleavage planes, so it breaks into flat chips. Those chips grind down to clay and silt and are deposited as mud once again."}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"Slate’s cleavage makes it weather into flat plates rather than round cobbles."}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Splits along cleavage"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Chips ground to silt and clay"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Transport and deposition"}},{"k":"tx_slate_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Compaction to mudstone"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Igneous rock (model outcome)"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Melting, then crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"Above ~900 °C"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"In the standard rock cycle any rock can melt and re-crystallize as igneous rock, and that is the pathway shown here."}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Granite, basalt or shale give cleaner examples of the melting pathway."}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_caveat","v":{"s":"Marble is calcite, so like limestone it tends to release CO₂ (decarbonation) rather than melt cleanly at crustal depths. Carbonate magmas are real but rare and mantle-derived."}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Strong heating"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Calcite becomes unstable"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Melt or CO₂ release"}},{"k":"tx_marble_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Crystallization"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Coarse Marble"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Continued recrystallization"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"500–700 °C"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Marble is already calcite, so more heat does not create new minerals — it just lets existing crystals grow larger by consuming their neighbours. Calcite grains are blocky, so even under great pressure they cannot align into foliation."}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Marble never develops banding no matter how high the grade — blocky minerals cannot line up."}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Reheating"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Grain boundaries migrate"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"Small crystals absorbed"}},{"k":"tx_marble_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Coarse sugary texture"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Limestone (re-precipitated)"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Carbonate dissolution and re-precipitation"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Rainwater with dissolved CO₂"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Like limestone, marble dissolves in weak carbonic acid rather than crumbling into grains. The dissolved calcium is carried off and re-precipitated as new carbonate rock."}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"This is why marble statues and headstones lose their detail in acidic rain."}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Acid rain contact"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Calcite dissolves"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Carried in solution"}},{"k":"tx_marble_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Re-precipitated as carbonate"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_product","v":{"s":"Migmatite, then Granite"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_process","v":{"s":"Partial melting (anatexis)"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_conditions","v":{"s":"700–800 °C — the top of the metamorphic range"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_change","v":{"s":"Gneiss sits right at the boundary where metamorphism ends and melting begins. The light quartz-feldspar bands melt first while the dark bands stay solid, producing migmatite — literally a mixed rock, half metamorphic and half igneous."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_evidence","v":{"s":"Migmatite is the visible proof of where the rock cycle’s two branches meet."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_stage0","v":{"s":"Temperature hits the solidus"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_stage1","v":{"s":"Light bands begin to melt"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_stage2","v":{"s":"Migmatite — part melt, part solid"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_melting_cooling_stage3","v":{"s":"Melt separates and crystallizes"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_product","v":{"s":"Migmatite"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_process","v":{"s":"Ultra-high-grade metamorphism"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_conditions","v":{"s":"700–800 °C"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_time","v":{"s":"Millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_change","v":{"s":"Gneiss is already the highest common grade, so pushing further starts partial melting instead of making a new solid rock. The result is migmatite, which marks the upper limit of metamorphism."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_evidence","v":{"s":"Wispy, folded light-coloured veins cutting through darker gneiss are frozen melt."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_stage0","v":{"s":"Already high grade"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_stage1","v":{"s":"Approaching the solidus"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_stage2","v":{"s":"First melt in felsic bands"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_heat_pressure_stage3","v":{"s":"Migmatite forms"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_product","v":{"s":"Sandstone and Shale"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_process","v":{"s":"Hydrolysis, transport, lithification"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_conditions","v":{"s":"Surface conditions"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_time","v":{"s":"1,000s–millions of years"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_change","v":{"s":"Gneiss weathers much like granite because it holds the same minerals: feldspar breaks down to clay while quartz survives as sand. The banding is destroyed entirely — sediment keeps no memory of it."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_evidence","v":{"s":"Sediment records the minerals of its parent rock, but not its texture."}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_stage0","v":{"s":"Bands split along weak micas"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_stage1","v":{"s":"Feldspar → clay, quartz freed"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_stage2","v":{"s":"Rivers sort the load"}},{"k":"tx_gneiss_weathering_erosion_stage3","v":{"s":"Burial and cementation"}},{"k":"weathering_erosion","v":{"s":"Weathering & Erosion"}},{"k":"heat_pressure","v":{"s":"Heat & Pressure"}},{"k":"melting_cooling","v":{"s":"Melting & Cooling"}}],"id":571}},{"k":"rocks","v":{"o":[{"k":"rcopt_granite_igneous_coarse","v":{"s":"Granite (igneous, coarse)"}},{"k":"rcopt_limestone_sedimentary","v":{"s":"Limestone (sedimentary)"}},{"k":"rcopt_basalt_igneous","v":{"s":"Basalt (igneous)"}},{"k":"rcopt_heat_and_pressure_at_depth","v":{"s":"Heat & pressure at depth"}},{"k":"rcopt_it_must_be_weathered","v":{"s":"It must be weathered"}},{"k":"rcopt_it_must_be_compressed_further","v":{"s":"It must be compressed further"}},{"k":"rcopt_rock_age","v":{"s":"Rock age"}},{"k":"rcopt_mineral_hardness","v":{"s":"Mineral hardness"}},{"k":"rcopt_crystal_size_and_shape","v":{"s":"Crystal size and shape"}},{"k":"rcopt_it_must_melt_then_cool","v":{"s":"It must melt, then cool"}},{"k":"q_breccia_vs_conglomerate","v":{"s":"Breccia and conglomerate have the same grain sizes and the same cement. What separates them?"}},{"k":"qopt_breccia_corners_sharp","v":{"s":"The fragments in breccia still have sharp corners"}},{"k":"qopt_breccia_bigger_grains","v":{"s":"Breccia has much larger grains"}},{"k":"qopt_breccia_no_cement","v":{"s":"Breccia has no cement holding it together"}},{"k":"qopt_breccia_metamorphic","v":{"s":"Breccia is metamorphic and conglomerate is sedimentary"}},{"k":"correct_breccia_corners","v":{"s":"Correct! Rounding takes distance, so sharp corners mean the pieces were buried close to where they broke — a scree slope or a fault zone."}},{"k":"incorrect_breccia_bigger_grains","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Both hold large fragments in a finer matrix; size is what they share, not what separates them."}},{"k":"incorrect_breccia_no_cement","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Both are cemented clastic rocks. Without cement you have loose scree, not a rock."}},{"k":"incorrect_breccia_metamorphic","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Both are sedimentary. Neither has been recrystallized by heat and pressure."}},{"k":"q_gritty_but_not_splitting","v":{"s":"A fine-grained rock feels gritty between your teeth but will not split into sheets. What is it?"}},{"k":"correct_siltstone_grit","v":{"s":"Correct! Silt is the size between sand and clay: too fine to pick out by eye, coarse enough to feel, and it does not split the way shale does."}},{"k":"incorrect_siltstone_shale","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Shale is finer still and its whole signature is splitting into thin sheets."}},{"k":"incorrect_siltstone_sandstone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. You can see sandstone grains without a lens; silt grains you can only feel."}},{"k":"incorrect_siltstone_chalk","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Chalk is soft carbonate made of plankton shells, and it is not gritty."}},{"k":"q_mohs_five_tooth_enamel","v":{"s":"Which mineral is the Mohs 5 reference, and the material your tooth enamel is built from?"}},{"k":"correct_apatite_enamel","v":{"s":"Correct! Apatite is Mohs 5, so the hardest tissue in your body sits on the very scale that runs from talc to diamond."}},{"k":"incorrect_apatite_fluorite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Fluorite is the step below, at Mohs 4."}},{"k":"incorrect_apatite_calcite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Calcite is Mohs 3 — a copper coin will scratch it."}},{"k":"incorrect_apatite_quartz","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz is Mohs 7, hard enough to scratch a steel nail."}},{"k":"q_graphite_diamond_same_element","v":{"s":"Graphite is Mohs 1 to 2 and diamond is Mohs 10, yet both are pure carbon. What accounts for the difference?"}},{"k":"qopt_atoms_bonded_differently","v":{"s":"The atoms are bonded in completely different arrangements"}},{"k":"qopt_different_elements","v":{"s":"They are made of different elements"}},{"k":"qopt_diamond_more_impurities","v":{"s":"Diamond contains more impurities"}},{"k":"qopt_graphite_is_younger","v":{"s":"Graphite formed more recently"}},{"k":"correct_graphite_arrangement","v":{"s":"Correct! Diamond bonds every carbon to four others in a rigid net; graphite bonds each to three in sheets that barely hold to one another. Arrangement, not composition."}},{"k":"incorrect_graphite_elements","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Both are pure carbon — that is exactly what makes the comparison worth making."}},{"k":"incorrect_graphite_impurities","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Impurities colour a diamond; they do not explain a nine-step gap in hardness."}},{"k":"incorrect_graphite_age","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Age does not set hardness. A mineral is as hard as its bonds, whenever it grew."}},{"k":"q_green_mineral_fizzes","v":{"s":"A green mineral fizzes in dilute acid, and its powder is green as well. What is it?"}},{"k":"correct_malachite_green_powder","v":{"s":"Correct! Malachite is a copper carbonate, so it fizzes — and it is one of the few minerals whose powder is as green as the specimen."}},{"k":"incorrect_malachite_olivine","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Olivine is green but it is a silicate, so it does not fizz, and its streak is white."}},{"k":"incorrect_malachite_fluorite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Fluorite comes in green, but it is a fluoride and its streak is white."}},{"k":"incorrect_malachite_biotite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Biotite is a dark mica with a white streak, and it does not react with acid."}},{"k":"q_blue_mineral_becomes_malachite","v":{"s":"Which deep blue mineral slowly turns INTO malachite as it takes up water?"}},{"k":"correct_azurite_to_malachite","v":{"s":"Correct! Azurite and malachite are the same copper carbonate in different proportions, which is why blue skies in old paintings sometimes look green today."}},{"k":"incorrect_azurite_fluorite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Fluorite can be blue, but it is stable and does not convert to a copper carbonate."}},{"k":"incorrect_azurite_corundum","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Blue corundum is sapphire, one of the most durable minerals there is."}},{"k":"incorrect_azurite_halite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Halite can look blue, but it is a salt: it dissolves in water rather than altering to malachite."}},{"k":"qopt_organic","v":{"s":"Organic"}},{"k":"qopt_compaction_and_cementation","v":{"s":"Compaction and cementation"}},{"k":"qopt_melting_and_recrystallizing","v":{"s":"Melting and recrystallizing"}},{"k":"qopt_erosion","v":{"s":"Erosion"}},{"k":"qopt_very_quickly","v":{"s":"Very quickly"}},{"k":"qopt_extremely_slowly","v":{"s":"Extremely slowly"}},{"k":"qopt_underground","v":{"s":"Underground"}},{"k":"qopt_underwater","v":{"s":"Underwater"}},{"k":"qopt_mineral","v":{"s":"Mineral"}},{"k":"qopt_fools_gold","v":{"s":"Fool's gold"}},{"k":"qopt_white_gold","v":{"s":"White gold"}},{"k":"qopt_rose_gold","v":{"s":"Rose gold"}},{"k":"qopt_black_gold","v":{"s":"Black gold"}},{"k":"qopt_salt","v":{"s":"Salt"}},{"k":"qopt_oil","v":{"s":"Oil"}},{"k":"qopt_hematite_iron_oxide","v":{"s":"Hematite (iron oxide)"}},{"k":"qopt_pyrite_iron_sulfide","v":{"s":"Pyrite (iron sulfide)"}},{"k":"qopt_galena_lead_sulfide","v":{"s":"Galena (lead sulfide)"}},{"k":"qopt_halite_sodium_chloride","v":{"s":"Halite (sodium chloride)"}},{"k":"qopt_microscopic_plankton","v":{"s":"Microscopic plankton"}},{"k":"qopt_snails_and_other_molluscs","v":{"s":"Snails and other molluscs"}},{"k":"qopt_clams","v":{"s":"Clams"}},{"k":"qopt_coral","v":{"s":"Coral"}},{"k":"qopt_salt_and_pepper","v":{"s":"Salt and pepper"}},{"k":"qopt_solid_black_throughout","v":{"s":"Solid black throughout"}},{"k":"qopt_striped","v":{"s":"Striped"}},{"k":"qopt_glassy","v":{"s":"Glassy"}},{"k":"qopt_emerald","v":{"s":"Emerald"}},{"k":"qopt_jade","v":{"s":"Jade"}},{"k":"qopt_green_glass","v":{"s":"Green glass"}},{"k":"qopt_the_colosseum","v":{"s":"The Colosseum"}},{"k":"qopt_the_great_pyramids","v":{"s":"The Great Pyramids"}},{"k":"qopt_stonehenge","v":{"s":"Stonehenge"}},{"k":"qopt_taj_mahal","v":{"s":"Taj Mahal"}},{"k":"qopt_aligned_mica_flakes","v":{"s":"Aligned mica flakes"}},{"k":"qopt_embedded_quartz_crystals","v":{"s":"Embedded quartz crystals"}},{"k":"qopt_gold_inclusions","v":{"s":"Gold inclusions"}},{"k":"qopt_diamond_dust","v":{"s":"Diamond dust"}},{"k":"qopt_piezoelectric_effect","v":{"s":"Piezoelectric effect"}},{"k":"qopt_a_magnetic_field_coil","v":{"s":"A magnetic field coil"}},{"k":"qopt_battery_power","v":{"s":"Battery power"}},{"k":"qopt_high_density","v":{"s":"High density"}},{"k":"qopt_naica_mine_mexico","v":{"s":"Naica Mine, Mexico"}},{"k":"qopt_mount_everest_nepal","v":{"s":"Mount Everest, Nepal"}},{"k":"qopt_grand_canyon","v":{"s":"Grand Canyon"}},{"k":"qopt_sahara_desert","v":{"s":"Sahara Desert"}},{"k":"qopt_an_allowance_connected_with_salt","v":{"s":"An allowance connected with salt"}},{"k":"qopt_a_payment_made_in_silver_coin_only","v":{"s":"A payment made in silver coin only"}},{"k":"qopt_a_gold_coin","v":{"s":"A gold coin"}},{"k":"qopt_a_stone_trading_fee","v":{"s":"A stone-trading fee"}},{"k":"qopt_the_andes_mountains","v":{"s":"The Andes Mountains"}},{"k":"qopt_andean_people","v":{"s":"Andean people"}},{"k":"qopt_a_scientist_named_ande","v":{"s":"A scientist named Ande"}},{"k":"qopt_an_ancient_city","v":{"s":"An ancient city"}},{"k":"qopt_volcanic_ash","v":{"s":"Volcanic ash"}},{"k":"qopt_river_sand_and_silt","v":{"s":"River sand and silt"}},{"k":"qopt_coral_reef","v":{"s":"Coral reef"}},{"k":"qopt_glacier_ice","v":{"s":"Glacier ice"}},{"k":"qopt_12_dodecahedral","v":{"s":"12 (dodecahedral)"}},{"k":"qopt_4_tetrahedral","v":{"s":"4 (tetrahedral)"}},{"k":"qopt_6_cubic","v":{"s":"6 (cubic)"}},{"k":"qopt_8_octahedral_form","v":{"s":"8 (octahedral form)"}},{"k":"ts_aria_a","v":{"s":" in thin section at about "}},{"k":"ts_aria_b","v":{"s":" times magnification, "}},{"k":"ts_aria_xpl","v":{"s":"under crossed polars. "}},{"k":"ts_aria_ppl","v":{"s":"in plane-polarized light. "}},{"k":"sp_aria_toohard","v":{"s":" is harder than the porcelain plate, so it scratches the plate instead of leaving a powder streak."}},{"k":"sp_aria_leaves","v":{"s":" leaves a "}},{"k":"sp_aria_tail","v":{"s":" streak, next to its outward colour for comparison."}},{"k":"sp_aria_empty","v":{"s":"An empty unglazed porcelain streak plate, ready for testing."}},{"k":"sc_aria_progress","v":{"s":"Scratch test in progress: "}},{"k":"sc_aria_hardness","v":{"s":", hardness "}},{"k":"sc_aria_across","v":{"s":", drawn across "}},{"k":"sc_aria_at","v":{"s":" at hardness "}},{"k":"sc_aria_cut","v":{"s":" cut a groove into "}},{"k":"sc_aria_harder","v":{"s":", because the modeled reference is harder."}},{"k":"sc_aria_and","v":{"s":" and "}},{"k":"sc_aria_both","v":{"s":" both have modeled hardness "}},{"k":"sc_aria_retest","v":{"s":". The broken trace is a borderline result, so retest with a different reference."}},{"k":"sc_aria_smear","v":{"s":" left only its own smear on "}},{"k":"sc_aria_softer","v":{"s":", because it is softer."}},{"k":"fz_aria_ready","v":{"s":"A pipette of dilute hydrochloric acid above the specimen, ready to test."}},{"k":"fz_aria_on","v":{"s":"Acid on "}},{"k":"fz_aria_bubbles","v":{"s":" releases a stream of carbon dioxide bubbles."}},{"k":"fz_aria_beads","v":{"s":"Acid beads on the surface of "}},{"k":"fz_aria_nobubbles","v":{"s":" with no bubbles, so no carbonate is present."}},{"k":"lat_aria_cell","v":{"s":" unit cell: "}},{"k":"sys_cubic","v":{"s":"cubic"}},{"k":"sys_isometric","v":{"s":"isometric"}},{"k":"sys_hexagonal","v":{"s":"hexagonal"}},{"k":"sys_trigonal","v":{"s":"trigonal"}},{"k":"sys_rhombohedral","v":{"s":"rhombohedral"}},{"k":"sys_orthorhombic","v":{"s":"orthorhombic"}},{"k":"sys_monoclinic","v":{"s":"monoclinic"}},{"k":"sys_triclinic","v":{"s":"triclinic"}},{"k":"geo_note_cubic","v":{"s":"three equal axes, all at right angles"}},{"k":"geo_note_isometric","v":{"s":"three equal axes, all at right angles"}},{"k":"geo_note_hexagonal","v":{"s":"six-fold symmetry about a long vertical axis"}},{"k":"geo_note_trigonal","v":{"s":"three-fold symmetry, axes equally inclined"}},{"k":"geo_note_rhombohedral","v":{"s":"three equal axes, none at right angles"}},{"k":"geo_note_orthorhombic","v":{"s":"three unequal axes, all at right angles"}},{"k":"geo_note_monoclinic","v":{"s":"three unequal axes, one pair not at right angles"}},{"k":"geo_note_triclinic","v":{"s":"three unequal axes, none at right angles"}},{"k":"thin_look_granite","v":{"s":"Interlocking grains with no preferred direction and no space between them — that texture alone says it crystallised slowly from a melt."}},{"k":"thin_look_diorite","v":{"s":"Interlocking, and much darker overall than granite because there is almost no quartz."}},{"k":"thin_look_basalt","v":{"s":"Tiny lath-shaped feldspar crystals in a fine groundmass — chilled too fast for anything to grow large."}},{"k":"thin_look_andesite","v":{"s":"A few larger crystals sitting in a much finer groundmass: two cooling rates recorded in one rock."}},{"k":"thin_look_rhyolite","v":{"s":"Glassy groundmass with scattered quartz and feldspar — erupted, not intruded."}},{"k":"thin_look_obsidian","v":{"s":"No crystals at all. Under crossed polars the whole field stays black however far you rotate, because glass is not a crystal."}},{"k":"thin_look_pumice","v":{"s":"Glass threaded with frozen gas bubbles — mostly holes. Those voids are why it floats."}},{"k":"thin_look_tuff","v":{"s":"Broken shards of volcanic glass welded together — an ash fall turned to rock. The fragments are angular because they were never carried anywhere."}},{"k":"thin_look_gabbro","v":{"s":"Big interlocking crystals: lath-shaped plagioclase with candy-stripe twins, pale green pyroxene between them, and opaque grains that stay black in both modes. The same minerals as basalt, given time to grow."}},{"k":"thin_look_sandstone","v":{"s":"ROUNDED grains with cement between them — the rounding happened during transport, and the space between grains is the giveaway that this was once loose sand."}},{"k":"thin_look_conglom","v":{"s":"Large rounded clasts of several different rocks, set in a finer matrix."}},{"k":"thin_look_breccia","v":{"s":"Sharp-cornered fragments packed in cement. Put it beside conglomerate at the same magnification: identical idea, rounded clasts. Corners survive only when the pieces did not travel."}},{"k":"thin_look_siltstone","v":{"s":"Grains you can only just resolve at 100x: far bigger than the clay in shale, far smaller than the sand in sandstone. They are still ANGULAR, because transport cannot round a grain this fine."}},{"k":"thin_look_shale","v":{"s":"Clay too fine to resolve even at this magnification, with the flakes weakly lined up."}},{"k":"thin_look_limestone","v":{"s":"Calcite everywhere, often with fossil fragments still recognisable. Watch the relief flicker as you rotate — that is calcite."}},{"k":"thin_look_chalk","v":{"s":"At 400x the \"mud\" resolves into countless plates from single-celled plankton."}},{"k":"thin_look_travertine","v":{"s":"Banded calcite precipitated from water, often with open cavities."}},{"k":"thin_look_coal","v":{"s":"Black in plane light AND under crossed polars, because coal is opaque and no light gets through it at all. The pale specks are the clay and quartz dust that blew into the swamp with the plants. Opacity is the observation here, and it is why coal is studied in reflected light."}},{"k":"thin_look_marble","v":{"s":"Calcite recrystallised into a tight interlocking mosaic — the fossils and bedding are gone."}},{"k":"thin_look_quartzite","v":{"s":"Quartz grains fused directly to each other with no cement left between them. Compare with sandstone."}},{"k":"thin_look_slate","v":{"s":"Microscopic micas all rotated into the same plane — that alignment IS the cleavage."}},{"k":"thin_look_phyllite","v":{"s":"The micas have grown just big enough to catch the light, which is the silky sheen you see in the hand specimen."}},{"k":"thin_look_schist","v":{"s":"Mica flakes now large and strongly aligned, often wrapping around garnets."}},{"k":"thin_look_gneiss","v":{"s":"Light and dark minerals segregated into separate bands — the banding you see with the naked eye, at grain scale."}},{"k":"opt_note_quartz","v":{"s":"colourless, no cleavage; grey to white interference colours"}},{"k":"opt_note_feldspar","v":{"s":"colourless with cleavage traces; grey, and plagioclase shows candy-stripe twins"}},{"k":"opt_note_mica","v":{"s":"one perfect cleavage; vivid 2nd-order colours"}},{"k":"opt_note_biotite","v":{"s":"brown and pleochroic; mottled dark extinction"}},{"k":"opt_note_calcite","v":{"s":"very high relief that changes as you rotate; pearly high-order colours"}},{"k":"opt_note_olivine","v":{"s":"colourless to pale green, cracked; bright 2nd-3rd order colours"}},{"k":"opt_note_pyroxene","v":{"s":"pale green, two cleavages at right angles"}},{"k":"opt_note_amphibole","v":{"s":"green and pleochroic, two cleavages at 60/120 degrees"}},{"k":"opt_note_garnet","v":{"s":"high relief, no cleavage — and STAYS BLACK under crossed polars at every angle"}},{"k":"opt_note_magnetite","v":{"s":"opaque — black in both modes because no light gets through at all"}},{"k":"opt_note_organic","v":{"s":"coal macerals are opaque in transmitted light, so they stay black in both modes; coal petrology is done in REFLECTED light for exactly this reason"}},{"k":"opt_note_clay","v":{"s":"too fine to resolve individually; a dull mottled mass"}},{"k":"opt_note_glass","v":{"s":"volcanic glass is not crystalline, so it is isotropic and stays black"}},{"k":"opt_note_cement","v":{"s":"the carbonate or silica cement holding the grains together"}},{"k":"lat_why_halite","v":{"s":"Na⁺ and Cl⁻ alternate in every direction. The bonds are equally strong along all three axes, so halite cleaves into perfect cubes."}},{"k":"lat_why_galena","v":{"s":"Same rock-salt packing as halite, with lead and sulfur. That shared arrangement is why galena also breaks into cubes."}},{"k":"lat_why_fluorite","v":{"s":"Calcium sits on a face-centred cube with fluoride filling all eight tetrahedral holes. The weakest planes run diagonally, so fluorite cleaves into octahedra, not cubes."}},{"k":"lat_why_graphite","v":{"s":"The same element as diamond, arranged completely differently. Each carbon bonds to only THREE others, in flat hexagonal sheets, and the sheets are held to each other by almost nothing. Inside a sheet the bonds are even stronger than diamond’s; between sheets there is a gap more than twice the bond length with no bond drawn across it, because there is none. That gap is the whole difference: graphite is Mohs 1 to 2 and rubs off on paper, while diamond is Mohs 10."}},{"k":"lat_why_diamond","v":{"s":"Every carbon is bonded to four others in a rigid three-dimensional net. Nothing in the structure is weak, which is why diamond is the hardest mineral at Mohs 10."}},{"k":"lat_why_pyrite","v":{"s":"Iron on a face-centred cube with sulfur in bonded PAIRS (S₂). The paired sulfur is what makes it a disulfide rather than a simple sulfide."}},{"k":"lat_why_malachite","v":{"s":"A copper carbonate, so the flat triangular CO₃ group drawn here is the right motif and the reason malachite fizzes in acid. What the model does NOT show is the rest of the formula: malachite is a BASIC carbonate, with hydroxyl groups bonded to the copper as well, and its real layers are more complex than calcite’s neat stack."}},{"k":"lat_why_azurite","v":{"s":"The same carbonate group and the same copper as malachite, in a different proportion: three coppers and two CO₃ groups instead of two and one. That small difference in the formula is the whole difference between blue and green, and azurite converts to malachite over time by taking up water."}},{"k":"lat_why_calcite","v":{"s":"Layers of calcium alternate with flat triangular CO₃ groups. The layers are stacked at a slant, which is why calcite always breaks into leaning rhombs."}},{"k":"lat_why_mica","v":{"s":"Strongly bonded silicate sheets held together only weakly between layers. That contrast is the whole story: mica peels into transparent flakes but is tough within a sheet."}},{"k":"lat_why_biotite","v":{"s":"The same silicate sheets as muscovite, with iron and magnesium filling the sites aluminium holds there. That swap changes almost nothing you can measure and everything you can see: it is why biotite is black and muscovite is silvery, while both peel into flexible sheets at Mohs 2.5 to 3."}},{"k":"lat_why_talc","v":{"s":"Silicate sheets with almost nothing holding one sheet to the next, so they slide over each other. That is why talc is the softest mineral at Mohs 1 and feels slippery."}},{"k":"lat_why_quartz","v":{"s":"Every silicon sits at the centre of an oxygen tetrahedron, and every tetrahedron shares all four corners with its neighbours. The framework has no weak plane, so quartz fractures like glass instead of cleaving."}},{"k":"lat_why_gypsum","v":{"s":"Layers of calcium sulfate separated by sheets of water molecules. The water layers are the weak planes gypsum splits along."}},{"k":"lat_why_garnet","v":{"s":"A nesosilicate like olivine — isolated SiO₄ tetrahedra — but held together by TWO different cation sites, a larger one and a smaller one, packed tightly in three dimensions. No chains, no sheets, no framework means no plane of weakness anywhere: garnet has no cleavage at all and fractures instead, and that even packing is why it reaches Mohs 7 and grows those equant twelve-sided crystals."}},{"k":"lat_why_topaz","v":{"s":"Isolated SiO₄ tetrahedra linked by aluminium, with fluorine completing the aluminium’s coordination. Strong bonding in every direction gives Mohs 8 — but the fluorine and hydroxyl sit in layers, and that single plane of weaker bonds is why topaz has one perfect cleavage. Gem cutters have to orient around it."}},{"k":"lat_why_apatite","v":{"s":"A phosphate, not a silicate: isolated PO₄ tetrahedra linked by calcium, the same island motif olivine has with SiO₄. What this model does NOT draw is the feature apatite is named for. Columns of calcium leave open channels running along the long axis, and fluorine, chlorine or a hydroxyl group sits inside them. Which one fills the channel is the entire difference between fluorapatite, chlorapatite and hydroxylapatite."}},{"k":"lat_why_magnetite","v":{"s":"Magnetite is an inverse spinel: Fe³⁺ fills tetrahedral A sites, while equal numbers of Fe²⁺ and Fe³⁺ share twice as many octahedral B sites. A- and B-site magnetic moments point in OPPOSITE directions. The Fe³⁺ contributions largely cancel, leaving a net Fe²⁺ contribution — why magnetite can form a lodestone. The two B-site colours track the formal 1:1 count; they are not fixed room-temperature charge positions."}},{"k":"lat_why_feldspar","v":{"s":"A framework of corner-linked tetrahedra, like quartz — except aluminium substitutes for some of the silicon. Aluminium carries one less positive charge, so potassium, sodium or calcium sits in the cavities to balance it. That substitution is the entire difference from quartz, and it is why feldspar breaks along two clean planes while quartz has none."}},{"k":"lat_why_sulfur","v":{"s":"Sulfur is a MOLECULAR crystal: eight atoms bonded into a puckered S₈ crown, and only weak attractions holding one ring to the next. Strong bonds inside the ring, almost nothing between them — which is why sulfur is Mohs 2, crumbles easily, and melts at just 115 °C."}},{"k":"lat_why_olivine","v":{"s":"A nesosilicate: no SiO₄ tetrahedron shares an oxygen with another one. They are islands, and the magnesium and iron bonded between them are the only thing linking the structure. With no linked framework and no sheets, olivine has no good cleavage direction — and those exposed cation sites are why it weathers away faster than any other common silicate."}},{"k":"lat_why_corundum","v":{"s":"Oxygen packed as tightly as spheres can be, with aluminium filling two thirds of the gaps between them. Dense packing plus short, strong Al–O bonds in every direction is what makes corundum Mohs 9 — ruby and sapphire are this structure with a trace of chromium or iron for colour."}},{"k":"lat_why_hematite","v":{"s":"The same close-packed architecture as corundum, with iron in place of aluminium. Identical geometry, different cation — and because Fe–O bonds are weaker than Al–O, hematite is around Mohs 6 rather than 9. The iron is also what gives it that red-brown streak."}},{"k":"atom_Na","v":{"s":"Sodium (Na⁺)"}},{"k":"atom_Cl","v":{"s":"Chloride (Cl⁻)"}},{"k":"atom_Pb","v":{"s":"Lead (Pb²⁺)"}},{"k":"atom_S","v":{"s":"Sulfur (S)"}},{"k":"atom_Ca","v":{"s":"Calcium (Ca²⁺)"}},{"k":"atom_F","v":{"s":"Fluoride (F⁻)"}},{"k":"atom_C","v":{"s":"Carbon (C)"}},{"k":"atom_O","v":{"s":"Oxygen (O)"}},{"k":"atom_Si","v":{"s":"Silicon (Si)"}},{"k":"atom_P","v":{"s":"Phosphorus (P)"}},{"k":"atom_Fe","v":{"s":"Iron (Fe)"}},{"k":"atom_Cu","v":{"s":"Copper (Cu²⁺)"}},{"k":"atom_Mg","v":{"s":"Magnesium (Mg)"}},{"k":"atom_Al","v":{"s":"Aluminium (Al)"}},{"k":"atom_K","v":{"s":"Potassium (K⁺)"}},{"k":"atom_Fe3A","v":{"s":"Iron Fe³⁺ (tetrahedral A site)"}},{"k":"atom_Fe3B","v":{"s":"Iron Fe³⁺ (octahedral B site)"}},{"k":"atom_Fe2B","v":{"s":"Iron Fe²⁺ (octahedral B site)"}},{"k":"atom_X","v":{"s":"Lattice point"}},{"k":"tsmin_quartz","v":{"s":"quartz"}},{"k":"tsmin_feldspar","v":{"s":"feldspar"}},{"k":"tsmin_mica","v":{"s":"mica"}},{"k":"tsmin_biotite","v":{"s":"biotite"}},{"k":"tsmin_calcite","v":{"s":"calcite"}},{"k":"tsmin_olivine","v":{"s":"olivine"}},{"k":"tsmin_pyroxene","v":{"s":"pyroxene"}},{"k":"tsmin_amphibole","v":{"s":"amphibole"}},{"k":"tsmin_garnet","v":{"s":"garnet"}},{"k":"tsmin_magnetite","v":{"s":"magnetite"}},{"k":"tsmin_organic","v":{"s":"organic"}},{"k":"tsmin_clay","v":{"s":"clay"}},{"k":"tsmin_glass","v":{"s":"glass"}},{"k":"tsmin_cement","v":{"s":"cement"}},{"k":"thin_minerals_present","v":{"s":"Minerals present: "}},{"k":"lat_aria_structure","v":{"s":" atomic structure: "}},{"k":"lat_cell_a","v":{"s":" crystallises in the "}},{"k":"lat_cell_b","v":{"s":" system — "}},{"k":"lat_cell_c","v":{"s":". That symmetry can influence its observed form and cleavage."}},{"k":"mdesc_quartz","v":{"s":"The second most abundant mineral in the crust of Earth. Forms distinctive six-sided prismatic crystals with pointed terminations. Extremely resistant to weathering. Comes in many colored varieties: amethyst (purple), citrine (yellow), rose quartz (pink), smoky quartz (brown)."}},{"k":"muses_quartz","v":{"s":"Electronics (oscillators, watches), glassmaking, abrasives, gemstones"}},{"k":"mfact_quartz","v":{"s":"Quartz is piezoelectric (when squeezed, it generates an electric charge). This property makes quartz watches accurate to within 15 seconds per month!"}},{"k":"mwhere_quartz","v":{"s":"Found in virtually all rock types worldwide. Major deposits in Brazil, Arkansas (USA), Madagascar, and the Alps."}},{"k":"mdesc_feldspar","v":{"s":"This reference specimen represents orthoclase, a potassium feldspar. Feldspars are the most abundant mineral group in Earth’s crust and commonly show two cleavage directions near 90°. Orthoclase is often pink, white, or gray."}},{"k":"muses_feldspar","v":{"s":"Ceramics, glass, porcelain, scouring powders, dental products"}},{"k":"mfact_feldspar","v":{"s":"The name feldspar comes from the German words for \"field\" and \"splinter.\" Moonstone and labradorite are feldspar gemstones!"}},{"k":"mwhere_feldspar","v":{"s":"Common in granite, rhyolite, gneiss, pegmatites, and many other igneous and metamorphic rocks worldwide."}},{"k":"mdesc_mica","v":{"s":"Sheet silicate that peels into thin, flexible, transparent sheets. The \"sparkly\" mineral in rocks. Two common types: muscovite (light/clear) and biotite (dark/black). Perfect basal cleavage produces incredibly thin layers."}},{"k":"muses_mica","v":{"s":"Electrical insulation, cosmetics (shimmer), paint filler, window material (historically)"}},{"k":"mfact_mica","v":{"s":"Before glass windows were common, thin sheets of muscovite mica were used as window panes in medieval Russia, hence \"Muscovy glass\" → muscovite!"}},{"k":"mwhere_mica","v":{"s":"Common in granites, schists, pegmatites. Major deposits in India, Brazil, Russia, and the USA."}},{"k":"mdesc_biotite","v":{"s":"The dark mica. It peels into flexible sheets exactly as muscovite does, and shares its hardness, its white streak and its one perfect cleavage, but iron and magnesium in the structure make it brown to black instead of silvery. In a granite the black flakes are biotite and the pale ones are muscovite."}},{"k":"muses_biotite","v":{"s":"Mainly a rock-forming mineral rather than an ore; used in potassium-argon dating and as a filler"}},{"k":"mfact_biotite","v":{"s":"Biotite is a favourite mineral for dating rocks. It takes potassium into its structure and then holds on to the argon that potassium decays into, so a single flake records when its rock last cooled."}},{"k":"mwhere_biotite","v":{"s":"Granites, schists and gneisses worldwide. Most coarse-grained igneous and metamorphic rocks contain some."}},{"k":"mdesc_calcite","v":{"s":"The primary mineral in limestone and marble. Shows perfect rhombohedral cleavage, always breaks into parallelogram-shaped pieces. Fizzes vigorously when dilute acid is applied. Some varieties show double refraction (text appears doubled through clear crystals)."}},{"k":"muses_calcite","v":{"s":"Cement/concrete, lime production, optical instruments, antacid tablets (Tums)"}},{"k":"mfact_calcite","v":{"s":"Iceland spar (transparent calcite) creates double images! Vikings may have used it as a \"sunstone\" to navigate on cloudy days by detecting polarized skylight."}},{"k":"mwhere_calcite","v":{"s":"Limestone caves (stalactites/stalagmites), coral reefs, chalk cliffs, marble deposits worldwide."}},{"k":"mdesc_malachite","v":{"s":"A copper carbonate, and one of the few minerals whose powder is as green as the specimen. It usually grows as banded, kidney-shaped masses rather than crystals, and it fizzes in dilute acid because it IS a carbonate. Where you find it, copper ore is usually nearby."}},{"k":"muses_malachite","v":{"s":"Copper ore, gemstone and carving stone, historic green pigment"}},{"k":"mfact_malachite","v":{"s":"Ground malachite was the green pigment in ancient Egyptian eye paint and in medieval European paintings, long before anyone knew it was a copper ore."}},{"k":"mwhere_malachite","v":{"s":"The oxidised upper zone of copper deposits. Famous from the Urals, Katanga in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, and Bisbee in Arizona."}},{"k":"mdesc_azurite","v":{"s":"The blue copper carbonate, and malachite’s constant companion: the two form in the same place, and azurite slowly turns INTO malachite as it takes up water. Like malachite it fizzes in dilute acid. Deep blue specimen, pale blue powder."}},{"k":"muses_azurite","v":{"s":"Copper ore, gemstone, historic blue pigment"}},{"k":"mfact_azurite","v":{"s":"Blue skies in old European paintings sometimes look green today, because the azurite pigment altered to malachite on the canvas over centuries."}},{"k":"mwhere_azurite","v":{"s":"The same oxidised copper zones as malachite, usually with it. Chessy in France gave azurite its old name, chessylite."}},{"k":"mdesc_halite","v":{"s":"Common table salt forms perfect cubic crystals. Never taste an unknown mineral specimen; it may be contaminated or unsafe. Halite forms when shallow seas or salt lakes evaporate and can be colorless, white, pink, blue, or red because of impurities."}},{"k":"muses_halite","v":{"s":"Food seasoning/preservation, road de-icing, chemical industry, water softening"}},{"k":"mfact_halite","v":{"s":"The English word \"salary\" comes through Latin salarium, an allowance associated with buying salt—not evidence that Roman soldiers were paid in salt."}},{"k":"mwhere_halite","v":{"s":"Evaporite deposits in arid regions: Great Salt Lake, Dead Sea, salt mines in Poland (Wieliczka), Germany, and Louisiana."}},{"k":"mdesc_pyrite","v":{"s":"Iron sulfide with a brilliant metallic brass-yellow color. Forms perfect cubes, pyritohedrons, and octahedrons. Produces sparks when struck against steel (name from Greek \"pyr\" = fire). Commonly mistaken for gold but much harder and lighter."}},{"k":"muses_pyrite","v":{"s":"Sulfuric acid production, electronics (early crystal radios), decorative stone, gold indicator mineral"}},{"k":"mfact_pyrite","v":{"s":"Called \"fool’s gold\" because miners confused it with real gold. You can tell them apart: gold is soft (scratches with a knife), pyrite is hard. Gold leaves a yellow streak, pyrite leaves a greenish-black streak!"}},{"k":"mwhere_pyrite","v":{"s":"Found in all rock types. Often found alongside real gold deposits! Common in coal, hydrothermal veins, and sedimentary rocks."}},{"k":"mdesc_talc","v":{"s":"The softest known mineral, number 1 on the Mohs scale. Can be scratched with a fingernail! Has a soapy, greasy feel. Forms flat, foliated masses. Color ranges from white to pale green to gray. Metamorphic mineral formed from magnesium-rich rocks."}},{"k":"muses_talc","v":{"s":"Talcum powder, ceramics, paint filler, paper coating, cosmetics"}},{"k":"mfact_talc","v":{"s":"Soapstone (used for carving and countertops) is a rock made mostly of talc. It was used by ancient cultures worldwide to carve cooking vessels because it retains heat well!"}},{"k":"mwhere_talc","v":{"s":"Metamorphic rocks (ultramafic environments). Major deposits in China, India, USA (Vermont), France, and Brazil."}},{"k":"mdesc_graphite","v":{"s":"Pure carbon, and the softest common mineral after talc. It is greasy to the touch, marks paper, and conducts electricity, which almost no other non-metal mineral does. Diamond is the same element: everything different about the two comes from how the atoms are joined."}},{"k":"muses_graphite","v":{"s":"Pencil \"lead\", dry lubricant, crucibles, electrodes, lithium-ion battery anodes"}},{"k":"mfact_graphite","v":{"s":"Graphite and diamond are both pure carbon, so the same element gives you Mohs 1 to 2 and Mohs 10. Arrangement, not composition, is doing all of the work."}},{"k":"mwhere_graphite","v":{"s":"Metamorphosed carbon-rich sediments; marbles and schists. Major sources in China, Mozambique, Madagascar and Sri Lanka."}},{"k":"mdesc_diamond","v":{"s":"Pure crystallized carbon, the hardest natural substance on Earth. Forms deep in the mantle (150+ km below surface) under extreme pressure and temperature. Brought to surface by violent volcanic eruptions in kimberlite pipes. High refractive index creates \"fire\" (rainbow flashes)."}},{"k":"muses_diamond","v":{"s":"Gemstones, cutting/grinding tools, drill bits, thermal conductors, optical windows"}},{"k":"mfact_diamond","v":{"s":"Diamond and graphite (pencil lead) are both pure carbon! The only difference is how the carbon atoms are arranged. Diamond is the hardest mineral; graphite is one of the softest. Same atoms, completely different properties!"}},{"k":"mwhere_diamond","v":{"s":"Kimberlite pipes in South Africa, Russia, Australia, Canada, Botswana. Also found in river gravels (alluvial deposits)."}},{"k":"mdesc_magnetite","v":{"s":"The most magnetic naturally occurring mineral on Earth. Strongly attracted to magnets and can itself act as a natural magnet (\"lodestone\"). Black, heavy, and opaque. Important iron ore mineral. Octahedral crystal habit."}},{"k":"muses_magnetite","v":{"s":"Iron/steel production, magnetic recording media, heavy concrete, water purification"}},{"k":"mfact_magnetite","v":{"s":"Lodestone (naturally magnetized magnetite) was the first compass! Ancient Chinese and Greek navigators used floating lodestones to find north. Magnetite crystals have even been found in the brains of pigeons and sea turtles, helping them navigate!"}},{"k":"mwhere_magnetite","v":{"s":"Igneous and metamorphic rocks worldwide. Major deposits in Sweden (Kiruna), Australia, Brazil, South Africa, and Minnesota (USA)."}},{"k":"mdesc_hematite","v":{"s":"The most important iron ore mineral. Name from Greek \"haima\" (blood) due to its red streak. Can appear metallic silver-gray (specular hematite) or earthy red-brown. Always produces a distinctive red-brown streak regardless of surface color."}},{"k":"muses_hematite","v":{"s":"Iron/steel production (primary ore), pigment (red ochre), polishing compound (jeweler’s rouge), radiation shielding"}},{"k":"mfact_hematite","v":{"s":"Mars looks red because its surface materials contain oxidized iron minerals. Hematite occurs on Mars, but newer evidence suggests ferrihydrite may also be an important source of the planet’s red dust. Hematite was also used as a pigment by prehistoric humans."}},{"k":"mwhere_hematite","v":{"s":"Banded iron formations, volcanic rocks, red soils. Lake Superior region (USA), Minas Gerais (Brazil), Pilbara (Australia), Mars!"}},{"k":"mdesc_garnet","v":{"s":"A group of silicate minerals known for their beautiful dodecahedral crystals (12-sided). Most commonly deep red (almandine), but can be green (tsavorite), orange (spessartine), or even color-changing. Very hard and durable. Excellent for identifying metamorphic grade."}},{"k":"muses_garnet","v":{"s":"Abrasive blasting (sandpaper, waterjet cutting), gemstones, water filtration, indicator mineral in geology"}},{"k":"mfact_garnet","v":{"s":"Garnets grow in metamorphic rocks and their size indicates how much heat and pressure the rock experienced. Geologists use garnet composition like a geological thermometer! Some rare garnets change color from blue-green in daylight to purple under incandescent light."}},{"k":"mwhere_garnet","v":{"s":"Schists, gneisses, contact metamorphic zones. Major gem deposits in India, Sri Lanka, Tanzania, Madagascar, and Idaho (USA)."}},{"k":"mdesc_olivine","v":{"s":"Olive-green mineral abundant in the upper mantle of Earth. One of the first minerals to crystallize from cooling magma. Forms small glassy grains in basalt. Gem variety is called peridot. Weathers quickly at the surface, which is why it is rare in sedimentary rocks."}},{"k":"muses_olivine","v":{"s":"Gemstone (peridot), refractory bricks, CO₂ capture research, foundry sand"}},{"k":"mfact_olivine","v":{"s":"Olivine is a dominant mineral in much of Earth's upper mantle, but bridgmanite deeper in the mantle is Earth's most abundant mineral overall. The green sand beaches of Hawaii (Papakōlea Beach) are made of tiny olivine crystals eroded from volcanic rock!"}},{"k":"mwhere_olivine","v":{"s":"Basalt, peridotite, meteorites. Hawaii, Canary Islands, Pakistan (peridot gems), mantle xenoliths worldwide."}},{"k":"mdesc_fluorite","v":{"s":"Known as the \"most colorful mineral in the world\", comes in virtually every color: purple, green, blue, yellow, pink, and even colorless. Forms perfect cubic and octahedral crystals. Often fluorescent under UV light (the word \"fluorescence\" comes from fluorite!). Four directions of perfect cleavage."}},{"k":"muses_fluorite","v":{"s":"Steelmaking flux, hydrofluoric acid production, optical lenses, gemstone, decorative carvings"}},{"k":"mfact_fluorite","v":{"s":"Fluorite literally invented the word \"fluorescence\"! In 1852, George Stokes described the glow of fluorite under UV light and coined the term from the name of the mineral. The element fluorine is also named after fluorite!"}},{"k":"mwhere_fluorite","v":{"s":"Hydrothermal veins, limestone cavities. Major deposits in China, Mexico, South Africa, Derbyshire (England, \"Blue John\"), and Illinois (USA)."}},{"k":"mdesc_galena","v":{"s":"Primary ore of lead. Very dense (heavy for its size) with perfect cubic cleavage, so it cleaves into tiny cubes. Bright metallic silver color when fresh, tarnishes to dull gray. Lead-gray streak. Often found with silver as an impurity, making it a source of silver too."}},{"k":"muses_galena","v":{"s":"Lead production, ammunition, batteries, radiation shielding, early radio crystal detectors"}},{"k":"mfact_galena","v":{"s":"Before transistors were invented, galena crystals were used in \"crystal radio\" sets! A thin wire (\"cat’s whisker\") touching a galena crystal could detect radio signals without any battery or electricity. Galena was also used by ancient Egyptians as kohl eyeliner!"}},{"k":"mwhere_galena","v":{"s":"Hydrothermal veins, limestone replacement deposits. Missouri (USA, largest lead deposit), Broken Hill (Australia), Germany, Mexico."}},{"k":"mdesc_apatite","v":{"s":"The reference mineral for Mohs 5, and the mineral your bones and tooth enamel are built from. Grows as six-sided prisms, most often green or blue-green, and is soft enough to be scratched by a glass plate but not by a copper coin."}},{"k":"muses_apatite","v":{"s":"Phosphate fertilizer, phosphoric acid, gemstones"}},{"k":"mfact_apatite","v":{"s":"Tooth enamel is a form of apatite, so the hardest tissue in your body sits at 5 on the very scale that runs from talc to diamond."}},{"k":"mwhere_apatite","v":{"s":"An accessory mineral in almost every igneous rock, and the main ore of phosphorus. Major deposits in Morocco, the Kola Peninsula in Russia, and Florida."}},{"k":"mdesc_gypsum","v":{"s":"A very soft evaporite mineral (can be scratched with a fingernail). Forms in a variety of habits: tabular crystals (selenite), fibrous masses (satin spar), and granular masses (alabaster). Transparent selenite crystals can be enormous. Contains water in its crystal structure."}},{"k":"muses_gypsum","v":{"s":"Drywall/plasterboard, plaster of Paris, cement, fertilizer, alabaster carvings"}},{"k":"mfact_gypsum","v":{"s":"The Naica Mine in Mexico contains selenite gypsum crystals up to 12 meters (39 feet) long and weighing 55 tons, the largest crystals ever discovered on Earth! The cave is so hot (58°C/136°F) that humans can only survive inside for about 10 minutes!"}},{"k":"mwhere_gypsum","v":{"s":"Evaporite deposits, desert roses (sand-included crystals), cave formations. Major deposits in USA, Mexico, Spain, Italy, and Nova Scotia."}},{"k":"mdesc_sulfur","v":{"s":"Native element with a distinctive bright yellow color. Very light and brittle, with a low melting point (115°C). Burning sulfur makes pungent sulfur dioxide gas; the rotten-egg odor at some volcanic sites comes from hydrogen sulfide, not sulfur itself. Associated with volcanic activity and hot springs. One of the few minerals that occurs as a native element."}},{"k":"muses_sulfur","v":{"s":"Sulfuric acid (most widely used chemical), gunpowder, rubber vulcanization, fungicides, matches"}},{"k":"mfact_sulfur","v":{"s":"Sulfur was known to ancient civilizations as \"brimstone\" (burning stone). It is mentioned in the Bible and in the Odyssey by Homer! The moon Io of Jupiter is covered in sulfur from its 400+ active volcanoes, giving it a bright yellow-orange appearance."}},{"k":"mwhere_sulfur","v":{"s":"Volcanic fumaroles, hot springs, evaporite domes (Gulf Coast USA), Sicily, Japan, Indonesia."}},{"k":"mdesc_corundum","v":{"s":"Second hardest natural mineral after diamond. Pure corundum is colorless, but trace impurities create spectacular gemstones: chromium makes ruby (red), iron and titanium make sapphire (blue). Can occur in many other colors too. Extremely durable and resistant to chemical weathering."}},{"k":"muses_corundum","v":{"s":"Gemstones (ruby/sapphire), watch bearings, abrasive (emery), laser rods, sandpaper"}},{"k":"mfact_corundum","v":{"s":"Ruby and sapphire are the SAME mineral! The only difference is trace element impurities, where 0.01% chromium makes a ruby, while iron+titanium make a sapphire. A \"padparadscha\" sapphire (pink-orange) is among the rarest gems in the world!"}},{"k":"mwhere_corundum","v":{"s":"Metamorphic and igneous rocks. Major ruby deposits in Myanmar, Mozambique. Sapphires from Kashmir, Sri Lanka, Montana (USA), Australia."}},{"k":"mdesc_topaz","v":{"s":"Hard silicate mineral prized as a gemstone. Naturally colorless, yellow, orange, or blue (most blue topaz on the market is heat-treated). Contains fluorine and hydroxyl in its structure. Forms beautiful prismatic crystals with vertical striations. Perfect basal cleavage."}},{"k":"muses_topaz","v":{"s":"Gemstones, Mohs hardness reference (#8), decorative carvings, optical components"}},{"k":"mfact_topaz","v":{"s":"The 6.2 kg (31,000-carat) El-Dorado Topaz from Brazil is one of the world's largest faceted topazes; it was cut from a much heavier rough crystal. Imperial topaz (a rare orange-pink variety from Ouro Preto, Brazil) is among the most valuable colored gemstones."}},{"k":"mwhere_topaz","v":{"s":"Granite pegmatites, rhyolite cavities, alluvial deposits. Major sources: Brazil (Minas Gerais), Pakistan, Russia (Ural Mts), Utah (USA)."}},{"k":"desc_granite","v":{"s":"Intrusive igneous rock with visible quartz, feldspar, and mica crystals. Forms deep underground from slowly cooling magma."}},{"k":"uses_granite","v":{"s":"Countertops, monuments, building stone"}},{"k":"desc_basalt","v":{"s":"Extrusive igneous rock, the most common volcanic rock. Forms when lava cools quickly at the surface."}},{"k":"uses_basalt","v":{"s":"Road aggregate, construction fill"}},{"k":"desc_obsidian","v":{"s":"Volcanic glass formed when lava cools extremely rapidly. Conchoidal fracture."}},{"k":"uses_obsidian","v":{"s":"Surgical scalpels, jewelry, ancient tools"}},{"k":"desc_pumice","v":{"s":"Light, porous volcanic rock full of gas bubbles. So light it can float on water!"}},{"k":"uses_pumice","v":{"s":"Abrasive cleaning, lightweight aggregate"}},{"k":"desc_rhyolite","v":{"s":"Extrusive equivalent of granite. Light-colored fine-grained volcanic rock, often with flow banding. Rich in silica (>69%). Erupts explosively due to high viscosity."}},{"k":"uses_rhyolite","v":{"s":"Aggregate, decorative stone, gemstone (thundereggs)"}},{"k":"desc_diorite","v":{"s":"Intrusive igneous rock with a \"salt and pepper\" appearance. Intermediate composition between granite and gabbro. Contains plagioclase feldspar and hornblende."}},{"k":"uses_diorite","v":{"s":"Building stone, cobblestones, ancient sculptures (Inca)"}},{"k":"desc_gabbro","v":{"s":"Coarse-grained intrusive rock with the same composition as basalt: plagioclase, pyroxene and often olivine. The same magma makes basalt when it erupts and gabbro when it cools slowly at depth. Most of the ocean crust beneath the basalt layer is gabbro."}},{"k":"uses_gabbro","v":{"s":"Building stone, road aggregate, polished slabs sold as \"black granite\""}},{"k":"desc_andesite","v":{"s":"Intermediate volcanic rock named after the Andes Mountains. Common at convergent plate boundaries. Often contains visible phenocrysts in a fine matrix (porphyritic texture)."}},{"k":"uses_andesite","v":{"s":"Construction aggregate, monuments"}},{"k":"desc_tuff","v":{"s":"Consolidated volcanic ash. Formed when explosive eruptions blast fine particles into the air, which settle and lithify. Can contain pumice fragments and glass shards."}},{"k":"uses_tuff","v":{"s":"Building stone (ancient Rome), lightweight concrete, water filtration"}},{"k":"desc_sandstone","v":{"s":"Made of sand-sized quartz grains cemented together. Often shows cross-bedding from ancient dunes or rivers."}},{"k":"uses_sandstone","v":{"s":"Building stone, flagstone, aquifers"}},{"k":"desc_limestone","v":{"s":"Composed mainly of calcite (CaCO₃). Often contains fossils. Fizzes with acid!"}},{"k":"uses_limestone","v":{"s":"Cement, lime, building stone, chalk"}},{"k":"desc_siltstone","v":{"s":"The grain size between sandstone and shale. Silt grains are too small to pick out by eye but big enough to feel gritty between your teeth, and unlike shale it does not split into sheets. Grain size is the entire classification here: sand you can see, silt you can feel, clay you can only smear."}},{"k":"uses_siltstone","v":{"s":"Brick and tile clay, construction aggregate"}},{"k":"desc_shale","v":{"s":"Made of compressed clay and silt. Splits into thin layers (fissile). Most common sedimentary rock."}},{"k":"uses_shale","v":{"s":"Bricks, pottery, oil/gas source rock"}},{"k":"desc_conglom","v":{"s":"Contains large rounded pebbles cemented in a fine matrix. Tells us about ancient fast-flowing rivers."}},{"k":"uses_conglom","v":{"s":"Construction aggregate, decorative stone"}},{"k":"desc_breccia","v":{"s":"Large ANGULAR fragments cemented in a finer matrix. Compare it with conglomerate: same sizes, same cement, but these corners are still sharp. Rounding takes distance, so breccia forms where the pieces were buried close to where they broke, such as a scree slope or a fault zone."}},{"k":"uses_breccia","v":{"s":"Decorative stone, construction aggregate"}},{"k":"desc_chalk","v":{"s":"Soft, white limestone made of microscopic plankton shells (coccoliths). The White Cliffs of Dover are chalk. Extremely fine-grained, each grain is a tiny fossil!"}},{"k":"uses_chalk","v":{"s":"Blackboard chalk, whiting, soil amendment, toothpaste"}},{"k":"desc_travertine","v":{"s":"Chemical sedimentary rock deposited from mineral-rich hot springs and cave systems. Often has a banded, porous appearance. Forms stalactites and stalagmites in caves."}},{"k":"uses_travertine","v":{"s":"Flooring, countertops, building facades (Colosseum in Rome)"}},{"k":"desc_coal","v":{"s":"The one common rock made of once-living material instead of mineral grains. Plants piled up in a swamp faster than they could rot, and burial squeezed and heated the peat that formed. More burial means a higher rank: peat, then lignite, then the bituminous coal shown here, then anthracite. Light for a rock, dull with brighter bands, and it burns."}},{"k":"uses_coal","v":{"s":"Fuel for electricity, coke for steelmaking"}},{"k":"desc_marble","v":{"s":"Metamorphosed limestone. Interlocking calcite crystals give it a sugary texture. Used by sculptors for millennia."}},{"k":"uses_marble","v":{"s":"Sculpture, flooring, tombstones"}},{"k":"desc_slate","v":{"s":"Metamorphosed shale. Excellent foliation, splits into flat, smooth sheets."}},{"k":"uses_slate","v":{"s":"Roofing tiles, chalkboards, flooring"}},{"k":"desc_quartzite","v":{"s":"Metamorphosed sandstone. Extremely hard, even harder than granite. Quartz grains fuse together."}},{"k":"uses_quartzite","v":{"s":"Railroad ballast, decorative stone"}},{"k":"desc_gneiss","v":{"s":"Shows distinct light and dark mineral banding. Forms under extreme heat and pressure deep in the crust."}},{"k":"uses_gneiss","v":{"s":"Decorative stone, construction"}},{"k":"desc_schist","v":{"s":"Medium-grade metamorphic rock with visible, aligned mica flakes that give it a sparkly, shiny appearance. Forms from shale under moderate heat and pressure. Named for its tendency to split (Greek \"schizein\" = to split)."}},{"k":"uses_schist","v":{"s":"Decorative landscaping, flagstone, historical millstones"}},{"k":"desc_phyllite","v":{"s":"Between slate and schist in metamorphic grade. Has a distinctive silky, satiny sheen from microscopic mica crystals. Crinkled foliation surface (crenulations). The stepping stone between low and medium metamorphism."}},{"k":"uses_phyllite","v":{"s":"Decorative stone, garden paths, grave markers"}},{"k":"sp_plate_scratched","v":{"s":"plate scratched — no powder"}},{"k":"sp_unglazed","v":{"s":"unglazed porcelain"}},{"k":"sp_looks_like","v":{"s":"looks like"}},{"k":"sp_streak","v":{"s":"streak"}},{"k":"fizz_no_gas","v":{"s":"no gas released"}},{"k":"weath_cap_minimal","v":{"s":"edges stay sharp"}},{"k":"weath_cap_phys","v":{"s":"ice wedges split it into angular blocks"}},{"k":"weath_cap_chem","v":{"s":"acid dissolves it — rounded and pitted"}},{"k":"weath_cap_mixed","v":{"s":"both signatures: cracks and rounding"}},{"k":"weath_scene_minimal","v":{"s":"A rock outcrop with sharp, intact edges under a clear sky. Stable conditions, so the surface persists."}},{"k":"weath_scene_physDom","v":{"s":"A rock outcrop split by straight ice-filled fractures, with angular blocks fallen as scree at its base. Freeze-thaw is breaking it apart mechanically."}},{"k":"weath_scene_chemDom","v":{"s":"A rock outcrop with a rounded, slumped profile, pitted surface and a solution hollow at the base, under acidic rain. Minerals are dissolving away."}},{"k":"weath_scene_mixed","v":{"s":"A rock outcrop showing both signatures at once: a fracture splitting it and rounded, pitted surfaces, with a little angular debris."}},{"k":"challenge_types_explored_name","v":{"s":"Petrologist"}},{"k":"challenge_types_explored_desc","v":{"s":"Examine all 3 rock types (Igneous, Sedimentary, Metamorphic)"}},{"k":"challenge_specimens_examined_name","v":{"s":"Rock Collector"}},{"k":"challenge_specimens_examined_desc","v":{"s":"Examine 5+ rock specimens"}},{"k":"challenge_quiz_ace_name","v":{"s":"Earth Science Ace"}},{"k":"challenge_quiz_ace_desc","v":{"s":"Correctly answer 3 questions in the quiz"}},{"k":"challenge_vocab_studied_name","v":{"s":"Vocabulary Master"}},{"k":"challenge_vocab_studied_desc","v":{"s":"Study 3 key terminology definitions"}},{"k":"challenge_wb_identify_name","v":{"s":"Field Mineralogist"}},{"k":"challenge_wb_identify_desc","v":{"s":"Identify 2 unknown specimens at the Mineral Workbench"}},{"k":"challenge_cycle_interact_name","v":{"s":"Cycle Creator"}},{"k":"challenge_cycle_interact_desc","v":{"s":"Perform 3 operations in the Rock Cycle simulator"}},{"k":"vocab_term_igneous","v":{"s":"Igneous"}},{"k":"vocab_def_igneous","v":{"s":"Rock formed from the cooling and solidification of molten magma or lava."}},{"k":"vocab_term_sedimentary","v":{"s":"Sedimentary"}},{"k":"vocab_def_sedimentary","v":{"s":"Rock formed by the accumulation, compaction, and cementation of mineral and organic particles over time."}},{"k":"vocab_term_metamorphic","v":{"s":"Metamorphic"}},{"k":"vocab_def_metamorphic","v":{"s":"Rock formed when pre-existing rocks are altered by intense heat and pressure without melting."}},{"k":"vocab_term_lithification","v":{"s":"Lithification"}},{"k":"vocab_def_lithification","v":{"s":"The process of turning loose sediment into solid rock through compaction and cementation."}},{"k":"vocab_term_foliation","v":{"s":"Foliation"}},{"k":"vocab_def_foliation","v":{"s":"The layered or banded texture in metamorphic rocks caused by the alignment of minerals under heat and pressure."}},{"k":"vocab_term_piezoelectric","v":{"s":"Piezoelectric"}},{"k":"vocab_def_piezoelectric","v":{"s":"The property of certain materials (like quartz) to generate an electrical charge when mechanically squeezed."}},{"k":"vocab_term_evaporite","v":{"s":"Evaporite"}},{"k":"vocab_def_evaporite","v":{"s":"A chemical sedimentary rock formed by the precipitation of minerals as water evaporates from a shallow basin."}},{"k":"vocab_term_crystallization","v":{"s":"Crystallization"}},{"k":"vocab_def_crystallization","v":{"s":"The process by which atoms arrange into a highly structured crystal lattice as magma cools."}},{"k":"vocab_term_hardness","v":{"s":"Hardness"}},{"k":"vocab_def_hardness","v":{"s":"A measure of a mineral's resistance to scratching, rated from 1 to 10 on the Mohs scale."}},{"k":"vocab_term_streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"vocab_def_streak","v":{"s":"The color of a mineral in powdered form, tested by rubbing it across an unglazed porcelain plate."}},{"k":"vocab_term_luster","v":{"s":"Luster"}},{"k":"vocab_def_luster","v":{"s":"The way light reflects off a mineral's surface (metallic, vitreous, pearly, earthy, etc.)."}},{"k":"sr_challenges_updated","v":{"s":"Challenges updated. You have completed "}},{"k":"sr_challenges_of","v":{"s":" of "}},{"k":"sr_challenges_tail","v":{"s":" challenges. Research points: "}},{"k":"sr_drill_new","v":{"s":"New specimen shown. "}},{"k":"sr_drill_choose","v":{"s":". Choose its name from four options."}},{"k":"sr_mystery_ready","v":{"s":"Mystery rock ready. First clue revealed."}},{"k":"sr_clue","v":{"s":"Clue "}},{"k":"sr_clue_of","v":{"s":" of "}},{"k":"sr_mystery_correct","v":{"s":"Correct! The mystery rock was "}},{"k":"sr_mystery_wrong","v":{"s":"Not quite. Next clue revealed."}},{"k":"sr_mystery_reveal","v":{"s":"Answer revealed: "}},{"k":"texture_gloss_coarse-grained","v":{"s":"coarse interlocking crystals you can see without a lens"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_fine-grained","v":{"s":"grains too fine to tell apart by eye"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_glassy","v":{"s":"smooth volcanic glass with curved, shell-like fracture"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_vesicular","v":{"s":"full of frozen gas bubbles"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_clastic","v":{"s":"cemented sand grains in visible beds"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_clastic-coarse","v":{"s":"rounded pebbles set in a finer matrix"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_clastic-angular","v":{"s":"sharp-cornered fragments set in a finer matrix"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_bioclastic","v":{"s":"packed with shell and fossil fragments"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_clastic-fine","v":{"s":"grains too fine to pick out by eye, and no splitting layers"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_organic-banded","v":{"s":"dull black rock banded with brighter, glassier layers"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_fine-layered","v":{"s":"very thin flat layers that split apart"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_crystalline","v":{"s":"tightly interlocking sugary crystals"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_foliated","v":{"s":"flat minerals all lined up in wavy layers"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_non-foliated","v":{"s":"interlocking crystals with no layering"}},{"k":"texture_gloss_banded","v":{"s":"alternating light and dark mineral bands"}},{"k":"motion_pause","v":{"s":"Pause motion"}},{"k":"motion_resume","v":{"s":"Resume motion"}},{"k":"motion_paused_sr","v":{"s":"Scene motion paused. The cross-section is still fully readable."}},{"k":"motion_resumed_sr","v":{"s":"Scene motion resumed."}},{"k":"wb_pool_candidates_sr","v":{"s":"candidates."}},{"k":"wb_impasse_title","v":{"s":"No candidate fits every observation."}},{"k":"wb_impasse_hardness","v":{"s":"Two scratch results cannot both be true, so the hardness bracket has closed on nothing. Clear the hardness trials and bracket again from a fresh reference."}},{"k":"wb_impasse_body","v":{"s":"The specimen on the bench IS one of these minerals, so one record must not match what the bench actually showed. Look again at a single property; you do not have to start over."}},{"k":"wb_impasse_clear_hardness","v":{"s":"Clear hardness trials and retest"}},{"k":"wb_impasse_look","v":{"s":"Look again: "}},{"k":"rock_hardness_label","v":{"s":"Scratch resistance (approx.)"}},{"k":"rock_hardness_hint","v":{"s":"Mohs is defined for MINERALS. A rock is a mixture, so the figure depends on which mineral the point lands on and on how well the grains are cemented."}},{"k":"rock_hardness_scale","v":{"s":"Roughly where that sits on the Mohs scale"}},{"k":"malachite","v":{"s":"Malachite"}},{"k":"azurite","v":{"s":"Azurite"}},{"k":"wb_streak_choice_powder-green","v":{"s":"Green"}},{"k":"wb_streak_choice_powder-pale-blue","v":{"s":"Pale blue"}},{"k":"biotite","v":{"s":"Biotite (Dark Mica)"}},{"k":"mohs_scale_title","v":{"s":"The Mohs scale, mineral by mineral"}},{"k":"mohs_scale_body","v":{"s":"Every step of the scale is a real mineral, from talc at 1 to diamond at 10, and all ten are in this catalogue. Open a step to see what defines it. The steps are a ranking, not a measurement: diamond is far harder than corundum, not one step harder."}},{"k":"mohs_scale_aria","v":{"s":"Mohs index minerals from 1 to 10"}},{"k":"coal","v":{"s":"Coal"}},{"k":"siltstone","v":{"s":"Siltstone"}},{"k":"graphite","v":{"s":"Graphite"}},{"k":"gabbro","v":{"s":"Gabbro"}},{"k":"breccia","v":{"s":"Breccia"}},{"k":"apatite","v":{"s":"Apatite"}},{"k":"ls_not_to_scale","v":{"s":"Vertical scale is exaggerated: the cross-section is schematic, not to scale."}},{"k":"wb_eff_title","v":{"s":"Experimental design review"}},{"k":"wb_eff_used","v":{"s":"Tests you ran: "}},{"k":"wb_eff_which","v":{"s":"That path: "}},{"k":"wb_eff_min","v":{"s":"Shortest sufficient path, visible only in hindsight: "}},{"k":"wb_eff_join","v":{"s":", "}},{"k":"wb_eff_lean","v":{"s":"You matched it while the answer was still open, which is what efficient experimental design looks like."}},{"k":"wb_eff_longer","v":{"s":"Nobody could have known it in advance, so the extra tests were not wasted. Independent properties that agree make an identification much harder to overturn."}},{"k":"igneous","v":{"s":"Igneous"}},{"k":"formed_from_cooled_magma_or","v":{"s":"Formed from cooled magma or lava"}},{"k":"sedimentary","v":{"s":"Sedimentary"}},{"k":"formed_from_compressed_layers_of","v":{"s":"Formed from compressed layers of sediment"}},{"k":"metamorphic","v":{"s":"Metamorphic"}},{"k":"formed_when_existing_rocks_change","v":{"s":"Formed when existing rocks change under heat & pressure"}},{"k":"granite","v":{"s":"Granite"}},{"k":"basalt","v":{"s":"Basalt"}},{"k":"obsidian","v":{"s":"Obsidian"}},{"k":"pumice","v":{"s":"Pumice"}},{"k":"rhyolite","v":{"s":"Rhyolite"}},{"k":"diorite","v":{"s":"Diorite"}},{"k":"intrusive_igneous_rock_with_a","v":{"s":"Intrusive igneous rock with a "}},{"k":"andesite","v":{"s":"Andesite"}},{"k":"tuff","v":{"s":"Tuff"}},{"k":"sandstone","v":{"s":"Sandstone"}},{"k":"limestone","v":{"s":"Limestone"}},{"k":"shale","v":{"s":"Shale"}},{"k":"conglomerate","v":{"s":"Conglomerate"}},{"k":"chalk","v":{"s":"Chalk"}},{"k":"travertine","v":{"s":"Travertine"}},{"k":"marble","v":{"s":"Marble"}},{"k":"slate","v":{"s":"Slate"}},{"k":"quartzite","v":{"s":"Quartzite"}},{"k":"gneiss","v":{"s":"Gneiss"}},{"k":"schist","v":{"s":"Schist"}},{"k":"phyllite","v":{"s":"Phyllite"}},{"k":"quartz","v":{"s":"Quartz"}},{"k":"feldspar","v":{"s":"Feldspar"}},{"k":"mica_muscovite","v":{"s":"Mica (Muscovite)"}},{"k":"calcite","v":{"s":"Calcite"}},{"k":"halite","v":{"s":"Halite"}},{"k":"sulfur","v":{"s":"Sulfur"}},{"k":"pyrite","v":{"s":"Pyrite"}},{"k":"talc","v":{"s":"Talc"}},{"k":"diamond","v":{"s":"Diamond"}},{"k":"magnetite","v":{"s":"Magnetite"}},{"k":"hematite","v":{"s":"Hematite"}},{"k":"the_most_important_iron_ore","v":{"s":"The most important iron ore mineral. Name from Greek "}},{"k":"garnet","v":{"s":"Garnet"}},{"k":"olivine","v":{"s":"Olivine"}},{"k":"fluorite","v":{"s":"Fluorite"}},{"k":"known_as_the","v":{"s":"Known as the "}},{"k":"galena","v":{"s":"Galena"}},{"k":"gypsum","v":{"s":"Gypsum"}},{"k":"corundum","v":{"s":"Corundum"}},{"k":"topaz","v":{"s":"Topaz"}},{"k":"hardness_mohs","v":{"s":"Hardness (Mohs)"}},{"k":"texture","v":{"s":"Texture"}},{"k":"uses","v":{"s":"Uses"}},{"k":"hardness","v":{"s":"Hardness"}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"luster","v":{"s":"Luster"}},{"k":"rocks","v":{"s":"Rocks"}},{"k":"ai_connection_error","v":{"s":"Connection error. Please try again."}},{"k":"ai_offline_no_key","v":{"s":"AI Petrologist is currently offline. Key not configured."}},{"k":"ai_no_response","v":{"s":"I could not generate a response. Try again!"}},{"k":"which_rock_type_forms_from_cooled_magma","v":{"s":"Which rock type forms from cooled magma?"}},{"k":"correct_igneous_rocks_solidify_from_molten_magma","v":{"s":"Correct! Igneous rocks solidify from molten magma."}},{"k":"incorrect_sedimentary_rocks_form_from_compressed_layers_of","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sedimentary rocks form from compressed layers of sediment."}},{"k":"incorrect_metamorphic_rocks_form_from_existing_rocks_changed","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Metamorphic rocks form from existing rocks changed by heat and pressure."}},{"k":"incorrect_organic_materials_form_coal_but_not_directly","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Organic materials form coal but not directly from cooled magma."}},{"k":"what_process_turns_sediment_into_sedimentary_rock","v":{"s":"What process turns sediment into sedimentary rock?"}},{"k":"correct_compaction_and_cementation_bind_sediment_into_rock","v":{"s":"Correct! Compaction and cementation bind sediment into rock."}},{"k":"incorrect_melting_produces_magma_leading_to_igneous_rocks","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Melting produces magma, leading to igneous rocks."}},{"k":"incorrect_cooling_solidifies_magma_into_igneous_rocks","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Cooling solidifies magma into igneous rocks."}},{"k":"incorrect_erosion_breaks_rocks_down_rather_than_building","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Erosion breaks rocks down rather than building them."}},{"k":"marble_is_a_metamorphic_form_of_which_rock","v":{"s":"Marble is a metamorphic form of which rock?"}},{"k":"correct_limestone_transforms_into_marble_under_heat_and","v":{"s":"Correct! Limestone transforms into marble under heat and pressure."}},{"k":"incorrect_sandstone_metamorphoses_into_quartzite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sandstone metamorphoses into quartzite."}},{"k":"incorrect_granite_is_igneous_and_can_metamorphose_into","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite is igneous and can metamorphose into gneiss."}},{"k":"incorrect_basalt_is_volcanic_igneous_and_does_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt is volcanic igneous and does not form marble."}},{"k":"which_mineral_is_the_hardest_on_the_mohs","v":{"s":"Which mineral is the hardest on the Mohs scale?"}},{"k":"correct_diamond_is_rated_at_10_on_the","v":{"s":"Correct! Diamond is rated at 10 on the Mohs hardness scale."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartz_is_hard_7_but_not_the","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz is hard (7) but not the hardest."}},{"k":"incorrect_corundum_is_very_hard_9_but_softer","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Corundum is very hard (9) but softer than diamond."}},{"k":"incorrect_topaz_is_hard_8_but_softer_than","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Topaz is hard (8) but softer than corundum and diamond."}},{"k":"what_is_the_softest_mineral","v":{"s":"What is the softest mineral?"}},{"k":"correct_talc_is_rated_at_1_on_the","v":{"s":"Correct! Talc is rated at 1 on the Mohs hardness scale."}},{"k":"incorrect_gypsum_is_rated_at_2_which_is","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Gypsum is rated at 2, which is harder than talc."}},{"k":"incorrect_calcite_is_rated_at_3_which_is","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Calcite is rated at 3, which is harder than talc and gypsum."}},{"k":"incorrect_halite_is_table_salt_rated_at_2","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Halite is table salt, rated at 2.5, harder than talc."}},{"k":"obsidian_forms_when_lava_cools","v":{"s":"Obsidian forms when lava cools..."}},{"k":"correct_obsidian_is_volcanic_glass_formed_by_extremely","v":{"s":"Correct! Obsidian is volcanic glass formed by extremely rapid cooling."}},{"k":"incorrect_slow_cooling_underground_produces_large_coarse_grains","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Slow cooling underground produces large coarse grains."}},{"k":"incorrect_intrusion_underground_is_slow_while_obsidian_is","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Intrusion underground is slow, while obsidian is volcanic."}},{"k":"incorrect_underwater_cooling_can_form_pillow_basalt_but","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Underwater cooling can form pillow basalt, but rapid air/surface cooling forms obsidian."}},{"k":"which_rock_can_float_on_water","v":{"s":"Which rock can float on water?"}},{"k":"correct_pumice_is_filled_with_gas_pockets_vesicles","v":{"s":"Correct! Pumice is filled with gas pockets (vesicles) and is less dense than water."}},{"k":"incorrect_basalt_is_dense_and_will_sink","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt is dense and will sink."}},{"k":"incorrect_marble_is_dense_metamorphic_rock_and_will","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Marble is dense metamorphic rock and will sink."}},{"k":"incorrect_granite_is_dense_intrusive_igneous_rock_and","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite is dense intrusive igneous rock and will sink."}},{"k":"what_type_of_rock_is_shale","v":{"s":"What type of rock is shale?"}},{"k":"correct_shale_is_a_fine_grained_clastic_sedimentary","v":{"s":"Correct! Shale is a fine-grained clastic sedimentary rock made of mud and clay."}},{"k":"incorrect_igneous_rocks_form_from_cooled_magma_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Igneous rocks form from cooled magma, not mud deposits."}},{"k":"incorrect_metamorphic_rocks_form_under_heat_and_pressure","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Metamorphic rocks form under heat and pressure."}},{"k":"incorrect_shale_is_a_rock_composed_of_minerals","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Shale is a rock composed of minerals, not a single mineral."}},{"k":"pyrite_is_also_known_as","v":{"s":"Pyrite is also known as..."}},{"k":"correct_pyrite_has_a_golden_metallic_luster_that","v":{"s":"Correct! Pyrite has a golden metallic luster that resembles gold."}},{"k":"incorrect_white_gold_is_a_real_gold_alloy","v":{"s":"Incorrect. White gold is a real gold alloy."}},{"k":"incorrect_rose_gold_is_gold_mixed_with_copper","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Rose gold is gold mixed with copper."}},{"k":"incorrect_black_gold_refers_to_crude_oil","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Black gold refers to crude oil."}},{"k":"which_rock_shows_distinct_banding","v":{"s":"Which rock shows distinct banding?"}},{"k":"correct_gneiss_displays_prominent_mineral_banding_from_intense","v":{"s":"Correct! Gneiss displays prominent mineral banding from intense metamorphic heat and pressure."}},{"k":"incorrect_granite_is_massive_and_does_not_show","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite is massive and does not show metamorphic banding."}},{"k":"incorrect_basalt_is_fine_grained_volcanic_rock_without","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt is fine-grained volcanic rock without layers."}},{"k":"incorrect_slate_is_foliated_but_splits_into_thin","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Slate is foliated but splits into thin sheets instead of displaying thick bands."}},{"k":"limestone_fizzes_when_you_add","v":{"s":"Limestone fizzes when you add..."}},{"k":"correct_hydrochloric_acid_reacts_with_calcium_carbonate_in","v":{"s":"Correct! Hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate in limestone to release CO2 gas."}},{"k":"incorrect_water_does_not_react_chemically_to_cause","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Water does not react chemically to cause limestone to fizz."}},{"k":"incorrect_salt_does_not_react_with_carbonates","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Salt does not react with carbonates."}},{"k":"incorrect_oil_does_not_react_with_carbonates","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Oil does not react with carbonates."}},{"k":"quartzite_is_metamorphosed","v":{"s":"Quartzite is metamorphosed..."}},{"k":"correct_sandstone_fuses_under_heat_and_pressure_into","v":{"s":"Correct! Sandstone fuses under heat and pressure into quartzite."}},{"k":"incorrect_limestone_metamorphoses_into_marble","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Limestone metamorphoses into marble."}},{"k":"incorrect_shale_metamorphoses_into_slate","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Shale metamorphoses into slate."}},{"k":"incorrect_granite_is_igneous_and_does_not_form","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite is igneous and does not form quartzite."}},{"k":"rhyolite_is_the_extrusive_equivalent_of","v":{"s":"Rhyolite is the extrusive equivalent of..."}},{"k":"correct_both_granite_and_rhyolite_are_high_silica","v":{"s":"Correct! Both granite and rhyolite are high-silica rocks; granite is intrusive, rhyolite is extrusive."}},{"k":"incorrect_basalt_is_extrusive_and_equivalent_to_intrusive","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt is extrusive and equivalent to intrusive gabbro."}},{"k":"incorrect_gabbro_is_intrusive_and_equivalent_to_extrusive","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Gabbro is intrusive and equivalent to extrusive basalt."}},{"k":"incorrect_diorite_is_intrusive_and_equivalent_to_extrusive","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Diorite is intrusive and equivalent to extrusive andesite."}},{"k":"which_mineral_is_naturally_magnetic","v":{"s":"Which mineral is naturally magnetic?"}},{"k":"correct_magnetite_is_a_magnetic_iron_oxide_mineral","v":{"s":"Correct! Magnetite is a magnetic iron oxide mineral."}},{"k":"incorrect_hematite_contains_iron_but_is_not_strongly","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Hematite contains iron but is not strongly magnetic."}},{"k":"incorrect_pyrite_is_an_iron_sulfide_and_is","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Pyrite is an iron sulfide and is not magnetic."}},{"k":"incorrect_galena_is_lead_sulfide_and_is_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Galena is lead sulfide and is not magnetic."}},{"k":"ruby_and_sapphire_are_both_varieties_of","v":{"s":"Ruby and sapphire are both varieties of..."}},{"k":"correct_rubies_and_sapphires_are_gemstone_forms_of","v":{"s":"Correct! Rubies and sapphires are gemstone forms of corundum (hardness 9)."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartz_forms_amethyst_and_citrine_not_ruby","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz forms amethyst and citrine, not ruby."}},{"k":"incorrect_diamond_is_pure_carbon","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Diamond is pure carbon."}},{"k":"incorrect_topaz_is_silicate_and_has_a_different","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Topaz is silicate and has a different composition."}},{"k":"what_gives_mars_its_red_color","v":{"s":"Which iron-oxide mineral has been identified on Mars?"}},{"k":"correct_hematite_dust_covers_the_martian_surface_giving","v":{"s":"Correct! Hematite is one iron-oxide mineral identified on Mars; the planet’s overall red color comes from oxidized iron minerals."}},{"k":"incorrect_general_rust_is_iron_oxide_but_hematite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Pyrite is an iron sulfide, not the iron oxide named in this question."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_sand_is_red_due_to_hematite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Galena is lead sulfide, not an iron oxide."}},{"k":"incorrect_volcanic_dust_on_mars_is_not_the","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Halite is sodium chloride, not an iron oxide."}},{"k":"the_word_fluorescence_comes_from_which_mineral","v":{"s":"The word \"fluorescence\" comes from which mineral?"}},{"k":"correct_george_stokes_named_fluorescence_after_studying_fluorite","v":{"s":"Correct! George Stokes named fluorescence after studying fluorite under ultraviolet light."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartz_does_not_commonly_show_fluorescence","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz does not commonly show fluorescence."}},{"k":"incorrect_diamond_can_fluoresce_but_was_not_the","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Diamond can fluoresce but was not the origin of the term."}},{"k":"incorrect_calcite_can_fluoresce_but_was_not_the","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Calcite can fluoresce but was not the origin of the term."}},{"k":"chalk_is_made_of_tiny_shells_from","v":{"s":"Chalk is made of tiny shells from..."}},{"k":"correct_chalk_is_composed_of_tiny_coccolith_shells","v":{"s":"Correct! Chalk is composed of tiny coccolith shells from microscopic marine plankton."}},{"k":"incorrect_snail_shells_are_too_large_and_form","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Snail shells are too large and form coquina."}},{"k":"incorrect_clam_shells_form_coquina_or_fossiliferous_limestone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Clam shells form coquina or fossiliferous limestone."}},{"k":"incorrect_coral_reefs_form_reef_limestone_not_chalk","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Coral reefs form reef limestone, not chalk."}},{"k":"diorite_has_what_distinctive_appearance","v":{"s":"Diorite has what distinctive appearance?"}},{"k":"correct_diorite_is_intrusive_with_a_speckled_salt","v":{"s":"Correct! Diorite is intrusive with a speckled salt-and-pepper look from light plagioclase and dark hornblende."}},{"k":"incorrect_basalt_is_solid_black","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt is solid black."}},{"k":"incorrect_gneiss_is_striped","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Gneiss is striped."}},{"k":"incorrect_obsidian_is_glassy","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Obsidian is glassy."}},{"k":"which_mineral_was_used_in_early_crystal_radios","v":{"s":"Which mineral was used in early crystal radios?"}},{"k":"correct_galena_was_used_as_a_point_contact","v":{"s":"Correct! Galena was used as a point-contact semiconductor crystal in early radios."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartz_is_used_for_oscillation_not_crystal","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz is used for oscillation, not crystal detection."}},{"k":"incorrect_diamond_is_not_a_suitable_semiconductor_for","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Diamond is not a suitable semiconductor for crystal radios."}},{"k":"incorrect_pyrite_was_not_the_standard_crystal_for","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Pyrite was not the standard crystal for early radios."}},{"k":"the_green_beaches_of_hawaii_are_made_of","v":{"s":"The green beaches of Hawaii are made of..."}},{"k":"correct_olivine_crystals_eroded_from_basaltic_lava_create","v":{"s":"Correct! Olivine crystals eroded from basaltic lava create green sand beaches."}},{"k":"incorrect_emerald_is_a_rare_beryl_mineral_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Emerald is a rare beryl mineral, not found in beach sand."}},{"k":"incorrect_jade_is_metamorphic_and_does_not_form","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Jade is metamorphic and does not form Hawaii beaches."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_sand_is_natural_olivine_not_man","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The sand is natural olivine, not man-made green glass."}},{"k":"which_building_was_made_from_travertine","v":{"s":"Which building was made from travertine?"}},{"k":"correct_the_colosseum_in_rome_was_constructed_largely","v":{"s":"Correct! The Colosseum in Rome was constructed largely of travertine limestone."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_pyramids_are_made_of_standard_limestone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The Pyramids are made of standard limestone and granite."}},{"k":"incorrect_stonehenge_is_made_of_sarsen_stones_and","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Stonehenge is made of sarsen stones and bluestones."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_taj_mahal_is_made_of_marble","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The Taj Mahal is made of marble."}},{"k":"schist_gets_its_sparkly_appearance_from","v":{"s":"Schist gets its sparkly appearance from..."}},{"k":"correct_aligned_muscovite_and_biotite_mica_flakes_reflect","v":{"s":"Correct! Aligned muscovite and biotite mica flakes reflect light, making schist sparkle."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartz_crystals_are_glassy_but_do_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartz crystals are glassy but do not cause the characteristic schist sheen."}},{"k":"incorrect_schist_does_not_contain_gold_inclusions_as","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Schist does not contain gold inclusions as a rule."}},{"k":"incorrect_diamond_dust_is_not_present_in_schist","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Diamond dust is not present in schist."}},{"k":"what_makes_quartz_watches_accurate","v":{"s":"What makes quartz watches accurate?"}},{"k":"correct_squeezing_quartz_generates_an_electric_charge_causing","v":{"s":"Correct! Squeezing quartz generates an electric charge, causing precise vibrations."}},{"k":"incorrect_magnetic_fields_do_not_drive_quartz_oscillations","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Magnetic fields do not drive quartz oscillations directly."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_battery_power_is_just_the_source","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The battery power is just the source, but the quartz crystal regulation provides the accuracy."}},{"k":"incorrect_density_is_not_related_to_timekeeping_accuracy","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Density is not related to timekeeping accuracy."}},{"k":"where_are_the_largest_crystals_ever_found","v":{"s":"Where are the largest crystals ever found?"}},{"k":"correct_gypsum_crystals_up_to_12_meters_long","v":{"s":"Correct! Gypsum crystals up to 12 meters long grow in the extreme heat of the Naica Mine."}},{"k":"incorrect_mount_everest_does_not_host_giant_caves","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Mount Everest does not host giant caves of giant crystals."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_grand_canyon_features_stratified_sedimentary_rocks","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The Grand Canyon features stratified sedimentary rocks."}},{"k":"incorrect_the_sahara_desert_is_sand_covered_rather","v":{"s":"Incorrect. The Sahara Desert is sand-covered rather than hosting giant gypsum crystal caves."}},{"k":"the_word_salary_comes_from_the_latin_word","v":{"s":"What was the Latin salarium associated with?"}},{"k":"correct_roman_soldiers_were_sometimes_paid_in_salt","v":{"s":"Correct! Latin salarium was an allowance associated with salt—not evidence that Roman soldiers were literally paid in salt."}},{"k":"incorrect_silver_was_money_but_not_the_root","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Silver was money but not the root of salary."}},{"k":"incorrect_gold_was_money_but_not_the_root","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Gold was money but not the root of salary."}},{"k":"incorrect_stone_was_not_the_root_of_salary","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Stone was not the root of salary."}},{"k":"andesite_is_named_after","v":{"s":"Andesite is named after..."}},{"k":"correct_andesite_is_volcanic_rock_typical_of_the","v":{"s":"Correct! Andesite is volcanic rock typical of the Andes subduction zone."}},{"k":"incorrect_it_was_named_after_the_location_rather","v":{"s":"Incorrect. It was named after the location rather than the people."}},{"k":"incorrect_there_is_no_scientist_named_ande","v":{"s":"Incorrect. There is no scientist named Ande."}},{"k":"incorrect_it_was_named_after_the_mountain_range","v":{"s":"Incorrect. It was named after the mountain range."}},{"k":"tuff_is_made_from_consolidated","v":{"s":"Tuff is made from consolidated..."}},{"k":"correct_tuff_is_igneous_rock_composed_of_compacted","v":{"s":"Correct! Tuff is igneous rock composed of compacted volcanic ash."}},{"k":"incorrect_river_sand_forms_sandstone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. River sand forms sandstone."}},{"k":"incorrect_coral_reefs_form_limestone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Coral reefs form limestone."}},{"k":"incorrect_glacier_ice_forms_glacial_till","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Glacier ice forms glacial till."}},{"k":"which_metamorphic_rock_comes_between_slate_and_schist","v":{"s":"Which metamorphic rock comes between slate and schist?"}},{"k":"correct_phyllite_represents_low_to_medium_grade_metamorphism","v":{"s":"Correct! Phyllite represents low-to-medium grade metamorphism, between slate and schist."}},{"k":"incorrect_marble_is_non_foliated_and_forms_from","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Marble is non-foliated and forms from limestone."}},{"k":"incorrect_gneiss_is_high_grade_metamorphism_occurring_after","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Gneiss is high-grade metamorphism, occurring after schist."}},{"k":"incorrect_quartzite_is_non_foliated_metamorphosed_sandstone","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Quartzite is non-foliated metamorphosed sandstone."}},{"k":"garnet_crystals_commonly_have_how_many_sides","v":{"s":"Garnet crystals commonly have how many sides?"}},{"k":"correct_garnet_crystals_typically_grow_into_12_sided","v":{"s":"Correct! Garnet crystals typically grow into 12-sided dodecahedrons."}},{"k":"incorrect_tetrahedrons_have_4_sides_not_characteristic_of","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Tetrahedrons have 4 sides, not characteristic of garnet."}},{"k":"incorrect_cubic_crystals_6_sides_are_typical_of","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Cubic crystals (6 sides) are typical of halite or pyrite."}},{"k":"incorrect_octahedral_crystals_8_sides_are_typical_of","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Octahedral crystals (8 sides) are typical of fluorite or diamond."}},{"k":"rock_cycle_landscape_aria","v":{"s":"Rock cycle landscape. Press 1 for volcano igneous rocks, 2 for river delta sedimentary rocks, 3 for mountain core metamorphic rocks."}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"rocks_minerals_explorer","v":{"s":"Rocks & Minerals Explorer"}},{"k":"open_3d_voxel_cross_section","v":{"s":"Open the 3D voxel cross-section of the crust"}},{"k":"open_geology_explorer_3d","v":{"s":"Open Geology Explorer — 3D voxel cross-section"}},{"k":"explore_in_3d","v":{"s":"Explore in 3D"}},{"k":"mode_landscape","v":{"s":"Landscape"}},{"k":"mode_rocks","v":{"s":"Rocks"}},{"k":"mode_minerals","v":{"s":"Minerals"}},{"k":"mystery_rock","v":{"s":"Mystery Rock"}},{"k":"weathering_lab","v":{"s":"Weathering Lab"}},{"k":"mode_quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"switch_to_prefix","v":{"s":"Switch to "}},{"k":"mode_suffix","v":{"s":" mode"}},{"k":"mystery","v":{"s":"Mystery"}},{"k":"weathering","v":{"s":"Weathering"}},{"k":"challenges","v":{"s":"challenges"}},{"k":"hero_landscape_title","v":{"s":"Landscape, the geology you can SEE"}},{"k":"hero_landscape_hint","v":{"s":"Volcano, river delta, mountain face. Surface features tell you what’s underneath. Plate tectonics + erosion + time = every landscape. The Hawaiian volcanoes are 30+ million years old; Mt. Etna is 500K."}},{"k":"hero_rocks_title","v":{"s":"Rocks: igneous, sedimentary, metamorphic"}},{"k":"hero_rocks_hint","v":{"s":"Igneous (cooled magma: granite, basalt), sedimentary (compressed layers: sandstone, limestone), metamorphic (heat + pressure: marble, slate). The rock cycle moves stones between all three over millions of years."}},{"k":"hero_minerals_title","v":{"s":"Minerals, the building blocks of rocks"}},{"k":"hero_minerals_hint","v":{"s":"Mohs scale 1-10: talc soft, diamond hardest. Streak, luster, cleavage, hardness, color = the 5 ID tests. Quartz is 12% of Earth’s crust; you carry it in every grain of sand."}},{"k":"hero_mystery_title","v":{"s":"Mystery Rock, detective ID"}},{"k":"hero_mystery_hint","v":{"s":"Real geology workflow: observe (color, crystals, layers), test (hardness, streak, fizz with HCl for carbonate), classify. The fizz test alone separates limestone from a pile of look-alikes."}},{"k":"hero_quiz_title","v":{"s":"Quiz, graded ID + classification"}},{"k":"hero_quiz_hint","v":{"s":"NGSS MS-ESS2-1: rock cycle as material system. AP ES practice: matching rocks to environment of formation. Builds the visual library so you can ID a rock at the Grand Canyon by sight."}},{"k":"hero_weath_title","v":{"s":"Weathering, how rock comes apart"}},{"k":"hero_weath_hint","v":{"s":"Physical weathering breaks rock without changing it — ice wedging prises blocks apart and leaves ANGULAR debris. Chemical weathering dissolves minerals instead, rounding and pitting the surface. Climate decides which one wins."}},{"k":"landscape_click_zones_hint","v":{"s":"Click landscape zones to explore rock types. Hover to see labels. Keyboard: Tab to canvas, then 1=Volcano, 2=River, 3=Mountain."}},{"k":"rock_cycle_diagram_aria","v":{"s":"Rock cycle diagram — click a rock type or process to explore how rocks transform."}},{"k":"the_rock_cycle","v":{"s":"The Rock Cycle"}},{"k":"rock_cycle_explanation","v":{"s":"Rocks continuously transform: Igneous rocks weather into sediment → Sediment compacts into Sedimentary rocks → Heat & pressure create Metamorphic rocks → Melting creates magma → Cooling forms new Igneous rocks. The cycle never stops!"}},{"k":"filter_all","v":{"s":"All"}},{"k":"visual_id_region","v":{"s":"Visual rock identification drill"}},{"k":"visual_id_title","v":{"s":"Visual ID drill"}},{"k":"visual_id_correct","v":{"s":"correct"}},{"k":"visual_id_intro","v":{"s":"No clues, no AI needed — just the specimen. Name it from what you can see. Wrong options are usually the same rock type, so look at texture and grain size, not colour alone."}},{"k":"visual_id_start","v":{"s":"Start drill"}},{"k":"visual_id_prompt","v":{"s":"Which specimen is this?"}},{"k":"visual_id_right","v":{"s":"Correct — "}},{"k":"visual_id_wrong","v":{"s":"It was "}},{"k":"visual_id_next","v":{"s":"Next specimen"}},{"k":"rock_texture_close_up","v":{"s":"Rock texture close-up"}},{"k":"mohs_hardness_scale","v":{"s":"Mohs Hardness Scale"}},{"k":"mohs_min_talc","v":{"s":"1 (Talc)"}},{"k":"mohs_max_diamond","v":{"s":"10 (Diamond)"}},{"k":"thin_title","v":{"s":"Thin section — polarizing microscope"}},{"k":"thin_intro","v":{"s":"A slice of the rock ground to 30 micrometres — thin enough for light to pass through. This is how the minerals in a rock are actually identified."}},{"k":"thin_mode_aria","v":{"s":"Illumination mode"}},{"k":"thin_ppl","v":{"s":"Plane light"}},{"k":"thin_xpl","v":{"s":"Crossed polars"}},{"k":"thin_stage","v":{"s":"Stage angle: "}},{"k":"thin_stage_aria","v":{"s":"Microscope stage angle in degrees; use crossed polars to observe extinction"}},{"k":"thin_extinction","v":{"s":"Why rotate? Under crossed polars, turn the stage and watch individual grains darken and brighten. A crystal reaches extinction when it turns black. Anything that stays black at every angle is isotropic or opaque."}},{"k":"thin_ppl_hint","v":{"s":"Plane light shows natural colour, relief and cleavage. In this model, stage rotation is used to investigate extinction: choose Crossed polars, then drag from 0° to 90° and watch different grains turn black."}},{"k":"thin_what_you_see","v":{"s":"What you're looking at: "}},{"k":"thin_assemblage","v":{"s":"Minerals in this section"}},{"k":"speed_slow_label","v":{"s":"Slow (Intrusive)"}},{"k":"speed_slow_desc","v":{"s":"Magma loses heat slowly at depth, so a few crystals have time to grow large."}},{"k":"speed_slow_where","v":{"s":"Deep underground"}},{"k":"speed_slow_atoms","v":{"s":"Lots of time to migrate and join an ordered crystal lattice"}},{"k":"speed_slow_texture","v":{"s":"Coarse-grained; large crystals visible to the eye"}},{"k":"speed_slow_example","v":{"s":"Granite or gabbro"}},{"k":"speed_slow_visual","v":{"s":"Few large crystals"}},{"k":"speed_medium_label","v":{"s":"Moderate"}},{"k":"speed_medium_desc","v":{"s":"A shallow intrusion cools faster, leaving less growth time and medium-sized crystals."}},{"k":"speed_medium_where","v":{"s":"Shallow underground intrusion"}},{"k":"speed_medium_atoms","v":{"s":"Some time to organize, but crystal growth stops sooner"}},{"k":"speed_medium_texture","v":{"s":"Medium-grained; crystals are smaller but still distinct"}},{"k":"speed_medium_example","v":{"s":"Diabase (dolerite)"}},{"k":"speed_medium_visual","v":{"s":"More medium crystals"}},{"k":"speed_fast_label","v":{"s":"Fast (Extrusive)"}},{"k":"speed_fast_desc","v":{"s":"Lava cools quickly at the surface, producing many crystals too small to see easily."}},{"k":"speed_fast_where","v":{"s":"At or near Earth's surface"}},{"k":"speed_fast_atoms","v":{"s":"Little time to move before the melt becomes solid"}},{"k":"speed_fast_texture","v":{"s":"Fine-grained; microscopic crystals"}},{"k":"speed_fast_example","v":{"s":"Basalt or rhyolite"}},{"k":"speed_fast_visual","v":{"s":"Many tiny crystals"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_label","v":{"s":"Quenched (Glassy)"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_desc","v":{"s":"Quenching locks the disordered melt in place before a crystal lattice can form."}},{"k":"speed_rapid_where","v":{"s":"A chilled lava margin or contact with water"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_atoms","v":{"s":"Almost no time to organize into repeating structures"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_texture","v":{"s":"Glassy; no mineral crystals"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_example","v":{"s":"Obsidian or volcanic glass"}},{"k":"speed_rapid_visual","v":{"s":"Glass — no crystals"}},{"k":"magma_cooling_simulator_title","v":{"s":"Magma Cooling & Crystallization Simulator"}},{"k":"cooling_intro","v":{"s":"Change only the cooling rate and follow the evidence chain: time for atoms to move → crystal size → igneous texture."}},{"k":"cooling_current_specimen","v":{"s":"Current specimen: "}},{"k":"cooling_context","v":{"s":"Use the model to isolate cooling rate, then compare its predicted texture with this specimen. Chemistry, dissolved gas, and eruption style can also shape an igneous rock."}},{"k":"cooling_how_title","v":{"s":"How to use this model"}},{"k":"cooling_walkthrough_choose","v":{"s":"Choose a cooling history."}},{"k":"cooling_walkthrough_run","v":{"s":"Run solidification and watch crystal size change."}},{"k":"cooling_walkthrough_compare","v":{"s":"Compare the predicted texture with the specimen and explain why."}},{"k":"cooling_choose_rate","v":{"s":"Cooling history controls"}},{"k":"cooling_run_step","v":{"s":"Live solidification model"}},{"k":"cooling_rate_group","v":{"s":"Cooling history"}},{"k":"cooling_environment","v":{"s":"Environment"}},{"k":"cooling_atom_time","v":{"s":"Time for atoms"}},{"k":"cooling_result_texture","v":{"s":"Resulting texture"}},{"k":"cooling_example","v":{"s":"Rock examples: "}},{"k":"cooling_why_title","v":{"s":"Why geologists care"}},{"k":"cooling_why","v":{"s":"Crystal size is a record of cooling history. Large visible crystals point to slow cooling underground; microscopic crystals point to fast cooling after eruption; glass records quenching so rapid that crystals never formed. Cooling rate controls texture, while magma chemistry helps determine which minerals and rock name result."}},{"k":"cooling_compare_prompt","v":{"s":"Try Slow and Quenched back-to-back: which result has visible crystals, and what did the atoms have time to do?"}},{"k":"cooling_phase_molten","v":{"s":"Molten magma"}},{"k":"cooling_phase_nuclei","v":{"s":"Crystal nuclei forming"}},{"k":"cooling_phase_growing","v":{"s":"Crystals growing"}},{"k":"cooling_status_ready","v":{"s":"Ready to run: the model begins with molten magma."}},{"k":"cooling_status_active","v":{"s":"Cooling in progress: watch the melt fade as crystals nucleate and grow."}},{"k":"cooling_status_complete","v":{"s":"Model complete: "}},{"k":"crystal_cooling_diagram_aria","v":{"s":"Crystal cooling-rate model — slower cooling grows larger crystals."}},{"k":"cooling_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Solidification model progress"}},{"k":"cooling_ellipsis","v":{"s":"Cooling..."}},{"k":"run_solidification","v":{"s":"Run solidification"}},{"k":"replay_solidification","v":{"s":"Replay solidification"}},{"k":"acid_fizz_test_lab","v":{"s":"Acid Fizz Test Lab"}},{"k":"acid_fizz_intro","v":{"s":"Use the virtual dilute-HCl dropper. A fizz means a carbonate, not a particular mineral: calcite, malachite and azurite all react at once. Some other carbonates, such as dolomite, react only weakly or when powdered, so no fizz is weaker evidence than a fizz."}},{"k":"fizz_positive","v":{"s":"Fizz! The acid reacted with calcium carbonate in the specimen, releasing carbon dioxide gas:"}},{"k":"fizz_no_reaction","v":{"s":"No immediate visible fizz. That argues against calcite in this model; some other carbonates react weakly or only when powdered."}},{"k":"dropping_acid","v":{"s":"Dropping Acid..."}},{"k":"drop_hcl_acid","v":{"s":"Drop HCl Acid"}},{"k":"bubbling_reaction_active","v":{"s":"Bubbling reaction active..."}},{"k":"ask_ai_petrologist","v":{"s":"Ask the AI Petrologist"}},{"k":"query_ai_about_prefix","v":{"s":"Query the AI about "}},{"k":"query_ai_suffix","v":{"s":"'s geologic origin, chemical properties, or tectonic significance."}},{"k":"ask_petrologist_question","v":{"s":"Ask the AI Petrologist a question"}},{"k":"ask_question_placeholder","v":{"s":"Ask a question (e.g., How does this form?)..."}},{"k":"thinking_ellipsis","v":{"s":"Thinking..."}},{"k":"ask_button","v":{"s":"Ask"}},{"k":"formula_label","v":{"s":"Formula: "}},{"k":"mineral_cross_section_aria","v":{"s":"Mineral cross-section"}},{"k":"crystal_system","v":{"s":"Crystal System"}},{"k":"uses_heading","v":{"s":"Uses"}},{"k":"fun_fact_heading","v":{"s":"Fun Fact"}},{"k":"where_found_heading","v":{"s":"Where Found"}},{"k":"mohs_position","v":{"s":"Mohs Position"}},{"k":"mohs_scratch_test_lab","v":{"s":"Mohs Hardness Scratch Test Lab"}},{"k":"mohs_scratch_intro","v":{"s":"Select a scratch tool and run the test. A harder tool leaves a scratch, a softer tool does not, and equal modeled hardness is a borderline result that should be retested."}},{"k":"tool_fingernail","v":{"s":"Fingernail"}},{"k":"tool_copper_penny","v":{"s":"Copper Penny"}},{"k":"tool_steel_nail","v":{"s":"Steel Nail"}},{"k":"tool_streak_plate","v":{"s":"Streak Plate"}},{"k":"tool_masonry_drill","v":{"s":"Masonry Drill"}},{"k":"tool_diamond_scribe","v":{"s":"Diamond Scribe"}},{"k":"scratch_success_a","v":{"s":"Result: Scratch created! The "}},{"k":"scratch_success_b","v":{"s":"successfully scratched "}},{"k":"scratch_fail_a","v":{"s":"Result: No scratch! The "}},{"k":"scratch_fail_b","v":{"s":"rubbed off on "}},{"k":"scratch_fail_c","v":{"s":"without leaving a mark."}},{"k":"active_tool_label","v":{"s":"Active Tool: "}},{"k":"hardness_word","v":{"s":"Hardness"}},{"k":"run_scratch_again","v":{"s":"Test again"}},{"k":"run_scratch_test","v":{"s":"Run Scratch Test"}},{"k":"scratch_see_structure","v":{"s":"Why is it this hard? Scroll to 3D crystal structure below — "}},{"k":"scratch_hard_hint","v":{"s":"strongly bonded in every direction leaves nothing to break along."}},{"k":"scratch_soft_hint","v":{"s":"look for the weak gaps between the strongly bonded parts; that is what gives way."}},{"k":"scratch_mid_hint","v":{"s":"the bond strength and how evenly it is spread set where this sits on the scale."}},{"k":"crystal3d_title","v":{"s":"3D crystal structure"}},{"k":"crystal3d_intro_cell","v":{"s":"Drag to rotate the unit cell — the smallest repeating box of this mineral's crystal system."}},{"k":"crystal3d_intro_exact","v":{"s":"This is how the atoms are actually stacked inside the mineral. Drag to rotate."}},{"k":"crystal3d_intro_model","v":{"s":"A simplified teaching model: it preserves the key arrangement described below, but not every atom or exact position. Drag to rotate."}},{"k":"crystal3d_controls","v":{"s":"Crystal view controls"}},{"k":"crystal3d_left","v":{"s":"Rotate left"}},{"k":"crystal3d_right","v":{"s":"Rotate right"}},{"k":"crystal3d_up","v":{"s":"Tilt up"}},{"k":"crystal3d_down","v":{"s":"Tilt down"}},{"k":"crystal3d_in","v":{"s":"Zoom in"}},{"k":"crystal3d_out","v":{"s":"Zoom out"}},{"k":"crystal3d_reset","v":{"s":"Reset view"}},{"k":"crystal3d_why","v":{"s":"Why it matters: "}},{"k":"crystal3d_model_limit","v":{"s":"Model limit: this shows the unit-cell geometry of the "}},{"k":"crystal3d_model_limit_b","v":{"s":" system, not this mineral's full atomic arrangement — that structure is more complex than this view is built to show."}},{"k":"crystal3d_host_stale_label","v":{"s":"3D unavailable: "}},{"k":"crystal3d_host_stale","v":{"s":"this build's STEAM Lab host is older than this tool and does not provide the 3D viewer. Everything else on this page works normally; the structure is described in full above."}},{"k":"crystal3d_offline_note","v":{"s":"If the 3D view stays blank, the engine is served from a CDN your network may block — every other panel on this page still works offline."}},{"k":"streak_plate_test_lab","v":{"s":"Streak Plate Test Lab"}},{"k":"streak_test_intro","v":{"s":"Scratch the mineral across an unglazed porcelain streak plate (modeled Mohs 6.5). Softer minerals leave powder whose color can aid identification; harder minerals scratch the plate, so no reliable powder streak is produced."}},{"k":"powder_streak_result","v":{"s":"Powder Streak Result: "}},{"k":"scratching_plate","v":{"s":"Scratching plate..."}},{"k":"perform_streak_test","v":{"s":"Perform Streak Test"}},{"k":"streak_vs_colour","v":{"s":"Compare the two chips: the powder colour is the reliable identifier, because a mineral's outward colour can vary with impurities while its streak does not."}},{"k":"ai_tutor_unavailable","v":{"s":"AI tutor is not available. Check back when online."}},{"k":"ai_no_clues","v":{"s":"The AI returned no clues. Try again."}},{"k":"could_not_reach_ai","v":{"s":"Could not reach AI tutor. Try again in a moment."}},{"k":"mystery_rock_challenge_aria","v":{"s":"Mystery Rock challenge"}},{"k":"mystery_rock_challenge_title","v":{"s":"Mystery Rock Challenge"}},{"k":"reading_level_label","v":{"s":"Reading level: "}},{"k":"mystery_intro","v":{"s":"The AI tutor picks a rock and gives you 3 clues. Read each clue, then click a rock from the grid below to guess. Wrong guesses reveal the next clue. Earn 15 XP for a correct ID."}},{"k":"ready_to_test","v":{"s":"Ready to test your rock knowledge?"}},{"k":"start_mystery_aria","v":{"s":"Start Mystery Rock challenge"}},{"k":"start_challenge","v":{"s":"Start Challenge"}},{"k":"ai_thinking_clues","v":{"s":"AI tutor is thinking up clues..."}},{"k":"clues_label","v":{"s":"Clues"}},{"k":"clue_label","v":{"s":"Clue "}},{"k":"reveal_next_clue","v":{"s":"Reveal next clue"}},{"k":"correct_it_was","v":{"s":"Correct! It was "}},{"k":"the_answer_was","v":{"s":"The answer was "}},{"k":"start_new_mystery_aria","v":{"s":"Start a new Mystery Rock challenge"}},{"k":"new_mystery","v":{"s":"New Mystery"}},{"k":"click_rock_matches_clues","v":{"s":"Click the rock you think matches the clues:"}},{"k":"rock_guess_options_aria","v":{"s":"Rock guess options"}},{"k":"guess_label","v":{"s":"Guess "}},{"k":"give_up_aria","v":{"s":"Give up and reveal the answer"}},{"k":"give_up_show_answer","v":{"s":"Give up · show answer"}},{"k":"quiz_region_aria","v":{"s":"Rock identification quiz. Press 1 through 4 to answer, or N for next."}},{"k":"correct_exclaim","v":{"s":"Correct!"}},{"k":"incorrect","v":{"s":"Incorrect."}},{"k":"correct_plus_xp","v":{"s":"Correct! +10 XP"}},{"k":"question_label","v":{"s":"Question "}},{"k":"answer_label","v":{"s":"Answer "}},{"k":"concept_focus_label","v":{"s":"Concept Focus: "}},{"k":"study_term","v":{"s":"Study Term (+5 RP)"}},{"k":"next_question_aria","v":{"s":"Next question (shortcut: N)"}},{"k":"next_question","v":{"s":"Next Question"}},{"k":"weath_chem_label","v":{"s":"Chemical-dominated"}},{"k":"weath_chem_desc","v":{"s":"Acidic rain dissolves minerals. Karst landscapes form."}},{"k":"weath_phys_label","v":{"s":"Physical-dominated"}},{"k":"weath_phys_desc","v":{"s":"Freeze-thaw cycles fracture rock mechanically."}},{"k":"weath_mixed_label","v":{"s":"Mixed weathering"}},{"k":"weath_mixed_desc","v":{"s":"Both processes active. Typical temperate climate."}},{"k":"weath_minimal_label","v":{"s":"Minimal weathering"}},{"k":"weath_minimal_desc","v":{"s":"Stable conditions. Rock surfaces persist."}},{"k":"rock_weathering_discovery","v":{"s":"Rock weathering discovery"}},{"k":"weathering_intro","v":{"s":"Adjust temperature swings, rainfall, and rain pH. Widget classifies dominant weathering mode into 4 discrete categories. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"weath_temp_swing","v":{"s":"Temp swing (°C)"}},{"k":"weath_rainfall","v":{"s":"Rainfall (mm/yr)"}},{"k":"weath_rain_ph","v":{"s":"Rain pH"}},{"k":"weath_log","v":{"s":"Log"}},{"k":"weath_reset","v":{"s":"Reset"}},{"k":"weath_log_title","v":{"s":"Logged trials"}},{"k":"weath_log_capped","v":{"s":"showing the last 8"}},{"k":"weath_log_clear","v":{"s":"Clear"}},{"k":"weath_log_temp","v":{"s":"ΔT"}},{"k":"weath_log_rain","v":{"s":"rain"}},{"k":"weath_log_ph","v":{"s":"pH"}},{"k":"hypothesis_input","v":{"s":"Weathering climate hypothesis"}},{"k":"weath_hypothesis_placeholder","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: What climate produces chemical vs physical dominance?"}},{"k":"weath_stuck_btn","v":{"s":"Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"weath_prompt_ph","v":{"s":"Try pH=4 (acid rain). Does that change the mode?"}},{"k":"weath_prompt_temp","v":{"s":"Why does temperature swing matter more in arid climates?"}},{"k":"weath_understand_label","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explanation_input","v":{"s":"Weathering climate explanation"}},{"k":"weath_explanation_placeholder","v":{"s":"Explain how climate selects which weathering mode dominates."}},{"k":"weath_design_note","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 4-state weathering marker; no rate score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"selected_word","v":{"s":"selected"}},{"k":"mission_compare_title","v":{"s":"Compare all three rock families"}},{"k":"mission_compare_detail","v":{"s":"Select each node and look for evidence of how it formed."}},{"k":"mission_trace_title","v":{"s":"Trace a transformation"}},{"k":"mission_trace_detail","v":{"s":"Choose an arrow to connect process, energy, and time."}},{"k":"mission_test_title","v":{"s":"Test the transformation machine"}},{"k":"mission_test_detail","v":{"s":"Run another pathway and compare its inputs and products."}},{"k":"mission_explain_title","v":{"s":"Explain the branching cycle"}},{"k":"mission_explain_detail","v":{"s":"Use evidence to show why the rock cycle has many valid paths."}},{"k":"rock_cycle_title","v":{"s":"Rock Cycle"}},{"k":"animated_badge","v":{"s":"ANIMATED"}},{"k":"earth_systems_mission","v":{"s":"Earth systems mission"}},{"k":"mission_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Rock cycle mission progress"}},{"k":"metric_families","v":{"s":"Families"}},{"k":"metric_process","v":{"s":"Process"}},{"k":"metric_transforms","v":{"s":"Transforms"}},{"k":"evidence_route_aria","v":{"s":"Rock cycle evidence route"}},{"k":"evidence_route","v":{"s":"Evidence route"}},{"k":"evidence_step_observe","v":{"s":"Observe texture and composition"}},{"k":"evidence_step_connect","v":{"s":"Connect process to energy and time"}},{"k":"evidence_step_explain","v":{"s":"Explain more than one valid pathway"}},{"k":"rc_canvas_aria","v":{"s":"Rock cycle diagram: an Earth cross-section with a magma chamber below. Three rock family nodes — igneous at the top, metamorphic at lower left, sedimentary at lower right — are joined by six curved arrows, one per transformation pathway."}},{"k":"rc_canvas_keys","v":{"s":"Press 1 for igneous, 2 for sedimentary, 3 for metamorphic."}},{"k":"rc_canvas_selected","v":{"s":"Currently selected:"}},{"k":"family_rocks_heading","v":{"s":"{family} Rocks"}},{"k":"crystals_label","v":{"s":"Crystals"}},{"k":"real_uses_label","v":{"s":"Real Uses"}},{"k":"transformation_processes","v":{"s":"Transformation Processes"}},{"k":"processes_intro","v":{"s":"Every pathway is real. The first three are the familiar loop; the last three are marked direct branch and skip a step entirely."}},{"k":"process_direct_branch","v":{"s":"direct branch"}},{"k":"branching_network_title","v":{"s":"A branching network, not a one-way circle"}},{"k":"branching_network_body","v":{"s":"ANY rock can become ANY other rock — the diagram's 6 arrows show every path. Which one happens depends on the process (the geological agent), not on a fixed order."}},{"k":"common_myths","v":{"s":"Common myths"}},{"k":"myth_rocks_never_change","v":{"s":"“Rocks never change.” They change constantly — just over thousands to millions of years."}},{"k":"myth_one_way","v":{"s":"“The cycle only goes one way.” It can run in any direction (follow the arrows)."}},{"k":"myth_soil_is_rock","v":{"s":"“Soil is a rock.” Soil is weathered rock plus organic matter — not a rock itself."}},{"k":"myth_lava_magma","v":{"s":"“Lava and magma are the same.” Magma is molten rock UNDERGROUND; once it erupts it's lava."}},{"k":"machine_pick_agent","v":{"s":"Pick an agent of change"}},{"k":"transformation_machine_title","v":{"s":"Rock Transformation Machine"}},{"k":"transformation_machine_intro2","v":{"s":"Load a named rock specimen, choose an agent of change, and run the machine. Each pairing produces a specific named product with its real conditions, timescale and field evidence."}},{"k":"starting_specimen","v":{"s":"Starting specimen"}},{"k":"geological_agent","v":{"s":"Geological Agent"}},{"k":"transforming_ellipsis","v":{"s":"Transforming..."}},{"k":"transform_btn","v":{"s":"Transform!"}},{"k":"machine_reset","v":{"s":"Reset machine"}},{"k":"transformation_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Transformation progress"}},{"k":"machine_conditions","v":{"s":"Conditions"}},{"k":"machine_timescale","v":{"s":"Timescale"}},{"k":"machine_what_changes","v":{"s":"What changes: "}},{"k":"machine_how_you_know","v":{"s":"How you'd know: "}},{"k":"machine_model_limit","v":{"s":"Model limit: "}},{"k":"start_rock_cycle_quiz_aria","v":{"s":"Start rock cycle quiz"}},{"k":"rc_which_rock_type_forms_from_cooled_magma_lava","v":{"s":"Which rock type forms from cooled magma/lava?"}},{"k":"rc_correct_igneous_rocks_solidify_directly_from_cooled_magma","v":{"s":"Correct! Igneous rocks solidify directly from cooled magma."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_sedimentary_rocks_form_from_accumulated_layer_deposits","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sedimentary rocks form from accumulated layer deposits."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_metamorphic_rocks_form_from_existing_rocks_altered","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Metamorphic rocks form from existing rocks altered by heat and pressure."}},{"k":"rc_which_rock_type_often_contains_fossils","v":{"s":"Which rock type often contains fossils?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_magma_temperatures_destroy_fossil_evidence_in_igneous","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Magma temperatures destroy fossil evidence in igneous rocks."}},{"k":"rc_correct_fossils_are_preserved_in_layers_of_sedimentary","v":{"s":"Correct! Fossils are preserved in layers of sedimentary rock."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_heat_and_pressure_deform_and_destroy_fossils","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Heat and pressure deform and destroy fossils in metamorphic rocks."}},{"k":"rc_which_rock_type_forms_under_heat_and_pressure","v":{"s":"Which rock type forms under heat and pressure?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_igneous_rocks_form_from_cooling_magma_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Igneous rocks form from cooling magma, not solid alteration."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_sedimentary_rocks_form_from_compaction_of_loose","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sedimentary rocks form from compaction of loose sediment."}},{"k":"rc_correct_heat_and_pressure_change_pre_existing_rocks","v":{"s":"Correct! Heat and pressure change pre-existing rocks into metamorphic ones."}},{"k":"rc_granite_is_an_example_of_which_rock_type","v":{"s":"Granite is an example of which rock type?"}},{"k":"rc_correct_granite_is_a_coarse_grained_intrusive_igneous","v":{"s":"Correct! Granite is a coarse-grained intrusive igneous rock."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_granite_crystallizes_from_magma_and_is_not","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite crystallizes from magma and is not sedimentary."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_granite_is_igneous_its_metamorphic_equivalent_is","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite is igneous; its metamorphic equivalent is gneiss."}},{"k":"rc_marble_forms_from_which_rock","v":{"s":"Marble forms from which rock?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_granite_transforms_into_gneiss","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Granite transforms into gneiss."}},{"k":"rc_correct_limestone_transforms_into_marble_under_metamorphism","v":{"s":"Correct! Limestone transforms into marble under metamorphism."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_basalt_transforms_into_greenstone_or_amphibolite","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Basalt transforms into greenstone or amphibolite."}},{"k":"rc_what_breaks_rocks_into_sediment","v":{"s":"What breaks rocks into sediment?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_heat_and_pressure_trigger_metamorphic_alteration","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Heat and pressure trigger metamorphic alteration."}},{"k":"rc_correct_wind_water_and_ice_weather_rocks_down","v":{"s":"Correct! Wind, water, and ice weather rocks down into sediment particles."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_melting_creates_magma_which_forms_igneous_rocks","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Melting creates magma, which forms igneous rocks."}},{"k":"rc_what_must_happen_for_metamorphic_rock_to_become","v":{"s":"What must happen for metamorphic rock to become igneous?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_weathering_turns_metamorphic_rocks_into_sedimentary_ones","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Weathering turns metamorphic rocks into sedimentary ones."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_compression_leads_to_metamorphism_not_igneous_rock","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Compression leads to metamorphism, not igneous rock."}},{"k":"rc_correct_metamorphic_rocks_must_melt_into_magma_then","v":{"s":"Correct! Metamorphic rocks must melt into magma, then cool and solidify."}},{"k":"rc_what_is_the_mohs_scale_used_to_measure","v":{"s":"What is the Mohs scale used to measure?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_radiometric_dating_measures_rock_age_not_the","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Radiometric dating measures rock age, not the Mohs scale."}},{"k":"rc_correct_the_mohs_scale_rates_scratch_resistance_from","v":{"s":"Correct! The Mohs scale rates scratch resistance from 1 to 10."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_crystallization_rate_determines_crystal_size","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Crystallization rate determines crystal size."}},{"k":"rc_which_rock_is_used_for_countertops","v":{"s":"Which rock is used for countertops?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_sandstone_is_too_porous_for_durable_countertops","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sandstone is too porous for durable countertops."}},{"k":"rc_correct_granite_is_extremely_hard_heat_resistant_and","v":{"s":"Correct! Granite is extremely hard, heat-resistant, and durable."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_slate_is_used_for_roofing_and_writing","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Slate is used for roofing and writing boards, not typically heavy kitchen countertops."}},{"k":"rc_the_white_cliffs_of_dover_are_made_of","v":{"s":"The White Cliffs of Dover are made of which sedimentary rock?"}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_sandstone_is_granular_and_not_white_chalky","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Sandstone is granular and not white/chalky."}},{"k":"rc_incorrect_limestone_is_related_but_the_cliffs_are","v":{"s":"Incorrect. Limestone is related, but the cliffs are specifically soft chalk."}},{"k":"rc_correct_the_cliffs_are_made_of_chalk_formed","v":{"s":"Correct! The cliffs are made of chalk, formed from marine micro-fossils."}},{"k":"quiz_mode","v":{"s":"Quiz Mode"}},{"k":"correct_count_suffix","v":{"s":"correct"}},{"k":"select_answer_label","v":{"s":"Select answer: "}},{"k":"hero_wb_hint","v":{"s":"Streak, scratch hardness, acid, magnet, and lens on an unknown specimen. Your notebook of observations eliminates suspects until only one mineral fits, the same way a field geologist works."}},{"k":"hero_wb_title","v":{"s":"Mineral Workbench, evidence-first ID"}},{"k":"mode_workbench","v":{"s":"Workbench"}},{"k":"wb_bench_aria","v":{"s":"Laboratory bench with an unknown specimen showing "}},{"k":"wb_bench_aria_end","v":{"s":" surface. Test results are described in the field notebook below."}},{"k":"wb_bench_aria_mid","v":{"s":" and a "}},{"k":"wb_best_next","v":{"s":"Best next move"}},{"k":"wb_bracket","v":{"s":"Hardness constraint: "}},{"k":"wb_candidate_help","v":{"s":"Compare the unknown with the candidates still supported by every observation in your notebook."}},{"k":"wb_candidates_fit","v":{"s":"fit the evidence"}},{"k":"wb_cards_shown_sr","v":{"s":" candidate cards shown."}},{"k":"wb_cer_label","v":{"s":"Claim · Evidence · Reasoning"}},{"k":"wb_choose_claim","v":{"s":"Choose one supported candidate to build your claim."}},{"k":"wb_choose_help","v":{"s":"Selecting a card does not submit it. You will review the evidence here first."}},{"k":"wb_claim_rejected","v":{"s":"Review rejected claim evidence"}},{"k":"wb_claim_changed_sr","v":{"s":"Recheck the evidence, reasoning, and confidence before making a revised claim."}},{"k":"wb_claim_supported","v":{"s":"Evidence-supported identification"}},{"k":"wb_clear","v":{"s":"↺ Clear evidence"}},{"k":"wb_clear_aria","v":{"s":"Clear observations and retest the same specimen"}},{"k":"wb_clear_hardness","v":{"s":"Clear hardness trials and retest"}},{"k":"wb_clear_recheck","v":{"s":"Clear observations and recheck"}},{"k":"wb_close_forecast","v":{"s":"Close"}},{"k":"wb_compare_board","v":{"s":"Evidence comparison board"}},{"k":"wb_confirms_only","v":{"s":"Confirms, but does not narrow alone"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_acid","v":{"s":"Acid"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_aria","v":{"s":"Evidence coverage by property type"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_density","v":{"s":"Density"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_hardness","v":{"s":"Hardness"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_luster","v":{"s":"Luster"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_magnetism","v":{"s":"Magnetism"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_streak","v":{"s":"Streak"}},{"k":"wb_coverage_title","v":{"s":"Evidence coverage"}},{"k":"wb_diagnostic_end","v":{"s":" candidates by itself. A useful test creates a strong split among possibilities."}},{"k":"wb_diagnostic_mid","v":{"s":" rules out "}},{"k":"wb_diagnostic_title","v":{"s":"Most diagnostic so far"}},{"k":"wb_eliminated","v":{"s":"eliminated"}},{"k":"wb_eliminated_by","v":{"s":"Review why set aside · "}},{"k":"wb_elimination_progress","v":{"s":"Candidate elimination progress"}},{"k":"wb_ev_d_heavy","v":{"s":" (noticeably heavy)"}},{"k":"wb_ev_d_vheavy","v":{"s":" (remarkably heavy)"}},{"k":"wb_ev_density","v":{"s":"Density calculation: "}},{"k":"wb_ev_fizz","v":{"s":"Acid: immediate fizz (calcite response in this set)"}},{"k":"wb_ev_lens","v":{"s":"Lens: luster "}},{"k":"wb_ev_marked","v":{"s":"left a scratch"}},{"k":"wb_ev_nofizz","v":{"s":"Acid: no immediate visible fizz"}},{"k":"wb_ev_nomark","v":{"s":"no mark"}},{"k":"wb_ev_nopull","v":{"s":"Magnet: no attraction"}},{"k":"wb_ev_pull","v":{"s":"Magnet: strong pull"}},{"k":"wb_ev_streak","v":{"s":"Streak: "}},{"k":"wb_evidence_cleared_sr","v":{"s":"Observations cleared. The same specimen remains on the bench."}},{"k":"wb_evidence_label","v":{"s":"Evidence: "}},{"k":"wb_fact_density","v":{"s":"Density: "}},{"k":"wb_fact_fizz","v":{"s":"Acid: fizzes"}},{"k":"wb_fact_luster","v":{"s":"Luster: "}},{"k":"wb_fact_mohs","v":{"s":"Mohs: "}},{"k":"wb_fact_nofizz","v":{"s":"Acid: no fizz"}},{"k":"wb_fact_nopull","v":{"s":"Magnet: none"}},{"k":"wb_fact_pull","v":{"s":"Magnet: pull"}},{"k":"wb_fact_streak","v":{"s":"Streak: "}},{"k":"wb_field_station","v":{"s":"FIELD STATION 01"}},{"k":"wb_filter_all","v":{"s":"All"}},{"k":"wb_filter_aria","v":{"s":"Choose which candidate cards to show"}},{"k":"wb_filter_help","v":{"s":"Active shortlist cards match the current observations and have not been submitted already. Set aside preserves measurement conflicts and rejected claims for review; rejection feedback is not a physical property."}},{"k":"wb_filter_setaside","v":{"s":"Set aside"}},{"k":"wb_filter_shortlist","v":{"s":"Active shortlist"}},{"k":"wb_form_blocky","v":{"s":"blocky form with near-right-angle cleavage"}},{"k":"wb_form_dodecahedral","v":{"s":"dodecahedral form"}},{"k":"wb_form_micaceous","v":{"s":"thin, sheet-like form"}},{"k":"wb_form_octahedral","v":{"s":"octahedral form"}},{"k":"wb_form_reference_suffix","v":{"s":" reference form"}},{"k":"wb_form_unknown","v":{"s":"unknown reference form"}},{"k":"wb_hint","v":{"s":"Use Active shortlist for a focused decision. Set aside preserves eliminated and rejected cards with the property that conflicted, so evidence is never hidden from review."}},{"k":"wb_intro_body","v":{"s":"An unknown specimen, real instruments, and a field notebook. Run the tests a geologist would, watch your evidence eliminate suspects, then name the mineral."}},{"k":"wb_intro_eyebrow","v":{"s":"Evidence-first mineral identification"}},{"k":"wb_intro_title","v":{"s":"The Mineral Workbench"}},{"k":"wb_kind_acid","v":{"s":"acid reaction"}},{"k":"wb_kind_density","v":{"s":"density"}},{"k":"wb_kind_hardness","v":{"s":"hardness"}},{"k":"wb_kind_luster","v":{"s":"luster"}},{"k":"wb_kind_magnet","v":{"s":"magnetism"}},{"k":"wb_kind_streak","v":{"s":"streak"}},{"k":"wb_meta_acid","v":{"s":"Virtual calcite-response check"}},{"k":"wb_meta_density","v":{"s":"Calculate mass ÷ volume"}},{"k":"wb_meta_lens","v":{"s":"Observe luster + crystal form"}},{"k":"wb_meta_magnet","v":{"s":"Check for magnetic pull"}},{"k":"wb_meta_streak","v":{"s":"Check powder color or plate scratch"}},{"k":"wb_mismatch_aria","v":{"s":"Does not match the "}},{"k":"wb_mismatch_aria_end","v":{"s":" observation."}},{"k":"wb_mohs_value","v":{"s":"Mohs"}},{"k":"wb_my_claim","v":{"s":"My claim: the unknown is "}},{"k":"wb_new_specimen_sr","v":{"s":"A new unknown specimen is on the bench. Choose an instrument to begin testing."}},{"k":"wb_next","v":{"s":"🔄 Next specimen"}},{"k":"wb_next_acid","v":{"s":"Check for calcite’s acid response"}},{"k":"wb_next_acid_detail","v":{"s":"Fizzing means a carbonate. In the standard set calcite is the only one, so a fizz names it; the challenge set adds two copper carbonates, so there a fizz narrows the field instead of finishing it."}},{"k":"wb_next_claim","v":{"s":"Your evidence is ready"}},{"k":"wb_next_claim_detail","v":{"s":"Compare the remaining cards with your notebook, then make the claim your evidence supports."}},{"k":"wb_next_claim_detail_one","v":{"s":"Compare the remaining card with your notebook, then make the claim your evidence supports."}},{"k":"wb_next_density","v":{"s":"Compare heft with density"}},{"k":"wb_next_density_detail","v":{"s":"Mass divided by displaced volume helps separate minerals that look alike."}},{"k":"wb_next_lens","v":{"s":"Start with the hand lens"}},{"k":"wb_next_lens_detail","v":{"s":"Luster is a fast first split: does the surface reflect like metal, glass, pearl, or wax?"}},{"k":"wb_next_magnet","v":{"s":"Check magnetism"}},{"k":"wb_next_magnet_detail","v":{"s":"A strong pull is unusually diagnostic and can settle a magnetite hypothesis."}},{"k":"wb_next_scratch","v":{"s":"Bracket the hardness"}},{"k":"wb_next_scratch_detail","v":{"s":"Try the glass reference at modeled Mohs 5.5, then choose a softer or harder calibrated point from the result."}},{"k":"wb_next_streak","v":{"s":"Run the streak plate"}},{"k":"wb_next_streak_detail","v":{"s":"Softer minerals leave diagnostic powder; a harder specimen grooves the plate and provides a hardness clue instead."}},{"k":"wb_no_evidence_yet","v":{"s":"No measured properties yet."}},{"k":"wb_no_measured_facts","v":{"s":"Run a test to compare properties"}},{"k":"wb_not_ready_sr","v":{"s":"Measure at least two different property types before submitting a claim."}},{"k":"wb_notebook","v":{"s":"Field notebook"}},{"k":"wb_notebook_empty","v":{"s":"No observations yet. Every test you run is recorded here as evidence."}},{"k":"wb_notebook_empty_title","v":{"s":"Your first observation goes here."}},{"k":"wb_notebook_list_aria","v":{"s":"Recorded evidence grouped by property"}},{"k":"wb_observations","v":{"s":"observations"}},{"k":"wb_observing_now","v":{"s":"Observation in progress"}},{"k":"wb_of_six_types","v":{"s":" of 6 confirmed property types"}},{"k":"wb_on_own","v":{"s":" on its own"}},{"k":"wb_open","v":{"s":"🪨 Put a specimen on the bench"}},{"k":"wb_out_aria","v":{"s":"Review why this candidate was set aside: "}},{"k":"wb_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Mineral investigation progress"}},{"k":"wb_property_types_short","v":{"s":"confirmed types"}},{"k":"wb_ready","v":{"s":"Ready to claim"}},{"k":"wb_ready_sr","v":{"s":"At least two different property types have been measured."}},{"k":"wb_reasoning_label","v":{"s":"Reasoning: "}},{"k":"wb_reasoning_prompt","v":{"s":"Reasoning check: why did the most diagnostic observation separate more candidates than surface color would?"}},{"k":"wb_reasoning_sentence","v":{"s":"Each observation should match the candidate’s reference properties."}},{"k":"wb_recommended_aria","v":{"s":"Recommended next test. "}},{"k":"wb_reconcile_evidence","v":{"s":"Recheck the conflicting observations"}},{"k":"wb_reconcile_evidence_detail","v":{"s":"No reference candidate fits every recorded observation. Clear and rerun the measurements instead of making an unsupported claim."}},{"k":"wb_reconcile_hardness","v":{"s":"Resolve the conflicting scratch results"}},{"k":"wb_reconcile_hardness_detail","v":{"s":"The recorded bounds cannot all be true at once. Clear only the hardness trials, then bracket again from a fresh reference."}},{"k":"wb_rejected_aria","v":{"s":"Review unresolved evidence for the rejected claim: "}},{"k":"wb_result_nomark","v":{"s":"No mark observed"}},{"k":"wb_result_plate_derived","v":{"s":"Bound recorded from the streak-plate groove"}},{"k":"wb_result_scratch","v":{"s":"✓ left a scratch"}},{"k":"wb_revise_body","v":{"s":" shared the evidence you had, but it was not this specimen. Use "}},{"k":"wb_revise_body2","v":{"s":" to distinguish the remaining possibilities."}},{"k":"wb_revise_title","v":{"s":"Revise the claim—do not restart."}},{"k":"wb_rules_out","v":{"s":"Rules out "}},{"k":"wb_running_acid","v":{"s":"Watching for an acid reaction…"}},{"k":"wb_running_claim","v":{"s":"That claim does not fit yet—compare the remaining evidence."}},{"k":"wb_running_density","v":{"s":"Measuring mass and displaced volume…"}},{"k":"wb_running_lens","v":{"s":"Examining luster and crystal form…"}},{"k":"wb_running_magnet","v":{"s":"Checking for magnetic pull…"}},{"k":"wb_running_scratch","v":{"s":"Checking whether the reference leaves a true scratch…"}},{"k":"wb_running_streak","v":{"s":"Running the streak-plate test…"}},{"k":"wb_safety","v":{"s":"This is a virtual lab. In a physical lab, use only teacher-approved specimens, wear eye protection, avoid making dust—especially from lead-bearing minerals such as galena—wash hands, and never taste an unknown. Acid tests require teacher supervision and a tiny test area."}},{"k":"wb_scale_reference","v":{"s":"VISUAL SCALE REFERENCE"}},{"k":"wb_scratch_aria","v":{"s":"Try to scratch the specimen with "}},{"k":"wb_scratch_help","v":{"s":"Find one reference that leaves no mark and the next harder reference that scratches. A modeled near-match narrows to H ≈ the reference but should be confirmed. The porcelain reference is the same plate: a groove without powder means H > about 6.5."}},{"k":"wb_scratch_set","v":{"s":"Mohs scratch reference set"}},{"k":"wb_select_aria","v":{"s":"Select as a possible claim: "}},{"k":"wb_selected","v":{"s":"Selected for claim"}},{"k":"wb_selected_aria","v":{"s":"Selected for your claim: "}},{"k":"wb_selected_sr","v":{"s":"Selected "}},{"k":"wb_selected_sr2","v":{"s":" as a possible claim. Review the claim builder before submitting."}},{"k":"wb_sequence_aria","v":{"s":"Mineral identification sequence"}},{"k":"wb_setaside_help","v":{"s":"These cards are here for review. Switch to Active shortlist to select a supported claim."}},{"k":"wb_solved","v":{"s":"Identified: "}},{"k":"wb_solved_body","v":{"s":"Named from "}},{"k":"wb_solved_body2","v":{"s":" measured property types. Solved so far: "}},{"k":"wb_sr_density","v":{"s":"Mass divided by displaced volume: "}},{"k":"wb_sr_fizz","v":{"s":"Immediate carbon dioxide fizz supports calcite in this reference set."}},{"k":"wb_hardness_cleared_sr","v":{"s":"Conflicting hardness trials cleared. The other observations are preserved; bracket hardness again."}},{"k":"wb_sr_lens","v":{"s":"Under the lens the luster is "}},{"k":"wb_sr_lens2","v":{"s":"Observed reference form: "}},{"k":"wb_sr_nofizz","v":{"s":"No immediate visible fizz; this argues against calcite in this reference set."}},{"k":"wb_sr_nopull","v":{"s":"The magnet has no effect."}},{"k":"wb_sr_not_scratched","v":{"s":"No mark. The specimen is harder than "}},{"k":"wb_sr_pull","v":{"s":"The specimen jumps to the magnet. Strongly magnetic."}},{"k":"wb_sr_scratched","v":{"s":"It left a scratch. The specimen is softer than "}},{"k":"wb_sr_solved","v":{"s":"Correct. The specimen is "}},{"k":"wb_sr_streak","v":{"s":"Streak-plate test complete. Result: "}},{"k":"wb_sr_wrong","v":{"s":"Not "}},{"k":"wb_sr_wrong2","v":{"s":"Your evidence still fits it, so run a test that would tell them apart."}},{"k":"wb_step_claim","v":{"s":"Make a claim"}},{"k":"wb_step_claim_detail","v":{"s":"Name the mineral and support your choice."}},{"k":"wb_step_claim_short","v":{"s":"Claim"}},{"k":"wb_step_compare","v":{"s":"Compare evidence"}},{"k":"wb_step_compare_detail","v":{"s":"Notice which candidates no longer fit."}},{"k":"wb_step_compare_short","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"wb_step_observe","v":{"s":"Observe & test"}},{"k":"wb_step_observe_detail","v":{"s":"Use an instrument to collect a property."}},{"k":"wb_step_observe_short","v":{"s":"Observe"}},{"k":"wb_step_strengthen","v":{"s":"Strengthen your claim"}},{"k":"wb_still_fit","v":{"s":"still fit"}},{"k":"wb_still_possible","v":{"s":"Still supported"}},{"k":"wb_streak_recorded","v":{"s":"STREAK RECORDED"}},{"k":"wb_submit_claim","v":{"s":"Submit evidence-based claim"}},{"k":"wb_suspects","v":{"s":"Candidate minerals"}},{"k":"wb_swap","v":{"s":"🔄 New unknown"}},{"k":"wb_swap_aria","v":{"s":"Put away this specimen and draw a different unknown"}},{"k":"wb_t_acid","v":{"s":"Acid dropper"}},{"k":"wb_t_acid_aria","v":{"s":"Apply a virtual drop of dilute hydrochloric acid"}},{"k":"wb_t_balance"