r answer"}},{"k":"submit","v":{"s":"✔ Submit"}},{"k":"prev","v":{"s":"← Prev"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"true","v":{"s":"✔ TRUE"}},{"k":"false","v":{"s":"✘ FALSE"}},{"k":"prev_2","v":{"s":"← Prev"}},{"k":"next_2","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"unknown_tab","v":{"s":"Unknown tab"}},{"k":"circular","v":{"s":"Circular"}},{"k":"eccentricity_0_05_essentially_circular","v":{"s":"Eccentricity < 0.05 — essentially circular orbit. Earth (e=0.017) and Venus (e=0.007) live here."}},{"k":"elliptical","v":{"s":"Elliptical"}},{"k":"mildly_elliptical_mercury_e_0_21_and_m","v":{"s":"Mildly elliptical — Mercury (e=0.21) and Mars (e=0.09). Noticeable speed variation across orbit."}},{"k":"eccentric","v":{"s":"Eccentric"}},{"k":"highly_elliptical_pluto_e_0_25_many_tn","v":{"s":"Highly elliptical — Pluto (e=0.25), many TNOs. Speed at perihelion >> aphelion."}},{"k":"cometary","v":{"s":"Cometary"}},{"k":"halley_class_orbit_halley_e_0_97_spend","v":{"s":"Halley-class orbit (Halley e=0.97). Spends most of its time far from Sun, brief perihelion passage."}},{"k":"parabolic_hyperbolic","v":{"s":"Parabolic / hyperbolic"}},{"k":"e_1_means_object_barely_bound_paraboli","v":{"s":"e → 1 means object barely bound (parabolic) or unbound (hyperbolic) — could be interstellar."}},{"k":"kepler_inquiry_orbital_mechanics_sandb","v":{"s":"🔬 Kepler Inquiry — Orbital Mechanics Sandbox"}},{"k":"set_semi_major_axis_eccentricity_and_c","v":{"s":"Set semi-major axis, eccentricity, and central-body mass. Predict the orbital class — circular, elliptical, eccentric, cometary. No score, no reveal."}},{"k":"period","v":{"s":"Period"}},{"k":"perihelion","v":{"s":"Perihelion"}},{"k":"aphelion","v":{"s":"Aphelion"}},{"k":"v_ratio_p_a","v":{"s":"v ratio (p/a)"}},{"k":"sun","v":{"s":"☉ Sun"}},{"k":"empty_focus","v":{"s":"empty focus"}},{"k":"semi_major_a","v":{"s":"Semi-major a"}},{"k":"eccentricity_e","v":{"s":"Eccentricity e"}},{"k":"central_mass","v":{"s":"Central mass"}},{"k":"log_this_orbit","v":{"s":"📋 Log this orbit"}},{"k":"reset_earth","v":{"s":"Reset (Earth)"}},{"k":"your_hypothesis_which_slider_has_more_","v":{"s":"Your hypothesis (which slider has more effect on PERIOD vs SHAPE?)"}},{"k":"e_g_e_only_changes_shape_period_is_det","v":{"s":"e.g., e only changes shape — period is determined by a and mass alone..."}},{"k":"i_m_stuck_show_open_questions","v":{"s":"🤔 I'm stuck — show open questions"}},{"k":"open_questions_no_answer_key","v":{"s":"Open questions (no answer key)"}},{"k":"does_eccentricity_change_orbital_perio","v":{"s":"Does eccentricity change orbital PERIOD? Try e=0 vs e=0.5 at same a."}},{"k":"halley_s_comet_has_a_17_8_au_e_0_97_wh","v":{"s":"Halley's comet has a=17.8 AU, e=0.97. What does that imply for its period — and how often it visits perihelion?"}},{"k":"if_the_sun_lost_half_its_mass_instantl","v":{"s":"If the Sun lost half its mass instantly, what would happen to Earth's orbital period?"}},{"k":"at_what_e_value_does_an_orbit_become_u","v":{"s":"At what e value does an orbit become unbound? What determines the boundary?"}},{"k":"i_can_explain_why_this_a_e_m_combinati","v":{"s":"I can explain why this a/e/M combination yields this orbital class."}},{"k":"explain_in_your_own_words","v":{"s":"Explain in your own words..."}},{"k":"inquiry_widget_no_score_no_reveal_no_a","v":{"s":"Inquiry widget — no score, no reveal, no answer dump. T = √(a³/M) per Kepler III with M in solar masses, a in AU, T in years. Vis-viva used for v at perihelion / aphelion."}},{"k":"full_orrery_animated_solar_system","v":{"s":"Full orrery — animated solar system"}},{"k":"all_8_planets_to_scale_size_orbit_watc","v":{"s":"Eight planets plus Pluto follow measured orbital shapes and relative speeds. The main map shares one orbital-distance scale; markers are compressed and clamped, and the inner-system inset is magnified."}},{"k":"kepler_i_orbits_are_ellipses","v":{"s":"Kepler I — orbits are ellipses"}},{"k":"every_planet_orbits_in_an_ellipse_with","v":{"s":"Every planet orbits in an ellipse with the Sun at one focus. The other focus is empty space. Earth's orbit is barely-eccentric (e ≈ 0.017)."}},{"k":"kepler_ii_equal_areas_in_equal_times","v":{"s":"Kepler II — equal areas in equal times"}},{"k":"a_line_from_planet_to_sun_sweeps_equal","v":{"s":"A line from planet to Sun sweeps equal areas in equal times — meaning planets move FASTER near the Sun (perihelion) and SLOWER far away (aphelion)."}},{"k":"kepler_iii_period_semi_major_axis","v":{"s":"Kepler III — period² ∝ semi-major-axis³"}},{"k":"t_a_when_t_is_in_years_and_a_is_in_au_","v":{"s":"T² = a³ when T is in years and a is in AU. Earth's T=1, a=1 makes the math beautifully clean. Doubling orbit size multiplies period by ~2.83."}},{"k":"workshop_build_your_own_orbit","v":{"s":"Workshop — build your own orbit"}},{"k":"set_semi_major_axis_eccentricity_the_o","v":{"s":"Set semi-major axis + eccentricity; the orrery shows the orbit in real time. Try e=0.9 — that's comet territory. Try e=0 — perfect circle."}},{"k":"hohmann_transfers_actual_rocket_math","v":{"s":"Hohmann transfers — actual rocket math"}},{"k":"the_minimum_energy_way_to_travel_betwe","v":{"s":"The minimum-energy way to travel between two circular orbits. Two burns: leave the inner orbit at perihelion of transfer, arrive at the outer orbit at aphelion."}},{"k":"challenges_apply_kepler_s_laws","v":{"s":"Challenges — apply Kepler's laws"}},{"k":"multi_step_problems_predict_periods_fr","v":{"s":"Multi-step problems: predict periods from semi-major axis, identify perihelion vs aphelion speeds, compute transfer-orbit Δv. AP Physics 1 + 2 territory."}},{"k":"true_false_concept_check","v":{"s":"True / False — concept check"}},{"k":"quick_discrimination_quiz_on_common_ke","v":{"s":"Quick discrimination quiz on common Kepler misconceptions. Targets the orbital myths (\"closer = slower,\" \"orbits are circles,\" \"all orbits go the same direction\")."}},{"k":"kepler_inquiry_orbital_mechanics_sandb_2","v":{"s":"Kepler Inquiry — orbita