oute still takes model time."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_grow","v":{"s":"Collect nearby cloud droplets. Growth must make the parcel heavy enough to fall before you can choose a landing route."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_help","v":{"s":"Guide my movement can help with steering. Resume the simulation when you are ready to continue."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_land","v":{"s":"Aim for {surface} as you fall. The ground directly underneath determines the route; wind and remaining falling time can limit your landing."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_mismatch","v":{"s":"Your selected landing goal differs from this challenge."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_nucleus","v":{"s":"Move toward a glowing condensation nucleus. Reaching cloud base alone does not create a cloud droplet."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_realign","v":{"s":"Use challenge landing goal"}},{"k":"pilot_hint_release","v":{"s":"You have reached the leaf. Let the water gain enough energy to return to the air as vapor; observe the change to finish this challenge."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_rise","v":{"s":"Rise toward cloud base. The model parcel cools as it rises; condensation also needs a nucleus."}},{"k":"pilot_hint_step","v":{"s":"Step {step} of {total}"}},{"k":"pilot_hint_title","v":{"s":"Challenge hint"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_boundary","v":{"s":"These are separate journey observations, not a controlled experiment. Starting conditions may differ, and model times are compressed."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_changes","v":{"s":"Observed changes"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_claim","v":{"s":"My comparison"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_claim_prompt","v":{"s":"How did the water pathways differ? Describe one similarity or difference."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_climates","v":{"s":"These records also use different starting climates."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_destination","v":{"s":"Destination observed: {form}"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_download","v":{"s":"Download pathway comparison"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_empty","v":{"s":"Complete two different journey challenges to compare your own observations here."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_evidence","v":{"s":"Supporting observations"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_evidence_prompt","v":{"s":"Which recorded changes support your comparison? Refer to both journeys."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_first","v":{"s":"First saved pathway"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_intro","v":{"s":"Pin two completed challenges to compare their observed pathways. Replaying a challenge will not change these pinned records."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_limit","v":{"s":"Model limitation"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_limit_prompt","v":{"s":"What can these observations not establish about water in the real world?"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_pin","v":{"s":"Pin selected results"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_pinned","v":{"s":"Pinned observation"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_review","v":{"s":"Your writing is preserved when you pin new results. Review it against the selected evidence."}},{"k":"pilot_rc_second","v":{"s":"Second saved pathway"}},{"k":"pilot_rc_title","v":{"s":"Compare saved pathways"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_guiding","v":{"s":"Guiding movement"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_hide_map","v":{"s":"Hide landing map"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_manual","v":{"s":"Manual flight"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_options","v":{"s":"Flight options"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_paused","v":{"s":"Journey paused"}},{"k":"pilot_refine_ready","v":{"s":"Assist ready"}},{"k":"pilot_map_above","v":{"s":"Target above you"}},{"k":"pilot_map_ahead","v":{"s":"Target ahead of your view"}},{"k":"pilot_map_aria","v":{"s":"Model landing zones. The white triangle marks your position and viewing direction; the gold ring marks the current destination. This schematic shows landing behavior, not terrain elevation."}},{"k":"pilot_map_behind","v":{"s":"Target behind your view"}},{"k":"pilot_map_below","v":{"s":"Target below you"}},{"k":"pilot_map_here","v":{"s":"Near the target"}},{"k":"pilot_map_left","v":{"s":"Target to your left"}},{"k":"pilot_map_path","v":{"s":"Follow the current water pathway"}},{"k":"pilot_map_right","v":{"s":"Target to your right"}},{"k":"pilot_map_symbols","v":{"s":"▲ You and view direction · ○ Destination"}},{"k":"pilot_map_title","v":{"s":"Landing zones · schematic"}},{"k":"pilot_map_toggle","v":{"s":"Landing-zone map"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_measurements","v":{"s":"Model altitude: {m} m · temperature: {c} °C · RH: {rh}%"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_nucleus_recorded","v":{"s":"Condensation nucleus captured"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_active","v":{"s":"In progress"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_cloud_goal","v":{"s":"Reach cloud base as vapor and meet a condensation nucleus."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_cloud_insight","v":{"s":"In this model, cooling to saturation and meeting a nucleus allows cloud water to form. A cloud contains tiny droplets or ice; water vapor itself is invisible."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_cloud_step","v":{"s":"Observe vapor become a droplet or ice"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_cloud_title","v":{"s":"Condensation detective"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_complete","v":{"s":"Observed"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_export","v":{"s":"Download challenge evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_interrupted","v":{"s":"Restart needed"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_intro","v":{"s":"Start from your current place in the journey. Only new changes count. Movement assistance and learning pauses are optional."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_observed","v":{"s":"Challenge observed. Open Journey challenges to explain your evidence."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_enter","v":{"s":"Enter the plant route"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_goal","v":{"s":"Choose the plant pathway, move through the plant, and observe water return to the air."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_insight","v":{"s":"Plants take up liquid water through roots and release vapor from leaves by transpiration. This teaching route compresses soil uptake, plant transport, and their natural timescales."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_leaf","v":{"s":"Reach the leaf"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_title","v":{"s":"Return through a leaf"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_plant_vapor","v":{"s":"Return as vapor"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_readings","v":{"s":"Recorded model readings"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_reflection","v":{"s":"My explanation"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_reflection_prompt","v":{"s":"Explain what caused the changes. Which observation supports your explanation?"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_replay","v":{"s":"Replay challenge"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_restart_copy","v":{"s":"Resetting, restoring, or changing climate interrupted this attempt. Restart the challenge to collect a new sequence."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_runoff_collect","v":{"s":"Reach collected water"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_runoff_goal","v":{"s":"Land on hard ground, then follow surface flow to collected water."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_runoff_insight","v":{"s":"Runoff travels over the surface into streams and other water stores. This route does not require water to pass through soil first."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_runoff_land","v":{"s":"Land as runoff"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_runoff_title","v":{"s":"Runoff to stream"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_saved","v":{"s":"Saved result"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_saved_named","v":{"s":"View saved result: {title}"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_soil_goal","v":{"s":"Land on permeable ground and follow soil water toward groundwater."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_soil_groundwater","v":{"s":"Reach groundwater"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_soil_insight","v":{"s":"Infiltration enters the soil; further downward movement can recharge groundwater. Real infiltrated water can also be stored, evaporate, or enter roots, so infiltration does not always mean recharge."}},{"k":"pilot_quest_soil_land","v":{"s":"Enter soil"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_soil_title","v":{"s":"Below the surface"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_start","v":{"s":"Start challenge"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_start_named","v":{"s":"Start challenge: {title}"}},{"k":"pilot_quest_title","v":{"s":"Journey challenges"}},{"k":"pilot_landing_goal_v2","v":{"s":"Chosen goal: {surface}. Steer before touching down."}},{"k":"pilot_notice_continue","v":{"s":"Continue journey"}},{"k":"pilot_notice_energy","v":{"s":"Energy transfer"}},{"k":"pilot_notice_evidence","v":{"s":"Model reading at the change"}},{"k":"pilot_notice_paused","v":{"s":"Journey paused · notice the change"}},{"k":"pilot_notice_prompt","v":{"s":"What condition caused this change? Use the recorded evidence to explain it."}},{"k":"pilot_notice_toggle","v":{"s":"Pause at changes"}},{"k":"pilot_scene_larger","v":{"s":"Larger view"}},{"k":"pilot_scene_smaller","v":{"s":"Smaller view"}},{"k":"pilot_climate_drawer","v":{"s":"Starting climate"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_cloudbase","v":{"s":"Cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_drops","v":{"s":"Nearby cloud droplets"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_hard","v":{"s":"Hard ground"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_help","v":{"s":"The gold ring marks a navigation aid. Assistance uses ordinary movement; phase changes still need the right conditions. Manual controls take over while held. Wind and falling time can limit where you land. Pause stops the journey."}},{"k":"pilot_nav_landing","v":{"s":"Aim to land on"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_nucleus","v":{"s":"Condensation nucleus"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_off","v":{"s":"Guide my movement"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_on","v":{"s":"Movement assist on"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_percolate","v":{"s":"Pores toward the water table"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_plant","v":{"s":"Plant pathway"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_return","v":{"s":"Return to the atmosphere"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_runoff","v":{"s":"Downhill stream route"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_soil","v":{"s":"Permeable ground"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_spring","v":{"s":"Groundwater toward a spring"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_sun","v":{"s":"Sunlit water"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_target","v":{"s":"Next destination"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_title","v":{"s":"Navigation assistance"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_water","v":{"s":"Open water"}},{"k":"pilot_nav_waypoint","v":{"s":"Show waypoint"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_environment_copy","v":{"s":"Use the same rise and steering strategy to compare these environments. Presets change several conditions together; they do not isolate the effect of humidity or temperature."}},{"k":"investigation_index_note","v":{"s":"Teaching indices out of 100: tendencies, not water volumes or flood predictions."}},{"k":"investigation_baseline","v":{"s":"Urban baseline"}},{"k":"investigation_baseline_restore","v":{"s":"Restore urban cover and the recorded storm rainfall before saving the baseline."}},{"k":"investigation_boundary","v":{"s":"Independent teaching indices, not percentages of rainfall or flood forecasts. Infiltration does not automatically become groundwater recharge."}},{"k":"investigation_choose_cover","v":{"s":"Test a land-cover change. Keep rainfall, soil saturation, permeability, slope, sunlight, temperature, and wind fixed."}},{"k":"investigation_compare_prompt","v":{"s":"Watch the same storm over the changed cover. Compare surface flow and infiltration with your saved urban baseline."}},{"k":"investigation_comparison","v":{"s":"Changed land cover"}},{"k":"investigation_complete_step","v":{"s":"Recorded"}},{"k":"investigation_download","v":{"s":"Download evidence"}},{"k":"investigation_evidence_table","v":{"s":"Recorded land tests — independent indices out of 100"}},{"k":"investigation_explain_prompt","v":{"s":"What changed? Cite both indices, explain the land-cover effect, and name one thing this model cannot tell you."}},{"k":"investigation_explain_step","v":{"s":"Explain"}},{"k":"investigation_held_fixed","v":{"s":"Recorded starting inputs"}},{"k":"investigation_infiltration","v":{"s":"Infiltration opportunity"}},{"k":"investigation_intro","v":{"s":"Follow a storm, change one land-cover setting, and explain the difference."}},{"k":"investigation_invitation","v":{"s":"Connected water investigation"}},{"k":"investigation_kept","v":{"s":"Your prediction and recorded evidence stay with this investigation."}},{"k":"investigation_kicker","v":{"s":"Storm to stream · guided investigation"}},{"k":"investigation_learning_tools","v":{"s":"Learning guide, grade level, and achievements"}},{"k":"investigation_less","v":{"s":"Less runoff"}},{"k":"investigation_more","v":{"s":"More runoff"}},{"k":"investigation_new","v":{"s":"Start a new investigation"}},{"k":"investigation_notebook","v":{"s":"Evidence notebook"}},{"k":"investigation_observe_land","v":{"s":"Watch water move over urban land and into the soil. Trace a droplet or inspect the map, then record your baseline."}},{"k":"investigation_observe_storm","v":{"s":"Watch a particle fall through the temperature layers. This investigation needs liquid rain reaching the ground."}},{"k":"investigation_permeability","v":{"s":"permeability"}},{"k":"investigation_plan_boundary","v":{"s":"This controlled land-cover test is separate from the ten-year campaign below. It does not estimate a real planting project."}},{"k":"investigation_plan_step","v":{"s":"Choose cover"}},{"k":"investigation_predict_step","v":{"s":"Predict"}},{"k":"investigation_prediction","v":{"s":"Prediction"}},{"k":"investigation_prediction_hint","v":{"s":"A prediction is a starting idea. Unexpected results are useful evidence."}},{"k":"investigation_progress","v":{"s":"Investigation progress"}},{"k":"investigation_question","v":{"s":"With the same rain and soil conditions, what will vegetated cover do to runoff compared with urban cover?"}},{"k":"investigation_rainfall","v":{"s":"rainfall"}},{"k":"investigation_record_baseline","v":{"s":"Record urban baseline"}},{"k":"investigation_record_comparison","v":{"s":"Record comparison and explain"}},{"k":"investigation_record_storm","v":{"s":"Record storm and follow the water"}},{"k":"investigation_recorded","v":{"s":"Investigation recorded. Review or download your evidence."}},{"k":"investigation_reflection","v":{"s":"My explanation"}},{"k":"investigation_restore_test","v":{"s":"Restore fair test"}},{"k":"investigation_restore_urban","v":{"s":"Restore urban rain setup"}},{"k":"investigation_return","v":{"s":"Return to investigation step"}},{"k":"investigation_runoff","v":{"s":"Runoff tendency"}},{"k":"investigation_same","v":{"s":"Little or no change"}},{"k":"investigation_saturation","v":{"s":"soil saturation"}},{"k":"investigation_save_explanation","v":{"s":"Save explanation"}},{"k":"investigation_save_prediction","v":{"s":"Save prediction and observe"}},{"k":"investigation_slope","v":{"s":"slope"}},{"k":"investigation_start","v":{"s":"Start investigation"}},{"k":"investigation_started","v":{"s":"Investigation started. Predict how changing urban cover to vegetation will affect runoff."}},{"k":"investigation_storm_bridge","v":{"s":"The storm intensity index supplies the same rainfall input to both land tests. It is an illustrative link, not a measured rainfall rate."}},{"k":"investigation_storm_evidence","v":{"s":"Storm evidence"}},{"k":"investigation_storm_step","v":{"s":"Observe storm"}},{"k":"investigation_test","v":{"s":"Test"}},{"k":"investigation_test_step","v":{"s":"Compare"}},{"k":"investigation_title","v":{"s":"Where will the stormwater go?"}},{"k":"investigation_trace","v":{"s":"Trace a droplet"}},{"k":"investigation_trace_step","v":{"s":"Trace water"}},{"k":"investigation_try_forest","v":{"s":"Test forest cover"}},{"k":"investigation_try_grass","v":{"s":"Test grass cover"}},{"k":"investigation_unfair","v":{"s":"Other inputs changed, or the selected cover is missing. Restore the fair test before recording evidence."}},{"k":"investigation_use_rain","v":{"s":"Use gentle rain"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_environment_title","v":{"s":"Comparing environments"}},{"k":"pilot_form_cloud_label_v2","v":{"s":"Cloud parcel"}},{"k":"pilot_form_ice_science_v2","v":{"s":"This model turns cloud water into ice at its freezing boundary. Real cloud droplets can remain liquid below 0°C. Ice can grow by deposition of vapor, while freezing changes liquid water into ice."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_cloud_parcel","v":{"s":"Your parcel contains cloud droplets. Collect droplets to grow a falling particle, or explore colder air above."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_vapor_climb_model","v":{"s":"Rise with the air. This model places cloud base near {m} m, where the rising parcel cools to saturation."}},{"k":"measure_the_storm","v":{"s":"Measure the storm"}},{"k":"no_lightning_flash_is_modeled_yet","v":{"s":"No lightning flash is modeled yet, so there is no flash-to-thunder interval to measure. Increase cloud depth, moisture, or updraft to build electrical activity."}},{"k":"interactive_storm_chamber","v":{"s":"Interactive storm chamber"}},{"k":"precipitation_lab_title","v":{"s":"🌧️ Precipitation Lab"}},{"k":"build_a_cloud_shape_the_temperature","v":{"s":"Build a cloud, shape the temperature profile, and watch water reach the surface as rain, snow, wintry mix, freezing rain, hail, or virga."}},{"k":"precipitation_lab_visual_mode","v":{"s":"Precipitation lab visual mode"}},{"k":"two_d_chamber","v":{"s":"2D chamber"}},{"k":"view_this_setup_in_3d_cloud_chamber","v":{"s":"View this setup in 3D cloud chamber"}},{"k":"three_d_cloud_chamber","v":{"s":"3D cloud chamber"}},{"k":"continue_this_precipitation_setup","v":{"s":"Continue this precipitation setup into the full 3D water journey"}},{"k":"full_journey_arrow","v":{"s":"Full journey →"}},{"k":"interactive_3d_cloud_chamber_showing","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D cloud chamber showing cloud formation, airflow, thermal layers, and precipitation."}},{"k":"animated_cross_section_of_cloud_growth","v":{"s":"Animated cross-section of cloud growth, airflow, and precipitation."}},{"k":"choose_a_3d_science_viewpoint","v":{"s":"Choose a 3D science viewpoint"}},{"k":"intensity_index","v":{"s":"Intensity index"}},{"k":"move_storm_lifecycle_backward","v":{"s":"Move storm lifecycle backward"}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"Previous"}},{"k":"static","v":{"s":"Static"}},{"k":"pause_time","v":{"s":"Pause time"}},{"k":"play_storm","v":{"s":"Play storm"}},{"k":"move_storm_lifecycle_forward","v":{"s":"Move storm lifecycle forward"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"qualitative_ground_accumulation","v":{"s":"Qualitative ground accumulation indices"}},{"k":"no_comparison_baseline_saved_yet","v":{"s":"No comparison baseline saved yet."}},{"k":"comparison_ready_the_highlighted_deltas","v":{"s":"Comparison ready: the highlighted deltas are your evidence."}},{"k":"baseline_saved_change_one_control","v":{"s":"Baseline saved: change one control to create a testable difference."}},{"k":"follow_the_highlighted_droplet_and_choose","v":{"s":"Follow the highlighted droplet and choose its path when it reaches the ground."}},{"k":"watch_water_move_between_ocean_air_land","v":{"s":"Watch water move between ocean, air, land, and plants. Then try one change to see what shifts."}},{"k":"start_here","v":{"s":"Start here"}},{"k":"choose_one_way_to_explore","v":{"s":"Choose one way to explore"}},{"k":"play_the_animated_water_cycle","v":{"s":"Play the animated water cycle"}},{"k":"watch_the_cycle","v":{"s":"▶ Watch the cycle"}},{"k":"see_water_move_through_the_system","v":{"s":"See water move through the system"}},{"k":"open_climate_controls_and_run_a_one_var","v":{"s":"Open climate controls and run a one-variable experiment"}},{"k":"change_the_weather","v":{"s":"Change the weather"}},{"k":"test_sunlight_temperature_or_wind","v":{"s":"Test sunlight, temperature, or wind"}},{"k":"systems_view_six_connected_water_cycle","v":{"s":"Systems view: six connected water-cycle processes"}},{"k":"exit_focus_canvas","v":{"s":"Exit Focus Canvas"}},{"k":"focus_canvas","v":{"s":"Focus Canvas"}},{"k":"exit_focus_canvas_mode","v":{"s":"Exit Focus Canvas mode"}},{"k":"enter_focus_canvas_mode","v":{"s":"Enter Focus Canvas mode"}},{"k":"full_explorer","v":{"s":"↙ Full explorer"}},{"k":"focus_canvas_button","v":{"s":"🎯 Focus Canvas"}},{"k":"update_scenario_baseline","v":{"s":"Update scenario baseline"}},{"k":"save_current_scenario_as_baseline","v":{"s":"Save current scenario as baseline"}},{"k":"update_scenario_comparison_baseline","v":{"s":"Update scenario comparison baseline"}},{"k":"save_current_scenario_as_comparison","v":{"s":"Save current scenario as comparison baseline"}},{"k":"baseline_button","v":{"s":"↻ Baseline"}},{"k":"set_baseline_button","v":{"s":"⊕ Set baseline"}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_fb1","v":{"s":"Wind helps speed up evaporation by carrying moist air away, but solar energy is the heat source that drives the phase change."}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_fb3","v":{"s":"Gravity pulls water downward (precipitation, runoff), whereas solar energy drives it upward via evaporation."}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_fb4","v":{"s":"The Moon causes ocean tides but does not heat water to drive evaporation."}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_opt1","v":{"s":"Wind"}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_opt2","v":{"s":"Solar energy"}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_opt3","v":{"s":"Gravity"}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_opt4","v":{"s":"Moon"}},{"k":"quiz_35_1_q","v":{"s":"What drives evaporation?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_fb1","v":{"s":"Evaporation is liquid water turning into invisible gas. Clouds are formed when this gas cools and turns back to liquid."}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_fb2","v":{"s":"Precipitation is rain or snow falling out of clouds, not the process that forms the clouds themselves."}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_fb4","v":{"s":"Infiltration is water soaking into the soil, which is the opposite of cloud formation."}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_opt1","v":{"s":"Evaporation"}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_opt2","v":{"s":"Precipitation"}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_opt3","v":{"s":"Condensation"}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_opt4","v":{"s":"Infiltration"}},{"k":"quiz_35_2_q","v":{"s":"What forms clouds?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_fb1","v":{"s":"Lakes evaporate water, but oceans cover over 70% of Earth's surface and contain 97% of its water, making them the primary source."}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_fb2","v":{"s":"Rivers flow to the sea and have small surface areas compared to the vast oceans."}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_fb4","v":{"s":"Soil releases moisture (evapotranspiration), but oceans are by far the largest source of atmospheric moisture."}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_opt1","v":{"s":"Lakes"}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_opt2","v":{"s":"Rivers"}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_opt3","v":{"s":"Oceans"}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_opt4","v":{"s":"Soil"}},{"k":"quiz_35_3_q","v":{"s":"Where does most evaporation occur?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_fb1","v":{"s":"Rain falling is precipitation. Transpiration is water rising up through plants and escaping from their leaves."}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_fb3","v":{"s":"Snow melting is a phase change from solid to liquid, not related to plant transpiration."}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_fb4","v":{"s":"Rivers flowing is runoff or collection, not a biological release of water."}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_opt1","v":{"s":"Rain falling"}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_opt2","v":{"s":"Water release from plants"}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_opt3","v":{"s":"Snow melting"}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_opt4","v":{"s":"Rivers flowing"}},{"k":"quiz_35_4_q","v":{"s":"What is transpiration?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_fb2","v":{"s":"Freshwater is much scarcer! Only 3% of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is frozen in ice caps."}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_fb3","v":{"s":"A quarter of Earth's water is not fresh. Over 97% is salty ocean water."}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_fb4","v":{"s":"Half of Earth's water is not fresh. Freshwater is a tiny fraction of global water."}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_opt1","v":{"s":"3%"}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_opt2","v":{"s":"10%"}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_opt3","v":{"s":"25%"}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_opt4","v":{"s":"50%"}},{"k":"quiz_35_5_q","v":{"s":"How much of Earth's water is freshwater?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_fb1","v":{"s":"Clouds are made of condensed water droplets. Stomata are biological structures on plant leaves."}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_fb3","v":{"s":"Underground channels are part of aquifers. Stomata are tiny leaf pores used for gas exchange."}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_fb4","v":{"s":"Rain droplets are precipitation. Stomata are pores that let plants release water vapor."}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_opt1","v":{"s":"Types of clouds"}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_opt2","v":{"s":"Tiny pores on leaves"}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_opt3","v":{"s":"Underground rivers"}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_opt4","v":{"s":"Rain droplets"}},{"k":"quiz_35_6_q","v":{"s":"What are stomata?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_fb2","v":{"s":"Freezing is liquid water turning to solid ice. Sublimation bypasses the liquid state entirely."}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_fb3","v":{"s":"Rain evaporating is liquid turning to gas, whereas sublimation starts with solid ice or snow."}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_fb4","v":{"s":"Cloud formation is condensation (gas to liquid), not solid to gas."}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_opt1","v":{"s":"Ice turning directly to vapor"}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_opt2","v":{"s":"Water freezing"}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_opt3","v":{"s":"Rain evaporating"}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_opt4","v":{"s":"Clouds forming"}},{"k":"quiz_35_7_q","v":{"s":"What is sublimation?"}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_fb1","v":{"s":"Removing trees reduces the total leaf area, which decreases transpiration and makes the local climate drier."}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_fb3","v":{"s":"Fewer trees mean less moisture is pumped into the air, leading to fewer clouds and less local rainfall."}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_fb4","v":{"s":"Trees are key hydrologic pumps. Removing them severely disrupts local water cycles and increases flooding."}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_opt1","v":{"s":"Increases evaporation"}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_opt2","v":{"s":"Reduces transpiration and increases runoff"}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_opt3","v":{"s":"Creates more clouds"}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_opt4","v":{"s":"Has no effect"}},{"k":"quiz_35_8_q","v":{"s":"How does deforestation affect the water cycle?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_fb1","v":{"s":"Wind enhances the evaporation rate by removing the boundary layer of moist air, but solar radiation is the thermodynamic driver."}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_fb3","v":{"s":"Gravity is a downward force driving infiltration and precipitation, whereas solar energy drives water upward."}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_fb4","v":{"s":"The Moon drives tides but does not supply the heat energy required for the latent heat of vaporization."}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_opt1","v":{"s":"Wind"}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_opt2","v":{"s":"Solar energy"}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_opt3","v":{"s":"Gravity"}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_opt4","v":{"s":"Moon"}},{"k":"quiz_68_1_q","v":{"s":"What drives evaporation?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_fb1","v":{"s":"Evaporation is liquid transitioning to gas. Clouds form when this gas cools and condenses back into liquid droplets."}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_fb2","v":{"s":"Precipitation occurs when cloud droplets grow too heavy and fall, not when clouds form."}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_fb4","v":{"s":"Infiltration is surface water soaking into ground soil, unrelated to atmospheric clouds."}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_opt1","v":{"s":"Evaporation"}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_opt2","v":{"s":"Precipitation"}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_opt3","v":{"s":"Condensation"}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_opt4","v":{"s":"Infiltration"}},{"k":"quiz_68_2_q","v":{"s":"What forms clouds?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_fb1","v":{"s":"Lakes contribute a tiny fraction of global evaporation compared to the vast surface area of the oceans."}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_fb2","v":{"s":"Rivers represent a tiny portion of global surface water and account for very little evaporation."}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_fb4","v":{"s":"Soil moisture evaporation is limited by capillary action and plant coverage."}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_opt1","v":{"s":"Lakes"}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_opt2","v":{"s":"Rivers"}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_opt3","v":{"s":"Oceans"}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_opt4","v":{"s":"Soil"}},{"k":"quiz_68_3_q","v":{"s":"Where does most evaporation occur?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_fb1","v":{"s":"Rain falling is precipitation. Transpiration is water vapor release from plant stomata."}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_fb3","v":{"s":"Melting is a solid-to-liquid transition, not a biological release of moisture."}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_fb4","v":{"s":"River flow is runoff and collection, not a plant-driven water transport process."}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_opt1","v":{"s":"Rain falling"}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_opt2","v":{"s":"Water release from plants"}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_opt3","v":{"s":"Snow melting"}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_opt4","v":{"s":"Rivers flowing"}},{"k":"quiz_68_4_q","v":{"s":"What is transpiration?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_fb2","v":{"s":"Freshwater is much scarcer! Only 3% is fresh, and about 68% of that is locked in glaciers and ice sheets."}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_fb3","v":{"s":"Over 97% of Earth's water is salty ocean water. Fresh water is a tiny portion."}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_fb4","v":{"s":"Half of Earth's water is saline. Freshwater is a scarce resource."}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_opt1","v":{"s":"3%"}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_opt2","v":{"s":"10%"}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_opt3","v":{"s":"25%"}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_opt4","v":{"s":"50%"}},{"k":"quiz_68_5_q","v":{"s":"How much of Earth's water is freshwater?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_fb1","v":{"s":"Clouds are made of condensed vapor. Stomata are microscopic pores in plant epidermal layers."}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_fb3","v":{"s":"Subsurface channels are aquifers. Stomata are biological valves regulating leaf transpiration."}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_fb4","v":{"s":"Rain droplets are precipitation. Stomata are microscopic openings on leaves."}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_opt1","v":{"s":"Types of clouds"}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_opt2","v":{"s":"Tiny pores on leaves"}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_opt3","v":{"s":"Underground rivers"}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_opt4","v":{"s":"Rain droplets"}},{"k":"quiz_68_6_q","v":{"s":"What are stomata?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_fb2","v":{"s":"Freezing is liquid to solid. Sublimation is the transition from solid directly to gas."}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_fb3","v":{"s":"Rain evaporating is liquid to gas, whereas sublimation starts with solid ice or snow."}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_fb4","v":{"s":"Cloud formation is condensation, which is gas to liquid."}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_opt1","v":{"s":"Ice turning directly to vapor"}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_opt2","v":{"s":"Water freezing"}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_opt3","v":{"s":"Rain evaporating"}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_opt4","v":{"s":"Clouds forming"}},{"k":"quiz_68_7_q","v":{"s":"What is sublimation?"}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_fb1","v":{"s":"Without tree leaves to transpire and block wind, overall evapotranspiration drops, drying the local climate."}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_fb3","v":{"s":"Deforestation reduces the water pump effect, decreasing atmospheric humidity and cloud formation."}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_fb4","v":{"s":"Trees are vital hydrological regulators. Deforestation leads to severe soil erosion and immediate flooding."}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_opt1","v":{"s":"Increases evaporation"}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_opt2","v":{"s":"Reduces transpiration and increases runoff"}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_opt3","v":{"s":"Creates more clouds"}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_opt4","v":{"s":"Has no effect"}},{"k":"quiz_68_8_q","v":{"s":"How does deforestation affect the water cycle?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_fb1","v":{"s":"This rate is too low. The average environmental lapse rate in the troposphere is 6.5°C per kilometer."}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_fb3","v":{"s":"This is the dry adiabatic lapse rate (9.8°C/km) for dry rising air, not the environmental profile."}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_fb4","v":{"s":"This cooling rate is too high. The average atmospheric cooling is around 6.5°C per 1000m."}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_opt1","v":{"s":"~2°C per 1000m"}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_opt2","v":{"s":"~6.5°C per 1000m"}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_opt3","v":{"s":"~10°C per 1000m"}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_opt4","v":{"s":"~15°C per 1000m"}},{"k":"quiz_912_1_q","v":{"s":"At what rate does air temperature decrease with altitude (environmental lapse rate)?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_fb2","v":{"s":"Boyle's Law relates gas pressure to volume, not fluid flow through soils."}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_fb3","v":{"s":"Ohm's Law relates voltage and current, though it shares mathematical forms with Darcy's Law."}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_fb4","v":{"s":"Bernoulli's equation applies to open pipe flow, not flow within saturated media."}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_opt1","v":{"s":"Darcy's Law"}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_opt2","v":{"s":"Boyle's Law"}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_opt3","v":{"s":"Ohm's Law"}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_opt4","v":{"s":"Bernoulli's Principle"}},{"k":"quiz_912_2_q","v":{"s":"What law governs groundwater flow through saturated porous media?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_fb1","v":{"s":"This value is too low. Water requires about 2.45 megajoules per kilogram to vaporize."}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_fb3","v":{"s":"This is the specific heat capacity of liquid water, not its latent heat of vaporization."}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_fb4","v":{"s":"This is close to the latent heat of fusion (melting ice) which is 0.334 MJ/kg."}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_opt1","v":{"s":"~1.0 MJ/kg"}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_opt2","v":{"s":"~2.45 MJ/kg"}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_opt3","v":{"s":"~4.18 MJ/kg"}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_opt4","v":{"s":"~0.33 MJ/kg"}},{"k":"quiz_912_3_q","v":{"s":"What is the latent heat of vaporization of water at 20°C?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_fb1","v":{"s":"This is too low. Saturation vapor pressure increases by approximately 7% per degree of heating."}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_fb3","v":{"s":"This is too high. The capacity grows exponentially but is about 7% per degree Celsius."}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_fb4","v":{"s":"This is too high. A 1 degree Celsius increase corresponds to a 7% capacity expansion."}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_opt1","v":{"s":"~2%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_opt2","v":{"s":"~7%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_opt3","v":{"s":"~15%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_opt4","v":{"s":"~25%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_4_q","v":{"s":"The Clausius-Clapeyron relation predicts saturation vapor pressure increases by what per °C?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_fb1","v":{"s":"Navier-Stokes equations model momentum in open fluid dynamics, not unsaturated flow in soils."}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_fb3","v":{"s":"Bernoulli's equation relates pressure and speed in open inviscid flows."}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_fb4","v":{"s":"Poiseuille's equation describes flow through open cylindrical pipes."}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_opt1","v":{"s":"Navier-Stokes"}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_opt2","v":{"s":"Richards' equation"}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_opt3","v":{"s":"Bernoulli's equation"}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_opt4","v":{"s":"Poiseuille's equation"}},{"k":"quiz_912_5_q","v":{"s":"What equation extends Darcy's Law to unsaturated flow?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_fb1","v":{"s":"This is too low. Average global cloud albedo is significant, reflecting about 30% of incoming light."}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_fb3","v":{"s":"While specific storm clouds can be highly reflective, the global average is around 30%."}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_fb4","v":{"s":"Only the densest storm clouds reflect 90%, whereas the global average is much lower."}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_opt1","v":{"s":"Reflects ~5%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_opt2","v":{"s":"Reflects ~30%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_opt3","v":{"s":"Reflects ~60%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_opt4","v":{"s":"Reflects ~90%"}},{"k":"quiz_912_6_q","v":{"s":"What is cloud albedo's approximate effect on solar radiation?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_fb1","v":{"s":"This is the atmospheric residence time before precipitation, not the ocean residence time."}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_fb2","v":{"s":"This is too short. Due to ocean volume, the average water molecule remains there for about 3,200 years."}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_fb4","v":{"s":"This is too long. Oceanic circulation and evaporation cycle molecules much faster."}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_opt1","v":{"s":"~9 days"}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_opt2","v":{"s":"~100 years"}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_opt3","v":{"s":"~3,200 years"}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_opt4","v":{"s":"~1 million years"}},{"k":"quiz_912_7_q","v":{"s":"What is the average residence time of a water molecule in the ocean?"}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_fb2","v":{"s":"This is a hydrological partition ratio, not the thermodynamic Bowen ratio."}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_fb3","v":{"s":"This is a global water budget balance, not the Bowen ratio."}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_fb4","v":{"s":"This relates to cloud cover fraction, not the Bowen heat flux ratio."}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_opt1","v":{"s":"Ratio of sensible to latent heat flux"}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_opt2","v":{"s":"Ratio of runoff to infiltration"}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_opt3","v":{"s":"Ratio of evaporation to precipitation"}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_opt4","v":{"s":"Ratio of cloud cover to clear sky"}},{"k":"quiz_912_8_q","v":{"s":"What is the Bowen ratio?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_fb1","v":{"s":"While some water soaks into the ground, puddles on sidewalks and streets mostly disappear because the sun heats them up into vapor."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_fb3","v":{"s":"Wind can help water evaporate faster by moving air, but the sun's heat is the main reason liquid water changes into gas."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_fb4","v":{"s":"Water molecules never sleep! The sun's energy makes them move faster and float up into the sky."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_opt1","v":{"s":"The ground drinks it"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_opt2","v":{"s":"The sun heats the water"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_opt3","v":{"s":"Wind blows it away"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_opt4","v":{"s":"It goes to sleep"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_1_q","v":{"s":"What makes puddles disappear on sunny days?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_fb1","v":{"s":"Clouds look soft like cotton, but they are actually made of billions of tiny liquid water droplets floating in the air."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_fb3","v":{"s":"Smoke comes from fires, but clouds in the sky are made of clean water droplets and ice crystals."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_fb4","v":{"s":"Air bubbles are trapped inside water, but clouds are water droplets trapped in the air!"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_opt1","v":{"s":"Cotton"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_opt2","v":{"s":"Tiny water drops"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_opt3","v":{"s":"Smoke"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_opt4","v":{"s":"Air bubbles"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_2_q","v":{"s":"What are clouds made of?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_fb1","v":{"s":"Stars are huge, burning suns far away in space. They do not fall from clouds!"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_fb3","v":{"s":"Leaves fall from trees in autumn, not from clouds in the sky."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_fb4","v":{"s":"Rocks are heavy parts of the ground. Only liquid or frozen water falls from clouds."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_opt1","v":{"s":"Stars"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_opt2","v":{"s":"Rain and snow"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_opt3","v":{"s":"Leaves"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_opt4","v":{"s":"Rocks"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_3_q","v":{"s":"What falls from clouds?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_fb1","v":{"s":"Water does not vanish! It collects in lakes, flows down rivers, and fills the oceans."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_fb2","v":{"s":"Rain must collect on the ground first before the sun can heat it to rise back up later."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_fb4","v":{"s":"Earth's gravity keeps water on our planet. It collects in oceans and lakes rather than escaping into space."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_opt1","v":{"s":"It disappears"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_opt2","v":{"s":"Back up to the sky"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_opt3","v":{"s":"Rivers, lakes, and oceans"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_opt4","v":{"s":"Into outer space"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_4_q","v":{"s":"Where does rain go after it falls?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_fb1","v":{"s":"Leaves can absorb a tiny bit of moisture, but plants get almost all their water by drinking it from the soil through their roots."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_fb3","v":{"s":"Flowers attract bees and make seeds, but they do not drink water from the soil."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_fb4","v":{"s":"All living things need water to survive, including plants!"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_opt1","v":{"s":"Through their leaves"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_opt2","v":{"s":"Through their roots"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_opt3","v":{"s":"Through their flowers"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_opt4","v":{"s":"They don't drink water"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_5_q","v":{"s":"How do plants drink water?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_fb1","v":{"s":"The sun provides warm heat, which warms water up instead of freezing it into ice."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_fb3","v":{"s":"Algae and plants can make water look green, but the sun heats it up so it evaporates."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_fb4","v":{"s":"Ocean water is already salty because of dissolved minerals, not because of the sun."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_opt1","v":{"s":"Freezes it"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_opt2","v":{"s":"Heats it up so it rises as vapor"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_opt3","v":{"s":"Turns it green"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_opt4","v":{"s":"Makes it salty"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_6_q","v":{"s":"What does the sun do to ocean water?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_fb2","v":{"s":"Stars are massive bodies in space, whereas water vapor just condenses into cloud droplets."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_fb3","v":{"s":"Water vapor gas is invisible, but when it cools and condenses, it becomes visible liquid droplets (clouds)."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_fb4","v":{"s":"Water does not catch fire! Cooling vapor turns back into liquid water."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_opt1","v":{"s":"It turns into cloud drops"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_opt2","v":{"s":"It becomes a star"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_opt3","v":{"s":"It stays invisible"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_opt4","v":{"s":"It catches fire"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_7_q","v":{"s":"What happens when water vapor gets cold up high?"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_fb1","v":{"s":"Groundwater is part of the cycle. It flows slowly and emerges at natural springs or is pumped up through wells."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_fb3","v":{"s":"Digger wells do reach groundwater, but natural springs bubble up to the surface without any digging."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_fb4","v":{"s":"Springs flow continuously, even on sunny days, because groundwater moves very slowly."}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_opt1","v":{"s":"No, never"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_opt2","v":{"s":"Yes, through springs and wells"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_opt3","v":{"s":"Only if you dig"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_opt4","v":{"s":"Only on rainy days"}},{"k":"quiz_k2_8_q","v":{"s":"Can water underground come back up?"}},{"k":"sw_diff_coordinator_desc","v":{"s":"18 hours / year, standard events. Default."}},{"k":"sw_diff_coordinator_label","v":{"s":"Watershed Coordinator"}},{"k":"sw_diff_director_desc","v":{"s":"14 hours / year, harsher events. Real constraint."}},{"k":"sw_diff_director_label","v":{"s":"Watershed Director"}},{"k":"sw_diff_volunteer_desc","v":{"s":"24 hours / year, gentler events. For first runs."}},{"k":"sw_diff_volunteer_label","v":{"s":"New Volunteer"}},{"k":"sw_ev_algalBloom_desc","v":{"s":"A cyanobacteria bloom closed swim beaches and prompted advisories. Public support shifts toward stronger watershed protection."}},{"k":"sw_ev_algalBloom_name","v":{"s":"Cyanobacteria bloom"}},{"k":"sw_ev_beaverExpand_desc","v":{"s":"Beavers expanded their territory and built three new dam complexes in the floodplain."}},{"k":"sw_ev_beaverExpand_name","v":{"s":"Beaver complex expands"}},{"k":"sw_ev_drought_desc","v":{"s":"Low summer flows raised stream temperatures and concentrated pollutants. Cold-water species took a hit."}},{"k":"sw_ev_drought_name","v":{"s":"Drought year"}},{"k":"sw_ev_erosionEvent_desc","v":{"s":"A bend in the river undercut a road shoulder. Public attention focuses on streambank stabilization."}},{"k":"sw_ev_erosionEvent_name","v":{"s":"Major bank erosion"}},{"k":"sw_ev_farmSold_desc","v":{"s":"A long-running family dairy operation sold to a residential developer. BMP gains on that land reset."}},{"k":"sw_ev_farmSold_name","v":{"s":"Farm sold for development"}},{"k":"sw_ev_fundingBump_desc","v":{"s":"A federal grant lands. Stewardship hours next year will be +5."}},{"k":"sw_ev_fundingBump_name","v":{"s":"EPA / FEMA grant"}},{"k":"sw_ev_majorFlood_desc","v":{"s":"A 10-year flood scoured stream banks and washed sediment downstream. Buffers without good root systems lost ground."}},{"k":"sw_ev_majorFlood_name","v":{"s":"Major flood"}},{"k":"sw_ev_salmonReturn_desc","v":{"s":"Returning Atlantic salmon (or alewife runs) detected in the mainstem. Major morale boost and federal attention."}},{"k":"sw_ev_salmonReturn_name","v":{"s":"Atlantic salmon detected"}},{"k":"sw_ev_sewageRelease_desc","v":{"s":"A wastewater treatment plant bypass during a heavy storm released untreated discharge. Mainstem quality drops."}},{"k":"sw_ev_sewageRelease_name","v":{"s":"Sewage discharge"}},{"k":"sw_ev_volunteerSurge_desc","v":{"s":"A successful River Day brought 200+ volunteers. Citizen monitoring + cleanup boost across the board."}},{"k":"sw_ev_volunteerSurge_name","v":{"s":"Volunteer surge"}},{"k":"sw_fb_agCleansUp_msg","v":{"s":"Lower agricultural runoff cleaned up the river mainstem."}},{"k":"sw_fb_beaverHelpsFloodplain_msg","v":{"s":"Beaver-built wetlands attenuated flood pulses and improved mainstem water quality."}},{"k":"sw_fb_bufferFeedsHeadwaters_msg","v":{"s":"Healthy forest buffers cooled and cleaned headwater streams."}},{"k":"sw_fb_runRestoration_msg","v":{"s":"Connected, shaded river segments support documented anadromous fish returns."}},{"k":"sw_hint_agCleansUp_fired","v":{"s":"Farm runoff dropped enough (agricultural quality over 60) that the mainstem cleaned up on its own — less nitrogen, phosphorus, and sediment reaching the river."}},{"k":"sw_hint_agCleansUp_near","v":{"s":"Agricultural quality is {v}. BMP outreach is cheap (4h) and would tip it past 60, cleaning the mainstem via the runoff feedback."}},{"k":"sw_hint_beaverHelpsFloodplain_fired","v":{"s":"Your floodplain wetlands crossed 60% — beaver-built storage slowed the flood pulses and let sediment settle, improving mainstem water quality downstream."}},{"k":"sw_hint_beaverHelpsFloodplain_near","v":{"s":"Floodplain wetlands sit at {v}. A single Beaver Dam Analog would push past 60 and start cleaning the mainstem for you."}},{"k":"sw_hint_bufferFeedsHeadwaters_fired","v":{"s":"Your forest buffers crossed 70% quality — shade cooled the water and roots filtered it, so cleaner cold water flowed downhill into the headwaters (+quality there, for free)."}},{"k":"sw_hint_bufferFeedsHeadwaters_near","v":{"s":"Forest-buffer quality is at {v}. Get it past 70 (one riparian buffer planting) and it will cool and clean the headwaters automatically every year after."}},{"k":"sw_hint_runRestoration_fired","v":{"s":"A connected, shaded mainstem (connectivity over 60, with healthy buffers) is now supporting documented anadromous fish returns — morale rose across every component."}},{"k":"sw_hint_runRestoration_near","v":{"s":"Mainstem connectivity is {v}. Cross 60 with buffers already healthy and you unlock fish returns plus a support boost watershed-wide."}},{"k":"sw_tech_beaverDamAnalog_desc","v":{"s":"Build a low-cost wood-and-stone structure that mimics beaver dam function. Encourages real beaver recolonization. Restores wet meadow conditions."}},{"k":"sw_tech_bmpOutreach_desc","v":{"s":"Work with farmers on Best Management Practices: cover crops, livestock fencing, manure storage, buffer easements. Real Maine programs."}},{"k":"sw_tech_bufferPlant_desc","v":{"s":"Plant native trees and shrubs along stream banks. Slow buildup that pays off in shade, bank stability, and nutrient filtering for decades."}},{"k":"sw_tech_citizenScience_desc","v":{"s":"Train volunteer water-quality monitors. Slow but builds long-term community support and detects problems early."}},{"k":"sw_tech_damRemoval_desc","v":{"s":"Remove or breach a barrier dam. Huge connectivity gain. Politically expensive: some landowners and recreational users will be upset."}},{"k":"sw_tech_easement_desc","v":{"s":"Pay a landowner to permanently protect a riparian or upland parcel. The single highest-impact and highest-cost intervention."}},{"k":"sw_tech_fishPassage_desc","v":{"s":"Build a fish ladder or nature-like bypass around a barrier. Cheaper than dam removal and politically easier, but less effective for some species."}},{"k":"sw_tech_publicEd_desc","v":{"s":"Watershed festivals, school programs, paddle events. Build community ownership of the watershed."}},{"k":"sw_tech_rest_desc","v":{"s":"No active intervention this year. Some natural recovery; some drift."}},{"k":"sw_tech_stormwater_desc","v":{"s":"Install rain gardens, swales, permeable pavement, or detention basins in developed areas. Slows and filters stormwater pulses."}},{"k":"vocab_aquifer","v":{"s":"An underground layer of water-bearing permeable rock, gravel, sand, or silt from which groundwater can be extracted."}},{"k":"vocab_bowen_ratio","v":{"s":"The ratio of sensible heat to latent heat loss from the surface, determining local climate feedbacks."}},{"k":"vocab_clausius_clapeyron","v":{"s":"The thermodynamic relationship showing that atmospheric moisture capacity increases by about 7% per degree Celsius of warming."}},{"k":"vocab_collection","v":{"s":"The accumulation of water in oceans, lakes, rivers, and underground reservoirs, completing the surface loop."}},{"k":"vocab_condensation","v":{"s":"The process where water vapor gas cools and changes back into liquid water droplets, forming clouds."}},{"k":"vocab_darcy_s_law","v":{"s":"A mathematical equation that describes the flow of a fluid through a porous medium, governing groundwater."}},{"k":"vocab_evaporation","v":{"s":"The process where liquid water absorbs thermal energy and changes into water vapor gas, rising into the atmosphere."}},{"k":"vocab_infiltration","v":{"s":"The process by which water on the ground surface enters soil and rock; some infiltrated water can contribute to groundwater recharge."}},{"k":"vocab_latent_heat","v":{"s":"The heat energy absorbed or released by water during a phase change (like liquid to gas) without changing temperature."}},{"k":"vocab_pfas","v":{"s":"Synthetic per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances that contaminate water supplies and resist natural breakdown."}},{"k":"vocab_precipitation","v":{"s":"Water falling from clouds to Earth's surface as rain, snow, sleet, or hail when droplets grow too heavy."}},{"k":"vocab_riparian_buffer","v":{"s":"A vegetated area next to a water body, usually forested, which helps shade streams and filter run-off."}},{"k":"vocab_sublimation","v":{"s":"The direct transition of water from solid ice or snow into water vapor gas, bypassing the liquid phase."}},{"k":"vocab_transpiration","v":{"s":"The evaporation of water from plant leaves through tiny pores called stomata, acting as a natural pump."}},{"k":"vocab_watershed","v":{"s":"An area of land where all of the water that falls in it drains off into a common outlet like a river or bay."}},{"k":"ws_agriculturalWatershed_casework","v":{"s":"Maine has roughly 7,500 farms covering about 1.3 million acres. The Maine Soil and Water Conservation Districts operate the state-side BMP outreach; USDA NRCS provides federal cost-share. Dairy farms in the Sebasticook and Kennebec watersheds have implemented manure-handling and buffer projects with measurable downstream quality improvement; comparable work in the St. John watershed has helped Aroostook potato production."}},{"k":"ws_agriculturalWatershed_desc","v":{"s":"Dairy farms, hay fields, row crops, blueberry barrens. The dominant land use in central Maine watersheds. Manure runoff, fertilizer, sediment from tilled land all flow downstream. BMPs (Best Management Practices) can cut runoff by 50-80%."}},{"k":"ws_agriculturalWatershed_knowledge","v":{"s":"Agricultural land delivers three primary watershed insults: sediment from tilled or overgrazed land, nutrients (nitrogen and phosphorus) from manure and fertilizer, and pathogens from livestock waste. Best Management Practices include cover cropping, contour farming, livestock fencing from streams, manure storage upgrades, riparian buffer easements, and reduced-till or no-till cropping. Documented BMP implementations cut watershed nutrient export by 50 to 80 percent on participating farms."}},{"k":"ws_agriculturalWatershed_moderncontext","v":{"s":"Farm consolidation pressures BMP adoption (the smallest farms have the thinnest margins to invest in capital improvements). PFAS contamination from historic biosolid spreading has surfaced as a major Maine farm-water issue post-2022, with state-led testing and remediation programs. The Maine Farmland Trust links farmland protection to watershed protection."}},{"k":"ws_agriculturalWatershed_role","v":{"s":"Nutrient + sediment source"}},{"k":"ws_floodplainWetlands_casework","v":{"s":"Beaver populations have recovered to perhaps 10 to 15 million across North America but remain far below historic in most Northeast watersheds. Beaver Dam Analog (BDA) restoration mimics beaver work with imported wood, rock, and posts; it is increasingly used where beavers have not naturally recolonized. The Methow Beaver Project in Washington and similar Maine pilots have shown that BDAs can trigger natural beaver return within 2 to 4 years."}},{"k":"ws_floodplainWetlands_desc","v":{"s":"Beaver dam complexes and adjacent wet meadows. Slow flood pulses, recharge groundwater, filter nutrients, support amphibians, waterfowl, moose, otter. Beaver Dam Analogs (BDAs) mimic this work where beavers have not returned."}},{"k":"ws_floodplainWetlands_knowledge","v":{"s":"Beaver-built wetlands are the textbook example of ecosystem engineering. A single beaver complex can create up to 10 acres of wet meadow that stores flood water, recharges groundwater, traps sediment, filters nutrients, and supports moose, waterfowl, river otter, brook trout, and amphibians. Wetland complexes also act as firebreaks during dry years. North American beaver populations were estimated at 60 to 400 million pre-contact; the European fur trade crashed them to under 100,000 by 1900."}},{"k":"ws_floodplainWetlands_moderncontext","v":{"s":"Beavers face conflict with road managers and downstream landowners over flooding. Lethal trapping continues in Maine. Beaver Deceiver flow-control devices are the non-lethal alternative; Wabanaki communities have led some of the strongest beaver-protection advocacy in the region. Climate-resilience planners increasingly cite beavers as low-cost natural infrastructure."}},{"k":"ws_floodplainWetlands_role","v":{"s":"Beaver-built flood storage"}},{"k":"ws_forestBuffer_casework","v":{"s":"Maine's Shoreland Zoning Act (1971) regulates the first 75 feet around great ponds and 250 feet around rivers, but enforcement is uneven and exemptions for development are routine. Land trust easements have been more effective than regulation in many Maine watersheds. The Maine Coast Heritage Trust and Atlantic Salmon Federation have funded buffer-replanting on hundreds of farm streams; cover-cropping plus tree-row plantings cut runoff measurably within 3 to 5 years."}},{"k":"ws_forestBuffer_desc","v":{"s":"The strip of mature forest along stream banks. Shade keeps water cold, roots stabilize banks, leaf litter feeds aquatic insects, wood falls in to create habitat. A 50-foot intact buffer is the single most cost-effective stream protection."}},{"k":"ws_forestBuffer_knowledge","v":{"s":"Riparian buffers do five distinct jobs at once: shade keeps water cold for trout and salmon parr, root systems stabilize banks against erosion, leaf litter is the primary food source for stream insects (which feed fish), woody debris falls in to create pools and cover, and the buffer filters runoff from adjacent agricultural and developed land. The pioneering research by Allan and others established that even a 30-foot intact buffer captures most of the runoff-quality benefit, and a 100-foot buffer provides the full hydrological function."}},{"k":"ws_forestBuffer_moderncontext","v":{"s":"Buffer policy in Maine remains fragmented across jurisdictions. The strongest buffer protections often come from voluntary landowner agreements rather than zoning. Climate-driven storm events make buffers MORE important (they hold the streambank during high flows), so the federal Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act has lifted buffer-restoration funding."}},{"k":"ws_forestBuffer_role","v":{"s":"Riparian shade and filter"}},{"k":"ws_headwaterStreams_casework","v":{"s":"The Eastern Brook Trout Joint Venture maps the status of native populations across the species range. Maine retains an unusually large portion of historic native brook trout habitat compared to the rest of the Northeast. Most successful headwater protection has come from upper-watershed conservation easements and replacement of undersized culverts that act as warm-water bottlenecks."}},{"k":"ws_headwaterStreams_desc","v":{"s":"High-elevation forested streams. Native brook trout, native eastern brook trout, water temperature below 20°C. The cleanest water in the watershed; everything downstream is shaped by what happens here."}},{"k":"ws_headwaterStreams_knowledge","v":{"s":"Headwater streams are first-order channels: small enough to step across, fed by springs and seeps, almost always shaded by mature forest. They make up roughly 60 to 80 percent of the total stream-mile length in a typical Maine watershed but receive a fraction of the regulatory attention. Native brook trout require water below about 20°C, dissolved oxygen above 7 mg/L, and woody debris for cover. Every degree of warming pushes their range north and uphill."}},{"k":"ws_headwaterStreams_moderncontext","v":{"s":"Climate change is the central long-term threat to Maine headwater streams. Several Maine Audubon and Wabanaki community projects have led culvert replacement and shade-tree planting campaigns. The 2023 Maine Climate Action Plan named cold-water-fishery protection as a priority but funding has lagged."}},{"k":"ws_headwaterStreams_role","v":{"s":"Cold-water indicator"}},{"k":"ws_riverMainstem_casework","v":{"s":"The Penobscot River Restoration Project (Penobscot Nation, NGOs, hydro companies) removed Veazie Dam in 2012 and Great Works Dam in 2013 while preserving most generation through upgrades elsewhere. River herring returns increased over 1000-fold in the first decade post-removal. The Kennebec saw Edwards Dam come down in 1999 and Fort Halifax in 2008. The Sebasticook tributary alone now hosts the largest river-herring run on the East Coast."}},{"k":"ws_riverMainstem_desc","v":{"s":"The big channel through the watershed. Historically the route for Atlantic salmon, alewife, sea-run brook trout, eels, sturgeon. In Maine, dam barriers block most of these runs; recent removals (Edwards Dam 1999, Fort Halifax 2008, Veazie 2012, Great Works 2013) reopened sections."}},{"k":"ws_riverMainstem_knowledge","v":{"s":"Anadromous fish (born in fresh water, mature at sea, return upstream to spawn) include Atlantic salmon, alewife, blueback herring, American shad, sea lamprey, and sea-run brook trout. Each species has different barrier-passage tolerance: alewife can use modest fish ladders; Atlantic salmon need near-full passage; sturgeon need almost-complete connectivity. Dam barriers degrade water quality upstream too: stagnant impoundments warm, accumulate sediment, and lose dissolved oxygen."}},{"k":"ws_riverMainstem_moderncontext","v":{"s":"The Penobscot Nation has led the legal, political, and ecological work on its ancestral river. Ongoing dam-removal campaigns target the Mattaceunk, Milford, and lower Kennebec dams. NOAA and the Atlantic Salmon Federation track returns annually; numbers are recovering but still well below historic."}},{"k":"ws_riverMainstem_role","v":{"s":"Migratory fish corridor"}},{"k":"ws_suburbanEdges_casework","v":{"s":"Portland, ME has documented stream impairment along the Capisic Brook and Stroudwater drainages tied directly to impervious cover. Green Stormwater Infrastructure (rain gardens, swales, permeable pavement, detention basins, green roofs) can offset 50 to 80 percent of the conventional pulse. The Maine Stormwater BMP Manual is the regulatory reference; municipal stormwater (MS4) permits require larger towns to implement."}},{"k":"ws_suburbanEdges_desc","v":{"s":"Subdivisions, parking lots, lawns. Impervious surfaces deliver pulses of warm polluted water to streams during storms. Lawn fertilizer and pet waste are the modern eutrophication inputs. Green stormwater infrastructure can offset the impact."}},{"k":"ws_suburbanEdges_knowledge","v":{"s":"Impervious surface (roads, roofs, parking lots, driveways) shapes urban and suburban hydrology more than any other variable. Above 10 percent watershed-wide impervious cover, stream biology measurably degrades; above 25 percent, most native fish populations are gone. Stormwater pulses are warm, fast, and pollutant-laden: lawn fertilizer, dog waste, vehicle drip, road salt, sediment from construction. Conventional drainage (curb, gutter, pipe) delivers all of it directly to streams."}},{"k":"ws_suburbanEdges_moderncontext","v":{"s":"Most suburban watershed work in Maine happens at municipal scale through MS4 permits, town stormwater ordinances, and watershed-association advocacy. Climate-resilience funding under the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act has dramatically increased available capital for retrofit. The biggest challenge is older developments built before stormwater regulation that have no easy retrofit path."}},{"k":"ws_suburbanEdges_role","v":{"s":"Stormwater + impervious surface"}},{"k":"pilot_back","v":{"s":"Back"}},{"k":"pilot_freezing_short","v":{"s":"freezing"}},{"k":"pilot_fwd","v":{"s":"Fwd"}},{"k":"mode_3d_simulation","v":{"s":"3D SIMULATION"}},{"k":"mode_nav_label","v":{"s":"Water Cycle modes"}},{"k":"pilot_coverage","v":{"s":"{done} / {total}"}},{"k":"pilot_do_this_next","v":{"s":"Next goal"}},{"k":"pilot_flight_controls","v":{"s":"Flight controls"}},{"k":"pilot_flight_deck","v":{"s":"Flight deck"}},{"k":"pilot_humidity","v":{"s":"Humidity"}},{"k":"pilot_loops_many","v":{"s":"{n} full cycles"}},{"k":"pilot_loops_one","v":{"s":"{n} full cycle"}},{"k":"pilot_open_system_map_instead","v":{"s":"Open the System Map instead"}},{"k":"pilot_starting_conditions","v":{"s":"Starting conditions"}},{"k":"pilot_you","v":{"s":"You"}},{"k":"sect_nav_label","v":{"s":"Explorer sections"}},{"k":"pilot_air","v":{"s":"Air"}},{"k":"pilot_altitude","v":{"s":"Altitude"}},{"k":"pilot_saturation","v":{"s":"Saturation"}},{"k":"sect_10_years_of_one_watershed","v":{"s":"10 years of one watershed"}},{"k":"sect_be_the_water","v":{"s":"Be the Water"}},{"k":"sect_build_rain_snow_and_hail","v":{"s":"Build rain, snow, and hail"}},{"k":"sect_explore","v":{"s":"Explore"}},{"k":"sect_fly_one_parcel_through_every_form","v":{"s":"Pilot one parcel through changing states and pathways"}},{"k":"sect_steward","v":{"s":"Steward"}},{"k":"sect_water_worlds","v":{"s":"Water Worlds"}},{"k":"sect_water_worlds_blurb","v":{"s":"Edit a valley and investigate repeatable storms"}},{"k":"sect_storm_lab","v":{"s":"Storm Lab"}},{"k":"sect_the_whole_system_at_once","v":{"s":"The whole system at once"}},{"k":"mode_aria","v":{"s":"{label}. {blurb}"}},{"k":"mode_aria_active","v":{"s":"{label}. {blurb}. Currently open."}},{"k":"pilot_alt_state","v":{"s":"You are {form} at {alt} metres. Parcel temperature {you} degrees Celsius against surrounding air at {air}. Relative humidity {rh} percent. "}},{"k":"pilot_cloud_announce","v":{"s":"You are a cloud. {n} droplets have gathered around you at {m} metres. Keep collecting to grow heavy enough to fall."}},{"k":"pilot_model_note","v":{"s":"The heights above are computed from this scenario using the standard lapse rates (environment {env}°C/km, dry {dry}°C/km, saturated {moist}°C/km) and Espy’s equation for the lifting condensation level. Distances and droplet sizes are drawn at a teaching scale. Surface, soil, groundwater, and plant residence times are compressed so their real sequence can be explored in one session."}},{"k":"pilot_scenario_announce","v":{"s":"{label} selected. Surface {temp} degrees, humidity {rh} percent, cloud base {m} metres."}},{"k":"sect_aria","v":{"s":"{label}. {blurb}"}},{"k":"sect_aria_active","v":{"s":"{label}. {blurb}. Currently shown."}},{"k":"mode_announce","v":{"s":"{label} opened. {blurb}."}},{"k":"pilot_3d_is_unavailable_on_this_device","v":{"s":"3D is unavailable on this device."}},{"k":"pilot_3d_view_interrupted","v":{"s":"3D view interrupted"}},{"k":"pilot_graphics_connection_lost","v":{"s":"The 3D view lost its graphics connection. Your journey progress is saved; retry the view or continue in the System Map."}},{"k":"pilot_retry_3d_view","v":{"s":"Retry 3D view"}},{"k":"pilot_retrying_3d_view","v":{"s":"Retrying the 3D view. Your journey progress is preserved."}},{"k":"pilot_alt_cloudbase","v":{"s":" Cloud base is at {m} metres"}},{"k":"pilot_alt_freezing","v":{"s":", freezing level at {m} metres"}},{"k":"pilot_alt_lfc","v":{"s":", free convection at {m} metres"}},{"k":"pilot_be_the_water","v":{"s":"💧 Be the Water"}},{"k":"pilot_be_the_water_pilot_one_parcel_of_water","v":{"s":"Be the Water. Pilot one water parcel through changing states and pathways in the water cycle."}},{"k":"pilot_below_cloud_base","v":{"s":"{m} m below the cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_buoyant_warmer_than_the_air_rising_on","v":{"s":"↑ Buoyant: warmer than the air, rising on your own"}},{"k":"pilot_cloud_base","v":{"s":"CLOUD BASE"}},{"k":"pilot_cloud_base_2","v":{"s":"cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_cloud_base_lcl","v":{"s":"Cloud base (LCL)"}},{"k":"pilot_cloud_toast","v":{"s":"☁️ You are a cloud — {m} m up"}},{"k":"pilot_dew_point","v":{"s":"Dew point"}},{"k":"pilot_droplets_collected","v":{"s":"Droplets collected"}},{"k":"pilot_droplets_progress","v":{"s":"{n} / {total} to become a cloud"}},{"k":"pilot_energy_absorbed","v":{"s":"Energy absorbed"}},{"k":"pilot_escaping_as_vapour","v":{"s":"Escaping as vapour"}},{"k":"pilot_fly_one_parcel_through_every_form_wate","v":{"s":"Pilot one parcel through changing states and pathways"}},{"k":"pilot_form_cloud_label","v":{"s":"Cloud"}},{"k":"pilot_form_cloud_science","v":{"s":"Billions of droplets like you together scatter every wavelength of light, which is why a cloud looks white. Latent heat released as you condensed is now warming the parcel and driving it upward on its own."}},{"k":"pilot_form_droplet_label","v":{"s":"Cloud droplet"}},{"k":"pilot_form_droplet_science","v":{"s":"You condensed onto a speck of dust, salt, or smoke - a cloud condensation nucleus. You are about 20 micrometres across, roughly a fifth the width of a human hair, and light enough to stay aloft."}},{"k":"pilot_form_groundwater_label","v":{"s":"Groundwater"}},{"k":"pilot_form_groundwater_science","v":{"s":"Below the water table you move through connected pores in rock - often only metres per year. Groundwater is not an underground river; it is saturated rock, and it can hold water for thousands of years."}},{"k":"pilot_form_ice_label","v":{"s":"Ice crystal"}},{"k":"pilot_form_ice_science","v":{"s":"Above the freezing level you deposited into a hexagonal ice crystal. Ice holds water vapour less readily than liquid does, so you grow at the droplets’ expense - the Bergeron process behind most rain outside the tropics."}},{"k":"pilot_form_liquid_label","v":{"s":"Liquid water"}},{"k":"pilot_form_liquid_science","v":{"s":"You are liquid water at the surface, held together by hydrogen bonds. Molecules at the top are always escaping and returning; evaporation happens when more leave than come back."}},{"k":"pilot_form_plant_label","v":{"s":"Inside a plant"}},{"k":"pilot_form_plant_science","v":{"s":"A root hair took you up, and you are being pulled through the xylem by tension created as water evaporates from the leaves above you - a continuous thread of water metres tall."}},{"k":"pilot_form_rain_label","v":{"s":"Raindrop"}},{"k":"pilot_form_rain_science","v":{"s":"You collided and merged with enough droplets that gravity beat the updraft. A raindrop is roughly a million cloud droplets, which is why clouds can hang around for hours before it rains."}},{"k":"pilot_form_runoff_label","v":{"s":"Surface runoff"}},{"k":"pilot_form_runoff_science","v":{"s":"You landed faster than the ground could absorb you, so you are flowing downhill over the surface toward a stream. Runoff carries sediment and whatever the land surface holds."}},{"k":"pilot_form_snow_label","v":{"s":"Snowflake"}},{"k":"pilot_form_snow_science_v2","v":{"s":"You are falling as solid ice. This enlarged crystal with six branches is one example; real snow has many shapes and can contain joined crystals. Temperature and moisture shape crystal growth. In this model, entering air above freezing melts you into rain."}},{"k":"pilot_form_snow_science","v":{"s":"You fell through air that stayed below freezing the whole way down, so you never melted. Every branch grew in the same conditions at the same moment, which is what makes a snowflake symmetrical."}},{"k":"pilot_form_soil_label","v":{"s":"Soil water"}},{"k":"pilot_form_soil_science","v":{"s":"You soaked into pore spaces between soil grains. How fast depends on permeability: sand takes you in quickly, clay and pavement turn you back into runoff."}},{"k":"pilot_form_transpiring_label","v":{"s":"Transpiring"}},{"k":"pilot_form_transpiring_science","v":{"s":"You are leaving through a stoma on the underside of a leaf and becoming vapour again. Plants return a large share of land precipitation to the air this way."}},{"k":"pilot_form_vapor_label","v":{"s":"Water vapour"}},{"k":"pilot_form_vapor_science","v":{"s":"You are a gas, and gaseous water is invisible - the white you can see above a kettle or in a cloud is already condensed liquid. You rise because warm, moist air is less dense than dry air."}},{"k":"pilot_free_convection","v":{"s":"FREE CONVECTION"}},{"k":"pilot_free_convection_2","v":{"s":"free convection"}},{"k":"pilot_free_convection_3","v":{"s":"Free convection"}},{"k":"pilot_freezing_level","v":{"s":"FREEZING LEVEL"}},{"k":"pilot_freezing_level_2","v":{"s":"Freezing level"}},{"k":"pilot_heavy_enough_gravity_wins","v":{"s":"Heavy enough — gravity wins"}},{"k":"pilot_keep_collecting","v":{"s":"Keep collecting: {n} droplets so far"}},{"k":"pilot_ladder_km","v":{"s":"{n} km"}},{"k":"pilot_move_back_toward_the_camera","v":{"s":"Move back, toward the camera. Also S or Down Arrow."}},{"k":"pilot_move_forward_away_from_the_camera","v":{"s":"Move forward, away from the camera. Also W or Up Arrow."}},{"k":"pilot_muted","v":{"s":"🔇 Muted"}},{"k":"pilot_needs_lift","v":{"s":"↓ Needs lift: {d}°C cooler than the air"}},{"k":"pilot_nucleus_found","v":{"s":"Nucleus found"}},{"k":"pilot_obj_cloud","v":{"s":"You are a cloud. Keep collecting to grow heavy enough that gravity finally beats the updraft, or ride the lift higher and freeze."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_droplet","v":{"s":"You are a cloud droplet. Collide with the other droplets to grow. Keep climbing: at {m} m the latent heat you released makes you buoyant and you stop needing thrust."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_droplet_free","v":{"s":"Above {m} m you are warmer than the air around you and rising on your own - that is latent heat from condensing. Collide with other droplets to grow."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_droplet_nearly","v":{"s":"Keep sweeping up droplets. At {n} you and the droplets around you are a visible cloud."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_groundwater","v":{"s":"You are groundwater, moving through pores in rock - metres per year, not per second. Find a spring or a well to return."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_ice","v":{"s":"You are above the freezing level at {m} m. Ice grows faster than the droplets around it - keep collecting, then fall."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_liquid","v":{"s":"Hold in the sunlit shallows. Sunlight, dry air, and wind are all loading you with enough energy to escape as vapour."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_liquid_nearly","v":{"s":"Almost vapour - stay in the sunlit water until the energy bar fills."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_plant","v":{"s":"A root took you up. Ride the xylem to a leaf."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_rain","v":{"s":"Falling as rain. Steer toward open water, permeable ground, or a plant - each sends you down a different pathway."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_runoff","v":{"s":"You could not soak in, so you are running off the surface toward a stream. Follow the slope downhill."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_snow","v":{"s":"Falling as snow. If you pass below {m} m you will melt into rain on the way down."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_soil","v":{"s":"You infiltrated. Move down toward the water table, or let a root take you up."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_transpiring","v":{"s":"Leaving through a stoma. In a moment you are vapour again and the cycle repeats."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_vapor_climb","v":{"s":"Climb. At {m} m the air has cooled to your dew point and condensation can start - not one metre lower."}},{"k":"pilot_obj_vapor_nucleus","v":{"s":"You are above the cloud base and saturated, but water vapour will not condense on nothing. Fly into a condensation nucleus - the drifting specks of dust and sea salt."}},{"k":"pilot_one_flight_cannot_reach_every_stage_wh","v":{"s":"One flight cannot reach every stage: where you land decides which pathway you take. Fly it again and land somewhere else."}},{"k":"pilot_pause","v":{"s":"⏸ Pause"}},{"k":"pilot_pause_the_simulation","v":{"s":"Pause the simulation"}},{"k":"pilot_pilot_sound_muted","v":{"s":"Pilot sound muted."}},{"k":"pilot_pilot_sound_on","v":{"s":"Pilot sound on."}},{"k":"pilot_reset","v":{"s":"↻ Reset"}},{"k":"pilot_reset_this_run_and_start_again_as_liqu","v":{"s":"Reset this run and start again as liquid water"}},{"k":"pilot_resume","v":{"s":"▶ Resume"}},{"k":"pilot_resume_the_simulation","v":{"s":"Resume the simulation"}},{"k":"pilot_rise_also_space_or_w","v":{"s":"Rise. Also Space or E."}},{"k":"pilot_run_reset_you_are_liquid_water_at_the","v":{"s":"Run reset. You are liquid water at the sea surface."}},{"k":"pilot_saturated_now_find_a_nucleus","v":{"s":"Saturated — now find a nucleus"}},{"k":"pilot_sc_desertBasin_blurb","v":{"s":"Very dry air lifts the cloud base far above your head, and falling drops evaporate on the way down."}},{"k":"pilot_sc_desertBasin_label","v":{"s":"Desert basin"}},{"k":"pilot_sc_mountainWinter_blurb","v":{"s":"Cold and dry. The freezing level is near the ground, so you become ice, not rain."}},{"k":"pilot_sc_mountainWinter_label","v":{"s":"Mountain winter"}},{"k":"pilot_sc_temperateCoast_blurb","v":{"s":"Mild sea, drier air. You must climb further before anything condenses."}},{"k":"pilot_sc_temperateCoast_label","v":{"s":"Temperate coast"}},{"k":"pilot_sc_tropicalOcean_blurb","v":{"s":"Hot sea, humid air, a low cloud base. The easiest place to become a cloud."}},{"k":"pilot_sc_tropicalOcean_label","v":{"s":"Tropical ocean"}},{"k":"pilot_scenario_aria","v":{"s":"{label}. {blurb} Cloud base {m} metres."}},{"k":"pilot_scenario_stats","v":{"s":"{temp}°C · {rh}% RH · base {m} m"}},{"k":"pilot_sink_also_shift_or_s","v":{"s":"Sink. Also Shift or Q."}},{"k":"pilot_sound","v":{"s":"🔊 Sound"}},{"k":"pilot_sound_muted_select_to_hear_a_cue_on_ea","v":{"s":"Sound muted. Select to hear a cue on each change of form."}},{"k":"pilot_sound_on_each_change_of_form_plays_a_c","v":{"s":"Sound on. Each change of form plays a cue. Select to mute."}},{"k":"pilot_stage_experienced","v":{"s":"{stage}: experienced"}},{"k":"pilot_stage_not_yet_experienced","v":{"s":"{stage}: not yet experienced"}},{"k":"pilot_stages_you_have_been_through","v":{"s":"Stages you have been through"}},{"k":"pilot_steer_left_also_a_or_left_arrow","v":{"s":"Steer left. Also A or Left Arrow."}},{"k":"pilot_steer_right_also_d_or_right_arrow","v":{"s":"Steer right. Also D or Right Arrow."}},{"k":"pilot_sunlit_water","v":{"s":"SUNLIT WATER"}},{"k":"pilot_sunlit_water_charges_you_fastest","v":{"s":"Sunlit water charges you fastest"}},{"k":"pilot_text_description_of_the_3d_scene","v":{"s":"Text description of the 3D scene"}},{"k":"pilot_w_a_s_d_arrowup_arrowdown_arrowleft_ar","v":{"s":"W A S D F B ArrowUp ArrowDown ArrowLeft ArrowRight Space Shift"}},{"k":"pilot_webgl_is_unavailable_on_this_device_th","v":{"s":"WebGL is unavailable on this device. The System Map view teaches the same six stages without 3D."}},{"k":"pilot_weight_vs_updraft","v":{"s":"Weight vs updraft"}},{"k":"pilot_what_you_are","v":{"s":"What you are right now: {form}"}},{"k":"pilot_you_are_cooler_than_the_air_around_you","v":{"s":"You are cooler than the air around you, so you need lift to keep climbing."}},{"k":"pilot_you_are_warmer_than_the_air_around_you","v":{"s":"You are warmer than the air around you and rising on your own."}},{"k":"pilot_you_have_now_been_through_all_six_stag","v":{"s":"You have now been through all six stages. Different landings send you down different pathways — try landing on bare ground, on a forest, and in the lake to compare how long each one holds you."}},{"k":"sect_announce","v":{"s":"{label} shown. {blurb}."}},{"k":"sect_change_the_conditions","v":{"s":"Change the conditions"}},{"k":"sect_check_yourself","v":{"s":"Check yourself"}},{"k":"sect_climate_and_land_controls","v":{"s":"Climate and land controls"}},{"k":"sect_follow_one_droplet","v":{"s":"Follow one droplet"}},{"k":"sect_journey_mode_and_the_tutor","v":{"s":"Journey mode and the tutor"}},{"k":"sect_quiz_and_water_myths","v":{"s":"Quiz and water myths"}},{"k":"sect_readouts_comparison_next_test","v":{"s":"Readouts, comparison, next test"}},{"k":"sect_what_the_data_shows","v":{"s":"What the data shows"}},{"k":"a_quiet_year","v":{"s":"A Quiet Year"}},{"k":"no_major_event_routine_fieldwork_stead","v":{"s":"No major event. Routine fieldwork, steady progress."}},{"k":"watershed_recovery","v":{"s":"Watershed Recovery"}},{"k":"the_watershed_is_healing_across_the_bo","v":{"s":"The watershed is healing across the board. Headwaters are cold and clean. The mainstem carries fish again. Beaver wetlands are doing the floodplain work. This is what watershed-scale recovery looks like when timing and community come together."}},{"k":"recovering_watershed","v":{"s":"Recovering Watershed"}},{"k":"most_components_are_improving_a_few_st","v":{"s":"Most components are improving. A few still need work. The trajectory is good and the community is engaged."}},{"k":"mixed_recovery","v":{"s":"Mixed Recovery"}},{"k":"some_wins_some_gaps_real_watershed_wor","v":{"s":"Some wins, some gaps. Real watershed work is rarely uniform; some pieces improved while others stalled."}},{"k":"slipping_watershed","v":{"s":"Slipping Watershed"}},{"k":"average_quality_is_low_and_few_compone","v":{"s":"Average quality is low and few components reached recovery thresholds. This is how watersheds degrade quietly when stewardship cannot keep up with pressures."}},{"k":"initial_conditions_look_workable_use_y","v":{"s":"Initial conditions look workable. Use Year 1 for citizen-science monitoring and education to build community support before spending it on contested actions like dam removal."}},{"k":"watershed_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Watershed deep-dive"}},{"k":"close","v":{"s":"✕ Close"}},{"k":"hydrology","v":{"s":"💧 Hydrology"}},{"k":"case_work","v":{"s":"📰 Case work"}},{"k":"maine_context","v":{"s":"🌍 Maine context"}},{"k":"what_you_can_do_for_this_component","v":{"s":"🛠 What you can do for this component"}},{"k":"component_quality_across_the_10_year_c","v":{"s":"📈 Component quality across the 10-year campaign"}},{"k":"year_by_year_quality_trend_chart_by_wa","v":{"s":"Year-by-year quality trend chart by watershed component"}},{"k":"ai_watershed_educator_is_reading_your_","v":{"s":"⏳ AI watershed educator is reading your watershed data..."}},{"k":"ai_watershed_reading","v":{"s":"AI Watershed Reading"}},{"k":"ai_watershed_reading_2","v":{"s":"AI Watershed Reading"}},{"k":"re_read","v":{"s":"↻ Re-read"}},{"k":"ai_watershed_science_educator","v":{"s":"AI watershed-science educator. "}},{"k":"it_is_not_a_wabanaki_person_not_a_real","v":{"s":"It is not a Wabanaki person, not a real watershed coordinator or hydrologist, and does not speak for any Wabanaki nation, agency, or organization."}},{"k":"for_authoritative_voices_on_maine_wate","v":{"s":" For authoritative voices on Maine watershed work, consult Penobscot Nation Cultural and Historic Preservation Department, Wabanaki Public Health and Wellness, Atlantic Salmon Federation, Maine Coast Heritage Trust, Maine Audubon, Maine Soil and Water Conservation Districts, and the Maine Department of Environmental Protection."}},{"k":"water_cycle_explorer","v":{"s":"← Water Cycle Explorer"}},{"k":"watershed_steward_maine_campaign","v":{"s":"💧 Watershed Steward: Maine campaign"}},{"k":"watershed_steward_10_year_maine_campai","v":{"s":"Watershed Steward: 10-year Maine campaign"}},{"k":"six_watershed_components_ten_years_rea","v":{"s":"Six watershed components, ten years, real Maine pressures: dam barriers, agricultural runoff, suburban stormwater, climate-driven floods and droughts. "}},{"k":"hydrological_feedback_rules_tie_them_t","v":{"s":"Hydrological feedback rules tie them together."}},{"k":"healthy_buffers_cool_headwater_streams","v":{"s":" Healthy buffers cool headwater streams. Beaver wetlands attenuate floods and clean the mainstem. Low ag runoff lets the river breathe. Connected, shaded rivers bring back salmon and alewife runs. Outcomes are hand-authored teaching indices, not field measurements, forecasts, or project guarantees."}},{"k":"watershed_deep_dive_2","v":{"s":"📚 Watershed deep-dive →"}},{"k":"difficulty","v":{"s":"Difficulty"}},{"k":"begin_10_year_watershed_campaign","v":{"s":"💧 Begin 10-year Watershed Campaign"}},{"k":"water_cycle_explorer_2","v":{"s":"← Water Cycle Explorer"}},{"k":"watershed_steward_debrief","v":{"s":"💧 Watershed Steward: Debrief"}},{"k":"what_if_you_had_done_nothing_for_10_ye","v":{"s":"↔ What if you had done nothing for 10 years?"}},{"k":"your_campaign","v":{"s":"Your campaign"}},{"k":"pure_neglect","v":{"s":"Pure neglect"}},{"k":"components_at_75_quality","v":{"s":"Components at 75+ quality: "}},{"k":"connectivity_boosts","v":{"s":"Connectivity boosts: "}},{"k":"new_campaign","v":{"s":"↻ New campaign"}},{"k":"replay_same_conditions","v":{"s":"🔁 Replay same conditions"}},{"k":"campaign_seed","v":{"s":"Campaign seed: "}},{"k":"water_cycle_explorer_3","v":{"s":"← Water Cycle Explorer"}},{"k":"hydrological_feedback_rules_this_year","v":{"s":"🔄 Hydrological feedback rules this year"}},{"k":"what_changed_this_year","v":{"s":"What changed this year"}},{"k":"water_cycle_explorer_4","v":{"s":"← Water Cycle Explorer"}},{"k":"year_1_priority","v":{"s":"Year 1 priority: "}},{"k":"dismiss_tip","v":{"s":"Dismiss tip"}},{"k":"year","v":{"s":"Year"}},{"k":"hours_left","v":{"s":"Hours left"}},{"k":"connectivity_boosts_2","v":{"s":"Connectivity boosts"}},{"k":"ask_ai_watershed_educator_to_read_your","v":{"s":"Ask AI watershed educator to read your watershed data"}},{"k":"ai_watershed_science_educator_reads_yo","v":{"s":"AI watershed-science educator reads your current state"}},{"k":"end_this_year","v":{"s":"End this year"}},{"k":"end_year","v":{"s":"End Year →"}},{"k":"watershed_deep_dive_3","v":{"s":"Watershed deep-dive"}},{"k":"watershed_wide_actions","v":{"s":"🛠 Watershed-wide actions"}},{"k":"no_actions_yet_this_year_pick_a_compon","v":{"s":"No actions yet this year. Pick a component, pick a technique."}},{"k":"clear_very_light","v":{"s":"☀️ Clear / very light"}},{"k":"insufficient_condensation_too_warm_or_","v":{"s":"Insufficient condensation — too warm or too dry."}},{"k":"light_drizzle","v":{"s":"🌦️ Light drizzle"}},{"k":"slow_condensation_steady_but_gentle_pr","v":{"s":"Slow condensation. Steady but gentle precipitation."}},{"k":"moderate_rain","v":{"s":"🌧️ Moderate rain"}},{"k":"strong_condensation_typical_rainfall","v":{"s":"Strong condensation. Typical rainfall."}},{"k":"storm_burst","v":{"s":"⛈️ Storm burst"}},{"k":"high_moisture_cool_temp_strong_lift_he","v":{"s":"High moisture + cool temp + strong lift → heavy convective precipitation."}},{"k":"back_to_explorer","v":{"s":"← Back to Explorer"}},{"k":"precipitation_discovery","v":{"s":"🌧️ Precipitation discovery"}},{"k":"adjust_cloud_moisture_temperature_and_","v":{"s":"Adjust cloud moisture, temperature, and wind. Widget classifies precipitation into one of four discrete types. No score, no reveal — sweep and notice."}},{"k":"moisture","v":{"s":"Moisture (%)"}},{"k":"temp_c","v":{"s":"Temp (°C)"}},{"k":"wind_m_s","v":{"s":"Wind (m/s)"}},{"k":"log","v":{"s":"📋 Log"}},{"k":"reset","v":{"s":"↺ Reset"}},{"k":"hypothesis_free_text_what_triggers_a_s","v":{"s":"Hypothesis (free text): What triggers a storm vs drizzle?"}},{"k":"stuck_show_open_prompts","v":{"s":"🤔 Stuck — show open prompts"}},{"k":"hold_two_sliders_steady_move_one_watch","v":{"s":"Hold two sliders steady. Move one. Watch."}},{"k":"find_two_settings_producing_the_same_t","v":{"s":"Find two settings producing the same type."}},{"k":"real_storms_need_both_moisture_and_ins","v":{"s":"Real storms need both moisture and instability. Investigate."}},{"k":"i_understand_explain_in_own_words","v":{"s":"I understand — explain in own words"}},{"k":"explain_how_moisture_temperature_and_w","v":{"s":"Explain how moisture, temperature, and wind jointly determine precipitation type."}},{"k":"design_note_discrete_4_type_precipitat","v":{"s":"Design note: discrete 4-type precipitation marker; no rainfall-rate score; no reveal — by design."}},{"k":"river_path","v":{"s":"🌊 River Path"}},{"k":"go_underground","v":{"s":"🪴 Go Underground"}},{"k":"enter_plant","v":{"s":"🌿 Enter Plant"}},{"k":"evaporation","v":{"s":"↑ Evaporation"}},{"k":"condensation","v":{"s":"☁ Condensation"}},{"k":"precipitation","v":{"s":"↓ Precipitation"}},{"k":"collection","v":{"s":"🌊 Collection"}},{"k":"transpiration","v":{"s":"🌿 Transpiration"}},{"k":"infiltration","v":{"s":"🪴 Infiltration"}},{"k":"water_cycle_keyboard_shortcuts_1_throu","v":{"s":"Water Cycle. Keyboard shortcuts: 1 through 6 select a stage, G starts or stops the Guided Walkthrough, J toggles Journey mode, R U P choose your journey path, Escape exits Focus Canvas mode."}},{"k":"back_to_tools","v":{"s":"Back to tools"}},{"k":"water_cycle","v":{"s":"🌊 Water Cycle"}},{"k":"switch_to_watershed_steward_10_year_ca","v":{"s":"Switch to Watershed Steward 10-year campaign"}},{"k":"watershed_steward","v":{"s":"💧 Watershed Steward →"}},{"k":"switch_to_precipitation_discovery_widg","v":{"s":"Switch to Precipitation Discovery widget"}},{"k":"precipitation_lab","v":{"s":"🌧️ Precipitation Lab →"}},{"k":"grade","v":{"s":"🎓 Grade:"}},{"k":"snow","v":{"s":"❄️ SNOW"}},{"k":"storm","v":{"s":"⚡ STORM"}},{"k":"night","v":{"s":"🌙 NIGHT"}},{"k":"climate_lab","v":{"s":"Climate Lab"}},{"k":"night_2","v":{"s":"🌙 Night"}},{"k":"bright","v":{"s":"☀️ Bright"}},{"k":"calm","v":{"s":"Calm"}},{"k":"gale","v":{"s":"🌪 Gale"}},{"k":"snow_active","v":{"s":"❄️ Snow active"}},{"k":"thunderstorm","v":{"s":"⚡ Thunderstorm"}},{"k":"rainbow","v":{"s":"🌈 Rainbow"}},{"k":"stars_visible","v":{"s":"⭐ Stars visible"}},{"k":"fog","v":{"s":"🌫️ Fog"}},{"k":"water_cycle_stages","v":{"s":"Water cycle stages"}},{"k":"journey_mode","v":{"s":"Journey Mode"}},{"k":"play_as_water","v":{"s":"FOLLOW ONE DROPLET"}},{"k":"start_journey_mode_shortcut_j","v":{"s":"Start Journey mode (shortcut: J)"}},{"k":"start_journey_j","v":{"s":"▶ Play the droplet journey (J)"}},{"k":"end_journey_mode_shortcut_j","v":{"s":"End Journey mode (shortcut: J)"}},{"k":"end_journey_j","v":{"s":"⏹ End Journey (J)"}},{"k":"ride_one_droplet_from_the_ocean_back_t","v":{"s":"ride one droplet from the ocean back to the ocean. You will start in the ocean, evaporate, drift into a cloud, fall as rain, then pick a path at the ground (river runoff, underground infiltration, or absorbed by a plant). Each path takes a different amount of time. Complete the loop to log a journey; 3 loops unlocks the Journey badge."}},{"k":"you_rode_the_same_water_molecule_the_w","v":{"s":"You rode the SAME water molecule the whole way around — it changed form (liquid → vapor → liquid) but was never created or destroyed. Every drop you drink has been cycling for billions of years."}},{"k":"choose_river_runoff_path_shortcut_r","v":{"s":"Choose River Runoff path (shortcut: R)"}},{"k":"river_runoff_r","v":{"s":"River Runoff (R)"}},{"k":"fast_path","v":{"s":"Fast path!"}},{"k":"choose_underground_infiltration_path_s","v":{"s":"Choose Underground infiltration path (shortcut: U)"}},{"k":"underground_u","v":{"s":"Underground (U)"}},{"k":"slow_deep","v":{"s":"Slow + deep"}},{"k":"choose_plant_absorption_path_shortcut_","v":{"s":"Choose Plant absorption path (shortcut: P)"}},{"k":"enter_plant_p","v":{"s":"Enter Plant (P)"}},{"k":"start_another_loop","v":{"s":"Start Another Loop"}},{"k":"start_another_loop_2","v":{"s":"🔄 Start Another Loop"}},{"k":"become_a_water_droplet_and_travel_thro","v":{"s":"Become a water droplet and travel through the entire water cycle! Make choices at each stage and learn the science behind each transformation."}},{"k":"ask_the_ai_hydrologist_tutor","v":{"s":"Ask the AI Hydrologist Tutor"}},{"k":"ask_a_question_e_g_why_do_clouds_float","v":{"s":"Ask a question (e.g., Why do clouds float?)..."}},{"k":"water_budget_live","v":{"s":"Scenario Readout"}},{"k":"evaporation_2","v":{"s":"Evaporation index"}},{"k":"precip_type","v":{"s":"Precip Type"}},{"k":"runoff","v":{"s":"Runoff"}},{"k":"gw_recharge","v":{"s":"GW Recharge"}},{"k":"water_is_conserved_runoff_what_soaks_i","v":{"s":"💧 Water is conserved — runoff + what soaks in ≈ 100% of the rain reaching the ground. None disappears; it just takes different paths back to the sea."}},{"k":"start_water_cycle_quiz","v":{"s":"Start water cycle quiz"}},{"k":"generate_ai_quiz_question","v":{"s":"Generate AI quiz question"}},{"k":"ai_generated","v":{"s":"🧠 AI-GENERATED"}},{"k":"study_term_5_rp","v":{"s":"📖 Study Term (+5 RP)"}},{"k":"snapshot","v":{"s":"Snapshot"}},{"k":"snapshot_2","v":{"s":"📸 Snapshot"}},{"k":"headwaterStreams_desc","v":{"s":"High-elevation forested streams. Native brook trout, native eastern brook trout, water temperature below 20°C. The cleanest water in the watershed; everything downstream is shaped by what happens here."}},{"k":"riverMainstem_desc","v":{"s":"The big channel through the watershed. Historically the route for Atlantic salmon, alewife, sea-run brook trout, eels, sturgeon. In Maine, dam barriers block most of these runs; recent removals (Edwards Dam 1999, Fort Halifax 2008, Veazie 2012, Great Works 2013) reopened sections."}},{"k":"floodplainWetlands_desc","v":{"s":"Beaver dam complexes and adjacent wet meadows. Slow flood pulses, recharge groundwater, filter nutrients, support amphibians, waterfowl, moose, otter. Beaver Dam Analogs (BDAs) mimic this work where beavers have not returned."}},{"k":"forestBuffer_desc","v":{"s":"The strip of mature forest along stream banks. Shade keeps water cold, roots stabilize banks, leaf litter feeds aquatic insects, wood falls in to create habitat. A 50-foot intact buffer is the single most cost-effective stream protection."}},{"k":"agriculturalWatershed_desc","v":{"s":"Dairy farms, hay fields, row crops, blueberry barrens. The dominant land use in central Maine watersheds. Manure runoff, fertilizer, sediment from tilled land all flow downstream. BMPs (Best Management Practices) can cut runoff by 50-80%."}},{"k":"suburbanEdges_desc","v":{"s":"Subdivisions, parking lots, lawns. Impervious surfaces deliver pulses of warm polluted water to streams during storms. Lawn fertilizer and pet waste are the modern eutrophication inputs. Green stormwater infrastructure can offset the impact."}},{"k":"volunteer_desc","v":{"s":"24 hours / year, gentler events. For first runs."}},{"k":"coordinator_desc","v":{"s":"18 hours / year, standard events. Default."}},{"k":"director_desc","v":{"s":"14 hours / year, harsher events. Real constraint."}},{"k":"cycle_mission","v":{"s":"Cycle mission"}},{"k":"live_cycle_lab","v":{"s":"Live cycle lab"}},{"k":"track_the_droplet","v":{"s":"Track the droplet"}},{"k":"build_the_water_cycle","v":{"s":"Build the water cycle"}},{"k":"stage","v":{"s":"Stage"}},{"k":"weather","v":{"s":"Weather"}},{"k":"evaporation_rate","v":{"s":"Evaporation"}},{"k":"progress","v":{"s":"Progress"}},{"k":"live_cycle_model","v":{"s":"Live cycle model"}},{"k":"solar_intensity_slider","v":{"s":"Solar intensity"}},{"k":"temperature_slider_celsius","v":{"s":"Temperature in degrees Celsius"}},{"k":"wind_speed_slider","v":{"s":"Wind speed multiplier"}},{"k":"water_myths","v":{"s":"🧠 Water Myths — true or false?"}},{"k":"start_water_myth","v":{"s":"Start a water myth question"}},{"k":"choose_a_view","v":{"s":"Choose a view"}},{"k":"compare_the_system_or_follow_one","v":{"s":"Compare the whole system or follow one droplet."}},{"k":"system_map","v":{"s":"System Map"}},{"k":"droplet_journey","v":{"s":"Droplet Journey"}},{"k":"cycle_stages","v":{"s":"Cycle stages"}},{"k":"journey_focus","v":{"s":"Journey focus"}},{"k":"selected_process","v":{"s":"Selected process"}},{"k":"track_the_droplet_in_the_scene_below","v":{"s":"Track the droplet in the scene below; use the controls to pause, move, or refocus the camera."}},{"k":"choose_a_stage_to_spotlight","v":{"s":"Choose a stage to spotlight its movement in the live model."}},{"k":"how_to_read_the_model","v":{"s":"How to read the model"}},{"k":"watch_for","v":{"s":"Watch for"}},{"k":"use_drag_or_arrow_keys_to_explore","v":{"s":"Use drag or arrow keys to explore; highlighted routes are clickable. Press F to follow the droplet."}},{"k":"follow_the_moving_water_between_stores","v":{"s":"Follow the moving water between stores; the selected process is highlighted."}},{"k":"reset_climate_settings","v":{"s":"Reset climate settings"}},{"k":"pilot_alt_land_state","v":{"s":"You are {form}, {pct} percent through {route}. Storage: {storage}. Driver: {driver}. Natural timescale: {time}; the simulator compresses residence time without skipping the pathway. "}},{"k":"pilot_alt_location_wind","v":{"s":" You are over {surface}. Wind carries airborne water inland at {speed} metres per second."}},{"k":"pilot_aquifer_to_spring","v":{"s":"Aquifer → spring"}},{"k":"pilot_become_one_parcel","v":{"s":"Become one parcel of water"}},{"k":"pilot_begin_follow","v":{"s":"Begin - see your parcel"}},{"k":"pilot_begin_water","v":{"s":"Begin - look through water"}},{"k":"pilot_camera_view","v":{"s":"Camera view"}},{"k":"pilot_current_pathway","v":{"s":"Current pathway"}},{"k":"pilot_transition_meter","v":{"s":"Transition meter"}},{"k":"pilot_descent_to_surface","v":{"s":"Descent to surface"}},{"k":"pilot_driver","v":{"s":"Driver"}},{"k":"pilot_experience_started","v":{"s":"Be the Water started. You are liquid water in the sunlit ocean. Follow the Next goal panel at the top right of the scene."}},{"k":"pilot_interactive_3d_simulation","v":{"s":"Interactive 3D water-cycle simulation"}},{"k":"pilot_journey_guide","v":{"s":"Journey guide"}},{"k":"pilot_last_change","v":{"s":"Last change"}},{"k":"pilot_journey_started","v":{"s":"Journey started"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_kicker","v":{"s":"Four-climate challenge"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_title","v":{"s":"Collect evidence across climates"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_complete","v":{"s":"Investigation complete"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_building","v":{"s":"Building evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_progress","v":{"s":"{done} of {total} climate journeys pinned"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_checklist_aria","v":{"s":"Climate evidence checklist"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_step_pinned","v":{"s":"Evidence pinned"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_step_live","v":{"s":"Live now - pin this journey"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_mission_step_next","v":{"s":"Not tested yet"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_fair_test_title","v":{"s":"Fair-test rule"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_fair_test_copy","v":{"s":"Use the same rise and steering strategy in every climate so starting conditions are the main variable."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_next_current","v":{"s":"Next: pin this live {scenario} journey using the button above."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_next_switch","v":{"s":"Next: run {scenario} with the same flight strategy."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_next_button","v":{"s":"Test {scenario}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_kicker","v":{"s":"Claim - evidence - reasoning"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_title","v":{"s":"Climate conclusion"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_claim_label","v":{"s":"Claim"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_claim","v":{"s":"{low} forms cloud at a lower altitude than {high} in this model."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_evidence_label","v":{"s":"Evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_evidence","v":{"s":"{low}: {lowM} m cloud base at {lowRH}% humidity. {high}: {highM} m at {highRH}% humidity."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_reasoning_label","v":{"s":"Reasoning"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_reasoning","v":{"s":"Air that starts closer to saturation needs less cooling before water vapor condenses, so its cloud base is lower."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_method_title","v":{"s":"Separate climate from piloting"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_method_copy","v":{"s":"{scenario} recorded the most stage evidence ({done} of {total}). Stage coverage reflects the route you flew, so it is not proof of a climate effect."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_conclusion_complete","v":{"s":"Four-climate evidence set complete. Your claim now spans every starting environment."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_use_in_notebook","v":{"s":"Use this conclusion in Journey Notebook"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_notebook_added_announce","v":{"s":"Climate conclusion added to your reflection. Review or revise it."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_notebook_preserved_announce","v":{"s":"Journey Notebook reflection focused. Your existing writing was preserved."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_locked_title","v":{"s":"Pin one more climate to unlock a conclusion"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_synthesis_locked_copy","v":{"s":"A scientific comparison needs evidence from at least two starting conditions."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_pin_announce","v":{"s":"{scenario} journey pinned for comparison."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_clear_announce","v":{"s":"Pinned scenario comparisons cleared."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_kicker","v":{"s":"Cross-scenario investigation"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_title","v":{"s":"Scenario comparison lab"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_intro","v":{"s":"Pin evidence from this journey, switch climates, and compare what the same parcel experiences. Pins stay while you explore these scenarios."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_update","v":{"s":"Update current pin"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_pin","v":{"s":"Pin current journey"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_clear","v":{"s":"Clear pinned journeys"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cloud_insight_title","v":{"s":"Humidity changes the climb"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cloud_insight","v":{"s":"{low} forms cloud near {lowM} m; {high} near {highM} m because drier air starts farther from saturation."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_insight_title","v":{"s":"Temperature changes the phase"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_insight","v":{"s":"{scenario} reaches freezing near {m} m, so rising water can become ice sooner."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_kicker","v":{"s":"Shared model scale"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_title","v":{"s":"Climate sky profile"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_spread","v":{"s":"{value} m cloud-base spread"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_legend","v":{"s":"Climate sky profile legend"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_legend_cloud","v":{"s":"Cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_legend_freezing","v":{"s":"Freezing level"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_legend_journey","v":{"s":"Live or pinned parcel"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_legend_probe","v":{"s":"Altitude probe"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_kicker","v":{"s":"Interactive threshold check"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_label","v":{"s":"Altitude probe"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_prompt","v":{"s":"Move one altitude through every climate"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_aria_label","v":{"s":"Shared altitude probe"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_aria_value","v":{"s":"{altitude} metres"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_presets","v":{"s":"Altitude probe presets"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_preset_aria","v":{"s":"Set altitude probe to {altitude}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_science","v":{"s":"Thresholds describe environmental conditions, not a guaranteed parcel phase. Supercooled liquid can remain below 0°C."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_state_air","v":{"s":"Below cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_state_cold","v":{"s":"Cold air, no cloud yet"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_state_cloud","v":{"s":"Liquid cloud possible"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_state_ice","v":{"s":"Cloud ice possible"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_detail_air","v":{"s":"{temp}°C air · condensation threshold not reached"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_detail_cold","v":{"s":"{temp}°C air · subfreezing but below cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_detail_cloud","v":{"s":"{temp}°C air · above cloud base, below freezing"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_detail_ice","v":{"s":"{temp}°C air · above cloud base and freezing level"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_summary_item","v":{"s":"{scenario}: {result}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_scenario_aria","v":{"s":"At {altitude} metres: {result}. {detail}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_verdict_title","v":{"s":"Conditions at this altitude"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_crossing","v":{"s":"At {altitude}, the probe is above cloud base in {cloud} of {total} climates and at or above the freezing level in {freezing}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_state_count","v":{"s":"{count} / {total} climates"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_condition_aria","v":{"s":"{label}: {count} of {total} climates at {altitude}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_probe_temperature","v":{"s":"{temp}°C"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_cloud","v":{"s":"cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_freezing","v":{"s":"freezing level"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_event","v":{"s":"{scenario} {threshold}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoints_kicker","v":{"s":"Boundary navigator"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoints_title","v":{"s":"Atmospheric waypoints"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoints_hint","v":{"s":"Jump to the first 100 m probe step at or above each boundary"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoints_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Atmospheric thresholds crossed"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoints_count","v":{"s":"{crossed} / {total} crossed"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_previous","v":{"s":"Previous"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_next","v":{"s":"Next"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_button_aria","v":{"s":"Move probe to the {scenario} {threshold} crossing step at {altitude}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_card","v":{"s":"{threshold} · {altitude}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_status_here","v":{"s":"This probe step crosses: {events}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_status_next","v":{"s":"Next crossing: {scenario} {threshold} at the {altitude} probe step; climb {distance}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_waypoint_status_complete","v":{"s":"Every modeled cloud-base and freezing crossing is at or below this altitude."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_kicker","v":{"s":"Temperature evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_title","v":{"s":"Thermal cross-section"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_hint","v":{"s":"Same altitude, one shared model scale"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_spread","v":{"s":"{spread}°C spread"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_scale","v":{"s":"Shared ambient-temperature scale"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_zero","v":{"s":"0°C"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_surface","v":{"s":"Surface {temp}"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_climate_aria","v":{"s":"{scenario}: {current} at {altitude}; surface starting temperature {surface}; model cooling {drop}°C."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_thermal_summary","v":{"s":"At {altitude}, {cold} is coldest at {coldTemp}; {warm} is warmest at {warmTemp}. The {spread}°C spread comes from different surface starting temperatures in this shared-lapse-rate model."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_kicker","v":{"s":"Model rule"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_title","v":{"s":"Altitude cooling"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_rate","v":{"s":"{rate}°C / km"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_altitude","v":{"s":"{altitude} km climbed"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_result","v":{"s":"{drop}°C cooler"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_equation_aria","v":{"s":"{rate} degrees Celsius per kilometre multiplied by {altitude} kilometres equals {drop} degrees Celsius cooler."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cooling_summary","v":{"s":"Every climate cools by the same {drop}°C at this altitude because the model applies one environmental lapse rate. Different surface temperatures keep the markers separated."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_none","v":{"s":"none"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_state_confirmed","v":{"s":"Evidence matched"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_state_retry","v":{"s":"Check the line"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_state_ready","v":{"s":"Make your call"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_prompt","v":{"s":"How many climate markers sit at or below 0°C at {altitude}?"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_confirmed","v":{"s":"Confirmed: {count} of {total} climate profiles sit at or below 0°C at {altitude}: {climates}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_retry","v":{"s":"Try again. Count the markers on or left of the dashed 0°C line."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_hint","v":{"s":"Read the dashed freezing line, then choose 0–4."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_kicker","v":{"s":"Science challenge"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_title","v":{"s":"Freeze-line forecast"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_choices_aria","v":{"s":"Freeze-line forecast choices"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_choice_aria","v":{"s":"Forecast {count} climate profiles at or below 0°C"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_profiles","v":{"s":"profiles"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freeze_scope","v":{"s":"Counts ambient temperature markers only. Below-freezing air does not guarantee that every water parcel is ice."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_pinned","v":{"s":"Pinned: {form} at {altitude} m"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_live","v":{"s":"Live: {form} at {altitude} m"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_empty","v":{"s":"No journey evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_scenario_aria","v":{"s":"{scenario}. Cloud base {cloud} m. Freezing {freezing}. {evidence}."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_profile_caption","v":{"s":"All four climates use the same 0-{ceiling} km model scale. Tinted sky above each marker shows where that threshold has been crossed; higher markers require a farther climb."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_above_model","v":{"s":"Above model"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_pinned","v":{"s":"Pinned"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_live","v":{"s":"Live now"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_empty_short","v":{"s":"Not pinned"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_starting_air","v":{"s":"Starting air"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_surface","v":{"s":"Surface"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_cloud_base","v":{"s":"Cloud base"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_freezing","v":{"s":"Freezing"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_pinned_evidence","v":{"s":"Pinned evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_live_evidence","v":{"s":"Live evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_no_evidence","v":{"s":"No journey evidence yet"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_form","v":{"s":"Form"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_optics","v":{"s":"Optics"}},{"k":"pilot_compare_empty","v":{"s":"Run this scenario, then pin its evidence for comparison."}},{"k":"pilot_compare_run","v":{"s":"Run this scenario"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_challenge_kicker","v":{"s":"Atmospheric optics challenge"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_challenge_title","v":{"s":"Create a double rainbow"}},{"k":"scene_rainbow_rule","v":{"s":"RAINBOW · 42° OPPOSITE THE SUN"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_challenge_intro","v":{"s":"Use rain, sunlight, and viewing geometry to build both bows - then explain why they differ."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_progress","v":{"s":"{done} / 4 conditions"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_progress_aria","v":{"s":"Double-rainbow challenge progress"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_complete","v":{"s":"Double rainbow created. Your optical evidence is saved in the Journey Notebook."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_primary","v":{"s":"Primary bow found. Fine-tune the antisolar view to reveal the fainter outer bow."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_aim","v":{"s":"Rain and sunlight are ready. Face directly opposite the Sun and look through the rain."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_sun","v":{"s":"Liquid rain is ready. You still need bright sunlight with the Sun below 42 degrees."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_recreate","v":{"s":"Primary-bow evidence is saved. Become liquid rain again to complete the second bow."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_status_rain","v":{"s":"First become liquid rain: grow a cloud heavy enough for gravity to win."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_rain","v":{"s":"Liquid rain"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_rain_detail","v":{"s":"A curtain of spherical drops"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_sun","v":{"s":"Bright, low Sun"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_sun_detail","v":{"s":"Sun altitude below 42°"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_primary","v":{"s":"Primary bow"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_primary_detail","v":{"s":"42° · one reflection"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_secondary","v":{"s":"Secondary bow"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_step_secondary_detail","v":{"s":"51° · two reflections"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_created","v":{"s":"Double rainbow created"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_fine_tune","v":{"s":"Fine-tune the second bow"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_face_sun","v":{"s":"Face opposite the Sun"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_alignment","v":{"s":"Antisolar alignment: {pct}%"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_science_summary","v":{"s":"Open the raindrop ray lab"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_aria","v":{"s":"Rainbow viewing geometry: the Sun is behind the observer, who looks in the opposite direction toward rain."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_antisolar_point_short","v":{"s":"ANTISOLAR POINT"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_optics_locked","v":{"s":"OPTICS LOCKED"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_double_found","v":{"s":"DOUBLE RAINBOW FOUND"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_sun_short","v":{"s":"SUN"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_you_short","v":{"s":"YOU"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_rain_short","v":{"s":"RAIN"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_sun","v":{"s":"Sun bright and behind you"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_look","v":{"s":"Look opposite the Sun"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_geometry_rain","v":{"s":"Liquid rain ahead"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_primary_diagram_aria","v":{"s":"Schematic primary rainbow ray: sunlight refracts into a raindrop, reflects once, and refracts back toward the observer."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_secondary_diagram_aria","v":{"s":"Schematic secondary rainbow ray: sunlight refracts into a raindrop, reflects at two points inside, and exits with reversed color order."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_raylab_note","v":{"s":"Ray paths are schematic, not to scale. Both bows include refraction as light enters and leaves the drop."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_raylab_compare","v":{"s":"Compare the exits"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_raylab_compare_detail","v":{"s":"The extra reflection changes the exit angle, spreads energy across a wider arc, and reverses the visible color order."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_observation_captured","v":{"s":"Double-rainbow observation captured"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_observation_copy","v":{"s":"These measurements persist when the rain ends and are included in your downloaded Journey Notebook."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_receipt_scenario","v":{"s":"Scenario"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_receipt_sun","v":{"s":"Sun altitude"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_receipt_alignment","v":{"s":"Best alignment"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_receipt_paths","v":{"s":"Ray paths"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_receipt_paths_value","v":{"s":"Primary: 42° / 1 reflection · Secondary: 51° / 2 reflections"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_use_evidence","v":{"s":"Use this evidence in your notebook"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_notebook_focus_announce","v":{"s":"Journey Notebook reflection focused. Use the captured rainbow measurements as evidence."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_primary_science","v":{"s":"Primary · about 42°"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_primary_science_detail","v":{"s":"Light refracts, reflects once inside each drop, then refracts out."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_secondary_science","v":{"s":"Secondary · about 51°"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_secondary_science_detail","v":{"s":"Two internal reflections make it fainter, wider, and reverse its color order."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_dark_band","v":{"s":"Alexander's dark band"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_dark_band_detail","v":{"s":"Fewer rainbow rays reach your eye in the region between the two bows."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_science_note","v":{"s":"The bows are centered on the antisolar point - directly opposite the Sun. Every observer sees their own rainbow geometry."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_primary_label","v":{"s":"PRIMARY · 42°"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_secondary_label","v":{"s":"SECONDARY · 51° · REVERSED"}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_alt_double","v":{"s":"A double rainbow is visible opposite the Sun: a bright primary bow near 42 degrees and a fainter, wider secondary near 51 degrees with reversed colors. The band between them is darker."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_alt_primary","v":{"s":"A primary rainbow is visible opposite the Sun near 42 degrees. Fine alignment may reveal a fainter secondary bow outside it."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_align_announce","v":{"s":"Water view aimed opposite the Sun. Look through the rain for the primary bow."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_fine_tune_announce","v":{"s":"View centered more closely on the antisolar point. Look for the fainter outer bow."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_primary_announce","v":{"s":"Primary rainbow visible near 42 degrees from the antisolar point. Fine-tune your view to reveal the secondary bow."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_double_announce","v":{"s":"Double rainbow created. The fainter outer bow is wider and its colors are reversed."}},{"k":"pilot_rainbow_double_toast","v":{"s":"Double rainbow created - two internal reflections, reversed colors!"}},{"k":"pilot_now","v":{"s":"Now"}},{"k":"pilot_next_goal","v":{"s":"Next goal"}},{"k":"pilot_local_learning_record","v":{"s":"Local learning record"}},{"k":"pilot_live_water_state","v":{"s":"Live water state"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook","v":{"s":"Journey notebook"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_intro","v":{"s":"Keep the pathway evidence, explain what changed in your thinking, and resume this journey later."}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_storage_unavailable","v":{"s":"Storage unavailable"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_autosave_off","v":{"s":"Autosave off"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_saved_device","v":{"s":"Saved on this device"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_autosave_ready","v":{"s":"Autosave ready"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_summary","v":{"s":"Journey notebook summary"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_evidence_progress","v":{"s":"Stage evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_stage_progress","v":{"s":"Water-cycle stages recorded"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_stages","v":{"s":"Stages"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_cycles","v":{"s":"Cycles"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_pathways","v":{"s":"Pathways"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_climates","v":{"s":"Climates"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_changes","v":{"s":"Changes"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_compare_title","v":{"s":"Climate comparison evidence"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_compare_count","v":{"s":"{done} of {total} climates pinned"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_compare_journey","v":{"s":"{form} at {altitude} m - {stages} of {total} stages"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_compare_empty","v":{"s":"Pin a climate journey in the Comparison Lab to add evidence here."}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_compare_hint","v":{"s":"Pin at least two climates to unlock a claim, evidence, and reasoning receipt."}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_reflection_label","v":{"s":"What changed in your thinking?"}},{"k":"pilot_notebook_reflection_placeholder","v":{"s":"Use evidence from a phase change, pathway, or scenari