ext"}},{"k":"a11y_teacher_look_fors_reviewed","v":{"s":"Teacher look-fors reviewed"}}],"id":597}},{"k":"weldlab","v":{"o":[{"k":"a11y_inspection_sample_total_defects_to_find_found_s","v":{"s":"Inspection sample. {value1}. Total defects to find: {value2}. Found so far: {value3}."}},{"k":"a11y_score_out_of","v":{"s":"Score: {value1} out of {value2}"}},{"k":"sr_module_explored","v":{"s":"Module explored: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_selected","v":{"s":"Selected {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_process","v":{"s":"Process: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_material","v":{"s":"Material: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_thickness","v":{"s":"Thickness {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_view","v":{"s":"View: {value1} ({value2})"}},{"k":"sr_joint","v":{"s":"Joint: {value1} ({value2})"}},{"k":"sr_position","v":{"s":"Position: {value1} ({value2})"}},{"k":"sr_identified","v":{"s":"Identified {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_loaded","v":{"s":"Loaded {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_identified_zone","v":{"s":"Identified zone: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_quiz_complete_score_of","v":{"s":"Quiz complete. Score: {value1} of {value2}"}},{"k":"sr_challenge_loaded","v":{"s":"Challenge {value1} loaded"}},{"k":"sr_new_personal_best_for_tier_percent","v":{"s":"New personal best for {value1} tier: {value2} percent"}},{"k":"sr_run_complete_score_percent","v":{"s":"Run complete. Score: {value1} percent."}},{"k":"sr_speed_challenge_started_tier_seconds","v":{"s":"Speed challenge started, {value1} tier, {value2} seconds"}},{"k":"sr_code","v":{"s":"Code: {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_showing","v":{"s":"Showing {value1}"}},{"k":"sr_no_defect_at_that_point","v":{"s":"No defect at that point."}},{"k":"sr_reset_sample","v":{"s":"Reset sample"}},{"k":"sr_all_defects_revealed","v":{"s":"All defects revealed"}},{"k":"sr_joint_matching_quiz_started","v":{"s":"Joint matching quiz started"}},{"k":"sr_returned_to_catalog","v":{"s":"Returned to catalog"}},{"k":"sr_run_stopped_early","v":{"s":"Run stopped early"}},{"k":"sr_not_quite_see_the_explanation","v":{"s":"Not quite — see the explanation."}},{"k":"sr_restarted_at_scenario_1","v":{"s":"Restarted at scenario 1."}},{"k":"sr_scenario_quiz_reset","v":{"s":"Scenario quiz reset"}},{"k":"sr_returned_to_path_picker","v":{"s":"Returned to path picker"}},{"k":"sr_audit_reset","v":{"s":"Audit reset"}},{"k":"sr_quiz_restarted_score_reset","v":{"s":"Quiz restarted. Score reset."}},{"k":"sr_quiz_reset","v":{"s":"Quiz reset"}},{"k":"back_to_weldlab_menu","v":{"s":"Back to WeldLab menu"}},{"k":"menu","v":{"s":"← Menu"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_discussion_questions_sta","v":{"s":"Teacher Notes — discussion questions, standards alignment, and extension activities"}},{"k":"teacher_notes_click_to_expand","v":{"s":"🍎 Teacher Notes — click to expand"}},{"k":"print_this_module_page_includes_teache","v":{"s":"Print this module page (includes Teacher Notes)"}},{"k":"print","v":{"s":"🖨️ Print"}},{"k":"ngss_cte_standards","v":{"s":"NGSS / CTE Standards"}},{"k":"discussion_questions","v":{"s":"Discussion Questions"}},{"k":"watch_for_these_misconceptions","v":{"s":"Watch for these misconceptions"}},{"k":"extension_activity","v":{"s":"Extension Activity"}},{"k":"heat_input_calculator","v":{"s":"Heat Input Calculator"}},{"k":"the_math_that_drives_every_weld","v":{"s":"The math that drives every weld"}},{"k":"voltage_amperage_time_gives_you_total_","v":{"s":"Voltage × amperage × time gives you total heat per inch of weld. Too low: lack of fusion. Too high: warping, grain coarsening, hot cracking. This is the single most important number in arc welding — every certified welder knows it."}},{"k":"weld_bead_lab","v":{"s":"Weld Bead Lab"}},{"k":"live_bead_simulator_drag_your_arc","v":{"s":"Live bead simulator — drag your arc"}},{"k":"pick_a_process_material_and_plate_thic","v":{"s":"Pick a process, material, and plate thickness. Set voltage, amperage, travel speed. Watch a top-down bead form on the plate with accurate geometry — width, reinforcement height, ripple spacing — all driven by the heat-input physics. Score on penetration, uniformity, and spatter."}},{"k":"defect_hunt_lab","v":{"s":"Defect Hunt Lab"}},{"k":"inspector_training_click_to_identify","v":{"s":"Inspector training — click to identify"}},{"k":"look_at_a_finished_bead_click_the_defe","v":{"s":"Look at a finished bead. Click the defects you can spot. Each defect (porosity, undercut, lack of fusion, cold lap, crater crack, overlap) has a hover card explaining what causes it and how to fix it. Same skill that AWS-certified welding inspectors use."}},{"k":"speed_challenge","v":{"s":"Speed Challenge"}},{"k":"timed_precision_welding","v":{"s":"Timed precision welding"}},{"k":"a_60_second_timed_run_the_torch_travel","v":{"s":"A 60-second timed run. The torch travels automatically; you adjust voltage and amperage on the fly to keep the bead in spec. Score combines penetration, uniformity, and spatter control. Three tiers: Apprentice (forgiving), Pro (tight tolerance), Bath Iron Works (no margin for error). Personal-best persists across sessions."}},{"k":"underwater_welding","v":{"s":"Underwater Welding"}},{"k":"specialty_career_wet_hyperbaric","v":{"s":"Specialty career — wet & hyperbaric"}},{"k":"the_highest_paying_tier_of_welding_80_","v":{"s":"The highest-paying tier of welding ($80-200K+) and the most dangerous. Explore wet vs dry techniques, hyperbaric pressure effects on the arc, hydrogen-embrittlement risk, the unique safety hazards (electric shock through water, decompression sickness, hydrogen pocket explosions), and the path to becoming a commercial diver-welder."}},{"k":"process_comparison","v":{"s":"Process Comparison"}},{"k":"mig_vs_tig_vs_stick_vs_oxy","v":{"s":"MIG vs TIG vs Stick vs Oxy"}},{"k":"side_by_side_card_matrix_typical_use_m","v":{"s":"Side-by-side card matrix: typical use, materials, skill ceiling, equipment cost, portability, joint quality. Pick the right process for the job."}},{"k":"joint_configuration","v":{"s":"Joint Configuration"}},{"k":"5_joints_4_positions","v":{"s":"5 joints × 4 positions"}},{"k":"butt_lap_corner_edge_t_joint_position_","v":{"s":"Butt, lap, corner, edge, T-joint. Position codes 1F/2F/3F/4F (flat, horizontal, vertical, overhead). Quiz mode to match symbol → joint."}},{"k":"welding_symbols_reader","v":{"s":"Welding Symbols Reader"}},{"k":"aws_a2_4_blueprint_decoder","v":{"s":"AWS A2.4 blueprint decoder"}},{"k":"read_a_real_engineering_blueprint_deco","v":{"s":"Read a real engineering blueprint. Decode the welding symbol on the leader line: process? size? both sides? field weld? Critical shop-floor literacy."}},{"k":"ppe_safety","v":{"s":"PPE & Safety"}},{"k":"osha_1910_252_essentials","v":{"s":"OSHA 1910.252 essentials"}},{"k":"auto_darkening_helmet_fr_clothing_leat","v":{"s":"Auto-darkening helmet, FR clothing, leather gloves, respirator, ventilation, fire watch. Hazard scenarios with branch decisions: arc flash, fume exposure, hot work, confined space."}},{"k":"career_pathways","v":{"s":"Career Pathways"}},{"k":"maine_welding_careers","v":{"s":"Maine welding careers"}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_hiring_eastern_maine_c","v":{"s":"Bath Iron Works hiring, Eastern Maine Community College welding program, AWS certification ladder, salary bands by certification, apprenticeship vs trade school paths."}},{"k":"process_sleuth","v":{"s":"Process Sleuth"}},{"k":"10_vignettes_pick_the_right_process","v":{"s":"10 vignettes — pick the right process"}},{"k":"10_real_world_welding_scenarios_for_ea","v":{"s":"10 real-world welding scenarios. For each, pick the correct process from MIG / TIG / Stick / FCAW / Resistance. Vignettes target the canonical decisions: stainless food-service, structural outdoor in wind, aluminum boat hull, sanitary brewery pipe, cast-iron engine block crack, automotive sheet metal, agricultural repair on rusty steel."}},{"k":"defect_diagnose","v":{"s":"Defect Diagnose"}},{"k":"10_defects_identify_the_root_cause","v":{"s":"10 defects — identify the root cause"}},{"k":"complement_to_defect_hunt_that_one_tea","v":{"s":"Complement to Defect Hunt: that one teaches what defects LOOK like; this one teaches what CAUSED them. 10 weld defects (porosity, undercut, lack of fusion, burn-through, crater crack, overlap, slag inclusion, spatter, distortion, sugar oxidation). Pick the cause from 6 categories (heat too high/low, travel too fast/slow, contamination, technique)."}},{"k":"welder_s_defect_catalog","v":{"s":"Welder's Defect Catalog"}},{"k":"your_personal_log_of_every_defect_type","v":{"s":"Your personal log of every defect type you have ID'd"}},{"k":"a_persistent_catalog_of_welding_discon","v":{"s":"A persistent catalog of welding discontinuities you have correctly identified across every Defect Hunt sample. CWI-style mental library. Crosses sessions when you save your project."}},{"k":"welding_metallurgy","v":{"s":"Welding Metallurgy"}},{"k":"carbon_stainless_aluminum_exotics_haz_","v":{"s":"Carbon, stainless, aluminum, exotics + HAZ + PWHT"}},{"k":"the_science_of_what_heat_does_to_metal","v":{"s":"The science of what heat does to metal. Phase diagrams, carbon equivalent (CE) for crack-susceptibility, HAZ properties, sensitization in stainless, hot vs cold cracking, post-weld heat treatment (PWHT). Differentiates a welder who follows a procedure from one who understands it."}},{"k":"codes_standards","v":{"s":"Codes + Standards"}},{"k":"aws_d1_1_asme_ix_api_1104","v":{"s":"AWS D1.1, ASME IX, API 1104"}},{"k":"the_legal_frameworks_that_govern_what_","v":{"s":"The legal frameworks that govern what welds must be. AWS D1.1 (structural steel), ASME Section IX (pressure vessels), API 1104 (pipelines). How to read a Welding Procedure Specification (WPS) + Procedure Qualification Record (PQR), what each section means, why violations get welders fired."}},{"k":"welder_qualification_prep","v":{"s":"Welder Qualification Prep"}},{"k":"aws_performance_written_test_prep","v":{"s":"AWS performance + written test prep"}},{"k":"how_welder_qualification_tests_work_aw","v":{"s":"How welder qualification tests work — AWS D1.1, ASME, in-house. What a 6G pipe coupon looks like, how it gets bend-tested + visually inspected, what gets you passed or failed. Plus 30 written-exam practice questions covering the topics every certified welder must know."}},{"k":"pipe_welding_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Pipe Welding Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"5g_6g_6gr_orbital_pipeline","v":{"s":"5G / 6G / 6GR + orbital + pipeline"}},{"k":"pipe_is_its_own_world_fixed_positions_","v":{"s":"Pipe is its own world — fixed positions (1G/2G/5G/6G/6GR), root pass + hot pass + fill + cap technique, downhill vs uphill, pipeline welding (cross-country), orbital welding (semi-auto for nuclear + pharma). The highest-paid welding specialty when combined with X-ray-quality CWI standards."}},{"k":"robotic_automated_welding","v":{"s":"Robotic + Automated Welding"}},{"k":"fanuc_abb_kuka_programming_future","v":{"s":"FANUC, ABB, KUKA — programming + future"}},{"k":"industrial_robotic_welding_is_automati","v":{"s":"Industrial robotic welding is automating high-volume production (auto, appliance, structural). Brands (FANUC, ABB, KUKA, Yaskawa), teach-pendant programming, vision systems, collaborative cobots. Career impact: traditional welders aren't replaced — robot programmers + technicians are growing fast."}},{"k":"welding_inspection_cwi","v":{"s":"Welding Inspection (CWI)"}},{"k":"aws_certified_welding_inspector_prep","v":{"s":"AWS Certified Welding Inspector prep"}},{"k":"the_certified_welding_inspector_cwi_is","v":{"s":"The Certified Welding Inspector (CWI) is the gatekeeper. ~$70-110K pay, $1,150 exam, 3 parts (fundamentals, code book, hands-on). Visual inspection (VT), 5 NDT methods (PT, MT, UT, RT, ET), code interpretation, ethical responsibilities. Top of the welding career ladder for someone who reads + writes well."}},{"k":"consumables_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Consumables Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"electrodes_wires_gases_flux_what_to_bu","v":{"s":"Electrodes, wires, gases, flux — what to buy + when"}},{"k":"picking_the_right_consumable_is_half_t","v":{"s":"Picking the right consumable is half the welding decision. Every electrode classification (E6010, 6011, 6013, 7018, 7024), wire (ER70S-2/3/6, FCAW), shielding gas mixes, flux types. Practical: cost per pound, deposition rate, storage requirements, what each is for. Same Welder + same machine + wrong consumable = bad weld."}},{"k":"maine_welding_ecosystem","v":{"s":"Maine Welding Ecosystem"}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_emcc_employers_apprent","v":{"s":"Bath Iron Works, EMCC, employers, apprenticeships"}},{"k":"deep_dive_into_maine_s_welding_economy","v":{"s":"Deep-dive into Maine's welding economy: Bath Iron Works (US Navy destroyers, 6,500+ employees), Cianbro, Pratt & Whitney, Maine Maritime Academy, EMCC + KVCC + SMCC welding programs, apprenticeship pathways, salary ranges by employer, Maine Apprenticeship Council, MaineWorks workforce programs. The real Maine welding career map."}},{"k":"safety_health_deep_dive","v":{"s":"Safety + Health Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"fume_noise_ergonomics_vision_cancer_ri","v":{"s":"Fume, noise, ergonomics, vision, cancer risk"}},{"k":"beyond_ppe_basics_welding_fume_mangane","v":{"s":"Beyond PPE basics: welding fume (manganese, hexavalent chromium, nickel), noise exposure + hearing loss, ergonomic injuries (back, shoulder, wrist), arc-eye + cataracts, skin cancer, hot metal burns, electrocution risks, confined space, fire watch protocols, OSHA + NIOSH regs, long-term career health planning. Welders are 30%+ more likely to develop lung cancer — not a footnote."}},{"k":"welding_math_blueprints","v":{"s":"Welding Math + Blueprints"}},{"k":"fractions_decimals_geometry_blueprint_","v":{"s":"Fractions, decimals, geometry, blueprint reading"}},{"k":"the_math_drawing_literacy_every_welder","v":{"s":"The math + drawing literacy every welder needs: fractions/decimals/measurement, geometry (angles, circles, area), trigonometry basics for layout, reading welding blueprints (orthographic views, dimensioning, tolerances, welding symbols). The shop-floor literacy that distinguishes a welder who follows specs from one who interprets them."}},{"k":"welder_career_stories","v":{"s":"Welder Career Stories"}},{"k":"12_real_welder_profiles_paths","v":{"s":"12 real welder profiles + paths"}},{"k":"12_narrative_profiles_of_working_welde","v":{"s":"12 narrative profiles of working welders: shipyard journeyman, pipeline traveler, nuclear specialist, ironworker, female pipefitter, artist welder, welding instructor, mining welder, motorcycle frame builder, race car fabricator, sculptor, marine welder. What they actually do, what they earn, what they wish they'd known, how they got there."}},{"k":"soon","v":{"s":"Soon"}},{"k":"welding_metal_joining_heat_input_physi","v":{"s":"Welding & metal joining — heat-input physics, bead geometry, defect ID, AWS symbols, OSHA-aligned safety, and Maine welding careers (Bath Iron Works, EMCC, AWS)."}},{"k":"your_tier","v":{"s":"Your tier"}},{"k":"try_next","v":{"s":"→ Try next: "}},{"k":"open","v":{"s":"Open →"}},{"k":"defects_id_d","v":{"s":"Defects ID'd"}},{"k":"welder_s_defect_catalog_2","v":{"s":"Welder's Defect Catalog"}},{"k":"core_simulators","v":{"s":"Core Simulators"}},{"k":"quick_labs","v":{"s":"Quick Labs"}},{"k":"all_10_modules_live_from_heat_input_ph","v":{"s":"All 10 modules live. From heat-input physics to AWS welding symbols to Maine welding careers, plus underwater specialty + timed Speed Challenge — explore in any order."}},{"k":"mig_gmaw","v":{"s":"MIG / GMAW"}},{"k":"low_spatter_most_arc_energy_reaches_th","v":{"s":"Low spatter, most arc energy reaches the weld."}},{"k":"tig_gtaw","v":{"s":"TIG / GTAW"}},{"k":"open_arc_significant_radiative_loss","v":{"s":"Open arc, significant radiative loss."}},{"k":"stick_smaw","v":{"s":"Stick / SMAW"}},{"k":"slag_retains_heat_high_transfer_effici","v":{"s":"Slag retains heat, high transfer efficiency."}},{"k":"flame_heating_much_heat_is_lost_to_con","v":{"s":"Flame heating; much heat is lost to convection."}},{"k":"heat_input_calculator_2","v":{"s":"Heat Input Calculator"}},{"k":"the_most_important_number_in_arc_weldi","v":{"s":"The most important number in arc welding"}},{"k":"heat_input_measured_in_kilojoules_per_","v":{"s":"Heat input — measured in kilojoules per inch (kJ/in) — controls how deep your weld penetrates, how big the heat-affected zone gets, and whether you risk warping or cracking. Every certified welder calculates this. Every welding procedure specification (WPS) lists it."}},{"k":"gross_hi_kj_in_v_a_60_travel_speed_in_","v":{"s":"Gross HI (kJ/in) = (V × A × 60) ÷ (Travel Speed in/min × 1000)"}},{"k":"net_hi_gross_hi_arc_efficiency","v":{"s":"Net HI = Gross HI × arc efficiency η"}},{"k":"welding_process","v":{"s":"Welding Process"}},{"k":"welding_process_2","v":{"s":"Welding process"}},{"k":"voltage","v":{"s":"Voltage"}},{"k":"typical_mig_18_26v_tig_12_22v_stick_18","v":{"s":"Typical: MIG 18-26V, TIG 12-22V, Stick 18-30V"}},{"k":"amperage","v":{"s":"Amperage"}},{"k":"higher_amps_deeper_penetration_more_he","v":{"s":"Higher amps = deeper penetration, more heat"}},{"k":"travel_speed","v":{"s":"Travel Speed"}},{"k":"faster_travel_lower_heat_input_per_inc","v":{"s":"Faster travel = lower heat input per inch"}},{"k":"gross_heat_input","v":{"s":"Gross Heat Input"}},{"k":"arc_efficiency","v":{"s":"Arc Efficiency"}},{"k":"net_heat_input","v":{"s":"Net Heat Input"}},{"k":"heat_input_tier","v":{"s":"Heat Input Tier"}},{"k":"worked_example_verify_by_hand","v":{"s":"Worked Example — verify by hand"}},{"k":"weld_bead_lab_2","v":{"s":"Weld Bead Lab"}},{"k":"process","v":{"s":"Process"}},{"k":"welding_process_3","v":{"s":"Welding process"}},{"k":"material","v":{"s":"Material"}},{"k":"base_metal","v":{"s":"Base metal"}},{"k":"plate_thickness","v":{"s":"Plate Thickness"}},{"k":"plate_thickness_2","v":{"s":"Plate thickness"}},{"k":"voltage_2","v":{"s":"Voltage"}},{"k":"amperage_2","v":{"s":"Amperage"}},{"k":"travel_speed_2","v":{"s":"Travel Speed"}},{"k":"bead_view_mode","v":{"s":"Bead view mode"}},{"k":"3d_scene","v":{"s":"3D Scene"}},{"k":"helmet_pov","v":{"s":"Helmet POV"}},{"k":"3d_scene_configuration","v":{"s":"3D Scene Configuration"}},{"k":"joint_type","v":{"s":"Joint Type"}},{"k":"joint_type_2","v":{"s":"Joint type"}},{"k":"butt","v":{"s":"Butt"}},{"k":"edge_to_edge","v":{"s":"Edge to edge"}},{"k":"overlap","v":{"s":"Overlap"}},{"k":"perpendicular","v":{"s":"Perpendicular"}},{"k":"corner","v":{"s":"Corner"}},{"k":"edge","v":{"s":"Edge"}},{"k":"stacked_edge","v":{"s":"Stacked edge"}},{"k":"weld_position","v":{"s":"Weld Position"}},{"k":"welding_position","v":{"s":"Welding position"}},{"k":"flat_gravity_helps","v":{"s":"Flat — gravity helps"}},{"k":"horizontal_bead_runs_sideways","v":{"s":"Horizontal — bead runs sideways"}},{"k":"vertical_fight_gravity","v":{"s":"Vertical — fight gravity"}},{"k":"overhead_gravity_fights_you","v":{"s":"Overhead — gravity fights you"}},{"k":"show_real_time_defects","v":{"s":"🔍 Show real-time defects"}},{"k":"bad_parameters_visible_bad_bead","v":{"s":"— bad parameters → visible bad bead"}},{"k":"show_cross_section","v":{"s":"🪓 Show cross-section"}},{"k":"x_ray_view_of_penetration_haz_crown","v":{"s":"— X-ray view of penetration + HAZ + crown"}},{"k":"net_hi","v":{"s":"Net HI"}},{"k":"bead_width","v":{"s":"Bead Width"}},{"k":"reinforcement","v":{"s":"Reinforcement"}},{"k":"penetration","v":{"s":"Penetration"}},{"k":"score","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"inspector_scorecard","v":{"s":"Inspector Scorecard"}},{"k":"penetration_2","v":{"s":"Penetration"}},{"k":"uniformity","v":{"s":"Uniformity"}},{"k":"even_ripple_spacing_steady_travel_spee","v":{"s":"Even ripple spacing — steady travel speed"}},{"k":"spatter_control","v":{"s":"Spatter Control"}},{"k":"about_this_material","v":{"s":"About this material"}},{"k":"burnthrough","v":{"s":"Burnthrough"}},{"k":"hole_punched_through","v":{"s":"hole punched through"}},{"k":"lack_of_fusion","v":{"s":"Lack of fusion"}},{"k":"cold_weld_no_bond","v":{"s":"cold weld, no bond"}},{"k":"undercut","v":{"s":"Undercut"}},{"k":"groove_next_to_bead","v":{"s":"groove next to bead"}},{"k":"overlap_2","v":{"s":"Overlap"}},{"k":"bead_overhangs_edge","v":{"s":"bead overhangs edge"}},{"k":"spatter","v":{"s":"Spatter"}},{"k":"molten_splatter","v":{"s":"molten splatter"}},{"k":"porosity","v":{"s":"Porosity"}},{"k":"gas_bubbles_in_bead","v":{"s":"gas bubbles in bead"}},{"k":"loading_3d_scene","v":{"s":"Loading 3D scene…"}},{"k":"3d_view_unavailable","v":{"s":"3D view unavailable"}},{"k":"three_js_failed_to_load_cdn_blocked_of","v":{"s":"Three.js failed to load (CDN blocked, offline, or WebGL not supported on this device). Switch back to top-down view above."}},{"k":"retry_3d_mode","v":{"s":"Retry 3D Mode"}},{"k":"reset_camera_to_default_angle","v":{"s":"Reset camera to default angle"}},{"k":"reset_camera","v":{"s":"↺ Reset camera"}},{"k":"drag_to_orbit","v":{"s":"🖱 Drag to orbit"}},{"k":"wheel_to_zoom","v":{"s":"⚙ Wheel to zoom"}},{"k":"arrow_keys_for_keyboard_control","v":{"s":"⌨ Arrow keys + / − for keyboard control"}},{"k":"same_v_a_ts_controls_above_drive_both_","v":{"s":"— same V/A/TS controls above drive both views"}},{"k":"cross_section_profile_view_showing_pen","v":{"s":"Cross-section profile view showing penetration depth, heat-affected zone, and reinforcement crown"}},{"k":"the_cross_section_view_is_what_an_insp","v":{"s":"The cross-section view is what an inspector sees when they cut the weld in half + polish + etch with nital (carbon steel) or oxalic (stainless). "}},{"k":"the_bright_dome_above_the_plate_is_the","v":{"s":"The bright dome above the plate is the "}},{"k":"reinforcement_crown","v":{"s":"reinforcement crown"}},{"k":"excess_filler_stacked_above_the_surfac","v":{"s":" (excess filler stacked above the surface — should be ~1–3 mm for code). "}},{"k":"the_orange_halo_is_the","v":{"s":"The orange halo is the "}},{"k":"heat_affected_zone_haz","v":{"s":"heat-affected zone (HAZ)"}},{"k":"base_metal_that_didn_t_melt_but_reache","v":{"s":" — base metal that didn't melt but reached temperatures high enough to change grain structure + properties. "}},{"k":"the_depth_into_the_plate_is_the","v":{"s":"The depth into the plate is the "}},{"k":"how_deep_into_the_base_metal_the_fusio","v":{"s":" — how deep into the base metal the fusion actually went."}},{"k":"defect_overlay_legend","v":{"s":"🔍 Defect Overlay Legend"}},{"k":"defects_appear_when_your_parameters_cr","v":{"s":"⚠ Defects appear when your parameters cross unsafe thresholds. Try "}},{"k":"v_14_a_300_ts_4_on_1_8_steel","v":{"s":"V=14, A=300, TS=4 on 1/8\" steel"}},{"k":"to_trigger_burnthrough_try","v":{"s":" to trigger burnthrough. Try "}},{"k":"v_14_a_80_ts_22_on_3_8_steel","v":{"s":"V=14, A=80, TS=22 on 3/8\" steel"}},{"k":"to_trigger_lack_of_fusion_real_welders","v":{"s":" to trigger lack of fusion. Real welders read their bead this way — every defect has a parameter cause."}},{"k":"what_you_are_seeing","v":{"s":"🥽 What you are seeing"}},{"k":"this_is_approximately_what_a_welder_se","v":{"s":"This is approximately what a welder sees through an auto-darkening hood with the lens at Shade 11. The lens darkens within about 1 millisecond of arc strike. Most of the visual world goes black; the arc and the immediate weld pool are nearly the only things visible. The faint rectangle is the plate; the dark shape in the lower-right is your gloved hand on the torch."}},{"k":"why_so_dark","v":{"s":"Why so dark?"}},{"k":"a_welding_arc_is_roughly_as_bright_as_","v":{"s":"A welding arc is roughly as bright as the surface of the sun. Without lens shading, retinal damage happens in milliseconds. Shade 11 reduces visible light by ~99.9% and blocks UV / IR."}},{"k":"why_this_teaches_the_craft","v":{"s":"Why this teaches the craft"}},{"k":"you_can_t_see_fine_detail_once_the_arc","v":{"s":"You can't see fine detail once the arc is lit — you set up your torch position, work angle, and travel speed BEFORE striking, then run by muscle memory + the small visible region around the pool. That is what years of practice build."}},{"k":"auto_vs_fixed_shade","v":{"s":"Auto vs fixed-shade"}},{"k":"old_fixed_shade_hoods_stayed_at_shade_","v":{"s":"Old fixed-shade hoods stayed at Shade 11 always — you flipped them down only after striking. Many old welders developed cataracts or had 'flash burn' incidents. Auto-darkening (1980s+) lets you see clearly until the moment you strike."}},{"k":"porosity_2","v":{"s":"Porosity"}},{"k":"undercut_2","v":{"s":"Undercut"}},{"k":"lack_of_fusion_2","v":{"s":"Lack of Fusion"}},{"k":"cold_lap","v":{"s":"Cold Lap"}},{"k":"crater_crack","v":{"s":"Crater Crack"}},{"k":"overlap_3","v":{"s":"Overlap"}},{"k":"sample_a_fillet_weld_on_t_joint_mild_s","v":{"s":"Sample A — Fillet weld on T-joint, mild steel"}},{"k":"sample_b_butt_weld_3_8_plate","v":{"s":"Sample B — Butt weld, 3/8\" plate"}},{"k":"sample_c_lap_joint_aluminum","v":{"s":"Sample C — Lap joint, aluminum"}},{"k":"defect_hunt_lab_2","v":{"s":"Defect Hunt Lab"}},{"k":"inspection_sample","v":{"s":"Inspection Sample"}},{"k":"select_sample_to_inspect","v":{"s":"Select sample to inspect"}},{"k":"inspect_next_zone_keyboard","v":{"s":"🔎 Inspect next zone (keyboard)"}},{"k":"keyboard_alternative_identifies_the_ne","v":{"s":"Keyboard alternative — identifies the next un-found defect."}},{"k":"defects_found","v":{"s":"Defects Found"}},{"k":"score_2","v":{"s":"Score"}},{"k":"defect_reports_cause_remediation","v":{"s":"Defect Reports — Cause & Remediation"}},{"k":"six_common_defect_types_quick_referenc","v":{"s":"Six common defect types — quick reference"}},{"k":"mig_gmaw_2","v":{"s":"MIG / GMAW"}},{"k":"tig_gtaw_2","v":{"s":"TIG / GTAW"}},{"k":"stick_smaw_2","v":{"s":"Stick / SMAW"}},{"k":"oxy_fuel_ofw","v":{"s":"Oxy-Fuel / OFW"}},{"k":"skill_required","v":{"s":"Skill required"}},{"k":"welding_speed","v":{"s":"Welding speed"}},{"k":"joint_quality","v":{"s":"Joint quality"}},{"k":"portability","v":{"s":"Portability"}},{"k":"equipment_cost","v":{"s":"Equipment cost"}},{"k":"easy_to_learn","v":{"s":"Easy to learn"}},{"k":"process_comparison_2","v":{"s":"Process Comparison"}},{"k":"pick_the_right_process_for_the_job","v":{"s":"Pick the right process for the job"}},{"k":"no_process_is_best_each_one_is_best_fo","v":{"s":"No process is \"best\" — each one is best for some jobs. Hiring managers test exactly this: \"I need to weld 16-gauge stainless for a food-service line. What process and why?\" If you can answer questions like that, you know welding."}},{"k":"process_2","v":{"s":"Process"}},{"k":"best_for","v":{"s":"Best For"}},{"k":"materials","v":{"s":"Materials"}},{"k":"side_by_side_comparison","v":{"s":"Side-by-side comparison"}},{"k":"attribute","v":{"s":"Attribute"}},{"k":"stick","v":{"s":"Stick"}},{"k":"each_row_scored_1_5_more_dots_more_of_","v":{"s":"Each row scored 1-5. More dots = more of that attribute."}},{"k":"strengths","v":{"s":"✓ Strengths"}},{"k":"limitations","v":{"s":"× Limitations"}},{"k":"equipment_setup","v":{"s":"Equipment & Setup"}},{"k":"real_world_example","v":{"s":"Real-world example"}},{"k":"quick_decision_guide","v":{"s":"Quick decision guide"}},{"k":"butt_joint","v":{"s":"Butt Joint"}},{"k":"two_pieces_of_metal_lying_in_the_same_","v":{"s":"Two pieces of metal lying in the same plane, edge-to-edge. The most common joint in pressure vessels, pipe, ship hull plate, and any application where the two pieces must form one continuous surface."}},{"k":"lap_joint","v":{"s":"Lap Joint"}},{"k":"one_piece_of_metal_overlapping_another","v":{"s":"One piece of metal overlapping another, with weld along the edge of the top piece. Common in sheet metal, auto body, and any application where you can't fit a butt joint."}},{"k":"two_pieces_meeting_perpendicular_formi","v":{"s":"Two pieces meeting perpendicular, forming a T-shape. The \"fillet weld\" you see on furniture, brackets, gussets, and any structural intersection."}},{"k":"corner_joint","v":{"s":"Corner Joint"}},{"k":"two_pieces_meeting_at_right_angles_at_","v":{"s":"Two pieces meeting at right angles, at their edges (not perpendicular through the middle like a T-joint). Forms an L-shape. Common in box fabrication."}},{"k":"edge_joint","v":{"s":"Edge Joint"}},{"k":"two_pieces_lying_parallel_with_their_e","v":{"s":"Two pieces lying parallel, with their edges aligned and welded along that edge. Used for sealing thin-gauge sheet metal where strength is secondary."}},{"k":"flat_fillet","v":{"s":"Flat Fillet"}},{"k":"joint_horizontal_weld_at_45_to_vertica","v":{"s":"Joint horizontal, weld at 45° to vertical. Easiest position. Gravity helps."}},{"k":"horizontal_fillet","v":{"s":"Horizontal Fillet"}},{"k":"joint_vertical_but_the_weld_itself_run","v":{"s":"Joint vertical, but the weld itself runs horizontal along the joint."}},{"k":"vertical_fillet","v":{"s":"Vertical Fillet"}},{"k":"joint_vertical_welder_works_up_or_down","v":{"s":"Joint vertical, welder works up or down. Up is harder but stronger."}},{"k":"overhead_fillet","v":{"s":"Overhead Fillet"}},{"k":"joint_above_welder_s_head_hardest_posi","v":{"s":"Joint above welder's head. Hardest position — molten metal fights gravity."}},{"k":"flat_groove","v":{"s":"Flat Groove"}},{"k":"pipe_or_plate_horizontal_plate_flat_on","v":{"s":"Pipe or plate horizontal. Plate flat on table, pipe rotated under torch."}},{"k":"horizontal_groove","v":{"s":"Horizontal Groove"}},{"k":"pipe_vertical_plate_vertical_weld_runs","v":{"s":"Pipe vertical / plate vertical, weld runs horizontal around / across."}},{"k":"pipe_fixed_horizontal","v":{"s":"Pipe Fixed Horizontal"}},{"k":"pipe_horizontal_fixed_won_t_rotate_wel","v":{"s":"Pipe horizontal, fixed (won't rotate). Welder works around — flat → vertical → overhead in one pass."}},{"k":"pipe_fixed_45","v":{"s":"Pipe Fixed 45°"}},{"k":"pipe_at_45_angle_fixed_the_test_positi","v":{"s":"Pipe at 45° angle, fixed. The \"test\" position for AWS pipe certification — combines all difficulties."}},{"k":"joint_catalog_quiz","v":{"s":"Joint Catalog — Quiz"}},{"k":"back_to_catalog","v":{"s":"← Back to Catalog"}},{"k":"your_answers","v":{"s":"Your answers"}},{"k":"badge_earned_joint_reader","v":{"s":"🏅 Badge earned: Joint Reader"}},{"k":"retry_quiz","v":{"s":"🔄 Retry Quiz"}},{"k":"identify_this_joint","v":{"s":"Identify this joint"}},{"k":"cross_section_diagram_of_a_welded_join","v":{"s":"Cross-section diagram of a welded joint. Identify which joint configuration this represents."}},{"k":"joint_type_choices","v":{"s":"Joint type choices"}},{"k":"joint_configuration_catalog","v":{"s":"Joint Configuration Catalog"}},{"k":"the_five_fundamental_joint_configurati","v":{"s":"The five fundamental joint configurations"}},{"k":"every_welded_structure_on_earth_is_bui","v":{"s":"Every welded structure on Earth is built from some combination of these five joints. Recognize them at a glance and you're halfway to reading any blueprint or fabrication drawing."}},{"k":"joint_type_tabs","v":{"s":"Joint type tabs"}},{"k":"weld_types_used","v":{"s":"Weld types used"}},{"k":"common_applications","v":{"s":"Common applications"}},{"k":"watch_for_these_defects","v":{"s":"Watch for these defects"}},{"k":"aws_position_codes_what_1f_3g_6g_mean_","v":{"s":"AWS Position Codes — what 1F / 3G / 6G mean on a print"}},{"k":"aws_certification_trivia","v":{"s":"AWS certification trivia: "}},{"k":"a_welder_certified_for","v":{"s":"A welder certified for "}},{"k":"45_fixed_pipe_is_automatically_qualifi","v":{"s":" (45° fixed pipe) is automatically qualified for all easier positions. The reverse is not true."}},{"k":"test_yourself_joint_matching_quiz","v":{"s":"Test yourself — Joint Matching Quiz"}},{"k":"5_randomized_cross_section_diagrams_ma","v":{"s":"5 randomized cross-section diagrams. Match each to the right joint name. Real welding-test prep."}},{"k":"start_quiz","v":{"s":"🎯 Start Quiz"}},{"k":"welding_symbol_anatomy_diagram","v":{"s":"Welding symbol anatomy diagram"}},{"k":"arrow_joint","v":{"s":"Arrow → joint"}},{"k":"field_weld","v":{"s":"+ Field weld"}},{"k":"size_6_mm","v":{"s":"Size 6 (mm)"}},{"k":"arrow_side","v":{"s":"Arrow side ↑"}},{"k":"other_side","v":{"s":"Other side ↓"}},{"k":"process_mig_gmaw","v":{"s":"Process: MIG (GMAW)"}},{"k":"fillet_weld","v":{"s":"Fillet weld"}},{"k":"square_groove","v":{"s":"Square groove"}},{"k":"bevel_groove","v":{"s":"Bevel groove"}},{"k":"plug_slot","v":{"s":"Plug / slot"}},{"k":"field_weld_flag","v":{"s":"Field weld flag"}},{"k":"flush_contour","v":{"s":"Flush contour"}},{"k":"convex_contour","v":{"s":"Convex contour"}},{"k":"welding_symbols_reader_2","v":{"s":"Welding Symbols Reader"}},{"k":"symbol_reader_sections","v":{"s":"Symbol Reader sections"}},{"k":"1_anatomy_of_a_symbol","v":{"s":"1. Anatomy of a Symbol"}},{"k":"2_common_symbols","v":{"s":"2. Common Symbols"}},{"k":"3_decoder_practice","v":{"s":"3. Decoder Practice"}},{"k":"aws_a2_4_the_welding_symbol_explained","v":{"s":"AWS A2.4 — the welding symbol explained"}},{"k":"every_welded_part_on_every_engineering","v":{"s":"Every welded part on every engineering drawing in the country uses this same compact language. The reference line is the spine; everything else hangs off it. Master the parts and you can read any print."}},{"k":"common_weld_symbols_visual_reference","v":{"s":"Common weld symbols — visual reference"}},{"k":"memory_hooks_for_common_symbols","v":{"s":"Memory hooks for common symbols"}},{"k":"challenge_complete","v":{"s":"✓ Challenge complete"}},{"k":"you_decoded_all_parts_of_this_symbol_c","v":{"s":"You decoded all parts of this symbol correctly. Move on to the next challenge."}},{"k":"one_or_more_answers_above_are_wrong_re","v":{"s":"⚠ One or more answers above are wrong. Re-read the symbol and try again."}},{"k":"auto_darkening_welding_helmet","v":{"s":"Auto-darkening welding helmet"}},{"k":"flame_resistant_fr_jacket_coveralls","v":{"s":"Flame-resistant (FR) jacket / coveralls"}},{"k":"leather_welding_gloves","v":{"s":"Leather welding gloves"}},{"k":"respirator_when_required","v":{"s":"Respirator (when required)"}},{"k":"local_exhaust_ventilation","v":{"s":"Local exhaust ventilation"}},{"k":"fire_watch_extinguisher","v":{"s":"Fire watch + extinguisher"}},{"k":"steel_toe_high_top_boots","v":{"s":"Steel-toe high-top boots"}},{"k":"arc_flash_bystander_exposure","v":{"s":"Arc Flash / Bystander Exposure"}},{"k":"stop_welding_brief_them_on_flash_burn_","v":{"s":"Stop welding, brief them on flash burn risk, set up a portable welding curtain before resuming."}},{"k":"tell_them_they_re_probably_fine_since_","v":{"s":"Tell them they're probably fine since it was brief. Resume welding."}},{"k":"hand_them_your_spare_helmet_and_contin","v":{"s":"Hand them your spare helmet and continue welding while they walk through."}},{"k":"galvanized_steel_no_respirator","v":{"s":"Galvanized Steel + No Respirator"}},{"k":"refuse_to_weld_unless_you_can_grind_th","v":{"s":"Refuse to weld unless you can grind the galvanizing off the joint area first AND have a respirator OR work outdoors with strong wind."}},{"k":"open_the_garage_door_for_ventilation_a","v":{"s":"Open the garage door for ventilation and weld it quickly."}},{"k":"hold_your_breath_while_welding_the_bea","v":{"s":"Hold your breath while welding the bead."}},{"k":"hot_work_without_a_fire_watch","v":{"s":"Hot Work Without a Fire Watch"}},{"k":"stop_either_move_the_cardboard_install","v":{"s":"Stop. Either move the cardboard, install a spark-blanket curtain, or wait for the supervisor (or another fire watch) to return before continuing."}},{"k":"just_finish_the_bead_it_s_only_a_few_i","v":{"s":"Just finish the bead — it's only a few inches."}},{"k":"move_5_more_feet_from_the_boxes_and_we","v":{"s":"Move 5 more feet from the boxes and weld."}},{"k":"confined_space_welding","v":{"s":"Confined Space Welding"}},{"k":"refuse_until_proper_confined_space_ent","v":{"s":"Refuse until proper confined-space entry is set up: atmospheric testing, lockout/tagout, ventilation, attendant outside, retrieval line. \"Hot work\" + \"confined space\" + \"container that held flammables\" requires the strictest protocol."}},{"k":"sniff_the_drum_if_you_don_t_smell_oil_","v":{"s":"Sniff the drum. If you don't smell oil, weld it."}},{"k":"fill_the_drum_with_water_first_then_we","v":{"s":"Fill the drum with water first, then weld the dry section above the water line."}},{"k":"ppe_safety_2","v":{"s":"PPE & Safety"}},{"k":"welding_can_hurt_or_kill_you_if_you_sk","v":{"s":"Welding can hurt or kill you if you skip the gear."}},{"k":"arc_burns_the_retina_in_seconds_galvan","v":{"s":"Arc burns the retina in seconds. Galvanized fumes cause \"zinc fume fever.\" Stainless fumes are a known carcinogen (hexavalent chromium). Hot-work sparks travel 30+ feet and smolder for hours. Every PPE rule below has a body count behind it."}},{"k":"ppe_safety_sections","v":{"s":"PPE & Safety sections"}},{"k":"1_gear_up","v":{"s":"1. Gear Up"}},{"k":"2_hazard_scenarios","v":{"s":"2. Hazard Scenarios"}},{"k":"3_quick_reference","v":{"s":"3. Quick Reference"}},{"k":"gear_up_every_item_is_non_negotiable","v":{"s":"Gear up — every item is non-negotiable"}},{"k":"hood_gloves_jacket_boots_respirator_wh","v":{"s":"Hood, gloves, jacket, boots, respirator (when needed). Skip one and you do not weld today. Arc burns retinas in seconds."}},{"k":"hazard_scenarios_what_could_go_wrong","v":{"s":"Hazard scenarios — what could go wrong"}},{"k":"galvanized_fumes_hexavalent_chromium_f","v":{"s":"Galvanized fumes, hexavalent chromium from stainless, hot-work sparks at 30+ ft. Each scenario is from a documented incident."}},{"k":"quick_reference_pin_to_your_locker","v":{"s":"Quick reference — pin to your locker"}},{"k":"material_process_lethal_fume_table_hot","v":{"s":"Material × process × lethal-fume table, hot-work fire-watch rules, OSHA 1910.252 cheat-sheet — the things you actually look up at 7 AM."}},{"k":"click_each_item_as_you_put_it_on","v":{"s":"Click each item as you put it on"}},{"k":"geared_up_ready_to_weld","v":{"s":"✓ Geared up. Ready to weld."}},{"k":"required","v":{"s":"Required"}},{"k":"when_req","v":{"s":"When req."}},{"k":"response_choices","v":{"s":"Response choices"}},{"k":"welding_safety_quick_reference_card","v":{"s":"Welding-safety quick reference card"}},{"k":"authoritative_sources_to_bookmark","v":{"s":"Authoritative sources to bookmark"}},{"k":"entry_helper","v":{"s":"Entry / Helper"}},{"k":"aws_d1_1_structural_cert","v":{"s":"AWS D1.1 Structural Cert"}},{"k":"6g_pipe_stainless_cert","v":{"s":"6G Pipe / Stainless Cert"}},{"k":"cwi_welding_inspector","v":{"s":"CWI (Welding Inspector)"}},{"k":"specialty_underwater_aerospace","v":{"s":"Specialty / Underwater / Aerospace"}},{"k":"trade_school_community_college","v":{"s":"Trade school / community college"}},{"k":"eastern_maine_community_college_emcc_i","v":{"s":"Eastern Maine Community College (EMCC) in Bangor offers a 2-year Welding Technology AAS program (and a shorter 1-year certificate). Hands-on shop time, AWS certification testing built in. Pell-eligible."}},{"k":"apprenticeship","v":{"s":"Apprenticeship"}},{"k":"earn_while_you_learn_registered_appren","v":{"s":"Earn while you learn. Registered apprenticeships at unions (Iron Workers Local 7), at shops (Cianbro, BIW), or via Maine Apprenticeship Program (state-registered). Paid wages from day one, escalating as you certify."}},{"k":"military","v":{"s":"Military"}},{"k":"u_s_navy_hull_maintenance_technician_h","v":{"s":"U.S. Navy Hull Maintenance Technician (HT) rate is essentially a paid welding career — Navy pays for training, gives you 4-6 years of experience welding on ships. After service, BIW, Portsmouth Naval, and civilian shipyards actively recruit Navy HTs."}},{"k":"self_taught_certified","v":{"s":"Self-taught + certified"}},{"k":"buy_a_welder_watch_hours_of_youtube_we","v":{"s":"Buy a welder, watch hours of YouTube (Weld.com, Tim Welds, Pacific Arc Tig), practice on scrap. When ready, walk into an AWS-accredited test facility and pay to test. Pass D1.1 = certified, regardless of how you learned."}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_bath","v":{"s":"Bath Iron Works (Bath)"}},{"k":"cianbro_pittsfield_statewide","v":{"s":"Cianbro (Pittsfield + statewide)"}},{"k":"nd_paper_old_town","v":{"s":"ND Paper (Old Town)"}},{"k":"portland_yacht_services","v":{"s":"Portland Yacht Services"}},{"k":"pratt_whitney_north_berwick","v":{"s":"Pratt & Whitney (North Berwick)"}},{"k":"reed_reed_woolwich","v":{"s":"Reed & Reed (Woolwich)"}},{"k":"career_pathways_2","v":{"s":"Career Pathways"}},{"k":"welding_in_maine","v":{"s":"Welding in Maine"}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_hires_hundreds_of_weld","v":{"s":"Bath Iron Works hires hundreds of welders a year for US Navy destroyer construction. Cianbro, regional marine yards, paper mills, and pipeline contractors all post welding openings. AWS D1.1 certification + a high-school diploma can land a $50-70K job at age 19. 6G TIG cert can push it to $90K+. No four-year degree required."}},{"k":"career_pathway_sections","v":{"s":"Career pathway sections"}},{"k":"1_why_welding","v":{"s":"1. Why Welding"}},{"k":"2_cert_ladder","v":{"s":"2. Cert Ladder"}},{"k":"3_entry_paths","v":{"s":"3. Entry Paths"}},{"k":"4_me_employers","v":{"s":"4. ME Employers"}},{"k":"starting_pay_with_cert","v":{"s":"starting pay with cert"}},{"k":"mid_career_typical","v":{"s":"mid-career typical"}},{"k":"four_year_degrees_needed","v":{"s":"four-year degrees needed"}},{"k":"what_s_the_day_to_day_actually_like","v":{"s":"What's the day-to-day actually like?"}},{"k":"the_good","v":{"s":"✓ The good"}},{"k":"tangible_result_every_day_you_can_see_","v":{"s":"Tangible result every day — you can SEE what you built"}},{"k":"strong_demand_low_layoff_risk_in_skill","v":{"s":"Strong demand, low layoff risk in skilled trades"}},{"k":"skills_transfer_geographically_every_s","v":{"s":"Skills transfer geographically — every shipyard / mill needs welders"}},{"k":"path_to_your_own_shop_contractor_work_","v":{"s":"Path to your own shop / contractor work after experience"}},{"k":"overtime_premium_pay_common_in_shipyar","v":{"s":"Overtime + premium pay common in shipyards"}},{"k":"the_hard_parts","v":{"s":"⚠ The hard parts"}},{"k":"physical_work_bend_kneel_hold_position","v":{"s":"Physical work — bend, kneel, hold positions, lift"}},{"k":"hot_sweaty_fume_y_environment","v":{"s":"Hot, sweaty, fume-y environment"}},{"k":"vision_and_respiratory_health_require_","v":{"s":"Vision and respiratory health require lifelong PPE discipline"}},{"k":"shift_work_common_in_production_shops","v":{"s":"Shift work common in production shops"}},{"k":"joint_stress_knees_back_shoulders_accu","v":{"s":"Joint stress (knees, back, shoulders) accumulates over decades"}},{"k":"a_first_day_on_the_floor","v":{"s":"A first day on the floor"}},{"k":"composite_bath_iron_works_maine","v":{"s":"Composite — Bath Iron Works, Maine"}},{"k":"composite_voice_drawn_from_biw_emcc_we","v":{"s":"Composite voice drawn from BIW + EMCC welder accounts. Names changed; everything else is from the floor."}},{"k":"six_fifteen_on_a_monday_morning_you_pa","v":{"s":"Six-fifteen on a Monday morning. You park under the gantry crane that built the "}},{"k":"arleigh_burke","v":{"s":"Arleigh Burke"}},{"k":"class_hulls_and_watched","v":{"s":"-class hulls and watched "}},{"k":"zumwalts","v":{"s":"Zumwalts"}},{"k":"slide_down_the_ways_the_shift_whistle_","v":{"s":" slide down the ways. The shift whistle goes at six-thirty. You're early because your dad worked here for thirty-one years and told you, \"If you're on time, you're late.\""}},{"k":"your_assignor_walks_you_onto_the_deck_","v":{"s":"Your assignor walks you onto the deck. The "}},{"k":"you_ll_be_welding_on_for_the_next_year","v":{"s":" you'll be welding on for the next year is parked on stands the size of school buses. It is bigger than you imagined and somehow also smaller — closer up than the photos. Inside, the smell hits first: hot metal, weld fume, machine oil. The sound second: arc gouging from two bays over (a sound like the world's loudest staple gun), grinders, the ventilation rumble."}},{"k":"by_lunch_you_have_not_welded_anything_","v":{"s":"By lunch you have not welded anything. You have learned where the eyewash station is, where the fire extinguishers live, what the alarm signals mean, where to clip your harness when you go up. You have watched Maria run a 6G stainless joint that you could not have done if your life depended on it. She makes it look like she's painting a wall."}},{"k":"after_lunch_she_lets_you_strike_your_f","v":{"s":"After lunch she lets you strike your first arc on a piece of scrap. Your hands shake. The bead looks terrible. She nods once and says, \"Better than mine on day one.\" You spend the rest of the afternoon practicing on scrap, stopping every fifteen minutes to drink water (the booth gets to 90 °F by 3 pm), watching the people around you do something that takes them about three years to learn well."}},{"k":"the_shift_whistle_goes_at_three_you_ha","v":{"s":"The shift whistle goes at three. You have a small cut on your wrist where the leather glove rode up. You have not earned anything you could put on a wall. You have learned the layout of the bay, the names of four people, and that this work is going to be harder and slower than every YouTube video made it look. On the way to your car you see the gantry crane swing a bow section toward the dry dock. You will help build that, eventually. Not this week. Maybe not this year. Eventually."}},{"k":"what_this_captures","v":{"s":"What this captures"}},{"k":"pace_scale_sensory_load_the_specific_s","v":{"s":"Pace, scale, sensory load, the specific safety culture of a shipyard. The \"do not touch anything\" first day is real practice."}},{"k":"what_it_leaves_out","v":{"s":"What it leaves out"}},{"k":"union_vs_non_union_politics_shift_diff","v":{"s":"Union vs non-union politics, shift differentials, deployments to other yards, what happens when a hull is delayed. Real, but not the day-one story."}},{"k":"discussion","v":{"s":"Discussion"}},{"k":"what_in_this_story_would_draw_you_in_w","v":{"s":"What in this story would draw you in? What would push you away? What questions would you want to ask Maria before signing on?"}},{"k":"welding_career_ladder_typical_progress","v":{"s":"Welding career ladder — typical progression"}},{"k":"how_to_get_there","v":{"s":"How to get there: "}},{"k":"about_these_pay_bands","v":{"s":"About these pay bands"}},{"k":"wages_are_2025_ranges_based_on_us_bure","v":{"s":"Wages are 2025 ranges based on US Bureau of Labor Statistics, AWS surveys, and Maine-specific employer data. Actual pay varies by employer, location, overtime, and whether shop is union. BIW + heavy industrial trends to the higher end of each band."}},{"k":"pros","v":{"s":"✓ Pros"}},{"k":"cons","v":{"s":"× Cons"}},{"k":"maine_specific","v":{"s":"Maine specific: "}},{"k":"maine_welding_employers_sample_of_wher","v":{"s":"Maine welding employers — sample of where welders work in-state"}},{"k":"other_places_to_look","v":{"s":"Other places to look"}},{"k":"careercenter_maine_mainecareercenter_g","v":{"s":"CareerCenter Maine (mainecareercenter.gov) lists open welding positions statewide. Indeed and ZipRecruiter aggregate shop postings. Most large employers (BIW, Cianbro, Pratt) have dedicated career portals where you can apply directly without a recruiter. Many small-town shops still rely on word-of-mouth — walking in and asking is not unusual or unwelcome in this trade."}},{"k":"underwater_welding_2","v":{"s":"Underwater Welding"}},{"k":"welding_under_the_sea","v":{"s":"Welding under the sea"}},{"k":"commercial_underwater_welders_earn_80_","v":{"s":"Commercial underwater welders earn $80-200K+ but they earn every penny. The work is dangerous, the certifications stack up, and the physics are unforgiving — water cools your weld 5-8 times faster than air, dissolved hydrogen embrittles the steel, and the arc itself is electrically conductive in saltwater. Two techniques dominate: WET (you and the torch are both in the water) and HYPERBARIC (a sealed dry chamber keeps water out)."}},{"k":"underwater_welding_sections","v":{"s":"Underwater welding sections"}},{"k":"wet_welding","v":{"s":"Wet Welding"}},{"k":"you_re_in_the_water_the_torch_is_in_th","v":{"s":"You're in the water. The torch is in the water. The metal is in the water. Stick (SMAW) is the dominant process — the flux coating creates a tiny gas bubble around the arc that excludes water just long enough to fuse the metal. Used for emergency repairs, inspection follow-up work, and routine maintenance on offshore structures."}},{"k":"pros_2","v":{"s":"✓ Pros: "}},{"k":"lower_setup_cost_fast_deployment_for_e","v":{"s":"Lower setup cost. Fast deployment for emergency repair. Most accessible certification path."}},{"k":"cons_2","v":{"s":"× Cons: "}},{"k":"lower_weld_quality_class_b_by_us_navy_","v":{"s":"Lower weld quality (Class B by US Navy spec). Hydrogen embrittlement risk. Practical depth limit ~100 ft. Lower pay than hyperbaric."}},{"k":"smaw_with_e6013_e7014_waterproofed_ele","v":{"s":"SMAW with E6013 / E7014 waterproofed electrodes"}},{"k":"hyperbaric_dry_welding","v":{"s":"Hyperbaric (Dry) Welding"}},{"k":"a_sealed_pressurized_chamber_called_a_","v":{"s":"A sealed pressurized chamber (called a \"habitat\") is lowered around the joint. Water is displaced by inert gas (argon-helium mix). The diver enters the dry habitat and welds normally — usually GTAW (TIG) for code-quality results. Used for permanent repairs on pipelines, oil rig riser sections, and ship hulls."}},{"k":"pros_3","v":{"s":"✓ Pros: "}},{"k":"code_quality_welds_class_a_works_at_an","v":{"s":"Code-quality welds (Class A). Works at any practical depth. Can use any process (TIG, MIG, Stick). Highest pay."}},{"k":"cons_3","v":{"s":"× Cons: "}},{"k":"massive_setup_cost_chamber_support_shi","v":{"s":"Massive setup cost (chamber + support ship). Long mobilization time. Decompression schedule for diver. Niche specialty — fewer jobs."}},{"k":"gtaw_tig_most_common_smaw_gmaw_also_po","v":{"s":"GTAW (TIG) most common; SMAW + GMAW also possible in dry chamber"}},{"k":"technique","v":{"s":"Technique"}},{"k":"underwater_welding_technique","v":{"s":"Underwater welding technique"}},{"k":"depth","v":{"s":"Depth"}},{"k":"0_ft","v":{"s":"0 ft"}},{"k":"100_ft","v":{"s":"100 ft"}},{"k":"200_ft","v":{"s":"200 ft"}},{"k":"300_ft","v":{"s":"300 ft"}},{"k":"pressure","v":{"s":"Pressure"}},{"k":"cooling_rate","v":{"s":"Cooling Rate"}},{"k":"hydrogen_risk","v":{"s":"Hydrogen Risk"}},{"k":"visibility","v":{"s":"Visibility"}},{"k":"beyond_practical_wet_welding_limits","v":{"s":"Beyond practical wet-welding limits"}},{"k":"past_100_ft_wet_welding_becomes_commer","v":{"s":"Past ~100 ft, wet welding becomes commercially impractical. Hydrogen embrittlement risk, narcosis at depth, and code-quality issues mean industry switches to hyperbaric chambers or saturation diving for permanent repairs."}},{"k":"why_depth_matters_so_much","v":{"s":"Why depth matters so much"}},{"k":"underwater_welding_fatality_rate_is_am","v":{"s":"Underwater welding fatality rate is among the highest in any trade."}},{"k":"industry_estimates_put_fatality_rates_","v":{"s":"Industry estimates put fatality rates 5-10× higher than topside welding. The hazards stack: drowning + electric shock + decompression sickness + explosion risk + structural collapse + hypothermia. This isn't a scare tactic — it's why the pay is what it is."}},{"k":"electric_shock","v":{"s":"Electric shock"}},{"k":"saltwater_conducts_equipment_must_be_d","v":{"s":"Saltwater conducts. Equipment must be DC negative polarity, fully insulated, with automatic dead-man cutoff. AC welding underwater is illegal — fatal nearly every time."}},{"k":"hydrogen_explosion","v":{"s":"Hydrogen explosion"}},{"k":"arc_dissociates_water_into_h_o_if_the_","v":{"s":"Arc dissociates water into H₂ + O₂. If the gas pocket gets trapped (hull cavity, pipe), it can detonate. Multiple recorded fatalities. Procedures include venting cavities before lighting up."}},{"k":"decompression_sickness","v":{"s":"Decompression sickness"}},{"k":"working_at_depth_on_compressed_gas_the","v":{"s":"Working at depth on compressed gas, the diver-welder must follow strict ascent / decompression schedules. \"Bends\" can cause permanent neurological damage or death. Hyperbaric work uses saturation diving."}},{"k":"hypothermia","v":{"s":"Hypothermia"}},{"k":"cold_water_long_shifts_4_8h_underwater","v":{"s":"Cold water + long shifts (4-8h underwater is common). Dry suits, hot-water suits, and constant monitoring required."}},{"k":"visibility_disorientation","v":{"s":"Visibility / disorientation"}},{"k":"stirred_up_sediment_can_cut_visibility","v":{"s":"Stirred-up sediment can cut visibility to inches. Diver works by feel. Tether lines + comm checks every 60 seconds standard practice."}},{"k":"object_strikes_collapse","v":{"s":"Object strikes / collapse"}},{"k":"strong_currents_surge_from_passing_ves","v":{"s":"Strong currents, surge from passing vessels, structural failure of the work piece. Always two divers + topside attendant + retrieval line."}},{"k":"the_standards_that_exist_for_a_reason","v":{"s":"The standards that exist for a reason"}},{"k":"commercial_diver_welder","v":{"s":"commercial diver-welder"}},{"k":"commercial_dive_school","v":{"s":"commercial dive school"}},{"k":"to_journeyman_level","v":{"s":"to journeyman level"}},{"k":"the_path_step_by_step","v":{"s":"The path — step by step"}},{"k":"maine_specific_note","v":{"s":"Maine-specific note"}},{"k":"commercial_dive_welding_work_in_maine_","v":{"s":"Commercial dive-welding work in Maine is rare — most opportunities are Gulf Coast offshore oil, Pacific Northwest fishing/marine, or pipeline work nationwide. A Maine kid pursuing this path will likely relocate. Bath Iron Works does occasional underwater hull inspection but most repair is dry-docked. Best entry: get topside experience at BIW or Cianbro, then apply to dive school out of state."}},{"k":"apprentice","v":{"s":"Apprentice"}},{"k":"learning_the_feel_wide_tolerance_windo","v":{"s":"Learning the feel — wide tolerance window, slower travel."}},{"k":"production_grade_tight_tolerance_faste","v":{"s":"Production-grade — tight tolerance, faster travel."}},{"k":"bath_iron_works","v":{"s":"Bath Iron Works"}},{"k":"ship_hull_certified_minimal_margin_for","v":{"s":"Ship-hull certified — minimal margin for error, fastest travel."}},{"k":"mig_gmaw_3","v":{"s":"MIG (GMAW)"}},{"k":"tig_gtaw_3","v":{"s":"TIG (GTAW)"}},{"k":"stick_smaw_3","v":{"s":"Stick (SMAW)"}},{"k":"flux_cored_fcaw","v":{"s":"Flux-cored (FCAW)"}},{"k":"resistance_spot","v":{"s":"Resistance / Spot"}},{"k":"process_sleuth_2","v":{"s":"Process Sleuth"}},{"k":"10_welding_scenarios_pick_the_right_pr","v":{"s":"10 welding scenarios — pick the right process"}},{"k":"for_each_scenario_pick_the_welding_pro","v":{"s":"For each scenario, pick the welding process from MIG / TIG / Stick / FCAW / Resistance. Coaching after each pick names what makes this process the right choice and what would make a different process fit instead."}},{"k":"the_five_processes","v":{"s":"The five processes"}},{"k":"start_vignette_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🕵️ Start — vignette 1 of 10"}},{"k":"process_sleuth_3","v":{"s":"Process Sleuth"}},{"k":"vignette","v":{"s":"Vignette "}},{"k":"score_3","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_right_welding_process","v":{"s":"Pick the right welding process"}},{"k":"all_10_vignettes_complete","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 vignettes complete"}},{"k":"restart","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_vignette","v":{"s":"➡️ Next vignette"}},{"k":"heat_too_high","v":{"s":"Heat too high"}},{"k":"heat_too_low","v":{"s":"Heat too low"}},{"k":"travel_too_slow","v":{"s":"Travel too slow"}},{"k":"travel_too_fast","v":{"s":"Travel too fast"}},{"k":"contamination","v":{"s":"Contamination"}},{"k":"technique_error","v":{"s":"Technique error"}},{"k":"defect_diagnose_2","v":{"s":"Defect Diagnose"}},{"k":"10_weld_defects_identify_the_root_caus","v":{"s":"10 weld defects — identify the root cause"}},{"k":"visual_id_is_one_skill_defect_hunt_tea","v":{"s":"Visual ID is one skill (Defect Hunt teaches it). ROOT-CAUSE diagnosis is the next-level skill that turns welders into CWI inspectors. For each defect, pick the most likely cause from 6 categories. Coaching cites the fix and how to prevent the defect on the next weld."}},{"k":"the_six_cause_categories","v":{"s":"The six cause categories"}},{"k":"start_defect_1_of_10","v":{"s":"🔬 Start — defect 1 of 10"}},{"k":"defect_diagnose_3","v":{"s":"Defect Diagnose"}},{"k":"defect","v":{"s":"Defect "}},{"k":"score_4","v":{"s":"Score "}},{"k":"accuracy_2","v":{"s":"Accuracy "}},{"k":"streak_2","v":{"s":"Streak "}},{"k":"best_2","v":{"s":"Best "}},{"k":"pick_the_root_cause","v":{"s":"Pick the root cause"}},{"k":"all_10_defects_diagnosed","v":{"s":"🏆 All 10 defects diagnosed"}},{"k":"restart_2","v":{"s":"🔄 Restart"}},{"k":"next_defect","v":{"s":"➡️ Next defect"}},{"k":"speed_challenge_2","v":{"s":"Speed Challenge"}},{"k":"time_pressure_precision_welding","v":{"s":"Time-pressure precision welding"}},{"k":"the_torch_travels_at_tier_specified_sp","v":{"s":"The torch travels at tier-specified speed. You adjust voltage and amperage live to keep the bead in spec. Each 250 ms sample is graded; final score = % time in-spec, minus penalty for average deviation. Personal best persists per tier."}},{"k":"difficulty_tier","v":{"s":"Difficulty Tier"}},{"k":"difficulty_tier_2","v":{"s":"Difficulty tier"}},{"k":"run_timer","v":{"s":"Run Timer"}},{"k":"stop_run","v":{"s":"⏹ Stop run"}},{"k":"samples","v":{"s":"Samples"}},{"k":"tier","v":{"s":"Tier"}},{"k":"coming_soon","v":{"s":"Coming Soon"}},{"k":"ships_in_a_future_weldlab_phase","v":{"s":"Ships in a future WeldLab phase."}},{"k":"welder_s_defect_catalog_3","v":{"s":"Welder's Defect Catalog"}},{"k":"defect_types_id_d","v":{"s":"defect types ID'd"}},{"k":"your_defect_catalog","v":{"s":"Your Defect Catalog"}},{"k":"every_welding_discontinuity_you_correc","v":{"s":"Every welding discontinuity you correctly identify in Defect Hunt Lab lands here. CWI inspectors keep mental catalogs like this their whole careers — yours starts now."}},{"k":"start_inspecting","v":{"s":"Start inspecting →"}},{"k":"not_yet_identified","v":{"s":"Not yet identified"}},{"k":"open_defect_hunt","v":{"s":"🔍 Open Defect Hunt"}},{"k":"process_comparison_3","v":{"s":"⚖️ Process comparison"}},{"k":"new_defect_identified","v":{"s":"New defect identified"}},{"k":"overview","v":{"s":"📋 Overview"}},{"k":"what_metallurgy_means_for_welding","v":{"s":"What metallurgy means for welding"}},{"k":"phase_diagrams","v":{"s":"🌡 Phase diagrams"}},{"k":"iron_carbon_austenite_martensite","v":{"s":"Iron-carbon + austenite + martensite"}},{"k":"haz_microstructure","v":{"s":"🔥 HAZ microstructure"}},{"k":"what_heat_does_to_the_base_metal","v":{"s":"What heat does to the base metal"}},{"k":"carbon_equivalent","v":{"s":"🧮 Carbon Equivalent"}},{"k":"ce_formula_for_crack_susceptibility","v":{"s":"CE formula for crack-susceptibility"}},{"k":"stainless_sensitization","v":{"s":"🥄 Stainless sensitization"}},{"k":"chromium_carbide_precipitation","v":{"s":"Chromium-carbide precipitation"}},{"k":"aluminum_oxidation","v":{"s":"🌫 Aluminum oxidation"}},{"k":"why_aluminum_is_harder_than_steel","v":{"s":"Why aluminum is harder than steel"}},{"k":"hot_cold_cracking","v":{"s":"💥 Hot + cold cracking"}},{"k":"two_distinct_failure_modes","v":{"s":"Two distinct failure modes"}},{"k":"pwht","v":{"s":"🌡 PWHT"}},{"k":"post_weld_heat_treatment_when_needed","v":{"s":"Post-weld heat treatment + when needed"}},{"k":"welding_metallurgy_is_the_science_of_w","v":{"s":"Welding metallurgy is the science of what happens to metal when you heat it past its melting point, mix in filler, and let it cool. Most welding failures happen here — not in arc striking, not in technique — but in metallurgical changes the welder didn't understand. A skilled welder + bad metallurgy choices = cracked weld. A novice + correct procedure = sound weld."}},{"k":"this_module_covers_the_science_the_wel","v":{"s":"This module covers the science. The Weld Bead Lab covers the technique. Codes + Standards covers the legal framework. All three are layers in becoming a qualified welder."}},{"k":"three_regions_every_weld_has","v":{"s":"Three regions every weld has"}},{"k":"1_fusion_zone_fz","v":{"s":"1. Fusion zone (FZ): "}},{"k":"the_metal_that_actually_melted_mixed_w","v":{"s":"The metal that actually melted + mixed with filler + resolidified. Properties are partly base metal, partly filler metal (dilution)."}},{"k":"2_heat_affected_zone_haz","v":{"s":"2. Heat-Affected Zone (HAZ): "}},{"k":"base_metal_that_didn_t_melt_but_got_ho","v":{"s":"Base metal that didn't melt but got hot enough to change microstructure. The most failure-prone region of any weld."}},{"k":"3_unaffected_base_metal_bm","v":{"s":"3. Unaffected base metal (BM): "}},{"k":"cooler_region_original_properties_reta","v":{"s":"Cooler region, original properties retained. Should be the strongest region of a well-designed welded part."}},{"k":"the_iron_carbon_phase_diagram_simplifi","v":{"s":"🌡 The iron-carbon phase diagram (simplified)"}},{"k":"steel_iron_carbon_usually_0_05_2_carbo","v":{"s":"Steel = iron + carbon (usually 0.05-2% carbon). The phase that's stable at any given temperature depends on carbon content + cooling rate. Welding takes the metal through ALL of these phases as it heats up + cools down — that's why microstructure changes."}},{"k":"austenite_fe","v":{"s":"Austenite (γ-Fe): "}},{"k":"above_1340_f_face_centered_cubic_cryst","v":{"s":"Above ~1340°F. Face-centered cubic crystal. Dissolves carbon readily. Non-magnetic. Most ductile phase."}},{"k":"ferrite_fe","v":{"s":"Ferrite (α-Fe): "}},{"k":"below_1340_f_body_centered_cubic_low_c","v":{"s":"Below ~1340°F. Body-centered cubic. Low carbon solubility. Magnetic. Soft + ductile."}},{"k":"mixture_of_ferrite_cementite_fe3c_in_a","v":{"s":"Mixture of ferrite + cementite (Fe3C) in alternating lamellar layers. Forms by slow cooling from austenite."}},{"k":"super_saturated_body_centered_tetragon","v":{"s":"Super-saturated body-centered tetragonal phase. Forms when austenite cools too fast for carbon to diffuse out. "}},{"k":"extremely_hard_brittle_the_villain_of_","v":{"s":"Extremely hard + brittle. The villain of welding."}},{"k":"most_weld_cracking_is_martensite_relat","v":{"s":" Most weld cracking is martensite-related."}},{"k":"intermediate_cooling_rate_acicular_str","v":{"s":"Intermediate cooling rate. Acicular structure. Stronger than pearlite, tougher than martensite."}},{"k":"why_this_matters_for_welding","v":{"s":"⚠ Why this matters for welding"}},{"k":"when_you_weld_carbon_steel_the_haz_hea","v":{"s":"When you weld carbon steel, the HAZ heats above 1340°F (full austenite). When you stop welding, that region cools. If it cools FAST (thin section, cold base metal, no preheat) — martensite forms in the HAZ → brittle → crack. Higher carbon content = more martensite = more crack risk. "}},{"k":"this_is_why_preheat_matters","v":{"s":"This is why preheat matters"}},{"k":"slowing_the_cooling_rate_prevents_mart","v":{"s":": slowing the cooling rate prevents martensite formation."}},{"k":"haz_microstructure_what_the_heat_actua","v":{"s":"🔥 HAZ microstructure — what the heat actually does"}},{"k":"the_haz_is_a_graded_region_closest_to_","v":{"s":"The HAZ is a graded region. Closest to the fusion zone = hottest. Furthest = barely warmed. Each sub-zone has different microstructure:"}},{"k":"practical_takeaway","v":{"s":"💡 Practical takeaway"}},{"k":"the_haz_extends_3_5x_the_weld_bead_wid","v":{"s":"The HAZ extends ~3-5x the weld bead width into the base metal. Pre-existing flaws (notches, sharp corners, weld toes) within the HAZ are stress concentrators that initiate cracks. Welding inspectors look for HAZ cracking via magnetic particle (MT) or dye penetrant (PT) testing."}},{"k":"carbon_equivalent_ce_predicts_crack_ri","v":{"s":"🧮 Carbon Equivalent (CE) — predicts crack risk"}},{"k":"ce_is_a_weighted_sum_of_alloying_eleme","v":{"s":"CE is a weighted sum of alloying elements that estimates how susceptible a steel is to hydrogen-induced cracking + martensite formation. Different formulas for different steel families. The most common in welding is the IIW (International Institute of Welding) formula:"}},{"k":"ce_c_mn_6_cr_mo_v_5_ni_cu_15","v":{"s":"CE = C + Mn/6 + (Cr + Mo + V)/5 + (Ni + Cu)/15"}},{"k":"where_each_element_is_in_weight_percen","v":{"s":"where each element is in weight percent"}},{"k":"weldable_without_preheat_in_most_condi","v":{"s":"Weldable without preheat in most conditions. Common mild steel (A36, A572)."}},{"k":"preheat_may_be_needed_depending_on_thi","v":{"s":"Preheat may be needed depending on thickness + restraint. Use low-hydrogen electrodes (E7018)."}},{"k":"preheat_required_interpass_temperature","v":{"s":"Preheat required + interpass temperature control. Low-hydrogen practice mandatory. PWHT often required."}},{"k":"aggressive_preheat_400_600_f_strict_hy","v":{"s":"Aggressive preheat (~400-600°F). Strict hydrogen control. PWHT mandatory. Often requires special procedures or alternative materials."}},{"k":"real_world_example_2","v":{"s":"💡 Real-world example"}},{"k":"aisi_4140_a_common_alloy_steel_for_sha","v":{"s":"AISI 4140 (a common alloy steel for shafts + gears): C=0.40, Mn=0.85, Cr=0.95, Mo=0.20. CE = 0.40 + 0.142 + 0.230 = 0.77. VERY HIGH — Bath Iron Works welders working on 4140 components use 300°F preheat + low-hydrogen electrodes + post-weld stress relief. Skipping any of these = cracked weld."}},{"k":"stainless_steel_sensitization","v":{"s":"🥄 Stainless steel sensitization"}},{"k":"stainless_steel_resists_corrosion_beca","v":{"s":"Stainless steel resists corrosion because chromium forms a self-healing oxide layer (Cr2O3) on the surface. But heat between ~800-1500°F causes chromium to combine with carbon to form chromium carbides (Cr23C6) that precipitate at grain boundaries. This locally depletes chromium → the depleted regions are no longer corrosion-resistant → \"intergranular corrosion\" attacks the part months/years later."}},{"k":"the_800_1500_f_problem","v":{"s":"⚠ The 800-1500°F problem"}},{"k":"welding_inherently_puts_the_haz_throug","v":{"s":"Welding inherently puts the HAZ through this temperature range. The longer it stays there, the more sensitization."}},{"k":"three_solutions_1_low_heat_input_weldi","v":{"s":"Three solutions: (1) Low-heat-input welding (TIG, fast travel, no preheat). (2) \"L-grade\" stainless (304L, 316L) — low carbon (<0.03%) = less carbide formation. (3) \"Stabilized\" grades (321, 347) — contain Ti or Nb that form carbides preferentially, sparing Cr."}},{"k":"sugar_oxidation_on_the_back_side","v":{"s":"🌡 Sugar / oxidation on the back side"}},{"k":"stainless_tig_welding_requires_inert_g","v":{"s":"Stainless TIG welding requires inert gas (argon) on BOTH sides of the joint. If the back side sees air at red heat, the surface oxidizes to a sugary black scale that's rust-prone. Sanitary welders use \"purging\" — flow argon through pipe interior during welding. Critical for food + pharma + nuclear pipe welding."}},{"k":"aluminum_oxide_layer_why_aluminum_is_h","v":{"s":"🌫 Aluminum oxide layer (why aluminum is hard)"}},{"k":"aluminum_forms_an_oxide_skin_al2o3_wit","v":{"s":"Aluminum forms an oxide skin (Al2O3) within MILLISECONDS of exposure to air. The oxide melts at ~3700°F (vs aluminum metal at ~1220°F). If you don't remove the oxide first, you're trying to melt aluminum through a refractory ceramic shell. Result: incomplete fusion + porosity + ugly bead."}},{"k":"stainless_wire_brush_immediately_befor","v":{"s":"Stainless wire brush IMMEDIATELY before welding. Brush moves only one direction. Use brush DEDICATED to aluminum (mild steel brush = contamination). Brushed surface stays clean for ~30-60 sec, then re-oxidizes."}},{"k":"aluminum_specific_etching_solution_rem","v":{"s":"Aluminum-specific etching solution removes oxide. More thorough but adds prep time. Used for critical aerospace welds."}},{"k":"alternating_current_tig_includes_a_cle","v":{"s":"Alternating current TIG includes a \"cleaning\" half-cycle where electrons flow FROM the workpiece, blasting oxide off via cathodic etching. Visible as bright cleaning zone around the arc. Pure DC TIG can't do this — must use AC."}},{"k":"thermal_conductivity_penalty","v":{"s":"🌡 Thermal conductivity penalty"}},{"k":"aluminum_conducts_heat_2_4_faster_than","v":{"s":"Aluminum conducts heat ~2.4× faster than steel. The heat you put in spreads out instead of staying at the joint. You need MORE amperage + thicker filler to compensate, AND you need to weld faster before the heat propagates everywhere + distorts the part. Aluminum welding is fundamentally harder than steel — pay reflects this (specialized aluminum welders earn 15-30% premium)."}},{"k":"hot_cracking_vs_cold_cracking","v":{"s":"💥 Hot cracking vs cold cracking"}},{"k":"welds_crack_two_distinct_ways_at_two_d","v":{"s":"Welds crack two distinct ways at two distinct times. Confusing them = wrong fix = re-cracked weld."}},{"k":"hot_cracking_solidification_cracking","v":{"s":"🔥 Hot Cracking (Solidification Cracking)"}},{"k":"during_solidification_while_metal_is_s","v":{"s":"During solidification, while metal is still partially molten. Cracks appear immediately after welding."}},{"k":"centerline_of_bead_longitudinal_center","v":{"s":"Centerline of bead (longitudinal centerline cracks) or crater (at end of run when arc breaks)."}},{"k":"low_melting_point_eutectics_sulfur_pho","v":{"s":"Low-melting-point eutectics (sulfur, phosphorus, lead contaminants; or excess sulfur in base metal) remain liquid while surrounding metal solidifies + contracts. Tensile stress pulls the still-liquid film apart."}},{"k":"use_cleaner_base_metal_low_sulfur_use_","v":{"s":"Use cleaner base metal (low-sulfur). Use convex bead profile (not concave — concave allows top-surface tension). Reduce restraint. Cap crater (back up arc into crater before breaking)."}},{"k":"cold_cracking_hydrogen_induced_crackin","v":{"s":"❄ Cold Cracking (Hydrogen-Induced Cracking, HIC)"}},{"k":"hours_to_days_after_welding_often_call","v":{"s":"Hours to DAYS after welding. Often called \"delayed cracking\" because it appears overnight or even later."}},{"k":"haz_parallel_to_fusion_line_hard_to_se","v":{"s":"HAZ, parallel to fusion line. Hard to see initially; often discovered by inspection."}},{"k":"three_things_together_1_hydrogen_in_th","v":{"s":"Three things together: (1) hydrogen in the weld pool (from moisture in flux, contaminated wire, base metal), (2) martensitic microstructure in HAZ, (3) tensile residual stress. All three = crack. Remove any one = no crack."}},{"k":"low_hydrogen_electrodes_e7018_stored_h","v":{"s":"Low-hydrogen electrodes (E7018, stored hot). Preheat to slow cooling rate (prevent martensite). Stress relief PWHT. For high-restraint welds use \"buttering\" technique (build up filler before final pass)."}},{"k":"post_weld_heat_treatment_pwht","v":{"s":"🌡 Post-Weld Heat Treatment (PWHT)"}},{"k":"pwht_heating_the_welded_part_to_a_targ","v":{"s":"PWHT = heating the welded part to a target temperature, holding for a specified time, then slow cooling. Different PWHT types serve different purposes:"}},{"k":"stress_relief","v":{"s":"Stress relief"}},{"k":"hydrogen_bake_out","v":{"s":"Hydrogen bake-out"}},{"k":"normalize","v":{"s":"Normalize"}},{"k":"anneal","v":{"s":"Anneal"}},{"k":"solution_treat_stainless","v":{"s":"Solution treat (stainless)"}},{"k":"pwht_economics","v":{"s":"💡 PWHT economics"}},{"k":"pwht_is_expensive_large_parts_need_wal","v":{"s":"PWHT is EXPENSIVE — large parts need walk-in furnaces, slow heat-up + hold + slow cool cycles take days, energy cost is significant. Welding shops factor PWHT into bids ($1-5/lb extra for code work). Some material/joint combinations avoid PWHT specifically to skip this cost (e.g., choosing nickel-based filler that doesn't require stress relief)."}},{"k":"welding_metallurgy_2","v":{"s":"Welding Metallurgy"}},{"k":"general_provisions","v":{"s":"General Provisions"}},{"k":"scope_definitions_contract_documents","v":{"s":"Scope, definitions, contract documents."}},{"k":"design_of_welded_connections","v":{"s":"Design of Welded Connections"}},{"k":"joint_geometry_allowable_stresses_fati","v":{"s":"Joint geometry, allowable stresses, fatigue."}},{"k":"pre_qualified_wps","v":{"s":"Pre-Qualified WPS"}},{"k":"pre_qualified_procedures_that_don_t_re","v":{"s":"Pre-qualified procedures that don't require shop qualification."}},{"k":"qualification","v":{"s":"Qualification"}},{"k":"how_to_qualify_a_wps_a_welder_this_is_","v":{"s":"How to qualify a WPS + a welder. THIS is the section every welder lives by."}},{"k":"fabrication","v":{"s":"Fabrication"}},{"k":"workmanship_cleaning_fit_up_preheat_in","v":{"s":"Workmanship: cleaning, fit-up, preheat, interpass, tacks, cleaning between passes."}},{"k":"inspection","v":{"s":"Inspection"}},{"k":"who_when_how_vt_mt_pt_ut_rt_acceptance","v":{"s":"Who, when, how. VT, MT, PT, UT, RT acceptance criteria."}},{"k":"stud_welding","v":{"s":"Stud Welding"}},{"k":"specialty_section_on_shear_stud_nelson","v":{"s":"Specialty section on shear stud + nelson stud welding."}},{"k":"strengthening_repair","v":{"s":"Strengthening + Repair"}},{"k":"how_to_weld_on_existing_structures","v":{"s":"How to weld on existing structures."}},{"k":"tubular_structures","v":{"s":"Tubular Structures"}},{"k":"hollow_structural_sections_hss","v":{"s":"Hollow structural sections (HSS)."}},{"k":"statically_loaded_structures","v":{"s":"Statically Loaded Structures"}},{"k":"buildings_dead_load_structures","v":{"s":"Buildings, dead-load structures."}},{"k":"cyclically_loaded_structures","v":{"s":"Cyclically Loaded Structures"}},{"k":"bridges_cranes_fatigue_driven","v":{"s":"Bridges, cranes, fatigue-driven."}},{"k":"general_requirements","v":{"s":"General Requirements"}},{"k":"scope_definitions","v":{"s":"Scope + definitions."}},{"k":"welding_procedure_qualifications","v":{"s":"Welding Procedure Qualifications"}},{"k":"wps_pqr_requirements_essential_variabl","v":{"s":"WPS + PQR requirements. ESSENTIAL variables that require requalification if changed (base metal type, filler metal, electrode classification, joint design, position, electrical characteristics, technique)."}},{"k":"welding_performance_qualifications","v":{"s":"Welding Performance Qualifications"}},{"k":"how_welders_qualify_performance_variab","v":{"s":"How welders qualify. Performance variables (process, base metal, position, thickness range, backing)."}},{"k":"variables","v":{"s":"Variables"}},{"k":"lists_all_variables_whether_each_is_es","v":{"s":"Lists ALL variables + whether each is essential, supplementary essential, or nonessential."}},{"k":"specific_process_requirements","v":{"s":"Specific Process Requirements"}},{"k":"process_specific_smaw_gmaw_gtaw_etc_ru","v":{"s":"Process-specific (SMAW, GMAW, GTAW, etc.) rules."}},{"k":"general_definitions","v":{"s":"General + Definitions"}},{"k":"scope_references_terms","v":{"s":"Scope, references, terms."}},{"k":"qualification_of_welding_procedures","v":{"s":"Qualification of Welding Procedures"}},{"k":"pqr_wps_for_pipeline_welds","v":{"s":"PQR + WPS for pipeline welds."}},{"k":"qualification_of_welders","v":{"s":"Qualification of Welders"}},{"k":"performance_test_requirements_bend_tes","v":{"s":"Performance test requirements. Bend tests, nick-break tests."}},{"k":"design_preparation","v":{"s":"Design + Preparation"}},{"k":"joint_geometry_for_cross_country_pipel","v":{"s":"Joint geometry for cross-country pipeline."}},{"k":"inspection_testing","v":{"s":"Inspection + Testing"}},{"k":"visual_radiographic_acceptance_criteri","v":{"s":"Visual + radiographic + acceptance criteria. Most stringent X-ray standards in the welding industry."}},{"k":"acceptance_standards","v":{"s":"Acceptance Standards"}},{"k":"what_constitutes_an_acceptable_weld_de","v":{"s":"What constitutes an acceptable weld defect vs reject."}},{"k":"repair_removal","v":{"s":"Repair + Removal"}},{"k":"how_to_handle_defects_without_compromi","v":{"s":"How to handle defects without compromising line integrity."}},{"k":"procedures_for_ndt","v":{"s":"Procedures for NDT"}},{"k":"non_destructive_testing_methodologies","v":{"s":"Non-destructive testing methodologies."}},{"k":"welding_procedure_specification_wps_th","v":{"s":"📋 Welding Procedure Specification (WPS) — the \"recipe\""}},{"k":"a_wps_is_a_written_procedure_that_spec","v":{"s":"A WPS is a written procedure that specifies HOW a particular weld must be made. Includes: process, base metal range, filler metal, joint design, preheat, interpass temperature, electrical settings (V, A, polarity), travel speed, position, gas (if applicable), post-weld heat treatment. Every welder on a code job follows a WPS."}},{"k":"procedure_qualification_record_pqr_the","v":{"s":"📜 Procedure Qualification Record (PQR) — the \"proof\""}},{"k":"a_pqr_documents_the_actual_test_welds_","v":{"s":"A PQR documents the actual test welds + mechanical test results that prove the WPS produces sound welds. Includes: actual values used (not ranges), tensile test results, bend test results, charpy impact results, hardness, sometimes macroetch. The PQR validates the WPS — without a PQR, the WPS is just a piece of paper."}},{"k":"welder_performance_qualification_wpq_t","v":{"s":"🎓 Welder Performance Qualification (WPQ) — the \"license\""}},{"k":"a_wpq_documents_that_a_specific_welder","v":{"s":"A WPQ documents that a specific welder has demonstrated the ability to produce sound welds per a specific WPS, in specific positions + thicknesses. The welder's \"card.\" When you hear \"I'm 6G certified,\" that's a WPQ. Expires (must re-test every 6 months unless continuous experience documented)."}},{"k":"engineer_writes_wps_shop_welds_test_co","v":{"s":"Engineer writes WPS → shop welds test coupons per WPS → coupons tested + results recorded → if all pass, PQR issued + WPS qualified. Welder welds qualification coupons per WPS → coupons tested → if pass, WPQ issued for that welder + that WPS."}},{"k":"codes_standards_2","v":{"s":"Codes + Standards"}},{"k":"aws_d1_1","v":{"s":"AWS D1.1"}},{"k":"asme_section_ix","v":{"s":"ASME Section IX"}},{"k":"api_1104","v":{"s":"API 1104"}},{"k":"scope","v":{"s":"Scope"}},{"k":"why_it_matters","v":{"s":"Why it matters"}},{"k":"section_structure","v":{"s":"Section structure"}},{"k":"who_uses_it","v":{"s":"Who uses it"}},{"k":"welder_qualification_prep_2","v":{"s":"Welder Qualification Prep"}},{"k":"overview_2","v":{"s":"📋 Overview"}},{"k":"practice_quiz_30_q","v":{"s":"📝 Practice Quiz (30 q)"}},{"k":"what_is_welder_qualification","v":{"s":"What is welder qualification?"}},{"k":"welder_qualification_the_formal_proces","v":{"s":"Welder qualification = the formal process of proving you can produce welds that meet a specific code (AWS, ASME, API) on specific materials in specific positions. Without current qualification, you cannot legally weld on most jobs that require code-quality work."}},{"k":"the_test_process","v":{"s":"🎯 The test process"}},{"k":"choose_the_qualification","v":{"s":"Choose the qualification: "}},{"k":"wps_position_e_g_6g_material_thickness","v":{"s":"WPS, position (e.g., 6G), material thickness, process. Each combination is its own qualification."}},{"k":"weld_test_coupons","v":{"s":"Weld test coupons: "}},{"k":"per_the_wps_on_the_specified_material_","v":{"s":"Per the WPS, on the specified material + position. Usually 2-3 coupons (one for visual, one or more for bend testing)."}},{"k":"visual_inspection","v":{"s":"Visual inspection: "}},{"k":"independent_inspector_checks_for_visib","v":{"s":"Independent inspector checks for visible defects (cracks, undercut, porosity, incomplete fusion). Must be defect-free per code acceptance criteria."}},{"k":"bend_tests","v":{"s":"Bend tests: "}},{"k":"cut_strips_from_the_weld_bend_them_aro","v":{"s":"Cut strips from the weld + bend them around a mandrel. The face + root of the weld are stretched. If they crack open or show fissures larger than allowed, you fail."}},{"k":"rt_or_ut_some_certifications","v":{"s":"RT or UT (some certifications): "}},{"k":"for_pipe_critical_applications_x_ray_r","v":{"s":"For pipe + critical applications, X-ray (RT) or ultrasonic (UT) testing replaces or supplements bend tests."}},{"k":"pass_wpq_issued","v":{"s":"Pass = WPQ issued: "}},{"k":"you_get_a_welder_performance_qualifica","v":{"s":"You get a Welder Performance Qualification card good for 6 months (extends with documented continuous use)."}},{"k":"most_marketable_qualifications","v":{"s":"🪈 Most marketable qualifications"}},{"k":"6g_pipe_at_45_fixed","v":{"s":"6G — Pipe at 45° fixed"}},{"k":"6gr_restricted_6g_with_backing_ring","v":{"s":"6GR — Restricted 6G with backing ring"}},{"k":"d1_1_4g_overhead_groove","v":{"s":"D1.1 4G (overhead groove)"}},{"k":"asme_6g_tig_root_stick_fill","v":{"s":"ASME 6G TIG root + Stick fill"}},{"k":"welder_qual_prep_practice_quiz","v":{"s":"Welder Qual Prep — Practice Quiz"}},{"k":"overview_3","v":{"s":"← Overview"}},{"k":"explanation","v":{"s":"💡 Explanation: "}},{"k":"previous","v":{"s":"← Previous"}},{"k":"next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"why_pipe_is_its_own_world","v":{"s":"📋 Why pipe is its own world"}},{"k":"pipe_positions_1g_2g_5g_6g_6gr","v":{"s":"🪈 Pipe positions (1G/2G/5G/6G/6GR)"}},{"k":"root_hot_fill_cap","v":{"s":"🥞 Root + hot + fill + cap"}},{"k":"uphill_vs_downhill","v":{"s":"⬆⬇ Uphill vs downhill"}},{"k":"pipeline_welding_cross_country","v":{"s":"🛢 Pipeline welding (cross-country)"}},{"k":"orbital_automated_pipe","v":{"s":"⚙ Orbital + automated pipe"}},{"k":"specialty_pipe_work_pay","v":{"s":"⭐ Specialty pipe work + pay"}},{"k":"pipe_welding_is_its_own_discipline_wit","v":{"s":"Pipe welding is its own discipline within the welding trade. The geometry is different (curved surface, not flat), the technique demands constant adjustment as the bead climbs around the circumference, multiple passes build up the joint, and the consequences of a bad weld are higher — a pipe weld holding pressurized gas at 2,000 psi cannot leak."}},{"k":"a_welder_who_can_do_6g_on_small_diamet","v":{"s":"A welder who can do 6G on small-diameter pipe (2-3\" Sch 80) with X-ray-quality results is among the highest-paid skilled tradespeople in North America. Pipeline + nuclear + refinery work routinely pays $40-80/hr + per-diem + travel."}},{"k":"why_pipe_is_harder_than_plate","v":{"s":"Why pipe is harder than plate"}},{"k":"curved_surface","v":{"s":"Curved surface: "}},{"k":"travel_angle_continuously_changes_as_b","v":{"s":"Travel angle continuously changes as bead progresses around. Body position changes too."}},{"k":"multiple_passes","v":{"s":"Multiple passes: "}},{"k":"even_thin_wall_pipe_requires_2_4_passe","v":{"s":"Even thin-wall pipe requires 2-4 passes (root + hot + cap). Each pass must fuse with previous without inclusion."}},{"k":"position_constraint","v":{"s":"Position constraint: "}},{"k":"pipe_often_can_t_be_repositioned_the_w","v":{"s":"Pipe often can't be repositioned. The welder must adapt to whatever position the joint presents (5G + 6G fixed)."}},{"k":"x_ray_inspection","v":{"s":"X-ray inspection: "}},{"k":"most_code_pipe_welds_are_100_radiograp","v":{"s":"Most code pipe welds are 100% radiographed. Inclusions or lack of fusion that wouldn't fail plate-weld VT will fail pipe RT."}},{"k":"root_back_side","v":{"s":"Root + back side: "}},{"k":"pipe_joints_typically_can_t_be_welded_","v":{"s":"Pipe joints typically can't be welded from inside. The root pass must penetrate fully from outside with no excess or insufficient penetration."}},{"k":"pipe_position_designations","v":{"s":"🪈 Pipe position designations"}},{"k":"pipe_welding_adds_positions_beyond_the","v":{"s":"Pipe welding adds positions beyond the plate 1G/2G/3G/4G:"}},{"k":"horizontal_pipe_rolled","v":{"s":"Horizontal pipe rolled"}},{"k":"pipe_axis_is_horizontal_pipe_is_rotate","v":{"s":"Pipe axis is horizontal; pipe is rotated so weld is always in the flat position. Easiest pipe welding — like welding plate."}},{"k":"vertical_pipe","v":{"s":"Vertical pipe"}},{"k":"pipe_axis_is_vertical_weld_runs_around","v":{"s":"Pipe axis is vertical; weld runs around the pipe in a horizontal plane. Steady position, but you weld all the way around."}},{"k":"horizontal_pipe_fixed","v":{"s":"Horizontal pipe fixed"}},{"k":"pipe_axis_horizontal_pipe_cannot_rotat","v":{"s":"Pipe axis horizontal; pipe CANNOT rotate. Bead must travel from 6 o'clock UP through 9, 12, 3 back to 6. Must change technique as gravity changes."}},{"k":"pipe_at_45_fixed","v":{"s":"Pipe at 45°, fixed"}},{"k":"pipe_axis_at_45_cannot_rotate_most_dem","v":{"s":"Pipe axis at 45°. Cannot rotate. Most demanding position. Welder constantly adjusts technique, body position, electrode angle. The \"challenge\" qualification — passes for all other positions."}},{"k":"6g_with_restriction_ring","v":{"s":"6G with restriction ring"}},{"k":"6g_position_with_a_metal_ring_placed_a","v":{"s":"6G position with a metal ring placed around the pipe near the joint, restricting electrode access. The most punishing welding qualification commonly required. Used for nuclear + critical pressure vessel work."}},{"k":"the_pass_structure_of_pipe_welding","v":{"s":"🥞 The pass structure of pipe welding"}},{"k":"pipe_groove_welds_build_up_in_distinct","v":{"s":"Pipe groove welds build up in distinct passes. Each pass has its own purpose + technique:"}},{"k":"first_pass_at_the_very_bottom_of_the_j","v":{"s":"First pass at the very bottom of the joint groove. Must fully penetrate without excess + without insufficient penetration. Often TIG (cleanest control) for first pass on quality work. Determines whether the weld will pass X-ray. Most critical + most-attention-paid pass."}},{"k":"immediately_after_root_burns_out_any_i","v":{"s":"Immediately after root — burns out any inclusions in the root + fuses root face to the groove walls. Slightly hotter than root. Sometimes called \"burn-in pass.\""}},{"k":"multiple_passes_depending_on_wall_thic","v":{"s":"Multiple passes (depending on wall thickness) that fill the groove up to ~1/8\" below the surface. Higher amperage, faster travel, more material deposited per pass. Pipe up to 1\" thick may need 6+ fill passes."}},{"k":"final_passes_that_build_the_bead_sligh","v":{"s":"Final passes that build the bead slightly above the original surface (reinforcement crown). Smaller passes, weaving for uniform appearance. The \"show pass\" — what inspectors see first."}},{"k":"interpass_cleaning","v":{"s":"💡 Interpass cleaning"}},{"k":"between_passes_the_welder_must_chip_sl","v":{"s":"Between passes, the welder MUST chip slag + grind any defects + wire-brush the previous pass. Slag inclusions caused by skipping interpass cleaning are the #1 cause of pipe weld rejects in X-ray inspection."}},{"k":"uphill_vs_downhill_technique","v":{"s":"⬆⬇ Uphill vs Downhill technique"}},{"k":"in_vertical_5g_6g_pipe_positions_the_w","v":{"s":"In vertical + 5G/6G pipe positions, the welder chooses whether to weld UPHILL (from 6 o'clock to 12) or DOWNHILL (from 12 to 6). They're completely different techniques."}},{"k":"uphill","v":{"s":"⬆ UPHILL"}},{"k":"slower_travel_speed","v":{"s":"• Slower travel speed"}},{"k":"higher_heat_input_per_inch","v":{"s":"• Higher heat input per inch"}},{"k":"better_penetration","v":{"s":"• Better penetration"}},{"k":"wider_bead","v":{"s":"• Wider bead"}},{"k":"used_for_structural_heavier_sections_a","v":{"s":"• Used for structural + heavier sections (AWS D1.1 + most ASME)"}},{"k":"better_for_fill_cap_passes","v":{"s":"• Better for fill + cap passes"}},{"k":"downhill","v":{"s":"⬇ DOWNHILL"}},{"k":"faster_travel_speed","v":{"s":"• Faster travel speed"}},{"k":"lower_heat_input_per_inch","v":{"s":"• Lower heat input per inch"}},{"k":"less_penetration","v":{"s":"• Less penetration"}},{"k":"narrower_bead","v":{"s":"• Narrower bead"}},{"k":"used_for_thin_wall_pipe_cross_country_","v":{"s":"• Used for thin-wall pipe (cross-country pipeline, X65 + X70 grades)"}},{"k":"better_for_root_passes_on_high_strengt","v":{"s":"• Better for root passes on high-strength steel — avoids HAZ softening"}},{"k":"common_mistake","v":{"s":"⚠ Common mistake"}},{"k":"downhill_is_faster_easier_but_provides","v":{"s":"Downhill is FASTER + EASIER but provides LESS penetration. Welders new to pipe often default to downhill, get acceptable visual appearance, but fail X-ray due to lack of fusion at the root. Code-required uphill is uphill for a reason. Check your WPS."}},{"k":"cross_country_pipeline_welding","v":{"s":"🛢 Cross-country pipeline welding"}},{"k":"pipeline_welding_is_its_own_subculture","v":{"s":"Pipeline welding is its own subculture within pipe welding. Cross-country (oil + gas transmission) pipelines run from gas fields/wellheads to refineries/distribution. The work is mobile (move along the right-of-way as construction progresses), outdoor, often remote, and pays exceptionally well."}},{"k":"the_pipeline_weld_crew_structure","v":{"s":"🔧 The pipeline weld crew structure"}},{"k":"stringer_bead","v":{"s":"Stringer bead: "}},{"k":"first_welder_lays_the_root_pass_on_the","v":{"s":"First welder lays the root pass on the joint as soon as alignment clamps come off. Usually downhill stick (E6010 cellulosic electrode) for fast root."}},{"k":"hot_pass","v":{"s":"Hot pass: "}},{"k":"second_welder_follows_immediately_adds","v":{"s":"Second welder follows immediately + adds hot pass. Burns out any defects from root."}},{"k":"filler_hands","v":{"s":"Filler hands: "}},{"k":"2_4_welders_add_fill_passes_simultaneo","v":{"s":"2-4 welders add fill passes simultaneously (different sides of the joint). Speed is critical — pipeline construction is paid per foot of pipe laid."}},{"k":"cap_hand","v":{"s":"Cap hand: "}},{"k":"final_welder_does_cap_pass_cleanest_mo","v":{"s":"Final welder does cap pass. Cleanest + most visible — also the welder whose work everyone sees."}},{"k":"tie_in_welder","v":{"s":"Tie-in welder: "}},{"k":"specialist_who_joins_separate_pipeline","v":{"s":"Specialist who joins separate pipeline sections together. Often the hardest welds (no alignment clamps, must fit existing pipe). Senior position."}},{"k":"the_pay_reality","v":{"s":"💰 The pay reality"}},{"k":"pipeline_welders_typically_earn_40_60_","v":{"s":"Pipeline welders typically earn $40-60/hr + per-diem ($100-150/day for housing + meals) + overtime. A pipeline welder working 60 hr weeks for 6 months can clear $130-180K. The other side: brutal travel (months from home), physical demands (mostly outdoor + manual), industry cycles (boom/bust with oil prices), + safety risks (heavy equipment, gas, isolation)."}},{"k":"how_to_get_into_pipeline","v":{"s":"🎓 How to get into pipeline"}},{"k":"most_pipeline_welders_come_from_1_weld","v":{"s":"Most pipeline welders come from: (1) Welding schools that specifically prepare pipeline welders (Tulsa Welding School, Pipeliners Local 798 training, Hobart Institute). (2) Union 798 apprenticeship (UA Pipeliners). (3) Non-union \"merit shop\" companies (Henkels & McCoy, Michels, Hunt). Almost always requires 6G qualification + cellulosic stick experience."}},{"k":"orbital_welding_automated_tig_on_pipe","v":{"s":"⚙ Orbital welding — automated TIG on pipe"}},{"k":"orbital_welding_is_automated_tig_weldi","v":{"s":"Orbital welding is automated TIG welding where the torch travels around the pipe on a track. The welder sets parameters (current, travel speed, gas flow, electrode position) and the machine executes the weld with absolute repeatability. Used for high-precision applications where every joint must be identical: semiconductor fab pipe, pharmaceutical clean-in-place piping, nuclear, aerospace."}},{"k":"how_it_works","v":{"s":"🔧 How it works"}},{"k":"1_torch_is_mounted_in_a_weld_head_smal","v":{"s":"1. Torch is mounted in a \"weld head\" — small machine that clamps around the pipe + has a track that the torch travels along."}},{"k":"2_operator_not_technically_a_welder_di","v":{"s":"2. Operator (not technically a welder — different certification) programs the weld parameters per pipe size + material."}},{"k":"3_machine_welds_the_joint_while_operat","v":{"s":"3. Machine welds the joint while operator observes (sometimes a screen showing weld pool). Operator can intervene if needed."}},{"k":"4_data_logged_for_every_weld_current_v","v":{"s":"4. Data logged for every weld — current, voltage, travel speed, time, position. Critical for traceability in regulated industries."}},{"k":"pros_cons","v":{"s":"💡 Pros + cons"}},{"k":"extreme_repeatability_every_weld_looks","v":{"s":"Extreme repeatability. Every weld looks identical. Faster than manual for high-volume work. Less skill required to operate than to manually weld. Comprehensive data logging for quality audit."}},{"k":"capital_cost_30k_150k_per_system_setup","v":{"s":"Capital cost ($30K-150K per system). Setup time per joint can negate speed advantage on small batches. Limited to specific pipe sizes + joint configurations. Doesn't handle joint misalignment well (manual welder can compensate)."}},{"k":"career_path","v":{"s":"💰 Career path"}},{"k":"orbital_operator_technician_roles_pay_","v":{"s":"Orbital operator + technician roles pay $35-60/hr. Lower-pressure than pure welding (no body strain) + more analytical (parameters + troubleshooting). Niche skill — Arc Machines + Liburdi + Magnatech are the main equipment OEMs; training programs available through them. Growing fast in semiconductor + biotech + nuclear."}},{"k":"specialty_pipe_pay_scale","v":{"s":"⭐ Specialty pipe + pay scale"}},{"k":"nuclear_pipe_welder","v":{"s":"Nuclear pipe welder"}},{"k":"pipeline_tie_in_welder","v":{"s":"Pipeline tie-in welder"}},{"k":"refinery_turnaround_welder","v":{"s":"Refinery turnaround welder"}},{"k":"pharmaceutical_clean_room_welder","v":{"s":"Pharmaceutical clean-room welder"}},{"k":"subsea_pipeline_welder","v":{"s":"Subsea pipeline welder"}},{"k":"ndt_certified_pipe_welder","v":{"s":"NDT-certified pipe welder"}},{"k":"pipe_welding_deep_dive_2","v":{"s":"Pipe Welding Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"overview_4","v":{"s":"📋 Overview"}},{"k":"major_brands","v":{"s":"🏭 Major brands"}},{"k":"programming","v":{"s":"⌨ Programming"}},{"k":"vision_adaptive_control","v":{"s":"👁 Vision + adaptive control"}},{"k":"collaborative_cobots","v":{"s":"🤝 Collaborative cobots"}},{"k":"careers_in_robotic_welding","v":{"s":"💼 Careers in robotic welding"}},{"k":"industrial_robotic_welding_has_been_tr","v":{"s":"Industrial robotic welding has been transforming high-volume manufacturing since the 1980s. Automotive body assembly, appliance fabrication, structural steel, agricultural equipment, defense — all heavily robotic. A robot can weld 24/7 with consistent quality + speed manual welders can't match."}},{"k":"what_robots_do_better_than_humans","v":{"s":"What robots do better than humans"}},{"k":"every_weld_identical_to_the_last_no_fa","v":{"s":"Every weld identical to the last. No fatigue-induced variation."}},{"k":"2_5x_faster_than_skilled_manual_welder","v":{"s":"2-5x faster than skilled manual welder for repeating same joint."}},{"k":"heavy_awkward_access","v":{"s":"Heavy/awkward access: "}},{"k":"overhead_welds_confined_spaces_dangero","v":{"s":"Overhead welds, confined spaces, dangerous atmospheres."}},{"k":"tireless_production","v":{"s":"Tireless production: "}},{"k":"24_7_with_only_routine_maintenance_bre","v":{"s":"24/7 with only routine maintenance breaks."}},{"k":"data_logging","v":{"s":"Data logging: "}},{"k":"every_weld_parameter_recorded_for_trac","v":{"s":"Every weld parameter recorded for traceability."}},{"k":"what_humans_still_do_better","v":{"s":"What humans still do better"}},{"k":"joint_misalignment_varying_fit_up_unex","v":{"s":"Joint misalignment, varying fit-up, unexpected gaps — manual welder compensates real-time. Most robots cannot."}},{"k":"low_volume_work","v":{"s":"Low-volume work: "}},{"k":"setup_programming_time_for_1_10_unique","v":{"s":"Setup + programming time for 1-10 unique pieces > manual welding time."}},{"k":"field_work_repair","v":{"s":"Field work + repair: "}},{"k":"robots_can_t_go_to_a_job_site_to_fix_a","v":{"s":"Robots can't go to a job site to fix a damaged pipeline or weld a structural beam in place."}},{"k":"diagnostic_judgment","v":{"s":"Diagnostic judgment: "}},{"k":"when_the_weld_looks_wrong_deciding_wha","v":{"s":"When the weld looks wrong, deciding what to do (re-weld? grind out? abort?) requires welding judgment."}},{"k":"variable_joints","v":{"s":"Variable joints: "}},{"k":"custom_fabrication_art_metalwork_sculp","v":{"s":"Custom fabrication, art metalwork, sculpture, restoration."}},{"k":"career_impact_framing","v":{"s":"💡 Career-impact framing"}},{"k":"robotic_welding_doesn_t_eliminate_weld","v":{"s":"Robotic welding doesn't eliminate welders — it changes what welders do. Automotive body shops largely run on robots, but every shop still has manual welders for fixtures, prototyping, repair, and complex custom work. The bigger career shift is: ROBOT PROGRAMMERS + TECHNICIANS are a growing role drawing from welding experience + adding programming knowledge."}},{"k":"the_major_industrial_robot_brands","v":{"s":"🏭 The major industrial robot brands"}},{"k":"industrial_robots_are_dominated_by_a_h","v":{"s":"Industrial robots are dominated by a handful of OEMs. Each has its own teach-pendant interface + programming language, but core concepts transfer."}},{"k":"yaskawa_motoman","v":{"s":"Yaskawa Motoman"}},{"k":"lincoln_esab_miller","v":{"s":"Lincoln + ESAB + Miller"}},{"k":"how_robotic_welding_gets_programmed","v":{"s":"⌨ How robotic welding gets programmed"}},{"k":"there_are_three_main_approaches_often_","v":{"s":"There are three main approaches, often combined:"}},{"k":"operator_moves_robot_manually_with_joy","v":{"s":"Operator moves robot manually with joystick on teach pendant, recording waypoints. At each waypoint, they specify: position, weld start/stop, weld parameters (V, A, TS), motion type (joint vs linear). Most common for small batch + simpler parts."}},{"k":"using_software_like_fanuc_roboguide_ab","v":{"s":"Using software like FANUC Roboguide, ABB RobotStudio, etc., programmer creates the weld path on a virtual model of the robot + part. Tested in simulation. Code uploaded to robot when ready."}},{"k":"robot_uses_sensors_laser_scanners_visi","v":{"s":"Robot uses sensors (laser scanners, vision systems, touch probes) to find joint location + characteristics, then welds adaptively. Operator sets parameters but robot figures out path."}},{"k":"practical_reality","v":{"s":"💡 Practical reality"}},{"k":"most_production_welding_cells_use_a_co","v":{"s":"Most production welding cells use a combination: offline programming for the bulk of the path + teach pendant fine-tuning at the actual robot + optional vision for parts with known fit-up variation. Welder/programmer/technician roles often combine all three skills."}},{"k":"vision_adaptive_control_2","v":{"s":"👁 Vision + adaptive control"}},{"k":"the_hardest_problem_in_robotic_welding","v":{"s":"The hardest problem in robotic welding is variation — every part isn't perfectly identical. Gaps vary, fit-up varies, fixtures drift, parts arrive bent. Vision systems + adaptive control let the robot respond to actual joint conditions instead of blindly following a pre-programmed path."}},{"k":"a_laser_line_projected_ahead_of_the_to","v":{"s":"A laser line projected ahead of the torch + a camera detects where the line falls on the part. The control system calculates where the actual joint is + adjusts torch path. Used for long longitudinal welds where part may bow slightly."}},{"k":"robot_touches_the_part_with_the_weldin","v":{"s":"Robot touches the part with the welding wire (or a separate probe) to establish reference points before welding. Slower setup but very accurate. Used when part position is uncertain."}},{"k":"the_robot_weaves_the_arc_monitors_volt","v":{"s":"The robot weaves the arc + monitors voltage. When arc length differs side-to-side, it indicates joint asymmetry. Robot adjusts position. No additional sensor needed — uses the arc itself. Common for fillet welds."}},{"k":"cameras_projected_light_pattern_build_","v":{"s":"Cameras + projected light pattern build a 3D model of the part in real-time. Robot uses this to plan path. Most advanced + expensive systems ($50-200K added cost)."}},{"k":"collaborative_cobots_universal_robots_","v":{"s":"🤝 Collaborative cobots (Universal Robots, etc.)"}},{"k":"collaborative_robots_cobots_are_lighte","v":{"s":"Collaborative robots (\"cobots\") are lighter, slower, force-limited robots designed to work alongside humans without safety cages. Easier to program (often by guiding the arm with your hand to teach positions). Used widely for low-volume welding in small shops that couldn't afford traditional robot cells."}},{"k":"universal_robots_ur","v":{"s":"Universal Robots (UR)"}},{"k":"danish_company_ur5e_ur10e_ur16e_are_po","v":{"s":"Danish company; UR5e + UR10e + UR16e are popular for welding. PolyScope interface — easiest cobot programming on market. Major adoption in small fab shops."}},{"k":"abb_gofa","v":{"s":"ABB GoFa"}},{"k":"abb_s_cobot_line_crb_15000_good_integr","v":{"s":"ABB's cobot line. CRB 15000. Good integration with ABB welding systems."}},{"k":"fanuc_crx","v":{"s":"FANUC CRX"}},{"k":"fanuc_s_cobot_line_crx_10ia_crx_25ia_e","v":{"s":"FANUC's cobot line. CRX-10iA + CRX-25iA. Easier programming than FANUC industrial."}},{"k":"yumi_abb","v":{"s":"YuMi (ABB)"}},{"k":"dual_arm_cobot_for_assembly_delicate_w","v":{"s":"Dual-arm cobot for assembly + delicate work. Less commonly used for welding but emerging."}},{"k":"why_cobots_are_democratizing_automatio","v":{"s":"💡 Why cobots are democratizing automation"}},{"k":"traditional_welding_robots_200k_turn_k","v":{"s":"Traditional welding robots: ~$200K turn-key cell, weeks of programming, safety cage, requires dedicated programmer. Cobots: ~$50K turn-key, hours of programming, no cage needed, programmed by existing welder. Small shops can now afford automation that was previously out of reach. Result: more shops automating, but cobots typically supplement (not replace) skilled manual welders."}},{"k":"careers_in_robotic_automated_welding","v":{"s":"💼 Careers in robotic + automated welding"}},{"k":"loads_unloads_parts_monitors_cell_oper","v":{"s":"Loads/unloads parts, monitors cell operation, performs minor adjustments + maintenance. Entry-level. Often a starting point."}},{"k":"programs_new_parts_teaches_paths_troub","v":{"s":"Programs new parts + teaches paths + troubleshoots. Often a welder who learned programming. Strong demand."}},{"k":"designs_welding_cells_specs_equipment_","v":{"s":"Designs welding cells, specs equipment, validates processes, manages multiple cells. Engineering degree typical."}},{"k":"designs_commissions_vision_sensor_syst","v":{"s":"Designs + commissions vision + sensor systems on welding cells. Specialty subset of robotic engineering."}},{"k":"travels_to_customer_sites_for_installa","v":{"s":"Travels to customer sites for installation + commissioning + troubleshooting of robot welding cells. FANUC, ABB, Lincoln Electric, etc."}},{"k":"in_a_small_fab_shop_the_person_who_set","v":{"s":"In a small fab shop, the person who set up + maintains the cobot is often the highest-skilled welder (also programs + maintains). Growing role in small to mid-size fab."}},{"k":"path_to_robotic_welding","v":{"s":"💡 Path to robotic welding"}},{"k":"the_strongest_profile_start_as_a_manua","v":{"s":"The strongest profile: start as a manual welder (build technique knowledge), then learn one specific robot system (FANUC training, ABB training, etc.). Welding programs at community colleges increasingly include robot programming modules. EMCC in Maine has FANUC-certified instructors. Tulsa Welding School + Lincoln Electric Welding Tech School offer dedicated automation tracks."}},{"k":"robotic_automated_welding_2","v":{"s":"Robotic + Automated Welding"}},{"k":"the_cwi_role","v":{"s":"📋 The CWI role"}},{"k":"visual_inspection_vt","v":{"s":"👁 Visual inspection (VT)"}},{"k":"pt_mt_surface_defects","v":{"s":"🪞 PT + MT — surface defects"}},{"k":"ultrasonic_ut","v":{"s":"📡 Ultrasonic (UT)"}},{"k":"radiographic_rt","v":{"s":"☢ Radiographic (RT)"}},{"k":"eddy_current_et","v":{"s":"🧲 Eddy current (ET)"}},{"k":"the_cwi_exam","v":{"s":"📝 The CWI exam"}},{"k":"the_certified_welding_inspector_cwi_is_2","v":{"s":"The Certified Welding Inspector (CWI) is the gatekeeper between a finished weld and the customer who accepts it. CWIs apply codes (AWS D1.1, ASME, API) to determine acceptance + rejection of welds, oversee non-destructive testing, audit welder qualifications, and sign off on completed work. Often the only path to certify a fabrication shop's output."}},{"k":"cwi_pay_career","v":{"s":"💰 CWI pay + career"}},{"k":"salary_range","v":{"s":"Salary range: "}},{"k":"60_110k_per_year_typical_us_senior_cwi","v":{"s":"$60-110K per year typical (US). Senior CWIs at refineries + nuclear plants $100-150K+. Independent CWI consultants often bill $90-200/hr for inspection work."}},{"k":"work_environment","v":{"s":"Work environment: "}},{"k":"mix_of_shop_floor_field_office_writing","v":{"s":"Mix of shop floor + field + office (writing reports). Less physically demanding than welding. Some travel."}},{"k":"typical_entry","v":{"s":"Typical entry: "}},{"k":"experienced_welder_5_10_years_who_deci","v":{"s":"Experienced welder (5-10 years) who decides to move into inspection. The shift from making welds to judging them."}},{"k":"how_to_become_cwi","v":{"s":"🎓 How to become CWI"}},{"k":"aws_certified_welding_inspector_requir","v":{"s":"AWS Certified Welding Inspector — requires combination of education + welding experience (e.g., 5 yr welding + HS = qualified; 2 yr welding + Associate degree in welding tech = qualified). Pass 3-part exam ($1,150 fee). Renew every 3 yr. Maintained by continuing education + active inspection work."}},{"k":"visual_inspection_vt_the_first_most_us","v":{"s":"👁 Visual inspection (VT) — the first + most-used method"}},{"k":"visual_is_the_most_used_most_misunders","v":{"s":"Visual is the most-used + most-misunderstood NDT method. Done with the naked eye + simple tools (flashlight, magnifier, weld gauge, mirror). Detects surface-breaking defects only. Despite simplicity, requires extensive training to do reliably."}},{"k":"the_inspector_s_vt_toolkit","v":{"s":"🔧 The inspector's VT toolkit"}},{"k":"adequate_lighting","v":{"s":"Adequate lighting: "}},{"k":"50_fc_foot_candles_minimum_per_aws_b5_","v":{"s":"~50 fc (foot-candles) minimum per AWS B5.1. Often flashlight + ambient room light."}},{"k":"5_10x_magnifier","v":{"s":"5-10x magnifier: "}},{"k":"for_close_inspection_of_small_features","v":{"s":"For close inspection of small features."}},{"k":"mirror_on_stick","v":{"s":"Mirror on stick: "}},{"k":"for_visualizing_back_side_of_welds_ins","v":{"s":"For visualizing back side of welds, inside pipe, etc."}},{"k":"weld_gauge","v":{"s":"Weld gauge: "}},{"k":"bridge_cam_cambridge_or_fillet_gauge_m","v":{"s":"Bridge cam, Cambridge, or fillet gauge. Measures bead reinforcement, undercut depth, fillet leg size."}},{"k":"pit_gauge","v":{"s":"Pit gauge: "}},{"k":"calipers_depth_gauge_for_measuring_def","v":{"s":"Calipers + depth gauge for measuring defect dimensions."}},{"k":"for_inspecting_inside_enclosed_spaces_","v":{"s":"For inspecting inside enclosed spaces (pipe, vessel interior)."}},{"k":"code_book_acceptance_criteria","v":{"s":"Code book + acceptance criteria: "}},{"k":"reference_for_what_s_acceptable_what_s","v":{"s":"Reference for what's acceptable + what's not. Cannot work without this."}},{"k":"what_vt_catches","v":{"s":"💡 What VT catches"}},{"k":"surface_breaking_defects_undercut_over","v":{"s":"Surface-breaking defects: undercut, overlap, excess reinforcement, insufficient reinforcement, surface porosity, surface cracks, weld profile irregularities, lack of fusion at surface, weld dimension non-conformance (leg size, throat). VT cannot see internal defects — that's what other NDT methods are for."}},{"k":"surface_defect_detection_pt_mt","v":{"s":"🪞 Surface defect detection — PT + MT"}},{"k":"pt_liquid_penetrant_testing","v":{"s":"PT — Liquid Penetrant Testing"}},{"k":"clean_surface_apply_colored_or_fluores","v":{"s":"Clean surface → apply colored or fluorescent penetrant → let it seep into defects → wipe excess → apply developer (white powder) → defects show as colored or fluorescent lines."}},{"k":"surface_breaking_defects_only_cracks_p","v":{"s":"Surface-breaking defects only. Cracks, porosity, lack of fusion at surface, laps."}},{"k":"any_non_porous_material_common_on_stai","v":{"s":"Any non-porous material. Common on stainless steel, aluminum, nickel alloys. Not useful on porous materials (cast iron)."}},{"k":"cheap_simple_no_specialized_equipment","v":{"s":"Cheap, simple, no specialized equipment."}},{"k":"surface_only_time_consuming_hazardous_","v":{"s":"Surface only. Time-consuming. Hazardous chemicals."}},{"k":"mt_magnetic_particle_testing","v":{"s":"MT — Magnetic Particle Testing"}},{"k":"magnetize_the_part_with_an_electromagn","v":{"s":"Magnetize the part with an electromagnet → apply iron particles (dry powder or wet suspension) → particles cluster at flux leakage (where field exits at defects) → defects show as colored lines."}},{"k":"surface_near_surface_within_1_8_defect","v":{"s":"Surface + near-surface (within ~1/8\") defects. Cracks oriented perpendicular to magnetic field."}},{"k":"ferromagnetic_materials_only_carbon_st","v":{"s":"Ferromagnetic materials only — carbon steel, alloy steel, some stainless (martensitic). Won't work on aluminum, copper, austenitic stainless 304/316."}},{"k":"fast_sensitive_catches_near_surface_de","v":{"s":"Fast, sensitive, catches near-surface defects PT can't."}},{"k":"only_works_on_magnetic_materials_demag","v":{"s":"Only works on magnetic materials. Demagnetization needed after test for some applications."}},{"k":"pt_vs_mt_selection","v":{"s":"💡 PT vs MT selection"}},{"k":"if_material_is_magnetic_carbon_steel_a","v":{"s":"If material is magnetic (carbon steel, alloy steel) — use MT (faster, more sensitive, finds near-surface). If material is non-magnetic (aluminum, copper, austenitic stainless) — must use PT. Some shops use both: MT for production speed, PT for verification of suspect areas."}},{"k":"ultrasonic_testing_ut","v":{"s":"📡 Ultrasonic Testing (UT)"}},{"k":"ut_uses_high_frequency_sound_waves_1_1","v":{"s":"UT uses high-frequency sound waves (1-10 MHz) injected into the part via a transducer with coupling gel. Sound reflects off defects + back surface. Time-of-flight + amplitude indicate defect depth + size. Most sensitive volumetric NDT method."}},{"k":"how_ut_works","v":{"s":"🔧 How UT works"}},{"k":"1_transducer_piezoelectric_crystal_pre","v":{"s":"1. Transducer (piezoelectric crystal) pressed against surface with couplant gel."}},{"k":"2_transducer_pulses_sound_into_the_par","v":{"s":"2. Transducer pulses sound into the part. Sound travels through metal, reflects off opposite surface, returns."}},{"k":"3_defects_in_the_path_also_reflect_sho","v":{"s":"3. Defects in the path also reflect — show up earlier than back surface."}},{"k":"4_crt_screen_or_now_lcd_shows_time_of_","v":{"s":"4. CRT screen (or now LCD) shows time-of-flight + amplitude. Skilled operator interprets."}},{"k":"5_calibration_against_reference_blocks","v":{"s":"5. Calibration against reference blocks essential — defect size estimates depend on calibration."}},{"k":"pros_cons_2","v":{"s":"💡 Pros + cons"}},{"k":"detects_internal_defects_measures_dept","v":{"s":"Detects internal defects + measures depth + size. Sensitive (can find very small defects). Portable. Real-time results. Safe (no radiation)."}},{"k":"requires_couplant_gel_oil_so_messy_sur","v":{"s":"Requires couplant (gel/oil) so messy. Surface must be smooth. Highly skilled operator needed. Complex geometries (T-joints, intersections) tricky. Doesn't produce permanent record (unlike RT)."}},{"k":"paut_phased_array_ut","v":{"s":"🆕 PAUT — Phased Array UT"}},{"k":"modern_ut_often_uses_phased_array_prob","v":{"s":"Modern UT often uses Phased Array probes (multi-element transducers) + computerized scanning. Generates 2D imagery of defects (vs traditional A-scan single trace). Faster + more visual + better reporting. Increasingly replaces RT for pipe inspection. PAUT certification is the growing path."}},{"k":"radiographic_testing_rt","v":{"s":"☢ Radiographic Testing (RT)"}},{"k":"rt_uses_x_rays_or_gamma_rays_to_image_","v":{"s":"RT uses X-rays or gamma rays to image weld interiors. The part is placed between the radiation source + film/digital detector. Denser metal absorbs more radiation → shows as light areas on the film. Defects (less dense) appear as dark areas. The traditional gold standard for pipe weld inspection."}},{"k":"two_source_types","v":{"s":"🔧 Two source types"}},{"k":"x_ray_electronic","v":{"s":"X-ray (electronic): "}},{"k":"x_rays_generated_by_accelerating_elect","v":{"s":"X-rays generated by accelerating electrons. Source can be turned off when not in use. Used in shops + controlled environments. Better image quality."}},{"k":"gamma_isotope","v":{"s":"Gamma (isotope): "}},{"k":"radioactive_isotopes_ir_192_se_75_co_6","v":{"s":"Radioactive isotopes (Ir-192, Se-75, Co-60) emit gamma rays continuously. Used in field for pipeline + remote work. Source MUST be shielded — radiation incidents have killed pipeline workers."}},{"k":"radiation_safety","v":{"s":"⚠ Radiation safety"}},{"k":"rt_is_the_most_dangerous_ndt_method_ra","v":{"s":"RT is the most dangerous NDT method. Radiation exposure is cumulative + cancer-causing. Strict procedures: exclusion zones (typically 100+ ft radius), dosimeter badges, lead shielding, only certified RT technicians operate. NRC + OSHA highly regulated."}},{"k":"pros_cons_3","v":{"s":"💡 Pros + cons"}},{"k":"creates_permanent_film_digital_record_","v":{"s":"Creates permanent film/digital record (legal documentation). Detects volumetric defects (porosity, slag inclusions, lack of fusion, cracks transverse to bead). Standard for code-required pipe work."}},{"k":"radiation_hazard_requires_expensive_eq","v":{"s":"Radiation hazard. Requires expensive equipment + lead shielding. Time-consuming setup. Cracks parallel to film direction may not show. Less sensitive than PAUT for many crack types."}},{"k":"eddy_current_testing_et","v":{"s":"🧲 Eddy Current Testing (ET)"}},{"k":"et_induces_eddy_currents_in_conductive","v":{"s":"ET induces eddy currents in conductive material via electromagnetic coil. Surface + near-surface defects disrupt the eddy current pattern + the coil senses the change. Used heavily in aerospace + nuclear for crack detection + heat exchanger tube inspection."}},{"k":"how_et_works","v":{"s":"🔧 How ET works"}},{"k":"1_coil_generates_alternating_magnetic_","v":{"s":"1. Coil generates alternating magnetic field near surface of conductive material."}},{"k":"2_eddy_currents_induced_in_the_materia","v":{"s":"2. Eddy currents induced in the material flow in circular patterns parallel to surface."}},{"k":"3_defects_cracks_voids_conductivity_ch","v":{"s":"3. Defects (cracks, voids, conductivity changes) disrupt eddy current flow → impedance of coil changes."}},{"k":"4_coil_impedance_change_is_displayed_o","v":{"s":"4. Coil impedance change is displayed on instrument; operator interprets."}},{"k":"use_cases","v":{"s":"💡 Use cases"}},{"k":"common_applications_2","v":{"s":"Common applications: "}},{"k":"heat_exchanger_tube_inspection_most_co","v":{"s":"Heat exchanger tube inspection (most common ET use), aerospace structural inspection (fast surface crack detection), conductivity measurement (sorting alloys), coating thickness measurement."}},{"k":"only_conductive_materials_depth_limite","v":{"s":"Only conductive materials. Depth limited to ~1/4\" (skin effect). Surface must be accessible. Calibration sensitive."}},{"k":"the_aws_cwi_exam","v":{"s":"📝 The AWS CWI Exam"}},{"k":"the_cwi_exam_is_one_of_the_more_demand","v":{"s":"The CWI exam is one of the more demanding professional certifications in welding. Three parts, each 2 hours, given over 9 hours one day:"}},{"k":"150_multiple_choice_questions_welding_","v":{"s":"150 multiple-choice questions. Welding processes, metallurgy, weld design, distortion + residual stress, weld + base metal discontinuities, destructive + non-destructive examination, joint design, welding symbols, basic codes + standards. Open book — bring AWS reference materials."}},{"k":"46_multiple_choice_visual_inspection_o","v":{"s":"46 multiple choice. Visual inspection of weld samples — identify defects, measure dimensions, apply acceptance criteria. Uses physical weld coupons in the test room. Inspector applies code criteria to actual welds."}},{"k":"60_multiple_choice_based_on_a_code_boo","v":{"s":"60 multiple choice. Based on a code book chosen at registration — usually AWS D1.1 or ASME IX or API 1104. Tests ability to find + apply specific code provisions. Open book — must navigate the code efficiently."}},{"k":"pass_rates","v":{"s":"⚠ Pass rates"}},{"k":"approximately_50_65_pass_on_first_atte","v":{"s":"Approximately 50-65% pass on first attempt. Part C (code book) is typically the hardest — many candidates have never used a code book under time pressure. ~$1,150 fee per exam attempt. AWS-approved prep courses ($500-3000) significantly improve pass rates."}},{"k":"preparation_strategy","v":{"s":"🎓 Preparation strategy"}},{"k":"1_buy_the_aws_wi_manual_online_self_as","v":{"s":"1. Buy the AWS WI Manual + Online Self-Assessment ($150). Take + review repeatedly."}},{"k":"2_take_an_aws_approved_seminar_one_wee","v":{"s":"2. Take an AWS-approved seminar (one week immersive). $1500-3000. Highest ROI for first-time test-takers."}},{"k":"3_for_part_c_practice_with_the_actual_","v":{"s":"3. For Part C: practice with the actual code book you'll bring. Tab key sections. Time yourself looking up references."}},{"k":"4_for_part_b_get_hands_on_with_weld_sa","v":{"s":"4. For Part B: get hands-on with weld samples + practice applying acceptance criteria. Some local AWS sections offer practice clinics."}},{"k":"5_use_aws_qc1_aws_b5_1_specification_f","v":{"s":"5. Use AWS QC1 + AWS B5.1 (Specification for the Qualification of Welding Inspectors) as study foundations."}},{"k":"welding_inspection_cwi_prep","v":{"s":"Welding Inspection / CWI Prep"}},{"k":"overview_5","v":{"s":"📋 Overview"}},{"k":"stick_electrodes","v":{"s":"🪄 Stick electrodes"}},{"k":"mig_fcaw_wires","v":{"s":"🪢 MIG + FCAW wires"}},{"k":"tig_filler_rods_tungsten","v":{"s":"✏ TIG filler rods + tungsten"}},{"k":"shielding_gases","v":{"s":"💨 Shielding gases"}},{"k":"storage_handling","v":{"s":"📦 Storage + handling"}},{"k":"cost_economics","v":{"s":"💰 Cost economics"}},{"k":"pipeline_root_pass_on_cross_country_li","v":{"s":"Pipeline root pass on cross-country line"}},{"k":"structural_beam_to_column_weld_for_hig","v":{"s":"Structural beam-to-column weld for high-rise"}},{"k":"auto_body_repair_on_rusty_quarter_pane","v":{"s":"Auto body repair on rusty quarter panel"}},{"k":"stainless_food_grade_dairy_tank_repair","v":{"s":"Stainless food-grade dairy tank repair"}},{"k":"aluminum_extrusion_bracket_6061_t6","v":{"s":"Aluminum extrusion bracket (6061-T6)"}},{"k":"welding_rebar_5_for_a_foundation_repai","v":{"s":"Welding rebar (#5) for a foundation repair"}},{"k":"tig_welding_1_8_aluminum_boat_hull_ac","v":{"s":"TIG-welding 1/8\" aluminum boat hull (AC)"}},{"k":"high_production_fillet_welding_on_1_2_","v":{"s":"High-production fillet welding on 1/2\" plate"}},{"k":"welding_consumables_electrodes_wires_r","v":{"s":"Welding consumables (electrodes, wires, rods, gases, flux) are the variable cost of every weld. Picking the right consumable for the job is half the welder's skill — wrong electrode + same machine + same hands = bad weld + cracked joint + wasted time + rejected work."}},{"k":"this_module_covers_the_consumables_sys","v":{"s":"This module covers the consumables system in depth: electrode classifications (what the numbers mean), wire selection, shielding gas mixes, storage requirements, cost economics. Every welder needs working fluency."}},{"k":"quick_consumable_map_by_process","v":{"s":"🎯 Quick consumable map by process"}},{"k":"smaw_stick","v":{"s":"SMAW (stick): "}},{"k":"coated_electrodes_e6010_6011_6013_7018","v":{"s":"Coated electrodes (E6010, 6011, 6013, 7018, 7024). No shielding gas (flux coating provides). Available in 1/16\" to 3/16\" diameters."}},{"k":"gmaw_mig","v":{"s":"GMAW (MIG): "}},{"k":"solid_wire_er70s_3_s_6_on_spool_shield","v":{"s":"Solid wire (ER70S-3, S-6) on spool + shielding gas (CO2, C25, argon mixes). Wire 0.024\" to 0.045\" typical."}},{"k":"fcaw_flux_cored","v":{"s":"FCAW (Flux-Cored): "}},{"k":"tubular_wire_with_flux_inside_two_type","v":{"s":"Tubular wire with flux inside. Two types: self-shielded (E71T-11) needs no gas; gas-shielded (E70T-1) uses CO2."}},{"k":"gtaw_tig","v":{"s":"GTAW (TIG): "}},{"k":"tungsten_electrode_non_consumable_sepa","v":{"s":"Tungsten electrode (non-consumable) + separate filler rod (ER70S-2/3/6, ER308 for stainless, ER4043/5356 for aluminum) + shielding gas (100% argon usually)."}},{"k":"ofw_oxy_fuel","v":{"s":"OFW (Oxy-Fuel): "}},{"k":"no_electrode_torch_combines_oxygen_ace","v":{"s":"No electrode — torch combines oxygen + acetylene. Filler rod separate (RG45 for steel, similar to ER70S series)."}},{"k":"stick_smaw_electrode_classifications_d","v":{"s":"🪄 Stick (SMAW) electrode classifications — decoding AWS A5.1"}},{"k":"aws_electrode_classification_looks_lik","v":{"s":"AWS electrode classification looks like \"E7018.\" Each character means something:"}},{"k":"e70_18","v":{"s":"E70 18"}},{"k":"electrode","v":{"s":"= Electrode"}},{"k":"70_000_psi_minimum_tensile_strength_e8","v":{"s":"= 70,000 psi minimum tensile strength (E80 = 80 ksi, etc.)"}},{"k":"all_positions_2_horizontal_flat_only_4","v":{"s":"= All positions (2 = horizontal + flat only, 4 = flat + horizontal + overhead vertical-down)"}},{"k":"coating_type_recommended_current_see_t","v":{"s":"= Coating type + recommended current — see table below"}},{"k":"the_major_stick_electrodes_you_ll_enco","v":{"s":"The major stick electrodes you'll encounter:"}},{"k":"picking_the_right_stick_electrode","v":{"s":"💡 Picking the right stick electrode"}},{"k":"quick_farm_repair_work","v":{"s":"Quick farm/repair work: "}},{"k":"e6011_works_on_any_machine_dirty_metal","v":{"s":"E6011 (works on any machine, dirty metal OK)."}},{"k":"pipeline_root_pass","v":{"s":"Pipeline root pass: "}},{"k":"e6010_deep_penetration_fast_freeze","v":{"s":"E6010 (deep penetration, fast freeze)."}},{"k":"structural_code_work","v":{"s":"Structural code work: "}},{"k":"e7018_low_hydrogen_smooth_bead_aws_req","v":{"s":"E7018 (low hydrogen, smooth bead, AWS-required)."}},{"k":"production_fillets_flat_position","v":{"s":"Production fillets, flat position: "}},{"k":"e7024_drag_rod_speed","v":{"s":"E7024 (drag-rod speed)."}},{"k":"sheet_metal_cosmetic","v":{"s":"Sheet metal cosmetic: "}},{"k":"e6013_smooth_low_penetration","v":{"s":"E6013 (smooth, low penetration)."}},{"k":"high_strength_alloy_steel","v":{"s":"High-strength alloy steel: "}},{"k":"e8018_b2_or_higher_with_preheat_low_hy","v":{"s":"E8018-B2 or higher with preheat + low-hydrogen practice."}},{"k":"mig_gmaw_fcaw_wire_classifications_aws","v":{"s":"🪢 MIG (GMAW) + FCAW wire classifications — AWS A5.18 / A5.20"}},{"k":"mig_wires_are_solid_or_composite_fcaw_","v":{"s":"MIG wires are solid or composite (FCAW). Both are continuous-feed from a spool. AWS classification looks like \"ER70S-6\":"}},{"k":"er_70_s_6","v":{"s":"ER 70 S - 6"}},{"k":"electrode_rod_solid_wire_usable_as_tig","v":{"s":"= Electrode + Rod (solid wire; usable as TIG filler too)"}},{"k":"70_ksi_tensile_strength","v":{"s":"= 70 ksi tensile strength"}},{"k":"solid_wire_vs_c_for_composite_cored","v":{"s":"= Solid wire (vs C for composite/cored)"}},{"k":"chemistry_different_additives_mn_si_ti","v":{"s":"= Chemistry — different additives (Mn, Si, Ti, Al, Zr) for different needs"}},{"k":"common_solid_mig_wires","v":{"s":"Common solid MIG wires:"}},{"k":"triple_deoxidized_al_ti_zr_added_most_","v":{"s":"Triple-deoxidized (Al, Ti, Zr added). Most clean MIG wire. Best for: contaminated/rusty base metal, primer-coated steel. Often used by farm + repair welders."}},{"k":"standard_mig_wire_modest_deoxidation_b","v":{"s":"Standard MIG wire. Modest deoxidation. Best for: clean, new mild steel in fabrication shops. Most economical."}},{"k":"higher_mn_si_than_s_3_better_for_light","v":{"s":"Higher Mn + Si than S-3. Better for: light mill scale on steel. Slightly stronger weld."}},{"k":"highest_mn_si_content_best_for_rusty_d","v":{"s":"Highest Mn + Si content. Best for: rusty/dirty/galvanized steel, autobody work. The #1-selling MIG wire because it tolerates real-world conditions."}},{"k":"specialty_high_mn_for_thicker_steel_le","v":{"s":"Specialty — high Mn for thicker steel. Less common."}},{"k":"er308_308l","v":{"s":"ER308 / 308L"}},{"k":"stainless_mig_wire_for_304_304l_stainl","v":{"s":"Stainless MIG wire for 304/304L stainless. 308 has higher carbon; 308L is low-carbon (recommended for code work to avoid sensitization)."}},{"k":"er316_316l","v":{"s":"ER316 / 316L"}},{"k":"stainless_mig_wire_for_316_316l_more_c","v":{"s":"Stainless MIG wire for 316/316L (more corrosion-resistant grade — molybdenum added). For marine + chemical applications."}},{"k":"aluminum_mig_wire_5_silicon_best_for_6","v":{"s":"Aluminum MIG wire — 5% silicon. Best for: 6061 + 6063 aluminum (most extrusions). Easy to use; bead has darker tinge."}},{"k":"aluminum_mig_wire_5_magnesium_best_for","v":{"s":"Aluminum MIG wire — 5% magnesium. Best for: 5052 + 5083 + 6061 (some). Stronger than 4043, harder to use. Better for marine + structural."}},{"k":"fcaw_wires_flux_cored","v":{"s":"FCAW wires (flux-cored):"}},{"k":"gas_shielded_fcaw_smooth_bead_low_spat","v":{"s":"Gas-shielded FCAW. Smooth bead, low spatter, slag covers bead for protection while cooling. Best for: indoor production welding on plate. Faster than solid MIG."}},{"k":"like_e70t_1_but_all_positions_1_vs_0_i","v":{"s":"Like E70T-1 but all-positions (1 vs 0 in classification = all-position). Common for structural fabrication."}},{"k":"self_shielded_fcaw_no_gas_needed_best_","v":{"s":"Self-shielded FCAW (no gas needed). Best for: outdoor work, field repair, wind-affected environments. Less smooth bead than gas-shielded but no gas cylinder to lug around."}},{"k":"self_shielded_all_positions_fcaw_used_","v":{"s":"Self-shielded all-positions FCAW. Used in homeowner-grade MIG machines without gas. Cheap + tolerant but bead quality limited."}},{"k":"wire_diameter_selection","v":{"s":"💡 Wire diameter selection"}},{"k":"0_024_thin_sheet_metal_autobody_22_ga_","v":{"s":"0.024\" — thin sheet metal (autobody, 22 ga). 0.030\" — light gauge (16-12 ga). 0.035\" — most common general work (1/8\"-3/8\"). 0.045\" — heavy plate (3/8\"+) + production work. Bigger wire = more amperage required = more deposition rate but less control."}},{"k":"tig_gtaw_consumables_rods_tungsten_gas","v":{"s":"✏ TIG (GTAW) consumables — rods + tungsten + gas"}},{"k":"tig_uses_a_non_consumable_tungsten_ele","v":{"s":"TIG uses a non-consumable tungsten electrode (doesn't become part of the weld) + a separate filler rod that the welder dips into the puddle. Both selection + tungsten preparation matter."}},{"k":"tungsten_electrodes_color_coded_tip","v":{"s":"Tungsten electrodes — color-coded tip!"}},{"k":"2_thoriated_ewth_2","v":{"s":"2% Thoriated (EWTh-2)"}},{"k":"2_lanthanated_ewla_2","v":{"s":"2% Lanthanated (EWLa-2)"}},{"k":"2_ceriated_ewce_2","v":{"s":"2% Ceriated (EWCe-2)"}},{"k":"1_5_lanthanated_ewla_1_5","v":{"s":"1.5% Lanthanated (EWLa-1.5)"}},{"k":"pure_tungsten_ewp","v":{"s":"Pure tungsten (EWP)"}},{"k":"0_8_zirconiated_ewzr_1","v":{"s":"0.8% Zirconiated (EWZr-1)"}},{"k":"tungsten_preparation","v":{"s":"💡 Tungsten preparation"}},{"k":"dc_tig_grind_tungsten_to_a_sharp_point","v":{"s":"DC TIG: grind tungsten to a sharp point (pencil-tip). Grinding marks should run lengthwise (along electrode), not circumferentially (around). AC TIG: ball the tip by striking arc on scrap; the heat melts the tip into a smooth ball. Dirty/contaminated tungsten = poor arc start + bad weld. Re-grind every time you touch the filler rod by accident."}},{"k":"tig_filler_rods","v":{"s":"TIG filler rods:"}},{"k":"tig_filler_rods_use_the_same_classific","v":{"s":"TIG filler rods use the same classification as MIG wires (ER70S-2, ER70S-6, ER308, ER4043, etc.). The rod is 36\" long, ~1/16\" to 3/32\" diameter, fed manually into the weld puddle by the welder's other hand. The choice depends on base metal + intended weld properties."}},{"k":"shielding_gas_mixes_what_to_use_for_wh","v":{"s":"💨 Shielding gas mixes — what to use for what"}},{"k":"shielding_gas_protects_the_molten_weld","v":{"s":"Shielding gas protects the molten weld pool from atmospheric oxygen + nitrogen. The wrong gas = porosity + poor penetration + bad arc characteristics."}},{"k":"deep_pen","v":{"s":"Deep pen: "}},{"k":"flow_rate_basics","v":{"s":"💡 Flow rate basics"}},{"k":"typical_20_35_cfh_cubic_feet_hour_for_","v":{"s":"Typical: 20-35 CFH (cubic feet/hour) for MIG. 15-25 CFH for TIG. Too low = porosity. Too high = turbulent flow that SUCKS atmosphere in = porosity. Both extremes cause same defect. Sweet spot is your machine's recommended range adjusted for breeze + nozzle distance."}},{"k":"consumable_storage_handling_why_it_mat","v":{"s":"📦 Consumable storage + handling — why it matters"}},{"k":"damp_consumables_cause_hydrogen_induce","v":{"s":"Damp consumables cause hydrogen-induced cold cracking. Contaminated consumables cause weld defects. Most \"the welder couldn't get good penetration\" complaints are actually consumable problems."}},{"k":"damp_e7018_the_killer","v":{"s":"⚠ Damp E7018 — the killer"}},{"k":"a_welder_uses_e7018_from_a_can_that_s_","v":{"s":"A welder uses E7018 from a can that's been open for 3 days. The coating has absorbed moisture. When struck, the moisture dissociates → hydrogen enters the weld pool → diffuses into HAZ → 24-48 hr later, cold crack. Weld fails inspection or fails in service. NEVER trust E7018 from an unsealed can. When in doubt, rebake."}},{"k":"consumable_economics_cost_per_pound_of","v":{"s":"💰 Consumable economics — cost per pound of weld"}},{"k":"for_estimating_purposes_welders_shops_","v":{"s":"For estimating purposes, welders + shops calculate \"cost per pound of deposited weld metal.\" Includes consumable cost + labor cost + machine time. Process choice has huge cost implications."}},{"k":"lowest_deposit_rate_lots_of_waste_stub","v":{"s":"Lowest deposit rate. Lots of waste (stubs + flux). But low equipment cost + portable."}},{"k":"faster_than_stick_most_popular_for_pla","v":{"s":"Faster than stick. Most popular for plate fabrication. Higher equipment cost (machine + gas)."}},{"k":"much_faster_than_short_circuit_flat_ho","v":{"s":"Much faster than short-circuit. Flat + horizontal only. Heavy fabrication."}},{"k":"fastest_of_common_processes_heavy_indu","v":{"s":"Fastest of common processes. Heavy industrial work (structural, shipbuilding, mining equipment)."}},{"k":"lowest_deposit_rate_highest_cost_per_p","v":{"s":"Lowest deposit rate, highest cost per pound. ONLY used where quality + appearance + corrosion resistance justify cost."}},{"k":"lowest_cost_per_pound_automated_only_u","v":{"s":"Lowest cost per pound. Automated only. Used for huge plates, ships, pipe seam welding."}},{"k":"cost_vs_quality_trade_off","v":{"s":"💡 Cost vs quality trade-off"}},{"k":"engineers_spec_the_cheapest_process_th","v":{"s":"Engineers spec the cheapest process that meets requirements. A non-critical fillet weld on plate = MIG short-circuit. A pressure vessel root pass = TIG (5x cost, but the only way to meet code). A 10,000 ft pipeline = FCAW with mechanized travel. Smart welding shops bid jobs knowing these tradeoffs."}},{"k":"browse_topics","v":{"s":"📋 Browse topics"}},{"k":"pick_the_right_consumable","v":{"s":"🎯 Pick the right consumable"}},{"k":"consumables_deep_dive_2","v":{"s":"Consumables Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"consumables_mode","v":{"s":"Consumables mode"}},{"k":"which_consumable_combination_best_fits","v":{"s":"Which consumable combination best fits this job?"}},{"k":"consumable_options","v":{"s":"Consumable options"}},{"k":"correct","v":{"s":"✓ correct"}},{"k":"your_pick","v":{"s":"✗ your pick"}},{"k":"restart_scenarios","v":{"s":"↺ Restart scenarios"}},{"k":"overview_6","v":{"s":"🦞 Overview"}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_2","v":{"s":"🚢 Bath Iron Works"}},{"k":"maine_shipyards","v":{"s":"⚓ Maine shipyards"}},{"k":"emcc_training_pipelines","v":{"s":"🎓 EMCC + training pipelines"}},{"k":"other_maine_employers","v":{"s":"🏭 Other Maine employers"}},{"k":"unions_apprenticeships","v":{"s":"🤝 Unions + apprenticeships"}},{"k":"your_own_shop","v":{"s":"🛠 Your own shop"}},{"k":"biw_internal_training_production_welde","v":{"s":"BIW Internal Training → Production welder"}},{"k":"application_interview_process_drug_tes","v":{"s":"Application + interview process. Drug test + math test typical."}},{"k":"paid_during_the_16_week_program_weldin","v":{"s":"PAID during the 16-week program. Welding from day one. Cohort-based learning."}},{"k":"on_the_floor_welding_hulls_bulkheads_p","v":{"s":"On the floor welding hulls, bulkheads, piping. IAM Local S6 member."}},{"k":"annual_income_60_70k_with_regular_ot_c","v":{"s":"Annual income ~$60-70K with regular OT. Comfortable life in Bath area."}},{"k":"annual_70_80k_pension_begins_vesting_a","v":{"s":"Annual ~$70-80K. Pension begins vesting at 5 years employment."}},{"k":"annual_75_95k_pension_is_yours_regardl","v":{"s":"Annual $75-95K. Pension is yours regardless of future moves. Healthcare excellent."}},{"k":"emcc_2_year_boatyard_tig_welder","v":{"s":"EMCC 2-year → Boatyard TIG welder"}},{"k":"full_time_student_may_work_part_time_m","v":{"s":"Full-time student. May work part-time. Maine Promise scholarship covers most for income-eligible students."}},{"k":"building_tig_skills_portfolio_networki","v":{"s":"Building TIG skills + portfolio. Networking with shops."}},{"k":"annual_46_54k_most_yards_include_healt","v":{"s":"Annual $46-54K. Most yards include healthcare. Some bonus on yacht delivery."}},{"k":"annual_52_62k_aluminum_tig_skill_comma","v":{"s":"Annual $52-62K. Aluminum TIG skill commands premium."}},{"k":"annual_58_70k_path_to_lead_foreman_ope","v":{"s":"Annual $58-70K. Path to lead/foreman opening up."}},{"k":"ua_local_716_pipefitter_apprenticeship","v":{"s":"UA Local 716 Pipefitter Apprenticeship"}},{"k":"most_applicants_are_19_25_some_18_year","v":{"s":"Most applicants are 19-25. Some 18-year-olds get in. Apply through UA Local 716 directly."}},{"k":"earning_while_learning_no_tuition_full","v":{"s":"Earning while learning. No tuition. Full healthcare from day 1."}},{"k":"annual_54_62k_per_diem_on_travel_work","v":{"s":"Annual $54-62K. Per diem on travel work."}},{"k":"annual_62_72k_6g_pipe_qual_is_the_big_","v":{"s":"Annual $62-72K. 6G pipe qual is the big milestone."}},{"k":"annual_72_82k_approaching_journey_rate","v":{"s":"Annual $72-82K. Approaching journey rate."}},{"k":"annual_90_120k_typical_top_earners_120","v":{"s":"Annual $90-120K typical. Top earners $120-150K with travel work. Defined-benefit pension."}},{"k":"hs_cte_small_fab_shop_mobile_welding","v":{"s":"HS CTE → Small fab shop → Mobile welding"}},{"k":"no_college_debt_aws_quals_already_in_p","v":{"s":"No college debt. AWS quals already in pocket."}},{"k":"annual_37_46k_learning_real_world_weld","v":{"s":"Annual $37-46K. Learning real-world welding speed + variety."}},{"k":"annual_46_54k_considering_whether_to_s","v":{"s":"Annual $46-54K. Considering whether to stay long-term or move on."}},{"k":"if_mobile_invest_savings_into_truck_eq","v":{"s":"If mobile: invest savings into truck + equipment ($10-20K). High risk; high freedom."}},{"k":"word_of_mouth_facebook_marketplace_fir","v":{"s":"Word-of-mouth + Facebook marketplace. First year is hard. Year 2-3 typically much better."}},{"k":"full_freedom_over_schedule_hard_in_lea","v":{"s":"Full freedom over schedule. Hard in lean weeks. Plan for taxes (quarterly)."}},{"k":"maine_has_one_of_the_densest_skilled_t","v":{"s":"Maine has one of the densest skilled-trades welding economies in the Northeast. Shipbuilding (Bath Iron Works alone employs ~6,500), boatbuilding (Hodgdon, Sabre, Lyman-Morse), heavy industry (paper mills, food processing), and a thick small-shop ecosystem (fabrication shops, marine repair, custom work) all need welders right now."}},{"k":"this_module_maps_where_welders_work_in","v":{"s":"This module maps where welders work in Maine, what each path actually pays, and how students get in. Real names + real numbers + real entry routes — not generic \"you could work in welding\" career fluff."}},{"k":"the_maine_welding_landscape_at_a_glanc","v":{"s":"🎯 The Maine welding landscape at a glance"}},{"k":"biw_bath_dominates_designed_structural","v":{"s":"BIW (Bath) dominates. Designed structural + pipefitting welds for Navy destroyers. ~6,500 employees, IAM Local S6 union. Aggressive hiring."}},{"k":"boatbuilding_mid_coast","v":{"s":"Boatbuilding (mid-coast): "}},{"k":"hodgdon_yachts_east_boothbay_lyman_mor","v":{"s":"Hodgdon Yachts (East Boothbay), Lyman-Morse (Thomaston), Sabre (Raymond), Front Street Shipyard (Belfast). Aluminum + stainless TIG primarily. Smaller crews, more variety."}},{"k":"heavy_industry","v":{"s":"Heavy industry: "}},{"k":"paper_mills_sappi_westbrook_verso_pixe","v":{"s":"Paper mills (Sappi Westbrook, Verso/Pixelle Jay), Cianbro (statewide construction), Reed & Reed (large structural), food processing (Idexx, Tom's of Maine)."}},{"k":"marine_fabrication_shops","v":{"s":"Marine + fabrication shops: "}},{"k":"hundreds_of_small_shops_truck_bodies_s","v":{"s":"Hundreds of small shops. Truck bodies, snow plows, repair welding, custom fab. Often family-owned. Lower formal credentials, faster entry."}},{"k":"pipeline_cross_country","v":{"s":"Pipeline + cross-country: "}},{"k":"less_common_in_maine_but_maine_welders","v":{"s":"Less common in Maine but Maine welders frequently travel — Connecticut, Quebec, Pennsylvania for big-pay union work."}},{"k":"bath_iron_works_biw_maine_s_anchor_emp","v":{"s":"🚢 Bath Iron Works (BIW) — Maine's anchor employer"}},{"k":"biw_a_general_dynamics_company_builds_","v":{"s":"BIW (a General Dynamics company) builds Arleigh Burke-class destroyers (DDG-51) for the US Navy. Founded 1884. Located on the Kennebec River in Bath. Largest private employer in Maine."}},{"k":"the_numbers","v":{"s":"The numbers"}},{"k":"6_500_largest_maine_private_employer","v":{"s":"~6,500 (largest Maine private employer)"}},{"k":"welders_specifically","v":{"s":"Welders specifically: "}},{"k":"600_800_varies_with_production_cycle_a","v":{"s":"~600-800 (varies with production cycle). Always hiring."}},{"k":"entry_welder_pay_step_1_22_25_hr_benef","v":{"s":"Entry welder (Pay Step 1) ~$22-25/hr + benefits. Top-step structural welder ~$36-42/hr + benefits + shift differential. With OT (common during build push), 6-figure income achievable."}},{"k":"full_health_pension_iam_defined_benefi","v":{"s":"Full health, pension (IAM defined-benefit), 401k match, paid time off. Pension specifically is rare in modern manufacturing — a big draw."}},{"k":"international_association_of_machinist","v":{"s":"International Association of Machinists Local S6. Largest single private-sector union local in Maine."}},{"k":"what_you_weld_at_biw","v":{"s":"What you weld at BIW"}},{"k":"hull_plating_hsla_65_hsla_80_hy_80_hy_","v":{"s":"Hull plating (HSLA-65, HSLA-80, HY-80, HY-100 high-strength low-alloy steel). Internal structure (bulkheads, frames). Piping systems (carbon steel + stainless + copper-nickel). Aluminum superstructure (sometimes). Every weld is inspected. Code work: NAVSEA Technical Publication T9074 (Navy welding standards) + AWS D1.6 (stainless) + ASME IX (pipe)."}},{"k":"how_to_get_in","v":{"s":"How to get in"}},{"k":"biw_runs_its_own_training_school_16_we","v":{"s":"BIW runs its own training school. ~16-week program. Paid while you train ($18-19/hr starting). After completion + qualification, you join production. Application: biwjobs.com or BIW HR (207-443-3311). HUGE entry channel — no prior welding required for some entries."}},{"k":"eastern_maine_community_college_weldin","v":{"s":"Eastern Maine Community College welding program (Bangor). 2-year associate. BIW actively recruits EMCC grads. Tuition ~$3,500/yr — Maine residents get further discount."}},{"k":"many_maine_ctes_region_10_mid_coast_sc","v":{"s":"Many Maine CTEs (Region 10, Mid-Coast School of Technology, Hancock County Technical Center) feed BIW directly. Counselors have established relationships."}},{"k":"experienced_welders_with_navsea_aws_d1","v":{"s":"Experienced welders with NAVSEA, AWS D1.1 or D1.6 quals can apply directly + skip portions of internal training. Pay starts higher (Step 3-5 depending on experience)."}},{"k":"reality_check","v":{"s":"💡 Reality check"}},{"k":"biw_work_is_hard_outdoor_in_maine_wint","v":{"s":"BIW work is hard. Outdoor in Maine winter (heated tents help but cold). Shift work (1st/2nd/3rd, mandatory OT). Strict drug testing (Navy contract). Physical (carrying gear up + down hulls). Trade-offs are real wages, full benefits, pension, and a job that builds Navy ships."}},{"k":"maine_shipyards_boatbuilders_mid_coast","v":{"s":"⚓ Maine shipyards + boatbuilders — mid-coast cluster"}},{"k":"beyond_biw_maine_s_mid_coast_has_one_o","v":{"s":"Beyond BIW, Maine's mid-coast has one of the densest boatbuilding clusters in North America. Most are aluminum or steel construction; some composite. Welders here typically do TIG (aluminum + stainless), often work alongside designers + engineers, build everything from megayachts to Navy support craft."}},{"k":"hodgdon_yachts_east_boothbay","v":{"s":"Hodgdon Yachts (East Boothbay)"}},{"k":"lyman_morse_thomaston","v":{"s":"Lyman-Morse (Thomaston)"}},{"k":"sabre_yachts_raymond","v":{"s":"Sabre Yachts (Raymond)"}},{"k":"front_street_shipyard_belfast","v":{"s":"Front Street Shipyard (Belfast)"}},{"k":"steve_french_yacht_services_brooklin","v":{"s":"Steve French Yacht Services (Brooklin)"}},{"k":"hinckley_yachts_trenton_southwest_harb","v":{"s":"Hinckley Yachts (Trenton + Southwest Harbor)"}},{"k":"brooklin_boat_yard_brooklin","v":{"s":"Brooklin Boat Yard (Brooklin)"}},{"k":"washburn_doughty_east_boothbay","v":{"s":"Washburn & Doughty (East Boothbay)"}},{"k":"welders_do","v":{"s":"Welders do: "}},{"k":"boatyard_vs_shipyard_culture","v":{"s":"💡 Boatyard vs shipyard culture"}},{"k":"shipyards_biw_washburn_doughty_bigger_","v":{"s":"Shipyards (BIW, Washburn & Doughty) = bigger crews, structured shifts, union, strict code work. Boatyards = smaller crews, more creative latitude, often non-union, more variety per project. Both pay well. Personality fit matters as much as skill fit."}},{"k":"welder_training_pipelines_in_maine","v":{"s":"🎓 Welder training pipelines in Maine"}},{"k":"multiple_training_paths_get_maine_stud","v":{"s":"Multiple training paths get Maine students into paid welding work within 6 months to 2 years. Most options are inexpensive or free; some are paid-while-you-learn."}},{"k":"eastern_maine_community_college_emcc_b","v":{"s":"Eastern Maine Community College (EMCC, Bangor)"}},{"k":"southern_maine_community_college_smcc_","v":{"s":"Southern Maine Community College (SMCC, South Portland)"}},{"k":"washington_county_community_college_ca","v":{"s":"Washington County Community College (Calais)"}},{"k":"northern_maine_community_college_presq","v":{"s":"Northern Maine Community College (Presque Isle)"}},{"k":"cte_high_school_programs_region_by_reg","v":{"s":"CTE high school programs (region by region)"}},{"k":"biw_internal_training","v":{"s":"BIW Internal Training"}},{"k":"job_corps_welding_programs","v":{"s":"Job Corps welding programs"}},{"k":"apprenticeship_union_sponsored","v":{"s":"Apprenticeship (union sponsored)"}},{"k":"which_path_for_which_student","v":{"s":"💡 Which path for which student"}},{"k":"knows_welding_goal_by_sophomore_year_c","v":{"s":"Knows welding = goal by sophomore year + cost-sensitive: HS CTE → direct hire or BIW Training. Adult learner / career changer: EMCC + SMCC. Wants top pay + no debt: union apprenticeship (highly competitive entry). Wants BIW specifically + soon: BIW Internal Training. No family support + needs structure: Job Corps."}},{"k":"other_maine_welding_employers_beyond_s","v":{"s":"🏭 Other Maine welding employers beyond shipyards"}},{"k":"maine_s_welding_workforce_extends_well","v":{"s":"Maine's welding workforce extends well beyond shipbuilding. Heavy construction, paper/pulp, food processing, road maintenance, agriculture, custom fab — all hire welders, often year-round, often with shorter training requirements."}},{"k":"unions_apprenticeships_in_maine","v":{"s":"🤝 Unions + apprenticeships in Maine"}},{"k":"union_apprenticeships_pay_you_to_learn","v":{"s":"Union apprenticeships pay you to learn — usually 3-5 years to journeyman. Top pay nationally is in union work; Maine is a moderate-density union state with several active welding-relevant locals."}},{"k":"union_apprenticeship_reality","v":{"s":"💡 Union apprenticeship reality"}},{"k":"application_windows_are_tight_usually_","v":{"s":"Application windows are tight (usually 1-2 weeks each year). Selection is competitive (aptitude test + interview). Once in, you cannot easily switch. Travel is common (jobs follow construction). Pay scales formally each year — you know exactly what you'll earn through journey-out. Pension + healthcare are gold-standard. Many of the highest-paid welders in Maine are pipefitters + boilermakers."}},{"k":"starting_your_own_welding_shop_in_main","v":{"s":"🛠 Starting your own welding shop in Maine"}},{"k":"many_maine_welders_eventually_open_the","v":{"s":"Many Maine welders eventually open their own shops — sometimes after 5-15 years industry experience, sometimes immediately from CTE/EMCC. Small shop welding is hard work but it is one of the lowest-barrier paths to small business ownership."}},{"k":"what_you_need_to_start","v":{"s":"What you need to start"}},{"k":"mig_machine_miller_211_lincoln_210mp_1","v":{"s":"MIG machine (Miller 211 / Lincoln 210MP ~$1,200-1,500), TIG/stick combo (Miller Multimatic 220 ~$2,500), oxy-acetylene rig (~$500), grinder, plasma cutter (~$1,000), helmet, gloves, cart, vise, table. Used equipment cuts cost 30-50%."}},{"k":"garage_bay_rent_or_own_24x24_ft_minimu","v":{"s":"Garage bay (rent or own). 24x24 ft minimum for production work. Concrete floor (NOT wood). Adequate electrical (50A 240V minimum, 100A better). Ventilation. Some welders work outdoors May-Oct + heated bay rest of year."}},{"k":"general_liability_600_1200_yr_workers_","v":{"s":"General liability (~$600-1200/yr). Workers comp if employees. Some shops add product liability (welds that fail in service)."}},{"k":"sole_proprietorship_or_llc_175_filing_","v":{"s":"Sole proprietorship or LLC ($175 filing in Maine). Sales tax permit (Maine Revenue Services). EIN if hiring. Not complicated."}},{"k":"first_year_word_of_mouth_facebook_mark","v":{"s":"First year: word of mouth, Facebook marketplace, repair calls, custom truck/trailer work. Mid-coast + rural Maine has steady demand. Marine work seasonal but lucrative spring/fall."}},{"k":"pricing_your_work","v":{"s":"Pricing your work"}},{"k":"shop_labor_60_95_hr_maine_2026_field_m","v":{"s":"Shop labor: $60-95/hr (Maine, 2026). Field/mobile work: $85-125/hr + truck charge. Repair welding (often emergencies): premium. Don't underprice — your equipment + skill + insurance cost real money. Track time honestly: first hour on every job is usually setup + parts diagnosis."}},{"k":"what_goes_wrong","v":{"s":"💡 What goes wrong"}},{"k":"underpricing_maine_welders_chronically","v":{"s":"Underpricing (Maine welders chronically underbid each other). No quoting discipline (verbal estimates that become disputes). Equipment failure (one fried machine = month of lost income if no backup). Cash flow gaps (some customers pay 60-90 days). Don't take on jobs you don't have the right equipment for. Don't weld something safety-critical without the qualifications."}},{"k":"browse_topics_2","v":{"s":"🦞 Browse topics"}},{"k":"5_year_roadmap","v":{"s":"🗺 5-Year Roadmap"}},{"k":"maine_welding_ecosystem_2","v":{"s":"Maine Welding Ecosystem"}},{"k":"ecosystem_mode","v":{"s":"Ecosystem mode"}},{"k":"pick_a_path_we_ll_walk_you_through_yea","v":{"s":"Pick a path. We'll walk you through year 0 → year 5+ with the realistic milestones, decisions, and pay (2026 Maine dollars). Real progressions drawn from Maine Dept of Labor + AWS workforce data + actual employer pay schedules."}},{"k":"pick_a_different_path","v":{"s":"← Pick a different path"}},{"k":"trade_offs","v":{"s":"⚖ Trade-offs"}},{"k":"reality_check_2","v":{"s":"💡 Reality check"}},{"k":"pay_ranges_are_2026_maine_averages_dra","v":{"s":"Pay ranges are 2026 Maine averages drawn from BIW/IAM contracts, UA Local 716 wage scales, Maine DOL occupational data, and AWS workforce reports. Real outcomes vary with shift, overtime, employer, and individual performance. Use this as a planning tool, not a guarantee."}},{"k":"overview_7","v":{"s":"⚕️ Overview"}},{"k":"fume_lungs","v":{"s":"☁ Fume + lungs"}},{"k":"arc_flash_eyes","v":{"s":"👁 Arc flash + eyes"}},{"k":"noise_hearing","v":{"s":"🔊 Noise + hearing"}},{"k":"burns_uv","v":{"s":"🔥 Burns + UV"}},{"k":"ergonomics_posture","v":{"s":"🦴 Ergonomics + posture"}},{"k":"electrical_confined","v":{"s":"⚡ Electrical + confined"}},{"k":"mental_workplace","v":{"s":"🧠 Mental + workplace"}},{"k":"fume_ventilation","v":{"s":"☁ Fume + ventilation"}},{"k":"vision_arc_protection","v":{"s":"👁 Vision + arc protection"}},{"k":"hearing_protection","v":{"s":"🔊 Hearing protection"}},{"k":"electrical_cable_safety","v":{"s":"⚡ Electrical + cable safety"}},{"k":"fire_hot_work","v":{"s":"🔥 Fire + hot-work"}},{"k":"ergonomics_breaks","v":{"s":"🦴 Ergonomics + breaks"}},{"k":"welding_is_more_hazardous_than_most_pe","v":{"s":"Welding is more hazardous than most people realize. The visible risks (arc flash, burns, fire) are real but actually less likely to disable you than the cumulative ones (fume inhalation, hearing loss, joint damage, vibration). PPE & Safety covered the basics. This module goes deeper into what welders actually get hurt by, and what protects you over a 40-year career."}},{"k":"the_chronic_risks_will_affect_career_l","v":{"s":"⚠ The chronic risks (will affect career-long welders)"}},{"k":"manganese_exposure","v":{"s":"Manganese exposure: "}},{"k":"neurological_parkinson_s_like_symptoms","v":{"s":"Neurological — Parkinson's-like symptoms after years of high exposure. From MIG/stick steel welding fume. NIOSH-cited."}},{"k":"hexavalent_chromium_cr_vi","v":{"s":"Hexavalent chromium (Cr-VI): "}},{"k":"carcinogenic_from_stainless_welding_fu","v":{"s":"Carcinogenic. From stainless welding fume (hot Cr → Cr-VI). OSHA-regulated since 2006."}},{"k":"welder_s_lung","v":{"s":"Welder's lung: "}},{"k":"long_term_fume_exposure_bronchitis_ast","v":{"s":"Long-term fume exposure → bronchitis, asthma, increased pneumonia risk."}},{"k":"noise_induced_hearing_loss","v":{"s":"Noise-induced hearing loss: "}},{"k":"welding_itself_is_moderate_85_95_db_gr","v":{"s":"Welding itself is moderate (85-95 dB); grinding (95-110 dB) + chipping + adjacent processes push past safe limits."}},{"k":"joint_back_damage","v":{"s":"Joint + back damage: "}},{"k":"awkward_positions_held_for_hours_most_","v":{"s":"Awkward positions held for hours. Most welders develop chronic back, shoulder, or knee issues by age 50."}},{"k":"what_protects_you_career_long","v":{"s":"✓ What protects you (career-long)"}},{"k":"1_always_use_local_exhaust_ventilation","v":{"s":"1. ALWAYS use local exhaust ventilation (LEV) on every weld, not just stainless. 2. Wear hearing protection on the shop floor, not just when grinding. 3. Take micro-breaks every 30-60 min for posture reset. 4. Get a doctor who knows occupational medicine; do annual spirometry. 5. Know your OSHA right to a clean shop + report violations without retaliation."}},{"k":"welding_fume_what_s_in_it_why_it_matte","v":{"s":"☁ Welding fume — what's in it + why it matters"}},{"k":"welding_fume_is_a_complex_chemical_mix","v":{"s":"Welding fume is a complex chemical mix. The base composition depends on (1) base metal, (2) electrode/filler, (3) coatings/contamination, (4) shielding gas. NIOSH + ACGIH track Permissible Exposure Limits (PELs) for each constituent."}},{"k":"major_fume_constituents_by_metal","v":{"s":"Major fume constituents by metal"}},{"k":"fume_contains","v":{"s":"Fume contains: "}},{"k":"ventilation_hierarchy_most_least_prote","v":{"s":"Ventilation hierarchy (most → least protective)"}},{"k":"1_source_capture_lev","v":{"s":"1. Source capture LEV: "}},{"k":"fume_extractor_with_capture_hood_withi","v":{"s":"Fume extractor with capture hood within 6-12\" of the arc. REMOVES fume before it enters breathing zone. Gold standard. Examples: Lincoln X-Tractor, Miller FILTAIR. ~$1,500-5,000."}},{"k":"2_on_gun_extraction","v":{"s":"2. On-gun extraction: "}},{"k":"mig_gun_with_built_in_extraction_port_","v":{"s":"MIG gun with built-in extraction port. Heavier gun + needs hose to extractor. Excellent — captures at source."}},{"k":"3_downdraft_table_backdraft_bench","v":{"s":"3. Downdraft table / backdraft bench: "}},{"k":"workbench_that_pulls_fume_downward_goo","v":{"s":"Workbench that pulls fume downward. Good for parts you can move to it."}},{"k":"4_general_room_ventilation","v":{"s":"4. General room ventilation: "}},{"k":"wall_fans_makeup_air_dilutes_fume_but_","v":{"s":"Wall fans, makeup air. Dilutes fume but doesn't prevent breathing-zone exposure. MINIMUM acceptable."}},{"k":"5_papr_powered_air_purifying_respirato","v":{"s":"5. PAPR (Powered Air-Purifying Respirator): "}},{"k":"helmet_with_hepa_filter_blower_wear_wh","v":{"s":"Helmet with HEPA filter blower. WEAR when extraction not feasible. ~$1,200-2,000."}},{"k":"6_half_mask_n95_p100","v":{"s":"6. Half-mask N95/P100: "}},{"k":"fit_tested_respirator_compatible_with_","v":{"s":"Fit-tested respirator. Compatible with most welding hoods. Last-resort when LEV not available; uncomfortable for long welds."}},{"k":"arc_flash_eye_protection_beyond_wear_a","v":{"s":"👁 Arc flash + eye protection — beyond \"wear a hood\""}},{"k":"welding_produces_uv_260_400_nm_visible","v":{"s":"Welding produces UV (260-400 nm) + visible (400-700 nm) + IR (700-2500 nm). All three damage eyes. Damage from UV is the most common — arc flash (photokeratitis) feels like sand in your eyes 6-12 hours later. Repeated exposure → cataracts + retinal damage."}},{"k":"welding_helmet_shade_selection","v":{"s":"Welding helmet shade selection"}},{"k":"light_farm_grade_work_small_repairs","v":{"s":"Light farm-grade work, small repairs."}},{"k":"most_common_general_structural_work","v":{"s":"Most common — general structural work."}},{"k":"heavy_plate","v":{"s":"Heavy plate."}},{"k":"light_auto_body_sheet_metal","v":{"s":"Light auto-body, sheet metal."}},{"k":"general_plate_work","v":{"s":"General plate work."}},{"k":"heavy_fabrication","v":{"s":"Heavy fabrication."}},{"k":"stainless_thin_gauge_ac_aluminum_start","v":{"s":"Stainless thin gauge, AC aluminum start."}},{"k":"most_common_tig_work","v":{"s":"Most common TIG work."}},{"k":"heavier_tig","v":{"s":"Heavier TIG."}},{"k":"plasma_arc_is_intense_bright_uv_heavy","v":{"s":"Plasma arc is INTENSE — bright + UV-heavy."}},{"k":"no_electric_arc_much_less_uv_visible_f","v":{"s":"No electric arc — much less UV; visible flame is what you're protecting against."}},{"k":"auto_darkening_helmets_features_that_m","v":{"s":"Auto-darkening helmets — features that matter"}},{"k":"switching_speed","v":{"s":"Switching speed: "}},{"k":"1_10_000_sec_or_faster_older_budget_he","v":{"s":"1/10,000 sec or faster. Older budget helmets at 1/3,600 sec — slow enough to flash your eyes. Spend the money."}},{"k":"viewing_area","v":{"s":"Viewing area: "}},{"k":"bigger_better_visibility_4_3_minimum_4","v":{"s":"Bigger = better visibility. 4×3\" minimum; 4×4\" or 5×4\" preferred for pipe + structural work."}},{"k":"sensor_count","v":{"s":"Sensor count: "}},{"k":"4_sensors_better_for_awkward_positions","v":{"s":"4 sensors = better for awkward positions. 2 sensors fine for bench work."}},{"k":"variable_shade","v":{"s":"Variable shade: "}},{"k":"shade_9_13_range_covers_all_common_pro","v":{"s":"Shade 9-13 range covers all common processes. Lower (5-8) range needed for grinding mode + light TIG."}},{"k":"solar_battery","v":{"s":"Solar + battery: "}},{"k":"solar_charges_battery_during_arc_time_","v":{"s":"Solar charges battery during arc time. Replaceable batteries (lithium) are better than non-replaceable."}},{"k":"true_color_vs_amber","v":{"s":"TRUE COLOR vs amber: "}},{"k":"true_color_1_1_1_1_or_1_1_1_2_optical_","v":{"s":"True-color (1/1/1/1 or 1/1/1/2 optical class) = much easier to see puddle + edges. Worth the upgrade."}},{"k":"brands_worth_considering","v":{"s":"Brands worth considering: "}},{"k":"lincoln_viking_3350_series_miller_digi","v":{"s":"Lincoln Viking 3350 series, Miller Digital Infinity / Elite, Optrel Crystal 2.0, Speedglas 9100XXi. ~$200-500."}},{"k":"adjacent_welder_protection","v":{"s":"💡 Adjacent-welder protection"}},{"k":"a_welder_30_ft_away_from_your_arc_stil","v":{"s":"A welder 30 ft away from your arc still gets flashed if line-of-sight. Use welding screens / curtains. If you walk into a shop where someone is welding without a screen, that's a real OSHA concern. Photokeratitis is acute + painful — most welders get it at least once early in their career."}},{"k":"noise_exposure_hearing_loss_silent_car","v":{"s":"🔊 Noise exposure + hearing loss — silent career-killer"}},{"k":"osha_action_level_is_85_dba_8_hr_time_","v":{"s":"OSHA action level is 85 dBA (8-hr time-weighted). Above that = hearing conservation program required. Welding shops routinely exceed this from adjacent processes — not necessarily from welding itself."}},{"k":"typical_shop_noise_levels","v":{"s":"Typical shop noise levels"}},{"k":"doubling_rule","v":{"s":"💡 Doubling rule"}},{"k":"every_5_db_increase_half_the_allowed_e","v":{"s":"Every 5 dB increase = HALF the allowed exposure time. 85 dB = 8 hr. 90 dB = 4 hr. 95 dB = 2 hr. 100 dB = 1 hr. 110 dB = 15 min. A welder who spends 2 hours grinding without protection has already exceeded daily safe exposure."}},{"k":"hearing_protection_options","v":{"s":"Hearing protection options: "}},{"k":"foam_plugs_nrr_28_33_cheap_comfortable","v":{"s":"Foam plugs (NRR 28-33, cheap, comfortable for long use). Moldable silicone (NRR 22-27, reusable). Earmuffs (NRR 20-30, easy on/off, can stack over plugs for combined ~35 NRR). Double protection (plugs + muffs) for grinding + air arc gouging."}},{"k":"burns_uv_thermal_injury","v":{"s":"🔥 Burns, UV, + thermal injury"}},{"k":"direct_burns_from_spatter_hot_metal_ar","v":{"s":"Direct burns from spatter + hot metal are the most common acute injury in welding. UV burns (\"sunburn under your shirt\") accumulate over a shift. Long-term skin cancer risk for unprotected welders is elevated 2-4× general population."}},{"k":"burn_categories_you_ll_encounter","v":{"s":"Burn categories you'll encounter"}},{"k":"fire_prevention","v":{"s":"💡 Fire prevention"}},{"k":"hot_work_permits_are_required_in_many_","v":{"s":"Hot work permits are required in many industrial settings. Sweep area before welding (especially flammable debris). Have ABC fire extinguisher within 35 ft. Hot work watch (someone else watching for fire) for 30 min minimum after stopping. Most shop fires start hours after welding from smoldering material — not during welding itself."}},{"k":"ergonomics_chronic_injury_the_slow_ero","v":{"s":"🦴 Ergonomics + chronic injury — the slow erosion"}},{"k":"most_welders_who_leave_the_trade_early","v":{"s":"Most welders who leave the trade early do so for orthopedic reasons, not acute injury. Welding positions (overhead, kneeling, bent over) accumulate damage over years. Smart welders protect themselves with deliberate ergonomics from day one."}},{"k":"highest_risk_welding_positions","v":{"s":"Highest-risk welding positions"}},{"k":"career_preserving_habits","v":{"s":"💡 Career-preserving habits"}},{"k":"daily_stretching_especially_neck_shoul","v":{"s":"• Daily stretching, especially neck + shoulders + lower back (5 min morning + 5 min evening)."}},{"k":"hydrate_welding_shops_are_hot_dehydrat","v":{"s":"• Hydrate (welding shops are hot; dehydration multiplies fatigue)."}},{"k":"use_the_positioner_turning_gear_every_","v":{"s":"• Use the positioner / turning gear EVERY time, not just when supervisor watching."}},{"k":"welder_s_stool_chair_for_any_bench_wor","v":{"s":"• Welder's stool / chair for any bench work that lasts >30 min."}},{"k":"anti_fatigue_mats_on_concrete_floor","v":{"s":"• Anti-fatigue mats on concrete floor."}},{"k":"off_season_weight_training_lower_back_","v":{"s":"• Off-season: weight training (lower back + core), yoga (flexibility), swimming (low-impact cardio)."}},{"k":"if_something_hurts_repeatedly_see_a_do","v":{"s":"• If something hurts repeatedly: see a doctor BEFORE it becomes a chronic issue."}},{"k":"electrical_safety_confined_space_the_r","v":{"s":"⚡ Electrical safety + confined space — the rarely-discussed risks"}},{"k":"welding_involves_significant_voltage_c","v":{"s":"Welding involves significant voltage + current. Most welders never get electrocuted, but the conditions for it exist constantly. Confined-space welding adds atmospheric hazards on top of everything else."}},{"k":"electrical_hazards_specific_to_welding","v":{"s":"Electrical hazards specific to welding"}},{"k":"welding_machines_have_60_100v_at_the_e","v":{"s":"Welding machines have 60-100V at the electrode when not arcing. Enough to shock you, especially if you're sweaty or standing on wet ground. Worse with AC than DC."}},{"k":"welding_outside_in_rain_on_damp_concre","v":{"s":"Welding outside in rain, on damp concrete, in tanks with residual moisture = significantly higher shock risk. Use rubber-soled boots, dry gloves, insulating mats."}},{"k":"if_multiple_welding_machines_share_gro","v":{"s":"If multiple welding machines share grounds + work clamps are crossed, current can flow through your body. Always work-clamp directly to the workpiece, not through ladders or staging."}},{"k":"worn_welding_leads_exposing_copper_dam","v":{"s":"Worn welding leads exposing copper, damaged work clamps, cracked electrode holders = direct contact path. Inspect every shift. Replace cables when insulation cracks (don't patch)."}},{"k":"never_strike_an_arc_on_a_compressed_ga","v":{"s":"NEVER strike an arc on a compressed gas cylinder (even empty). Even a brief arc strike can cause failure under pressure."}},{"k":"confined_space_welding_the_compounded_","v":{"s":"Confined-space welding — the compounded hazard"}},{"k":"tanks_ship_hulls_boilers_storage_vesse","v":{"s":"Tanks, ship hulls, boilers, storage vessels — enclosed spaces not designed for human occupancy with limited entry/exit."}},{"k":"osha_29_cfr_1910_146","v":{"s":"OSHA 29 CFR 1910.146: "}},{"k":"permit_required_confined_space_atmosph","v":{"s":"Permit-required confined space. Atmosphere testing (O2, LEL, toxics) before + during entry. Continuous ventilation. Rescue plan + standby attendant."}},{"k":"welding_specific_issues","v":{"s":"Welding-specific issues: "}},{"k":"shielding_gases_argon_co2_displace_oxy","v":{"s":"Shielding gases (argon, CO2) DISPLACE oxygen — silent killer. Heavier-than-air gases pool in low spots. Fume + ozone accumulate fast. Sparks can ignite trapped vapors."}},{"k":"1_confined_space_permit_2_atmospheric_","v":{"s":"(1) Confined space permit, (2) atmospheric testing, (3) forced ventilation, (4) standby attendant outside with rescue equipment, (5) communication (radio or signal rope), (6) NEVER alone, (7) machine ON/OFF accessible from outside."}},{"k":"most_confined_space_fatalities_happen_","v":{"s":"Most confined-space fatalities happen to the would-be rescuer who entered without PPE. If something goes wrong, signal — don't enter."}},{"k":"mental_health_workplace_dynamics_the_l","v":{"s":"🧠 Mental health, workplace dynamics, + the long career"}},{"k":"welding_shops_shipyards_construction_s","v":{"s":"Welding shops + shipyards + construction sites have distinct cultures. Some are welcoming + supportive; some are rough + exclusionary. Career-long welders need realistic strategies for the workplace itself, not just the welding."}},{"k":"mental_emotional_realities_of_trade_wo","v":{"s":"Mental + emotional realities of trade work"}},{"k":"production_welding_is_mentally_repetit","v":{"s":"Production welding is mentally repetitive. Brain wants stimulation. Common coping: podcasts on Bluetooth bone-conduction headphones (compatible with hood), planning side projects in head, social conversation during breaks. Some welders take additional certifications (CWI inspector path) to add intellectual variety."}},{"k":"trade_culture_historically_still_inclu","v":{"s":"Trade culture historically + still includes drinking, opioid use (often from work-related injuries → pain pills). Risk factor for transitioning off pain meds → alcohol → harder substances. Be aware. Many shops drug-test (especially safety-sensitive work like BIW). Maine SAMHSA helpline: 1-800-499-0027. Free, confidential."}},{"k":"construction_has_the_highest_male_suic","v":{"s":"Construction has the highest male suicide rate of any US industry (~53 per 100,000 — about 4× general rate). Combination of isolation, pain, substance use, masculinity culture that discourages help-seeking. AFSP + Construction Industry Alliance for Suicide Prevention have free resources. If you're struggling: 988 (Suicide & Crisis Lifeline) — text or call."}},{"k":"still_happens_especially_in_older_shop","v":{"s":"Still happens, especially in older shops. New welders often pranked (welded boots to floor, etc.) — usually meant good-naturedly but can cross into harassment. Know your rights: workplace harassment based on protected class (sex, race, disability, sexual orientation) is illegal. Maine Human Rights Commission: 207-624-6290."}},{"k":"maine_s_welding_workforce_is_5_7_women","v":{"s":"Maine's welding workforce is ~5-7% women (slowly growing). Some shops are excellent; some still have culture work to do. Resources: Women in Welding (national organization), Maine Tradeswomen Association, AWS Women in Welding Committee. LGBTQ+ welders: Pride At Work (AFL-CIO affiliate) has resources for union members."}},{"k":"many_welders_estimated_20_are_dyslexic","v":{"s":"Many welders (estimated 20%+) are dyslexic, ADHD, autistic, or have other neurodivergent profiles. Welding rewards visual-spatial thinking + focused attention + tactile skill — often a great fit. Reasonable accommodations are protected by ADA. Common requests: written instructions, quieter break area, predictable schedule."}},{"k":"welding_pays_well_but_can_demand_long_","v":{"s":"Welding pays well but can demand: long shifts, OT, travel for shutdown work. Many welders earn high incomes by working unsustainable hours. Plan early career around saving for shop equipment, education, real estate — assets that produce income beyond your body. The welders who do best long-term have a Plan B beyond their hands."}},{"k":"help_is_available_no_stigma_needed","v":{"s":"🟢 Help is available — no stigma needed"}},{"k":"988_suicide_crisis_lifeline_call_or_te","v":{"s":"988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline (call or text). SAMHSA National Helpline 1-800-662-4357 (substance use, free, 24/7). Maine 211 (any social service). Most union locals + employers have Employee Assistance Programs (EAPs) — free + confidential counseling, usually 4-8 sessions per issue. Use them. Welders need mental health resources as much as anyone — often more."}},{"k":"browse_topics_3","v":{"s":"⚕️ Browse topics"}},{"k":"how_safe_is_your_shop","v":{"s":"🛡 How safe is your shop?"}},{"k":"safety_health_deep_dive_2","v":{"s":"Safety + Health Deep-Dive"}},{"k":"safety_mode","v":{"s":"Safety mode"}},{"k":"walk_through_your_actual_shop_classroo","v":{"s":"Walk through your actual shop, classroom shop, or any shop you've worked in. Answer Yes / No / Not applicable for each. We'll score your environment overall + by category. Not a replacement for an OSHA-level audit, but a strong starting checklist."}},{"k":"reset_audit","v":{"s":"↺ Reset audit"}},{"k":"reporting_concerns","v":{"s":"Reporting concerns: "}},{"k":"osha_accepts_complaints_from_any_worke","v":{"s":"OSHA accepts complaints from any worker or member of the public. Online: osha.gov/workers/file-complaint. Anonymous reports protected by law. State plan in Maine: Maine Department of Labor (also handles state-level safety)."}},{"k":"overview_8","v":{"s":"📐 Overview"}},{"k":"shop_arithmetic","v":{"s":"🧮 Shop arithmetic"}},{"k":"measurement_tolerance","v":{"s":"📏 Measurement + tolerance"}},{"k":"geometry_angles","v":{"s":"📐 Geometry + angles"}},{"k":"blueprint_reading","v":{"s":"📋 Blueprint reading"}},{"k":"symbol_fluency","v":{"s":"🔣 Symbol fluency"}},{"k":"estimating_bidding","v":{"s":"🧾 Estimating + bidding"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_mean","v":{"s":"What does this symbol mean?"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_mean_2","v":{"s":"What does this symbol mean?"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_mean_3","v":{"s":"What does this symbol mean?"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_mean_4","v":{"s":"What does this symbol mean?"}},{"k":"a_1_16_root_opening_is_added_to_the_v_","v":{"s":"A 1/16 root opening is added to the V-groove. What changes?"}},{"k":"what_does_the_circle_at_the_junction_t","v":{"s":"What does the circle at the junction tell the welder?"}},{"k":"what_does_the_filled_black_flag_at_the","v":{"s":"What does the filled (black) flag at the junction tell the welder?"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_mean_5","v":{"s":"What does this symbol mean?"}},{"k":"what_does_the_tail_gtaw_wps_22_specify","v":{"s":"What does the tail \"GTAW · WPS-22\" specify?"}},{"k":"what_does_this_symbol_bevel_angle_only","v":{"s":"What does this symbol (bevel angle only on arrow plate) mean?"}},{"k":"arrow_joint_2","v":{"s":"arrow → joint"}},{"k":"a_skilled_welder_reads_prints_does_sho","v":{"s":"A skilled welder reads prints, does shop math, and works in fractions + decimals + angles without slowing down. Most failed welder qualifications + most rejected fabrications fail because of math/measurement errors, not bad welds."}},{"k":"this_module_covers_the_math_you_actual","v":{"s":"This module covers the math you actually use: fractions in fab work, measurement tools + tolerance, geometry for cutting + fitting, blueprint conventions, weld symbol fluency, and estimating jobs."}},{"k":"what_welders_actually_use","v":{"s":"🎯 What welders actually use"}},{"k":"fractions_1_16_3_32_1_8_3_16_1_4_etc_e","v":{"s":"• Fractions (1/16, 3/32, 1/8, 3/16, 1/4, etc.) — everywhere"}},{"k":"decimals_especially_for_tolerance_0_03","v":{"s":"• Decimals (especially for tolerance: ±0.030)"}},{"k":"right_triangles_pythagorean_theorem_fo","v":{"s":"• Right triangles + Pythagorean theorem (for cuts + miters)"}},{"k":"trigonometry_basics_sin_cos_tan_for_an","v":{"s":"• Trigonometry basics (sin/cos/tan for angled cuts)"}},{"k":"area_volume_for_filler_material_calcs","v":{"s":"• Area + volume (for filler material calcs)"}},{"k":"cost_per_pound_cost_per_hour_for_biddi","v":{"s":"• Cost-per-pound + cost-per-hour (for bidding)"}},{"k":"shop_arithmetic_the_daily_math","v":{"s":"🧮 Shop arithmetic — the daily math"}},{"k":"fraction_decimal_conversions_memorize","v":{"s":"Fraction-decimal conversions (memorize)"}},{"k":"practice_problems","v":{"s":"Practice problems"}},{"k":"measurement_tools_tolerance","v":{"s":"📏 Measurement tools + tolerance"}},{"k":"tools_when_to_use_what","v":{"s":"Tools — when to use what"}},{"k":"tolerance_what_means","v":{"s":"Tolerance — what ± means"}},{"k":"24_000_0_030_23_970_to_24_030_60_thou_","v":{"s":"\"24.000 ± 0.030\" = 23.970 to 24.030 (60 thou window)."}},{"k":"24_000_0_020_0_000_24_000_to_24_020_20","v":{"s":"\"24.000 +0.020/-0.000\" = 24.000 to 24.020 (20 thou window, only positive allowed)."}},{"k":"common_shop_tolerances","v":{"s":"Common shop tolerances: "}},{"k":"structural_plate_1_16_length_pipe_cuts","v":{"s":"Structural plate ±1/16\" length. Pipe cuts ±1/32\". Precision fab ±0.020\". Aerospace ±0.005\"."}},{"k":"why_it_matters_2","v":{"s":"Why it matters: "}},{"k":"if_you_make_5_parts_each_1_16_oversize","v":{"s":"If you make 5 parts each 1/16\" oversize, your final 20-ft assembly is 5/16\" off — past tolerance, scrap."}},{"k":"geometry_angles_for_welders","v":{"s":"📐 Geometry + angles for welders"}},{"k":"pythagorean_theorem_daily_use","v":{"s":"Pythagorean theorem — daily use"}},{"k":"a_b_c_where_c_is_the_hypotenuse","v":{"s":"a² + b² = c² where c is the hypotenuse."}},{"k":"diagonal_of_a_3_ft_4_ft_rectangle_3_4_","v":{"s":"Diagonal of a 3 ft × 4 ft rectangle = √(3² + 4²) = √25 = 5 ft. Square check: a frame that measures 3×4 should diagonal exactly 5 (or 5'-0\")."}},{"k":"3_4_5_rule","v":{"s":"3-4-5 rule: "}},{"k":"field_shortcut_any_3_4_5_triangle_is_s","v":{"s":"Field shortcut — any 3-4-5 triangle is square. Measure 3 along one leg + 4 along the other; the diagonal must be exactly 5. Bigger versions: 6-8-10, 9-12-15, 12-16-20."}},{"k":"triangle_types_you_cut","v":{"s":"Triangle types you cut"}},{"k":"right_triangle_90_2_others","v":{"s":"Right triangle (90° + 2 others)"}},{"k":"isoceles_2_equal_sides","v":{"s":"Isoceles (2 equal sides)"}},{"k":"equilateral_3_equal_sides_all_60","v":{"s":"Equilateral (3 equal sides, all 60°)"}},{"k":"trigonometry_when_you_need_it","v":{"s":"Trigonometry — when you need it"}},{"k":"soh_cah_toa_sin_opp_hyp_cos_adj_hyp_ta","v":{"s":"SOH-CAH-TOA: sin = opp/hyp, cos = adj/hyp, tan = opp/adj."}},{"k":"practical_example","v":{"s":"Practical example: "}},{"k":"you_need_a_brace_at_30_from_horizontal","v":{"s":"You need a brace at 30° from horizontal, climbing 12\" vertical. Hypotenuse (brace length) = 12 / sin(30°) = 12 / 0.5 = 24\". Horizontal run = 12 / tan(30°) = 12 / 0.577 = 20.78\"."}},{"k":"bevel_cut_for_30","v":{"s":"Bevel cut for 30°: "}},{"k":"a_1_2_thick_plate_beveled_at_30_from_v","v":{"s":"A 1/2\" thick plate beveled at 30° from vertical: bevel face = 0.5 / cos(30°) = 0.5 / 0.866 = 0.577\" — that's the long dimension of the beveled face."}},{"k":"reading_blueprints_drawings","v":{"s":"📋 Reading blueprints + drawings"}},{"k":"most_welding_mistakes_are_misread_prin","v":{"s":"Most welding mistakes are misread prints, not bad welding. Knowing the conventions saves time + scrap."}},{"k":"anatomy_of_a_blueprint","v":{"s":"Anatomy of a blueprint"}},{"k":"drawing_number_revision_letter_a_b_c_d","v":{"s":"Drawing number, revision letter (A, B, C...), date, drawn-by, checked-by, scale, material specification, weight. ALWAYS check revision letter — old prints have killed parts."}},{"k":"lists_each_revision_with_date_descript","v":{"s":"Lists each revision with date + description + initials. Compare to your drawing — if the part you're holding doesn't match the print revision, STOP."}},{"k":"lists_every_part_by_number_quantity_ma","v":{"s":"Lists every part by number + quantity + material spec. Cross-reference part labels in the drawing."}},{"k":"standard_arrangement_top_view_above_fr","v":{"s":"Standard arrangement: top view above front view, right side view to the right of front view. Third-angle projection (US standard) vs first-angle (European) — verify symbol in title block."}},{"k":"cutaway_showing_internal_features_sect","v":{"s":"Cutaway showing internal features. Section line on parent view, \"A-A\" or \"B-B\" labels identify which cutting plane."}},{"k":"in_inches_or_mm_for_international_dime","v":{"s":"In inches (or mm for international). Dimensions to important features, not to arbitrary points. \"TYP\" or \"TYPICAL\" = applies to multiple similar features."}},{"k":"default_tolerances_stated_near_title_b","v":{"s":"Default tolerances stated near title block (e.g., \"X.XX = ±0.030 unless noted\"). Specific tolerances override defaults."}},{"k":"per_aws_a2_4_symbol_direction_matters_","v":{"s":"Per AWS A2.4. Symbol direction matters — see Symbols module. Multiple welds get multiple symbols."}},{"k":"general_notes_near_title_block_apply_t","v":{"s":"GENERAL NOTES near title block apply to whole drawing. LOCAL notes near specific features apply only there. Read them ALL before starting."}},{"k":"pre_cut_checklist","v":{"s":"💡 Pre-cut checklist"}},{"k":"1_revision_letter_matches_bom_work_ord","v":{"s":"1. ✓ Revision letter matches BOM + work order"}},{"k":"2_material_spec_matches_stock_you_re_c","v":{"s":"2. ✓ Material spec matches stock you're cutting from"}},{"k":"3_all_dimensions_noted_no_missing_meas","v":{"s":"3. ✓ All dimensions noted (no missing measurements)"}},{"k":"4_tolerance_understood_default_or_spec","v":{"s":"4. ✓ Tolerance understood (default or specific)"}},{"k":"5_weld_symbols_clear_process_size_leng","v":{"s":"5. ✓ Weld symbols clear (process, size, length, location)"}},{"k":"6_cut_drill_operations_sequenced_some_","v":{"s":"6. ✓ Cut + drill operations sequenced (some must precede others)"}},{"k":"aws_weld_symbol_fluency","v":{"s":"🔣 AWS weld symbol fluency"}},{"k":"aws_a2_4_weld_symbols_are_a_language_o","v":{"s":"AWS A2.4 weld symbols are a language. Once fluent, you read a symbol in 2 seconds. Until then, every print is a translation exercise. (Symbols Reader module covers each symbol in interactive detail; this section drills the key reading rules.)"}},{"k":"the_4_anchor_concepts","v":{"s":"The 4 anchor concepts"}},{"k":"1_reference_line","v":{"s":"1. Reference line: "}},{"k":"horizontal_line_with_arrow_on_one_end_","v":{"s":"Horizontal line with arrow on one end. This is the structural element of every weld symbol."}},{"k":"2_arrow_side_vs_other_side","v":{"s":"2. Arrow side vs other side: "}},{"k":"symbols_below_the_line_arrow_side_the_","v":{"s":"Symbols BELOW the line = arrow side (the side the arrow points to). ABOVE the line = other side. BOTH = weld on both sides."}},{"k":"3_symbol_shape_weld_type","v":{"s":"3. Symbol shape = weld type: "}},{"k":"triangle_fillet_square_square_groove_v","v":{"s":"Triangle = fillet, square = square groove, \"V\" = V groove, half-V = bevel groove, U = U groove, circle = plug/slot."}},{"k":"4_numbers_tail","v":{"s":"4. Numbers + tail: "}},{"k":"number_left_of_symbol_size_leg_length_","v":{"s":"Number left of symbol = size (leg length for fillet, depth for groove). Number right = length / pitch (for intermittent). Tail = specification reference (process, procedure number)."}},{"k":"common_compound_symbols","v":{"s":"Common compound symbols"}},{"k":"estimating_bidding_work","v":{"s":"🧾 Estimating + bidding work"}},{"k":"whether_bidding_a_job_for_a_customer_o","v":{"s":"Whether bidding a job for a customer or estimating internal hours for a supervisor, welders need to translate prints into time + materials. Bad estimates = lost money OR lost bids."}},{"k":"the_estimating_formula","v":{"s":"The estimating formula"}},{"k":"total_material_markup_labor_hr_rate_ov","v":{"s":"Total = (Material × markup) + (Labor hr × rate) + Overhead + Profit"}},{"k":"steel_filler_gas_consumables_add_15_25","v":{"s":"Steel + filler + gas + consumables. Add 15-25% markup for handling + waste."}},{"k":"labor_hours","v":{"s":"Labor hours: "}},{"k":"time_per_foot_of_weld_number_of_feet_s","v":{"s":"Time-per-foot of weld × number of feet + setup time + cleanup. Include grinding, fitting, tacking."}},{"k":"shop_rate","v":{"s":"Shop rate: "}},{"k":"65_95_hr_maine_welding_shop_2026_inclu","v":{"s":"$65-95/hr (Maine welding shop, 2026). Includes machinery cost, insurance, utilities, taxes."}},{"k":"already_in_shop_rate_but_big_jobs_may_","v":{"s":"Already in shop rate but big jobs may need separate line for travel, permits, special equipment rental."}},{"k":"10_25_on_top_of_cost_basis_the_this_is","v":{"s":"10-25% on top of cost basis. The \"this is worth doing\" number."}},{"k":"time_estimates_by_process_approximate_","v":{"s":"Time estimates by process (approximate, plate work)"}},{"k":"the_estimating_mistakes","v":{"s":"💡 The estimating mistakes"}},{"k":"forgetting_setup_cleanup_often_20_40_o","v":{"s":"Forgetting setup/cleanup (often 20-40% of total time). Forgetting fitting + tacking before welding starts. Underestimating multi-pass welds (root + fill + cap = 3+ passes). Not factoring inspection + repair time. Not pricing in consumables (especially gas cylinder rental + delivery)."}},{"k":"browse_topics_4","v":{"s":"📐 Browse topics"}},{"k":"symbol_match_quiz","v":{"s":"🔣 Symbol Match quiz"}},{"k":"welding_math_blueprints_2","v":{"s":"Welding Math + Blueprints"}},{"k":"math_blueprint_mode","v":{"s":"Math + Blueprint mode"}},{"k":"choose_the_meaning","v":{"s":"Choose the meaning"}},{"k":"correct_2","v":{"s":"✓ correct"}},{"k":"your_pick_2","v":{"s":"✗ your pick"}},{"k":"restart_quiz","v":{"s":"↺ Restart quiz"}},{"k":"marcus_t","v":{"s":"Marcus T."}},{"k":"sarah_k","v":{"s":"Sarah K."}},{"k":"diego_r","v":{"s":"Diego R."}},{"k":"patricia_l","v":{"s":"Patricia L."}},{"k":"jamal_f","v":{"s":"Jamal F."}},{"k":"rebecca_w","v":{"s":"Rebecca W."}},{"k":"kevin_s","v":{"s":"Kevin S."}},{"k":"tyrone_j","v":{"s":"Tyrone J."}},{"k":"sophie_b","v":{"s":"Sophie B."}},{"k":"mike_h","v":{"s":"Mike H."}},{"k":"cassidy_m","v":{"s":"Cassidy M."}},{"k":"lin_c","v":{"s":"Lin C."}},{"k":"rosa_p","v":{"s":"Rosa P."}},{"k":"daniel_o","v":{"s":"Daniel O."}},{"k":"steady_work_great_benefits","v":{"s":"🛡 Steady work, great benefits"}},{"k":"pension_healthcare_predictable_schedul","v":{"s":"Pension, healthcare, predictable schedule"}},{"k":"hands_on_creative_work_no_boss","v":{"s":"🎨 Hands-on creative work, no boss"}},{"k":"build_things_set_your_own_hours_varied","v":{"s":"Build things, set your own hours, varied projects"}},{"k":"highest_pay_even_if_i_travel","v":{"s":"💰 Highest pay even if I travel"}},{"k":"top_dollar_willing_to_be_away_from_hom","v":{"s":"Top dollar, willing to be away from home for it"}},{"k":"help_or_teach_others","v":{"s":"🤝 Help or teach others"}},{"k":"inspector_teacher_mentor_roles","v":{"s":"Inspector, teacher, mentor roles"}},{"k":"start_working_asap_after_hs","v":{"s":"⚡ Start working ASAP after HS"}},{"k":"cte_direct_hire_training_biw_small_sho","v":{"s":"CTE + direct-hire training (BIW, small shops)"}},{"k":"2_years_of_community_college","v":{"s":"🎓 ~2 years of community college"}},{"k":"emcc_smcc_county_college_welding_progr","v":{"s":"EMCC, SMCC, county college welding programs"}},{"k":"paid_apprenticeship_3_5_yrs","v":{"s":"🤝 Paid apprenticeship (3-5 yrs)"}},{"k":"union_ua_pipefitter_boilermaker_iam","v":{"s":"Union: UA pipefitter, Boilermaker, IAM"}},{"k":"4_year_bachelor_s_degree","v":{"s":"📚 4-year bachelor's degree"}},{"k":"welding_engineering_art_sculpture_desi","v":{"s":"Welding engineering, art/sculpture, design-side"}},{"k":"big_shipyard_or_factory","v":{"s":"🏭 Big shipyard or factory"}},{"k":"biw_pratt_whitney_cianbro_modules","v":{"s":"BIW, Pratt & Whitney, Cianbro modules"}},{"k":"small_shop_or_boatyard","v":{"s":"🛠 Small shop or boatyard"}},{"k":"hodgdon_mid_coast_boat_builders_family","v":{"s":"Hodgdon, mid-coast boat builders, family fab shops"}},{"k":"my_own_truck_my_own_customers","v":{"s":"🚐 My own truck, my own customers"}},{"k":"mobile_welding_custom_builds_food_truc","v":{"s":"Mobile welding, custom builds, food trucks"}},{"k":"outdoor_varied_locations","v":{"s":"🌲 Outdoor, varied locations"}},{"k":"bridges_pipelines_traveling_shutdown_w","v":{"s":"Bridges, pipelines, traveling shutdown work"}},{"k":"browse_all_profiles","v":{"s":"📖 Browse all profiles"}},{"k":"find_your_fit","v":{"s":"🎯 Find Your Fit"}},{"k":"welder_career_stories_2","v":{"s":"Welder Career Stories"}},{"k":"real_welders_real_paths_real_wages_the","v":{"s":"Real welders. Real paths. Real wages. These profiles are composites drawn from documented Maine + New England welding career patterns + AWS workforce reports + Maine Department of Labor occupational data. Each one represents a viable path — not the only path, but proven ones. Names are pseudonyms; situations are representative."}},{"k":"career_stories_mode","v":{"s":"Career Stories mode"}},{"k":"answer_3_quick_questions_we_ll_show_yo","v":{"s":"Answer 3 quick questions. We'll show you the welders whose paths fit your answers best — not the only fits, just the closest matches in this set of 14."}},{"k":"your_top_matches","v":{"s":"🎯 Your top matches"}},{"k":"start_over","v":{"s":"↺ Start over"}},{"k":"no_matches_try_different_answers","v":{"s":"No matches — try different answers."}},{"k":"read_full_profile","v":{"s":"Read full profile →"}},{"k":"reality_check_3","v":{"s":"Reality check: "}},{"k":"these_are_starting_points_not_destinat","v":{"s":"These are starting points, not destinations. Most welders end up somewhere none of these 14 quite predicted. The quiz tells you which paths are worth researching first — not which one you must take."}},{"k":"path","v":{"s":"Path"}},{"k":"in_their_words","v":{"s":"In their words"}},{"k":"advice_for_students","v":{"s":"💡 Advice for students"}},{"k":"cold_weld_insufficient_fusion","v":{"s":"🧊 Cold weld (insufficient fusion)"}},{"k":"not_enough_heat_cold_lap_defects_poor_","v":{"s":"Not enough heat. Cold lap defects, poor penetration."}},{"k":"optimal_heat_input","v":{"s":"🟢 Optimal heat input"}},{"k":"good_penetration_fusion_minimal_distor","v":{"s":"Good penetration, fusion, minimal distortion."}},{"k":"excessive_heat","v":{"s":"🟠 Excessive heat"}},{"k":"high_distortion_wide_haz_possible_unde","v":{"s":"High distortion, wide HAZ, possible undercut."}},{"k":"burn_through_risk","v":{"s":"🔥 Burn-through risk"}},{"k":"excessive_base_metal_melts_through","v":{"s":"Excessive — base metal melts through."}},{"k":"hypothesis_how_does_travel_speed_compe","v":{"s":"Hypothesis: How does travel speed compensate for amperage?"}},{"k":"explain_how_amperage_voltage_and_speed","v":{"s":"Explain how amperage, voltage, and speed compose heat input."}},{"k":"tilt_camera_up","v":{"s":"Tilt camera up"}},{"k":"tilt_camera_down","v":{"s":"Tilt camera down"}},{"k":"touch_swipe_sideways_tilt_buttons","v":{"s":"👆 Touch: swipe sideways to orbit, ▲▼ to tilt"}},{"k":"el_carbon","v":{"s":"Carbon"}},{"k":"el_manganese","v":{"s":"Manganese"}},{"k":"el_chromium","v":{"s":"Chromium"}},{"k":"el_molybdenum","v":{"s":"Molybdenum"}},{"k":"el_vanadium","v":{"s":"Vanadium"}},{"k":"el_nickel","v":{"s":"Nickel"}},{"k":"el_copper","v":{"s":"Copper"}},{"k":"ce_calculator","v":{"s":"🧮 Work it out — enter a composition"}},{"k":"weight_percent","v":{"s":"weight percent"}},{"k":"ce_caveat","v":{"s":"CE is one input, not the whole decision. Section thickness, joint restraint and the hydrogen level of your consumables all move the preheat requirement. Check the governing code (AWS D1.1 Annex) before setting a procedure."}},{"k":"butt_img","v":{"s":"Butt joint: two plates meeting edge to edge in the same plane, welded along the seam"}},{"k":"ch1_img","v":{"s":"Weld symbol: a horizontal reference line with a leader angling down to an arrowhead. A triangle sits below the reference line, with the text 1/4 to its left"}},{"k":"ch2_img","v":{"s":"Weld symbol: a horizontal reference line with a leader angling down to an arrowhead. An open circle marks the point where they meet and a small flag flies from it. A triangle sits below the reference line, with the text 3/16 to its left"}},{"k":"ch3_img","v":{"s":"Weld symbol: a horizontal reference line with a leader angling down to an arrowhead. A V opens below the reference line and a mirrored V opens above it, each marked 3/8"}},{"k":"ch4_img","v":{"s":"Weld symbol: a horizontal reference line with a leader angling down to an arrowhead and an open circle where they meet. Below the line is a V with a curve bulging downward beneath it, marked 1/2. The right end forks into a tail carrying the text GTAW-6G"}},{"k":"corner_img","v":{"s":"Corner joint: two plates meeting at an outside corner, welded along the outer seam"}},{"k":"edge_img","v":{"s":"Edge joint: two plates face to face with edges aligned, welded along the top edge"}},{"k":"lap_img","v":{"s":"Lap joint: one plate overlapping another, welded along the overlapping edge"}},{"k":"tee_img","v":{"s":"T-joint: a vertical plate meeting a horizontal plate at a right angle, welded where they meet"}}],"id":598}},{"k":"wheelandfire","v":{"o":[{"k":"sr_shaping_is_unavailable_after_the_clay_has_dried","v":{"s":"Shaping is unavailable after the clay has dried."}},{"k":"sr_nothing_to_undo","v":{"s":"Nothing to undo."}},{"k":"sr_pottery_action_undone","v":{"s":"Pottery action undone."}},{"k":"sr_nothing_to_redo","v":{"s":"Nothing to redo."}},{"k":"sr_pottery_action_redone","v":{"s":"Pottery action redone."}},{"k":"sr_no_earlier_safe_checkpoint_is_available_load_fres","v":{"s":"No earlier safe checkpoint is available. Load fresh clay or a saved journal record."}},{"k":"sr_restored_the_last_checkpoint_before_modeled_failu","v":{"s":"Restored the last checkpoint before modeled failure. Change one input before repeating the trial."}},{"k":"sr_focused_the_highest_risk_ring_in_shape","v":{"s":"Focused the highest-risk ring in Shape."}},{"k":"sr_loaded_a_safer_comparative_touch_setup_