verify_alignment_and_p","v":{"s":"Capture corridor, 15 meters. Verify alignment and positive stopping margin."}},{"k":"sr_controlled_hatch_challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Controlled hatch challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_controlled_handrail_stop_challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Controlled handrail stop challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_cargo_pouch_caught_carry_it_to_the_marked_unity_r","v":{"s":"Cargo pouch caught. Carry it to the marked Unity restraint while braced."}},{"k":"sr_cargo_pouch_secured_loose_object_management_chall","v":{"s":"Cargo pouch secured. Loose-object management challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_loose_cargo_avoided_navigation_challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Loose cargo avoided. Navigation challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_transfer_tether_clipped_center_both_body_and_bag","v":{"s":"Transfer tether clipped. Center both body and bag through the Harmony-Destiny hatch."}},{"k":"sr_bulky_transfer_cancelled_before_a_cross_room_trai","v":{"s":"Bulky transfer cancelled before a cross-room training move. Bag returned to Harmony staging; contact and completion counts did not change."}},{"k":"sr_bulky_hatch_transfer_complete_transfer_bag_docked","v":{"s":"Bulky hatch transfer complete. Transfer bag docked in Destiny."}},{"k":"sr_reaction_torque_detected_recover_catch_the_nearby","v":{"s":"Reaction torque detected. Recover, catch the nearby rail, then retry the filter service."}},{"k":"sr_harmony_airflow_blocked_a_loose_item_is_at_the_ai","v":{"s":"Harmony airflow blocked. A loose item is at the air return."}},{"k":"sr_cabin_item_caught_carry_it_to_its_matching_harmon","v":{"s":"Cabin item caught. Carry it to its matching Harmony restraint and brace on a rail."}},{"k":"sr_harmony_cabin_stow_complete_all_three_items_restr","v":{"s":"Harmony cabin stow complete. All three items restrained and airflow clear."}},{"k":"sr_orientation_recovery_challenge_complete","v":{"s":"Orientation recovery challenge complete."}},{"k":"sr_five_module_free_flight_route_complete","v":{"s":"Five-module free-flight route complete."}},{"k":"sr_accessible_interior_diagram_opened_at_the_saved_f","v":{"s":"Accessible interior diagram opened at the saved flight room."}},{"k":"sr_interactive_3_d_free_flight_view_restored_at_the","v":{"s":"Interactive 3-D free-flight view restored at the saved flight room."}},{"k":"sr_free_flight_route_restarted_in_harmony_crew_jobs","v":{"s":"Free-flight route restarted in Harmony. Crew jobs were kept."}},{"k":"sr_3_d_cabin_stow_is_active_use_x_to_catch_or_secure","v":{"s":"3-D cabin stow is active. Use X to catch or secure the nearest item."}},{"k":"a11y_thruster_controls","v":{"s":"Thruster controls"}},{"k":"a11y_handrail_route","v":{"s":"Handrail route"}},{"k":"a11y_interactive_3_d_interior_of_the_international_s","v":{"s":"Interactive 3-D interior of the International Space Station. Training push controls add velocity for exploration; actual crew translate by pushing and pulling handrails. White gloved forearms and task props show your current body task: tucked, holding a rail, carrying cargo, using the torque tool, holding the water injector, or framing Earth with a handheld camera. In-world destination signs mark the forward, port, and nadir branches; labeled deck and overhead surfaces plus a numeric roll readout preserve orientation. Follow Harmony through Destiny and Unity, turn port into Tranquility, then move nadir into Cupola. Linear and rotational momentum continue until you counter-push or catch a nearby rail. In Harmony, use X to catch the nearest loose sleeping bag, tablet, or washcloth within zero point six five meters and below zero point two meters per second relative speed, then rail-brace at its matching restraint and press X again. Airflow risk is shown with color, shape, and text. Match a loose pouch velocity before catching and securing it. At the Destiny plant glovebox, stabilize, face the lower wick port, then hold V and release between 2.7 and 3.3 milliliters. At the Tranquility filter, brace on a rail before applying torque. In Cupola, approach the camera control, hold the nearby rail, center the selected Earth target, then hold P for a one point two second training frame lock."}},{"k":"a11y_virtual_navigation_push_controls","v":{"s":"Virtual navigation push controls"}},{"k":"a11y_free_flight_safety_assists","v":{"s":"Free-flight safety assists"}},{"k":"a11y_choose_a_bulky_hatch_transfer_method","v":{"s":"Choose a bulky hatch-transfer method"}},{"k":"a11y_life_support_telemetry_channels","v":{"s":"Life-support telemetry channels"}},{"k":"a11y_maintenance_actions","v":{"s":"Maintenance actions"}},{"k":"a11y_cabin_items_to_secure","v":{"s":"Cabin items to secure"}},{"k":"a11y_earth_observation_targets","v":{"s":"Earth observation targets"}},{"k":"a11y_crew_shift_progress","v":{"s":"Crew shift progress"}},{"k":"a11y_learning_guidance_level","v":{"s":"Learning guidance level"}},{"k":"a11y_interior_view_mode","v":{"s":"Interior view mode"}},{"k":"a11y_highlight_a_station_system_flow","v":{"s":"Highlight a station system flow"}},{"k":"a11y_mission_scenario_presets","v":{"s":"Mission scenario presets"}},{"k":"a11y_mission_replay_events","v":{"s":"Mission replay events"}},{"k":"a11y_emergency_scenario","v":{"s":"Emergency scenario"}},{"k":"a11y_emergency_actions","v":{"s":"Emergency actions"}},{"k":"a11y_inspect_a_rendezvous_hold_point","v":{"s":"Inspect a rendezvous hold point"}},{"k":"a11y_station_flight_rules","v":{"s":"Station flight rules"}},{"k":"a11y_operations_simulation","v":{"s":"Operations simulation"}},{"k":"a11y_3d_station_view_controls","v":{"s":"3D station view controls"}},{"k":"a11y_station_modules","v":{"s":"Station modules"}},{"k":"a11y_daily_schedule","v":{"s":"Daily schedule"}},{"k":"a11y_select_solar_activity_for_the_drag_model","v":{"s":"Select solar activity for the drag model"}},{"k":"a11y_select_a_reboost_plan","v":{"s":"Select a reboost plan"}},{"k":"a11y_station_systems","v":{"s":"Station systems"}},{"k":"a11y_question_for_mission_control","v":{"s":"Question for Mission Control"}},{"k":"a11y_inclination_presets","v":{"s":"Inclination presets"}},{"k":"a11y_questions_answered","v":{"s":"Questions answered"}},{"k":"initializing","v":{"s":"🛰️ Docking with the station…"}},{"k":"dock_win_toast","v":{"s":"Soft capture confirmed — docking complete!"}},{"k":"dock_win","v":{"s":"Soft capture! Contact at {speed} m/s — gentle enough for the docking latches."}},{"k":"dock_bonk","v":{"s":"Contact too fast or off-axis ({speed} m/s). Real vehicles would abort long before this — try arriving under 0.6 m/s, centered."}},{"k":"dock_drift","v":{"s":"Drifted out of the approach zone. Notice HOW you drifted — in orbit, thrusting forward raises you and slows you."}},{"k":"dock_start_sr","v":{"s":"New approach started: 190 meters from the docking port, closing at 1 meter per second."}},{"k":"eva_o2_out","v":{"s":"Suit consumables exhausted — EVA aborted. Real spacewalks budget every breath; try a cleaner run."}},{"k":"eva_win_toast","v":{"s":"Pump module secured — EVA objective complete!"}},{"k":"scene_day","v":{"s":"☀️ Daylit Pacific"}},{"k":"scene_night","v":{"s":"🌃 Night — city lights"}},{"k":"scene_aurora","v":{"s":"🟢 Aurora pass"}},{"k":"inside_cupola","v":{"s":"Inside the Cupola"}},{"k":"cupola_aria","v":{"s":"View from inside the Cupola dome: six angled windows around a large round center window, looking down at Earth. Scene: "}},{"k":"shutters_closed","v":{"s":"Shutters closed"}},{"k":"close_shutters","v":{"s":"Close debris shutters"}},{"k":"shutter_note","v":{"s":"Every window has an external shutter, closed whenever the Cupola is unattended — a micrometeoroid pit in this glass would be very bad news."}},{"k":"spot_bag","v":{"s":"Sleeping bag"}},{"k":"spot_laptop","v":{"s":"Laptop"}},{"k":"spot_vent","v":{"s":"Air vent"}},{"k":"spot_gear","v":{"s":"Personal kit"}},{"k":"spot_photos","v":{"s":"Family photos"}},{"k":"inside_cabin","v":{"s":"Inside a crew sleep cabin (Harmony)"}},{"k":"cabin_aria","v":{"s":"Cutaway of a crew sleep cabin, about the size of a phone booth. Tap the hotspot buttons to inspect items."}},{"k":"missions_intro","v":{"s":"Two hands-on missions. Both are simplified but honest: the docking sim runs the real relative-motion equations (with orbit effects sped up so you can feel them), and the spacewalk enforces the real two-tether safety rule."}},{"k":"mission_dock","v":{"s":"Mission 1 — Dock the cargo capsule"}},{"k":"dock_help","v":{"s":"Fly the capsule (left side) onto the glowing port. Arrow keys / WASD or hold a thruster button with Space or Enter: → thrusts forward, ← brakes, ↑/↓ steer radially. Dock slower than 0.6 m/s, inside the corridor. With ORBITAL PHYSICS ON, watch the counter-intuitive part: thrusting forward also pushes you upward off the approach line — orbits are not roads."}},{"k":"dock_aria","v":{"s":"Docking simulator. Use arrow keys or W A S D to thrust. Goal: reach the docking port slower than 0.6 meters per second."}},{"k":"dock_retry","v":{"s":"New approach"}},{"k":"dock_real_on","v":{"s":"Orbital physics ON"}},{"k":"dock_real_off","v":{"s":"Video-game mode (physics OFF)"}},{"k":"mission_report","v":{"s":"Mission report: "}},{"k":"dock_science","v":{"s":"🔬 The science: relative motion near an orbiting target follows the Clohessy-Wiltshire equations — thrust toward the target and you drift off-axis, because raising your speed raises your orbit. Real approaches are therefore slow, computed, and rehearsed. Try predicting your drift BEFORE toggling physics on: that is the whole discipline of rendezvous in one toggle."}},{"k":"mission_eva","v":{"s":"Mission 2 — Spacewalk: replace the failed pump"}},{"k":"eva_help","v":{"s":"An ammonia pump on the truss has failed. Translate hand-over-hand from the Quest airlock to the worksite and torque 4 bolts — while respecting the real rule that keeps astronauts alive: at least one tether clipped AT ALL TIMES. Clip the free tether ahead, then move. Every action costs suit consumables."}},{"k":"eva_start","v":{"s":"Open the hatch"}},{"k":"eva_o2","v":{"s":"Suit consumables: "}},{"k":"eva_bolts","v":{"s":"bolts"}},{"k":"eva_clip","v":{"s":"Clip tether "}},{"k":"eva_ahead","v":{"s":" ahead"}},{"k":"eva_translate","v":{"s":"Translate forward"}},{"k":"eva_torque","v":{"s":"Torque bolt"}},{"k":"eva_restart","v":{"s":"Restart EVA"}},{"k":"eva_win","v":{"s":"✅ Pump secured with {pct}% consumables to spare. Real EVAs run 6-8 hours with the same discipline you just practiced: clip, verify, move, repeat — hundreds of times."}},{"k":"eva_science","v":{"s":"🔬 The science: nothing about a spacewalk is casual. Astronauts pre-breathe pure O₂ for hours (decompression safety), gloves stiffen every grip like squeezing a tennis ball for 7 hours, and the two-tether rule exists because in orbit a slip does not mean falling — it means becoming a slowly departing satellite."}},{"k":"interior_kicker","v":{"s":"Your crew shift"}},{"k":"interior_title","v":{"s":"Float inside. Work like an astronaut."}},{"k":"interior_intro","v":{"s":"Move through five real station spaces. Inspect what is around you, make a crew decision, and learn the science from the result."}},{"k":"interior_route","v":{"s":"Interior station route"}},{"k":"tab_interior","v":{"s":"Inside: Crew Shift"}},{"k":"tab_operations","v":{"s":"Mission Operations"}},{"k":"tab_map","v":{"s":"3-D Station"}},{"k":"tab_day","v":{"s":"A Day Aboard"}},{"k":"tab_systems","v":{"s":"Systems & Challenges"}},{"k":"tab_orbit","v":{"s":"Orbit Lab"}},{"k":"tab_missions","v":{"s":"Missions"}},{"k":"tab_history","v":{"s":"History & Future"}},{"k":"tab_quiz","v":{"s":"Quiz"}},{"k":"map_intro","v":{"s":"A schematic (not to scale) 3-D map of the real station. Drag or use the arrow keys to rotate, plus and minus to zoom, and Home to return to the overview. The module buttons provide an equivalent non-canvas inspection path."}},{"k":"canvas_aria","v":{"s":"Interactive 3-D model of the International Space Station."}},{"k":"fact","v":{"s":"Worth knowing: "}},{"k":"eng","v":{"s":"Engineering spotlight: "}},{"k":"fast_facts","v":{"s":"📊 Fast facts"}},{"k":"day_intro","v":{"s":"The station runs on GMT — a compromise between Houston and Moscow. Step through a typical workday. Every line has a WHY: nothing aboard is done a certain way by accident."}},{"k":"why","v":{"s":"Why: "}},{"k":"earlier","v":{"s":"Earlier"}},{"k":"later","v":{"s":"Later"}},{"k":"day_notes","v":{"s":"🌍 The strangest parts of normal life"}},{"k":"note_sunrise","v":{"s":"16 sunrises and sunsets every day — crews use clocks and window shades, not the sky, to know when to sleep."}},{"k":"note_taste","v":{"s":"Food tastes bland aboard: fluid shifts stuff up sinuses like a permanent head cold, so hot sauce and shrimp cocktail are prized."}},{"k":"note_height","v":{"s":"Spines stretch without gravity — astronauts return up to ~3 cm taller (temporarily, and often with backaches)."}},{"k":"note_laundry","v":{"s":"There is no laundry. Clothes are worn for days, then packed into cargo ships that burn up on reentry as shooting stars."}},{"k":"sys_intro","v":{"s":"The station is a closed-loop machine that must make its own air, recycle its own water, shed its own heat, and hold its own orientation — forever, with no hardware store. Each system below is an engineering-design case study (NGSS MS-ETS1)."}},{"k":"numbers","v":{"s":"By the numbers: "}},{"k":"design_challenge","v":{"s":"Design challenge: "}},{"k":"ask_mc","v":{"s":"🎧 Ask Mission Control"}},{"k":"ask_ph","v":{"s":"How do they fix a leak? What happens in a fire? Can you see the station from my town?"}},{"k":"ask_wait","v":{"s":"Standing by…"}},{"k":"ask_go","v":{"s":"📡 Call Mission Control"}},{"k":"orbit_intro","v":{"s":"Being in orbit is not about being high — it is about being FAST. The station falls around Earth continuously; the crew floats because station and astronaut fall together. Slide the altitude and watch real physics (v = √(GM/r)) respond."}},{"k":"orbit_lab","v":{"s":"🧮 Orbit designer"}},{"k":"altitude","v":{"s":"Orbital altitude"}},{"k":"tradeoff","v":{"s":"Trade-off report: "}},{"k":"orbit_note","v":{"s":"Notice the counter-intuitive part: LOWER orbits are FASTER. To catch up with something ahead of you in orbit, you briefly slow down and drop lower. Orbital mechanics breaks driving intuition — which is why dockings are computed, not eyeballed."}},{"k":"why_400","v":{"s":"🎯 Why ~400 km?"}},{"k":"why1","v":{"s":"Low enough for cargo and crew rockets to carry useful mass, and below the worst radiation zones."}},{"k":"why2","v":{"s":"High enough that drag only costs ~50-100 m/day — manageable with periodic reboosts."}},{"k":"why3","v":{"s":"The 51.6° orbit tilt was chosen so Russian launches from Baikonur can reach it — the orbit itself encodes the partnership."}},{"k":"timeline","v":{"s":"📜 Assembly to retirement"}},{"k":"legacy","v":{"s":"🌍 Why it matters"}},{"k":"legacy_body","v":{"s":"The station is the most expensive object ever built (well over $100 billion) and arguably the most ambitious peacetime engineering partnership in history: 15 nations keeping hardware and crews alive together for a quarter century, through every political storm on the ground below. Its deepest lesson is not any single experiment — it is proof that humans can build and operate a permanent home off Earth. Everything learned aboard, from water recycling to bone loss, is a down payment on the Moon and Mars."}},{"k":"spot_note","v":{"s":"💡 You can SEE the station: it is the third-brightest object in the sky after the Sun and Moon. NASA’s \"Spot the Station\" service lists pass times for your town — it looks like a brilliant star crossing the sky in a few minutes, moving too smoothly to be a plane."}},{"k":"quiz_done","v":{"s":"🏁 Debrief"}},{"k":"quiz_great","v":{"s":"Flight-controller material. Quest objective complete!"}},{"k":"quiz_retry","v":{"s":"Every controller trains on repetitions — revisit the tabs and fly it again."}},{"k":"quiz_again","v":{"s":"🔁 Run it again"}},{"k":"quiz_toast","v":{"s":"Station quiz aced: "}},{"k":"quiz_finish","v":{"s":"Finish →"}},{"k":"quiz_next","v":{"s":"Next →"}},{"k":"tab_selected","v":{"s":"section opened."}},{"k":"hero_kicker","v":{"s":"Expedition learning console / live simulation"}},{"k":"title","v":{"s":"Space Station — a permanent home off Earth"}},{"k":"subtitle","v":{"s":"The International Space Station: 420 tonnes of engineering falling around the planet at 7.66 km/s, continuously inhabited for over 25 years."}},{"k":"orbit_status","v":{"s":"Current station reference data"}},{"k":"sections","v":{"s":"Space Station sections"}},{"k":"earth_note","v":{"s":"About the planet below: the coastlines are real, drawn from the public-domain Natural Earth dataset, and the city lights sit at the real coordinates of major metropolitan areas. The surface colouring, sea ice and cloud bands are schematic — a picture of where deserts, storm tracks and ice tend to be, not measured data. The station is drawn hanging above the globe rather than at a real point on its path; for where it actually flies, open Orbit Lab and look at the ground track."}},{"k":"inclination","v":{"s":"Orbital inclination"}},{"k":"inclination_lab","v":{"s":"🌍 Inclination: which world you fly over"}},{"k":"inclination_why","v":{"s":"Why 51.6° and not something lower? Baikonur sits at about 46°N, and a rocket cannot reach an inclination below its own launch latitude without an expensive change of plane afterwards — so from there, nothing under 46° is on the table. The usual explanation given for the extra few degrees is what lies under the flight path: a launch due east from Baikonur would drop its spent stages over populated land and toward the Chinese border. What the partnership settled on passes over every continent except Antarctica and about {pct}% of Earth’s land. Its northern limit runs almost exactly through London at 51.5°N — Berlin, at 52.5°N, never passes directly underneath."}}],"id":609}},{"k":"arithmetic","v":{"o":[{"k":"selected","v":{"s":" selected."}},{"k":"arithmetic_strategy_challenge","v":{"s":"Arithmetic strategy challenge"}},{"k":"correct_the_exact_answer_and_estimate_","v":{"s":"Correct. The exact answer and estimate are consistent."}},{"k":"your_exact_answer_is_correct_revise_th","v":{"s":"Your exact answer is correct. Revise the estimate so it is close enough to check reasonableness."}},{"k":"not_yet_compare_your_answer_with_your_","v":{"s":"Not yet. Compare your answer with your estimate and try a strategy."}},{"k":"retrying_missed_problem","v":{"s":"Retrying a missed practice problem."}},{"k":"arithmetic_error_detective","v":{"s":"Arithmetic error detective"}},{"k":"correct_diagnosis","v":{"s":"Correct diagnosis. "}},{"k":"that_is_not_the_key_error_read_the_wor","v":{"s":"That is not the key error. Read the work one place at a time."}},{"k":"retrying_missed_diagnosis","v":{"s":"Retrying a missed diagnosis."}},{"k":"arithmetic_word_problem","v":{"s":"Arithmetic word problem"}},{"k":"correct_the_operation_and_context_answ","v":{"s":"Correct. The operation and context answer both fit the story."}},{"k":"reconsider_which_operation_represents_","v":{"s":"Reconsider which operation represents the relationship in the story."}},{"k":"the_operation_fits_recheck_how_the_con","v":{"s":"The operation fits. Recheck how the context changes the numerical result."}},{"k":"chosen_for_this_story","v":{"s":" chosen for this story."}},{"k":"opening_a_missed_story_for_another_try","v":{"s":"Opening a missed story for another try."}},{"k":"choose_an_operation","v":{"s":"Choose an operation"}},{"k":"think_how_many_groups_of","v":{"s":"Think: how many groups of "}},{"k":"fit_in","v":{"s":" fit in "}},{"k":"place_value_table_and_text_alternative","v":{"s":"Place-value table and text alternative"}},{"k":"place_value_alignment_for","v":{"s":"Place-value alignment for "}},{"k":"and","v":{"s":" and "}},{"k":"number","v":{"s":"Number"}},{"k":"second","v":{"s":" second"}},{"k":"place_value_decomposition"